// Rounds Codex Resident Mode - MASTER dataset (all specialties merged for deployment)
// 1308 entries across 23 specialties. Single RES_DATA array; app filters by .sec.
// Medical-QA corrections: cardiology + thoracic (pneumothorax/aortic/mesothelioma) + gs (breast axilla SOUND/INSEMA/SENOMAC).
const RES_DATA=[{"id":"em-cardiac-arrest","name":"Cardiac Arrest & Post-Arrest Care","sec":"em","present":["Unresponsive, apneic/agonal, pulseless; may be witnessed collapse or found down","Shockable (VF/pVT) vs non-shockable (asystole/PEA) determines pathway","Consider context: overdose, trauma, drowning, hyperkalemia (dialysis pt)"],"dx":["Rhythm check q2min during high-quality CPR (rate 100-120, depth 5-6cm, full recoil, minimal pauses)","ETCO2: <10 mmHg suggests poor CPR quality; abrupt rise ~40 signals ROSC","POCUS during pulse check for tamponade, PE (RV strain), hypovolemia — keep pauses <10s","Hunt reversible Hs & Ts: hypoxia, hypovolemia, H+ (acidosis), hyper/hypoK, hypothermia; tension PTX, tamponade, toxins, thrombosis (MI/PE)"],"tx":["VF/pVT: defibrillate 200J biphasic immediately, CPR 2min between shocks","Epinephrine 1mg IV/IO q3-5min (immediately in non-shockable; after 2nd shock in shockable)","Amiodarone 300mg then 150mg (or lidocaine 1-1.5mg/kg) for refractory VF/pVT","Treat cause: calcium+insulin/glucose+bicarb for hyperK; needle/finger thoracostomy for tension PTX; lytics for suspected massive PE","Post-ROSC: 12-lead ECG (cath lab if STEMI), MAP ≥65, SpO2 92-98%, avoid hyperoxia, temperature control 32-37.5°C, EEG if not following commands"],"pearls":["Compressions and early defibrillation are the only interventions proven to improve neuro-intact survival","Don't call it on hypothermic arrest — warm to ≥32°C first","Refractory VF: change pad vector, consider dual sequential defibrillation and esmolol"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA ACLS Guidelines (2020, updated)","u":"https://cpr.heart.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Cardiac Arrest","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/cardiacarrest.html"}]},{"id":"em-acs","name":"Chest Pain / Acute Coronary Syndrome","sec":"em","present":["Pressure-like substernal pain ± radiation to arm/jaw, diaphoresis, dyspnea, nausea","Atypical in women, elderly, diabetics: fatigue, epigastric pain, isolated dyspnea","Always screen the lethal six: ACS, dissection, PE, tension PTX, tamponade, esophageal rupture"],"dx":["ECG within 10 min of arrival; repeat q15-30min if ongoing pain — evolving changes matter","STEMI: ST elevation ≥1mm in 2 contiguous leads (V2-V3: ≥2mm men/≥1.5mm women); know equivalents (posterior MI, Sgarbossa, de Winter, Wellens)","hs-troponin 0/1-2h rule-out algorithm; single undetectable + nonischemic ECG + low HEART score can discharge","HEART score risk-stratifies: 0-3 low (~1-2% MACE), 4-6 moderate, ≥7 high"],"tx":["Aspirin 324mg chewed for all suspected ACS (NNT ~42 for mortality in MI)","STEMI: activate cath lab — door-to-balloon <90 min (or lytics <30 min if PCI >120 min away)","NSTEMI: anticoagulate (heparin), P2Y12 per cardiology, early invasive if high-risk (GRACE)","Nitroglycerin for ongoing pain (avoid in RV infarct/recent PDE5i); morphine sparingly","High-intensity statin; beta-blocker within 24h if no shock/heart failure"],"pearls":["A normal initial troponin does not exclude ACS — timing from symptom onset matters","New LBBB alone is no longer a STEMI equivalent; apply Sgarbossa criteria","Right-sided leads (V4R) in inferior STEMI — RV infarct is preload-dependent"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC Chest Pain Guideline (2021)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Heart Attack","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/heartattack.html"}]},{"id":"em-sepsis","name":"Sepsis & Septic Shock","sec":"em","present":["Suspected infection + organ dysfunction: altered mentation, hypotension, tachypnea, oliguria, mottling","qSOFA ≥2 (RR≥22, SBP≤100, AMS) flags risk; SIRS remains sensitive for screening","Septic shock: vasopressors required for MAP ≥65 + lactate >2 despite fluids"],"dx":["Lactate at presentation and repeat if >2 (clearance guides resuscitation)","Two sets blood cultures BEFORE antibiotics (don't delay abx >45min for this)","Source hunt: CXR, UA, skin/soft tissue exam, abdominal imaging, LP if meningitis suspected","SOFA score defines organ dysfunction (Sepsis-3)"],"tx":["Broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour of recognition — each hour of delay increases mortality in shock","30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid for hypotension or lactate ≥4, then dynamic reassessment (passive leg raise, pulse pressure variation) — not blind boluses","Norepinephrine first-line vasopressor, target MAP ≥65; add vasopressin 0.03 U/min if escalating","Hydrocortisone 200mg/day if refractory shock on pressors","Source control within 6-12h: drain abscess, remove infected line, decompress obstructed urinary tract/biliary tree"],"pearls":["Normal blood pressure doesn't exclude sepsis — young patients compensate until they crash","Cryptic shock: normotensive with lactate ≥4 carries near-shock mortality","Push-dose or peripheral norepi is acceptable early — don't let a central line delay pressors"],"refs":[{"t":"Surviving Sepsis Campaign Guidelines (2021)","u":"https://www.sccm.org"},{"t":"CDC: Sepsis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/sepsis"}]},{"id":"em-abd-pain","name":"Undifferentiated Abdominal Pain","sec":"em","present":["Location-driven ddx: RUQ (biliary, hepatitis), epigastric (PUD, pancreatitis, ACS!), RLQ (appy, TOA, torsion), LLQ (diverticulitis), diffuse (obstruction, mesenteric ischemia, DKA, perforation)","Peritonitis: rigidity, rebound, pain with cough/heel-tap — surgical until proven otherwise","Elderly present late and atypically — lower threshold for imaging"],"dx":["Every reproductive-age female: urine HCG before imaging decisions","Labs: CBC, BMP, LFTs, lipase, UA; lactate if ischemia/sepsis concern","CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast is the ED workhorse; US first for RUQ and pelvic/testicular pathology","Upright CXR for free air if perforation suspected (misses up to 20% — CT far more sensitive)"],"tx":["Analgesia early — opioids do NOT mask surgical findings (myth debunked)","Antiemetics, NPO, IV fluids while workup proceeds","Peritonitis/free air/ischemia: broad-spectrum abx + emergent surgical consult","Serial exams and return precautions for the well-appearing nondiagnostic workup — up to 25% leave without a specific diagnosis"],"pearls":["Pain out of proportion to exam in an elderly patient with AF = mesenteric ischemia until excluded (CTA, not routine CT)","Always examine hernial orifices and testicles — missed incarcerated hernia and torsion are classic errors","Epigastric pain + risk factors: get the ECG — inferior MI masquerades as 'gastritis'"],"refs":[{"t":"ACEP Clinical Policies","u":"https://www.acep.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Abdominal Pain","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/abdominalpain.html"}]},{"id":"em-cap","name":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia","sec":"em","present":["Fever, productive cough, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea; focal crackles/egophony","Elderly: confusion or falls without fever — check a temperature and an X-ray","Post-influenza deterioration suggests staph (incl. MRSA) superinfection"],"dx":["CXR infiltrate confirms (may lag in dehydration); POCUS/CT more sensitive","CURB-65 (confusion, urea>7, RR≥30, BP<90/60, ≥65y) or PSI for disposition: 0-1 outpatient, ≥2 admit, ≥3 consider ICU","Severe CAP (IDSA/ATS major criteria): mechanical ventilation or septic shock → ICU","Blood cultures/sputum for severe or MRSA/Pseudomonas risk; legionella & pneumococcal urinary antigens when severe; procalcitonin does not gate abx in the ED"],"tx":["Outpatient healthy: amoxicillin 1g TID or doxycycline; comorbidities: amox-clav + macrolide, or respiratory FQ","Inpatient non-severe: ceftriaxone + azithromycin (or respiratory FQ monotherapy)","Severe: beta-lactam + macrolide; add vancomycin/linezolid if MRSA risk, antipseudomonal beta-lactam if Pseudomonas risk (prior isolation, bronchiectasis)","First abx dose in the ED for admitted patients; steroids for severe CAP with high inflammatory markers (CAPE COD trial benefit)"],"pearls":["Failure to improve by 72h: think empyema (get the US), obstruction, or wrong bug (TB, PJP, fungal)","A 'normal' CXR early doesn't exclude pneumonia in the toxic dehydrated patient","Aspiration events need coverage only when a true pneumonia declares — chemical pneumonitis clears without abx"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA/ATS CAP Guidelines (2019)","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pneumonia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/pneumonia.html"}]},{"id":"em-asthma","name":"Asthma Exacerbation","sec":"em","present":["Dyspnea, wheeze, cough, chest tightness; speech in phrases→words tracks severity","Severe: accessory muscles, tripoding, silent chest (ominous), inability to lie flat","Risk flags: prior intubation/ICU, ≥2 hospitalizations/year, current oral steroids"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis — peak flow vs personal best (<50% severe) when obtainable","Normalizing pCO2 on gas in a tachypneic asthmatic = fatigue = impending failure","CXR only for focal findings, fever, or failure to respond (r/o PTX, pneumonia)"],"tx":["Albuterol continuous or stacked (2.5-5mg neb q20min ×3) + ipratropium 0.5mg ×3 (ED evidence for combo)","Systemic steroids within 1h: prednisone 40-60mg PO (IV methylpred only if can't take PO)","Severe: magnesium sulfate 2g IV over 20 min; epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg IM if peri-arrest/anaphylaxis overlap","NIV trial cautiously; intubate for exhaustion — ketamine induction, large ETT, then LOW rate (6-8), long expiratory time, permissive hypercapnia; watch for breath-stacking/auto-PEEP","Hypotension on the vent: disconnect circuit and press the chest — auto-PEEP until proven otherwise; then check tension PTX"],"pearls":["The quiet asthmatic is the dying asthmatic — no wheeze can mean no air movement","Discharge with steroids (5-7 days), SABA, inhaler technique check, and early follow-up — most bounce-backs are treatment-plan failures","Ventilated asthmatics die from barotrauma and auto-PEEP, not hypercapnia — tolerate the CO2"],"refs":[{"t":"GINA Report (updated annually)","u":"https://ginasthma.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Asthma","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/asthma.html"}]},{"id":"em-copd","name":"COPD Exacerbation","sec":"em","present":["Increased dyspnea, sputum volume, and/or sputum purulence (cardinal symptoms) in a known/suspected COPD patient","Look for triggers: viral URI (most common), pneumonia, PE (consider in 'exacerbation' without trigger), heart failure","Severe: accessory muscle use, paradoxical breathing, cyanosis, AMS (CO2 narcosis)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; ABG/VBG for severity (acute respiratory acidosis pH<7.35 with pCO2>45 → NIV territory)","CXR to exclude pneumonia/PTX; ECG + troponin/BNP when cardiac overlap suspected","D-dimer/CTA when no clear trigger — occult PE found in a meaningful minority of 'exacerbations'"],"tx":["Titrate O2 to SpO2 88-92% — over-oxygenation worsens hypercapnia (V/Q + Haldane), but never withhold O2 from the hypoxic patient","Bronchodilators: albuterol + ipratropium nebs, repeat/stack","Prednisone 40mg ×5 days (REDUCE trial — 5 days noninferior)","Antibiotics if purulent sputum or ventilatory support: azithromycin, doxycycline, or amox-clav ×5 days","NIV (BiPAP) for pH<7.35/pCO2>45: reduces intubation and mortality — the strongest-evidence intervention; intubate for NIV failure, arrest, or inability to protect airway"],"pearls":["BiPAP early, not as a last resort — reassess gas at 1h; failure to improve pH predicts intubation","'COPD exacerbation' with clear lungs and no cough: hunt for PE","Check inhaler technique and arrange steroids+abx to complete outpatient course on every discharge"],"refs":[{"t":"GOLD Report (updated annually)","u":"https://goldcopd.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: COPD","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/copd.html"}]},{"id":"em-adhf","name":"Acute Decompensated Heart Failure","sec":"em","present":["Dyspnea, orthopnea, PND, edema, weight gain; classify wet/dry × warm/cold","SCAPE (sympathetic crashing acute pulmonary edema): abrupt severe distress, hypertensive, flash pulmonary edema","Cold+wet (cardiogenic shock physiology) is the dangerous minority — different pathway"],"dx":["POCUS: diffuse B-lines + plethoric IVC + reduced EF is faster and better than CXR","BNP/NT-proBNP for ambiguity (obesity lowers, renal failure raises); troponin for precipitating ACS","ECG for ischemia/arrhythmia trigger; CXR for congestion and alternatives","Hunt the precipitant: FAILURE — Forgot meds, Arrhythmia/Anemia, Ischemia/Infection, Lifestyle (salt), Upregulation (pregnancy/hyperthyroid), Renal failure, Embolus"],"tx":["Warm+wet: IV loop diuretic — at least 2× total daily home dose as IV bolus (DOSE trial), furosemide-naive: 40mg IV","SCAPE: high-dose nitroglycerin (start 100+ mcg/min IV or repeated 400mcg SL) + BiPAP — turns patients around in minutes; diuretic is secondary","BiPAP for moderate-severe distress: reduces intubation","Cold+wet: small fluid challenge only if truly preload-deplete; inotropes (dobutamine/milrinone) + pressors, early cardiology/shock team","Hold/reduce beta-blocker only in shock or marked hypotension — continue in simple congestion"],"pearls":["Most ADHF is hypertensive and vasoconstricted — vasodilation, not just diuresis, is the fix","Check the creatinine but don't let it stop decongestion — worsening renal function during effective diuresis is usually benign","Ultrafiltration and admission pathways vary — know your shop's heart failure protocol"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HFSA Heart Failure Guideline (2022)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Heart Failure","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/heartfailure.html"}]},{"id":"em-afib-rvr","name":"Atrial Fibrillation with RVR","sec":"em","present":["Palpitations, dyspnea, chest discomfort, lightheadedness; irregularly irregular pulse","Ask onset time (<48h matters for cardioversion), prior episodes, anticoagulation status","Unstable = hypotension, ischemic chest pain, pulmonary edema, AMS attributable to the rhythm"],"dx":["ECG: irregularly irregular, no P waves; rate often 110-170","Hunt drivers: sepsis, PE, hyperthyroid, alcohol, ischemia, hypovolemia — RVR is often the symptom, not the disease","TSH, electrolytes (K, Mg), troponin selectively; POCUS for EF and volume status"],"tx":["Unstable: synchronized cardioversion 100-200J biphasic — do not delay for anticoagulation","Rate control: diltiazem 0.25mg/kg IV over 2min (repeat 0.35mg/kg), then drip 5-15mg/h; or metoprolol 5mg IV q5min ×3","Avoid AV-nodal blockers in pre-excited AF (wide, bizarre, very fast) — use procainamide or cardiovert","Secondary RVR (sepsis, hypovolemia): treat the cause first; nodal blockade can precipitate collapse","Rhythm control candidates (<48h onset or anticoagulated ≥3wk or TEE-cleared): electrical or pharmacologic cardioversion; start anticoagulation by CHA2DS2-VASc before discharge decisions"],"pearls":["The sick septic patient in AF-RVR needs fluids and antibiotics, not diltiazem","Borderline pressure? Push calcium first or use amiodarone; phenylephrine can support BP during dilt","New AF diagnosed in the ED: arrange anticoagulation and cardiology follow-up — stroke prevention is the ED's lasting contribution"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HRS AF Guideline (2023)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Atrial Fibrillation","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/atrialfibrillation.html"}]},{"id":"em-svt","name":"SVT & Tachyarrhythmias","sec":"em","present":["Abrupt-onset regular palpitations, chest fluttering, lightheadedness, anxiety; rates often 150-220","AVNRT most common; ask about prior episodes and terminating maneuvers","Wide-complex tachycardia is VT until proven otherwise — especially with structural heart disease"],"dx":["ECG: regular narrow-complex tachycardia, P waves buried/retrograde","Regular narrow at exactly ~150: think 2:1 atrial flutter — look for sawtooth","Wide-complex: age >35, known CAD/CHF, AV dissociation, fusion/capture beats all favor VT; when unsure, treat as VT","Post-conversion ECG: hunt for WPW (short PR, delta wave) — changes disposition and drug choices"],"tx":["Stable narrow SVT: modified Valsalva (REVERT: 15s strain then supine + leg raise, ~43% conversion), then adenosine 6mg rapid flush → 12mg","Adenosine warning counseling ('brief awful feeling'); record continuous rhythm strip during administration","Alternatives: diltiazem or metoprolol IV","Unstable any tachyarrhythmia: synchronized cardioversion (SVT/flutter often converts at 50-100J)","Stable VT: procainamide (PROCAMIO favors it) or amiodarone 150mg over 10 min; pre-excited AF: procainamide or cardioversion — never AV nodal blockers"],"pearls":["Adenosine unmasks flutter when it 'fails' — the transient AV block reveals sawtooth waves","Never give verapamil/diltiazem to wide-complex tachycardia of uncertain type","First-episode SVT converts and feels well: ECG for WPW, then discharge with EP referral is reasonable"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HRS SVT Guideline (2015)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Arrhythmia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/arrhythmia.html"}]},{"id":"em-brady","name":"Bradycardia & AV Blocks","sec":"em","present":["Fatigue, lightheadedness, syncope/presyncope, dyspnea; may be asymptomatic incidental finding","Unstable: hypotension, AMS, ischemic chest pain, acute heart failure attributable to rate","Med list is half the diagnosis: beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, digoxin, clonidine, antiarrhythmics"],"dx":["ECG classification: sinus brady, Mobitz I (progressive PR, benign-ish), Mobitz II (dropped beats without PR change — dangerous), 3rd-degree (AV dissociation)","Check K+ (hyperkalemia mimics/causes blocks), digoxin level when relevant, troponin for inferior MI (RCA supplies the AV node)","Lyme titers in endemic areas with new heart block in the young"],"tx":["Unstable: atropine 1mg IV q3-5min (max 3mg) — often fails in Mobitz II/3rd degree and heart-transplant patients","Transcutaneous pacing next: sedate, confirm electrical AND mechanical capture (pulse with paced beats)","Epinephrine 2-10 mcg/min or dopamine 5-20 mcg/kg/min as bridge infusions","Overdose-driven: calcium + glucagon (beta-blocker), calcium + high-dose insulin euglycemia (CCB), digoxin-Fab","Mobitz II and complete heart block: cardiology for transvenous pacer regardless of current stability"],"pearls":["Mobitz I with good pressure can usually be watched; Mobitz II can degenerate without warning — pads on and cards called","Bradycardia + hypotension + hyperkalemia (BRASH syndrome): treat the K+ and give fluids/pressors — pacing often unnecessary","Inferior STEMI with heart block usually recovers with reperfusion — get them to the lab"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HRS Bradycardia Guideline (2018)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Bradycardia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/slowheartrate.html"}]},{"id":"em-stroke","name":"Acute Ischemic Stroke & TIA","sec":"em","present":["Sudden focal deficit: face droop, arm drift, speech disturbance (BE-FAST adds balance/eyes)","Establish LAST KNOWN WELL precisely — it drives every treatment decision","Mimics: hypoglycemia (check glucose immediately), seizure/Todd's, migraine, Bell's palsy"],"dx":["Non-contrast head CT to exclude hemorrhage; CTA head/neck for LVO in potential thrombectomy candidates; CT perfusion for late-window selection","NIHSS documents severity and tracks change","Glucose, coags/anticoagulant history, ECG (AF), troponin","TIA: ABCD2 + vessel imaging; carotid stenosis and AF are the actionable findings"],"tx":["Thrombolysis (alteplase 0.9mg/kg or tenecteplase 0.25mg/kg) within 4.5h of LKW if no exclusions; BP must be <185/110 pre-lytic (nicardipine/labetalol/clevidipine)","LVO: endovascular thrombectomy up to 24h with favorable imaging (DAWN/DEFUSE-3) — transfer fast if not a thrombectomy center","Not lytic-eligible: aspirin 325mg (after CT); high-risk TIA/minor stroke: DAPT ×21 days (CHANCE/POINT)","Permissive hypertension to 220/120 if no lytics; treat glucose 140-180, temperature, and swallow-screen before PO","Post-lytic: neuro checks, BP <180/105, no antithrombotics ×24h"],"pearls":["Hypoglycemia is the mandatory 30-second rule-out before stroke activation proceeds","Aggressive BP lowering below guideline thresholds extends the infarct — resist the urge","Posterior circulation strokes (vertigo, diplopia, ataxia) are missed most — HINTS beats early MRI in continuous vertigo"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Acute Ischemic Stroke Guideline (2019)","u":"https://www.stroke.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Stroke","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/stroke.html"}]},{"id":"em-seizure","name":"Seizure & Status Epilepticus","sec":"em","present":["Generalized tonic-clonic activity, postictal confusion; tongue biting (lateral) and incontinence support true seizure","Status epilepticus: ≥5 min continuous or recurrent without return to baseline — treat aggressively","First seizure vs known epilepsy (level check, missed doses) vs provoked (withdrawal, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, eclampsia)"],"dx":["Immediate glucose; BMP (Na!), Mg, Ca, pregnancy test, anticonvulsant levels, tox screen","CT head for first seizure, trauma, anticoagulation, focal features, or failure to wake","LP if febrile/immunocompromised/meningismus; EEG for persistent AMS — nonconvulsive status hides in 'postictal' patients","Prolactin and lactate (early) can support true seizure vs syncope/PNES"],"tx":["ABCs, position lateral, O2, don't force anything in the mouth","Benzos FIRST and adequately dosed: lorazepam 4mg IV (repeat ×1) or midazolam 10mg IM if no access (RAMPART)","Second line (ESETT — all equivalent): levetiracetam 60mg/kg (max 4.5g), fosphenytoin 20mg PE/kg, or valproate 40mg/kg","Refractory: RSI (propofol/ketamine; avoid long-acting paralytic masking seizures) + continuous infusion (propofol/midazolam) with EEG","Special causes: eclampsia → magnesium 4-6g; INH overdose → pyridoxine; hyponatremia → 3% saline 100mL boluses"],"pearls":["Underdosed benzodiazepines are the most common error in status — give the full weight-based dose","The patient who seized and won't wake up needs an EEG, not just observation","First unprovoked seizure, normal exam/imaging/labs: most go home without meds but with driving counseling and neurology follow-up"],"refs":[{"t":"AES Status Epilepticus Guideline","u":"https://www.aesnet.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Seizures","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/seizures.html"}]},{"id":"em-syncope","name":"Syncope","sec":"em","present":["Abrupt transient LOC with loss of tone and rapid complete recovery — anything slower or incomplete is not simple syncope","Reflex (prodrome, trigger, standing), orthostatic (positional, volume loss, meds), cardiac (exertional, no prodrome, palpitations, family hx sudden death)","Red flags: exertional onset, chest pain, dyspnea, back pain (dissection/AAA), GI bleed, severe headache"],"dx":["ECG on everyone — hunt: long/short QT, Brugada, WPW, HCM (LVH + deep septal Q), ARVC (epsilon), heart block, ischemia","Orthostatic vitals, hemoglobin, glucose, pregnancy test; troponin/BNP only when cardiac cause suspected","POCUS if concern for tamponade, PE, AAA, severe AS","Risk scores (Canadian Syncope Risk Score) support judgment; convulsive syncope commonly mimics seizure"],"tx":["Reflex/orthostatic with normal ECG: reassure, volume, education — no routine imaging or admission","Cardiac-suspicious: monitored bed, serial ECGs, cardiology; structural cause on POCUS drives disposition","Fix the found cause: pace the block, resuscitate the GI bleed, PE pathway, replace volume","Older adults on rate/BP meds: review the med list — polypharmacy is a leading cause"],"pearls":["Syncope while supine or during exertion is cardiac until proven otherwise","A normal ECG and unremarkable history make dangerous causes unlikely — most young reflex syncope needs nothing further","Ask about the 3 P's (posture, provocation, prodrome) — the history makes the diagnosis in most cases"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA/HRS Syncope Guideline (2017)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Fainting","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/fainting.html"}]},{"id":"em-vertigo","name":"Dizziness & Vertigo","sec":"em","present":["Clarify the timing-and-trigger pattern, not the word 'dizzy': episodic positional (BPPV), continuous spontaneous (vestibular neuritis vs posterior stroke), episodic spontaneous (Meniere, TIA)","BPPV: seconds-long spells with head position change","Neuritis: continuous vertigo, nausea, gait unsteadiness over hours-days, often post-viral"],"dx":["HINTS exam ONLY in continuous vertigo with nystagmus: normal head impulse, direction-changing nystagmus, or skew deviation = central → MRI","HINTS (done correctly) outperforms early MRI for posterior stroke; DWI-MRI misses up to 20% in first 48h","Dix-Hallpike diagnoses posterior canal BPPV (upbeat-torsional nystagmus, latency, fatigability)","Walk every dizzy patient — inability to walk unaided is central until proven otherwise; screen for the 'deadly D's': diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, dysmetria"],"tx":["BPPV: Epley maneuver at bedside (NNT ~2-3) — teach self-Epley; avoid meclizine (prolongs central compensation)","Vestibular neuritis: short steroid taper sometimes used; brief symptomatic control (antihistamine/antiemetic ≤3 days), then vestibular rehab","Central features/HINTS-central: stroke pathway — MRI, neurology, admit","Meniere: symptomatic control, ENT referral, salt restriction counseling"],"pearls":["The most dangerous phrase in the chart is 'dizziness, likely peripheral' without a documented exam","Head impulse test is only meaningful in the continuously vertiginous patient — a normal (no-saccade) test there is BAD (central)","Isolated vertigo can be the only symptom of a cerebellar stroke, especially with vascular risk factors"],"refs":[{"t":"GRACE-3 Guideline: Acute Dizziness (SAEM 2023)","u":"https://www.saem.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Dizziness and Vertigo","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/dizzinessandvertigo.html"}]},{"id":"em-headache-sah","name":"Headache & SAH Rule-Out","sec":"em","present":["Most are primary (migraine, tension, cluster) — the job is finding the deadly minority","Thunderclap (maximal <1 min): SAH, RCVS, dissection, venous thrombosis — always investigate","Red flags (SNNOOP10): systemic signs, neuro deficits, new >50y, onset thunderclap, pattern change, positional, pregnancy, immunosuppression/cancer, anticoagulation"],"dx":["Suspected SAH: non-contrast CT — within 6h of onset on modern scanners, sensitivity approaches 100% (read by attending radiologist)","Beyond 6h: CT then LP (xanthochromia, elevated opening pressure) or CTA per shared decision — Ottawa SAH rule is highly sensitive for who needs any workup","Venous thrombosis (postpartum, OCPs, dehydration): CT/MR venogram; temporal arteritis (>50, jaw claudication): ESR/CRP → steroids before biopsy","Papilledema on fundoscopy/POCUS optic nerve sheath: think elevated ICP, IIH, mass"],"tx":["Migraine cocktail: prochlorperazine or metoclopramide 10mg IV + diphenhydramine 25mg + ketorolac; fluids; dark quiet room — avoid opioids","Refractory: valproate, magnesium, dexamethasone (reduces 72h recurrence), nerve blocks","Confirmed SAH: neurosurgery emergently, SBP <160 (nicardipine), nimodipine 60mg q4h (delayed vasospasm), analgesia, HOB 30°","Cluster: high-flow O2 12-15L NRB + sumatriptan SC"],"pearls":["'Worst headache of life' earns a workup even with a normal neuro exam","A responding headache does not exclude SAH — sentinel bleeds improve with analgesia too","Dexamethasone before discharge cuts migraine bounce-backs"],"refs":[{"t":"ACEP Clinical Policy: Acute Headache (2019)","u":"https://www.acep.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Headache","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/headache.html"}]},{"id":"em-tbi","name":"Head Trauma / TBI & Concussion","sec":"em","present":["Spectrum: concussion (GCS 15, transient symptoms) → severe TBI (GCS ≤8)","Ask mechanism, LOC, amnesia, vomiting, anticoagulants/antiplatelets, age","Deterioration pattern matters: lucid interval then decline = epidural until proven otherwise"],"dx":["Canadian CT Head Rule (GCS<15 at 2h, suspected open/depressed fracture, basilar skull signs, vomiting ≥2, age ≥65, dangerous mechanism, amnesia >30min) — apply to minor head injury","ALL anticoagulated head injuries: scan; consider delayed scan/observation for DOACs with initial negative CT","Basilar skull fracture signs: raccoon eyes, Battle sign, hemotympanum, CSF rhinorrhea","PECARN for children — observation over radiation when low-risk"],"tx":["Severe TBI: prevent secondary injury — avoid hypoxia (SpO2 ≥94) and hypotension (SBP ≥110 per BTF); single episodes worsen mortality dramatically","Herniation signs (blown pupil, Cushing reflex): hypertonic saline 3% or mannitol 1g/kg, brief hyperventilation to pCO2 ~30-35 as bridge, emergent neurosurgery","Reverse anticoagulation: warfarin → 4-factor PCC + vitamin K; DOAC → PCC or specific reversal (andexanet/idarucizumab)","Concussion: symptom-guided rest 24-48h then graded return; no same-day return to play; written discharge instructions to a sober adult","TXA within 3h for moderate TBI reasonable (CRASH-3 subgroup)"],"pearls":["One episode of hypotension can double severe-TBI mortality — treat the pressure like a code","Elderly on anticoagulants with 'trivial' mechanisms harbor significant bleeds — image liberally","Post-concussive patients recover faster with early light activity than strict cocooning"],"refs":[{"t":"Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://braintrauma.org"},{"t":"CDC HEADS UP: Concussion","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/headsup"}]},{"id":"em-cspine","name":"Cervical Spine Injury & Trauma Evaluation","sec":"em","present":["Blunt trauma: apply ATLS primary survey (ABCDE) before anything else — airway with c-spine control, breathing, circulation, disability, exposure","Midline neck tenderness, focal deficits, distracting injury, intoxication change clearance pathway","High-risk mechanisms: axial load (diving), high-speed MVC, fall >3ft/5 stairs, age ≥65"],"dx":["Clinical clearance by NEXUS (no midline tenderness, no deficit, normal alertness, no intoxication, no distracting injury) or Canadian C-Spine Rule (adds mechanism + rotation test)","Imaging when indicated: CT c-spine (plain films are obsolete for adults)","Persistent midline pain/deficit with negative CT: MRI + spine consult; maintain precautions","Obtunded patient: modern evidence supports collar removal after a truly negative high-quality CT (institutional practice varies)","eFAST in the unstable blunt trauma patient; CT pan-scan per mechanism"],"tx":["Immobilize suspected injuries; treat the whole trauma patient — hemorrhage kills faster than the spine","Unstable fracture/cord injury: spine surgery consult, maintain MAP 85-90 (neurogenic shock: fluids + norepinephrine)","Neurogenic shock = hypotension + bradycardia + warm skin (vs hemorrhagic: cold + tachycardic) — but assume bleeding first","Steroids for cord injury are no longer routinely recommended","Document a dated/timed neuro exam — it is the baseline every later decision references"],"pearls":["The collar is a treatment with complications (aspiration, ICP, ulcers) — clear the spine as soon as clinically possible","Ligamentous injury hides behind normal CTs in the symptomatic patient — respect persistent midline tenderness","Elderly ground-level falls break C1/C2 — image liberally over 65"],"refs":[{"t":"ATLS (ACS Committee on Trauma)","u":"https://www.facs.org"},{"t":"EAST Trauma Guidelines","u":"https://www.east.org"}]},{"id":"em-back-pain","name":"Low Back Pain & Cauda Equina","sec":"em","present":["Most is benign musculoskeletal; the screen is for the dangerous few","Red flags: saddle anesthesia, urinary retention/incontinence, bilateral leg symptoms (cauda equina); fever/IVDU/recent instrumentation (epidural abscess); cancer history + new pain (mets); age >50 + weight loss; trauma; anticoagulation (epidural hematoma)","Aortic causes masquerade as back pain: tearing pain, syncope, pulsatile mass — think AAA/dissection in the >50 vasculopath"],"dx":["No red flags: NO imaging — evidence-based and guideline-backed; imaging correlates poorly with symptoms and drives harm","Cauda equina suspicion: post-void residual (>200-300mL concerning) + EMERGENT MRI whole spine; if MRI unavailable, transfer","Suspected epidural abscess: ESR/CRP (sensitive), blood cultures, MRI with contrast","Suspected AAA: bedside aorta ultrasound in minutes"],"tx":["Benign LBP: NSAIDs + activity as tolerated (bed rest worsens outcomes), heat, reassurance with explicit return precautions; avoid opioids/benzos; no routine muscle relaxant superiority","Cauda equina/cord compression: dexamethasone 10mg IV (malignant compression), emergent spine surgery consult — decompression within 24-48h preserves function","Epidural abscess: abx after cultures (vanc + ceftriaxone/cefepime) + emergent spine consult","Radiculopathy without deficits: conservative care 6 weeks before considering imaging/referral"],"pearls":["Ask every back pain patient two questions: any trouble urinating, any numbness in the saddle area — document the answers","Epidural abscess is missed on first visit in a majority of cases — fever + spine pain + risk factor = MRI","The 'benign exam' does not rule out AAA — the aorta ultrasound takes 90 seconds"],"refs":[{"t":"ACP Low Back Pain Guideline","u":"https://www.acponline.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Back Pain","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/backpain.html"}]},{"id":"em-fractures","name":"Fractures & Dislocations","sec":"em","present":["Deformity, swelling, focal bony tenderness, loss of function after trauma","Describe like a consultant: bone, location (proximal/mid/distal third), pattern (transverse/oblique/spiral/comminuted), displacement/angulation, intra-articular extension, open vs closed","High-risk occult injuries: scaphoid (snuffbox), radial head (posterior fat pad), hip in the elderly (MRI if XR negative but can't bear weight)"],"dx":["X-ray with joint above and below; CT for intra-articular detail and occult injury","Neurovascular exam BEFORE and AFTER every manipulation — document","Named nerve checks: axillary (shoulder dislocation), radial (humeral shaft), median (supracondylar/distal radius), peroneal (knee/fibular head)","Knee dislocation (often self-reduced): ABI ≥0.9 or CTA — popliteal artery injury is limb-threatening"],"tx":["Analgesia early; hematoma blocks, intra-articular blocks, regional anesthesia, procedural sedation as needed","Reduce dislocations promptly (shoulder, patella, ankle, hip) — the neurovascularly compromised limb is reduced NOW, not after imaging","Open fractures: IV cefazolin 2g within 1 hour (add gram-negative coverage for severe contamination), tetanus, saline-moistened dressing, splint, urgent ortho","Splint in position of function with post-reduction films; never circumferential-cast acute injuries","Compartment syndrome (pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch, tense compartment): remove constriction, emergent fasciotomy consult — pressures support, don't replace, clinical judgment"],"pearls":["The 6 P's of compartment syndrome appear late — pain out of proportion IS the early finding","Snuffbox tenderness with a normal X-ray still gets a thumb spica and follow-up — scaphoid nonunion is a career-long problem","Always image the joint above and below a long-bone fracture; Maisonneuve hides at the proximal fibula"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Clinical Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Fractures","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/fractures.html"}]},{"id":"em-lacerations","name":"Lacerations & Wound Management","sec":"em","present":["Assess mechanism (crush vs sharp), contamination, time since injury, tetanus status, hand dominance for upper-extremity wounds","Examine BEFORE anesthesia: distal neurovascular status, tendon function through full ROM (a partially lacerated tendon works — until it ruptures)","High-risk wounds: bites, plantar puncture through shoes (Pseudomonas), fight bite over MCP (assume joint violation), high-pressure injection injuries (surgical emergency despite benign appearance)"],"dx":["Explore to the base of every wound in a bloodless field; X-ray for radiopaque FB or suspected fracture, US for radiolucent FB (wood)","Irrigation is the intervention that prevents infection: 250-1000mL saline or potable tap water (equivalent evidence) with pressure","Lidocaine max 4.5mg/kg plain / 7mg/kg with epi; buffered + warmed + slow injection hurts less; epinephrine in digits is safe in healthy patients"],"tx":["Primary closure timeframes (guides, not laws): face up to ~24h, other sites ~12-18h if clean; heavily contaminated/bite wounds often heal by secondary intention or delayed closure","Material/removal: face 6-0 (5 days), scalp staples (7-10d), trunk/extremity 4-0/5-0 (10-14d over joints); absorbables acceptable in children's faces; skin glue for low-tension clean edges","Prophylactic antibiotics only for: bites to hand/face, through-and-through oral wounds, open fractures, heavily contaminated wounds, immunocompromised — routine wounds get irrigation, not abx (amox-clav when indicated)","Tetanus: Tdap if >5y (dirty) or >10y (clean); add TIG for dirty wounds with unknown/incomplete primary series","Fight bites: X-ray (tooth/fracture), irrigate joint concern in OR, amox-clav, hand surgery consult — never primarily close"],"pearls":["Irrigation volume and pressure prevent more infections than any antibiotic","Document tendon exam through full range of motion — the 'works fine' partial laceration is a lawsuit classic","Wounds heal from side to side, not end to end — a gaping clean wound closed late still beats an infected one closed early"],"refs":[{"t":"ACEP Wound Management Resources","u":"https://www.acep.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Wounds and Injuries","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/woundsandinjuries.html"}]},{"id":"em-burns","name":"Burns & Inhalation Injury","sec":"em","present":["Depth: superficial (red, painful, no blisters), partial-thickness (blisters, wet, very painful), full-thickness (leathery, insensate)","TBSA by rule of 9s (adult) or palm-of-patient ≈1%; only partial + full thickness count","Inhalation red flags: enclosed-space fire, facial burns, singed nasal hairs, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness/stridor — airway can occlude over hours"],"dx":["Carboxyhemoglobin level (pulse ox lies in CO poisoning); lactate for cyanide (house fires: elevated lactate >8-10 suggests it)","Serial airway assessment; early nasopharyngoscopy/laryngoscopy when inhalation suspected","Electrical burns: ECG, CK (rhabdo), deep injury exceeds surface appearance"],"tx":["Stop the burning, remove jewelry/clothing, cool water briefly (no ice), then keep the patient WARM — hypothermia kills burn patients","Intubate EARLY for airway burns with progressive symptoms — delayed airways become impossible; use an uncut large ETT","Parkland: 4mL/kg × %TBSA lactated Ringer's (half over first 8h from burn time) for ≥20% TBSA — titrate to urine output 0.5mL/kg/h; over-resuscitation causes compartment syndromes","CO poisoning: 100% O2 (halves CO half-life); hyperbarics for severe cases; cyanide toxicity: hydroxocobalamin 5g IV","Circumferential full-thickness burns compromising perfusion/ventilation: escharotomy","Dress partial-thickness with topical antimicrobial (bacitracin/silver); tetanus for everyone; transfer per ABA criteria (>10% TBSA partial, any significant full-thickness, face/hands/feet/genitalia/joints, electrical, chemical, inhalation)"],"pearls":["The pulse ox reads 100% while carboxyhemoglobin poisons the patient — order the co-oximetry","Fluid formulas are starting points; the Foley is the resuscitation monitor","Pain management is grossly underdosed in burns — partial-thickness burns are among the most painful injuries in medicine"],"refs":[{"t":"American Burn Association Guidelines","u":"https://ameriburn.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Burns","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/burns.html"}]},{"id":"em-appendicitis","name":"Appendicitis","sec":"em","present":["Classic march: periumbilical pain → anorexia/nausea → migration to RLQ over 12-24h (present in ~50-60%)","McBurney tenderness; Rovsing, psoas (retrocecal), obturator (pelvic) signs","Atypical in pregnancy (displaced appendix), elderly (blunted findings), young children (perforate early)"],"dx":["Alvarado/AIR scores structure risk; no lab rules it out — WBC can be normal","CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast: sensitivity ~95%+ (appendix >6mm, wall enhancement, fat stranding, appendicolith)","US first in children and pregnancy (graded compression); MRI in pregnancy when US equivocal","Check HCG and examine testicles/pelvis — torsion and ectopic are the classic mimics"],"tx":["NPO, IV fluids, analgesia (give the opioid — it does not mask the diagnosis), antiemetics","Antibiotics once diagnosed: e.g., ceftriaxone + metronidazole (or piperacillin-tazobactam if perforated/complicated)","Uncomplicated: laparoscopic appendectomy remains standard; antibiotics-first is a legitimate shared-decision option (CODA: ~29% appendectomy by 90 days, appendicolith predicts failure)","Perforated with abscess: percutaneous drainage + IV abx, interval appendectomy discussion","Pregnant patients: appendectomy regardless of trimester — perforation threatens the fetus far more than surgery"],"pearls":["The appendicitis that 'suddenly got better' may have perforated — the calm before peritonitis","Anorexia is soft but useful: the hungry patient asking for food is rarely the one with appendicitis","In young women, US/MRI-first pathways avoid radiating the ovaries when feasible"],"refs":[{"t":"CODA Trial (NEJM 2020)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Appendicitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/appendicitis.html"}]},{"id":"em-cholecystitis","name":"Acute Cholecystitis & Biliary Colic","sec":"em","present":["Biliary colic: postprandial RUQ/epigastric pain, steady 1-6h, radiating to right scapula, resolves — no fever, benign exam","Cholecystitis: pain persists >6h, fever, focal RUQ tenderness, Murphy's sign","Choledocholithiasis/cholangitis: add jaundice; Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain) ± hypotension and AMS (Reynolds pentad) = ascending cholangitis = emergency"],"dx":["RUQ ultrasound first: stones, wall >3mm, pericholecystic fluid, sonographic Murphy's; HIDA when US equivocal (nonvisualization = cystic duct obstruction)","Labs: CBC, LFTs, lipase; elevated bilirubin/alk phos or CBD >6mm suggests ductal stone → MRCP or EUS, then ERCP","Tokyo Guidelines grade severity and guide timing","Lipase excludes the great mimic; ECG for the inferior-MI-as-'gallbladder' trap"],"tx":["Biliary colic: NSAIDs (ketorolac 15-30mg IV — as effective as opioids here), antiemetics; discharge with surgical follow-up for elective cholecystectomy","Cholecystitis: NPO, IVF, IV abx (ceftriaxone + metronidazole or piperacillin-tazobactam), analgesia, surgical consult","Early laparoscopic cholecystectomy (within 72h, ideally same admission) beats delayed surgery — shorter stay, no increase in complications","Nonoperative candidates (prohibitive risk): percutaneous cholecystostomy","Cholangitis: resuscitate + abx + urgent biliary decompression (ERCP) — this is the one that dies in the waiting room"],"pearls":["A normal WBC and no fever do not exclude cholecystitis — the ultrasound and the exam decide","Gallstones on US with a normal exam explain nothing by themselves — 10-15% of adults have asymptomatic stones","Rising bilirubin + gallstones = think ductal stone; get the MRCP before the OR"],"refs":[{"t":"Tokyo Guidelines 2018 (TG18)","u":"https://www.jshbps.jp"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Gallstones","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/gallstones.html"}]},{"id":"em-pancreatitis","name":"Acute Pancreatitis","sec":"em","present":["Constant severe epigastric pain boring to the back, worse supine, better leaning forward; nausea/vomiting","Etiology hunt: gallstones (~40%) and alcohol (~30%) dominate; then hypertriglyceridemia (>1000), post-ERCP, drugs, hypercalcemia","Severe disease clues at presentation: SIRS, hemoconcentration, BUN rising, altered mentation"],"dx":["Diagnosis = 2 of 3: characteristic pain, lipase ≥3× upper limit, imaging findings — CT is NOT required to diagnose","Lipase height does not predict severity; BISAP and admission BUN/hematocrit trends do","RUQ ultrasound on every first episode (gallstones change management); triglycerides and calcium on the etiology panel","CT with contrast reserved for diagnostic uncertainty or failure to improve at 48-72h (necrosis assessment)"],"tx":["Fluids: moderate LR resuscitation (WATERFALL trial — aggressive fluids increased overload without benefit); target urine output and hemodynamics, not a fixed formula","Analgesia (opioids fine), antiemetics; early oral feeding as tolerated (low-fat) — 'gut rest' is obsolete; enteral > parenteral if support needed","NO prophylactic antibiotics for pancreatitis or even sterile necrosis — treat only documented infection","Gallstone pancreatitis + cholangitis: ERCP within 24h; without cholangitis: no early ERCP; cholecystectomy same admission for mild gallstone pancreatitis (delay = ~20% recurrence risk)","Hypertriglyceridemic: insulin infusion ± apheresis; severe/necrotizing or organ failure: ICU"],"pearls":["The admission lipase of 10,000 in a comfortable patient matters less than the BUN that climbs overnight","Discharge counseling for alcohol cessation is an actual intervention — recurrent alcoholic pancreatitis is the default without it","Same-admission cholecystectomy for mild gallstone pancreatitis is guideline-directed and constantly forgotten"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG Acute Pancreatitis Guideline","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pancreatitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/pancreatitis.html"}]},{"id":"em-sbo","name":"Small Bowel Obstruction","sec":"em","present":["Crampy periumbilical pain, vomiting (bilious = proximal), distension, obstipation","Prior abdominal surgery (adhesions ~60-70% of cases) or hernia history; no prior surgery → suspect hernia, malignancy, or Crohn's","Danger signs: constant (not crampy) pain, fever, peritonitis, hemodynamic change — think strangulation"],"dx":["CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast: transition point, dilated loops >2.5-3cm, distal collapse; identifies closed-loop and ischemia (pneumatosis, reduced wall enhancement)","Examine ALL hernial orifices — incarcerated femoral hernias hide in obese patients","Lactate for ischemia concern (normal doesn't exclude early strangulation)","Upright films (air-fluid levels) are quick but miss ~20% — CT is the standard"],"tx":["NPO, IV fluid resuscitation, electrolyte repletion, antiemetics, analgesia","NG decompression for significant vomiting/distension — relieves symptoms and reduces aspiration","Uncomplicated adhesive SBO: nonoperative trial 48-72h succeeds in ~70-80%; Gastrografin challenge is both diagnostic and mildly therapeutic","Surgery now: strangulation signs, closed-loop, peritonitis, incarcerated hernia, no prior surgery with clear transition point","Broad-spectrum abx only when ischemia/perforation suspected or heading to OR"],"pearls":["Crampy pain that becomes constant is the classic strangulation story — reimage or consult, don't reassure","The 'partial SBO' vomiting feculent material is not partial","Virgin abdomen + SBO = surgical consult regardless of how well they look"],"refs":[{"t":"Bologna Guidelines: Adhesive SBO (WSES)","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Intestinal Obstruction","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000260.htm"}]},{"id":"em-gib","name":"GI Bleeding (Upper & Lower)","sec":"em","present":["Upper (proximal to ligament of Treitz): hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis, melena; lower: hematochezia (but brisk UGIB causes hematochezia + instability in ~10%)","Risk context: cirrhosis (varices), NSAIDs/anticoagulants, prior bleeds, alcohol, aortic graft (aortoenteric fistula until excluded)","Syncope, orthostasis, or ongoing hematemesis flag severity — the first BP can be deceptively normal"],"dx":["Rectal exam with stool inspection — settles melena vs bright red blood arguments","CBC (initial Hgb lags true loss), BUN/Cr ratio >30 favors upper source, coags, type & screen (crossmatch if unstable), fibrinogen in massive bleeding","Glasgow-Blatchford score: 0-1 can be considered for outpatient endoscopy pathway","Unstable with negative/nondiagnostic upper workup: CT angiography localizes active lower bleeding (needs ~0.3-0.5 mL/min)"],"tx":["Two large-bore IVs; restrictive transfusion threshold Hgb <7 (Villanueva — better survival) except exsanguination or active ACS (<8)","Massive bleed: activate MTP, balanced ratios, reverse anticoagulation (warfarin → 4F-PCC + vit K; DOACs → PCC/specific agents)","Suspected variceal: octreotide 50mcg bolus + drip, ceftriaxone 1g (mortality benefit), urgent endoscopy; refractory: balloon tamponade as bridge to TIPS","Nonvariceal UGIB: pantoprazole 80mg + drip or intermittent high-dose; erythromycin 250mg IV pre-scope improves visualization; endoscopy within 24h","Airway: intubate the obtunded actively vomiting bleeder BEFORE endoscopy, not during"],"pearls":["A normal hemoglobin in a brisk bleeder means the labs haven't caught up — treat the vitals","Melena can persist days after bleeding stops; rising BUN with stable Hgb ≠ rebleed by itself","Cirrhotic + GI bleed gets antibiotics every time — it prevents SBP and lowers mortality"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG Upper GI Bleeding Guideline (2021)","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: GI Bleeding","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/gastrointestinalbleeding.html"}]},{"id":"em-diverticulitis","name":"Diverticulitis","sec":"em","present":["LLQ pain (sigmoid predominance), low-grade fever, change in bowel habits; 'left-sided appendicitis'","Complicated features: high fever, peritonitis, palpable mass (abscess), pneumaturia/fecaluria (colovesical fistula)","Right-sided diverticulitis occurs (younger, Asian populations) and mimics appendicitis"],"dx":["CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast: wall thickening, fat stranding, diverticula; grades complications (Hinchey: abscess, purulent/feculent peritonitis)","Labs: CBC, BMP, UA; lactate when septic","Clinical diagnosis without CT is acceptable for classic recurrent mild episodes"],"tx":["Uncomplicated, well, tolerating PO, immunocompetent: outpatient — and selective NO-antibiotic management is now guideline-supported (AVOD/DIABOLO); when treating: amox-clav or cipro+metronidazole ×4-7d","Admit: complicated disease, immunosuppressed, failure of outpatient care, can't tolerate PO — IV abx (ceftriaxone+metronidazole or pip-tazo)","Abscess ≥3-4cm: percutaneous drainage + IV abx","Perforation with peritonitis: resuscitate, broad abx, emergent surgery (Hartmann's vs resection + anastomosis)","Discharge counseling: colonoscopy 6-8 weeks after resolution of a first complicated episode (exclude neoplasm); fiber; nuts/seeds/popcorn restriction is a myth"],"pearls":["The immunosuppressed patient with 'mild' diverticulitis perforates quietly — admit liberally","Recurrent 'diverticulitis' never imaged deserves one CT — mimics include colon cancer and ischemic colitis","Free air on CT ≠ automatic OR if peritonitis is absent and the patient is stable — surgical consult decides"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Diverticulitis Guideline (2020)","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Diverticulosis and Diverticulitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/diverticulosisanddiverticulitis.html"}]},{"id":"em-renal-colic","name":"Renal Colic / Nephrolithiasis","sec":"em","present":["Sudden severe unilateral flank pain radiating to groin, colicky, writhing (peritonitis patients lie still — colic patients can't)","Nausea/vomiting, hematuria (gross or microscopic ~85%; absence doesn't exclude)","The imposter list: AAA (>50y first 'stone'), dissection, torsion, ectopic, pyelonephritis"],"dx":["Low-dose non-contrast CT is the standard: stone size + location drive management; US first in pregnancy, children, and known recurrent stone formers","UA: hematuria, and crucially NITRITES/pyuria — infected obstructed stone is an emergency","BMP for renal function; the solitary-kidney patient with an obstructing stone is a different disease","First-time stone or age >50: glance at the aorta on the CT (or bedside US) before anchoring"],"tx":["NSAIDs are first-line and superior for colic: ketorolac 15-30mg IV (avoid in AKI/pregnancy); add opioids for breakthrough; IV lidocaine as adjunct in refractory cases","Antiemetics; routine IV fluid 'flushing' doesn't push stones out","Medical expulsive therapy: tamsulosin 0.4mg for distal stones 5-10mm (benefit concentrated there); ≤5mm pass spontaneously ~80-90%","Discharge with strainer, urology follow-up: stones ≤10mm, controlled pain, no infection, normal renal function","EMERGENT urology + decompression (stent or nephrostomy): obstructing stone + infection/fever, AKI, solitary kidney, intractable pain — the infected obstructed kidney is pus under pressure"],"pearls":["Fever + hydronephrosis + stone = source control tonight, not antibiotics alone","The 60-year-old with new 'renal colic' and a soft crit gets an aorta looked at","Counsel fluids >2.5L/day — recurrence is ~50% at 5-10 years without prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"AUA Surgical Management of Stones Guideline","u":"https://www.auanet.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Kidney Stones","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/kidneystones.html"}]},{"id":"em-uti-pyelo","name":"UTI & Pyelonephritis","sec":"em","present":["Cystitis: dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic discomfort, no fever","Pyelonephritis: fever, chills, flank pain, CVA tenderness, nausea/vomiting ± cystitis symptoms","Elderly: confusion alone is NOT a UTI without urinary symptoms/systemic signs — asymptomatic bacteriuria is ubiquitous and overtreated"],"dx":["UA (nitrites specific, leukocyte esterase sensitive); culture for pyelonephritis, complicated UTI, pregnancy, recurrent infections — not routine simple cystitis","Complicated = structural/functional abnormality, catheter, stone, pregnancy, male with fever/systemic illness, immunosuppression","Imaging (CT) for: sepsis, suspected obstruction/stone, failure to improve at 48-72h (perinephric abscess, emphysematous pyelonephritis)","Pregnancy test — changes drug choices and disposition (pyelo in pregnancy admits)"],"tx":["Simple cystitis: nitrofurantoin 100mg BID ×5d or TMP-SMX DS BID ×3d (if local resistance <20%) or fosfomycin 3g ×1; fluoroquinolones NOT first-line for cystitis","Outpatient pyelonephritis: ceftriaxone 1g IV in ED, then oral FQ or TMP-SMX pending culture; recheck at 48-72h","Admit pyelo: sepsis, intractable vomiting, pregnancy, obstruction, failed outpatient therapy — IV ceftriaxone (pip-tazo/carbapenem if resistance risk)","Obstructed infected system: emergent urology decompression (see renal colic)","Asymptomatic bacteriuria: treat ONLY pregnancy and pre-urologic procedures — treating the smelly urine of the asymptomatic nursing-home patient breeds resistance"],"pearls":["Male + fever + urinary symptoms: examine the prostate once, gently — acute prostatitis changes duration (2-4 weeks) and drug penetration requirements","The catheterized patient's UA is uninterpretable — culture + clinical picture decide","Pyelo that isn't better at 72h on correct abx has an abscess or a stone until the CT says otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA UTI Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Urinary Tract Infections","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/urinarytractinfections.html"}]},{"id":"em-torsion","name":"Testicular Torsion","sec":"em","present":["Sudden severe unilateral testicular/lower abdominal pain, often with nausea/vomiting; may wake from sleep","Peak bimodal: neonates and adolescents 12-18, but occurs at any age","High-riding transverse testis, absent cremasteric reflex, diffuse tenderness; intermittent torsion causes recurrent self-resolving episodes"],"dx":["This is a CLINICAL diagnosis — high suspicion goes to urology/OR without waiting for imaging","Doppler US when presentation is equivocal: absent/decreased flow (sensitivity ~90% — a 'normal' study does not exclude torsion with a good story)","TWIST score (swelling, hard testis, absent cremasteric, nausea, high-riding) stratifies","Ddx: epididymitis (gradual, pyuria, relief with elevation — unreliable), torsed appendix testis (blue dot), hernia, orchitis"],"tx":["Time is testis: salvage ~90-100% <6h, ~50% at 12h, <10% >24h — every consult/imaging decision is on that clock","Emergent urology consult + NPO the moment torsion is seriously entertained","Manual detorsion if surgical delay: 'open the book' (medial to lateral) — relief of pain suggests success, but may need >360° and direction is wrong in up to a third; surgery proceeds regardless","OR: detorsion + bilateral orchiopexy (the contralateral testis shares the bell-clapper anomaly)","Abdominal-pain-only presentations in boys: examine the scrotum — always"],"pearls":["Every adolescent male 'abdominal pain' gets a genital exam documented","A negative ultrasound with a classic story still gets the urology call","Intermittent torsion (recurrent transient pain) merits urgent urology referral for elective pexy"],"refs":[{"t":"AUA Education: Testicular Torsion","u":"https://www.auanet.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Testicular Torsion","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000517.htm"}]},{"id":"em-ectopic","name":"Ectopic Pregnancy","sec":"em","present":["Abdominal/pelvic pain + vaginal bleeding + amenorrhea in reproductive-age — but any combination, and 30% have no bleeding","Risk factors: prior ectopic, PID, tubal surgery, IVF, IUD (pregnancy WITH IUD is ectopic until proven otherwise) — half have none","Ruptured: peritonitis, shoulder pain (diaphragmatic irritation), syncope, shock"],"dx":["Urine HCG on every reproductive-age abdomen/syncope/bleeding — no exceptions","Transvaginal US: intrauterine pregnancy essentially excludes ectopic (heterotopic ~1/4000 naturally, but ~1/100 with IVF)","No IUP + quantitative HCG above discriminatory zone (~3500): high concern; below it with no IUP = pregnancy of unknown location → 48h HCG + repeat US (normal IUP roughly doubles q48h)","Free fluid in Morison's pouch on FAST + positive HCG + shock = OR, not more imaging"],"tx":["Unstable/ruptured: 2 large-bore IVs, type & cross, transfuse as needed, emergent OB-GYN for operative management","Methotrexate candidates (with OB): hemodynamically stable, unruptured, no fetal cardiac activity, mass <3.5cm, HCG typically <5000, reliable follow-up, normal renal/hepatic function","Surgical (salpingostomy/salpingectomy) for MTX contraindications or failure","Rh-negative patients: Rho(D) immune globulin","Discharge PUL patients only with explicit ectopic precautions and CONFIRMED 48h follow-up"],"pearls":["The pregnancy test is the pelvic exam of the 21st century — order it before the CT, the lytics, the MTX for the 'rheumatoid flare'","An 'appropriate' HCG rise does not exclude ectopic; a falling HCG does not exclude rupture","Heterotopic pregnancy: seeing an IUP in an IVF patient with free fluid does not end the workup"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Practice Bulletin: Ectopic Pregnancy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Ectopic Pregnancy","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ectopicpregnancy.html"}]},{"id":"em-miscarriage","name":"First-Trimester Bleeding & Miscarriage","sec":"em","present":["Vaginal bleeding ± cramping <13 weeks; ~25% of pregnancies bleed in the first trimester and about half of those continue normally","Classify: threatened (closed os, viable IUP), inevitable (open os), incomplete (partial passage), complete (empty uterus after documented IUP), missed (nonviable, retained)","Heavy bleeding with clots/tissue at the os can cause cervical shock (vagal bradycardia + hypotension) — remove the tissue"],"dx":["Quantitative HCG + transvaginal US: establish location first (ectopic exclusion is job #1), then viability","Viability criteria (avoid premature death calls): CRL ≥7mm without cardiac activity or mean sac diameter ≥25mm without embryo = failed pregnancy; below thresholds → repeat imaging in 7-10 days","Speculum exam: os open/closed, tissue, quantify bleeding; bimanual for size/tenderness","CBC, type & screen (Rh status), coags only if heavy ongoing loss"],"tx":["Threatened: expectant management, precautions, OB follow-up — no evidence for bed rest or progesterone in unselected patients","Confirmed early pregnancy loss — three equivalent options by patient preference: expectant (up to 80% complete in 8 weeks), medical (mifepristone 200mg + misoprostol 800mcg vaginal — combination markedly more effective), or aspiration","Hemorrhage/instability/infection: resuscitate + urgent suction evacuation; remove visible os tissue with ring forceps immediately for cervical shock","Rh-negative: Rho(D) immune globulin 50-120mcg in first trimester (per current ACOG, practice varies — follow local protocol)","Compassionate communication and bereavement resources are part of the treatment — 'miscarriage is common and not caused by anything you did'"],"pearls":["Never diagnose miscarriage on HCG trend alone with an undocumented location — ectopics fall too","The words used in these conversations are remembered for years — sit down, avoid 'products of conception' at the bedside","Bleeding + prior IUP on US at an outside facility still deserves location confirmation in YOUR notes"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Practice Bulletin: Early Pregnancy Loss","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Miscarriage","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/miscarriage.html"}]},{"id":"em-pid-torsion","name":"PID & Ovarian Torsion","sec":"em","present":["PID: lower abdominal/pelvic pain, discharge, dyspareunia, fever; onset often during/after menses; RUQ pain variant = Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis","Ovarian torsion: sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain + nausea/vomiting, often with known cyst/mass or ovulation induction; pain may wax/wane with intermittent torsion","Both are clinical traps: PID underdiagnosed (low threshold mandated), torsion misdiagnosed as gastroenteritis/renal colic"],"dx":["PID minimum criteria (CDC): pelvic pain + cervical motion, uterine, OR adnexal tenderness — treat empirically at that threshold; NAAT for gonorrhea/chlamydia/trich, HIV & syphilis screening","Tubo-ovarian abscess: severe illness, mass, failure to improve → transvaginal US or CT","Torsion: pelvic US with Doppler — enlarged ovary is the most consistent finding; PRESERVED arterial flow does NOT exclude torsion (dual supply, venous obstruction first)","HCG on both pathways always"],"tx":["PID outpatient: ceftriaxone 500mg IM + doxycycline 100mg BID ×14d + metronidazole 500mg BID ×14d (current CDC regimen); treat partners, abstinence counseling, reassess 72h","Admit PID: pregnancy, TOA, severe illness/vomiting, failed outpatient therapy — IV ceftriaxone + doxy + metronidazole","TOA: IV abx; drainage for large abscess or no response in 48-72h","Torsion: emergent gynecology for laparoscopic detorsion — ovaries recover function even when they look dusky; salvage is time-dependent","IUD stays in for mild-moderate PID with close follow-up (removal if no improvement 48-72h)"],"pearls":["Undertreated PID costs fertility — the diagnostic bar is deliberately low; treat the 'probable' case","Normal Doppler flow torsion is a classic miss — the story and the big ovary trump the flow report","Adolescents with PID need empiric treatment and STI counseling regardless of stated sexual history"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC STI Treatment Guidelines (2021)","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/pelvicinflammatorydisease.html"}]},{"id":"em-dka-hhs","name":"DKA & HHS","sec":"em","present":["DKA: polyuria/polydipsia, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, Kussmaul respirations, fruity breath; evolves over hours-days; increasingly seen in T2DM and on SGLT2 inhibitors (euglycemic DKA — glucose can be <250!)","HHS: older T2DM, days-weeks of dehydration, profound hyperglycemia (>600), hyperosmolarity (>320), AMS, minimal ketosis","BOTH: hunt the precipitant — infection, infarction (MI, stroke, gut), insulin omission, new diabetes, drugs (steroids, SGLT2i)"],"dx":["DKA triad: glucose >250 (unless euglycemic), anion-gap metabolic acidosis (pH <7.3, bicarb <18), ketonemia (beta-hydroxybutyrate)","VBG suffices; BMP q2-4h during treatment — the gap, not the glucose, defines resolution","Corrected sodium (add ~2.4 per 100 glucose over 100); ECG for hyperkalemia and silent MI; infectious workup per clinical picture","HHS: effective osmolality >320 with pH >7.3 and minimal ketones"],"tx":["Fluids first: 1-1.5L NS or LR in the first hour (LR may resolve acidosis faster), then rate by hemodynamics; add dextrose when glucose ~200-250","POTASSIUM before insulin: K <3.3 → hold insulin, replete; 3.3-5.2 → add 20-30 mEq/L to fluids; total-body K is always depleted despite the serum number","Insulin: 0.1 U/kg/h infusion (bolus optional/omittable); target glucose fall 50-75/h; continue until gap closes — then overlap subcutaneous basal insulin 1-2h before stopping the drip","Bicarbonate only for pH <6.9 (controversial even there); phosphate only if <1 with symptoms","HHS: slower correction — fluids do most of the work; drop osmolality ≤3 mOsm/kg/h; watch for cerebral edema in young DKA (headache, AMS during treatment → mannitol/3% saline)"],"pearls":["The drip gets turned off before basal insulin is on board — and the DKA comes right back; overlap is the whole game","Normal glucose does not exclude DKA on SGLT2 inhibitors — check the ketones and the gap","DKA abdominal pain resolves with treatment; pain that persists after the gap closes is a real abdomen"],"refs":[{"t":"ADA Hyperglycemic Crises Consensus","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Diabetic Ketoacidosis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000320.htm"}]},{"id":"em-hypoglycemia","name":"Hypoglycemia","sec":"em","present":["Adrenergic: tremor, diaphoresis, palpitations, anxiety; neuroglycopenic: confusion, slurred speech, focal deficits (stroke mimic), seizure, coma","Whipple triad: symptoms + low glucose + resolution with correction","Etiology: diabetic med mismatch (most), sulfonylureas, renal failure (insulin accumulates), sepsis, alcohol, adrenal insufficiency, insulinoma, factitious"],"dx":["POC glucose on every AMS/seizure/stroke-alert/'psych' patient — the fastest save in medicine","Review the med list: sulfonylureas and long-acting insulins define observation time","No diabetes history: draw critical labs BEFORE dextrose when feasible (insulin, C-peptide, cortisol) — C-peptide separates endogenous from exogenous insulin","BMP for renal function; consider adrenal axis in refractory cases"],"tx":["Awake + protecting airway: oral fast carbs 15-20g, recheck 15 min, then complex carb + protein meal","IV: D50 25g (or D10 100-250mL — gentler on veins, less overshoot); recheck q15-30min","No access: glucagon 1mg IM (fails in depleted glycogen — alcoholics, malnourished) or intranasal glucagon","Sulfonylurea overdose: octreotide 50-100mcg SC q6-8h blocks rebound hypoglycemia — plus admission; refeed and observe minimum 24h","Disposition: short-acting insulin mismatch with a meal and stable rechecks can go home with dose adjustment + follow-up; sulfonylurea, long-acting insulin, renal failure, or no clear cause = observe/admit"],"pearls":["Every sulfonylurea hypoglycemic bounces back after one amp of D50 — the drug outlasts the dextrose by many hours","Alcoholics get thiamine, but never delay dextrose waiting for it","Recurrent unexplained hypoglycemia in a nondiabetic: think factitious (check who has access to insulin) and insulinoma — send the critical sample"],"refs":[{"t":"Endocrine Society: Hypoglycemic Disorders","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hypoglycemia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/hypoglycemia.html"}]},{"id":"em-lytes","name":"Electrolyte Emergencies (HyperK, HypoNa)","sec":"em","present":["Hyperkalemia: often asymptomatic until arrhythmia; weakness, paresthesias; suspect in renal failure, missed dialysis, ACEi/ARB/spironolactone, crush injury, tumor lysis","Severe hyponatremia: nausea, headache, confusion → seizures, coma (cerebral edema); rate of development matters more than the number","Ask duration: acute (<48h) hyponatremia tolerates faster correction; chronic demands slow correction (osmotic demyelination risk)"],"dx":["HyperK ECG progression: peaked T's → PR prolongation → P-wave loss → QRS widening → sine wave; but ECG can be deceptively normal — treat the number AND the tracing","Exclude pseudohyperkalemia (hemolyzed sample) when clinically incongruent — repeat, but treat first if ECG changes","HypoNa workup: serum osm (true hypotonic?), urine osm (ADH on?), urine Na (volume status/SIADH), glucose (translocational), cortisol/TSH when unexplained","Classify hypoNa by volume: hypovolemic (GI loss, diuretics), euvolemic (SIADH, beer potomania), hypervolemic (CHF, cirrhosis)"],"tx":["HyperK with ECG changes: calcium gluconate 3g IV (or CaCl 1g via good line) FIRST — stabilizes membrane in minutes, repeat if ECG unchanged","Shift: insulin 5-10U IV + D50 (5U in renal failure reduces hypoglycemia), albuterol 10-20mg neb; bicarbonate only if acidotic","Remove: emergent dialysis for renal failure/refractory; K-binders (patiromer/SZC) work in hours-days, not minutes; loop diuretics if urine flows","HypoNa with seizures/severe symptoms: 3% saline 100-150mL bolus, repeat ×2 PRN — small rapid rises stop seizures","Correction caps: ≤8 mEq/L per 24h (chronic); overshoot (large water diuresis) → D5W ± desmopressin clamp; asymptomatic chronic hypoNa is fixed slowly on the floor, not in the ED"],"pearls":["Calcium first, always, when the ECG shows hyperkalemia — everything else is minutes-to-hours slower","The wide-QRS 'STEMI-mimic' in a dialysis patient is hyperkalemia until the K+ results back","Symptomatic hypoNa gets hypertonic saline regardless of chronicity — the seizure is the emergency; the correction limit governs the next 24h, not the first bolus"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO & AHA Hyperkalemia Resources","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"European Hyponatremia Guidelines","u":"https://academic.oup.com/ndt"}]},{"id":"em-anaphylaxis","name":"Anaphylaxis & Angioedema","sec":"em","present":["Anaphylaxis: acute onset with skin/mucosa (hives, flushing, lip/tongue swelling) PLUS respiratory compromise or hypotension — OR two+ systems after likely allergen; GI symptoms count","Common triggers: foods, stings, drugs; biphasic reactions recur hours later in ~5%","Angioedema without urticaria: think bradykinin-mediated (ACE inhibitor — can start years into therapy; hereditary angioedema) — epinephrine-resistant"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis — do not wait for labs; serum tryptase (drawn 1-2h) supports retrospectively but never gates treatment","Airway assessment front and center: voice change, stridor, drooling, tongue/floor-of-mouth involvement","Distinguish histaminergic (urticaria, itch, responds to epi) from bradykinin angioedema (isolated swelling, ACEi history, family history)"],"tx":["Epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg IM anterolateral thigh IMMEDIATELY — delay is the dominant factor in anaphylaxis deaths; repeat q5-15min; no absolute contraindication in anaphylaxis","Refractory after 2-3 IM doses: epinephrine infusion 0.05-0.1 mcg/kg/min; add glucagon for beta-blocked patients","Adjuncts (never substitutes): H1 (cetirizine/diphenhydramine), H2 blocker, steroids (may not prevent biphasic but standard), nebulized albuterol for bronchospasm; aggressive fluids for distributive shock","Airway: intubate early for progressive angioedema — expect a difficult airway, double setup with surgical airway ready, most experienced operator","ACEi angioedema: stop the drug forever, airway-first management, supportive (evidence for icatibant/FFP is mixed); HAE: C1-INH concentrate, icatibant, or ecallantide","Observation 4-6h (longer for severe/biphasic risk); discharge with epinephrine auto-injector ×2, teaching, and allergy referral — every time"],"pearls":["IM epi is underused and underdosed out of misplaced fear — the dangerous drug is the one not given","Normotension does not exclude anaphylaxis — vomiting + hives after a bee sting is enough","The ACEi angioedema tongue that 'looks stable' can occlude in minutes — disposition is about airway trajectory, not the current picture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAAAI/ACAAI Anaphylaxis Practice Parameter","u":"https://www.aaaai.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Anaphylaxis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000844.htm"}]},{"id":"em-tox","name":"Toxicologic Emergencies","sec":"em","present":["Approach every overdose with: what, how much, when, what else, why (intent) — and treat the patient, not just the poison","Toxidromes: opioid (miosis, respiratory depression, coma), sympathomimetic (agitation, mydriasis, diaphoresis, hyperthermia), anticholinergic (dry, flushed, mydriasis, delirium, retention), cholinergic (SLUDGE/killer B's), sedative-hypnotic","Undifferentiated AMS: glucose, naloxone trial, consider CO"],"dx":["ECG on every intentional ingestion: QRS >100ms (TCA/sodium-channel blockade → bicarb), QTc prolongation (torsades risk)","Acetaminophen level at 4h post-ingestion on ALL intentional overdoses (silent early, lethal late) + salicylate level; BMP for gap acidosis","Toxic alcohols (gap acidosis + osmolar gap): methanol/ethylene glycol — send levels, start fomepizole empirically when suspicion real","Poison control (1-800-222-1222) early — documents recommendations and helps disposition"],"tx":["Supportive care saves more overdose patients than antidotes: airway, ventilation, fluids, temperature control, benzos for sympathomimetic agitation","Naloxone: 0.04-0.4mg IV titrated to RESPIRATIONS (not consciousness) in dependent patients; infusion for long-acting opioids; observe for renarcotization","Acetaminophen: N-acetylcysteine by nomogram (Rumack-Matthew at 4h+) — near-universal antidote efficacy when started <8-10h","Sodium-channel blockade (wide QRS): sodium bicarbonate 1-2 mEq/kg boluses; salicylates: bicarb drip for urine alkalinization, dialysis for severe; beta-blocker/CCB: calcium, glucagon, high-dose insulin euglycemia, lipid emulsion late","Activated charcoal only within ~1h, awake, cooperative, non-caustic — never in the obtunded unprotected airway","Intentional ingestions: medical clearance then psychiatric evaluation, never discharged while at risk"],"pearls":["The acetaminophen level you don't send is the transplant list referral in three days","'Found down' + gap acidosis + normal lactate: run the tox differential, not just sepsis","QRS widening in an overdose is bicarb NOW — don't wait for the drug ID"],"refs":[{"t":"America's Poison Centers 1-800-222-1222","u":"https://poisonhelp.org"},{"t":"ACMT Resources","u":"https://www.acmt.net"}]},{"id":"em-etoh","name":"Alcohol Intoxication & Withdrawal","sec":"em","present":["Intoxication: disinhibition → ataxia, slurred speech → stupor, respiratory depression; NEVER attribute AMS to alcohol alone until trauma, hypoglycemia, and co-ingestion are addressed","Withdrawal timeline: tremor/anxiety 6-12h; hallucinosis 12-24h; seizures 12-48h; delirium tremens 48-96h (autonomic storm + delirium — mortality up to 5% treated)","Risk factors for severe withdrawal: prior DTs/withdrawal seizures, heavy daily use, comorbid illness"],"dx":["Glucose immediately; head CT for any trauma evidence, focal findings, or failure to metabolize toward sobriety on serial exams","BMP, magnesium, phosphorus (refeeding), LFTs, lipase; consider CIWA-Ar scoring to drive treatment","Withdrawal seizure workup: first-time seizures get full evaluation — 'alcohol withdrawal seizure' is partly a diagnosis of exclusion","Wernicke's (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia — any ONE suffices): clinical diagnosis, treat empirically"],"tx":["Intoxication: supportive — position lateral, monitor, serial exams; document a sober(ing) neuro exam before discharge; thiamine + folate + electrolyte repletion for chronic users","Withdrawal: benzodiazepines symptom-triggered by CIWA (diazepam 10-20mg or lorazepam 2-4mg escalating) — front-loading with diazepam gives a smoother auto-taper","Refractory/DTs: escalating benzos → phenobarbital (65-260mg IV increments or 10mg/kg load per protocol) — excellent evidence, watch airway; dexmedetomidine as adjunct only","Wernicke prophylaxis: thiamine 100-200mg IV; treatment dose 500mg IV TID — give before/with glucose in the depleted (practical rule)","Withdrawal seizures: benzos treat and prevent recurrence — phenytoin does NOT work for withdrawal seizures","Discharge planning: warm handoff to addiction resources; naltrexone/acamprosate referrals reduce relapse — the ED visit is a treatable moment"],"pearls":["The 'drunk and sleeping it off' patient with a subdural is the classic ED tragedy — examine, image liberally, re-examine","DTs don't happen at hour 6 — the agitated tremulous patient at hour 6 is the one who gets DTs at hour 60 if undertreated","Phenobarbital is the move when benzo requirements climb — don't chase with 40th mg of lorazepam"],"refs":[{"t":"ASAM Alcohol Withdrawal Guideline (2020)","u":"https://www.asam.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Alcohol Withdrawal","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000764.htm"}]},{"id":"em-agitation","name":"Agitation, Psychosis & Behavioral Emergencies","sec":"em","present":["Agitation is a symptom, not a diagnosis: hypoglycemia, hypoxia, intoxication/withdrawal, head injury, seizure, sepsis, thyroid storm all masquerade as 'psych'","Medical mimics red flags: abnormal vitals, age >45 with first psychiatric presentation, disorientation, visual hallucinations, focal findings","Excited delirium-type presentations (severe agitation + hyperthermia) are medical emergencies with real mortality"],"dx":["POC glucose + full vitals + pulse ox on every behavioral patient before the psych label sticks","Targeted medical screening: history and exam drive labs — routine 'clearance' panels on alert, oriented, known psychiatric patients add little","Consider: tox (sympathomimetics, anticholinergics), CIWA context, pregnancy test, head imaging for trauma/focality","Document capacity assessments when patients refuse care — capacity is decision-specific"],"tx":["Verbal de-escalation FIRST (BETA guidelines): one calm voice, space, offers of food/warmth — restraint and medication are what happens when de-escalation is skipped or fails","Oral meds when accepted (respect and efficacy): risperidone 2mg or olanzapine 10mg ± lorazepam","IM when required: droperidol 5-10mg (fast, effective — ACEP-supported) or olanzapine 10mg or haloperidol 5mg + lorazepam 2mg; ketamine 4-5mg/kg IM for severe undifferentiated agitation with airborne monitoring readiness","Physical restraint: least restrictive, trained team of 5, never prone pressure, face-up, frequent reassessment, early medication to shorten restraint time — document per policy","After control: NOW the medical exam happens — hyperthermia, rhabdo (CK), injury survey","Suicidality: safe environment, belongings secured, structured risk assessment (e.g., C-SSRS), 1:1 observation as indicated — discharge only with a documented safety plan and follow-up"],"pearls":["The combative patient who suddenly goes quiet is decompensating, not cooperating — check vitals immediately","Droperidol's black-box era is over per emergency medicine literature — it remains one of the fastest, most reliable agents","First-episode psychosis after 45 is organic until proven otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"ACEP & AAEP BETA De-escalation Guidelines","u":"https://www.acep.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Mental Health Emergencies","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/mentalhealth.html"}]},{"id":"em-pe-dvt","name":"Pulmonary Embolism & DVT","sec":"em","present":["PE: pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, tachycardia, hemoptysis; syncope suggests large clot; can be subtle ('can't catch my breath')","DVT: unilateral calf/thigh swelling, pain, warmth; >2cm circumference difference matters","Risk: recent surgery/immobilization, malignancy, OCPs/pregnancy, prior VTE, thrombophilia"],"dx":["Pretest probability FIRST: Wells or Geneva → low probability + PERC-negative = done, no D-dimer","Low/intermediate: age-adjusted D-dimer (age×10 over 50y) — negative excludes; positive → CTPA","YEARS/pregnancy-adapted algorithms reduce imaging in pregnancy; V/Q scan when CTPA contraindicated","Risk-stratify confirmed PE: sPESI, RV strain (CT or echo), troponin/BNP → massive (hypotension), submassive (RV strain, normotensive), low-risk","DVT: whole-leg compression US; isolated distal DVT can be surveilled vs treated"],"tx":["Low-risk PE (sPESI 0, no strain): DOAC and DISCHARGE from the ED is guideline-supported (apixaban 10mg BID ×7d → 5mg BID; rivaroxaban 15 BID ×21d → 20 daily)","Submassive: admit, anticoagulate (heparin drip if intervention contemplated), monitor for deterioration; catheter-directed options case-by-case","Massive (SBP <90): systemic thrombolysis (alteplase 100mg/2h) unless absolute contraindication; peri-arrest: 50mg bolus; mechanical/ECMO pathways where available","Anticoagulation contraindicated: IVC filter","Cancer-associated: DOACs acceptable (LMWH for GI/GU luminal tumors); pregnancy: LMWH (DOACs contraindicated)","DVT: DOAC, compression stockings for symptoms; phlegmasia cerulea dolens (massive ileofemoral, blue leg) → vascular emergency, catheter-directed therapy"],"pearls":["The PERC rule exists to STOP testing in low-risk patients — the D-dimer you shouldn't have sent creates the CT you didn't need","Syncope + new RBBB/S1Q3T3 + clear lungs: think PE before 'vasovagal'","Tachycardia that doesn't resolve with fluids and analgesia deserves an explanation before discharge"],"refs":[{"t":"ESC/AHA PE Guidelines","u":"https://www.escardio.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pulmonary Embolism","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/pulmonaryembolism.html"}]},{"id":"em-dissection","name":"Aortic Dissection","sec":"em","present":["Abrupt severe chest/back pain, maximal at onset, tearing/ripping, may MIGRATE as dissection propagates","Pain + neuro deficit, pain + limb ischemia, pain above and below diaphragm, syncope with chest pain — think dissection","Risks: hypertension (most), connective tissue disease (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos), bicuspid aortic valve, cocaine, pregnancy, prior cardiac surgery"],"dx":["Exam: pulse deficits/BP differential >20mmHg between arms (present in minority — absence excludes nothing), new aortic regurgitation murmur, tamponade signs","CXR: widened mediastinum in ~60% — a normal CXR does not exclude; ADD-RS score + D-dimer pathway can risk-stratify low-probability patients","CT angiography chest/abdomen/pelvis is the definitive ED study; TEE for the too-unstable-to-travel","Classify: Stanford A (ascending — surgical) vs B (descending only — usually medical)"],"tx":["Anti-impulse therapy immediately, sequence matters: RATE first with esmolol infusion (target HR <60), THEN pressure (nicardipine/clevidipine) to SBP 100-120 — vasodilator before beta-blockade worsens shear","Analgesia (fentanyl) is hemodynamic therapy here","Type A: emergent cardiothoracic surgery — mortality rises 1-2% per hour untreated; mobilize transfer early if no CT surgery in-house","Type B uncomplicated: ICU medical management; complicated (malperfusion, rupture, refractory pain/HTN) → TEVAR","Tamponade from proximal dissection: pericardiocentesis is temporizing at best and can worsen — the treatment is the OR"],"pearls":["The 'STEMI' with pain into the back and an aortic regurg murmur: look at the aorta before lytics/heparin — dissection into a coronary mimics inferior MI","Beta-block before vasodilating, every time","Chest pain + any neurologic finding = aorta on the differential in writing"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC Aortic Disease Guideline (2022)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Aortic Dissection","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000181.htm"}]},{"id":"em-aaa","name":"Ruptured Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm","sec":"em","present":["Classic triad (pain + hypotension + pulsatile mass) in <50% — the common story is an older smoker with abdominal/back/flank pain ± syncope","Misdiagnosis patterns: 'renal colic' (flank pain, hematuria can occur), 'diverticulitis', 'musculoskeletal back pain', 'syncope workup'","Contained retroperitoneal rupture can look deceptively stable — for a while"],"dx":["Bedside aortic ultrasound: >3cm aneurysmal, >5cm with symptoms = presume rupture; US sees the aneurysm, not reliably the rupture — a symptomatic aneurysm IS the answer","Stable enough for CT: CTA defines anatomy for open vs endovascular repair (EVAR)","Type & cross 6+ units, activate MTP pathways early","First-time 'renal colic' over 60: image the aorta before the diagnosis sticks"],"tx":["Unstable + known/US-confirmed aneurysm: straight to OR/hybrid suite — no CT, vascular surgery called from the bedside","Permissive hypotension (SBP ~80-90, mentating) until proximal control — aggressive crystalloid pops the clot; transfuse blood, not saline","Two large-bore IVs/MTP, reverse anticoagulation, analgesia with hemodynamic care","EVAR increasingly first-line for ruptures with suitable anatomy; transfer decisions depend on local vascular capability — call early, ship fast","Symptomatic non-ruptured aneurysm: urgent vascular consult, admission, BP control — these rupture on the observation unit"],"pearls":["The AAA exam is 90 seconds of ultrasound — make it reflexive in the >60 abdominal/back/flank pain patient","Hematuria does not exclude AAA — the aneurysm can irritate the ureter","Syncope in an old smoker: aorta, GI bleed, PE, arrhythmia — in that frame, not 'vasovagal'"],"refs":[{"t":"SVS AAA Guidelines","u":"https://vascular.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000162.htm"}]},{"id":"em-htn-emergency","name":"Hypertensive Emergency","sec":"em","present":["Hypertensive EMERGENCY = severe HTN + acute target-organ damage: encephalopathy, ICH/stroke, ACS, pulmonary edema, dissection, AKI, eclampsia, MAHA","Severe asymptomatic hypertension (no organ damage) is NOT an emergency — it's the more common and more mismanaged entity","Symptoms directing the workup: headache/AMS/seizure (brain), chest pain (heart/aorta), dyspnea (lungs), oliguria (kidneys), pregnancy (preeclampsia)"],"dx":["Confirm with proper cuff technique both arms; fundoscopy for papilledema/hemorrhages","Targeted testing by symptoms: BMP + UA (AKI, proteinuria), troponin/ECG, CXR, head CT for neuro findings, smear + platelets + LDH if MAHA suspected","Asymptomatic severe HTN: minimal/no routine testing per ACEP — screening panels rarely change management","Pregnancy ≥20wk + BP ≥140/90: preeclampsia evaluation (protein, platelets, LFTs, creatinine)"],"tx":["True emergency: IV titratable agents, reduce MAP ~10-20% in first hour, further 5-15% over 23h — EXCEPT: dissection (rapid to SBP ~120 + HR <60), ICH (SBP toward 140 per guidelines), ischemic stroke (only >220/120, or >185/110 pre-lytic)","Agent by syndrome: nicardipine/clevidipine (neuro, most), esmolol (dissection, with vasodilator after), nitroglycerin (pulmonary edema/ACS), labetalol (broadly useful, avoid in cocaine per tradition)","Severe preeclampsia/eclampsia: labetalol IV or hydralazine + magnesium 4-6g load → 2g/h; delivery is the cure — OB emergently","Severe ASYMPTOMATIC hypertension: restart/adjust ORAL therapy, arrange follow-up within days — acute IV lowering harms (watershed infarcts); do not treat the number","Never drop chronic severe hypertension to 'normal' acutely — autoregulation is right-shifted"],"pearls":["The question is never 'how high' — it's 'what organ is being damaged right now'","Asymptomatic 220/110 discharged on amlodipine with follow-up is good medicine; the same patient given IV hydralazine to 130 is a stroke risk","Headache alone with a normal neuro exam is not target-organ damage"],"refs":[{"t":"ACEP Clinical Policy: Asymptomatic Elevated BP","u":"https://www.acep.org"},{"t":"AHA Hypertension Guideline","u":"https://www.heart.org"}]},{"id":"em-ptx","name":"Pneumothorax","sec":"em","present":["Sudden pleuritic chest pain + dyspnea; primary spontaneous (tall thin young smokers), secondary (COPD, cystic lung disease — worse tolerated), traumatic, iatrogenic (post-line, post-biopsy)","Tension: severe distress, hypotension, distended neck veins, tracheal deviation (late), unilateral absent breath sounds — a clinical diagnosis treated before imaging","Ventilated patient who acutely deteriorates: think tension PTX (DOPE mnemonic)"],"dx":["Stable: chest X-ray (expiratory films unnecessary); POCUS (absent lung sliding, barcode sign) is faster and more sensitive than supine CXR — invaluable in trauma","CT defines size accurately and finds blebs/underlying disease; size estimates (>2cm at hilum ≈ large per BTS) guide strategy","Tension physiology: DO NOT wait for imaging"],"tx":["Tension: immediate needle decompression (2nd intercostal midclavicular or 4th/5th anterior axillary — better wall penetration) followed ALWAYS by chest tube","Primary spontaneous, small, minimal symptoms: high-flow O2 + observation 3-6h with repeat film, or even conservative outpatient management (PSP trial — many resolve untreated); reliable follow-up required","Primary, larger/symptomatic: needle aspiration or small-bore pigtail (8-14Fr) — small-bore ≈ large-bore for spontaneous PTX with less pain; ambulatory valve devices increasingly used","Secondary PTX: lower threshold for tube + admission (poor reserve)","Traumatic: chest tube for most; occult (CT-only) PTX in stable non-ventilated patients can be observed","Recurrence counseling: no diving ever (unless definitively treated), no flying until resolved, smoking cessation — recurrence ~30%; thoracic surgery referral after 2nd episode or persistent leak"],"pearls":["The needle decompression that 'didn't hiss' doesn't exclude tension — put the finger or tube in","Lung sliding on ultrasound rules OUT pneumothorax at that interspace in seconds","Small primary PTX in a comfortable patient no longer mandates a tube — practice has genuinely changed"],"refs":[{"t":"BTS Pleural Disease Guideline (2023)","u":"https://www.brit-thoracic.org.uk"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Collapsed Lung","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/collapsedlung.html"}]},{"id":"em-cellulitis","name":"Cellulitis & Abscess","sec":"em","present":["Cellulitis: spreading warm erythema, poorly demarcated, tender; usually unilateral — 'bilateral cellulitis' is nearly always stasis dermatitis","Abscess: fluctuant tender mass ± surrounding cellulitis; furuncle/carbuncle spectrum","Red flags for necrotizing infection: pain out of proportion, rapid progression, bullae, crepitus, skin anesthesia, systemic toxicity — see the necrotizing entry"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; POCUS distinguishes abscess (cobblestoning alone = cellulitis; discrete collection = drainable) and changes management in ~1/4 of cases","Mark the border with a pen + timestamp — progression tracking beats memory","Cultures from drained pus guide failures; blood cultures only for systemic toxicity/immunocompromise","Mimics: DVT (unilateral swollen leg — US when uncertain), gout, stasis dermatitis (bilateral, chronic, itchy > tender)"],"tx":["Abscess: incision & drainage is the cure — adequate incision, break loculations, irrigation; packing small abscesses adds pain without benefit; loop drainage is an elegant alternative","Post-I&D antibiotics (TMP-SMX or doxycycline): modest absolute benefit (NNT ~14 for cure) — favor when >2cm, surrounding cellulitis, immunocompromise, systemic features","Nonpurulent cellulitis: cephalexin 500mg QID (strep-directed — MRSA coverage adds nothing for simple nonpurulent per trials); purulent: add MRSA coverage","IV (ceftriaxone/cefazolin; vancomycin if MRSA risk factors) for systemic illness, rapid progression, failed PO, immunocompromise","Elevate the limb (underrated, effective), treat tinea pedis portal of entry, reassess 48-72h — most 'failures' are too-early reassessment or a missed abscess"],"pearls":["Both legs red = look for stasis dermatitis, not double cellulitis","Erythema may extend transiently after starting abx — worsening PAIN or systemic signs define true failure","The 'cellulitis' that failed two antibiotic courses needs an ultrasound and a rethink, not a third antibiotic"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Skin & Soft Tissue Infection Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Cellulitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/cellulitis.html"}]},{"id":"em-septic-joint","name":"Septic Arthritis vs Acute Gout","sec":"em","present":["Both: acutely hot, swollen, exquisitely tender joint with painful ROM — clinical overlap is the whole problem","Septic risk: prosthetic joint, recent instrumentation, bacteremia risk (IVDU, endocarditis), immunosuppression, overlying cellulitis","Gout patterns: podagra (1st MTP), prior identical attacks, triggers (alcohol, purines, diuretics, post-surgical) — but gout history does NOT exclude concurrent infection"],"dx":["Arthrocentesis is the decision — tap the hot joint: cell count/diff, Gram stain, culture, crystals","Synovial WBC: >50k with >90% PMNs = septic until cultures say otherwise; 2k-50k inflammatory (gout/pseudogout live here); crystals (negatively birefringent needles = gout; rhomboid positive = CPPD) — crystals AND infection coexist in ~1-2%","Serum uric acid is useless acutely (normal in 1/3 of flares); ESR/CRP elevated in both","Prosthetic joint concern: do NOT tap through cellulitis or without orthopedics involvement — call first; X-ray for chondrocalcinosis, effusion, erosions"],"tx":["Septic arthritis: IV abx AFTER the tap (vancomycin + ceftriaxone typical; add pseudomonal coverage for IVDU) + urgent orthopedic washout/serial aspiration — cartilage is destroyed in days","Gonococcal arthritis (young, sexually active, migratory ± tenosynovitis/pustules): ceftriaxone + NAAT all sites","Gout flare: NSAIDs full-dose (naproxen 500 BID), colchicine 1.2mg then 0.6mg at 1h, or prednisone 30-40mg ×5d — comorbidities pick the agent; intra-articular steroid for single accessible joint AFTER infection excluded","Do NOT stop allopurinol during a flare if already on it; do not start urate-lowering in the ED (rheum/PCP decision with coverage)","Disposition: septic joints admit; gout goes home with a plan and follow-up"],"pearls":["The tap you talk yourself out of is the diagnosis you miss — when in doubt, needle in joint","'It's just his gout' has preceded many septic joint disasters — fever + first-ever joint or unwell patient = aspirate","Steroids into an infected joint is the classic catastrophe — crystals in the syringe don't fully exonerate; wait for the Gram stain when suspicion is real"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR Gout Guideline (2020)","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Infectious Arthritis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/infectiousarthritis.html"}]},{"id":"em-epistaxis","name":"Epistaxis & ENT Emergencies","sec":"em","present":["Anterior (90%+, Kiesselbach plexus): visible unilateral ooze; posterior: bilateral flow, blood down the pharynx, older/anticoagulated patients — can be life-threatening","Ask: anticoagulants/antiplatelets, hypertension, trauma/picking, prior episodes, bleeding diathesis","Other ENT emergencies in this bucket: peritonsillar abscess (hot potato voice, trismus, uvular deviation), post-tonsillectomy bleed (day 5-10 — surgical emergency), Ludwig angina (woody submandibular swelling, drooling)"],"dx":["Position, blow out clots, inspect with nasal speculum + good light — identify anterior source","Labs only when indicated: INR on warfarin, CBC for heavy/recurrent bleeding, type & screen for posterior bleeds","Recurrent unilateral epistaxis + nasal obstruction in a teen male: juvenile angiofibroma — refer, don't biopsy","Peritonsillar abscess: clinical ± POCUS (intraoral) distinguishing abscess from cellulitis"],"tx":["Anterior stepwise: blow clots → oxymetazoline 2 sprays → direct pressure (squeeze cartilaginous ala) 10-15 min timed, sitting forward — this alone stops most","Visible vessel: silver nitrate cautery 5-10s (one side of septum only — bilateral risks perforation)","Continued bleeding: TXA-soaked pledgets or nasal tampon/anterior pack; dissolvable packing for the anticoagulated; traditional packs → consider anti-staph prophylaxis per local practice, ENT follow-up 48-72h","Posterior: dual-balloon posterior device or Foley technique, admit monitored, ENT urgently — posterior packs cause vagal/hypoxic events","Peritonsillar abscess: needle aspiration or I&D + steroids + abx (amox-clav/clinda); Ludwig/deep space: secure airway early, IV abx, OR — sit them up, awake technique readiness","Anticoagulation: usually continue for minor controlled bleeds with a real indication; reverse for life-threatening hemorrhage"],"pearls":["Most 'failed pressure' was pressure on the nasal bones, not the ala — show the patient where to squeeze and watch the clock","Bleeding controlled + normal vitals: resist the urge to check INR on every well warfarin patient with a settled anterior bleed","The post-tonsillectomy bleed that 'stopped' is the herald bleed — ENT and the OR are the disposition, not discharge"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Epistaxis Guideline (2020)","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Nosebleed","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003106.htm"}]},{"id":"em-eye","name":"Eye Emergencies","sec":"em","present":["The vision-threatening short list: acute angle-closure glaucoma (painful red eye, halos, fixed mid-dilated pupil, rock-hard globe, vomiting), central retinal artery occlusion (sudden painless monocular blindness), retinal detachment (flashes, floaters, curtain), chemical burns, endophthalmitis (post-op/post-injection pain + hypopyon), globe rupture","Corneal abrasion/foreign body: pain, tearing, photophobia, foreign-body sensation after trauma/grinding","Contact lens wearers with a red painful eye: Pseudomonas keratitis until proven otherwise"],"dx":["Every eye complaint: VISUAL ACUITY both eyes (the vital sign of the eye), pupils (RAPD), pressures unless globe rupture suspected, fluorescein exam","Angle closure: IOP often 40-70 (normal 10-21); CRAO: RAPD + cherry-red spot/pale retina; POCUS: retinal detachment, lens dislocation, vitreous hemorrhage (never pressure the suspected ruptured globe)","Globe rupture signs: peaked/teardrop pupil, 360° subconjunctival hemorrhage, extruding contents, Seidel sign (fluorescein waterfall)","Fluorescein: abrasions stain; dendrites = HSV keratitis (no steroids!); flip the lid for the hiding foreign body"],"tx":["CHEMICAL BURN: irrigate IMMEDIATELY (before acuity, before everything) — liters of saline/LR via Morgan lens until pH 7.0-7.5 stable ×30min; alkali is worse than acid; then ophthalmology","Angle closure: ophtho emergently + pressure-lowering cocktail: topical timolol + apraclonidine + pilocarpine, plus acetazolamide 500mg IV, ± mannitol — definitive is laser iridotomy","CRAO: stroke pathway (it IS a stroke) — emergent ophtho/neuro, ocular massage/paracentesis are low-evidence temporizers; hunt the embolic source","Globe rupture: shield (no patch, no pressure, no drops), NPO, antiemetics (prevent Valsalva), IV abx (vanc + ceftazidime), tetanus, emergent ophtho","Corneal abrasion: topical antibiotic (erythromycin; tobramycin/fluoroquinolone for contact lens wearers — cover Pseudomonas), oral analgesia, NO patching, NEVER dispense topical anesthetics; 24-48h follow-up","Retinal detachment/endophthalmitis: emergent ophthalmology — hours matter for the macula"],"pearls":["Irrigation delayed for triage paperwork is vision lost — chemical exposure goes straight to the eyewash","Documented visual acuity protects the patient and the chart — 'unable to assess' is rarely true with a pinhole","The tetracaine that fixed the pain in the ED destroys the cornea at home — comfort in the department, never a take-home"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO EyeWiki & Preferred Practice Patterns","u":"https://eyewiki.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Eye Emergencies","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000054.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-lap-chole","name":"Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Symptomatic cholelithiasis (biliary colic), acute cholecystitis, gallstone pancreatitis (same admission), biliary dyskinesia, polyps ≥1cm","Acute cholecystitis: persistent RUQ pain >6h, fever, Murphy's sign","Elective for recurrent colic — most common general surgery operation (~750k/yr US)"],"dx":["RUQ US: stones, wall >3mm, pericholecystic fluid; HIDA for equivocal cases or dyskinesia (EF <35%)","LFTs/bilirubin/CBD diameter screen for choledocholithiasis → MRCP/EUS ± ERCP before or during surgery","Tokyo Guidelines grade cholecystitis severity and operative timing"],"tx":["Early cholecystectomy (<72h, ideally same admission) for acute cholecystitis — better outcomes than delayed","Perioperative abx for acute cases (ceftriaxone+metronidazole or equivalent); none needed post-op for uncomplicated elective","Prohibitive operative risk: percutaneous cholecystostomy, interval reassessment"],"tech":["4-port technique: umbilical camera, subxiphoid working, two right subcostal retraction ports; reverse Trendelenburg, left tilt","THE CRITICAL VIEW OF SAFETY before clipping anything: hepatocystic triangle cleared of fat/fibrous tissue, lower third of gallbladder off the cystic plate, exactly TWO structures entering the gallbladder","Clip cystic duct and artery ×2 proximal, ×1 distal; divide; dissect gallbladder off the fossa with hook/spatula cautery","Difficult gallbladder bailouts: subtotal (fenestrating or reconstituting) cholecystectomy, fundus-first, or open conversion — conversion is judgment, not failure","Intraoperative cholangiogram or ICG fluorescence for anatomic uncertainty or CBD stone concern"],"after":["Outpatient or 23h stay for elective; diet as tolerated same day, early ambulation","Shoulder-tip pain from CO2 is expected 24-48h; return precautions: fever, jaundice, worsening pain, bilious drainage from wounds","Bile leak presentation (day 2-7: pain, fever, bloating, elevated bili): US/CT → HIDA → ERCP with stent for cystic duct stump leaks","Retained CBD stone: jaundice/pancreatitis post-op → ERCP","Back to work 1-2 weeks laparoscopic; no lifting >10-15 lbs ×2 weeks (port-site hernia prevention)"],"pearls":["Bile duct injury (~0.3%) is the defining complication of a surgical career — the Critical View is non-negotiable; when the anatomy won't declare itself, bail out","The 'easy chole' on the schedule perforates and drains pus; the inflamed one dissects easily — respect them all","Stump the audience: hot gallbladder + cirrhosis + coagulopathy = cholecystostomy tube is the courageous answer"],"refs":[{"t":"SAGES Safe Cholecystectomy Program","u":"https://www.sages.org"},{"t":"Tokyo Guidelines 2018","u":"https://www.jshbps.jp"}]},{"id":"gs-appendectomy","name":"Appendectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Acute appendicitis: periumbilical → RLQ migration, anorexia, low-grade fever; perforation risk rises after 24-48h of symptoms","Complicated: perforation, abscess, phlegmon — different pathway than simple disease","Interval appendectomy after drained abscess: selective (r/o neoplasm >40y with colonoscopy)"],"dx":["CT with IV contrast (adults): >6mm, wall enhancement, stranding, appendicolith; US first-line in children/pregnancy; MRI for equivocal pregnancy cases","Alvarado/AIR scores structure assessment; HCG in all reproductive-age women","Perforation markers: abscess/free fluid, extraluminal air, ill patient"],"tx":["Uncomplicated: laparoscopic appendectomy within 24h (in-hospital delay ≤24h doesn't increase perforation); abx while awaiting OR","Antibiotics-first is a shared-decision alternative (CODA: ~29% crossover by 90d; appendicolith predicts failure) — surgery remains standard in most US practice","Perforated with organized abscess: percutaneous drain + IV→PO abx, colonoscopy if >40y, selective interval appy","Perforated free/diffuse peritonitis: prompt operation + washout"],"tech":["3-port lap: umbilical camera, suprapubic and LLQ working ports","Identify appendix at cecal taeniae convergence; window the mesoappendix at the base","Divide mesoappendix (energy device or endoloop+cautery); divide appendix at base with stapler or between endoloops — a clean base with no residual stump appendicitis risk","Base too inflamed/necrotic: staple across healthy cecum (partial cecectomy); irrigate; retrieve in a bag (never through the port bare)","Normal-appearing appendix during exploration for RLQ pain: take it anyway (microscopic disease, removes future diagnostic confusion) and survey for Meckel's, adnexa, terminal ileum (Crohn's)"],"after":["Uncomplicated: home same day or next morning; diet immediately; abx stop post-op (no benefit to continued doses)","Perforated: 4 days IV-capable abx after source control (STOP-IT trial); watch for post-op abscess (POD 5-7 fevers, ileus → CT, IR drain)","Return to activity 1-2 weeks lap; stump appendicitis is rare but real — 'history of appendectomy' doesn't fully exclude the diagnosis","Pathology follows every specimen: incidental neoplasm (carcinoid/NET most common) may need completion right hemicolectomy by size/margin criteria"],"pearls":["The appendicolith in the pelvis you didn't retrieve becomes the chronic abscess that never resolves — count and collect","Fecalith + failed abx-first management is the CODA lesson: pick surgical candidates wisely","In pregnancy: operate in any trimester — the fetus fears perforation, not laparoscopy"],"refs":[{"t":"CODA Collaborative (NEJM 2020)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"},{"t":"WSES Jerusalem Guidelines: Appendicitis","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"}]},{"id":"gs-inguinal-hernia","name":"Inguinal Hernia Repair (Open & TEP/TAPP)","sec":"gs","present":["Groin bulge worse with standing/Valsalva, reducible ± ache; indirect (through internal ring, lateral to inferior epigastrics) vs direct (Hesselbach triangle, medial)","Incarcerated: irreducible ± obstruction; strangulated: irreducible + toxicity/skin changes — emergency","Watchful waiting is acceptable for minimally symptomatic men (crossover to surgery ~70% by 10y, but low acute risk)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis standing + Valsalva; US/CT/MRI for occult hernias or diagnostic uncertainty","Distinguish femoral hernia (below inguinal ligament, higher strangulation risk — repair, don't watch, especially women)","Preop: anticoagulation plan, urinary symptoms (post-op retention risk)"],"tx":["Symptomatic: elective repair; mesh-based repairs are standard (recurrence without mesh is multiples higher)","Incarcerated without strangulation: attempt reduction (analgesia, Trendelenburg, steady pressure), then urgent-elective repair; strangulation → emergent operation, no reduction attempts of dead bowel back into the abdomen","Approach choice: open (Lichtenstein) under local possible; laparoscopic (TEP/TAPP) for bilateral, recurrent-after-open, faster recovery"],"tech":["Lichtenstein (open, mesh, tension-free): oblique incision, open external oblique, protect ilioinguinal nerve, mobilize cord, reduce/ligate indirect sac (high ligation), flat mesh from pubic tubercle overlap medial to beyond internal ring, slit for cord, tack per technique","TEP (totally extraperitoneal): balloon-develop preperitoneal space, 3 midline ports, reduce sac, wide mesh coverage of myopectineal orifice without entering peritoneum","TAPP (transabdominal preperitoneal): intraperitoneal access, peritoneal flap, same mesh coverage, close flap over mesh","KNOW the danger zones: triangle of doom (vas/vessels — no tacks), triangle of pain (lateral — cutaneous nerves), corona mortis near Cooper's","Strangulated repair: assess bowel viability before mesh decision — contaminated field may push toward tissue repair (Shouldice/Bassini) or biologic"],"after":["Outpatient; expect bruising to scrotum/labia, seroma that mimics recurrence (US distinguishes, most resorb)","Urinary retention is the most common early complication (elderly, spinal anesthesia) — confirm void before discharge","No heavy lifting ~2-4 weeks (evidence increasingly permissive — pain-guided return); driving when off opioids and able to emergency-brake","Chronic postherniorrhaphy pain (~10% meaningful): early = nerve irritation/mesh; refer persistent pain to hernia specialist — avoid reflex reoperation","Recurrence: bulge returning after interval — image, repair via the OPPOSITE plane (open failure → lap redo and vice versa)"],"pearls":["In the groin, the nerve you don't see is the one you tacked — no fixation below the iliopubic tract laterally","Femoral hernias masquerade as inguinal and strangulate quietly — in women, look twice","An 'incarcerated hernia' that reduces en masse (sac and all) still strangulates — re-examine and image the questionable reduction"],"refs":[{"t":"HerniaSurge International Guidelines","u":"https://www.herniasurge.com"},{"t":"SAGES Groin Hernia Resources","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"gs-ventral-hernia","name":"Ventral / Incisional Hernia Repair","sec":"gs","present":["Bulge at prior incision (incisional — up to 20% of laparotomies) or spontaneous (primary ventral: epigastric, spigelian)","Symptoms: bulge, pain, intermittent obstruction; skin changes/thinning over large hernias","Risks: obesity, smoking, diabetes, SSI at index operation, immunosuppression — all modifiable targets before elective repair"],"dx":["CT defines defect size/number ('Swiss cheese'), content, domain loss, rectus width — the operative planning study","Optimize first (elective): BMI ideally <35-40, HbA1c <8, smoking cessation ≥4 weeks (hard stop in many practices)","Small (<1cm) asymptomatic primary defects can be watched"],"tx":["Symptomatic/enlarging: repair; incarceration/obstruction: urgent","Mesh repair standard for defects ≥1-2cm — suture-only recurrence is unacceptable except tiny defects","Approach: laparoscopic/robotic IPOM or retromuscular (open Rives-Stoppa or robotic eTEP/TARUP) — retromuscular positions increasingly favored; complex/loss of domain → component separation (see complex abdominal wall entry)"],"tech":["Principles: reduce contents, clear adhesions off the abdominal wall circumferentially, measure the true defect, wide mesh overlap (≥5cm each direction), close the defect when feasible (restores midline function)","Rives-Stoppa retromuscular: open posterior rectus sheaths, dissect retrorectus plane to linea semilunaris, close posterior sheath, sublay mesh, close anterior fascia over drain","Lap IPOM-plus: adhesiolysis (cold scissors near bowel), primary defect closure with transfascial sutures, coated mesh intraperitoneal with 5cm overlap, tacks/sutures dual fixation","Enterotomy during adhesiolysis: repair, then judgment — clean-contaminated may proceed with sublay/synthetic depending on spillage; gross contamination → staged","Robotic eTEP: crossover access to retrorectus space, defect closure with barbed suture, uncoated mesh extraperitoneal"],"after":["Binder for comfort/seroma control ×4-6 weeks; drains (open retromuscular) out by output criteria","Seroma is near-universal after big repairs — aspirate only if symptomatic/infected (introducing infection into mesh is the disaster)","Activity: core-heavy lifting restricted 4-6 weeks; early walking always","Mesh infection (fever, wound drainage, sinus): CT, abx, attempt salvage for macroporous extra-peritoneal mesh; explant when chronic","Recurrence surveillance: exam ± CT — most within 3 years; reinforce weight/smoking maintenance"],"pearls":["The hernia is a symptom of the abdominal wall's biology — un-optimized repairs recur regardless of technique","Overlap is measured from defect edge AFTER closure, not from where the mesh happens to land","Chronic sinus over an old mesh = infected mesh until proven otherwise; antibiotics alone rarely cure it"],"refs":[{"t":"AHSQC / ACHQC Hernia Resources","u":"https://achqc.org"},{"t":"SAGES Ventral Hernia Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"gs-umbilical-hernia","name":"Umbilical Hernia Repair","sec":"gs","present":["Periumbilical bulge/pain; congenital (usually closes by age 4-5) vs acquired adult (obesity, pregnancy, ascites)","Cirrhotic with ascites + umbilical hernia is a special danger: skin thinning, rupture (Flood syndrome), incarceration after paracentesis","Most adult umbilical hernias slowly enlarge — repair symptomatic ones electively"],"dx":["Clinical; US/CT for uncertainty, obesity, or preop planning of larger defects","In cirrhotics: characterize ascites control, MELD, transplant candidacy — repair timing is a multidisciplinary decision","Defect size drives technique: <1cm vs 1-4cm vs larger"],"tx":["Children: observe to 4-5 years (most close); repair if persistent, >1.5-2cm, or incarceration","Adults symptomatic: elective repair; incarceration → urgent","Cirrhotics: control ascites FIRST (diuretics, TIPS if needed) — elective repair with controlled ascites beats emergency repair of ruptured hernia in every series"],"tech":["Small (<1cm): primary suture repair (interrupted permanent/slow-absorbing) acceptable","≥1cm: mesh reduces recurrence roughly by half (flat preperitoneal patch or plug alternatives; avoid old 'plug in peritoneum' habits)","Curvilinear infraumbilical incision, dissect sac off the umbilical stalk, reduce/invert or excise sac, clear fascial edges, preperitoneal pocket for flat mesh, close fascia over it, tack umbilicus down to fascia for cosmesis","Larger/recurrent: treat as ventral hernia (lap/robotic with wide overlap)","Incarcerated: assess content viability; omental incarceration → resect compromised omentum; bowel compromise converts the operation"],"after":["Outpatient; bruising and a firm 'lump' of healing tissue are expected — counsel or the patient thinks it recurred day 5","Lifting restriction ~2-4 weeks; obesity/ascites counseling to protect the repair","Cirrhotics: monitor for ascites leak (infection risk into repair) — persistent leak needs control, not observation","Recurrence higher with suture-only, obesity, ascites — mesh threshold generous"],"pearls":["The cirrhotic's umbilical hernia is a window into the abdomen — leaks, ruptures, and infects; fix the ascites, then the hernia","In kids, coins taped to hernias fix nothing and macerate skin — reassure the parents instead","Palpate for a second midline (epigastric) defect before closing — they travel in pairs"],"refs":[{"t":"European/American Umbilical Hernia Guidelines (EHS/AHS)","u":"https://www.europeanherniasociety.eu"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Umbilical Hernia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001559.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-femoral-hernia","name":"Femoral & Incarcerated Groin Hernia Repair","sec":"gs","present":["Femoral: bulge below the inguinal ligament, medial thigh crease; female predominance; often presents ALREADY incarcerated/strangulated (up to 40% emergent)","Incarcerated groin hernia: irreducible mass ± obstruction symptoms; strangulation adds pain out of proportion, erythema, toxicity","Elderly woman + SBO + no prior surgery: examine the groins — the missed femoral hernia is a classic"],"dx":["Exam differentiates poorly from inguinal in practice — imaging (US/CT) helps in the obese or ambiguous groin","CT for the obstructed patient: hernia content, transition point, ischemia signs","Femoral canal boundaries (inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament, Cooper's, femoral vein) define the tight unforgiving neck — hence the strangulation risk"],"tx":["ALL femoral hernias: repair on diagnosis (no watchful waiting) — strangulation risk too high","Incarcerated, no strangulation signs: one gentle reduction attempt with analgesia/positioning → urgent repair same admission","Strangulation suspected: resuscitate, abx, emergent OR — do NOT forcibly reduce (dead bowel returned to the abdomen declares itself late)"],"tech":["Elective femoral: open infra-inguinal (Lockwood), trans-inguinal (Lotheissen), or high (McEvedy) approaches — or laparoscopic (TEP/TAPP) which covers the femoral space elegantly with one mesh over the whole myopectineal orifice","McVay (Cooper's ligament repair) is the classic tissue repair addressing the femoral space when mesh is unwanted/contaminated","Emergent: McEvedy or midline/laparoscopy gives bowel access; open sac BEFORE reducing to inspect content viability","Nonviable bowel: resect via the same access or convert to laparotomy/lap-assisted; contaminated field pushes toward tissue repair or biologic/absorbable mesh per judgment","Release the constricting ring carefully — lacunar ligament division risks an aberrant obturator artery (corona mortis)"],"after":["Uncomplicated elective: outpatient, same expectations as inguinal repair","Post-bowel-resection: inpatient, NGT per ileus, abx short-course, monitor anastomosis","Watch the 'reduced en masse' patient: persistent pain/obstipation after apparent reduction → CT — the loop may still be trapped in the sac","Counsel on the contralateral groin — femoral hernias are bilateral often enough to examine and image liberally"],"pearls":["A groin hernia in a woman is femoral until proven otherwise — and femoral means operate","The strangulated hernia's toxicity is sometimes the only sign; the overlying skin can look innocent","During emergent repair, find the bowel FIRST — the loop that slipped back inside during induction still needs inspection (laparoscope through the sac works)"],"refs":[{"t":"HerniaSurge International Guidelines","u":"https://www.herniasurge.com"},{"t":"WSES Emergency Hernia Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"}]},{"id":"gs-diag-lap","name":"Diagnostic Laparoscopy","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: acute abdomen with unclear diagnosis (esp. young women — appendicitis vs adnexal), stable penetrating trauma to assess peritoneal violation/diaphragm, staging of malignancy (gastric, pancreatic, peritoneal disease), chronic pain workup (selective), second-look for mesenteric ischemia","Increasingly the FIRST move in equivocal peritonitis — diagnosis and treatment in one setting"],"dx":["Preop: exhaust reasonable imaging first (CT/US); laparoscopy answers what imaging can't — peritoneal surfaces, small perforations, diaphragm, adnexa, bowel viability","Trauma: left thoracoabdominal penetrating wounds → laparoscopy specifically excludes diaphragm injury (CT is poor at this)","Staging: washings for cytology + inspection of peritoneum/liver surface changes management in a large minority of gastric/pancreatic cancers before futile laparotomy"],"tx":["Systematic exploration converts to the therapeutic operation when pathology is found — appendectomy, patch, washout, adnexal surgery, hernia reduction","Negative laparoscopy in the acute abdomen is a RESULT, not a failure — it prevents observation of a surgical belly or laparotomy for a medical one"],"tech":["Entry: open Hasson at umbilicus (safest teaching default) or Veress with checks; adjust entry away from scars (prior midline → Palmer's point LUQ)","Pneumoperitoneum 12-15 mmHg; 30° scope; systematic survey: run all four quadrants, pelvis (uterus/adnexa/bladder), both diaphragms, liver surfaces, stomach, spleen tip","Run the small bowel proximal-to-distal with atraumatic graspers hand-over-hand from ligament of Treitz — the discipline that finds the second injury/the Meckel's","Add ports as the findings demand; irrigate/culture free fluid; biopsy suspicious peritoneal nodules","Convert to open without apology for: uncontrolled bleeding, inability to visualize, extensive adhesions in an unstable patient"],"after":["Uncomplicated negative diagnostic lap: home same day/next morning; incorporate the answer (surgical disease excluded) into ongoing medical workup","Post-therapeutic: per the definitive procedure performed","Counsel about the small missed-injury rate — return precautions matter after negative trauma laparoscopy","Port-site metastasis is rare and mostly a marker of disease biology — not a reason to avoid staging laparoscopy"],"pearls":["The operation is only as good as the bowel run — 'looked around and it was fine' misses the back-wall perforation","In penetrating trauma, laparoscopy's best answer is the intact diaphragm and peritoneum — it changes disposition from admission to discharge","Frozen-section the unexpected nodule before the big resection proceeds on assumptions"],"refs":[{"t":"SAGES Diagnostic Laparoscopy Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"},{"t":"EAST Penetrating Trauma Resources","u":"https://www.east.org"}]},{"id":"gs-ex-lap","name":"Exploratory Laparotomy (Trauma & Acute Abdomen)","sec":"gs","present":["Trauma indications: hemodynamic instability + positive FAST, peritonitis, evisceration, gunshot traversing the peritoneum, refractory shock with abdominal source","Acute abdomen: diffuse peritonitis, free air with instability, mesenteric ischemia, uncontrolled GI hemorrhage after endoscopic/IR failure","The damage-control decision (abbreviated laparotomy) is made EARLY: the lethal triad — acidosis (pH<7.2), hypothermia (<34°C), coagulopathy — is prevented, not treated"],"dx":["Unstable: FAST + judgment — the OR is the diagnostic test; stable: CT defines injuries and may divert to nonoperative/IR management","Massive transfusion triggers (ABC score, shock index) activate early balanced resuscitation","Communicate with anesthesia constantly — the operation's tempo is set by the physiology, not the anatomy"],"tx":["Trauma laparotomy sequence: control HEMORRHAGE, control CONTAMINATION, then reconstruct if physiology allows — or pack and leave","Nontrauma: source control (resect, patch, drain, washout) proportional to physiology; damage-control applies to the septic abdomen too","Resuscitate with blood products in balanced ratios; TXA within 3h in trauma hemorrhage"],"tech":["Generous midline incision, evacuate blood, PACK ALL FOUR QUADRANTS immediately, then unpack systematically starting AWAY from the suspected bleeding","Hemorrhage control toolbox: direct pressure, Pringle maneuver (portal triad clamp) for liver, splenectomy for shattered spleen in the unstable, vascular clamps/shunts, pelvic packing; supraceliac aortic control for exsanguination","Contamination: rapid staple/whipstitch of bowel injuries (no anastomoses in damage control), pack, temporary abdominal closure (negative-pressure dressing)","Full trauma survey when stable enough: run bowel ligament of Treitz to rectum, open lesser sac, mobilize duodenum (Kocher) for central hematomas, evaluate retroperitoneal zones by mechanism (penetrating zone II/III explored, blunt selective)","Definitive reconstruction at 24-48h relook once warm, resuscitated, coagulation restored: anastomoses, ostomies as needed, sequential closure — fight for fascial closure before the abdomen 'freezes'"],"after":["ICU: rewarm, correct coagulopathy (TEG-guided), watch abdominal compartment syndrome (bladder pressure >20-25 + organ dysfunction → open the abdomen)","Planned relook 24-48h for damage control; open abdomens lose protein and fluid — nutrition early (enteral when bowel in continuity)","Post-laparotomy expectations: ileus days, incisional hernia rate substantial long-term — but the survival came first","Missed injury vigilance: unexplained fevers, rising WBC, drain changes → re-image; the second look is cheaper than the fourth"],"pearls":["Pack first, think second — the quadrant packing that controls venous bleeding buys the anesthesia catch-up time that saves the case","The best damage-control decision is made 10 minutes into the case, not 3 hours in","In the medical peritonitis laparotomy, do enough and no more — heroic reconstructions in septic shock die of the reconstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"ATLS / ACS Committee on Trauma","u":"https://www.facs.org"},{"t":"WSES Damage Control Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"}]},{"id":"gs-right-colectomy","name":"Right Colectomy / Ileocecectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: right-sided colon cancer, cecal volvulus, appendiceal neoplasms with base involvement, terminal ileal Crohn's (ileocecectomy), bleeding/ischemia of the right colon","Right colon cancers present late: anemia + fatigue (occult bleeding) more than obstruction","Crohn's ileocecectomy: obstructing fibrostenotic disease or penetrating complications after medical failure"],"dx":["Colonoscopy with biopsy + tattoo distal to lesion; CT chest/abd/pelvis staging; CEA baseline","Complete colonoscopy to exclude synchronous lesions (or CT colonography if obstructed, scope the remnant post-op)","Crohn's: MR/CT enterography maps disease extent; optimize nutrition, wean steroids when feasible before elective resection"],"tx":["Oncologic right colectomy: resect with ≥12 lymph nodes, high vascular ligation (ileocolic ± right branch of middle colic), 5-7cm margins — laparoscopic equivalent to open oncologically","Cecal volvulus: resection (right colectomy) — detorsion/pexy alone recurs","Crohn's: resect grossly diseased bowel only (2cm margins) — microscopic margins don't predict recurrence; preserve length"],"tech":["Lap medial-to-lateral approach: identify/ligate ileocolic pedicle at its origin off the SMA/SMV, develop the retromesenteric plane over the duodenum (PROTECT it and the ureter/gonadals), take right branch of middle colic for extended indications","Mobilize hepatic flexure, divide terminal ileum and transverse colon with staplers","Anastomosis: side-to-side stapled ileocolic (isoperistaltic or antiperistaltic) or handsewn — check perfusion (ICG where available), no tension, close mesenteric defect per practice","Crohn's: consider Kono-S anastomosis (recurrence data favorable); handle thickened mesentery with suture-ligation/energy respect — the Crohn's mesentery bleeds","Intracorporeal vs extracorporeal anastomosis: intracorporeal trends toward fewer wound/extraction complications"],"after":["Enhanced recovery: early feeding + ambulation POD0-1, minimize opioids (blocks/multimodal), expect discharge POD2-4 lap","Anastomotic leak (2-5%): POD3-7 fever, tachycardia (often the ONLY early sign), rising WBC/CRP, ileus → CT with contrast; contained → drain + abx; free → return to OR","Oncology follow-up: pathology review, adjuvant chemo for stage III (and high-risk II), CEA + colonoscopy surveillance schedule","Crohn's: post-op prophylaxis discussion (biologics for high-risk) + colonoscopy at 6-12 months (Rutgeerts score drives escalation)"],"pearls":["Persistent tachycardia after colectomy is a leak until proven otherwise — the 'anxiety' diagnosis has buried patients","The duodenum is the right colectomy's landmine — see it, name it, protect it during the medial dissection","In Crohn's, the operation treats complications, not the disease — resect conservatively, plan medically"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Colon Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ASCRS Colectomy Resources","u":"https://fascrs.org"}]},{"id":"gs-left-colectomy","name":"Left Colectomy & Sigmoidectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: left colon/sigmoid cancer, recurrent/complicated diverticulitis, sigmoid volvulus, ischemia","Left-sided cancers: change in caliber/habits, obstruction, hematochezia","Sigmoid volvulus: massive distension, elderly/institutionalized, 'coffee bean' on imaging — endoscopic detorsion first, then semi-elective resection same admission (recurrence ~60% untreated)"],"dx":["Colonoscopy + tattoo; staging CT + CEA for cancer; complete colon evaluation","Diverticulitis: operate for the DISEASE COURSE (smoldering, fistula, stricture, immunosuppression), not an episode count — elective decisions individualized after CT-proven episodes","Obstructed left colon: gastrografin study/CT distinguishes pseudo-obstruction; assess cecal diameter (>12cm = perforation risk)"],"tx":["Elective oncologic: high ligation of IMA (or superior rectal preserving left colic per anatomy), ≥12 nodes, anastomosis with splenic flexure mobilization as needed for reach","Obstructing cancer options: resection + primary anastomosis ± diversion, Hartmann's, or endoscopic stent as bridge-to-surgery in selected patients","Elective diverticular resection: resect to the top of the RECTUM distally (retained sigmoid = recurrence), proximal margin to soft compliant bowel — not every diverticulum"],"tech":["Medial-to-lateral: score at sacral promontory, enter the avascular presacral-mesocolic plane, IDENTIFY THE LEFT URETER before any pedicle division (stents for reoperative/inflamed pelvis are a phone call, not a weakness), ligate IMA/IMV","Splenic flexure mobilization for tension-free reach: enter lesser sac, take renocolic/splenocolic attachments — gentle traction (the splenic capsule tear is a classic)","Divide at the upper rectum (taeniae splay = rectum), EEA circular stapled colorectal anastomosis: sized dilators, purse-string or anvil in proximal colon, pass stapler transanally, marry without twist","TEST THE ANASTOMOSIS: air leak test under saline with proximal occlusion + inspect donuts — a positive test found in the OR is a fixable problem","Consider diverting loop ileostomy for low/tenuous anastomoses, immunosuppressed, or leak-test concerns"],"after":["ERAS pathway as with right colectomy; expect POD2-5 discharge uncomplicated","Leak surveillance identical — left-sided/colorectal anastomoses leak more than ileocolic; drainage/diversion/redo per severity","Volvulus patients: address the underlying colonic dysmotility expectations; recurrence after resection is rare (vs pexy)","Diverticular disease counseling: fiber, weight, and the reassurance that resection targeted the sigmoid — recurrence elsewhere is uncommon after proper distal margin"],"pearls":["The distal margin for diverticulitis is the rectum — leaving distal sigmoid is the technical cause of 'recurrent diverticulitis after resection'","Find the ureter early, and find it again before firing anything","Splenic flexure bleeding usually starts with enthusiasm, not anatomy — retract the omentum, not the spleen"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Diverticulitis & Colectomy Guidelines","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"NCCN Colon Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"gs-lar","name":"Low Anterior Resection (LAR)","sec":"gs","present":["Rectal cancer (mid/upper, and low with sphincter preservation feasible) — the operation that removes the rectum and reconnects","Rectal bleeding, tenesmus, caliber change; locally advanced disease found on staging MRI","Modern pathway: many locally advanced rectal cancers get total neoadjuvant therapy (TNT) first; complete clinical responders may enter watch-and-wait protocols"],"dx":["Rigid/flexible proctoscopy measures the EXACT distance from the anal verge (drives every decision); biopsy","Pelvic MRI: T/N stage, circumferential resection margin (CRM) threat, extramural vascular invasion; CT chest/abd for distant disease; CEA","Full colonoscopy for synchronous lesions; multidisciplinary tumor board — rectal cancer is a team disease"],"tx":["Neoadjuvant (chemo)radiation/TNT for threatened CRM, T3-4, node-positive; then TME surgery 8-12+ weeks later","Total mesorectal excision (TME) is the oncologic core: intact mesorectal envelope, sharp dissection — halved local recurrence historically","Distal margin: 2cm acceptable, 1cm for low tumors after CRT with clear frozen section"],"tech":["High ligation of IMA, splenic flexure mobilization (routine for LAR — reach matters), IMV division near ligament of Treitz","TME planes: the 'holy plane' between visceral and parietal pelvic fascia — sharp scissors/energy under direct vision, protect hypogastric nerves (posterolateral, sexual/urinary function), sweep the mesorectum intact","Anterior dissection along Denonvilliers' fascia (protect seminal vesicles/prostate or vagina); circumferential mobilization below the tumor","Distal division with reticulating stapler (avoid crossing multiple fires low in the pelvis), EEA coloanal/colorectal anastomosis ± colonic J-pouch/side-to-end for low reconstructions","DIVERTING LOOP ILEOSTOMY for most low anastomoses (≤5-7cm), post-radiation, positive leak test, or judgment — it doesn't prevent leaks but blunts their consequence"],"after":["ERAS; monitor for leak (presentation may be delayed with diversion — pelvic abscess, failure to thrive)","Low anterior resection syndrome (LARS — frequency, urgency, clustering, incontinence): counsel EVERYONE preop; manage with fiber, loperamide, pelvic floor therapy, transanal irrigation for severe cases","Ileostomy education: dehydration is the #1 readmission — high-output pathway (>1200mL/day → fluids, loperamide, psyllium); reversal ~2-3+ months after healed anastomosis confirmed (contrast enema/scope)","Surveillance: CEA, imaging, endoscopy per NCCN; sexual/urinary dysfunction screening — ask, because patients won't volunteer it"],"pearls":["The specimen photo of an intact, shiny mesorectum is the operation's report card","Nerve injury happens where the dissection wanders off the holy plane — anterolateral at the vesicles and lateral stalks","LARS discussed preoperatively is expected physiology; discovered postoperatively it's a complication — same symptoms, different conversation"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Rectal Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ASCRS Rectal Cancer Resources","u":"https://fascrs.org"}]},{"id":"gs-apr","name":"Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)","sec":"gs","present":["Distal rectal/anal canal cancers invading the sphincter complex or too low for restorative resection; recurrent anal SCC after chemoradiation (salvage APR); incontinent patients where restoration is pointless","End result: no anus, permanent end colostomy — the counseling is as important as the operation","Anal SCC is treated FIRST with definitive chemoradiation (Nigro) — APR is for persistence/recurrence"],"dx":["MRI pelvis defines sphincter/levator involvement; EUA when tolerance limits exam; biopsy histology matters (adeno vs SCC = different algorithms)","PET/CT often added for anal SCC recurrence staging","Preop stoma-site marking by ostomy nurse (sitting, standing, belt line) — a poorly sited stoma is a daily complication for life"],"tx":["Neoadjuvant per rectal adenocarcinoma pathways; salvage APR for anal SCC after confirmed persistent disease (post-CRT changes can take 6 months to declare — biopsy-proven before committing)","Extralevator APR (ELAPE) for advanced low tumors: wider perineal cylinder, fewer positive margins/'waists' on the specimen","Perineal reconstruction planning (primary closure vs flap — VRAM/gracilis) especially after radiation"],"tech":["Abdominal phase: standard TME dissection to the levators, sigmoid divided, end colostomy fashioned through rectus, pelvic floor dissection stops WITHOUT coning in toward the tumor","Perineal phase (lone-star retractor, often lithotomy or prone-jackknife for ELAPE): elliptical incision around closed anus, purse-string the anus shut first, dissect ischioanal fat to levators, divide levators widely laterally, connect with the abdominal plane, deliver specimen","Watch anteriorly at the prostate/vagina (the classic injury plane) and the urethra in males; posteriorly stay off the coccyx bleeding","Irradiated perineum or large defect: myocutaneous flap fills dead space and brings unirradiated blood supply — plan jointly with plastics BEFORE the gap exists","Meticulous perineal hemostasis + drainage; close in layers without tension"],"after":["Perineal wound is the defining morbidity (breakdown in up to 30-40% after radiation): sitz care, offloading cushions, wound care team early; flap protocols when performed","Colostomy teaching intensively before discharge; expect pouching pattern to mature over weeks","Phantom rectal sensations are common and normal — warn patients","Urinary/sexual dysfunction rates exceed LAR (wider pelvic dissection) — screen and refer","Surveillance per histology-specific guidelines; inspect the perineum at every visit (perineal recurrence is visible/palpable early)"],"pearls":["The stoma site marked in clinic beats any site chosen through a laparotomy incision with the patient supine and asleep","'Waisting' of the APR specimen at the levels of the levators = the plane cut into tumor territory — ELAPE exists to prevent exactly that","Delayed perineal healing after radiation is the rule, not a surprise — set the timeline expectation at months"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Rectal & Anal Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ASCRS APR Resources","u":"https://fascrs.org"}]},{"id":"gs-hartmanns","name":"Hartmann's Procedure","sec":"gs","present":["The septic pelvis operation: perforated diverticulitis with feculent/purulent peritonitis (Hinchey III-IV), obstructing/perforated sigmoid cancer in the unstable, anastomotic catastrophe, ischemia — resect the sigmoid, staple the rectum closed, end colostomy","Chosen when anastomosis is unsafe: hemodynamic instability, gross contamination, immunosuppression, questionable tissue","Alternative in selected stable Hinchey III: laparoscopic lavage (controversial) or resection + primary anastomosis with diverting ileostomy — trial data (LADIES/DIVERTI) supports RPA+DI in the right patient"],"dx":["CT with contrast: free air, feculent contamination, abscess mapping","Physiology drives the decision more than anatomy: lactate, pressors, coagulopathy → damage-control mindset","Intraoperative findings finalize the choice — the decision tree lives in the OR"],"tx":["Resuscitate + broad-spectrum abx en route to source control; the operation IS the sepsis treatment","Resect the perforated segment to healthy margins (distal = upper rectum, again)","Damage-control variant: resect, staple both ends, temporary closure, decide reconstruction at relook when physiology recovers"],"tech":["Midline laparotomy (lap-assisted feasible in stable patients), evacuate contamination, control the perforation early (clamp/staple across it)","Mobilize sigmoid, identify LEFT URETER (inflamed, retracted planes — stents help in redo/phlegmonous pelvises), ligate vessels at convenient level (oncologic ligation if cancer suspected — treat unknowns as cancer)","Divide upper rectum with stapler; LEAVE LANDMARKS for the reversal: long nonabsorbable sutures on the rectal stump, tack stump high if possible — future-you is the beneficiary","Washout thoroughly; end colostomy through rectus at the pre-marked/best-judgment site with adequate spout and no tension; drains to the pelvis per contamination","Record stump length and anatomy in the op note in detail — the reversal surgeon (often you) reads it in 6 months"],"after":["Sepsis recovery: ICU per severity, abx 4 days after source control (STOP-IT), wound often managed open/NPWT in contamination","Colostomy education + high rate of parastomal issues long-term — counsel","Rectal stump 'blowout' presents as pelvic sepsis — low threshold to image; some divert stump secretions via rectal tube early per practice","Hartmann's REVERSAL: 3-6+ months later, only ~50-60% ever get reversed (frailty, adhesions) — set honest expectations at the first operation; preop before reversal: stump evaluation (contrast/scope), nutrition, smoking","Reversal is a major operation: adhesiolysis, stump identification (those landmarks), EEA anastomosis ± diversion"],"pearls":["The best Hartmann's is the one whose op note makes the reversal easy — landmark the stump like you'll be the one hunting for it","Telling the patient 'temporary colostomy' without the reversal statistics writes a check that half of them never cash","Primary anastomosis with diversion in the stable Hinchey III patient is defensible modern surgery — know the trials, know your patient"],"refs":[{"t":"WSES Diverticulitis Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"},{"t":"ASCRS Emergency Colorectal Surgery","u":"https://fascrs.org"}]},{"id":"gs-ostomy","name":"Ostomy Creation & Reversal","sec":"gs","present":["Ostomies protect (diverting loop ileostomy over a low anastomosis), decompress (obstruction), or divert permanently (end colostomy after APR)","Loop ileostomy: easiest to create/reverse, high-output physiology; loop colostomy: bulkier, lower output; end stomas: definitive or staged","The stoma is the operation the patient lives with — siting, education, and follow-up determine quality of life"],"dx":["Preoperative marking by WOC/ostomy nurse whenever remotely possible: through the rectus, away from creases/scars/belt, visible to the patient sitting","Before ANY reversal: confirm distal continuity/anastomotic healing (contrast enema and/or endoscopy), sphincter adequacy for colorectal reversals, nutritional readiness","Assess the abdominal wall — parastomal hernia complicates both the stoma and its reversal"],"tx":["Creation principles: adequate mobilization for a TENSION-FREE stoma, viable well-perfused bowel, spout everted (ileostomy 2-3cm — flush ileostomies leak and destroy skin)","Reversal timing: loop ileostomy typically 8-12+ weeks after index surgery once distal healing confirmed (earlier protocols exist); Hartmann's reversal 3-6+ months","High-output stoma management is medical: fluid/electrolyte protocol, antimotility agents, dietitian"],"tech":["Trephine through rectus: disc of skin, split fascia cruciate ~2 fingerbreadths, deliver bowel without twist (check mesentery orientation)","Loop ileostomy: deliver loop over rod/bridge per practice, open distal-transverse, evert proximal limb to a Brooke spout with 3-point sutures","End colostomy: adequate spout even for colon (flush colostomies retract), mature primarily; consider prophylactic mesh in permanent end colostomies (parastomal hernia reduction — evidence favorable)","Loop ileostomy reversal: peristomal incision, mobilize both limbs off fascia, stapled side-to-side functional end-to-end (or handsewn), drop back, close fascia, PURSE-STRING skin closure (SSI rates markedly lower than linear)","Hartmann's reversal: full adhesiolysis, find/refresh the rectal stump (landmarks!), EEA circular anastomosis, leak-test"],"after":["New ostomy: staged teaching (appliance changes, output tracking), dehydration prevention pathway for ileostomies (#1 readmission cause: target output <1200mL/d, oral rehydration solutions, loperamide scheduled not PRN)","Peristomal skin: most problems are appliance-fit problems — WOC nurse beats steroid cream","Watch early: stoma necrosis (dusky → assess depth with test tube/scope light — above fascia observe, below fascia return to OR), retraction, high output","Late: parastomal hernia (most permanent stomas eventually — repair for symptoms/obstruction, mesh-based), prolapse, stenosis","Post-reversal: expect irregular bowel habits weeks-months (esp. after long diversion — 'disuse' rectum); LARS management after low anastomoses"],"pearls":["No patient ever complained the stoma spout was too well-everted — flush stomas are the gift that keeps on leaking","The ileostomy dehydration readmission is preventable at discharge with a written output protocol — hand it to them","Before reversing anything, prove the downstream plumbing works — a contrast enema costs an afternoon; a missed stricture costs a redo"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Ostomy Guidelines","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"United Ostomy Associations of America","u":"https://www.ostomy.org"}]},{"id":"gs-sbr","name":"Small Bowel Resection & Anastomosis","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: ischemia (strangulation, mesenteric occlusion), perforation, Crohn's complications, tumors (GIST, NET, adenocarcinoma), Meckel's complications, trauma, radiation injury","The recurring intraoperative question of general surgery: is this bowel viable, and where do I cut?","Short bowel awareness: every centimeter matters in the multiply-resected patient — measure and document remaining length"],"dx":["CT with contrast: pneumatosis, portal venous gas, mesenteric vessel cutoff, closed loops","Intraop viability: color, peristalsis, mesenteric pulsation/bleeding at cut edge, Doppler, ICG fluorescence where available; marginal bowel → warm packs + reassess in 10-15 min, or plan a second look","NETs: often multifocal — palpate the ENTIRE small bowel"],"tx":["Resect to clearly viable, bleeding margins; oncologic resections take the wedge of mesentery/nodes (adenocarcinoma, NET)","Uncertain viability + sick patient: staple both ends, damage control, SECOND-LOOK laparotomy at 24-48h — the plan is made before leaving the OR, not contingent on deterioration","Crohn's: conservative margins (2cm grossly normal), strictureplasty (Heineke-Mikulicz short / Finney longer) to preserve length in fibrostenotic disease without perforation"],"tech":["Resection: score mesentery in a V toward the base (wider for oncologic), seal/ligate vessels, divide bowel with GIA at slight angle favoring antimesenteric perfusion","Stapled side-to-side functional end-to-end: align antimesenteric borders, GIA through enterotomies, close common channel with TA/GIA, offset staple lines, check the crotch (the classic bleeding point)","Handsewn end-to-end (single or two-layer): full-thickness bites catching submucosa (the strength layer), mesenteric-side attention, size mismatch handled with Cheatle slit","Close the mesenteric defect (internal hernia prevention) — debated in some contexts, cheap insurance in most","Principles trump technique choice: TENSION-FREE, WELL-PERFUSED, HEALTHY EDGES, accurate apposition — stapled vs handsewn outcomes equivalent in elective settings"],"after":["Ileus expected 2-4 days; feed when awake bowel signals (ERAS: early attempts safe); NGT selectively, not routinely","Leak window POD3-7: tachycardia, pain, fever, drain character change → CT; proximal leaks are fluid/electrolyte disasters — control sepsis, drain, consider proximal diversion/fistula management","Massive resection: track length remaining (<180-200cm = risk; <100cm with no colon = likely parenteral support) — early nutrition/SBS team involvement, PPI (hypergastrinemia), antimotility strategy","Crohn's: same post-op prophylaxis conversation as ileocecectomy"],"pearls":["The anastomosis heals on blood supply and lies about it until POD5 — when the margin oozed dark blood, resect more","In the mesentery of a NET, the nodal mass is often bigger than the primary — chase the mesentery, palpate the whole bowel for satellites","Strictureplasty in Crohn's is bowel currency saved — resect what perforates, strictureplasty what obstructs"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS/SSAT Small Bowel Resources","u":"https://ssat.com"},{"t":"ECCO Crohn's Surgical Guidelines","u":"https://www.ecco-ibd.eu"}]},{"id":"gs-loa","name":"Lysis of Adhesions","sec":"gs","present":["Adhesive SBO failing nonoperative management (48-72h), recurrent obstructions, strangulation signs at presentation, chronic pain (rarely operative — outcomes poor for pain alone)","Adhesions cause ~60-70% of SBOs in operated abdomens; the operation risks creating what it treats","Virgin-abdomen 'adhesive' SBO deserves suspicion of another cause (tumor, hernia, gallstone ileus)"],"dx":["CT: transition point, free fluid, mesenteric edema/swirl, reduced enhancement (ischemia), closed loop (two transition points — operate, don't watch)","Gastrografin challenge in uncomplicated adhesive SBO: contrast in colon by 8-24h predicts nonoperative success and is mildly therapeutic","Serial exams remain the monitor — worsening pain/tenderness overrides any imaging schedule"],"tx":["Operate for: strangulation/closed loop at presentation, failed 48-72h nonoperative trial, worsening exam/labs","Laparoscopic approach in experienced hands for favorable anatomy (first-time obstruction, single-band expectation, non-distended enough to work) — convert liberally","The goal is TARGETED lysis: free the obstruction, run the bowel, resect the dead — not a total adhesiolysis crusade"],"tech":["Entry away from scars (Palmer's point, or open cutdown well clear of prior midline); first look determines feasibility — massively dilated loops + no working space = open","Find COLLAPSED distal bowel and walk it back to the transition point — collapsed bowel leads to the culprit band; dilated bowel leads everywhere","Sharp dissection (cold scissors) for bowel-to-bowel adhesions; energy devices sparingly near bowel wall; traction-countertraction in the correct areolar plane","Serosal tears: repair seromuscular; full-thickness enterotomy: repair transversely or resect segment if multiple/compromised — COUNT and address every injury before closing (the missed enterotomy is the lethal one)","After relief: run the entire small bowel LOT to ICV, verify the released segment pinks up, decompress retrograde into NGT if massively dilated (closure and ventilation)"],"after":["Ileus proportional to manipulation; feed on function; NGT per distension/vomiting","Watch for delayed (POD2-5) presentation of a missed thermal injury or enterotomy: new fever, tachycardia, peritonitis → immediate CT/OR — do not rationalize it as ileus","Recurrence counseling: adhesive obstructions recur (~15-30% long-term) regardless of technique; adhesion barriers (hyaluronate films) modestly reduce adhesion burden — used selectively","Chronic pain patients: set expectations low preop or decline — adhesiolysis for pain alone helps a minority and reoperates many"],"pearls":["The transition point found on CT is a suggestion; the transition point found by walking collapsed bowel is the diagnosis","Every difficult adhesiolysis ends with the same question — 'did I make a hole I haven't found?' Answer it with a systematic re-run, not optimism","The best adhesiolysis is the one done sharply in the right plane; planes are earned with patience, not power"],"refs":[{"t":"Bologna Guidelines: Adhesive SBO (WSES)","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"},{"t":"EAST SBO Management Guidelines","u":"https://www.east.org"}]},{"id":"gs-graham-patch","name":"Perforated Peptic Ulcer Repair (Graham Patch)","sec":"gs","present":["Sudden severe epigastric pain → rigid boardlike abdomen; free air under diaphragm (~80%); antecedent NSAIDs, H. pylori, smoking","Elderly/steroid-dependent may present blunted — 'silent perforation' with sepsis","Duodenal (most common site for perforation) vs gastric ulcer perforation — gastric adds a malignancy question"],"dx":["Upright CXR free air is quick; CT with oral contrast when equivocal (finds the sealed microperforation too)","Labs: leukocytosis, lactate, amylase mildly up (DDx pancreatitis)","Sealed perforation in a stable, non-peritonitic patient (contrast doesn't leak): selective nonoperative management with NGT, PPI, abx — serial exams, low threshold to abandon"],"tx":["Resuscitate: fluids, IV PPI infusion, broad-spectrum abx + antifungal consideration (perforations >24h), NGT decompression","Definitive: prompt operative source control — laparoscopic patch is now the default in experienced centers for standard perforations","H. pylori eradication after recovery is the RECURRENCE operation — test and treat, always"],"tech":["Laparoscopic (or open) exploration: find the perforation (usually anterior duodenal bulb/prejunctional); thorough washout of all quadrants (the peritonitis is the disease)","Graham patch: pedicled omentum drawn over the perforation, secured with 3-4 interrupted sutures placed through healthy edges TIED OVER the omental plug (modified Graham: close the hole first, then patch) — omentum is the seal, sutures just hold it","Do NOT try to excise/close large friable defects primarily — big (>2cm) or destroyed duodenum escalates: controlled fistula (tube duodenostomy), pyloric exclusion, or resection — call for help early","GASTRIC ulcer perforation: BIOPSY the edge (malignancy in ~5-10%) then patch/wedge-excise per size/location","Leak-test with air/methylene blue via NGT; drain per contamination/judgment"],"after":["NGT until function returns; PPI IV→PO for 4-8 weeks minimum; abx per source-control principles","Water-soluble contrast study before feeding is selective (large repairs, concern) rather than routine","H. pylori: test (stool antigen/biopsy) and eradicate; STOP NSAIDs or co-protect if truly unavoidable; smoking cessation","Gastric ulcer pathology follow-up mandatory — and endoscopy at 6-8 weeks for gastric ulcers to confirm healing/exclude missed cancer","Failure to improve (fever, tachycardia, drain bile): CT — patch leak → drain/redo/divert per anatomy"],"pearls":["Wash out the abdomen like the ulcer was incidental — patients die of the peritonitis, not the hole","The gastric ulcer you patched without biopsy may be the cancer you staged with a 3-month delay","A patch under tension on friable duodenum fails by POD4 — when the tissue says no, change operations, don't add sutures"],"refs":[{"t":"WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"},{"t":"SAGES Foregut Resources","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"gs-peg","name":"Gastrostomy / PEG Placement","sec":"gs","present":["Durable enteral access (>4-6 weeks anticipated): neurologic dysphagia (stroke, ALS), head/neck cancer (often pre-treatment), trauma, decompression (malignant obstruction 'venting PEG')","The ethics precede the procedure: PEG in advanced dementia does not improve survival or aspiration — goals-of-care conversation is part of the consult","Contraindications: uncorrectable coagulopathy, no safe window (interposed colon/liver), gastric outlet obstruction (for feeding), limited prognosis without comfort rationale"],"dx":["Confirm indication + anatomy: prior gastric surgery changes everything (consider surgical/laparoscopic or jejunal access)","Review anticoagulation/antiplatelets (procedural bleeding plan), ascites (relative contraindication — drain/optimize first)","Aspiration-risk patients may need post-pyloric (PEG-J/DPEJ) instead — feeding the stomach doesn't prevent aspirating saliva"],"tx":["PEG (endoscopic pull technique) is the workhorse; image-guided (IR) gastrostomy or surgical (open Stamm/laparoscopic) when endoscopy blocked or anatomy hostile","Peri-procedural single-dose antibiotic (cefazolin) reduces peristomal infection — evidence-backed","Venting gastrostomy for malignant obstruction is a palliative kindness — different consent conversation, same technique"],"tech":["Pull-PEG: gastroscope insufflates stomach against abdominal wall; TRANSILLUMINATION + finger indentation identify the safe spot (LUQ, below costal margin); SAFE-TRACT: needle advances under aspiration — air return must coincide with needle visible in stomach (early air = interposed bowel — abort/re-site)","Wire through needle, snared, pulled out the mouth; PEG tube pulled retrograde through esophagus into position; external bumper snugged to 1-2cm marking WITHOUT tension (tension = necrosis/buried bumper)","Stamm (open/lap): purse-strings ×2 in anterior gastric body, tube via separate stab, tack stomach to abdominal wall at 3-4 points","Document the cm-at-skin number — it is the reference for every future 'is it migrated?' call"],"after":["Feeds may start within 4 hours (evidence supports early use); flush protocols prevent clogging","Peristomal care: keep bumper snug-not-tight, rotate/advance per protocol to prevent BURIED BUMPER (fixed, painful, non-rotating tube → endoscopic release)","Dislodgement rules the phone calls: tract matures ~4 weeks; EARLY dislodgement = do NOT blindly replace (false passage → peritonitis) — endoscopic/radiologic replacement; MATURE tract = replace promptly (closes in hours), confirm position (aspirate/pH or contrast) before feeding","Peristomal leakage: fix the fit and gastric emptying — upsizing the tube enlarges the hole and worsens it (classic error)","Site infection common/minor (abx, care); necrotizing infection rare but recognized by pain + spreading erythema → surgical emergency"],"pearls":["The needle that returns air before you see it in the stomach just found the colon — abort, re-site, and be glad you aspirated","Buried bumper is a tension complication placed at insertion — the free-spinning, gently-advancing tube never buries","A PEG placed to 'prevent aspiration' in dementia prevents nothing — have the honest conversation the consult is actually asking for"],"refs":[{"t":"ASGE PEG Guidelines","u":"https://www.asge.org"},{"t":"ASPEN Enteral Access Resources","u":"https://www.nutritioncare.org"}]},{"id":"gs-sleeve","name":"Sleeve Gastrectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Most common bariatric operation worldwide: resects the greater curvature, leaving a ~100-150mL tubular stomach","Indications (updated ASMBS/IFSO 2022): BMI ≥35 regardless of comorbidity, BMI 30-34.9 with metabolic disease; expected excess weight loss ~60-70%","Mechanism: restriction + ghrelin reduction (fundus resected) + accelerated emptying — metabolic effects beyond volume"],"dx":["Multidisciplinary preop: nutrition education, psychological evaluation, comorbidity optimization (sleep study snoring/OSA screen, HbA1c, cardiac risk)","GERD assessment matters: significant reflux/Barrett's favors bypass instead (sleeve worsens reflux in a meaningful fraction) — EGD preop per program","VTE risk-stratify; smoking cessation mandatory (ulcer/leak risk)"],"tx":["Sleeve chosen for: standard metabolic disease, transplant candidates needing weight loss, higher-risk patients (shorter, no anastomosis), IBD; bypass favored for: severe GERD, poorly controlled T2DM (slight edge), revisional needs","Comorbidity remission expectations: T2DM remission substantial (sleeve slightly under RYGB), HTN/OSA improvement majority"],"tech":["5-port or reduced-port lap; divide gastrocolic/gastrosplenic ligaments off the greater curve (energy device) from ~4-6cm proximal to pylorus up to the angle of His, fully mobilizing the fundus (leaving posterior fundus = retained reservoir = failure)","Bougie 32-40Fr along the lesser curve calibrates the sleeve; stapler fires begin 4-6cm from pylorus (preserve the antral pump), staying uniformly beside the bougie — DO NOT NARROW AT THE INCISURA (the stricture/kink point) and DO NOT chase the last millimeter at the GE junction (leak point #1: proximal staple line near His)","Graduated staple heights (thicker antrum → thinner fundus); reinforce staple line per practice (buttress/oversew — bleeding reduction clearer than leak reduction)","Inspect line, test per practice (methylene blue/air), retrieve specimen; hiatal hernia found intraop → repair it concurrently","Final sleeve should lie straight without spiral/corkscrew rotation"],"after":["Enhanced recovery: ambulate day 0, liquids per protocol POD0-1, discharge POD1-2; staged diet (liquid→puree→soft→regular over ~4-6 weeks)","LEAK is the feared complication (~1%): tachycardia >120, fever, left shoulder pain, unwell — POD1 to week 6 (proximal, near His) → CT with oral contrast; management: sepsis control, drain, stent/endoluminal therapy, rarely reoperation — chronic leaks are referral-center disease","Lifelong: multivitamin, B12, iron, D/calcium; labs at 3/6/12 months then annually; dumping less than bypass but counsel sugars anyway","GERD surveillance: new/worsening reflux common — PPIs, and conversion to RYGB for refractory cases","Weight recurrence years out: nutrition-first evaluation, then revisional options"],"pearls":["Leaks live at the top of the staple line and present as tachycardia before anything else — the post-sleeve HR of 130 is a contrast study, not anxiety","The incisura narrowing you accepted at fire #3 is the regurgitation clinic visit at month 3","Reflux history is the fork in the road preop — sleeving severe GERD trades weight for misery"],"refs":[{"t":"ASMBS Guidelines & 2022 Indications","u":"https://asmbs.org"},{"t":"SAGES Bariatric Resources","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"gs-rygb","name":"Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass","sec":"gs","present":["The metabolic benchmark: small gastric pouch + Roux limb rerouting; expected excess weight loss ~70-80%, strongest T2DM remission data","Preferred over sleeve for: significant GERD, T2DM (marginal edge), revision of failed sleeve/band","Anatomy creates its own long-term physiology: dumping, marginal ulcers, internal hernias, malabsorption — the follow-up is lifelong by design"],"dx":["Same multidisciplinary preop as sleeve; EGD per program (pouch excludes the remnant from future endoscopic reach — know the baseline)","Absolute attention to smoking/NSAIDs history: both are marginal-ulcer engines — many programs require documented cessation","T2DM patients: plan perioperative med de-escalation (remission can begin in days)"],"tx":["Standard construction: 15-30mL lesser-curve pouch, 75-150cm Roux (alimentary) limb, 30-50cm biliopancreatic limb — longer limbs add malabsorption and risk","Comorbidity outcomes: diabetes remission majority (DiRECT-type magnitude), OSA/HTN/lipids improve, MACE and mortality reductions in cohort data"],"tech":["Create pouch: divide stomach with staplers off a calibrated bougie, small vertical lesser-curve-based pouch excluding fundus (pouch dilation = late failure); the remnant stays in situ, vascularized","Jejunum divided 30-50cm past ligament of Treitz; Roux limb brought up ANTECOLIC-ANTEGASTRIC (fewer internal hernia spaces) or retrocolic per practice","Gastrojejunostomy: linear-stapled, circular-stapled (EEA 25mm), or handsewn — calibrated ~1-1.2cm; jejunojejunostomy side-to-side stapled at chosen Roux length; close the common-channel enterotomy meticulously","CLOSE THE MESENTERIC DEFECTS (jejunojejunostomy mesentery + Petersen's space) with permanent suture — internal hernia prevention is a closure discipline (SM-BOSS/long-term data support)","Leak-test the GJ (air under saline/endoscopy); inspect orientation — a twisted Roux is a chronic mystery pain generator"],"after":["Pathways as sleeve; leak vigilance identical (GJ + JJ are the sites — tachycardia rule applies)","Marginal ulcer (pouch/GJ, up to 5-10%): epigastric pain, dysphagia, bleed — EGD; treat PPI + sucralfate, eliminate smoking/NSAIDs forever; perforated marginal ulcer = patch + washout","INTERNAL HERNIA is the signature late emergency: intermittent crampy pain ± normal imaging; CT 'mesenteric swirl'; any bypass patient with acute abdominal pain and no diagnosis gets diagnostic laparoscopy — closed-loop through Petersen's kills","Nutrition lifelong: B12, iron (menstruating women especially), calcium/D, thiamine in vomiting patients (Wernicke's after bariatric surgery is real); annual labs forever","Dumping syndrome: early (osmotic — teach small low-sugar meals) vs late (reactive hypoglycemia — dietary/acarbose); excluded remnant/duodenum are endoscopically unreachable — biliary disease planning changes (lap-assisted ERCP)"],"pearls":["The bypass patient with crampy pain and a 'normal CT' is an internal hernia until a laparoscope says otherwise","Marginal ulcers are caused in clinic (smoking, NSAIDs) and cured in clinic — the OR just handles the failures","Closing both mesenteric defects takes five minutes and prevents the worst phone call in bariatric surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"ASMBS Clinical Resources","u":"https://asmbs.org"},{"t":"SAGES RYGB Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"gs-nissen","name":"Nissen Fundoplication","sec":"gs","present":["GERD refractory to/dependent on PPIs, volume regurgitation, extraesophageal symptoms (selected), PPI intolerance — a QUALITY-of-life operation demanding correct patient selection","Typical heartburn + good PPI response predicts surgical success; atypical symptoms + no PPI response predicts disappointment","Often combined with hiatal hernia repair (most GERD patients have one)"],"dx":["THE WORKUP IS THE OPERATION: EGD (esophagitis, Barrett's, hernia anatomy), pH monitoring OFF PPI (proves pathologic acid exposure — DeMeester), high-resolution MANOMETRY (excludes achalasia/aperistalsis — the catastrophe prevented), ± barium swallow","Skipping manometry and wrapping an achalasia patient is the classic avoidable disaster","Barrett's changes surveillance planning, not necessarily the operation"],"tx":["Nissen (360° wrap) is standard for normal motility; partial wraps (Toupet 270° posterior, Dor anterior) for impaired motility or side-effect minimization — slightly less durable reflux control, less dysphagia/gas-bloat","Alternatives discussed honestly: continued PPI, magnetic sphincter augmentation (LINX) in selected anatomy","Redo fundoplication is a different, harder operation — get the first one right"],"tech":["5-port lap; divide gastrohepatic ligament (watch replaced left hepatic artery), dissect BOTH crura, circumferential esophageal mobilization for ≥2-3cm TENSION-FREE intra-abdominal esophagus (short esophagus → extended mediastinal mobilization; rarely Collis)","Divide short gastrics to fully mobilize the fundus (a tethered wrap twists)","Close the crura posteriorly (interrupted, over an appropriately sized bougie 52-60Fr passed carefully at this stage)","FLOPPY 360° wrap: geometric fundus-behind-esophagus 'shoeshine' to confirm no twist, 2-2.5cm wrap secured with 2-3 sutures catching esophagus (not vagus), over the bougie — SHORT and FLOPPY are the operative adjectives","Final checks: wrap sits below diaphragm without tension, no twist, vagi identified/preserved"],"after":["Diet progression liquid→soft over 2-4 weeks; EARLY dysphagia is expected (edema) — counsel preop so nobody panics at week 1; persistent dysphagia >6-8 weeks → esophagram ± dilation","Gas-bloat syndrome (can't burp/vomit): usually improves over months — simethicone, avoid carbonation; severe persistent → wrap revision conversation","Retching precautions early (acute wrap herniation): aggressive antiemetics on discharge","Symptom recurrence years later: esophagram + EGD — wrap slipped/herniated/loosened; redo in experienced hands","Stop the PPI — that was the point; recurrence of true acid symptoms gets objective retesting, not reflex restart"],"pearls":["Manometry before every wrap — the achalasia you wrapped is the dysphagia you own","Short, floppy, over a bougie, with mobilized fundus: nearly every bad Nissen violated one of those four","The best redo fundoplication is the referral to someone who does them weekly"],"refs":[{"t":"SAGES GERD Surgery Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: GERD Surgery","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002925.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-phh","name":"Hiatal / Paraesophageal Hernia Repair","sec":"gs","present":["Type I sliding (GE junction migrates — the GERD hernia) vs II-IV paraesophageal (fundus ± other organs herniate beside/above; type IV includes colon/spleen etc.)","PEH symptoms: postprandial chest pressure, early satiety, dyspnea, anemia (Cameron erosions), dysphagia","ACUTE presentations: obstruction, gastric volvulus (Borchardt triad: pain, retching without vomitus, NGT won't pass) — surgical emergency"],"dx":["Contrast esophagram defines anatomy/volvulus; EGD (Cameron lesions, Barrett's, GE junction position); CT for the acute presentation","Manometry when feasible for elective repairs (wrap choice); pH testing selective","Symptomatic PEH: repair; asymptomatic large PEH: individualized (watchful waiting acceptable in the frail elderly — annualized strangulation risk is low but real)"],"tx":["Elective lap repair for symptomatic PEH; emergent for volvulus/strangulation (attempt endoscopic/NGT decompression as a bridge — may convert emergency to urgent)","Principles: reduce content, EXCISE THE SAC, restore intra-abdominal esophageal length, close crura, add fundoplication (anti-reflux + anchoring)","Recurrence is the honest conversation: radiographic recurrence common (up to 30-50% long-term), symptomatic redo much less"],"tech":["Reduce herniated stomach gently (traction on stomach, countertraction on sac — the sac delivers the stomach); complete SAC EXCISION from the mediastinum (leaving it = leaving the recurrence scaffold) while sparing pleura/vagi","High mediastinal esophageal mobilization until ≥2.5-3cm tension-free intra-abdominal length; true short esophagus after full mobilization → Collis gastroplasty (rare when mobilization is committed)","Crural closure: posterior interrupted permanent sutures without tension — pledgets/relaxing incisions for wide gaps; MESH at the hiatus is selective and non-permanent-material if used (synthetic mesh erosion into esophagus is a famous catastrophe — avoid bridging with permanent mesh)","Fundoplication per motility (Nissen/Toupet); gastropexy ± gastrostomy alone for the frail patient needing volvulus prevention without full repair","Emergent necrosis: resect compromised stomach — damage-control foregut principles"],"after":["Similar to fundoplication: staged diet, retching control, early esophagram per practice or for any concerning symptom","Watch respiratory function early in the elderly (large PEH repair unmasks/recovers pulmonary compression)","Delayed recurrence surveillance is symptom-driven; small radiographic recurrences without symptoms are usually observed","Acute post-op chest pain + retching = acute re-herniation until imaged — same-day esophagram/CT"],"pearls":["The sac is the hernia — leave it and it refills; excise it and the stomach follows almost willingly","Tension recurs, mobilization endures: earn the esophageal length in the mediastinum rather than accepting a strained repair","Borchardt's triad with an NGT that won't pass is an OR consult, not an overnight observation"],"refs":[{"t":"SAGES Paraesophageal Hernia Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hiatal Hernia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/hiatalhernia.html"}]},{"id":"gs-heller","name":"Heller Myotomy","sec":"gs","present":["Achalasia: progressive dysphagia to solids AND liquids, regurgitation of undigested food, weight loss, chest pain; often misdiagnosed as GERD for years","Failed LES relaxation + absent peristalsis — the outflow obstruction is functional, not mechanical","Pseudoachalasia caution: rapid onset, older age, marked weight loss → exclude GE junction malignancy"],"dx":["High-resolution manometry is definitive: integrated relaxation pressure elevated + failed peristalsis; Chicago classification types I-III (type II best outcomes, type III spastic — longer myotomy/POEM consideration)","Barium esophagram: bird's beak taper, dilated esophagus, poor emptying (timed barium column)","EGD to exclude pseudoachalasia/candidiasis and assess the mucosa before anyone cuts"],"tx":["Definitive options counseled honestly: laparoscopic Heller + partial fundoplication, POEM (endoscopic myotomy — best for type III/spastic; trades higher reflux), pneumatic dilation (graded, effective, retreatment common); botox only for the frail (months of benefit, scars the plane)","Heller+Dor remains the durable surgical standard with reflux protection","End-stage sigmoid megaesophagus failing everything: esophagectomy conversation"],"tech":["Lap approach as for hiatal surgery; expose the GE junction, clear the anterior fat pad, identify/protect the anterior vagus","Myotomy: divide longitudinal then circular muscle fibers on the ANTERIOR esophagus — 5-6cm on esophagus + 2-3cm ONTO THE CARDIA (the gastric extension is where failures live) — meticulous plane just above mucosa (hook/energy at low settings, cold where prudent)","The mucosa should bulge freely through the completed myotomy; separate muscle edges laterally","PERFORATION CHECK: endoscopic insufflation under saline ± methylene blue — a recognized mucosal injury is a suture repair + Dor coverage; unrecognized is a mediastinitis","Add PARTIAL fundoplication (Dor anterior — also patches the myotomy; or Toupet) — a full Nissen atop an aperistaltic esophagus is an obstruction"],"after":["Liquids POD0-1, esophagram per practice, soft diet advancing over 1-2 weeks","Dysphagia relief is typically immediate and gratifying — persistent/recurrent dysphagia: esophagram + manometry (incomplete gastric-side myotomy is the usual culprit) → dilation or completion myotomy/POEM","Reflux surveillance long-term (even with Dor): symptoms or periodic pH/EGD — the aperistaltic esophagus handles acid poorly, and Barrett's risk persists","Lifelong reality: achalasia is palliated, not cured — esophagus remains aperistaltic; eat upright, chew thoroughly, late surveillance for the small cancer risk in longstanding disease"],"pearls":["Failures are gastric-side failures — the myotomy that stopped at the junction relieved nothing","Never Nissen an achalasia patient; the wrap must be partial atop a motor-dead esophagus","Sudden fever/chest pain after myotomy = leak until contrast proves otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"SAGES Achalasia Guidelines","u":"https://www.sages.org"},{"t":"ISDE Achalasia Resources","u":"https://isde.net"}]},{"id":"gs-gastrectomy","name":"Partial & Total Gastrectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Gastric adenocarcinoma (dominant indication), GIST too large/unfavorable for wedge, refractory complicated ulcer disease (rare now), prophylactic total gastrectomy for CDH1 mutation carriers","Cancer presentations: weight loss, early satiety, iron-deficiency anemia, epigastric pain — late by the time symptoms declare","Siewert classification at the GE junction directs esophagectomy-vs-gastrectomy territory"],"dx":["EGD + biopsies; EUS for T/N; CT chest/abd/pelvis; STAGING LAPAROSCOPY with washings before curative-intent resection of ≥T1b/advanced lesions (peritoneal disease changes everything and CT misses it)","HER2/MSI/PD-L1 profiling — perioperative systemic therapy (FLOT) is standard for locally advanced disease","Nutrition baseline: many present depleted — prehabilitate"],"tx":["Distal cancers: subtotal (distal) gastrectomy with 5cm margins; proximal/diffuse: total gastrectomy; diffuse-type/linitis needs generous margins with frozen sections","D2 LYMPHADENECTOMY (perigastric + named left gastric/hepatic/celiac/splenic stations, ≥15-16 nodes) is the standard-of-care oncologic dissection","Perioperative chemotherapy (FLOT4) for ≥cT2 or node-positive — surgery is one act of a systemic play"],"tech":["Distal gastrectomy: divide greater omentum (bursectomy selective), ligate right gastroepiploic at origin, right gastric, left gastric AT THE CELIAC ORIGIN with nodal sweep, duodenal division 1-2cm past pylorus (secure stump), proximal division with margins — reconstruct Billroth II with Braun or ROUX-EN-Y (favored: less bile reflux)","Total gastrectomy: add short gastrics/spleen-preserving splenic hilar dissection, esophageal division with frozen margin — ROUX-EN-Y ESOPHAGOJEJUNOSTOMY (EEA circular or linear/handsewn), 50-60cm Roux to prevent bile reflux, jejunojejunostomy, ± feeding jejunostomy for anticipated nutrition needs","Duodenal stump security is its own religion: healthy tissue, consider reinforcement — the stump blowout is a feared POD3-7 event","GIST: wedge/segmental resection with negative margins, NO lymphadenectomy needed (nodal spread rare) — organ-sparing whenever anatomy allows","Minimally invasive gastrectomy equivalent in experienced centers (KLASS/CLASS trials)"],"after":["ERAS-adapted: early enteral nutrition (J-tube if placed), drains per anastomosis philosophy; leak surveillance (esophagojejunostomy = the total gastrectomy's Achilles) — tachycardia/fever/drain amylase → contrast CT; managed by drainage/stent/reoperation by severity","Duodenal stump leak: sepsis + bilious drain output → drainage/tube duodenostomy control","Post-gastrectomy syndromes to actively manage: dumping (small dry meals, low simple sugars), bile reflux (Roux prevents most), afferent limb syndrome (post-BII), delayed emptying","LIFELONG nutrition: B12 injections mandatory post-total (no intrinsic factor), iron/calcium/D monitoring, pancreatic enzyme consideration for maldigestion, expected 10-15% body weight loss post-total — dietitian is a permanent teammate","Oncology: pathology review, adjuvant completion (FLOT), surveillance imaging/tumor boards"],"pearls":["Staging laparoscopy before the big incision — finding carcinomatosis at laparotomy is a preventable operation","The D2 dissection is won at the named vessels, not the perigastric fat — count stations, not just nodes","Every total gastrectomy patient without B12 supplementation is a neurologic complication on a 2-3 year timer"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Gastric Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"JGCA Gastric Cancer Treatment Guidelines","u":"https://www.jgca.jp"}]},{"id":"gs-esophagectomy","name":"Esophagectomy (Overview)","sec":"gs","present":["Esophageal cancer (adenocarcinoma distal/GEJ — the Western epidemic; squamous mid-proximal), end-stage benign disease (caustic stricture, failed achalasia), perforation salvage","Progressive solid dysphagia + weight loss is the classic story — EGD on first presentation, not after a PPI trial","One of surgery's highest-stakes elective operations: multidisciplinary by necessity, volume-outcome relationship is real"],"dx":["EGD + biopsy; EUS (T/N), PET-CT (distant disease), staging laparoscopy for GEJ tumors per program","Neoadjuvant chemoradiation (CROSS) or perioperative chemo (FLOT) for locally advanced disease is standard — upfront surgery only for early lesions","Fitness assessment is rigorous: cardiopulmonary testing, nutrition (pre-op supplementation/J-tube for the obstructed), smoking/alcohol cessation — prehabilitation changes outcomes"],"tx":["Approach by tumor location + surgeon: IVOR LEWIS (laparotomy/laparoscopy + right thoracotomy/scopy — intrathoracic anastomosis; workhorse for distal/GEJ), McKEOWN (three-field, cervical anastomosis — more proximal tumors), TRANSHIATAL (abdomen + neck, no thoracotomy — cervical anastomosis)","Minimally invasive/hybrid esophagectomy reduces pulmonary complications (MIRO/TIME trials)","T1a disease: endoscopic resection (EMR/ESD) spares the esophagus — surgery for deeper/node-risk disease"],"tech":["Abdominal phase: mobilize stomach on the RIGHT GASTROEPIPLOIC ARCADE (the conduit's life-line — protect it with religious care), divide left gastric with nodal dissection, Kocher maneuver, pyloric drainage per practice (pyloroplasty/botox/none — debated), construct a 4-5cm gastric CONDUIT along the greater curve","Mediastinal dissection en bloc with periesophageal nodes; thoracic duct territory awareness (chyle leak prevention/ligation per practice)","Anastomosis (intrathoracic or cervical): EEA circular, linear side-to-side, or handsewn — tension-free, well-perfused conduit tip (ICG perfusion assessment increasingly standard)","Feeding jejunostomy placed at the index operation in most programs — the nutrition bridge for the leak that hasn't happened yet","Drains: chest tube(s); NGT decompressing the conduit positioned before leaving the OR"],"after":["ICU/stepdown pathway; aggressive pulmonary toilet (pneumonia is the volume killer), early mobilization, chest tube management","ANASTOMOTIC LEAK (5-15%): fever, tachycardia, arrhythmia (new AF post-esophagectomy = leak until proven otherwise), drain change → CT + contrast/endoscopy; management spectrum: NPO/abx → stent/endoluminal vac → drainage/reoperation; cervical leaks drain via the neck more forgivingly","Conduit necrosis (rare, catastrophic): endoscopic assessment when suspicion — takedown/diversion if dead","Chyle leak: milky chest output rising with feeds → medium-chain diet/NPO+TPN, octreotide, thoracic duct ligation/embolization if high-output persists","Recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy (cervical dissections): hoarseness + aspiration — ENT evaluation, swallow therapy; delayed conduit emptying/dumping: promotility, diet engineering","Long-term: reflux precautions (head-of-bed elevation forever), strictures dilated endoscopically, surveillance per oncology; nutrition follow-up with the J-tube weaned as oral intake proves itself"],"pearls":["The gastroepiploic arcade is the whole operation — one careless energy fire on it converts a conduit into a problem list","New-onset atrial fibrillation on POD3 after esophagectomy is a leak announcement, not a cardiology consult","Esophagectomy outcomes are made before the OR (neoadjuvant, nutrition, prehab) and after it (pulmonary toilet, leak vigilance) — the operation is the middle chapter"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Esophageal Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ESTS/STS Esophagectomy Resources","u":"https://www.sts.org"}]},{"id":"gs-whipple","name":"Whipple Procedure (Pancreaticoduodenectomy)","sec":"gs","present":["Pancreatic head/uncinate adenocarcinoma, distal cholangiocarcinoma, ampullary and duodenal cancers, selected IPMN/NETs, chronic pancreatitis with head mass","Classic story: painless jaundice + weight loss ± new diabetes; Courvoisier gallbladder","One of surgery's biggest operations — outcomes track with center volume and multidisciplinary care"],"dx":["Pancreas-protocol CT (arterial+venous phases): resectability = relationship to SMA/celiac (arterial abutment/encasement) and SMV/PV (venous involvement graded); borderline-resectable definitions drive neoadjuvant therapy","EUS-FNA for tissue (required before neoadjuvant); CA 19-9 (with normal bilirubin); staging chest CT; diagnostic laparoscopy selectively","Biliary drainage (ERCP+stent) only for cholangitis, deep jaundice with delayed surgery, or neoadjuvant pathway — not reflexively"],"tx":["Upfront resection for clearly resectable disease per program vs growing use of neoadjuvant (FOLFIRINOX/gem-nabpaclitaxel) especially borderline","Adjuvant chemotherapy (mFOLFIRINOX — PRODIGE-24) for the fit; surgery alone is rarely curative-intent complete therapy","Unresectable at exploration: document, biopsy, palliate (biliary/gastric bypass or stents) — the operation changes, not ends"],"tech":["Assessment first: laparoscopy/inspection for occult metastases, Kocher maneuver to assess SMA relationship, SMV/PV tunnel behind the neck — 'no touch of no return' points identified BEFORE committing (dividing the neck)","Resection: cholecystectomy + CBD division, gastric antrum (classic) or duodenum-preserving pylorus (PPPD — equivalent outcomes), jejunum past ligament of Treitz, pancreatic neck over the vein, uncinate off the SMA with meticulous ligation (the bleeding and the margin live here); regional lymphadenectomy travels with the specimen","Reconstruction (one loop, three anastomoses in order): PANCREATICOJEJUNOSTOMY (duct-to-mucosa most common — the operation's Achilles), hepaticojejunostomy, gastro/duodenojejunostomy","Soft gland + small duct = highest fistula risk — technical adjuncts (stents, sealants) per practice; drains near the PJ standard in most hands","Venous resection/reconstruction for limited SMV/PV involvement in experienced centers"],"after":["POPF (postoperative pancreatic fistula) is the signature complication: drain amylase >3× serum on POD3 defines biochemical leak; grade B/C when clinically relevant — manage with drainage, nutrition, octreotide per practice, IR for collections","Delayed gastric emptying (up to 20-30%): NGT patience, promotility agents, rule out an intra-abdominal collection driving it (CT before labeling it primary)","POST-PANCREATECTOMY HEMORRHAGE: early = technical; DELAYED (POD7-14+, often heralded by a 'sentinel bleed' in the drain) = pseudoaneurysm (GDA stump classically) until proven otherwise → CT angiogram + IR embolization emergently — not a return to the OR first","Chyle leak, wound issues, pancreatic insufficiency (enzymes), new diabetes surveillance; nutrition support pathway","Oncology completion: pathology review, adjuvant therapy, surveillance CA 19-9 + imaging"],"pearls":["The sentinel drain bleed on POD10 is the GDA pseudoaneurysm clearing its throat — CTA now, not a hemoglobin recheck in 6 hours","Resectability is decided at the SMA, not the vein — arterial involvement is the wall","A soft normal gland fistulizes more than the hard obstructed one — the 'easy' Whipple is the leaky one"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ISGPS Definitions (POPF/DGE/PPH)","u":"https://www.isgps.info"}]},{"id":"gs-distal-panc","name":"Distal Pancreatectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Body/tail lesions: adenocarcinoma, NETs, mucinous/serous cystic neoplasms, IPMN, chronic pancreatitis complications","Often found incidentally (cysts) — the workup decides observation vs operation","Body/tail cancers present late (no jaundice to announce them) — many are unresectable at diagnosis"],"dx":["Pancreas-protocol CT/MRI-MRCP characterizes cysts (worrisome features: size ≥3cm, enhancing nodule, dilated duct, growth); EUS-FNA with cyst fluid analysis (CEA, amylase, molecular) when it changes management","Fukuoka/international guidelines direct IPMN/cyst surveillance vs resection","NET workup: functional labs by syndrome + DOTATATE PET for localization/staging"],"tx":["Cancer: distal pancreatectomy WITH SPLENECTOMY (nodal basin travels with splenic vessels) + adjuvant therapy — RAMPS (radical antegrade modular pancreatosplenectomy) posture for oncologic planes","Benign/low-grade: spleen-PRESERVING distal pancreatectomy (Kimura: preserve splenic vessels; Warshaw: divide vessels, spleen lives on short gastrics — watch for gastric varices later)","Minimally invasive (lap/robotic) is standard for benign/low-grade and increasingly for cancer (LEOPARD/DIPLOMA data)"],"tech":["Lesser sac entry, mobilize the inferior/superior pancreatic borders, identify splenic vein/artery relationships","Cancer: early splenic artery control, dissect the retroperitoneal plane anterior to Gerota's/adrenal (RAMPS deep plane if invaded), en bloc spleen, node sweep to the celiac/SMA margin","Parenchymal division: stapler (reinforced) vs energy+suture — fistula rates stubbornly similar (~20-30% biochemical) regardless of technique religion; oversew/duct ligation per practice","Spleen preservation: patient vessel-branch ligation (Kimura) demands unhurried dissection; Warshaw is the pragmatic bailout — confirm spleen perfusion at the end","Drain to the stump in most practices"],"after":["Pancreatic fistula surveillance identical in principle to Whipple (drain amylase POD3): most are biochemical and resolve; clinically relevant → keep drains, nutrition, IR for undrained collections","POST-SPLENECTOMY duties when spleen went: vaccines (pneumococcal, meningococcal, Hib — ideally 14 days post-op if not given pre-op), OPSI counseling card, fever-means-antibiotics education","New diabetes/exocrine insufficiency risk proportional to remnant — screen symptoms + HbA1c","Warshaw patients: late gastric varices possibility — remember it when they present with GI bleeding years on"],"pearls":["The distal stump leaks in a quarter of patients no matter whose stapler you worship — drains and patience beat denial","Spleen preservation is elegant until the oncology suffers — cancer takes the spleen, no negotiation","Vaccinate before discharge, not 'at follow-up' — the no-show rate is the OPSI rate"],"refs":[{"t":"International IPMN/Cyst Guidelines (Fukuoka/Kyoto)","u":"https://www.iap.or.jp"},{"t":"NCCN Pancreatic & NET Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"gs-liver-resection","name":"Liver Resection (Segmentectomy / Lobectomy)","sec":"gs","present":["Colorectal liver metastases (most common Western indication), HCC (in preserved function/transplant-ineligible), intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, symptomatic benign lesions (adenoma ≥5cm, symptomatic hemangioma rarely)","Resectability is about the FUTURE LIVER REMNANT (FLR), not lesion count: ≥20-25% healthy liver, ≥30-40% diseased/post-chemo/cirrhotic","HCC decisions run through liver function (Child-Pugh, portal hypertension markers, MELD) and transplant criteria — tumor board disease"],"dx":["Liver-protocol MRI (best lesion characterization/count) + CT chest/abd staging; CEA (CRLM), AFP (HCC); percutaneous biopsy NOT needed for classic imaging HCC in cirrhosis","Volumetry for planned major resections; portal vein embolization (PVE) grows an inadequate FLR over 4-6 weeks","CRLM: systemic therapy sequencing (peri-operative chemo), disappearing metastases mapped BEFORE they vanish"],"tx":["Parenchymal-sparing resection philosophy for CRLM (survival equivalent to formal lobectomy, preserves salvage options); anatomic resection favored for HCC (portal-tributary spread)","Ablation (microwave/RFA) for small deep lesions or as resection's partner; staged hepatectomy/ALPPS for extensive bilobar disease in selected patients","Cirrhotics: resect small, peripheral, well-compensated; transplant pathway when criteria fit"],"tech":["Intraoperative ultrasound FIRST and always — finds occult lesions, maps vessels, redraws the plan in a meaningful fraction of cases","Inflow control: Pringle maneuver (portal triad clamp, intermittent 15min on/5 off) tames bleeding; LOW CVP anesthesia (<5 cmH2O) is half the hemostasis — partnership with anesthesia explicitly","Parenchymal transection: crush-clamp, energy devices, CUSA, staplers for pedicles/hepatic veins per preference — clip/tie what you meet, respect the hepatic veins' thin walls","Anatomic resections follow portal pedicles (Glissonean approach); non-anatomic follow the tumor with margin — either way know segmental anatomy (Couinaud) cold","Air embolism vigilance during hepatic vein work (that's the CVP conversation again); argon/hemostatics to the raw surface; drains selective"],"after":["Watch the triad of liver-specific trouble: BLEEDING (early — trend hemoglobin, drains), BILE LEAK (POD3-7: bilious drain/collection → drain adequately, most close; ERCP+stent for ongoing duct leaks), LIVER FAILURE (the FLR bet: rising bilirubin/INR + encephalopathy POD3-5 = post-hepatectomy liver failure — supportive, avoid nephrotoxins, ICU)","'50-50 criteria' (POD5 PT<50%/bili>50 µmol/L) flags mortality risk","Phosphate drops with regeneration — replete; nutrition early; DVT prophylaxis despite the INR blip (they're prothrombotic, not anticoagulated)","Oncologic follow-up: CRLM surveillance (CEA + imaging — re-resection of recurrence is real and curative-intent), HCC surveillance in the cirrhotic remnant continues forever"],"pearls":["The ultrasound probe is the most important retractor in liver surgery — scan before you burn bridges","Blood loss is an anesthesia-surgery duet: a CVP of 12 makes every hepatic vein a fountain","Bile leaks declare themselves in the drain you almost didn't leave — after complex transections, leave it"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Hepatobiliary Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"IHPBA Resources","u":"https://www.ihpba.org"}]},{"id":"gs-splenectomy","name":"Splenectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Trauma (the unstable shattered spleen), hematologic disease (ITP refractory to medical therapy — the classic elective indication, hereditary spherocytosis, selected lymphomas/hypersplenism), splenic artery aneurysm, symptomatic massive splenomegaly, en bloc with distal pancreatectomy","Blunt trauma splenic injuries are now MOSTLY nonoperative (grade, stability, and angioembolization decide) — operative splenectomy is for physiology, not grade alone","Elective ITP splenectomy: durable response in ~60-70%"],"dx":["Trauma: FAST + CT grading (AAST I-V, contrast blush = angio candidate); serial exams/hemoglobin for nonoperative candidates","Elective: confirm diagnosis with hematology, pre-op vaccinate (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Hib) ≥14 days BEFORE surgery — the elective advantage","Massive spleen: imaging for size/hilar anatomy, plan for accessory spleens (10-15% — the cause of ITP 'failure')"],"tx":["Trauma: unstable + splenic source = splenectomy without apology; stable with blush = angioembolization; observation protocols for the rest","Elective lap splenectomy is standard for normal-to-moderately enlarged spleens; massive (>20-25cm) may need hand-assist/open","Partial splenectomy/splenorrhaphy in children where feasible (immunity preservation weighs heavier)"],"tech":["Lap (right lateral decubitus 'hanging spleen'): divide splenocolic, short gastrics (energy device — respect the gastric wall), splenophrenic attachments; hilum LAST","Hilar control: stapler across the hilum (protect the pancreatic TAIL — it lives in the hilum; identify it before firing) or individual vessel ligation for favorable anatomy","SEARCH FOR ACCESSORY SPLEENS systematically (hilum, tail of pancreas, omentum, mesentery) in hematologic cases — before removing the main spleen distorts the field","Specimen: morcellate in a bag for benign disease; intact retrieval when pathology matters (lymphoma)","Trauma open: midline, pack, deliver the spleen medially by taking lateral attachments bluntly, clamp the hilum, out — speed is the technique"],"after":["Vaccines if not done pre-op: give at ~14 days post-op (better response than immediate); document relentlessly","OPSI (overwhelming post-splenectomy infection) counseling is a survival intervention: lifetime risk small but lethal — fever card, immediate antibiotics for febrile illness, some carry standby amoxicillin-clavulanate; annual flu, boosters per schedule","THROMBOCYTOSIS post-op: expected, peaks POD7-14 — aspirin commonly at >1000k per practice; PORTAL/SPLENIC VEIN THROMBOSIS (esp. massive spleens, myeloproliferative disease): new vague abdominal pain/fever POD5-30 → CT with contrast, anticoagulate","Left lower lobe atelectasis/effusion and pancreatic tail issues (fistula from a stapled tail) are the local complications — drain amylase if a collection appears","ITP: platelet response usually within days; hematology co-follows"],"pearls":["The pancreatic tail is inside the hilar staple line you're about to fire — look for it every time","The ITP that relapses after splenectomy has an accessory spleen until a nuclear scan says otherwise","OPSI education outlives the operation: the handshake at discharge should include the fever speech"],"refs":[{"t":"EAST Splenic Injury Guidelines","u":"https://www.east.org"},{"t":"CDC Asplenia Vaccination Schedule","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines"}]},{"id":"gs-adrenalectomy","name":"Adrenalectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Functional tumors: pheochromocytoma, aldosteronoma (Conn), cortisol-producing adenoma (Cushing); nonfunctional adenomas ≥4cm or growing/suspicious; adrenocortical carcinoma (open, en bloc); metastasectomy (selected)","Incidentaloma pathway feeds this operation: every adrenal mass gets a functional workup + imaging characterization","Pheo classic triad (episodic headache, sweating, tachycardia) + hypertension — but many are found on imaging first"],"dx":["Biochemical FIRST, always: plasma/urine metanephrines (pheo), aldosterone:renin ratio ± confirmatory testing and ADRENAL VEIN SAMPLING for lateralization (Conn — imaging lies about which side), 1mg dexamethasone suppression (cortisol)","Imaging: unenhanced CT HU <10 = lipid-rich benign; washout characteristics; size/heterogeneity/growth flag carcinoma; MIBG/DOTATATE for pheo extra-adrenal disease","NEVER biopsy a possible pheo; biopsy adrenal masses only for suspected metastasis AFTER pheo excluded biochemically"],"tx":["Pheochromocytoma: ALPHA-BLOCKADE FIRST (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin 10-14 days, liberal salt/fluid to re-expand volume), beta-blockade only AFTER alpha (unopposed alpha crisis otherwise) — the preop is the operation","Conn: lap adrenalectomy for lateralized disease cures/improves most; bilateral disease = medical (spironolactone)","Carcinoma suspicion: OPEN en bloc resection, no capsule violation — laparoscopy is contraindicated for known/likely ACC"],"tech":["Lateral transperitoneal lap (most common): fall-away exposure; RIGHT side: mobilize liver, the short fat adrenal vein enters the IVC directly — early careful control (the 2mm structure that defines the operation); LEFT side: mobilize spleen/pancreas medially, vein drains to renal vein — longer, kinder","Pheo: MINIMAL GLAND MANIPULATION, vein control early when feasible, constant anesthesia dialogue (pressure surges with handling; precipitous drop after vein ligation — they're pre-loaded with your alpha-blockade for exactly this)","Retroperitoneoscopic (posterior) approach: no peritoneal entry, ideal for bilateral/small tumors/hostile abdomens","Dissect outside the periadrenal fat plane; the gland tears if grasped directly — handle the fat, not the organ","Cortical-sparing (partial) adrenalectomy for bilateral/hereditary pheo (MEN2, VHL) preserves steroid independence"],"after":["Pheo: ICU-level observation first night — HYPOTENSION (residual alpha-blockade + volume) treated with fluids first; HYPOGLYCEMIA rebound (catecholamine withdrawal unmasks insulin) — check glucoses; repeat metanephrines at follow-up, genetic testing (30-40% hereditary)","Cushing adenoma: the contralateral gland is SUPPRESSED — steroid coverage and a slow taper over months; adrenal-insufficiency education (stress dosing, medical alert)","Conn: potassium normalizes fast — stop supplements/spironolactone, recheck; hypertension improves over months (cure ~40-50%, improvement most)","Bilateral adrenalectomy = lifelong steroid + mineralocorticoid replacement and crisis training","Standard lap recovery otherwise; pathology review for every gland"],"pearls":["The pheo preop (alpha, then beta, then volume) prevents the intraop crisis — an unblocked pheo found the hard way is an anesthesia emergency you caused in clinic","Right adrenal vein: short, posterior, straight into the cava — the reason right adrenalectomy humbles people","Post-Cushing patients are adrenally insufficient by design — the taper plan leaves the hospital with them in writing"],"refs":[{"t":"AACE/AAES Adrenal Incidentaloma Guidelines","u":"https://www.aace.com"},{"t":"Endocrine Society Pheochromocytoma Guideline","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"}]},{"id":"gs-thyroidectomy","name":"Thyroidectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Differentiated thyroid cancer (papillary dominant), indeterminate/suspicious nodules (Bethesda IV-V-VI), compressive goiter (dysphagia, dyspnea, substernal), Graves' refractory to/rejecting medical/radioiodine therapy, toxic nodules","Nodules are ubiquitous; cancers are the minority — the workup exists to operate on the right necks","Voice matters: document baseline, ask about prior neck surgery (the contralateral cord may already be quietly paralyzed)"],"dx":["TSH first (suppressed → uptake scan for toxic nodule pathway); ultrasound with TI-RADS risk stratification drives FNA thresholds","FNA cytology by Bethesda: II observe, III repeat/molecular testing, IV-VI toward surgery; molecular panels rescue many indeterminates from diagnostic lobectomy","Cancer staging: US of central + lateral compartments (found lateral nodes change the operation to include neck dissection); calcitonin if medullary suspected; vocal cord exam pre-op for cancer/reoperation/voice symptoms"],"tx":["Lobectomy is sufficient for: unilateral benign disease, toxic nodule, low-risk small (<4cm, no extrathyroidal extension/nodes) differentiated cancers per ATA — total thyroidectomy for bilateral disease, larger/higher-risk cancers, Graves', when RAI planned","Medullary: total + central dissection minimum; anaplastic: airway + multidisciplinary urgency","Graves' pre-op: euthyroid on antithyroid drugs, potassium iodide (SSKI/Lugol's) 7-10 days pre-op shrinks vascularity"],"tech":["Collar incision, raise subplatysmal flaps, open midline raphe, mobilize the lobe medially","THE TWO STRUCTURES THAT DEFINE THE OPERATION: recurrent laryngeal nerve (identify in the tracheoesophageal groove — right runs more obliquely; nerve monitoring per practice, eyes always) and PARATHYROIDS (peach-colored, preserve on their vascular pedicle; devascularized gland → mince + autotransplant into SCM and say so in the note)","Ligate superior pole vessels CLOSE to the capsule (external branch of superior laryngeal nerve — the 'high note nerve' — runs nearby), middle vein, inferior vessels branch-by-branch preserving parathyroid supply","Berry's ligament is the RLN's danger zone — the nerve is closest where the traction is greatest; small bites, no blind clamps, no energy fired at the groove","Central neck dissection (level VI) when node-positive/medullary: clear the compartment with both nerves and all parathyroids accounted for"],"after":["POST-TOTAL: calcium watch — symptomatic hypocalcemia (perioral tingling, Chvostek/Trousseau) POD0-2; protocols use PTH at 4h post-op to predict (low PTH → start calcium+calcitriol preemptively); severe symptoms → IV calcium gluconate","HEMATOMA is the airway emergency (first 24h): expanding neck, pressure, stridor → open the incision AT THE BEDSIDE (staple removers live at the bedside for a reason), then OR — the airway dies from compression before anesthesia arrives","Voice: hoarseness from RLN neuropraxia usually recovers in weeks-months; persistent → laryngoscopy + speech pathology; bilateral RLN injury = stridor at extubation = surgical airway readiness","Levothyroxine: after total (weight-based ~1.6mcg/kg, TSH target per cancer risk); after lobectomy ~20-30% eventually need it — check TSH at 6 weeks","Cancer completion: pathology review, RAI decisions, thyroglobulin surveillance with endocrinology"],"pearls":["The parathyroid you're not sure you saw is in the specimen — inspect the gland surface before it goes to pathology, and autotransplant generously","Post-thyroidectomy stridor is opened first and discussed later — the bedside decompression is the algorithm","Ligate the superior pole ON the capsule: the patient who can no longer sing project blames a nerve most surgeons never mention"],"refs":[{"t":"ATA Thyroid Nodule & Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.thyroid.org"},{"t":"AAES Resources","u":"https://www.endocrinesurgery.org"}]},{"id":"gs-parathyroidectomy","name":"Parathyroidectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Primary hyperparathyroidism: hypercalcemia + inappropriately non-suppressed PTH — 'stones, bones, groans, psychiatric overtones,' though most today are asymptomatic labs","Operate for: symptomatic disease, or asymptomatic meeting criteria (Ca >1 above normal, osteoporosis/fracture, eGFR<60, stones/nephrocalcinosis, age <50)","Single adenoma ~85%; four-gland hyperplasia ~10-15% (think MEN); carcinoma rare (very high Ca, palpable mass)"],"dx":["Biochemical diagnosis FIRST and alone: calcium + PTH + vitamin D + 24h urine calcium (excludes FHH — familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, the trap that doesn't need surgery: Ca/Cr clearance ratio <0.01)","Localization is for the SURGEON, not the diagnosis: US + sestamibi/4D-CT concordance enables focused exploration; negative imaging does NOT mean no disease — it means bilateral exploration","Secondary/tertiary (renal) hyperparathyroidism is a different algorithm (medical first; subtotal or total+auto-transplant when operating)"],"tx":["Focused (minimally invasive) parathyroidectomy for concordantly localized single adenomas — with INTRAOPERATIVE PTH monitoring (half-life ~4 min: >50% drop from baseline AND into normal range at 10-15 min post-excision = cure, close)","Bilateral four-gland exploration for: discordant/negative imaging, MEN/familial, lithium disease, failed focal cure criteria","Hyperplasia: subtotal (3.5 gland) resection or total + forearm autotransplant"],"tech":["Focused: small incision over the target, find the adenoma along expected anatomy — superior glands posterior/deep to RLN plane near cricothyroid junction; inferior glands anterior/ventral, near thymic tongue","Handle by the fat, take the vascular pedicle, DON'T rupture the capsule (parathyromatosis seeds recurrence)","ioPTH discipline: baseline, pre-excision, +5/+10/+15 — a failed drop means more disease: convert to exploration, no rationalizing the curve","Missing gland hunt (know it before you need it): retroesophageal, carotid sheath, intrathymic (inferior), undescended, intrathyroidal — thymectomy tongue delivery for the missing inferior","RLN awareness identical to thyroidectomy — same neighborhood, same rules"],"after":["Outpatient/23h typical; calcium checks: transient hypocalcemia common — supplement per protocol; HUNGRY BONE SYNDROME after severe long-standing disease (profound Ca/Phos/Mg drops for days-weeks): aggressive repletion, expect it in the high-alk-phos, big-adenoma patient","Cure surveillance: calcium + PTH at 2 weeks and 6 months (persistent = never cured, missed gland; recurrent = returns >6 months — re-imaging + referral-level redo surgery)","Redo necks are high-stakes: re-localize with 2 concordant studies, nerve monitoring, consider selective venous sampling","Bone density improves post-cure — reassure and re-scan at intervals; stones risk falls"],"pearls":["FHH is the operation that should never have happened — the 24h urine calcium is the cheapest complication prevention in endocrine surgery","The ioPTH that 'almost dropped 50%' is a second adenoma laughing at you — explore","Superior glands hide behind the nerve plane, inferior glands hide in the thymus — 'missing' glands follow embryology, not luck"],"refs":[{"t":"AAES Primary Hyperparathyroidism Guidelines","u":"https://www.endocrinesurgery.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hyperparathyroidism","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001215.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-lumpectomy","name":"Breast Lumpectomy (Partial Mastectomy)","sec":"gs","present":["Breast-conserving surgery for early-stage invasive cancer and DCIS — WITH radiation, survival equivalent to mastectomy (NSABP B-06 lineage; modern data trend favorable)","Candidacy: tumor-to-breast ratio permitting acceptable cosmesis, ability to receive radiation (no prior chest RT, not early pregnancy), no diffuse disease","Neoadjuvant systemic therapy downstages many to conservation — the sequence conversation happens at diagnosis"],"dx":["Diagnostic mammography + US ± MRI (selective); CORE NEEDLE biopsy (never excisional as first move) with clip placement","Receptors (ER/PR/HER2) + grade define the systemic plan before any incision; genetic counseling triggers (age, family history, triple-negative) checked at diagnosis","Non-palpable lesions need LOCALIZATION: wire, radioactive/magnetic seed, or radar reflector placed pre-op"],"tx":["Lumpectomy + margin clearance + adjuvant RADIATION is the package — declining radiation reopens the mastectomy conversation","Margins: 'no ink on tumor' for invasive cancer (SSO/ASTRO), 2mm for DCIS — re-excise positive margins","Axilla handled per SLNB entry; neoadjuvant responders get response-adapted surgery"],"tech":["Incision planned for cosmesis AND oncology (along Langer's/RSTLs, within a future mastectomy footprint if conversion ever needed); oncoplastic techniques (tissue rearrangement, therapeutic mammoplasty) extend conservation to bigger resections","Excise the lesion with a rim of normal tissue as ONE ORIENTED SPECIMEN — 6-point orientation (sutures/clips per protocol), no fragmenting","SPECIMEN RADIOGRAPH before closing for localized lesions: confirms target + clip retrieval and gauges margins — while the patient is still asleep and re-excision is a 5-minute affair","Cavity shave margins (circumferential additional shaves) halve positive-margin rates in trials — per practice","Clips to the cavity bed (radiation targeting), layered closure without drains for standard lumpectomy"],"after":["Outpatient; bruising/seroma expected — aspirate only symptomatic collections","Pathology drives the next moves: margins (re-excision ~10-20%), receptors confirm systemic plan; radiation oncology consult on the calendar before discharge paperwork settles","Radiation typically starts weeks after healing (sequence flexes around chemotherapy)","Surveillance: annual mammography (first post-treatment at ~6-12 months), clinical exams; endocrine therapy adherence conversations for ER+ disease are a survival intervention","Cosmetic/functional issues (fat necrosis lump mimicking recurrence — image it; chronic pain): address, don't dismiss"],"pearls":["The oriented specimen and its radiograph are the difference between one operation and two","'No ink on tumor' means exactly that — chasing wider negative margins in invasive disease adds deformity, not survival","A lumpectomy without a radiation plan is half an operation — confirm the whole package is consented, scheduled, and believed in"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Breast Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"SSO/ASTRO Margin Consensus","u":"https://www.surgonc.org"}]},{"id":"gs-mastectomy","name":"Mastectomy (Simple / Modified Radical)","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: patient choice, large tumor-to-breast ratio, multicentric disease, radiation contraindicated/prior, inflammatory breast cancer (post-neoadjuvant), risk-reduction (BRCA and high-penetrance carriers)","Types: simple/total (breast alone), modified radical (+ axillary dissection), skin-sparing and NIPPLE-SPARING variants enabling immediate reconstruction","Reconstruction discussion is part of the cancer consult, not an afterthought — immediate vs delayed, implant vs autologous, radiation's impact on each"],"dx":["Same diagnostic package as conservation; MRI selectively for extent/occult disease","Nipple-sparing candidacy: tumor-nipple distance, no nipple involvement clinically/on imaging; inflammatory cancer excludes skin-sparing approaches","Plastic surgery co-consult early for immediate reconstruction pathways"],"tx":["Oncologic equivalence with conservation+RT for eligible patients — the choice is values-based; document the shared decision","Post-mastectomy radiation (PMRT) for: ≥4 positive nodes (and often 1-3), T3-4, positive margins — PMRT likelihood shapes reconstruction choice/timing","Risk-reducing mastectomy: ~90-95% risk reduction; SLNB not routinely needed for prophylactic sides"],"tech":["Flap plane: between subcutaneous fat and breast parenchyma (oncoplastic 'mastectomy plane') — VIABLE flaps are the operation: too thin necroses, too thick leaves breast tissue; perfusion assessment (ICG) in skin/nipple-sparing cases","Boundaries: clavicle to inframammary fold, sternum to latissimus edge; take pectoralis FASCIA with the specimen (muscle stays)","Modified radical: en bloc level I-II axillary contents (see axillary entry — nerves: long thoracic, thoracodorsal, intercostobrachial)","Nipple-sparing: retroareolar margin sent separately — positive = the nipple goes; incision placement (inframammary favored) protects perfusion","Drains under flaps ± axilla; immediate reconstruction proceeds with plastics (expander/implant ± ADM, or autologous)"],"after":["Drains out by output criteria (<30mL/day typical); seroma after removal → aspirate as needed","FLAP NECROSIS watch (first week): demarcating dusky edges — small areas heal by care, full-thickness needs debridement (and threatens implants beneath — escalate with plastics early)","Post-mastectomy pain syndrome and axillary web syndrome are real — early PT referral, validate and treat","No BP/blood draws mandates on the ADS-side arm are tradition more than evidence for SLNB-only patients — follow institutional lymphedema guidance; formal ALND patients get lymphedema surveillance/referral","Pathology → PMRT decision → systemic therapy sequencing; prosthesis/bra fitting resources and psychosocial support belong in the discharge packet"],"pearls":["The mastectomy flap is a perfusion experiment you graded with your scissors — respect the plane and doubt the dusky edge early","PMRT changes reconstruction physics — the expander conversation must anticipate radiation before the first incision","Prophylactic surgery removes risk, not certainty — the counseling includes the small residual and the surveillance that continues"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Breast Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"ASBrS Mastectomy Resources","u":"https://www.breastsurgeons.org"}]},{"id":"gs-slnb","name":"Sentinel Node Biopsy & Axillary Dissection","sec":"gs","present":["SLNB: staging the clinically node-negative axilla in invasive breast cancer (and melanoma ≥T1b) — the node(s) that drain first tell the story for the basin","ALND (levels I-II): clinically positive persistent disease, SLNB-positive outside Z0011-type criteria, recurrence, inflammatory cancer","The modern arc is DE-ESCALATION: Z0011 (1-2 positive SLNs with lumpectomy+RT → no ALND), AMAROS (axillary RT ≈ ALND with less lymphedema), SENOMAC (ALND-omission extended to selected mastectomy patients with 1-2 +SLN), and now SLNB OMISSION itself in selected early-stage cN0 disease (SOUND/INSEMA; 2025 ASCO update - small tumor, negative axillary US, favorable biology)"],"dx":["Clinically suspicious nodes: US + FNA/core FIRST — proven disease pre-op changes the plan (and neoadjuvant may still de-escalate: clipped-node targeted axillary dissection after response)","Dual-tracer mapping (technetium sulfur colloid + blue dye) maximizes identification (~97-99%); single-agent acceptable per experience","Neoadjuvant setting: SLNB after therapy with dual tracer + ≥3 nodes + clipped node retrieval keeps false-negatives acceptable"],"tx":["SLN positive: apply trial criteria honestly — Z0011 patients (T1-2, 1-2 +SLN, BCT with whole-breast RT) skip ALND; mastectomy patients with +SLN → axillary RT (AMAROS) vs ALND discussion","Melanoma: positive SLN → surveillance US (MSLT-II) over completion dissection for most","ALND remains for gross/persistent disease — do it completely when you do it"],"tech":["SLNB: inject tracer (peritumoral/subareolar), gamma-probe transcutaneous mapping, small axillary incision, follow counts + blue channels to the sentinel node(s) — remove all nodes ≥10% of the hottest count + any palpably suspicious node (the 'hard brown node' outranks the isotope)","Confirm bed counts drop appropriately; send for pathology (frozen section selectively — de-escalation trials reduced its urgency)","ALND: levels I-II en bloc (level III only for gross disease) — borders: axillary vein superiorly, latissimus laterally, chest wall medially","PROTECT THE NAMED STRUCTURES: long thoracic nerve (medial, on serratus — winged scapula), thoracodorsal bundle (posterolateral — latissimus function), intercostobrachial nerves (sensory — sacrifice = numb inner arm, warn preop), axillary vein","Clip the lymphatic leash inferiorly (lymphedema-conscious technique); drain for ALND, not SLNB"],"after":["SLNB: outpatient, minimal restrictions; blue dye counseling (urine/skin tint transient; rare anaphylaxis intraop is anesthesia's shared briefing)","ALND: drain management, early shoulder ROM protocol (frozen shoulder prevention) balanced against seroma","LYMPHEDEMA surveillance is the lifelong assignment (ALND ~15-25%, +RT higher; SLNB ~5%): baseline limb measurements, early-referral triggers, compression resources — early treatment changes trajectory","Axillary web syndrome (cording): weeks post-op, PT fixes it — name it so the patient isn't frightened","Pathology completes staging → medical/radiation oncology sequencing"],"pearls":["Palpate the axilla before trusting the probe — the replaced node is cold, hard, and the whole point","Z0011 and AMAROS are practiced, not just cited: applying them precisely spares arms; applying them loosely under-treats axillae","The long thoracic nerve hugs the chest wall exactly where suction retracts — retract with sponges, not vigor"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOSOG Z0011 / EORTC AMAROS Trials","u":"https://www.swog.org"},{"t":"NCCN Breast Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"gs-hemorrhoids","name":"Hemorrhoidectomy","sec":"gs","present":["Internal hemorrhoids by grade: I bleed, II prolapse-reduce spontaneously, III require manual reduction, IV irreducible; external disease = pain/thrombosis/skin tags","Surgical candidates: grade III-IV, mixed disease, failure of office therapy, thrombosed external hemorrhoid within ~72h (excision relieves)","BLEEDING PER RECTUM IS NOT HEMORRHOIDS UNTIL THE COLON IS CLEARED when risk/age/symptom criteria say so — anemia, change in habits, or age-appropriate screening gaps get endoscopy"],"dx":["Anoscopy grades internal disease; DRE for masses/tone","Rule out the mimics: fissure (pain out of proportion, posterior midline), abscess/fistula, prolapse, condyloma, anal cancer (any atypical/indurated lesion gets biopsy)","Optimize first: fiber 25-35g, water, no straining/phone-toilet time — half of 'surgical' hemorrhoids improve"],"tx":["Office ladder for grades I-III: rubber band ligation (internal, above dentate — effective, repeatable), sclerotherapy/infrared for bleeding grade I-II; NEVER band external disease (somatic innervation = severe pain)","Excisional hemorrhoidectomy (Ferguson closed/Milligan-Morgan open) for grade III-IV/mixed — most durable, most painful","Alternatives: stapled hemorrhoidopexy (less pain, higher recurrence, distinct complications), doppler-guided artery ligation — selection conversations","Thrombosed external <72h: elliptical EXCISION (not incision — clot re-forms) under local; >72h: supportive (sitz, analgesia) as pain is already crescendoed"],"tech":["Excisional: elliptical excision of the hemorrhoidal column from external skin to pedicle, PRESERVE SKIN BRIDGES between columns (take all three carelessly = anal stenosis, the career complication), suture-ligate the pedicle, close (Ferguson) or leave open (Milligan-Morgan) — both work","Depth discipline: stay on the hemorrhoidal plane, protect the internal sphincter fibers beneath (visible white fibers = warning)","Local anesthetic blocks (pudendal/perianal with long-acting agents ± liposomal bupivacaine) are the difference between recovery at home and readmission for pain","Banding technique: apply above the dentate line, confirm placement painless BEFORE deploying — pain on grasp = too low, reposition"],"after":["The honest speech: excisional hemorrhoidectomy hurts for 1-2 weeks — multimodal analgesia (NSAIDs scheduled, topical diltiazem/nifedipine for spasm, minimal opioids), sitz baths, fiber + stool softeners from day 0 (the first bowel movement is the boss fight)","URINARY RETENTION is the most common early complication (pelvic floor spasm + fluids + opioids): limit IV fluids intraop, confirm void before discharge","Bleeding: minor spotting expected; significant delayed bleed POD5-10 (band/eschar separation) → return, exam under anesthesia if brisk","Watch: fever/urinary symptoms/severe pain = exclude pelvic sepsis (rare, lethal, classically post-banding — immediate evaluation)","Long-term: fiber forever; recurrence managed by ladder again"],"pearls":["Skin bridges are sacred — the stenosed anus is a lifelong reproach from a 30-second decision","Post-banding fever + retention + escalating pain is pelvic sepsis until examined — the phone call no one may sit on","The colonoscopy question comes before the anoscope leaves the drawer: 'hemorrhoidal bleeding' has hidden many cancers"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Hemorrhoid Guidelines","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hemorrhoids","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/hemorrhoids.html"}]},{"id":"gs-fistula","name":"Anal Fistulotomy & Seton Placement","sec":"gs","present":["Fistula-in-ano: chronic draining perianal opening after an abscess (30-50% of drained abscesses fistulize), recurrent abscesses at the same site","Goodsall's rule roughly predicts tracts (anterior external openings → radial; posterior → curve to posterior midline) — trust it loosely","CROHN'S CHANGES EVERYTHING: multiple/complex/recurrent fistulas or atypical anatomy → evaluate for Crohn's before aggressive surgery"],"dx":["EUA (exam under anesthesia) is the diagnostic core: identify external and INTERNAL openings (gentle probe, hydrogen peroxide via external opening bubbles at the internal), classify by Parks (intersphincteric, transsphincteric, suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric)","MRI pelvis for complex/recurrent/Crohn's disease — maps tracts and hidden collections","Continence assessment BEFORE cutting any muscle: prior obstetric injury, prior fistula surgery, baseline function — the sphincter budget is finite"],"tx":["The eternal trade: CURE vs CONTINENCE — lay open more muscle = heal more, leak more","Simple low fistulas (<30% sphincter, intersphincteric/low trans): fistulotomy cures ~90%+","Complex/high tracts: draining seton first (controls sepsis, matures tract), then definitive: LIFT procedure, advancement flap, plug/glue (modest success) — staged thinking","Crohn's fistulas: setons + BIOLOGICS (medical disease, surgical drainage) — aggressive fistulotomy in Crohn's costs continence for nothing"],"tech":["Fistulotomy: probe the tract from external to internal opening, confirm the muscle involved is safely divisible, lay open over the probe, curette granulation, marsupialize edges (faster healing)","When the probe angles high or the muscle bulk above it is substantial — STOP: place a seton instead of finishing a fistulotomy your patient's continence can't afford","Draining seton: vessel loop/suture through the tract, tied loosely as a wick (not cutting) — sepsis control while plans mature","LIFT: intersphincteric groove incision, isolate the tract between sphincters, ligate/divide both sides, close the internal-side defect — sphincter-sparing, ~60-70% success","Advancement flap: excise internal opening, mobilize a mucosal/submucosal (± partial muscle) flap, cover the defect — for high tracts with good tissue"],"after":["Open wounds heal by secondary intention over WEEKS: sitz baths, irrigation, dressing routine, fiber/softeners — set the timeline honestly (6-12 weeks is normal)","Seton care: it stays for weeks-months; drainage around it is its job, not a failure — schedule the definitive-plan follow-up before discharge","Watch: recurrent abscess (undrained extension → re-EUA/MRI), non-healing >3 months (wrong internal opening found, Crohn's, rarely malignancy — biopsy chronic tracts)","Continence check-ins after any muscle division: minor flatus incontinence is common transiently; solid stool incontinence is a complication conversation","Crohn's co-management with GI is continuous"],"pearls":["You can always cut more muscle later; you can never put it back — when in doubt, seton","The internal opening is the fistula: fail to find and address it and the tract laughs at everything else you did","The chronic fistula that won't heal despite good surgery gets a biopsy and a Crohn's workup, in that order"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Anorectal Abscess & Fistula Guidelines","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"ECCO Perianal Crohn's Resources","u":"https://www.ecco-ibd.eu"}]},{"id":"gs-perianal-abscess","name":"Perianal Abscess I&D","sec":"gs","present":["Cryptoglandular origin (infected anal gland at the dentate line) spreading to spaces: perianal (most common — superficial, exquisitely tender lump), ischioanal (buttock fullness/cellulitis), intersphincteric (severe rectal pain, normal external exam), supralevator (pelvic origin — ill patient, minimal external findings)","Throbbing anal pain, worse sitting/defecation ± fever; fluctuance may be absent in deep abscesses","'Severe anal pain with a normal external exam' = intersphincteric abscess until EUA/imaging says otherwise"],"dx":["Clinical for superficial disease; CT/MRI pelvis for suspected deep (supralevator/ischioanal extension), recurrent disease, immunocompromised, or the toxic patient","DRE (as tolerated) for boggy tenderness/mass; screen the story for Crohn's and immunosuppression","Glucose check in the recurrent/severe — undiagnosed diabetes rides with anorectal sepsis; necrotizing spread (crepitus, spreading erythema, toxicity) escalates to the Fournier pathway immediately"],"tx":["Incision and drainage — TODAY: antibiotics do not drain pus, and anorectal sepsis in the diabetic/immunocompromised advances by the hour","Antibiotics ADJUNCTIVE for: surrounding cellulitis, systemic signs, immunocompromise, valve/hardware concerns — after drainage; also evidence they modestly reduce later fistula formation","Do NOT chase/probe for a fistula in the acutely inflamed field — the false passage you create becomes the complex fistula you couldn't find; drainage now, fistula assessment at follow-up/EUA"],"tech":["Superficial: cruciate or elliptical incision over maximal fluctuance AS CLOSE TO THE ANAL VERGE as the abscess allows (shortens any future fistula tract), evacuate, break loculations gently, irrigate — packing lightly once for hemostasis; repeated deep packing adds pain without healing benefit","Ischioanal: adequate incision, may need counter-drainage; bilateral ('horseshoe') disease: drain the deep postanal space (the keystone) + counter incisions","Intersphincteric: drain INTERNALLY via internal sphincterotomy over the abscess into the anal canal — external incisions miss it","Supralevator: define the ORIGIN first (imaging): pelvic source drains toward the rectum, upward cryptoglandular extension drains via ischioanal route — draining the wrong direction creates an extrasphincteric fistula (the disaster tract)","EUA is the setting for anything deep, recurrent, or intolerable awake"],"after":["Sitz baths TID + after BMs, dressing/hygiene routine, fiber + softeners, analgesia; most wounds close in 2-4 weeks","The 30-50% fistula speech happens at discharge: persistent drainage >6-8 weeks = fistula → planned EUA — forewarned patients return for follow-up instead of urgent care","Diabetics/immunocompromised: 24-48h wound checks, low threshold for repeat imaging/EUA if not clearly improving","Recurrent same-site abscess = untreated fistula until proven otherwise","Crohn's suspicion (multiple, complex, atypical): GI referral from the follow-up chair"],"pearls":["Antibiotics without drainage is how a perianal abscess becomes Fournier's over a weekend","Drain close to the verge — future-you probing a short tract will send thanks","The patient in 10/10 anal pain with a 'normal exam' has pus between the sphincters — believe the pain, book the EUA"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Anorectal Abscess Guidelines","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"WSES Skin/Soft Tissue Infection Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"}]},{"id":"gs-pilonidal","name":"Pilonidal Disease Excision","sec":"gs","present":["Midline gluteal cleft pits ± chronic sinus drainage; acute abscess (tender fluctuant mass off-midline typically); recurrent flares in hirsute young adults, drivers/prolonged sitters","Disease of hair and friction, not congenital cysts — management logic follows that biology","Spectrum: asymptomatic pits (leave alone) → acute abscess → chronic/recurrent sinus disease"],"dx":["Clinical; probe chronic tracts gently at elective evaluation; MRI only for extensive/recurrent atypical disease","The atypical non-healing wound with raised edges after years of disease: biopsy (rare SCC in chronic disease)","Distinguish from perianal fistula (tracts orient cephalad from the cleft, not toward the anal canal) and hidradenitis"],"tx":["Acute abscess: I&D off-midline over fluctuance — simple, bedside/minor OR; definitive surgery is NEVER done through acute pus","Chronic disease escalation ladder: hair control + hygiene → pit-picking/minimal procedures (Gips, sinusectomy) → excision with OFF-MIDLINE closure (Karydakis, Bascom cleft-lift) → flaps (Limberg) for recurrent/extensive disease","Midline excision left open heals slowly (weeks-months) and midline closures break down — the field has moved decisively OFF the midline","Nonoperative pillars for everyone: laser/epilation hair removal (recurrence reduction evidence), cleft hygiene, weight/sitting modification"],"tech":["I&D: off-midline incision, evacuate, remove visible hair nests, light packing/none, no heroics","Pit-picking (Gips): excise the midline pits with tiny trephines + lateral drainage incision for the cavity — day-case, low morbidity, respectable success for limited disease","Karydakis/cleft-lift concept: asymmetric elliptical excision of diseased tissue, mobilize a flap to close OFF-midline, flattening the cleft (removes the anaerobic hair-milling environment) — closed suction drain per extent","Limberg rhomboid flap: rhomboid excision to fascia + transposition flap for big/recurrent disease — mark awake/standing, flatten the cleft, tension-free inset","Technical religion: eliminate the midline wound, flatten the cleft, obliterate dead space, control hair"],"after":["Open wounds: packing/dressing routine + weekly checks; closed/flap repairs: drain management, no direct pressure/prolonged sitting per protocol, activity graded over 2-4 weeks","HAIR CONTROL IS AFTERCARE: shaving/depilation/laser of the cleft margins for months — the recurrence prevention that outlives the sutures","Wound breakdown of midline portions is common and managed with care, not panic; true recurrence → escalate the ladder (flap territory)","Counsel realistic timelines and recurrence rates (10-30% lifetime depending on method/behavior) — surprise recurrences feel like failures; predicted ones feel like biology"],"pearls":["Pilonidal surgery is won off the midline — every durable technique is a way of not leaving a wound in the ditch","Definitive excision through an acute abscess trades one operation for three — drain now, plan later","The laser hair appointment prevents more recurrences than the scalpel did"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCRS Pilonidal Disease Guideline","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pilonidal Sinus","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001233.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-skin-excision","name":"Skin & Soft Tissue Excision (Lipoma, Cyst, Melanoma WLE)","sec":"gs","present":["Lipoma: soft, mobile, painless subcutaneous mass (excise for symptoms, growth, cosmesis, or diagnostic doubt — rapid growth/size >5cm/deep/firm = image and biopsy: sarcoma question)","Epidermoid cyst: mobile nodule with central punctum; inflamed cysts get incision/drainage or steroid, EXCISION only when quiescent","Melanoma: the excision with rules — after biopsy-proven diagnosis, wide local excision by Breslow depth"],"dx":["Suspicious pigmented lesions: NARROW-margin excisional/full-thickness biopsy first (1-3mm) — never shave a suspected melanoma thin or wide-excise before staging thinking","Melanoma staging: Breslow depth drives everything (margins + SLNB threshold ≥0.8mm with ulceration or ≥1mm)","Soft tissue mass red flags (deep to fascia, >5cm, firm, fixed, rapid growth): MRI + core biopsy BEFORE excision — the 'lipoma' that was sarcoma, excised piecemeal, is a preventable tragedy"],"tx":["Melanoma WLE margins: in situ 0.5-1cm; ≤1mm Breslow → 1cm; 1-2mm → 1-2cm; >2mm → 2cm — down to fascia","SLNB coordinated at the SAME anesthetic as WLE when indicated (map before the lymphatics are disturbed)","Benign lesions: complete excision with the capsule (lipoma) or entire cyst wall (leave wall = recurrence)"],"tech":["Orient the ellipse along relaxed skin tension lines with 3:1 length:width; melanoma WLE incisions on extremities run LONGITUDINALLY (future lymphatic/re-excision planning)","Lipoma: incise, dissect the capsule circumferentially, deliver whole — deep/intramuscular ones earn their MRI in the OR when planes surprise you","Cyst: elliptical incision including the punctum, dissect the intact wall without rupture; ruptured = irrigate and chase remaining wall","Melanoma WLE: measured margins from the biopsy scar/lesion edges marked BEFORE infiltration distorts, excise as one block to fascia, ORIENT the specimen for pathology","Close in layers without tension; know when the defect needs more than you (flap/graft territory → coordinate, don't improvise on a cancer bed)"],"after":["Standard wound care; pathology review on EVERYTHING excised — the routine lipoma reads benign 99 times and changes a life the hundredth","Melanoma: margins clear? SLNB result? → staging completion, dermatology surveillance (full-body exams), oncology for node-positive (adjuvant immunotherapy era)","Cyst/lipoma recurrence = retained wall/capsule — re-excise properly","Teach sun protection + self-examination at melanoma visits; first-degree-relative screening counseling"],"pearls":["Every excised specimen goes in formalin, no exceptions — the pathology jar is cheaper than the missed sarcoma deposition","Biopsy first, then WLE with measured margins: sequence is the standard of care in melanoma, and shortcuts unravel staging","A deep, firm, growing 'lipoma' is a sarcoma consult, not a lunchtime excision"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Melanoma Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Skin Lesion Removal","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002964.htm"}]},{"id":"gs-nec-fasc","name":"Debridement of Necrotizing Soft Tissue Infection","sec":"gs","present":["PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to visible findings is the cardinal early sign; then: rapid progression, tense edema beyond erythema borders, bullae, skin dusky/anesthetic, crepitus (variable), systemic toxicity out of scale","Fournier's gangrene = perineal/genital variant (diabetics, perianal sources) — same disease, same urgency","Types: polymicrobial (type I — diabetics, perineal), group A strep (type II — can hit the healthy young, 'flu-like' prodrome then fulminant), vibrio (water exposure, cirrhotics)"],"dx":["THIS IS A CLINICAL/SURGICAL DIAGNOSIS: high suspicion → OR for exploration; imaging must never delay the incision in the toxic patient","Adjuncts when genuinely equivocal AND stable: CT (fascial gas/edema/non-enhancement), LRINEC score (supports, never excludes)","Finger test/bedside cut-down: dishwater fluid, gray non-bleeding fascia, tissue planes that separate with a finger = diagnosis made","Labs: lactate, CK, sodium (low), WBC — sepsis panel; blood + tissue cultures from the OR"],"tx":["The treatment is SURGERY: emergent radical debridement — hours matter, mortality climbs with every OR delay (20-30%+ overall)","Antibiotics adjunctive: vancomycin + piperacillin-tazobactam (or carbapenem) + CLINDAMYCIN (toxin suppression for strep/staph); IVIG considered for strep toxic shock per practice","Aggressive sepsis resuscitation runs in parallel — the OR is part of the resuscitation"],"tech":["Longitudinal incisions over the worst area, extend until VIABLE bleeding tissue with adherent planes is reached in every direction — debride ALL gray/non-bleeding/foul fascia and nonviable tissue; the specimen looks like too much because enough always does","Test planes with the finger — easy separation = still infected territory; chase it","Fournier's: debride perineum widely, testicles usually survive (separate blood supply — tunnel them subcutaneously/protect), diverting colostomy or fecal management for fecally contaminated wounds per extent","PLAN THE RETURN: pack/NPWT, leave open, mandatory second look at 12-24h — average patients need 2-4 debridements; 'one heroic debridement' thinking kills","Amputation is on the table for limb-destructive disease with physiologic collapse — life over limb, decided early with a witness"],"after":["ICU sepsis care; scheduled re-explorations until a clean, stable wound at two consecutive looks","Nutrition aggressively (protein losses are massive — think burn-level support), glucose control, culture-directed antibiotic narrowing","Wound phase: NPWT, staged closure/grafting/flaps with plastics once biology permits — reconstruction is a later chapter, not the same book's first pages","Mortality/morbidity conversations with family early and honestly; multi-service coordination (urology for Fournier's, ID, plastics)","Survivors: rehab, psychological support, scar/function follow-up for the long haul"],"pearls":["The eyes make this diagnosis before any scan — severe pain plus a sick patient plus a soft-tissue story earns an operation, not an MRI slot","Debride to bleeding, plan to return: the second look is booked in the first operation's op note","Clindamycin is there to shut the toxin factory — add it up front in the toxic patient, don't wait for speciation"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Necrotizing SSTI Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"WSES NSTI Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"}]},{"id":"gs-fasciotomy","name":"Fasciotomy (Extremity Compartment Syndrome)","sec":"gs","present":["Compartment syndrome: pressure within a fixed fascial compartment exceeds perfusion — after fractures (tibia classically), crush, reperfusion (post-vascular repair), burns, tight casts, prolonged compression (found-down)","EARLY: pain out of proportion + PAIN WITH PASSIVE STRETCH of compartment muscles, tense swollen compartment, paresthesias; LATE (dead-limb signs): pallor, pulselessness, paralysis — pulses present until the end","The awake reliable exam is the monitor; the obtunded/blocked patient needs pressures or prophylactic release"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis in the examinable patient — serial documented exams by the same standard","Compartment pressures for the unexaminable: delta pressure (diastolic BP − compartment pressure) <30 mmHg = release; absolute >30 supportive","CK/myoglobin track muscle death and rhabdo kidney risk; remove circumferential dressings/bivalve casts IMMEDIATELY as the first 'procedure'","The clock: irreversible muscle injury begins ~4-6h of ischemia — decision speed is the outcome"],"tx":["Suspicion + supporting exam = fasciotomy; the threshold errs early — a negative fasciotomy is a scar, a missed compartment syndrome is a contracted useless limb and a lawsuit built on 'we watched'","Post-revascularization limbs (prolonged ischemia): prophylactic fasciotomy at the index vascular operation is often right","Treat rhabdomyolysis in parallel: volume, urine output targets, electrolyte surveillance"],"tech":["LEG (4 compartments) — two-incision technique: LATERAL incision (anterior + lateral compartments — identify/protect the superficial peroneal nerve near the intermuscular septum), MEDIAL incision 1-2cm posterior to tibia (superficial + DEEP posterior — the deep posterior release under soleus origin is the one commonly missed; take down the soleal bridge and see FDL/tib post to prove it)","Full-length skin AND fascial releases — short 'mini' releases under intact skin defeat the purpose; muscle should bulge freely","FOREARM: volar (lazy-S from biceps aponeurosis to carpal tunnel — release lacertus, superficial + deep flexors, carpal tunnel) ± dorsal; hand/foot/thigh compartments per pattern of injury","Debride clearly dead muscle (contract-color-consistency-capacity to bleed); dress open (NPWT/moist), NEVER close primarily at the index operation","Document compartment softness + nerve status post-release"],"after":["Return 48-72h: reassess muscle viability, further debridement PRN; closure by delayed primary (gradual approximation techniques) or split-thickness graft when swelling permits","Rhabdo management: fluids to urine output, potassium/calcium vigilance, renal function tracking","Elevate to heart level (not above — perfusion pressure matters now), splint in function, early therapy once closed/grafted","Late sequelae owned honestly: nerve deficits, contractures (Volkmann's in the missed forearm), chronic swelling — rehab referral early","The missed deep posterior compartment presents as claw toes and a plantar-numb foot months later — the exam at release prevents the clinic revelation"],"pearls":["Passive stretch pain in a high-risk limb is the diagnosis knocking — pulses and pretty toes are alibis, not evidence","Release everything, full length, and prove the deep posterior compartment — the fasciotomy that missed one compartment counts as zero","When debating fasciotomy at 2am, the debate is the indication"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS/OTA Compartment Syndrome Resources","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"EAST Extremity Trauma Guidelines","u":"https://www.east.org"}]},{"id":"gs-amputation","name":"Lower Extremity Amputation (Toe / BKA / AKA)","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: non-reconstructable critical limb ischemia, non-salvageable diabetic foot sepsis (the 'life over limb' presentation), necrotizing infection, trauma with unreconstructable limb, refractory osteomyelitis, malignancy","The decision balances: healing potential (perfusion), rehabilitation potential (BKA rehab ≫ AKA — preserve the knee whenever biology allows), and sepsis control urgency","Frame it right: amputation is a RECONSTRUCTIVE operation toward a functional prosthetic limb, not a defeat — the framing changes consent and recovery"],"dx":["Perfusion mapping picks the level: pulses, ABI/toe pressures (toe >30, TcPO2 >30-40 favor healing), angiography when revascularization might lower the level","Sepsis source mapping: X-ray/MRI for osteomyelitis extent, probe-to-bone; glucose/nutrition (albumin) as healing predictors","Vascular surgery co-evaluation: revascularize-then-minor-amputate beats primary major amputation when anatomy allows"],"tx":["Wet gangrene/uncontrolled sepsis: URGENT debridement/guillotine amputation for source control, formalize the stump when stable (two-stage in the septic)","Dry gangrene/chronic: optimize (perfusion, glucose, nutrition) then single-stage definitive amputation at the healing level","Level selection is a rehab decision as much as vascular: BKA if the knee + tissue can heal; AKA for non-healing BKA territory or non-ambulators with flexion contractures"],"tech":["Toe/ray: tennis-racquet incisions, disarticulate/resect through healthy bone, preserve plantar skin for closure, send bone margin cultures/path","BKA (posterior flap/Burgess): tibia divided ~10-15cm below tubercle with anterior bevel, fibula 1-2cm shorter, LONG POSTERIOR MYOCUTANEOUS FLAP (gastrocnemius — the flap IS the operation) rotated over the bone ends, myodesis/myoplasty, handle nerves with traction-neurectomy (cut high, let retract — neuroma prevention), drain per practice","AKA: fish-mouth flaps, femur division mid-thigh (length preserves lever arm), MYODESIS OF ADDUCTORS to the femur (prevents the abducted femur that ruins prosthetic fit), anterior-posterior muscle balance","Hemostasis meticulously (these bleed into flaps), no tension closure, soft dressing ± knee immobilizer (BKA — flexion contracture prevention from hour one)","Guillotine stage (sepsis): circular division above infection, leave open, plan formalization at 5-7 days"],"after":["Stump care: compression/shrinker shaping when incision permits, KNEE EXTENSION protocol for BKA (contracture = prosthetic failure), prone lying for AKA hip extension","EARLY PT/rehab + prosthetist referral — the pathway to walking starts in the hospital, not at the 6-week visit; pre-prosthetic conditioning matters","Phantom limb sensation is near-universal, phantom PAIN common: preemptive multimodal analgesia (gabapentinoids, regional catheters), mirror therapy, validate and treat","Wound surveillance: breakdown/nonhealing → reassess perfusion before blaming technique (the vascular consult returns); infection → debride early","The systemic sentence: an amputation for vascular disease predicts cardiovascular mortality — risk-factor management and contralateral-limb surveillance (daily foot checks, podiatry) are the survival care; depression screening is part of follow-up"],"pearls":["The knee is worth fighting for: BKA patients walk at multiples the rate of AKA — level decisions are rehabilitation decisions","In sepsis, cut fast and formalize later — the two-stage amputation is humility that saves lives","The contralateral foot is the next operation unless someone starts examining it today"],"refs":[{"t":"SVS Chronic Limb-Threatening Ischemia Guidelines","u":"https://vascular.org"},{"t":"Amputee Coalition Resources","u":"https://www.amputee-coalition.org"}]},{"id":"gs-trach","name":"Tracheostomy","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: prolonged mechanical ventilation (the ICU conversation typically at 7-14 days of anticipated ongoing need), upper airway obstruction, airway protection/secretion management, adjunct in major head/neck surgery","Benefits over prolonged ETT: comfort, less sedation, secretion access, speech/swallow pathways, weaning facilitation — timing trials (TracMan) show no mortality magic; individualized","Emergency surgical airway (cricothyroidotomy) is a different operation with different rules — this entry is the elective/semi-elective one"],"dx":["Anatomy assessment: neck landmarks, prior surgery/radiation, goiter, high-riding innominate artery (pulsate check), obesity (extended-length tube planning), coagulation status/anticoagulation timing","Ventilator status: FiO2/PEEP requirements shape timing and technique choice","Goals-of-care alignment BEFORE the operation — a tracheostomy is a trajectory statement"],"tx":["Percutaneous dilational tracheostomy (PDT) at the bedside with bronchoscopic guidance = standard for favorable-anatomy ICU patients (outcomes ≈ open, resource-friendly)","Open surgical tracheostomy for: difficult anatomy, coagulopathy concerns, prior neck surgery/radiation, pediatric, emergency conversion","Timing: 'early vs late' resolved into individualized — commit when the ventilation trajectory declares itself"],"tech":["Open: transverse incision ~2cm below cricoid, midline vertical dissection between straps, divide/retract thyroid isthmus, EXPOSE rings 2-4; tracheal entry between rings 2-3 (window/Björk flap/vertical per practice — NEVER through ring 1/cricoid = subglottic stenosis)","STAY MAY-SUTURES to tracheal edges (the lifeline for early dislodgement recannulation) + mark them L/R; insert tube under vision, confirm ETCO2 + bilateral sounds BEFORE the ETT (withdrawn to just above) leaves the cords","PDT: bronchoscope in the ETT visualizes everything — midline needle between rings 1-2/2-3 confirmed endoscopically, wire, serial/single dilation (Ciaglia), tube over dilator, scope confirms intratracheal placement","FiO2 ≤40% + no cautery near an open airway = airway fire discipline","Secure with ties AND sutures per practice; obesity: proximal-extended tubes prevent the tip riding anterior"],"after":["THE FIRST 7 DAYS RULE: an immature tract that decannulates is a lost airway — bedside sign, stay sutures visible, same-size + smaller tube AND the algorithm at the bedside: attempt oral intubation FIRST for early dislodgement (blind stoma re-insertion creates false passages); after ~7 days the tract recannulates safely","First tube change at 5-7+ days by an experienced operator; cuff management/downsizing/fenestration toward speech (speaking valve trials with speech pathology — cuff DOWN always with valve on)","BLEEDING: minor stomal common; ANY brisk/pulsatile bleed after POD3 = tracheoinnominate fistula until excluded (herald bleed!) → overinflate cuff/finger compression against sternum + emergency surgery — the once-a-career catastrophe everyone must know cold","Secretion care: humidification, suction protocols, inner-cannula cleaning; stoma skin care","Decannulation pathway: cap trials with downsized tube, effective cough, airway patency (scope if doubt) → decannulate, occlusive dressing, stoma closes in days; long-term: tracheal stenosis surveillance for the symptomatic"],"pearls":["The herald bleed from a trach on POD10 is the innominate artery introducing itself — cuff up, finger in, OR now","Early accidental decannulation is an intubation from above, not a poke from outside — the false passage kills faster than the apnea","Stay sutures cost two minutes and convert a 3am airway disaster into a controlled recannulation"],"refs":[{"t":"SCCM/ATS Tracheostomy Resources","u":"https://www.sccm.org"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Tracheostomy Care Guidelines","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"gs-chest-tube","name":"Chest Tube Thoracostomy","sec":"gs","present":["Indications: pneumothorax (tension, traumatic, symptomatic/enlarging spontaneous), hemothorax, large/infected effusions (empyema), post-thoracic surgery drainage","Tension physiology (see EM PTX) is decompressed BEFORE imaging; everything else earns a look first","Size philosophy modernized: small-bore (14Fr pigtail) equals large-bore for pneumothorax and many effusions; large-bore (28-32Fr) retains its place in hemothorax/trauma"],"dx":["CXR/POCUS/CT define the target; ULTRASOUND-mark effusions (site + depth) — the marked spot moves with position, so mark in the drainage position","Coagulation review for the elective tube (correct what's correctable; emergencies proceed)","Know the diaphragm's excursion — the 'effusion' below the diaphragm is a liver"],"tx":["Pneumothorax: pigtail/small-bore, water seal early; hemothorax: 28-32Fr, monitor output against thoracotomy triggers (>1500mL initial or >200mL/h ×2-4h = OR conversation)","Empyema: drain + intrapleural tPA/DNase (MIST2) for loculated collections; VATS decortication when tubes+lytics fail","Post-pull or persistent air leak management is half the skill (below)"],"tech":["SAFE TRIANGLE (anterior to latissimus, lateral to pectoralis, above the 5th intercostal space at anterior axillary line) — tubes placed low find the abdomen","Open technique: local generously (skin THROUGH pleura + rib periosteum), incision over the rib BELOW the target space, blunt dissect with Kelly OVER THE TOP OF THE RIB (neurovascular bundle hides below each rib), controlled pop through pleura, FINGER SWEEP confirms lung/adhesions before any tube passes, direct tube posterior-apical (PTX) or posterior-basal (fluid), all fenestrations inside","Pigtail/Seldinger: US/landmark site, needle over the rib with aspiration confirming, wire, dilate, catheter — same rib-top religion","Connect to drainage system: 20cmH2O suction vs water seal per indication; SECURE with heavy suture + dressing (the tube that pulls out chose its own adventure)","Confirm: fogging, swing, output, and the post-placement CXR (tip position, last hole inside, lung expansion)"],"after":["Daily assessment discipline: output volume/character, AIR LEAK (bubbling on water seal — continuous vs forced-exhalation only), swing (patency), dressing/site","Removal criteria: lung expanded + leak resolved (water-seal trial per practice) + output below threshold (<100-200mL/day serous, indication-dependent) → pull at peak inspiration/Valsalva with occlusive dressing pre-positioned, post-pull CXR per practice","Persistent air leak >3-5 days: dedicated evaluation (tube position/last hole out? large leak = bronchopleural concern) → thoracic surgery conversation (blood patch/valves/VATS)","Clogged/non-draining tube with retained hemothorax: early VATS evacuation (POD3-7 window) beats a second tube into clot (retained hemothorax → empyema/trapped lung)","Complications catalog honestly: malposition (fissure/abdomen/lung parenchyma — imaging when function disappoints), re-expansion pulmonary edema (drain massive effusions in stages), bleeding (intercostal vessel — the below-rib error)"],"pearls":["Over the rib, finger first, fenestrations inside — three rules that prevent ninety percent of chest tube grief","A tube that stops swinging is a decoration — flush per protocol, image, or replace before the effusion organizes","The second chest tube for retained hemothorax is usually a VATS postponed — call thoracic surgery at the first failed tube, not the third"],"refs":[{"t":"BTS Pleural Procedure Guidelines","u":"https://www.brit-thoracic.org.uk"},{"t":"EAST Hemothorax Management","u":"https://www.east.org"}]},{"id":"gs-central-line","name":"Central Venous Access & Port Placement","sec":"gs","present":["Central access for: vasopressors/irritant infusions, TPN, hemodialysis (temporary/tunneled), long-term chemotherapy (ports), difficult peripheral access, monitoring","Site selection is a complication conversation: IJ (US-friendly workhorse), subclavian (lowest infection, pneumothorax + compressibility trade-offs, avoid in dialysis-future patients — vein stenosis), femoral (fast, 'dirty' reputation partly rehabilitated, DVT consideration)","Ports/tunneled lines: implanted reservoirs for cyclical long-term therapy — different insertion, same principles"],"dx":["Pre-procedure: coagulation status (correct for elective; US-guided IJ tolerates moderate abnormality), prior lines/stenosis history (dialysis patients: MAP THE VEINS, protect the arms — fistula future), ipsilateral pathology","ULTRASOUND: patency + compressibility check before draping — the thrombosed IJ found after three passes was findable in three seconds"],"tx":["Ultrasound-guided insertion is the standard of care where anatomy permits — landmark-only technique persists for subclavian by preference/necessity","Full barrier precautions + chlorhexidine (the checklist isn't bureaucracy — CLABSI lines die by bundles)","Right-size the device: fewest lumens necessary, smallest effective catheter (thrombosis scales with catheter:vein ratio)"],"tech":["Seldinger religion: needle under real-time US (in-plane or out-of-plane, TIP ALWAYS SEEN), venous confirmation (compressibility/color/pressure waveform/manometry — dark blood is not proof), wire advances WITHOUT resistance (resistance = stop, reassess; ectopy = withdraw wire slightly), NEVER LET GO OF THE WIRE","Dilate skin+tract only (the dilator that follows the wire's curve gently; forced dilation lacerates veins), catheter to appropriate depth (right IJ ~13-15cm, left longer), aspirate/flush all lumens, secure + biopatch + dressing","Tip target: cavoatrial junction (CXR: ~2 vertebral bodies below carina) — confirm before high-risk infusions; post-procedure CXR for pneumothorax (subclavian/IJ)","PORT: tunneled from chest-wall pocket to venotomy (IJ/subclavian), reservoir sutured to pectoral fascia in a pocket sized snugly (seroma vs erosion balance), access with Huber needle confirming blood return; fluoroscopic confirmation standard","Tunneled dialysis catheters: retrograde tunnel, cuff positioned subcutaneously (infection barrier + anchor)"],"after":["Daily necessity review — the best CLABSI prevention is the line that came out yesterday; site inspection, dressing integrity per protocol","Complication vigilance: PNEUMOTHORAX (post-procedure CXR/symptoms), arterial injury (pulsatile/bright — if a DILATED arterial catheter: LEAVE IT, call vascular — pulling it bedside converts a problem into an exsanguination), arrhythmia (wire depth), air embolism (Trendelenburg + occlusive technique on removal too)","Line infection: erythema/purulence/unexplained fevers → cultures (paired peripheral + line), removal decisions per organism/necessity; ports: pocket infection = device usually comes out","Catheter dysfunction: no aspiration → flush protocol/tPA dwell/reposition study (fibrin sheath); catheter-associated DVT: anticoagulate, line stays if needed+functional per guidance","REMOVAL is a procedure: supine/Trendelenburg for neck lines, exhale/Valsalva, occlusive dressing, pressure — the fatal air embolism on removal is a preventable classic"],"pearls":["See the needle tip or you're guessing with a 7Fr consequence — the whole point of the ultrasound is the tip","The wire never leaves your hand; the guidewire retained in the patient is the never-event with your name on it","Dilated the artery? Freeze, leave it, phone vascular — heroism at the bedside is how carotid injuries become strokes"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC CLABSI Prevention Guidelines","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/infectioncontrol"},{"t":"ASA/SCCM Central Line Resources","u":"https://www.sccm.org"}]},{"id":"gs-chole-tube","name":"Cholecystostomy Tube Placement","sec":"gs","present":["Acute cholecystitis in the patient too sick/frail for cholecystectomy (severe comorbidity, late presentation, ICU physiology): source control without an operation","Also: acalculous cholecystitis (ICU disease — the septic ventilated patient with RUQ findings/unexplained sepsis), bridge-to-surgery in grade III cholecystitis","A management fork, not just a procedure: tube-first patients need a downstream plan (interval cholecystectomy vs tube destiny)"],"dx":["US: distension, wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, sonographic Murphy's; HIDA when the picture is murky; CT for complications (perforation, emphysematous change)","Acalculous disease in the ICU is a diagnosis of suspicion — unexplained sepsis + any gallbladder abnormality on US in the right host","Anatomy for route planning: transhepatic (through liver into gallbladder — tamponade effect, tract stability, standard) vs transperitoneal"],"tx":["IR percutaneous cholecystostomy under US/fluoro is the standard route; surgical (open/lap) cholecystostomy is the rare bailout (e.g., the aborted impossible cholecystectomy)","Antibiotics + resuscitation continue — the tube is source control, not the whole treatment","CHOCOLATE trial context: in high-risk-but-operable patients, lap chole outperformed tube placement — the tube is for the truly prohibitive, not the merely risky"],"tech":["Transhepatic route preferred: through segment V/VI liver parenchyma into the gallbladder body — the liver tract seals bile and stabilizes the catheter","Trocar or Seldinger technique: needle under US into the gallbladder, bile aspirated (culture it), wire coiled, dilate, LOCKING pigtail catheter deployed and locked, secured to skin; contrast confirms position (gentle — septic gallbladders dislike pressure)","Drain to gravity; document the tract route for the eventual surgeon (transhepatic tracts change the interval-chole dissection story)","Surgical Stamm-type cholecystostomy (when done): fundus purse-string, tube in, fundus tacked to abdominal wall"],"after":["Expect clinical improvement in 24-72h — failure to improve = re-image (tube position? undrained complication? wrong diagnosis?)","Tube care: flush protocols, secure against the accidental yank; MATURE TRACT ~4-6 weeks before removal/exchange decisions","The downstream fork, decided deliberately: (1) INTERVAL CHOLECYSTECTOMY at 6-8+ weeks for the recovered operable patient (tube stays until surgery); (2) tube removal after tract maturity + confirmed CYSTIC DUCT PATENCY (cholangiogram through the tube) in calculous disease — recurrence risk persists with stones in place; (3) acalculous disease with patent duct: remove after recovery, usually no surgery needed","Dislodged early tube (immature tract): bile peritonitis risk — image, IR replacement promptly, clinical surveillance","The frail patient's honest conversation: some tubes are permanent companions; goals-of-care alignment beats optimistic scheduling"],"pearls":["A cholecystostomy without a plan is a problem postponed — write the fork (interval surgery vs duct-patency-then-pull) into the discharge summary","Transhepatic is your friend: the liver seals what the peritoneum would announce","The acalculous ICU gallbladder improves fast after drainage — if it didn't, the sepsis source was never the gallbladder"],"refs":[{"t":"Tokyo Guidelines: Gallbladder Drainage","u":"https://www.jshbps.jp"},{"t":"SIR Percutaneous Cholecystostomy Resources","u":"https://www.sirweb.org"}]},{"id":"gs-perf-repair","name":"Bowel Perforation Repair & Damage Control","sec":"gs","present":["Sources: perforated ulcer (see Graham patch), diverticular (see Hartmann's), iatrogenic (colonoscopy — the call that interrupts clinic), foreign body, ischemia, malignancy, anastomotic dehiscence","Presentation spectrum: sudden peritonitis with free air → contained/walled-off smoldering sepsis","Colonoscopic perforations split by mechanism: mechanical tears (sigmoid, larger) vs post-polypectomy thermal (delayed, sometimes serosal-only)"],"dx":["CT with contrast: free air location points to the source (perihepatic/foregut vs pelvic/colonic), contained collection vs free contamination, extravasation","Post-colonoscopy: immediate pain/distension → image now; POST-POLYPECTOMY SYNDROME (serositis without perforation: pain + fever + leukocytosis, NO free perforation on CT) is treated with antibiotics/observation, not laparotomy — distinguishing it saves operations","Physiology assessment runs in parallel: lactate, pressors, coagulopathy — the damage-control triggers"],"tx":["Free perforation + peritonitis: resuscitate + broad-spectrum abx + operative source control","SELECTED nonoperative candidates: contained perforation, stable, localized findings (some duodenal seals, diverticular abscesses → drain, contained colonoscopic perforations with endoscopic clips already placed) — serial exams with a surgeon watching, abandon early for progression","Operative menu scales with contamination + physiology: primary repair (fresh, clean, small) → resection + anastomosis (stable, limited contamination) → resection + diversion (feculent peritonitis, shock, tenuous tissue) → damage control (staple/pack/leave open, return when alive)"],"tech":["Principles regardless of site: CONTROL spillage first (clamp/staple across the hole), thorough washout of all quadrants, assess tissue honestly (edema, perfusion, tension)","Small fresh perforations (colonoscopic tear, foreign body): debride edges to healthy tissue, transverse primary closure in 1-2 layers ± omental buttress; laparoscopic repair appropriate in stable early presentations","Segmental disease (diverticular, ischemic, malignant): resect the segment — the malignant perforation is resected ONCOLOGICALLY (nodes, margins) even in the emergency; diversion decisions per the Hartmann's logic","Anastomose vs divert honestly: pressors, feculent contamination, malnutrition, and tissue quality all vote — 'protect it with a loop' is a legitimate middle path","Damage control: contamination controlled, no reconstruction, temporary closure (NPWT), ICU, relook 24-48h — anastomoses are built in warm, resuscitated patients"],"after":["Source-control antibiotics: 4 days after adequate control (STOP-IT) — extended courses treat the surgeon, not the patient","Leak/recurrent sepsis surveillance: fevers, drain changes, ileus beyond expectation → CT; undrained collections → IR","Open abdomen pathway when used: sequential closure fights, nutrition early, fascial closure before the abdomen freezes (see ex-lap entry)","Colonoscopic perforation follow-through: the polyp pathology still matters — the perforation doesn't cancel the cancer question that started the scope","Ostomy pathways when diverted (education, reversal planning per Hartmann's/ostomy entries)"],"pearls":["Control contamination in the first five minutes — the washout and decisions come easier with the hole clamped","Post-polypectomy syndrome is the perforation that isn't: recognize it and spare the laparotomy","The anastomosis built on pressors in stool is a leak with a timestamp — divert or damage-control without shame"],"refs":[{"t":"WSES Emergency Surgery Guidelines","u":"https://www.wses.org.uk"},{"t":"ASGE Perforation Management","u":"https://www.asge.org"}]},{"id":"gs-pd-catheter","name":"Peritoneal Dialysis Catheter Placement","sec":"gs","present":["ESRD patients choosing peritoneal dialysis: home-based therapy, hemodynamic gentleness, residual renal function preservation — the catheter is the fistula-equivalent lifeline","Referral timing matters: 'urgent-start PD' can begin within days-weeks of placement with low-volume supine protocols — an alternative to the tunneled HD catheter for the crashing-onto-dialysis patient","Candidacy: intact peritoneum (extensive adhesions/prior mesh complicate), adequate support/dexterity/home situation, hernias fixed before/at placement"],"dx":["Abdominal history mapping: prior operations predict adhesions (laparoscopic placement earns its keep here — adhesiolysis + directed placement)","Examine for hernias (repair concurrently — PD pressure finds every defect) and plan the EXIT SITE awake/sitting: away from belt line, folds, ostomies; downward or lateral facing","Coordinate with nephrology/PD nursing on catheter type (coiled-tip double-cuff standard) and training timeline"],"tx":["Techniques: laparoscopic (preferred where available — direct vision, adhesiolysis, rectus sheath tunneling, omentopexy adjuncts), open (mini-laparotomy), percutaneous/fluoroscopic (IR/nephrology in favorable abdomens)","Advanced laparoscopic adjuncts reduce failure: rectus sheath tunnel (prevents tip migration), selective omentopexy/omentectomy (prevents omental wrap), adhesiolysis","Timing: ideally ≥2 weeks before planned use (tissue ingrowth into cuffs); urgent-start protocols bend this with low-volume recumbent exchanges"],"tech":["Laparoscopic: umbilical/paramedian camera, catheter introduced through a RECTUS SHEATH TUNNEL (long oblique intramuscular path) entering the peritoneum low, coiled tip directed into the TRUE PELVIS under vision (deepest point — where dialysate pools), test irrigation/drainage on table","DEEP CUFF within the rectus muscle (not peritoneal, not subcutaneous), SUPERFICIAL CUFF 2-4cm from exit site; subcutaneous tunnel gently curved, exit site small + downward/lateral, NO SUTURE AT THE EXIT (sutures breed exit-site infection)","Omentum flirting with the tip → omentopexy (tack it high) — the five extra minutes that prevent the outflow-failure reoperation","Open (Stamm-style): mini-laparotomy through rectus, purse-string at peritoneal entry, same cuff/tunnel/exit religion","Test function before leaving: instill + drain — sluggish drainage on the table is a repositioning opportunity, not a hope"],"after":["Healing phase: exit site dressings undisturbed initially (PD nursing protocols), no submersion; flushes per unit protocol until full-volume training","Break-in ideally 2+ weeks; urgent-start: low-volume supine exchanges avoid leaks","EARLY problems: pericatheter LEAK (dialysate at exit/subcutaneous — rest the catheter, lower volumes, most seal), OUTFLOW FAILURE (constipation FIRST — treat aggressively before imaging; then migration/omental wrap → X-ray for tip position, laparoscopic rescue), exit-site infection (topical protocols, cultures)","PERITONITIS is the program-defining complication: cloudy effluent + abdominal pain → effluent cell count/culture THROUGH the catheter, intraperitoneal antibiotics per ISPD — most treated with catheter in place; refractory/fungal/tunnel infections = catheter removal","Long-term partnership: hernia surveillance, exit care, catheter survival tracking with the PD program — surgical responsiveness keeps patients on home therapy"],"pearls":["The catheter lives or dies by geography: deep cuff in muscle, tip in the pelvis, exit pointing down — violate one and meet the patient again in the OR","Outflow failure is constipation until the laxatives fail — the KUB before the laxative trial is a step skipped backward","Cloudy fluid is peritonitis tonight, not at the morning clinic — the effluent goes to the lab through the catheter, and treatment starts empirically"],"refs":[{"t":"ISPD Catheter & Peritonitis Guidelines","u":"https://ispd.org"},{"t":"NKF Peritoneal Dialysis Resources","u":"https://www.kidney.org"}]},{"id":"gs-component-sep","name":"Complex Abdominal Wall: Component Separation","sec":"gs","present":["The giant/recurrent ventral hernia beyond standard repair: defects >8-10cm, loss of domain, multiply recurrent, previously infected fields, enterocutaneous fistula takedowns","Patients accumulate: obesity, prior mesh, stomas, radiated/scarred tissue — the operation is the exit from a spiral, planned once and properly","Loss of domain: viscera living outside the abdominal cavity — reduction has respiratory/compartment physiology consequences"],"dx":["CT with defect measurements + volumetrics (hernia sac : abdominal cavity ratios flag loss of domain), muscle quality, prior mesh mapping","PREHABILITATION is half the operation: BMI targets (<35-40 per practice), smoking cessation ≥4 weeks HARD STOP, HbA1c <7.5-8, nutrition repletion, physical conditioning — elective complex repair earns the delay","Adjuncts for loss of domain: preoperative progressive pneumoperitoneum and/or botulinum toxin to the lateral wall muscles (chemical relaxation expands the container over 2-4 weeks)"],"tx":["Goal: restore the midline (functional abdominal wall) + WIDE mesh reinforcement in a well-vascularized plane","Anterior component separation (Ramirez, ideally perforator-sparing) vs POSTERIOR component separation with TRANSVERSUS ABDOMINIS RELEASE (TAR) — TAR dominates modern complex practice: massive sublay space, preserved perforators, lower wound morbidity","Contaminated fields: staged vs single-stage with appropriate mesh choice (macroporous synthetic increasingly used in clean-contaminated; biologic/absorbable synthetics per judgment) — decision-making is the expertise"],"tech":["Posterior approach (TAR): retrorectus dissection (Rives-Stoppa start) to the linea semilunaris; incise the POSTERIOR LAMELLA of the internal oblique just medial to the neurovascular bundles, divide the TRANSVERSUS ABDOMINIS muscle, enter the pre-transversalis/preperitoneal plane — dissection extends to psoas laterally, ribs superiorly, space of Retzius inferiorly = a continuous circumferential pocket","Close the POSTERIOR layer (visceral coverage — buttonholes repaired meticulously), giant mesh (30×30cm class) diamond in the sublay pocket with minimal/no fixation (pressure holds it), close the ANTERIOR fascia at the midline = linea alba restored, drains in the pocket","Anterior (Ramirez): external oblique released lateral to semilunaris via lipocutaneous flaps (perforator-sparing tunnels reduce the skin necrosis toll) — rectus advances 5-10cm per side","PROTECT the neurovascular bundles (they define the TAR incision line — cut medial to them, never through); protect semilunaris integrity (full-thickness violation = lateral hernia, the unfixable complication)","Physiology watch at closure: peak airway pressure rise >6-9 with fascial approximation = abdominal hypertension warning — the botox/pneumoperitoneum patients close easier; bail-out = bridged mesh rather than compartment syndrome"],"after":["ICU-level observation for big reductions: airway pressures, urine output, bladder pressures if concern — abdominal compartment syndrome after heroic closure is the feared spiral","Binder, drains until output criteria, aggressive pulmonary toilet + early ambulation (these patients splint), VTE prophylaxis diligently (high-risk population)","Wound morbidity is the currency: seroma (expectant unless infected), skin edge issues (anterior releases), SSI protocols — most managed without touching the mesh; deep mesh infection with macroporous sublay mesh: drainage + abx salvages many","Activity: core restrictions 6+ weeks, graded return; long-term surveillance for recurrence (single digits in expert TAR series with optimization — the number that justifies all the preparation)","The patient bought this outcome with prehab — reinforce maintenance (weight, glucose, no smoking) as repair protection"],"pearls":["TAR's whole geometry hangs on one line: incise medial to the neurovascular bundles, and the plane opens; wander lateral and the semilunaris — and the repair — is lost","The best component separation is the one delayed until the patient earned it: the smoking, sugar, and BMI conversation IS surgical technique","Rising vent pressures at fascial closure are the abdomen voting no — bridge gracefully rather than close triumphantly into compartment syndrome"],"refs":[{"t":"ACHQC / AHS Complex Hernia Resources","u":"https://achqc.org"},{"t":"SAGES Abdominal Wall Reconstruction","u":"https://www.sages.org"}]},{"id":"im-sepsis","name":"Sepsis & Bacteremia (Inpatient Management)","sec":"im","present":["Ward course after ED resuscitation: the admitting job is source confirmation, antibiotic stewardship, and catching the deteriorator","Ward-onset sepsis hides in soft signs: new confusion, falling urine output, creeping tachycardia, 'not looking right' to the nurse — respond to the concern, not just the score","Bacteremia follow-through: gram-positive cocci in clusters (staph until proven otherwise) changes the day's plan"],"dx":["Daily reassessment: lactate clearance, culture results, source-control adequacy (is the abscess drained? line out? urine flowing?)","S. aureus bacteremia is a MANDATORY bundle: repeat cultures q48h until clear, echocardiography (TTE, TEE if prosthetic valve/persistent), ID consult (mortality benefit shown), hunt metastatic foci — duration counts from FIRST NEGATIVE culture","Candidemia: ophthalmology exam, line removal, repeat cultures; enterococcal/strep bacteremia: endocarditis thinking (see IE entry)","Persistent fever/bacteremia on 'appropriate' abx = undrained source or endovascular focus, not a dosing problem"],"tx":["De-escalate deliberately: narrow to culture data at 48-72h — stewardship is a daily active decision, not a passive default","Duration by syndrome: uncomplicated gram-negative bacteremia 7 days (per trials), uncomplicated S. aureus 14 days from clearance (complicated = 4-6 weeks), source-controlled intra-abdominal 4 days (STOP-IT)","IV-to-PO switch for susceptible organisms with good bioavailability (FQs, TMP-SMX, linezolid) once stable + eating — the PICC avoided is a complication avoided","Fluid stewardship post-resuscitation: de-resuscitate the recovered (the POD3 sepsis patient 8L positive needs diuresis, not maintenance fluids)","Escalation triggers written into the plan: MAP/urine/lactate/mentation thresholds for ICU re-evaluation"],"pearls":["The most dangerous day of ward sepsis is day 2 — resuscitated enough to leave the ICU radar, sick enough to crash; build your own early-warning habits","Staph aureus in even one bottle is never a contaminant until proven — the 'probable contaminant' that seeded a valve is a classic morbidity conference","Every sepsis discharge needs a source narrative: what grew, what treated it, what's the stop date — the next admission depends on that paragraph"],"refs":[{"t":"Surviving Sepsis Campaign (2021)","u":"https://www.sccm.org"},{"t":"IDSA Bacteremia Resources","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"}]},{"id":"im-adhf","name":"Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The admission is decongestion + trajectory change: most patients leave the hospital still congested, and residual congestion predicts readmission","Characterize: EF (HFrEF vs HFpEF), etiology if new (ischemia, valves, tachyarrhythmia, toxins, infiltrative), precipitant of THIS episode","Daily assessment currency: weights (same scale, same clothes), strict I/Os, JVP, orthopnea trend"],"dx":["New diagnosis workup: echo, ischemic evaluation as appropriate, TSH, iron studies (deficiency treatment improves outcomes independent of anemia), consider amyloid in the LVH + low-voltage + neuropathy phenotype","Track decongestion: daily BMP (the creatinine bump during effective diuresis is usually permissive), natriuretic peptide trend supports (falling ≥30% pre-discharge is favorable)","Bedside POCUS (B-lines, IVC) beats the physical exam for residual congestion; spot urine sodium 2h post-diuretic (<50-70 mEq/L = inadequate response) modernizes titration"],"tx":["Diuresis: IV loop at ≥2× home dose (DOSE), reassess within hours — inadequate output → DOUBLE the dose before shortening the interval; sequential nephron blockade (add thiazide/metolazone) for loop resistance; target sustained net negative until euvolemic (not a fixed number of days)","GDMT is the inpatient job, not the outpatient homework: initiate/uptitrate the four pillars (ARNI/ACEi, beta-blocker once euvolemic, MRA, SGLT2i — SGLT2i safely started in-house and reduces readmission) — in-hospital initiation triples long-term adherence (STRONG-HF logic: intensify early, follow early)","Hold/reduce beta-blockers only for shock/bradycardia — continuing through decompensation is associated with better outcomes","Address the precipitant list explicitly in the chart (ischemia, AF, infection, NSAIDs, dietary/med lapse)","Discharge criteria honestly applied: euvolemic (or near), stable oral regimen ≥24h, GDMT plan, follow-up within 7 days (the readmission-prevention appointment), daily-weight action plan taught"],"pearls":["A creatinine rise with brisk diuresis is hemoconcentration and usually good news; a creatinine rise with poor output is cardiorenal trouble — the urine output tells you which movie you're in","Discharging on the home diuretic dose that failed is scheduling the readmission — most patients need more than they came in on","The four pillars started at discharge are worth more than any single inpatient intervention — 'defer to outpatient' is where GDMT goes to die"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guideline (2022)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Heart Failure","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/heartfailure.html"}]},{"id":"im-pneumonia","name":"Pneumonia: CAP, HAP & Aspiration (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted CAP: the inpatient arc is confirmation, appropriate empiric coverage, de-escalation, and the failure-to-improve algorithm","HAP (≥48h after admission) / VAP: different flora (resistant gram-negatives, MRSA) and different empiric rules","Aspiration events: distinguish chemical pneumonitis (immediate, often clears in 24-48h without abx) from aspiration pneumonia (develops/persists — treat)"],"dx":["Severity re-check on arrival to the floor (the ED CURB-65 can age badly overnight); IDSA/ATS severe criteria drive ICU","Microbiology worth sending in admitted patients: blood cultures + sputum for severe/risk-factor patients, urinary antigens (pneumococcus, Legionella) in severe disease, respiratory viral panel in season (influenza changes therapy)","MRSA/Pseudomonas risk = prior isolation or recent hospitalization + IV abx: culture and cover, then DE-escalate with negative cultures at 48h (the nasal MRSA PCR's negative predictive value ~95%+ is a vancomycin-stopping tool)","Failure to improve by 72h: wrong bug (TB? PJP? fungal?), wrong diagnosis (PE, cancer, organizing pneumonia), or complication (empyema — get the ultrasound; abscess) — re-image, tap effusions"],"tx":["Non-severe inpatient: beta-lactam (ceftriaxone) + macrolide, or respiratory FQ; severe: beta-lactam + macrolide preferred; add coverage per risk as above","Steroids for severe CAP with marked inflammation (CAPE COD: hydrocortisone reduced mortality) — know your ICU's adoption","Duration 5 days for uncomplicated CAP responding by day 3 (afebrile 48h, stable vitals) — longer courses treat anxiety; HAP/VAP 7 days","IV-to-PO switch when improving + tolerating PO (same-day discharge after switch is fine); pneumococcal/influenza/COVID vaccination status fixed before discharge","Aspiration pneumonia: standard CAP coverage suffices for most (anaerobic add-ons only for empyema/abscess/severe periodontal disease per current IDSA thinking); address the mechanism — swallow evaluation, aspiration precautions, med review (sedatives)"],"pearls":["The parapneumonic effusion you didn't sample is the empyema you'll drain next week — pH/glucose/culture the meaningful ones early","A negative MRSA nasal swab is an antibiotic timeout notice — stopping vancomycin is a decision, make it","'Aspiration' witnessed at dinner with an infiltrate at midnight is chemistry, not bacteria — reassess at 48h before committing to a course"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA/ATS CAP Guidelines (2019) & HAP/VAP (2016)","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pneumonia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/pneumonia.html"}]},{"id":"im-copd","name":"COPD Exacerbation (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted for: respiratory acidosis/NIV need, failed outpatient therapy, comorbidity burden, inadequate home support","The admission has two jobs: treat this exacerbation, and bend the trajectory (exacerbations beget exacerbations — the post-discharge plan is the medicine)","Frequent exacerbators (≥2/yr) are a phenotype needing escalated maintenance, not just repeated rescue"],"dx":["Gas the acidotic/somnolent (VBG acceptable for trends); target SpO2 88-92% with documented orders (the well-meaning 100% mask is a CO2 narcosis machine)","Hunt the trigger and the mimics: CXR (pneumonia, PTX, edema), consider PE in the triggerless deteriorator, BNP/echo for the cardiac overlap patient","Eosinophil count predicts steroid responsiveness (≥300 favors benefit; <100 argues steroids add little — emerging practice)","Spirometry is for the OUTPATIENT — but confirm the diagnosis exists somewhere in the record (many 'COPD' patients never had one)"],"tx":["Bronchodilators (albuterol+ipratropium), prednisone 40mg ×5 days (REDUCE — five days, not fourteen), antibiotics for increased purulence or ventilatory support (azithromycin/doxycycline/amox-clav ×5d)","NIV early for pH <7.35 with hypercapnia: the mortality-reducing intervention — reassess at 1-2h; failure to improve pH/RR = ICU/intubation conversation before the crash","The discharge bundle IS the treatment: inhaler TECHNIQUE observed (not asked about), maintenance escalation per GOLD group (LABA/LAMA backbone; add ICS for frequent exacerbators with eosinophilia), smoking cessation pharmacotherapy started in-house, vaccination update, pulmonary rehab referral (mortality/readmission benefit — chronically under-referred)","Home O2 evaluation if SpO2 ≤88% at rest on discharge (mortality benefit in chronic hypoxemia); roflumilast/azithromycin-chronic for the frequent-exacerbator phenotype per outpatient follow-up","Goals-of-care conversation for the advanced patient (recurrent admissions, home O2, weight loss): the honest trajectory talk belongs on the ward, not the ICU at 3am"],"pearls":["The inhaler demonstration finds the problem the medication list hides — watch them use it before renewing anything","pH, not pCO2, picks who needs NIV — the chronic retainer at their baseline needs a comparison gas, not a ventilator","Pulmonary rehab referral is the most under-prescribed mortality-benefit intervention on the medicine service"],"refs":[{"t":"GOLD Report (updated annually)","u":"https://goldcopd.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: COPD","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/copd.html"}]},{"id":"im-asthma","name":"Asthma Exacerbation (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted asthma = severe disease or severe circumstances: the floor course is short, the risk assessment is the point","Risk-stratify every admission: prior intubation/ICU, ≥2 hospitalizations or ≥3 ED visits/year, current/recent oral steroids, SABA overuse (>1 canister/month = red flag), poor follow-up","Ward deterioration signs: rising RR with quiet chest, inability to speak sentences, normalizing pCO2 in a tachypneic patient — ICU now"],"dx":["Serial objective assessment: peak flows against personal best where feasible; continuous pulse ox; gas the tiring patient","Review the phenotype: allergic triggers, aspirin-exacerbated disease, eosinophil counts (biologic-eligibility screening for the severe), vocal cord dysfunction mimics in the recurrent 'refractory' admitter","Confirm the diagnosis exists: normal spirometry ever documented? The 'asthma' that never varies may be something else (obliterative bronchiolitis, tracheal pathology)"],"tx":["Continue SABA q4-6h + taper frequency by response; systemic steroids: prednisone 40-60mg daily ×5-7 days (no taper needed for short courses)","THE INPATIENT LEVERAGE IS THE CONTROLLER PLAN: start/escalate ICS-containing therapy before discharge — SMART/MART strategy (ICS-formoterol as both maintenance and reliever) reduces exacerbations vs SABA-only rescue (GINA now recommends against SABA-alone at every step)","Observed inhaler technique + spacer provision + written asthma action plan — the trio that prevents the next admission","Trigger remediation honestly: smoking/vaping cessation, occupational exposures, NSAID avoidance in AERD, allergen strategies","Discharge threshold: SABA no more than q4h, walking SpO2 stable, technique verified, controller in hand (not 'prescribed' — IN HAND), follow-up within 1-4 weeks; severe/recurrent → pulmonology + biologic evaluation (anti-IgE/IL-5/IL-4R for the eligible)"],"pearls":["The SABA-only asthmatic admitted twice this year is a guideline violation walking — ICS-formoterol changes their trajectory","Discharge steroids finish in 5-7 days; the ICS you started lasts — one is the rescue, the other is the treatment","A quiet chest in a distressed asthmatic is pre-arrest, not improvement — the floor-to-ICU call is made on work of breathing, not the number"],"refs":[{"t":"GINA Report (updated annually)","u":"https://ginasthma.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Asthma","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/asthma.html"}]},{"id":"im-nstemi","name":"NSTEMI & Unstable Angina (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The medicine service co-manages: risk stratification, medical optimization, timing of angiography, and the secondary-prevention discharge","Spectrum: unstable angina (ischemic symptoms, negative troponins) → NSTEMI (troponin+); both = ACS pathway","Type 2 MI (demand ischemia from sepsis/tachyarrhythmia/anemia) is NOT ACS — treat the driver, don't reflexively cath (the most common troponin consult)"],"dx":["Serial hs-troponins + ECGs; GRACE score risk-stratifies and times invasive strategy (very high risk — instability, refractory angina, dynamic ECG → urgent cath; high risk → early invasive <24h)","Echo for LV function (drives GDMT and prognosis)","Distinguish the troponin taxonomies deliberately in the chart: type 1 (plaque event), type 2 (supply-demand), chronic myocardial injury (flat elevated pattern — CKD, HF) — the label directs everything downstream"],"tx":["Antithrombotic backbone: aspirin 81 + P2Y12 (ticagrelor/clopidogrel per pathway and bleeding risk) + anticoagulation (heparin/enoxaparin) until revascularized/decided","Anti-ischemic: beta-blocker within 24h (absent shock/decompensation), nitrates for symptoms, high-intensity statin day 1","Timing per risk (above); radial-access PCI era logistics — hold nothing for breakfast, communicate with cardiology about anticoagulant timing","SECONDARY PREVENTION IS THE DISCHARGE: DAPT duration plan documented (typically 12 months, modified by bleeding), high-intensity statin, ACEi/ARB (EF↓, DM, HTN, CKD), beta-blocker, SGLT2i/GLP-1 for the diabetic with ASCVD, cardiac rehab REFERRAL (mortality benefit, chronically underused), smoking cessation pharmacotherapy","The bleeding-risk conversation (HAS-BLED thinking, PPI for high-GI-risk on DAPT) is medicine's contribution to the cath lab's plan"],"pearls":["The septic 80-year-old with troponin 0.4 needs sepsis treatment, not a heparin drip — type 2 MI mislabeled as ACS causes real bleeding harm","Cardiac rehab referral at discharge has a mortality benefit rivaling the statin — it's a checkbox that saves lives and nobody clicks it","DAPT without a documented duration and PPI plan is a GI bleed with a schedule"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA NSTE-ACS Guidelines","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"Fourth Universal Definition of MI","u":"https://www.escardio.org"}]},{"id":"im-afib","name":"Atrial Fibrillation & Flutter (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Inpatient AF flavors: chronic AF admitted for something else (continue/adjust therapy), new AF discovered on telemetry, post-operative AF, RVR complicating acute illness","Secondary AF (sepsis, PE, hyperthyroid, alcohol, post-op) is a symptom — rate control supports while the driver is treated","The admission is the anticoagulation decision point: more strokes are prevented by fixing this than by anything else in the AF chart"],"dx":["ECG confirmation (flutter's sawtooth at 2:1 hides at 150); telemetry burden characterization","New AF workup: TSH, echo (structure, LA size, valves), electrolytes, sleep apnea screening (the unasked question in half of AF), alcohol history honestly taken","CHA2DS2-VASc on every chart — calculated, documented, and acted on (score ≥2 men/≥3 women → anticoagulate; the 'aspirin instead' era is over)"],"tx":["Rate control targets: lenient <110 resting (RACE II) for the asymptomatic — beta-blocker or diltiazem (avoid non-DHP CCBs in HFrEF); digoxin as adjunct in the hypotensive/HF patient","Rhythm control's expanding role: EARLY rhythm control improves outcomes in newly diagnosed AF (EAST-AFNET 4) — cardiology referral for cardioversion/antiarrhythmics/ablation is a real inpatient decision, not a punt; ablation first-line conversation for symptomatic paroxysmal AF","Anticoagulation: DOACs first-line (apixaban/rivaroxaban; warfarin for mechanical valves/moderate-severe mitral stenosis); renal dosing checked TWICE (the apixaban dose-reduction criteria are botched constantly); post-op AF after non-cardiac surgery still counts for stroke risk — don't dismiss it","Cardioversion timing rules: <48h onset or anticoagulated ≥3 weeks or TEE-cleared; anticoagulate ≥4 weeks after regardless","Alcohol reduction (abstinence reduced AF burden in trials), weight/OSA management — the lifestyle prescriptions with rhythm outcomes"],"pearls":["The AF found 'incidentally' on post-op telemetry has the same stroke risk as clinic AF — the discharge without an anticoagulation decision is the stroke consult next year","Rate-control the septic patient by treating the sepsis — the diltiazem that dropped their pressure treated the monitor, not the patient","Apixaban dose reduction needs 2 of 3 (age ≥80, weight ≤60kg, Cr ≥1.5) — the under-dosed DOAC is a placebo with a copay"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA/HRS AF Guideline (2023)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Atrial Fibrillation","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/atrialfibrillation.html"}]},{"id":"im-htn","name":"Hypertension: Inpatient Management & Urgency","sec":"im","present":["The commonest inpatient BP story: chronic hypertension + pain/anxiety/held meds/full bladder reading high on the ward — NOT an emergency","True hypertensive emergency (organ damage) belongs in the EM/ICU pathway; everything else is about the OUTPATIENT regimen","The teachable scandal: PRN IV antihypertensives (hydralazine/labetalol 'for SBP>160') on stable ward patients cause overshoot, hypoperfusion, and harm — evidence-backed deprescribing target"],"dx":["Recheck properly (rested, correct cuff, arm supported) before treating any number; screen for the reversible ward drivers (pain, urinary retention, withdrawal, missed home meds)","Asymptomatic elevation: NO reflexive workup — assess target organs via history + existing labs","Resistant hypertension (≥3 drugs including a diuretic): adherence first (the honest conversation), then secondary causes: primary aldosteronism (screen liberally — aldosterone:renin; it's in 10-20% of resistant HTN), OSA, renal artery stenosis, CKD, drugs (NSAIDs, decongestants, stimulants)"],"tx":["Ward elevation without organ damage: resume/adjust the ORAL regimen, treat the driver, and stop writing PRN IV hydralazine — intensify the home regimen instead of spot-treating the monitor","Discharge intensification done right: evidence supports tightening chronic therapy at discharge for persistent elevation, with follow-up — not for transient illness-related readings (the SPRINT patient and the delirious dehydrated patient are different people)","Regimen-building outpatient logic applies inpatient: ACEi/ARB + CCB + thiazide-like (chlorthalidone/indapamide) backbone; spironolactone is the 4th drug for resistant disease (PATHWAY-2)","Set the target honestly: <130/80 for most (SPRINT-informed) with individualized frailty adjustments","Every antihypertensive change lands in the discharge summary with a recheck date — titration without follow-up is drift"],"pearls":["Treat the patient's chronic risk, not tonight's telemetry — the PRN hydralazine order is a habit, not a treatment","An aldosterone:renin ratio on the resistant-HTN patient finds the most under-diagnosed curable hypertension in medicine","The BP med stopped during admission for a soft reason and never restarted is a top-five medication-reconciliation error — reconcile at discharge like it matters, because it does"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA Hypertension Guideline","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: High Blood Pressure","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/highbloodpressure.html"}]},{"id":"im-dm","name":"Type 2 Diabetes & Inpatient Glycemic Management","sec":"im","present":["Inpatient hyperglycemia worsens outcomes; inpatient HYPOglycemia kills faster — the management philosophy is 'safely moderate'","The admission is a diabetes audit: regimen adequacy, complication screening status, and the discharge upgrade opportunity (SGLT2i/GLP-1 eligibility)","Steroid-induced hyperglycemia (predictable afternoon/evening spikes) and NPO/tube-feed logistics are the daily puzzles"],"dx":["A1c on admission if none in 3 months — it separates stress hyperglycemia from undiagnosed/undertreated diabetes and sets the discharge plan","Glucose targets inpatient: 140-180 for most (NICE-SUGAR taught the tight-control lesson); POC checks matched to nutrition pattern","Screen the chart for the eligibility flags: ASCVD/HF/CKD → the SGLT2i/GLP-1 conversation is now inpatient business"],"tx":["BASAL-BOLUS insulin (weight-based ~0.3-0.5 U/kg/day split basal/prandial) beats sliding-scale-only — the RABBIT-2 lesson still ignored on wards everywhere; correction scale SUPPLEMENTS, never substitutes","Hold the pitfalls: metformin peri-contrast/AKI, SGLT2i held for acute illness/surgery (euglycemic DKA — restart at discharge!), sulfonylureas in the NPO/renal patient (hypoglycemia machines)","Steroid coverage: NPH timed with the morning prednisone maps the spike elegantly; tube feeds: basal + q4-6h regular coverage matched to feed schedule (and a dextrose contingency order for feed interruptions)","HYPOGLYCEMIA PROTOCOLS: every insulin order set pairs with one; recurrent lows → cut doses 20%, hunt the cause (renal decline, improving illness, missed meals)","DISCHARGE IS THE INTERVENTION: A1c-driven regimen upgrade, SGLT2i for the HF/CKD patient (outcome drugs, not sugar drugs), GLP-1 for the ASCVD/obesity phenotype, teach-back on insulin/hypoglycemia, supplies IN HAND, follow-up within 1-4 weeks"],"pearls":["Sliding scale alone is documentation of hyperglycemia, not treatment of it — basal-bolus from day one for the insulin-requiring","The SGLT2i held on admission and absent from the discharge summary is a heart-failure readmission with a paper trail","An admission A1c of 10 is not 'stress hyperglycemia' — it's the outpatient plan failing in front of you; upgrade it before the door"],"refs":[{"t":"ADA Standards of Care (updated annually)","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Diabetes","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/diabetes.html"}]},{"id":"im-dka","name":"DKA & HHS (Inpatient Course)","sec":"im","present":["The medicine floor inherits DKA at the transition: drip running or recently off — the dangerous hours are the HANDOFFS","Gap closed ≠ done: the subcutaneous transition, the precipitant workup, and the never-again discharge plan are the ward's deliverables","Recurrent DKA is a systems disease: insulin access, cost, regimen complexity, psychiatric comorbidity — treat the system or readmit the patient"],"dx":["Resolution criteria before any transition: glucose <200-250 PLUS two of: bicarb ≥15, pH >7.3, anion gap closed — the GAP is the boss, not the glucose","Precipitant checklist completed in writing: infection screen, ischemia (ECG ± troponin), adherence/access history, SGLT2i use (euglycemic presentations), new-onset T1DM workup (autoantibodies, C-peptide) when the story fits","q2-4h BMPs until stable; potassium/phosphate trends through the transition"],"tx":["THE OVERLAP RULE: basal insulin subcutaneously 1-2 HOURS BEFORE stopping the drip (no overlap = rebound ketoacidosis by morning — the classic preventable bounce-back); if home basal dose known and adequate, some protocols give it early during the drip","Transition dosing: home regimen if it was working, else weight-based 0.5-0.6 U/kg/day total split basal/prandial","Restart nutrition and match prandial insulin; potassium repletion continues (total-body deficit outlasts the drip by days)","DISCHARGE ENGINEERING: insulin affordability/access solved before the door (the $300 copay is the readmission), glucometer + ketone strips + sick-day rules taught (never stop basal insulin when ill — the single most valuable sentence in diabetes education), endocrinology/diabetes-educator follow-up within days-weeks","SGLT2i-associated DKA: drug stopped, counseled, documented allergy-style in the chart per judgment"],"pearls":["Every 3am DKA rebound has the same op note: the drip stopped and the basal insulin was 'due at 9' — overlap is the whole transition","The gap that won't close on a running drip means the drip isn't actually running (line, pump, bag) or something is still driving ketosis — go look at the pump yourself","Recurrent DKA admissions are asking a social question in a chemical language — the answer is in access and psychology, not the correction scale"],"refs":[{"t":"ADA Hyperglycemic Crises Consensus","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Diabetic Ketoacidosis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000320.htm"}]},{"id":"im-aki","name":"Acute Kidney Injury","sec":"im","present":["KDIGO stages by creatinine rise (≥0.3 in 48h or ≥1.5× baseline) or urine output — the ward's most common consult-generator","The classic buckets still work: prerenal (volume, cardiorenal, hepatorenal), intrinsic (ATN from ischemia/sepsis/toxins, AIN, GN, rhabdo), postrenal (obstruction — the one you never miss because you always image)","The chart tells the story: contrast timing, new drugs (the AIN lineup: PPIs, NSAIDs, antibiotics), hypotensive episodes, procedures"],"dx":["The basic four: urinalysis with MICROSCOPY (muddy brown casts = ATN, WBC casts = AIN, RBC casts/dysmorphic = GN — escalate fast), renal ultrasound (hydronephrosis), bladder scan (the 10-second postrenal answer), volume exam honestly done","FENa/FEUrea support but don't rule (diuretics, sepsis confound); urine eosinophils are unreliable for AIN — the drug timeline is the better test","GN suspicion (RBC casts, proteinuria, systemic features): complement, ANCA, anti-GBM, hepatitis serologies + NEPHROLOGY TODAY (crescentic GN is dialysis-by-Friday disease)","Rhabdo: CK, urine myoglobin; myeloma in the unexplained AKI over 50: SPEP/free light chains"],"tx":["Treat the bucket: volume for prerenal (balanced crystalloids), relieve obstruction (Foley/nephrostomy — watch post-obstructive diuresis), stop the offender (AIN: discontinue + steroids per nephrology in the non-resolving)","NEPHROTOXIN HYGIENE written as orders: hold NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB (during AKI — restart before discharge when appropriate, see pearl), adjust vancomycin/aminoglycosides with levels, review EVERYTHING renally-dosed (the DOAC, the gabapentin, the metformin)","Contrast decisions: modern data show low AKI risk from IV contrast in most — don't withhold the diagnostic scan the patient needs for a marginal creatinine","Dialysis indications unchanged (AEIOU: acidosis, electrolytes [K+], intoxications, overload, uremia [pericarditis, encephalopathy]) — involve nephrology BEFORE the emergency","Track: daily weights/I/Os, urine output trend (the earliest signal both directions), potassium/acid-base"],"pearls":["The bladder scan is the highest-yield 10 seconds in the AKI workup — post-renal AKI missed for want of a Foley is indefensible","The ACEi held for AKI and never restarted costs more life-years than the AKI did — the discharge summary must say 'restart lisinopril at follow-up' in words","Muddy brown casts don't need a biopsy; RBC casts need a nephrologist before lunch"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO AKI Guideline","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Kidney Failure","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/kidneyfailure.html"}]},{"id":"im-ckd","name":"Chronic Kidney Disease & Complications","sec":"im","present":["CKD staged by eGFR (G1-5) AND albuminuria (A1-3) — the heat-map that predicts progression and drives therapy intensity","The inpatient CKD patient needs: renal dosing vigilance, AKI-on-CKD prevention, complication management (K+, acidosis, anemia, bone-mineral), and preparation trajectory (access, transplant referral) protected","Medicine's quiet triumph: the drugs that slow CKD now genuinely work — prescribing them is the job"],"dx":["Confirm chronicity (prior creatinines, small echogenic kidneys, anemia/bone markers) vs AKI-on-CKD (treat the acute reversibly)","Etiology matters when atypical: rapid progression, active sediment, heavy proteinuria without diabetes → nephrology workup ± biopsy","Complication surveillance panel: potassium, bicarbonate, hemoglobin + iron studies, calcium/phosphate/PTH/vitamin D, uACR trend"],"tx":["THE DISEASE-MODIFYING STACK — verify each on every CKD chart: ACEi/ARB (albuminuric disease, titrated to max tolerated — a creatinine bump ≤30% is expected, not a stop signal), SGLT2i (DAPA-CKD/EMPA-KIDNEY: slows progression WITH or WITHOUT diabetes — the biggest nephrology advance in 20 years), finerenone (diabetic CKD with albuminuria), GLP-1 (diabetic kidney/CV benefit), statin per KDIGO, BP <120-130 systolic per tolerance","Complication management: hyperkalemia (diet, diuretics, K-binders — patiromer/SZC let you KEEP the ACEi rather than sacrifice it), metabolic acidosis (bicarb when <22 — slows progression), anemia (iron FIRST — IV iron liberally; ESA for Hgb <10 with targets ~10-11, not normal), mineral-bone (phosphate management, PTH trends per stage)","PROTECT THE FUTURE: no PICC lines/subclavian sticks in advanced CKD (vein preservation for fistulas — write it on the chart), nephrology referral by eGFR <30 (late referral worsens survival), access planning and transplant evaluation EARLY (preemptive transplant is the best dialysis)","Renal dosing sweep on admission and discharge (the gabapentin, the DOAC, the antibiotics)","Avoid: NSAIDs (say it at every discharge), gadolinium caution in advanced stages, bowel-prep phosphate"],"pearls":["Stopping the ACEi for a 25% creatinine rise trades a number for nephrons — the bump is the drug working","An SGLT2i missing from a proteinuric CKD chart in this era is an error of omission with a survival cost","The forearm veins of a stage 4 CKD patient are surgical territory — every 'easy' PICC is a future fistula that won't mature"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO CKD Guideline (2024)","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"NKF Resources","u":"https://www.kidney.org"}]},{"id":"im-hyponatremia","name":"Hyponatremia (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The most common inpatient electrolyte disorder and the most commonly mishandled — the ward version is usually chronic, and chronic demands slow","Symptoms scale with rate more than number: chronic 118 walking and talking vs acute 125 seizing","Ward-acquired hypoNa: hypotonic fluids + ADH-stimulating states (pain, nausea, post-op) — largely iatrogenic and preventable"],"dx":["The algorithm in order, every time: serum osmolality (true hypotonic?), urine osmolality (>100 = ADH active), urine sodium (>30 with euvolemia = SIADH; <20 = volume/effective-volume depletion), volume exam","SIADH is a diagnosis of exclusion requiring: euvolemia, normal thyroid/adrenal (check TSH + AM cortisol — adrenal insufficiency mimics it and steroids fix it), no diuretics; then hunt the cause (drugs — SSRIs are everywhere; small cell lung cancer; CNS/pulmonary disease)","Special phenotypes recognized on sight: thiazide-induced (elderly, weeks after starting), beer potomania/tea-and-toast (low solute — urine osm LOW), psychogenic polydipsia","Symptom severity assessment drives urgency, not the absolute number"],"tx":["Severe symptoms (seizure, coma) any chronicity: 3% saline 100-150mL boluses to raise Na ~4-6 quickly — the emergency is the brain, then slow down","CORRECTION LIMITS: ≤8 mEq/L per 24h (≤6 in high-risk for osmotic demyelination: alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalemia, cirrhosis, very low starting Na) — q4-6h sodium checks while actively correcting","OVERCORRECTION RESCUE (the skill that prevents tragedy): brisk water diuresis after fixing the cause (fluids in the volume-deplete, stopping the thiazide) drives overshoot → D5W ± desmopressin 2mcg to clamp — or run proactive DDAVP-clamp protocols in high-risk correction","By cause: SIADH → fluid restriction, salt tabs ± loop, urea; refractory → tolvaptan with care (inpatient initiation, no restriction with it); volume depletion → isotonic fluids EXPECTING the autocorrection; hypervolemic (HF/cirrhosis) → restriction + treat the disease","The prevention order: no hypotonic maintenance fluids in ADH-rich states"],"pearls":["The dangerous hyponatremia patient is the one CORRECTING briskly on their own — the thiazide stopped and the kidneys opened the floodgates; check at 4 hours, not tomorrow","Urine osmolality is the poor man's ADH level — memorize what high and low mean and the algorithm runs itself","Cortisol before the SIADH label: adrenal insufficiency called SIADH is a missed diagnosis with a lethal ceiling"],"refs":[{"t":"European Hyponatremia Guidelines","u":"https://academic.oup.com"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hyponatremia","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000394.htm"}]},{"id":"im-hyperk","name":"Hyperkalemia & Electrolyte Disorders (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Ward hyperkalemia arrives with its causes attached: CKD/AKI + RAAS inhibitors + K-sparing diuretics + TMP-SMX + tissue breakdown — the chart review IS the workup","Chronic mild hyperkalemia in CKD is managed, not panicked over; acute rises with ECG changes follow the emergency pathway (see EM entry)","The parallel ward problems: hypokalemia (diuretics, GI losses — remember MAGNESIUM), hypomagnesemia, phosphate disorders (refeeding!)"],"dx":["Repeat the surprising value while treating the concerning one; ECG for K ≥6 or rapid rise","Cause taxonomy: decreased excretion (CKD/AKI, hypoaldosteronism — type 4 RTA in the diabetic with mild CKD and disproportionate K+, drugs), shift (acidosis, insulin deficiency, beta-blockade), load (hemolysis, rhabdo, tumor lysis)","Hypokalemia refractory to repletion = check and fix magnesium first (K+ pours out the kidney without it)","Refeeding risk screen (malnourished, alcohol use, prolonged NPO): phosphate/K/Mg before and during nutrition — the phosphate crash on day 2 of feeding is predictable and preventable"],"tx":["Acute algorithm per EM entry (calcium → shift → remove); the WARD additions: modern K-binders (patiromer, sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) work in hours-days and — critically — ENABLE KEEPING the ACEi/ARB/MRA that the patient's heart and kidneys need (the old reflex of sacrificing RAAS inhibition for mild hyperkalemia costs outcomes)","Sodium polystyrene (kayexalate): weak evidence, GI necrosis risk — largely superseded where newer binders are available","Chronic strategy for the cardiorenal patient: dietary counseling (real foods listed, not a photocopy), loop diuretic adequacy, binder maintenance, bicarbonate for acidosis — built WITH nephrology","Hypokalemia repletion: PO preferred (40-80 mEq/day divided), IV max 10-20/h with monitoring; replete Mg alongside; hunt the diuretic/GI driver","Refeeding protocol: start low (10-20 kcal/kg), replete electrolytes prophylactically, thiamine before calories, daily phos/K/Mg ×3-5 days"],"pearls":["The potassium of 5.4 that gets the lisinopril stopped forever is a bad trade — bind the potassium, keep the outcome drug","Hypokalemia that won't fix is hypomagnesemia wearing a disguise — Mg first, then the K+ sticks","Tumor lysis doesn't wait for oncology rounds: the new leukemia with rising K/phos/uric acid needs fluids, rasburicase, and a monitored bed tonight"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO Potassium Management Resources","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"ASPEN Refeeding Syndrome Consensus","u":"https://www.nutritioncare.org"}]},{"id":"im-uti","name":"UTI & Pyelonephritis (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted pyelonephritis/complicated UTI: sepsis criteria, obstruction risk, vomiting, pregnancy, failed outpatient therapy","The ward's twin discipline: treat true infection well, and DON'T treat asymptomatic bacteriuria (the antibiotic stewardship hill worth dying on — exceptions: pregnancy, pre-urologic procedures)","CAUTI thinking: the catheterized patient's positive urine needs symptoms referable beyond the urine (fever without alternative, flank pain, new systemic signs) — and the catheter needs a daily necessity review"],"dx":["Urine culture BEFORE antibiotics on all admitted UTIs; blood cultures for pyelonephritis/sepsis","IMAGE (CT) for: septic presentation, known/suspected stones, obstruction risk, male complicated infections, or failure to defervesce by 48-72h (perinephric abscess, emphysematous pyelonephritis — the diabetic's necrotizing variant needing urology)","Delirium + bacteriuria in the elderly WITHOUT urinary/systemic signs: hunt the real cause of delirium (meds, dehydration, other infection) — 'UTI' as the default explanation misses the actual diagnosis","Review prior cultures for resistance history — the empiric choice is personalized by the microbiology record"],"tx":["Empiric inpatient: ceftriaxone for most; prior resistant organisms/healthcare exposure → pip-tazo or carbapenem (ESBL history = carbapenem); septic shock → broad then narrow","DE-ESCALATE at 48h to the narrowest culture-directed agent; IV→PO switch when improving + tolerating (high-bioavailability options per susceptibilities)","Duration honestly short: pyelonephritis 5-7 days (FQ) or 7-10 (beta-lactam); complicated UTI 7 days when source-controlled and responding — the 14-day reflex is history for most","OBSTRUCTED + INFECTED = urology decompression emergently (the ward patient with stone + fever isn't 'on antibiotics, monitoring' — they're pre-procedural)","Catheters: out if not needed (the best CAUTI treatment), exchanged if chronic and infected; recurrent UTI outpatient plan (post-menopausal vaginal estrogen, prophylaxis conversations) referred properly"],"pearls":["The urine that 'grew something' in a patient with no symptoms is colonization — treating it breeds the resistant organism that causes the real infection later","Fever persisting at 72h on correct antibiotics is anatomy, not microbiology: image for the abscess or the stone","The Foley placed in the ED 'for monitoring' and forgotten is the CAUTI — necessity review is a daily rounding line"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA UTI & Asymptomatic Bacteriuria Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"CDC CAUTI Prevention","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/infectioncontrol"}]},{"id":"im-cellulitis","name":"Cellulitis & Skin/Soft Tissue Infection (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted for: systemic signs, rapid progression, failed outpatient therapy, immunocompromise, or the diabetic foot (its own disease — see osteomyelitis entry)","The inpatient value-add: right diagnosis (a third of admitted 'cellulitis' isn't), right duration (short), and recurrence prevention","Necrotizing red flags reviewed on EVERY exam (pain out of proportion, bullae, crepitus, toxicity) — the surgery call that can't wait for rounds"],"dx":["Mimic hunt before the second antibiotic: stasis dermatitis (bilateral, chronic), DVT (ultrasound), gout, contact dermatitis, lymphedema flares — mark the border, date it, and reassess against it","Blood cultures for the toxic/immunocompromised; POCUS/imaging for occult abscess in the non-responder","Purulent (abscess-associated, staph/MRSA logic) vs nonpurulent (strep logic) drives empiric choice"],"tx":["Nonpurulent inpatient: cefazolin IV (strep + MSSA) — vancomycin ONLY for MRSA risk factors, purulence, or severe sepsis (reflex vancomycin for every red leg is stewardship failure); purulent: drain what's drainable + MRSA coverage","Elevate the limb (the free intervention that works), treat the PORTAL: tinea pedis (look between the toes on rounds), xerosis, wounds","IV→PO at 48-72h improvement (cephalexin/TMP-SMX per pattern); TOTAL duration 5-6 days for uncomplicated cellulitis responding — extended courses add resistance, not cure","Expect 48-72h before erythema recedes (it may extend early WITHOUT treatment failure — worsening pain/systemic signs define failure, not the pen mark alone)","RECURRENT cellulitis (≥3-4/year, usually same leg with lymphedema/venous disease): compression therapy (the actual fix), portal control, and prophylactic penicillin conversation (PATCH trial) — the discharge that prevents the next four admissions"],"pearls":["Bilateral red legs without fever is stasis dermatitis until proven otherwise — steroids and compression, not vancomycin and a PICC","The erythema that grew 2cm on day 2 while the pain improved is treatment working — resist the escalation reflex","Compression stockings prevent more cellulitis readmissions than any antibiotic choice — prescribe them like they're medicine, because they are"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA SSTI Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Cellulitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/cellulitis.html"}]},{"id":"im-cdiff","name":"Clostridioides difficile Colitis","sec":"im","present":["New diarrhea (≥3 unformed stools/24h) + antibiotic exposure (clindamycin, FQs, cephalosporins classically — but any, up to months later) ± PPI use, age, hospitalization","Severity spectrum: mild diarrhea → colitis → fulminant (hypotension, ileus, megacolon) — the ileus patient may STOP stooling as they worsen (the deceptive 'improvement')","Recurrence is the disease's signature: 15-25% after first treatment, higher after each subsequent episode"],"dx":["Test ONLY diarrheal stool in patients not on laxatives (the two-step: GDH/toxin EIA ± PCR — PCR-positive/toxin-negative may be colonization; clinical judgment adjudicates)","NO test of cure, NO retesting the asymptomatic — positive PCRs linger for weeks","Severity staging (IDSA): WBC ≥15k or Cr ≥1.5 = severe; hypotension/shock/ileus/megacolon = fulminant","Fulminant assessment: lactate, imaging (colonic dilation >7cm), early surgical consultation — the colectomy conversation happens BEFORE the arrest"],"tx":["First episode: FIDAXOMICIN preferred (lower recurrence) or oral vancomycin 125 QID ×10d; metronidazole only when neither available (mild disease)","STOP the inciting antibiotic if at all possible (the single most effective intervention); reassess the PPI's necessity","Fulminant: vancomycin 500 PO QID + IV metronidazole; ileus → add vancomycin retention enemas; EARLY surgery consult — subtotal colectomy or diverting loop ileostomy with vancomycin lavage for the deteriorating (delayed operation = mortality)","RECURRENCE ladder: first recurrence → fidaxomicin (or tapered-pulsed vancomycin); BEZLOTOXUMAB adjunct for high recurrence risk; ≥2 recurrences → FECAL MICROBIOTA THERAPY (FMT/approved live biotherapeutics — cure rates ~80-90%+, the standard for multiply recurrent disease)","Infection control: contact precautions, SOAP AND WATER hands (alcohol doesn't kill spores), private room; future-antibiotic warning documented in the chart"],"pearls":["The C. diff patient who stopped having diarrhea but looks worse has an ileus, not a cure — examine the abdomen, get the lactate","Treating the PCR instead of the patient turns colonization into a vancomycin course — insist on real diarrhea before the test","Surgery consulted at 'fulminant' on day 1 saves more colons than surgery consulted at 'crashing' on day 3"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA/SHEA C. difficile Guidelines (2021 update)","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"CDC C. diff Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/cdiff"}]},{"id":"im-gib","name":"GI Bleeding (Inpatient Management)","sec":"im","present":["The medicine service owns: resuscitation continuity, endoscopy timing coordination, anticoagulation decisions both directions, and the rebleed watch","Upper vs lower logic per EM entry; the inpatient additions are risk scores for disposition (Glasgow-Blatchford low scores may never have needed the bed) and post-endoscopy management by stigmata","Anticoagulated bleeders are the daily puzzle: reverse, hold, and — the forgotten step — RESTART planning"],"dx":["Trend hemoglobins q6-8h while active (the first Hgb lies); coags/fibrinogen in the massive; BUN:Cr supports upper source","Endoscopy findings drive everything after: high-risk stigmata (active bleeding, visible vessel) vs clean-based ulcer (eats today, PPI PO, early discharge candidate)","Obscure bleeding pathway when EGD+colonoscopy negative: repeat look, capsule endoscopy, CTA if active — small bowel sources (angiodysplasias, Dieulafoy) are found by persistence","H. pylori testing on every ulcer (biopsy/stool antigen) — and remember false negatives during acute bleeding: retest if negative"],"tx":["Restrictive transfusion (Hgb <7; <8 with ACS) — Villanueva showed MORE transfusion = MORE rebleeding in portal hypertension; platelets <50 with active bleeding","PPI after endoscopy by findings: high-risk stigmata → 72h high-dose (drip or intermittent IV — equivalent), then oral taper; clean base → standard oral","Variceal follow-through: octreotide ×3-5d, ceftriaxone ×7d (mortality benefit), nonselective beta-blocker + banding surveillance program before discharge — the secondary prophylaxis that prevents the next exsanguination","ANTICOAGULATION RESTART (the evidence-backed counterintuition): warfarin/DOAC resumption at ~3-7 days post-hemostasis in AF/VTE patients REDUCES mortality and thrombosis without prohibitive rebleeding — the discharge summary that says 'hold anticoagulation, PCP to address' is an abdication; write the restart date","Aspirin for secondary CV prevention: continue/resume early (stopping it trades GI rebleeding for MI); the ulcer patient on DAPT needs the cardiology-GI conversation documented, plus a PPI indefinitely"],"pearls":["The rebleed declares itself with tachycardia and a lavage-colored NG output before the hemoglobin moves — vitals trend beats lab trend","An anticoagulant held at discharge without a restart date has a 30-day stroke plan attached","H. pylori eradication is the cure; the PPI was the truce — confirm eradication testing happens (the follow-up everyone forgets)"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG Upper GI Bleeding Guideline (2021)","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"Baveno Portal Hypertension Consensus","u":"https://www.easl.eu"}]},{"id":"im-cirrhosis","name":"Cirrhosis & Complications","sec":"im","present":["The decompensations that admit: ascites/volume, hepatic encephalopathy, variceal bleeding (see GIB), SBP, hepatorenal physiology, hyponatremia","Every admission is staged (Child-Pugh, MELD-Na — the transplant currency) and every decompensation is a transplant-referral trigger reviewed","The background rhythm: HCC surveillance (US ± AFP q6mo), variceal screening status, vaccination completeness"],"dx":["DIAGNOSTIC PARACENTESIS on EVERY admitted cirrhotic with ascites within 24h regardless of symptoms (SBP is silent in a third; early tap = mortality benefit): cell count/diff, culture in blood-culture bottles at bedside, albumin (SAAG), total protein","SBP = PMN ≥250 — treat; secondary peritonitis suspicion (very high PMN, polymicrobial, low glucose/high LDH/CEA) → image before assuming","New/worsening encephalopathy workup: infection hunt (tap included), GI bleed, constipation, meds (opioids/benzos), electrolytes — ammonia levels do NOT grade or guide (a clinical diagnosis; the serial ammonia is a habit, not medicine)","AKI in cirrhosis: prerenal vs ATN vs hepatorenal (HRS-AKI = diagnosis of exclusion after 48h albumin challenge + no other cause)"],"tx":["Ascites: sodium restriction 2g + spironolactone:furosemide (100:40 ratio, titrate) — NO NSAIDs ever; large-volume paracentesis + ALBUMIN (6-8g/L removed when >5L taken)","SBP: ceftriaxone/cefotaxime ×5d + ALBUMIN 1.5g/kg day 1, 1g/kg day 3 (renal protection, mortality benefit); then SECONDARY PROPHYLAXIS for life (daily norfloxacin/ciprofloxacin or TMP-SMX) — the discharge med that prevents the next admission","Encephalopathy: lactulose titrated to 2-3 soft stools (the number matters), add RIFAXIMIN after the second episode (readmission reduction) — and treat the precipitant, not just the symptom","HRS-AKI: albumin + vasoconstrictor (terlipressin where available — CONFIRM trial; else midodrine+octreotide+albumin), transplant evaluation urgently (HRS is a transplant indication conversation)","The comprehensive cirrhosis discharge: diuretic doses with lab follow-up, lactulose/rifaximin teaching, SBP prophylaxis when indicated, beta-blocker/banding program, HCC surveillance scheduled, alcohol treatment engaged (the etiologic therapy), transplant referral status documented"],"pearls":["The admission paracentesis skipped 'because they're comfortable' is how silent SBP becomes septic shock on hospital day 2 — tap them all","Serial ammonia levels on a lactulose drip is theater — count the stools and examine the patient instead","Every decompensation is the liver asking for a transplant evaluation — the referral not sent is the MELD points wasted"],"refs":[{"t":"AASLD Practice Guidances (Ascites/HE/HRS)","u":"https://www.aasld.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Cirrhosis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/cirrhosis.html"}]},{"id":"im-hepatitis","name":"Acute Hepatitis & Abnormal Liver Tests","sec":"im","present":["Patterns sort the differential: hepatocellular (AST/ALT-predominant), cholestatic (alk phos/bili — image the ducts), infiltrative (isolated alk phos), mixed","The magnitude taxonomy for ALT/AST in the thousands: ischemic ('shock liver' — check the hemodynamic history), acetaminophen, acute viral, rarer (autoimmune, Budd-Chiari, HSV in the immunosuppressed/pregnant)","ACUTE LIVER FAILURE = coagulopathy (INR ≥1.5) + encephalopathy without prior cirrhosis — a transplant-center phone call, not a floor observation"],"dx":["First-pass panel for significant hepatocellular injury: acetaminophen level (+honest dosing history including combination products), viral serologies (HAV IgM, HBsAg + core IgM, HCV RNA, HEV in the right setting), autoimmune (ANA, anti-smooth muscle, IgG), ceruloplasmin (<40y), ferritin/iron, pregnancy test (HELLP/AFLP change everything)","Cholestatic pattern: ultrasound first (ducts) → dilated = obstructive workup (MRCP/ERCP: stones, strictures, malignancy); non-dilated = AMA (PBC), drug review, infiltrative consideration","DILI is a chart-review diagnosis: timeline every drug/supplement (the herbal weight-loss tea, the bodybuilding stack — ask specifically); NAFLD/MASLD is the commonest mild chronic elevation (fibrosis-stage with FIB-4 → elastography, don't just reassure)","AST:ALT >2 with modest elevations suggests alcohol-associated; alcoholic hepatitis severity by Maddrey DF/MELD"],"tx":["Acetaminophen: N-acetylcysteine early and liberally (also consider in non-acetaminophen ALF — evidence for benefit); nomogram for single ingestions, treat empirically for staggered/unclear","Acute liver failure bundle: NAC, glucose monitoring (they drop), INR without FFP unless bleeding (it's the prognostic marker), avoid sedation, neuro checks (cerebral edema kills), TRANSFER to transplant center early — King's College criteria thinking","Alcoholic hepatitis: nutrition aggressively (the intervention with consistent benefit), prednisolone for severe (DF ≥32) reassessed at day 7 (Lille score stops futile steroids), infection surveillance; addiction medicine engaged (the survival treatment)","Autoimmune: steroids per hepatology; Wilson/Budd-Chiari/HSV: specific urgent pathways","Chronic discoveries managed forward: HBV/HCV treatment linkage (HCV cure is 8-12 weeks of pills — losing the patient to follow-up is losing the cure), MASLD → weight/metabolic program + fibrosis staging"],"pearls":["ALT 4000 with an unremarkable-feeling patient after a hypotensive episode is shock liver — the trend resolves as fast as it rose; don't launch the full serologic armada before looking at the blood pressure history","The 'negative' acetaminophen level drawn early or in a staggered ingestion excludes nothing — the history and the NAC threshold do the work","Every abnormal LFT panel discharged with 'recheck outpatient' needs an owner and a date — the HCV RNA nobody followed up is a cirrhosis with a timestamp"],"refs":[{"t":"AASLD/ACG Liver Chemistry Guidelines","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"AASLD Acute Liver Failure Guidance","u":"https://www.aasld.org"}]},{"id":"im-pancreatitis","name":"Acute Pancreatitis (Inpatient Course)","sec":"im","present":["The floor course after ED diagnosis: fluid stewardship, early feeding, severity surveillance, and the etiology-fix-before-discharge rule","Trajectory fork at 48-72h: most resolve (mild); the minority declare severe disease (persistent organ failure) or local complications — the ward's job is catching the fork early","Etiology drives the discharge: gallstones (cholecystectomy THIS admission for mild), alcohol (treatment linkage), triglycerides (>1000 — specific pathway), drugs, post-ERCP, idiopathic (age-appropriate malignancy thought in the >40 first episode)"],"dx":["Severity tracking beats admission scores: BUN trend (rising = under-resuscitated or severe disease), creatinine, SIRS persistence at 48h — the bedside trajectory is the best prognosticator","NO routine early CT (doesn't change early management, underestimates necrosis) — CT with contrast at 72h+ for the non-improver (necrosis assessment) or diagnostic doubt","RUQ ultrasound on every first episode (stones); triglycerides + calcium on the etiology panel; MRCP/EUS for the 'idiopathic' before it's labeled such","Infected necrosis suspicion (fever/deterioration week 2-4): gas in the collection on CT; culture-guided from drains — not routine FNA anymore"],"tx":["Fluids MODERATE (WATERFALL): LR at goal-directed rates, reassess — the 10L pancreatitis resuscitations of the past caused abdominal hypertension and worse outcomes","FEED EARLY: oral low-fat as tolerated within 24-72h (tolerance, not lipase, gates the tray); can't eat by day 3-5 → enteral (NG acceptable — jejunal not required) — enteral feeding REDUCES infection vs TPN (the gut barrier is the treatment)","NO prophylactic antibiotics for pancreatitis or sterile necrosis — antibiotics for documented infection (infected necrosis: carbapenem-class penetration logic)","Infected/symptomatic necrosis: DELAY intervention 4+ weeks for walling-off when possible, then step-up (percutaneous drain → endoscopic transmural necrosectomy/minimally invasive) — PANTER/step-up trials buried open necrosectomy","THE DISCHARGE RULES: mild gallstone pancreatitis → SAME-ADMISSION cholecystectomy (PONCHO: interval waiting = ~20% recurrence/readmission); hypertriglyceridemic → fibrate + insulin-pathway follow-up + apheresis relationships for the extreme; alcohol → treatment linkage as prescription-level care"],"pearls":["The BUN that rises on day 2 outranks every admission severity score — reassess volume and level of care","Feeding the pancreatitis patient early is treatment, not a reward for a normal lipase — the gut fed is the infection prevented","Discharging mild gallstone pancreatitis without a cholecystectomy date is scheduling the readmission — book the OR before the ride home"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG/AGA Acute Pancreatitis Guidelines","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"Step-Up Approach (PANTER, NEJM)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"}]},{"id":"im-vte","name":"Venous Thromboembolism (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The medicine service inherits: confirmed PE/DVT needing risk-stratified management, anticoagulation initiation/teaching, and the provoked-vs-unprovoked determination that sets duration","Also owns PREVENTION: every medical admission gets a VTE risk assessment (Padua/IMPROVE thinking) and appropriate prophylaxis — hospital-acquired VTE is a quality failure","Submassive/intermediate-risk PE on the floor: the deterioration-watch admission (troponin/RV strain positive, normotensive)"],"dx":["Risk stratification completed, not assumed: sPESI, troponin/BNP, RV assessment (CT ratio or echo) — drives monitoring intensity and escalation planning","Provoked (surgery, immobilization, hospitalization, estrogen, pregnancy) vs unprovoked taxonomy determines duration — document the reasoning, not just the label","Thrombophilia testing: NOT during the acute event/on anticoagulation and NOT for provoked VTE — selective, outpatient, when it changes management; APS testing when the phenotype fits (arterial+venous, recurrent loss) since it changes drug choice","Cancer screening after unprovoked VTE: age-appropriate screening + symptom-directed workup (not pan-scanning — SOME trial)","Upper extremity/catheter-associated DVT: the line question (needed? functional?) shapes management"],"tx":["DOAC first-line (apixaban/rivaroxaban with lead-in dosing, or LMWH-bridge to dabigatran/edoxaban); cancer-associated: DOACs acceptable (LMWH for GI/GU luminal lesions); APS/mechanical valves/pregnancy: NOT DOACs (warfarin/LMWH respectively)","Duration by provocation: provoked-transient → 3 months and STOP; unprovoked → extended anticoagulation with annual risk-benefit review (reduced-dose apixaban/rivaroxaban after 6 months lowers bleeding while keeping efficacy); second unprovoked → indefinite","Submassive floor management: anticoagulate, monitor with explicit escalation criteria (hypotension, rising O2 needs → ICU/catheter-directed conversation); massive → per EM entry","IVC filters ONLY for anticoagulation contraindication — with a RETRIEVAL plan documented (the permanent 'temporary' filter is a complication factory)","The discharge package: teach-back on the drug, interaction counseling, duration + stop/review date in the summary, compression stockings for symptomatic DVT (symptom relief, not PTS prevention per SOX), follow-up owner named"],"pearls":["The provoked-vs-unprovoked sentence in your discharge summary is worth more than the drug choice — it decides whether anticoagulation ends at 3 months or never","Thrombophilia panels sent during the acute clot on a heparin drip produce expensive noise — test later, selectively, or not at all","An IVC filter without a retrieval appointment is a future IVC thrombosis with a serial number"],"refs":[{"t":"CHEST VTE Guidelines","u":"https://journal.chestnet.org"},{"t":"ASH VTE Guidelines","u":"https://www.hematology.org"}]},{"id":"im-anticoag","name":"Anticoagulation Management & Reversal","sec":"im","present":["The cross-cutting inpatient skill: periprocedural decisions, bleeding management, drug interactions, renal dosing, and the transitions between agents","The daily consults: 'holding for procedure — bridge?', 'INR 8, no bleeding', 'GI bleed on apixaban', 'new AKI on dabigatran'","Medication errors cluster here: anticoagulants are perennial top-3 harm drugs — double-checks are culture, not insult"],"dx":["Know what they're on and WHY (indication drives every risk-benefit): AF stroke prevention vs recent VTE vs mechanical valve are different games","Renal function on every anticoagulated chart (DOAC accumulation in AKI; enoxaparin dosing); drug interactions swept (the DOAC + diltiazem + fluconazole stack)","Assays when needed: anti-Xa for LMWH in extremes (pregnancy, obesity, renal failure); DOAC levels rarely available — timing of last dose is the working assay","Bleeding assessment: site, severity, hemodynamics — 'major' (critical site, Hgb drop ≥2, transfusion) triggers reversal pathways"],"tx":["WARFARIN management: INR high without bleeding → hold ± small PO vitamin K (2.5mg) for INR >10, just hold for 4.5-10 (evidence-based restraint); major bleeding → 4-FACTOR PCC (faster, less volume than FFP) + vitamin K 10mg IV","DOAC bleeding: dabigatran → idarucizumab; Xa inhibitors → andexanet (where available/selected) or 4F-PCC (widely practiced); the universal adjuncts: stop drug, source control, time (short half-lives are the built-in reversal — 12-24h clears most with normal kidneys)","PERIPROCEDURAL: most DOACs → hold 24-48h by procedure risk + renal function, NO bridging; warfarin → hold 5 days, BRIDGE ONLY the truly high-risk (mechanical mitral, recent stroke/VTE <3mo, severe thrombophilia) — BRIDGE trial: routine bridging = bleeding without benefit","Transitions done precisely: warfarin→DOAC when INR <2; DOAC→heparin at next scheduled dose; overlap rules for VTE warfarin initiation (5 days + INR ≥2 ×24h)","HIT changes everything: stop ALL heparin (flushes included), non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban/bivalirudin/fondaparinux) — see thrombocytopenia entry"],"pearls":["The INR of 9 in a well patient needs a held dose and a recheck, not FFP and a scare — reversal intensity matches bleeding, not numbers","Routine bridging is a habit the BRIDGE trial should have killed — bridge the mechanical mitral valve, not the CHA2DS2-VASc of 4","Every anticoagulation hold order needs a partner: the restart order with a date — the indefinite hold is the stroke nobody ordered"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC Anticoagulation Decision Pathways","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"ASH Anticoagulation Guidelines","u":"https://www.hematology.org"}]},{"id":"im-anemia","name":"Anemia Workup & Transfusion Medicine","sec":"im","present":["The inpatient anemia consult sorts three questions: is it acute (bleeding/hemolysis — urgent) or chronic? what's the mechanism? does it need transfusion TODAY?","MCV + retic count is still the fastest map: micro (iron, thal, chronic disease), macro (B12/folate, meds, MDS, liver/thyroid, retic-driven), normo (chronic disease, renal, marrow, dilutional)","Hospital-acquired anemia is real: daily phlebotomy + hemodilution — the trending Hgb drop without a source is often us"],"dx":["Retic FIRST: high (losing or lysing — find the bleed or run hemolysis labs: LDH, haptoglobin, indirect bili, smear) vs low (production problem)","Iron studies interpreted like an adult: ferritin <30 = deficient, full stop; ferritin 30-100 + high inflammation may still be deficient (TSAT <20 supports); IRON DEFICIENCY IS A FINDING, NOT A DIAGNOSIS — the source hunt (GI evaluation in men + postmenopausal women) is mandatory","Hemolysis positive → direct antiglobulin test splits immune (warm/cold AIHA, drug) from non-immune (MAHA — the smear with schistocytes is an EMERGENCY branch: TTP/HUS/DIC, see platelets entry; mechanical valves; hypersplenism)","B12: check MMA when borderline; smear review yourself (the pathologist's phone number is a diagnostic instrument); unexplained cytopenias/macrocytosis → SPEP, hematology, marrow conversation"],"tx":["RESTRICTIVE transfusion (Hgb <7; <7.5-8 cardiac surgery/ACS per protocol) — TRICC/TRISS-era evidence is consistent: more blood is not more better; symptomatic thresholds individualized","Transfusion practicalities owned: consent conversation, single-unit-then-reassess culture, reaction recognition (febrile vs TRALI vs TACO — the volume-overloaded transfusion is the commonest harm: slow rates + diuretic cover in the susceptible)","IRON: IV liberally in the hospital (functional deficiency, intolerance, ongoing losses, CKD/HF — where oral fails or is futile); oral iron correctly (once daily or alternate-day dosing absorbs BETTER than TID — hepcidin physiology), with the source workup scheduled","B12 repletion (parenteral for neuro symptoms/pernicious anemia), folate; treat the driver (renal → see CKD entry ESA logic; inflammation → the underlying disease)","The discharge line every anemia admission deserves: mechanism named, source workup owner assigned (the colonoscopy referral in writing), repletion plan with recheck date"],"pearls":["Iron deficiency in a man or postmenopausal woman has a GI lesion until endoscopy says otherwise — the ferritin of 8 treated with pills and no scope is a missed cancer pathway","One unit, reassess, repeat if needed — the two-unit reflex doubles exposure for symptoms one unit fixes","The smear you look at yourself finds the schistocytes the automated differential buried — MAHA is a diagnosis made by eyes"],"refs":[{"t":"AABB Transfusion Guidelines","u":"https://www.aabb.org"},{"t":"ASH Anemia Resources","u":"https://www.hematology.org"}]},{"id":"im-plt","name":"Thrombocytopenia (incl. HIT)","sec":"im","present":["The inpatient approach triages by context + kinetics: new admission with low platelets (chronic ITP? cirrhosis? MDS?) vs the hospitalized patient whose platelets FELL (the dangerous version — drugs, HIT, sepsis/DIC, MAHA)","The two never-miss emergencies: TTP (MAHA + thrombocytopenia is enough — pentad not required) and HIT (thrombosis risk, not bleeding risk)","Pseudothrombocytopenia (EDTA clumping) is the 30-second exclusion: smear + citrate-tube recount"],"dx":["Smear FIRST on every new thrombocytopenia: schistocytes → MAHA pathway (TTP/HUS/DIC/HELLP) — PLASMIC score + ADAMTS13 sent, but treatment doesn't wait for the send-out; clumps → pseudo; blasts → hematology now","HIT: 4Ts score (timing 5-10 days post-heparin, degree of fall ~50%, thrombosis, no other cause) — low score EXCLUDES (stop testing), intermediate/high → immunoassay (PF4) → functional confirmation (SRA); the sequence prevents both missed HIT and the over-called HIT that strands patients on argatroban","DIC labs (fibrinogen, D-dimer, coags) in the septic/obstetric/malignant context","Drug timeline honestly built: heparin exposures (including flushes/line locks), vancomycin, linezolid, quinine; ITP remains a diagnosis of exclusion"],"tx":["TTP suspected: PLASMA EXCHANGE + steroids STARTED on clinical suspicion (mortality untreated ~90%, treated <10%) — caplacizumab per hematology; platelet transfusion relatively contraindicated (feeds the thrombosis) absent life-threatening bleeding","HIT: STOP ALL HEPARIN everywhere (flushes, coated lines, prophylaxis) + START a non-heparin anticoagulant at therapeutic dose (argatroban/bivalirudin; fondaparinux/DOACs in selected stable patients) — even WITHOUT thrombosis (30-50% thrombose untreated); NO warfarin until platelets recover ≥150 (venous gangrene); duplex the legs (silent DVT common); document the allergy permanently","ITP: treat the COUNT + BLEEDING, not the number alone — steroids ± IVIG for active bleeding/very low counts; TPO agonists/rituximab down-pathway with hematology","Transfusion thresholds: <10 prophylactic (stable), <50 for procedures/active bleeding, <100 for CNS — and almost never in TTP/HIT (see above)","Sepsis/DIC: treat the driver; cryoprecipitate for fibrinogen <100-150 with bleeding"],"pearls":["Schistocytes + low platelets = TTP until ADAMTS13 says otherwise — the plasma exchange you start tonight is the outcome","A low 4Ts score means STOP — the PF4 sent 'to be safe' on a score of 2 generates the false positive that costs a week of argatroban and a real bleeding risk","In HIT, stopping heparin without starting something else is half a treatment — the thrombosis happens in the gap"],"refs":[{"t":"ASH ITP/HIT/TTP Guidelines","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"ISTH DIC Resources","u":"https://www.isth.org"}]},{"id":"im-stroke","name":"Stroke & TIA (Medicine Co-Management)","sec":"im","present":["Medicine's share of the stroke admission: secondary-prevention engineering, complication prevention (aspiration, VTE, delirium), comorbidity management, and the etiologic workup follow-through","TIA/minor stroke on the medicine service: the high-risk window is NOW (90-day stroke risk front-loaded into the first week) — workup is urgent, not elective","Post-thrombolysis/thrombectomy patients arrive with BP parameters and antithrombotic timing rules — know them before writing orders"],"dx":["Etiology determines prevention: vessel imaging reviewed (carotid stenosis → revascularization referral within 2 weeks for symptomatic 70-99%), echo with bubble study when cryptogenic (PFO conversation for the young), rhythm monitoring escalation (telemetry → prolonged ambulatory/implantable monitors — occult AF is the findable cause)","Cryptogenic stroke in the young: the expanded panel (dissection imaging, hypercoagulable evaluation per hematology, drug screen, endocarditis thinking)","Dysphagia SCREEN before anything PO (aspiration pneumonia is the killer complication); mood screening (post-stroke depression is common, treatable, and outcome-relevant)"],"tx":["Antithrombotics by syndrome: minor stroke/high-risk TIA → DAPT ×21 days then mono (CHANCE/POINT); AF-related → anticoagulation (timing post-infarct sized by infarct volume — the 1-3-6-12 day heuristic with neurology); post-lytic: antithrombotics held 24h","The prevention stack completed before discharge: high-intensity statin (SPARCL), BP program (<130/80 chronically — permissive acutely per neurology), SGLT2i/GLP-1 for the diabetic, smoking cessation, AF hunt scheduled","VTE prophylaxis (pharmacologic when hemorrhage allows), early mobilization with PT/OT, SLP-guided diet — the rehab referrals ARE stroke treatment","Carotid endarterectomy/stenting referral timed (benefit decays fast after the event)","Discharge with named follow-up: neurology, monitor results owner, rehab plan, and the driving conversation documented"],"pearls":["The occult AF found on the 30-day monitor changes the antithrombotic and prevents the second stroke — the monitor unordered is the recurrence unexplained","Statin, BP plan, DAPT clock, dysphagia screen: the four boxes that separate a stroke admission from a stroke observation","Symptomatic carotid stenosis is a 2-week deadline, not a routine referral — the surgery's benefit is front-loaded exactly like the risk"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Secondary Prevention Guidelines","u":"https://www.stroke.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Stroke","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/stroke.html"}]},{"id":"im-delirium","name":"Delirium","sec":"im","present":["Acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention + awareness — hyperactive (the one everyone notices), HYPOACTIVE (the one everyone misses — worse prognosis), or mixed","The vulnerable brain (age, dementia, sensory impairment) + the insult (infection, drugs, metabolic, urinary retention/fecal impaction, pain, sleep disruption) = the ward's commonest complication","Delirium is a medical emergency wearing a behavioral costume: it doubles mortality risk and accelerates cognitive decline"],"dx":["Screen actively (CAM/4AT — attention testing: months backward, days backward), because the quiet confused patient self-selects out of attention","The precipitant hunt is the workup: med review FIRST (anticholinergics, benzos, opioids, steroids — the Beers list walk-through), infection screen driven by exam (not the reflex pan-culture), metabolic panel, bladder scan + rectal exam (retention/impaction — the embarrassingly common reversible pair), pain assessment","Neuroimaging/LP/EEG SELECTIVELY: focal signs, head trauma, fever+meningismus, or no cause found (nonconvulsive status hides in 'encephalopathy')","Distinguish from dementia (chronic, attention relatively preserved) — collateral history is the instrument; delirium ON dementia is the common reality"],"tx":["NONPHARMACOLOGIC IS THE TREATMENT (HELP-model bundle, evidence-backed): reorientation, glasses + hearing aids ON, sleep protocol (lights/noise/no 4am vitals when safe), early mobilization, hydration/nutrition, family presence, line/catheter/restraint minimization","Treat every found cause; schedule the bowel regimen and toileting","Antipsychotics do NOT treat delirium (trials neutral for duration/severity) — reserve low-dose haloperidol/atypicals for severe distress or safety threat, briefly, with a documented stop plan; NEVER benzos except alcohol/benzo withdrawal","Restraints worsen delirium — a last resort with exit criteria, not a nursing convenience","The discharge duties: delirium documented as a diagnosis (it predicts everything), deliriogenic meds deprescribed IN WRITING, family counseled that recovery takes weeks-months, cognitive follow-up arranged"],"pearls":["Hypoactive delirium is missed on rounds because the patient is 'pleasantly resting' — test attention on everyone over 70, every day","The bladder scan and the rectal exam solve more 3am agitation than haloperidol ever has","The quetiapine started for delirium and living on in the discharge med list is how hospitals create long-term antipsychotic users — deprescribe before the door"],"refs":[{"t":"AGS Delirium Resources / HELP Program","u":"https://www.americangeriatrics.org"},{"t":"NICE Delirium Guideline","u":"https://www.nice.org.uk"}]},{"id":"im-dementia","name":"Dementia & Behavioral Disturbance (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The admitted dementia patient decompensates from the admission itself: unfamiliar environment + sleep disruption + tethers = behavioral crisis risk — prevention beats response","New cognitive concern flagged inpatient: distinguish delirium (fix first, THEN assess baseline) from underlying dementia — never diagnose dementia during acute illness","Behavioral and psychological symptoms (agitation, sundowning, resistance to care) are COMMUNICATIONS: pain, fear, unmet needs speaking through behavior"],"dx":["Collateral history is the gold standard: timeline (years vs weeks), functional decline pattern (IADLs first), safety events (driving, wandering, finances)","The reversible screen when workup is owed: TSH, B12, metabolic panel, depression assessment ('pseudodementia'), med review, structural imaging once — plus the syndrome recognition (Lewy body: fluctuations + visual hallucinations + parkinsonism = ANTIPSYCHOTIC SENSITIVITY warning; NPH triad; frontotemporal behavioral variants)","Rapid progression (weeks-months) is NOT typical Alzheimer's — expand the differential (autoimmune encephalitis, prion, structural)","Capacity assessed decision-by-decision, documented — dementia ≠ global incapacity"],"tx":["Behavioral first, always: pain treated empirically (undertreated pain drives half of 'agitation'), routines preserved, family/sitter presence, DICE approach (Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate)","Antipsychotics for dementia carry a mortality black box: reserved for danger/severe distress after nonpharmacologic failure, lowest dose, shortest course, documented risk discussion — and NEVER reflexively in Lewy body disease","Deprescribe the harmful (anticholinergics, benzos, sliding-scale-everything); cholinesterase inhibitors/memantine are outpatient decisions with realistic expectation-setting","THE HOSPITALIST'S REAL DELIVERABLES: safety evaluation (home supports, wandering risk), caregiver assessment (burnout screening — the caregiver is the second patient), goals-of-care trajectory conversation started early (feeding decisions, rehospitalization preferences — see EOL entry), and the PCP/geriatrics/neurology handoff with the cognitive concern in the summary","Elder abuse vigilance when the story doesn't fit"],"pearls":["'Agitation' in dementia is a symptom with a differential, not a haloperidol deficiency — examine for pain, retention, impaction, and fear first","The hospital is where dementia families finally hear the word out loud — saying it clearly, kindly, and in the discharge summary changes the next two years","Lewy body + antipsychotic = potential catastrophe; when hallucinations come with parkinsonism, the reflex order is the error"],"refs":[{"t":"Alzheimer's Association Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.alz.org"},{"t":"AGS Beers Criteria","u":"https://www.americangeriatrics.org"}]},{"id":"im-etoh-withdrawal","name":"Alcohol Withdrawal (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Every admission screens (AUDIT-C/single question) — the elective surgery patient nobody asked seizes on POD2","Timeline discipline: tremor/anxiety 6-12h → hallucinosis 12-24h → seizures 12-48h → DTs 48-96h (autonomic storm + delirium; mortality real even treated)","Risk-stratify on arrival (PAWSS score): prior DTs/withdrawal seizures, heavy daily use, comorbid illness — high risk earns proactive, not reactive, management"],"dx":["CIWA-Ar drives symptom-triggered dosing — but CIWA requires a communicative patient (the intubated/delirious need fixed or RASS-based protocols)","The withdrawal mimics excluded in parallel: sepsis, hypoglycemia, thyroid storm, stimulant toxicity, hepatic encephalopathy (the cirrhotic 'withdrawal' that's actually HE inverts the treatment)","Labs: BMP, Mg/Phos (replete — often depleted), LFTs, lipase; Wernicke vigilance (confusion/ataxia/eye findings — ANY one suffices)"],"tx":["Symptom-triggered benzodiazepines (front-loaded diazepam for the smooth auto-taper, lorazepam for advanced cirrhosis) — symptom-triggered beats fixed-schedule for total dose and duration","PHENOBARBITAL: the escalation answer for benzodiazepine-resistant withdrawal and increasingly a primary protocol (predictable kinetics, GABA + anti-glutamate) — know your institution's pathway; refractory DTs → ICU, dexmedetomidine as ADJUNCT (not monotherapy — it masks, doesn't treat), propofol if intubated","Thiamine BEFORE/with glucose in the depleted: prophylaxis 100-200mg IV; Wernicke TREATMENT dose 500mg IV TID ×3d — under-dosing treatment-level Wernicke is the classic error","Replete Mg (facilitates thiamine function + seizure threshold), K, Phos; the withdrawal seizure gets benzos (phenytoin does NOT work for withdrawal seizures)","THE ADMISSION IS THE TREATABLE MOMENT: naltrexone or acamprosate STARTED inpatient (evidence supports inpatient initiation), warm handoff to addiction medicine/peer support, and the discharge that names alcohol use disorder as the diagnosis it is"],"pearls":["The CIWA of 6 at hour 8 in a high-PAWSS patient is the DTs of hour 60 announcing itself — risk scores choose the protocol, not the current score alone","When benzo doses climb past 40mg diazepam-equivalents without control, the answer is phenobarbital, not benzo dose 41","Anti-craving medication started at discharge doubles the odds this admission was the last one — the naltrexone conversation is the mortality intervention"],"refs":[{"t":"ASAM Alcohol Withdrawal Guideline (2020)","u":"https://www.asam.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Alcohol Withdrawal","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000764.htm"}]},{"id":"im-oud","name":"Opioid Use Disorder (Inpatient Management)","sec":"im","present":["The hospitalized patient with OUD: withdrawal management, pain management (they still get real analgesia), infection complications (endocarditis, osteo, SSTI), and the treatment-initiation opportunity","Withdrawal (COWS-scored): myalgias, rhinorrhea, mydriasis, GI distress, restlessness — miserable and destabilizing but rarely lethal; UNTREATED withdrawal drives premature discharge (patient-directed discharge = interrupted antibiotics = mortality)","Fentanyl-era pharmacology changed the playbook: lipophilic accumulation complicates buprenorphine timing"],"dx":["COWS drives induction timing; urine toxicology interpreted with humility (fentanyl analogs miss on standard panels; confirmatory testing when it matters)","The infection survey in the febrile person who injects: blood cultures, echo thinking (see IE), spine exam/MRI threshold low (epidural abscess), HIV/HCV/HBV testing offered universally","Pain assessment taken seriously: OUD + acute pain (the fracture, the abscess) needs MORE analgesia than opioid-naive patients, not less — tolerance is physiology, not moral failure"],"tx":["BUPRENORPHINE initiation inpatient: traditional (COWS ≥8-12, then 2-4mg titrating) or low-dose/'micro-induction' overlap strategies for the fentanyl-exposed or those on full agonists — precipitated withdrawal managed with MORE buprenorphine + adjuncts; METHADONE alternative pathway (can initiate inpatient; coordinate the clinic slot before discharge — the dose without a clinic is a cliff)","X-waiver era is over: any prescriber can discharge buprenorphine — the prescription IN HAND with a named follow-up is the standard","Acute pain on OUD treatment: CONTINUE buprenorphine/methadone + add multimodal (NSAIDs, acetaminophen, regional, ketamine) + short-acting full agonists at adequate doses when needed — splitting buprenorphine TID uses its analgesic pharmacology","Withdrawal adjuncts while titrating: clonidine, ondansetron, loperamide, NSAIDs","HARM REDUCTION IS DISCHARGE PLANNING: NALOXONE dispensed (not prescribed — dispensed), overdose education (tolerance loss after abstinence), syringe service referral, HCV treatment linkage; the patient-directed discharge negotiated (partial treatment + oral antibiotics + follow-up beats a signed-out nothing)"],"pearls":["Buprenorphine started in the hospital doubles treatment engagement at 30 days — the induction is the intervention this admission was for","Withholding opioid analgesia from the OUD patient with a surgical abdomen isn't stewardship, it's undertreatment with a stigma diagnosis","The naloxone kit handed to the patient at the door is the cheapest mortality intervention in the building"],"refs":[{"t":"ASAM National Practice Guideline (OUD)","u":"https://www.asam.org"},{"t":"SAMHSA Treatment Resources","u":"https://www.samhsa.gov"}]},{"id":"im-syncope","name":"Syncope Workup (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["Admitted syncope = someone judged the cause potentially cardiac/dangerous — the inpatient job is efficient risk-directed evaluation, not the shotgun","The history retaken properly solves most: true syncope (rapid onset, brief, complete recovery) vs seizure (aura, tonic-clonic, prolonged confusion) vs fall (no LOC) — witnesses called, not just referenced","High-risk features justifying the bed: exertional/supine onset, palpitations preceding, family history of sudden death, structural disease, concerning ECG"],"dx":["Telemetry with a DEFINED question and duration — the arrhythmia yield concentrates in the first 24-48h; symptom-rhythm correlation is the goal","Echo for suspected structural disease (murmur, HF history, abnormal ECG); ischemic evaluation only when ischemia is actually suggested (routine stress testing in syncope is low-yield theater)","ECG review like a cardiologist: intervals (QT), pre-excitation, Brugada pattern, epsilon waves, HCM voltage, blocks — the 12-lead is the highest-yield test in syncope","Orthostatics DONE PROPERLY (1 and 3 minutes standing); carotid sinus massage in the older patient with compatible story (monitored); the workup NOT indicated: routine head CT, carotid dopplers, EEG without seizure features — document their omission as reasoning, not oversight","Unexplained + high-risk after full workup: prolonged monitoring (30-day event/implantable loop recorder) and EP referral"],"tx":["Treat what's found: pacemaker pathways (symptomatic bradycardia/blocks per device guidelines), EP for inducible/documented ventricular arrhythmias, structural disease per its own algorithm","Reflex/vasovagal (most, even among admissions): education (prodrome recognition, counterpressure maneuvers), hydration/salt liberalization when appropriate, med-list deprescribing (the alpha-blocker + diuretic + nitrate stack in the 80-year-old IS the diagnosis)","Orthostatic hypotension: volume, med adjustment, compression garments, slow-rise counseling; midodrine/droxidopa for the refractory neurogenic","Driving counseling per local regulations documented — the conversation nobody wants and everybody needs","Disposition honesty: low-risk syncope admitted 'for obs' accrues cost without yield — discharge with the right outpatient monitor beats a third day of telemetry"],"pearls":["The witness on the phone is worth more than the head CT — two minutes of collateral history reclassifies half of 'syncope'","Syncope with injury (facial trauma, no protective response) argues for sudden cardiac mechanism — the bruise pattern is data","The medication list is the most treatable cause of syncope in anyone over 75 — deprescribing is the procedure"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA/HRS Syncope Guideline (2017)","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"ESC Syncope Guidelines","u":"https://www.escardio.org"}]},{"id":"im-thyroid","name":"Thyroid Disease (Hypo/Hyper/Storm/Myxedema)","sec":"im","present":["Ward spectrum: the incidental TSH abnormality (interpret in context), symptomatic hypo/hyperthyroidism, and the two emergencies — thyroid storm and myxedema coma — that kill when labeled 'sepsis' or 'altered mental status'","STORM: hyperthyroidism + decompensation (fever, tachycardia/AF out of proportion, agitation→delirium, GI symptoms, heart failure) with a precipitant (infection, surgery, iodine load, withdrawal of antithyroid drugs) — Burch-Wartofsky supports the clinical call","MYXEDEMA COMA: the hypothyroid elderly patient in winter with hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, hypoventilation (CO2 retention), hyponatremia, AMS — often triggered by infection/sedatives"],"dx":["TSH + free T4 together for the sick inpatient; NONTHYROIDAL ILLNESS ('sick euthyroid' — low T3, variable TSH) confounds every ICU panel: don't diagnose or treat thyroid disease from labs drawn during critical illness unless the clinical syndrome demands it — recheck in recovery","Hyperthyroid etiology after stabilization: receptor antibodies (Graves), uptake scan when not contraindicated (recent iodine/contrast blocks it), nodular disease","Storm/myxedema are CLINICAL diagnoses — treatment precedes confirmatory levels; draw cortisol before steroids in myxedema (coexisting adrenal insufficiency)","Amiodarone thyroid disease is its own beast (type 1 vs 2) — endocrinology early"],"tx":["THYROID STORM sequence matters: beta-blockade (propranolol — also blocks T4→T3), THIONAMIDE first (PTU favored acutely — blocks conversion; load then q4h), THEN iodine ≥1 HOUR AFTER thionamide (iodine before blockade feeds the fire — the classic sequencing error), hydrocortisone (conversion blockade + adrenal reserve), cooling, precipitant treatment, ICU","MYXEDEMA COMA: IV levothyroxine load (200-400mcg then daily; ± T3 in selected), HYDROCORTISONE FIRST/CONCURRENT (until adrenal insufficiency excluded — thyroxine before steroids can precipitate crisis), passive rewarming, ventilatory support readiness, treat the trigger — mortality 25-50% even treated, ICU always","Routine hypothyroidism: weight-based levothyroxine (1.6mcg/kg; start low in elderly/CAD), TSH recheck 6 weeks, absorption counseling (empty stomach, separated from calcium/iron/PPIs)","Routine hyperthyroidism: methimazole (except first-trimester/storm), beta-blockade for symptoms, definitive-therapy referral (RAI vs surgery) — agranulocytosis warning documented (fever+sore throat = stop drug, check CBC)","Subclinical disease: treat by TSH degree + age + symptoms per guidelines, mostly an outpatient titration"],"pearls":["Iodine before the thionamide in storm stocks the factory you're trying to shut — sequence is the treatment","Myxedema coma gets hydrocortisone before thyroxine — the reverse order can convert hypothyroidism into adrenal crisis","The TSH drawn in the ICU is a hostage of the illness — recheck it in clinic before anyone starts lifelong levothyroxine"],"refs":[{"t":"ATA Hyper/Hypothyroidism Guidelines","u":"https://www.thyroid.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Thyroid Diseases","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/thyroiddiseases.html"}]},{"id":"im-adrenal","name":"Adrenal Insufficiency","sec":"im","present":["The great inpatient mimic: refractory hypotension, hyponatremia + hyperkalemia (primary), hypoglycemia, eosinophilia, fatigue/nausea — 'culture-negative sepsis that responds to steroids'","Etiology map: primary (Addison's — autoimmune, infectious, hemorrhage [anticoagulated patient with flank pain + shock], mets), secondary (pituitary), and the commonest of all: EXOGENOUS steroid suppression (including inhaled/topical/injected — the med list nobody counted)","ADRENAL CRISIS: shock disproportionate to illness in someone with (often unrecognized) AI + a stressor — treatment cannot wait for testing"],"dx":["Crisis suspected: draw cortisol + ACTH, THEN TREAT — do not await results (a random cortisol <5 in shock strongly supports; >18-20 argues against)","Stable workup: morning cortisol (<3 diagnostic, >15-18 excludes) → cosyntropin stimulation (250mcg; 30-60min cortisol ≥18-20 normal); ACTH level separates primary (high) from central (low/normal)","Primary confirmed: check the axis partners (aldosterone/renin — mineralocorticoid need), 21-hydroxylase antibodies, adrenal imaging when non-autoimmune suspected","The chronic steroid patient's risk math: >5mg prednisone-equivalent for >3-4 weeks anytime in the past year = suppression until proven otherwise"],"tx":["CRISIS: hydrocortisone 100mg IV bolus → 50mg q6h (or 200mg/24h infusion) + aggressive saline (often liters — they're salt-deplete) + dextrose for hypoglycemia + trigger treatment; improvement within hours is diagnostic-adjacent","No separate mineralocorticoid needed at stress-dose hydrocortisone (>50mg/day covers it); fludrocortisone joins at maintenance transition for primary AI","SICK-DAY/STRESS DOSING is the discharge curriculum: double-triple oral dosing for febrile illness, injectable hydrocortisone kit + training for vomiting, medical alert identification — the education IS the mortality prevention","Perioperative/procedural stress dosing scaled to surgical severity (minor: little/none beyond usual; major: 50-100mg then taper) — write the plan for the chart, don't leave it to the anesthesia morning-of scramble","Maintenance: hydrocortisone 15-25mg/day split (largest dose on waking) + fludrocortisone (primary); taper strategies for iatrogenic suppression run through endocrinology patience"],"pearls":["Hypotension + hyponatremia + hyperkalemia is Addison's spelling its own name — the cosyntropin test is the confirmation, not the discovery","The 'adrenal-insufficient' patient is on the med list: prednisone courses, steroid injections, high-dose inhaled fluticasone — count them all before the workup","Treat the suspected crisis first and test around it — hydrocortisone given to someone who didn't need it costs nothing; withheld from someone who did costs everything"],"refs":[{"t":"Endocrine Society Adrenal Insufficiency Guideline","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Addison Disease","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/addisondisease.html"}]},{"id":"im-osteo","name":"Osteomyelitis & Diabetic Foot Infection","sec":"im","present":["Routes: contiguous (the diabetic foot ulcer — the ward's dominant version), hematogenous (vertebral — the back pain + fever + bacteremia triad), post-traumatic/hardware-associated","DIABETIC FOOT: ulcer depth/duration, probe-to-bone, peripheral pulses, neuropathy — infection severity (IDSA mild/moderate/severe) and vascular status decide everything","VERTEBRAL osteomyelitis/discitis: back pain + fever ± bacteremia (S. aureus classically) — epidural abscess screening (new radicular pain, weakness, sphincter symptoms) is the daily exam"],"dx":["PROBE-TO-BONE + ESR/CRP + plain films first (films lag 2 weeks); MRI is the imaging standard (osteo + abscess + extent); nuclear/CT when MRI blocked","THE MICROBIOLOGIC PRINCIPLE: bone/deep cultures BEFORE antibiotics whenever safely possible (image-guided or operative biopsy — swabs of ulcer surfaces grow colonizers, not answers); blood cultures ×2 (a positive spares the biopsy in vertebral disease)","Hold antibiotics in the STABLE patient until cultures obtained (vertebral osteo without sepsis: waiting for biopsy is correct, not negligent)","Vascular assessment on every diabetic foot (ABIs/toe pressures) — infection control without perfusion fails; Charcot arthropathy is the great mimic (warm swollen foot, NO ulcer/systemic signs — MRI distinguishes; treating Charcot as infection with months of antibiotics is a recurring error)"],"tx":["Empiric only when systemic illness demands: vancomycin + gram-negative coverage sized to severity/chronicity; then CULTURE-DIRECTED narrowing (the whole point of the biopsy)","Duration modernized: 6 weeks for most vertebral osteo (not 8-12 by reflex); diabetic foot osteo post-resection of infected bone: days-2 weeks (clean margins) vs 6 weeks (residual infected bone); OVIVA changed the game — ORAL therapy with high-bioavailability agents non-inferior to prolonged IV for many (the PICC is not the treatment; the drug is)","SOURCE CONTROL PARTNERSHIPS: podiatry/surgery debridement (infected bone removed beats infected bone bathed), revascularization when ischemic (vascular consult BEFORE the amputation conversation), epidural abscess with deficits → emergent spine surgery","Offloading is treatment (total contact casting for plantar ulcers), glycemic control, wound care team","The foot saved this admission needs the SYSTEM: podiatry follow-up, therapeutic footwear, daily self-exams taught, the contralateral foot examined — see the amputation entry's final pearl"],"pearls":["Culture the bone, not the ulcer — the swab that grew four organisms directed six weeks of wrong antibiotics","New neuro findings in vertebral osteo convert a medicine admission into a surgical emergency — the daily strength exam is the monitoring plan","OVIVA freed patients from six-week PICCs; the reflexive IV course despite susceptible oral options is habit, not evidence"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Diabetic Foot & Vertebral Osteomyelitis Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"OVIVA Trial (NEJM 2019)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"}]},{"id":"im-endocarditis","name":"Infective Endocarditis","sec":"im","present":["Suspect in: bacteremia with typical organisms (S. aureus, viridans strep, enterococcus), fever + new murmur/embolic phenomena, persistent fever in the injection-drug-use/prosthetic-valve/device patient","Embolic calling cards: stroke, splenic/renal infarcts, janeway lesions, splinter hemorrhages, glomerulonephritis — the exam findings worth actually examining for","Right-sided disease (IDU): septic pulmonary emboli (the 'multifocal pneumonia' with cavitation)"],"dx":["Modified Duke criteria organize; blood cultures ×3 sets BEFORE antibiotics from separate sites (the culture-negative endocarditis you created with early ceftriaxone haunts the whole admission)","Echo pathway: TTE first → TEE for prosthetic valves, high suspicion with negative TTE, intracardiac device, or abscess concern (new AV block on the DAILY ECG = aortic root abscess until excluded)","S. AUREUS BACTEREMIA has its own mandatory bundle (see sepsis entry) — endocarditis evaluation is part of it","Culture-negative workup (prior antibiotics dominate; then HACEK-era serologies: Coxiella, Bartonella, Brucella); PET-CT aids prosthetic/device diagnosis"],"tx":["Prolonged bactericidal therapy pathogen-directed (4-6 weeks typical, counted from first negative culture); ID consultation standard (associated with mortality benefit)","THE SURGERY QUESTION asked early and repeatedly — indications: heart failure from valve destruction (the strongest), uncontrolled infection (abscess, persistent cultures >5-7d), large vegetation + embolization risk (>10mm with emboli); EARLY surgery in appropriate patients beats deferred (EASE trial logic) — the endocarditis team huddle (cardiology + CT surgery + ID) is the structure","Anticoagulation nuance: do NOT anticoagulate for endocarditis itself; ongoing indications individualized (mechanical valve continues cautiously; hold after embolic stroke per neuro-timing)","Partial oral transition (POET): stabilized left-sided disease can complete therapy orally in selected patients — changing the 6-week-PICC default","Device infections: extraction, not salvage, for most; DENTAL evaluation before discharge (the source and the prevention); prophylaxis counseling for the highest-risk cardiac lesions per AHA"],"pearls":["The daily ECG in aortic valve endocarditis is abscess surveillance — the PR interval that grew 60ms overnight is a surgical consult, not an interesting finding","Cultures before antibiotics is the whole diagnostic architecture: three sets drawn 'after the first dose' cost the organism's name for six weeks","Surgery delayed until 'the antibiotics finish' in a patient with valve destruction and HF misunderstands the disease — the indication is now, not at week 6"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ESC Infective Endocarditis Guidelines","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"POET Trial (NEJM 2019)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"}]},{"id":"im-meningitis","name":"Meningitis & Encephalitis","sec":"im","present":["Bacterial meningitis: fever + headache + neck stiffness + AMS (the full tetrad is minority; two of four is most) — hours matter","Encephalitis distinguished by BRAIN dysfunction: altered behavior/personality, focal deficits, seizures — HSV until proven otherwise","The immunocompromised/elderly present atypically (Listeria coverage logic); the healthcare-associated version (post-neurosurgery, shunts) has different flora"],"dx":["THE SEQUENCE THAT SAVES LIVES: cultures → dexamethasone + empiric antibiotics → THEN CT-if-indicated → LP; antibiotics are NEVER delayed for imaging or LP logistics (CSF cultures sterilize in hours, but CSF cell counts/chemistry stay informative — treat first, tap fast after)","CT before LP only for: immunocompromise, new seizure, focal deficit, papilledema, significantly altered consciousness","CSF interpretation: bacterial (neutrophilic pleocytosis, glucose low, protein high), viral (lymphocytic, normal glucose), HSV PCR on everyone with encephalitis features; multiplex panels accelerate; opening pressure documented (cryptococcus management hinges on it)","AUTOIMMUNE encephalitis in the subacute psychiatric-neurologic syndrome (anti-NMDA — young patients, psychiatric onset, movement disorder, autonomic instability): antibody panels + the ovarian teratoma hunt; new refractory status or limbic phenotype → immunology thinking early"],"tx":["Empiric bacterial: vancomycin + ceftriaxone (meningitic dosing: 2g q12h) + AMPICILLIN if >50y/immunocompromised/pregnant (Listeria); post-neurosurgical: vancomycin + cefepime/meropenem","DEXAMETHASONE 10mg q6h ×4d with/before the first antibiotic dose (pneumococcal mortality/hearing benefit) — given first, questioned later; discontinue if non-pneumococcal","ACYCLOVIR 10mg/kg q8h for ANY encephalitis suspicion at presentation (renal-dosed, pre-hydrated) — the days-late acyclovir is the disability; treat empirically while PCR pends (and repeat the PCR at 72h if early tap was negative and suspicion persists)","Narrow by CSF/culture results; durations by organism (pneumococcus 10-14d, meningococcus 7, Listeria 21+); droplet precautions + close-contact prophylaxis for meningococcus (the public-health phone call is part of the treatment)","Autoimmune confirmed/probable: steroids/IVIG/PLEX escalation with neurology; tumor removal when found"],"pearls":["No patient ever died from early antibiotics and dexamethasone; patients die every year from 'waiting for the scan before the LP before the drugs'","Encephalitis behavior + a negative early HSV PCR still gets acyclovir — the first-48-hour false negative is textbook","The young woman with new psychosis, seizures, and autonomic storms has anti-NMDA encephalitis until the antibody returns — psychiatry and neurology share this patient"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Meningitis/Encephalitis Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Meningitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/meningitis.html"}]},{"id":"im-hiv","name":"HIV & Opportunistic Infections","sec":"im","present":["Two inpatient archetypes: the established patient admitted with anything (ART continuity + interaction sweep is YOUR job), and the new/advanced presentation (the OI unmasking undiagnosed AIDS)","The CD4 count is the differential's index: <200 (PJP, toxo risk begins), <100 (toxoplasma encephalitis, cryptococcus), <50 (MAC, CMV retinitis)","PJP phenotype: subacute dyspnea + dry cough + hypoxia out of proportion (the walk test desaturation), LDH up, bilateral infiltrates"],"dx":["TEST UNIVERSALLY: every admission is an HIV-testing opportunity (opt-out per CDC) — the advanced presentations were admitted 'for something else' three times before someone sent the test","New diagnosis inpatient panel: CD4, viral load, genotype, coinfection screen (HBV — changes ART, HCV, syphilis, TB testing), toxoplasma IgG, cryptococcal antigen if CD4 <100 (serum CrAg screening)","OI workups: PJP (induced sputum/BAL for PCP PCR/stains; beta-D-glucan supports), cryptococcal meningitis (serum/CSF CrAg, OPENING PRESSURE — the management variable), CNS lesions (toxo IgG + imaging pattern → empiric toxo treatment with response assessment vs lymphoma workup)","Admitted on ART: exact regimen continued (interruptions breed resistance) + interaction sweep (rifampin, PPIs with rilpivirine, polyvalent cations with integrase inhibitors — the antacid that dropped the dolutegravir level)"],"tx":["PJP: TMP-SMX high-dose ×21d; PREDNISONE when PaO2 <70/A-a >35 (started with antibiotics, not after deterioration); alternatives for sulfa allergy per severity","Cryptococcal meningitis: liposomal amphotericin + flucytosine induction → fluconazole; SERIAL LPs to control pressure (repeat until <20-25 or symptoms controlled — pressure management is mortality management)","ART TIMING: start within ~2 weeks of most OI treatment (earlier is better for most — but DELAY 4-6 weeks in cryptococcal and TB meningitis: IRIS in a closed box kills); never stop ART for the admission without an ID conversation","IRIS recognized not panicked over: paradoxical worsening weeks after ART start = usually treat through + steroids for severe","THE LINKAGE IS THE OUTCOME: new diagnoses leave with ART started or a fast-track ID appointment (days, not months), OI prophylaxis by CD4 (TMP-SMX <200; azithromycin-MAC prophylaxis largely retired in the ART era), vaccination plan, partner-services conversation handled with dignity"],"pearls":["The 'community-acquired pneumonia' that desaturates walking to the bathroom with an LDH of 600 is PJP asking for an HIV test","In cryptococcal meningitis the LP is therapy — the pressure you didn't recheck is the blindness/death you didn't prevent","Every missed HIV test in a compatible admission is a stage-3 diagnosis deferred — opt-out testing is the standard because clinician suspicion demonstrably isn't sensitive enough"],"refs":[{"t":"NIH/CDC/IDSA OI Guidelines","u":"https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov"},{"t":"CDC HIV Testing Recommendations","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv"}]},{"id":"im-tb","name":"Tuberculosis Evaluation","sec":"im","present":["The inpatient TB question is usually: 'could this be TB, and does this patient need airborne isolation?' — the epidemiology (birth country, incarceration, homelessness, immunosuppression, known contact) + syndrome (subacute cough, fevers, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis) answers it","Reactivation pattern: upper-lobe/cavitary disease; but immunosuppressed and elderly present atypically (lower lobes, miliary, extrapulmonary)","Extrapulmonary reminders: lymphadenitis, pleural effusion (lymphocytic exudate), vertebral (Pott's), GU, peritoneal, meningeal — TB belongs on more differentials than it appears on"],"dx":["ISOLATE FIRST, sort later: airborne isolation + N95s on suspicion — the exposure investigation for the un-isolated smear-positive patient costs more than every precaution combined","Respiratory sampling: sputum ×2-3 (including early morning) for AFB smear + NAAT (GeneXpert — rapid, detects rifampin resistance) + culture (the gold standard that also enables full susceptibilities); induced sputum/bronchoscopy for the non-producer","IGRA/TST test for INFECTION not disease — a negative doesn't exclude active TB (anergy in the sick), a positive doesn't confirm it; LTBI screening logic is different from active-case evaluation","Extrapulmonary sampling: pleural fluid ADA + biopsy (fluid AFB yield is poor), lymph node excision/FNA, CSF studies for meningitis (high protein, low glucose, lymphocytic)","HIV testing on every TB evaluation (the syndemic); baseline LFTs/vision assessment before therapy"],"tx":["Active disease: RIPE (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) + pyridoxine ×2 months → INH/rifampin continuation (4 more months standard) — initiated WITH public health notification (mandatory reporting; DOT programs are the adherence infrastructure)","Drug-resistance signals (prior treatment, high-burden-country exposure, contact with resistant case, Xpert rifampin flag) → expert consultation before regimen commitment","Monitoring built in: LFTs (hepatotoxicity triad — INH/RIF/PZA), visual acuity/color (ethambutol), the drug-interaction sweep (rifampin guts DOACs, ART, azoles, warfarin)","TB meningitis/pericarditis: add steroids; ART timing per HIV entry","LTBI treated properly when found: short regimens (3HP weekly INH-rifapentine, 4R daily rifampin) beat 9 months of INH on completion rates — the LTBI treated today is the reactivation admission prevented"],"pearls":["The homeless patient with a cavitary upper lobe and three weeks of cough sat in a shared room for two days — isolation on suspicion is cheap; the exposure workup is not","A negative IGRA in a sick patient excludes nothing — active TB is diagnosed by finding the organism, not by immunologic memory","Rifampin is the interaction bomb in the med list: the therapeutic INR, the suppressed viral load, and the DOAC all detonate quietly — sweep every drug on day one"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC TB Guidelines","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/tb"},{"t":"WHO TB Resources","u":"https://www.who.int/health-topics/tuberculosis"}]},{"id":"im-fuo","name":"Fever of Unknown Origin","sec":"im","present":["Classic definition modernized: fever ≥38.3 on multiple occasions, >3 weeks, undiagnosed after appropriate initial workup — the inpatient version includes the 'fever without a source' consult on hospital day 5","The big three buckets, roughly equal thirds: INFECTION (occult abscess, endocarditis, TB, osteomyelitis), MALIGNANCY (lymphoma above all — with its B-symptom cousins), CONNECTIVE TISSUE DISEASE (Still's disease, giant cell arteritis in the elderly, vasculitis) — plus drug fever and factitious in the remainder","The elderly FUO leans GCA/malignancy; the returned traveler, the injection drug user, and the immunocompromised each carry their own list"],"dx":["THE HISTORY RE-TAKEN IS THE HIGHEST-YIELD TEST: exposures (animals, travel, TB contacts, tick habitat), drugs (including supplements — drug fever has no distinguishing pattern), family history (periodic fevers), the review of systems done slowly","Verify the fever (pattern documentation; factitious excluded by observed measurement when suspicion exists); STOP nonessential drugs as a diagnostic act (drug fever resolves in 48-96h)","Structured tiers instead of shotgun: repeat cultures held longer + serologies by exposure, ESR/CRP/ferritin (sky-high ferritin → Still's/HLH thinking), SPEP, HIV, IGRA, echo when endocarditis plausible → then CT chest/abdomen/pelvis → then FDG-PET/CT (the modern FUO workhorse — localizes the biopsy target in a majority of persistent cases)","BIOPSY what's abnormal (node excision > FNA for lymphoma architecture; temporal artery in the >50 with compatible symptoms — treat GCA empirically while arranging it; marrow when cytopenias/HLH features)","HLH on the radar for the febrile-cytopenic-ferritin-5000 patient (HScore) — it changes tempo entirely"],"tx":["THE DISCIPLINE: no empiric antibiotics in the stable FUO patient — undirected antibiotics obscure cultures, breed C. diff, and delay the real answer; treat empirically only for neutropenia, hemodynamic instability, or suspected GCA (steroids to save vision) and culture-negative endocarditis pathways","Treat what's found — FUO management is diagnosis; the therapeutics belong to the answer","NAPROXEN test and steroid trials are last resorts with informed reasoning, not week-one moves (steroids in undiagnosed lymphoma melt the biopsy target and cost the diagnosis)","Serial reassessment beats serial escalation: many FUOs declare themselves with time + repeat exam (the new murmur on day 6, the node that grew)","The undiagnosed-but-well patient can be worked up as an outpatient — prolonged admission for a stable mystery adds risk without tempo"],"pearls":["The steroid trial that made the fever vanish also made the lymphoma unstageable — biopsy before empiric immunosuppression, always","In the febrile patient over 60 with a high ESR, headaches, or jaw claudication: prednisone today, temporal artery biopsy this week — vision doesn't wait for pathology","Half of solved FUOs are solved by a finding that was available in week one and looked at properly in week three — re-examine, re-take the history, re-read the films"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA/ACP FUO Reviews","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Fever","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html"}]},{"id":"im-gout","name":"Gout & Crystal Arthropathies (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The hospital is a gout machine: diuretics, volume shifts, surgery, held home medications — the post-op day 2 hot first MTP or knee is the archetype","Polyarticular/febrile gout mimics sepsis convincingly (fevers, leukocytosis) — and coexists with it rarely but consequentially (see EM septic-joint entry: the tap adjudicates)","CPPD (pseudogout) prefers the hospitalized elderly knee/wrist — same tap, different crystals, similar management"],"dx":["Arthrocentesis remains the standard: crystals + cell count + culture (the inpatient hot joint earns a needle — 'his usual gout' has covered for septic arthritis too many times)","Uric acid during the flare misleads (normal in a third) — check it 2+ weeks later to guide chronic therapy; chondrocalcinosis on films supports CPPD","New CPPD without obvious cause: check calcium/PTH, iron studies, magnesium (the secondary-cause screen)","The med-list archaeology: which held/started drug precipitated this (stopped allopurinol on admission? started thiazide?)"],"tx":["Inpatient flare toolkit chosen by comorbidity, not habit: steroids (prednisone 30-40mg ×5d, or intra-articular after infection excluded — often the cleanest inpatient choice), colchicine low-dose (1.2 then 0.6 at 1h; renal/interaction caution — the clarithromycin+colchicine combination is toxic), NSAIDs (the option usually eliminated by the CKD/anticoagulation/GI context that put them in the hospital)","DO NOT stop allopurinol during flares (the classic error — continuing is guideline-directed); DO NOT start urate-lowering therapy mid-crisis without coverage, but DO arrange it: the recurrent-gout patient leaves with a urate-lowering plan (allopurinol starting low with flare prophylaxis, titrated to urate <6) and a named titrator","Anti-inflammatory prophylaxis (colchicine 0.6 daily or low-dose steroid) bridges ULT initiation ×3-6 months","CPPD flares: same anti-inflammatory menu; chronic recurrent → colchicine prophylaxis + treat the underlying metabolic driver","The diuretic conversation: if gout recurs on chronic thiazide/loop, revisit necessity/alternatives with the outpatient prescriber (documented, not just mused)"],"pearls":["The allopurinol held 'because of the flare' converts a 5-day problem into a 5-week one — continue it, treat the flare around it","Steroids treat gout in the renal-failure anticoagulated patient that NSAIDs and full-dose colchicine cannot touch — the comorbidity list picks the drug","A hot joint plus fever in the hospital is a tap, not a diagnosis-by-history — crystals and culture can both be true"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR Gout Guideline (2020)","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Gout","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/gout.html"}]},{"id":"im-ra","name":"Rheumatoid Arthritis & Inflammatory Arthritis (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The admitted RA patient is rarely admitted FOR RA — the inpatient craft is managing the disease's baggage: immunosuppression + infection, perioperative biologic timing, steroid stress questions, and the cervical spine warning","New inflammatory arthritis presenting inpatient: symmetric small-joint synovitis + morning stiffness >30-60min — early recognition and rheumatology referral change the erosion trajectory (the window is real)","The RA red-flag reflexes: C1-C2 instability (pre-intubation imaging in longstanding disease — the flexion that shifts the odontoid), septic arthritis risk (RA joints get infected MORE and hurt-signal LESS on immunosuppression)"],"dx":["New suspicion workup: RF + anti-CCP (specificity), ESR/CRP, hand/foot films (baseline erosions), viral arthritis excluded (parvovirus, hepatitis) — but referral doesn't wait for seropositivity (seronegative RA exists)","The sick RA patient's fever: infection FIRST (immunosuppression lowers the signal), flare second — joint tap for any disproportionate single joint","Drug-specific admission screens: methotrexate (CBC, LFTs, the accidental-daily-dosing catastrophe check — confirm WEEKLY dosing on every med rec), biologics (TB/HBV reactivation history)","Extra-articular disease on the differential when RA patients decompensate: ILD (the dyspneic RA patient), pericarditis, vasculitis, Felty's"],"tx":["PERIOPERATIVE/SEPSIS BIOLOGIC RULES: hold biologics through serious infection (restart when treated/recovered); elective surgery timed to the dosing-interval trough (ACR/AAHKS guidance) — methotrexate CONTINUES through most surgery (stopping it flares disease without reducing complications)","Steroid stress-dose thinking for the chronically-dosed (see adrenal entry); PJP prophylaxis conversations for the steroid+biologic combinations","Flares inpatient: verify it's a flare (tap the disproportionate joint), then bridge steroids at the lowest effective dose with rheumatology alignment — inpatient DMARD escalation belongs to the rheumatologist, inpatient steroid restraint belongs to everyone","MED REC IS PATIENT SAFETY here specifically: weekly methotrexate + folate documented unambiguously (daily-dosed methotrexate is a recurring fatal error), biologic schedules preserved, no live vaccines on immunosuppression","The new-arthritis discharge: rheumatology referral expedited (weeks matter), NSAIDs/low-dose steroid bridge per judgment, and the explicit words 'possible inflammatory arthritis — needs DMARD evaluation' in the summary"],"pearls":["Methotrexate is WEEKLY — the transcription error that makes it daily has killed patients; say the word 'weekly' out loud at med rec","The RA neck extended for intubation without imaging is a spinal cord bet — longstanding disease gets flexion-extension films or fiberoptic planning","An immunosuppressed RA patient's 'flare' with fever gets cultures and a tap before it gets more steroids"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR RA Guidelines & Perioperative Guidance","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Rheumatoid Arthritis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/rheumatoidarthritis.html"}]},{"id":"im-sle","name":"SLE & Connective Tissue Disease Flares","sec":"im","present":["The admitted lupus patient's central question: FLARE vs INFECTION vs DRUG EFFECT — often simultaneously, always consequentially (immunosuppression cuts both ways)","Organ-threatening flares that change tempo: lupus nephritis (new proteinuria/hematuria/rising creatinine), NPSLE (seizures, psychosis), hematologic (severe cytopenias), serositis, and the APS overlay (thrombosis in a lupus patient = antiphospholipid workup)","Pregnancy + SLE is co-managed high-risk territory (preeclampsia vs flare is its own algorithm)"],"dx":["Flare serology pattern: rising anti-dsDNA + FALLING complements (C3/C4) supports activity; ESR up with quiet CRP leans flare (CRP elevation leans infection/serositis) — patterns support, cultures decide","Every admission: urinalysis with sediment + urine protein:creatinine (the silent nephritis screen — lupus kidneys whisper); cytopenias worked up (flare vs drug vs MAHA — the lupus patient with schistocytes triggers the TTP pathway)","Infection mimicry respected: the immunosuppressed lupus patient's fever gets cultured like a neutropenic's; PJP/opportunistic thinking scales with the regimen","APS antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, beta-2-glycoprotein) with any thrombosis/pregnancy-loss history — it changes anticoagulation choice (warfarin, NOT DOACs, for high-risk APS)","New nephritis-range findings → nephrology + biopsy planning (class drives therapy)"],"tx":["HYDROXYCHLOROQUINE continues through nearly everything (it's the survival drug of lupus — stopped only for true toxicity); verify it's on the list and the retinopathy screening is current","Flare treatment scaled to organ threat with rheumatology: mild-moderate (NSAIDs cautiously, low-moderate steroids), organ-threatening (pulse methylprednisolone + induction immunosuppression — mycophenolate/cyclophosphamide for nephritis, belimumab/voclosporin era options per specialist)","Steroids at the lowest dose that controls — the damage index accumulates from disease AND from prednisone; the taper plan is written before the pulse is given","The prevention housekeeping while admitted: PJP prophylaxis on heavy regimens, vaccination status (no live vaccines), bone protection on chronic steroids, CV risk addressed (premature atherosclerosis is a lupus killer — statins per risk)","Pregnancy-capable patients: teratogen sweep (mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, warfarin), contraception/planning conversation documented, aspirin for preeclampsia prevention in pregnancy"],"pearls":["Falling complements with rising dsDNA is the flare fingerprint — but the febrile lupus patient still gets cultures before the steroid pulse","Hydroxychloroquine held at admission and lost at discharge is the flare and the mortality signal of next year — protect it like an anticoagulant","In lupus, CRP that's truly high argues infection or serositis — the discordant ESR/CRP pair is a diagnostic instrument, not a lab quirk"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR/EULAR SLE & Nephritis Guidelines","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Lupus","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/lupus.html"}]},{"id":"im-onc-emergencies","name":"Oncologic Emergencies","sec":"im","present":["The five the medicine service must own: NEUTROPENIC FEVER, cord compression, SVC syndrome, tumor lysis, hypercalcemia of malignancy — each with an hours-matter window","Neutropenic fever: single temp ≥38.3 (or ≥38 sustained) + ANC <500 (or expected drop) — a sepsis syndrome until proven otherwise; the exam may be silent (no pus without neutrophils)","Cord compression: back pain in a cancer patient IS the early symptom (precedes weakness by weeks) — the pain-only stage is the salvageable stage"],"dx":["Neutropenic fever: cultures (peripheral + every lumen), targeted exam (mouth, perineum — NO routine rectal exam, skin, lines), CXR; MASCC/CISNE scores identify the low-risk outpatient-eligible minority","Cord compression: whole-spine MRI (multifocal disease in a third) EMERGENTLY for the cancer patient with new back pain + any neuro symptom — and urgently for pain alone with high-risk histology","Tumor lysis (spontaneous or treatment-triggered — high-burden leukemias/lymphomas): K/phos/uric acid/Ca + creatinine q6-8h in the at-risk window; Cairo-Bishop framework","Hypercalcemia: albumin-corrected/ionized, PTH (suppressed in malignancy), PTHrP vs bony mets vs calcitriol-mediated (lymphoma) taxonomy","SVC syndrome: face/arm swelling, distended chest veins — CT venography; tissue diagnosis BEFORE radiation when airway/stability allows (steroids/RT can melt the lymphoma biopsy)"],"tx":["NEUTROPENIC FEVER: anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam (cefepime/pip-tazo/meropenem) WITHIN THE HOUR — vancomycin added only for specific indications (line infection, skin/soft tissue, pneumonia, instability); de-escalate/stop per recovery and source; antifungals join at 4-7 days of persistent fever","CORD COMPRESSION: dexamethasone 10mg IV then 4mg q6h ON SUSPICION (before the MRI finishes) + emergent radiation oncology AND spine surgery consults (surgery-then-radiation beats radiation alone for selected patients — Patchell) — ambulatory status at treatment predicts ambulatory status forever","TUMOR LYSIS: aggressive fluids, rasburicase for high-risk/established (allopurinol for prophylaxis of moderate risk), electrolyte management per protocols, renal replacement early when refractory — prevention protocols BEFORE the chemo, not after the potassium","HYPERCALCEMIA: saline expansion first, calcitonin (fast, tachyphylaxis in 48h) + IV bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid — slower, durable); denosumab for bisphosphonate-refractory/renal failure; treat the cancer or the calcium returns","SVC: elevate head, steroids for lymphoma/airway compromise, stenting for severe symptoms, histology-directed therapy — the emergency is the airway, the treatment is the diagnosis"],"pearls":["The neutropenic patient looks fine until they arrest — the hour-one beta-lactam is the entire margin","Back pain in myeloma/prostate/breast/lung cancer is cord compression until MRI proves otherwise — the dexamethasone given on suspicion costs little; the paralysis of waiting costs everything","Rasburicase before high-burden chemo is cheaper than dialysis after it — risk-stratify every induction"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA/ASCO Neutropenic Fever Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"NCCN Supportive Care Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"im-malignancy-workup","name":"New Malignancy Workup (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The admission that changes a life: the mass, the adenopathy, the unexplained weight loss, the incidental finding — medicine's job is efficient tissue-directed evaluation + honest communication + tight linkage","The inpatient question is TEMPO: who needs the workup completed in-house (unstable, rapidly progressive, oncologic-emergency-adjacent, or unable to navigate outpatient) vs expedited outpatient pathways","B-symptom syndromes (fevers, drenching sweats, >10% weight loss) + bulky adenopathy = lymphoma tempo — days matter"],"dx":["TISSUE IS THE ISSUE, and the RIGHT tissue: biopsy the most accessible lesion that will give the highest-stage answer (the liver lesion outranks the lung primary for staging); EXCISIONAL/core biopsy for suspected lymphoma (FNA cytology cannot read architecture — the inadequate FNA is a week lost)","Image for staging before biopsy when it changes the target (CT chest/abd/pelvis; brain when symptoms/histology dictate)","Tumor markers used honestly: NOT screening tools — supportive/monitoring roles (PSA, CA-125 in a pelvic mass context, AFP/beta-hCG in the young man with a mediastinal/testicular mass — the curable one never missed, and testicular ultrasound for any retroperitoneal mystery mass in a young man)","Unknown primary logic: immunohistochemistry directs; treatable subsets hunted (germ cell, lymphoma, hormone-responsive)","BEFORE treatment starts elsewhere: fertility preservation flagged for the young, hepatitis B screening (reactivation under chemo/rituximab), baseline organ function"],"tx":["Steroids WITHHELD until tissue is secured in suspected lymphoma/leukemia (the pre-biopsy dexamethasone that melted the node and delayed the diagnosis is a recurring tragedy — see FUO entry) — exceptions only for airway/cord emergencies with oncology at the bedside","The communication IS a procedure: the word 'cancer' said clearly when established, prognosis uncertainty handled honestly ('we need the pathology before anyone can tell you what this means'), SPIKES-style structure, family present per patient preference — and NEVER a pathology result delivered by a covering intern reading a chart cold","Multidisciplinary linkage before discharge: oncology appointment MADE (named, dated — not 'referral placed'), pathology follow-up owner assigned, port/staging logistics mapped","Symptom management doesn't wait for staging: pain control, antiemetics, nutrition, VTE risk (malignancy is prothrombotic — prophylaxis inpatient, risk-stratified thinking outpatient)","PALLIATIVE CARE INTRODUCED EARLY as an extra layer (not a surrender): concurrent palliative care improves quality of life AND survival in advanced disease (Temel) — normalize it at diagnosis"],"pearls":["The FNA that returned 'atypical cells, recommend excisional biopsy' was a predictable week's delay — suspected lymphoma gets the surgical biopsy first","A new cancer diagnosis without a dated oncology appointment is a patient lost to the system at their most vulnerable — make the call, write the date","Early palliative care is the intervention that improved both quality AND quantity of life — offering it late because 'they're not there yet' misreads the evidence"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Guidelines by Site","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Temel et al., Early Palliative Care (NEJM 2010)","u":"https://www.nejm.org"}]},{"id":"im-pleural","name":"Pleural Effusion & Thoracentesis","sec":"im","present":["The effusion's question is always WHY: transudate (the failing pump/liver/kidney — treat the disease) vs exudate (the pleura itself is diseased — infection, malignancy, PE, autoimmune)","Presentation: dyspnea, pleuritic pain, cough; the massive effusion with mediastinal shift and the bilateral small effusions of volume overload are different consults","Parapneumonic timing is destiny: free-flowing → loculating → organized (the effusion that waits becomes the empyema that needs surgery)"],"dx":["TAP THE UNEXPLAINED: new unilateral effusion without an obvious transudative context gets a diagnostic thoracentesis (ultrasound-guided as standard — marks, depth, and fewer pneumothoraces)","LIGHT'S CRITERIA (exudate if any: fluid/serum protein >0.5, fluid/serum LDH >0.6, fluid LDH >2/3 serum ULN) — sensitive for exudate, misclassifies some diuresed heart failure (serum-fluid albumin gradient >1.2 reclaims them)","The fluid panel by suspicion: cell count/diff, pH (parapneumonic triage: <7.2 = drain), glucose (low: empyema, malignancy, RA), culture, cytology (×2 taps ~60-70% yield in malignancy — pleuroscopy when cytology fails and suspicion persists), ADA (TB), triglycerides (chylothorax)","Bilateral effusions in classic heart failure: treat first, tap only the atypical (fever, pleurisy, unilateral disproportion, failure to resolve)","CT with contrast for the complex/loculated/suspicious"],"tx":["Therapeutic thoracentesis for dyspnea relief: drain to symptom relief; large-volume drainage monitored (chest discomfort/cough → stop; re-expansion pulmonary edema is rare but real — the routine 1.5L ceiling is tradition-plus-prudence, pleural manometry where practiced)","PARAPNEUMONIC ALGORITHM: pH <7.2, glucose <60, positive gram stain/culture, loculation, or frank pus → chest tube (small-bore acceptable) + intrapleural tPA/DNase (MIST2) for incomplete drainage → VATS decortication for failure — escalation measured in days, not weeks","MALIGNANT effusion (prognosis-honest management): recurrent symptomatic → indwelling pleural catheter (outpatient autonomy, high auto-pleurodesis rates) or talc pleurodesis per patient priorities (TIME2/AMPLE inform the choice) — repeated taps alone for the short-prognosis patient only","Chylothorax/hepatic hydrothorax/TB pleuritis: cause-specific pathways (the hepatic hydrothorax is a TIPS/transplant conversation, NOT a chest tube — the tube that never stops draining)","Post-procedure: routine CXR only for symptoms/air aspiration/multiple passes; document fluid appearance + studies sent"],"pearls":["The parapneumonic effusion re-imaged 'in a few days' organized on schedule — pH and drainage decisions belong to the first 24-48 hours","A chest tube in hepatic hydrothorax is a protein-losing, never-healing mistake — recognize the cirrhotic effusion before the pigtail goes in","Ultrasound turned thoracentesis from a landmark gamble into a guided procedure — no marks, no tap"],"refs":[{"t":"BTS Pleural Disease Guideline (2023)","u":"https://www.brit-thoracic.org.uk"},{"t":"ATS Malignant Effusion Guidelines","u":"https://www.thoracic.org"}]},{"id":"im-ild","name":"Interstitial Lung Disease & Hypoxemia Workup","sec":"im","present":["The inpatient ILD encounters: the new diagnosis (subacute dyspnea + dry cough + crackles + fibrotic imaging), the ACUTE EXACERBATION of known fibrosis (rapid worsening, new ground-glass — high mortality), and the 'hypoxemia out of proportion' consult","Exam signatures: velcro-like bibasilar crackles, clubbing (fibrotic phenotypes); the connective-tissue review of systems is mandatory (Raynaud's, sclerodactyly, arthritis, myositis symptoms — the hands tell half the story)","Drug/exposure archaeology: amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, bleomycin, checkpoint inhibitors (pneumonitis era), birds/mold/hot tubs (hypersensitivity), occupational dusts"],"dx":["HRCT is the diagnostic centerpiece: UIP pattern (basal/peripheral reticulation + honeycombing) vs NSIP (ground-glass predominant) vs organizing/hypersensitivity patterns — the pattern + context often IS the diagnosis (definite UIP + right story = IPF without biopsy)","Serologic screen on every new ILD: ANA, RF/CCP, myositis panel when suspected (anti-synthetase syndrome hides as 'idiopathic' ILD + mechanic's hands + arthritis), hypersensitivity precipitins by exposure","The hypoxemia framework applied honestly: A-a gradient, response to O2 (shunt doesn't correct), orthodeoxia (hepatopulmonary), the PE always considered (CTA vs V/Q per context) — not every infiltrate + hypoxemia is 'multifocal pneumonia'","ACUTE EXACERBATION workup = exclusion: infection (BAL when tolerated), PE, heart failure, aspiration — the 'triggered vs idiopathic' sorting","MULTIDISCIPLINARY DISCUSSION (pulm + radiology + path) is the diagnostic standard — the inpatient job is assembling the data, not forcing the label; biopsy (cryobiopsy/surgical) reserved for management-changing uncertainty in fit patients"],"tx":["Acute exacerbation of IPF: supportive core (oxygen, treat the treatable trigger) + empiric antibiotics commonly + steroids by convention (evidence thin) — honest prognosis framing (in-hospital mortality high) and early goals-of-care/palliative involvement; mechanical ventilation in progressive fibrosis rarely changes the destination (the pre-intubation conversation belongs BEFORE the crash)","Disease-directed paths set up from the ward: IPF → antifibrotics (nintedanib/pirfenidone — slow FVC decline; ILD-clinic initiation), CTD-ILD/inflammatory phenotypes → immunosuppression with rheum/pulm, hypersensitivity → EXPOSURE REMOVAL (the treatment that's a conversation)","The universal ILD bundle before discharge: ambulatory O2 assessment (walk test — the resting sat lies), pulmonary rehab referral, vaccination update, antireflux/aspiration attention, ILD-clinic linkage (named, dated), TRANSPLANT referral early for progressive fibrosis (referred late = referred never), smoking cessation","Checkpoint-inhibitor pneumonitis: grade-based steroids + oncology co-management (hold vs discontinue immunotherapy decisions)","Avoid the reflex: long steroid courses for undifferentiated fibrosis without a diagnosis — phenotype first (steroids treat inflammation, not established fibrosis, and definite-UIP IPF was HARMED by triple therapy in PANTHER)"],"pearls":["The 'recurrent pneumonia' with peripheral basal reticulation on the third CT was ILD on the first one — pattern recognition on imaging is the referral trigger","Examine the hands of every unexplained-ILD patient: mechanic's hands, Raynaud's, and sclerodactyly convert 'idiopathic' into treatable CTD-ILD","Prednisone for definite-UIP IPF is not neutral — PANTHER taught that the reflexive steroid harms; get the phenotype before the immunosuppression"],"refs":[{"t":"ATS/ERS ILD & IPF Guidelines","u":"https://www.thoracic.org"},{"t":"Pulmonary Fibrosis Foundation","u":"https://www.pulmonaryfibrosis.org"}]},{"id":"im-osa","name":"OSA & Obesity Hypoventilation (Inpatient)","sec":"im","present":["The inpatient reveal: witnessed apneas on the ward, refractory hypertension, unexplained polycythemia, post-op respiratory events, morning headaches — sleep apnea is diagnosed in hospitals mostly by accident and should be by design","OBESITY HYPOVENTILATION SYNDROME (OHS): BMI ≥30 + daytime hypercapnia (pCO2 >45) without another cause — the 'COPD' admission with clean spirometry, a bicarb of 34, and a BMI of 45; often presents DECOMPENSATED (acute-on-chronic hypercapnic failure + cor pulmonale)","The overlap patients (OSA+COPD, OSA+HF) carry compounded risk"],"dx":["Screen deliberately (STOP-BANG) in: refractory HTN, AF, HF, pre-op bariatric/major surgery, pulmonary hypertension workups, the unexplained hypersomnolent","The bicarbonate is the OHS breadcrumb: chronic elevation without diuretic/vomiting cause → check a gas; polycythemia similarly","Decompensated OHS: gas confirms hypercapnia; exclude contributors (sedatives — the med list did this to half of them, hypothyroidism, neuromuscular weakness); echo for the cor pulmonale burden","Formal sleep testing: inpatient sleep studies where available for the decompensated (empiric therapy shouldn't wait); otherwise EXPEDITED outpatient polysomnography with a date, not a suggestion"],"tx":["Decompensated hypercapnic OHS: BiPAP (not CPAP acutely) — settings titrated to ventilation (backup rate for the profound); avoid over-oxygenation (target 88-92%); diurese the cor pulmonale volume; this admission's survival intervention is positive pressure","SEDATIVE STEWARDSHIP: the OSA/OHS patient's opioid + benzodiazepine orders are respiratory events waiting — minimize, monitor (continuous oximetry/capnography post-op per risk), and flag the chart","Stable OSA inpatient: CONTINUE home CPAP in the hospital (bring the machine or match settings — the surgical patient whose CPAP stayed home is the rapid-response at 3am), restart before discharge if interrupted","The discharge that changes trajectory: PAP therapy arranged with an actual delivery/titration plan, adherence counseling (the first month predicts the first year), weight-management referral (including bariatric surgery conversation for OHS — the disease-modifying option), driving-sleepiness counseling documented","OHS long-term: PAP therapy reduces mortality; untreated OHS has heart-failure-level survival curves — treat it with that seriousness"],"pearls":["A bicarbonate of 33 in an obese patient is a blood gas waiting to be drawn — chronic hypercapnia announces itself in the BMP","The post-op OSA patient on a PCA without monitoring is the code blue the risk assessment predicted — screen before surgery, monitor after","'COPD exacerbation' with normal spirometry and a BMI of 48 is obesity hypoventilation wearing a borrowed diagnosis — the label change changes the treatment and the survival"],"refs":[{"t":"ATS OHS Guideline (2019)","u":"https://www.thoracic.org"},{"t":"AASM Resources","u":"https://aasm.org"}]},{"id":"im-alf","name":"Acute Liver Failure","sec":"im","present":["Definition: severe acute liver injury + COAGULOPATHY (INR ≥1.5) + ENCEPHALOPATHY, without pre-existing cirrhosis — a different disease from decompensated chronic liver disease, with different tempo and rules","Etiology drives both treatment and prognosis: acetaminophen (most common US — best transplant-free survival), viral, drug/DILI, autoimmune, Wilson's (young + hemolysis + low alk phos), Budd-Chiari, pregnancy syndromes (HELLP/AFLP), ischemic","The tempo is the danger: hyperacute cases progress from jaundice to cerebral edema in days — the floor patient who becomes confused is an ICU patient who is becoming a transplant patient"],"dx":["The etiologic panel STAT (see hepatitis entry) with acetaminophen level + honest ingestion history front and center; pregnancy test; Wilson's screen in the young","ENCEPHALOPATHY GRADE is the vital sign: grade I-II (confusion, asterixis) → ICU-level monitoring; grade III-IV (somnolence→coma) → intubation planning + cerebral edema precautions (the ALF brain swells — unlike cirrhotic HE)","Track: glucose (they drop — the failing liver stops making it; scheduled checks + dextrose infusions), lactate, phosphate (very low phosphate paradoxically accompanies regeneration in acetaminophen ALF), ammonia (in ALF, unlike cirrhosis, arterial ammonia >150-200 correlates with cerebral edema risk), INR (the prognostic instrument — which is why...)","...NO FFP for the number: correcting the INR without bleeding erases your prognostic marker and doesn't help — correct only for procedures/active bleeding","KING'S COLLEGE CRITERIA thinking (acetaminophen: pH <7.3 after resuscitation, or INR >6.5 + Cr >3.4 + grade III-IV; non-acetaminophen versions) = the transplant-listing conversation triggers"],"tx":["CALL THE TRANSPLANT CENTER EARLY — the defining management decision of ALF is geographic: transfer before grade III encephalopathy, before pressors, before the transfer becomes impossible; listing evaluation runs parallel to treatment, not after it","N-ACETYLCYSTEINE: immediately for acetaminophen (any suspicion, any timeline) AND for non-acetaminophen ALF (transplant-free survival benefit in early-grade encephalopathy) — the default drug of ALF","Cerebral edema precautions (grade III-IV): HOB 30°, quiet environment, avoid stimulation, hypertonic saline targeting Na 145-155 per protocol, intubate for airway protection (propofol-based — it helps ICP), treat surges (mannitol/hypertonic), temperature control","Etiology-specific: mushroom (silibinin/penicillin pathways), autoimmune (steroids), HSV (acyclovir empirically in the febrile/pregnant/immunosuppressed until excluded), Budd-Chiari (anticoagulation/TIPS/transplant), pregnancy syndromes (DELIVERY is the treatment), Wilson's fulminant (transplant — medical therapy doesn't rescue it)","Infection vigilance (surveillance cultures — ALF patients get septic quietly and it takes them off transplant lists), stress ulcer prophylaxis, early renal replacement for the failing kidneys (CRRT preferred — ICP stability)"],"pearls":["The most important procedure in ALF is a phone call — transfer windows close at the speed of encephalopathy grades","NAC is not just for acetaminophen anymore — the non-acetaminophen ALF patient in early encephalopathy gets it too","The FFP that 'fixed' the INR of 4 fixed nothing and blinded everyone — in ALF the coagulopathy is the prognostic dashboard; correct bleeding, not numbers"],"refs":[{"t":"AASLD Acute Liver Failure Guidance","u":"https://www.aasld.org"},{"t":"King's College Criteria (classic reference)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc"}]},{"id":"im-nephrotic","name":"Nephrotic & Nephritic Syndromes","sec":"im","present":["NEPHROTIC: proteinuria >3.5g/day + hypoalbuminemia + edema (± hyperlipidemia) — the frothy urine + anasarca admission; adult etiologies: diabetic nephropathy (commonest overall), membranous, FSGS, minimal change, amyloid/myeloma-related","NEPHRITIC: hematuria (dysmorphic RBCs/casts) + hypertension + AKI ± subnephrotic proteinuria — the inflamed glomerulus; the RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE version (RPGN: days-weeks creatinine doubling) is a nephrology emergency measured in nephron-days","The overlap and the systemic clues: recent infection (post-infectious GN), hemoptysis (pulmonary-renal syndromes — anti-GBM, ANCA vasculitis), rash/arthralgias (lupus, IgA vasculitis), hepatitis serologies"],"dx":["Quantify + characterize: urine protein:creatinine ratio, microscopy PERSONALLY REVIEWED (dysmorphic RBCs and casts are diagnoses the automated analyzer won't make), albumin, complements","The serologic sweep frames the biopsy: C3/C4 (low → lupus, post-infectious, MPGN/cryo), ANA/anti-dsDNA, ANCA (MPO/PR3), anti-GBM, hepatitis B/C, HIV, SPEP/free light chains (the >50 nephrotic patient is myeloma/amyloid until screened), PLA2R antibody (primary membranous — the serology that sometimes spares the biopsy)","KIDNEY BIOPSY is the definitive answer for most adult glomerular disease — expedited (same-admission) for RPGN/unexplained AKI with active sediment; diabetic patients with the classic trajectory (long-standing DM + retinopathy + slow proteinuria) often reasonably go unbiopsied, but atypical features (rapid change, active sediment, no retinopathy) buy the needle","RPGN suspicion = same-day nephrology + serologies STAT — anti-GBM and ANCA titers change treatment tonight"],"tx":["RPGN: pulse methylprednisolone on clinical suspicion with nephrology, then disease-directed induction (cyclophosphamide/rituximab for ANCA; plasma exchange + immunosuppression for anti-GBM, especially with hemorrhage or severe renal failure) — dialysis-dependence is decided by treatment speed","Nephrotic supportive care (every etiology): RAAS blockade for proteinuria, loop diuretics for edema (often high-dose/combination — gut edema impairs absorption; IV when severe), sodium restriction, statin for the lipids, and the COMPLICATION SURVEILLANCE: thrombosis risk (membranous especially — renal vein thrombosis/PE; prophylactic anticoagulation when albumin <2-2.5 per risk tools), infection risk (urinary immunoglobulin loss — pneumococcal vaccination), AKI vigilance","Disease-specific with nephrology: minimal change (steroids — the rapid responder), membranous (risk-stratified rituximab/cyclophosphamide era), FSGS (primary vs adaptive distinction matters), lupus nephritis (see SLE entry), diabetic (the modern stack — see CKD entry: SGLT2i + finerenone changed this disease)","Post-infectious GN: supportive + treat residual infection (most children recover; adults less reliably)","Edema mobilized at a sustainable pace with daily weights — the anasarca resolved in 48 hours is an AKI in 72"],"pearls":["Dysmorphic RBCs and red cell casts turn 'hematuria' into 'glomerulonephritis' — the microscope you look through yourself is a consult-grade instrument","RPGN loses nephrons by the day: the serologies sent tonight and the biopsy done tomorrow are the difference between CKD and dialysis","The nephrotic patient with new flank pain or unexplained dyspnea has clotted — membranous nephropathy anticoagulation thresholds exist because renal vein thrombosis doesn't announce itself politely"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO Glomerular Disease Guideline (2021)","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Glomerular Diseases","u":"https://medlineplus.gov/kidneydiseases.html"}]},{"id":"im-goc","name":"Goals of Care & End-of-Life Management","sec":"im","present":["The skill that defines internists: aligning medical care with what matters to the patient — done early and well it prevents suffering; done late and badly it becomes the 2am code discussion with strangers","The triggers for a serious-illness conversation TODAY: the 'surprise question' (would you be surprised if this patient died within a year? — no = talk now), recurrent admissions, advanced organ failure, metastatic disease, frailty spirals, ICU-eligibility questions","Code status is the LAST line of the conversation, not the first — goals first, then the treatments that serve them"],"dx":["Prognostication done honestly: disease trajectories differ (cancer's cliff vs organ-failure's sawtooth decline vs frailty's slow slope) — communicate ranges ('hours-to-days', 'weeks-to-months') not false precision","CAPACITY assessed for THIS decision (understand, appreciate, reason, express a choice) — and when absent: surrogate hierarchy per jurisdiction + SUBSTITUTED JUDGMENT framing ('what would SHE say if she could sit up and hear this?' — not 'what do you want for her'; the reframe lifts guilt off families)","Document what exists and find it: advance directives, POLST/MOLST, prior conversations in the chart — the beautifully documented conversation nobody reads is a conversation that didn't happen; put it where the next clinician looks","Symptom assessment at end of life is active medicine: pain, dyspnea, secretions, delirium, existential distress — each has an exam and a treatment"],"tx":["THE CONVERSATION IS A PROCEDURE with technique (SPIKES/Serious Illness Conversation Guide): setup (sit, silence pagers, right people present), perception ('what's your understanding of where things are?'), invitation, knowledge (headline first, no jargon, then STOP), empathy (respond to emotion before returning to information — 'I wish' statements), summary/strategy — and the 70% listening ratio","Frame recommendations, don't present menus: 'given what you've told me matters to him, I recommend we focus on comfort and not attempt resuscitation' beats the à-la-carte list of interventions read like a waiver — patients and families deserve medical judgment, not just options","HOSPICE demystified in plain language: comfort-focused care wherever the patient lives, with team support — eligibility (prognosis ≤6 months if disease runs its course), and the evidence: hospice and early palliative care improve quality of life and sometimes length (the 'giving up' framing is factually wrong)","COMFORT-FOCUSED CARE done to medicine's standard: opioids titrated for dyspnea/pain (morphine 2.5-5mg q1-2h starting doses in the naive — treating dyspnea is not euthanasia and undertreating it is not caution), glycopyrrolate/scopolamine for secretions, haloperidol for terminal delirium/nausea, deprescribing everything that no longer serves (the statin at hour-of-death is a symbol of unexamined momentum), stop the vitals/labs/fingersticks that serve the chart and hurt the patient","FAMILY CARE is patient care: time-limited trials framed explicitly ('we'll continue X for 72 hours and reassess against the goals we named'), the death pronounced with dignity and the family given time, bereavement resources — and debrief your own team (the resident who ran their first death deserves five minutes of processing too)"],"pearls":["'What's your understanding of where things are?' is the single highest-yield question in medicine — the answer tells you exactly where the conversation must begin","Families asked 'what do you want us to do' carry guilt for decades; families asked 'what would he tell us' carry his voice — substituted judgment is a kindness with an evidence base","Comfort care is not the absence of medicine, it's a different medicine done with the same rigor — the dyspneic dying patient deserves titrated opioids with the same urgency the septic patient deserves antibiotics"],"refs":[{"t":"Serious Illness Conversation Guide (Ariadne Labs)","u":"https://www.ariadnelabs.org"},{"t":"CAPC Palliative Care Resources","u":"https://www.capc.org"}]},{"id":"anes-preop-eval","name":"Preoperative Evaluation & Risk Stratification","sec":"anes","present":["Every patient before anesthesia: history, exam, airway assessment, functional capacity, and risk stratification proportional to patient and surgical risk.","Key screening targets: cardiac risk, pulmonary risk (OSA via STOP-BANG), anemia, frailty, anticoagulants and high-risk home meds (GLP-1 agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors, insulin)."],"dx":["Functional capacity: METs from history or the Duke Activity Status Index; <4 METs (cannot climb 2 flights) flags need for further cardiac consideration in elevated-risk surgery.","Cardiac risk: RCRI (Lee) and NSQIP MICA/ACS surgical risk calculators; stepwise ACC/AHA algorithm - proceed if low risk or adequate functional capacity; test only if results change management.","ASA Physical Status I-VI communicates comorbidity burden; add E for emergency.","Labs and studies only as indicated by disease and procedure - routine testing for low-risk surgery is low-value care (avoid the shotgun preop panel)."],"tx":["Optimize before elective surgery: treat anemia (iron), glycemic control, smoking cessation (any abstinence helps; >4-8 weeks best), prehabilitation in frail patients.","Medication management: continue beta-blockers and most antihypertensives (hold ACEi/ARB morning-of per many protocols), hold SGLT2i 3-4 days (euglycemic DKA), manage anticoagulation with ASRA/ACC bridging principles.","Fasting: clear liquids until 2 h, light meal 6 h, fatty meal 8 h (ASA); encourage clear carbohydrate drinks in ERAS pathways."],"tech":["GLP-1 receptor agonists: per ASA/multisociety guidance, assess for delayed gastric emptying; hold daily dosing day-of and weekly dosing ~1 week when feasible, or treat as full stomach (RSI or gastric ultrasound to check contents).","Gastric point-of-care ultrasound: antrum in right lateral decubitus; clear antrum or <1.5 mL/kg supports low aspiration risk."],"after":["Document plan A/B airway strategy, anticipated disposition (PACU vs ICU), and postoperative pain plan at the preop visit.","High-risk patients: arrange postoperative troponin surveillance and continuous monitoring where indicated (see perioperative myocardial injury)."],"pearls":["The preop visit changes outcomes mostly by cancelling the wrong operations and optimizing the right patients - not by ordering tests.","A patient who climbs stairs daily with groceries needs no stress test for a hernia repair.","Never let a normal ECG reassure you about a story of new exertional chest pain - the history outranks the tracing."],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA Perioperative CV Management Guideline","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"ASA Preoperative Fasting & Preanesthesia Evaluation","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Preoperative Evaluation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-difficult-airway","name":"Airway Assessment & the Difficult Airway Algorithm","sec":"anes","present":["Predicted difficulty: prior difficult intubation, Mallampati III-IV, thyromental distance <6 cm, mouth opening <3 cm, limited neck extension, beard, OSA, radiation changes, angioedema, anterior mediastinal mass.","Unanticipated difficulty declares itself after induction - the algorithm exists for this moment."],"dx":["Bedside assessment: 3-3-2 rule, Mallampati, upper lip bite test, neck mobility; no single test is sensitive - combine and respect gestalt.","MACOCHA score predicts difficult intubation in ICU patients.","Distinguish four difficulties independently: difficult ventilation, difficult laryngoscopy, difficult SGA, difficult surgical access - trouble with two or more mandates an awake technique."],"tx":["ASA 2022 algorithm entry decision: awake intubation when difficult intubation is anticipated PLUS one of difficult ventilation, aspiration risk, rapid desaturation, or difficult rescue.","After failed attempts with adequate oxygenation: limit attempts (3+1), change something each time (blade, position, operator, adjunct), place SGA to rescue oxygenation.","Cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate (CICO): declare it aloud, call for help, proceed immediately to front-of-neck access - delay is the fatal error (NAP4)."],"tech":["Awake intubation: glycopyrrolate early, topical lidocaine to airway, cautious sedation that preserves ventilation (dexmedetomidine or remifentanil), flexible scope or awake VL; verify tube, then induce.","Optimize every first attempt: ramped position (ear-to-sternal-notch), preoxygenation to EtO2 >85-90%, apneic oxygenation via nasal cannula, bougie ready, suction on."],"after":["After a difficult airway: document precisely (grade, devices, what worked), tell the patient in writing, enter airway alert in the chart, and extubate with an explicit strategy (airway exchange catheter for high-risk cases)."],"pearls":["The most dangerous airway plan is the vague one - say plan A/B/C out loud before induction.","Waking the patient up is a triumph, not a failure.","When you reach for the third different laryngoscope, your hands should already be thinking about the neck."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA 2022 Difficult Airway Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"Difficult Airway Society Guidelines","u":"https://das.uk.com"},{"t":"NAP4 Major Airway Complications","u":"https://www.nationalauditprojects.org.uk"}]},{"id":"anes-rsi","name":"Rapid Sequence Induction & Intubation","sec":"anes","present":["Indicated when aspiration risk is elevated: full stomach, bowel obstruction, symptomatic reflux, pregnancy >~16-20 weeks, gastroparesis (diabetes, GLP-1 agonists), trauma, altered consciousness."],"dx":["Confirm the indication and the airway assessment simultaneously - RSI in a predicted difficult airway is a deliberate, senior-level decision (consider awake technique instead).","Gastric ultrasound can de-escalate: an empty antrum may permit a standard induction."],"tx":["Preoxygenate 3 minutes tidal breathing or 8 vital-capacity breaths to EtO2 >85-90%; add apneic oxygenation (nasal cannula 15 L/min) in high-risk patients.","Induction + paralysis in immediate succession without mask ventilation: propofol, etomidate, or ketamine per hemodynamics, then succinylcholine 1-1.5 mg/kg or rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg.","Cricoid pressure remains guideline-mentioned but evidence-weak; apply if practiced, release immediately if the view is poor.","Gentle low-pressure mask ventilation (<15-20 cmH2O) is acceptable in modified RSI, especially in patients who desaturate fast (obesity, pregnancy, sepsis, children)."],"tech":["Position ramped, suction under the pillow, two working laryngoscopes, bougie on the field.","First-attempt success is the goal: video laryngoscopy improves first-pass success (DEVICE trial) - use it liberally.","Confirm with continuous waveform capnography; auscultation alone is not confirmation."],"after":["Post-intubation: secure tube, recheck hemodynamics (induction + positive pressure = hypotension), set lung-protective ventilation, gastric decompression if obstructed."],"pearls":["Rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg matches succinylcholine onset and sugammadex 16 mg/kg can reverse it - the sux monopoly on RSI is over.","The desaturating obese patient was never adequately preoxygenated - fix the position first.","In shock, ketamine and half-dose everything; induction agents kill before laryngoscopes do."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rapid Sequence Induction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"DEVICE Trial (VL vs DL)","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ASA Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-vl-afoi","name":"Video Laryngoscopy & Awake Fiberoptic Intubation","sec":"anes","present":["VL: routine and rescue laryngoscopy, cervical spine precautions, teaching (shared view), predicted moderate difficulty.","Awake flexible intubation: predicted difficult airway with any red flag (difficult ventilation, aspiration risk, rapid desaturation), fixed neck, airway tumor, angioedema, unstable c-spine with difficult anatomy."],"dx":["Choose the blade: Macintosh-style VL when standard geometry is fine; hyperangulated blade (GlideScope-type) for anterior larynges - remember hyperangulated blades show a great view that can still be hard to intubate.","Awake technique selection: flexible scope (nasal or oral) vs awake VL - awake VL is faster with good mouth opening; flexible scope wins in limited mouth opening and distorted anatomy."],"tx":["Awake preparation: explain honestly, glycopyrrolate 0.2 mg IV early, oxygen throughout (nasal cannula or high-flow), topicalize with lidocaine (atomizer, gargle, spray-as-you-go; respect a total dose ceiling and count every route), light titratable sedation preserving spontaneous ventilation (dexmedetomidine, low-dose remifentanil)."],"tech":["Hyperangulated VL: insert midline, do not sweep tongue, deliberately keep the glottis in the upper half of the screen, use a rigid hyperangulated stylet, rotate the tube off the stylet as it passes the cords.","Flexible scope: railroad a reinforced or smaller tube with the bevel rotated; the commonest hang-up is the right arytenoid - withdraw 1 cm, rotate 90 degrees counterclockwise, advance.","Confirm position with capnography, then induce anesthesia only after confirmation in awake technique."],"after":["Awake cases: document topicalization doses and patient tolerance; observe for lidocaine toxicity and post-procedure stridor.","After VL rescue of a failed DL, record which blade and stylet succeeded - it becomes the patient's plan A."],"pearls":["A perfect camera view with an unpassable tube means the geometry is wrong - change the stylet shape or drop to a Macintosh-style blade and a bougie.","The awake airway is lost by oversedation, not by undersedation - the patient breathing is your safety net.","Antisialagogue first: topicalization fails on a wet airway."],"refs":[{"t":"Difficult Airway Society: Awake Tracheal Intubation Guideline","u":"https://das.uk.com"},{"t":"ASA 2022 Difficult Airway Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Video Laryngoscopy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-lma","name":"Supraglottic Airways (LMA)","sec":"anes","present":["Primary airway for fasted patients in short-to-medium peripheral surgery with supine access; rescue device in failed intubation and failed ventilation (ASA/DAS algorithms); conduit for intubation."],"dx":["Contraindication check: significant aspiration risk (full stomach, obstruction, severe reflux, late pregnancy), poor pulmonary compliance or high resistance needing pressures >~20-25 cmH2O, glottic/subglottic pathology, prone or restricted-access cases (relative, experience-dependent).","Size by weight: 3 (30-50 kg), 4 (50-70 kg), 5 (70-100 kg) - when between sizes, larger with less cuff air usually seals better."],"tx":["Second-generation devices (ProSeal, i-gel, LMA Supreme) with gastric drain channels and higher seal pressures are the modern default, and mandatory for any marginal case.","Anesthesia depth must blunt airway reflexes before insertion (propofol is the friendliest agent); laryngospasm on a light patient is the classic error."],"tech":["Insert along the palate with the cuff deflated or semi-inflated, seat in the hypopharynx, inflate to cuff pressure <60 cmH2O (manometer, not feel).","Check: square capnograph, chest rise, audible leak pressure >20 cmH2O, gastric tube passes the drain channel (2nd gen), bite block in place.","As rescue: an SGA that restores oxygenation converts a crisis into a decision point - stabilize, then choose wake-up, intubate through it (Aintree catheter or flexible scope), or proceed."],"after":["Remove deep or awake by unit protocol - not in between (excitement stage = laryngospasm).","PACU: watch for sore throat, lingual/hypoglossal nerve symptoms from overinflated cuffs; document seal pressure and device size."],"pearls":["Cuff pressure creep with nitrous oxide - recheck with a manometer.","A leaking LMA usually needs depth or repositioning, not more air.","The gastric channel bubbling with ventilation means the tip is folded or malpositioned - reinsert."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngeal Mask Airway","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"Difficult Airway Society Guidelines","u":"https://das.uk.com"},{"t":"APSF: Supraglottic Airway Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-fona","name":"Front-of-Neck Access (Emergency Cricothyrotomy)","sec":"anes","present":["Cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate (CICO): failed intubation, failed mask ventilation, failed SGA, saturation falling - the end of every airway algorithm and the step most often started too late (NAP4)."],"dx":["Declare CICO out loud to the room; assign one person to the neck while a final SGA attempt proceeds.","Identify the cricothyroid membrane BEFORE induction in every predicted difficult airway - palpation (laryngeal handshake: stabilize larynx, walk down thyroid cartilage to the membrane) or mark with ultrasound in obese/distorted necks."],"tx":["Adults: scalpel-bougie-tube cricothyrotomy is the DAS-endorsed default over needle techniques - faster, definitive, familiar equipment.","Children under ~10-12: cricothyroid membrane is tiny - needle/cannula oxygenation techniques with specialized low-pressure insufflation are preferred; surgical airway means tracheostomy by the most experienced hands available."],"tech":["Scalpel technique (palpable membrane): transverse stab through the cricothyroid membrane with a #10 blade, rotate blade 90 degrees, slide bougie beside the blade into the trachea, railroad a 6.0 cuffed tube, confirm with capnography.","Impalpable membrane: 8-10 cm vertical midline incision, blunt dissect with fingers to find the larynx, then proceed as above.","Continue oxygen from above throughout; the SGA holding partial oxygenation buys the seconds the neck needs."],"after":["A cricothyrotomy is converted to tracheostomy or trans-laryngeal tube in controlled conditions, typically within 24-72 h.","Debrief the team, document timeline honestly, complete an airway alert and patient notification."],"pearls":["The decision is the hard part; the procedure is 45 seconds of committed cuts.","Practice the laryngeal handshake on every patient you anesthetize - the day you need it, your fingers must already know the way.","Oxygenation, not intubation, is the goal of the algorithm - say it when panic pushes toward a fourth laryngoscopy."],"refs":[{"t":"DAS Guidelines: CICO / eFONA","u":"https://das.uk.com"},{"t":"ASA 2022 Difficult Airway Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cricothyroidotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-induction-agents","name":"Induction Agents (Propofol, Etomidate, Ketamine)","sec":"anes","present":["Choice of induction agent is a hemodynamic decision first: the healthy outpatient, the septic hypotensive patient, and the tamponade patient need different drugs and different doses."],"dx":["Assess volume status, sympathetic dependence (shock states live on their catecholamines), reactive airways, ICP/CV concerns, and allergy history (egg/soy allergy is NOT a propofol contraindication in current practice)."],"tx":["Propofol 1.5-2.5 mg/kg IV: smooth, antiemetic, suppresses airway reflexes (best for SGA); dose-dependent vasodilation and myocardial depression - halve or quarter the dose in the elderly, hypovolemic, or beta-blocked, and titrate to effect.","Etomidate 0.2-0.3 mg/kg: hemodynamically stable induction; causes adrenal suppression (single dose measurably suppresses cortisol synthesis - weigh in septic shock), myoclonus, PONV; no analgesia.","Ketamine 1-2 mg/kg IV: dissociative, sympathomimetic (preserves BP via catecholamine release), bronchodilates, preserves respiratory drive; direct myocardial depressant unmasked in catecholamine-depleted shock - use 0.5-1 mg/kg there; secretions, emergence phenomena (blunt with benzodiazepine or propofol co-administration)."],"tech":["Co-induction: fentanyl 1-2 mcg/kg or lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg blunts laryngoscopy response and spares hypnotic dose; low-dose midazolam spares propofol.","In shock: ketamine or etomidate at reduced dose, push vasopressor in hand, expect the blood pressure to fall regardless - induction unmasks hypovolemia."],"after":["Watch the 5 minutes after induction as vigilantly as laryngoscopy: this is when the pressure bottoms out (vasodilation + positive pressure + hemorrhage)."],"pearls":["There is no safe induction drug, only safe doses and prepared hands.","Etomidate is stable because it does nothing - including nothing for the intubation response; cover it with opioid.","Ketamine is the shock drug until the shock is catecholamine-deplete - then nothing is the shock drug; consider awake technique with topicalization."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Propofol / Etomidate / Ketamine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"FDA Prescribing Information (DailyMed)","u":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-nmb","name":"Neuromuscular Blockade & Reversal","sec":"anes","present":["Paralysis for intubation, surgical relaxation, and ventilator synchrony; the corresponding hazard is residual blockade in PACU - historically present in up to ~30-40% of patients without quantitative monitoring."],"dx":["Quantitative twitch monitoring is now the standard (ASA 2023): train-of-four RATIO >=0.9 at the adductor pollicis defines adequate recovery; count and fade by feel are unreliable.","Depth language: deep block = post-tetanic count 1-2; moderate = TOF count 1-3; recovery = TOFR >=0.9."],"tx":["Succinylcholine 1-1.5 mg/kg: fastest onset/offset; risks hyperkalemia (burns, denervation, immobilization, myopathies - lethal in these), MH triggering, bradycardia on repeat dosing, phase II block; avoid in children routinely (undiagnosed dystrophinopathy).","Rocuronium 0.6 mg/kg (intubating) / 1.2 mg/kg (RSI): aminosteroid workhorse, reversible at any depth with sugammadex.","Cisatracurium: Hofmann elimination - the choice in combined renal + hepatic failure.","Reversal: sugammadex 2 mg/kg (TOF count >=2), 4 mg/kg (deep block, PTC 1-2), 16 mg/kg (immediate rescue after RSI rocuronium); neostigmine 50 mcg/kg + glycopyrrolate only when TOF count is 4 with minimal fade - it has a ceiling and cannot reverse deep block."],"tech":["Monitor at the hand, not the face - facial muscles recover early and lie to you.","Sugammadex binds progesterone: counsel patients on hormonal contraception (7 days backup) - an ASA-highlighted, frequently-missed conversation."],"after":["Extubate only with TOFR >=0.9, sustained head lift is NOT sufficient evidence.","PACU signs of residual block: floppy, uncoordinated swallowing, hypoxemia with an obstructing pattern - reparalyze recognition: give sugammadex, support ventilation."],"pearls":["The patient breathing does not mean the patient can protect the airway - diaphragm recovers first, pharynx last.","Renal failure: rocuronium lingers, sugammadex-rocuronium complex is renally cleared but reversal still works and dialyzes; cisatracurium sidesteps it all.","If sux is contraindicated and you cannot say why in one sentence, look it up before you push it."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA 2023 Neuromuscular Blockade Monitoring & Antagonism Guideline","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neuromuscular Blockade","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"DailyMed: Sugammadex","u":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"anes-volatiles","name":"Volatile Anesthetics & MAC","sec":"anes","present":["Sevoflurane, desflurane, and isoflurane maintain most general anesthetics; understanding MAC, solubility, and agent-specific quirks is core anesthesia literacy."],"dx":["MAC = alveolar concentration preventing movement to incision in 50% of patients: sevoflurane ~2%, desflurane ~6%, isoflurane ~1.2%; MAC-awake ~0.3-0.4 MAC; MAC falls ~6% per decade of age, with opioids, alpha-2 agonists, hypothermia; rises with chronic alcohol, hyperthermia, red hair (classically), infant age (peak ~6 months).","Blood:gas solubility sets speed: desflurane (0.42) < sevoflurane (0.65) < isoflurane (1.4) - lower solubility = faster on/off."],"tx":["Sevoflurane: non-pungent, the inhalational induction agent (pediatrics, needle-phobic adults), bronchodilator; historic compound A concern with low fresh gas flows no longer restricts practice meaningfully.","Desflurane: fastest emergence, but pungent (laryngospasm if used for induction, avoid in reactive airways), sympathetic surge with rapid overpressurization, highest greenhouse footprint - many institutions have retired it.","Isoflurane: cheap, stable, slow; still common for long cases and outside high-income settings.","All volatiles: dose-dependent vasodilation and hypotension, uterine relaxation (>1 MAC worsens atony), MH triggers, potentiate NMB, PONV contribution vs TIVA."],"tech":["Overpressure technique speeds induction (deliver higher-than-target concentration transiently); second gas effect with nitrous accelerates uptake.","Low fresh-gas-flow anesthesia (0.5-1 L/min) cuts cost and emissions - titrate with end-tidal agent monitoring, mind circuit water/CO2 absorbent."],"after":["Emergence tracks washout: high flows, stop agent early per solubility, ensure NMB reversed and analgesia loaded before the agent is off.","Volatile-heavy anesthetics without prophylaxis = PONV in risk patients; apply Apfel-guided multimodal prophylaxis."],"pearls":["End-tidal agent is the only depth number volatile anesthesia gives you free - keep age-adjusted MAC >=0.7 with NMB on board to prevent awareness.","The circuit is a reservoir: at 0.5 L flows, dialing the vaporizer moves the patient minutes later - anticipate.","Every volatile is a tocolytic: on the way to cesarean hemorrhage, cut the agent and switch toward TIVA."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Inhalational Anesthetic Agents","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: Low-Flow Anesthesia","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-tiva","name":"Total Intravenous Anesthesia (TIVA)","sec":"anes","present":["Indications: MH susceptibility, severe PONV history, neuromonitoring cases (motor/somatosensory evoked potentials degraded by volatiles), airway surgery with an open circuit, transport/NORA locations without scavenging, patient preference."],"dx":["Plan depth monitoring: without end-tidal gas, processed EEG (BIS/SedLine target ~40-60) is recommended, especially with NMB - NAP5 found accidental awareness clustered in paralyzed TIVA patients with infusion mishaps."],"tx":["Standard recipe: propofol infusion (TCI effect-site 2.5-6 mcg/mL where available; manual ~100-200 mcg/kg/min after bolus, titrated) plus remifentanil (0.05-0.3 mcg/kg/min) or fentanyl boluses.","Adjuncts spare propofol and improve analgesia: lidocaine infusion, ketamine 0.1-0.3 mg/kg/h, dexmedetomidine 0.2-0.7 mcg/kg/h (expect bradycardia, slower wake).","Load long-acting analgesia before stopping remifentanil - its context-INSENSITIVE half-time (~3-4 min regardless of duration) means analgesia evaporates on arrival in PACU; opioid-induced hyperalgesia is dose-related."],"tech":["Dedicated IV or visible carrier line with anti-reflux valve; the classic disaster is a disconnected/tissued line silently infusing the mattress - keep the pump and the IV site visible, alarm limits on.","Context-sensitive half-time governs wake-up: propofol's rises modestly with duration; plan earlier taper for long cases; process EEG trends guide the taper."],"after":["Emergence: patients wake clearer with less PONV than volatiles; remifentanil rebound pain and shivering are the two PACU ambushes - preempt both.","Document total propofol dose in long ICU-adjacent cases; propofol infusion syndrome is an ICU-duration disease but the habit of tracking starts in the OR."],"pearls":["TIVA without a depth monitor in a paralyzed patient is flying IFR without instruments.","Check the IV site every time you would have checked the vaporizer.","Remifentanil ending is an event - treat it like one, with fentanyl/hydromorphone aboard 20 minutes before."],"refs":[{"t":"NAP5: Accidental Awareness During GA","u":"https://www.nationalauditprojects.org.uk"},{"t":"StatPearls: Total Intravenous Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: Medication Safety / Infusion Errors","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-opioids","name":"Opioids in Anesthesia (Fentanyl, Remifentanil, Multimodal Sparing)","sec":"anes","present":["Intraoperative opioids blunt sympathetic responses to laryngoscopy and surgery and anchor analgesia; the modern arc is opioid-sparing multimodal anesthesia, not opioid-free dogma."],"dx":["Match kinetics to the stimulus timeline: laryngoscopy (fentanyl 1-2 mcg/kg 3 min prior), continuous intense stimulus (remifentanil infusion), postoperative pain (morphine/hydromorphone timed 20-30+ min before emergence).","Screen risk: OSA, opioid tolerance/OUD (buprenorphine patients: continue buprenorphine, add full agonists at higher doses per current consensus), elderly (halve doses)."],"tx":["Fentanyl: boluses 25-100 mcg; context-sensitive half-time balloons with cumulative dosing - large total doses resurface in PACU.","Remifentanil 0.05-0.3 mcg/kg/min: esterase-metabolized, offset in minutes regardless of duration; ideal for neuro/airway cases needing rapid wake; causes bradycardia/rigidity at bolus, hyperalgesia at high dose.","Hydromorphone 0.2-0.4 mg IV increments or morphine for post-op tail; avoid morphine in renal failure (M6G accumulation).","Multimodal backbone every case unless contraindicated: acetaminophen, NSAID (celecoxib/ketorolac - discuss bleeding with surgeon), dexamethasone, local/regional anesthesia, +/- lidocaine, ketamine, dexmedetomidine infusions."],"tech":["Chest wall rigidity from rapid high-dose fentanyl/remi: treat with NMB and ventilation, not more opioid.","In spontaneous ventilation cases, titrate to respiratory rate 10-14 - the rate is your opioid assay."],"after":["PACU: respiratory depression peaks when stimulus stops - the patient who was 'light' at drapes-down becomes apneic in the elevator; quantitative monitoring (capnography) for OSA/high-dose cases.","Discharge opioid stewardship: smallest effective quantity, multimodal continuation."],"pearls":["Opioids treat nociception, not hypertension from a light anesthetic - know which one you are chasing.","Remifentanil off = pain on: pre-load the long game.","The opioid-tolerant patient needs MORE opioid plus MORE multimodal, not judgment."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Opioid Anesthesia Agents","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASRA/ASA Acute Pain Resources","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"CDC Opioid Prescribing","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"anes-la-last","name":"Local Anesthetics & LAST","sec":"anes","present":["LAST presents on a spectrum minutes (or, with catheters/infusions, hours) after local anesthetic administration: perioral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, agitation -> seizures -> apnea -> hypotension, bradyarrhythmia, VT/VF, asystole; cardiac collapse can be the FIRST sign with bupivacaine."],"dx":["Know maximum doses cold (with epinephrine / plain): lidocaine 7 / 4.5 mg/kg; bupivacaine 3 / 2.5 mg/kg; ropivacaine ~3 mg/kg; mepivacaine 7 / 4.5 mg/kg - and that toxicity is additive across agents and routes.","Risk rises with vascular sites (intercostal > caudal > epidural > brachial plexus), extremes of age, low muscle mass, severe cardiac/hepatic disease, and bilateral or high-volume truncal blocks."],"tx":["ASRA LAST checklist: stop injecting, call for help and the lipid emulsion, manage airway with 100% O2 (hypoxia and acidosis potentiate toxicity), suppress seizures with benzodiazepines (avoid propofol boluses in instability).","Lipid emulsion 20%: 100 mL bolus over 2-3 min (patients >70 kg; 1.5 mL/kg if smaller) then infusion 200-250 mL over 15-20 min; repeat bolus and double infusion for ongoing instability; ceiling ~12 mL/kg.","Cardiac arrest from LAST: standard ACLS modified - epinephrine in SMALL doses (<=1 mcg/kg), AVOID vasopressin, avoid lidocaine (obviously) and beta/calcium blockers; prolonged CPR is warranted - consider ECMO early; bupivacaine arrests can survive 60+ minutes of good CPR."],"tech":["Prevention: ultrasound guidance, incremental injection 3-5 mL with aspiration, intravascular test dose where applicable, dose to lean body weight, run a timer mentally for delayed presentations."],"after":["Observe >=2 h after significant symptoms, >=4-6 h after cardiovascular events; report to the LipidRescue registry; document total mg of every local given by every provider - surgeon infiltration counts."],"pearls":["The surgeon's 'a little local' plus your block plus the lidocaine infusion is one arithmetic problem with one denominator: the patient's kilograms.","Seizure in the block room is LAST until proven otherwise - lipid lives where blocks happen.","Ropivacaine and levobupivacaine are less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine, not non-toxic."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA LAST Checklist & Practice Advisory","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Local Anesthetic Toxicity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: LAST Prevention","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-spinal","name":"Spinal Anesthesia","sec":"anes","present":["Single-shot subarachnoid block for surgery below ~T10 (and up to T4-6 for cesarean): joint replacement, urologic, gynecologic, hernia, obstetric procedures - dense, fast, reliable."],"dx":["Absolute contraindications: patient refusal, infection at site, frank coagulopathy or anticoagulation outside ASRA timing, severe uncorrected hypovolemia, critical fixed-output lesions (severe AS - relative and technique-dependent), elevated ICP with mass effect.","ASRA anticoagulation timing highlights: hold prophylactic LMWH 12 h, therapeutic LMWH 24 h, DOACs ~72 h (apixaban/rivaroxaban), clopidogrel 5-7 days; aspirin alone is fine."],"tx":["Drug: hyperbaric bupivacaine 0.75% 10-15 mg (1.4-2 mL) for most; adjuncts - fentanyl 10-25 mcg sharpens the block, preservative-free (intrathecal) morphine 100-200 mcg buys 18-24 h analgesia at the cost of pruritus, PONV, and delayed respiratory depression monitoring.","Hypotension + bradycardia = sympathectomy +/- Bezold-Jarisch reflex: co-load fluids, phenylephrine or norepinephrine infusion for cesarean-level blocks, atropine/ephedrine for bradycardic hypotension, and treat high spinal aggressively (support ventilation, pressors, prepare to intubate)."],"tech":["Midline at L3-4/L4-5 (below conus; Tuffier's line approximates L4), pencil-point 25-27 g needle through an introducer, clear CSF, stabilize, inject over 10-15 s; paramedian approach rescues calcified ligaments.","Baricity + position steer spread: hyperbaric solutions follow gravity - sitting prolongs sacral density, immediate supine with table tilt sets thoracic height; check level with cold/pinprick before incision."],"after":["Motor recovery precedes bladder recovery: ambulate with assistance first time, watch for urinary retention.","PDPH risk: young, female, cutting needles, multiple passes - positional headache 1-3 days out; conservative care first, epidural blood patch (~70-90% effective) for severe cases.","Intrathecal morphine patients need respiratory monitoring per institutional protocol for 18-24 h."],"pearls":["A spinal that is patchy at 10 minutes will not improve at 20 - have the general anesthesia conversation early, not at incision.","The blood pressure falls in proportion to the block height and the starting volume status - a co-load running WITH the injection beats a preload.","Paresthesia on injection: stop, withdraw, redirect - never inject against pain."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Regional Anesthesia & Anticoagulation Guidelines","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA Neuraxial Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-epidural","name":"Epidural Anesthesia & Analgesia","sec":"anes","present":["Catheter-based segmental block: labor analgesia, cesarean anesthesia (dosed up), major thoracic/abdominal surgery analgesia (reduces pulmonary complications after thoracotomy/laparotomy), rib fractures."],"dx":["Same neuraxial contraindications and ASRA anticoagulation timing as spinal - MORE consequential because a catheter must also come OUT within timing windows (remove epidural only when anticoagulation timing permits; restart drug after removal per drug-specific intervals).","Level targets: labor T10-L1 plus sacral for stage 2; thoracotomy T4-6; upper abdominal T6-8 - place the catheter at the middle of the desired band."],"tx":["Dosing: local anesthetic volume determines spread (~1-2 mL per segment lumbar), concentration determines density; labor runs dilute (bupivacaine 0.0625-0.125% + fentanyl 2 mcg/mL) as PIEB/PCEA; surgical anesthesia needs 2% lidocaine or 0.5% bupivacaine/ropivacaine incrementally.","Hypotension: fluid + vasopressor, reduce infusion; unilateral block: pull catheter 1 cm, re-bolus in lateral position."],"tech":["Loss of resistance to saline (or air) at the ligamentum flavum; thread catheter 4-6 cm into the space; ALWAYS test dose: 3 mL lidocaine 1.5% with epinephrine 1:200,000 - heart rate jump >20% = intravascular, dense rapid motor block = intrathecal.","Every top-up is a fresh test: aspirate, give incrementally - catheters migrate mid-case.","Wet tap (accidental dural puncture, ~1%): thread intrathecal catheter and label it LOUDLY, or resite; either way counsel about the ~50-80% PDPH risk."],"after":["Run epidurals with a monitoring bundle: sensory level, motor exam, BP, site checks - a rising level or dense legs on a dilute infusion means migration; new back pain + motor deficit = STAT MRI for epidural hematoma (surgical window ~8 h from deficit onset to laminectomy for recovery).","Remove catheter per anticoagulation timing; document tip intact."],"pearls":["The epidural hematoma you catch is the one you examined for - dense motor block that outlives the infusion is an emergency, not an observation.","Volume spreads, concentration numbs.","A labor epidural that never worked is in the wrong place - replace it before the crash cesarean finds you both."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Anticoagulation Guidelines (4th ed)","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epidural Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"SOAP Practice Resources","u":"https://www.soap.org"}]},{"id":"anes-cse-labor","name":"Combined Spinal-Epidural & Labor Analgesia","sec":"anes","present":["Laboring patients requesting analgesia at any stage (no arbitrary dilation threshold - ACOG/ASA: maternal request suffices); CSE when rapid onset matters (advanced labor, distressed multip) with catheter backup for duration."],"dx":["Pre-block check: platelets trend (neuraxial generally acceptable >=70k with stable counts per SOAP consensus), preeclampsia labs, fetal status, airway exam (every laboring patient is a potential GA cesarean)."],"tx":["CSE: intrathecal fentanyl 10-25 mcg +/- bupivacaine 1.25-2.5 mg gives analgesia in ~3-5 min; then run the epidural (dilute bupivacaine + fentanyl, PIEB with PCA boluses gives less motor block than continuous infusions).","Dural puncture epidural (DPE, 25-26 g puncture without intrathecal dosing) speeds sacral spread with fewer side effects than CSE - increasingly the default in some units.","Side effects: pruritus (opioid-mediated - nalbuphine 2.5-5 mg), hypotension (left uterine displacement + phenylephrine/ephedrine + fluids), transient fetal bradycardia after CSE (uterine tone spike from rapid catecholamine fall) - usually resolves with pressure/tocolysis support; prepare, don't panic."],"tech":["Needle-through-needle at L3-4/L4-5; confirm CSF at the spinal needle, dose, withdraw, thread catheter; the CSE catheter is 'untested' until the spinal recedes - test and label accordingly for cesarean conversion decisions.","For urgent cesarean with a WORKING epidural: top up with 2% lidocaine + epi + bicarbonate (+/- fentanyl) - surgical level in 5-10 min; a catheter that limped through labor does not deserve your trust in the OR."],"after":["Post-partum: motor check before ambulation, catheter removal timing vs VTE prophylaxis, PDPH surveillance after any dural puncture; debrief failed catheters honestly in the chart for the next anesthetist."],"pearls":["The labor epidural is also your cesarean plan A and your postpartum hemorrhage plan A - maintain it like the airway it replaces.","Fetal bradycardia 5 minutes after your CSE: check tone, resuscitate in utero (position, pressure, fluids, tocolytic if hypertonus) - the block is rarely the fetus's real problem.","Never let a beautiful spinal level distract from a rising spinal level."],"refs":[{"t":"SOAP / ASA Obstetric Anesthesia Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.soap.org"},{"t":"ACOG Obstetric Analgesia Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Labor Analgesia / CSE","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-pnb-upper","name":"Peripheral Nerve Blocks: Upper Extremity","sec":"anes","present":["Brachial plexus blocks by surgical territory: shoulder (interscalene), mid-humerus to hand (supraclavicular, infraclavicular), hand/forearm rescue (axillary, distal blocks)."],"dx":["Choose by level: interscalene covers C5-7 (shoulder) but spares ulnar; supraclavicular is the 'spinal of the arm' (whole plexus at the trunks); infraclavicular suits catheters and below-elbow work; axillary needs separate musculocutaneous injection.","Screen respiratory reserve: interscalene causes ipsilateral phrenic palsy in ~100% (hemidiaphragm paralysis) - avoid or use low-volume/suprascapular alternatives in severe COPD or contralateral diaphragm dysfunction."],"tx":["Drug: ropivacaine 0.5% (surgical) or 0.2% (analgesic), 15-25 mL typical single-shot; adjuncts dexamethasone (IV or perineural) and dexmedetomidine extend duration; catheters for major shoulder/elbow surgery.","Complication profile: interscalene - phrenic palsy, Horner's, recurrent laryngeal hoarseness; supraclavicular - pneumothorax (respect the 'corner pocket' technique with the pleura in view); all - LAST, nerve injury (rare, mostly transient)."],"tech":["Ultrasound-guided, in-plane needle with the tip visible ALWAYS; inject only with low opening pressure, no paresthesia, and visible perineural (not intraneural) spread in 3-5 mL increments with aspiration.","Interscalene: C5-6 roots between scalenes at the level of the cricoid ('traffic light' sign); supraclavicular: plexus lateral to the subclavian artery on the first rib - deposit lateral and deep to the sheath, watch the pleura."],"after":["Sling and counsel: the insensate arm cannot protect itself - no leaning, no heat packs, expect block resolution at 8-24 h (longer with adjuncts) with rebound pain: schedule multimodal analgesia BEFORE it wears off.","Document a post-block neuro exam at follow-up; persistent deficits get early documentation, reassurance for expected neurapraxia timelines, and neurology referral if progressive."],"pearls":["Interscalene for shoulder surgery in a patient who cannot tolerate losing half their diaphragm is a respiratory event you scheduled.","The corner pocket keeps the supraclavicular off the chest tube service.","Rebound pain at 2 AM is a planning failure at 2 PM."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Regional Anesthesia Resources","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brachial Plexus Blocks","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASRA LAST Checklist","u":"https://www.asra.com"}]},{"id":"anes-pnb-lower","name":"Peripheral Nerve Blocks: Lower Extremity","sec":"anes","present":["Hip fracture analgesia (fascia iliaca / femoral), knee arthroplasty pathways (adductor canal + periarticular +/- IPACK), foot and ankle surgery (popliteal sciatic + saphenous), and combinations replacing neuraxial where anticoagulation forbids it."],"dx":["Map the surgery to the nerves: femoral nerve (anterior thigh, knee), obturator (medial thigh), lateral femoral cutaneous (lateral thigh), sciatic -> tibial + common peroneal (everything below the knee except the saphenous strip).","Motor-sparing decisions drive modern choices: adductor canal (mostly sensory to the knee) preserves quadriceps for early ambulation vs femoral block's fall risk."],"tx":["Fascia iliaca or femoral block for hip fracture in the ED/preop reduces opioid use and delirium - guideline-supported, do it early.","Knee arthroplasty: adductor canal block/catheter + surgeon periarticular infiltration +/- IPACK (posterior capsule) is the ERAS-standard motor-sparing recipe.","Foot/ankle: popliteal sciatic (block above the bifurcation where tibial and peroneal share a sheath) + saphenous for medial coverage; ropivacaine 0.5% 15-20 mL popliteal."],"tech":["Popliteal: ultrasound at the apex of the fossa, trace the tibial and common peroneal to their union, needle in-plane, circumferential spread around the shared paraneural sheath.","Fascia iliaca (suprainguinal): two-pop or ultrasound 'bowtie', high volume dilute local (30-40 mL 0.25%) - a plane block, volume is the drug.","All lower-limb blocks: fall precautions ordered in the chart, knee immobilizer where quads are dense-blocked."],"after":["Ambulation only with PT and assist until motor function objectively returns; catheter patients need daily site checks and a disconnection plan.","Same rebound-pain scheduling as the arm: multimodal on the clock before the block dies."],"pearls":["A femoral block without a fall-precaution order is half an order.","Blocked toes still need pulses: compartment syndrome pain can be masked - in high-risk tibia cases coordinate with surgery on whether/what to block and monitor pressures liberally.","The saphenous nerve ruins more 'complete' ankle blocks than any textbook admits."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Regional Anesthesia Resources","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lower Extremity Blocks","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAOS/Hip Fracture Analgesia Guidance","u":"https://www.aaos.org"}]},{"id":"anes-truncal-blocks","name":"Truncal Blocks (TAP, ESP, Paravertebral, PECS)","sec":"anes","present":["Fascial plane and paravertebral blocks for abdominal, thoracic, breast, and rib fracture analgesia - opioid-sparing components of ERAS pathways, and neuraxial alternatives in anticoagulated patients (plane blocks have permissive ASRA risk categorization vs epidurals)."],"dx":["Choose by dermatomes and depth of analgesia needed: TAP (T10-L1 somatic abdominal wall - misses visceral pain), rectus sheath (periumbilical midline), quadratus lumborum (broader, some visceral spread), erector spinae plane (paraspinal, versatile T-spine levels for thorax/abdomen/ribs), paravertebral (unilateral 'hemi-epidural' density for thoracotomy/breast/ribs), PECS I/II - interpectoral/serratus (breast and axilla)."],"tx":["Dosing reality: plane blocks are volume-hungry (20-30 mL per side of dilute ropivacaine/bupivacaine) - BILATERAL blocks approach maximum doses fast; calculate mg/kg before, not after.","Rib fractures: ESP or paravertebral catheter early improves pulmonary mechanics - pair with incentive spirometry and multimodal per trauma pathways."],"tech":["All ultrasound-guided, in-plane, hydrodissection confirming the correct plane (local should spread as a lens lifting the fascia, not a blob in muscle).","Paravertebral: transverse process as backstop, pleura in view, watch the pleura DEPRESS with injection; pneumothorax is the feared complication - ESP trades some density for distance from the pleura.","Surgeon-placed wound catheters and liposomal bupivacaine are alternatives; do not stack them with your plane blocks without doing the LAST math."],"after":["Duration 12-24 h single shot (longer with adjuncts/catheters); schedule the multimodal handoff.","Bilateral high-volume patients: observe for delayed LAST (absorption peaks ~30-90 min for plane blocks)."],"pearls":["Fascial plane blocks fail in the plane you almost reached - hydrodissect until the anatomy proves itself.","TAP blocks treat the wall, not the viscera - the laparotomy patient still hurts inside without systemic or neuraxial coverage.","Bilateral TAP + surgeon infiltration is the classic route to plane-block LAST."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Regional & Anticoagulation Resources","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: TAP / ESP / Paravertebral Blocks","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASRA LAST Checklist","u":"https://www.asra.com"}]},{"id":"anes-mac-sedation","name":"Monitored Anesthesia Care & Procedural Sedation","sec":"anes","present":["Sedation for endoscopy, cataracts, cardiology and radiology procedures, minor surgery with local - the continuum from anxiolysis to general anesthesia, where patients drift deeper than anyone ordered."],"dx":["Pre-sedation assessment identical in rigor to GA: airway exam, NPO status, OSA screening (STOP-BANG), aspiration risk - MAC does not lower the standard, it raises the vigilance requirement.","Recognize the continuum (ASA definitions): moderate sedation (purposeful response to voice/touch, airway maintained) vs deep (response to repeated/painful stimulus, airway MAY need support) - providers must rescue from one level deeper than intended."],"tx":["Agents: propofol 25-100 mcg/kg/min +/- small boluses (apnea lives one bolus away), dexmedetomidine 0.2-0.7 mcg/kg/h (respiratory-sparing, bradycardia, slow offset), ketamine low-dose (analgesic, secretions), midazolam-fentanyl for lighter targets; combinations are synergistic in depression - reduce doses when stacking.","Oversedation/apnea response ladder: stimulate, jaw thrust, airway adjunct, bag-mask, LMA/intubate - equipment for every rung physically present before the first milligram."],"tech":["Capnography is REQUIRED for moderate and deep sedation (ASA standard) - the sat lags apnea by minutes on oxygen; the capnograph tells the truth in seconds.","Surgical fire triad in facial/airway MAC cases: open oxygen + drapes + cautery - keep FiO2 <=30% where feasible, seal or divert oxygen, communicate with the surgeon before every cautery-near-face case (ASA closed claims recurrent disaster)."],"after":["Discharge criteria (Aldrete/PADSS): stable vitals, return to baseline consciousness, ambulation per baseline, escort present; dexmedetomidine patients linger - plan the slot accordingly.","Document maximum sedation depth reached, not just target."],"pearls":["The most dangerous anesthetic in the building is the 'quick MAC' in a dark room far from help.","Propofol has no reversal agent - your hands and an LMA are the reversal agent.","If the case cannot tolerate the patient moving, it is not a sedation case - book the general."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA Continuum of Depth of Sedation & Monitoring Standards","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: OR Fire Prevention / Sedation Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Procedural Sedation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-monitoring","name":"Standard ASA Monitoring & Depth of Anesthesia","sec":"anes","present":["Every anesthetic, every location: qualified personnel continuously present, plus continuous evaluation of oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, and temperature (ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring)."],"dx":["Oxygenation: pulse oximetry with audible tone + inspired O2 analyzer with low-limit alarm on the machine.","Ventilation: continuous waveform capnography for every GA (and moderate/deep sedation); disconnect alarms on mechanical ventilation.","Circulation: continuous ECG, BP at least every 5 minutes, plus a perfusion check (pleth, art line, or auscultation).","Temperature: monitor whenever clinically significant change is intended, anticipated, or suspected - practically, any case over ~30-60 min (core sites: nasopharyngeal, esophageal, bladder)."],"tx":["Escalate monitoring with the patient/surgery: arterial line (beat-to-beat need, frequent gases, planned pressors), central access (vasoactive infusions, poor access), quantitative NMB monitor whenever relaxants are given (ASA 2023 standard), processed EEG for TIVA-with-NMB and consider in the elderly to avoid deep-anesthesia burden."],"tech":["Alarms are monitoring: set limits you would want to be woken by, never silence indefinitely - NAP/closed-claims cases repeatedly feature disabled alarms.","Depth by triangulation: end-tidal agent (>=0.7 age-adjusted MAC) or processed EEG 40-60 + hemodynamic trend + clinical signs; no single number is 'asleep'."],"after":["Monitoring continues through transport and PACU handoff: the corridor between OR and PACU is the least-monitored dangerous real estate in the hospital - take the oximeter.","Structured handoff (patient, procedure, anesthetic, lines, meds due, concerns) reduces information loss - use your unit's tool every time."],"pearls":["The pulse ox tone is a continuous monitor only if you can hear it - keep the volume where the pitch change reaches you mid-task.","Treat the patient, then believe the monitor: a flat art line with a good pleth is a kinked line, a flat both is a code.","The cheapest monitor in the room is your hand on the reservoir bag and your eyes on the field."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"ASA 2023 NMB Monitoring Guideline","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF Newsletter: Monitoring & Alarms","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-art-lines","name":"Arterial Lines & Invasive Hemodynamic Monitoring","sec":"anes","present":["Indications: anticipated large hemodynamic swings (major vascular, cardiac, spine, transplant), vasoactive infusions, deliberate pressure targets (neuro), frequent blood gases, non-invasive BP failure (obesity, arrhythmia, burns)."],"dx":["Site selection: radial first (collateral flow; the modified Allen test is traditional but poorly predictive - documented practice varies), then dorsalis pedis, femoral, axillary; avoid end-artery sites and infected/injured limbs.","Waveform literacy: square-wave (fast-flush) test for damping - underdamped (extra oscillations: overshoots SBP), overdamped (sluggish: bubbles, clots, kinks - understates SBP); MAP is the most damping-resistant number."],"tx":["Dynamic indices from the arterial trace: pulse pressure variation / stroke volume variation >12-13% predicts fluid responsiveness ONLY in controlled ventilation >=8 mL/kg, sinus rhythm, closed chest - outside those conditions use passive leg raise or a fluid challenge with stroke volume monitoring."],"tech":["Ultrasound guidance raises first-pass success; 20 g catheter over wire (integrated or Seldinger); transducer zeroed to atmosphere and leveled at the phlebostatic axis (4th interspace, mid-axillary) - in sitting neuro cases level at the external auditory meatus to read cerebral perfusion honestly.","Secure visibly: an arterial disconnect exsanguinates quietly under the drapes - line, connections, and pressure bag inspected as part of your room check."],"after":["Daily necessity review; remove when the indication resolves - complications (thrombosis, distal ischemia, infection, pseudoaneurysm) scale with dwell time.","Post-removal: pressure 5+ minutes, perfusion check documented (color, cap refill, pulse ox pleth on that hand)."],"pearls":["The art line is a drug: it has indications, complications, and a stop date.","Cold white fingers distal to your radial line is an emergency consult, not an observation note.","A beautiful number from a mis-leveled transducer is precision without accuracy - re-level after every table move."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Arterial Line Placement & Monitoring","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: Hemodynamic Monitoring Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-intraop-hypotension","name":"Intraoperative Hypotension Management & Vasopressors","sec":"anes","present":["Hypotension after induction and during maintenance is the commonest intraoperative event; observational data associate MAP <65 mmHg (even brief cumulative exposure) with myocardial injury, AKI, and mortality - treat promptly, prevent preferentially."],"dx":["Run the pump-pipes-tank differential in seconds: vasodilation (anesthetic depth, neuraxial, sepsis, anaphylaxis), hypovolemia/hemorrhage (check the field and suction canisters), obstructive (pneumothorax, embolism, insufflation, surgical compression - LOOK at the screen and TALK to the surgeon), pump failure (ischemia, arrhythmia).","Cross-check the number: cuff on the same arm as the drip? Art line damped or mis-leveled? Then believe it and move."],"tx":["Match pressor to mechanism: phenylephrine 50-200 mcg boluses (vasodilation with adequate HR), ephedrine 5-10 mg (hypotension WITH bradycardia), norepinephrine dilute infusion or 4-8 mcg boluses (the modern workhorse - peripheral administration of dilute norepinephrine through a good IV is accepted short-term practice), epinephrine for anaphylaxis/arrest physiology, vasopressin adjunct in catecholamine-refractory vasoplegia (ACEi/ARB patients).","Simultaneously treat causes: lighten anesthetic if deep, fluid bolus if responsive/bleeding, relieve obstruction, call the surgeon's hands off the cava."],"tech":["Prevention beats rescue: reduced induction dosing in the vulnerable, co-loading with neuraxial, prophylactic infusion for cesarean spinals, art line before induction when you expect the fall."],"after":["Chart the exposure honestly (duration below threshold) and extend surveillance in high-risk patients: post-op troponin where indicated, urine output and creatinine follow-up - intraoperative hypotension is a postoperative disease."],"pearls":["Hypotension is a diagnosis deferred: every phenylephrine bolus should buy you thinking time, not replace the thinking.","The suction canister and the surgical field are hemodynamic monitors.","Treat the MAP the patient's organs live at, not the MAP the textbook lives at - the chronic hypertensive perfuses higher."],"refs":[{"t":"Perioperative Quality Initiative (POQI) BP Consensus","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Intraoperative Hypotension / Vasopressors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-fluids-gdt","name":"Intraoperative Fluid Management & Goal-Directed Therapy","sec":"anes","present":["Every anesthetic requires a fluid strategy: maintenance, replacement of losses, and hemodynamic optimization - both hypovolemia (hypoperfusion, AKI) and salt-water overload (ileus, edema, anastomotic stress) carry harm."],"dx":["Assess responsiveness, not just pressure: PPV/SVV >12-13% (with validity conditions), passive leg raise, or a 250 mL challenge with stroke-volume response >10%; static CVP numbers do not predict responsiveness.","Balanced crystalloids (LR/Plasma-Lyte) over normal saline as default - large datasets (SMART, SALT-ED) associate chloride-rich saline with renal harm; saline retains niche roles (hypochloremic alkalosis, some neuro cases)."],"tx":["Modern baseline for major surgery: moderately liberal beats aggressively restrictive - RELIEF found zero-balance restriction increased AKI; practical target ~6-8 mL/kg/h in open abdominal surgery tapering with GDT, plus measured losses; ERAS carbohydrate loading upstream shrinks the deficit myth (classic NPO 'deficit replacement' is out).","Colloids: albumin selectively (cirrhosis paradigms, post-crystalloid in vasoplegia by preference); synthetic starches are out of routine use (renal harm).","GDT: in high-risk major surgery, titrating fluid +/- inotropes to stroke volume optimization reduces complications - the algorithm matters less than having one."],"tech":["Track the running ledger out loud at intervals: in (crystalloid, colloid, products) vs out (blood loss, urine, evaporative estimate) vs dynamic indices - fluid management by ledger AND physiology, never either alone."],"after":["Handoff the fluid story quantitatively to PACU/ICU: net balance, responsiveness status, ongoing loss expectations - day-1 weight and creatinine grade your work.","Post-op oliguria: assess responsiveness before reflex boluses; the stressed post-surgical kidney concentrates appropriately for hours."],"pearls":["The kidney is the organ your fluid strategy is graded on - by both too little AND too much.","Urine output is a trend instrument, not a bolus trigger.","Saline is a drug with a chloride side effect; LR is compatible with far more than folklore claims."],"refs":[{"t":"RELIEF / SMART Trials","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ERAS Society Guidelines","u":"https://erassociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Perioperative Fluid Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-transfusion-mtp","name":"Blood Transfusion & Massive Transfusion in the OR","sec":"anes","present":["From single-unit decisions in stable anemia to massive transfusion in exsanguinating hemorrhage (trauma, obstetric, vascular, hepatic) - the anesthesiologist runs the resuscitation while the surgeon controls the tap."],"dx":["Restrictive triggers for the stable patient: Hb ~7 g/dL (TRICC and successors), ~7.5-8 in cardiac surgery/ischemic disease - transfuse to physiology (ongoing loss, ischemia signs, mixed venous trends), one unit at a time with reassessment.","Recognize massive hemorrhage early and ACTIVATE the MTP by criteria/gestalt (e.g., ABC score, shock index, visible uncontrolled loss) - the protocol's power is logistics: products moving before you are behind."],"tx":["Massive transfusion: balanced ratio resuscitation approximating 1:1:1 plasma:platelets:RBCs (PROPPR), early TXA in trauma within 3 h (CRASH-2) and obstetric hemorrhage (WOMAN), calcium replacement aggressively (citrate chelation - hypocalcemia is the reliably lethal electrolyte of MTP; keep ionized Ca >1.1), warm everything (hypothermia-acidosis-coagulopathy triad), permissive hypotension until surgical control where compatible with the brain/pregnancy.","Goal-directed correction once the storm allows: TEG/ROTEM-guided fibrinogen (cryoprecipitate/concentrate - fibrinogen first in obstetric hemorrhage, target >2 g/L), platelets, FFP/PCC by results.","Emergency release: O-neg (children/childbearing) or O-pos (males) uncrossmatched now beats perfect blood later."],"tech":["Rapid infuser + fluid warmer assembled before the case that might need it; large-bore access above the diaphragm in abdominal/pelvic bleeding; cell salvage where available and appropriate.","Assign roles out loud: one brain on ratios/labs/calcium, hands on products; labs (gas, ionized Ca, lactate, TEG, fibrinogen) cycling every 30 min."],"after":["Reactions: TACO (volume, hypertension, edema - the commonest killer: diurese, slow down) vs TRALI (inflammatory hypoxemia within 6 h - support, report) vs hemolytic (fever, flank pain, hemoglobinuria under GA may show only as hypotension + oozing: stop, recheck, send workup); febrile and allergic reactions managed symptomatically.","Debrief and document times, products, and calcium - the MTP audit improves the next one."],"pearls":["In massive hemorrhage the most dangerous fluid is crystalloid and the most forgotten drug is calcium.","Activate the MTP one crisis earlier than feels justified - deactivating is free.","Under anesthesia the transfusion reaction has no voice: unexplained hypotension plus new oozing IS the symptom."],"refs":[{"t":"PROPPR / CRASH-2 / TRICC Trials","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"AABB Transfusion Guidelines","u":"https://www.aabb.org"},{"t":"ASA Perioperative Blood Management Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-mh","name":"Malignant Hyperthermia","sec":"anes","present":["Hypermetabolic crisis in susceptible patients (RYR1/CACNA1S variants, autosomal dominant) triggered by volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine: EARLIEST signs are unexplained rising EtCO2 despite increased ventilation + tachycardia + masseter/generalized rigidity; hyperthermia is a LATE sign; can begin intraoperatively or in early recovery."],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis under fire - supportive findings: mixed respiratory/metabolic acidosis, hyperkalemia, myoglobinuria (cola urine), CK elevation, arrhythmias; consider the mimics briefly (light anesthesia, hypoventilation, sepsis, thyroid storm, serotonin syndrome, NMS) but do not delay dantrolene for certainty.","Isolated masseter spasm after succinylcholine: controversial spectrum - at minimum abort elective triggers, monitor CK/myoglobin, counsel and refer."],"tx":["Declare the emergency, call for help and the MH cart, dial the MHAUS hotline (1-800-644-9737) early.","STOP triggering agents, hyperventilate with 100% O2 at flows >=10 L/min, convert to TIVA; insert activated charcoal filters on both limbs of the circuit if available (or change machine when practical - filters are faster).","DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV rapid bolus, repeat every 5-10 min until EtCO2, rigidity, and heart rate break (occasionally cumulative >10 mg/kg); assign dedicated hands to reconstitute (newer concentrated formulations mix far faster than legacy vials).","Treat the chemistry: hyperkalemia (calcium, insulin/dextrose, hyperventilation), acidosis (ventilation +/- bicarbonate), arrhythmias (standard agents but AVOID calcium-channel blockers with dantrolene - hyperkalemic cardiovascular collapse), active cooling if T >39 C (stop at 38 to avoid overshoot), fluids + urine output >1-2 mL/kg/h for myoglobinuria."],"tech":["Prevention in known/suspected susceptibility: trigger-free anesthetic (TIVA, non-depolarizing NMB), machine prepared per manufacturer flush protocols or charcoal filters, temperature monitored, dantrolene immediately available - susceptible patients can safely have surgery anywhere these conditions are met."],"after":["ICU >=24 h after an episode: recrudescence occurs in up to ~20%; continue dantrolene 1 mg/kg q4-6h or infusion for 24 h, serial CK/potassium/myoglobin, watch for DIC (grave sign).","Afterwards: refer patient AND family for counseling and testing (caffeine-halothane contracture test at referral centers; genetic testing first-line increasingly), register with MHAUS, document the event in permanent flags."],"pearls":["The capnograph diagnoses MH before the thermometer knows anything is wrong.","Dantrolene delayed is the whole ballgame - mortality tracks minutes to first dose.","Every OR you ever work in: know where the dantrolene lives before you need it - that walk is your first-day orientation."],"refs":[{"t":"MHAUS Emergency Protocols","u":"https://www.mhaus.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Malignant Hyperthermia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-anaphylaxis","name":"Anaphylaxis Under Anesthesia","sec":"anes","present":["Sudden hypotension (often the ONLY sign under GA), bronchospasm with rising airway pressures, tachycardia (or paradoxical bradycardia), rash/flushing hidden under drapes, falling EtCO2 from low output - typically within minutes of a culprit: neuromuscular blockers (rocuronium leads), antibiotics (cefazolin at incision), chlorhexidine, latex, sugammadex, dyes, colloids."],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis - treat first; the classic triad (hypotension + bronchospasm + skin signs) is often incomplete under anesthesia.","Confirm later: serial tryptase at ~1 h, 4 h, and >=24 h baseline; refer for allergy testing (skin/specific IgE) 4-6 weeks out to identify the culprit and safe alternatives - critical because re-exposure is routine in surgical patients."],"tx":["EPINEPHRINE is the disease-modifying drug: under anesthesia give IV - 10-100 mcg boluses titrated (grade-dependent), escalating to infusion 0.05-0.1+ mcg/kg/min for refractory shock; do not defer to antihistamines/steroids (adjuncts only, they do not treat the shock).","Simultaneously: stop the suspected trigger (halt the antibiotic, remove chlorhexidine sources, consider sugammadex timing), call for help, 100% O2, aggressive crystalloid (liters - massive extravasation), lift legs.","Refractory: norepinephrine/vasopressin add-on, glucagon 1-2 mg IV for beta-blocked patients, methylene blue described in vasoplegia; cardiac arrest = ACLS with the epinephrine you were already giving, and prolonged CPR is justified (young hearts, reversible cause)."],"tech":["Airway decision early: angioedema progresses - secure the tube while anatomy permits.","Rocuronium anaphylaxis nuance: sugammadex encapsulation is described but NOT a substitute for epinephrine - shock physiology persists."],"after":["ICU observation for biphasic reactions; document the timeline, all agents with times, tryptase draws, and a prominent allergy flag; anesthesia allergy clinic referral letter listing every drug given - the next anesthetist depends on it."],"pearls":["Unexplained refractory hypotension after induction is anaphylaxis until proven otherwise - look under the drapes at the skin.","The tryptase you do not draw is the diagnosis you can never prove.","Cefazolin given at skin prep is the anaphylaxis whose timing frames rocuronium - keep both on the suspect list; testing sorts it out."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA/Anaphylaxis Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"NAP6: Perioperative Anaphylaxis","u":"https://www.nationalauditprojects.org.uk"},{"t":"StatPearls: Perioperative Anaphylaxis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-bronchospasm-laryngospasm","name":"Bronchospasm & Laryngospasm","sec":"anes","present":["Laryngospasm: reflex glottic closure at light anesthesia - induction/emergence, especially children, URI, secretions, airway manipulation; presents as crowing stridor then silent obstruction with rocking chest and no capnograph.","Bronchospasm: wheeze or silent chest with high peak pressures, up-sloping (shark-fin) capnograph, difficult exhalation - triggers: airway instrumentation in asthmatics/smokers, light anesthesia, aspiration, anaphylaxis, carinal stimulation."],"dx":["First move for any intubated 'bronchospasm': rule out the mimics - kinked/obstructed tube (pass a suction catheter), endobronchial migration, breathing against the ventilator, pneumothorax, anaphylaxis (check BP/skin), aspiration.","Laryngospasm is diagnosed by the silence: no air movement, no EtCO2, effort without result."],"tx":["Laryngospasm ladder (seconds matter, especially pediatric desaturation): remove stimulus, 100% O2 with CPAP 10-20 cmH2O and jaw thrust at Larson's point (pressure behind the earlobes) -> deepen with propofol 0.5-1 mg/kg -> succinylcholine 0.1-0.5 mg/kg IV (or 4 mg/kg IM if no access) and ventilate/intubate; anticipate the bradycardia in children (atropine ready).","Bronchospasm: 100% O2, deepen anesthesia (volatile - sevoflurane is a bronchodilator - or propofol/ketamine), salbutamol via circuit adapter (many puffs needed to pass the tube), epinephrine IV titrated for severe/hypotensive cases, ventilator strategy for air-trapping: low rate, long expiratory time, permissive hypercapnia; steroids for the aftermath.","Negative pressure pulmonary edema follows relieved obstruction - pink frothy secretions and hypoxemia post-laryngospasm: supportive, CPAP, usually resolves in 24-48 h."],"tech":["Prevention beats treatment: extubate deep OR fully awake (not between), suction the pharynx before emergence, IV lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg blunts reflexes, delay elective anesthesia ~2-4+ weeks after significant URI in children where feasible."],"after":["Post-laryngospasm: observe for NPPE; document the event and what broke it.","Post-bronchospasm: optimize outpatient therapy before the next anesthetic; severe asthmatics earn a stress-dose and pre-treatment plan in the record."],"pearls":["A silent chest is worse than a wheezing one - in both diseases.","Larson's maneuver plus CPAP breaks most laryngospasm before the succinylcholine syringe is found - but find the syringe anyway.","The 'bronchospasm' that is actually a kinked tube has killed patients while albuterol was being sprayed - pass the catheter first."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngospasm / Bronchospasm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: Airway Emergencies","u":"https://www.apsf.org"},{"t":"DAS Paediatric Airway Guidelines","u":"https://das.uk.com"}]},{"id":"anes-aspiration","name":"Aspiration & Pulmonary Complications","sec":"anes","present":["Gastric contents in the airway at induction, maintenance with an unprotected airway, or emergence: seen material at laryngoscopy, hypoxemia, bronchospasm, high pressures, later infiltrates (right lower lobe classically supine; dependent segments by position); risk peaks with full stomach, obstruction, pregnancy, emergency surgery, GLP-1 agonists, difficult airway management with insufflation."],"dx":["Witnessed events are clinical; unwitnessed suspicion arises from unexplained hypoxemia + infiltrate within hours.","Grade severity by oxygen requirement and hemodynamics over the first 2 h - most aspiration is chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson), NOT immediate bacterial pneumonia."],"tx":["Immediate: head-down or lateral, suction the pharynx and trachea BEFORE positive-pressure breaths where feasible, secure the airway, 100% O2, PEEP for oxygenation; bronchoscopy only for particulate obstruction (lavage does not help acid injury).","Do NOT give prophylactic antibiotics or steroids for uncomplicated aspiration - antibiotics are for the patient who declares infection (persistent/worsening infiltrate, fever, purulence at 48 h) or special populations (obstruction, feculent material).","Disposition by trajectory: asymptomatic with normal oxygenation at 2 h can often proceed/recover routinely; symptomatic patients get monitored beds and lung-protective ventilation if intubated (ARDS is the severe pathway)."],"tech":["Prevention architecture: fasting guidelines, gastric ultrasound for ambiguity, RSI for risk, second-generation SGA gastric drains, NG decompression of obstruction BEFORE induction, extubate awake with intact reflexes in risk patients."],"after":["Document what was seen and suctioned, gas exchange trend, and antibiotic decision rationale; debrief system contributors (missed GLP-1, fasting failure) into the preop process."],"pearls":["The 2-hour mark sorts pneumonitis from trouble - reassess and write it down.","Suction before you bag: the first breath drives the acid distal.","Reflex antibiotics for every aspiration create resistant pneumonia for the few who actually need treatment later."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA Fasting & Aspiration Risk Guidance","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Aspiration Pneumonitis/Pneumonia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: GLP-1 and Aspiration","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-or-arrest","name":"Intraoperative Cardiac Arrest & OR Crisis Management","sec":"anes","present":["Arrest in the OR differs from the ward: witnessed, monitored, with a short causal menu - anesthetic overdose/high spinal, hypoxemia/airway loss, hemorrhage, anaphylaxis, embolism (gas/thrombus/fat/cement/amniotic), vagal/oculocardiac reflexes, electrolytes (hyperkalemia), tension pneumothorax, MH, LAST."],"dx":["Recognition cues under anesthesia: sudden EtCO2 collapse (the earliest and most reliable arrest sign), lost pleth/art line, ECG deterioration; pulse checks are unreliable under drapes - believe the capnograph.","Diagnose the cause WHILE compressing: run the OR-specific differential aloud; TEE/TTE during arrest identifies tamponade, RV strain (embolism), hypovolemia, standstill."],"tx":["Declare the arrest, call for help/code cart, 100% O2, stop anesthetic agents, start compressions (backboard, or internal massage discussions in open chest/abdomen with the surgeon), standard ACLS energies and epinephrine - with cause-specific forks:","High spinal/anesthetic overdose: epinephrine early, aggressive pressors; LAST: lipid emulsion + small-dose epi, no vasopressin; hyperkalemia: calcium chloride, insulin/dextrose; MH: dantrolene; anaphylaxis: epinephrine + fluids; gas embolism: flood field, left-lateral head-down, aspirate central line; hemorrhage: MTP + surgical control; tension pneumothorax: decompress.","Crisis resource management: name a leader, closed-loop communication, use the emergency manual/cognitive aid (Stanford/ariadne-type OR manuals demonstrably improve performance) - assign a reader."],"tech":["Pre-brief every high-risk case: who compresses, where the defibrillator lives, MTP trigger criteria; keep the arrest lines of the emergency manual physically in the room."],"after":["Post-arrest: ICU, targeted temperature/oxygenation/pressure management, cause-directed workup, structured debrief the same day (team, timeline, systems fixes) - OR arrests have better survival than ward arrests; audit yours to keep it that way.","Documentation: precise timeline, doses, rhythms, and the presumed cause with evidence."],"pearls":["The capnograph called the arrest before anyone said the word - and its return calls ROSC first too.","In the OR the arrest has a REASON and the reason is on a short list - say the list out loud.","Cognitive aids are not for the weak; they are for the human."],"refs":[{"t":"AHA ACLS Guidelines","u":"https://cpr.heart.org"},{"t":"Emergency Manuals Implementation Collaborative","u":"https://emergencymanuals.org"},{"t":"ASA/APSF Crisis Resources","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-or-hypoxemia","name":"Hypoxemia in the OR (Differential & Management)","sec":"anes","present":["Falling SpO2 under anesthesia - the universal alarm with a finite differential: delivery problems (circuit, FiO2, tube), ventilation problems (bronchospasm, secretions, endobronchial intubation), shunt (atelectasis, aspiration, edema, pneumothorax), perfusion signal problems (low output, probe artifact)."],"dx":["Sweep the system machine-to-alveolus in one pass: FiO2 analyzer reading? Circuit connected, capnograph shape present? Tube depth (endobronchial = commonest new intraop hypoxemia after repositioning - listen and check depth at teeth), cuff, patency (suction catheter)? Then chest: auscultate, look at pressures and loops.","Cross-check the number: pleth quality, probe position, cold extremity, methylene blue/nail polish artifacts - but NEVER attribute to artifact without completing the sweep.","Ultrasound answers the stubborn cases in minutes: sliding (pneumothorax), B-lines (edema), consolidation/atelectasis, plus a cardiac view for shunt/output."],"tx":["Universal first moves: FiO2 100%, hand-ventilate on the bag (feel compliance - the bag is diagnostic), confirm bilateral air entry, suction the tube.","Cause-directed: recruit atelectasis (recruitment maneuver then PEEP 5-10 - atelectasis is near-universal under GA, worse in obesity/laparoscopy/Trendelenburg), pull back the endobronchial tube, treat bronchospasm, decompress pneumothorax, treat the failing circulation (hypoxemia from low mixed venous saturation improves with output, not oxygen).","Refractory: exclude the rare shunts (intracardiac via TEE with bubble study), consider advanced modes/ICU ventilator, and in one-lung scenarios apply the OLV hypoxemia ladder."],"tech":["Prevention: FiO2 titrated but not phobic, PEEP from the start in at-risk bodies, recruitment after intubation and disconnections, head-up positioning where surgery permits."],"after":["Persistent unexplained desaturation earns a post-op chest imaging and a documented differential, not a shrug; handoff the FiO2/PEEP requirements explicitly to PACU."],"pearls":["The hand on the bag learns the diagnosis faster than the eye on the monitor.","New hypoxemia after the table turned is a mainstem bronchus until the tape measure says otherwise.","Treat the patient while you troubleshoot the probe - artifact is a diagnosis of exclusion."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intraoperative Hypoxemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: Airway/Oxygenation Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"},{"t":"ASA Monitoring Standards","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-olv-thoracic","name":"One-Lung Ventilation & Thoracic Anesthesia","sec":"anes","present":["Lung isolation for thoracic surgery (lobectomy, VATS, esophagectomy), airway protection (hemorrhage, pus), and bronchopleural fistula; the defining challenges are hypoxemia during OLV and lung-protective ventilation of a single lung."],"dx":["Absolute vs relative isolation indications; choose device: left-sided double-lumen tube (DLT, 35-39 Fr by height/sex) is default for most; bronchial blockers for difficult airways, existing ETTs, pediatric sizes, and planned postoperative ventilation.","Predict OLV hypoxemia: right-sided surgery (bigger lung down... actually ventilating the LEFT), poor preop V/Q, supine (vs lateral) position, low DLCO."],"tx":["OLV settings: TV 4-6 mL/kg predicted weight to the ventilated lung, PEEP 5-10, plateau <25-30, permissive hypercapnia, FiO2 titrated from high starting point downward; avoid both collapse and overdistension - both injure.","OLV hypoxemia ladder (after verifying tube position!): FiO2 100% -> recheck DLT with the scope -> recruit the ventilated lung + optimize PEEP -> CPAP 5-10 or intermittent O2 insufflation to the operative lung (warn the surgeon - it partially inflates the field) -> intermittent two-lung ventilation -> clamp the operative pulmonary artery (with the surgeon) in extremis.","Fluid discipline: restrictive-to-moderate strategy in lung resection/esophagectomy (post-pneumonectomy pulmonary edema association) balanced against perfusion; thoracic epidural or paravertebral catheter for thoracotomy analgesia is standard."],"tech":["DLT placement: intubate, rotate 90 degrees left as the tip passes the cords, advance to depth (~29 cm average adult, height-scaled), then BRONCHOSCOPY confirms - tracheal lumen view: blue bronchial cuff just visible in the LEFT mainstem, clear right upper lobe orifice check when relevant; reconfirm after EVERY position change.","Lateral positioning: axillary roll, neutral neck, pressure points; the tube migrates with the head - scope again after turning."],"after":["Re-expansion at closure under direct vision, gentle pressures; extubation preferred in the OR for most lung resections; post-op: chest tube management awareness, aggressive analgesia + physiotherapy (atelectasis/pneumonia are THE complications), watch for the 3 AM emergencies: hemorrhage into the chest, cardiac herniation after pneumonectomy (positional arrest - do not lay operative side down), and post-pneumonectomy edema."],"pearls":["Ninety percent of OLV hypoxemia is the tube in the wrong place - the fiberscope is step one, not step four.","The down lung drowns and the up lung dries: both are your ventilator settings' fault.","After pneumonectomy, the chest tube (if any) is NEVER put to routine suction - mediastinal shift arrests the heart."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: One-Lung Ventilation / Double-Lumen Tubes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ERAS Society: Thoracic Guidelines","u":"https://erassociety.org"},{"t":"ASRA: Thoracic Analgesia","u":"https://www.asra.com"}]},{"id":"anes-cardiac","name":"Cardiac Anesthesia Essentials (CPB Basics, TEE Roles)","sec":"anes","present":["Anesthesia for CABG, valve surgery, and increasingly structural interventions (TAVR, MitraClip); resident-level fluency means the cardiopulmonary bypass sequence, anticoagulation ritual, separation pharmacology, and what TEE answers."],"dx":["Preop synthesis: ventricular function, valve lesions (each dictates hemodynamic goals - e.g., severe AS: maintain preload/afterload/sinus rhythm, avoid tachycardia and vasodilation; severe MR: forward flow likes lower afterload and faster rates), coronary anatomy, carotid disease, baseline creatinine and hematocrit (transfusion/AKI risk).","Intraop TEE roles: confirm/refine valve pathology, ventricular filling and function in real time, de-airing surveillance, immediate post-repair assessment (residual regurgitation, gradients, SAM after mitral repair), diagnose the crashing patient (tamponade, RV failure, hypovolemia)."],"tx":["Pre-bypass: standard induction adapted to the lesion (slow, titrated, pressor in hand), large-bore access + art line pre-induction for tight lesions, heparin 300-400 units/kg from a CENTRAL/confirmed line, target ACT >400-480 s before cannulation; antifibrinolytic (TXA) per protocol.","On bypass: anesthesia continues (volatile via the pump or TIVA - awareness risk is real), MAP typically 50-80 per patient/institution, monitor ACT, glucose, temperature, urine, cerebral oximetry where used.","Separation from CPB checklist: warm (>=36), rhythm (pace if needed), rate 80-100, labs/K+/Ca2+/Hct acceptable, ventilating with visual lung inflation, de-aired (TEE), inotropes/pressors chosen for the ventricle you saw; then protamine 1 mg per 100 units heparin SLOWLY - watch for protamine reactions (hypotension, catastrophic pulmonary hypertension) - and confirm ACT return to baseline.","Failure to separate: diagnose with TEE (RV vs LV failure, valve issue, graft ischemia - new wall motion), escalate inotropes/pulmonary vasodilators, IABP/mechanical support conversation early."],"tech":["Cannulation and cross-clamp moments demand YOUR eyes on the field and pressure control (systolic ~90-100 for aortic cannulation); communication with perfusion is closed-loop: heparin given/ACT/flows/pressures repeated back."],"after":["Transport intubated with full monitoring and drugs; ICU handoff is a formal structured event (lines, pacing settings, bleeding expectations, target pressures).","First hours: bleeding vs tamponade vigilance (rising chest tube output OR suddenly stopping output with rising filling pressures both alarm), atrial fibrillation prophylaxis per pathway, glucose control."],"pearls":["Every valve lesion is a sentence: what it wants, what kills it - recite it before induction.","Protamine is the most dangerous routine drug in cardiac anesthesia: slowly, with the surgeon aware, and never through the line that fed the heparin-primed pump.","The TEE probe answers 'why is the pressure low' faster than any drug fixes it."],"refs":[{"t":"ASE/SCA TEE Guidelines","u":"https://www.asecho.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiopulmonary Bypass / Cardiac Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"STS/SCA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.sts.org"}]},{"id":"anes-neuro","name":"Neuroanesthesia (ICP, Positioning, Neuromonitoring)","sec":"anes","present":["Craniotomy (tumor, vascular, trauma), complex spine with neuromonitoring, and the shared physiology: cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP), autoregulation, and anesthetic effects on CBF and evoked potentials."],"dx":["Preop: baseline neuro exam DOCUMENTED (the postop exam is graded against it), ICP status (imaging midline shift, symptoms), seizure history/levels, positioning demands (prone, park-bench, sitting - the sitting position adds venous air embolism physiology).","Choose the monitoring montage with surgery: SSEPs (dorsal columns), MEPs (motor tracts - preclude NMB after intubation, favor TIVA; volatile agents degrade signals dose-dependently, keep <=0.5 MAC if used), EMG (nerve root/facial nerve), EEG burst suppression on request."],"tx":["ICP management bundle: head up 30 degrees, neutral neck (venous drainage), PaCO2 ~35 (hyperventilate to 30-32 only as a temporizing bridge to definitive therapy - vasoconstriction risks ischemia), osmotherapy (mannitol 0.25-1 g/kg or hypertonic saline coordinated with the surgeon), adequate MAP for CPP 60-70, avoid hypoosmolar fluids and hyperglycemia, deep-but-not-obstructed anesthesia (coughing/bucking spikes ICP - deep extubation vs fully-awake-smooth strategies).","Anesthetic selection: TIVA (propofol-remifentanil) is the neuromonitoring default and reduces CBF/ICP; maintain temperature normal, seizure prophylaxis per surgeon, TXA per protocol in some spine cases.","Emergence: rapid, smooth, examinable - the wake-up IS the monitor; remifentanil makes 'tube tolerated, patient following commands' achievable."],"tech":["Sitting/steep head-up cases: precordial Doppler + EtCO2 vigilance for venous air embolism - sudden EtCO2 fall + Doppler chirp: flood the field/saline-soaked packs, lower the head, compress jugulars, aspirate the (pre-placed) central line, support pressure; hemodynamic transducer leveled at the ear for cerebral honesty.","MEP cases: bite block (tongue lacerations), warn everyone the patient will twitch."],"after":["PACU/ICU: neuro checks on a schedule against the documented baseline; a new deficit or declining GCS = imaging and the surgeon NOW; watch sodium (DI/SIADH/cerebral salt wasting after pituitary/SAH surgery), pain control that preserves the exam."],"pearls":["The neurosurgical emergence is a performance with one acceptable review: 'following commands, not coughing.'","Hyperventilation is a loan against cerebral blood flow - short term only, repay quickly.","When the Doppler sings and the EtCO2 drops in the sitting position, the diagnosis has been made for you - act, don't ponder."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Neuroanesthesia / ICP Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"SNACC Practice Resources","u":"https://www.snacc.org"},{"t":"Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://braintrauma.org"}]},{"id":"anes-ob","name":"Obstetric Anesthesia (Cesarean, Preeclampsia)","sec":"anes","present":["Cesarean delivery (scheduled to crash), labor analgesia complications, and hypertensive disease of pregnancy - superimposed on pregnancy physiology: aortocaval compression, full-stomach status, difficult airway prevalence, 40-50% more plasma volume, hypercoagulability."],"dx":["Urgency stratification drives technique: scheduled/urgent cesarean -> neuraxial (spinal or epidural top-up) strongly preferred; true crash with no functioning catheter -> general anesthesia with RSI; a WORKING labor epidural topped up with 2% lidocaine+epi+bicarb reaches surgical level in 5-10 min and outruns many 'crash' GAs.","Preeclampsia assessment: severity features (BP >=160/110, headache, visual changes, RUQ pain, pulmonary edema), platelet trend (neuraxial generally acceptable >=70k stable), HELLP labs, magnesium status."],"tx":["Spinal for cesarean: hyperbaric bupivacaine ~10-12 mg + fentanyl 10-15 mcg + preservative-free morphine 100-150 mcg; PROPHYLACTIC phenylephrine or norepinephrine infusion + left uterine displacement + co-load prevents the expected hypotension (consensus standard).","GA for cesarean: aspiration prophylaxis (sodium citrate +/- famotidine/metoclopramide), ramped preoxygenation, RSI with smaller tube ready, volatile <=~0.75-1 MAC after delivery balanced with oxytocin (volatiles relax the uterus), and REMEMBER: awareness risk is historically highest here - depth vigilance.","Preeclampsia specifics: magnesium continues through surgery (potentiates NMB - reduce/monitor), treat severe BP (labetalol/hydralazine/nifedipine) targeting <160/110 without crashing placental perfusion, ATTENUATE the intubation pressor response (remifentanil 1 mcg/kg or labetalol - intracranial hemorrhage is a leading killer), restrictive fluids (pulmonary edema risk), neuraxial preferred over GA when platelets permit.","Obstetric hemorrhage readiness: oxytocin infusion (not fast bolus - hypotension), escalate methylergonovine (avoid in hypertension)/carboprost (avoid in asthma)/misoprostol, TXA within 3 h (WOMAN trial), MTP with fibrinogen-first thinking (target >2 g/L)."],"tech":["Left uterine displacement until delivery in every supine pregnant patient; difficult airway kit and second-generation SGA in every OB OR; post-spinal fetal bradycardia protocol posted (pressure, position, tocolysis if hypertonic)."],"after":["Intrathecal morphine monitoring per protocol 18-24 h; preeclamptics remain seizure/edema risks 48-72 h postpartum (magnesium typically 24 h) - the disease does not end at delivery; debrief every GA cesarean and every catheter that failed."],"pearls":["The best treatment for post-spinal hypotension is the infusion you started before the hypotension.","In severe preeclampsia the laryngoscope is a hypertensive weapon - blunt it or the blood pressure will spike into the brain.","A labor epidural is a cesarean anesthetic on retainer - test it, maintain it, trust it only if it earned trust."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA/SOAP Obstetric Anesthesia Guidelines","u":"https://www.soap.org"},{"t":"ACOG Hypertension in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"WOMAN Trial (TXA in PPH)","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"anes-peds","name":"Pediatric Anesthesia Essentials","sec":"anes","present":["Children are not small adults: proportionally larger head/occiput and tongue, higher larynx (C3-4), narrowest at the cricoid (functionally), double the oxygen consumption (desaturate in seconds), immature autonomic responses where bradycardia IS the hypotension."],"dx":["Preop: URI decision-making (recent/active URI raises laryngospasm/bronchospasm risk - weigh severity, airway plans, and delay elective cases ~2-4 weeks for significant symptoms), fasting per guidelines (clear liquids increasingly liberalized toward 1-2 h - follow your institution), premedication (midazolam 0.5 mg/kg PO) vs parental presence vs distraction.","Weight-based EVERYTHING: drugs in mg/kg, tube size (cuffed microcuff-era: age/4 + 3.5 cuffed), depth (age/2 + 12 cm oral), fluids (4-2-1 maintenance)."],"tx":["Inhalational induction with sevoflurane is the standard needle-free start; IV placed once deep; propofol induction when IV exists.","The pediatric crises are airway crises: laryngospasm (CPAP + Larson's + propofol + succinylcholine IV 0.1-0.5 mg/kg or IM 4 mg/kg with atropine ready), bradycardia = treat OXYGENATION first (hypoxia until proven otherwise), then atropine/epinephrine.","Emergence delirium (sevoflurane-prone, ages 2-6): distinguish from pain; prevention with propofol slug at the end, dexmedetomidine, or TIVA; it self-limits but injures - protect the child, calm the parents.","Fluids: isotonic (LR/Plasma-Lyte) intraop with glucose only for neonates/at-risk (avoid both hypoglycemia AND hyponatremic hypotonic errors)."],"tech":["Airway: shoulder roll for the big occiput (not sniffing pillows), cuffed tubes standard with cuff pressure monitored, uncuffed leak checks historical; SGAs work brilliantly for suitable cases.","Circuit and equipment scaled: appropriate bag size, precision infusions, temperature management aggressively (surface-area losses)."],"after":["PACU with pediatric-capable staff: laryngospasm/stridor watch post-extubation, apnea monitoring for ex-premature infants (post-conceptual age thresholds per policy - typically overnight monitoring under ~60 weeks PCA), parents reunited early - they are the best sedation.","Pain scales for the pre-verbal (FLACC), regional/caudal blocks underused - advocate."],"pearls":["A desaturating child bradys, a bradying child arrests: the interval is seconds and the treatment is oxygen.","Calculate the emergency drugs BEFORE induction and tape the sheet where eyes land - arithmetic under stress fails.","The crying child in PACU has one of three problems - pain, delirium, or a parent not yet arrived - sort them in that order."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"SPA (Pediatric Anesthesia Society) Resources","u":"https://www.pedsanesthesia.org"},{"t":"DAS Paediatric Airway Guidelines","u":"https://das.uk.com"}]},{"id":"anes-geriatric","name":"Geriatric Anesthesia & Perioperative Neurocognitive Disorders","sec":"anes","present":["The fastest-growing surgical population: reduced physiologic reserve across organs, altered pharmacology (smaller central compartment, slower clearance, heightened brain sensitivity), and the signature complications - postoperative delirium (up to 20-50% after major surgery in the vulnerable) and longer-term neurocognitive disorder."],"dx":["Preop beyond ASA class: frailty screening (gait speed, FRAIL scale, clinical frailty scale) predicts outcomes better than age; baseline cognition documented (Mini-Cog) - you cannot diagnose postoperative change without a baseline; polypharmacy audit (deprescribe anticholinergics/benzodiazepines where possible); goals-of-care alignment for major surgery in frailty."],"tx":["Dosing rule: start at 25-50% reductions of induction agents, titrate slowly (arm-brain time is prolonged - impatience causes stacking and hypotension); MAC requirement falls ~6% per decade.","Delirium prevention bundle (multicomponent, evidence-based): avoid benzodiazepines and anticholinergics, adequate MULTIMODAL analgesia (pain causes delirium), maintain perfusion, normothermia, early reorientation - glasses/hearing aids returned in PACU, sleep protection, early mobilization; depth monitoring to avoid unnecessarily deep anesthesia is reasonable (evidence mixed but burden-free).","Regional/neuraxial where suitable reduces systemic exposure; there is no proven cognitive superiority of regional vs carefully-dosed general - conduct of anesthesia matters more than the label."],"tech":["Hemodynamics: treat the pressure the patient's organs live at - chronic hypertensives autoregulate high; sustained MAP dips harm kidneys/brain/heart with less reserve to forgive; art line threshold lower.","Positioning and skin: osteoporotic spines, fragile skin, contractures - pad and move deliberately; temperature drops faster and matters more."],"after":["PACU/ward: delirium screening (CAM/4AT) on schedule - HYPOACTIVE delirium (quiet, withdrawn) outnumbers and outkills the agitated form and gets missed; treat causes (pain, retention, hypoxemia, meds) before drugs; antipsychotics only for distressing/dangerous symptoms, briefly.","Counsel patients/families beforehand that transient cognitive change is common - forewarned families report and cope better."],"pearls":["In the elderly, the loading dose is where the harm lives - halve it and wait for the brain to answer.","The quiet, sleepy postoperative patient may be the sickest one on the unit - screen, don't assume serenity.","Hearing aids and glasses are delirium prophylaxis with zero side effects."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA Perioperative Brain Health Initiative","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"AGS Postoperative Delirium Guidelines","u":"https://www.americangeriatrics.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Geriatric Anesthesia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-obesity-osa","name":"Anesthesia for the Obese Patient & OSA","sec":"anes","present":["Rising BMI changes every phase: difficult mask ventilation, rapid desaturation (low FRC + high consumption), aspiration considerations, altered drug dosing, positioning/equipment logistics, and the OSA overlap that turns opioids into respiratory hazards."],"dx":["Screen OSA universally: STOP-BANG >=3 (>=5 high risk) - most surgical OSA is UNdiagnosed; assess for obesity hypoventilation (bicarbonate elevation is the cheap clue), right heart strain in severe cases.","Airway assessment: neck circumference >40-42 cm predicts difficulty; plan ramped positioning and VL-first strategies; IV access and cuff sizing surveyed early."],"tx":["Drug dosing by the right scalar: propofol induction on LEAN/adjusted body weight (maintenance closer to total), succinylcholine on TOTAL body weight, rocuronium and opioids on LEAN/ideal - a taped dosing card avoids arithmetic-by-vibes.","Anesthetic conduct: short-acting agents (desflurane/sevoflurane/propofol-remi), aggressive multimodal opioid-sparing (acetaminophen, NSAIDs, dexmedetomidine, regional blocks whenever the surgery permits), quantitative NMB reversal to TOFR >=0.9 non-negotiable.","Ventilation: PEEP 8-10 from the start, recruitment maneuvers after intubation, TV on PREDICTED body weight, slight reverse Trendelenburg; extubate AWAKE, sitting up, fully reversed, with CPAP available."],"tech":["Preoxygenation is the whole game: 25-30 degree head-up ramp (ear-to-sternal-notch), tight mask to EtO2 >=90%, apneic oxygenation via nasal cannula through laryngoscopy - properly done, the desaturation cliff moves minutes away.","Home CPAP comes WITH the patient to PACU; positioning equipment (bariatric table, gel ramps, extra hands) arranged before, not during."],"after":["PACU: extended monitoring with continuous oximetry +/- capnography for OSA patients on opioids; recovery semi-upright; resume CPAP immediately with sedation aboard; ambulatory surgery eligibility per SAMBA criteria (treated/optimized OSA with manageable postop opioid needs can go home; unoptimized severe OSA for painful surgery should not).","VTE prophylaxis weight-adjusted; wound and positioning-injury checks."],"pearls":["The obese patient's oxygen reserve is a countdown you shorten with flat positioning - build the ramp every time.","Opioids given to untreated OSA at discharge continue anesthesia unsupervised at home.","Dose the body the drug actually distributes to, not the body on the scale."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA OSA Practice Guidelines","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"SAMBA Ambulatory OSA Consensus","u":"https://www.sambahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Obesity Anesthesia / STOP-BANG","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-trauma","name":"Trauma Anesthesia & Damage-Control Resuscitation","sec":"anes","present":["The exsanguinating, hypothermic, acidotic patient arriving to the OR mid-resuscitation: anesthesia becomes the continuation of trauma resuscitation with a tube - hemorrhage control, balanced products, and physiologic rescue running simultaneously."],"dx":["Rapid synthesis on arrival: mechanism, prehospital course, airway status, FAST/imaging, blood already given, TBI presence (changes pressure targets), and the lethal-triad trajectory (temp, pH/lactate, coagulopathy).","Recognize occult shock: normal pressure with narrowing pulse pressure, rising lactate, base deficit - young patients compensate until they fall off the cliff."],"tx":["Damage-control resuscitation principles: permissive hypotension (SBP ~80-90 / MAP ~50-60) UNTIL surgical hemorrhage control - EXCEPT TBI (CPP needs: SBP >=100-110) and spinal cord injury; minimize crystalloid; balanced products early (1:1:1, PROPPR), TXA within 3 h (CRASH-2), calcium with the blood, warm everything.","Induction of the shocked trauma patient: ketamine 0.5-1 mg/kg or reduced etomidate + rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg RSI with manual in-line stabilization for suspected c-spine; expect pressure collapse - push-dose pressors drawn; sometimes 'induction' is scopolamine/ketamine-only in the periarrest.","Ongoing case management: art line when hands allow (do not delay incision for lines in exsanguination), TEG/ROTEM-guided correction once tempo permits, communicate the physiologic scoreboard to the surgeon (temperature, pH, products in) - the decision to bail to damage-control packing is joint.","Specific injuries: tension pneumothorax decompression before/at induction if suspected (positive pressure converts occult to arrest), open globe (avoid pressure spikes - controversial sux), burns (massive fluid protocols, sux contraindicated after ~48 h)."],"tech":["Room readiness is the skill: rapid infuser primed, blood in cooler, airway cart, pressors diluted, warm OR (~26-28 C for the exposed patient), MTP communication line open; assign roles before the door opens."],"after":["Damage-control patients go to ICU packed, open, and intubated: handoff includes products given, calcium/potassium status, next-look timing, pressure targets, and TEG trajectory; anticipate abdominal compartment syndrome (rising airway pressures + oliguria + tense abdomen), ongoing rewarming, and staged returns."],"pearls":["Crystalloid in hemorrhage dilutes the clot, cools the core, and reassures only the monitor.","The blood pressure that satisfies you may be washing off the only clot the patient made - lower until control, except the brain.","Calcium is the trauma electrolyte: every few units of citrated products, check and replace."],"refs":[{"t":"ATLS / ACS Trauma Resources","u":"https://www.facs.org"},{"t":"PROPPR / CRASH-2 Trials","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Damage Control Resuscitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-ponv","name":"Postoperative Nausea & Vomiting (PONV)","sec":"anes","present":["The most common patient-remembered complication (~30% overall, up to 80% in high-risk untreated): misery, delayed discharge, unplanned admission, and rare mechanical harms (wound dehiscence, aspiration, esophageal injury)."],"dx":["Risk-stratify with Apfel (adults): female sex, nonsmoker, history of PONV/motion sickness, expected postoperative opioids - 0-4 factors -> ~10/20/40/60/80% risk; pediatric analog (POVOC): surgery >=30 min, age >=3, strabismus surgery, history in child/relatives.","Surgical amplifiers: laparoscopy, gynecologic, strabismus, middle ear, prolonged volatile exposure, nitrous oxide."],"tx":["Modern consensus (4th PONV guideline) simplified: give MULTIMODAL prophylaxis (2+ agents from different classes) to most patients rather than gatekeeping - dexamethasone 4-8 mg at induction + ondansetron 4 mg at closure is the universal base; add for higher risk: TIVA with propofol, NK1 antagonist (aprepitant - long half-life, good for discharge-prone procedures), scopolamine patch (mind elderly/glaucoma), dropping nitrous/volatile exposure, opioid-sparing multimodal analgesia, adequate hydration.","RESCUE rule: a failed prophylactic class does not get re-dosed within 6 h - switch classes (e.g., ondansetron failed -> promethazine, droperidol 0.625 mg (QT awareness), haloperidol low-dose, or propofol 20 mg subhypnotic in monitored settings).","Special notes: dexamethasone single-dose is safe in diabetics with a glucose check; droperidol's black-box reputation exceeds its low-dose risk but monitor QT."],"tech":["Build it into the anesthetic plan preop, not the PACU apology: document Apfel factors and the chosen bundle on the record; ERAS pathways bake this in."],"after":["PACU persistent vomiting: reassess beyond receptors - pain spikes, hypotension, gastric distension (suction), ileus, opioid stacking, and rarely increased ICP; discharge with rescue prescription (ondansetron ODT) after high-risk ambulatory cases; document what worked for the NEXT anesthetic."],"pearls":["PONV prophylaxis is now closer to universal precautions than to a risk-gated privilege.","Repeating the drug that already failed treats the chart, not the patient - change the receptor.","The cheapest antiemetics are propofol, hydration, and fewer opioids."],"refs":[{"t":"Fourth Consensus Guidelines for PONV Management","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"SAMBA Resources","u":"https://www.sambahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: PONV","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-postop-pain","name":"Postoperative Pain Management & Multimodal Analgesia","sec":"anes","present":["Every surgical patient; the goals are humane comfort, functional recovery (breathe, cough, walk), and preventing both undertreatment and the opioid-dependence pipeline - acute pain services exist at this intersection."],"dx":["Assess pain as function, not just a number: interference with deep breathing/movement/sleep; distinguish incisional, visceral, neuropathic (burning/shooting - responds to different drugs), and rebound-after-block patterns; screen risk for severe pain/persistent opioid use: preoperative opioid tolerance, chronic pain, anxiety/catastrophizing, young age, certain operations (thoracotomy, amputation, mastectomy - also the chronic post-surgical pain list)."],"tx":["Multimodal scaffolding for essentially everyone: scheduled acetaminophen 1 g q6-8h + NSAID (celecoxib/ibuprofen/ketorolac - coordinate bleeding/renal/bone-healing concerns with the surgeon; blanket NSAID bans are increasingly unsupported) + regional/local anesthesia (single-shot, catheter, or surgeon infiltration) as the base.","Opioids as the flexible layer on top: oral first when tolerating (oxycodone 5-10 mg), IV PCA for the truly NPO/severe (morphine/hydromorphone demand-only for opioid-naive - basal infusions raise respiratory events), dose-adjust for age/renal function/OSA.","Adjuncts by phenotype: ketamine infusion 0.1-0.3 mg/kg/h for the opioid-tolerant/highest-pain surgeries, lidocaine infusion in some abdominal pathways, gabapentinoids NO LONGER routine (sedation/respiratory depression synergy with opioids exceeds modest benefit - reserve for neuropathic-predominant cases), dexmedetomidine, and always: ice, elevation, mobilization, expectation-setting.","Opioid-tolerant/OUD patients: continue baseline (including buprenorphine - modern consensus: DO NOT stop it; add full agonists at higher doses as needed), lean harder on regional + ketamine + non-opioids, involve acute pain service early."],"tech":["Time the layers: long-acting analgesia loaded BEFORE remifentanil/blocks expire; rebound pain after single-shot blocks is scheduled for - multimodal on the clock and patient warned.","Prescribing stewardship at discharge: smallest effective opioid quantity procedure-matched, disposal instructions, no automatic refills."],"after":["Daily reassessment de-escalating to oral multimodal; persistent unexpected pain triggers examination (compartment syndrome, infection, hematoma, nerve injury) BEFORE dose escalation; flag high-risk patients for transitional pain follow-up - persistent post-surgical opioid use starts with an unexamined refill."],"pearls":["Pain that outruns the expected trajectory is a diagnosis, not a dosing problem.","The best opioid-sparing drug is a working regional block; the second best is a scheduled cheap one actually given on schedule.","Comfortable enough to cough and walk beats comatose and 'pain-free' by every outcome that matters."],"refs":[{"t":"APS/ASA Postoperative Pain Management Guideline","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ASRA Acute Pain Resources","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"CDC Opioid Prescribing","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"anes-pacu-emergencies","name":"PACU Emergencies (Delayed Emergence, Stridor, Hypoxemia)","sec":"anes","present":["The PACU is where anesthesia's debts come due: obstructed airways, residual drugs, hidden bleeding, and the patient who will not wake up - the anesthetist on call owns all of it."],"dx":["Delayed emergence differential (work it systematically): drugs (residual volatile/propofol, opioid pupils+bradypnea, benzodiazepine, residual NMB - put the twitch monitor ON, scopolamine patch forgotten), metabolic (glucose FIRST - hypoglycemia mimics everything; CO2 narcosis on a gas; sodium; temperature), neurologic (stroke/bleed - focal exam, pupils; nonconvulsive seizure) - if drugs and metabolism are clean, image the brain.","Post-extubation stridor: laryngospasm, laryngeal edema (prolonged/traumatic intubation, prone/steep-Trendelenburg cases, fluid-heavy resuscitations), vocal cord palsy/paradoxical motion, and after THYROID/CAROTID/NECK surgery: expanding hematoma compressing the airway (a surgical airway emergency).","PACU hypoxemia ladder mirrors the OR: obstruction/OSA (commonest), atelectasis, residual NMB (uncoordinated, floppy - TOFR it), opioid hypoventilation (capnograph/rate), aspiration, edema (NPPE after obstruction; TACO after transfusion), pneumothorax after lines/laparoscopy."],"tx":["Delayed emergence: support airway/ventilation while diagnosing; targeted reversal - naloxone in 40 mcg increments (full 0.4 mg boluses precipitate hypertension/edema/pain crises), flumazenil 0.2 mg cautiously (seizure risk in dependence), sugammadex for residual rocuronium; glucose corrected immediately.","Stridor: humidified O2, sit up, nebulized epinephrine (2.5-5 mL of 1:1000), dexamethasone 4-8 mg IV, CPAP/heliox as bridges; reintubate EARLY with a smaller tube if progressing - and for the neck hematoma: OPEN THE WOUND at the bedside (release the clot) while mobilizing the OR; do not sedate-and-wait a compressing airway.","Hypoxemia: sit up, jaw thrust/nasal airway, CPAP for OSA (home machine to PACU), reverse the reversible (sugammadex, careful naloxone), recruit atelectasis, treat the specific lesion; escalate to HFNC/NIV/reintubation on trajectory, not on despair."],"tech":["Handoffs armed for prediction: tell PACU which debt is coming (OSA + morphine, difficult airway grade, blocks in place, EBL and last gas) - the emergency you predicted is the one they catch early."],"after":["Every PACU emergency gets a note (what, why, response), an updated plan for the ward (monitoring level, standing orders), and a threshold decision: who stays in step-down; unresolved delayed emergence is an ICU admission with neurology in motion."],"pearls":["Check the glucose before you philosophize about the brain.","Naloxone is a titration, not a verdict.","The strangling neck hematoma is treated with scissors at the bedside, not with a stat page and hope."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Delayed Emergence / PACU Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"ASA PACU Standards","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: Postanesthesia Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-emergence-delirium","name":"Emergence Delirium & Agitation","sec":"anes","present":["Two distinct beasts: pediatric emergence delirium (2-6 years, sevoflurane era - thrashing, inconsolable, no eye contact, not comforted by parents, 10-30 min self-limited) and adult emergence agitation (young men, ETT/catheter irritation, alcohol/substance history, anxiety, breath-holding surgeries like ENT/oral) - both dangerous to wounds, lines, staff, and the patient."],"dx":["Pediatric: distinguish delirium from PAIN and fear - PAED scale scores the delirium features (eye contact, purposefulness, awareness); a child who calms with parents/analgesia was hurting or scared, not delirious.","Adult: exclude the ORGANIC drivers first - hypoxemia (oximeter before haloperidol, always), hypercapnia, hypoglycemia, full bladder, pain, residual NMB (terrifying paralyzed-awake agitation - TOF it), withdrawal (alcohol/benzos/nicotine), stroke; agitation is a symptom until proven a syndrome."],"tx":["Pediatric prevention: propofol 1 mg/kg slug at the end of sevoflurane anesthesia, dexmedetomidine (0.3-0.5 mcg/kg), TIVA for the repeat offender, adequate analgesia (caudal/regional), calm low-stimulation wake-up, parents at the bedside as reunion not restraint.","Pediatric treatment: protect from injury, dim and quiet, small propofol or dexmedetomidine doses if severe/prolonged - and clock it: typical episodes burn out in 10-30 min.","Adult treatment after organics excluded: reorient with a calm single voice, treat pain, remove the removable irritants (deflate/remove catheter when appropriate, extubate the ready patient rather than wrestling the tube), dexmedetomidine is the elegant drug (cooperative sedation without respiratory depression), small haloperidol/droperidol for danger-level agitation; physical restraint minimal, brief, documented."],"tech":["Anticipate by history: prior emergence agitation recurs - write the plan (TIVA, dexmedetomidine running at closure, early tube out or deep extubation strategy, security-aware staffing for the high-risk)."],"after":["Document episode, triggers found, and what worked - it changes the next anesthetic; pediatric parents get the 'this was distressing but expected and harmless' debrief; adult patients get screened later for awareness/distressing recall if the agitation suggested it."],"pearls":["Agitation with a falling sat is hypoxemia wearing a behavioral costume.","The paralyzed-but-awake patient fights with their blood pressure before their fists - unexplained hypertension+tachycardia at 'emergence' deserves a TOF and a hypnotic NOW.","For the thrashing 4-year-old: safety, patience, and a parent beat pharmacology in most rounds."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Emergence Delirium","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"SPA Pediatric Resources","u":"https://www.pedsanesthesia.org"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-periop-meds","name":"Perioperative Medication Management (Anticoagulants, GLP-1s, Home Meds)","sec":"anes","present":["Every preop visit ends with the medication list interrogation: what continues, what stops, when, and what bridges - errors here cancel cases, cause strokes, or cause bleeding."],"dx":["Triage the list into: continue (most cardiac/pulmonary/psych/thyroid/reflux meds), hold-by-clock (anticoagulants/antiplatelets, diabetes drugs), hold-by-risk (SGLT2i, GLP-1), and special-handling (steroids, MAOIs, buprenorphine, immunosuppressants).","Anticoagulant timing (neuraxial per ASRA; surgical bleeding risk per procedure): warfarin 5 days (INR check; bridge with LMWH only for high thrombotic risk - mechanical mitral, recent VTE/stroke, per CHEST/BRIDGE lessons: most AF does NOT need bridging), DOACs 24-48 h for standard/high-bleed surgery (~72 h for neuraxial; longer in renal impairment for dabigatran), prophylactic LMWH 12 h / therapeutic 24 h, clopidogrel/ticagrelor 5 days, prasugrel 7, ASPIRIN continue for secondary prevention in most non-closed-space surgery; coronary stents: defer elective surgery (ideally >=6 months DES; minimum windows per ACC) and never stop DAPT early without cardiology."],"tx":["Diabetes: hold oral agents day-of (metformin per local policy), SGLT2 inhibitors 3-4 days (euglycemic DKA - check ketones if suspicious), basal insulin reduced ~20-25%, sliding correction perioperatively; GLP-1 agonists per ASA/multisociety guidance - hold daily day-of/weekly ~1 week where practical OR proceed with full-stomach precautions/gastric ultrasound (do not blanket-cancel).","Cardiovascular: continue beta-blockers (withdrawal harms; do not START high-dose day-of - POISE), continue statins, hold ACEi/ARB the morning for most GA (refractory hypotension) unless heart-failure indication argues otherwise; clonidine continues (rebound).","Steroids: chronic >=5 mg prednisone equivalents - continue home dose; stress-dose (e.g., hydrocortisone 50-100 mg then taper) for major surgery/HPA-suppression signs rather than reflexively for everyone.","Psych/neuro: continue SSRIs/SNRIs/antipsychotics/antiepileptics/parkinson meds ON TIME (levodopa gaps are disasters); MAOIs - modern practice often continues with an MAOI-safe anesthetic (avoid meperidine, indirect sympathomimetics); lithium levels checked; buprenorphine CONTINUES."],"tech":["Write times, not vibes: each held drug gets a last-dose timestamp and a restart plan in the chart; the preop phone call the day before catches the aspirin swallowed with breakfast."],"after":["Restart matrix postop: anticoagulation restarted per bleeding-risk timeline with the surgeon (prophylactic doses usually within 24 h; therapeutic per procedure), home meds resumed as GI function allows with renal function rechecked, held SGLT2i restarted when eating and stable; document the plan so the intern at 2 AM is not guessing."],"pearls":["Bridging anticoagulation is the exception that everyone treats as the rule - most AF patients just pause and resume.","The forgotten SGLT2 inhibitor causes the normoglycemic acidosis nobody thinks to test for - check ketones.","Parkinson's medication is dosed by the clock, and the clock does not stop for surgery."],"refs":[{"t":"ASRA Anticoagulation Guidelines","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"ACC/AHA Perioperative Guidelines","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"ASA GLP-1 Perioperative Guidance","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"anes-mins","name":"Perioperative Myocardial Injury & Troponin Surveillance","sec":"anes","present":["Myocardial injury after noncardiac surgery (MINS): troponin elevation within 30 days postop from ischemic mechanisms - mostly SILENT (>=80% have no chest pain, masked by analgesia/sedation), affecting roughly 1 in 5-7 elevated-risk patients, and strongly associated with 30-day mortality."],"dx":["Who to surveil (per CCS and supporting VISION data): elevated-risk patients (age >=65, or 45-64 with significant cardiovascular disease, elevated NT-proBNP where used, high RCRI) undergoing inpatient noncardiac surgery - troponin before surgery (baseline) and daily x 2-3 days after.","Interpret the elevation: MINS = ischemic-pattern injury (supply-demand mismatch type 2 predominates; plaque events type 1 minority) - exclude nonischemic troponin causes (sepsis, PE, renal, myocarditis); ECG, symptoms review, and hemodynamic context classify; formal MI criteria (ischemic evidence) define the subset with the worst prognosis."],"tx":["Intraoperative prevention is the anesthesiologist's lever: avoid sustained hypotension (MAP <65 associations), avoid marked tachycardia and anemia extremes, temperature and pain control - the injury is mostly a mismatch disease and the mismatch happens on your watch.","Confirmed MINS management (evidence-informed): optimize medical therapy - aspirin and statin for most, cardiology involvement, manage the precipitants (anemia, hypoxemia, sepsis, hypotension); dabigatran reduced vascular events post-MINS in MANAGE (bleeding-tradeoff conversation); type 1 events (dynamic ECG, plaque physiology) go down the ACS pathway with surgical-bleeding-adjusted antithrombotics and invasive timing."],"tech":["Build the pathway, not the heroics: standing troponin orders for qualifying patients, an interpretation cascade (who gets called for what delta), and unit protocols - surveillance without a response plan is expensive anxiety."],"after":["Before discharge: cardiology follow-up arranged, risk-factor therapy documented (statin intensity, BP, antiplatelet decisions), and the patient TOLD - a silent event with a 30-day mortality signal deserves an informed patient and PCP letter.","Long view: MINS flags a vascular patient for life - secondary prevention is the durable intervention."],"pearls":["The postoperative MI does not announce itself - the troponin you did not send is the one you will not treat.","Prevention is boring: pressure, pulse, hemoglobin, temperature, pain - guarded for 48 hours.","A troponin leak in a septic, tachycardic POD1 patient is a mismatch to fix, not automatically a cath to book."],"refs":[{"t":"CCS Perioperative Cardiac Risk Guidelines","u":"https://ccs.ca"},{"t":"VISION / MANAGE Studies","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ACC Perioperative Resources","u":"https://www.acc.org"}]},{"id":"anes-temperature","name":"Perioperative Temperature Management & Shivering","sec":"anes","present":["Inadvertent perioperative hypothermia (<36 C) is near-universal without prevention: anesthesia abolishes behavioral thermoregulation, vasodilation redistributes core heat to the periphery (the phase-1 drop of 1-1.5 C in the first hour), then the cold OR drains the rest."],"dx":["Monitor core temperature for any anesthetic where change is anticipated (practically: cases >30-60 min, all neuraxial majors, all pediatrics): nasopharyngeal/esophageal (GA), bladder/zero-heat-flux alternatives; skin lags and lies.","Know the costs of 35-point-something: surgical site infection (classic Kurz data), coagulopathy (enzymes are thermolabile - the bleeding cold patient), transfusion increase, MI/adverse cardiac association, drug metabolism slowed (NMB duration prolonged), shivering (oxygen consumption up 2-4x), recovery delayed, patients remember being cold more than almost anything."],"tx":["PREWARMING is the highest-yield move: 20-30+ min of forced-air warming before induction fills the peripheral heat reservoir and blunts redistribution - the drop you prevent beats the drop you chase.","Intraop: forced-air warming as the workhorse, fluid warmers for >~1 L or any blood, ambient temperature raised for pediatric/burn/trauma exposure, humidified circuits marginal, cover everything not being operated on.","Shivering treatment (PACU): warm first (it is usually thermogenic), then meperidine 12.5-25 mg IV (the classic anti-shivering opioid), dexmedetomidine, or clonidine; rule out the non-thermal mimics (transfusion reaction, sepsis rigors, emergence phenomena)."],"tech":["Neuraxial anesthesia hypothermia is sneaky: vasodilated, redistributing, and the patient cannot shiver below the block while feeling deceptively 'warm' - monitor and warm cesareans and spinals like the general anesthetics they thermally are.","Deliberate exceptions exist (cardiac surgery cooling, targeted temperature management) - normothermia is the rule for everyone else."],"after":["PACU: continue active warming to >=36 C before discharge criteria are met; document temperatures like the vital sign it is; cold + oozing = check the temperature before ordering the plasma (warm the patient, help the enzymes)."],"pearls":["The 30 minutes of prewarming does more than the 3 hours of intraop chasing.","A cold coagulopathy will not appear on a lab warmed to 37 degrees before testing - treat the patient's temperature, not the machine's.","Shivering quadruples oxygen demand in exactly the patients least able to pay for it."],"refs":[{"t":"NICE/ASA Perioperative Hypothermia Guidance","u":"https://www.nice.org.uk"},{"t":"StatPearls: Perioperative Hypothermia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"APSF: Temperature Management","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-positioning","name":"Positioning Injuries & Peripheral Nerve Protection","sec":"anes","present":["The anesthetized patient cannot complain, shift, or protect a stretched nerve for hours: peripheral nerve injury (ulnar > brachial plexus > lumbosacral in claims data), pressure injuries, eye injuries, and position-specific physiologic insults are anesthesia-team liabilities in every case."],"dx":["Preop: document pre-existing neuropathy, diabetes, prior injuries, and range-of-motion limits BEFORE positioning (the post-op deficit needs a baseline); identify high-risk positions: prone (eyes, brachial plexus, abdominal pressure), lithotomy (peroneal nerve at the fibular head, compartment syndrome in long cases), lateral (down-side plexus/eye/ear, axillary roll), steep Trendelenburg (brachial plexus from shoulder braces, facial/airway edema, ocular pressure), sitting (VAE, sciatic stretch).","Postop new deficit: examine and map it (sensory/motor, which nerve territory), check for reversible compression ongoing, distinguish neurapraxia (majority - resolves days-to-weeks) from concerning dense/progressive deficits; compartment syndrome after prolonged lithotomy/lateral cases presents as pain-out-of-proportion in the leg - pressure measurement and fasciotomy thinking, not reassurance."],"tx":["Prevention is positioning craft: ulnar - arms supinated/neutral, elbows padded, <90 degrees abduction; brachial plexus - avoid contralateral head rotation with abducted arms, no shoulder braces pressing the plexus in Trendelenburg (use bean-bag/non-slip alternatives); peroneal - pad the fibular head in lithotomy, limit time; eyes - taped/protected, NO pressure on the globe (prone headrests checked and RE-checked - ischemic optic neuropathy and central retinal artery occlusion are the blinding disasters, associated with prone spine surgery, long duration, hypotension, anemia)."],"tech":["The team lifts and turns on a coordinated count with the airway owner commanding; after EVERY reposition: re-check eyes, ears, nose (prone tubes/pillows), genitals/breasts, lines and tube depth, pressure points, and pulses in abnormal arm positions; document positioning and padding in the record (medicolegally: what was done, not just 'padded appropriately').","Long cases: scheduled position micro-checks (e.g., hourly eye/pressure-point sweep) written into the flow."],"after":["Postop nerve deficit protocol: examine, document precisely, reassure for classic mild neurapraxia with follow-up scheduled, neurology referral + electrodiagnostics (timed ~3+ weeks for meaningful EMG) for significant/progressive lesions; visual complaint after prone surgery = ophthalmology EMERGENTLY.","Report through the safety system - positioning injuries are system-preventable and trend-trackable."],"pearls":["The ulnar nerve is the tort of anesthesia - pad it, supinate it, and write down that you did.","After the table moves, everything you checked is unchecked.","Post-prone visual loss is never 'wait and see' - it is ophthalmology at whatever hour it is."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA Practice Advisory: Perioperative Peripheral Neuropathies","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"ASA Practice Advisory: Perioperative Visual Loss","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Patient Positioning Injuries","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-awareness","name":"Awareness Under Anesthesia & Depth Monitoring","sec":"anes","present":["Accidental awareness with recall during intended general anesthesia: incidence ~1-2/1000 by structured interview (lower by spontaneous report - NAP5), concentrated in: paralyzed patients, RSI/emergency surgery, cesarean under GA, cardiac surgery, TIVA with infusion failures, hemodynamically-limited 'light' anesthetics; consequences include PTSD in a substantial fraction - this is a psychological injury with an anesthetic cause."],"dx":["Intraoperative suspicion: unexplained hypertension/tachycardia/tearing/sweating in a paralyzed patient (unreliable but actionable), infusion pump/IV inspection in TIVA, end-tidal agent unexpectedly low; the paralyzed patient has no other voice.","Postoperative detection: modified Brice interview questions when suspicion or risk existed ('last thing you remember before sleep, first on waking, anything in between, dreams?') - asked at PACU AND at a delayed interval (recall can surface late)."],"tx":["Prevention bundle: end-tidal agent monitoring with LOW-AGENT ALARMS enabled (age-adjusted MAC >=0.7 with NMB), processed EEG (BIS/SedLine 40-60) for TIVA-with-paralysis (NICE-endorsed use case) and reasonable in high-risk GAs, checklists for vaporizer filling and TIVA line/pump integrity (visible IV site, anti-reflux valves, occlusion alarms), minimize paralysis when not needed and monitor it quantitatively, and treat 'the pressure is too low to give more anesthetic' as a pressor problem, not a hypnosis-rationing problem (ketamine/midazolam amnesia strategies in the shocked patient).","If awareness is suspected DURING the case: deepen immediately, benzodiazepine amnestic dose is reasonable, verify delivery systems, document."],"tech":["Depth is triangulated, never single-sourced: agent analysis or pEEG + hemodynamic trend + clinical signs; both monitor types fail (pEEG confounded by ketamine/dexmedetomidine/NMB artifacts and age extremes)."],"after":["A patient reporting awareness gets: belief, an honest explanation of what likely happened, documentation, early psychological support referral (early intervention mitigates PTSD trajectory), institutional reporting, and the record flagged for future anesthetics - dismissal is the second injury.","Debrief the delivery-system cause: most TIVA awareness is a plumbing failure that will recur until fixed."],"pearls":["In the paralyzed patient the anesthetic proof burden is on YOU - alarm the agent, monitor the brain, or both.","'The pressure can't tolerate anesthesia' is how awareness cases begin - support the pressure, deliver amnesia.","Believe the patient who reports awareness: the interview costs minutes, the dismissal costs them years."],"refs":[{"t":"NAP5: Accidental Awareness During GA","u":"https://www.nationalauditprojects.org.uk"},{"t":"ASA Practice Advisory: Intraoperative Awareness","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: TIVA Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"}]},{"id":"anes-machine-vent","name":"Anesthesia Machine, Ventilator Modes & Circuit Problems","sec":"anes","present":["The machine is your co-anesthetist: gas delivery, vaporization, ventilation, scavenging, and monitoring in one stack - and machine/circuit problems (disconnection, leak, obstruction, valve failure, absorbent exhaustion) present as patient crises until proven mechanical."],"dx":["Pre-use check (automated + manual per manufacturer/FDA-style checkout): pipeline and cylinder pressures (backup O2 cylinder open-check-close, >~1000 psi), flowmeters/hypoxic guard, vaporizer filled/seated/interlocked, circuit leak and compliance test, valves (unidirectional flutter), absorbent color/age, scavenging, suction, AMBU BAG PRESENT (the ultimate backup), ventilator function.","Intraop problem patterns: sudden low-pressure/apnea alarm + absent EtCO2 = disconnection (commonest, usually at the Y-piece); rising baseline EtCO2 (inspired CO2 >0) = exhausted absorbent or incompetent expiratory valve; high peak pressures = kink/secretion/bronchospasm/light patient vs circuit obstruction (differentiate: disconnect and hand-bag with the self-inflating bag - if it resolves, the machine was the patient's problem); sustained high pressure = stuck APL/ventilator relief failure (barotrauma in seconds - open the circuit)."],"tx":["The universal machine-crisis move: DISCONNECT from the machine and ventilate with the self-inflating (Ambu) bag on cylinder/wall O2 while a colleague troubleshoots - never debug a machine through a desaturating patient; convert to TIVA if the vaporizer/delivery is suspect.","Hypoxic delivery events (pipeline crossover - rare, historic, lethal): oxygen analyzer reading is the ONLY trustworthy FiO2 - if inspired O2 falls despite 'O2' flowing, disconnect from the pipeline, open the cylinder, and hand-ventilate."],"tech":["Ventilator mode literacy: volume control (guaranteed TV, variable pressure - watch peaks with changing compliance), pressure control (guaranteed pressure, variable TV - watch volumes with insufflation/position), PSV for spontaneous LMA/deep-extubation phases, PCV-VG hybrids common; set: TV 6-8 mL/kg PREDICTED weight, PEEP ~5 baseline, rate to EtCO2, and re-set after every position/pneumoperitoneum change.","Fresh gas flow economics and safety: low flows save agent and heat/humidity but demand analyzer trust; know YOUR machine's fresh-gas coupling behavior."],"after":["Any machine event: swap or tag-out the machine, report through equipment safety, and document the timeline - the next patient inherits your troubleshooting; incorporate the failure into the departmental teaching file (machine stories are the best simulation scripts)."],"pearls":["The self-inflating bag on the back of the machine is the most important ventilator in the room.","Inspired CO2 above zero is the machine confessing - change the absorbent or find the valve.","When the alarm and the patient disagree, believe the patient; when the FiO2 analyzer and the pipeline label disagree, believe the analyzer."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA/FDA Anesthesia Machine Pre-Use Checkout","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"APSF: Machine & Equipment Safety","u":"https://www.apsf.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anesthesia Machine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-pocus","name":"Point-of-Care Ultrasound for the Anesthesiologist","sec":"anes","present":["The probe answers perioperative questions in minutes that used to take consults and transport: gastric contents before induction, cardiac function in the hypotensive, lung pathology in the desaturating, airway anatomy before FONA, volume responsiveness, and every vascular access and regional block you place."],"dx":["Gastric POCUS: antrum in supine then RIGHT lateral decubitus - empty/flat antrum = low risk; clear fluid graded by antral cross-section (qualitative grade 0-2; calculated volumes <1.5 mL/kg reassuring); solid content = full stomach, plan accordingly (the GLP-1-era tiebreaker).","Focused cardiac (FoCUS/FATE views): the hypotension differential in four windows - hyperdynamic-empty (hypovolemia/vasoplegia), globally poor LV, RV dilated/strained (embolism - with McConnell's pattern), pericardial effusion/tamponade physiology; qualitative gestalt is the resident-attainable skill.","Lung ultrasound: sliding present rules out pneumothorax AT THAT POINT (absent sliding + lung point rules in), B-lines = interstitial fluid (edema), consolidation/effusion patterns - faster and more sensitive than the supine chest film for most PACU/OR questions.","Airway ultrasound: cricothyroid membrane identification and marking in the anticipated difficult airway (transverse 'string of pearls' to midline) - do it BEFORE induction in the neck you cannot palpate."],"tx":["Integrate, don't ornament: the scan changes a decision (RSI vs routine, fluids vs pressor vs inotrope, needle decompression vs recruit, block location) and gets documented with the decision it changed; escalate to formal echo/radiology for definitive characterization."],"tech":["Machine craft: linear probe (vascular/airway/blocks), phased array (cardiac), curvilinear (gastric/abdomen/lung depth); optimize depth/gain before interpreting; save representative images per institutional documentation standards.","Skill maintenance is deliberate: scan normals routinely, pair findings against formal studies, log toward institutional/ASA-framework competencies - POCUS confidence without calibration is its own hazard."],"after":["Handoffs include the sonographic findings and their timestamps ('sliding present at 14:00' ages fast after central lines and positive pressure); repeat the scan when the clinical question repeats."],"pearls":["The stomach you scanned beats the fasting history the patient estimated.","In undifferentiated hypotension the probe sorts empty from failing from compressed faster than any drug trial does.","Mark the cricothyroid membrane while everything is calm - future-you, mid-CICO, sends thanks."],"refs":[{"t":"ASA POCUS Resources & Certificate Framework","u":"https://www.asahq.org"},{"t":"ASRA/ASE POCUS Guidance","u":"https://www.asra.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Gastric / Lung / Cardiac POCUS","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"anes-eras","name":"Enhanced Recovery (ERAS) & the Anesthesiologist's Role","sec":"anes","present":["ERAS pathways are multidisciplinary bundles (20+ elements) that shorten stay and complications after major surgery - and the anesthesiologist owns or co-owns the highest-leverage elements: fasting/carbohydrate strategy, multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia, fluid management, PONV prevention, normothermia, and early recovery milestones."],"dx":["Know the bundle for your service line (colorectal is the archetype; ERAS Society guidelines exist across specialties): preop counseling + expectation setting, no prolonged fasting (clears to 2 h + carbohydrate drink), minimal/no bowel prep variance by procedure, no routine premedication sedatives (they delay recovery), VTE and antibiotic prophylaxis on time.","Audit thinking: ERAS works via COMPLIANCE - units that measure element adherence and outcomes (registry/audit cycles) keep the gains; the pathway on paper saves no one."],"tx":["Intraop anesthetic elements: opioid-sparing multimodal core (regional/neuraxial or infiltration analgesia matched to the operation - e.g., mid-thoracic epidural for open, TAP/wound catheters + systemic multimodal for laparoscopic), short-acting agents titrated for rapid emergence, lung-protective ventilation, goal-directed/near-zero-balance-avoiding fluid strategy (moderately liberal baseline, GDT in the high-risk), active normothermia, glucose kept reasonable, multimodal PONV prophylaxis as default, quantitative NMB reversal (residual paralysis sabotages early mobilization and breathing).","Postop elements the anesthesia/pain team drives: scheduled acetaminophen+NSAID, opioids as rescue not scaffold, early oral intake (often day 0), lines/catheters OUT early (urinary catheters especially - they tether mobilization), ambulation targets from day 0-1, ileus prevention (chewing gum trivia aside: opioid minimization is the real lever)."],"tech":["Be the pathway's anesthetic conscience: verify the carb drink happened, write the multimodal orders BEFORE PACU, flag deviations (the 'NPO after midnight' order that crept back) - pathway drift is constant and silent.","Personalize inside the protocol: ERAS is a default, not a cage - the frail, the opioid-tolerant, and the complex get the bundle ADAPTED and documented, not abandoned."],"after":["Contribute to the audit loop: length of stay, complications, opioid consumption, compliance dashboards; feed failures back (the block that didn't work, the PONV cluster) - ERAS is a quality-improvement engine wearing a clinical-pathway costume; residents who learn the audit habit run better systems forever."],"pearls":["ERAS is mostly the disciplined removal of harmful traditions: starving, drowning, over-sedating, and tethering patients.","The elements are ordinary; the outcomes come from doing all of them, every time, and measuring it.","Your multimodal plan written at 7 AM determines whether the patient walks at 5 PM."],"refs":[{"t":"ERAS Society Guidelines","u":"https://erassociety.org"},{"t":"ASER/POQI Consensus Statements","u":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"t":"ASA Practice Resources","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"cards-ecg-interpretation","name":"ECG Interpretation","sec":"cards","present":["The 12-lead ECG is the foundational cardiac test; a systematic read covers rate, rhythm, axis, intervals (PR, QRS, QT), and morphology (P waves, QRS, ST segments, T waves)","It diagnoses arrhythmias, conduction disease, ischemia/infarction (by territory), chamber enlargement, and electrolyte/drug effects","Territory localization: inferior (II, III, aVF), anterior/septal (V1-V4), lateral (I, aVL, V5-V6) - maps coronary distributions"],"dx":["Rate/rhythm first (regular vs irregular, P-QRS relationship), then axis and intervals; a prolonged QTc predisposes to torsades de pointes","ISCHEMIA/INFARCTION: ST ELEVATION (transmural/STEMI), ST depression/T-wave inversion (ischemia/NSTEMI); reciprocal changes support true infarction; new LBBB with symptoms is a STEMI-equivalent consideration","Recognize high-risk patterns: Wellens (LAD), de Winter T-waves, Brugada, hyperkalemia (peaked T -> widening), Wolff-Parkinson-White (short PR, delta wave)"],"tx":["Act on the diagnosis: activate reperfusion for STEMI, treat arrhythmias per rhythm, correct electrolytes/drug effects, and compare with prior ECGs","Serial ECGs for evolving symptoms (a single normal ECG does not exclude ACS)","Escalate ambiguous cases with biomarkers, imaging, or continuous monitoring"],"pearls":["Localize infarcts by leads: inferior (II/III/aVF), anteroseptal (V1-V4), lateral (I/aVL/V5-V6); look for reciprocal ST depression to confirm true STEMI","Prolonged QTc risks torsades - review QT-prolonging drugs and electrolytes (low K, Mg, Ca)","New LBBB or a paced rhythm complicates STEMI reading - use Sgarbossa criteria; a single normal ECG does not rule out ACS (repeat serially)","Learn can't-miss patterns: Wellens, de Winter, Brugada, WPW, and hyperkalemia's progression"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Electrocardiogram","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549803/"},{"t":"StatPearls: ECG Interpretation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537260/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiac-biomarkers-chest-pain","name":"Cardiac Biomarkers & Chest Pain Evaluation","sec":"cards","present":["Acute chest pain evaluation risk-stratifies for ACS and other life-threats (aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism, tension pneumothorax, esophageal rupture) using history, ECG, and biomarkers","HIGH-SENSITIVITY CARDIAC TROPONIN is the key biomarker for myocardial injury - it is sensitive and specific for myocyte necrosis but not specific to type 1 MI (it rises in many conditions)","Structured pathways (e.g., HEART score, ESC 0/1-hour hs-troponin algorithms) combine risk factors, ECG, and troponin to rule in/out efficiently"],"dx":["Serial hs-troponin (baseline and 1-3 hours) detects a rising/falling pattern indicating acute myocardial injury; a stable elevated level suggests chronic injury","Interpret troponin in context: elevations occur in heart failure, myocarditis, PE, sepsis, renal failure, and tachyarrhythmia (type 2 MI/non-ischemic injury) - not only type 1 MI","Use validated risk scores (HEART) and ECG to guide disposition; consider CT coronary angiography for low-intermediate risk to exclude CAD"],"tx":["Rule-in ACS: treat per STEMI/NSTEMI pathways; rule-out low-risk chest pain safely for discharge with follow-up","Address alternative diagnoses (dissection, PE) with targeted testing when suspected","Distinguish type 1 MI (plaque rupture -> needs antithrombotic/revascularization) from type 2 (supply-demand mismatch -> treat the underlying stressor)"],"pearls":["High-sensitivity troponin detects a rising/falling pattern of acute injury - a single elevated value is not automatically a type 1 MI (many non-ischemic causes)","Type 1 MI (plaque rupture) differs from type 2 (supply-demand mismatch, e.g., tachycardia/anemia/sepsis) - management differs","Use structured pathways (HEART score, ESC 0/1-hour algorithm) to rule ACS in/out efficiently","Always consider aortic dissection and PE in acute chest pain - a missed dissection is catastrophic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Biomarkers","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545216/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Troponin","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507805/"}]},{"id":"cards-stress-testing","name":"Cardiac Stress Testing","sec":"cards","present":["Stress testing evaluates for inducible myocardial ischemia and risk-stratifies known/suspected coronary disease; modalities combine a STRESSOR (exercise or pharmacologic) with a DETECTION method (ECG, echo, or nuclear perfusion)","EXERCISE is preferred when the patient can exercise (provides functional capacity/prognostic data); PHARMACOLOGIC stress (vasodilator - regadenoson/adenosine/dipyridamole for perfusion imaging; dobutamine for stress echo) is used when they cannot","Choice of imaging adjunct depends on baseline ECG interpretability and the clinical question"],"dx":["Exercise ECG alone is limited if the baseline ECG is uninterpretable (LBBB, paced, LVH with strain, digoxin, pre-excitation) - add imaging (echo or nuclear)","Positive findings: exercise-induced ST depression, wall-motion abnormalities (stress echo), or reversible perfusion defects (nuclear) indicate ischemia; fixed defects suggest prior infarction","High-risk results (large ischemic burden, early/marked ST changes, hypotension, poor functional capacity) prompt angiography"],"tx":["Use results to guide management: medical optimization for mild/moderate ischemia, and angiography +/- revascularization for high-risk or refractory ischemia","Avoid stress testing when it will not change management or when angiography is already indicated (e.g., ongoing ACS - do not stress-test unstable patients)","Match modality to the patient (exercise capacity, ECG interpretability, imaging availability)"],"pearls":["Exercise is preferred over pharmacologic stress when feasible - functional capacity is powerful prognostic information","An uninterpretable baseline ECG (LBBB, paced, LVH strain, digoxin) requires an imaging adjunct (echo or nuclear), not plain exercise ECG","Vasodilator stress (regadenoson/adenosine) is for perfusion imaging; dobutamine for stress echo when vasodilators are contraindicated (e.g., severe reactive airway disease)","Do NOT stress-test unstable ACS, severe symptomatic AS, or when angiography is already clearly indicated"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Exercise Stress Testing","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482500/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pharmacologic Stress Testing","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558921/"}]},{"id":"cards-echocardiography","name":"Echocardiography","sec":"cards","present":["Echocardiography uses ultrasound to assess cardiac structure and function; TRANSTHORACIC (TTE) is the first-line, noninvasive workhorse and TRANSESOPHAGEAL (TEE) gives superior posterior/valve/atrial detail","It quantifies LEFT VENTRICULAR EJECTION FRACTION, wall motion, chamber sizes, valve function (stenosis/regurgitation), diastolic function, pericardial effusion, and estimates pulmonary pressures","Point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) rapidly assesses effusion/tamponade, gross function, and volume status at the bedside"],"dx":["TTE: measure LVEF, regional wall-motion abnormalities (coronary territories), valve gradients/regurgitation, diastolic parameters, and RV function; Doppler estimates pressures/gradients","TEE indications: suspected endocarditis (vegetations), cardiac source of embolism/LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE thrombus (before cardioversion), prosthetic valve evaluation, and intraprocedural guidance","Stress echo detects inducible wall-motion abnormalities (ischemia) or contractile reserve"],"tx":["Use findings to drive management: guideline thresholds for valve intervention, HF classification by EF, and detection of complications (thrombus, effusion, vegetation)","TEE before cardioversion of AF >48 hours (or uncertain duration) if not adequately anticoagulated - to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus","Serial echo to follow LV function, valve disease progression, and therapy response"],"pearls":["TTE is first-line; TEE is superior for endocarditis vegetations, left atrial appendage thrombus, prosthetic valves, and intraprocedural guidance","Exclude left atrial appendage thrombus with TEE before cardioverting AF of >48 hours/unknown duration in inadequately anticoagulated patients","LVEF classifies heart failure (reduced vs preserved) and drives device/therapy eligibility","POCUS quickly identifies pericardial tamponade physiology (RV diastolic collapse, IVC plethora) at the bedside"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Echocardiography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558940/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Transesophageal Echocardiography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539756/"}]},{"id":"cards-advanced-imaging-ct-cmr","name":"Advanced Cardiac Imaging - CT, CMR & Nuclear","sec":"cards","present":["Advanced imaging complements echo: CORONARY CT ANGIOGRAPHY (CCTA) is excellent for EXCLUDING coronary disease (high negative predictive value) in low-intermediate-risk chest pain; the coronary artery calcium score refines risk in primary prevention","CARDIAC MRI (CMR) is the reference standard for ventricular volumes/function and for TISSUE CHARACTERIZATION (late gadolinium enhancement patterns distinguish infarct from myocarditis, infiltration, and cardiomyopathies)","NUCLEAR cardiology (SPECT/PET myocardial perfusion) assesses ischemia and viability; specialized scans diagnose cardiac amyloidosis (pyrophosphate/PYP for transthyretin) and sarcoidosis (FDG-PET)"],"dx":["CCTA: rules out obstructive CAD with high NPV; calcium score (Agatston) stratifies ASCVD risk (a zero score is reassuring in primary prevention)","CMR: quantifies EF/volumes and characterizes myocardium - subendocardial/transmural LGE (infarct) vs mid-wall/patchy LGE (non-ischemic: myocarditis, sarcoid, HCM), and mapping for amyloid/edema","Nuclear: reversible defects (ischemia) vs fixed (scar); viability imaging guides revascularization benefit; bone-avid tracer uptake supports ATTR amyloidosis"],"tx":["Use CCTA to safely avoid invasive angiography in low-intermediate-risk patients; use ischemia/viability data to select patients for revascularization","CMR tissue characterization directs diagnosis-specific therapy (e.g., immunosuppression for sarcoid, ATTR-directed therapy for amyloid)","Choose the modality by the question (anatomy vs ischemia vs viability vs tissue characterization) and radiation/contrast considerations"],"pearls":["Coronary CT angiography has a high negative predictive value - great for ruling OUT CAD in low-intermediate-risk chest pain; a zero calcium score is reassuring in primary prevention","CMR is the reference standard for volumes/function and for tissue characterization - LGE pattern distinguishes ischemic (subendocardial/transmural) from non-ischemic (mid-wall/patchy) disease","PYP/pyrophosphate scintigraphy diagnoses transthyretin (ATTR) cardiac amyloidosis noninvasively; FDG-PET evaluates cardiac sarcoidosis","Match modality to the clinical question: anatomy (CCTA), ischemia (nuclear/stress), viability (PET/CMR), tissue (CMR)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Magnetic Resonance Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556122/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Coronary CT Angiography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544277/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiac-catheterization","name":"Cardiac Catheterization & Coronary Angiography","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiac catheterization is the invasive assessment of coronary anatomy (coronary angiography), hemodynamics (pressures, cardiac output, shunts), and structural/valve disease; it is both diagnostic and a platform for intervention (PCI)","Access is usually RADIAL (preferred - fewer bleeding/vascular complications and lower mortality than femoral) or femoral","It defines the coronary lesions that guide medical therapy vs PCI vs surgical (CABG) revascularization"],"dx":["Coronary angiography visualizes stenoses; PHYSIOLOGIC assessment (FRACTIONAL FLOW RESERVE/FFR or instantaneous wave-free ratio/iFR) determines whether an intermediate lesion is flow-limiting and warrants intervention","Right heart catheterization measures filling pressures, pulmonary pressures/resistance, and cardiac output (shock, pulmonary hypertension, HF, shunt, transplant evaluation)","Left ventriculography/hemodynamics and structural assessment as needed"],"tx":["Use anatomy + physiology to decide: medical therapy, PCI, or CABG (heart-team for complex/left-main/multivessel disease)","FFR/iFR-guided PCI improves outcomes over angiography alone for intermediate lesions - defer non-flow-limiting stenoses","Manage periprocedural anticoagulation, contrast use (renal protection), and access-site care"],"pearls":["Radial access is preferred over femoral - lower bleeding/vascular complications and mortality benefit","FFR/iFR assesses whether an intermediate stenosis is flow-limiting - defer non-ischemic lesions, treat significant ones (physiology beats eyeballing)","Right heart catheterization is the gold standard for filling pressures, pulmonary pressures/resistance, and cardiac output","Watch complications: bleeding/access-site, contrast nephropathy, coronary dissection, stroke, and (rare) cholesterol emboli"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Catheterization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556151/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fractional Flow Reserve","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551659/"}]},{"id":"cards-stable-ischemic-heart-disease","name":"Stable Ischemic Heart Disease","sec":"cards","present":["Stable ischemic heart disease (chronic coronary syndrome) is stable, predictable angina from fixed coronary stenoses causing demand-related ischemia, without the acute plaque rupture of ACS","Angina is typically substernal, provoked by exertion/emotion, and relieved by rest/nitroglycerin; atypical/anginal-equivalent presentations occur (dyspnea, especially in women, diabetics, elderly)","The goals are symptom relief and prevention of MI/death through risk-factor control"],"dx":["Assess pretest probability; noninvasive testing (stress imaging or coronary CT angiography) confirms ischemia/anatomy; invasive angiography for high-risk features or refractory symptoms","Quantify ischemic burden and LV function; identify high-risk anatomy (left main, proximal LAD, multivessel)","Optimize evaluation of risk factors (lipids, diabetes, blood pressure, smoking)"],"tx":["GUIDELINE-DIRECTED MEDICAL THERAPY is the foundation: antianginals (beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, nitrates, ranolazine) plus event prevention (high-intensity statin, aspirin, and risk-factor control)","REVASCULARIZATION (PCI or CABG) for refractory symptoms despite medical therapy or for high-risk anatomy (left main, multivessel with reduced EF/diabetes -> CABG); ISCHEMIA trial showed no mortality benefit of routine early revascularization over optimal medical therapy in stable disease","Cardiac rehabilitation and aggressive secondary prevention"],"pearls":["Optimal medical therapy is the foundation; the ISCHEMIA trial found routine revascularization did not reduce death/MI vs medical therapy in stable disease (revascularize for symptoms or high-risk anatomy)","Left main or multivessel disease (especially with diabetes/reduced EF) favors CABG over PCI (SYNTAX/FREEDOM)","High-intensity statin, aspirin, and risk-factor control prevent events; antianginals relieve symptoms","Women, diabetics, and the elderly often present atypically (dyspnea/anginal equivalents)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stable Angina","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554558/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Coronary Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560672/"}]},{"id":"cards-acs-nstemi","name":"Acute Coronary Syndrome - UA/NSTEMI","sec":"cards","present":["Non-ST-elevation ACS spans UNSTABLE ANGINA (ischemic symptoms without biomarker rise) and NSTEMI (myocardial injury with a rising/falling troponin) - usually from non-occlusive/partial thrombus on a ruptured plaque","Presents with rest/crescendo angina or an anginal equivalent; ECG may show ST depression or T-wave inversion (or be nondiagnostic)","Risk stratification (TIMI/GRACE scores) guides timing of invasive management"],"dx":["Serial ECGs and hs-troponin establish the diagnosis (rising/falling pattern = NSTEMI; normal = unstable angina if symptoms are ischemic); continuous monitoring for arrhythmia","Risk-stratify with GRACE/TIMI; identify high-risk features (dynamic ECG changes, ongoing pain, hemodynamic/electrical instability, elevated/rising troponin)","Echo for wall motion/EF; exclude alternative causes"],"tx":["Anti-ischemic and antithrombotic therapy: aspirin + a P2Y12 inhibitor (DAPT), anticoagulation (heparin), beta-blocker, nitrates, high-intensity statin; add oxygen only if hypoxic","INVASIVE strategy (coronary angiography +/- PCI): IMMEDIATE (<2 h) for instability/refractory ischemia; EARLY (<24 h) for high-risk (e.g., GRACE >140, rising troponin, dynamic ECG); an ischemia-guided approach for low-risk","Secondary prevention and risk-factor modification"],"pearls":["NSTEMI has a rising/falling troponin; unstable angina has ischemic symptoms without biomarker rise - both are non-occlusive plaque events","Risk-stratify with GRACE/TIMI: high-risk features (dynamic ECG changes, rising troponin, instability) prompt early (<24 h) invasive angiography","Dual antiplatelet therapy + anticoagulation is the antithrombotic backbone; add high-intensity statin","Unlike STEMI, most NSTEMI does not need immediate reperfusion - but instability/refractory ischemia does (immediate angiography)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513228/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Unstable Angina","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442000/"}]},{"id":"cards-stemi","name":"ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI)","sec":"cards","present":["STEMI is acute transmural ischemia from complete/near-complete thrombotic occlusion of a coronary artery - a time-critical emergency where 'time is muscle'","Diagnosis: ischemic symptoms + ST-segment ELEVATION in contiguous leads (or new LBBB with ischemia by Sgarbossa); localize the culprit territory by leads","Immediate priority is REPERFUSION to restore flow and salvage myocardium"],"dx":["Obtain and act on a 12-lead ECG within ~10 minutes of arrival; do not wait for troponin to treat a clear STEMI","Localize the infarct/culprit (inferior/anterior/lateral) and watch for high-risk features: RV infarction (inferior STEMI - preload-dependent, avoid nitrates), and mechanical complications","Continuous monitoring for arrhythmia and hemodynamic instability"],"tx":["PRIMARY PCI is the preferred reperfusion strategy if achievable within guideline times (door-to-balloon <=90 min, or <=120 min if transferred); FIBRINOLYSIS if timely PCI is unavailable (then transfer for angiography)","Adjuncts: aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor (DAPT), anticoagulation, high-intensity statin; beta-blocker/ACE inhibitor as tolerated; avoid nitrates in RV infarction/hypotension","Rapid transfer, activation of the cath lab, and treatment of complications"],"pearls":["Primary PCI is preferred (door-to-balloon <=90 min); if timely PCI is unavailable, give fibrinolysis (within ~30 min door-to-needle) then transfer","Obtain the ECG within 10 minutes and treat a clear STEMI without waiting for troponin - time is muscle","Inferior STEMI: check right-sided leads for RV infarction - it is preload-dependent, so AVOID nitrates and give fluids for hypotension","New LBBB with ischemic symptoms may be a STEMI-equivalent - apply Sgarbossa criteria"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532281/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Myocardial Infarction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459269/"}]},{"id":"cards-pci-revascularization","name":"PCI & Coronary Revascularization","sec":"cards","present":["Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) restores coronary flow via balloon angioplasty and (usually) DRUG-ELUTING STENT placement; it is the reperfusion strategy for STEMI and treats significant lesions in ACS and selected stable disease","Revascularization choice (PCI vs CABG) depends on anatomy, complexity (SYNTAX score), diabetes, and LV function - decided by the heart team for complex disease","Post-PCI antithrombotic therapy prevents stent thrombosis"],"dx":["Define lesion significance (angiography + FFR/iFR for intermediate lesions) and complexity; assess LV function, diabetes, and comorbidity","Heart-team evaluation for left main/multivessel disease (PCI vs CABG); identify high-risk features favoring surgery","Assess bleeding vs ischemic risk to tailor antiplatelet duration"],"tx":["PCI with drug-eluting stents for STEMI culprit, high-risk NSTEMI, and symptomatic/high-risk stable lesions; CABG preferred for left main/complex multivessel disease (especially diabetes/reduced EF)","DUAL ANTIPLATELET THERAPY (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) after stenting - duration balanced against bleeding risk; never abruptly stop DAPT early (stent thrombosis risk)","Optimize secondary prevention (statin, risk factors, cardiac rehab)"],"pearls":["Drug-eluting stents require dual antiplatelet therapy - premature discontinuation risks catastrophic stent thrombosis","PCI treats the STEMI culprit and high-risk lesions; CABG is preferred for left main/complex multivessel disease (SYNTAX/FREEDOM, especially with diabetes)","FFR/iFR-guided PCI (treat only flow-limiting lesions) improves outcomes vs angiography alone","Balance ischemic vs bleeding risk when setting DAPT duration; complete revascularization decisions are heart-team based for complex anatomy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Percutaneous Coronary Intervention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556123/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Coronary Stents","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507804/"}]},{"id":"cards-post-mi-complications","name":"Post-MI Complications","sec":"cards","present":["After myocardial infarction, complications are ELECTRICAL (arrhythmias, conduction block), MECHANICAL (pump failure and structural rupture), and inflammatory/other - timing helps predict which","MECHANICAL complications (typically days 3-7, or earlier with reperfusion) include PAPILLARY MUSCLE RUPTURE (acute severe mitral regurgitation), VENTRICULAR SEPTAL RUPTURE (new VSD), and FREE-WALL RUPTURE (tamponade/sudden death) - catastrophic and surgical emergencies","Others: cardiogenic shock, LV thrombus (anterior MI), pericarditis (early peri-infarction and later Dressler syndrome), and heart failure/remodeling"],"dx":["A new murmur with hemodynamic deterioration after MI signals a mechanical complication - urgent ECHO distinguishes papillary muscle rupture (acute MR) from ventricular septal rupture (VSD with a step-up on right-heart cath)","Monitor for arrhythmias/conduction block (inferior MI -> AV block; anterior MI -> bundle branch/high-grade block); free-wall rupture presents as tamponade/PEA arrest","Anterior MI: screen for LV thrombus (echo); recognize pericarditis patterns"],"tx":["Mechanical complications: hemodynamic stabilization (often mechanical support - IABP/Impella) and EMERGENT SURGICAL repair (papillary muscle/VSD/free-wall rupture)","Arrhythmias per ACLS/rhythm; conduction block may need temporary/permanent pacing; LV thrombus -> anticoagulation","Standard post-MI therapy (DAPT, high-intensity statin, beta-blocker, ACE inhibitor/ARB, mineralocorticoid antagonist for reduced EF) and cardiac rehab; treat post-MI pericarditis (avoid high-dose NSAIDs/steroids that impair healing)"],"pearls":["A new murmur + hemodynamic collapse days after MI = mechanical complication (papillary muscle rupture -> acute MR, or ventricular septal rupture -> VSD) - echo urgently, stabilize, and operate","Inferior MI causes AV block (often transient); anterior MI causes bundle-branch/high-grade block (worse prognosis)","Anterior MIs risk LV thrombus - anticoagulate; free-wall rupture presents as tamponade/PEA","Dressler syndrome (late immune pericarditis) is now uncommon; avoid high-dose NSAIDs/steroids acutely post-MI (impair healing)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Myocardial Infarction Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537011/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ventricular Septal Rupture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499835/"}]},{"id":"cards-antithrombotic-therapy","name":"Antithrombotic & Antiplatelet Therapy","sec":"cards","present":["Antithrombotic therapy spans ANTIPLATELET agents (aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors - clopidogrel, ticagrelor, prasugrel) and ANTICOAGULANTS (heparins, warfarin, DOACs); indications, combinations, and durations are high-yield","DUAL ANTIPLATELET THERAPY (DAPT: aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) is standard after ACS and coronary stenting to prevent stent thrombosis/recurrent events","The central tension is always ISCHEMIC vs BLEEDING risk - individualize agent choice and duration"],"dx":["Match therapy to indication: DAPT after ACS/PCI; anticoagulation for AF/VTE/mechanical valves; assess bleeding risk (e.g., HAS-BLED context) and drug interactions","Prasugrel is contraindicated with prior stroke/TIA; ticagrelor/prasugrel are more potent than clopidogrel (clopidogrel has variable CYP2C19 metabolism)","For patients needing both anticoagulation and antiplatelet therapy (e.g., AF + PCI), plan the combination and duration to minimize bleeding"],"tx":["ACS/PCI: aspirin + a potent P2Y12 inhibitor (ticagrelor/prasugrel preferred over clopidogrel in many ACS settings), duration balanced against bleeding risk","AF + recent PCI: typically SHORTEN triple therapy and transition to a DOAC + single antiplatelet ('double therapy') to reduce bleeding (per WOEST/AUGUSTUS-type data)","Mechanical heart valves require WARFARIN (DOACs are contraindicated); reverse agents available (idarucizumab for dabigatran, andexanet for factor Xa inhibitors, PCC/vitamin K for warfarin)"],"pearls":["DAPT (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) prevents stent thrombosis - do not stop it prematurely after stenting; balance ischemic vs bleeding risk for duration","Prasugrel is contraindicated with prior stroke/TIA; clopidogrel efficacy varies with CYP2C19 genotype","Mechanical valves need warfarin - DOACs are contraindicated (and are also avoided in significant mitral stenosis/valvular AF)","AF + PCI: minimize triple therapy - transition to DOAC + single antiplatelet to lower bleeding (WOEST/AUGUSTUS)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Antiplatelet Medications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537062/"},{"t":"StatPearls: P2Y12 Inhibitors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557845/"}]},{"id":"cards-lipid-management","name":"Lipid Management","sec":"cards","present":["Dyslipidemia, especially elevated LDL cholesterol, drives atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD); lipid lowering reduces events across primary and secondary prevention","STATINS are the foundation (high-intensity for ASCVD and high-risk primary prevention); add-on agents (ezetimibe, PCSK9 inhibitors, bempedoic acid) achieve lower LDL when needed","Very high triglycerides (>500-1000) risk pancreatitis and need separate management"],"dx":["Obtain a lipid panel; estimate 10-year ASCVD risk (via the PREVENT equations, which the 2025 ACC/AHA guidelines adopted in place of the pooled cohort equations) for primary prevention decisions; the coronary artery calcium score refines intermediate risk","Identify very-high-risk secondary-prevention patients (recent ACS, multiple events) who benefit from the most aggressive LDL targets","Screen for familial hypercholesterolemia (very high LDL, family history, early ASCVD) and secondary causes"],"tx":["SECONDARY prevention (established ASCVD): high-intensity statin, targeting a large LDL reduction (guidelines target LDL <55-70 mg/dL in very-high-risk patients); add ezetimibe then a PCSK9 inhibitor if not at goal","PRIMARY prevention: statin based on ASCVD risk, diabetes, LDL >=190, or age/risk enhancers; lifestyle for all","Severe hypertriglyceridemia: fibrates/omega-3 and lifestyle to prevent pancreatitis; icosapent ethyl reduces events in selected high-risk patients (REDUCE-IT)"],"pearls":["High-intensity statin is the foundation for ASCVD and high-risk primary prevention; 'lower is better' for LDL in very-high-risk patients (add ezetimibe, then PCSK9 inhibitors)","Statin for LDL >=190 (familial hypercholesterolemia), diabetes (age 40-75), or elevated 10-year ASCVD risk; coronary calcium score refines intermediate-risk decisions","Check for statin-associated muscle symptoms and manage (dose adjust, alternate agents); statins are not stopped for mild transaminase elevations","Triglycerides >500-1000 -> pancreatitis risk (fibrates/omega-3); icosapent ethyl reduces CV events in selected patients (REDUCE-IT)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hyperlipidemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560891/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Statin Medications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430940/"}]},{"id":"cards-hfref-gdmt","name":"Heart Failure with Reduced EF - GDMT","sec":"cards","present":["Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF, LVEF <=40%) is a syndrome of impaired systolic function with neurohormonal activation (RAAS and sympathetic) driving progression","Causes: ischemic (most common), and non-ischemic (hypertension, valvular, cardiomyopathies, toxins, tachycardia-mediated)","GUIDELINE-DIRECTED MEDICAL THERAPY (GDMT) improves survival and reduces hospitalizations - the core of management"],"dx":["Confirm reduced EF (echo) and evaluate etiology (ischemic workup, consider CMR); assess volume status and symptoms (NYHA class)","Labs: natriuretic peptides (BNP/NT-proBNP) support diagnosis and prognosis; renal function/electrolytes to guide therapy","Identify reversible causes (ischemia, tachyarrhythmia, valve disease, thyroid, toxins)"],"tx":["The FOUR PILLARS of GDMT (start and titrate all as tolerated): (1) ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) or ACE inhibitor/ARB, (2) beta-blocker (evidence-based: carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol), (3) mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, and (4) SGLT2 INHIBITOR","Diuretics for congestion/symptoms (do not improve survival); add hydralazine/nitrate (especially in Black patients) and ivabradine in selected patients","DEVICE therapy: ICD for primary prevention (EF <=35% despite GDMT), CRT for wide QRS/LBBB; advanced therapies (LVAD/transplant) for refractory disease"],"pearls":["Four pillars of HFrEF GDMT improve survival: ARNI/ACEi-ARB, evidence-based beta-blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist, and SGLT2 inhibitor - start early and titrate all","Diuretics relieve congestion but do NOT improve survival - use for symptoms","Reassess EF after GDMT before implanting a primary-prevention ICD (EF <=35% despite >=3 months of optimized therapy); CRT for LBBB/wide QRS","Monitor renal function and potassium (RAAS/MRA); do not withhold GDMT for asymptomatic mild changes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Heart Failure","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430873/"},{"t":"NHLBI: Heart Failure","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure"}]},{"id":"cards-hfpef","name":"Heart Failure with Preserved EF","sec":"cards","present":["Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF, LVEF >=50%) is HF with normal systolic function but impaired relaxation/filling (diastolic dysfunction) and elevated filling pressures","It is strongly associated with aging, HYPERTENSION, obesity, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and chronic kidney disease; it now accounts for about half of HF cases","Presentation mirrors HFrEF (dyspnea, congestion, exercise intolerance) but with preserved EF"],"dx":["Diagnose with HF signs/symptoms + preserved EF + evidence of elevated filling pressures/diastolic dysfunction (echo diastolic parameters, elevated natriuretic peptides) - scores (H2FPEF, HFA-PEFF) aid diagnosis","Exclude HFpEF mimics that change management: cardiac AMYLOIDOSIS (older, low-voltage ECG with LVH on echo, discordant), hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and constrictive pericarditis","Evaluate and treat comorbidities driving the syndrome"],"tx":["SGLT2 INHIBITORS reduce HF hospitalizations across the EF spectrum (EMPEROR-Preserved, DELIVER) - a cornerstone for HFpEF; diuretics for congestion","Aggressively treat comorbidities: hypertension, obesity (weight loss), atrial fibrillation (rate/rhythm), diabetes, and ischemia","Mineralocorticoid antagonists (finerenone reduced HF events in FINEARTS-HF) and ARNI have selective/modest benefit; individualize and enroll in exercise/weight management"],"pearls":["HFpEF = HF symptoms + EF >=50% + elevated filling pressures/diastolic dysfunction; driven by hypertension, obesity, diabetes, AF, and aging","SGLT2 inhibitors reduce HF hospitalizations across the EF spectrum (EMPEROR-Preserved, DELIVER) - a cornerstone of HFpEF therapy","Always exclude cardiac amyloidosis (low-voltage ECG with LVH, older patients) and constrictive pericarditis - they masquerade as HFpEF","Treating comorbidities (BP, weight, AF) is central; diuretics manage congestion"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Diastolic Heart Failure","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537226/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Heart Failure With Preserved Ejection Fraction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK599964/"}]},{"id":"cards-acute-decompensated-hf","name":"Acute Decompensated Heart Failure","sec":"cards","present":["Acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF) is worsening congestion (and/or hypoperfusion) requiring urgent therapy; most presentations are CONGESTION-predominant (volume overload) rather than low-output","Precipitants: dietary/medication nonadherence, ischemia/ACS, arrhythmia (AF), uncontrolled hypertension, infection, and progression of disease","Bedside profiles combine CONGESTION ('wet' vs 'dry') and PERFUSION ('warm' vs 'cold') to guide therapy"],"dx":["Assess volume status (JVP, edema, rales, congestion on exam/imaging) and perfusion (extremities, mentation, blood pressure, lactate); natriuretic peptides support the diagnosis","Identify the precipitant (ECG/troponin for ischemia/arrhythmia, infection screen); echo for new structural/functional changes","Classify as warm-wet (most common), cold-wet, warm-dry, or cold-dry"],"tx":["CONGESTION: IV loop DIURETICS are the cornerstone (bolus or infusion; adequate dosing); add vasodilators (nitroglycerin) for hypertensive acute HF/pulmonary edema","LOW PERFUSION (cold): inotropes (dobutamine/milrinone) for hypoperfusion, and escalate to mechanical support for cardiogenic shock","Continue/optimize GDMT as tolerated, treat the precipitant, and monitor renal function/electrolytes and diuretic response (urine output, weight)"],"pearls":["Most ADHF is congestion-driven ('wet') - IV loop diuretics are the mainstay; vasodilators (nitroglycerin) help hypertensive flash pulmonary edema","Use warm/cold (perfusion) and wet/dry (congestion) profiles to guide therapy - inotropes for the 'cold' (hypoperfused), diuretics for the 'wet'","Always find the precipitant: nonadherence, ischemia, arrhythmia, uncontrolled HTN, or infection","Avoid stopping GDMT unnecessarily; monitor diuretic response and renal function"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Heart Failure","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557710/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Pulmonary Edema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557611/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiogenic-shock","name":"Cardiogenic Shock","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiogenic shock is end-organ hypoperfusion from inadequate cardiac output despite adequate intravascular volume - most commonly from ACUTE MI with LV pump failure (also mechanical complications, decompensated HF, arrhythmia, myocarditis, and valve emergencies)","Hallmarks: hypotension, elevated filling pressures, low cardiac index, and hypoperfusion (cool extremities, oliguria, altered mentation, rising lactate)","It carries high mortality; staged classification (SCAI A-E) tracks severity"],"dx":["Assess hemodynamics (often with echo +/- right-heart catheterization: high filling pressures, low cardiac index/output); identify the cause (ECG/angiography for MI, echo for mechanical complications/valve failure)","Distinguish from other shock types (distributive/hypovolemic); rule out RV infarction and mechanical complications","Trend lactate and end-organ function; stage severity (SCAI)"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE: EARLY REVASCULARIZATION for MI-related shock (culprit-vessel PCI; the CULPRIT-SHOCK trial favors culprit-only over routine multivessel PCI), and surgery for mechanical complications/valve emergencies","Hemodynamic support: vasopressors (norepinephrine preferred first-line) and inotropes (dobutamine/milrinone); escalate to MECHANICAL CIRCULATORY SUPPORT (IABP, Impella, VA ECMO) for refractory shock - in STEMI-related shock the DanGer Shock trial (2024) showed a microaxial flow pump (Impella CP) improved 180-day survival, at the cost of more bleeding/vascular/renal complications","ICU care, careful volume management, and treat arrhythmias"],"pearls":["MI is the most common cause of cardiogenic shock - EARLY revascularization improves survival (culprit-only PCI per CULPRIT-SHOCK, not routine multivessel)","Norepinephrine is the preferred first-line vasopressor in cardiogenic shock; add inotropes for low output","Escalate refractory shock to mechanical support (IABP/Impella/VA ECMO); always exclude mechanical complications and RV infarction - DanGer Shock (2024) was the first RCT to show an MCS mortality benefit (Impella CP in selected STEMI-related shock)","Trend lactate and end-organ perfusion; SCAI staging (A-E) communicates severity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiogenic Shock","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482255/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Shock","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531492/"}]},{"id":"cards-advanced-hf-mcs","name":"Advanced Heart Failure & Mechanical Support (Medical Overview)","sec":"cards","present":["Advanced (stage D) heart failure is persistent severe symptoms despite optimized GDMT - marked by recurrent hospitalizations, intolerance of GDMT (hypotension/renal limits), inotrope dependence, and poor functional capacity","Recognizing advanced HF triggers timely referral for evaluation of TRANSPLANT and DURABLE MECHANICAL CIRCULATORY SUPPORT (LVAD) before end-organ damage develops","(Surgical implantation/technique for LVAD and transplant is covered under Thoracic Surgery - this entry is the medical recognition/selection view)"],"dx":["Identify advanced-HF markers (the 'I-NEED-HELP' clues: inotropes, NYHA III-IV/elevated peptides, end-organ dysfunction, EF very low, defibrillator shocks, hospitalizations, edema/escalating diuretics, low blood pressure, prognostic medication intolerance)","Formal evaluation: cardiopulmonary exercise testing (peak VO2), right-heart catheterization (hemodynamics, pulmonary vascular resistance), and end-organ/psychosocial assessment","Assess candidacy and contraindications (fixed pulmonary hypertension is a transplant contraindication; RV function predicts LVAD success)"],"tx":["Refer EARLY to an advanced-HF/transplant center; bridge with inotropes or temporary/durable MCS as needed","DURABLE LVAD (bridge to transplant or destination therapy) and heart TRANSPLANT are the definitive options (see Thoracic Surgery for implantation/technique)","Palliative care integration for symptom management and goals of care when appropriate"],"pearls":["Recognize advanced HF early (recurrent hospitalizations, inotrope dependence, GDMT intolerance, very low EF) and refer BEFORE irreversible end-organ damage","Fixed severe pulmonary hypertension contraindicates transplant; RV function is key to LVAD success (RV failure is the major post-LVAD risk)","Continuous-flow LVAD serves as bridge to transplant or destination therapy; cross-reference Thoracic Surgery for surgical detail","Integrate palliative care for symptom control and goals-of-care discussions in stage D disease"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Left Ventricular Assist Device","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499841/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Heart Transplantation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535406/"}]},{"id":"cards-dilated-cardiomyopathy","name":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy","sec":"cards","present":["Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is LV (or biventricular) dilation with systolic dysfunction not explained by loading conditions or coronary disease sufficient to cause it - a common cause of non-ischemic HFrEF","Causes: idiopathic, GENETIC/familial (e.g., titin, lamin A/C), toxic (ALCOHOL, chemotherapy - anthracyclines, cocaine), infectious/post-myocarditis, peripartum, tachycardia-mediated, and infiltrative/metabolic","It presents with heart failure, arrhythmias, conduction disease, and thromboembolism"],"dx":["Echo shows a dilated, poorly contracting ventricle; EXCLUDE ischemic cardiomyopathy (angiography/CT); CMR characterizes tissue (myocarditis, infiltration) and prognosticates (LGE)","Evaluate reversible/specific causes: thyroid, iron, alcohol/toxins, tachyarrhythmia, peripartum timing; family history and GENETIC testing/counseling (some genotypes - lamin A/C - carry high arrhythmic risk)","Assess arrhythmia burden (some genetic DCMs warrant earlier ICD consideration)"],"tx":["Treat as HFrEF with full GDMT (four pillars) and device therapy per EF/QRS; anticoagulation for LV thrombus/AF","Remove/treat the cause: alcohol cessation, rate control for tachycardia-mediated CM, treat myocarditis-related causes; peripartum CM has specific management/prognosis","Genetic counseling and family screening; consider earlier ICD in high-risk genotypes (e.g., lamin A/C) regardless of standard EF cutoffs"],"pearls":["DCM is dilation + systolic dysfunction not explained by loading/coronary disease - always exclude ischemic cardiomyopathy first","Look for reversible causes: alcohol, tachycardia-mediated, thyroid, peripartum, and toxins (anthracyclines)","Some genotypes (lamin A/C) carry high arrhythmic risk and may warrant earlier ICD - obtain family history and genetic testing","Manage as HFrEF with GDMT + devices; anticoagulate for LV thrombus/AF"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Dilated Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441911/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430843/"}]},{"id":"cards-hypertrophic-cardiomyopathy","name":"Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy","sec":"cards","present":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is a genetic (autosomal dominant sarcomere gene) disorder of LV hypertrophy not explained by loading conditions; a subset has dynamic LV OUTFLOW TRACT OBSTRUCTION (from septal hypertrophy + systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve)","It is a leading cause of SUDDEN CARDIAC DEATH IN YOUNG ATHLETES; symptoms include exertional dyspnea, angina, palpitations, and syncope","The murmur INCREASES with maneuvers that decrease preload/afterload (Valsalva, standing) - the opposite of most murmurs"],"dx":["Echo/CMR shows asymmetric septal (or other) hypertrophy; assess for outflow obstruction (resting/provoked gradients, systolic anterior motion) and mitral regurgitation","ECG shows LVH/repolarization changes; screen for SUDDEN-DEATH RISK factors (family history of SCD, unexplained syncope, massive LV wall thickness, non-sustained VT, abnormal blood-pressure response to exercise, extensive LGE on CMR)","Genetic testing/counseling and family screening (first-degree relatives)"],"tx":["Symptomatic obstruction: NEGATIVE INOTROPES/chronotropes - beta-blockers first-line, then non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers or disopyramide; CARDIAC MYOSIN INHIBITORS (mavacamten) reduce obstruction; AVOID vasodilators, diuretics, and digoxin (worsen dynamic obstruction)","Refractory obstruction: SEPTAL REDUCTION (surgical myectomy or alcohol septal ablation)","ICD for primary/secondary SUDDEN-DEATH prevention in high-risk patients; activity/sports counseling"],"pearls":["HCM murmur INCREASES with Valsalva/standing (decreased preload) and decreases with squatting/handgrip - opposite of aortic stenosis and MR","Avoid vasodilators, diuretics, and digoxin in obstructive HCM - they worsen the dynamic outflow gradient; use beta-blockers (then verapamil/disopyramide, or mavacamten)","Screen for SCD risk (family history of SCD, unexplained syncope, massive hypertrophy, NSVT, abnormal exercise BP, extensive LGE) - ICD for high risk","It is a leading cause of sudden death in young athletes; obtain genetic testing and screen first-degree relatives"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430788/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542193/"}]},{"id":"cards-restrictive-infiltrative-cardiomyopathy","name":"Restrictive & Infiltrative Cardiomyopathy","sec":"cards","present":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy is impaired ventricular FILLING (stiff, non-compliant ventricles) with preserved or near-preserved systolic function - causing predominantly right-heart failure signs and elevated filling pressures","The most important infiltrative cause is CARDIAC AMYLOIDOSIS (ATTR - transthyretin, and AL - light chain); others include sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, and endomyocardial diseases","It classically mimics (and must be distinguished from) CONSTRICTIVE PERICARDITIS - which is surgically curable"],"dx":["Echo/CMR: thickened, stiff ventricles with biatrial enlargement and restrictive filling; AMYLOID clues include increased wall thickness with LOW ECG VOLTAGE (a discordance), granular myocardium, and characteristic CMR/mapping and LGE","Diagnose amyloid subtype: bone scintigraphy (PYP) diagnoses ATTR noninvasively; ALWAYS exclude AL amyloid (serum/urine free light chains, immunofixation) because AL needs urgent hematologic treatment; FDG-PET/biopsy for sarcoidosis","Distinguish restriction from constriction (respirophasic septal shift/ventricular interdependence favors constriction; catheterization patterns) - management differs"],"tx":["Treat the underlying infiltration: ATTR amyloidosis - TTR stabilizers (tafamidis or acoramidis, the latter FDA-approved 2024) and TTR silencers (e.g., vutrisiran); AL amyloidosis - urgent hematology/chemotherapy; sarcoidosis - immunosuppression (steroids) +/- device therapy; hemochromatosis - iron removal","Supportive HF care: cautious diuresis for congestion; many amyloid patients are SENSITIVE to and poorly tolerate standard HF drugs (beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors) and digoxin (avoid)","Anticoagulation for AF (amyloid has high thromboembolic/AF risk); device/transplant in selected cases"],"pearls":["Increased LV wall thickness with LOW ECG voltage is a classic clue to cardiac amyloidosis (a striking discordance)","PYP/bone scintigraphy diagnoses ATTR amyloid noninvasively - but ALWAYS exclude AL amyloid first (free light chains/immunofixation); AL needs urgent chemotherapy","Distinguish restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis (surgically curable) - interdependence/imaging/cath patterns differ","Amyloid hearts tolerate standard HF meds poorly and are digoxin-sensitive; anticoagulate for AF given high thromboembolic risk"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Restrictive Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537234/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Amyloidosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574531/"}]},{"id":"cards-myocarditis","name":"Myocarditis","sec":"cards","present":["Myocarditis is inflammation of the myocardium, most often from VIRAL infection (or post-viral immune response); other causes include other infections, autoimmune/systemic disease, drugs/hypersensitivity, and IMMUNE CHECKPOINT INHIBITORS","Presentation ranges widely: a viral prodrome then chest pain (mimicking MI or pericarditis), heart failure, arrhythmias, or fulminant cardiogenic shock/sudden death","It is an important cause of DCM and of sudden death in young people"],"dx":["Elevated troponin, ECG changes, and wall-motion/functional abnormalities on echo; CARDIAC MRI (T2/edema and non-ischemic LGE by Lake Louise criteria) is the key noninvasive test","Coronary assessment to EXCLUDE ACS (myocarditis mimics MI with elevated troponin and normal coronaries - a form of MINOCA); ENDOMYOCARDIAL BIOPSY for fulminant/unexplained cases or when giant-cell/eosinophilic myocarditis is suspected","Identify the cause (viral, autoimmune, checkpoint-inhibitor exposure)"],"tx":["Mostly SUPPORTIVE: HF therapy (GDMT), arrhythmia management, and mechanical support/transplant for fulminant disease (many fulminant cases recover if bridged)","AVOID strenuous EXERCISE for ~3-6 months (exertion worsens viral myocarditis/arrhythmic risk); treat specific causes (immunosuppression for giant-cell, eosinophilic, sarcoid, or checkpoint-inhibitor myocarditis; stop the offending drug)","Standard supportive care; guideline-directed follow-up of recovery"],"pearls":["Myocarditis can mimic MI (elevated troponin, ECG changes) with normal coronaries (MINOCA) - CMR (Lake Louise criteria) is the key noninvasive test","Endomyocardial biopsy is reserved for fulminant/unexplained cases or suspected giant-cell/eosinophilic myocarditis (which need immunosuppression)","Advise avoiding strenuous exercise for ~3-6 months during recovery (reduces arrhythmic risk)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis is a serious, treatable cause - stop the drug and give high-dose steroids"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Myocarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534799/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Viral Myocarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459259/"}]},{"id":"cards-takotsubo","name":"Takotsubo (Stress) Cardiomyopathy","sec":"cards","present":["Takotsubo (stress-induced or 'broken heart') cardiomyopathy is acute, usually reversible LV dysfunction, classically with APICAL BALLOONING, typically triggered by intense EMOTIONAL or PHYSICAL stress","It predominantly affects POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN and mimics acute MI (chest pain, ECG changes - ST elevation/T-wave inversion, elevated troponin)","The mechanism is thought to be catecholamine-mediated myocardial stunning"],"dx":["It mimics ACS - CORONARY ANGIOGRAPHY shows NO obstructive disease that explains the regional dysfunction, while ventriculography/echo shows the characteristic apical ballooning (wall motion beyond a single coronary territory)","Troponin is elevated (usually modestly relative to the extent of wall-motion abnormality); ECG may show ST elevation/deep T-wave inversions and QT prolongation","Identify the stressor; exclude pheochromocytoma and myocarditis in atypical cases"],"tx":["Largely SUPPORTIVE - most recover LV function within days to weeks; treat heart failure and complications; if LV outflow obstruction is present, AVOID inotropes and use cautious beta-blockade","Manage complications: acute HF/pulmonary edema, arrhythmias, LV thrombus (anticoagulate), and (rarely) cardiogenic shock","Address the precipitating stressor; monitor QT and recovery with follow-up echo"],"pearls":["Takotsubo mimics STEMI in a postmenopausal woman after intense stress, but angiography shows no obstructive CAD and there is apical ballooning beyond a single coronary territory","It is usually reversible - supportive care with recovery over days to weeks; confirm improvement on follow-up echo","If dynamic LV outflow obstruction develops, AVOID inotropes (worsen it) - use cautious beta-blockade and fluids","Watch for LV thrombus, arrhythmias, QT prolongation, and (rare) shock during the acute phase"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430798/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stress Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562247/"}]},{"id":"cards-arrhythmogenic-cardiomyopathy","name":"Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy","sec":"cards","present":["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC) is a genetic (usually autosomal dominant DESMOSOMAL gene) disorder with fibrofatty replacement of (predominantly right) ventricular myocardium","It causes VENTRICULAR ARRHYTHMIAS and is an important cause of SUDDEN CARDIAC DEATH IN YOUNG PEOPLE AND ATHLETES; symptoms include palpitations, syncope, or aborted cardiac arrest, often exercise-related","Later stages develop RV (and sometimes LV) systolic dysfunction/heart failure"],"dx":["Diagnosis uses TASK FORCE CRITERIA integrating imaging (RV dilation/dysfunction/aneurysms), ECG findings (EPSILON WAVES, T-wave inversions in right precordial leads, ventricular arrhythmias with LBBB morphology), tissue, family history, and genetics","CMR characterizes RV structure/function and fibrofatty change; ambulatory monitoring/exercise testing assesses arrhythmia burden","Genetic testing and cascade family screening (desmosomal mutations)"],"tx":["EXERCISE RESTRICTION (endurance/competitive sport accelerates disease and arrhythmias) - a key, often-tested recommendation; beta-blockers and antiarrhythmics for arrhythmia suppression","ICD for secondary prevention (prior arrest/sustained VT) and high-risk primary prevention; catheter ablation for recurrent VT (adjunctive, not usually curative)","Heart failure therapy as RV/LV dysfunction develops; family screening/counseling"],"pearls":["ARVC is a desmosomal genetic cardiomyopathy causing ventricular arrhythmias/sudden death in young athletes - EXERCISE RESTRICTION slows progression and lowers arrhythmic risk","ECG clues: epsilon waves and T-wave inversions in V1-V3, with VT of LBBB morphology (RV origin)","Diagnosis is by integrated Task Force Criteria (imaging, ECG, arrhythmias, tissue, family history, genetics)","ICD for secondary prevention/high-risk patients; screen first-degree relatives (autosomal dominant)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470378/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Dysplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542241/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiorenal-syndrome","name":"Cardiorenal Syndrome","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiorenal syndrome describes the bidirectional interplay where dysfunction of the heart and kidneys drive each other's decline; a common scenario is worsening renal function during heart failure treatment","Mechanisms include reduced forward perfusion AND, importantly, VENOUS CONGESTION (elevated central venous pressure impairs renal perfusion) plus neurohormonal activation","It complicates decongestion in acute HF (rising creatinine during diuresis) and challenges GDMT tolerance"],"dx":["Assess both perfusion and CONGESTION (venous congestion is a major, sometimes dominant, driver of renal dysfunction in HF) - elevated JVP/CVP correlates with worse renal outcomes","Trend renal function, electrolytes, and diuretic response; distinguish true worsening renal function from expected creatinine changes with effective decongestion","Evaluate for other contributors (nephrotoxins, obstruction, intrinsic renal disease)"],"tx":["DECONGESTION is usually the priority - relieving venous congestion often improves renal function; use adequate loop diuretics (bolus/infusion), add a thiazide-type agent for diuretic resistance (sequential nephron blockade), and consider SGLT2 inhibitors","Do NOT reflexively stop GDMT for modest creatinine rises during decongestion (small increases can be acceptable if the patient is decongesting); avoid excessive over-diuresis causing hypoperfusion","Ultrafiltration for true diuretic-refractory congestion; treat the underlying cardiac disease"],"pearls":["Venous CONGESTION (high CVP), not just low output, drives renal dysfunction in HF - effective decongestion often IMPROVES renal function","Do not reflexively stop GDMT or diuresis for a modest creatinine rise if the patient is successfully decongesting","Overcome diuretic resistance with adequate loop dosing + sequential nephron blockade (add a thiazide); SGLT2 inhibitors help","Ultrafiltration is reserved for truly diuretic-refractory congestion; avoid over-diuresis causing hypoperfusion"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiorenal Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542305/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Diuretics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557838/"}]},{"id":"cards-atrial-fibrillation","name":"Atrial Fibrillation","sec":"cards","present":["Atrial fibrillation (AF) is a disorganized atrial rhythm (irregularly irregular, no distinct P waves) - the most common sustained arrhythmia; it causes palpitations, dyspnea, fatigue, and raises STROKE risk from atrial thrombus (left atrial appendage)","Classified as paroxysmal, persistent, long-standing persistent, or permanent; associated with hypertension, HF, valvular disease, hyperthyroidism, obesity/OSA, and alcohol","Management rests on three pillars: rate/rhythm control and STROKE PREVENTION (anticoagulation)"],"dx":["ECG confirms AF; assess for reversible triggers (thyroid, ischemia, infection, alcohol, electrolytes) and structural disease (echo)","Estimate stroke risk with CHA2DS2-VASc and bleeding risk (context, e.g., HAS-BLED) - risk drives anticoagulation decisions","Determine onset/duration (guides cardioversion safety) and symptom burden"],"tx":["STROKE PREVENTION: anticoagulate based on annual thromboembolic risk (>=2%/year) using a validated score such as CHA2DS2-VASc (>=2 in men / >=3 in women per the 2023 guideline); DOACs are preferred over warfarin EXCEPT in mechanical valves or moderate-severe (rheumatic) mitral stenosis (use warfarin)","RATE control (beta-blocker or non-dihydropyridine CCB; digoxin adjunct) vs RHYTHM control (antiarrhythmics or catheter ablation) - rhythm control is favored earlier in selected/symptomatic patients (EAST-AFNET 4) and in HF","CARDIOVERSION: if AF >48 hours/unknown duration and not anticoagulated, either anticoagulate >=3 weeks first or exclude left atrial appendage thrombus with TEE before cardioverting (then anticoagulate >=4 weeks after); left atrial appendage occlusion for anticoagulation-ineligible patients"],"pearls":["Anticoagulate by CHA2DS2-VASc; DOACs are preferred EXCEPT mechanical valves and moderate-severe mitral stenosis (warfarin)","Before cardioverting AF >48 h/unknown duration: anticoagulate >=3 weeks OR do a TEE to exclude LAA thrombus - then anticoagulate >=4 weeks after (stunned atrium)","Rate vs rhythm control: early rhythm control benefits selected symptomatic patients (EAST-AFNET 4) and those with HF; ablation is effective for symptomatic paroxysmal AF","Always seek reversible triggers (thyroid, alcohol, OSA, ischemia); treat obesity/OSA to reduce AF burden"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Fibrillation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526072/"},{"t":"StatPearls: CHA2DS2-VASc Score","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470410/"}]},{"id":"cards-atrial-flutter","name":"Atrial Flutter","sec":"cards","present":["Atrial flutter is a macro-reentrant atrial tachycardia; TYPICAL (cavotricuspid isthmus-dependent) flutter produces SAWTOOTH flutter waves (best seen in inferior leads) with an atrial rate around 300/min and often 2:1 AV conduction (ventricular rate ~150)","It causes similar symptoms and STROKE RISK to AF and frequently coexists with AF","A regular narrow-complex tachycardia at ~150/min should prompt suspicion of flutter with 2:1 block"],"dx":["ECG shows sawtooth flutter waves (negative in II/III/aVF for typical counterclockwise flutter); vagal maneuvers/adenosine can transiently increase AV block to unmask flutter waves","Assess for underlying disease and thromboembolic risk (apply the same anticoagulation principles as AF)","Distinguish typical (isthmus-dependent, ablatable) from atypical flutter"],"tx":["Rate control and anticoagulation as in AF (same CHA2DS2-VASc-based stroke prevention and pre-cardioversion precautions)","CATHETER ABLATION of the cavotricuspid isthmus is highly effective and often FIRST-LINE for typical flutter (high cure rate, low complication) - more definitive than for AF","Cardioversion (flutter converts at low energy) with the same anticoagulation safeguards"],"pearls":["Typical atrial flutter = sawtooth waves (inferior leads), atrial rate ~300 with 2:1 conduction giving a ventricular rate of ~150 - suspect it in any regular narrow tachycardia at ~150","Apply the SAME anticoagulation rules as AF (stroke risk is comparable); the two often coexist","Cavotricuspid isthmus ablation is highly effective and often first-line for typical flutter (higher cure rate than AF ablation)","Adenosine/vagal maneuvers unmask flutter waves by transiently increasing AV block"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Flutter","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK540985/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Supraventricular Tachycardia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441972/"}]},{"id":"cards-svt-wpw","name":"Supraventricular Tachycardia & WPW","sec":"cards","present":["Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia from a reentrant circuit - most commonly AV NODAL REENTRANT TACHYCARDIA (AVNRT) or AV REENTRANT TACHYCARDIA (AVRT) using an accessory pathway","WOLFF-PARKINSON-WHITE (WPW) is ventricular pre-excitation via an accessory pathway (short PR, delta wave, wide QRS on baseline ECG) that predisposes to AVRT and, dangerously, to rapid conduction of AF","Presents with abrupt palpitations, sometimes dizziness/dyspnea; usually hemodynamically tolerated but can be unstable"],"dx":["ECG during tachycardia: regular narrow-complex SVT; baseline ECG in WPW shows a short PR and delta wave (pre-excitation)","Vagal maneuvers/adenosine terminate AV-node-dependent SVT (diagnostic and therapeutic); a P-wave relationship helps subtype","Recognize PRE-EXCITED AF (irregular, wide, very rapid) - a can't-miss dangerous rhythm in WPW"],"tx":["Acute SVT: vagal maneuvers first, then ADENOSINE (blocks the AV node to terminate AVNRT/AVRT); synchronized cardioversion if unstable","WPW with PRE-EXCITED AF: AVOID AV-nodal blockers (adenosine, beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, digoxin) - they can accelerate conduction down the accessory pathway and precipitate VF; use procainamide or synchronized cardioversion","Definitive: CATHETER ABLATION of the reentrant circuit/accessory pathway (curative, first-line for recurrent/symptomatic SVT and symptomatic WPW)"],"pearls":["Adenosine terminates AV-node-dependent SVT (AVNRT/AVRT) - vagal maneuvers first, cardiovert if unstable","WPW = short PR + delta wave; in PRE-EXCITED AF, NEVER give AV-nodal blockers (adenosine/BB/CCB/digoxin) - they can trigger VF; use procainamide or cardioversion","Catheter ablation is curative and first-line for recurrent symptomatic SVT and symptomatic WPW","A regular narrow-complex tachycardia that breaks with adenosine is AV-node-dependent SVT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Wolff-Parkinson-White Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554437/"},{"t":"StatPearls: AV Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499936/"}]},{"id":"cards-ventricular-arrhythmias","name":"Ventricular Arrhythmias","sec":"cards","present":["Ventricular arrhythmias range from premature ventricular complexes (PVCs) to VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (VT, wide-complex) and VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION (VF) - the latter two are life-threatening","VT is monomorphic (usually scar-related, e.g., prior MI) or polymorphic; TORSADES DE POINTES is polymorphic VT in the setting of a prolonged QT","A wide-complex tachycardia should be treated as VT until proven otherwise (especially with structural heart disease)"],"dx":["ECG: wide-complex tachycardia; features favoring VT include AV dissociation, capture/fusion beats, and very wide QRS; a history of structural heart disease strongly favors VT","Assess for reversible causes: ischemia, electrolyte abnormalities (K, Mg), QT-prolonging drugs (torsades), and channelopathies (long QT, Brugada, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT)","Evaluate LV function/scar (echo/CMR) and ischemia; identify the substrate"],"tx":["UNSTABLE VT/VF: immediate DEFIBRILLATION (VF/pulseless VT) or synchronized cardioversion (unstable VT with a pulse) per ACLS; CPR as needed","STABLE monomorphic VT: antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, procainamide, lidocaine) and treat ischemia/reversible causes; TORSADES: IV MAGNESIUM, stop offending drugs, correct electrolytes, and overdrive pacing/isoproterenol","Long-term: correct substrate/ischemia, ICD for secondary prevention (and high-risk primary prevention), and catheter ablation for recurrent scar-related VT"],"pearls":["Treat a wide-complex tachycardia as VT until proven otherwise - especially with known structural heart disease","Unstable VT/VF: defibrillate (pulseless) or synchronized cardiovert (unstable with pulse) immediately per ACLS","Torsades de pointes (polymorphic VT with long QT): IV magnesium, stop QT-prolonging drugs, correct electrolytes","Look for reversible causes (ischemia, K/Mg, drugs) and channelopathies (long QT, Brugada, CPVT); ICD for secondary prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ventricular Tachycardia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532954/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Torsade de Pointes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459388/"}]},{"id":"cards-sudden-cardiac-death","name":"Sudden Cardiac Death Prevention","sec":"cards","present":["Sudden cardiac death (SCD) is unexpected death from a cardiac cause, usually a ventricular arrhythmia (VT/VF); coronary artery disease/prior MI is the most common substrate in adults","In the young/athletes, causes include hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, ARVC, channelopathies (long QT, Brugada, CPVT), and congenital coronary anomalies","Prevention targets the highest-risk substrates with implantable defibrillators and risk-factor/trigger management"],"dx":["Identify high-risk substrates: reduced LVEF (ischemic/non-ischemic cardiomyopathy), prior sustained VT/VF or resuscitated arrest, and inherited arrhythmia syndromes/cardiomyopathies","Risk assessment: LVEF (the dominant driver for primary prevention), plus disease-specific risk markers (HCM risk factors, long-QT features, family history of SCD)","Genetic testing/family screening for inherited syndromes; evaluate for reversible/ischemic causes"],"tx":["ICD for SECONDARY prevention (survivors of VT/VF arrest not due to a reversible cause) and PRIMARY prevention (e.g., LVEF <=35% despite >=3 months of GDMT, ischemic or non-ischemic; specific inherited-disease criteria)","Optimize GDMT/beta-blockers, revascularize ischemia, and manage disease-specific triggers (avoid QT-prolonging drugs in long QT; exercise restriction in ARVC/CPVT)","A WEARABLE defibrillator may bridge patients during the waiting period before permanent ICD decisions; public access defibrillation/CPR for community arrest"],"pearls":["Reduced LVEF (<=35% despite optimized GDMT) is the main primary-prevention ICD criterion; prior VT/VF arrest (not from a reversible cause) mandates a secondary-prevention ICD","Reassess EF AFTER >=3 months of GDMT (and after revascularization) before implanting a primary-prevention ICD - function may recover","In the young/athletes, think HCM, ARVC, channelopathies (long QT/Brugada/CPVT), and coronary anomalies","Disease-specific prevention: beta-blockers/exercise restriction (long QT, CPVT, ARVC), avoid QT-prolonging drugs (long QT), avoid triggers (Brugada - fever)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Sudden Cardiac Death","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507854/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillator","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459166/"}]},{"id":"cards-bradycardia-conduction","name":"Bradycardia & Conduction Blocks","sec":"cards","present":["Bradyarrhythmias arise from SINUS NODE DYSFUNCTION (sick sinus syndrome) or ATRIOVENTRICULAR (AV) block; symptoms include fatigue, dizziness, syncope, and exercise intolerance","AV block: FIRST-DEGREE (prolonged PR), SECOND-DEGREE Mobitz I (Wenckebach - progressive PR lengthening then a dropped beat, usually AV-nodal/benign) and Mobitz II (sudden dropped beats, infranodal - high risk of progression to complete block), and THIRD-DEGREE (complete AV dissociation)","Reversible causes are common: drugs (beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, digoxin), ischemia (especially inferior MI), hyperkalemia, hypothyroidism, and increased vagal tone"],"dx":["ECG classifies the block; localize (Mobitz I/AV-nodal vs Mobitz II/infranodal - the latter is unstable); ambulatory monitoring correlates symptoms with rhythm","Identify and treat REVERSIBLE causes (medications, ischemia, electrolytes, thyroid) before committing to a permanent device","Assess hemodynamic stability and symptom-rhythm correlation"],"tx":["UNSTABLE bradycardia: ATROPINE first-line; if refractory, transcutaneous PACING and/or chronotropic infusions (dopamine, epinephrine) per ACLS; treat reversible causes","PERMANENT PACEMAKER for symptomatic sinus node dysfunction and for high-grade AV block (Mobitz II, third-degree, or symptomatic) NOT due to a reversible cause","Note: atropine may be ineffective (or worsen) infranodal (Mobitz II/complete) block - be ready to pace"],"pearls":["Mobitz I (Wenckebach): progressive PR prolongation then a dropped beat - usually AV-nodal and benign; Mobitz II: sudden dropped beats, infranodal, high risk of complete block -> pacemaker","Always exclude reversible causes (drugs - BB/CCB/digoxin, ischemia, hyperkalemia, hypothyroidism, vagal tone) before a permanent pacemaker","Unstable bradycardia: atropine first, then transcutaneous pacing/chronotropes - but atropine often fails in infranodal (Mobitz II/complete) block, so be ready to pace","Permanent pacing for symptomatic sinus node dysfunction and high-grade/symptomatic AV block not from a reversible cause"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atrioventricular Block","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459147/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sick Sinus Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470599/"}]},{"id":"cards-pacemakers","name":"Pacemakers","sec":"cards","present":["Permanent pacemakers treat symptomatic bradyarrhythmias (sinus node dysfunction, high-grade AV block) by delivering electrical stimulation; leads are placed in the right atrium and/or right ventricle (or leadless in the RV)","A standardized code names the mode (e.g., DDD = dual-chamber sensing/pacing/dual response; VVI = ventricular pace/sense/inhibit); mode is chosen by the rhythm problem","Modern considerations include minimizing unnecessary RV pacing (which can worsen function) and conduction-system (His/left bundle) pacing"],"dx":["Confirm the pacing indication (symptomatic bradycardia/high-grade block not from a reversible cause); select single- vs dual-chamber based on the conduction problem and atrial rhythm","Interrogate devices for capture/sensing thresholds, lead integrity, battery, and pacing percentages","Recognize pacemaker malfunction on ECG: FAILURE TO CAPTURE (pacing spike without a following complex), FAILURE TO SENSE (inappropriate spikes), and failure to pace (no output when needed)"],"tx":["Implant the appropriate pacemaker/mode; program to minimize unnecessary RV pacing when AV conduction is intact; consider conduction-system pacing (His/left bundle) or biventricular pacing to avoid RV-pacing-induced dysfunction","Manage complications: lead dislodgement, pneumothorax, pocket hematoma/infection, and (late) lead issues; device/lead infection requires complete SYSTEM EXTRACTION plus antibiotics","MRI-conditional systems and perioperative magnet/management considerations"],"pearls":["Pacemaker codes: DDD (dual-chamber) vs VVI (ventricular) - choose by the conduction problem and atrial rhythm","ECG malfunctions: failure to capture (spike, no complex), failure to sense (inappropriate spikes) - recognize these patterns","Minimize unnecessary RV pacing (it can worsen ventricular function) - consider conduction-system or biventricular pacing in appropriate patients","Device/lead infection requires complete hardware extraction plus antibiotics - antibiotics alone are insufficient"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pacemaker","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526001/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Permanent Pacemaker Insertion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526048/"}]},{"id":"cards-icd-crt","name":"ICD & Cardiac Resynchronization Therapy","sec":"cards","present":["An IMPLANTABLE CARDIOVERTER-DEFIBRILLATOR (ICD) detects and terminates life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF) with antitachycardia pacing or shocks - for sudden-death prevention","CARDIAC RESYNCHRONIZATION THERAPY (CRT, biventricular pacing) adds a left-ventricular lead to resynchronize contraction in HFrEF patients with electrical dyssynchrony (wide QRS/LBBB), improving symptoms and survival","Devices are often combined (CRT-D)"],"dx":["ICD indications: secondary prevention (survived VT/VF not from a reversible cause) and primary prevention (LVEF <=35% despite GDMT, and disease-specific criteria)","CRT indications: HFrEF (LVEF <=35%) with LEFT BUNDLE BRANCH BLOCK and QRS >=150 ms on GDMT derive the greatest benefit (benefit is strongest with LBBB and wider QRS)","Assess GDMT optimization and reversible factors before implant; interrogate for appropriate vs inappropriate therapies"],"tx":["Implant ICD/CRT per indication; program to reduce INAPPROPRIATE SHOCKS (from AF/SVT or oversensing) - a major morbidity","Optimize GDMT alongside device therapy; CRT non-responders may need lead optimization/repositioning","Manage device complications (lead issues, infection - requiring extraction) and provide shock counseling/psychological support"],"pearls":["Primary-prevention ICD: LVEF <=35% despite >=3 months of GDMT (reassess EF first - it may recover); secondary prevention: prior VT/VF arrest not from a reversible cause","CRT benefits HFrEF with LBBB and wide QRS (>=150 ms) the most - it resynchronizes dyssynchronous contraction and improves survival","Minimize inappropriate ICD shocks (often triggered by AF/SVT) through programming and rate/rhythm control","Device infection mandates complete system extraction plus antibiotics"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Resynchronization Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553748/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillator Indications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507200/"}]},{"id":"cards-catheter-ablation","name":"Catheter Ablation","sec":"cards","present":["Catheter ablation delivers energy (radiofrequency or cryo, and newer PULSED-FIELD ablation) through catheters to destroy arrhythmogenic tissue/circuits - an increasingly first-line, often curative rhythm therapy","Highly effective targets: typical atrial flutter (cavotricuspid isthmus), AVNRT, accessory pathways (WPW), and focal atrial tachycardias; AF ablation centers on PULMONARY VEIN ISOLATION","Scar-related VT ablation reduces recurrent arrhythmia/ICD shocks in structural heart disease"],"dx":["Electrophysiology study maps the circuit/mechanism; imaging (echo/CT) defines anatomy and excludes left atrial appendage thrombus (TEE) before left-sided/AF ablation","Select candidates by arrhythmia type, symptom burden, and antiarrhythmic response/preference","Assess procedural risk and anticoagulation status"],"tx":["Ablation is first-line/curative for many SVTs (AVNRT, accessory pathway, typical flutter) and effective for symptomatic paroxysmal AF (pulmonary vein isolation), often superior to antiarrhythmic drugs for rhythm control/QOL","VT ablation for recurrent scar-related VT/ICD shocks (adjunct to ICD, not a replacement)","Periprocedural anticoagulation for left-sided/AF ablation; continue as indicated afterward"],"pearls":["Ablation is curative/first-line for AVNRT, accessory pathways (WPW), and typical atrial flutter (cavotricuspid isthmus)","AF ablation = pulmonary vein isolation; effective for symptomatic paroxysmal AF and often superior to antiarrhythmic drugs for rhythm control","VT ablation reduces recurrent scar-related VT/ICD shocks but complements (does not replace) an ICD","Exclude left atrial appendage thrombus (TEE) and manage anticoagulation before left-sided/AF ablation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Catheter Ablation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470203/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Vein Isolation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572075/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiac-arrest-acls","name":"Cardiac Arrest & ACLS","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiac arrest is the abrupt loss of cardiac function with no pulse; survival depends on the CHAIN OF SURVIVAL: early recognition, high-quality CPR, rapid DEFIBRILLATION of shockable rhythms, and post-arrest care","Rhythms split into SHOCKABLE (VF, pulseless VT) and NON-SHOCKABLE (asystole, pulseless electrical activity/PEA)","High-quality chest compressions (adequate rate/depth, minimal interruptions, full recoil) and early defibrillation drive outcomes"],"dx":["Confirm arrest (unresponsive, no pulse/normal breathing); attach a monitor/defibrillator to determine the rhythm (shockable vs non-shockable)","Search for and treat REVERSIBLE CAUSES - the Hs and Ts (Hypoxia, Hypovolemia, Hydrogen ions/acidosis, Hypo/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis - coronary/pulmonary)","Use end-tidal CO2 to gauge CPR quality and detect return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC)"],"tx":["SHOCKABLE (VF/pulseless VT): immediate DEFIBRILLATION + CPR; epinephrine every 3-5 min; antiarrhythmic (amiodarone or lidocaine) for refractory VF/VT","NON-SHOCKABLE (asystole/PEA): CPR + epinephrine every 3-5 min; do NOT shock; aggressively find/treat reversible causes","POST-ROSC care: support hemodynamics/oxygenation (avoid hyperoxia/hypotension), obtain an ECG and pursue emergent CORONARY ANGIOGRAPHY for STEMI/suspected cardiac cause, and use targeted temperature management/avoid fever"],"pearls":["High-quality compressions and early defibrillation of shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) are the biggest survival levers","Epinephrine every 3-5 min in all arrests; add amiodarone/lidocaine for refractory VF/pulseless VT; asystole/PEA are NOT shocked","Always hunt the reversible causes (Hs and Ts); use end-tidal CO2 to monitor CPR quality and detect ROSC","Post-ROSC: avoid hypotension/hyperoxia, get an ECG, pursue emergent angiography for a cardiac cause, and use targeted temperature management/avoid fever"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Arrest","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534866/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Advanced Cardiac Life Support","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459168/"}]},{"id":"cards-aortic-stenosis","name":"Aortic Stenosis","sec":"cards","present":["Aortic stenosis (AS) is obstruction of LV outflow, most often from CALCIFIC degeneration (elderly) or a congenital BICUSPID valve (younger); rheumatic disease is another cause","Classic symptoms mark advanced disease and poor prognosis if untreated: ANGINA, SYNCOPE, and heart failure (dyspnea) - the SAD triad","The murmur is a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids, with a delayed/weak carotid upstroke (pulsus parvus et tardus) in severe disease"],"dx":["ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY grades severity: severe AS is aortic valve area <=1.0 cm2, mean gradient >=40 mmHg, or peak jet velocity >=4 m/s; assess LV function and for low-flow/low-gradient AS (dobutamine echo)","Symptom status and LV function drive the timing of intervention; exercise testing (cautiously) can unmask symptoms in 'asymptomatic' severe AS","CT for valve calcium/anatomy and TAVR planning"],"tx":["Symptomatic severe AS or severe AS with LV dysfunction requires VALVE REPLACEMENT - medical therapy does not alter the natural history; there is no effective medical therapy to defer intervention","Choice of SURGICAL (SAVR) vs TRANSCATHETER (TAVR) aortic valve replacement is heart-team based (age, surgical risk, anatomy) - TAVR is now first-line for many across the risk spectrum (see Thoracic Surgery/structural entries)","Avoid aggressive preload reduction (nitrates/diuretics) in severe AS - these patients are preload-dependent; manage carefully"],"pearls":["Symptomatic severe AS (angina, syncope, heart failure) needs valve replacement - no medical therapy changes its natural history","Severe AS: valve area <=1.0 cm2, mean gradient >=40 mmHg, or velocity >=4 m/s; look for low-flow/low-gradient AS (dobutamine echo)","Pulsus parvus et tardus (weak, delayed carotid upstroke) signals severe AS; avoid aggressive preload reduction (preload-dependent)","TAVR vs SAVR is heart-team decided; TAVR is first-line for many patients (see Thoracic Surgery for surgical/structural detail)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557628/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bicuspid Aortic Valve","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534214/"}]},{"id":"cards-aortic-regurgitation","name":"Aortic Regurgitation","sec":"cards","present":["Aortic regurgitation (AR) is diastolic backflow into the LV from valve or aortic-root disease; CHRONIC AR causes gradual LV volume overload/dilation (long compensated phase), while ACUTE AR (endocarditis, dissection) causes sudden decompensation and cardiogenic shock","Causes: valve (bicuspid, rheumatic, endocarditis, degenerative) and aortic root (dilation, Marfan, dissection)","Chronic AR gives wide pulse pressure and multiple classic peripheral signs (bounding pulses, head bobbing, etc.); a decrescendo diastolic murmur at the left sternal border"],"dx":["Echocardiography grades severity, LV size/function, and the mechanism (valve vs root); acute AR is a hemodynamic emergency (tachycardia, pulmonary edema, no time for compensatory dilation)","Follow chronic AR with serial echo for LV size/EF; assess the aorta (CT/CMR) for root dilation","Wide pulse pressure and peripheral signs point to significant chronic AR"],"tx":["ACUTE severe AR (endocarditis/dissection): URGENT SURGERY - it is poorly tolerated; temporize (avoid intra-aortic balloon pump, which is contraindicated in AR) while arranging operation","CHRONIC AR: replace/repair for symptoms, or for LV dysfunction (EF <=55%) or significant LV dilation (end-systolic dimension thresholds) even if asymptomatic; treat/monitor the aortic root","Vasodilators for symptomatic patients not yet surgical or with hypertension; treat the underlying cause"],"pearls":["Acute severe AR (endocarditis, dissection) is a surgical emergency - it decompensates fast; the intra-aortic balloon pump is CONTRAINDICATED in AR","Chronic AR: operate for symptoms, LV dysfunction (EF <=55%), or significant LV dilation (end-systolic dimension) even when asymptomatic","Wide pulse pressure with bounding peripheral pulses signals significant chronic AR","Evaluate the aortic root (Marfan/dilation/dissection) as a cause and a co-target for surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Regurgitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555944/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Insufficiency","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553180/"}]},{"id":"cards-mitral-stenosis","name":"Mitral Stenosis","sec":"cards","present":["Mitral stenosis (MS) is obstruction of LV inflow; RHEUMATIC heart disease is the dominant cause worldwide (with characteristic commissural fusion), while calcific/degenerative MS occurs in the elderly","Obstruction raises left atrial pressure -> pulmonary congestion, atrial enlargement/AF, pulmonary hypertension, and right heart failure; symptoms worsen with tachycardia/increased flow (exercise, pregnancy, AF)","The murmur is a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex with an opening snap; a loud S1"],"dx":["Echocardiography grades severity (valve area, gradient, pulmonary pressures) and morphology (Wilkins score predicts suitability for balloon valvuloplasty); assess for left atrial thrombus (TEE)","Recognize decompensation triggers (new AF with rapid rate, pregnancy) that acutely worsen symptoms","Assess pulmonary hypertension and right heart consequences"],"tx":["Rate control (slow the heart to allow LV filling) and manage AF; ANTICOAGULATE for AF in MS with WARFARIN (rheumatic/valvular AF - DOACs are contraindicated in significant/rheumatic MS)","Intervention for symptomatic severe MS: PERCUTANEOUS MITRAL BALLOON VALVULOPLASTY for suitable pliable rheumatic valves (favorable Wilkins score, no significant MR/left atrial thrombus); otherwise surgical repair/replacement","Endocarditis and rheumatic-disease secondary prevention as appropriate"],"pearls":["Rheumatic disease is the leading cause of mitral stenosis worldwide; the murmur is a diastolic rumble with an opening snap","Anticoagulate AF in significant/rheumatic MS with WARFARIN - DOACs are contraindicated in moderate-severe (rheumatic) mitral stenosis","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty is preferred for suitable pliable rheumatic valves (favorable Wilkins score, no left atrial thrombus/significant MR)","Tachycardia and new AF acutely worsen MS (less filling time) - rate control is key; pregnancy commonly unmasks MS"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mitral Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430742/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rheumatic Mitral Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556005/"}]},{"id":"cards-mitral-regurgitation","name":"Mitral Regurgitation","sec":"cards","present":["Mitral regurgitation (MR) is systolic backflow into the left atrium; PRIMARY (degenerative - prolapse/flail, rheumatic, endocarditis) is a valve problem, while SECONDARY (functional) MR results from LV dilation/ischemia with structurally normal leaflets","ACUTE severe MR (papillary muscle rupture post-MI, chordal rupture, endocarditis) causes sudden pulmonary edema/shock; CHRONIC MR causes gradual LV volume overload","The murmur is a holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla"],"dx":["Echocardiography (often TEE) grades severity, defines the mechanism (primary vs secondary), and assesses LV size/function and repairability","Chronic primary MR: follow LV size/EF for the timing of surgery; a 'normal-low' EF in significant MR actually indicates LV dysfunction (the regurgitant path masks true function)","Acute MR: recognize the emergency (flash pulmonary edema, new murmur post-MI)"],"tx":["PRIMARY severe MR: mitral REPAIR (preferred over replacement) for symptoms or LV dysfunction/dilation (EF <=60% or LV end-systolic dimension threshold) even if asymptomatic - refer to repair centers (see Thoracic Surgery)","SECONDARY (functional) MR: optimize GDMT/revascularization first; TRANSCATHETER EDGE-TO-EDGE REPAIR (TEER/MitraClip) benefits selected patients (COAPT); surgery in selected cases","ACUTE severe MR: stabilize (afterload reduction, mechanical support) and URGENT surgery"],"pearls":["Primary severe MR: repair beats replacement - operate for symptoms or for LV dysfunction (EF <=60%) or dilation, even when asymptomatic (a 'normal-low' EF already signals dysfunction in MR)","Secondary/functional MR: optimize GDMT/revascularization first; transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (MitraClip) helps selected patients (COAPT)","Acute severe MR (post-MI papillary rupture, endocarditis) is a surgical emergency with flash pulmonary edema","The murmur is holosystolic at the apex radiating to the axilla; cross-reference Thoracic Surgery for surgical detail"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mitral Regurgitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430907/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mitral Valve Prolapse","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470288/"}]},{"id":"cards-tricuspid-pulmonic","name":"Tricuspid & Pulmonic Valve Disease","sec":"cards","present":["Right-heart valve disease is dominated by TRICUSPID REGURGITATION (TR), most commonly SECONDARY/functional (from RV/annular dilation due to pulmonary hypertension or left-heart disease) rather than primary","Primary TR causes: endocarditis (especially in injection drug users - right-sided), rheumatic disease, carcinoid, device-lead-related, and Ebstein anomaly; pulmonic valve disease is often congenital","Significant TR causes right heart failure signs: elevated JVP (prominent v wave), hepatomegaly, ascites, and peripheral edema"],"dx":["Echocardiography grades TR severity, RV size/function, and pulmonary pressures; identify primary vs secondary mechanism and any lead-related or endocarditis cause","Right-sided endocarditis: blood cultures and echo (large tricuspid vegetations, septic pulmonary emboli on imaging)","Assess the left-heart/pulmonary-hypertension driver of functional TR"],"tx":["SECONDARY TR: treat the underlying cause (left-heart disease, pulmonary hypertension, volume) - diuretics for congestion; address concomitantly during left-sided valve surgery when significant","Severe symptomatic primary/secondary TR: surgical repair/replacement in selected patients; emerging TRANSCATHETER tricuspid therapies for high-risk patients","Right-sided endocarditis: prolonged antibiotics; surgery for specific indications (see endocarditis/Thoracic entries)"],"pearls":["Most tricuspid regurgitation is SECONDARY (functional) from RV/annular dilation due to pulmonary hypertension or left-heart disease - treat the underlying driver","Right-sided (tricuspid) endocarditis is classically associated with injection drug use and causes septic pulmonary emboli","Significant TR causes right heart failure: elevated JVP, hepatomegaly, ascites, edema; diuretics manage congestion","Address significant TR during left-sided valve surgery; transcatheter tricuspid options are emerging for high-risk patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tricuspid Regurgitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459354/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tricuspid Valve Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560800/"}]},{"id":"cards-prosthetic-valves","name":"Prosthetic Heart Valves","sec":"cards","present":["Prosthetic valves are MECHANICAL (durable but require lifelong anticoagulation) or BIOPROSTHETIC (limited durability but no long-term anticoagulation); the choice balances durability against bleeding/anticoagulation burden and is guided by age and patient preference","Mechanical valves are generally favored in younger patients (durability); bioprosthetic in older patients or those who cannot take anticoagulation (or want to avoid it)","Complications include thrombosis, thromboembolism, bleeding, structural degeneration (bioprosthetic), paravalvular leak, and PROSTHETIC VALVE ENDOCARDITIS"],"dx":["Follow with clinical exam and echocardiography (baseline post-implant, then for symptoms/complications); TEE for suspected thrombosis, endocarditis, or paravalvular leak","Evaluate a new murmur, heart failure, hemolysis (paravalvular leak), or embolic/thrombotic events; assess INR control in mechanical valves","Prosthetic valve endocarditis: blood cultures + TEE (often needs surgery)"],"tx":["MECHANICAL valves: lifelong WARFARIN (DOACs are CONTRAINDICATED) with target INR by valve position/type (+/- low-dose aspirin); bridge for procedures per thrombotic risk","BIOPROSTHETIC valves: short-term anticoagulation/antiplatelet per guidelines then typically aspirin; watch for structural valve deterioration over time (valve-in-valve TAVR is an option for degeneration)","Prosthetic valve thrombosis: anticoagulation/fibrinolysis or surgery; endocarditis usually requires surgery"],"pearls":["Mechanical valves require lifelong warfarin - DOACs are contraindicated; target INR varies by valve position/type","Mechanical (durable, anticoagulation) vs bioprosthetic (no long-term anticoagulation, less durable) - choose by age and patient preference","Bioprosthetic degeneration can be treated with valve-in-valve TAVR in selected patients","New murmur/heart failure/hemolysis suggests paravalvular leak or dysfunction; prosthetic valve endocarditis usually needs surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Prosthetic Heart Valves","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560750/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mechanical Heart Valve","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562215/"}]},{"id":"cards-structural-transcatheter","name":"Structural & Transcatheter Interventions","sec":"cards","present":["Transcatheter structural interventions treat valve and structural disease without open surgery: TAVR (transcatheter aortic valve replacement), transcatheter mitral/tricuspid edge-to-edge repair (TEER/MitraClip), LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE OCCLUSION, PFO/ASD closure, and paravalvular leak closure","These expand options for high-risk/inoperable patients and, for TAVR, increasingly across the surgical-risk spectrum","A HEART TEAM (cardiology + cardiac surgery) selects patients and modality"],"dx":["Detailed imaging drives planning: CT for TAVR (annular sizing, access, coronary heights), TEE for mitral/appendage procedures; assess anatomy, access, and procedural risk","Confirm the disease severity/indication and surgical risk; heart-team evaluation","Screen for contraindications (thrombus, unfavorable anatomy)"],"tx":["TAVR for symptomatic severe aortic stenosis (first-line for many; see AS/Thoracic entries); TEER (MitraClip) for selected primary MR (high surgical risk) and secondary MR (COAPT)","LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE OCCLUSION (e.g., Watchman) for AF stroke prevention when long-term anticoagulation is contraindicated/not tolerated","PFO closure for selected cryptogenic stroke; transcatheter tricuspid and paravalvular-leak therapies for appropriate patients"],"pearls":["TAVR is first-line for many patients with severe symptomatic aortic stenosis (heart-team selected); watch for new conduction block/pacemaker and paravalvular leak","Transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (MitraClip) helps selected primary (high-risk) and secondary MR (COAPT)","Left atrial appendage occlusion is an alternative for AF stroke prevention when anticoagulation is contraindicated","A heart team (cardiology + surgery) selects patients and modality; cross-reference Thoracic Surgery for surgical options"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Transcatheter Aortic Valve Replacement","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431075/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Left Atrial Appendage Occlusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572123/"}]},{"id":"cards-pericarditis-effusion","name":"Acute Pericarditis & Pericardial Effusion","sec":"cards","present":["Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium; causes are often idiopathic/viral, plus post-MI (early and late/Dressler), uremia, autoimmune, malignancy, post-cardiac-injury, and tuberculosis","Classic features: sharp, PLEURITIC chest pain that is worse supine and BETTER LEANING FORWARD, a pericardial FRICTION RUB, and characteristic ECG changes","A pericardial EFFUSION may accompany it and, if large/rapid, can cause tamponade"],"dx":["Diagnose with >=2 of: typical chest pain, friction rub, ECG changes (DIFFUSE ST elevation with PR depression), and a new/worsening pericardial effusion","Echocardiography assesses effusion size and tamponade physiology; check inflammatory markers and troponin (myopericarditis if elevated)","Search for a specific cause when features suggest it (uremia, malignancy, TB, autoimmune)"],"tx":["Idiopathic/viral pericarditis: NSAIDs (or aspirin post-MI) PLUS COLCHICINE (colchicine reduces recurrence) as first-line; taper per response","Treat the underlying cause (dialysis for uremic pericarditis, etc.); reserve corticosteroids for specific indications/refractory autoimmune cases (steroids increase recurrence risk)","Avoid high-dose NSAIDs early post-MI (impair healing); restrict strenuous exercise until resolved"],"pearls":["Pericarditis pain is pleuritic, worse supine, and relieved by leaning forward; ECG shows diffuse ST elevation with PR depression, and there may be a friction rub","First-line therapy is NSAID (or aspirin post-MI) PLUS colchicine - colchicine reduces recurrence","Corticosteroids increase recurrence risk - reserve them for specific/refractory autoimmune cases","Treat the underlying cause; avoid high-dose NSAIDs immediately post-MI"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Pericarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431080/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pericardial Effusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431089/"}]},{"id":"cards-tamponade-constriction","name":"Cardiac Tamponade & Constrictive Pericarditis","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiac TAMPONADE is pericardial fluid (or blood) compressing the heart and impairing filling - obstructive shock; it can develop rapidly (small volume, e.g., hemorrhage/dissection) or slowly (large volume)","CONSTRICTIVE pericarditis is a rigid/scarred (often calcified) pericardium restricting diastolic filling, causing right-heart failure signs and mimicking restrictive cardiomyopathy","(Surgical pericardiocentesis technique/pericardiectomy is covered under Thoracic Surgery - this is the medical recognition/diagnosis view)"],"dx":["TAMPONADE: hypotension, elevated JVP, and muffled heart sounds (Beck's triad), PULSUS PARADOXUS (>10 mmHg inspiratory systolic drop), and echo showing effusion with diastolic RV/RA collapse and IVC plethora - a clinical/echo diagnosis","CONSTRICTION: elevated JVP with KUSSMAUL'S SIGN, pericardial knock, and imaging/cath showing ventricular interdependence, equalized diastolic pressures, and a 'dip-and-plateau'; distinguish from restrictive cardiomyopathy","CT/CMR shows pericardial thickening/calcification in constriction"],"tx":["TAMPONADE: urgent PERICARDIOCENTESIS (or surgical drainage) to relieve compression; give IV fluids to temporize; AVOID intubation/positive-pressure ventilation before drainage if possible (worsens hemodynamics)","CONSTRICTION: definitive treatment is surgical PERICARDIECTOMY for symptomatic patients (see Thoracic Surgery); trial of anti-inflammatory therapy for early/transient inflammatory constriction","Treat the underlying cause (malignancy, uremia, infection, post-cardiac injury)"],"pearls":["Tamponade: Beck's triad + pulsus paradoxus + echo diastolic chamber collapse -> urgent pericardiocentesis; avoid positive-pressure ventilation before drainage","A rapidly accumulating small effusion (hemorrhage/dissection) can tamponade with less fluid than a slowly accumulating large one","Constrictive pericarditis: Kussmaul's sign, pericardial knock, ventricular interdependence - definitive treatment is pericardiectomy (cross-ref Thoracic)","Distinguish constriction (surgically curable) from restrictive cardiomyopathy (medical) - imaging and hemodynamics differ"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Tamponade","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431090/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Constrictive Pericarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459307/"}]},{"id":"cards-infective-endocarditis-medical","name":"Infective Endocarditis (Medical)","sec":"cards","present":["Infective endocarditis (IE) is infection of the endocardium/valves, forming vegetations; risk factors include prosthetic valves, prior IE, structural/congenital heart disease, injection drug use (right-sided), and intravascular devices","Common organisms: Staphylococcus aureus (acute, aggressive), viridans streptococci (subacute, native valves), enterococci, and coagulase-negative staph (prosthetic/device)","Presentation ranges from acute sepsis to subacute constitutional illness; complications include heart failure, embolism, abscess, and immune phenomena"],"dx":["Apply the modified DUKE criteria integrating BLOOD CULTURES (obtain multiple sets before antibiotics) and ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY (TEE is more sensitive than TTE for vegetations, abscess, and prosthetic involvement)","Look for peripheral/immune signs (Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages) and embolic phenomena; image for septic emboli/abscesses","Identify the organism and source; assess for surgical indications"],"tx":["Prolonged targeted IV ANTIBIOTICS based on organism/susceptibility (empiric coverage adjusted after cultures); typically several weeks","SURGERY (see Thoracic Surgery) for the accepted indications: heart failure from valve dysfunction, uncontrolled/persistent infection or abscess, and prevention of embolism (large mobile vegetations, especially after embolism)","Antibiotic PROPHYLAXIS only for the HIGHEST-RISK patients (prosthetic valve/material, prior IE, certain congenital disease, transplant valvulopathy) undergoing high-risk dental procedures"],"pearls":["Diagnose with modified Duke criteria - multiple blood cultures before antibiotics + echocardiography (TEE beats TTE for vegetations/abscess/prosthetic valves)","Staph aureus causes acute, aggressive IE; viridans streptococci cause subacute native-valve IE; right-sided IE is linked to injection drug use","Surgical indications: heart failure, uncontrolled infection/abscess, and high embolic risk (large mobile vegetations) - cross-reference Thoracic Surgery","Endocarditis prophylaxis is reserved for the highest-risk patients (prosthetic valve/material, prior IE, certain congenital disease) before high-risk dental work"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Infective Endocarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557641/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endocarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459133/"}]},{"id":"cards-rheumatic-heart-disease","name":"Rheumatic Heart Disease","sec":"cards","present":["Rheumatic heart disease is chronic valve damage from ACUTE RHEUMATIC FEVER, an autoimmune sequela of untreated group A streptococcal PHARYNGITIS (molecular mimicry); it remains a major cause of valvular disease worldwide","Acute rheumatic fever is diagnosed by the JONES criteria (major: carditis, migratory polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules; minor: fever, arthralgia, elevated acute-phase reactants, prolonged PR) with evidence of preceding strep infection","Chronic valve involvement most often causes MITRAL STENOSIS (and mitral/aortic regurgitation), presenting years later"],"dx":["Acute rheumatic fever: apply Jones criteria + evidence of antecedent GAS infection (throat culture/rapid test, or anti-streptococcal antibodies - ASO)","Echocardiography detects carditis/valvulitis acutely and chronic valve lesions (classic rheumatic mitral stenosis with commissural fusion) later","Assess valve severity and cardiac consequences (AF, pulmonary hypertension) in chronic disease"],"tx":["ACUTE rheumatic fever: treat the streptococcal infection (penicillin), anti-inflammatories (aspirin/NSAIDs) for arthritis/carditis; manage heart failure/carditis","SECONDARY PROPHYLAXIS with long-acting penicillin (to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever and progressive valve damage) - continued for years, longer if carditis/valve disease is present","Manage chronic valve disease (balloon valvuloplasty/surgery for rheumatic mitral stenosis; see valve entries)"],"pearls":["Rheumatic fever follows untreated group A strep pharyngitis - prevent it by treating strep throat; diagnose with the Jones criteria + evidence of preceding GAS infection","The most common chronic rheumatic valve lesion is mitral stenosis (commissural fusion) presenting years later","Secondary prophylaxis with long-acting penicillin prevents recurrent rheumatic fever/progressive valve damage (duration depends on carditis/valve involvement)","Rheumatic heart disease remains a leading cause of valvular disease worldwide"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rheumatic Heart Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538286/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Rheumatic Fever","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532875/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiac-masses-tumors","name":"Cardiac Masses & Tumors","sec":"cards","present":["Cardiac masses include TUMORS (rare) and non-neoplastic masses (thrombus and vegetations, which are far more common); the first step is distinguishing these","METASTATIC tumors to the heart are much more common than primary cardiac tumors; among primary tumors, MYXOMA is the most common benign tumor in adults and angiosarcoma the most common primary malignant one","Presentations depend on location: obstruction (myxoma mimicking mitral stenosis), embolism, arrhythmia, or constitutional symptoms"],"dx":["Echocardiography (often TEE) identifies the mass, attachment, and mobility; CARDIAC MRI characterizes tissue and helps distinguish tumor from thrombus and benign from malignant","Myxoma: a mobile, pedunculated LEFT ATRIAL mass attached to the interatrial septum (fossa ovalis); papillary fibroelastoma is a small mobile valve-associated tumor (embolic risk)","Evaluate for a primary malignancy (metastases) and for thrombus/vegetation as more common mimics"],"tx":["Symptomatic/embolizing benign tumors (myxoma, papillary fibroelastoma): surgical resection (see Thoracic Surgery) - myxoma resection is generally curative","Malignant primary tumors (angiosarcoma): poor prognosis; multimodality therapy in selected cases; metastatic involvement - treat the primary malignancy (often palliative)","Distinguish and treat non-tumor masses appropriately (anticoagulation for thrombus, antibiotics/surgery for vegetations)"],"pearls":["Thrombus and vegetations are far more common than tumors - distinguish these first (echo + CMR tissue characterization)","Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults - usually left atrial (fossa ovalis), causing obstruction (mimics MS), embolism, or constitutional symptoms; resection is curative (cross-ref Thoracic)","Metastases to the heart are commoner than primary tumors; angiosarcoma is the most common primary malignant cardiac tumor","Papillary fibroelastoma (valve-associated) carries embolic risk and may warrant resection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534801/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Myxoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470198/"}]},{"id":"cards-syncope","name":"Syncope Evaluation","sec":"cards","present":["Syncope is transient loss of consciousness from global cerebral hypoperfusion, with spontaneous complete recovery; the crucial task is separating benign REFLEX (vasovagal) syncope from dangerous CARDIAC syncope","Categories: REFLEX/neurally-mediated (vasovagal, situational, carotid sinus - most common, benign), ORTHOSTATIC hypotension (volume/autonomic/medications), and CARDIAC (arrhythmia or structural/obstructive - the highest-risk group)","Red flags for cardiac syncope: exertional or supine onset, no prodrome, palpitations preceding, structural heart disease, abnormal ECG, and family history of sudden death"],"dx":["History, exam, ORTHOSTATIC vitals, and a 12-lead ECG are the core initial evaluation (ECG screens for arrhythmia substrate, ischemia, pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada, blocks)","Risk-stratify: reassure/outpatient for clear low-risk reflex syncope; admit/monitor high-risk features; targeted testing (echo for structural disease, ambulatory/implantable monitors for arrhythmia, exercise testing for exertional syncope)","Avoid low-yield reflex testing (e.g., routine head CT, carotid dopplers) unless focal neurology - most syncope is not from cerebrovascular disease"],"tx":["REFLEX/vasovagal: education, trigger avoidance, hydration/salt, counterpressure maneuvers; adjust hypotensive medications; midodrine/fludrocortisone in selected refractory cases","CARDIAC: treat the cause - pacemaker for bradyarrhythmia/high-grade block, ICD for high-risk ventricular arrhythmia/structural disease, and treat outflow obstruction (AS, HCM)","ORTHOSTATIC: volume repletion, medication review, and physical countermeasures"],"pearls":["Separate benign reflex/vasovagal syncope from dangerous cardiac syncope - the core initial tools are history, orthostatic vitals, and a 12-lead ECG","Red flags for cardiac syncope: exertional/supine onset, no prodrome, preceding palpitations, structural heart disease, abnormal ECG, and family history of sudden death","Most syncope is NOT cerebrovascular - avoid routine head CT/carotid studies unless focal neurologic findings","Cardiac syncope treatment is cause-directed (pacemaker, ICD, relieve outflow obstruction)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Syncope","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442006/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vasovagal Syncope","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470277/"}]},{"id":"cards-peripheral-artery-disease","name":"Peripheral Artery Disease (Medical)","sec":"cards","present":["Peripheral artery disease (PAD) is atherosclerotic narrowing of the limb (usually lower-extremity) arteries; it is a CORONARY-RISK EQUIVALENT marking systemic atherosclerosis and high cardiovascular event risk","Classic symptom is INTERMITTENT CLAUDICATION (reproducible exertional calf/leg pain relieved by rest); advanced disease causes rest pain, non-healing ulcers, and CRITICAL LIMB ISCHEMIA (threatened limb)","Many patients are asymptomatic or have atypical symptoms"],"dx":["The ANKLE-BRACHIAL INDEX (ABI) is the first-line test: <=0.90 is diagnostic of PAD; falsely elevated/non-compressible (>1.40) vessels occur with medial calcification (diabetes, CKD - use toe-brachial index)","Duplex ultrasound/CT/MR angiography for anatomy when revascularization is considered; assess cardiovascular risk factors","Acute limb ischemia (the 6 Ps) is a separate emergency requiring urgent revascularization"],"tx":["MEDICAL/risk-factor therapy for all: SMOKING CESSATION, high-intensity STATIN, antiplatelet therapy, blood-pressure and diabetes control, and a supervised EXERCISE (walking) program (first-line for claudication); cilostazol improves walking distance","Low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin reduces limb/cardiovascular events in selected patients","REVASCULARIZATION (endovascular or surgical) for lifestyle-limiting claudication refractory to therapy or for critical limb ischemia (limb salvage)"],"pearls":["PAD is a coronary-risk equivalent - aggressive risk-factor modification (statin, antiplatelet, smoking cessation) reduces cardiovascular events, not just limb symptoms","ABI <=0.90 diagnoses PAD; a non-compressible ABI (>1.40, from medial calcification in diabetes/CKD) requires the toe-brachial index","Supervised exercise therapy and cilostazol improve claudication; revascularize for refractory lifestyle-limiting symptoms or critical limb ischemia","Acute limb ischemia (6 Ps) is a limb emergency needing urgent revascularization (distinct from chronic PAD)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Artery Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430745/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Intermittent Claudication","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430778/"}]},{"id":"cards-aortic-disease-medical","name":"Aortic Disease (Medical)","sec":"cards","present":["Aortic disease includes ANEURYSMS (thoracic and abdominal) and ACUTE AORTIC SYNDROMES (dissection, intramural hematoma, penetrating ulcer); this entry is the medical recognition, risk-factor, and surveillance view (surgical/endovascular repair is under Thoracic/Vascular)","ACUTE AORTIC DISSECTION presents with sudden severe tearing chest/back pain; type A (ascending) is a surgical emergency, type B (descending) is usually managed medically unless complicated","Risk factors: hypertension (the dominant one), connective tissue disease (Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, vascular Ehlers-Danlos), bicuspid aortic valve, and atherosclerosis"],"dx":["Suspect dissection with abrupt tearing pain +/- pulse/blood-pressure differentials, new AR murmur, or malperfusion; CT ANGIOGRAPHY is the diagnostic test (a widened mediastinum on chest radiograph is a clue)","Aneurysms are often incidental - screen with imaging (ultrasound for AAA; CT/MR for thoracic); the US Preventive Services Task Force recommends one-time AAA ultrasound screening in men 65-75 who ever smoked","Follow aneurysm size/growth to time repair; screen families/connective-tissue-disease patients"],"tx":["ACUTE MANAGEMENT of dissection/acute syndromes: strict ANTI-IMPULSE therapy - lower heart rate first (IV beta-blocker) THEN blood pressure to reduce aortic wall stress; type A -> emergency surgery, complicated type B -> TEVAR (see Thoracic)","ANEURYSM medical management: blood-pressure control, smoking cessation, statins, and surveillance imaging; repair at size thresholds (lower for connective tissue disease/bicuspid or rapid growth)","Genetic evaluation/family screening and activity counseling in heritable aortopathy"],"pearls":["Acute aortic dissection: sudden tearing chest/back pain - CT angiography diagnoses it; type A (ascending) is a surgical emergency, type B (descending) is usually medical","Anti-impulse therapy: lower HEART RATE first (beta-blocker) THEN blood pressure to reduce aortic wall shear stress","One-time screening AAA ultrasound is recommended for men 65-75 who ever smoked; follow aneurysm size to time repair (lower thresholds for Marfan/bicuspid/rapid growth)","Hypertension is the dominant risk factor; think connective tissue disease (Marfan/Loeys-Dietz) and bicuspid aortopathy in younger patients - cross-reference Thoracic/Vascular for repair"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Dissection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441963/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470237/"}]},{"id":"cards-hypertension","name":"Hypertension","sec":"cards","present":["Hypertension is chronically elevated blood pressure and the leading modifiable risk factor for cardiovascular disease, stroke, heart failure, and chronic kidney disease; most is PRIMARY (essential)","The ACC/AHA thresholds: elevated (120-129/<80), stage 1 (130-139 or 80-89), stage 2 (>=140 or >=90); diagnosis requires proper measurement and confirmation (often out-of-office/ambulatory or home readings)","It is usually asymptomatic ('silent killer') - end-organ damage accrues over years"],"dx":["Confirm with accurate technique and out-of-office measurements (ambulatory/home BP) to exclude white-coat and detect masked hypertension","Assess cardiovascular risk (ASCVD), screen for end-organ damage (ECG/LVH, renal function/urine albumin, retinopathy), and consider secondary causes when features suggest (see next entry)","Establish overall risk to set treatment intensity/targets"],"tx":["LIFESTYLE for all: the DASH diet, sodium reduction, weight loss, physical activity, limiting alcohol, and smoking cessation","PHARMACOTHERAPY first-line classes: thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers; start two agents for stage 2 or markedly elevated BP; individualize by comorbidity (ACEi/ARB for diabetes/CKD/HF, etc.)","Target generally <130/80 in most patients (SPRINT supports intensive control in high-risk patients); avoid combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB"],"pearls":["First-line agents: thiazide-type diuretic, ACE inhibitor/ARB, and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; do NOT combine an ACEi with an ARB","Target <130/80 for most patients; SPRINT supports intensive control in high cardiovascular risk (balance against orthostasis/falls in the frail)","Confirm the diagnosis with out-of-office (ambulatory/home) readings to exclude white-coat and detect masked hypertension","Choose agents by comorbidity (ACEi/ARB for diabetes/CKD/HFrEF); lifestyle (DASH, sodium reduction, weight loss) is foundational"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539859/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Essential Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430818/"}]},{"id":"cards-resistant-secondary-hypertension","name":"Resistant & Secondary Hypertension","sec":"cards","present":["RESISTANT hypertension is BP above goal despite three optimally-dosed antihypertensives (including a diuretic), or controlled BP requiring four or more agents; confirm adherence and exclude white-coat effect first","SECONDARY hypertension is elevated BP from an identifiable cause; the most common is PRIMARY ALDOSTERONISM (underdiagnosed), followed by renal parenchymal/renovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnea, and endocrine causes","Clues prompting secondary workup: young onset, abrupt/severe/resistant hypertension, hypokalemia, or specific syndrome features"],"dx":["Confirm true resistance (adherence, proper technique, out-of-office BP, and remove BP-raising substances - NSAIDs, decongestants, stimulants, licorice, excess alcohol)","Screen for PRIMARY ALDOSTERONISM with the aldosterone-to-renin ratio (especially with hypokalemia/resistant HTN); evaluate renovascular disease, pheochromocytoma (metanephrines), Cushing, thyroid disease, coarctation, and obstructive sleep apnea as indicated","Targeted imaging/confirmatory testing per the suspected cause"],"tx":["RESISTANT HTN: optimize the regimen and ADD a MINERALOCORTICOID RECEPTOR ANTAGONIST (spironolactone) - the most effective add-on (PATHWAY-2); ensure a diuretic appropriate to renal function (chlorthalidone; loop diuretic in advanced CKD)","Treat the SPECIFIC secondary cause: adrenalectomy or MR antagonist for primary aldosteronism, revascularization for selected renovascular disease, CPAP for OSA, surgery for pheochromocytoma/coarctation/Cushing","Reinforce adherence and lifestyle; remove interfering substances"],"pearls":["Resistant hypertension: exclude pseudo-resistance (nonadherence, white-coat, poor technique, BP-raising drugs like NSAIDs/decongestants) before escalating","Spironolactone (mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist) is the most effective add-on for resistant hypertension (PATHWAY-2)","Primary aldosteronism is the most common secondary cause - screen with the aldosterone-to-renin ratio, especially with hypokalemia/resistant HTN","Young onset, severe/abrupt/resistant HTN, or hypokalemia should trigger a secondary-cause workup"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Resistant Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470145/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Secondary Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544305/"}]},{"id":"cards-hypertensive-emergencies","name":"Hypertensive Emergencies","sec":"cards","present":["A hypertensive EMERGENCY is severe blood pressure elevation (usually >180/120) WITH acute target-organ damage; a hypertensive URGENCY is severe elevation WITHOUT acute organ damage (managed with oral agents, not rapid IV lowering)","Target-organ damage includes hypertensive encephalopathy, intracranial hemorrhage/acute stroke, acute heart failure/pulmonary edema, ACS, AORTIC DISSECTION, acute kidney injury, and (in pregnancy) preeclampsia/eclampsia","The distinction (organ damage or not) determines the pace and route of treatment"],"dx":["Assess for acute end-organ damage: neuro exam/imaging (encephalopathy, stroke, hemorrhage), ECG/troponin (ACS), chest imaging (pulmonary edema, dissection - CT angiography), renal function, fundoscopy, and pregnancy status","Distinguish emergency (organ damage - needs IV therapy) from urgency (no acute damage - oral therapy, avoid overly rapid lowering)","Identify the specific syndrome, which dictates the BP target and drug"],"tx":["EMERGENCY: IV titratable agents in a monitored setting; the GENERAL target is to lower BP by ~10-20% in the first hour, then gradually - EXCEPT specific scenarios","SCENARIO-SPECIFIC: AORTIC DISSECTION needs rapid, aggressive control (heart rate first with a beta-blocker like esmolol/labetalol, then vasodilator, low SBP target); acute ISCHEMIC STROKE has permissive/limited lowering (avoid over-lowering); avoid rapid drops that cause hypoperfusion","URGENCY: resume/intensify ORAL therapy and arrange close follow-up - do NOT lower BP precipitously"],"pearls":["Hypertensive emergency = severe BP + acute target-organ damage (needs IV therapy); urgency = severe BP without organ damage (oral therapy, avoid rapid lowering)","General emergency target: reduce BP ~10-20% in the first hour, then gradually - overly rapid lowering causes organ hypoperfusion","Aortic dissection is the exception: rapid, aggressive control - heart rate first (esmolol/labetalol) THEN vasodilator, with a low SBP target","In acute ischemic stroke, BP lowering is limited/permissive - do not aggressively lower (risk of worsening penumbral perfusion)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertensive Emergency","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470371/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Malignant Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470400/"}]},{"id":"cards-pulmonary-hypertension","name":"Pulmonary Hypertension","sec":"cards","present":["Pulmonary hypertension (PH) is elevated pulmonary artery pressure (mean PAP >20 mmHg by right-heart catheterization); it is grouped by mechanism into 5 WHO GROUPS","Groups: 1 = pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH - idiopathic, heritable, drug/toxin, connective tissue disease, congenital); 2 = due to LEFT HEART DISEASE (the most common overall); 3 = due to LUNG DISEASE/hypoxia; 4 = chronic thromboembolic (CTEPH); 5 = multifactorial/unclear","Presents with progressive exertional dyspnea, fatigue, and later right heart failure signs"],"dx":["Echocardiography screens (estimated PA pressure, RV size/function); RIGHT-HEART CATHETERIZATION is required to confirm PH and to distinguish PRE-capillary (PAH/group 1) from POST-capillary (group 2, elevated wedge pressure) hemodynamics","Systematic workup to assign the group: left-heart disease (echo), lung disease (PFTs/CT), and CHRONIC THROMBOEMBOLIC disease (V/Q scan - the key test to not miss CTEPH, which is potentially curable)","Group 1 PAH: vasoreactivity testing and connective tissue/HIV/other-cause evaluation"],"tx":["TREAT BY GROUP - do NOT reflexively give PAH-specific drugs to group 2/3 PH (they can be harmful): Group 2 - treat the left-heart disease; Group 3 - treat the lung disease/hypoxia (oxygen); Group 4 (CTEPH) - pulmonary endarterectomy (potentially curative), balloon pulmonary angioplasty, or riociguat","GROUP 1 PAH: pulmonary vasodilator therapy targeting three pathways (endothelin receptor antagonists, PDE5 inhibitors/riociguat, and prostacyclin analogues), guided by risk stratification; the rare vasoreactive responders may use calcium-channel blockers","Supportive care (diuretics for RV failure, oxygen); refer PAH/CTEPH to expert centers"],"pearls":["Right-heart catheterization confirms PH and separates pre-capillary (PAH) from post-capillary (left-heart, group 2) disease - group 2 is the most common overall","Do NOT give PAH-specific vasodilators to group 2 (left heart) or group 3 (lung disease) PH - treat the underlying cause instead (can be harmful otherwise)","Always get a V/Q scan to screen for chronic thromboembolic PH (group 4/CTEPH) - it is potentially curable with pulmonary endarterectomy","Group 1 PAH uses combination pathway-targeted vasodilators at expert centers; only rare vasoreactive responders benefit from calcium-channel blockers"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482463/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551514/"}]},{"id":"cards-adult-congenital-heart-disease","name":"Adult Congenital Heart Disease (Medical)","sec":"cards","present":["Adult congenital heart disease (ACHD) includes patients with congenital lesions surviving to (or first presenting in) adulthood; common lesions are ATRIAL SEPTAL DEFECT (ASD), VENTRICULAR SEPTAL DEFECT (VSD), PATENT DUCTUS ARTERIOSUS, COARCTATION, and repaired complex disease (e.g., tetralogy of Fallot)","Left-to-right shunts (ASD/VSD/PDA), if large and untreated, cause pulmonary vascular disease progressing to EISENMENGER SYNDROME (shunt reversal, cyanosis) - a contraindication to shunt closure","(Surgical/structural repair is under Thoracic Surgery - this is the medical recognition, physiology, and long-term management view)"],"dx":["Echocardiography defines the lesion, shunt direction/magnitude (Qp:Qs), chamber enlargement, and pulmonary pressures; CT/CMR and catheterization refine anatomy/hemodynamics","ASD clues: fixed split S2, RV volume overload; coarctation: upper-extremity hypertension with brachial-femoral delay and rib notching; assess for Eisenmenger physiology (irreversible pulmonary hypertension)","Screen repaired-lesion patients for late complications (arrhythmia, residual shunt, valve/conduit dysfunction, pulmonary regurgitation after tetralogy repair)"],"tx":["Close hemodynamically significant left-to-right shunts BEFORE fixed pulmonary hypertension develops (device or surgical - see Thoracic); do NOT close once Eisenmenger physiology is established","Lifelong specialized ACHD follow-up for arrhythmia, heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, endocarditis risk, and (coarctation) re-coarctation/aneurysm and persistent hypertension","Eisenmenger/cyanotic patients: careful management (avoid dehydration/vasodilation, endocarditis precautions, pregnancy counseling); pregnancy counseling and contraception for higher-risk lesions"],"pearls":["Close significant left-to-right shunts (ASD/VSD/PDA) BEFORE fixed pulmonary hypertension develops - Eisenmenger physiology (shunt reversal, cyanosis) contraindicates closure","ASD: fixed split S2 and RV volume overload; coarctation: upper-extremity hypertension, brachial-femoral delay, rib notching","Repaired tetralogy of Fallot patients need surveillance for pulmonary regurgitation and arrhythmia (pulmonary valve replacement when indicated)","ACHD needs lifelong specialized follow-up; provide pregnancy/contraception counseling for higher-risk lesions (cross-reference Thoracic Surgery for repair)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Septal Defect","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535440/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Eisenmenger Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507800/"}]},{"id":"cards-preventive-cardiology","name":"Preventive Cardiology & ASCVD Risk","sec":"cards","present":["Preventive cardiology reduces atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) events by managing modifiable risk factors; PRIMARY prevention targets those without established disease, SECONDARY prevention those with known ASCVD","Major modifiable risk factors: hypertension, dyslipidemia (LDL), smoking, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and unhealthy diet; risk enhancers include family history, chronic inflammatory disease, and elevated lipoprotein(a)","Risk-based, shared decision-making guides intensity of therapy"],"dx":["Estimate cardiovascular risk with the PREVENT equations (which replaced the pooled cohort equations and estimate 10- and 30-year total CV risk including heart failure) for primary prevention; use RISK ENHANCERS and the CORONARY ARTERY CALCIUM score to refine borderline/intermediate-risk decisions (a zero calcium score can de-escalate; high scores escalate)","Screen and quantify individual risk factors (lipids, BP, glucose/HbA1c, smoking, weight/BMI); consider lipoprotein(a) once in a lifetime","Assess overall risk to individualize statin/aspirin and other preventive therapy"],"tx":["Foundation: lifestyle (heart-healthy diet, regular exercise, weight management, smoking cessation) for everyone; treat hypertension and dyslipidemia to risk-based targets","STATINS by risk (secondary prevention/high risk = high-intensity; primary prevention per risk/LDL/diabetes); SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists reduce cardiovascular events in diabetes/high-risk patients","ASPIRIN is largely reserved for SECONDARY prevention - routine aspirin for PRIMARY prevention is NO LONGER recommended for most (bleeding risk outweighs benefit), individualized only in selected higher-risk, low-bleeding patients"],"pearls":["Aspirin for PRIMARY prevention is no longer routinely recommended (bleeding risk) - it is mainly for secondary prevention","The coronary artery calcium score refines borderline/intermediate ASCVD risk - a zero score can justify de-escalating statin therapy","Statin intensity is risk-based (high-intensity for established ASCVD/high risk); SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists cut CV events in diabetes/high-risk patients","Lifestyle (diet, exercise, weight, smoking cessation) is foundational; check lipoprotein(a) once and use risk enhancers to individualize"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiovascular Disease Prevention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537177/"},{"t":"StatPearls: ASCVD Risk Assessment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556101/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardio-oncology","name":"Cardio-Oncology","sec":"cards","present":["Cardio-oncology manages the cardiovascular effects of cancer therapies and the overlap of cancer and heart disease; the goal is to deliver effective cancer treatment while minimizing/monitoring cardiotoxicity","ANTHRACYCLINES (doxorubicin) cause DOSE-DEPENDENT cardiomyopathy/HF (partly irreversible); HER2-targeted therapy (TRASTUZUMAB) causes usually reversible LV dysfunction (often additive with anthracyclines)","Other effects: checkpoint-inhibitor MYOCARDITIS (rare but often fulminant), VEGF/TKI-related hypertension, QT prolongation, vascular/thrombotic effects, and radiation-induced heart disease (late CAD, valve, pericardial, conduction disease)"],"dx":["Baseline cardiovascular risk assessment and LV function (echo - global longitudinal strain detects early dysfunction before EF falls); surveillance during and after cardiotoxic therapy per regimen","Monitor for cancer-therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (a defined drop in EF/strain); troponin/natriuretic peptides aid detection; recognize checkpoint-inhibitor myocarditis early (troponin rise, arrhythmia, heart failure)","Long-term surveillance for radiation heart disease in chest-irradiated survivors (late CAD/valve/pericardial disease)"],"tx":["Prevent/limit anthracycline cardiotoxicity (cumulative dose limits, consider cardioprotection); treat cancer-therapy cardiomyopathy with standard HF GDMT and cardiology-oncology co-management (often continue/resume cancer therapy when possible)","CHECKPOINT-INHIBITOR MYOCARDITIS: stop the drug and give high-dose corticosteroids urgently (high mortality)","Manage VEGF/TKI hypertension aggressively; anticoagulate cancer-associated VTE; surveil and treat radiation heart disease"],"pearls":["Anthracycline (doxorubicin) cardiotoxicity is dose-dependent and can be irreversible; trastuzumab (HER2) dysfunction is usually reversible and additive with anthracyclines","Global longitudinal strain detects early cardiotoxicity before EF declines - use it for surveillance","Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis is rare but often fulminant - stop the drug and give high-dose steroids urgently","Chest radiation causes late CAD, valve, pericardial, and conduction disease - surveil survivors long-term"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chemotherapy-Induced Cardiotoxicity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564329/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardio-oncology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574555/"}]},{"id":"cards-pregnancy-cardiovascular","name":"Pregnancy & Cardiovascular Disease","sec":"cards","present":["Pregnancy causes major cardiovascular changes (increased blood volume, cardiac output, and heart rate; decreased systemic vascular resistance) that can unmask or worsen heart disease and are a leading cause of maternal morbidity/mortality","Key entities: PERIPARTUM CARDIOMYOPATHY (new HFrEF late in pregnancy or the months after delivery), pre-existing valvular/congenital disease decompensating, hypertensive disorders (preeclampsia/eclampsia), and spontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD)","High-risk lesions (severe AS/MS, pulmonary hypertension/Eisenmenger, dilated aorta in Marfan, mechanical valves) require specialized planning"],"dx":["Risk-stratify pre-conception/early pregnancy (e.g., modified WHO classification) for known heart disease; echocardiography for new symptoms or murmurs","Recognize peripartum cardiomyopathy (new HF near term/postpartum after excluding other causes) and preeclampsia (hypertension + end-organ involvement); consider SCAD in peripartum ACS","Distinguish normal pregnancy symptoms from pathology; monitor high-risk lesions closely"],"tx":["Use pregnancy-SAFE therapies and AVOID teratogens: ACE inhibitors/ARBs/ARNI, mineralocorticoid antagonists, and the cardiac myosin inhibitor mavacamten are CONTRAINDICATED; warfarin is teratogenic (mechanical-valve anticoagulation in pregnancy is complex - specialized management)","Peripartum cardiomyopathy: HF therapy compatible with pregnancy/lactation, anticoagulation for very low EF (thrombus risk), and counseling on recurrence risk in future pregnancies","Multidisciplinary (cardio-obstetric) care and delivery planning; treat preeclampsia (safe antihypertensives, magnesium for seizure prophylaxis, delivery as definitive treatment)"],"pearls":["ACE inhibitors/ARBs/ARNI, mineralocorticoid antagonists, mavacamten, and warfarin are contraindicated/teratogenic in pregnancy - use pregnancy-safe alternatives","Peripartum cardiomyopathy is new HFrEF late in pregnancy or postpartum (diagnosis of exclusion); anticoagulate for very low EF and counsel on recurrence risk","High-risk lesions (severe AS/MS, pulmonary hypertension/Eisenmenger, Marfan with dilated aorta) need pre-conception counseling and cardio-obstetric care","Consider spontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD) in peripartum ACS; preeclampsia treatment is safe antihypertensives + magnesium, with delivery definitive"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Peripartum Cardiomyopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534770/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Disease in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537261/"}]},{"id":"cards-cardiovascular-systemic-disease","name":"Cardiovascular Effects of Systemic Disease","sec":"cards","present":["Many systemic conditions have important cardiovascular manifestations that a cardiologist must recognize; examples span endocrine, inflammatory, infiltrative, and connective tissue disease","Endocrine: THYROID disease (hyperthyroidism -> AF/high-output states; hypothyroidism -> bradycardia/effusion/dyslipidemia), acromegaly (cardiomyopathy), pheochromocytoma (catecholamine effects), and diabetes (accelerated ASCVD, cardiomyopathy)","Inflammatory/autoimmune (accelerated atherosclerosis and specific cardiac involvement in lupus, rheumatoid arthritis), infiltrative (amyloid, sarcoid, hemochromatosis), and connective tissue disease (aortopathy in Marfan/Loeys-Dietz)"],"dx":["Consider a systemic cause for unexplained cardiac findings: new AF (check thyroid), restrictive/thick-walled ventricle (amyloid/infiltration), heart block in a young patient (sarcoid/Lyme), and aortopathy (connective tissue disease)","Targeted testing: thyroid function, iron studies (hemochromatosis), amyloid workup (free light chains, PYP scan), sarcoid imaging (FDG-PET/CMR), and autoimmune serologies as indicated","Recognize accelerated ASCVD risk in chronic inflammatory disease (lupus, rheumatoid arthritis) and diabetes"],"tx":["Treat the underlying systemic disease AND its cardiac manifestation: restore euthyroidism (rate control/anticoagulation for thyrotoxic AF), iron removal for hemochromatosis, immunosuppression for cardiac sarcoid, and disease-specific amyloid therapy","Aggressively modify cardiovascular risk in chronic inflammatory disease and diabetes (they are ASCVD risk enhancers)","Coordinate multidisciplinary care; screen for cardiac involvement in high-risk systemic diseases"],"pearls":["Unexplained cardiac findings should prompt a systemic-disease search: new AF (thyroid), thick/restrictive ventricle (amyloid/infiltration), heart block in the young (sarcoid/Lyme), aortopathy (Marfan/Loeys-Dietz)","Hyperthyroidism causes AF/high-output states; hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, pericardial effusion, and dyslipidemia","Chronic inflammatory diseases (lupus, rheumatoid arthritis) and diabetes accelerate atherosclerosis - treat them as ASCVD risk enhancers","Treat the systemic cause and the cardiac manifestation together (euthyroidism, iron removal, immunosuppression for sarcoid, amyloid-directed therapy)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroid and the Heart","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537284/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Sarcoidosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574509/"}]},{"id":"cards-sports-cardiology","name":"Sports Cardiology & Athlete's Heart","sec":"cards","present":["Sports cardiology addresses cardiovascular care of athletes, including preparticipation screening, distinguishing ATHLETE'S HEART (physiologic adaptation) from pathology, and safe-return-to-play decisions","ATHLETE'S HEART is benign physiologic remodeling from chronic training (increased chamber size/wall thickness, resting bradycardia, and common benign ECG changes) - it must be distinguished from cardiomyopathies (HCM, ARVC) and channelopathies that cause sudden death","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes is most often due to HCM, ARVC, coronary anomalies, or channelopathies (and, in some regions, commotio cordis)"],"dx":["Preparticipation evaluation: history (exertional symptoms, syncope, family history of SCD/inherited disease) and exam +/- ECG (using athlete-specific ECG criteria to reduce false positives)","GRAY-ZONE distinction: features favoring PATHOLOGY over athlete's heart include marked LV wall thickness with a small cavity, systolic dysfunction, pathologic ECG patterns, lack of regression with detraining, and symptoms/family history - use echo/CMR, exercise testing, and detraining","Genetic testing/family screening when an inherited condition is suspected"],"tx":["Manage identified pathology per disease (HCM, ARVC, long QT, coronary anomaly) with individualized activity counseling and, where indicated, ICDs; increasingly this uses SHARED DECISION-MAKING rather than blanket disqualification","Ensure emergency preparedness: access to AEDs and CPR/emergency action plans at sporting venues (rapid defibrillation is life-saving, including for commotio cordis)","Treat reversible contributors and counsel on safe training"],"pearls":["Distinguish benign athlete's heart (physiologic remodeling, benign ECG changes, resting bradycardia) from HCM/ARVC/channelopathies - use athlete-specific ECG criteria, imaging, and detraining","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes is most often HCM, ARVC, coronary anomalies, or channelopathies (commotio cordis in some cases)","Features favoring pathology: marked wall thickness with small cavity, systolic dysfunction, pathologic ECG, symptoms, family history, and no regression with detraining","AED access and emergency action plans at venues save lives - rapid defibrillation is critical (including commotio cordis); return-to-play uses shared decision-making"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Athletic Heart Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541069/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sudden Cardiac Death in Athletes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544366/"}]},{"id":"derm-atopic-dermatitis","name":"Atopic Dermatitis (Eczema)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: ill-defined erythematous scaly patches and plaques with excoriation, lichenification (thickened skin with accentuated markings) in chronic disease, oozing/crusting when acute; xerosis is universal.","Distribution is age-dependent: infants - cheeks, scalp, extensor surfaces; children/adults - FLEXURAL (antecubital/popliteal fossae, neck, wrists, ankles); hand and eyelid dermatitis in adults.","The 'itch that rashes': pruritus is the defining symptom and drives the scratch-lichenify cycle; sleep disruption and atopic history (asthma, allergic rhinitis) support it."],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (Hanifin-Rajka or UK Working Party criteria): pruritus plus age-typical morphology/distribution, chronic-relapsing course, personal/family atopy.","Recognize complications on exam: eczema herpeticum (monomorphic punched-out erosions, systemically unwell - a dermatologic emergency), impetiginized eczema (honey crust, S. aureus), and erythroderma.","Biopsy rarely needed; consider patch testing when distribution suggests superimposed allergic contact dermatitis (eyelids, hands, recalcitrant cases), and consider cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in adult-onset 'eczema' that won't behave."],"tx":["Foundation: liberal emollients (ointments > creams), gentle non-soap cleansers, trigger avoidance, lukewarm short showers - the barrier is the disease.","Topical anti-inflammatories by site/severity: low-potency (hydrocortisone 2.5%) for face/folds, mid-to-high potency (triamcinolone, clobetasol) for body/lichenified plaques, in bursts; steroid-sparing calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, pimecrolimus) and crisaborole/topical JAK (ruxolitinib) for face/eyelids/maintenance (proactive twice-weekly to prior flare sites).","Escalation for moderate-severe: phototherapy (nbUVB), then systemic - dupilumab (IL-4/13, first-line biologic, excellent safety) or oral JAK inhibitors (upadacitinib/abrocitinib - faster, boxed-warning class); short systemic steroids only as a rescue bridge (rebound on withdrawal).","Treat the complications: eczema herpeticum - oral/IV acyclovir promptly; bacterial superinfection - anti-staph antibiotics + bleach baths for recurrent cases."],"pearls":["If 'eczema' isn't itchy, doubt the diagnosis; if it's unilateral or geometric, think contact.","Steroid phobia undertreats more atopic dermatitis than steroid overuse ever harms - quantify with fingertip units and set an end date.","Monomorphic punched-out erosions on an eczema patient is eczema herpeticum until proven otherwise - do not reach for a stronger steroid."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Atopic Dermatitis Guidelines","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Eczema","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-contact-dermatitis","name":"Contact Dermatitis (Allergic & Irritant)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: eczematous - erythema, vesicles, weeping when acute; scaling, fissuring, lichenification when chronic; SHARP borders and geometric/linear shapes betray an external cause (the classic linear vesicles of poison ivy, the rectangle under a bandage).","Distribution is the diagnosis: eyelids/face (airborne, transferred by hands, cosmetics), earlobes/wrist/umbilicus (nickel), dorsal feet (shoe rubber/chromate), hands (occupational).","Irritant (ICD, ~80%) burns/stings and is dose-dependent (soaps, solvents, saliva, frequent handwashing); allergic (ACD) itches and is a delayed type IV reaction appearing 24-72 h after exposure in the sensitized."],"dx":["History maps exposures to sites; the pattern (shape, location) often reveals the culprit before any test.","Patch testing is the gold standard for ACD (read at 48 h and 72-96 h - delayed reactions matter); the T.R.U.E. test screens common allergens, expanded panels for occupational/recalcitrant cases.","Distinguish from atopic dermatitis (flexural, atopic history) and from irritant (burning vs itching, everyone gets it with enough exposure vs only the sensitized)."],"tx":["Identify and eliminate the agent - the only curative step; provide allergen-specific avoidance lists (nickel, fragrance mix, methylisothiazolinone, formaldehyde releasers, neomycin) after positive patch tests.","Topical corticosteroids (mid-to-high potency to body, low to face) for localized disease; barrier repair and emollients for irritant hand dermatitis; vinyl gloves/cotton liners for occupational protection.","Severe/widespread acute ACD (e.g., extensive rhus dermatitis): a 2-3 WEEK oral steroid taper (shorter courses rebound); antihistamines help sleep more than itch."],"pearls":["Shape and site solve contact dermatitis - linear = plant, geometric = applied object, eyelids = something the hands carried.","'Hypoallergenic' and 'natural' products are frequent sensitizers - botanicals and essential oils patch-test positive constantly.","A steroid CREAM that isn't working may contain the very preservative (or neomycin) the patient reacts to - check the vehicle."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Contact Dermatitis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"American Contact Dermatitis Society","u":"https://www.contactderm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Allergic Contact Dermatitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-psoriasis","name":"Psoriasis (Plaque & Variants)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: well-demarcated erythematous plaques with SILVERY-WHITE micaceous scale; Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding when scale removed); symmetric on EXTENSOR surfaces (elbows, knees), scalp, lumbosacral, gluteal cleft, umbilicus.","Variants: guttate (raindrop papules post-streptococcal, younger patients), inverse (shiny non-scaly plaques in folds), pustular (sterile pustules - generalized von Zumbusch is a systemic emergency), erythrodermic (>90% BSA, thermoregulatory/metabolic risk), nail (pitting, oil drops, onycholysis), palmoplantar.","Koebner phenomenon: plaques at sites of trauma; ask about joint pain (psoriatic arthritis in up to ~30%, can precede or follow skin - dactylitis, enthesitis, DIP involvement)."],"dx":["Clinical; biopsy shows regular acanthosis, parakeratosis, Munro microabscesses if needed for atypical presentations.","Assess severity and impact: BSA and PASI, but also DLQI (a 'small' area on palms/genitals can be disabling); screen EVERY patient for psoriatic arthritis (PEST questionnaire) and for comorbidities - metabolic syndrome, cardiovascular risk, depression, IBD.","Guttate flares: check for streptococcal trigger; new/worsening psoriasis: review drug triggers (beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials, interferon, steroid withdrawal, TNF paradoxical)."],"tx":["Localized: topical corticosteroids + vitamin D analog (calcipotriene) combination is first-line; add tar, tazarotene, calcineurin inhibitors for face/folds; scalp formulations (solutions/foams).","Moderate-severe or PsA: phototherapy (nbUVB) for skin-only; systemics - methotrexate, apremilast, and the biologics that transformed the disease: TNF inhibitors, IL-17 (secukinumab/ixekizumab), IL-23 (guselkumab/risankizumab - high efficacy, favorable safety); choose by PsA presence, comorbidities (IBD favors IL-23/TNF, not IL-17), and infection screening (TB/hepatitis before biologics).","Emergencies: generalized pustular and erythrodermic psoriasis often need admission, fluid/thermoregulation support, and rapid systemic therapy (infliximab/cyclosporine act fastest)."],"pearls":["Always examine the nails, scalp, and gluteal cleft - they clinch psoriasis when the plaques are ambiguous, and reveal the disease patients hid.","Ask about joints at every visit: psoriatic arthritis is erosive and the window to prevent joint damage is early.","Abruptly stopping systemic steroids can precipitate pustular flare - psoriasis is a reason NOT to prescribe oral prednisone bursts."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD-NPF Psoriasis Guidelines","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"National Psoriasis Foundation","u":"https://www.psoriasis.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Psoriasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-seborrheic-dermatitis","name":"Seborrheic Dermatitis","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: erythematous patches with GREASY yellow scale in sebaceous-rich zones - scalp (dandruff to thick scale), eyebrows, nasolabial folds, glabella, ears/retroauricular, central chest, beard.","Infantile form: 'cradle cap' - thick greasy scalp scale, usually self-limited.","Chronic waxing/waning; flares with stress, cold/dry seasons; distribution is the tell - it hugs the oily central face and scalp."],"dx":["Clinical; the greasy scale + sebaceous distribution distinguishes it from psoriasis (drier silvery scale, extensor/sharp), rosacea (papulopustules, no scale), and tinea (KOH-positive, annular).","Severe, explosive, or treatment-resistant seborrheic dermatitis is a clue to underlying HIV (screen) or Parkinson's/neuroleptic-related states; Malassezia yeast is the pathogenic driver."],"tx":["Scalp: antifungal shampoos (ketoconazole 2%, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox) left on several minutes, rotated; add tar or salicylic acid for thick scale; topical steroid solutions/foams for flares.","Face/body: ketoconazole 2% cream, low-potency hydrocortisone for short flare control (avoid chronic facial steroids - atrophy/perioral dermatitis), topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus/pimecrolimus) for steroid-sparing maintenance.","It's controlled, not cured: set expectations for intermittent lifelong maintenance therapy."],"pearls":["Greasy yellow scale in the nasolabial folds and scalp = seborrheic dermatitis; silvery scale on elbows/knees = psoriasis - the scale quality and site separate them.","Sudden severe seborrheic dermatitis warrants an HIV test.","Chronic facial steroids trade seborrheic dermatitis for perioral dermatitis and atrophy - move to antifungals and calcineurin inhibitors for maintenance."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Seborrheic Dermatitis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Seborrheic Dermatitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Seborrheic Dermatitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-acne","name":"Acne Vulgaris","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology defines the type and the treatment: comedones (open blackheads/closed whiteheads - the non-inflammatory core lesion), inflammatory papules and pustules, and nodules/cysts in severe disease; distribution over sebaceous areas - face, chest, back.","Sequelae drive urgency: post-inflammatory erythema/hyperpigmentation and scarring (atrophic ice-pick/boxcar, hypertrophic/keloidal) - visible scarring argues for early aggressive therapy.","Pattern clues: predominantly comedonal along the jaw/perioral in adult women suggests hormonal contribution; monomorphic papulopustules on trunk/shoulders after steroids/supplements suggests drug-induced or Malassezia (fungal) folliculitis mimicking acne."],"dx":["Clinical, graded by lesion type and severity (mild comedonal -> moderate papulopustular -> severe nodulocystic).","Screen for hyperandrogenism (PCOS) when acne accompanies hirsutism, irregular menses, or is treatment-resistant in adult women; consider gram-negative folliculitis in patients worsening on long-term antibiotics.","Recognize the mimics: rosacea (no comedones, flushing/telangiectasia), perioral dermatitis, folliculitis."],"tx":["Match therapy to lesion type: comedonal - topical retinoid (adapalene/tretinoin, the backbone of nearly every regimen); inflammatory - add benzoyl peroxide (always pair with topical antibiotics to prevent resistance) +/- topical clindamycin, or newer topical clascoterone/minocycline foam.","Moderate-severe: oral antibiotics (doxycycline/minocycline) for a LIMITED course (~3-4 months, always with topical retinoid + BPO, never as monotherapy), hormonal therapy (combined OCPs, spironolactone - highly effective in adult women).","Severe nodulocystic or scarring/refractory: isotretinoin - the only durable-remission agent; requires iPLEDGE, strict contraception (teratogen), and monitoring (lipids, LFTs); counsel on transient initial flare and dryness.","Always address scarring/pigment: sun protection, treat active disease first, then resurfacing/procedures for scars."],"pearls":["Comedones are the pathognomonic acne lesion - no comedones, rethink the diagnosis (rosacea, folliculitis, perioral dermatitis).","Never treat inflammatory acne with a topical or oral antibiotic alone - pair with benzoyl peroxide and a retinoid to prevent resistance and relapse.","Scarring or deep nodules on the first visit is an indication to move toward isotretinoin early, not to cycle through antibiotics for a year."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Acne Management Guidelines","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acne Vulgaris","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"FDA: Isotretinoin / iPLEDGE","u":"https://www.fda.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-rosacea","name":"Rosacea","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology by subtype: erythematotelangiectatic (central facial FLUSHING, persistent erythema, telangiectasias, stinging), papulopustular (acne-like papules/pustules WITHOUT comedones on a red base), phymatous (sebaceous hyperplasia and thickening - rhinophyma), ocular (gritty, dry, lid margin telangiectasia/blepharitis).","Distribution: convexities of the CENTRAL face - cheeks, nose, chin, forehead; spares periocular skin.","Triggers reported: heat, sun, alcohol, spicy food, stress, hot drinks - flushing that outlasts the trigger."],"dx":["Clinical, by phenotype (persistent centrofacial erythema or phymatous change are diagnostic features).","The absence of comedones separates it from acne; the trigger-driven flushing and telangiectasia separate it from seborrheic dermatitis and lupus (check for photosensitive malar rash sparing nasolabial folds, systemic features if lupus suspected).","Always ask about eye symptoms - ocular rosacea is underdiagnosed and can threaten the cornea."],"tx":["General: gentle skin care, daily broad-spectrum sunscreen, trigger diary/avoidance - foundational and often underemphasized.","Erythema/flushing: topical brimonidine or oxymetazoline (alpha-agonists, transient), vascular laser/IPL for fixed telangiectasia.","Papulopustular: topical metronidazole, azelaic acid, ivermectin; oral doxycycline (anti-inflammatory sub-antimicrobial dosing) for moderate-severe.","Phymatous: physical modalities (laser, electrosurgery, surgical debulking) for established rhinophyma; isotretinoin in selected refractory cases.","Ocular: lid hygiene, warm compresses, artificial tears, oral doxycycline, ophthalmology referral for keratitis."],"pearls":["Papules and pustules WITHOUT comedones on a flushed central face is rosacea, not acne - and the distinction changes the treatment.","A photosensitive facial redness that SPARES the nasolabial folds is more likely lupus than rosacea - rosacea favors the convexities.","Ask every rosacea patient about their eyes; ocular rosacea can precede skin disease and damage the cornea."],"refs":[{"t":"National Rosacea Society / AAD Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rosacea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Rosacea","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-hidradenitis-suppurativa","name":"Hidradenitis Suppurativa","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: painful inflammatory nodules, abscesses, draining sinus tracts, and rope-like scarring; DOUBLE-ended (bridged) comedones are a hallmark of chronic disease.","Distribution: INTERTRIGINOUS, apocrine-bearing skin - axillae, inguinal/genital, perianal, inframammary, buttocks; bilateral and recurrent.","Chronic, relapsing, and profoundly impactful (pain, malodor, drainage, depression); often mislabeled as recurrent 'boils' for years before diagnosis."],"dx":["Clinical triad: typical lesions + typical sites + recurrence/chronicity (>=2 flares in 6 months); Hurley staging (I nodules/abscesses, II recurrent with tracts, III diffuse interconnected tracts) guides therapy.","Screen comorbidities: obesity, smoking, metabolic syndrome, PCOS, IBD, spondyloarthropathy, depression; culture only to guide antibiotics in acute superinfection (it's inflammatory, not primarily infectious)."],"tx":["Foundational: smoking cessation, weight management, loose clothing, antiseptic/chlorhexidine washes, pain control, wound care; treat flares of pain promptly.","Medical by stage: topical/oral clindamycin, oral tetracyclines, clindamycin-rifampin combination; hormonal (spironolactone/OCPs) in women; metformin adjunct; intralesional steroid for acute flares.","Moderate-severe: adalimumab (FDA-approved TNF inhibitor) and secukinumab (IL-17) are the biologics that changed prognosis; refractory disease -> other biologics in trials.","Surgical: incision and drainage for acute abscess relief (temporary), but definitive control of sinus tracts requires deroofing or wide local excision of affected sites - medical + surgical combined."],"pearls":["Recurrent 'boils' in the axillae and groin with scarring is hidradenitis suppurativa, not bad luck with staph - name it and stage it.","Smoking and obesity are the two most modifiable drivers - address them at every visit without shaming.","I&D relieves today's abscess but doesn't treat the disease; sinus tracts need deroofing/excision, and moderate disease needs a biologic."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD/HS Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hidradenitis Suppurativa","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hidradenitis Suppurativa","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-urticaria-angioedema","name":"Urticaria & Angioedema","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: WHEALS - transient edematous pink papules/plaques with pale centers and surrounding flare; the individual lesion is FLEETING (each resolves <24 h) and intensely pruritic; leaves no bruise or scale.","Angioedema: deeper dermal/subcutaneous swelling (lips, eyelids, tongue, hands, genitals), burning/tension more than itch, resolves over 24-72 h.","Acute (<6 weeks) vs chronic (>=6 weeks, most spontaneous/idiopathic); inducible forms (dermographism, cold, cholinergic, pressure, solar) reproduce with the trigger."],"dx":["Diagnosis is clinical and the KEY question is duration of the individual lesion: a wheal lasting >24 h, painful, or leaving bruising/pigment suggests urticarial VASCULITIS (biopsy).","Acute urticaria: usually infection, food, drug, or idiopathic - extensive workup NOT indicated; chronic spontaneous urticaria: limited targeted labs only (CBC, ESR/CRP); avoid shotgun allergy panels.","Angioedema WITHOUT wheals + no response to antihistamines: think bradykinin-mediated - ACE-inhibitor-induced or hereditary/acquired C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency (check C4, C1-INH); recurrent, family history, laryngeal involvement raise the stakes."],"tx":["Urticaria: second-generation H1 antihistamines are first-line, up-titrated to 4x standard dose before adding on; add H2 blocker or montelukast; refractory chronic spontaneous urticaria -> omalizumab (anti-IgE), then cyclosporine; short steroid bursts for severe acute flares only.","Anaphylaxis (urticaria/angioedema + airway/GI/hemodynamic involvement): intramuscular EPINEPHRINE immediately - antihistamines/steroids are adjuncts, not the treatment.","Bradykinin angioedema does NOT respond to epinephrine/antihistamines/steroids: stop the ACE inhibitor; hereditary angioedema attacks need specific therapy (C1-INH concentrate, icatibant, ecallantide) and airway vigilance."],"pearls":["The single most useful question in hives is 'how long does ONE spot last?' - over 24 hours, painful, or bruising means urticarial vasculitis, not ordinary urticaria.","Angioedema without hives that ignores antihistamines is bradykinin-mediated - look for the ACE inhibitor and check C1 inhibitor.","Chronic urticaria almost never has an allergic cause worth chasing with panels - up-dose the antihistamine and reassure."],"refs":[{"t":"AAAAI/Joint Task Force Urticaria Guidelines","u":"https://www.aaaai.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urticaria / Angioedema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"US HAEA (Hereditary Angioedema)","u":"https://www.haea.org"}]},{"id":"derm-drug-eruptions","name":"Drug Eruptions (Morbilliform & Fixed)","sec":"derm","present":["Morbilliform (exanthematous, ~most common): symmetric blanching pink macules/papules coalescing on the trunk spreading outward, mild pruritus, starting ~4-14 days after a new drug (sooner on re-exposure); NO mucosal/systemic involvement in simple cases.","Fixed drug eruption: one or few sharply demarcated round DUSKY red-brown plaques (sometimes bullous) that RECUR AT THE SAME SITE with each exposure (lips, genitals, hands common), leaving post-inflammatory pigment.","Always scan for the RED FLAGS that separate benign from severe: facial edema, skin pain/tenderness, dusky/target lesions, blisters, mucosal erosions, fever, lymphadenopathy."],"dx":["Diagnosis is clinical + a careful drug timeline (include OTC, supplements, recently stopped drugs); common culprits - antibiotics (beta-lactams, sulfonamides), allopurinol, anticonvulsants, NSAIDs.","Actively exclude the severe cutaneous adverse reactions: SJS/TEN (mucosae, skin pain, detachment), DRESS (facial edema, eosinophilia, organ involvement, later onset 2-8 weeks), AGEP (rapid sterile pustules); check CBC with differential, LFTs, creatinine when any red flag is present.","Fixed drug eruption is confirmed by history of recurrence at the same site; biopsy/patch or oral rechallenge only in selected cases."],"tx":["Stop the offending drug (and document a clear allergy label with the reaction type - it guides future prescribing and avoids over-labeling).","Simple morbilliform eruption: supportive - topical steroids, oral antihistamines, emollients; it fades over 1-2 weeks and does NOT mandate stopping a life-saving drug if benign and monitored (context-dependent, with specialist input).","Fixed drug eruption: avoid the culprit; topical steroids for active lesions; counsel on the recurring pigmented mark.","If ANY severe-reaction red flag appears: treat as an emergency - stop the drug, admit/escalate, and manage as SJS/TEN or DRESS."],"pearls":["The job in any drug rash is triage: benign morbilliform vs the killers - skin pain, mucosae, facial edema, and blisters are the alarms.","A recurring rash at the exact same spot with each course of a drug is a fixed drug eruption - the localization is the diagnosis.","Timing separates the severe reactions: SJS/TEN early with mucosae, DRESS late (weeks) with eosinophilia and organs, AGEP fast with pustules."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Drug Eruptions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Drug Reaction Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Drug Reactions","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-sjs-ten","name":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: painful dusky erythema and atypical targetoid macules progressing to flaccid bullae and full-thickness epidermal DETACHMENT with sheet-like sloughing; positive Nikolsky sign (skin shears with lateral pressure).","Hallmark: erosive involvement of >=2 MUCOSAL surfaces (oral, ocular, genital) - severe hemorrhagic crusted lips, conjunctivitis; often a prodrome of fever and malaise 1-3 days before skin.","Spectrum by body surface area detached: SJS <10%, overlap 10-30%, TEN >30%; a dermatologic-and-critical-care EMERGENCY."],"dx":["Clinical recognition is urgent; skin biopsy (full-thickness epidermal necrosis) confirms and excludes mimics (staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome - subcorneal split, spares mucosae, in children; autoimmune bullous disease; AGEP; erythema multiforme major).","Identify the trigger: usually a drug started 4 days-4 weeks prior - allopurinol, aromatic anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, lamotrigine, phenytoin), sulfonamides, nevirapine, NSAIDs (oxicams); occasionally Mycoplasma (especially mucosal-predominant in children).","Prognosticate with SCORTEN (age, malignancy, tachycardia, BSA, BUN, glucose, bicarbonate) within 24 h to estimate mortality and guide disposition."],"tx":["STOP the culprit drug immediately - earlier withdrawal improves survival - and stop all nonessential drugs.","Transfer to a BURN unit or ICU: aggressive supportive care is the backbone - fluid/electrolyte resuscitation, temperature control, nutrition, meticulous non-adherent wound care, analgesia, infection surveillance (avoid prophylactic antibiotics; avoid sulfa/silver sulfadiazine).","Multidisciplinary mucosal care is sight- and function-saving: urgent OPHTHALMOLOGY (prevent corneal scarring/symblepharon), plus urology/gynecology, ENT/oral care.","Immunomodulation is adjunctive and debated: cyclosporine and/or etanercept have the most supportive recent data; high-dose IVIG and systemic steroids are used variably - decisions with dermatology/burn specialists; supportive care outweighs any single agent."],"pearls":["Skin PAIN out of proportion, dusky color, and mucosal erosions in a patient on a new drug is SJS/TEN until proven otherwise - do not wait for sloughing.","Every SJS/TEN patient needs ophthalmology on day one - ocular sequelae blind more survivors than the skin kills.","The two highest-impact interventions are the earliest ones: stop the drug and get the patient to a burn unit."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / TEN","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD SJS/TEN Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Stevens-Johnson Syndrome","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-dress","name":"DRESS Syndrome (Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia & Systemic Symptoms)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: widespread morbilliform eruption that becomes confluent and infiltrated, with the tell-tale FACIAL EDEMA (periorbital swelling), and often progressing toward erythroderma; may develop pustules or purpura.","Systemic disease is the point: high fever, lymphadenopathy, and internal organ involvement - LIVER most common (hepatitis), also kidney, lung, heart, thyroid.","Timing is late and distinctive: 2-8 WEEKS after starting the culprit (much later than a simple drug rash), and can persist/relapse for weeks even after stopping."],"dx":["Recognize the syndrome (RegiSCAR criteria): drug exposure + rash + fever + lymphadenopathy + EOSINOPHILIA (or atypical lymphocytosis) + organ involvement.","Workup: CBC with differential (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytes), LFTs, creatinine, urinalysis; then chase organ involvement (thyroid function at baseline and weeks later - thyroiditis is delayed).","Classic culprits: aromatic anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine), allopurinol, sulfonamides, vancomycin, minocycline, dapsone; HHV-6 reactivation is associated and marks more severe/relapsing courses."],"tx":["STOP the culprit immediately and avoid cross-reacting drugs (aromatic anticonvulsants cross-react - substitute a non-aromatic agent for seizures).","Systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay for significant organ involvement (e.g., prednisone ~0.5-1 mg/kg/day) with a SLOW taper over weeks-to-months to prevent relapse; topical steroids + supportive care for skin-limited/milder disease.","Monitor organs over TIME: repeat LFTs/renal function and screen thyroid at 6-12 weeks (autoimmune sequelae are delayed); admit for significant organ dysfunction.","Counsel: lifelong avoidance of the drug and documented allergy; screen first-degree relatives for the same HLA risk where relevant (e.g., allopurinol/HLA-B*58:01, carbamazepine/HLA-B*15:02)."],"pearls":["Facial edema + eosinophilia + a rash weeks into a new drug is DRESS - the late timing fools people expecting a rash in the first days.","Recheck the thyroid weeks later: DRESS causes delayed autoimmune thyroiditis after everything else has settled.","Aromatic anticonvulsants cross-react - never swap carbamazepine for phenytoin in a DRESS patient."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: DRESS Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Severe Cutaneous Drug Reaction Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Drug Reactions","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-erythema-multiforme","name":"Erythema Multiforme","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: the TARGET (iris) lesion - three concentric zones: dusky/blistered center, pale edematous ring, erythematous outer halo; typical targets are the diagnostic feature.","Distribution: ACRAL and symmetric - dorsal hands, extensor forearms, palms, soles, elbows/knees, spreading centripetally (opposite of most drug rashes); EM major adds mucosal erosions (usually oral).","Abrupt onset, often recurrent; distinguish from SJS/TEN by the acral (not truncal) distribution, well-formed targets, limited BSA, and cause."],"dx":["Clinical; the key is that EM is usually INFECTION-triggered (HSV most common - preceding cold sore; Mycoplasma pneumoniae especially in children), whereas SJS/TEN is drug-triggered with truncal atypical targets and prominent skin detachment.","Ask about a preceding herpes labialis (recurrent EM is nearly always HSV-driven, even without a visible sore); check for Mycoplasma symptoms in younger patients with prominent mucosal disease.","Biopsy if atypical; workup limited unless mucosal/severe (then evaluate as for the SCARs)."],"tx":["Treat the trigger/symptoms: EM minor is self-limited (resolves ~2 weeks) - symptomatic care with topical steroids, antihistamines, oral rinses for mouth lesions.","HSV-associated RECURRENT EM: chronic suppressive antiviral therapy (acyclovir/valacyclovir) prevents recurrences - the definitive intervention.","Mycoplasma-associated: treat the infection; severe mucosal disease (Mycoplasma-induced rash and mucositis) needs supportive mucosal care and sometimes admission.","EM major with significant mucosal involvement: supportive care, ophthalmology if ocular; systemic steroids are used variably."],"pearls":["Well-formed target lesions on the hands and forearms is erythema multiforme; dusky truncal atypical targets with skin pain and sloughing is SJS/TEN - different cause, different danger.","Recurrent erythema multiforme is herpes until proven otherwise - suppress the virus even if you never see the cold sore.","EM is triggered by infections; SJS/TEN by drugs - anchoring on the cause prevents dangerous misclassification in both directions."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Erythema Multiforme","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Erythema Multiforme Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Erythema Multiforme","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-cellulitis-erysipelas","name":"Cellulitis & Erysipelas (Dermatology Perspective)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: warm, tender, non-fluctuant erythema with edema; cellulitis has ILL-DEFINED borders (deeper dermis/subcutis), erysipelas is SHARPLY demarcated, raised, and bright red (superficial dermis/lymphatics), classically on the face or shin.","Distribution: usually UNILATERAL lower leg (bilateral acute cellulitis is rare - doubt it); look for a portal of entry - tinea pedis maceration between toes, ulcer, trauma, eczema.","Systemic signs (fever, chills, leukocytosis) support infection; rapid spread, severe pain out of proportion, bullae, or crepitus signal necrotizing infection - a surgical emergency."],"dx":["Clinical; the great mimic is STASIS DERMATITIS / pseudocellulitis - bilateral, chronic, itchy > painful, with hemosiderin and scale, NOT febrile - misdiagnosis leads to unnecessary antibiotics and admissions.","Blood cultures low-yield except in systemic toxicity/immunocompromise; imaging (ultrasound) to exclude abscess/DVT; consider necrotizing fasciitis (LRINEC, rapid surgical consult) when pain, systemic toxicity, or skin necrosis outpace the exam.","Distinguish purulent (abscess, MRSA-driven) from non-purulent (streptococcal-predominant) - it changes antibiotic choice."],"tx":["Non-purulent cellulitis/erysipelas: cover beta-hemolytic streptococci (and MSSA) - cephalexin/penicillin/amoxicillin; erysipelas responds well to penicillin.","Purulent (abscess/MRSA risk): incision and drainage is primary for abscess, add MRSA coverage (TMP-SMX, doxycycline, or clindamycin); severe/systemic disease -> IV (vancomycin) and admission.","Treat the portal of entry - especially interdigital tinea pedis (topical antifungal) to prevent recurrent lower-leg cellulitis; elevate the limb, mark the border and re-examine (erythema may advance ~24-48 h before improving).","Recurrent cellulitis: address lymphedema/venous disease, treat tinea, consider prophylactic penicillin."],"pearls":["Bilateral, itchy, chronic 'cellulitis' that isn't febrile is stasis dermatitis - antibiotics won't help and steroids/compression will.","Always look between the toes: tinea pedis is the silent portal that seeds recurrent leg cellulitis.","Pain out of proportion, systemic toxicity, or skin necrosis means think necrotizing fasciitis and call surgery - imaging should not delay the operating room."],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Skin & Soft Tissue Infection Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cellulitis / Erysipelas","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Cellulitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-impetigo-folliculitis","name":"Impetigo & Folliculitis","sec":"derm","present":["Impetigo morphology: non-bullous - thin-walled pustules/vesicles that rupture to leave the classic golden-yellow HONEY-COLORED crust on an erythematous base, favoring the face/perioral and extremities; bullous - flaccid clear/turbid bullae that collapse into shallow erosions with a collarette of scale (toxin-mediated S. aureus).","Folliculitis morphology: follicular-centered papules and pustules, each pierced by a hair; distribution over beard, scalp, buttocks, thighs; 'hot tub' folliculitis (Pseudomonas) appears 1-3 days after immersion on covered areas.","Contagious and often in children (impetigo) or after shaving/occlusion/immersion (folliculitis); usually superficial and self-limited but can spread."],"dx":["Clinical; honey crust = impetigo, follicular pustules = folliculitis; culture for recurrent, extensive, or treatment-failing cases (MRSA, resistance) and to distinguish bacterial from other causes.","Recognize the mimics/variants: fungal folliculitis (Malassezia - itchy monomorphic papulopustules on trunk of young adults, often after antibiotics), eosinophilic folliculitis (HIV/immunosuppression), and 'razor bumps' pseudofolliculitis barbae (ingrown hairs, not primarily infectious).","Bullous impetigo and any bullae in a neonate/child prompt consideration of staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome if widespread + systemic."],"tx":["Impetigo, localized: topical mupirocin or retapamulin; ozenoxacin as an option; gentle crust removal and hygiene.","Impetigo, extensive/bullous/systemic: oral anti-staph/strep antibiotics (cephalexin, dicloxacillin; cover MRSA if suspected); decolonization for recurrent disease.","Bacterial folliculitis: warm compresses + topical antibiotics/antiseptics for mild; oral antibiotics for deeper/refractory; hot-tub (Pseudomonas) folliculitis is usually self-limited - stop exposure.","Fungal (Malassezia) folliculitis: TOPICAL/oral ANTIFUNGALS (it worsens with antibiotics); pseudofolliculitis barbae: shaving technique changes, laser hair removal."],"pearls":["Honey-colored crust is impetigo on sight - swab only if it's recurrent, widespread, or failing therapy.","Itchy monomorphic 'acne' on the trunk of a young adult that got worse on doxycycline is Malassezia folliculitis - switch to an antifungal.","Not all follicular pustules are bacterial: fungal, eosinophilic, and mechanical (ingrown hair) folliculitis each need a different approach."],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Skin & Soft Tissue Infection Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Impetigo / Folliculitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Impetigo","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-hsv-zoster","name":"Herpes Simplex & Herpes Zoster","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology (both): GROUPED vesicles on an erythematous base, evolving to pustules, erosions, and crusts; a monomorphic cluster at one stage.","HSV distribution: recurrent grouped vesicles at a fixed site - orolabial (HSV-1) or genital (HSV-2), with prodromal tingling; primary infection more extensive/systemic.","Zoster (VZV reactivation) distribution: painful vesicles in a UNILATERAL DERMATOMAL band that stops at the midline; prodromal neuritic pain precedes the rash; disseminated/multidermatomal disease flags immunosuppression."],"dx":["Clinical; confirm when needed with PCR (most sensitive) or direct fluorescent antibody from a vesicle; Tzanck (multinucleated giant cells) is fast but doesn't separate HSV from VZV.","Recognize the sight-/life-threatening variants: herpes zoster OPHTHALMICUS (V1 dermatome; Hutchinson sign = nasal tip lesions -> nasociliary involvement, ophthalmology urgently), zoster oticus (Ramsay Hunt - facial palsy + ear vesicles), eczema herpeticum (widespread HSV on atopic skin), and disseminated disease in the immunocompromised.","Genital HSV: counsel, test for co-infections; consider serology for counseling in select cases."],"tx":["Antivirals (acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir) work best started EARLY (within 72 h for zoster, or at prodrome/onset for HSV): shorten duration and reduce complications; higher-dose regimens for zoster.","HSV: episodic therapy for infrequent outbreaks; chronic SUPPRESSIVE therapy for frequent recurrences, HSV-associated erythema multiforme, or to reduce transmission.","Zoster complications: aggressive pain control and prevention of postherpetic neuralgia (the major morbidity); IV acyclovir + specialist care for ophthalmic, disseminated, or immunocompromised disease.","PREVENTION: recombinant zoster vaccine (Shingrix) for adults >=50 (and immunocompromised >=19) - highly effective at preventing zoster and PHN; counsel eligible patients."],"pearls":["Grouped vesicles in a band that stops at the midline is zoster; grouped vesicles that keep recurring at one spot is HSV - the pattern names the virus.","Vesicles on the tip of the nose (Hutchinson sign) predict eye involvement in ophthalmic zoster - get ophthalmology the same day.","Start antivirals within 72 hours of zoster onset and vaccinate the eligible - both reduce the postherpetic neuralgia that causes the lasting suffering."],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Herpes Zoster / Shingrix Recommendations","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Herpes Simplex / Herpes Zoster","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Shingles","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-molluscum-warts","name":"Molluscum Contagiosum & Warts (HPV)","sec":"derm","present":["Molluscum morphology: discrete dome-shaped, flesh-colored/pearly papules with central UMBILICATION; clusters in children (any site), or in the genital/pubic area of adults (sexually transmitted); widespread/giant lesions flag immunosuppression (HIV).","Wart morphology (HPV): common warts - firm hyperkeratotic papules with a rough surface and THROMBOSED CAPILLARIES (black dots) that interrupt skin lines; plantar warts (painful, inward, on pressure points); flat warts (small, smooth, on face/hands); anogenital condylomata (soft, cauliflower).","Both spread by contact/autoinoculation; molluscum may develop a surrounding eczema (molluscum dermatitis) or become inflamed as it clears (the 'BOTE' sign)."],"dx":["Clinical; the umbilicated pearly papule (molluscum) and the black-dotted keratotic papule interrupting dermatoglyphics (wart) are recognizable on sight; pare a wart to reveal thrombosed capillaries and confirm it isn't a corn/callus (which preserves skin lines).","Dermoscopy aids ambiguous cases; biopsy only when atypical or to exclude mimics (e.g., amelanotic melanoma masquerading as an 'wart', SCC in a non-healing genital/periungual lesion).","Genital lesions: counsel on HPV, screen per guidelines; extensive/refractory molluscum warrants HIV testing."],"tx":["Both are often SELF-LIMITED (immune clearance over months-years) - reassurance and watchful waiting is legitimate, especially for molluscum in young children.","Molluscum, if treated: cantharidin, cryotherapy, curettage, or topical agents; treat surrounding dermatitis to reduce spread; avoid aggressive scarring therapy on children's faces.","Warts: no single reliably curative therapy - first-line salicylic acid and/or cryotherapy (repeated), then intralesional immunotherapy (Candida antigen), cantharidin, or destructive/immune options for resistant warts; plantar warts need paring + persistence.","Anogenital warts: patient-applied imiquimod or podofilox, or provider-applied cryotherapy/TCA; HPV VACCINATION prevents the oncogenic and wart-causing types - recommend per schedule."],"pearls":["A pearly umbilicated papule is molluscum; a keratotic papule with black dots that erases the skin lines is a wart - the surface tells you which virus.","Widespread or giant molluscum in an adult is an HIV screen.","Warts and molluscum both often self-resolve - weigh the morbidity of treatment (pain, scarring in kids) against just waiting."],"refs":[{"t":"CDC HPV / Genital Warts Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Molluscum Contagiosum / Warts","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Warts Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"}]},{"id":"derm-tinea","name":"Tinea Infections (Corporis, Pedis, Capitis, Versicolor)","sec":"derm","present":["Tinea corporis morphology: ANNULAR plaque with a raised scaly ADVANCING border and central clearing ('ringworm'); tinea pedis: interdigital maceration/scaling, moccasin-distribution hyperkeratosis, or vesicles; tinea cruris spares the scrotum (unlike candida).","Tinea capitis (children): scaly patches with hair loss and broken hairs +/- black dots; kerion is a boggy inflamed pustular mass; posterior cervical lymphadenopathy supports it.","Tinea versicolor (Malassezia): hypo- OR hyperpigmented, finely scaly oval macules coalescing on the upper trunk/shoulders, more visible after sun (affected areas don't tan)."],"dx":["Confirm with KOH prep of a scale scraping (branching septate hyphae; 'spaghetti and meatballs' hyphae+spores in versicolor) - KOH before empiric steroids, which cause 'tinea incognito' (an atypical, spreading, poorly-scaly eruption from steroid-masked tinea).","Wood's lamp: some tinea capitis fluoresces; culture for capitis and refractory cases; the annular active border with central clearing distinguishes tinea corporis from nummular eczema, granuloma annulare (no scale), and psoriasis.","Scrape the ADVANCING edge for the highest yield."],"tx":["Localized tinea corporis/cruris/pedis: TOPICAL antifungals (azoles, terbinafine, ciclopirox) for 2-4 weeks past clearance; keep areas dry; treat footwear/tinea pedis to prevent cruris/onychomycosis reinfection.","Tinea capitis REQUIRES ORAL therapy (topicals don't penetrate the follicle): griseofulvin or terbinafine; add antifungal shampoo to reduce shedding; treat household contacts/fomites; kerion may need adjunctive steroids.","Extensive, follicular (Majocchi granuloma), or immunosuppressed disease: oral terbinafine/itraconazole.","Tinea versicolor: topical selenium sulfide/azole shampoos or creams; oral azoles for extensive/recurrent; counsel that dyspigmentation takes months to normalize AFTER the fungus is gone."],"pearls":["KOH before steroids: a steroid put on unrecognized tinea creates tinea incognito that spreads and confuses everyone.","Scrape the raised edge, not the clear center - that's where the hyphae live.","Tinea capitis and onychomycosis need ORAL antifungals; topical creams can't reach hair follicles or nail plate."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Tinea / Fungal Infection Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dermatophyte Infections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"CDC Fungal Diseases","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-candidal-intertrigo","name":"Candidal Intertrigo","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: bright, moist, beefy-red erythema in skin FOLDS with maceration and, crucially, SATELLITE pustules/papules just beyond the main plaque - the satellite lesions are the clue.","Distribution: intertriginous/occluded moist areas - inframammary, axillae, inguinal/genital, abdominal panniculus, gluteal cleft, web spaces; often itchy/burning.","Risk factors: obesity, diabetes, moisture/occlusion, incontinence, antibiotics, immunosuppression; candida (unlike tinea cruris) DOES involve the scrotum/labia."],"dx":["Clinical; KOH shows budding yeast with pseudohyphae; the satellite pustules distinguish candida from tinea cruris (advancing scaly border, spares scrotum), inverse psoriasis (well-demarcated, shiny, no satellites), seborrheic dermatitis, and erythrasma (Wood's lamp CORAL-RED fluorescence, Corynebacterium).","New or recalcitrant candidal intertrigo warrants a glucose/diabetes check."],"tx":["Dry the environment - the disease lives on moisture: air exposure, absorbent barriers, moisture-wicking, treat incontinence, weight management; drying agents (zinc oxide), antifungal powders in folds.","Topical antifungals (nystatin, azoles - clotrimazole, ketoconazole, miconazole) until clear; a low-potency topical steroid briefly for marked inflammation (not as monotherapy - steroids alone worsen it).","Extensive/refractory or immunocompromised: oral fluconazole; address the systemic driver (glycemic control)."],"pearls":["Satellite pustules beyond a red, macerated fold = candida; a dry advancing scaly ring that spares the scrotum = tinea - the border and satellites separate them.","If it involves the scrotum or labia, think candida, not tinea cruris.","No topical will win against a fold that stays wet - drying the environment is half the prescription."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Candidal Intertrigo","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Intertrigo Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Candidiasis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-scabies-lice","name":"Scabies & Lice","sec":"derm","present":["Scabies morphology: intensely pruritic (WORSE AT NIGHT) small papules, excoriations, and the pathognomonic BURROW (thin serpiginous gray line), favoring finger WEB SPACES, wrists/flexors, axillae, periumbilical, genitals (penile/scrotal nodules), and areolae; sparing the head in adults (but involving scalp/palms/soles in infants).","Crusted (Norwegian) scabies: thick hyperkeratotic crusts teeming with mites in the immunosuppressed/elderly - highly contagious, minimal itch.","Lice: head lice - nits (firmly adherent egg cases) on hair shafts + scalp itch; pubic lice - itching with lice/nits in coarse hair; body lice live in clothing seams (vector for typhus/trench fever)."],"dx":["Scabies: the itch out of proportion, characteristic distribution, burrows, and household members itching too; confirm by dermoscopy ('delta-wing jet' sign) or skin scraping/microscopy showing mite, eggs, or scybala - but treat on strong clinical suspicion without waiting.","Distinguish from atopic dermatitis, dermatitis herpetiformis, and papular urticaria; crusted scabies mimics psoriasis/eczema - a critical miss because it causes outbreaks.","Lice: visualize live lice/nits; wet-combing improves detection; body lice - examine clothing seams."],"tx":["Scabies: topical PERMETHRIN 5% applied neck-down (whole body incl. scalp in infants/elderly), left overnight, REPEATED in 1 week; oral IVERMECTIN (two doses ~1-2 weeks apart) for widespread disease, outbreaks, or crusted scabies (combine topical + oral for crusted).","Treat ALL close/household contacts SIMULTANEOUSLY (even if asymptomatic) and decontaminate: wash/hot-dry bedding and clothing, bag non-washables ~72 h; counsel that itch can persist 2-4 weeks after successful treatment (post-scabetic itch - not treatment failure; treat with steroids/antihistamines).","Head lice: topical pediculicides (permethrin, dimethicone, spinosad, topical ivermectin) with wet-combing and repeat treatment; pubic lice: topical treatment + treat partners/screen for other STIs; body lice: hygiene and launder/discard infested clothing."],"pearls":["Itch that's worst at night, spares the face, and has the household scratching too is scabies - find the burrow in the finger webs.","Post-treatment itch lasting weeks is expected (dead mite antigen), not failure - don't re-treat reflexively; do re-treat if new burrows appear.","Always treat contacts and the environment at the same time, or the mites just come back."],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Scabies / Lice Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Scabies / Pediculosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Scabies Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"}]},{"id":"derm-bullous-pemphigoid","name":"Bullous Pemphigoid","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: large, TENSE, fluid-filled bullae on erythematous or urticarial/normal skin that do NOT rupture easily (subepidermal split); intense pruritus, often on flexural surfaces, trunk, and thighs; mucosal involvement is uncommon and mild.","A prodromal, non-bullous phase of intense pruritus with urticarial or eczematous plaques can precede blisters by WEEKS-MONTHS - itch in an elderly patient is a common presentation.","Classic in the ELDERLY; Nikolsky sign NEGATIVE (contrast with pemphigus)."],"dx":["Biopsy TWO specimens: lesional skin for histology (subepidermal blister with eosinophils) and PERILESIONAL skin for direct immunofluorescence - the gold standard: LINEAR IgG and C3 along the basement membrane zone.","Serology: anti-BP180 and anti-BP230 (ELISA); indirect immunofluorescence on salt-split skin localizes to the blister ROOF (vs the floor in some other subepidermal diseases).","Consider drug-induced BP (checkpoint inhibitors, gliptins/DPP-4 inhibitors, diuretics, some antibiotics) - review the medication list."],"tx":["Localized/mild: high-potency topical corticosteroids (clobetasol) - as effective as systemic steroids with less toxicity, and preferred first-line for many patients.","Widespread/severe: systemic corticosteroids with steroid-sparing agents (doxycycline +/- nicotinamide as a lower-toxicity option; azathioprine, mycophenolate, methotrexate); rituximab and omalizumab for refractory disease.","Supportive wound care, infection surveillance, and treat the pruritus; taper slowly - it's a chronic relapsing disease; review and stop culprit drugs if drug-induced."],"pearls":["Tense blisters that resist rupture in an elderly, itchy patient is bullous pemphigoid; flaccid blisters that shear off (positive Nikolsky) is pemphigus - tension vs fragility separates them.","Perilesional (not lesional) skin goes for direct immunofluorescence - sample the wrong site and the diagnostic test comes back falsely negative.","Unexplained intractable itch in an older adult can be pre-bullous pemphigoid - keep it on the differential before any blisters appear."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bullous Pemphigoid","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Bullous Disease Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Bullous Pemphigoid","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-pemphigus-vulgaris","name":"Pemphigus Vulgaris","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: FLACCID, easily-ruptured bullae that quickly become painful erosions (the intact blister is rarely seen); intraepidermal split makes the skin fragile - Nikolsky sign POSITIVE (epidermis shears with lateral pressure).","MUCOSAL involvement is characteristic and often FIRST: painful, non-healing oral erosions (may precede skin by months), also conjunctival, nasal, esophageal, genital.","Chronic, potentially life-threatening (fluid loss, infection through erosions); typically middle-aged adults."],"dx":["Biopsy lesional skin (suprabasal acantholysis, 'row of tombstones') PLUS perilesional direct immunofluorescence: IgG in a 'CHICKEN-WIRE'/fishnet INTERCELLULAR pattern throughout the epidermis (contrast with the linear basement-membrane band of bullous pemphigoid).","Serology: anti-desmoglein 1 (skin) and desmoglein 3 (mucosa) antibodies by ELISA - correlate with activity and guide monitoring.","Recognize paraneoplastic pemphigus (severe intractable stomatitis + polymorphous skin + underlying lymphoproliferative malignancy) as a distinct, grave variant."],"tx":["Systemic immunosuppression is required (unlike localized BP): RITUXIMAB is now first-line (with steroids) and induces durable remissions - a practice-changing shift; systemic corticosteroids for control, with steroid-sparing agents (azathioprine, mycophenolate).","Meticulous wound/erosion care, oral care and analgesia for stomatitis, nutrition support, infection surveillance - the eroded barrier is the danger.","Monitor desmoglein titers and taper immunosuppression carefully; multidisciplinary care (ophthalmology, dentistry, ENT) for mucosal sites."],"pearls":["Flaccid blisters, positive Nikolsky, and painful mouth erosions is pemphigus vulgaris - the fragility and mucosal disease separate it from the tense, mucosa-sparing bullous pemphigoid.","Non-healing oral erosions can be the only sign for months - persistent painful stomatitis deserves a biopsy, not another course of thrush treatment.","Direct immunofluorescence pattern is the discriminator: intercellular fishnet = pemphigus, linear basement membrane = pemphigoid."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pemphigus Vulgaris","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Pemphigus Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pemphigus","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-dermatitis-herpetiformis","name":"Dermatitis Herpetiformis & Celiac Skin Disease","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: intensely pruritic, GROUPED ('herpetiform') papulovesicles on urticarial bases - but the intact vesicles are usually destroyed by scratching, so EXCORIATIONS and crusts dominate the picture.","Distribution is symmetric and diagnostic: EXTENSOR surfaces - elbows, knees, buttocks, scalp, shoulders.","The cutaneous expression of CELIAC disease/gluten sensitivity; burning/stinging itch; most patients have subclinical enteropathy even without GI symptoms."],"dx":["Biopsy PERILESIONAL skin for direct immunofluorescence - the gold standard: GRANULAR IgG (IgA) deposits in the DERMAL PAPILLAE; lesional histology shows neutrophilic microabscesses at the tips of dermal papillae.","Serology: anti-tissue transglutaminase (tTG-IgA) and anti-endomysial antibodies (shared with celiac); anti-epidermal transglutaminase is more specific for DH; check total IgA.","Screen for associated autoimmune disease (thyroid) and the enteropathy; the relentless extensor itch with excoriations should prompt the DIF."],"tx":["GLUTEN-FREE DIET is the cornerstone and the only therapy that treats the underlying disease, reduces medication need, and lowers long-term lymphoma risk - lifelong, with dietitian support.","DAPSONE gives rapid symptomatic relief of itch (often within days) while the diet takes effect - CHECK G6PD before starting (hemolysis) and monitor for methemoglobinemia/hemolysis/agranulocytosis.","Long-term: taper dapsone as the gluten-free diet controls disease; monitor for celiac complications and nutritional deficiencies."],"pearls":["Symmetric, ferociously itchy excoriations on the elbows, knees, and buttocks is dermatitis herpetiformis - biopsy perilesional skin for granular IgA before you dismiss it as neurotic excoriations.","Check G6PD before dapsone, every time.","The rash is celiac disease on the skin - the gluten-free diet, not just dapsone, is the real treatment and it lowers lymphoma risk."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Dermatitis Herpetiformis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"Celiac Disease Foundation","u":"https://celiac.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Dermatitis Herpetiformis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-lichen-planus","name":"Lichen Planus","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology - the '6 Ps': Pruritic, Polygonal, Planar (flat-topped), Purple (violaceous) Papules and Plaques, often with fine white lacy lines on the surface (WICKHAM STRIAE).","Distribution: flexor wrists/forearms, ankles/shins, lower back; Koebnerizes into linear streaks at scratch sites; oral LP - reticulate white lacy buccal patches or painful erosions; genital, nail (dystrophy, pterygium), and scalp (lichen planopilaris -> scarring alopecia) variants.","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation is prominent, especially in darker skin."],"dx":["Clinical + Wickham striae; biopsy shows a band-like ('lichenoid') lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction with sawtooth rete and basal damage; confirm oral/erosive/nail disease histologically.","Check for hepatitis C association (especially oral/erosive LP in endemic populations); review drugs for lichenoid DRUG eruptions (thiazides, ACE inhibitors, antimalarials, beta-blockers, checkpoint inhibitors) which mimic idiopathic LP.","Erosive oral and genital LP carry a small squamous cell carcinoma risk - monitor non-healing lesions."],"tx":["Cutaneous LP: high-potency topical (or intralesional) corticosteroids first-line; it is often self-limited over months-2 years; antihistamines/phototherapy for widespread pruritic disease; systemic steroids or retinoids/immunosuppressants for severe/erosive disease.","Oral LP: topical steroids (gels, rinses), topical calcineurin inhibitors; treat symptomatic erosive disease and surveil for malignant change.","Scalp (lichen planopilaris): treat early to prevent permanent scarring alopecia; nail LP: intralesional/systemic therapy to prevent irreversible pterygium.","Address post-inflammatory pigmentation with sun protection and time; stop culprit drugs in lichenoid drug eruption."],"pearls":["Purple, polygonal, pruritic, flat-topped papules with lacy white Wickham striae is lichen planus - the surface lines clinch it.","Check hepatitis C in oral/erosive lichen planus and review the med list for a lichenoid drug eruption that looks identical.","Erosive oral and genital lichen planus need long-term surveillance - chronic erosions can transform to squamous cell carcinoma."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lichen Planus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Lichen Planus Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Lichen Planus","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-pityriasis-rosea","name":"Pityriasis Rosea","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: begins with a solitary HERALD PATCH - a 2-5 cm oval salmon-pink plaque with a fine COLLARETTE of scale trailing inside the border - days to ~2 weeks before the generalized eruption.","Generalized phase: numerous smaller oval scaly plaques on the trunk/proximal limbs aligned along skin cleavage lines in a 'CHRISTMAS TREE' pattern on the back; mild or no itch; spares face/palms/soles typically.","Self-limited (6-8 weeks), young adults; a viral association (HHV-6/7) is proposed; a prodrome is sometimes reported."],"dx":["Clinical - herald patch + christmas-tree distribution + collarette scale are diagnostic; no testing usually needed.","The critical mimic to EXCLUDE is SECONDARY SYPHILIS - it can look identical BUT classically involves the PALMS AND SOLES, may have lymphadenopathy/mucous patches/condyloma lata, and occurs with risk factors: send RPR/treponemal serology if palms/soles involved, no herald patch, or any risk.","Also consider guttate psoriasis, nummular eczema, tinea (KOH the herald patch if solitary and scaly - it can be mistaken for tinea corporis), and pityriasis-rosea-like DRUG eruptions (ACE inhibitors, others)."],"tx":["Reassurance and education: it resolves on its own without scarring in 6-8 weeks - the main 'treatment' is confident diagnosis and expectation-setting.","Symptomatic: emollients, topical steroids and oral antihistamines for pruritus; a short course of UVB or possibly oral antivirals/macrolides has limited evidence for severe/extensive cases.","Counsel on possible post-inflammatory pigment change (esp. darker skin) and that a longer or atypical course warrants re-evaluation (and re-checking for syphilis/drug cause)."],"pearls":["A herald patch followed by a christmas-tree scaly eruption sparing palms and soles is pityriasis rosea - but if the palms and soles ARE involved, rule out secondary syphilis first.","The collarette of scale (attached at the outer edge, free inside) is the morphologic signature.","It's a diagnosis of confident reassurance - but a lonely scaly herald patch can be mistaken for tinea, so KOH if in doubt."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pityriasis Rosea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Pityriasis Rosea Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pityriasis Rosea","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-granuloma-annulare","name":"Granuloma Annulare","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: firm, smooth, flesh-colored to erythematous/violaceous DERMAL papules coalescing into ANNULAR (ring-shaped) plaques with NO surface scale or epidermal change - the lack of scale is the key distinguishing feature.","Distribution: localized form favors the dorsal HANDS and FEET, extensor extremities of children/young adults; generalized form is widespread on trunk/limbs (more common in older adults).","Asymptomatic or mildly itchy; chronic but usually self-limited (localized lesions often resolve over months-2 years without scarring)."],"dx":["Clinical; the NON-scaly annular plaque distinguishes it from tinea corporis (scaly advancing border, KOH-positive) - KOH is the fast bedside discriminator - and from nummular eczema, psoriasis, and sarcoidosis.","Biopsy if uncertain: palisaded granulomas around degenerated collagen with mucin.","Associations to consider (evidence variable): generalized/atypical GA has been linked to diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, thyroid disease, and rarely underlying infection/malignancy - screen the generalized cases (glucose/lipids/thyroid) with appropriate skepticism."],"tx":["Reassurance first - localized GA is benign and frequently self-resolves; setting expectations often suffices.","If treated: high-potency topical or intralesional corticosteroids for localized lesions; topical calcineurin inhibitors as steroid-sparing.","Generalized/refractory GA: phototherapy, and various systemic options (antimalarials, and others) with modest, variable evidence - manage expectations; treat associated metabolic conditions."],"pearls":["A ring without scale is granuloma annulare; a ring WITH a scaly advancing edge is tinea - do the KOH and save the patient a course of antifungal for nothing.","Localized granuloma annulare is benign and self-limited - the strongest intervention is often confident reassurance.","Reserve the diabetes/lipid/thyroid workup for generalized or atypical disease rather than every solitary ring."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Granuloma Annulare","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Granuloma Annulare Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Granuloma Annulare","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-vitiligo","name":"Vitiligo","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: sharply demarcated, completely DEPIGMENTED (chalk/milk-white, not merely hypopigmented) macules and patches; Wood's lamp accentuates them with bright blue-white fluorescence and reveals subclinical involvement.","Distribution patterns: non-segmental (symmetric - periorificial face, hands, elbows, knees, genitals; the common type) vs segmental (unilateral, dermatomal-ish, younger onset, stable); leukotrichia (white hairs) within patches signals follicular melanocyte loss.","Koebnerizes at sites of friction/trauma; asymptomatic but psychosocially heavy, especially in darker skin."],"dx":["Clinical + Wood's lamp; the total absence of pigment distinguishes vitiligo from hypopigmented mimics - tinea versicolor (fine scale, KOH+), pityriasis alba (ill-defined, on atopic children), post-inflammatory hypopigmentation (history of prior dermatosis), and (critically) the hypopigmented not depigmented patches of leprosy/CTCL.","Screen for associated autoimmune disease - THYROID especially (check TSH), also type 1 diabetes, pernicious anemia, Addison; biopsy rarely needed (absent melanocytes)."],"tx":["Set expectations: repigmentation is slow and best on the face/neck, poor on hands/feet; treatment stabilizes and can repigment but is not guaranteed.","Localized: topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus - preferred for face/eyelids); the topical JAK inhibitor RUXOLITINIB cream is FDA-approved for nonsegmental vitiligo and a significant advance.","Widespread: narrowband UVB phototherapy (the workhorse for extensive disease), often combined with topicals; surgical grafting for stable/segmental disease; depigmentation therapy only in extensive refractory cases by choice.","Always: photoprotection (depigmented skin burns and the contrast worsens), vitamin D, cosmetic camouflage, and psychological support; treat associated thyroid disease."],"pearls":["Chalk-white and Wood's-lamp-bright means true depigmentation (vitiligo); off-white with scale or ill-defined edges points to the hypopigmentation mimics.","Check a TSH in every new vitiligo patient - autoimmune thyroid disease travels with it.","The face repigments, the hands and feet usually don't - say so up front so the patient isn't chasing an impossible result."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Vitiligo Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vitiligo","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Vitiligo","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-melasma-pih","name":"Melasma & Post-Inflammatory Pigmentation","sec":"derm","present":["Melasma morphology: symmetric, ill-defined tan-to-brown reticulated HYPERpigmented patches; centrofacial (forehead, cheeks, upper lip, chin), malar, or mandibular; worse with sun and heat.","Triggers: UV, pregnancy ('mask of pregnancy'/chloasma), OCPs/hormones, and it strongly favors women with darker skin phototypes.","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH): tan-brown-to-gray macules/patches conforming to the SITE of prior inflammation (acne, eczema, trauma, procedures) - the shape follows the preceding dermatosis; darker skin is more prone and it lingers for months."],"dx":["Clinical; Wood's lamp historically separated epidermal (accentuated) from dermal (not accentuated) pigment, guiding prognosis (dermal/mixed is more stubborn).","Distinguish melasma (symmetric centrofacial, hormonal/UV) from PIH (follows prior inflammation), lentigines/ephelides, drug-induced pigmentation, and (for gray facial pigment) exogenous ochronosis from overused hydroquinone or lichen planus pigmentosus.","Manage expectations: both are chronic/relapsing and pigment resolves slowly."],"tx":["PHOTOPROTECTION is the foundation and the most important step for BOTH - broad-spectrum SPF with iron oxides/tint (blocks visible light, which drives pigment in darker skin), reapplication, hats; without it every other therapy fails.","Melasma: topical hydroquinone or triple combination (hydroquinone + retinoid + steroid) in cycles; adjuncts azelaic acid, tranexamic acid (topical/oral - screen thrombotic risk), cysteamine; CAUTIOUS procedures (gentle chemical peels, low-energy laser) - aggressive lasers can worsen melasma.","PIH: treat the UNDERLYING inflammatory disease first (ongoing acne keeps making new PIH), then topical lightening (retinoids, azelaic acid, hydroquinone) + sun protection + patience.","Avoid overtreatment: prolonged hydroquinone risks exogenous ochronosis; procedures in skin of color risk MORE PIH - go gentle."],"pearls":["No sunscreen, no result - and in darker skin it must block visible light (tinted/iron-oxide), because plain UV filters aren't enough for pigment.","Treat the acne/eczema before chasing the brown marks it leaves - PIH keeps regenerating while the primary disease is active.","Melasma punishes aggressive lasers - restraint and photoprotection beat firepower."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Melasma / Hyperpigmentation Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Melasma / Post-Inflammatory Hyperpigmentation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Melasma","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-alopecia-areata","name":"Alopecia Areata","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: sudden, sharply circumscribed, SMOOTH round-oval patches of NON-scarring hair loss - the scalp skin looks normal (no scale, no follicular scarring, follicular openings preserved).","Pathognomonic 'EXCLAMATION-MARK' hairs at the margins (tapered proximally); variants - alopecia totalis (whole scalp), universalis (all body hair), ophiasis (band along occipital/temporal hairline); nail pitting is common.","Autoimmune (T-cell attack on the hair bulb); unpredictable course - regrowth (often initially fine/white) and relapse; psychologically significant."],"dx":["Clinical; the smooth patch with preserved follicular ostia + exclamation-mark hairs + a positive pull test at the active edge; dermoscopy shows yellow dots, black dots, exclamation-mark and short vellus hairs.","Distinguish from SCARRING alopecias (lost follicular openings, permanent - lichen planopilaris, discoid lupus), tinea capitis (scale, broken hairs, KOH/culture+ in children), trichotillomania (irregular, broken hairs of varying length), and telogen effluvium (diffuse, not patchy).","Screen for associated autoimmune conditions (thyroid, vitiligo) as clinically indicated."],"tx":["Limited patches: intralesional corticosteroids (first-line for localized adult disease), topical high-potency steroids, topical minoxidil as adjunct; many limited cases regrow spontaneously.","Extensive/rapidly progressive or totalis/universalis: the oral JANUS KINASE (JAK) INHIBITORS (baricitinib, ritlecitinib - FDA-approved for severe alopecia areata) are the practice-changing advance; topical immunotherapy (diphencyprone/SADBE) is an alternative; systemic steroids give temporary control with relapse.","Counsel honestly about the unpredictable, relapsing course, offer wigs/camouflage and psychological support; regrown hair may initially be white and later repigment."],"pearls":["Smooth patch + preserved follicular openings + exclamation-mark hairs = alopecia areata (non-scarring); scarring alopecia loses the follicular openings and the hair permanently.","In a child with a scaly patch of hair loss, prove it's not tinea capitis (KOH/culture) before calling it alopecia areata.","JAK inhibitors changed the outlook for severe disease - the days of 'nothing works for totalis' are over."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Alopecia Areata Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Alopecia Areata","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"NIH/NIAMS: Alopecia Areata","u":"https://www.niams.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-androgenetic-telogen","name":"Androgenetic Alopecia & Telogen Effluvium","sec":"derm","present":["Androgenetic alopecia (AGA) morphology: gradual, PATTERNED, non-scarring thinning with follicular MINIATURIZATION - men: bitemporal recession + vertex (Norwood); women: diffuse central thinning with a retained frontal hairline and a widened part ('CHRISTMAS-TREE' pattern on the part, Ludwig).","Telogen effluvium (TE) morphology: DIFFUSE, generalized shedding of normal telogen (club) hairs 2-4 MONTHS after a trigger; the scalp looks normal, no patches, a strongly positive pull test globally; patient reports handfuls of hair, often overestimated but real.","AGA is chronic/progressive and genetic/hormonal; TE is acute and self-limited once the trigger resolves."],"dx":["AGA: clinical pattern + miniaturization on dermoscopy (hair-shaft diameter DIVERSITY); in women with AGA + hyperandrogenism signs (hirsutism, acne, irregular menses), evaluate for PCOS/androgen excess.","TE: identify the TRIGGER 2-4 months prior - childbirth, high fever/illness (incl. COVID), surgery, crash diet/weight loss, thyroid disease, iron deficiency, new drugs; check ferritin, TSH, and relevant labs; the shed is telogen (club) hairs.","Both are NON-scarring (follicular openings preserved); exclude scarring alopecia and rule out AA (patchy)."],"tx":["AGA: topical MINOXIDIL (men and women) and, in men, oral FINASTERIDE (5-alpha-reductase inhibitor - counsel on sexual side effects; teratogenic, avoid handling in pregnancy); low-level laser, oral minoxidil (off-label, growing use), spironolactone in women, and hair transplant for suitable candidates; early treatment preserves more.","TE: REASSURE - it recovers over months once the trigger is corrected; treat the cause (repair iron/ferritin, thyroid), stop culprit drugs; no specific hair drug needed, and regrowth is expected.","Set timelines: AGA drugs take 6-12 months and maintain rather than reverse; TE recovers over 3-6 months."],"pearls":["Patterned thinning with miniaturization is androgenetic; a diffuse shed 2-4 months after a stressor is telogen effluvium - the pattern vs the timeline separates them.","Chase ferritin and TSH in telogen effluvium - correctable deficiencies are the common, fixable causes.","Reassurance is the main treatment for telogen effluvium; the hair comes back once the trigger is gone - the hard part is the wait."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Hair Loss Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Androgenetic Alopecia / Telogen Effluvium","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Hair Loss","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-bcc","name":"Basal Cell Carcinoma","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology (nodular, commonest): a PEARLY, translucent papule/nodule with rolled borders, arborizing TELANGIECTASIAS over the surface, and often a central depression or ulcer ('rodent ulcer'); may be pigmented (mimics melanoma).","Other subtypes: superficial (thin pink-red scaly patch with a thread-like pearly rim, often trunk), morpheaform/infiltrative (scar-like, ill-defined, indistinct borders - deceptively large and aggressive).","Distribution: sun-exposed skin, especially the FACE (nose, cheeks, eyelids), head and neck; slow-growing, locally destructive, essentially never metastasizes but invades if neglected."],"dx":["Dermoscopy (arborizing vessels, blue-gray ovoid nests, leaf-like areas) raises suspicion; SHAVE or punch BIOPSY confirms and subtypes it - subtype matters because morpheaform/infiltrative are aggressive and need wider/Mohs margins.","Risk-stratify by NCCN low- vs high-risk features: location (the facial 'mask'/H-zone), size, borders, recurrence, aggressive histology, immunosuppression, prior radiation.","A non-healing, bleeding, or 'pimple that won't heal' on sun-damaged facial skin is BCC until biopsied."],"tx":["Surgical excision with appropriate margins for most; MOHS micrographic surgery for high-risk sites (face, recurrent, aggressive subtypes, ill-defined borders) - tissue-sparing with the highest cure rate.","Superficial low-risk BCC on trunk/limbs: topical imiquimod or 5-fluorouracil, curettage and electrodesiccation, or photodynamic therapy; radiation for non-surgical candidates.","Locally advanced or (rarely) metastatic BCC: HEDGEHOG PATHWAY inhibitors (vismodegib, sonidegib), and immunotherapy (cemiplimab) for refractory cases.","Prevention/surveillance: rigorous photoprotection and regular full-skin exams - a BCC predicts more skin cancers."],"pearls":["A pearly papule with fine branching telangiectasias that bleeds and won't heal on the face is basal cell carcinoma - biopsy the 'pimple' that keeps coming back.","Subtype dictates surgery: morpheaform/infiltrative BCCs are icebergs - the visible lesion underestimates the extent, so they go to Mohs.","BCC almost never kills by metastasis but destroys locally - on the face and near the eye, timely Mohs prevents disfigurement."],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Basal Cell Skin Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"AAD Skin Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Basal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-scc-ak","name":"Squamous Cell Carcinoma & Actinic Keratosis","sec":"derm","present":["Actinic keratosis (precursor) morphology: rough, gritty, ill-defined erythematous scaly macules/papules on chronically sun-damaged skin - often better FELT (sandpaper texture) than seen; scalp (bald men), face, ears, dorsal hands, forearms.","SCC in situ (Bowen disease): a well-demarcated persistent scaly erythematous patch/plaque.","Invasive SCC morphology: a firm, indurated, hyperkeratotic or ulcerated nodule/plaque that may be tender, bleed, or arise within an AK/scar/chronic wound; keratoacanthoma is a rapidly-growing crateriform variant."],"dx":["AK is clinical (rough scaly sun-damaged skin); a THICK, tender, indurated, bleeding, or rapidly growing AK-like lesion warrants biopsy to exclude invasive SCC.","Biopsy suspected SCC; risk-stratify (NCCN/BWH): size, depth, poor differentiation, perineural invasion, high-risk sites (ear, lip, temple), recurrence, and IMMUNOSUPPRESSION (transplant patients get numerous aggressive SCCs) - these predict metastatic potential (SCC, unlike BCC, can metastasize, especially lip/ear).","Consider SCC in any non-healing ulcer, chronic wound (Marjolin), or lesion on the lip/genital mucosa."],"tx":["AK (field treatment reduces SCC): cryotherapy for discrete lesions; FIELD therapy for multiple - topical 5-fluorouracil, imiquimod, tirbanibulin, or photodynamic therapy over the whole sun-damaged area.","SCC in situ: topical 5-FU/imiquimod, curettage/ED, or excision; invasive SCC: surgical EXCISION with adequate margins, MOHS for high-risk/facial/recurrent; radiation for non-surgical candidates.","Advanced/metastatic SCC: cemiplimab or pembrolizumab (PD-1 immunotherapy) is the major advance; multidisciplinary care and nodal evaluation for high-risk disease.","Transplant/immunosuppressed: aggressive surveillance, field therapy, and consider reducing immunosuppression + acitretin prophylaxis."],"pearls":["An actinic keratosis you can feel like sandpaper is a precursor; when it thickens, indurates, becomes tender, or bleeds, biopsy it for invasive SCC.","SCC metastasizes (unlike BCC) - lip, ear, and immunosuppressed patients are the high-risk group to watch.","Treat the field, not just the spot: scattered AKs mark sun-damaged skin that keeps making new lesions - 5-FU/PDT over the whole area lowers future SCC."],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Squamous Cell Skin Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"AAD Actinic Keratosis / SCC Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cutaneous SCC / Actinic Keratosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-melanoma-nevi","name":"Melanoma & Atypical Nevi","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology by the ABCDEs: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation (multiple shades, blue/black/white/red), Diameter >6 mm, and above all EVOLUTION (change in size/shape/color/symptoms); the 'UGLY DUCKLING' - the mole that looks different from the patient's others.","Subtypes: superficial spreading (commonest), nodular (rapidly growing, may be amelanotic pink - the dangerous mimic), lentigo maligna (slow, on chronically sun-damaged face of elderly), ACRAL lentiginous (palms/soles/nail unit - the type disproportionately affecting darker skin, often diagnosed late).","Nail melanoma clue - Hutchinson sign (pigment spreading onto the proximal nail fold) in a longitudinal melanonychia band."],"dx":["Dermoscopy improves accuracy (atypical network, blue-white veil, irregular streaks/dots); EXCISIONAL biopsy (narrow margins, full depth) is preferred for suspected melanoma - never a superficial shave that transects depth, because BRESLOW DEPTH drives everything.","Staging (AJCC): Breslow thickness, ulceration, mitotic activity, then sentinel lymph node biopsy for appropriate depths; imaging for higher stages; molecular testing (BRAF) in advanced disease guides therapy.","Atypical (dysplastic) nevi: irregular but benign moles that are RISK MARKERS (and occasional precursors) - the patient with many needs surveillance, not excision of every one; biopsy the changing/ugly-duckling lesion."],"tx":["Primary melanoma: WIDE LOCAL EXCISION with margins set by Breslow depth (e.g., in situ ~0.5-1 cm up to 2 cm for thick tumors); sentinel lymph node biopsy for staging in intermediate-thickness (and selected) tumors.","Advanced/metastatic: the revolution - IMMUNE CHECKPOINT INHIBITORS (anti-PD-1 +/- anti-CTLA-4) and BRAF/MEK-targeted therapy (for BRAF-mutant); adjuvant immunotherapy/targeted therapy for high-risk resected disease.","Prevention/surveillance: strict photoprotection, avoid tanning beds, total-body skin exams and dermoscopic monitoring for high-risk patients, patient self-exam education."],"pearls":["Evolution and the ugly-duckling sign catch more melanomas than any single ABCD letter - the mole that's changing or doesn't match its neighbors is the one to biopsy.","Never shave-transect a suspected melanoma - Breslow depth determines margins, sentinel node, and prognosis, and a partial shave can destroy it.","Amelanotic/nodular melanoma is pink and fast - and acral melanoma hides on soles and nails, so examine palms, soles, and nail units, especially in darker skin."],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Melanoma Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"AAD Melanoma Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Melanoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-ctcl","name":"Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma (Mycosis Fungoides)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: evolves slowly over YEARS through patch -> plaque -> tumor stages; early PATCHES are erythematous, finely scaly, often in a 'BATHING-SUIT' distribution (sun-PROTECTED sites - buttocks, trunk, proximal limbs), frequently with poikiloderma (mottled atrophy/telangiectasia/dyspigmentation).","Plaques are indurated and annular; tumors are nodular and may ulcerate; Sezary syndrome is the leukemic/erythrodermic variant (diffuse red skin, intense itch, circulating malignant cells, lymphadenopathy).","The great imitator - notoriously mimics eczema/psoriasis and is often treated as such for YEARS before diagnosis."],"dx":["High index of suspicion for a 'refractory eczema/psoriasis' that won't respond, especially non-sun-exposed patches with poikiloderma in an adult; multiple, repeated skin BIOPSIES over time are often needed (early histology is subtle - atypical lymphocytes, epidermotropism, Pautrier microabscesses).","Immunophenotyping and T-cell receptor gene rearrangement (clonality) support it; stage with the TNMB system (skin/node/viscera/blood), peripheral smear/flow for Sezary cells in erythrodermic disease.","Withhold long-term topical/systemic steroids that can obscure the histology if CTCL is genuinely suspected - biopsy first."],"tx":["Stage-driven and usually chronic/incurable but often indolent (early-stage MF has near-normal life expectancy) - the goal is control and quality of life.","Skin-directed (early stage): topical corticosteroids, topical chemotherapy (mechlorethamine), topical bexarotene, and PHOTOTHERAPY (nbUVB for patches, PUVA for plaques); localized radiation for isolated tumors.","Advanced/refractory: total skin electron beam therapy, systemic retinoids (bexarotene), interferon, HDAC inhibitors, brentuximab vedotin, mogamulizumab, and extracorporeal photopheresis for Sezary syndrome; hematology-oncology co-management.","Aggressive antipruritic and supportive care - the itch of advanced disease is severe."],"pearls":["'Eczema' or 'psoriasis' that never quite responds for years, in a bathing-suit (sun-protected) distribution with poikiloderma, deserves a biopsy for mycosis fungoides.","Early CTCL histology is subtle - one negative biopsy doesn't exclude it; re-biopsy over time off steroids.","Most early-stage mycosis fungoides is indolent - frame it as a chronic skin-directed condition, not an immediate death sentence, while staging the minority with systemic disease."],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Primary Cutaneous Lymphoma Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mycosis Fungoides","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"Cutaneous Lymphoma Foundation","u":"https://www.clfoundation.org"}]},{"id":"derm-dermatofibroma-benign","name":"Dermatofibroma & Benign Tumors","sec":"derm","present":["Dermatofibroma morphology: a firm, small (few mm-1 cm) dermal papule/nodule, pink-brown, often on the LEGS of adults; the DIMPLE (Fitzpatrick) SIGN - lateral pinch causes central dimpling/retraction - is characteristic; frequently follows minor trauma/insect bite.","Other common benign tumors residents encounter: acrochordons (skin tags - soft pedunculated papules in folds/neck/axillae), cherry angiomas (small bright-red vascular papules, increase with age), sebaceous hyperplasia (yellowish umbilicated papules on the face), lipomas (soft mobile subcutaneous nodules).","Generally asymptomatic, stable or slowly growing, cosmetically or mechanically bothersome rather than dangerous."],"dx":["Clinical + dermoscopy; the dimple sign and firm feel identify dermatofibroma; biopsy if features are atypical (large, rapidly growing, irregular) to exclude dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans (DFSP) or amelanotic melanoma/other malignancy.","Multiple eruptive dermatofibromas can occasionally associate with immunosuppression/autoimmune disease.","Recognize each benign entity's classic look so as not to over-biopsy - but keep threshold low for any changing or ulcerating 'benign' lesion."],"tx":["Reassurance is usually sufficient - most need no treatment once benign nature is confirmed.","Remove for symptoms/cosmesis/diagnostic uncertainty: excision for dermatofibroma (counsel it may recur or leave a scar), snip/cautery/cryo for skin tags, electrodesiccation/laser for cherry angiomas and sebaceous hyperplasia, excision for symptomatic lipomas.","When in doubt about a firm growing nodule, excise and send for histology rather than assume benign (DFSP masquerades as a plaque/nodule and needs wide/Mohs excision)."],"pearls":["A firm leg nodule that dimples inward when pinched is a dermatofibroma - the dimple sign saves an unnecessary biopsy.","'Benign' is a diagnosis, not an assumption - a growing, ulcerating, or atypical nodule gets sent to pathology (DFSP and amelanotic melanoma hide among benign lumps).","Don't excise every skin tag and angioma reflexively - confident recognition spares the patient scars and cost."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Benign Skin Growths Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dermatofibroma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Benign Skin Tumors","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-seborrheic-keratosis","name":"Seborrheic Keratosis","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: well-demarcated, waxy or verrucous papules/plaques with a 'STUCK-ON' appearance, tan to dark brown-black, often with visible keratin plugs/horn cysts on the surface; they sit on top of the skin rather than infiltrating it.","Distribution: trunk, face, scalp; increase in number with age; usually multiple; dermatosis papulosa nigra is the small facial variant common in darker skin.","Benign; itch or irritation from friction is the usual complaint; the LESER-TRELAT sign - sudden eruption of many SKs - is a rare paraneoplastic clue (GI adenocarcinoma), used cautiously."],"dx":["Clinical + dermoscopy (milia-like cysts, comedo-like openings, cerebriform/brain-like surface, sharp demarcation) - a highly recognizable pattern.","The critical task is not mistaking a pigmented, irregular, or changing SK for MELANOMA - when a 'seborrheic keratosis' is atypical, asymmetric, or evolving, biopsy it; melanoma can arise adjacent to or mimic SK.","Irritated/inflamed SKs can look alarming - dermoscopy and, if any doubt, biopsy resolve it."],"tx":["Reassurance - no treatment needed for asymptomatic benign SKs.","Remove for irritation/cosmesis/diagnostic doubt: cryotherapy, curettage/shave (send for histology if any melanoma concern), electrodesiccation; topical hydrogen peroxide 40% is an FDA-approved option for raised SKs.","When uncertain whether a pigmented lesion is an SK or melanoma, biopsy rather than destroy - cryo/curettage without histology can obliterate a melanoma masquerading as SK."],"pearls":["'Stuck-on', waxy, with keratin plugs and milia-like cysts on dermoscopy is a seborrheic keratosis - benign and sitting on top of the skin.","The one that matters is the SK that isn't: a pigmented, irregular, or changing 'SK' gets a biopsy, because melanoma imitates it.","A sudden shower of new seborrheic keratoses (Leser-Trelat) is a soft paraneoplastic flag - consider an age-appropriate malignancy review, without over-alarming."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Seborrheic Keratosis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Seborrheic Keratosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Seborrheic Keratosis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-cysts","name":"Epidermoid & Pilar Cysts","sec":"derm","present":["Epidermoid cyst morphology: a dermal-to-subcutaneous, mobile, dome-shaped nodule with a central PUNCTUM (dilated pore); expresses malodorous cheesy keratin if ruptured/squeezed; common on face, trunk, neck, scrotum.","Pilar (trichilemmal) cyst: firm, smooth, mobile subcutaneous nodule usually on the SCALP, no punctum, often multiple and familial.","Both are benign; the common acute presentation is a RUPTURED/inflamed cyst - red, hot, tender, fluctuant - which is a foreign-body inflammatory reaction, frequently sterile rather than truly infected."],"dx":["Clinical; the punctum (epidermoid) or scalp location + smooth mobility (pilar) identifies them; distinguish an inflamed ruptured cyst (foreign-body reaction to keratin) from a true abscess and from lipoma (softer, no punctum).","Multiple cysts + other features (osteomas, desmoids, GI polyps) raise Gardner syndrome; recurrent/atypical cysts get histology after removal."],"tx":["Asymptomatic cyst: no treatment needed; reassure.","Acutely inflamed/ruptured cyst: incision and drainage relieves a fluctuant lesion; intralesional corticosteroid can settle inflammation; antibiotics only if genuine surrounding cellulitis/infection (much inflammation is sterile foreign-body reaction) - do NOT attempt definitive excision while acutely inflamed (higher recurrence/difficult plane).","Definitive treatment is complete excision of the cyst WALL when quiescent - leaving sac remnants causes recurrence."],"pearls":["The central punctum and cheesy keratin identify an epidermoid cyst; a smooth scalp nodule without a punctum is usually pilar.","An acutely inflamed cyst is often a sterile foreign-body reaction, not an infection - drain it, and reserve antibiotics for true cellulitis.","Excise the whole sac when it's calm - operate on a hot cyst and it recurs because remnants of wall are left behind."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Epidermoid / Pilar Cyst","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Cyst Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Epidermoid Cysts","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-keloids-scars","name":"Keloids & Hypertrophic Scars","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: firm, rubbery, shiny, often hyperpigmented raised scars at sites of prior injury (piercings, surgery, acne, burns); hypertrophic scars stay WITHIN the original wound borders and tend to regress over time, whereas KELOIDS EXTEND BEYOND the original wound margins and persist/grow.","Distribution: high-tension and predisposed sites - earlobes, shoulders/deltoid, upper chest/sternum, upper back, jawline; more common and severe in darker skin phototypes and with family history.","Symptoms: itch, tenderness, pain, and significant cosmetic/psychological burden."],"dx":["Clinical; the discriminator is whether the scar stays within (hypertrophic) or grows beyond (keloid) the original wound; onset and course (hypertrophic often early and improving; keloid delayed and progressive) help.","Rarely biopsy (can itself provoke a keloid); exclude other infiltrative lesions if atypical."],"tx":["First-line: intralesional CORTICOSTEROID injections (triamcinolone), often serial, +/- 5-fluorouracil; silicone gel sheets/gel and pressure therapy (pressure earrings after earlobe keloid excision) as adjuncts/maintenance.","Procedural: cryotherapy, pulsed-dye/laser for erythema, and surgical excision ALWAYS COMBINED with adjuvant therapy (intralesional steroid, and for high-risk keloids postoperative radiation) - excision alone recurs and often returns larger.","Prevention in predisposed patients is key: minimize elective procedures at keloid-prone sites, tension-reducing closure, early silicone/pressure on new wounds."],"pearls":["Beyond the border = keloid; within the border = hypertrophic scar - and that single distinction predicts the stubbornness.","Never excise a keloid and stop there - excision without adjuvant steroid/radiation invites a bigger recurrence.","In keloid-prone, darker-skinned patients, prevention matters: think twice before elective piercings/procedures on the chest, shoulders, and earlobes."],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Keloids / Scars Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Keloid / Hypertrophic Scar","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Keloids","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-stasis-dermatitis-ulcers","name":"Venous Stasis Dermatitis & Leg Ulcers","sec":"derm","present":["Stasis dermatitis morphology: erythematous, scaly, eczematous, often WEEPING patches on the LOWER LEGS (medial ankle/gaiter area) over a background of chronic venous changes - edema, HEMOSIDERIN (brown) staining, varicosities, and lipodermatosclerosis (indurated, 'inverted champagne bottle' leg).","Venous ulcer morphology: shallow, irregular, exudative ulcers at the MEDIAL MALLEOLUS/gaiter area with granulation base and surrounding stasis changes; usually only mildly painful (contrast arterial ulcers).","Bilateral, chronic, itchy > painful; the classic and consequential misdiagnosis is bilateral 'cellulitis' (see cellulitis entry)."],"dx":["Clinical; recognize the venous insufficiency backdrop (edema, hemosiderin, varicosities, lipodermatosclerosis); ASSESS ARTERIAL supply before compression - check pulses and ankle-brachial index (ABI) to exclude significant peripheral arterial disease (compression can harm an ischemic leg).","Differentiate ulcer types: venous (medial malleolus, shallow, mild pain, stasis changes) vs arterial (distal/toes/pressure points, punched-out, painful, cool/pulseless) vs neuropathic (plantar pressure points, diabetic); biopsy chronic non-healing ulcers to exclude malignancy (Marjolin) or vasculitis/pyoderma gangrenosum.","Patch-test recalcitrant stasis dermatitis - these patients readily sensitize to topical antibiotics/medicaments applied to the leg."],"tx":["The disease is venous hypertension - COMPRESSION therapy (once arterial supply confirmed adequate by ABI) is the cornerstone for both stasis dermatitis and venous ulcers; leg ELEVATION, exercise, weight management.","Stasis dermatitis: mid-potency topical corticosteroids for the inflamed eczema + emollients; treat superimposed infection if present; AVOID the common allergen medicaments (neomycin, bacitracin).","Venous ulcers: compression + appropriate moist wound dressings + debridement; treat infection when clinically present; refer for venous intervention (ablation) to reduce recurrence.","Do not reflexively treat bilateral stasis dermatitis with antibiotics - it is not cellulitis; treat the venous disease."],"pearls":["Bilateral, itchy, brown-stained lower legs with scaling is stasis dermatitis, not cellulitis - compression and topical steroids, not antibiotics.","Check the ABI/pulses before you compress - compression on an ischemic arterial leg can cause necrosis.","A leg ulcer that won't heal despite good care needs a biopsy - chronic ulcers can harbor squamous cell carcinoma or be pyoderma gangrenosum in disguise."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stasis Dermatitis / Venous Ulcer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Leg Ulcer / Stasis Dermatitis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Venous Insufficiency / Leg Ulcers","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-pressure-injuries","name":"Pressure Injuries (Staging & Management)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology by NPUAP/NPIAP stage: Stage 1 - intact skin with NON-blanchable erythema; Stage 2 - partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis (shallow open ulcer or intact/ruptured blister); Stage 3 - full-thickness loss with visible fat; Stage 4 - full-thickness with exposed muscle/tendon/bone; Unstageable - base obscured by slough/eschar; Deep Tissue Injury - persistent non-blanchable deep maroon/purple discoloration or blood-filled blister.","Distribution: over BONY PROMINENCES under sustained pressure - sacrum, heels, ischium, greater trochanter, occiput (and medical-device-related sites); in immobile, malnourished, incontinent, or neurologically impaired patients.","Preventable in large part; a marker of care quality and a source of pain, infection, and osteomyelitis."],"dx":["Stage by the deepest visible tissue (do NOT stage an eschar-covered wound - it's unstageable until debrided; deep tissue injury may evolve rapidly); assess size, depth, undermining/tunneling, exudate, odor, and surrounding skin.","Evaluate for complications: local infection/cellulitis, and underlying OSTEOMYELITIS in deep non-healing wounds (imaging/bone biopsy); assess nutrition, perfusion, and the whole patient (Braden risk score)."],"tx":["PREVENTION and offloading are the core: repositioning schedules, pressure-redistributing support surfaces, heel elevation, moisture/incontinence management, nutrition optimization (protein/calories), and treating the immobility driver.","Wound care by stage/moisture: keep clean and appropriately moist; DEBRIDE devitalized tissue (sharp, enzymatic, autolytic) except stable dry heel eschar in an ischemic limb (leave intact); dressings matched to exudate; manage pain.","Treat infection when clinically present (not routine swab-guided antibiotics for colonized wounds); surgical consultation for large Stage 3/4 wounds (flap coverage) and for suspected osteomyelitis; multidisciplinary wound team."],"pearls":["Stage by the deepest tissue you can SEE - an eschar-covered wound is 'unstageable' until debrided, and deep tissue injury can look deceptively minor at the surface.","The treatment is offloading the pressure - the best dressing fails if the patient keeps lying on the wound.","Stable, dry heel eschar in a poorly perfused limb is left alone - debriding it can create a bigger, non-healing wound."],"refs":[{"t":"NPIAP Pressure Injury Staging & Guidelines","u":"https://npiap.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pressure Injury / Ulcer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pressure Sores","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-pyoderma-gangrenosum","name":"Pyoderma Gangrenosum","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: begins as a tender pustule/nodule that rapidly breaks down into a painful ULCER with a characteristic UNDERMINED, ragged, VIOLACEOUS (dusky purple) overhanging border and a purulent/necrotic base; PATHERGY - new or worsening lesions at sites of minor trauma (including surgery/debridement).","Distribution: classically the LOWER LEGS (pretibial), also peristomal and post-surgical sites; disproportionate PAIN.","Associated with systemic disease in ~50%: INFLAMMATORY BOWEL DISEASE, inflammatory arthritis, and hematologic disorders/malignancy (myelodysplasia, IgA gammopathy)."],"dx":["A DIAGNOSIS OF EXCLUSION - there is no confirmatory test; exclude infection (cultures, special stains), vasculitis, vasculopathy (antiphospholipid), venous/arterial ulcers, and malignancy; biopsy the EDGE (shows neutrophilic infiltrate) mainly to exclude mimics, accepting that biopsy itself can trigger pathergy.","Recognize the trap: PG is frequently MISDIAGNOSED as a necrotic infection or wound and taken to surgical debridement, which WORSENS it via pathergy - the rapidly enlarging, exquisitely painful, undermined violaceous ulcer that grows after every debridement is the clue.","Once diagnosed, screen for associated IBD, arthritis, and hematologic disease."],"tx":["Anti-inflammatory/immunosuppressive, NOT surgical: systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS and/or CYCLOSPORINE are first-line for rapidly progressive disease; localized disease may respond to potent topical/intralesional steroids or topical tacrolimus.","Refractory/associated disease: biologics - TNF inhibitors (infliximab, especially with IBD) and others; steroid-sparing agents for maintenance.","AVOID aggressive surgical debridement (pathergy); gentle wound care, pain control, treat the associated systemic disease; if surgery is unavoidable, cover with immunosuppression."],"pearls":["A rapidly enlarging, agonizingly painful ulcer with a dusky, undermined, violaceous border that gets WORSE after every debridement is pyoderma gangrenosum - stop cutting and start immunosuppressing.","Pathergy is the warning: PG punishes trauma, so the instinct to debride a 'necrotic infection' makes it explode.","Find the company it keeps - screen for inflammatory bowel disease, arthritis, and hematologic disease in every PG patient."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pyoderma Gangrenosum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Pyoderma Gangrenosum Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Pyoderma Gangrenosum","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-erythema-nodosum","name":"Erythema Nodosum","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: crops of tender, warm, ERYTHEMATOUS-to-violaceous SUBCUTANEOUS NODULES/plaques, poorly demarcated (felt more than sharply seen), that do NOT ulcerate and heal like a resolving bruise (evolving through bruise-like color changes - 'contusiform').","Distribution: bilateral, symmetric, over the EXTENSOR SHINS (pretibial); occasionally thighs/forearms.","A reactive septal PANNICULITIS; often with fever, arthralgias, malaise; more common in young women; acute and self-limited (weeks)."],"dx":["Clinical; it is a REACTION PATTERN, so the work is finding the trigger: streptococcal infection (common, check ASO/throat), sarcoidosis (chest imaging - Lofgren syndrome = EN + hilar adenopathy + arthritis), drugs (OCPs, sulfonamides), IBD, pregnancy, TB and other infections; a basic workup - CBC, ESR/CRP, ASO, chest X-ray, and targeted tests.","Biopsy (deep, incisional to capture subcutis) only if atypical - shows septal panniculitis without vasculitis; distinguish from other panniculitides and from cellulitis/nodular vasculitis."],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause (antibiotics for strep, address IBD, stop culprit drug) - EN itself is self-limited.","Symptomatic: NSAIDs, leg elevation, rest, compression; potassium iodide or a short steroid course for severe/persistent disease ONCE infection (especially TB) is excluded.","Reassure: most resolves over 3-6 weeks without scarring or ulceration."],"pearls":["Tender bruise-like nodules on both shins that never ulcerate is erythema nodosum - a skin sign pointing elsewhere, so hunt the trigger (strep, sarcoid, IBD, drugs, TB).","Get a chest X-ray: Lofgren syndrome (EN + hilar adenopathy + arthritis) is an acute, good-prognosis sarcoidosis you don't want to miss.","Exclude TB before reaching for systemic steroids or potassium iodide."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Erythema Nodosum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Erythema Nodosum Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Erythema Nodosum","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-cutaneous-vasculitis","name":"Cutaneous Vasculitis (Palpable Purpura)","sec":"derm","present":["Morphology: PALPABLE PURPURA - non-blanching, raised purpuric papules (you can feel them, distinguishing them from flat, non-inflammatory purpura); may progress to vesicles, pustules, necrosis, or shallow ulcers in severe disease.","Distribution: DEPENDENT and pressure areas - lower legs, ankles, buttocks, or beneath tight clothing; symmetric crops.","Small-vessel leukocytoclastic vasculitis is the prototype; ranges from skin-limited to a cutaneous sign of systemic vasculitis with organ involvement."],"dx":["Confirm with skin biopsy: lesional (~24-48 h old) for H&E (leukocytoclastic vasculitis - neutrophilic vessel-wall damage) PLUS perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (IgA deposits indicate IgA vasculitis/Henoch-Schonlein, which carries renal risk).","Then determine skin-limited vs systemic and find the cause: infections, DRUGS, connective tissue disease, IgA vasculitis, ANCA-associated vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia (check hepatitis C), and malignancy; workup - CBC, renal function/URINALYSIS (crucial for renal involvement), LFTs, ANA, ANCA, complement, cryoglobulins, hepatitis serologies, and age-appropriate cancer screen when indicated.","Assess for systemic involvement (renal, GI, pulmonary, neuro) - urinalysis for every patient (glomerulonephritis is the silent, serious complication)."],"tx":["Identify and remove/treat the trigger (stop the culprit drug, treat infection, treat hepatitis C in cryoglobulinemic vasculitis) - many skin-limited cases resolve with this alone.","Skin-limited: supportive - leg elevation, compression, NSAIDs, rest; topical/short systemic steroids for symptomatic disease; colchicine or dapsone for chronic/recurrent cutaneous vasculitis.","Systemic vasculitis with organ involvement: systemic immunosuppression (corticosteroids + steroid-sparing/immunomodulators, e.g., rituximab in ANCA-associated) - co-manage with rheumatology/nephrology.","Follow urinalysis/renal function over time even in apparently skin-limited IgA vasculitis - nephritis can appear later."],"pearls":["If you can FEEL the purpura, it's vasculitis until proven otherwise - flat purpura is a clotting/platelet or non-inflammatory problem, palpable purpura is inflamed vessels.","Do a urinalysis on every palpable-purpura patient - the kidney is where small-vessel vasculitis does its quiet damage.","Palpable purpura is a reaction pattern - biopsy confirms it, but your job is finding the drug, infection, hepatitis C, or systemic vasculitis behind it."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Leukocytoclastic Vasculitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Cutaneous Vasculitis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Vasculitis","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-cutaneous-lupus","name":"Cutaneous Lupus (Discoid, Subacute)","sec":"derm","present":["Discoid lupus (DLE) morphology: sharply demarcated, coin-shaped erythematous plaques with adherent SCALE, follicular plugging, and - critically - central ATROPHY, SCARRING, and dyspigmentation (hyper/hypo); on the face, scalp (SCARRING alopecia), and ears; heals with permanent scarring.","Subacute cutaneous lupus (SCLE) morphology: photosensitive, non-scarring annular-polycyclic or papulosquamous (psoriasiform) plaques on sun-exposed upper trunk/arms/neck; strongly PHOTOSENSITIVE, anti-Ro/SSA-associated, often DRUG-induced.","Acute cutaneous lupus: the transient malar 'butterfly' rash sparing the nasolabial folds, tracking with systemic disease."],"dx":["Biopsy (interface dermatitis) + direct immunofluorescence (lupus band) support it; correlate with serologies - ANA, anti-dsDNA/anti-Sm (systemic), anti-Ro/SSA (SCLE and neonatal lupus risk in pregnancy).","Stratify systemic risk: DLE is often skin-limited (a minority progress to SLE), SCLE has moderate SLE association; screen ALL cutaneous lupus for systemic disease (history, exam, CBC, renal function, urinalysis, complements) and review drugs in SCLE (hydrochlorothiazide, terbinafine, PPIs, TNF inhibitors, antifungals).","Distinguish from rosacea/seborrheic dermatitis (malar rash sparing vs involving nasolabial folds), psoriasis, and photodermatoses."],"tx":["PHOTOPROTECTION is fundamental (UV drives all forms) - broad-spectrum SPF, protective clothing, avoid sun; smoking cessation (smoking worsens disease and blunts antimalarial response).","First-line systemic: HYDROXYCHLOROQUINE (antimalarial) - baseline and periodic ophthalmologic screening for retinopathy; topical/intralesional corticosteroids and topical calcineurin inhibitors for active lesions.","Refractory: additional antimalarials (quinacrine), methotrexate, mycophenolate, thalidomide/lenalidomide, or belimumab; stop culprit drugs in drug-induced SCLE.","Treat DLE EARLY and aggressively - scarring alopecia and dyspigmentation are permanent; co-manage systemic disease with rheumatology."],"pearls":["Discoid lupus SCARS - early treatment prevents permanent alopecia and disfiguring pigment change, so don't watch it.","A malar rash that SPARES the nasolabial folds points to lupus; one that INVOLVES them is more likely rosacea or seborrheic dermatitis.","New photosensitive annular/psoriasiform plaques (SCLE) should prompt a drug review - hydrochlorothiazide, terbinafine, and others induce it."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Cutaneous Lupus Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"Lupus Foundation of America","u":"https://www.lupus.org"}]},{"id":"derm-dermatomyositis","name":"Dermatomyositis (Skin Signs)","sec":"derm","present":["Pathognomonic morphology: GOTTRON PAPULES - violaceous flat-topped papules/plaques over the KNUCKLES (MCP/IP joints, extensor); and the HELIOTROPE rash - violaceous periorbital erythema with edema of the eyelids.","Other signs: SHAWL sign (photodistributed erythema over upper back/shoulders) and V-sign (anterior neck/chest); ragged cuticles with dilated nailfold capillaries and periungual erythema; scalp involvement; 'mechanic's hands'; poikiloderma; often intense photosensitivity and pruritus.","Systemic: symmetric PROXIMAL muscle weakness (difficulty rising from chairs, combing hair) - though 'amyopathic' dermatomyositis has skin disease without clinical myositis."],"dx":["Recognize the specific skin signs (Gottron papules over the knuckles distinguish DM from lupus, which favors the skin BETWEEN the knuckles); confirm myositis with muscle enzymes (CK, aldolase), EMG, MRI, and/or muscle biopsy; myositis-specific antibodies (e.g., anti-Mi-2, anti-MDA5 - the last associated with amyopathic disease + rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease, anti-TIF1-gamma - malignancy-associated) refine phenotype and risk.","CRITICAL: dermatomyositis is a PARANEOPLASTIC disease in adults - perform an AGE-APPROPRIATE MALIGNANCY WORKUP (the risk is elevated especially around diagnosis; ovarian, lung, GI, nasopharyngeal, breast, etc.); screen for interstitial lung disease (PFTs, chest imaging), especially with anti-MDA5.","Distinguish from lupus and other photodermatoses/psoriasis by the distribution and specific signs."],"tx":["Skin: rigorous PHOTOPROTECTION (UV-driven), topical corticosteroids/calcineurin inhibitors, antipruritics; antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine) though cutaneous DM can be more refractory than lupus.","Systemic myositis: systemic corticosteroids + steroid-sparing immunosuppressants (methotrexate, mycophenolate, azathioprine, IVIG - IVIG has strong evidence); rituximab and others for refractory disease; aggressive treatment of interstitial lung disease with rheumatology/pulmonology.","Treat/monitor the associated malignancy and repeat cancer surveillance over the following years; multidisciplinary care."],"pearls":["Violaceous papules ON the knuckles (Gottron) + heliotrope eyelids is dermatomyositis - and lupus, by contrast, spares the knuckles and hits between them.","Adult dermatomyositis is paraneoplastic until proven otherwise - do the age-appropriate cancer hunt at diagnosis and keep looking.","Amyopathic dermatomyositis (skin without weakness), especially anti-MDA5 positive, can hide rapidly progressive lung disease - screen the lungs even when the muscles are fine."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Dermatomyositis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Dermatomyositis Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"NIH/NIAMS: Dermatomyositis","u":"https://www.niams.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-skin-signs-systemic","name":"Skin Signs of Systemic Disease","sec":"derm","present":["The skin as a window: ACANTHOSIS NIGRICANS - velvety, hyperpigmented thickening of flexural skin (neck, axillae) signaling insulin resistance/diabetes (or, if sudden and extensive, paraneoplastic - GI adenocarcinoma).","XANTHOMAS - yellow papules/plaques of lipid deposition (eruptive on extensors = high triglycerides; tendinous/xanthelasma = hypercholesterolemia/familial); NECROBIOSIS LIPOIDICA - atrophic yellow-brown shins in diabetes; pretibial myxedema and other thyroid skin signs; jaundice, spider angiomas/palmar erythema (liver); pyoderma gangrenosum/erythema nodosum (IBD).","GENERALIZED PRURITUS without a primary rash can signal systemic disease - cholestasis, uremia/CKD, thyroid disease, iron deficiency, polycythemia vera (aquagenic itch), and lymphoma/malignancy."],"dx":["Use the sign to direct the workup: acanthosis nigricans -> screen for diabetes/insulin resistance (and, if abrupt/florid with weight loss, malignancy); xanthomas -> fasting lipid panel and familial hyperlipidemia evaluation; new necrobiosis lipoidica -> glucose.","Generalized pruritus without rash gets a systemic screen: CBC (polycythemia, eosinophilia), LFTs/cholestasis markers, renal function, TSH, ferritin/iron studies, glucose, HIV, and age-appropriate malignancy/lymphoma evaluation (LDH, imaging as indicated) when persistent and unexplained.","Biopsy specific lesions (xanthoma, necrobiosis) to confirm when the diagnosis or systemic implication is uncertain."],"tx":["Treat the UNDERLYING systemic disease - the skin sign improves as the driver is controlled (glycemic control for acanthosis/necrobiosis, lipid-lowering for xanthomas, treating cholestasis/uremia for itch).","Symptomatic skin care: emollients and topical measures for necrobiosis; targeted antipruritic therapy while chasing the cause (emollients, topical steroids, and cause-specific agents - e.g., cholestyramine/rifampin/opioid antagonists for cholestatic itch, gabapentin/UVB for uremic itch).","Refer to the relevant specialty (endocrinology, hepatology, hematology, oncology) once the systemic association is identified; re-screen when a sign is abrupt or refractory (paraneoplastic possibilities)."],"pearls":["Velvety dark flexural skin (acanthosis nigricans) is usually insulin resistance - but if it erupts suddenly and floridly with weight loss, hunt for an internal (often gastric) cancer.","Generalized itch with NO rash is a systemic workup, not just a moisturizer - liver, kidney, thyroid, iron, blood counts, and lymphoma.","Read the skin as a report on the inside: xanthomas mean lipids, necrobiosis and acanthosis mean glucose, jaundice and spiders mean liver - the sign tells you which test to order."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cutaneous Manifestations of Systemic Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Skin Signs of Systemic Disease Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Itching / Pruritus","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-photosensitivity-pmle","name":"Photosensitivity & Polymorphous Light Eruption","sec":"derm","present":["Polymorphous light eruption (PMLE) morphology: pruritic erythematous papules, papulovesicles, or plaques (monomorphic within a given patient despite the name) appearing HOURS TO DAYS after sun exposure on intermittently sun-exposed skin (chest V, arms, dorsal hands) - classically in SPRING/early summer, improving as the season 'hardens' the skin.","Broader photosensitivity presentations: exaggerated sunburn in a photodistribution, sparing shaded areas (under chin, behind ears, covered skin) - the SHARP cutoff at clothing/sun lines is the tell.","Photosensitivity is a category: drug/chemical photosensitivity (phototoxic - exaggerated sunburn; photoallergic - eczematous), photoaggravated dermatoses (lupus, dermatomyositis), and metabolic (porphyria)."],"dx":["Recognize the PHOTODISTRIBUTION (sun-exposed, sharp cutoffs, sparing shadowed sites) as the unifying clue; timing and morphology narrow it - PMLE (delayed papules, seasonal), phototoxic drug reaction (immediate exaggerated burn), photoallergic (delayed eczematous).","Exclude the dangerous mimics that are photoaggravated: LUPUS and DERMATOMYOSITIS (check ANA, anti-Ro, specific signs) and PORPHYRIA (porphyria cutanea tarda - blistering/fragility on dorsal hands, check porphyrins) - PMLE is a diagnosis that shouldn't be made until connective tissue disease and porphyria are considered.","Review photosensitizing DRUGS (doxycycline, thiazides, fluoroquinolones, NSAIDs, voriconazole, amiodarone) and topical/chemical agents; phototesting in specialized settings for unclear cases."],"tx":["PHOTOPROTECTION is the cornerstone for all: broad-spectrum (UVA-covering) high-SPF sunscreen, protective clothing/hats, sun avoidance at peak hours; identify and stop photosensitizing drugs/agents.","PMLE: prevention with photoprotection and, for prone patients, spring 'hardening' with graduated sun exposure or prophylactic phototherapy (nbUVB/PUVA) before high-exposure season; topical steroids and antihistamines for active flares; hydroxychloroquine for severe recurrent disease.","Treat photoaggravated systemic disease (lupus/DM) on its own terms; manage porphyria specifically.","Educate: the eruption is UV-driven and largely preventable with disciplined sun protection."],"pearls":["A rash that maps to sun exposure with sharp cutoffs at clothing lines and spares the shaded chin/behind-ears is photodistributed - the pattern is the diagnosis.","Don't call it PMLE until you've considered lupus, dermatomyositis, and porphyria - photosensitivity can be the first sign of connective tissue or metabolic disease.","Always review the medication list for photosensitizers - doxycycline, thiazides, and fluoroquinolones turn ordinary sun into an exaggerated burn."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Polymorphous Light Eruption / Photosensitivity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Photosensitivity / Sun Protection Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Sun Sensitivity / Photodermatoses","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-burns-sunburn","name":"Burns & Sunburn (Dermatology Management)","sec":"derm","present":["Burn depth by morphology: SUPERFICIAL (first-degree) - erythema, painful, dry, no blisters (typical sunburn), blanches; SUPERFICIAL PARTIAL-THICKNESS (second) - moist, red, BLISTERS, very painful, blanches; DEEP PARTIAL-THICKNESS - drier, mottled red/white, less sensate; FULL-THICKNESS (third) - leathery, white/charred, INSENSATE, non-blanching.","Sunburn: delayed (hours) photodistributed erythema, tenderness, edema, sometimes blistering; severe/extensive sunburn can cause systemic symptoms; a marker of significant UV damage and future skin-cancer risk.","Assess extent (body surface area) and the whole patient for larger/deeper burns (airway, fluids)."],"dx":["Estimate depth (appearance, blanching, sensation, blistering) and BSA (rule of nines/palm method) - these drive disposition; recognize when a burn EXCEEDS outpatient care (large BSA, full-thickness, circumferential, face/hands/genitals/joints, electrical/chemical, inhalation, extremes of age) and needs a BURN CENTER (ABA referral criteria).","For sunburn, look for photosensitizing drugs and consider phototoxic reactions if severity is out of proportion to exposure.","Consider non-accidental injury in pediatric burns with suspicious patterns."],"tx":["Sunburn/superficial burns: cool compresses, emollients/aloe, NSAIDs for pain/inflammation, hydration; topical steroids give modest relief; oral steroids not routinely helpful; the real treatment is prevention (photoprotection education, skin-cancer risk counseling).","Superficial partial-thickness burns (outpatient): cool running water for acute thermal injury, gentle cleansing, appropriate non-adherent moist dressings, blister management (leave small intact blisters; debride large/tense per practice), topical antimicrobials as indicated, pain control, tetanus update.","Deeper/large burns: fluid resuscitation, refer to burn center, surgical management (excision/grafting) - beyond dermatology; avoid routine prophylactic systemic antibiotics.","Follow burns for scarring/contracture and pigment change; counsel long-term photoprotection over healed/burned skin."],"pearls":["Depth and body-surface-area drive the decision: blistering, insensate, circumferential, or large burns and those on face/hands/genitals go to a burn center, not the clinic.","Leathery, white, painless skin is full-thickness - the absence of pain is a bad sign, not a good one.","Every bad sunburn is a skin-cancer risk conversation - treat the burn, then teach the photoprotection that prevents the next one and the melanoma after that."],"refs":[{"t":"American Burn Association Referral Criteria","u":"https://ameriburn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Burns / Sunburn","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Burns / Sunburn","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-nail-disorders","name":"Nail Disorders (Onychomycosis, Psoriatic Nails, Melanonychia)","sec":"derm","present":["Onychomycosis morphology: distal-lateral subungual thickening, yellow-brown discoloration, subungual hyperkeratotic debris, onycholysis (nail-bed separation); usually toenails, often with coexisting tinea pedis.","Psoriatic nail morphology: PITTING (irregular pits), 'OIL-DROP'/salmon patches, subungual hyperkeratosis, onycholysis, and splinter hemorrhages; a marker for coexisting/future psoriatic arthritis.","Melanonychia: a longitudinal pigmented (brown-black) band in the nail - most are benign (racial/ethnic, drug, trauma), but the task is to catch subungual MELANOMA (see red flags)."],"dx":["CONFIRM onychomycosis before months of systemic antifungals - KOH, PAS histology of nail clippings, or culture/PCR (up to half of dystrophic nails are NOT fungal - psoriasis, trauma, lichen planus mimic it); treating the wrong thing wastes months and drug exposure.","Melanonychia RED FLAGS (ABCDEF - suggest biopsy of the nail MATRIX): single digit, Age (adult) with Band >3 mm/irregular/variegated color, Change/rapid growth, and especially HUTCHINSON SIGN (pigment onto the proximal/lateral nail fold) - refer/biopsy to exclude subungual melanoma (which is deadly and frequently missed as a 'bruise' or 'fungus').","Psoriatic nails: examine skin/scalp and ASK ABOUT JOINTS (nail psoriasis strongly associates with psoriatic arthritis)."],"tx":["Onychomycosis (confirmed): oral terbinafine (most effective; check LFTs, drug interactions) or itraconazole; topical efinaconazole/tavaborole/ciclopirox for mild disease or when oral is contraindicated; set expectations - toenails grow out over ~12 months and cure is imperfect/recurrent.","Psoriatic nails: high-potency topical steroids/vitamin D to the nail folds, intralesional steroid for matrix disease; systemic/biologic therapy for psoriasis often improves nails and treats associated arthritis.","Melanonychia: benign-appearing, stable, multiple-digit or clearly drug/trauma-related bands can be observed; anything with red flags goes for nail-matrix biopsy - do not dismiss a single evolving pigmented band as a bruise."],"pearls":["Prove it's fungus before committing to months of oral antifungals - up to half of dystrophic nails are psoriasis, lichen planus, or trauma, not onychomycosis.","A single pigmented nail band that's wide, irregular, changing, or spreads onto the nail fold (Hutchinson sign) is subungual melanoma until a matrix biopsy says otherwise.","Nail pits and oil drops should make you examine the skin and ask about the joints - nail psoriasis flags psoriatic arthritis."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Onychomycosis / Nail Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Nail Disorders Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Nail Diseases","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"derm-pediatric-exanthems","name":"Pediatric Exanthems","sec":"derm","present":["Measles (rubeola): prodrome of high fever + the 3 C's (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) + KOPLIK SPOTS (white papules on buccal mucosa), then a MORBILLIFORM rash spreading CEPHALOCAUDAD from the hairline/face downward; the sick, high-fever child - a reportable, vaccine-preventable, resurgent disease.","Fifth disease (erythema infectiosum, parvovirus B19): 'SLAPPED CHEEK' bright facial erythema then a lacy reticular rash on the extremities; child often well by rash onset.","Hand-foot-mouth (coxsackievirus): oral ulcers/vesicles + vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks; Roseola (HHV-6): high fever for ~3 days that BREAKS, THEN a rose-pink truncal rash appears as the child improves; scarlet fever (GAS): SANDPAPER rough erythema, perioral pallor, strawberry tongue, after strep pharyngitis."],"dx":["Diagnose largely by pattern, prodrome, and timing (rash-fever relationship): fever-then-rash-as-fever-breaks (roseola), slapped cheek (fifth), Koplik + 3 C's + cephalocaudad spread (measles), sandpaper + strawberry tongue (scarlet).","Confirm/act where it matters: MEASLES - notify public health, serology/PCR, because of contagion and complications; scarlet fever - test/treat GAS; parvovirus - counsel pregnant contacts (fetal risk/hydrops) and aplastic crisis risk in hemoglobinopathies/immunocompromised.","Distinguish viral exanthems from drug eruptions, Kawasaki disease (persistent fever >=5 days + conjunctivitis, mucositis, extremity changes, adenopathy - a can't-miss needing cardiology/IVIG), and other serious rashes (meningococcemia - petechiae/purpura + ill child)."],"tx":["Most are SUPPORTIVE and self-limited (fifth disease, hand-foot-mouth, roseola): antipyretics, hydration, comfort; counsel on course and contagion/return-to-school.","Specific actions: measles - supportive care + VITAMIN A, isolation, public-health reporting, post-exposure prophylaxis (MMR/immunoglobulin) for contacts; scarlet fever - antibiotics for GAS.","Counsel special populations: parvovirus exposure in pregnancy, sickle cell (aplastic crisis), and immunocompromised; recognize and urgently refer Kawasaki disease and any ill child with petechiae/purpura.","Reinforce VACCINATION (MMR) - measles is preventable and its resurgence is a vaccination-gap disease."],"pearls":["Let the fever-rash timing guide you: rash appearing AS the fever breaks is roseola; a slapped cheek is fifth disease; Koplik spots with the 3 C's is measles.","Fever >=5 days with red eyes, red lips/strawberry tongue, and extremity changes is Kawasaki until excluded - it needs IVIG to protect the coronary arteries, not reassurance.","Measles is back where vaccination lapses - recognize it, isolate, report, give vitamin A, and use the moment to close the immunization gap."],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Measles / Vaccine-Preventable Exanthems","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"AAP Red Book: Pediatric Infectious Diseases","u":"https://redbook.solutions.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Viral Exanthems","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"}]},{"id":"derm-procedures","name":"Dermatologic Procedures (Biopsy Techniques, Cryotherapy, Excisions)","sec":"derm","present":["The core resident procedural toolkit: SHAVE biopsy (raised/epidermal lesions, superficial BCC, SK), PUNCH biopsy (full-thickness cylinder for inflammatory dermatoses and to sample dermis), EXCISIONAL/incisional biopsy (suspected MELANOMA - full depth, or large lesions), CRYOTHERAPY (AKs, warts, SKs), curettage & electrodesiccation, and elliptical EXCISION with layered closure.","Indication drives technique: never shave a suspected melanoma (need Breslow depth); punch or excise for depth; sample the active edge of annular/inflammatory lesions; deep incisional wedge for panniculitis (erythema nodosum)."],"dx":["Match the tool to the question: diagnostic sampling (which biopsy captures the relevant layer?) vs therapeutic removal (margins?); mark the lesion and photograph/site-document BEFORE anesthetizing (wrong-site surgery is a real, preventable error - confirm site with the patient).","Screen pre-procedure: anticoagulants/antiplatelets (usually CONTINUE for most skin procedures - bleeding is controllable and thrombotic risk of stopping is worse), anesthetic allergy, pacemaker/ICD (electrocautery precautions), infection/implant risk, and pregnancy (epinephrine generally fine)."],"tx":["Anesthesia: buffered lidocaine with epinephrine (hemostasis, longer duration; safe on digits in current evidence with appropriate use), respecting maximum dosing (~7 mg/kg with epinephrine); slow injection, small needle, warm buffered solution reduce pain.","Choose the definitive procedure by diagnosis: Mohs for high-risk facial/recurrent NMSC; wide local excision with depth-based margins for melanoma; simple excision or destruction for benign/low-risk lesions."],"tech":["Shave: stabilize skin, use a flexible blade/DermaBlade parallel to the surface at the chosen depth; punch: stretch skin PERPENDICULAR to relaxed skin tension lines so the resulting circle becomes an ellipse for easier closure, rotate the punch to hub, lift and cut the base, close with a single suture; excision: design an ellipse ~3:1 length:width along tension lines with appropriate margins, undermine to reduce tension, close in layers (buried dermal + epidermal).","Cryotherapy: liquid nitrogen freeze-thaw cycles with a small margin, freeze time to lesion type (longer/harder for thicker lesions) - counsel on blistering, hypopigmentation (caution in darker skin), and pain.","Hemostasis: pressure, electrocautery, aluminum chloride for superficial sites; ensure specimen is oriented/labeled and sent in formalin (fresh/special media only for specific tests)."],"after":["Wound care: petrolatum + non-adherent dressing (NOT topical antibiotics routinely - neomycin/bacitracin are common allergens and no better than petrolatum); keep moist, clean daily; counsel on expected serosanguinous drainage vs infection signs.","Suture removal timing by site (face ~5-7 days, trunk/extremities ~10-14, back/high-tension longer); educate on scar care, sun protection over the scar (prevents hyperpigmentation), and activity/tension restrictions.","CLOSE THE LOOP on pathology: every biopsy needs a tracked result and a plan communicated to the patient - a melanoma lost to follow-up is the catastrophic procedural error; document margins and arrange re-excision if indicated."],"pearls":["Never shave a lesion you're worried is melanoma - you destroy the Breslow depth that dictates margins, sentinel node, and prognosis; punch or excise for full thickness.","Stretch the skin perpendicular to tension lines before a punch, and the round defect becomes an ellipse that closes clean.","The procedure isn't finished when the wound is closed - it's finished when the pathology result is tracked, acted on, and communicated; petrolatum beats antibiotic ointment for aftercare."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Skin Biopsy / Cryotherapy / Excision","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/"},{"t":"AAD Dermatologic Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Skin Biopsy","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"ent-emergency-airway","name":"Emergency Airway Management & Surgical Airway","sec":"ent","present":["The airway emergency is the defining ENT crisis: impending or actual obstruction from angioedema, deep neck/supraglottic INFECTION, TRAUMA, tumor, foreign body, bilateral vocal fold paralysis, or bleeding","Warning signs of a threatened airway: STRIDOR (inspiratory - supraglottic/glottic; biphasic - subglottic/tracheal), voice change/muffling, drooling, tripod positioning, accessory muscle use, and hypoxia/agitation (a LATE, ominous sign)","The feared scenario is 'CAN'T INTUBATE, CAN'T OXYGENATE' (CICO), which mandates an emergency surgical airway"],"dx":["Rapid assessment of the degree and LEVEL of obstruction and the likelihood of a DIFFICULT airway; flexible laryngoscopy (when safe) localizes the pathology","Anticipate difficulty (infection, edema, trauma, tumor, prior surgery/radiation) and plan BEFORE losing the airway; do not agitate a marginal pediatric airway (epiglottitis)","Have a failed-airway plan and equipment ready (difficult airway algorithm)"],"tx":["Secure the airway by the safest route for the situation: for an anticipated difficult airway, an AWAKE (often fiberoptic) intubation preserves spontaneous breathing; a controlled setting (OR) is safest for critical/pediatric airways ('double setup' with surgical airway ready)","SURGICAL AIRWAY when intubation fails or is impossible: CRICOTHYROTOMY is the emergency procedure of choice in ADULTS (fast, through the cricothyroid membrane); in young CHILDREN use NEEDLE cricothyrotomy (cricothyrotomy is contraindicated in small children); TRACHEOSTOMY is the controlled/elective surgical airway","Treat the underlying cause (steroids/epinephrine for angioedema, antibiotics/drainage for infection) in parallel"],"tech":["Awake fiberoptic intubation for the anticipated difficult airway; cricothyrotomy (emergency surgical airway in adults); tracheostomy (controlled surgical airway); needle cricothyrotomy with jet oxygenation as a temporizing measure in children","Double-setup approach in the OR for critical airways","Convert an emergency cricothyrotomy to a formal tracheostomy when stable"],"after":["Airway monitoring in a higher-acuity setting; TRACHEOSTOMY care (suctioning, humidification, tube changes) and a plan for eventual decannulation; convert cricothyrotomy to tracheostomy","Treat and reassess the underlying cause; manage complications (bleeding, tube dislodgement - a dislodged fresh trach is an emergency)","Communication and swallowing support for the tracheostomized patient"],"pearls":["Plan the airway BEFORE you lose it - awake fiberoptic intubation preserves breathing when difficulty is anticipated, and a 'double setup' keeps a surgical airway ready","Cricothyrotomy is the emergency surgical airway in adults; in small children it is contraindicated - use a needle cricothyrotomy","Hypoxia and agitation are late signs of airway obstruction - act on stridor, drooling, and voice change first","Never heavily sedate or lie flat a patient with a marginal airway (e.g., epiglottitis) - secure it in a controlled setting"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / Difficult Airway Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Surgical Airway / Cricothyrotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537350/"},{"t":"ASA Difficult Airway Algorithm","u":"https://www.asahq.org"}]},{"id":"ent-epistaxis","name":"Epistaxis","sec":"ent","present":["Nosebleed - extremely common; the critical distinction is ANTERIOR (from Kiesselbach's plexus / Little's area on the anterior septum - the vast majority, usually manageable) vs POSTERIOR (from the sphenopalatine artery region - less common but heavier, more likely in older/hypertensive/anticoagulated patients, with airway and hemodynamic risk)","Contributing factors: digital trauma, dry air, nasal steroid/oxygen use, HYPERTENSION, ANTICOAGULATION/antiplatelets, coagulopathy, hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT), and tumors (juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma in an adolescent male)","Posterior bleeds can pool posteriorly and threaten the airway"],"dx":["Assess hemodynamics and airway first in heavy bleeds; localize the source with good light, suction, and a decongestant/anesthetic spray (nasal endoscopy for posterior/refractory bleeds)","Review anticoagulation/antiplatelet use and coagulation status; consider a tumor with unilateral recurrent bleeding (biopsy JNA is dangerous - image first)","Distinguish anterior from posterior (drives treatment)"],"tx":["FIRST AID: firm continuous compression of the anterior nose (lean forward) for 10-15 minutes +/- topical vasoconstrictor; correct coagulopathy/hold anticoagulation as appropriate","ANTERIOR source: chemical (silver nitrate) or electro-CAUTERY of the visualized vessel, or absorbable/anterior PACKING; POSTERIOR source: posterior packing/balloon (ADMIT with airway monitoring), and, if refractory, endoscopic SPHENOPALATINE ARTERY LIGATION or embolization","Manage blood pressure and reversible factors"],"tech":["Nasal cautery (silver nitrate/electrocautery), anterior nasal packing, posterior packing/balloon catheter; endoscopic sphenopalatine (+/- anterior ethmoid) artery LIGATION or arterial EMBOLIZATION for refractory posterior bleeds","Endoscopic localization/control","Address the underlying lesion (tumor, HHT) definitively"],"after":["Packing removal at an interval (and antibiotics per practice for prolonged packing - toxic shock caution); monitor for rebleeding and, with posterior packing, airway/hypoxia","Address recurrence and the cause (humidification, blood pressure, anticoagulation review, HHT/tumor management)","Counsel on prevention (moisturization, avoid digital trauma)"],"pearls":["Most epistaxis is anterior (Kiesselbach) and stops with proper compression - lean forward and pinch the soft nose for a full 10-15 minutes","A heavy posterior bleed (older, hypertensive, anticoagulated) needs posterior packing and ADMISSION with airway monitoring - and escalation to SPA ligation/embolization if refractory","Unilateral recurrent epistaxis with a mass - especially an adolescent male - raises juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma; image before any biopsy (it is vascular)","Address the driver - anticoagulation, hypertension, and dryness - not just the current bleed"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Nosebleed (Epistaxis)","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epistaxis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK435997/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-deep-neck-infection","name":"Deep Neck Space Infections & Ludwig's Angina","sec":"ent","present":["Infections of the deep neck fascial spaces (parapharyngeal, retropharyngeal, submandibular, prevertebral) - potentially life-threatening from AIRWAY compromise, descent to MEDIASTINITIS, and vascular complications","LUDWIG'S ANGINA: rapidly spreading bilateral cellulitis of the submandibular/sublingual spaces (usually ODONTOGENIC) that elevates the floor of the mouth and TONGUE, threatening the airway - a true emergency","Complications: airway obstruction, descending mediastinitis, LEMIERRE syndrome (internal jugular vein septic thrombophlebitis, classically from Fusobacterium after pharyngitis), and carotid erosion"],"dx":["Assess the AIRWAY first (trismus, floor-of-mouth swelling, tongue displacement, stridor, drooling); CONTRAST CT of the neck (and chest if descent suspected) defines the space(s), abscess vs phlegmon, airway, and vascular involvement","Identify the source (dental, tonsillar, pharyngeal) and organisms (polymicrobial - aerobes/anaerobes); labs and cultures","Look for mediastinal extension and Lemierre (IJ thrombosis)"],"tx":["SECURE the AIRWAY early when threatened - often an awake technique or a controlled surgical airway/TRACHEOSTOMY (Ludwig's airways are difficult; do not delay)","IV broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS (covering oral flora incl anaerobes) and surgical DRAINAGE of abscesses (and the source, e.g., dental extraction); phlegmon may respond to antibiotics alone with close monitoring","Manage complications (mediastinal drainage, anticoagulation considerations for Lemierre)"],"tech":["Airway control (awake intubation/tracheostomy); incision and DRAINAGE of the involved neck spaces; source control (dental extraction, tonsillectomy)","Mediastinal drainage for descending infection (thoracic surgery)","Serial imaging/re-exploration as needed"],"after":["Airway monitoring and continued IV antibiotics (narrow to cultures); watch for progression/descent and vascular complications; nutritional support","Definitive source treatment (dental) and de-escalation; monitor for mediastinitis/Lemierre","Rehabilitation (swallowing, trismus) and follow-up"],"pearls":["Ludwig's angina is an airway emergency - a rapidly elevating floor of mouth and tongue from an odontogenic source; secure the airway early (often surgically) and do not lie the patient flat","Get a contrast CT to map the spaces, distinguish abscess from phlegmon, and assess airway/vascular involvement","Cover anaerobes and drain abscesses - deep neck infections are polymicrobial and often need surgical source control","Think Lemierre (Fusobacterium IJ thrombophlebitis) after pharyngitis, and watch for descending mediastinitis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Deep Neck Infection Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ludwig Angina / Deep Neck Infections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482354/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-peritonsillar-abscess","name":"Peritonsillar Abscess","sec":"ent","present":["The most common deep head-and-neck infection ('quinsy') - a collection of pus between the tonsillar capsule and the pharyngeal muscles, usually in adolescents/young adults, often following tonsillitis","Classic features: severe unilateral SORE THROAT, TRISMUS (difficulty opening the mouth), a muffled 'HOT POTATO' VOICE, drooling, and deviation of the swollen soft palate/uvula AWAY from the abscess","Distinguish from peritonsillar cellulitis (no drainable pus); rarely progresses to a deep neck infection/airway compromise"],"dx":["Largely a CLINICAL diagnosis (unilateral bulging, uvular deviation, trismus); intraoral ultrasound or CT can confirm/localize the collection when the exam is equivocal or a deeper infection is suspected","Assess the airway and hydration; consider CT if a deep neck extension is a concern","Differentiate abscess (drainable) from cellulitis (treat medically)"],"tx":["DRAINAGE (needle ASPIRATION or incision and drainage) is both diagnostic and therapeutic, plus ANTIBIOTICS (covering strep and oral anaerobes) and analgesia; corticosteroids reduce symptoms","Ensure adequate hydration; admit if unable to tolerate oral intake or airway concern","Recurrent PTA (or complicated cases) - consider interval or immediate ('quinsy') TONSILLECTOMY"],"tech":["Needle aspiration or incision and drainage of the abscess (topical/local anesthesia); quinsy tonsillectomy in select/recurrent cases","Ultrasound guidance if needed","Airway readiness for complicated cases"],"after":["Monitor for adequate drainage and symptom resolution (re-aspirate if recurs); complete antibiotics and ensure hydration","Counsel on RECURRENCE and the option of interval tonsillectomy for recurrent PTA or recurrent tonsillitis","Return precautions for worsening/airway symptoms"],"pearls":["Unilateral severe sore throat with trismus, a hot-potato voice, and uvular deviation is a peritonsillar abscess","Drainage (needle aspiration or I&D) plus antibiotics is the treatment - antibiotics alone treat only a peritonsillar cellulitis","Trismus and a muffled voice distinguish PTA from simple tonsillitis","Recurrent PTA is an indication to consider tonsillectomy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Peritonsillar Abscess Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Peritonsillar Abscess","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519520/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-epiglottitis","name":"Epiglottitis / Supraglottitis","sec":"ent","present":["Inflammation of the epiglottis/supraglottis - an airway EMERGENCY due to rapid supraglottic swelling; now seen MORE in ADULTS (Hib vaccination dramatically reduced pediatric cases, historically Haemophilus influenzae type b)","Presents with rapidly progressive SORE THROAT and severe ODYNOPHAGIA out of proportion to a relatively normal-looking oropharynx, DROOLING, a muffled ('hot potato') voice, fever, and (ominously) stridor and the TRIPOD/sniffing position","Children classically appear toxic, anxious, drooling, and refuse to lie down"],"dx":["In a stable adult, careful FLEXIBLE laryngoscopy (in a controlled setting) shows a swollen, erythematous epiglottis; the lateral neck radiograph may show the 'THUMBPRINT' sign but should NOT delay airway assessment","In CHILDREN with suspected epiglottitis, do NOT examine the throat, agitate, or lie the child down - keep them calm and go to a controlled setting (OR) for airway evaluation","Blood/epiglottic cultures once the airway is secured"],"tx":["SECURE the AIRWAY as the priority in a CONTROLLED setting (OR, with ENT/anesthesia and a surgical-airway backup) for significant obstruction - intubation over an unstable airway, tracheostomy if needed; many stable adults can be observed closely in an ICU","IV ANTIBIOTICS (covering H. influenzae and other pathogens - e.g., a third-generation cephalosporin) and CORTICOSTEROIDS to reduce edema; humidified oxygen","Do not agitate or heavily instrument a marginal airway before it is secured"],"tech":["Controlled airway management (intubation in the OR with surgical-airway backup, or tracheostomy); flexible laryngoscopy for diagnosis in stable patients","ICU airway monitoring for observed cases","Cultures and source management"],"after":["Airway monitoring in the ICU; continue antibiotics and steroids; plan extubation once swelling resolves (often confirmed by an air leak/repeat laryngoscopy)","Watch for abscess formation and complications; complete the antibiotic course","Vaccination counseling (Hib) where relevant"],"pearls":["Sore throat and odynophagia OUT OF PROPORTION to a normal-looking oropharynx, with drooling and a muffled voice, is epiglottitis until proven otherwise - now more common in adults","Do not agitate, examine the throat of, or lie down a child with suspected epiglottitis - manage the airway in a controlled OR setting","The thumbprint sign supports the diagnosis, but airway assessment/security comes before imaging","Secure the airway first, then treat with antibiotics and steroids"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Epiglottitis / Supraglottitis Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epiglottitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430960/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-angioedema","name":"Angioedema & Airway Compromise","sec":"ent","present":["Rapid, non-pitting swelling of the deep dermis/submucosa - dangerous when it involves the LIPS, TONGUE, floor of mouth, or LARYNX (airway threat); two mechanistic categories with different treatments","HISTAMINERGIC (mast-cell/allergic - part of anaphylaxis or allergic reactions, often with urticaria/itching) vs BRADYKININ-mediated (ACE-INHIBITOR-induced - can occur years into therapy - and HEREDITARY angioedema from C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency, typically WITHOUT urticaria)","Presents with facial/oral/laryngeal swelling, voice change, stridor, and dysphagia"],"dx":["Assess the AIRWAY (voice change, stridor, tongue/floor-of-mouth swelling) - flexible laryngoscopy to evaluate laryngeal involvement; determine the mechanism from history (medication - ACE inhibitor, family history, prior episodes, associated urticaria/anaphylaxis)","Histaminergic angioedema often has urticaria/pruritus and responds to allergic treatment; bradykinin-mediated (ACE-I/HAE) lacks urticaria and does NOT respond to epinephrine/antihistamines/steroids","Assess severity and progression"],"tx":["AIRWAY first - low threshold to secure the airway (awake fiberoptic or surgical airway) for progressive tongue/laryngeal swelling; observe/monitor milder cases","ALLERGIC/anaphylactic angioedema: EPINEPHRINE, antihistamines, and corticosteroids; BRADYKININ-mediated: STOP the ACE inhibitor (and avoid it forever), and for HAE/severe ACE-I angioedema use bradykinin-targeted agents (C1-esterase inhibitor, ICATIBANT, ecallantide) - the allergic drugs do not work","Fresh frozen plasma is an option when specific agents are unavailable"],"tech":["Airway management (awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway) for severe/progressive laryngeal edema; laryngoscopic assessment","Administration of mechanism-specific medications","ICU monitoring"],"after":["Airway monitoring until swelling resolves; DISCONTINUE the offending ACE inhibitor permanently and document the allergy; provide an anaphylaxis action plan/epinephrine autoinjector for allergic disease","HAE: long-term prophylaxis and specialist (allergy/immunology) referral; on-demand therapy plan","Patient education on triggers and recurrence"],"pearls":["Identify the mechanism - allergic (histaminergic, with urticaria) responds to epinephrine/antihistamines/steroids; ACE-inhibitor and hereditary (bradykinin) angioedema do NOT, and need bradykinin-targeted agents","ACE-inhibitor angioedema can appear years into therapy and typically has no urticaria - stop the drug permanently","Tongue and floor-of-mouth swelling with voice change is an airway threat - secure it early (awake fiberoptic/surgical)","Suspect hereditary angioedema with recurrent angioedema without urticaria and a family history - refer to allergy/immunology"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / Allergy: Angioedema Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Angioedema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482399/"},{"t":"US Hereditary Angioedema Association","u":"https://www.haea.org"}]},{"id":"ent-foreign-bodies","name":"Aerodigestive Foreign Bodies","sec":"ent","present":["Inhaled or swallowed foreign bodies - most common in young CHILDREN (and in adults with dentures/impaired swallowing). AIRWAY foreign bodies cause choking, cough, wheeze, and asymmetric breath sounds; ESOPHAGEAL foreign bodies cause dysphagia, drooling, food refusal, and chest/neck pain","A witnessed choking event may be absent - a persistent unilateral wheeze or recurrent pneumonia can be a retained airway foreign body","Two special EMERGENCIES: a BUTTON (disc) BATTERY (rapid caustic/liquefactive injury - urgent removal, especially in the esophagus) and high-powered MAGNETS"],"dx":["Airway FB: examine for asymmetric breath sounds/wheeze; radiographs (many organic FBs are radiolucent - look for hyperinflation/atelectasis, air trapping on expiratory/decubitus films); rigid BRONCHOSCOPY is diagnostic and therapeutic","Esophageal FB: radiographs (coins are radiopaque; identify a BUTTON BATTERY by its 'double-ring/halo' sign - a caustic emergency); localize and characterize","Maintain a high index of suspicion in children with unexplained respiratory symptoms"],"tx":["AIRWAY foreign body: urgent RIGID BRONCHOSCOPY for removal (with airway control); acute complete obstruction - basic life support choking maneuvers","ESOPHAGEAL foreign body: endoscopic removal; a BUTTON BATTERY in the esophagus is a surgical EMERGENCY (immediate removal to prevent perforation/fistula); sharp objects and impactions also need prompt endoscopy","Airway/mucosal protection and complication management"],"tech":["Rigid bronchoscopy (airway FB removal); rigid/flexible esophagoscopy (esophageal FB removal); urgent removal of button batteries and sharp/impacted objects","Airway control during instrumentation","Manage mucosal injury/complications"],"after":["Monitor for airway edema, retained fragments, aspiration, and (button battery) delayed injury/perforation/fistula - serial evaluation for caustic injury; antibiotics/observation as indicated","Counsel on choking prevention (small objects, food, batteries out of reach in children)","Follow-up for mucosal healing/strictures"],"pearls":["A persistent unilateral wheeze or recurrent focal pneumonia in a child is a retained airway foreign body until proven otherwise - even without a witnessed choking event","A button battery in the esophagus is an emergency - it causes rapid caustic injury; look for the double-ring sign and remove it immediately","Many aspirated foreign bodies are radiolucent - look for indirect signs (air trapping/atelectasis), and use rigid bronchoscopy","Prevention counseling matters - small objects, coins, magnets, and batteries are classic pediatric hazards"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Aerodigestive Foreign Body Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Airway / Esophageal Foreign Body","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459193/"},{"t":"NCPC Button Battery Guidance","u":"https://www.poison.org"}]},{"id":"ent-aom-ome","name":"Acute Otitis Media & Otitis Media with Effusion","sec":"ent","present":["ACUTE OTITIS MEDIA (AOM): a symptomatic middle-ear infection - one of the most common pediatric illnesses - with otalgia (ear tugging in infants), fever, irritability, and a BULGING, erythematous, immobile tympanic membrane (often after a viral URI); common pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis","OTITIS MEDIA WITH EFFUSION (OME): middle-ear FLUID WITHOUT acute infection - causes hearing loss and a sensation of fullness, common after AOM or with eustachian tube dysfunction; a dull/retracted TM with an air-fluid level","Complications of AOM: TM perforation, mastoiditis, and (rarely) intracranial spread and facial nerve involvement"],"dx":["Pneumatic OTOSCOPY (a bulging, immobile TM supports AOM; a retracted TM with reduced mobility and effusion supports OME) and tympanometry; distinguish AOM (acute inflammation) from OME (effusion without acute infection)","Assess HEARING and speech/language effects in children with persistent OME","Evaluate for complications (postauricular swelling/proptosis of the ear - mastoiditis; facial weakness)"],"tx":["AOM: adequate ANALGESIA; ANTIBIOTICS (high-dose AMOXICILLIN first-line, amoxicillin-clavulanate for treatment failure/risk) for young/severe/bilateral cases, with OBSERVATION ('watchful waiting') a reasonable option for older children with mild disease","OME: usually resolves spontaneously - OBSERVE for ~3 months; antibiotics/antihistamines are NOT effective for OME","Persistent OME with HEARING LOSS or recurrent AOM: TYMPANOSTOMY (ventilation) TUBES (+/- adenoidectomy)"],"tech":["Myringotomy with TYMPANOSTOMY (ventilation) tube placement for recurrent AOM or persistent OME with hearing loss; adenoidectomy as an adjunct in select children","Analgesia and antibiotic management for AOM","Audiologic assessment"],"after":["Reassess AOM for resolution and recurrence; monitor OME hearing and for tube patency/extrusion, otorrhea (treat with topical drops), and (rarely) persistent perforation after tubes","Address contributing factors (allergy, reflux, adenoid hypertrophy, smoke exposure) and speech/language for hearing-affected children","Follow-up audiology"],"pearls":["A bulging, immobile, erythematous TM defines AOM; a dull, retracted TM with an effusion and no acute inflammation is OME","Antibiotics and antihistamines do NOT clear OME - it resolves on its own or, if persistent with hearing loss, gets ventilation tubes","Watchful waiting is appropriate for AOM in older children with mild disease; treat young/severe/bilateral cases with high-dose amoxicillin","Persistent OME threatens speech/language via hearing loss - that, and recurrent AOM, drive tube placement"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / AAP Otitis Media with Effusion & Tympanostomy Tube CPGs","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Otitis Media","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470332/"},{"t":"AAP Otitis Media Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"ent-otitis-externa","name":"Otitis Externa & Necrotizing Otitis Externa","sec":"ent","present":["OTITIS EXTERNA ('swimmer's ear'): infection/inflammation of the external ear canal - ear PAIN, itching, discharge, and canal edema, with PAIN ON TRAGAL/pinna manipulation (which distinguishes it from otitis media); usually Pseudomonas aeruginosa or Staphylococcus, promoted by moisture/trauma","NECROTIZING (malignant) OTITIS EXTERNA: a life-threatening SKULL BASE OSTEOMYELITIS - typically in elderly DIABETICS or the immunocompromised, from Pseudomonas - with severe, deep, unrelenting pain, GRANULATION TISSUE at the bony-cartilaginous junction of the canal floor, and potential cranial nerve (facial, then lower CN) palsies","Fungal otitis externa (otomycosis - Aspergillus/Candida) is also common, especially after antibiotic drops"],"dx":["OE: clinical (canal edema/debris, tragal tenderness); culture for refractory cases; distinguish from otomycosis (visible fungal elements)","NECROTIZING OE: suspect in a diabetic/immunocompromised patient with severe otalgia and granulation tissue; CT (bony erosion) and MRI/nuclear bone scans assess skull base extent; markedly elevated ESR/CRP; biopsy granulation to exclude malignancy","Assess cranial nerves (facial nerve involvement is an ominous sign)"],"tx":["OE: aural TOILET (clean the canal), TOPICAL antibiotic/steroid drops (an ear WICK if the canal is very edematous), water precautions, and analgesia; otomycosis - antifungal drops/acidifying agents; systemic antibiotics generally unnecessary for uncomplicated OE","NECROTIZING OE: PROLONGED systemic ANTIPSEUDOMONAL therapy (weeks), tight GLUCOSE control, aural debridement, and follow-up imaging; surgical debridement for refractory disease","Correct predisposing factors"],"tech":["Aural toilet/microsuction and ear-wick placement (OE); debridement of granulation/necrotic tissue and biopsy (necrotizing OE)","Imaging-guided follow-up for skull base osteomyelitis","Manage cranial nerve/complication issues"],"after":["OE: dry-ear precautions and follow-up for resolution; recurrent OE - address moisture/trauma and consider acidifying drops","NECROTIZING OE: prolonged antibiotics with clinical/imaging (and inflammatory marker) monitoring to confirm resolution; watch for cranial neuropathies and relapse - it can be fatal","Diabetes/immune optimization"],"pearls":["Pain on tugging the pinna/tragus points to otitis externa (canal), not otitis media","Severe, deep, unrelenting ear pain with granulation tissue in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient is necrotizing (malignant) otitis externa - a skull base osteomyelitis that can kill","Necrotizing OE needs prolonged systemic antipseudomonal therapy, glucose control, and imaging follow-up - not just drops; cranial nerve palsy is an ominous sign","Treat uncomplicated otitis externa with aural toilet and topical drops (a wick if edematous) - systemic antibiotics are usually unnecessary"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Acute Otitis Externa","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Otitis Externa / Malignant Otitis Externa","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556055/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-chronic-om-cholesteatoma","name":"Chronic Otitis Media & Cholesteatoma","sec":"ent","present":["CHRONIC (SUPPURATIVE) OTITIS MEDIA: chronic middle-ear inflammation with a persistent TM PERFORATION and recurrent/persistent otorrhea and hearing loss","CHOLESTEATOMA: an abnormal, expanding collection of keratinizing SQUAMOUS epithelium in the middle ear/mastoid (from a retraction pocket - acquired - or congenital) that is locally EROSIVE and destructive despite being 'benign'","Presents with painless, often FOUL-smelling otorrhea, progressive hearing loss, and a retraction pocket/attic CRUST or debris; it erodes the ossicles and can cause serious complications"],"dx":["OTOSCOPY (retraction pocket, keratin debris, attic/marginal perforation, aural polyp) and microscopic exam; AUDIOGRAM (usually conductive hearing loss); high-resolution CT of the temporal bones defines extent, ossicular/bony erosion, and complications","Cholesteatoma is a clinical/otoscopic diagnosis confirmed at surgery; assess for complications (facial nerve, labyrinthine fistula - vertigo/positive fistula test, intracranial)","Culture otorrhea for treatment"],"tx":["CHOLESTEATOMA is a SURGICAL disease - it must be removed (medical therapy does not cure it): TYMPANOMASTOIDECTOMY (canal-wall-up or canal-wall-down) to eradicate disease, with ossicular reconstruction for hearing","Chronic suppurative OM (without cholesteatoma): aural toilet, topical antibiotics for otorrhea, and tympanoplasty to repair the perforation","Treat active infection perioperatively; manage complications"],"tech":["Tympanomastoidectomy (canal-wall-up preserves the ear canal but has higher recurrence; canal-wall-down gives better disease control but leaves a mastoid cavity) for cholesteatoma; ossiculoplasty/tympanoplasty for hearing","Second-look procedures for residual disease in canal-wall-up cases","Manage labyrinthine fistula/facial nerve at surgery"],"after":["SURVEILLANCE for RESIDUAL/RECURRENT cholesteatoma (clinical, imaging - non-echo-planar diffusion MRI, or second-look surgery), especially with canal-wall-up techniques; hearing rehabilitation","Canal-wall-down cavities need periodic cleaning; water precautions and treat recurrent otorrhea","Long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Painless, foul-smelling otorrhea with a retraction pocket/attic crust and progressive hearing loss is a cholesteatoma - and it is a surgical disease, not a course of drops","Cholesteatoma is histologically benign but locally destructive - it erodes ossicles and can cause facial palsy, labyrinthine fistula, and intracranial complications","Get a temporal bone CT to map extent and complications, and an audiogram for the (usually conductive) hearing loss","Cholesteatoma recurs - build in surveillance (imaging or second-look surgery), especially after canal-wall-up mastoidectomy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Chronic Otitis Media / Cholesteatoma Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cholesteatoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448108/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-tm-perforation","name":"Tympanic Membrane Perforation & Tympanoplasty","sec":"ent","present":["A hole in the eardrum from TRAUMA (barotrauma, slap/blast, cotton-swab/Q-tip injury, temporal bone fracture), INFECTION (acute otitis media), or persistent after tympanostomy tubes","Presents with conductive HEARING LOSS (proportional to perforation size), possible otorrhea (if infected), tinnitus, and sometimes a sensorineural component/vertigo with high-energy trauma (ossicular/inner-ear injury)","Most traumatic/small perforations heal SPONTANEOUSLY"],"dx":["Otoscopy to size and locate the perforation; AUDIOGRAM to quantify the (conductive) hearing loss and detect any sensorineural component (which suggests inner-ear/ossicular injury)","Assess for infection, cholesteatoma (marginal perforations/retraction), and, in trauma, facial nerve/vertigo (temporal bone injury)","Water precautions during healing"],"tx":["Most acute/small perforations - OBSERVE (they heal spontaneously) with DRY-EAR precautions; topical antibiotic drops if infected/draining","Persistent (failure to heal over months), large, or symptomatic perforations, or those causing recurrent infection - surgical repair (myringoplasty/TYMPANOPLASTY with a graft); OSSICULOPLASTY if there is ossicular discontinuity","Avoid ototoxic drops with an open perforation where possible"],"tech":["Tympanoplasty/myringoplasty (graft repair of the TM, e.g., temporalis fascia/cartilage); ossiculoplasty for ossicular chain discontinuity; paper-patch/office procedures for select small perforations","Otomicroscopic technique","Address associated pathology (cholesteatoma) at surgery"],"after":["Water precautions and follow-up for spontaneous healing or graft take; audiologic reassessment of hearing after repair","Monitor for persistent/recurrent perforation, graft failure, and (post-op) infection; hearing rehabilitation if needed","Counsel to avoid cotton-swab use"],"pearls":["Most traumatic and small perforations heal on their own - observe with dry-ear precautions rather than rushing to surgery","A sensorineural component or vertigo with a traumatic perforation suggests inner-ear/ossicular injury - get an audiogram and assess carefully","Persistent, large, or symptomatic perforations (or recurrent infection) are repaired with tympanoplasty","Counsel patients to stop using cotton swabs - a very common cause of perforation and canal trauma"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Tympanic Membrane Perforation Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tympanic Membrane Perforation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430863/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-otosclerosis","name":"Otosclerosis & Conductive Hearing Loss","sec":"ent","present":["OTOSCLEROSIS: abnormal remodeling of the otic capsule bone that FIXES the STAPES footplate, producing progressive CONDUCTIVE hearing loss - typically in young-to-middle-aged adults, more common in women and often FAMILIAL, and classically WORSENING during pregnancy","Clues: a normal-appearing TM, a positive family history, paradoxical better hearing in noise (paracusis of Willis), a CARHART NOTCH on audiometry, and a reddish promontory blush (Schwartze sign) in active disease","Part of the broader differential of CONDUCTIVE hearing loss: cerumen impaction, middle-ear effusion, TM perforation, ossicular discontinuity/fixation, and cholesteatoma"],"dx":["AUDIOGRAM shows conductive hearing loss with an AIR-BONE GAP (and a Carhart notch); TYMPANOMETRY is typically normal (distinguishing it from effusion/discontinuity); acoustic reflexes are often absent","Examine to exclude other conductive causes (cerumen, effusion, perforation); CT can confirm/assess otic capsule involvement in select cases","Weber lateralizes to the affected (conductive) ear"],"tx":["Options are HEARING AID (excellent, non-surgical) or surgery: STAPEDECTOMY/STAPEDOTOMY (removing/bypassing the fixed stapes with a prosthesis) reliably closes the air-bone gap and restores hearing","Sodium fluoride may be used to slow active otosclerosis in some cases","Individualize by patient preference and surgical risk"],"tech":["Stapedotomy/stapedectomy (create a fenestra in or remove the fixed stapes footplate and place a prosthesis to the incus); otomicroscopic technique","Hearing aid fitting as the non-surgical alternative","Manage intraoperative footplate/inner-ear issues"],"after":["Audiologic follow-up for hearing outcome; counsel on the surgical RISKS - sensorineural hearing loss/'dead ear', vertigo/imbalance, dysgeusia (chorda tympani), and prosthesis problems","Monitor the contralateral ear (often bilateral disease); manage recurrence/prosthesis displacement","Hearing aid option if surgery is declined/unsuccessful"],"pearls":["Progressive conductive hearing loss with a normal eardrum, a family history, and worsening in pregnancy is classic otosclerosis (stapes fixation)","The audiogram shows a conductive loss with a Carhart notch and normal tympanometry - the pattern that separates it from effusion or ossicular discontinuity","Offer a hearing aid or stapes surgery - both are effective; stapedotomy reliably closes the air-bone gap","Counsel on the small but real risk of sensorineural loss ('dead ear') with stapes surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Otosclerosis Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Otosclerosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560761/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-snhl","name":"Sensorineural & Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss","sec":"ent","present":["SENSORINEURAL hearing loss (SNHL) arises from the cochlea or auditory nerve: PRESBYCUSIS (age-related, high-frequency), NOISE-induced, OTOTOXIC (aminoglycosides, cisplatin, loop diuretics), genetic, and infectious/autoimmune causes","SUDDEN SENSORINEURAL HEARING LOSS (SSNHL) is an EMERGENCY: a rapid (within 72 hours) unilateral SNHL of >=30 dB over at least 3 contiguous frequencies - usually idiopathic, but a RETROCOCHLEAR lesion (vestibular schwannoma) must be excluded","Patients report muffled hearing, aural fullness, tinnitus, and sometimes vertigo"],"dx":["WEBER/RINNE and an AUDIOGRAM confirm a sensorineural pattern (Weber lateralizes AWAY from the affected ear in SNHL, Rinne air>bone); for SSNHL, distinguish it from cerumen/effusion (do not miss a treatable conductive cause)","SSNHL: obtain an MRI (with gadolinium) to exclude VESTIBULAR SCHWANNOMA/retrocochlear pathology; targeted workup for bilateral/atypical cases (autoimmune, infectious)","Identify ototoxic exposures and noise history"],"tx":["SSNHL is TIME-SENSITIVE: prompt CORTICOSTEROIDS (oral, and/or INTRATYMPANIC) improve recovery odds - start early (within days to a couple of weeks); intratympanic steroids are used as primary or salvage therapy","Chronic SNHL (presbycusis, noise-induced): HEARING AIDS, and COCHLEAR IMPLANTATION for severe-to-profound loss with poor aided benefit; HEARING PROTECTION and avoiding ototoxins for prevention","Treat identifiable causes (autoimmune, infectious)"],"tech":["Intratympanic steroid injection (primary or salvage for SSNHL); hearing aid fitting; cochlear implantation for severe-profound SNHL","Audiologic assessment and monitoring","MRI-guided workup for retrocochlear disease"],"after":["Follow audiograms for recovery (SSNHL) and progression; MRI results guide schwannoma surveillance; hearing rehabilitation and counseling","Prevention counseling (hearing protection, ototoxicity monitoring); manage tinnitus/balance symptoms","Long-term audiologic follow-up"],"pearls":["Sudden sensorineural hearing loss is an emergency - start corticosteroids promptly (the window is short) and do not dismiss it as 'wax' or an infection","Always get an MRI to rule out a vestibular schwannoma in unilateral SSNHL/asymmetric SNHL","Distinguish sensorineural from conductive at the bedside (Weber/Rinne) - a treatable conductive cause (cerumen, effusion) should not be missed","Prevent what you can - hearing protection and ototoxicity monitoring (aminoglycosides, cisplatin) matter"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Sudden Hearing Loss","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551709/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-tinnitus-rehab","name":"Tinnitus & Hearing Rehabilitation","sec":"ent","present":["TINNITUS: the perception of sound without an external source - usually SUBJECTIVE (associated with hearing loss/noise exposure) and benign, but some patterns are RED FLAGS: PULSATILE tinnitus (suggests a vascular cause - workup needed) and UNILATERAL tinnitus (raises concern for a retrocochlear lesion - vestibular schwannoma)","HEARING REHABILITATION addresses the associated/underlying hearing loss and includes amplification and implantable devices","Tinnitus is common, often distressing, and frequently linked to hearing loss"],"dx":["AUDIOGRAM for associated hearing loss; for PULSATILE tinnitus, imaging (CT/MRI/MRA-MRV or angiography) to evaluate vascular/structural causes (dural fistula, glomus tumor, venous, atherosclerotic); for UNILATERAL tinnitus/asymmetric hearing loss, MRI to exclude vestibular schwannoma","Assess the impact (validated questionnaires) and contributing factors (noise, ototoxins, TMJ, medications)","Distinguish objective (audible to the examiner) from subjective tinnitus"],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause where found; for bothersome subjective tinnitus: patient EDUCATION, SOUND therapy/maskers, hearing aids (amplification often reduces tinnitus), and cognitive behavioral therapy (the best-evidence treatment for tinnitus distress)","HEARING REHABILITATION options: HEARING AIDS (mainstay), BONE-ANCHORED hearing aids/BAHA (conductive/mixed loss or single-sided deafness), and COCHLEAR IMPLANTS (severe-profound sensorineural loss)","No drug reliably cures subjective tinnitus - avoid overpromising"],"tech":["Hearing aid fitting; bone-anchored hearing aid (BAHA) implantation; cochlear implantation; sound-therapy/masking devices","Imaging/intervention for a vascular cause of pulsatile tinnitus","CBT/sound therapy programs"],"after":["Ongoing audiologic rehabilitation and device follow-up; manage tinnitus distress with sound therapy/CBT; reassess for new red flags","Counsel realistically (subjective tinnitus is managed, not usually cured) and address sleep/mood impact","Hearing protection counseling"],"pearls":["Two tinnitus red flags demand workup: PULSATILE tinnitus (image for a vascular cause) and UNILATERAL tinnitus/asymmetric hearing loss (MRI for vestibular schwannoma)","Amplification (hearing aids) often improves subjective tinnitus by restoring ambient sound","Cognitive behavioral therapy has the best evidence for reducing tinnitus distress - and no drug reliably cures it","Match the rehabilitation device to the loss: hearing aids for most, BAHA for conductive/single-sided deafness, cochlear implants for severe-profound SNHL"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Tinnitus","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tinnitus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430809/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-vestibular-schwannoma","name":"Vestibular Schwannoma (Acoustic Neuroma)","sec":"ent","present":["A benign SCHWANNOMA of the vestibular portion of the eighth cranial nerve, arising in the internal auditory canal/cerebellopontine angle (this content shares with the neurosurgery framing, ENT/neurotology lens)","Presents with ASYMMETRIC sensorineural HEARING LOSS, unilateral TINNITUS, and imbalance/disequilibrium; larger tumors cause trigeminal (facial numbness), facial nerve, and eventually brainstem/hydrocephalus effects","BILATERAL vestibular schwannomas are pathognomonic of NEUROFIBROMATOSIS TYPE 2"],"dx":["MRI with GADOLINIUM of the internal auditory canals is the gold standard (a CPA/IAC enhancing mass); AUDIOGRAM shows asymmetric SNHL with disproportionate loss of word recognition; auditory brainstem response as an adjunct","Any ASYMMETRIC SNHL or unilateral tinnitus warrants MRI to exclude it","Assess tumor size, growth (serial MRI), hearing, and facial nerve function"],"tx":["Three strategies, individualized by tumor size/growth, hearing, age, and symptoms: OBSERVATION with serial MRI (small/asymptomatic - many grow slowly or not at all), stereotactic RADIOSURGERY (control growth, hearing/facial-nerve preservation goals), and MICROSURGICAL RESECTION (larger/growing tumors or brainstem compression)","Surgical approach (translabyrinthine, retrosigmoid, middle fossa) chosen by hearing status and tumor location - facial nerve preservation is a priority","Manage hydrocephalus/brainstem compression in large tumors"],"tech":["Microsurgical resection via translabyrinthine (sacrifices hearing), retrosigmoid, or middle fossa approach (hearing-preservation attempts); stereotactic radiosurgery (Gamma Knife) for growth control","Intraoperative facial nerve monitoring","Serial-MRI observation protocol"],"after":["Facial nerve function and HEARING monitoring; surveillance MRI (observation, or post-radiosurgery for continued control); vestibular rehabilitation for imbalance","Manage facial nerve weakness (eye protection) and hearing rehabilitation; watch for CSF leak/complications post-surgery","Genetic evaluation (NF2) for bilateral/young cases"],"pearls":["Asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss or unilateral tinnitus warrants an MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma - disproportionately poor word recognition is a clue","Three legitimate management paths: observe with serial MRI, radiosurgery, or microsurgery - matched to size/growth, hearing, and age","Facial nerve preservation is a central goal - approach and treatment choice weigh hearing and facial-nerve outcomes","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas mean neurofibromatosis type 2 - pursue genetic evaluation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / Neurotology: Vestibular Schwannoma","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acoustic Neuroma (Vestibular Schwannoma)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562312/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-facial-nerve-palsy","name":"Facial Nerve Paralysis (Bell's Palsy & Others)","sec":"ent","present":["Weakness of the facial nerve (CN VII) - the critical first step is distinguishing a LOWER-motor-neuron (peripheral) palsy, which involves the FOREHEAD, from an UPPER-motor-neuron (central/stroke) palsy, which SPARES the forehead (this content shares with the neurology framing, ENT lens)","BELL'S PALSY (idiopathic, HSV-associated) is the most common cause - acute, unilateral, complete forehead-involving weakness; RAMSAY HUNT syndrome (herpes zoster oticus) adds ear pain and vesicles and has a worse prognosis","Other causes: Lyme disease (can be bilateral), otitis media/cholesteatoma, temporal bone trauma, and tumors (parotid malignancy, schwannoma) - suggested by gradual onset, recurrence, or associated findings"],"dx":["Grade severity (HOUSE-BRACKMANN scale); confirm a peripheral (forehead-involving) pattern and examine the ear (vesicles - Ramsay Hunt; cholesteatoma/otitis), parotid (mass), and other cranial nerves","Bell's palsy is a clinical diagnosis; RED FLAGS prompting imaging/workup: GRADUAL onset, no recovery by ~3-4 months, RECURRENT palsy, other cranial neuropathies, or a parotid mass (tumor)","Test for Lyme in endemic areas/bilateral cases"],"tx":["BELL'S PALSY: oral CORTICOSTEROIDS started EARLY (within 72 hours) improve recovery; add ANTIVIRALS for severe cases and for Ramsay Hunt (zoster)","EYE PROTECTION is essential (incomplete closure -> exposure keratopathy): lubricating drops/ointment, taping the eye shut at night, and a moisture chamber","Treat the specific cause (antibiotics for Lyme/otitis, surgery for cholesteatoma/tumor); facial reanimation procedures for chronic paralysis"],"tech":["Eye-protection measures (and, for chronic palsy, upper-lid weight/tarsorrhaphy); facial reanimation surgery (nerve grafts/transfers, static/dynamic procedures) for non-recovering paralysis; decompression in select cases","Treat causative pathology surgically (cholesteatoma, tumor)","Physical therapy/facial retraining"],"after":["Most Bell's palsy recovers substantially over weeks-months; monitor recovery and manage EYE care throughout to prevent corneal injury; watch for SYNKINESIS (aberrant regeneration) and manage it","Reassess and IMAGE if no recovery by ~3-4 months, or if recurrent/atypical (exclude tumor)","Facial rehabilitation and reanimation for residual deficits"],"pearls":["Forehead involvement separates a peripheral (Bell's) palsy from a central (stroke) one, which spares the forehead - the single most important bedside distinction","Give early steroids for Bell's palsy (within 72 hours) and protect the eye - exposure keratopathy is the avoidable harm","Ear pain with vesicles is Ramsay Hunt (zoster) - add antivirals and expect a worse prognosis","Gradual onset, no recovery by 3-4 months, recurrence, or a parotid mass means image and search for a tumor - it is not simple Bell's palsy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Bell's Palsy","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bell Palsy / Facial Nerve Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482290/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-bppv","name":"Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV)","sec":"ent","present":["The MOST COMMON cause of vertigo: brief (seconds), intense SPINNING triggered by HEAD POSITION changes - rolling over in bed, lying down, looking up ('top-shelf vertigo'), or bending over","Caused by displaced otoconia (canaliths) in the semicircular canals (the POSTERIOR canal is most common), which move with gravity and generate spurious signals","There is NO hearing loss or persistent symptoms between episodes (features that would suggest another diagnosis)"],"dx":["The DIX-HALLPIKE maneuver diagnoses posterior-canal BPPV (reproduces vertigo with a characteristic transient UPBEAT-TORSIONAL nystagmus that FATIGUES); the supine roll test evaluates horizontal-canal BPPV","The history (brief, positional, no auditory symptoms) plus positional nystagmus confirms it; RED FLAGS (persistent vertigo, hearing loss, other neurologic signs, direction-changing/non-fatiguing nystagmus) point elsewhere (central causes)","No imaging is needed for classic BPPV"],"tx":["CANALITH REPOSITIONING maneuvers are curative and first-line: the EPLEY maneuver for posterior-canal BPPV (and the Lempert/BBQ-roll for horizontal canal) - they move the otoconia out of the canal","Reassurance and home repositioning/exercises; vestibular suppressant medications are generally NOT recommended (they do not treat the cause and can prolong recovery)","Rarely, surgery (canal plugging) for intractable disease"],"tech":["Epley (and other) canalith repositioning maneuvers - the definitive treatment; horizontal-canal repositioning; posterior semicircular canal occlusion surgery for refractory cases","Teach home repositioning exercises","Vestibular rehabilitation for residual imbalance"],"after":["Most respond to one or a few repositioning maneuvers; counsel on RECURRENCE (common) and teach home maneuvers; brief post-maneuver activity guidance","Reassess/consider other diagnoses if symptoms persist or red flags emerge","Vestibular rehabilitation for lingering unsteadiness"],"pearls":["Brief, positional spinning with NO hearing loss and a fatiguing upbeat-torsional nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike is posterior-canal BPPV","The Epley maneuver cures it - repositioning, not medication, is the treatment (vestibular suppressants can prolong recovery)","Direction-changing, non-fatiguing, or persistent nystagmus, or any hearing/neurologic signs, means it is NOT simple BPPV - think central","BPPV recurs - teach patients home repositioning maneuvers"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: BPPV","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470308/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-meniere-vestibular","name":"Meniere's Disease & Vestibular Neuritis/Labyrinthitis","sec":"ent","present":["Peripheral vestibular disorders. MENIERE'S DISEASE (endolymphatic hydrops): recurrent, spontaneous vertigo episodes lasting MINUTES TO HOURS, with FLUCTUATING low-frequency sensorineural HEARING LOSS, TINNITUS, and aural FULLNESS in the affected ear","VESTIBULAR NEURITIS: acute, single, PROLONGED vertigo (days) from inflammation of the vestibular nerve (often post-viral) WITHOUT hearing loss; LABYRINTHITIS is the same picture WITH hearing loss","The key clinical task is separating a peripheral cause from a central (stroke) cause of acute vertigo"],"dx":["MENIERE'S: clinical criteria (episodic vertigo + documented fluctuating low-frequency SNHL + tinnitus/fullness); audiogram; exclude other causes (MRI to rule out retrocochlear pathology)","ACUTE prolonged vertigo: the HINTS exam (Head-Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew) distinguishes a PERIPHERAL lesion (abnormal head impulse, unidirectional nystagmus, no skew) from a central/STROKE cause (a 'central' HINTS pattern is more sensitive than early MRI for posterior stroke)","Assess hearing (labyrinthitis vs neuritis) and neurologic signs"],"tx":["MENIERE'S: LOW-SODIUM diet and DIURETICS for maintenance; acute attacks - vestibular suppressants/antiemetics; step-up therapy - INTRATYMPANIC steroids, then intratympanic GENTAMICIN (chemical ablation) or surgery (endolymphatic sac procedures, labyrinthectomy) for refractory disease","VESTIBULAR NEURITIS/LABYRINTHITIS: brief vestibular suppressants for the acute phase only, corticosteroids (may aid recovery), and early VESTIBULAR REHABILITATION (central compensation); antivirals for labyrinthitis of viral/zoster origin","Do not use vestibular suppressants long-term (they impair compensation)"],"tech":["Intratympanic steroid or gentamicin injection (Meniere's); endolymphatic sac decompression, labyrinthectomy, or vestibular nerve section for refractory Meniere's; vestibular rehabilitation therapy","HINTS-guided triage (rule out stroke)","Audiologic monitoring"],"after":["MENIERE'S: monitor hearing (progressive SNHL) and attack frequency; counsel on the fluctuating, chronic course; VESTIBULAR NEURITIS: expect gradual recovery via central compensation aided by vestibular rehabilitation","Fall-safety and driving counseling during acute vertigo; manage residual imbalance","Long-term follow-up (hearing, recurrence)"],"pearls":["Meniere's = episodic vertigo (minutes-hours) WITH fluctuating low-frequency hearing loss, tinnitus, and aural fullness; vestibular neuritis = prolonged vertigo (days) WITHOUT hearing loss (labyrinthitis adds hearing loss)","In acute prolonged vertigo, a 'central' HINTS exam beats early MRI for catching posterior-circulation stroke - do the HINTS","Treat vestibular neuritis with early vestibular rehabilitation and only brief suppressants - long-term suppressants impair central compensation","Escalate refractory Meniere's stepwise: diet/diuretics -> intratympanic steroids -> gentamicin/surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Meniere's Disease","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Meniere Disease / Vestibular Neuritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536955/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-acute-rhinosinusitis","name":"Acute Rhinosinusitis & Complications","sec":"ent","present":["Acute inflammation of the nose and paranasal sinuses (<4 weeks) - VIRAL in the vast majority (resolving within ~10 days), with a minority becoming acute BACTERIAL rhinosinusitis (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis)","Features: nasal congestion/obstruction, purulent DISCHARGE, facial PAIN/pressure, and hyposmia; BACTERIAL infection is suggested by symptoms >10 days without improvement, 'DOUBLE WORSENING' (initial improvement then deterioration), or severe onset with high fever","Dangerous COMPLICATIONS spread from the sinuses to the ORBIT and INTRACRANIAL cavity"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis for uncomplicated disease (imaging is NOT routinely needed); reserve CONTRAST CT (and MRI) for suspected COMPLICATIONS, severe/refractory disease, or immunocompromise","Recognize COMPLICATIONS: ORBITAL (preseptal vs orbital cellulitis, subperiosteal/orbital ABSCESS - proptosis, painful/restricted eye movements, vision change - the Chandler classification), INTRACRANIAL (meningitis, epidural/subdural/brain abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis), and POTT'S PUFFY TUMOR (frontal bone osteomyelitis with forehead swelling)","Assess vision and neurologic status when complications are suspected"],"tx":["UNCOMPLICATED viral rhinosinusitis: supportive care (analgesia, saline irrigation, intranasal steroids, decongestants); WATCHFUL WAITING or antibiotics for bacterial disease","BACTERIAL rhinosinusitis meeting criteria: AMOXICILLIN-CLAVULANATE (first-line) with symptomatic care","COMPLICATIONS are emergencies: IV antibiotics and urgent SURGICAL drainage (endoscopic sinus surgery, abscess drainage) with ophthalmology/neurosurgery involvement as needed"],"tech":["Endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS) and abscess drainage for orbital/intracranial complications; surgical management of Pott's puffy tumor","Nasal endoscopy/culture for refractory disease","Multidisciplinary management of complications"],"after":["Uncomplicated disease resolves - reassess if not improving; COMPLICATIONS need close monitoring (serial vision/neuro exams), continued IV antibiotics, and follow-up imaging","Address predisposing factors (allergy, anatomy) and recurrence; evaluate for chronic rhinosinusitis if persistent","Vision and neurologic follow-up after complicated disease"],"pearls":["Most acute rhinosinusitis is viral and self-limited - antibiotics are for the minority with >10 days without improvement, double-worsening, or severe onset","Amoxicillin-clavulanate is first-line for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis - and imaging is NOT needed for uncomplicated disease","Proptosis, painful/restricted eye movements, or vision change means an orbital complication - urgent CT, IV antibiotics, and drainage","Forehead swelling (Pott's puffy tumor) and neurologic signs signal frontal osteomyelitis/intracranial spread - a surgical emergency"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Adult Sinusitis","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Sinusitis / Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547701/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-chronic-rhinosinusitis","name":"Chronic Rhinosinusitis & Nasal Polyps","sec":"ent","present":["CHRONIC RHINOSINUSITIS (CRS): sinonasal inflammation lasting >=12 weeks with >=2 cardinal symptoms - nasal obstruction, anterior/posterior discharge, facial pressure/pain, and reduced SMELL (hyposmia/anosmia)","Phenotypes: CRS WITH nasal polyps (CRSwNP) and CRS WITHOUT polyps (CRSsNP); a major endotype is TYPE 2 (eosinophilic) inflammation, associated with polyps, ASTHMA, and AERD (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease - SAMTER'S TRIAD: nasal polyps, asthma, and aspirin/NSAID sensitivity)","Nasal POLYPS are benign edematous outgrowths; a UNILATERAL polyp/mass warrants exclusion of a tumor"],"dx":["Diagnosis requires objective evidence of inflammation: nasal ENDOSCOPY (polyps, edema, purulent discharge) and/or CT of the sinuses (mucosal thickening, opacification) plus the symptom criteria","Characterize the endotype (eosinophilia, allergy, asthma/AERD); a UNILATERAL mass needs biopsy/imaging to exclude neoplasm (inverted papilloma, malignancy)","Assess for allergy and asthma comorbidity"],"tx":["Medical mainstay: saline IRRIGATION and INTRANASAL CORTICOSTEROIDS (topical, high-volume for polyps); short oral steroid courses for polyp flares; treat comorbid allergy/asthma; antibiotics for acute exacerbations","BIOLOGICS (e.g., dupilumab and other anti-type-2 agents) for severe CRSwNP; ENDOSCOPIC SINUS SURGERY (FESS) for disease refractory to medical therapy (improves access for topical therapy)","AERD: aspirin desensitization in selected patients"],"tech":["Functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS)/polypectomy for refractory disease; steroid-eluting implants; ongoing topical therapy delivery","Biologic administration for severe type-2 disease","Aspirin desensitization for AERD"],"after":["Long-term TOPICAL steroid/irrigation MAINTENANCE is essential - CRSwNP RECURS, and surgery is not a cure; monitor and treat comorbid asthma/allergy","Surveillance for polyp recurrence and for a unilateral mass; escalate to biologics for refractory type-2 disease","Ongoing multidisciplinary care (allergy/pulmonology)"],"pearls":["Chronic rhinosinusitis needs objective evidence (endoscopy or CT) plus symptoms - do not diagnose it on symptoms alone","Nasal polyps, asthma, and aspirin/NSAID sensitivity is Samter's triad (AERD) - a type-2 eosinophilic endotype that recurs and may benefit from biologics or aspirin desensitization","A UNILATERAL nasal polyp/mass is a tumor until proven otherwise - biopsy/image it (inverted papilloma, malignancy)","Surgery is not a cure for CRSwNP - long-term topical steroids/irrigation maintenance prevents recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Chronic Rhinosinusitis / Nasal Polyp Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Rhinosinusitis / Nasal Polyps","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441934/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-rhinitis","name":"Allergic & Non-Allergic Rhinitis","sec":"ent","present":["Inflammation of the nasal mucosa causing congestion, rhinorrhea, sneezing, and itching. ALLERGIC RHINITIS (IgE-mediated): sneezing, ITCHING (nose/eyes/palate), clear watery rhinorrhea, and congestion, seasonal or perennial, often with allergic 'shiners', a nasal crease, and boggy/pale turbinates with cobblestoning","NON-ALLERGIC RHINITIS: chronic nasal symptoms WITHOUT allergic sensitization - includes vasomotor rhinitis (triggered by temperature/odors), gustatory rhinitis (with eating), and rhinitis medicamentosa (rebound from overused topical decongestants)","Allergic rhinitis is very common and linked to asthma, sinusitis, and OME"],"dx":["Clinical history distinguishes the types (itching/sneezing and identifiable allergen triggers favor allergic); ALLERGY TESTING (skin-prick or specific IgE) confirms sensitization and guides avoidance/immunotherapy","Nasal exam (pale boggy turbinates in allergic; assess for polyps, septal deviation); identify rhinitis medicamentosa (history of chronic topical decongestant use)","Assess comorbid asthma, sinusitis, and conjunctivitis"],"tx":["ALLERGIC RHINITIS: allergen AVOIDANCE, INTRANASAL CORTICOSTEROIDS (the most effective single therapy), antihistamines (oral/intranasal), leukotriene antagonists, and saline; IMMUNOTHERAPY (subcutaneous or sublingual) for refractory/allergen-specific disease (potentially disease-modifying)","NON-ALLERGIC: intranasal steroids/antihistamines and ipratropium (for rhinorrhea); STOP overused topical decongestants for rhinitis medicamentosa (with a steroid taper)","Treat comorbidities (asthma, sinusitis)"],"tech":["No routine procedure; allergen immunotherapy (subcutaneous/sublingual); turbinate reduction for refractory turbinate hypertrophy contributing to obstruction","Allergy testing to guide immunotherapy","Address structural contributors surgically if needed"],"after":["Chronic management and adherence to intranasal steroids (best when used consistently); immunotherapy over years for durable benefit; avoid rebound from topical decongestants","Monitor and treat comorbid asthma/sinusitis; reassess response and escalate","Environmental control counseling"],"pearls":["Itching and sneezing with identifiable triggers favor ALLERGIC rhinitis; their absence with congestion/rhinorrhea suggests non-allergic","Intranasal corticosteroids are the single most effective therapy for allergic rhinitis - and work best used regularly","Rhinitis medicamentosa is rebound congestion from overusing topical decongestants - stop the spray (with a steroid bridge)","Immunotherapy is the disease-modifying option for refractory, allergen-specific allergic rhinitis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Allergic Rhinitis","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Allergic Rhinitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538186/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-nasal-obstruction","name":"Nasal Obstruction & Septal Deviation","sec":"ent","present":["A very common complaint with multiple contributors: a deviated nasal SEPTUM, inferior TURBINATE hypertrophy (allergic/vasomotor), NASAL VALVE collapse (dynamic narrowing at the internal/external valve), nasal POLYPS, adenoid hypertrophy (children), and mucosal disease","Often multifactorial - obstruction may fluctuate (turbinate cycling, allergy) or be fixed (structural deviation, valve collapse)","Presents with difficulty breathing through the nose, mouth breathing, snoring, and reduced quality of life/sleep"],"dx":["Anterior rhinoscopy and NASAL ENDOSCOPY to identify the site(s) of obstruction (septum, turbinates, valve, polyps, masses); the COTTLE maneuver (lateral cheek retraction improving airflow) suggests nasal valve compromise","Decongest to separate mucosal (reversible) from structural (fixed) obstruction; assess for allergy and, in children, adenoid hypertrophy","Exclude a unilateral mass/tumor"],"tx":["MEDICAL therapy first for mucosal/allergic contributors: intranasal corticosteroids, allergy treatment, and saline","SURGICAL correction of structural causes: SEPTOPLASTY (deviated septum), turbinate REDUCTION (hypertrophy), and nasal VALVE repair/spreader grafts (valve collapse); functional rhinoplasty for external deformity","Address polyps (see CRS) and adenoids (children) as indicated"],"tech":["Septoplasty (straighten/resect the deviated septum), inferior turbinate reduction (submucosal/outfracture), nasal valve repair (spreader grafts, batten grafts, functional rhinoplasty); adenoidectomy in children","Endoscopic assessment/technique","Combined structural procedures as needed"],"after":["Post-septoplasty care (splints/packing per practice, saline); reassess airflow and treat residual mucosal disease; recurrence of turbinate hypertrophy is possible","Manage the multifactorial contributors (allergy) to optimize the result","Follow-up for symptom relief"],"pearls":["Nasal obstruction is usually multifactorial - decongest to separate fixed structural causes (septum, valve) from reversible mucosal ones (turbinates, allergy)","Treat mucosal/allergic contributors medically first - many patients improve without surgery","The Cottle maneuver flags nasal valve collapse - a cause of obstruction that septoplasty alone will not fix","Correct the specific structural problem: septoplasty for the septum, turbinate reduction for hypertrophy, valve repair for collapse"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Nasal Obstruction Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Nasal Septal Deviation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542177/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-sinonasal-masses","name":"Sinonasal Masses & CSF Rhinorrhea","sec":"ent","present":["UNILATERAL sinonasal symptoms (obstruction, epistaxis, a mass, facial pain/numbness) should raise concern for a TUMOR: benign (INVERTED PAPILLOMA - unilateral, HPV-associated, ~can harbor squamous cell carcinoma; osteoma; juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma) and malignant (squamous cell carcinoma, ESTHESIONEUROBLASTOMA, adenoid cystic carcinoma, melanoma)","CSF RHINORRHEA: clear, watery unilateral nasal drainage from a skull-base defect (traumatic, iatrogenic/post-surgical, or spontaneous - often in obese women with elevated intracranial pressure), which risks ascending MENINGITIS","Red flags: unilateral disease, epistaxis, cranial neuropathies, orbital symptoms, and clear rhinorrhea"],"dx":["Nasal ENDOSCOPY and imaging (CT for bony detail, MRI for soft-tissue/intracranial extent); BIOPSY suspicious masses - but do NOT biopsy a suspected vascular lesion (juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma) in the office; image first","CSF rhinorrhea: confirm with BETA-2 TRANSFERRIN testing of the fluid (specific for CSF); high-resolution CT/MRI and intrathecal fluorescein to localize the defect","Stage neoplasms; assess for skull-base/orbital involvement"],"tx":["SINONASAL TUMORS: SURGICAL RESECTION (increasingly ENDOSCOPIC, sometimes open/craniofacial) - inverted papilloma needs complete removal (recurs, and can harbor malignancy); malignancies get multimodal therapy (surgery +/- radiation/chemotherapy)","CSF RHINORRHEA: many traumatic leaks resolve with conservative measures (bed rest, stool softeners, +/- lumbar drain); persistent/high-flow or spontaneous leaks need ENDOSCOPIC surgical REPAIR of the skull-base defect (and address elevated ICP in spontaneous leaks)","Meningitis prophylaxis considerations and vaccination"],"tech":["Endoscopic (or open/craniofacial) resection of sinonasal tumors; endoscopic skull-base CSF leak repair (grafts/flaps); embolization before resecting vascular tumors (JNA)","Beta-2 transferrin/fluorescein localization for CSF leaks","Multidisciplinary skull-base surgery"],"after":["Tumor SURVEILLANCE for recurrence (inverted papilloma recurs and can transform; malignancy needs oncologic follow-up); reconstruction/rehabilitation","CSF leak: monitor for recurrent leak and MENINGITIS; manage intracranial pressure in spontaneous cases","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["Unilateral nasal obstruction, epistaxis, or a mass is a tumor until proven otherwise - image and biopsy (but never biopsy a suspected angiofibroma in the office - it is vascular)","An inverted papilloma is unilateral, recurs, and can harbor squamous cell carcinoma - it needs complete surgical removal and surveillance","Clear unilateral rhinorrhea is CSF until proven otherwise - confirm with beta-2 transferrin and localize the skull-base defect; it risks meningitis","Spontaneous CSF leaks are often driven by elevated intracranial pressure (obese women) - repair the defect AND address the ICP"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Sinonasal Tumor / Skull Base Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Inverted Papilloma / CSF Rhinorrhea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448097/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-hoarseness-vocal-lesions","name":"Hoarseness & Benign Vocal Fold Lesions","sec":"ent","present":["HOARSENESS (dysphonia) - a change in voice quality; benign vocal fold lesions are a common cause: NODULES (bilateral, symmetric 'singer's/screamer's nodules' from vocal abuse/overuse), POLYPS (usually unilateral, from a phonotrauma event), CYSTS, REINKE'S EDEMA (diffuse edema in smokers), and vocal process GRANULOMAS (reflux/intubation-related)","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (HPV) and, importantly, MALIGNANCY (especially in smokers) also present with hoarseness","A change in voice reflects abnormal vocal fold vibration/closure"],"dx":["LARYNGOSCOPY (flexible, and videoSTROBOSCOPY for the mucosal wave) visualizes the lesion and vocal fold function - the essential examination","Any hoarseness persisting beyond ~2-4 weeks (especially in a SMOKER or with risk factors) mandates laryngoscopy to EXCLUDE CANCER - do not attribute persistent hoarseness to 'laryngitis'","Assess for contributing reflux, vocal behavior, and smoking"],"tx":["NODULES: VOICE THERAPY (behavioral) is first-line and often curative (they are use-related); vocal HYGIENE and treating reflux","POLYPS, CYSTS, and lesions failing conservative care: PHONOSURGERY (microlaryngoscopic excision) with voice therapy; Reinke's edema - smoking cessation +/- surgery; granulomas - reflux treatment/voice therapy (surgery often not needed)","Treat contributing reflux and eliminate vocal abuse/smoking"],"tech":["Microlaryngoscopy with phonomicrosurgical excision of polyps/cysts; laser or cold-steel techniques; injection/office procedures for select lesions","Voice therapy (speech-language pathology) as primary or adjunct treatment","Address reflux/behavioral contributors"],"after":["Voice REST/therapy after phonosurgery and behavioral modification to prevent RECURRENCE (nodules recur if vocal behavior is unchanged); ongoing vocal hygiene","Monitor voice outcome and lesion recurrence; reassess/biopsy any suspicious or non-resolving lesion","Smoking cessation and reflux control"],"pearls":["Hoarseness lasting beyond 2-4 weeks - especially in a smoker - needs laryngoscopy to rule out cancer; do not keep calling it laryngitis","Nodules are bilateral and use-related - voice therapy, not surgery, is first-line and often curative","Polyps and cysts are usually unilateral and often need phonosurgery when conservative measures fail","Fix the behavior (vocal abuse, smoking, reflux) or the benign lesions come back"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Hoarseness (Dysphonia)","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vocal Fold Nodules / Lesions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563209/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-vocal-fold-paralysis","name":"Vocal Fold Paralysis","sec":"ent","present":["Impaired vocal fold mobility from RECURRENT LARYNGEAL NERVE (or vagus) dysfunction. UNILATERAL paralysis: a breathy, weak voice, vocal fatigue, and ASPIRATION (poor glottic closure) - causes include THYROID/parathyroid, cardiac, and thoracic SURGERY, tumors along the nerve's course (thyroid, lung, mediastinum, skull base), and idiopathic (often post-viral)","The LEFT recurrent laryngeal nerve has a longer intrathoracic course (around the aortic arch) and is more commonly affected","BILATERAL paralysis: the folds sit near midline, so the voice may be near-normal but the AIRWAY is compromised (stridor) - potentially needing a tracheostomy"],"dx":["LARYNGOSCOPY confirms the immobile fold and its position; for a paralysis without an obvious surgical cause, IMAGE along the entire nerve course (CT/MRI from skull base to chest) to find a tumor; laryngeal EMG can assess prognosis","Assess GLOTTIC closure and ASPIRATION risk (unilateral) and the AIRWAY (bilateral)","Idiopathic paralysis is a diagnosis of exclusion (often recovers)"],"tx":["UNILATERAL: voice therapy, and for persistent glottic insufficiency/aspiration - vocal fold INJECTION augmentation (temporary/durable) or MEDIALIZATION laryngoplasty (THYROPLASTY, permanent) to improve closure","BILATERAL: prioritize the AIRWAY - options include TRACHEOSTOMY, and airway-widening procedures (cordotomy/arytenoidectomy) that trade voice for airway; treat any reversible cause","Await possible recovery (especially idiopathic) before permanent procedures when appropriate; treat the underlying cause (tumor)"],"tech":["Vocal fold injection augmentation (medialization); type I thyroplasty (medialization laryngoplasty) +/- arytenoid adduction; for bilateral paralysis - tracheostomy, transverse cordotomy, or arytenoidectomy","Laryngeal EMG for prognosis; imaging-guided workup","Treat the causative lesion"],"after":["Monitor voice, swallowing, and aspiration; unilateral idiopathic paralysis may recover over months (temporary injection can bridge); reassess before permanent medialization","Bilateral paralysis: airway monitoring and balancing airway vs voice; decannulation planning if applicable","Manage the underlying cause and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["A breathy voice with aspiration after thyroid/cardiac/thoracic surgery is unilateral vocal fold paralysis - and the left nerve's long thoracic course makes it more vulnerable","Unilateral paralysis without a clear surgical cause needs imaging along the whole nerve (skull base to chest) to exclude a tumor","Unilateral paralysis threatens the VOICE and swallowing; bilateral paralysis threatens the AIRWAY (folds near midline) and may need a tracheostomy","Bridge idiopathic unilateral paralysis with a temporary injection - it may recover, so delay permanent medialization when appropriate"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Vocal Fold Paralysis Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vocal Cord Paralysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441907/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-lpr","name":"Laryngopharyngeal Reflux","sec":"ent","present":["Retrograde flow of gastric contents to the LARYNGOPHARYNX causing laryngeal irritation - distinct from classic GERD in that HEARTBURN is often ABSENT ('silent reflux')","Symptoms: hoarseness, chronic throat CLEARING, GLOBUS (lump-in-throat sensation), chronic cough, postnasal-drip sensation, and mild dysphagia","Laryngoscopic signs: posterior laryngeal erythema/edema, interarytenoid changes, and pseudosulcus - captured by the Reflux Finding Score/Reflux Symptom Index (nonspecific)"],"dx":["Largely a CLINICAL diagnosis (symptom pattern + laryngoscopic findings); an empiric therapeutic TRIAL is a reasonable diagnostic step","pH/impedance testing (dual-probe) for refractory/atypical cases; laryngoscopy to exclude other laryngeal pathology (lesions, malignancy)","Recognize the findings are nonspecific - avoid over-diagnosis and rule out other causes of the symptoms"],"tx":["LIFESTYLE/DIETARY modification (weight loss, avoid late meals/triggers, elevate the head of bed) and acid suppression - PROTON PUMP INHIBITORS (often higher-dose/longer course than for GERD), H2 blockers, and alginates","Reassess response over weeks-months; refractory, well-documented reflux may warrant anti-reflux (fundoplication) surgery","Manage contributing behaviors (throat clearing, vocal hygiene)"],"tech":["No routine procedure - laryngoscopy for diagnosis, pH/impedance probe for refractory cases; anti-reflux surgery (fundoplication) in select refractory disease","Behavioral/dietary counseling","Voice therapy for associated muscle tension"],"after":["Chronic management with lifestyle measures and a stepwise medication plan; counsel that response can be gradual (weeks-months)","Reassess and de-escalate; investigate further if refractory (pH testing, other diagnoses)","Long-term reflux/behavioral control"],"pearls":["Laryngopharyngeal reflux is often 'silent' - hoarseness, throat clearing, globus, and cough WITHOUT heartburn","The laryngoscopic findings are nonspecific - avoid over-diagnosing LPR and exclude other causes (including malignancy) of persistent laryngeal symptoms","Treatment usually needs higher-dose, longer PPI courses than GERD plus lifestyle change - and response is gradual","Persistent hoarseness still needs laryngoscopy to rule out cancer - do not just treat empirically forever"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Laryngopharyngeal Reflux Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngopharyngeal Reflux","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519548/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-dysphagia","name":"Dysphagia & Pharyngoesophageal Disorders (incl Zenker)","sec":"ent","present":["Difficulty swallowing - separate OROPHARYNGEAL (transfer) dysphagia (trouble initiating the swallow, coughing/choking, nasal regurgitation, ASPIRATION - often neurologic/structural) from ESOPHAGEAL dysphagia (food sticking after the swallow)","ZENKER DIVERTICULUM: a posterior hypopharyngeal outpouching through Killian's dehiscence from cricopharyngeal dysfunction - typically older adults with dysphagia, REGURGITATION of undigested food, halitosis, gurgling, and aspiration/weight loss","Aspiration is the dangerous consequence of oropharyngeal dysphagia"],"dx":["Instrumented swallow evaluation: MODIFIED BARIUM SWALLOW (videofluoroscopy) and/or FLEXIBLE ENDOSCOPIC EVALUATION OF SWALLOWING (FEES) to assess mechanism, penetration/aspiration, and safety; esophagram/manometry for esophageal-phase and cricopharyngeal dysfunction","Zenker: barium esophagram shows the pouch; assess for malignancy in a chronic pouch","Identify the underlying cause (stroke/neurologic, structural, malignancy)"],"tx":["OROPHARYNGEAL dysphagia: SWALLOW THERAPY (speech-language pathology), diet/texture modification, and aspiration precautions; treat the underlying cause; feeding tube if unsafe swallow","ZENKER DIVERTICULUM: surgical - CRICOPHARYNGEAL MYOTOMY with diverticulectomy/diverticulopexy (open) OR ENDOSCOPIC stapling/laser diverticulotomy (division of the common wall/cricopharyngeus) - endoscopic approaches offer faster recovery","Address reflux and nutritional status"],"tech":["Endoscopic (stapler or CO2 laser) diverticulotomy with cricopharyngeal myotomy, or open diverticulectomy/diverticulopexy + myotomy (Zenker); swallow rehabilitation for oropharyngeal dysphagia","FEES/MBS-guided therapy","Feeding access when needed"],"after":["Zenker: diet advancement per protocol; monitor for RECURRENCE and complications (leak, mediastinitis - rare); ongoing swallow therapy for dysphagia","Aspiration precautions and nutritional monitoring; reassess swallow safety","Long-term follow-up (recurrence, aspiration)"],"pearls":["Separate oropharyngeal (transfer, coughing/aspiration) from esophageal (food sticking after swallow) dysphagia - they have different workups and teams","An older adult with dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, and halitosis has a Zenker diverticulum until proven otherwise - get a barium swallow","Zenker treatment always includes a cricopharyngeal MYOTOMY (the underlying dysfunction) - endoscopic approaches speed recovery","Aspiration is the danger of oropharyngeal dysphagia - FEES/MBS assess safety and guide diet/therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Dysphagia / Zenker Diverticulum Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Zenker Diverticulum / Dysphagia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499996/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-osa","name":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Snoring","sec":"ent","present":["Repetitive UPPER AIRWAY collapse during sleep causing apneas/hypopneas, intermittent hypoxia, and sleep fragmentation - a common, under-diagnosed disorder with major cardiovascular and neurocognitive consequences","Presents with SNORING, WITNESSED APNEAS, gasping/choking, and daytime SLEEPINESS (screen with STOP-BANG/Epworth); associated with obesity, retrognathia, large tonsils, and (in children) adenotonsillar hypertrophy","Untreated OSA raises risks of hypertension, arrhythmia, stroke, metabolic disease, and motor vehicle accidents"],"dx":["POLYSOMNOGRAPHY (the gold standard) or home sleep apnea testing quantifies severity by the APNEA-HYPOPNEA INDEX (AHI); assess oxygen desaturation and comorbidity","Examine the upper airway for the site(s) of obstruction (nose, palate/tonsils, tongue base) - drug-induced sleep endoscopy helps surgical planning; assess pediatric tonsil/adenoid size","Screen with validated questionnaires"],"tx":["ADULTS: CPAP is first-line (highly effective when tolerated), plus weight loss and positional therapy; ORAL APPLIANCES (mandibular advancement) for mild-moderate disease or CPAP intolerance","SURGERY for CPAP-intolerant/anatomically-appropriate patients: nasal surgery, UVULOPALATOPHARYNGOPLASTY (UPPP), HYPOGLOSSAL NERVE STIMULATION (upper airway stimulation), and maxillomandibular advancement","CHILDREN: ADENOTONSILLECTOMY is first-line for pediatric OSA from adenotonsillar hypertrophy"],"tech":["Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP), hypoglossal nerve stimulator implantation, maxillomandibular advancement, and nasal/tongue-base procedures (adults); ADENOTONSILLECTOMY (children); CPAP/oral appliance fitting","Drug-induced sleep endoscopy for site-specific planning","Multilevel/individualized surgery"],"after":["CPAP ADHERENCE support and follow-up (the main challenge); post-surgical or post-treatment repeat sleep study to confirm response; weight management","Monitor and treat cardiovascular/metabolic comorbidity; pediatric OSA - reassess after adenotonsillectomy (residual OSA in obese/syndromic children)","Long-term follow-up and driving-safety counseling"],"pearls":["Snoring with witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness needs a sleep study - the AHI grades severity and untreated OSA raises cardiovascular and accident risk","CPAP is first-line for adults (adherence is the battle); oral appliances and surgery (UPPP, hypoglossal nerve stimulation, MMA) are options for intolerance","Adenotonsillectomy is first-line for pediatric OSA from adenotonsillar hypertrophy","Localize the obstruction (drug-induced sleep endoscopy) before choosing airway surgery - one size does not fit all"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS OSA / Sleep Surgery Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Obstructive Sleep Apnea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459252/"},{"t":"AASM Sleep Medicine Resources","u":"https://aasm.org"}]},{"id":"ent-neck-mass","name":"Neck Mass Evaluation (Adult)","sec":"ent","present":["A neck mass in an ADULT is approached with a malignancy-first mindset: a persistent (>2-3 weeks), firm neck mass in an adult over ~40 - especially with tobacco/alcohol use or HPV risk - is METASTATIC head-and-neck cancer (usually squamous cell carcinoma) until proven otherwise","The differential also includes congenital cysts (which can mimic - a cystic node may be metastatic HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer or papillary thyroid cancer), inflammatory/infectious lymphadenopathy, lymphoma, thyroid, and salivary masses","Location and character (midline vs lateral, cystic vs solid, pulsatile) narrow the differential"],"dx":["History (smoking, alcohol, HPV risk, B symptoms, duration) and complete head-and-neck EXAM including flexible endoscopy to find a primary; FINE-NEEDLE ASPIRATION (FNA) is the initial tissue diagnosis of choice - do NOT do an OPEN excisional biopsy first (it can compromise later cancer treatment)","Contrast CT/MRI (and PET-CT) for staging/primary search; HPV/p16 and EBV testing on aspirate as indicated; EXAM UNDER ANESTHESIA/PANENDOSCOPY with directed biopsies for an unknown primary","Age and risk factors drive the aggressiveness of workup"],"tx":["Directed by the diagnosis: malignancy -> stage and treat per site (surgery +/- chemoradiation); lymphoma -> oncology; infectious/inflammatory -> treat the cause","For a metastatic squamous node with an occult primary, proceed with panendoscopy/directed biopsies (and tonsillectomy) to find the primary; treat per protocol","Avoid delaying cancer diagnosis - a persistent adult neck mass needs prompt workup"],"tech":["Fine-needle aspiration (initial); panendoscopy with directed biopsies and diagnostic tonsillectomy (unknown primary); definitive surgery/neck dissection per diagnosis","Imaging-guided FNA for deep/cystic masses","Excisional biopsy only when FNA is nondiagnostic and lymphoma is suspected (with planning)"],"after":["Complete staging and initiate treatment; surveillance per the underlying diagnosis; counseling on risk-factor modification (tobacco/alcohol)","Multidisciplinary tumor board for malignancy; follow-up per protocol","Address the primary site once identified"],"pearls":["A persistent neck mass in an adult over 40 is metastatic head-and-neck cancer until proven otherwise - do not just observe or antibiotic-treat it","FNA first, NOT open excisional biopsy - an unplanned open biopsy of a nodal metastasis can worsen oncologic outcomes","A cystic lateral neck mass in an adult can be metastatic HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer (or papillary thyroid cancer) - not just a benign branchial cyst","Scope the upper aerodigestive tract to find the primary, and test the aspirate for HPV/p16 and EBV"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Adult Neck Mass","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neck Mass","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513237/"},{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"ent-oral-cavity-cancer","name":"Oral Cavity Cancer & Premalignant Lesions","sec":"ent","present":["SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA of the oral cavity (tongue, floor of mouth, buccal mucosa, alveolus, hard palate) - strongly linked to TOBACCO, ALCOHOL, and BETEL nut; presents as a non-healing ULCER, mass, pain, bleeding, loose teeth, or referred OTALGIA","PREMALIGNANT lesions: LEUKOPLAKIA (a white patch that cannot be wiped off - BIOPSY it), ERYTHROPLAKIA (a red patch - HIGHER malignant potential than leukoplakia), and oral lichen planus/submucous fibrosis","Unlike oropharyngeal cancer, oral cavity SCC is largely NOT HPV-driven"],"dx":["Examination (visualize and PALPATE the lesion/neck) and BIOPSY of any suspicious or non-healing lesion (a persistent oral ulcer >2 weeks needs biopsy); imaging (CT/MRI) for extent and nodal disease; staging","Erythroplakia and mixed erythroleukoplakia carry the highest dysplasia/cancer risk - biopsy them","Assess mandibular invasion and neck nodes"],"tx":["PREMALIGNANT: excision/close surveillance of dysplastic lesions and TOBACCO/alcohol cessation (field cancerization risk)","CANCER: primarily SURGICAL resection (with adequate margins, +/- mandibulectomy) and NECK DISSECTION for at-risk necks, with RECONSTRUCTION (free flaps for larger defects); adjuvant RADIATION or chemoradiation for high-risk features (positive margins, extranodal extension)","Dental evaluation and prehabilitation before treatment"],"tech":["Wide local resection (glossectomy, floor-of-mouth resection, mandibulectomy as needed) with neck dissection; free-flap reconstruction; sentinel node biopsy in select early cancers","Adjuvant radiation/chemoradiation planning","Dental extraction/prophylaxis pre-radiation"],"after":["Speech and SWALLOWING rehabilitation; dental care and osteoradionecrosis prevention; surveillance for recurrence and SECOND primaries (field cancerization); tobacco/alcohol cessation","Monitor for treatment sequelae (xerostomia, trismus, dysphagia); nutritional support","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["A non-healing oral ulcer or a patch lasting more than 2 weeks needs a biopsy - do not keep treating it as an aphthous ulcer","Erythroplakia (red patch) has higher malignant potential than leukoplakia (white patch) - biopsy both, but respect the red one","Oral cavity SCC is treated primarily with surgery (+/- neck dissection and reconstruction), unlike the chemoradiation-favored oropharynx","Field cancerization means lifelong surveillance for second primaries - and tobacco/alcohol cessation is core therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Oral Cancer / Leukoplakia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430950/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-oropharyngeal-cancer","name":"Oropharyngeal Cancer (HPV-Related)","sec":"ent","present":["Squamous cell carcinoma of the oropharynx (palatine TONSIL, BASE OF TONGUE, soft palate) - now predominantly driven by HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS (HPV-16), a distinct disease from the tobacco/alcohol-related type","HPV-associated cancers occur in YOUNGER, often non-smoking patients, frequently present as a CYSTIC NECK NODE (with a small/occult primary), and have a substantially BETTER prognosis (p16-positive)","Presents with a neck mass, sore throat, dysphagia, referred otalgia, or a tonsil asymmetry/mass"],"dx":["Examination and flexible endoscopy; BIOPSY of the primary (or FNA of the neck node) with HPV/p16 testing (p16 immunohistochemistry as a surrogate) - which defines the disease and prognosis and drives a SEPARATE AJCC staging system for HPV+ disease","Imaging (CT/MRI, PET-CT) for staging and to find an occult primary (tonsillectomy/base-of-tongue mapping for unknown primary)","Assess smoking status (affects prognosis even in HPV+ disease)"],"tx":["Curative options: TRANSORAL surgery (TRANSORAL ROBOTIC SURGERY - TORS - for accessible primaries) with neck dissection, OR definitive (chemo)RADIATION - both give excellent outcomes for HPV+ disease","DE-ESCALATION strategies (reducing treatment intensity to limit toxicity while preserving the high cure rates) are an active area for HPV+ disease - via clinical trials/protocols","Adjuvant therapy based on pathology (extranodal extension, margins)"],"tech":["Transoral robotic surgery (TORS)/transoral laser microsurgery for the primary + neck dissection; definitive intensity-modulated radiation +/- chemotherapy","Diagnostic tonsillectomy/base-of-tongue mapping for unknown primary","Reconstruction as needed"],"after":["Surveillance for recurrence; manage treatment sequelae - dysphagia, XEROSTOMIA, and fibrosis (radiation) - with swallow therapy; the prognosis is favorable for HPV+ disease","Counsel on HPV and prevention (HPV vaccination reduces future disease); smoking cessation","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer is a distinct, rising disease - younger non-smokers, often a cystic neck node with an occult primary, and a much better prognosis","Test the tumor for p16/HPV - it changes staging (separate AJCC system), prognosis, and eligibility for de-escalation","Both TORS and definitive chemoradiation cure most HPV+ disease - the choice balances functional outcomes and toxicity","A cystic lateral neck node in an adult is metastatic HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer until proven otherwise - not a benign branchial cyst"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Oropharyngeal Cancer / HPV","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563268/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-laryngeal-cancer","name":"Laryngeal & Hypopharyngeal Cancer","sec":"ent","present":["LARYNGEAL squamous cell carcinoma - strongly TOBACCO-related; GLOTTIC cancer (vocal fold) presents EARLY with persistent HOARSENESS (a good prognostic feature - fewer nodes), while SUPRAGLOTTIC cancer presents later (dysphagia, referred otalgia, a neck node) due to richer lymphatics","HYPOPHARYNGEAL cancer (pyriform sinus, postcricoid, posterior wall) presents LATE with dysphagia, odynophagia, referred OTALGIA, and neck nodes - and has a POOR prognosis with a high rate of nodal/second-primary disease","Airway compromise and aspiration can complicate advanced disease"],"dx":["LARYNGOSCOPY (flexible +/- stroboscopy) and direct laryngoscopy with BIOPSY; imaging (CT/MRI, PET-CT) for extent, cartilage invasion, and nodal staging; assess vocal fold mobility","Any hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks (especially in a smoker) mandates laryngoscopy","Assess the airway and swallowing; panendoscopy for second primaries"],"tx":["EARLY-STAGE (T1-T2) glottic cancer: single-modality - RADIATION or transoral laser/partial laryngeal SURGERY, both with excellent cure and VOICE PRESERVATION","ADVANCED disease: ORGAN-PRESERVATION chemoRADIATION (with salvage surgery for failure) OR TOTAL LARYNGECTOMY (for extensive disease, cartilage invasion, or a nonfunctional larynx), with NECK DISSECTION","Hypopharyngeal cancer: multimodal therapy (surgery + adjuvant, or chemoradiation), often with reconstruction"],"tech":["Transoral laser microsurgery/partial laryngectomy (early disease); total laryngectomy (+ pharyngectomy for hypopharynx) with neck dissection and reconstruction; definitive/adjuvant (chemo)radiation","Airway management for obstructing tumors (tracheostomy)","Voice-restoration planning (tracheoesophageal puncture)"],"after":["VOICE and SWALLOWING rehabilitation - after total laryngectomy, voice restoration via TRACHEOESOPHAGEAL PUNCTURE/prosthesis, electrolarynx, or esophageal speech, and permanent STOMA care; surveillance for recurrence and second primaries","Manage radiation sequelae (xerostomia, dysphagia, stricture, hypothyroidism); nutritional support","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up and smoking cessation"],"pearls":["Persistent hoarseness in a smoker is glottic cancer until laryngoscopy proves otherwise - and glottic cancers present early with a good prognosis","Early glottic cancer is cured with single-modality radiation or transoral surgery, preserving the voice","A total laryngectomy patient breathes through a permanent stoma and needs planned voice rehabilitation (tracheoesophageal puncture/prosthesis, electrolarynx)","Hypopharyngeal cancer presents late with dysphagia and referred otalgia and carries a poor prognosis - keep a high index of suspicion"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngeal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526076/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-nasopharyngeal-carcinoma","name":"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","sec":"ent","present":["A DISTINCT head-and-neck cancer arising in the nasopharynx - strongly associated with EPSTEIN-BARR VIRUS (EBV) and ENDEMIC in Southern China/Southeast Asia (and with dietary/genetic factors), often the non-keratinizing type","Presents insidiously: a NECK MASS (upper cervical/retropharyngeal nodes - a very common presentation), EPISTAXIS/nasal obstruction, UNILATERAL serous OTITIS MEDIA (eustachian tube obstruction - a classic adult red flag), and CRANIAL NEUROPATHIES with skull-base extension","Unlike most head-and-neck SCC, it is not primarily tobacco/alcohol-driven and is managed non-surgically"],"dx":["NASOPHARYNGOSCOPY with BIOPSY of the primary; plasma EBV DNA (diagnostic/prognostic/surveillance marker); MRI (best for soft-tissue/skull-base extent) and PET-CT for staging","A UNILATERAL serous otitis media in an adult should prompt nasopharyngeal examination to exclude NPC","Assess cranial nerves and skull base"],"tx":["NASOPHARYNGEAL CARCINOMA IS RADIOSENSITIVE - the primary treatment is RADIATION (intensity-modulated) with concurrent/adjuvant CHEMOTHERAPY for advanced disease - NOT primary surgery (the location and radiosensitivity favor chemoradiation)","SURGERY (nasopharyngectomy) and re-irradiation are reserved for salvage of persistent/recurrent disease; neck dissection for residual nodal disease","EBV DNA monitoring guides response/surveillance"],"tech":["Definitive chemoradiation (IMRT) as primary treatment; salvage endoscopic/open nasopharyngectomy and neck dissection for recurrence","EBV DNA-guided monitoring","Manage skull-base extension multidisciplinarily"],"after":["Surveillance including plasma EBV DNA, endoscopy, and imaging; manage significant RADIATION sequelae (xerostomia, hearing loss, hypothyroidism, temporal lobe effects, trismus, cranial neuropathies)","Hearing rehabilitation and dental care; nutritional support","Long-term follow-up (late recurrence/second effects)"],"pearls":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is EBV-associated, endemic to Southern China/SE Asia, and RADIOSENSITIVE - treated with chemoradiation, not primary surgery","A unilateral serous otitis media in an adult is a red flag - examine the nasopharynx to exclude NPC","Neck node, epistaxis/nasal obstruction, unilateral ear effusion, and cranial neuropathies are the classic presenting cluster","Plasma EBV DNA is a useful diagnostic, prognostic, and surveillance marker"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459256/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-salivary-tumors","name":"Salivary Gland Tumors","sec":"ent","present":["Neoplasms of the major (PAROTID, submandibular, sublingual) and minor salivary glands; the PAROTID is the most common site and most parotid tumors are BENIGN, while the SMALLER the gland, the HIGHER the likelihood of MALIGNANCY (submandibular/sublingual/minor glands)","BENIGN: PLEOMORPHIC ADENOMA (the most common salivary tumor - a mobile, painless mass) and Warthin tumor (older male smokers, can be bilateral); MALIGNANT: mucoepidermoid carcinoma (most common salivary malignancy), adenoid cystic carcinoma (perineural spread), and others","FEATURES suggesting MALIGNANCY: pain, rapid growth, FACIAL NERVE weakness (in a parotid mass), fixation, and cervical nodes"],"dx":["FINE-NEEDLE ASPIRATION and MRI (best soft-tissue characterization) evaluate a salivary mass; facial nerve assessment for parotid masses (weakness suggests malignancy)","Distinguish benign from malignant to plan surgery and neck management","Assess for perineural spread (adenoid cystic) and nodal disease"],"tx":["PLEOMORPHIC ADENOMA: SURGICAL removal with a cuff of normal tissue (superficial/partial parotidectomy) - do NOT enucleate (recurs, and risk of malignant transformation over time)","Parotid tumors: superficial or total PAROTIDECTOMY with FACIAL NERVE PRESERVATION; submandibular tumors - gland excision; MALIGNANT tumors - resection + NECK DISSECTION (for high-grade/nodal disease) + adjuvant RADIATION","Balance completeness of resection against facial nerve preservation"],"tech":["Parotidectomy (superficial/total) with meticulous facial nerve dissection/monitoring; submandibular gland excision; neck dissection and adjuvant radiation for malignancy","Facial nerve grafting if the nerve must be sacrificed for cancer","Reconstruction as needed"],"after":["Monitor FACIAL NERVE function; manage FREY SYNDROME (gustatory sweating after parotidectomy), sialocele, and first-bite syndrome; surveillance for recurrence (pleomorphic adenoma recurs if incompletely excised; adenoid cystic recurs late)","Adjuvant radiation follow-up for malignancy; hearing/cosmetic considerations","Long-term follow-up (adenoid cystic has late recurrences/metastases)"],"pearls":["The smaller the salivary gland, the higher the chance a tumor is malignant - parotid tumors are mostly benign, minor-gland tumors often are not","Pleomorphic adenoma is removed with a cuff of normal tissue (parotidectomy), never enucleated - it recurs and can undergo malignant transformation","Facial nerve weakness in a parotid mass strongly suggests malignancy - and facial nerve preservation guides the operation","Adenoid cystic carcinoma spreads perineurally and recurs late - long-term surveillance is essential"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck Cancer / Salivary Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Salivary Gland Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538279/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-hn-cutaneous-malignancy","name":"Head & Neck Cutaneous Malignancy","sec":"ent","present":["Skin cancers of the head and neck (this content shares with the dermatology framing, with an ENT/surgical and nodal lens) - the head/face is the most sun-exposed, highest-incidence site","BASAL CELL CARCINOMA (most common - pearly, telangiectatic, rolled-border lesions on the nose/ear/cheek; locally destructive, rarely metastasizes); SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (can METASTASIZE to the PAROTID and neck nodes, with high-risk features); and MELANOMA (nodal/distant spread)","High-risk cutaneous SCC features: large size, depth, poor differentiation, PERINEURAL invasion, immunosuppression, and recurrence"],"dx":["BIOPSY confirms the diagnosis and depth; for high-risk SCC/melanoma assess regional nodes (the PAROTID is the first echelon for many facial/scalp/ear cutaneous SCCs) and image for nodal/perineural/deep spread","Assess perineural invasion (facial numbness/weakness) and margins","Sentinel node biopsy for appropriate melanomas"],"tx":["Complete excision with clear margins - MOHS micrographic surgery is preferred for the FACE (cosmetically sensitive, high-risk sites) to spare tissue while clearing margins; standard wide excision for others","High-risk SCC/melanoma with nodal/parotid metastasis: PAROTIDECTOMY and/or NECK DISSECTION +/- adjuvant RADIATION; systemic/immunotherapy for advanced disease","RECONSTRUCTION of facial defects (see facial plastics)"],"tech":["Mohs micrographic surgery/wide local excision; parotidectomy and neck dissection for nodal metastasis; sentinel node biopsy (melanoma); facial reconstruction (flaps/grafts)","Adjuvant radiation planning","Perineural/deep-margin management"],"after":["SURVEILLANCE for recurrence, new primaries, and regional/nodal spread; strict SUN PROTECTION and skin surveillance (field damage); manage immunosuppression risk","Reconstruction follow-up (function/cosmesis); adjuvant therapy monitoring","Long-term dermatologic and ENT follow-up"],"pearls":["Head and neck skin is the highest-incidence site for skin cancer - basal cell is most common, but cutaneous SCC can metastasize to the PAROTID and neck nodes","Mohs surgery is preferred on the face for margin control with tissue preservation","Facial cutaneous SCC drains to the parotid first - a parotid mass in a patient with prior facial skin cancer is metastasis until proven otherwise","High-risk SCC features (perineural invasion, immunosuppression, size/depth) drive nodal treatment and adjuvant radiation"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Cutaneous Malignancy Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441939/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS / Facial Plastic Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-thyroid-nodules-cancer","name":"Thyroid Nodules & Thyroid Cancer","sec":"ent","present":["Thyroid nodules are very common and mostly BENIGN (this content shares with the general-surgery/endocrine framing, ENT/head-and-neck-surgery lens); the task is to identify the minority that are malignant","THYROID CANCER types: PAPILLARY (most common, spreads to cervical lymph NODES, generally excellent prognosis), FOLLICULAR (hematogenous spread), MEDULLARY (from parafollicular C-cells - calcitonin, associated with MEN2/RET), and ANAPLASTIC (rare, aggressive, rapidly enlarging neck mass in the elderly - often fatal)","Presents as a palpable nodule/neck mass; hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement) or fixation raises concern for malignancy"],"dx":["Check TSH; ULTRASOUND characterizes the nodule (TI-RADS features - microcalcifications, hypoechogenicity, irregular margins, taller-than-wide) and the neck nodes; FINE-NEEDLE ASPIRATION (reported by the BETHESDA system) for suspicious nodules, with molecular testing for indeterminate results","A low TSH prompts a radioiodine scan (a hyperfunctioning 'hot' nodule is rarely cancer); assess vocal fold mobility before thyroid surgery","Medullary cancer - calcitonin/RET testing"],"tx":["BENIGN nodules: observe (serial ultrasound) or treat if compressive/hyperfunctioning; INDETERMINATE - molecular testing or diagnostic lobectomy","THYROID CANCER: THYROIDECTOMY (lobectomy for low-risk small cancers; total thyroidectomy for larger/higher-risk) +/- central/lateral NECK DISSECTION for nodal disease, then risk-adapted RADIOACTIVE IODINE and TSH SUPPRESSION","Protect the RECURRENT LARYNGEAL NERVES and PARATHYROIDS during surgery"],"tech":["Thyroid lobectomy or total thyroidectomy with recurrent laryngeal nerve monitoring and parathyroid preservation; central/lateral neck dissection for nodal disease","Radioactive iodine ablation (differentiated cancers)","Molecular testing for indeterminate nodules"],"after":["Thyroid hormone replacement (and TSH suppression for cancer); monitor CALCIUM for hypoparathyroidism and assess voice (recurrent laryngeal nerve) after surgery; radioactive iodine and thyroglobulin-based SURVEILLANCE for differentiated cancer","Long-term surveillance (ultrasound, thyroglobulin/calcitonin); manage recurrence","Genetic counseling for medullary/MEN2"],"pearls":["Most thyroid nodules are benign - ultrasound (TI-RADS) plus FNA (Bethesda) selects the few needing surgery","Papillary cancer spreads to neck nodes but has an excellent prognosis; anaplastic is a rapidly enlarging, often-fatal neck mass in the elderly","Protect the recurrent laryngeal nerves and parathyroids - and check voice and calcium after thyroidectomy","Medullary thyroid cancer (calcitonin, MEN2/RET) needs genetic evaluation and does not take up radioiodine"],"refs":[{"t":"ATA Thyroid Nodule & Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.thyroid.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroid Nodule / Thyroid Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500006/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Endocrine Surgery","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-hyperparathyroidism","name":"Hyperparathyroidism & Parathyroid Surgery","sec":"ent","present":["Excess parathyroid hormone (this content shares with the general-surgery/endocrine framing, head-and-neck-surgery lens). PRIMARY hyperparathyroidism (autonomous PTH, usually a single ADENOMA) causes HYPERCALCEMIA; SECONDARY (compensatory, from renal failure/vitamin D deficiency) and tertiary forms also occur","Primary disease is often ASYMPTOMATIC (found on routine calcium) or presents with the classic 'STONES (kidney), BONES (osteoporosis/pain), abdominal GROANS, and psychiatric MOANS'","A parathyroid CARCINOMA is rare (very high calcium/PTH with a palpable mass)"],"dx":["Biochemical diagnosis: elevated CALCIUM with an inappropriately elevated/non-suppressed PTH (and check vitamin D, 24-hour urine calcium to exclude familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia)","LOCALIZATION before surgery: sestamibi scan, neck ULTRASOUND, and/or 4D-CT to find the adenoma (enables focused surgery)","Assess end-organ effects (bone density, renal function/stones)"],"tx":["PARATHYROIDECTOMY is the definitive cure for primary hyperparathyroidism (indicated for symptomatic disease and per criteria for asymptomatic disease - age, calcium level, renal/bone involvement)","Approaches: MINIMALLY INVASIVE/focused parathyroidectomy (single localized adenoma) with INTRAOPERATIVE PTH monitoring to confirm cure, or bilateral exploration (multigland disease/non-localized)","Medical management (hydration, bisphosphonates, cinacalcet) when surgery is not pursued; treat secondary HPT causes"],"tech":["Minimally invasive/focused parathyroidectomy with intraoperative PTH monitoring; four-gland exploration for multigland disease; recurrent laryngeal nerve protection","Localization-guided approach","Subtotal/total parathyroidectomy for secondary/tertiary disease"],"after":["Monitor CALCIUM postoperatively (transient hypocalcemia, and 'HUNGRY BONE' syndrome after correcting severe disease); assess voice (recurrent laryngeal nerve)","Confirm biochemical cure; monitor for persistent/recurrent disease (especially multigland)","Bone-health and renal follow-up"],"pearls":["Elevated calcium with a non-suppressed PTH is primary hyperparathyroidism - and it is often found incidentally before the classic 'stones, bones, groans, and moans'","Localize the adenoma (sestamibi/ultrasound/4D-CT) to enable a focused parathyroidectomy with intraoperative PTH confirmation","Parathyroidectomy is the definitive cure - exclude familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (low urine calcium) first, which is NOT treated surgically","Watch for postoperative hypocalcemia and hungry-bone syndrome after correcting severe disease"],"refs":[{"t":"AAES Hyperparathyroidism Guidelines","u":"https://www.endocrinesurgery.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hyperparathyroidism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430757/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Head & Neck Endocrine Surgery","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"ent-salivary-disorders","name":"Salivary Gland Disorders (Sialadenitis, Sialolithiasis)","sec":"ent","present":["Non-neoplastic salivary disease. SIALOLITHIASIS (salivary STONES): most common in the SUBMANDIBULAR gland/Wharton's duct (thicker, alkaline saliva, uphill flow) - causing POSTPRANDIAL pain and swelling of the gland that subsides between meals","SIALADENITIS: acute bacterial (in dehydrated/debilitated/elderly patients - retrograde Staphylococcus, a tender swollen gland with purulent duct discharge), viral (MUMPS parotitis - bilateral), or chronic/recurrent; autoimmune (Sjogren syndrome)","Obstruction and reduced salivary flow predispose to infection"],"dx":["Clinical exam (milk the gland/duct for pus or stones); imaging - ULTRASOUND or non-contrast CT for stones, and SIALOGRAPHY/sialendoscopy to define ductal pathology; assess for autoimmune disease (Sjogren) in recurrent/bilateral cases","Distinguish obstructive (stone) from inflammatory/autoimmune causes","Exclude a neoplasm in a persistent salivary mass"],"tx":["ACUTE BACTERIAL sialadenitis: HYDRATION, warm compresses, gland MASSAGE, SIALAGOGUES (sour candies), and ANTIBIOTICS; drain an abscess","SIALOLITHIASIS: conservative measures (hydration, massage, sialagogues, heat) to pass small stones; SIALENDOSCOPY or transoral removal for accessible stones; gland EXCISION for recurrent/chronic disease or large hilar stones","Treat autoimmune disease (Sjogren) medically"],"tech":["Sialendoscopy (diagnostic and therapeutic stone removal/duct dilation); transoral duct stone removal; submandibular/parotid gland excision for refractory chronic disease; abscess drainage","Sialography for ductal mapping","Gland-preserving approaches when possible"],"after":["Recurrence prevention (hydration, salivary hygiene, sialagogues, treating xerostomia); monitor for recurrent stones/infection; gland function after conservative treatment","Manage underlying autoimmune disease; dental/oral care in xerostomia","Follow-up for persistent masses (exclude tumor)"],"pearls":["Postprandial gland swelling and pain that subsides between meals is an obstructing salivary stone - most often in the submandibular gland (Wharton's duct)","Acute bacterial sialadenitis strikes dehydrated/debilitated patients - treat with hydration, sialagogues, massage, and antibiotics","Sialendoscopy is a gland-preserving way to remove accessible stones and treat ductal disease","Bilateral parotid swelling suggests mumps or an autoimmune process (Sjogren) - and a persistent salivary mass still needs a tumor workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Salivary Gland Disorders Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sialadenitis / Sialolithiasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549845/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-tonsillitis-adenoids","name":"Tonsillitis, Adenoiditis & (Adeno)tonsillectomy","sec":"ent","present":["Common pediatric (and adult) throat/nasal problems. TONSILLITIS/pharyngitis: viral (most) vs GROUP A STREPTOCOCCAL (GABHS) - fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, no cough (Centor criteria); infectious MONONUCLEOSIS (EBV) causes marked tonsillar hypertrophy and can cause airway obstruction","ADENOID HYPERTROPHY: nasal obstruction, mouth breathing, hyponasal speech, snoring/OSA, and eustachian tube dysfunction (otitis media with effusion) in children","Recurrent throat infections and obstructive symptoms drive surgical referral"],"dx":["GABHS: rapid antigen test/throat culture (Centor/McIsaac to guide testing); recognize MONONUCLEOSIS (posterior nodes, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes, positive monospot) - do NOT give amoxicillin (causes a rash)","Assess tonsil/adenoid size and obstructive symptoms; a UNILATERALLY enlarging tonsil raises concern for malignancy (lymphoma)","Sleep assessment for suspected OSA"],"tx":["GABHS tonsillitis: penicillin/amoxicillin (avoid amoxicillin if mono is possible); supportive care for viral disease; steroids/airway management for obstructive mononucleosis","TONSILLECTOMY indications: recurrent throat infections (PARADISE criteria - ~7 in 1 year, 5/year for 2 years, or 3/year for 3 years), OSA/sleep-disordered breathing, recurrent peritonsillar abscess, and tonsil asymmetry/suspected malignancy; ADENOIDECTOMY for nasal obstruction, OSA, and recurrent OME/AOM","Watchful waiting for milder recurrent infections"],"tech":["Tonsillectomy (cold-steel, electrocautery, coblation, or intracapsular) and adenoidectomy; adenotonsillectomy for pediatric OSA","Airway management for obstructive mononucleosis","Assess/biopsy an asymmetric tonsil"],"after":["Post-tonsillectomy care: adequate ANALGESIA and HYDRATION (poor intake -> dehydration); the key complication is POST-TONSILLECTOMY HEMORRHAGE - PRIMARY (<24 hours) and SECONDARY (classically days 5-10 as the eschar sloughs) - a bleeding tonsil is an emergency (airway/hemorrhage)","Monitor for dehydration and pain control; reassess OSA after surgery","Return precautions for bleeding"],"pearls":["Do not give amoxicillin for presumed strep if mononucleosis is possible - it causes a florid rash; posterior nodes and splenomegaly are clues","Tonsillectomy indications are recurrent infection (Paradise criteria), OSA, recurrent PTA, and tonsil asymmetry (rule out lymphoma)","Post-tonsillectomy hemorrhage is the feared complication - primary (<24 h) and secondary (~days 5-10); treat a bleed as an airway/hemorrhage emergency","Adenoid hypertrophy drives nasal obstruction, OSA, and recurrent middle-ear effusions in children - adenoidectomy helps"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Tonsillectomy in Children","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tonsillitis / Tonsillectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544342/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-pediatric-airway","name":"Pediatric Airway (Laryngomalacia, Subglottic Stenosis, Stridor)","sec":"ent","present":["Pediatric STRIDOR localizes by phase: INSPIRATORY (supraglottic/glottic), BIPHASIC (subglottic/glottic), and EXPIRATORY (tracheal/lower). LARYNGOMALACIA is the most common cause of chronic INFANT stridor - inspiratory stridor worse when supine, feeding, or agitated, from collapse of floppy supraglottic tissue (omega-shaped epiglottis)","SUBGLOTTIC STENOSIS: congenital or (more often) ACQUIRED from prolonged intubation - biphasic stridor and recurrent 'croup'; vocal fold paralysis, tracheomalacia, laryngeal cleft, hemangioma, and vascular rings are other causes","Feeding difficulty, failure to thrive, cyanosis, and apnea signal severity"],"dx":["FLEXIBLE laryngoscopy (awake) evaluates the dynamic supraglottis/glottis (laryngomalacia, vocal fold mobility); rigid BRONCHOSCOPY/microlaryngoscopy under anesthesia is the gold standard for the subglottis/trachea and grading stenosis (Cotton-Myer)","Assess severity (feeding, growth, oxygenation, apnea); imaging (airway films, and echo/CT for vascular anomalies)","Consider reflux (contributes to laryngomalacia/stenosis)"],"tx":["LARYNGOMALACIA: most is mild and self-limited (resolves by ~12-18 months) - OBSERVE and treat reflux; SUPRAGLOTTOPLASTY for severe disease (failure to thrive, significant apnea/obstruction)","SUBGLOTTIC STENOSIS: endoscopic dilation/incision for select cases; open LARYNGOTRACHEAL RECONSTRUCTION or cricotracheal resection for significant stenosis; anterior cricoid split in neonates; a tracheostomy may be needed to secure the airway","Treat the specific lesion (propranolol for airway hemangioma, etc.)"],"tech":["Supraglottoplasty (laryngomalacia); endoscopic dilation/incision, laryngotracheal reconstruction, or cricotracheal resection (subglottic stenosis); tracheostomy for airway security","Rigid bronchoscopy for diagnosis/grading","Lesion-specific therapy"],"after":["Monitor GROWTH/FEEDING and airway; laryngomalacia - reassurance and reflux control with resolution expected; post-reconstruction airway surveillance and decannulation planning","Treat reflux; watch for restenosis and complications","Multidisciplinary follow-up (feeding, pulmonary)"],"pearls":["Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of infant stridor - inspiratory, worse supine/feeding/agitated, and usually self-resolving (supraglottoplasty for the severe few)","Localize stridor by phase: inspiratory (supraglottic), biphasic (subglottic/glottic), expiratory (tracheal)","Acquired subglottic stenosis usually follows prolonged intubation - it presents as biphasic stridor and recurrent 'croup'","Failure to thrive, apnea, or cyanosis marks a severe pediatric airway - evaluate with endoscopy/bronchoscopy and treat"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Pediatric Airway Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngomalacia / Subglottic Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544477/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-congenital-neck-masses","name":"Congenital Neck Masses","sec":"ent","present":["Neck masses present from developmental remnants - usually in CHILDREN/young adults. THYROGLOSSAL DUCT CYST: the most common congenital neck mass - MIDLINE, near the hyoid, that MOVES with SWALLOWING and TONGUE PROTRUSION (attached to the tract to the tongue base)","BRANCHIAL CLEFT CYST: a LATERAL neck mass (second cleft most common - along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid), often noticed after an upper respiratory infection; LYMPHATIC MALFORMATION (cystic hygroma - soft, compressible, transilluminating, often posterior triangle) and dermoid cysts also occur","These can become infected/enlarge; in an ADULT, a 'cyst' may actually be cystic metastatic cancer"],"dx":["ULTRASOUND (first-line, confirms cystic nature and location) and MRI for extent; for a thyroglossal duct cyst, confirm NORMAL thyroid tissue is present elsewhere (the cyst can contain the only functioning thyroid - do not remove ectopic thyroid unknowingly)","In an ADULT with a 'congenital-appearing' cystic neck mass, EXCLUDE cystic metastasis (HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer, papillary thyroid cancer) - FNA/workup","Assess for infection and airway (large lymphatic malformations)"],"tx":["THYROGLOSSAL DUCT CYST: the SISTRUNK PROCEDURE (excision of the cyst WITH the central hyoid bone and the tract to the tongue base) - removing the cyst alone leads to high recurrence","BRANCHIAL CLEFT CYST: complete surgical excision of the cyst and its tract; LYMPHATIC MALFORMATION: sclerotherapy and/or surgical excision (often staged; may recur/infiltrate)","Treat infection before elective excision"],"tech":["Sistrunk procedure (thyroglossal duct cyst); complete branchial cleft cyst/tract excision; sclerotherapy or excision (lymphatic malformation)","Ultrasound/MRI-guided planning","Airway management for large lesions"],"after":["Monitor for RECURRENCE (incomplete excision or missed tract) and infection; lymphatic malformations frequently recur/need repeat treatment","Confirm thyroid function if thyroid tissue was involved; pathology review (exclude malignancy in adults)","Follow-up for wound/cosmetic outcome"],"pearls":["A midline neck mass that moves with swallowing and tongue protrusion is a thyroglossal duct cyst - treat with the Sistrunk procedure (take the hyoid and tract, or it recurs)","Confirm there is normal thyroid tissue before removing a thyroglossal duct cyst - it can contain the patient's only functioning thyroid","A lateral neck cyst along the anterior SCM border is a branchial cleft cyst - but in an adult, exclude cystic metastatic cancer first","Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation) is soft, compressible, and transilluminates - and tends to recur/infiltrate"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Congenital Neck Mass Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroglossal Duct Cyst / Branchial Cleft Cyst","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499914/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-pediatric-hearing-loss","name":"Pediatric Hearing Loss & Cochlear Implantation","sec":"ent","present":["Hearing loss in children - critical because untreated loss impairs SPEECH and LANGUAGE development; causes are CONGENITAL (genetic - e.g., CONNEXIN 26/GJB2 non-syndromic, and syndromic; congenital CMV - the leading non-genetic cause; perinatal insults) and ACQUIRED (meningitis, ototoxicity, chronic otitis media with effusion)","May be conductive (effusion, atresia), sensorineural, or mixed; unilateral or bilateral","UNIVERSAL NEWBORN HEARING SCREENING enables early detection and intervention"],"dx":["NEWBORN SCREENING with otoacoustic emissions (OAE) and/or auditory brainstem response (ABR); diagnostic ABR/audiometry confirms type and degree; age-appropriate behavioral audiometry in older children","Etiologic workup: genetics (GJB2), congenital CMV testing (best within the first weeks of life), imaging (CT/MRI for cochlear/nerve anomalies), and ophthalmology/ECG per syndrome","Follow the 1-3-6 principle (screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3, intervene by 6)"],"tx":["Early AMPLIFICATION (hearing aids) and enrollment in early-intervention/aural habilitation for language development; treat conductive causes (ventilation tubes for persistent effusion, atresia repair/bone-conduction devices)","COCHLEAR IMPLANTATION for severe-to-profound sensorineural hearing loss with inadequate benefit from hearing aids - EARLY implantation (in infancy) yields the best language outcomes","Family-centered communication support (spoken language/sign per family choice)"],"tech":["Cochlear implantation (severe-profound SNHL); hearing aid fitting; bone-conduction devices/BAHA (atresia, conductive/mixed loss); ventilation tubes for effusion","Genetic/CMV-guided management","Aural habilitation programs"],"after":["Intensive speech-language HABILITATION and audiologic follow-up (device mapping/monitoring); family support and educational planning; monitor progressive/late-onset loss (e.g., CMV, enlarged vestibular aqueduct)","Serial developmental/language assessment; adjust interventions","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["Early detection and intervention protect language - follow the 1-3-6 rule (screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3, intervene by 6)","Connexin 26 (GJB2) is the most common genetic cause; congenital CMV is the leading non-genetic cause - and CMV testing is time-sensitive (first weeks of life)","Cochlear implantation for severe-profound SNHL works best when done early (infancy) for language outcomes","Some losses are progressive/late (CMV, enlarged vestibular aqueduct) - keep monitoring hearing over time"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / AAP Pediatric Hearing Loss Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Hearing Loss","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538285/"},{"t":"CDC EHDI (Early Hearing Detection)","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/hearing-loss-children"}]},{"id":"ent-pediatric-rhinology","name":"Pediatric Rhinology (Adenoid Hypertrophy, Choanal Atresia)","sec":"ent","present":["Pediatric nasal obstruction. ADENOID HYPERTROPHY: enlarged adenoids cause chronic nasal obstruction, MOUTH BREATHING, hyponasal speech, snoring/OSA, 'adenoid facies', and eustachian tube dysfunction with recurrent otitis media/effusion","CHOANAL ATRESIA: a bony/membranous blockage of the posterior nasal aperture; BILATERAL atresia is a NEONATAL EMERGENCY because newborns are OBLIGATE NASAL breathers - cyclical cyanosis that IMPROVES with CRYING (mouth open) and worsens with feeding, and inability to pass a catheter through the nose; associated with CHARGE syndrome","Pediatric rhinosinusitis and allergic rhinitis also contribute to nasal obstruction"],"dx":["Adenoids: nasal endoscopy or lateral neck radiograph; assess obstructive/sleep symptoms and middle-ear status","CHOANAL ATRESIA: failure to pass a catheter through the nose plus CT confirming the atretic plate; evaluate for CHARGE association (coloboma, heart defects, atresia choanae, retardation, genital/ear anomalies) in bilateral cases","Assess the airway/feeding in neonates"],"tx":["ADENOID HYPERTROPHY: ADENOIDECTOMY for significant nasal obstruction, OSA, or recurrent otitis media/effusion (often with tympanostomy tubes); intranasal steroids for milder disease","CHOANAL ATRESIA: BILATERAL is urgent - establish an oral airway (McGovern nipple/oral airway) and surgically repair (transnasal ENDOSCOPIC repair of the atretic plate); unilateral atresia can be repaired electively","Treat contributing rhinosinusitis/allergy"],"tech":["Adenoidectomy; transnasal endoscopic choanal atresia repair (+/- stenting); airway stabilization (oral airway/McGovern nipple) for bilateral atresia","Endoscopic technique","Manage restenosis"],"after":["Adenoidectomy - reassess nasal/sleep symptoms and ear disease; choanal atresia - monitor for RESTENOSIS (may need dilation/revision) and feeding/airway; CHARGE workup and multidisciplinary care","Treat ongoing allergy/sinus disease","Long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Bilateral choanal atresia is a neonatal emergency - newborns are obligate nasal breathers, so they have cyanosis that paradoxically improves with crying; secure an oral airway and repair","Inability to pass a catheter through the nose plus CT confirms choanal atresia - and bilateral cases need a CHARGE syndrome workup","Adenoid hypertrophy causes nasal obstruction, mouth breathing, OSA, and recurrent middle-ear effusions - adenoidectomy (often with tubes) helps","Choanal atresia repair can restenose - build in surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Pediatric Rhinology Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Choanal Atresia / Adenoid Hypertrophy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556133/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-nasal-fractures","name":"Nasal Fractures & Septal Hematoma","sec":"ent","present":["The nose is the MOST COMMONLY fractured facial bone; blunt trauma causes epistaxis, swelling, deformity (dorsal deviation/depression), and crepitus","The critical associated injury is a SEPTAL HEMATOMA - a boggy, bluish swelling of the nasal septum from blood collecting between the mucoperichondrium and cartilage; it must be found and drained URGENTLY","Untreated septal hematoma leads to septal cartilage NECROSIS (avascular/infected) and a SADDLE-NOSE deformity"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis - inspect and palpate for deformity and, crucially, examine the SEPTUM (after decongestion) for a hematoma in EVERY nasal trauma; imaging is usually unnecessary for isolated nasal fractures (reserve CT for suspected other facial fractures/CSF leak)","Assess for septal deviation, ongoing epistaxis, and associated injuries; rule out CSF rhinorrhea in higher-energy trauma","Re-examine after swelling subsides to judge deformity"],"tx":["SEPTAL HEMATOMA: urgent incision and DRAINAGE with packing/quilting to prevent reaccumulation, plus antibiotics - do not miss or delay this","NASAL FRACTURE: CLOSED REDUCTION for displaced fractures - performed early (within ~1-2 weeks in adults, sooner in children, before the bones set); definitive septorhinoplasty later for residual deformity/obstruction","Control epistaxis and manage pain/swelling"],"tech":["Incision and drainage of septal hematoma (with packing/quilting sutures); closed reduction of nasal fracture; delayed septoplasty/septorhinoplasty for residual deformity","Decongestion and septal exam technique","Address CSF leak if present"],"after":["Septal hematoma: monitor for reaccumulation, abscess, and eventual saddle-nose deformity; nasal fracture - reassess for residual deformity/obstruction once swelling resolves (septorhinoplasty later if needed)","Counsel on the timing window for closed reduction","Follow-up for cosmetic/functional outcome"],"pearls":["Examine the SEPTUM in every nasal fracture - a missed septal hematoma causes cartilage necrosis and a saddle-nose deformity; drain it urgently","Isolated nasal fractures usually need no imaging - the diagnosis and the septal exam are clinical","Closed reduction is time-sensitive - do it within about 1-2 weeks (sooner in children) before the fracture sets","Reassess after swelling subsides - residual deformity/obstruction is addressed with later septorhinoplasty"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Nasal Trauma Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Nasal Fracture / Septal Hematoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551700/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-mandible-fractures","name":"Mandible Fractures","sec":"ent","present":["The mandible is among the most commonly fractured facial bones (assault, falls, motor vehicle collisions); because it is a RING, fractures are often MULTIPLE (a fracture in one area implies another)","Common sites: CONDYLE, angle, body, and symphysis/parasymphysis; presents with MALOCCLUSION (the most sensitive sign - 'my bite feels off'), trismus, pain, a step-off, gingival lacerations/bleeding, and NUMBNESS of the lower lip/chin (inferior alveolar nerve)","A floor-of-mouth hematoma/ecchymosis is a clue to a mandible fracture"],"dx":["CT (or panoramic radiograph/Panorex) defines the fracture(s) - look for the SECOND fracture; assess OCCLUSION, dental status, and inferior alveolar nerve function","Evaluate the airway (bilateral parasymphyseal fractures can cause tongue-base collapse) and associated injuries","Document malocclusion and nerve deficits"],"tx":["Restore OCCLUSION as the primary goal: MAXILLOMANDIBULAR FIXATION (MMF - wiring the jaws in occlusion) and/or open reduction and internal FIXATION (ORIF with plates); many CONDYLAR fractures are managed CLOSED (MMF/functional therapy) to avoid facial nerve risk","Antibiotics for fractures communicating with the mouth (open fractures); dental management","Airway protection when needed"],"tech":["Open reduction and internal fixation (plates/screws) and/or maxillomandibular fixation; closed treatment (MMF/functional) for many condylar fractures","Airway management for unstable/bilateral fractures","Dental extraction/management in the fracture line as indicated"],"after":["Monitor OCCLUSION, diet (soft/liquid during healing), and oral hygiene; follow inferior alveolar NERVE recovery (numbness often improves); watch for NONUNION/malunion and INFECTION","MMF patients need wire cutters available (airway) and nutritional support","Follow-up for occlusion and hardware"],"pearls":["Malocclusion ('my bite is off') is the most sensitive sign of a mandible fracture - and because the mandible is a ring, look for a second fracture","Lower-lip/chin numbness signals inferior alveolar nerve involvement; a floor-of-mouth hematoma is another clue","The goal of treatment is restoring occlusion - via MMF and/or ORIF; many condylar fractures are treated closed to spare the facial nerve","Fractures open to the mouth need antibiotics - and bilateral parasymphyseal fractures can threaten the airway (tongue-base collapse)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / AO Facial Trauma: Mandible Fractures","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mandible Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507883/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-midface-fractures","name":"Midface (Le Fort), Orbital & Frontal Sinus Fractures","sec":"ent","present":["Higher-energy midface trauma. LE FORT fractures are transverse maxillary fracture patterns: LE FORT I (a floating palate - across the maxilla above the teeth), LE FORT II (pyramidal - through the nasofrontal region and orbital floors), and LE FORT III (craniofacial DISJUNCTION - separating the midface from the skull base); they cause malocclusion, midface mobility, and CSF leak","ORBITAL floor 'BLOWOUT' fractures: from a blow to the globe - ENOPHTHALMOS, DIPLOPIA, and infraorbital nerve numbness, with the danger of extraocular muscle ENTRAPMENT (a pediatric 'white-eyed blowout' with an oculocardiac reflex - bradycardia/nausea - is a surgical EMERGENCY)","FRONTAL SINUS fractures involve the anterior and/or posterior table (posterior-table/dural injury risks CSF leak, meningitis, and late mucocele); zygomaticomaxillary complex (tripod) fractures are also common"],"dx":["Maxillofacial CT is the imaging standard - define the Le Fort level, orbital floor/muscle entrapment, ZMC, and frontal sinus tables; test facial mobility, OCCLUSION, and infraorbital nerve","ORBITAL fractures: full EYE exam (visual acuity, motility, enophthalmos, and for entrapment - restricted upgaze with pain/bradycardia); rule out globe injury and retrobulbar hematoma (vision-threatening)","Assess for CSF leak (frontal/Le Fort) and associated intracranial/cervical injuries"],"tx":["LE FORT/ZMC/displaced fractures: open reduction and internal FIXATION to restore facial buttresses, projection, and OCCLUSION","ORBITAL floor: repair for significant enophthalmos, persistent diplopia, or large defects - and URGENTLY for entrapment (especially a pediatric white-eyed blowout); many small fractures without entrapment are observed","FRONTAL SINUS: management by table involvement and outflow tract (observation vs repair vs sinus obliteration/cranialization for posterior-table/dural injury); repair CSF leaks"],"tech":["Open reduction and internal fixation of Le Fort/ZMC fractures (restoring buttresses/occlusion); orbital floor reconstruction (implant/mesh) for entrapment/enophthalmos; frontal sinus repair, obliteration, or cranialization; CSF leak repair","Maxillomandibular fixation to re-establish occlusion","Multidisciplinary (ophthalmology/neurosurgery) management"],"after":["Monitor VISION/diplopia (orbital), OCCLUSION (Le Fort/ZMC), and CSF LEAK/meningitis and late MUCOCELE (frontal sinus); manage infraorbital numbness and cosmetic/functional outcomes","Sinus precautions and follow-up imaging where relevant; nutritional support during MMF","Long-term surveillance (frontal sinus mucocele can present years later)"],"pearls":["A pediatric 'white-eyed blowout' with entrapment (restricted upgaze, pain, bradycardia/nausea from the oculocardiac reflex) is a surgical EMERGENCY - the eye looks normal, so it is easily missed","Le Fort fractures are transverse maxillary patterns (I floating palate, II pyramidal, III craniofacial disjunction) - test for midface mobility and malocclusion and get a maxillofacial CT","Posterior-table frontal sinus fractures risk CSF leak, meningitis, and late mucocele - they need careful, often surgical, management","Always exclude globe injury and retrobulbar hematoma (a vision-threatening emergency) in orbital trauma before addressing the fracture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / AO Facial Trauma: Midface & Orbital Fractures","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Le Fort / Orbital Blowout / Frontal Sinus Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526060/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-facial-soft-tissue-temporal-trauma","name":"Auricular & Facial Soft-Tissue Trauma; Temporal Bone Trauma","sec":"ent","present":["SOFT-TISSUE facial trauma with structures at risk: an AURICULAR HEMATOMA (blood between perichondrium and cartilage - must be drained to prevent 'CAULIFLOWER EAR'), auricular lacerations (exposed cartilage needs coverage), and cheek lacerations that can injure the FACIAL NERVE and PAROTID (Stensen's) DUCT","TEMPORAL BONE fractures from head trauma: LONGITUDINAL (more common - conductive hearing loss, ossicular/TM injury, bleeding) vs TRANSVERSE (higher rate of SENSORINEURAL hearing loss, VERTIGO, and FACIAL NERVE paralysis); may cause CSF OTORRHEA and a Battle sign (postauricular ecchymosis)","Facial nerve status and its TIMING (immediate vs delayed) after temporal bone trauma guides management"],"dx":["Examine ear (hematoma, canal, TM, hemotympanum, CSF otorrhea), FACIAL NERVE function, and, for cheek lacerations, the facial nerve branches and parotid duct; CT of the TEMPORAL BONE for fractures and ossicular/facial nerve canal involvement; audiogram","Determine facial nerve onset: IMMEDIATE COMPLETE paralysis (suggests transection - consider exploration) vs DELAYED/incomplete (better prognosis - usually observed)","Assess for associated intracranial injury"],"tx":["AURICULAR HEMATOMA: prompt incision and DRAINAGE with a bolster/compression dressing to prevent reaccumulation and cauliflower ear; laceration repair with cartilage coverage; repair injured facial nerve branches and parotid duct primarily when identified","TEMPORAL BONE/facial nerve: DELAYED or incomplete paralysis - observe +/- steroids (good recovery); IMMEDIATE complete paralysis with radiographic transection - facial nerve EXPLORATION/decompression/repair; manage CSF leak (usually conservative first)","Hearing loss - address ossicular/TM injury (may need later tympanoplasty/ossiculoplasty)"],"tech":["Auricular hematoma drainage with bolster; laceration/cartilage repair; facial nerve exploration/decompression/grafting and parotid duct repair; delayed ossicular reconstruction for persistent conductive loss","CT-guided facial nerve/ossicular assessment","CSF leak management"],"after":["Monitor for hematoma reaccumulation (cauliflower ear), facial nerve recovery, hearing (audiometric follow-up), and CSF leak resolution/meningitis; eye protection if facial nerve palsy","Manage residual conductive loss (delayed reconstruction) and vestibular symptoms (rehabilitation)","Cosmetic and functional follow-up"],"pearls":["Drain an auricular hematoma promptly (with a bolster) - leaving it causes cauliflower-ear deformity","After temporal bone trauma, the TIMING of facial paralysis matters: immediate complete paralysis suggests transection (consider exploration); delayed/incomplete paralysis usually recovers with observation +/- steroids","Transverse temporal bone fractures cause more sensorineural loss, vertigo, and facial paralysis; longitudinal fractures cause more conductive loss and TM/ossicular injury","A deep cheek laceration can transect the facial nerve or parotid duct - examine and repair them"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Facial & Temporal Bone Trauma Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Temporal Bone Fracture / Auricular Hematoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536996/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-facial-plastics","name":"Facial Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery","sec":"ent","present":["Reconstruction and aesthetic surgery of the face - a core ENT domain covering defect RECONSTRUCTION (after Mohs excision of skin cancer, trauma, or ablative surgery), functional/cosmetic RHINOPLASTY, scar revision, and facial reanimation","Facial reconstruction respects AESTHETIC UNITS/subunits and the RECONSTRUCTIVE LADDER (from secondary-intention healing and primary closure, to skin grafts, to LOCAL/regional FLAPS, to microvascular free flaps)","Also includes otoplasty, microtia repair, and management of facial paralysis (reanimation)"],"dx":["Analyze the defect (size, depth, location, aesthetic subunit, exposed structures - cartilage/bone) and match the reconstruction to restore form and function; assess vascularity and prior radiation","For rhinoplasty, evaluate both the functional airway (nasal valve, septum) and aesthetics","Plan on the reconstructive ladder - the simplest option that gives the best result"],"tx":["Reconstruct facial defects with the appropriate technique: primary closure, SKIN GRAFTS (full/split thickness), LOCAL FLAPS (advancement, rotation, transposition, interpolated - e.g., paramedian forehead flap for the nose), and free tissue transfer for large/complex defects","RHINOPLASTY/septorhinoplasty for functional (airway) and cosmetic goals; facial reanimation (nerve grafts/transfers, static/dynamic procedures) for paralysis","Preserve function (eyelid, oral competence, nasal airway) alongside cosmesis"],"tech":["Local/regional flaps and skin grafts for facial defects; paramedian forehead/nasolabial flaps for nasal reconstruction; functional and cosmetic rhinoplasty; facial reanimation (nerve transfer, gracilis free flap, static slings); otoplasty/microtia repair","Reconstructive-ladder decision-making","Aesthetic-unit-based planning"],"after":["Wound care, flap/graft monitoring (viability, congestion), and scar management; staged procedures (e.g., interpolated flap division) and REVISION as needed for optimal cosmesis/function","Counsel on healing timelines and the possibility of revision; sun protection for scars","Long-term aesthetic and functional follow-up"],"pearls":["Reconstruct by aesthetic subunit and climb the reconstructive ladder - choose the simplest technique that restores both form and function","The paramedian forehead flap is a workhorse for larger nasal defects (e.g., after Mohs)","Rhinoplasty must address function (nasal valve, septum) as well as appearance - a pretty nose that cannot breathe is a failure","Preserve function first (eyelid closure, oral competence, nasal airway); cosmesis follows"],"refs":[{"t":"AAFPRS Facial Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.aafprs.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Local Flaps / Facial Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563135/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-otalgia","name":"Otalgia & Referred Ear Pain","sec":"ent","present":["Ear pain - either PRIMARY (from ear disease itself: otitis media, otitis externa, cerumen, trauma, mastoiditis) or SECONDARY/REFERRED (a NORMAL ear exam, with pain referred from elsewhere via the ear's rich sensory innervation)","The external/middle ear is innervated by cranial nerves V, VII, IX, and X and cervical nerves C2-C3 - so pain refers from the TMJ, TEETH, oral cavity/PHARYNX (tonsil), and LARYNX/hypopharynx","The crucial concern: referred otalgia with a normal ear exam in an adult with risk factors can signal a HEAD-AND-NECK MALIGNANCY"],"dx":["Examine the ear thoroughly - if the ear is NORMAL, the pain is REFERRED, and the source must be sought (TMJ, dental, throat); in an ADULT with a normal ear and risk factors (tobacco/alcohol, weight loss, dysphagia, hoarseness), perform LARYNGOSCOPY to exclude cancer","Evaluate TMJ, dentition, and the upper aerodigestive tract; consider trigeminal/glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Do not attribute otalgia with a normal ear to the ear"],"tx":["Treat the SOURCE: primary otologic disease per its cause; referred otalgia - address the TMJ (bite therapy), dental disease, pharyngitis/tonsil disease, reflux, or the identified pathology","When cancer is found, treat per head-and-neck oncology protocols","Analgesia while the source is addressed"],"tech":["No specific procedure - laryngoscopy/panendoscopy for cancer exclusion; treat the underlying source (dental, TMJ, ENT pathology) as indicated","Directed evaluation of the referral sites","Biopsy of any identified lesion"],"after":["Follow resolution once the source is treated; ensure malignancy has been excluded in at-risk adults with persistent referred otalgia","Manage chronic sources (TMJ, reflux) and reassess if pain persists","Reevaluate for occult pathology if unexplained"],"pearls":["Otalgia with a NORMAL ear exam is referred pain - the ear is innervated by CN V, VII, IX, X and C2-3, so look to the TMJ, teeth, throat, and larynx","In an adult with a normal ear and risk factors, referred otalgia warrants laryngoscopy to exclude a head-and-neck cancer - do not dismiss it","Persistent ear pain with a normal exam is a red flag, not a reassurance","Find and treat the source (TMJ, dental, pharyngeal, laryngeal) rather than treating the ear"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Otalgia Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Otalgia / Referred Ear Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549830/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-smell-taste","name":"Olfactory & Taste Dysfunction","sec":"ent","present":["Disorders of SMELL (anosmia/hyposmia/parosmia) and, less commonly, true TASTE loss - patients often report 'taste' loss that is actually SMELL loss (flavor is mostly olfaction)","Olfactory causes: CONDUCTIVE/sinonasal (nasal obstruction, chronic rhinosinusitis/polyps blocking odorant delivery), SENSORINEURAL (POST-VIRAL - including COVID-19 - the most common cause; head TRAUMA shearing the olfactory nerves at the cribriform plate), NEURODEGENERATIVE disease (an EARLY sign of PARKINSON'S and Alzheimer's), and congenital (Kallmann syndrome - anosmia with hypogonadism)","Loss of smell carries SAFETY risks (undetected smoke, gas, spoiled food) and quality-of-life/nutritional impact"],"dx":["History and NASAL ENDOSCOPY (identify sinonasal obstruction/inflammation - a treatable conductive cause) and objective SMELL testing (e.g., UPSIT); assess onset (post-viral, post-traumatic) and associated neurologic features","MRI when a mass or central/neurologic cause is suspected (e.g., an olfactory groove meningioma or esthesioneuroblastoma, or unexplained/progressive loss)","Distinguish conductive (sinonasal, often treatable) from sensorineural loss"],"tx":["Treat SINONASAL causes (intranasal/oral corticosteroids, treat chronic rhinosinusitis/polyps, surgery) - the most reversible category; POST-VIRAL loss - OLFACTORY TRAINING (structured smell retraining) aids recovery, and topical steroids are used","Counsel on SAFETY (smoke/gas detectors, check food dates) and nutrition; treat identified causes (mass, neurodegenerative referral)","Set realistic expectations - post-viral/post-traumatic recovery is variable and may be incomplete"],"tech":["No specific procedure - nasal endoscopy and imaging for evaluation; sinus surgery for obstructing polyps/CRS as a conductive cause","Olfactory training program","Treat the underlying pathology"],"after":["Monitor recovery (often gradual and partial for post-viral/traumatic loss); continue olfactory training; reinforce SAFETY measures and nutritional support","Reassess/image if progressive or unexplained (exclude a mass or neurodegenerative process)","Long-term follow-up for persistent dysfunction"],"pearls":["Most 'taste loss' is actually SMELL loss - flavor is largely olfactory","The common causes are sinonasal (treatable/conductive), post-viral (including COVID-19), and post-traumatic (cribriform shear); persistent smell loss can be an early sign of Parkinson's or Alzheimer's","Olfactory training helps post-viral recovery, and sinonasal causes (polyps/CRS) are the most reversible - treat them","Counsel on safety - anosmic patients cannot smell smoke, gas leaks, or spoiled food; ensure detectors are in place"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Olfactory Dysfunction Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anosmia / Olfactory Dysfunction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482152/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-vestibular-migraine","name":"Vestibular Migraine & Central Vertigo","sec":"ent","present":["VESTIBULAR MIGRAINE is a leading cause of recurrent spontaneous vertigo - episodes lasting minutes to hours to days, often WITHOUT the severe hearing loss/fullness of Meniere's, in patients with a MIGRAINE history and features (photophobia, phonophobia, visual aura, headache - which may or may not accompany the vertigo)","The broader task is separating PERIPHERAL vertigo (benign - BPPV, neuritis, Meniere's) from CENTRAL vertigo (brainstem/cerebellar - stroke, demyelination, tumor), which carries neurologic red flags","CENTRAL red flags: sustained direction-changing or vertical/pure-torsional nystagmus, inability to walk, and other neurologic deficits (dysarthria, diplopia, ataxia)"],"dx":["Vestibular migraine is a CLINICAL diagnosis (recurrent vestibular symptoms + migraine history/features + exclusion of other causes - per Barany Society/ICHD criteria); a normal interictal exam is typical","For ACUTE vertigo, the HINTS exam (Head-Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew) and gait assessment distinguish peripheral from CENTRAL causes - a 'central' HINTS pattern (normal head impulse, direction-changing nystagmus, skew) outperforms early MRI for posterior stroke; image (MRI) when central features are present","Screen for stroke risk factors in acute presentations"],"tx":["VESTIBULAR MIGRAINE: migraine-style management - lifestyle/TRIGGER avoidance, acute abortives, and PROPHYLAXIS (e.g., migraine preventives) for frequent episodes; vestibular rehabilitation for interictal imbalance","CENTRAL vertigo: treat the underlying cause (stroke pathway/neurology referral) - do not mislabel a central process as benign","Limit long-term vestibular suppressants (impair compensation)"],"tech":["No procedure - HINTS-guided triage and imaging (MRI) for central causes; vestibular rehabilitation therapy for chronic imbalance","Migraine prophylaxis management","Neurology/stroke pathway for central disease"],"after":["Vestibular migraine: track triggers/frequency and optimize prophylaxis; vestibular rehabilitation for residual symptoms; reassess if the pattern changes","Central vertigo: manage the underlying diagnosis and secondary prevention","Long-term follow-up and reassurance for the benign forms"],"pearls":["Vestibular migraine causes recurrent spontaneous vertigo in a migraineur - often without the hearing loss/fullness of Meniere's - and is managed like migraine (triggers + prophylaxis)","In acute vertigo, a 'central' HINTS exam beats early MRI for catching posterior-circulation stroke - do the HINTS and check gait","Central red flags (direction-changing/vertical nystagmus, inability to stand/walk, other neuro deficits) mean image and treat as central - not benign","Avoid long-term vestibular suppressants - they impair central compensation"],"refs":[{"t":"Barany Society / AAO-HNS Vestibular Migraine Criteria","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vestibular Migraine / Central Vertigo","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560896/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-rrp","name":"Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis & HPV Airway Disease","sec":"ent","present":["RECURRENT RESPIRATORY PAPILLOMATOSIS (RRP): benign but troublesome squamous papillomas of the aerodigestive tract (favoring the LARYNX/vocal folds) caused by HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS (HPV-6 and HPV-11)","JUVENILE-onset RRP (acquired perinatally from maternal genital HPV) presents in young children with progressive HOARSENESS, and - dangerously - STRIDOR and AIRWAY obstruction (papillomas can be misdiagnosed as asthma/croup); adult-onset RRP also occurs","The disease RECURS and requires repeated treatment; rare malignant transformation (especially HPV-11, distal spread)"],"dx":["LARYNGOSCOPY/bronchoscopy reveals the characteristic exophytic papillomas; biopsy confirms the diagnosis and HPV typing (11 has a worse course); assess airway and distal (tracheobronchial/pulmonary) spread","In a child with progressive hoarseness and stridor, examine the airway - do not just treat as asthma/croup","Track disease burden (staging scores) over serial procedures"],"tx":["Surgical DEBULKING to maintain the airway and voice while preserving normal tissue (microdebrider or CO2 laser via microlaryngoscopy) - the mainstay, done REPEATEDLY as it recurs; avoid tracheostomy if possible (can seed distal spread)","ADJUVANT therapies for aggressive disease: intralesional cidofovir, systemic bevacizumab, and the HPV VACCINE (which may reduce recurrences); interferon historically","Aim to control disease and lengthen intervals between procedures"],"tech":["Microlaryngoscopy with microdebrider or CO2 laser debulking (tissue-sparing); intralesional adjuvant injection (cidofovir/bevacizumab); systemic bevacizumab for severe disease","Airway management during procedures","HPV vaccination as adjunct"],"after":["Anticipate RECURRENCE with serial surveillance laryngoscopy and repeat debulking; monitor voice and airway; watch for distal spread and (rarely) malignant transformation","Counsel on the chronic, relapsing course; HPV vaccination and prevention counseling","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["Progressive hoarseness with stridor in a young child can be juvenile RRP (perinatal HPV-6/11) - examine the airway rather than assuming asthma or croup","RRP is treated by repeated tissue-sparing debulking (microdebrider/CO2 laser) - it recurs, so the goal is airway/voice control, not one-time cure","Avoid tracheostomy in RRP when possible - it can seed distal tracheobronchial spread","HPV-11 has a worse course; the HPV vaccine and adjuvants (cidofovir, bevacizumab) can reduce disease burden"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526046/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-jna","name":"Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma","sec":"ent","present":["JUVENILE NASOPHARYNGEAL ANGIOFIBROMA (JNA): a benign but locally aggressive, highly VASCULAR tumor of the nasopharynx/pterygopalatine fossa, occurring almost exclusively in ADOLESCENT MALES","Classic presentation: recurrent, often severe unilateral EPISTAXIS plus progressive nasal OBSTRUCTION and a nasopharyngeal mass; larger tumors extend to the pterygopalatine/infratemporal fossa, orbit, and skull base (cheek swelling, proptosis, cranial neuropathies)","Though histologically benign, it erodes bone and can be life-threatening from hemorrhage"],"dx":["IMAGING first: contrast CT/MRI shows an avidly enhancing nasopharyngeal mass with characteristic anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary wall (Holman-Miller sign) and defines skull-base/intracranial extent; angiography identifies the (usually internal maxillary artery) blood supply","DO NOT BIOPSY in the office - JNA is intensely vascular and biopsy risks torrential HEMORRHAGE; the diagnosis is made on imaging in the right clinical context (adolescent male with epistaxis and a nasopharyngeal mass)","Stage extent (e.g., Radkowski/Fisch) for planning"],"tx":["Preoperative EMBOLIZATION (to reduce vascularity/blood loss) followed by surgical RESECTION - increasingly ENDOSCOPIC for accessible tumors, open/combined approaches for extensive skull-base disease","Radiation is reserved for unresectable/intracranial disease; hormonal manipulation is historical","Meticulous hemostasis and blood-loss planning"],"tech":["Preoperative angiographic embolization; endoscopic (or open/craniofacial) resection; skull-base approaches for extensive disease","Imaging-based (no-biopsy) diagnosis","Blood-loss management"],"after":["SURVEILLANCE imaging for residual/recurrent tumor (JNA can recur, especially with skull-base extension); monitor for complications of resection and any radiation effects","Manage nasal function and any cranial nerve/orbital sequelae","Long-term follow-up through adolescence"],"pearls":["Recurrent severe epistaxis with a nasopharyngeal mass in an adolescent male is juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma until proven otherwise","NEVER biopsy a suspected JNA in the office - it is intensely vascular and can hemorrhage catastrophically; diagnose it on imaging","Anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary wall (Holman-Miller sign) on imaging is characteristic","Treat with preoperative embolization then resection (often endoscopic) - and follow with surveillance imaging for recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Sinonasal / Skull Base Tumor Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499996/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-cleft-lip-palate","name":"Cleft Lip & Palate","sec":"ent","present":["The most common congenital craniofacial anomalies - failure of fusion of the lip (CLEFT LIP, +/- alveolus) and/or the palate (CLEFT PALATE); may be unilateral or bilateral, and syndromic or non-syndromic (cleft palate more often syndromic - e.g., Pierre Robin sequence, Stickler, 22q11 deletion)","Consequences: FEEDING difficulty (poor suction with cleft palate), speech problems (velopharyngeal insufficiency - hypernasality), recurrent otitis media/EFFUSION and hearing loss (eustachian tube dysfunction is near-universal with cleft palate), and dental/facial growth issues","Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, cleft palate) can cause neonatal AIRWAY obstruction"],"dx":["Diagnosis is clinical (often prenatal ultrasound for cleft lip); examine the palate (including submucous cleft - bifid uvula, zona pellucida, notched hard palate) and assess FEEDING, AIRWAY (Robin sequence), and hearing/middle ear","Genetics/syndrome evaluation (especially isolated cleft palate); multidisciplinary CLEFT TEAM assessment","Serial hearing assessment (effusion is expected)"],"tx":["STAGED, TEAM-based repair: cleft LIP repair typically around ~3-6 months, cleft PALATE repair around ~9-12 months (to optimize speech before language develops); specialized feeding support (cleft bottles/nipples) meanwhile","Middle-ear disease managed with TYMPANOSTOMY TUBES (frequently needed); later care - speech therapy/velopharyngeal surgery for insufficiency, alveolar bone grafting, orthodontics, and rhinoplasty","Airway management for Robin sequence (positioning, mandibular distraction, or airway support)"],"tech":["Cleft lip repair and palatoplasty (staged); tympanostomy tube placement; velopharyngeal surgery (pharyngeal flap/sphincter pharyngoplasty) for insufficiency; alveolar bone grafting; mandibular distraction for Robin-sequence airway","Feeding-support devices","Team-based sequencing"],"after":["Long-term MULTIDISCIPLINARY cleft-team follow-up: SPEECH development (velopharyngeal function), HEARING/middle-ear surveillance, dental/orthodontic and facial-growth management, and psychosocial support","Monitor for velopharyngeal insufficiency and the need for secondary procedures","Care coordinated from infancy through adolescence"],"pearls":["Cleft palate brings near-universal eustachian tube dysfunction - expect chronic middle-ear effusions and hearing loss, and use tympanostomy tubes","Repair is staged and team-based: lip around 3-6 months, palate around 9-12 months (before speech develops)","Isolated cleft palate is more often syndromic - and Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, cleft palate) can obstruct the neonatal airway","Look for a submucous cleft (bifid uvula, notched hard palate) as a cause of hypernasal speech"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS / ACPA Cleft & Craniofacial Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cleft Lip and Palate","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547629/"},{"t":"American Cleft Palate-Craniofacial Association","u":"https://acpacares.org"}]},{"id":"ent-microtia-atresia","name":"Microtia & Congenital Aural Atresia","sec":"ent","present":["MICROTIA: congenital underdevelopment/malformation of the external ear (auricle), ranging from mild deformity to ANOTIA (absent ear); often accompanied by CONGENITAL AURAL ATRESIA (an absent/stenotic external auditory canal)","Aural atresia produces a maximal CONDUCTIVE hearing loss; because the inner ear (different embryologic origin) is usually normal, hearing can often be rehabilitated; may be isolated or syndromic (e.g., hemifacial microsomia/Goldenhar, Treacher Collins)","BILATERAL atresia threatens early hearing/speech development and needs prompt amplification"],"dx":["Examine the auricle and canal; AUDIOLOGIC assessment (ABR in infants) to define the conductive loss and confirm normal cochlear function; high-resolution temporal bone CT (typically later, before surgical planning) assesses middle-ear/ossicular anatomy and candidacy","Evaluate for associated anomalies/syndromes and facial nerve course; assess the contralateral ear","Determine unilateral vs bilateral (drives urgency of hearing intervention)"],"tx":["HEARING first: for BILATERAL atresia, early BONE-CONDUCTION hearing devices (softband, then implantable) are critical for speech/language; unilateral atresia - hearing device per benefit","AURICULAR RECONSTRUCTION options: autologous COSTAL CARTILAGE reconstruction (typically after age ~6-10 when cartilage/ear size suffices) or a prosthetic ear; atresia canal surgery (atresiaplasty) in selected favorable candidates, or an implantable bone-conduction device for hearing","Coordinate the sequence of ear reconstruction and any atresia/hearing surgery"],"tech":["Autologous rib-cartilage auricular reconstruction or prosthetic auricular rehabilitation; atresiaplasty (canal reconstruction) in select candidates; bone-conduction/implantable hearing devices","Temporal bone CT-based candidacy assessment","Staged reconstruction"],"after":["Ongoing audiologic rehabilitation and speech/language monitoring (especially bilateral cases); staged reconstruction follow-up and revision; psychosocial support","Monitor hearing device function; coordinate reconstruction timing with growth","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["Microtia often comes with aural atresia and a maximal conductive loss - but the inner ear is usually normal, so hearing can be rehabilitated","Bilateral atresia is a hearing/speech emergency of development - fit an early bone-conduction device (softband) promptly","Auricular reconstruction (rib cartilage or prosthesis) is typically staged and done later, once the ear/cartilage is large enough (~age 6-10)","Assess for syndromes (hemifacial microsomia, Treacher Collins) and the aberrant facial nerve course before atresia surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Microtia / Aural Atresia Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Microtia / Congenital Aural Atresia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562155/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-tmj-disorders","name":"Temporomandibular Joint Disorders","sec":"ent","present":["TEMPOROMANDIBULAR DISORDERS (TMD): a common group of conditions affecting the temporomandibular JOINT and the muscles of mastication - a frequent cause of facial pain and REFERRED OTALGIA (ear pain with a normal ear exam)","Presents with preauricular/jaw PAIN, joint CLICKING/popping or crepitus, limited or deviated mouth opening, and headache; often linked to BRUXISM (clenching/grinding), stress, malocclusion, and disc displacement or joint arthritis","Because of the joint's proximity to the ear, patients frequently present to ENT with 'ear pain' or 'ear fullness'"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis: palpate the joint and masticatory muscles, assess opening range/deviation and joint sounds, and confirm a NORMAL ear exam (identifying TMD as the source of referred otalgia); classify muscular (myofascial) vs intra-articular (disc/arthritis)","Imaging (MRI for disc/soft tissue, CT for bony/arthritic changes) for refractory or atypical cases; exclude other causes of facial pain","Screen for bruxism and contributing habits"],"tx":["CONSERVATIVE care is first-line and effective for most: patient education, soft diet, jaw rest, warm compresses, NSAIDs, stress management, physical therapy/jaw exercises, and OCCLUSAL SPLINTS/bite guards (for bruxism)","Refractory intra-articular disease: intra-articular injections, arthrocentesis/arthroscopy, and (rarely) open TMJ surgery; address dental/occlusal factors","Treat comorbid bruxism and myofascial pain"],"tech":["Occlusal splint/bite guard therapy; arthrocentesis/TMJ arthroscopy and intra-articular injections for refractory intra-articular disease; open TMJ surgery rarely","Physical therapy/jaw rehabilitation","Dental/occlusal management"],"after":["Most improve with conservative measures - reinforce habit change (clenching), continue splint/physical therapy, and reassess; escalate only for refractory disease","Manage stress/bruxism long-term; monitor for chronic pain patterns","Follow-up for persistent or progressive joint disease"],"pearls":["TMJ disorders are a common cause of referred otalgia - ear pain/fullness with a NORMAL ear exam should prompt a TMJ and masticatory-muscle exam","Most TMD responds to conservative care - soft diet, NSAIDs, jaw rest, physical therapy, stress/bruxism management, and a bite splint","Reserve injections, arthrocentesis/arthroscopy, and open surgery for refractory intra-articular disease","Address the driver (clenching/grinding, stress, occlusion) or symptoms recur"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Temporomandibular Disorders Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Temporomandibular Joint Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551612/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-chronic-cough-pvfm","name":"Chronic Cough & Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion","sec":"ent","present":["CHRONIC COUGH (>8 weeks) - after excluding smoking, ACE inhibitors, and chest pathology, the common 'upper airway' contributors are UPPER AIRWAY COUGH SYNDROME (postnasal drip), asthma/cough-variant asthma, and REFLUX (GERD/LPR); a subset have neurogenic/laryngeal HYPERSENSITIVITY (chronic cough hypersensitivity syndrome)","PARADOXICAL VOCAL FOLD MOTION (PVFM/vocal cord dysfunction): inappropriate ADDUCTION of the vocal folds during INSPIRATION, producing episodic DYSPNEA, INSPIRATORY stridor/wheeze, throat tightness, and voice change - frequently MISDIAGNOSED and treated as refractory ASTHMA","PVFM can cause dramatic, frightening episodes but the lungs are normal between attacks"],"dx":["Chronic cough: stepwise workup for the common causes (postnasal drip, asthma, reflux) and empiric treatment; laryngoscopy and consideration of laryngeal hypersensitivity when refractory","PVFM: LARYNGOSCOPY (ideally during symptoms/provocation) shows paradoxical inspiratory vocal fold ADDUCTION with a posterior glottic chink; pulmonary function may show a TRUNCATED/flattened INSPIRATORY flow-volume loop (vs the expiratory obstruction of asthma)","Distinguish PVFM from asthma (they can coexist) - normal oxygenation and lack of response to asthma therapy are clues"],"tx":["CHRONIC COUGH: treat the identified cause (nasal steroids/antihistamine for postnasal drip, asthma therapy, reflux management); for refractory neurogenic cough - SPEECH THERAPY (cough suppression techniques) and neuromodulators","PVFM: reassurance/education, SPEECH-LANGUAGE THERAPY (laryngeal control/breathing techniques - the mainstay), treat triggers (reflux, allergy, exercise), and address anxiety; acute episodes are self-limited","Avoid escalating asthma medications for what is actually PVFM"],"tech":["No procedure - laryngoscopy for diagnosis; speech-language (laryngeal control) therapy is the primary treatment for both refractory cough and PVFM","Treat contributing reflux/allergy/asthma","Botulinum toxin rarely for severe refractory PVFM"],"after":["Reinforce speech-therapy techniques and trigger control; reassess and treat coexisting asthma/reflux/allergy; manage anxiety where relevant","Monitor for symptom control and avoid unnecessary asthma escalation/intubation during PVFM episodes","Long-term follow-up for refractory cases"],"pearls":["Paradoxical vocal fold motion masquerades as refractory 'asthma' - inspiratory stridor, throat tightness, normal oxygenation, and a flattened inspiratory flow-volume loop are the clues; laryngoscopy during symptoms confirms it","Speech-language therapy (laryngeal control/breathing techniques) is the mainstay for PVFM - not more asthma medication","Chronic cough usually comes down to postnasal drip, asthma, or reflux (after excluding ACE inhibitors and smoking) - treat the cause","Refractory neurogenic cough responds to cough-suppression speech therapy and neuromodulators, reflecting laryngeal hypersensitivity"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Chronic Cough / PVFM Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion / Chronic Cough","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534789/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-tracheostomy","name":"Tracheostomy & Long-Term Airway Management","sec":"ent","present":["TRACHEOSTOMY - a surgical airway into the trachea - is performed for prolonged mechanical VENTILATION/weaning, upper airway OBSTRUCTION (tumor, bilateral vocal fold paralysis, severe OSA/craniofacial), airway protection with chronic aspiration, and pulmonary toilet","Enables ventilator weaning, reduces laryngeal injury from prolonged intubation, and improves comfort/communication for long-term airway patients","Carries important early and late complications and specific emergencies (obstruction, decannulation, bleeding)"],"dx":["Assess the indication and airway anatomy; choose open surgical vs percutaneous dilational technique and timing (e.g., after ~1-2 weeks of intubation when prolonged ventilation is anticipated)","Confirm tube position and ventilation; plan tube type/size (cuffed vs uncuffed, fenestrated) by need","Evaluate readiness for downsizing/decannulation over time"],"tx":["Perform the tracheostomy (open or percutaneous) and provide meticulous care: suctioning, HUMIDIFICATION, inner-cannula cleaning, stoma care, and scheduled tube changes (the FIRST tube change is delayed until the tract matures, ~5-7 days)","Manage emergencies: a DISLODGED fresh (immature) tracheostomy is an EMERGENCY (do not blindly force a tube into a false passage - ventilate from above/orally as needed); mucus plugging/obstruction - suction/replace inner cannula; TRACHEOINNOMINATE ARTERY FISTULA - herald bleed then massive hemorrhage (overinflate cuff/apply pressure, emergent surgery)","Plan weaning: capping trials, downsizing, and DECANNULATION when the airway/protection allows"],"tech":["Open surgical or percutaneous dilational tracheostomy; tube changes/downsizing; management of tracheoinnominate fistula (Utley maneuver/overinflation and emergent control); decannulation protocol","Cuff/fenestration management","Speaking-valve fitting"],"after":["Ongoing tracheostomy care and education (patient/caregiver), humidification, and communication/swallowing support (speaking valve, swallow evaluation); watch for stomal issues, granulation, and tracheal STENOSIS","Structured weaning and DECANNULATION when appropriate; monitor for late complications (tracheomalacia, stenosis, tracheocutaneous fistula)","Long-term airway follow-up"],"pearls":["A dislodged FRESH (immature) tracheostomy is an emergency - do not blindly force a tube into a false passage; ventilate orally/from above and get help (the first tube change waits ~5-7 days for the tract to mature)","Sentinel bleeding from a tracheostomy can herald a tracheoinnominate artery fistula - a catastrophic hemorrhage; overinflate the cuff/apply pressure and get emergent surgical control","Humidification and suctioning prevent mucus plugging - a common cause of tracheostomy obstruction","Plan for decannulation (capping/downsizing) once the airway and airway protection allow - and watch for late tracheal stenosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Tracheostomy Care Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tracheostomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559124/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-benign-oral-lesions","name":"Ranula, Mucocele & Benign Oral Cavity Lesions","sec":"ent","present":["Common benign oral cavity lesions. MUCOCELE: a mucus-retention/extravasation cyst - a bluish, soft, painless swelling typically on the LOWER LIP (from minor salivary gland trauma); recurrent as patients bite the area","RANULA: a mucus retention pseudocyst of the SUBLINGUAL gland on the FLOOR OF THE MOUTH (a translucent bluish 'frog-belly' swelling); a PLUNGING ranula extends through the mylohyoid into the neck, presenting as a neck mass","Other benign lesions: torus (palatinus/mandibularis - bony exostoses), fibroma (irritation), pyogenic granuloma, and papilloma - generally slow-growing and asymptomatic, but persistent/atypical lesions warrant biopsy to exclude malignancy"],"dx":["Largely CLINICAL diagnosis by appearance/location; imaging (ultrasound/MRI) for a ranula (define plunging component) or deep/atypical lesions; BIOPSY any lesion that is persistent, growing, indurated, ulcerated, or otherwise suspicious to exclude malignancy","Distinguish benign lesions from oral cancer (see oral cavity cancer)","Assess for recurrence/trauma source"],"tx":["MUCOCELE: often recurs - definitive treatment is EXCISION (with the associated minor salivary glands) or marsupialization; small ones may resolve","RANULA: marsupialization for simple ranulas, but EXCISION OF THE SUBLINGUAL GLAND is the most definitive treatment (lowest recurrence), especially for plunging ranulas","Other lesions: excision if symptomatic/enlarging or for diagnosis; tori only if interfering with dentures/function"],"tech":["Excision of mucocele with associated minor salivary glands; sublingual gland excision/marsupialization for ranula; excisional biopsy of other benign lesions","Imaging for plunging ranula","Send specimens for histology"],"after":["Monitor for RECURRENCE (mucoceles and ranulas recur if the source gland is not addressed); confirm benign histology; counsel to avoid the trauma source (lip biting)","Reassess any lesion that recurs atypically or fails to heal (exclude malignancy)","Routine follow-up"],"pearls":["A bluish, soft, recurrent swelling on the lower lip is a mucocele; a translucent floor-of-mouth swelling is a ranula (a plunging ranula presents as a neck mass)","Definitive treatment of a ranula is usually sublingual gland excision - marsupialization alone recurs more often","Address the source gland/trauma or these mucus cysts recur","Biopsy any persistent, growing, indurated, or ulcerated oral lesion - do not assume benign; oral cancer is the key exclusion"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Oral Cavity Lesion Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ranula / Mucocele / Benign Oral Lesions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560855/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"ent-free-flap-reconstruction","name":"Head & Neck Microvascular Free-Flap Reconstruction","sec":"ent","present":["MICROVASCULAR FREE-FLAP reconstruction restores large/complex defects after head-and-neck cancer ablation (or trauma) - transferring tissue with its own artery and vein, which are anastomosed under the microscope to neck vessels to restore FORM and FUNCTION (speech, swallowing, oral competence, coverage)","Common flaps: RADIAL FOREARM (thin, pliable - oral cavity/pharynx), FIBULA (bone - mandibular/maxillary reconstruction, allows dental implants), ANTEROLATERAL THIGH (bulk/soft tissue), and scapular/latissimus flaps","It sits at the top of the reconstructive ladder for defects too large for local/regional flaps"],"dx":["Analyze the defect (soft tissue vs composite bony, size, functional needs) and select the flap; assess DONOR-site suitability and vascular supply (e.g., Allen test for radial forearm; angiography/exam of the leg for fibula) and RECIPIENT neck vessels","Account for prior radiation/surgery (vessel-depleted neck) and comorbidities","Plan for dental rehabilitation (fibula) where relevant"],"tx":["Harvest the flap and perform microvascular ANASTOMOSIS to recipient vessels, insetting to reconstruct the defect; restore mandibular continuity (fibula), lining/coverage (radial forearm/ALT), and function","Meticulous flap monitoring in the early postoperative period to detect vascular compromise early (salvage depends on speed)","Coordinate with ablative/oncologic and dental teams"],"tech":["Radial forearm, fibula (osseous), anterolateral thigh, and scapular free flaps; microvascular arterial/venous anastomosis; osteocutaneous reconstruction with plating and planned dental implants","Flap monitoring (clinical exam, implantable Doppler)","Vein grafts/vessel selection in the vessel-depleted neck"],"after":["Intensive early FLAP MONITORING (color, temperature, capillary refill, Doppler) - VENOUS congestion or arterial compromise demands emergent RE-EXPLORATION for salvage; donor-site care; DVT and pulmonary prophylaxis","Rehabilitation of SPEECH and SWALLOWING, tracheostomy/decannulation, dental rehabilitation (implants on fibula), and management of any radiation effects","Long-term oncologic surveillance and functional follow-up"],"pearls":["Free-flap reconstruction restores form AND function after major head-and-neck ablation - match the flap to the defect (radial forearm for pliable lining, fibula for bone/dental implants, ALT for bulk)","Early flap monitoring is everything - venous congestion or arterial compromise needs emergent re-exploration; salvage is time-dependent","Plan around the vessel-depleted (previously irradiated/operated) neck - recipient vessels and vein grafts matter","The fibula flap allows mandibular reconstruction with future dental implants - plan dental rehabilitation from the start"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Microvascular Reconstruction Guidance","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Free Flap Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK552152/"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Patient Resources","u":"https://www.enthealth.org"}]},{"id":"fm-htn","name":"Hypertension (Outpatient Diagnosis & Titration)","sec":"fm","present":["Usually asymptomatic, found on screening; USPSTF: screen all adults >=18, annually if >=40 or high-risk","Confirm out-of-office before labeling: home BP monitoring (HBPM) or 24h ABPM to exclude white-coat (office high/home normal) and catch masked HTN (office normal/home high)","Proper technique: seated 5 min, feet flat, arm at heart level, correct cuff size, no caffeine/smoking 30 min prior; average >=2 readings on >=2 occasions"],"dx":["ACC/AHA 2017 thresholds: normal <120/80, elevated 120-129/<80, stage 1 130-139/80-89, stage 2 >=140/90","Initial workup: BMP, lipids, A1c, TSH, UA, urine ACR, ECG; calculate 10-yr ASCVD risk (PREVENT or pooled cohort) to guide stage 1 treatment decision","Screen for secondary HTN if onset <30, resistant (>=3 drugs incl diuretic), abrupt worsening, or clues: renin/aldosterone ratio (primary aldosteronism is the most common secondary cause), OSA screening (STOP-BANG), renal artery disease, drugs (NSAIDs, decongestants, stimulants, OCPs)"],"tx":["Lifestyle for all: DASH diet (-11 mmHg), Na <1.5-2.3 g/d, weight loss (~1 mmHg per kg), aerobic exercise 150 min/wk, limit alcohol","Start meds: stage 2 or stage 1 with clinical ASCVD/DM/CKD/10-yr risk >=10%; stage 2 or >20/10 above goal -> start 2 drugs (ideally single-pill combo, improves adherence)","First-line: thiazide (chlorthalidone/indapamide preferred over HCTZ), ACEi/ARB, or DHP-CCB (amlodipine); Black patients without CKD: thiazide or CCB first per ACC/AHA","Goal <130/80 for most (SPRINT); titrate q2-4 wks using home BP averages, not single office readings","Resistant HTN: confirm adherence + ABPM, maximize chlorthalidone, then add spironolactone 25 mg (PATHWAY-2)","Never combine ACEi + ARB; check K and Cr 2-4 wks after starting/titrating ACEi/ARB or MRA; up to 30% Cr rise is acceptable"],"pearls":["White-coat effect is why you should never titrate off one office reading — the home BP log is the real chart","HCTZ 12.5-25 mg is the weakest link; chlorthalidone at the same dose is ~2x more potent and evidence-backed","An unexplained hypokalemia in a hypertensive patient is primary aldosteronism until proven otherwise — check ARR before starting the MRA","NSAID use is the most common reversible cause of 'treatment failure' in clinic"],"refs":[{"t":"ACC/AHA High Blood Pressure Guideline","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"USPSTF Hypertension Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Essential Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539859/"}]},{"id":"fm-t2dm","name":"Type 2 Diabetes (Outpatient Management & Complication Screening)","sec":"fm","present":["Often asymptomatic on screening; USPSTF: screen adults 35-70 with overweight/obesity q3y; ADA adds risk-based screening (family hx, GDM, PCOS, high-risk ethnicity)","Classic symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss) suggest marked hyperglycemia; rule out T1DM/LADA in lean adults with rapid onset (GAD antibodies, C-peptide)","Diagnosis: A1c >=6.5%, FPG >=126, 2h OGTT >=200, or random >=200 with symptoms; asymptomatic requires 2 abnormal tests (same or separate samples); prediabetes A1c 5.7-6.4%"],"dx":["At diagnosis and annually: A1c q3mo if not at goal (q6mo if stable), urine ACR + eGFR, dilated eye exam (or retinal photo), monofilament + vibration foot exam, lipids, BP","A1c goal <7% for most; <8% or symptom-based for limited life expectancy/hypoglycemia risk; use CGM time-in-range >70% when available","Screen for comorbid: MASLD (FIB-4 per ADA), OSA, depression (PHQ-9); check B12 on long-term metformin"],"tx":["Metformin 500 mg daily -> titrate to 1000 mg BID over 2-4 wks with food (GI tolerance); avoid if eGFR <30, halve dose 30-45","Compelling-indication drugs regardless of A1c (ADA Standards): ASCVD -> GLP-1 RA (semaglutide, LEADER/SUSTAIN-6 class) or SGLT2i (EMPA-REG); HF or CKD (eGFR/ACR) -> SGLT2i (DAPA-HF, DAPA-CKD); obesity -> GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide (SURPASS/SURMOUNT)","Titrate GLP-1 RA slowly (e.g., semaglutide 0.25 mg wkly x4 wks then 0.5 -> 1 -> 2 mg) to limit nausea; hold before procedures per anesthesia guidance","Insulin when A1c >10%, glucose >=300, or catabolic: start basal 10 units or 0.1-0.2 u/kg qhs, titrate 2 units q3d to FPG 80-130","Sulfonylureas cheap but hypoglycemia + weight gain — avoid glyburide in elderly/CKD (Beers)","Deintensify in elderly on insulin/SU with tight A1c — hypoglycemia kills faster than an A1c of 7.8"],"pearls":["The eGFR/ACR pair changes the drug list — an ACR of 300 is an SGLT2i indication, not just a referral trigger","SGLT2i: hold for surgery/acute illness (euglycemic DKA), warn about genital mycotic infections, and expect a ~0.3 A1c 'cost' when eGFR is low — you're treating the kidney, not the sugar","Metformin-associated B12 deficiency mimics diabetic neuropathy — check B12 before blaming the diabetes","A rapidly rising A1c despite adherence in a lean adult = get GAD antibodies; LADA masquerades as 'metformin failure'"],"refs":[{"t":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"USPSTF Prediabetes and T2DM Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Type 2 Diabetes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513253/"}]},{"id":"fm-lipids","name":"Hyperlipidemia & ASCVD Risk Assessment","sec":"fm","present":["Asymptomatic; screen adults 40-75 with lipid panel + 10-yr ASCVD risk (pooled cohort equations or newer PREVENT); earlier if family hx premature ASCVD or FH suspicion","Nonfasting panel acceptable for screening; fasting if TG >400 or confirming severe hypertriglyceridemia","LDL >=190 at any age = probable familial hypercholesterolemia: treat regardless of risk score, screen first-degree relatives (cascade screening)"],"dx":["AHA/ACC 2018 groups: clinical ASCVD (secondary prevention), LDL >=190, DM age 40-75, primary prevention by 10-yr risk (<5% low, 5-7.5% borderline, 7.5-20% intermediate, >=20% high)","Risk enhancers tip intermediate patients toward statin: family hx premature ASCVD, CKD, metabolic syndrome, inflammatory disease (RA, psoriasis, HIV), South Asian ancestry, persistent LDL >=160, Lp(a) >=50, hsCRP >=2, preeclampsia/early menopause","Coronary artery calcium (CAC) score for shared decision-making when intermediate risk and statin-reluctant: CAC 0 (and no diabetes/smoking/FHx) -> can defer, recheck 5-10 yrs; CAC >=100 or >=75th percentile -> treat","Secondary causes before labeling: TSH, A1c, urine protein (nephrotic), alcohol, drugs (thiazides, retinoids, antipsychotics)"],"tx":["High-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40-80, rosuvastatin 20-40) for ASCVD, LDL >=190, or 10-yr risk >=20%; moderate-intensity for most DM and intermediate risk with enhancers","Goals: >=50% LDL reduction (high-intensity); secondary prevention very-high-risk threshold LDL <70 -> add ezetimibe (IMPROVE-IT), then PCSK9i (FOURIER/ODYSSEY) if still above","Recheck lipids 4-12 wks after start/change, then q3-12mo; nonadherence is the top cause of 'statin failure'","Statin intolerance: verify true myalgia (bilateral proximal, onset weeks, resolves off drug); rechallenge lower dose or alternate-day rosuvastatin; bempedoic acid (CLEAR Outcomes) for truly intolerant","TG 150-499: lifestyle + address secondary causes; TG >=500: fibrate + very-low-fat diet to prevent pancreatitis; icosapent ethyl (REDUCE-IT) for TG 135-499 on statin with ASCVD/DM+risk","Check CK only if symptomatic; baseline ALT, no routine LFT monitoring"],"pearls":["The statin discussion is a risk conversation, not a cholesterol conversation — patients anchor on the LDL number, the benefit tracks the absolute risk","CAC of zero is the most powerful 'de-risker' in the intermediate group — but it does not apply to smokers, diabetics, or strong family history","Muscle symptoms on statins: nocebo is real (SAMSON trial ~90% of symptom burden present on placebo) — rechallenge before abandoning the class","Check Lp(a) once in a lifetime, especially with family hx premature ASCVD — it reclassifies risk and flags the family"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC Cholesterol Guideline","u":"https://www.acc.org"},{"t":"USPSTF Statin Use for Primary Prevention","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hyperlipidemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559182/"}]},{"id":"fm-obesity","name":"Obesity & Weight Management (GLP-1 Era)","sec":"fm","present":["BMI >=30 (or >=27 with comorbidity for pharmacotherapy); BMI limitations: use waist circumference (>102 cm M, >88 cm F) and body composition context, lower thresholds for Asian patients (BMI >=27.5 high risk)","Screen for drivers and consequences: meds (antipsychotics, insulin, SU, steroids, some antidepressants), hypothyroidism, Cushing clues, binge-eating disorder, OSA (STOP-BANG), MASLD, PCOS, depression","Use patient-first language ('patient with obesity'); weight stigma is a documented barrier to care"],"dx":["Baseline: BP, A1c/FPG, lipids, ALT (MASLD: FIB-4 if abnormal or T2DM), TSH if symptomatic, consider OSA testing","Set outcome goals: 5-10% loss improves BP/A1c/lipids; 10-15%+ modifies OSA, MASH, and with newer agents approaches metabolic-surgery territory","Assess readiness and prior attempts; screen eating disorders (binge eating) before prescribing — stimulant-type agents can worsen purging"],"tx":["Foundation: intensive lifestyle intervention (USPSTF B: refer BMI >=30 to intensive multicomponent behavioral program), 500-750 kcal/d deficit, protein preservation + resistance training to protect lean mass","Pharmacotherapy (BMI >=30 or >=27 + comorbidity): semaglutide 2.4 mg wkly (STEP-1 ~15% loss), tirzepatide 5-15 mg wkly (SURMOUNT-1 ~20%); titrate monthly, manage nausea (small meals, slow titration), counsel on gallstones and muscle loss; SELECT trial: semaglutide cuts MACE in obesity + ASCVD without DM","Older agents when access limits: phentermine-topiramate (avoid pregnancy - teratogenic, check BP), naltrexone-bupropion (avoid opioids/seizures), phentermine short-term, orlistat rarely tolerated","Anticipate discontinuation regain (STEP-4: most weight regained off drug) — frame as chronic disease therapy like a statin, plan maintenance","Metabolic-bariatric surgery referral: BMI >=35 regardless of comorbidity or 30-34.9 with metabolic disease (ASMBS/IFSO 2022); sleeve and RYGB dominate; primary care owns long-term micronutrient monitoring (B12, iron, D, calcium)","Treat the comorbidity list in parallel — obesity visits should still titrate the BP and statin"],"pearls":["Ask permission before discussing weight — it doubles the odds the conversation goes anywhere","GLP-1 nausea is a titration problem, not a stop signal; plateaus at max dose are expected, not failure","Screen for binge-eating disorder before and during pharmacotherapy — the drugs suppress appetite, not the disorder","The most commonly missed reversible driver is the med list: swap mirtazapine/olanzapine/glyburide before adding a weight-loss drug"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Weight Loss Interventions","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"AGA / Obesity Pharmacotherapy Guidance","u":"https://gastro.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Obesity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459357/"}]},{"id":"fm-tobacco","name":"Tobacco Cessation & Vaping","sec":"fm","present":["Ask at every visit (USPSTF A: screen all adults, advise cessation, provide interventions); the 5 A's: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange follow-up","Assess dependence: time to first cigarette <30 min after waking and >=20/day predict high dependence; document pack-years (triggers lung cancer screening: 50-80 yo, >=20 pack-yrs, current or quit <15 yrs -> annual LDCT)","Ask separately about vaping/e-cigarettes and dual use, including adolescents (nicotine salts deliver high doses)"],"dx":["No labs needed; screen for comorbid depression/alcohol use (co-occurring and lower quit rates)","Motivational interviewing for ambivalence; even brief (<3 min) advice increases quit rates","Offer treatment to ALL smokers regardless of stated readiness — opt-out framing outperforms waiting for a quit date"],"tx":["Combination NRT is first-line workhorse: patch (21 mg if >=10 cigs/day, taper 21->14->7 over 8-12 wks) PLUS short-acting gum/lozenge 2-4 mg PRN cravings; start patch BEFORE quit date is acceptable","Varenicline is the most effective single agent (EAGLES): 0.5 mg daily x3d -> 0.5 BID x4d -> 1 mg BID x12 wks; nausea (take with food) and vivid dreams common; EAGLES debunked the neuropsychiatric black-box — safe in stable psychiatric illness","Bupropion SR 150 mg daily x3d then BID: good with comorbid depression or weight concern; avoid in seizure/eating disorders","Combine varenicline + NRT or extend therapy to 6 mo for relapse-prone patients; arrange follow-up within 1-2 wks of quit date (highest relapse window)","Quitline (1-800-QUIT-NOW) and text programs add ~5% absolute quit rate on top of meds","Vaping cessation: no FDA-approved therapy; extrapolate NRT/varenicline by nicotine load; for youth, behavioral programs (e.g., text-based) are the evidence base; never recommend starting vaping as cessation in a nonsmoker, though adult smokers who fully switch reduce toxicant exposure"],"pearls":["A 'failed quit' with meds usually means underdosed NRT — a pack-a-day smoker gets ~20-40 mg nicotine; a lone 14 mg patch is homeopathy","Smoking induces CYP1A2: quitting RAISES levels of clozapine, olanzapine, theophylline, caffeine — adjust and warn","Weight gain averages 4-5 kg post-quit; pre-empt with bupropion/varenicline choice and the exercise conversation, not by delaying the quit","Relapse is the rule (avg 8-11 attempts) — normalize it and re-prescribe the day they walk back in"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Tobacco Cessation in Adults","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"CDC Smoking & Tobacco Use","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco"},{"t":"StatPearls: Smoking Cessation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482442/"}]},{"id":"fm-prevention","name":"Adult Preventive Care & Screening Schedules (USPSTF)","sec":"fm","present":["Anchor the annual/periodic visit to USPSTF A/B recommendations (ACA-covered without cost-sharing); organize by age-sex bands rather than memorizing piecemeal","Every adult: BP at least annually, depression + anxiety screening (PHQ-2/GAD-2 gateway), unhealthy alcohol (AUDIT-C) and drug use, tobacco, HIV once 15-65, hepatitis C once 18-79, intimate partner violence (women of reproductive age), BMI","Latent TB screening in increased-risk adults (born in high-prevalence countries, congregate settings)"],"dx":["Cancer screening core set: colorectal 45-75 (colonoscopy q10y, FIT annual, or FIT-DNA q1-3y); breast: biennial mammography 40-74 (USPSTF 2024); cervical 21-65 (cytology q3y, or 30-65 hrHPV q5y); lung: annual LDCT 50-80 with >=20 pack-yrs current/quit <15y; prostate 55-69 shared decision (PSA, grade C)","Metabolic: diabetes/prediabetes 35-70 with overweight q3y; lipids + ASCVD risk 40-75; osteoporosis DXA women >=65 (younger if FRAX-elevated) and per 2025 update postmenopausal <65 with risk factors","AAA: one-time ultrasound men 65-75 who ever smoked; hepatitis B in high-risk; anxiety <65 (2023 addition)","Track intervals in the problem list — the interval IS the order (q10y colonoscopy vs annual FIT changes the recall system)"],"tx":["Convert positives into pathways: positive FIT -> diagnostic colonoscopy (do not repeat FIT); abnormal LDCT -> Lung-RADS-driven follow-up; PHQ-9 positive -> same-visit severity assessment and treatment plan","Chemoprevention: statin per ASCVD risk; low-dose aspirin primary prevention is now individualized 40-59 with >=10% risk only (grade C), not routine; folic acid 0.4-0.8 mg for anyone who could become pregnant","Behavioral referrals carry B grades: intensive behavioral counseling for obesity, healthy diet/activity for high CV risk, fall-prevention exercise for adults >=65 at risk","Stop-screening conversations are care too: colorectal 76-85 selective, stop 85+; cervical stops at 65 with adequate negatives; mammography beyond 75 is insufficient-evidence — frame by life expectancy (~10-yr rule for most cancer screening)"],"pearls":["Screening is a system, not a visit: use standing orders, registries, and the recall list — the patients who need screening most attend least","The 10-year life-expectancy rule prevents the 88-year-old screening colonoscopy nobody benefits from","Grade C means 'selectively offer' — PSA and aspirin are conversations, not checkboxes","When patients decline colonoscopy, offer FIT in the same breath — the best test is the one that gets done"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF A & B Recommendations","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"AAFP Clinical Preventive Services","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"CDC Preventive Care Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"fm-adult-imms","name":"Adult Immunizations","sec":"fm","present":["Review at every visit using the ACIP adult schedule; catch-up happens in primary care, not pharmacies alone","Core adult set: annual influenza (>=6 mo, all); COVID per current ACIP season guidance (risk/shared decision framing for younger adults, recommended for older/immunocompromised); Tdap once then Td/Tdap q10y (Tdap every pregnancy 27-36 wks); MMR/varicella if non-immune","Age-triggered: RSV single dose for >=75 (and 60-74 or 50-74 high-risk per updates); shingles (RZV, Shingrix) 2 doses >=50 (>=19 if immunocompromised); pneumococcal >=50 (2024 lowered from 65): PCV20 or PCV21 alone, or PCV15 -> PPSV23"],"dx":["Risk-based additions: hepatitis B universal 19-59 (and >=60 with risk); hepatitis A (travel, MSM, liver disease, drug use); HPV catch-up through 26, shared decision 27-45; MenACWY/MenB (asplenia, complement deficiency, eculizumab, college outbreaks); Hib (asplenia); consider newer meningococcal pentavalent where indicated","Immunocompromised/asplenia deserve a deliberate protocol visit: no live vaccines (MMR, varicella, live zoster is obsolete anyway), time vaccines around immunosuppression, RZV strongly indicated","Pregnancy: Tdap each pregnancy, influenza (inactivated), RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) 32-36 wks Sep-Jan OR nirsevimab for the infant; no live vaccines"],"tx":["True contraindications are rare (anaphylaxis to vaccine/component); egg allergy is NOT a contraindication to any influenza vaccine per current ACIP","Coadministration is fine for non-live vaccines (different sites); RZV reactogenicity is real — warn about 1-2 days of arm pain/myalgia so dose 2 happens (2-6 mo later)","Document refusals and readdress; a strong provider recommendation ('you are due for X today') is the single biggest predictor of uptake","Use state immunization registries to reconcile records; when in doubt with no records, revaccinate (extra doses of inactivated vaccines are safe) or use serology for MMR/varicella/HepB"],"pearls":["Pneumococcal at 50 is the update everyone misses — the 65 anchor is gone","Shingrix works even after zoster and after Zostavax — vaccinate once the acute episode settles","The RSV vaccine is (currently) not annual — one dose, and check the age/risk bands each season because they keep moving","Every pregnancy resets Tdap; 'I had it 3 years ago' does not carry maternal antibody to this baby"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/ACIP Adult Immunization Schedule","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines"},{"t":"Immunize.org Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.immunize.org"},{"t":"AAFP Immunizations","u":"https://www.aafp.org"}]},{"id":"fm-well-child","name":"Well-Child Visits & Developmental Milestones","sec":"fm","present":["Bright Futures periodicity: newborn (3-5 d), then 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 24, 30 mo, then annually; each visit = growth, development, physical exam, screening, anticipatory guidance, vaccines","Plot weight/length/HC on WHO curves <24 mo, CDC BMI curves >=2 yrs; crossing 2 major percentile lines or HC acceleration/deceleration warrants evaluation","Formal developmental screening (ASQ-3 or PEDS) at 9, 18, 30 mo; autism-specific (M-CHAT-R/F) at 18 and 24 mo; do not rely on surveillance alone"],"dx":["Milestone anchors (CDC 2022 = 75th percentile norms): social smile 2 mo, rolls ~6 mo (was 4), sits without support 9 mo, first words ~12 mo, walks ~15 mo (12-18 range), 2-word phrases 24 mo, 50% intelligible speech 2 yrs (fully intelligible to strangers ~4 yrs)","Red flags at any age: loss of skills (regression -> urgent eval), no babbling by 12 mo, no words by 16 mo, no pointing/joint attention by 12-15 mo, persistent primitive reflexes, hand dominance before 18 mo (hemiparesis clue)","Visit-anchored screens: maternal postpartum depression (EPDS) at 1-6 mo visits; lead level at 12 and 24 mo (risk-based per state); Hgb at 12 mo; dyslipidemia once 9-11 yrs; vision/hearing per schedule; SDOH/food insecurity screening","Failed screen -> simultaneous referrals: Early Intervention (<3 yrs, no diagnosis required) or school district (>=3 yrs) AND audiology (hearing loss mimics everything) AND diagnostic eval — do not sequence them"],"tx":["Anticipatory guidance by age: safe sleep (alone, back, crib, nothing in it) and no screen time <18-24 mo; solids ~6 mo with early allergen introduction (LEAP: early peanut prevents allergy); rear-facing car seat to at least 2 yrs; water/firearm safety (secured, locked, unloaded) at every visit","Fluoride varnish from first tooth q3-6mo in primary care (USPSTF B), dental home by age 1","Vitamin D 400 IU/d for breastfed infants; iron-fortified foods/supplement discussion at 4-6 mo for exclusively breastfed","Obesity trajectory: intervene early with family-based lifestyle counseling; AAP 2023 allows adolescent pharmacotherapy/surgery referral in severe obesity","Normalize the wide range of normal while acting on true flags — 'watchful waiting' for a missed milestone at a screening age is no longer the standard"],"pearls":["'He's a late talker like his dad' is the classic trap — screen, refer, and check hearing; you can always celebrate being wrong","Regression is never normal — loss of language or social skills gets an urgent autism/neurology pathway, not a recheck in 3 months","Early Intervention referral needs no diagnosis and is free — refer on suspicion, evaluation IS the workup","The 75th-percentile milestone rewrite means 'not meeting a milestone' is now a real signal, not a shrug"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Bright Futures","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"CDC Developmental Milestones","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/actearly"},{"t":"USPSTF Child Preventive Services","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"}]},{"id":"fm-child-imms","name":"Childhood Immunization Schedule & Vaccine Hesitancy","sec":"fm","present":["Backbone (ACIP/AAP): birth HepB; 2-4-6 mo DTaP/IPV/Hib/PCV/HepB + rotavirus (first dose by 15 wks, done by 8 mo); 12-15 mo MMR, varicella, HepA (2 doses 6 mo apart), Hib/PCV boosters; 4-6 yr DTaP/IPV/MMR/varicella boosters; 11-12 yr Tdap, HPV (2 doses if started <15, 3 if >=15 or immunocompromised), MenACWY (booster 16); MenB 16-23 shared decision","Seasonal: influenza annually >=6 mo (2 doses first season if <9 yrs); COVID per current ACIP; RSV protection = maternal Abrysvo OR infant nirsevimab (all <8 mo entering first season; high-risk 8-19 mo second season)","Hesitancy presents as delay requests, alternative schedules, or refusal — treat it as a spectrum, not a binary"],"dx":["Verify status via state registry every visit — sick visits count as vaccine visits (minor illness +/- low-grade fever is NOT a contraindication)","Catch-up rules: never restart a series, minimum intervals govern; live vaccines (MMR/varicella) — give same day or separate by >=28 days","True contraindications are few: anaphylaxis to prior dose/component; live vaccines in significant immunosuppression or pregnancy; rotavirus in SCID or hx intussusception; encephalopathy within 7 d of pertussis vaccine for further pertussis doses","Distinguish hesitancy drivers: safety fears (MMR-autism myth — Wakefield retracted, repeatedly refuted), 'too many too soon', needle distress, access/logistics masquerading as refusal"],"tx":["Presumptive announcement ('Sam gets three vaccines today') beats participatory framing ('what do you want to do about shots?') — strongest evidence-based communication move","Motivational interviewing for hesitancy: elicit specific concern, ask permission to share info, address that concern only, keep the door open; do not lecture or pile on data","Offer pain mitigation (breastfeeding/sucrose for infants, topical anesthetic, upright positioning, distraction) — needle fear is a fixable driver","Accept partial wins strategically: prioritize measles-containing and pertussis-containing vaccines first if forced to sequence; document refusal with AAP form and revisit EVERY visit — many refusers vaccinate within a year","Do not dismiss families from the practice for refusal as first response (AAP discourages); an unvaccinated child with a medical home is safer than one without"],"pearls":["The strongest predictor of vaccination is your clear recommendation — hedging language ('we could think about...') reads as doubt","A firm 'no' today is often a 'yes' in six months — keep offering without relitigating the argument","Check the registry before saying 'behind' — records scattered across states cause phantom delinquency","Nirsevimab is passive immunization, not a vaccine — frame it that way for RSV-hesitant families and uptake improves"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/ACIP Child & Adolescent Schedule","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines"},{"t":"AAP Immunizations & Hesitancy Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"Immunize.org","u":"https://www.immunize.org"}]},{"id":"fm-peds-fever","name":"Pediatric Fever (Outpatient Evaluation)","sec":"fm","present":["Fever = >=38.0 C (100.4 F) rectal; parental report of tactile fever has moderate reliability — believe documented home readings","The age bands drive everything: <29 days, 29-60 days, 61-90 days, 3-36 mo, >36 mo; plus the modifiers: ill appearance, incomplete immunization, immunocompromise, indwelling hardware","Most outpatient fevers are viral; the job is finding the febrile infant who needs the ED and the older child with an occult bacterial source (UTI is the most common)"],"dx":["Age <29 days: NOT an outpatient patient — direct ED referral for full sepsis evaluation (blood, urine, CSF cultures) and empiric antibiotics regardless of appearance","29-60 days: AAP 2021 febrile infant guideline — urinalysis, blood culture, inflammatory markers (procalcitonin preferred, +/- ANC/CRP); markers abnormal -> LP/ED; well-appearing with normal markers and negative UA may be managed with close 24 h follow-up per pathway — in practice, refer unless your system supports the pathway","3-36 mo, well-appearing, immunized: exam-directed testing only; catheterized UA/culture if UTI risk (girls <24 mo, uncircumcised boys <12 mo, circumcised <6 mo, fever >=39 without source, >48 h duration); occult bacteremia is rare in the PCV/Hib era","Red flags for serious bacterial infection/alternative dx: petechiae below nipple line, meningismus, respiratory distress, poor perfusion (cap refill >3 s), inconsolability, fever >5 days (think Kawasaki: 5 d fever + 4 of 5 — conjunctivitis, rash, oral changes, extremity changes, cervical node)","No routine CXR or blood work for well-appearing febrile children with a clinical source"],"tx":["Treat discomfort, not the number: acetaminophen 15 mg/kg q4-6h or ibuprofen 10 mg/kg q6-8h (>=6 mo); do not schedule routine alternating — fuels fever phobia and dosing errors","No aspirin (Reye); no OTC cough/cold meds <6 yrs; hydration and return precautions are the actual prescription","Fever phobia counseling: fever is a response, not a disease; height does not equal severity; febrile seizures are frightening but simple ones (<15 min, generalized, once/24 h) do not damage the brain and antipyretics do not prevent them","Concrete return precautions: any fever <2 mo (immediate), fever >5 d, trouble breathing, poor intake/urine output, petechiae, stiff neck, ill appearance when afebrile, or parental gut-level concern","Antibiotics only for a defined bacterial diagnosis (AOM, strep, UTI, CAP) — 'fever' is not an indication"],"pearls":["An ill-appearing child overrides every reassuring test; a well-appearing child does not fully override age <29 days — the neonate rules are absolute","UTI is the occult source: a 'fever without source' in a young girl or uncircumcised infant boy has the urine as the workup","Day 5 of fever means re-examine, re-take the history, and say the word Kawasaki out loud","How the child looks AFTER antipyretics is the most useful vital sign in the visit"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Febrile Infant Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fever in Children","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519500/"},{"t":"CDC Kawasaki Disease","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/kawasaki"}]},{"id":"fm-otitis","name":"Otitis Media & Otitis Externa","sec":"fm","present":["AOM: acute ear pain (tugging in preverbal), fever, irritability, often on the heels of a URI; peak 6-24 mo","OE ('swimmer's ear'): otalgia worse with tragal pressure/pinna traction, canal edema and debris, water exposure or trauma history; hearing muffled if canal occluded","Otitis media with effusion (OME): painless fluid, muffled hearing, flat tympanogram — NOT an infection, common for weeks post-AOM"],"dx":["AOM diagnosis (AAP): moderate-severe TM bulging OR new otorrhea not from OE; mild bulging + recent (<48 h) otalgia + intense erythema qualifies; a red TM alone (crying, fever) is not AOM — bulging is the money finding","Pneumatic otoscopy/tympanometry to confirm effusion when uncertain; distinguish AOM (bulging, opacified, +/- fever) from OME (neutral/retracted, air-fluid level, afebrile)","OE: canal tenderness + edema + debris; think malignant (necrotizing) OE in diabetics/immunocompromised with severe pain out of proportion, granulation at bone-cartilage junction, cranial neuropathy -> urgent ENT + imaging","Complications to catch: mastoiditis (postauricular tenderness/protruding ear -> ED), TM perforation (usually heals), cholesteatoma (chronic retraction pocket with keratin debris -> ENT)"],"tx":["AOM antibiotics: definitely treat <6 mo, any severe (toxic, otalgia >48 h, T >=39), bilateral <24 mo, or otorrhea; observation option (48-72 h with guaranteed follow-up and rescue script) for unilateral non-severe >=6 mo — shared decision, most resolve","First-line amoxicillin 90 mg/kg/day divided BID (high-dose beats pneumococcal resistance); amox-clav 90/6.4 if amoxicillin in last 30 d, concurrent purulent conjunctivitis (H. flu), or treatment failure at 48-72 h; non-anaphylactic PCN allergy -> cefdinir; duration 10 d if <2 yrs or perforation, 7 d 2-5 yrs, 5-7 d >=6 yrs","Pain is the primary outcome: dose ibuprofen/acetaminophen properly regardless of antibiotic decision","Recurrent AOM (3 in 6 mo or 4 in 12 mo): ENT referral for tympanostomy tubes; NO prophylactic antibiotics; fix modifiables (smoke exposure, supine bottle, pacifier, daycare timing); confirm pneumococcal + flu vaccination","OME: watchful waiting 3 mo (antibiotics, steroids, antihistamines all ineffective); persistent >3 mo or hearing/language concern -> audiology + ENT for tubes","OE: topical is king — fluoroquinolone drops (ofloxacin or cipro-dex) 7 d, safe with perforation/tubes; wick if canal swollen shut; analgesia; dry-ear precautions; oral antibiotics only for cellulitis beyond canal, immunocompromise, or failed topicals"],"pearls":["The observation option fails when follow-up is theoretical — write the safety-net prescription ('fill only if not better in 48-72 h') and it works","Cerumen removal is diagnostic work: you cannot call AOM on a TM you have not seen","Diabetic with OE pain that wakes them at night = necrotizing OE until proven otherwise; check the glucose you forgot to ask about","Post-AOM effusion at the 2-week recheck is normal biology, not treatment failure — resist the second antibiotic"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Acute Otitis Media Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"AAO-HNS Otitis Externa Guideline","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Otitis Media","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470332/"}]},{"id":"fm-pharyngitis","name":"Pharyngitis & Strep Testing","sec":"fm","present":["Sore throat +/- fever; task one is separating viral (majority, ~70-85% adults) from group A strep (GAS: ~5-15% adults, 20-30% children 5-15)","Viral clues: cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, hoarseness, oral ulcers, diarrhea; GAS clues: abrupt onset, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior nodes, palatal petechiae, scarlatiniform rash, absence of cough","Think beyond strep: infectious mononucleosis (teens, fatigue, posterior nodes, splenomegaly), acute HIV (risk + mono-like), gonococcal pharyngitis (exposure history), and the airway emergencies — peritonsillar abscess (hot-potato voice, trismus, uvular deviation), epiglottitis/deep space (drooling, stridor, tripod) -> ED"],"dx":["Centor/McIsaac scores select who to TEST, not who to treat: 0-1 no test, no antibiotics; >=2 (adults) or symptomatic children -> RADT; empiric antibiotics without testing is not guideline care (IDSA)","Children/adolescents: back up a negative RADT with culture (or use NAAT which needs no backup); adults: negative RADT needs no backup (low rheumatic fever risk)","Do NOT test: children <3 yrs (rheumatic fever essentially absent, carriage confounds) unless a GAS-positive household contact; obvious viral picture at any age; asymptomatic contacts","Mono workup when suspected: heterophile (Monospot — false negatives in week 1 and young children) +/- EBV serologies, CBC for atypical lymphocytosis; examine for splenomegaly before clearing sports"],"tx":["GAS confirmed: penicillin V 500 mg BID x10 d (adults) or amoxicillin 50 mg/kg (max 1 g) once daily x10 d (kids — taste and once-daily win); no GAS resistance to penicillin exists, ever","PCN allergy: cephalexin (non-anaphylactic), or azithromycin/clindamycin (anaphylactic) — check local macrolide resistance","Symptomatic care carries the visit: NSAIDs/acetaminophen, saltwater gargles, honey (>1 yr); single-dose dexamethasone 10 mg (0.6 mg/kg) for severe odynophagia is evidence-supported","Return to school/daycare after 12-24 h on antibiotics + afebrile; no routine test-of-cure; recurrent culture-positive episodes -> consider carrier state (treat only in special situations) vs true reinfection","Amoxicillin given during undiagnosed mono classically triggers a diffuse morbilliform rash — not a true allergy, but label-generating; mono itself: relative rest, no contact sports >=3-4 wks and until spleen non-palpable","Treating GAS shortens symptoms ~1 day; the real targets are rheumatic fever prevention, suppuration, and transmission — frame antibiotic decisions honestly"],"pearls":["The score gates the swab, the swab gates the script — breaking that chain in either direction is where stewardship dies","A 'positive strep test' in a 2-year-old with runny nose is usually carriage plus a virus — that's why you don't swab them","Sore throat + fatigue + posterior nodes in a teenager: examine the belly and hold the amoxicillin","Unilateral peritonsillar bulge with trismus is a drainage procedure, not a stronger antibiotic"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis Guideline","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"CDC Group A Strep","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep"},{"t":"StatPearls: Streptococcal Pharyngitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525997/"}]},{"id":"fm-sinusitis","name":"Acute Sinusitis & Rhinitis","sec":"fm","present":["Acute rhinosinusitis: nasal congestion, purulent drainage, facial pressure/pain, +/- fever after URI; >90-98% viral — purulence alone does NOT mean bacterial","Bacterial (ABRS) pattern (AAO-HNS/IDSA): symptoms >=10 days without improvement, OR severe onset (fever >=39 + purulence >=3-4 consecutive days), OR 'double worsening' (initial improvement then deterioration)","Allergic rhinitis: sneezing, clear rhinorrhea, itchy eyes/nose, pale boggy turbinates, seasonal/perennial pattern, allergic salute/shiners; nonallergic (vasomotor) rhinitis: triggers like temperature, odors, no itch"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis — no imaging for uncomplicated acute sinusitis (sinus films useless; CT reserved for complications or surgical planning); no routine cultures","Red flags -> ED/urgent imaging (CT + ophtho/ENT): periorbital edema/proptosis, vision change or painful EOM (orbital cellulitis), severe headache with neuro signs (intracranial extension), frontal swelling (Pott puffy tumor), immunocompromised/DKA with necrotic turbinates (mucormycosis)","Chronic rhinosinusitis (>=12 wks, 2+ cardinal symptoms) requires objective evidence — anterior rhinoscopy/endoscopy or CT; ask about smell loss and consider nasal polyps/aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","Allergic rhinitis is clinical; skin testing or serum specific IgE only when the answer changes therapy (immunotherapy candidacy, avoidance targets)"],"tx":["Viral/early presumed-viral: analgesics, saline irrigation (evidence-backed), intranasal steroid, +/- short decongestant course (oxymetazoline <=3-5 d to avoid rhinitis medicamentosa); antibiotics do not help and the visit is about explaining why","ABRS by criteria: watchful waiting with safety-net prescription is guideline-endorsed for reliable follow-up; when treating — amoxicillin-clavulanate 875/125 BID (high-dose 2 g BID if resistance risk: daycare, recent antibiotics, >65, immunocompromised) x5-7 d adults; PCN allergy: doxycycline or respiratory FQ (last line); NOT macrolides/TMP-SMX (pneumococcal resistance)","Failure at 3-5 d of appropriate antibiotics -> switch class, reassess diagnosis, consider CT/ENT","Allergic rhinitis ladder: intranasal corticosteroid daily is the single most effective agent (technique: aim lateral, away from septum); add intranasal antihistamine (azelastine) for breakthrough — combo beats either; oral second-gen antihistamines (cetirizine/loratadine/fexofenadine) for itch/sneeze; montelukast only when asthma coexists (boxed neuropsychiatric warning); allergen immunotherapy (SCIT/SLIT) for refractory/perennial — the only disease-modifying option","Chronic rhinosinusitis: daily high-volume saline + intranasal steroid for months, treat comorbid allergy/asthma; polyps -> steroid +/- biologic (dupilumab) via ENT; refer for endoscopy when medical therapy fails"],"pearls":["Green snot is dead neutrophils, not a bacterial species — the 10-day/double-worsening pattern is the actual test","Rhinitis medicamentosa hides in the social history: ask specifically about Afrin-daily-for-months before calling it refractory congestion","Intranasal steroid 'failures' are usually technique or adherence failures — it takes days-to-weeks and the bottle must point AWAY from the septum","Eye findings + sinusitis = same-day imaging; orbital cellulitis does not wait for the amoxicillin-clavulanate to work"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS Adult Sinusitis Guideline","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"IDSA Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Allergic Rhinitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538186/"}]},{"id":"fm-uri","name":"URI & Acute Bronchitis (Antibiotic Stewardship)","sec":"fm","present":["Common cold: rhinorrhea, congestion, sore throat, cough, low-grade fever, peaking day 2-3, lasting 7-10 d (cough to 2-3 wks); color change of secretions mid-course is normal","Acute bronchitis: cough +/- sputum up to 3 wks in a patient WITHOUT COPD, often post-viral; wheeze/rhonchi that clear with cough; >90% viral — 'bronchitis' is not an antibiotic diagnosis","The visit is often about work notes, symptom relief, and the antibiotic expectation — name the expectation early"],"dx":["Rule out pneumonia clinically: no CXR needed if vitals normal (HR <100, RR <24, T <38) and chest exam clear; abnormal vitals, focal findings, hypoxia, or ill appearance -> CXR","Pertussis when cough is paroxysmal, post-tussive emesis, inspiratory whoop, or >2-3 wks with exposure — NAAT nasopharyngeal swab; treat and report (macrolide reduces transmission, not symptoms after paroxysmal stage)","Influenza/COVID testing when result changes management (antivirals, isolation, high-risk contacts); consider asthma or postnasal-drip/GERD pathways when 'bronchitis' keeps recurring","Duration counseling is diagnostic armor: cough median 18 days — patients expect 7-9, and that gap drives the second visit and the unnecessary Z-pak"],"tx":["No antibiotics for colds or uncomplicated acute bronchitis (ACP/CDC) — zero benefit on cure, real harms (C. diff, resistance, adverse effects ~1 in 5)","Positive communication frame: name the diagnosis ('chest cold'), give a normal timeline, provide an action plan, and offer a contingency ('if fever returns after day X or breathing worsens, we reassess') — delayed prescriptions cut antibiotic use dramatically when you must compromise","Symptomatic toolkit: NSAIDs/acetaminophen, honey (>=1 yr — beats OTC cough meds in trials), intranasal saline, intranasal ipratropium for rhinorrhea, dextromethorphan modest benefit adults; benzonatate for cough (choking hazard <10 yrs); avoid codeine; nothing OTC for cough/cold <6 yrs","Bronchodilators only if wheeze/underlying airway disease — routine albuterol for bronchitis doesn't help the cough; oral steroids have no role in uncomplicated bronchitis","Vaccinate the encounter: flu/COVID/pertussis (Tdap) status while they're in the room — the URI visit is a capture opportunity"],"pearls":["'It's turned green' is the most common reason patients believe they need antibiotics — pre-empt it before they say it","A cough at 2.5 weeks with normal vitals needs reassurance and a timeline, not azithromycin; at 3+ weeks it needs a differential (pertussis, asthma, GERD, ACEi, postnasal drip)","Antibiotic 'success stories' are natural history wearing a costume — the illness was ending anyway; say this kindly and often","The 60-second normal-vitals check is your pneumonia rule-out and your documentation shield in one"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Antibiotic Use: Common Cold & Bronchitis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/antibiotic-use"},{"t":"ACP Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute RTIs","u":"https://www.acponline.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Bronchitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448067/"}]},{"id":"fm-flu-covid","name":"Influenza & COVID-19 (Outpatient Management)","sec":"fm","present":["Both: abrupt fever, myalgias, cough, sore throat, fatigue; influenza favors abrupt onset + myalgia, COVID favors smell/taste loss and longer incubation — clinically inseparable enough that testing decides","Define the high-risk roster that changes management: >=65, pregnancy (and <2 wks postpartum), chronic lung/heart/renal/liver disease, diabetes, obesity (BMI >=30-35), immunocompromise, children <2 (flu), unvaccinated status","Screen severity at triage: SpO2 <=93-94%, RR >=24-30, dyspnea, chest pain, confusion, poor intake -> ED, not clinic"],"dx":["Test when it changes management: NAAT (or combined flu/COVID/RSV panel) for high-risk patients within the treatment window, or when result alters isolation/work/contacts; rapid antigen COVID tests need repeat at 48 h if negative with symptoms","During flu season with high prevalence, empiric influenza treatment of high-risk patients is appropriate while awaiting/without testing (CDC)","No routine labs/CXR for well-appearing outpatients; CXR when pneumonia suspected clinically","Distinguish secondary bacterial pneumonia: biphasic illness — improving flu then new fever, productive cough, focal findings (classically S. aureus post-influenza)"],"tx":["Influenza antivirals — treat ALL high-risk regardless of duration, and healthy patients if within 48 h (shared decision): oseltamivir 75 mg BID x5 d (renal-dose if CrCl <60; weight-based peds), or single-dose baloxavir >=5-12 yrs per label (avoid in pregnancy); treat, don't test-and-wait, when clock matters","COVID antivirals for high-risk within 5 d of onset: nirmatrelvir-ritonavir 300/100 BID x5 d (eGFR 30-60: 150/100; avoid <30) — run the FULL interaction check (statins hold/adjust, tacrolimus, DOACs, amiodarone, colchicine); remdesivir IV x3 d when Paxlovid contraindicated; molnupiravir last-line (avoid pregnancy); rebound after Paxlovid is usually mild — re-isolate, no second course routinely","Symptomatic care for everyone else: antipyretics, fluids, rest; no steroids for outpatients without hypoxia (harm signal in mild disease)","Isolation/return (current CDC respiratory guidance): stay home until fever-free 24 h without antipyretics AND improving, then 5 days of added precautions (mask, distance); healthcare and institutional settings retain stricter rules","Post-exposure prophylaxis: oseltamivir for high-risk close contacts (institutional outbreaks especially); vaccinate the household while you're at it","Follow-up: recheck high-risk at 24-48 h (phone ok); pulse-ox loaner or home monitoring for borderline COVID patients; flag long-COVID symptoms >4-12 wks for structured follow-up rather than dismissal"],"pearls":["The Paxlovid decision is made in the med list, not the diagnosis — open the interaction checker before the prescription pad","'It's been 3 days so antivirals won't help' is wrong for every high-risk patient — the 48-hour rule is for the healthy","Flu that improves then re-fevers has earned a chest x-ray — post-influenza staph pneumonia is the one that kills the young","Test the pregnant patient, treat the pregnant patient — oseltamivir is recommended in pregnancy, and both infections are meaner there"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Influenza Antiviral Guidance","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/flu"},{"t":"NIH/IDSA COVID-19 Treatment Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"FDA Paxlovid Prescribing Information","u":"https://www.fda.gov"}]},{"id":"fm-cap-outpt","name":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (Outpatient)","sec":"fm","present":["Cough, fever, dyspnea, pleuritic pain, +/- sputum; elderly may show only confusion, falls, or tachypnea without fever","Exam: focal crackles, egophony, dullness; tachypnea (RR >24) is the most sensitive vital sign","Distinguish from bronchitis (normal vitals + clear exam) — CAP is an abnormal-vitals or focal-findings diagnosis confirmed on imaging"],"dx":["CXR (PA/lateral) to confirm infiltrate for outpatient diagnosis (ATS/IDSA 2019); no routine sputum/blood cultures, urinary antigens, or procalcitonin for outpatients","Site-of-care decision is the crux: CURB-65 (confusion, urea >7 mmol/L, RR >=30, BP <90/<=60, age >=65) — 0-1 outpatient, 2 consider admission; PSI is the better-validated tool where calculable; then override with the eyeball test: hypoxia (SpO2 <92%), unreliable intake/follow-up, frailty","Test influenza/COVID in season — a positive changes therapy (antiviral) and infection-control, and flu+CAP still needs the antibiotic","Repeat exam/CXR thinking for failure at 48-72 h: wrong bug (resistant, TB, fungal), wrong diagnosis (PE, CHF, malignancy, OP), or complication (effusion/empyema)"],"tx":["Healthy outpatient, no comorbidities/recent antibiotics: amoxicillin 1 g TID (first-line, ATS/IDSA) OR doxycycline 100 mg BID; macrolide monotherapy ONLY where pneumococcal macrolide resistance <25% (rare in the US — mostly avoid)","Comorbidities (chronic heart/lung/liver/renal disease, DM, alcohol use, asplenia, recent antibiotics): amoxicillin-clavulanate 875/125 BID PLUS macrolide (azithromycin 500 then 250) or doxycycline; OR respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin 750) monotherapy — reserve FQs (tendon, aorta, QT, C. diff)","Duration 5 days minimum, continue until afebrile + clinically stable 48-72 h — 5 d suffices for most; NOT 10-14","Supportive: antipyretics, hydration; counsel that cough lingers weeks and fatigue up to a month; safety-net instructions: worsening dyspnea, persistent fever >72 h on antibiotics, confusion -> return/ED","Follow-up call/visit at 48-72 h for treatment response; NO routine follow-up CXR in adults who recover — exception: consider ~6-8 wk imaging for smokers >50/weight loss (unmask malignancy) per clinical judgment","Prevent the next one: pneumococcal vaccine (PCV20/21 per age/risk), influenza/COVID vaccines, smoking cessation — the discharge conversation is the prevention visit"],"pearls":["CURB-65 was built to find who goes home safely, not who is 'sick' — a score of 0 with SpO2 88% still gets admitted; the oximeter outranks the score","Azithromycin-alone for CAP is a habit, not a guideline — US pneumococcal macrolide resistance killed it","The 48-72 h check is where outpatient CAP is won: no defervescence = re-image and rethink, don't just extend the course","Post-obstructive pneumonia hides behind 'recurrent CAP in the same lobe' — that patient needs a CT and often a bronchoscopy"],"refs":[{"t":"ATS/IDSA Community-Acquired Pneumonia Guideline","u":"https://www.thoracic.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Community-Acquired Pneumonia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430749/"},{"t":"CDC Pneumococcal Vaccination","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/pneumococcal"}]},{"id":"fm-asthma","name":"Asthma (Outpatient Management & Action Plans)","sec":"fm","present":["Episodic wheeze, cough (often nocturnal), chest tightness, dyspnea; triggers: viral URI, exercise, allergens, cold air, NSAIDs/aspirin (AERD triad with polyps), occupational exposures","Assess control at every visit: daytime symptoms >2x/wk, any night waking, SABA use >2x/wk, activity limitation — any of these = not well controlled; validated ACT score tracks it","Ask the two mortality questions: prior intubation/ICU for asthma, and >=2 SABA canisters/yr (a canister a month predicts death)"],"dx":["Confirm with spirometry: obstructive pattern (FEV1/FVC below LLN) with bronchodilator reversibility (FEV1 increase >=12% AND >=200 mL); normal spirometry with strong story -> bronchoprovocation (methacholine) or PEF variability; do not anchor an inhaler lifetime on a clinical guess","FeNO supports eosinophilic/T2 airway inflammation and adherence checks; CBC eosinophils + total IgE when stepping up or considering biologics","Classify severity by the step needed to control, not the first visit's drama; reassess in 4-6 wks after any change","Mimics to exclude when 'asthma' refuses to behave: inducible laryngeal obstruction/VCD (inspiratory stridor, flat inspiratory loop), COPD/ACO in smokers, cardiac dyspnea, GERD/postnasal cough, bronchiectasis"],"tx":["The paradigm shift: NO SABA-only treatment (GINA); every asthmatic gets inhaled corticosteroid exposure — SABA alone treats the spasm and abandons the inflammation that kills","GINA track 1 (preferred): ICS-formoterol as BOTH maintenance and reliever (MART/SMART — budesonide-formoterol); mild disease uses it PRN-only (SYGMA/Novel START evidence); step up by adding maintenance doses of the same inhaler","Alternative track: daily low-dose ICS + SABA reliever -> step to ICS-LABA -> add LAMA (triple) -> biologic referral; US airsupra (albuterol-budesonide) offers an anti-inflammatory reliever where MART access fails","Every step: technique check (have them demonstrate — most errors are technique), adherence check, trigger/comorbidity sweep (rhinitis, GERD, obesity, OSA, smoking/vaping), vaccinate (flu, COVID, pneumococcal per age)","Written asthma action plan for all: green (usual meds) / yellow (early escalation: quadruple ICS or start MART reliever pattern, when to call) / red (OCS 40-50 mg x5-7 d and same-day contact / ED criteria); short OCS bursts need no taper","Refer (pulm/allergy) for: uncontrolled on medium-dose ICS-LABA, biologic candidacy (severe eosinophilic/allergic — anti-IgE, anti-IL5, dupilumab), occupational asthma, AERD, or diagnostic doubt; exercise-induced alone: ICS-formoterol or SABA 15 min pre-exercise plus warm-up"],"pearls":["Count the canisters in the refill history — the EMR knows the truth the patient softens","An 'asthma exacerbation' with stridor and a normal SpO2 that resolves instantly is the larynx, not the bronchi — spirometry loops before more steroids","Every OCS burst is a strike against current therapy: two bursts a year means step up or refer, not another prednisone script","The action plan on the fridge saves more nights than the fourth controller — write it, print it, rehearse it"],"refs":[{"t":"GINA Global Strategy for Asthma","u":"https://ginasthma.org"},{"t":"NHLBI Asthma Management Guidelines","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Asthma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430901/"}]},{"id":"fm-copd","name":"COPD (Outpatient Management & Spirometry)","sec":"fm","present":["Progressive dyspnea, chronic cough/sputum in a patient with exposure (>=10-20 pack-yrs, biomass fuel); consider alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency in early-onset (<45), basilar emphysema, or family history — AATD level once in every COPD patient (GOLD)","Quantify symptoms (mMRC dyspnea scale, CAT score) and exacerbation history (# and severity in last yr) — these, not FEV1 alone, drive drug selection","Screen the fellow travelers: cardiovascular disease (the top killer of mild-moderate COPD), lung cancer (LDCT eligibility), osteoporosis, depression/anxiety, sarcopenia"],"dx":["Diagnosis REQUIRES spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7 (GOLD); severity by FEV1 %pred (GOLD 1 >=80, 2 50-79, 3 30-49, 4 <30); no spirometry = no diagnosis — half of clinical 'COPD' isn't","GOLD ABE groups: A (low symptoms, 0-1 moderate exacerbations), B (high symptoms, low risk), E (>=2 moderate or >=1 hospitalized exacerbation regardless of symptoms)","Blood eosinophils guide ICS: >=300 supports ICS benefit, <100 argues against; check during stability","CXR at diagnosis (rule-outs), not for routine follow-up; consider echo/BNP when dyspnea outpaces obstruction (HFpEF loves to hide in COPD)"],"tx":["The only mortality-movers: smoking cessation (pharmacotherapy + counseling at every visit), long-term oxygen for resting SpO2 <=88% (or <=89% with cor pulmonale/polycythemia — NOTT), pulmonary rehab after exacerbations, and appropriate vaccination (flu, COVID, pneumococcal, RSV >=50-60 per risk, Tdap)","Initial inhaled therapy (GOLD): group A — a bronchodilator; group B — LABA+LAMA combination; group E — LABA+LAMA, add ICS upfront if eos >=300 (triple therapy)","Escalate to triple (LABA+LAMA+ICS) for exacerbations on dual therapy with eos >=100 (IMPACT/ETHOS: fewer exacerbations, mortality signal); withdraw ICS if pneumonia, no exacerbations, and eos <100; never ICS monotherapy in COPD","Refractory adds: roflumilast (chronic bronchitis + severe obstruction + exacerbations), azithromycin MWF or daily in ex-smokers with frequent exacerbations (check QT, hearing), theophylline last resort","Exacerbation home management: increase SABA, prednisone 40 mg x5 d (REDUCE), antibiotics 5-7 d when increased purulence + volume/dyspnea (Anthonisen) — amox-clav or doxycycline/azithro per local patterns; hypoxia, accessory muscles, confusion, or failure -> ED","Refer: pulmonology for GOLD 3-4, frequent exacerbations, AATD, or valve/surgery evaluation (lung volume reduction, transplant); palliative-care conversation belongs in clinic, not the last ICU admission"],"pearls":["Treat the spirometer, not the wheeze: an inhaler cabinet without a confirmed FEV1/FVC ratio is where misdiagnosed HFpEF and obesity live","The eosinophil count is the ICS gatekeeper — and the pneumonia history is the ICS eviction notice","Pulmonary rehab is the most underprescribed therapy in the disease: it beats most drug escalations on symptoms and readmission","Two exacerbations in a year is a strategy failure, not bad luck — restage, re-drug, re-refer"],"refs":[{"t":"GOLD COPD Report","u":"https://goldcopd.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: COPD","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559281/"},{"t":"CDC COPD Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/copd"}]},{"id":"fm-gerd","name":"GERD & Dyspepsia","sec":"fm","present":["GERD: retrosternal burning, regurgitation, worse supine/post-prandial; extraesophageal masks: chronic cough, hoarseness, 'asthma', dental erosions — but attribute cautiously (poor PPI response for isolated ENT symptoms)","Dyspepsia: epigastric pain/burning, early satiety, post-prandial fullness (Rome IV) — overlapping but distinct pathway from GERD","ALARM features demand endoscopy, not empiric acid suppression: dysphagia, odynophagia, GI bleeding/melena, iron deficiency anemia, unintentional weight loss, persistent vomiting, family hx upper GI cancer, new symptoms >=60 (ACG age threshold for dyspepsia)"],"dx":["Typical GERD without alarms: presumptive diagnosis + 4-8 wk PPI trial IS the test; response supports it, but non-response means workup, not dose-doubling forever","Uninvestigated dyspepsia <60 without alarms: test-and-treat H. pylori (urea breath test or stool antigen — hold PPI 2 wks, antibiotics/bismuth 4 wks prior); positive -> eradicate; negative or persistent -> PPI trial; still refractory -> EGD","EGD indications beyond alarms: PPI-refractory GERD, screening for Barrett esophagus in chronic GERD + 3 risk factors (male, >50, white, central obesity, smoking, family hx); biopsy to confirm eradication pathology as needed","Refractory pathway: confirm adherence/timing first (30-60 min before breakfast), then ambulatory pH/impedance OFF therapy to separate true acid disease from functional heartburn; manometry before any surgical referral (rule out achalasia masquerading)"],"tx":["Lifestyle with actual evidence: weight loss (dose-response), head-of-bed elevation 6-8 in, avoid meals within 3 h of bed; trigger-food elimination only for triggers the patient actually notices","PPI once daily before breakfast x8 wks (omeprazole 20-40, pantoprazole 40); partial response -> optimize timing, consider BID before doubling down; then STEP DOWN — lowest effective dose, PRN, or switch to H2RA; famotidine for breakthrough nocturnal symptoms","Deprescribe deliberately: taper (not stop) PPIs to dodge rebound acid hypersecretion; long-term PPI only with an indication (Barrett, severe erosive LA C/D, bleeding prophylaxis in high-risk NSAID/antiplatelet users) — discuss the modest association-level risks (fractures, B12, Mg, C. diff) without theatrics","H. pylori eradication first-line: bismuth quadruple (PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole x14 d) or rifabutin triple/vonoprazan regimens where available — clarithromycin triple only with proven susceptibility; ALWAYS test-of-cure >=4 wks post-treatment (UBT/stool antigen)","Functional dyspepsia (most dyspepsia after negative workup): PPI trial, then TCA (amitriptyline 10-25 qhs), then prokinetic; explain the brain-gut mechanism — it legitimizes the diagnosis and the antidepressant","Surgical/endoscopic anti-reflux referral only for objectively confirmed GERD (pH-positive) with good PPI response or regurgitation-predominant disease"],"pearls":["The PPI taken at bedtime with no food is pharmacologically half-asleep — timing fixes half of 'refractory GERD'","Dysphagia is never GERD until the endoscope says so — eosinophilic esophagitis and cancer both start as 'heartburn with food sticking'","Test-of-cure for H. pylori is the most skipped step in primary care — an untested 'eradication' is a coin flip","Chronic cough + normal EGD + no heartburn rarely gets cured by a PPI — resist the indefinite empiric script"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG Clinical Guideline: GERD","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"ACG Guideline: Treatment of H. pylori","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: GERD","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441938/"}]},{"id":"fm-ibs","name":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","sec":"fm","present":["Rome IV: recurrent abdominal pain >=1 d/wk for 3 mo, associated with >=2 of — related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form; subtype by Bristol scale on abnormal-stool days: IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M","Long, fluctuating history often triggered by infection (post-infectious IBS), stress, or food; overlap with anxiety/depression, fibromyalgia, functional dyspepsia is the rule","Alarm features that break the pattern: onset >=50, GI bleeding, nocturnal diarrhea waking from sleep, unintentional weight loss, progressive symptoms, family hx of CRC/IBD/celiac, iron deficiency"],"dx":["Make a POSITIVE diagnosis (ACG): limited targeted testing, not exclusion-by-colonoscopy — CBC, CRP (or fecal calprotectin for IBS-D to exclude IBD), celiac serology (tTG-IgA + total IgA) for D/M subtypes; age-appropriate CRC screening as scheduled","IBS-D additions when suspicion: Giardia antigen (exposure), consider bile acid diarrhea (empiric sequestrant trial is pragmatic); NO routine food-allergy panels or breath testing","Colonoscopy ONLY for alarm features, abnormal initial tests, or due screening — a normal calprotectin + negative celiac in a classic young patient ends the workup","Deliver the diagnosis with confidence and a mechanism (disorder of gut-brain interaction, visceral hypersensitivity) — diagnostic confidence is itself therapeutic and stops the scope-shopping cycle"],"tx":["Universal base: education + reassurance, soluble fiber (psyllium — insoluble bran often worsens), regular meals/sleep/exercise; trial low-FODMAP diet 4-6 wks WITH structured reintroduction (ideally dietitian-guided — indefinite restriction breeds disordered eating and micronutrient gaps)","IBS-D: loperamide for stool frequency (not pain); rifaximin 550 TID x14 d (TARGET trials, retreatable); eluxadoline (avoid: no gallbladder, alcohol >3/d, pancreatitis hx); alosetron reserved for severe refractory women (ischemic colitis risk); bile acid sequestrant when that flavor fits","IBS-C: PEG for stools (not pain); step to secretagogues — linaclotide or lubiprostone or plecanatide (pain + stool benefit); tenapanor as newer option","Pain across subtypes: antispasmodics PRN (dicyclomine, hyoscyamine, peppermint oil — enteric-coated); central neuromodulator for moderate-severe: TCA first (amitriptyline/nortriptyline 10-25 mg qhs, titrate — ATLANTIS trial supports primary-care TCA use); SSRIs help comorbid anxiety more than IBS itself","Brain-gut therapies with real effect sizes: CBT for IBS and gut-directed hypnotherapy (app-based versions count) — offer them as legitimate treatment, not as 'it's in your head'","Follow up in 4-6 wks initially: symptom diaries, expectation-setting (management, not cure), and one named clinician — continuity is the anti-doctor-shopping drug"],"pearls":["Nocturnal diarrhea that wakes the patient is organic until proven otherwise — true IBS sleeps through the night","Calprotectin is the 30-dollar test that saves the 3000-dollar colonoscopy in the 28-year-old with diarrhea","Prescribe the TCA with a script for the conversation too: 'nerve-pain-modulator for the gut at a fraction of antidepressant doses' — or the bottle hits the trash after one Google","The low-FODMAP diet without reintroduction is a nutrition problem you created — phase 2 and 3 are the actual diet"],"refs":[{"t":"ACG Clinical Guideline: IBS","u":"https://gi.org"},{"t":"AGA IBS Pharmacologic Management","u":"https://gastro.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Irritable Bowel Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534810/"}]},{"id":"fm-constipation","name":"Constipation & Hemorrhoids (Clinic Management)","sec":"fm","present":["Constipation (Rome IV functional): <3 SBMs/wk plus straining, lumpy/hard stools (Bristol 1-2), incomplete evacuation, blockage sensation, or manual maneuvers in >25% of defecations","Sort primary (normal-transit most common, slow-transit, defecatory/pelvic-floor dysfunction) from secondary: meds (opioids, anticholinergics, CCBs, iron, ondansetron), hypothyroidism, hypercalcemia, DM, Parkinson, mechanical obstruction","Hemorrhoids: internal (painless bright-red bleeding with defecation, +/- prolapse — grade I-IV) vs external (visible, hurt when thrombosed: acute exquisitely tender blue perianal nodule)"],"dx":["Constipation alarms -> colonoscopy: blood mixed in stool/iron deficiency, weight loss, onset >=50 without prior screening, family hx CRC, acute change in caliber; otherwise labs limited (TSH, calcium, glucose) and no routine imaging","Refractory constipation: DRE is the most skipped test — assess tone, paradoxical puborectalis contraction on push, impaction, mass; suspected defecatory dysfunction -> anorectal manometry + balloon expulsion (biofeedback fixes what laxatives can't)","Hemorrhoid diagnosis needs eyes and a finger: external inspection, DRE, anoscopy for internal grading; NEVER attribute rectal bleeding to hemorrhoids without at least anoscopy — and colonoscopy for anyone with alarms, age-appropriate screening due, or bleeding without a convincing anal source","Anal fissure mimics thrombosed hemorrhoid pain: tearing pain WITH defecation + posterior midline tear on gentle traction — different disease, different treatment"],"tx":["Constipation ladder: fiber to 25-30 g/d (psyllium best evidence) + fluids + toilet training (post-meal timing, footstool to unkink the anorectal angle); then PEG 17 g daily (best-evidence osmotic, titratable) > lactulose (bloating); stimulants (senna, bisacodyl) are safe for regular use — the 'colon damage' myth is dead","Refractory: secretagogues (linaclotide, lubiprostone, plecanatide); opioid-induced constipation -> scheduled osmotic + stimulant from opioid day one, escalate to PAMORA (naloxegol, methylnaltrexone) — fiber alone worsens OIC","Impaction first when present: disimpaction (manual/enemas from below) before maintenance from above","Internal hemorrhoids: fiber + water + no prolonged straining/phone-on-toilet fixes most grade I-II; topical short courses only (steroid <=1 wk); office rubber band ligation for persistent grade I-III bleeding/prolapse (most effective office procedure); surgery for grade IV/refractory III","Thrombosed external hemorrhoid: excision (not incision) within 72 h of onset relieves fastest; after 72 h — sitz baths, analgesia, stool softeners, time (resolves 1-2 wks)","Anal fissure: sitz baths + fiber + topical nitroglycerin 0.4% or diltiazem 2% BID x6-8 wks (headache warning with NTG); chronic/refractory -> botulinum toxin or lateral internal sphincterotomy"],"pearls":["The med list causes more constipation than the colon does — recon the opioids, iron, and verapamil before adding a third laxative","A patient 'straining for years' who fails every laxative has pelvic floor dyssynergia until manometry says otherwise — biofeedback is the cure laxatives pretend to be","'It's just hemorrhoids' is how right-sided colon cancers buy 12 months — anoscopy plus screening status check, every time","For thrombosed external hemorrhoids the clock matters: day 2 is a procedure, day 6 is sympathy and sitz baths"],"refs":[{"t":"AGA/ACG Chronic Idiopathic Constipation Guideline","u":"https://gastro.org"},{"t":"ASCRS Hemorrhoids Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://fascrs.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Constipation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513291/"}]},{"id":"fm-low-back","name":"Low Back Pain (Primary Care Approach)","sec":"fm","present":["Acute (<4 wks), subacute (4-12), chronic (>12); >85% is nonspecific mechanical LBP — the visit's job is triage into nonspecific vs radicular vs specific pathology","Radicular pattern: pain below the knee in a dermatome, positive straight-leg raise (crossed SLR more specific), +/- focal weakness/reflex change — L5 (dorsiflexion, EHL) and S1 (plantarflexion, ankle jerk) dominate","Red flags in clusters, not isolation: cauda equina (urinary retention with overflow, saddle anesthesia, bilateral leg symptoms — same-day MRI/ED), infection (fever, IVDU, recent instrumentation, immunosuppression), fracture (osteoporosis, steroids, significant trauma, age >65-75), malignancy (cancer history is the strongest; weight loss, night pain), inflammatory (age <45, insidious onset >3 mo, morning stiffness >30-60 min, better with exercise -> axSpA pathway: HLA-B27, CRP, SI imaging)"],"dx":["NO imaging for nonspecific LBP <6 wks without red flags (ACP/Choosing Wisely) — early MRI finds incidental disc findings in half of asymptomatic adults, anchors patients to anatomy, and worsens outcomes","Image when it changes management: immediate MRI for cauda equina/infection/cancer suspicion or progressive neuro deficit; radiographs +/- labs (ESR/CRP) for fracture or inflammatory suspicion; radiculopathy gets MRI only when epidural injection or surgery is actually on the table (i.e., after 4-6 wks of failed conservative care)","Examine functionally: gait, heel/toe walk, SLR, focal neuro; map deficits to a single root — multi-level or bilateral findings escalate","Screen prognosis, not just pathology: STarT Back or the psychosocial 'yellow flags' (fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, work dissatisfaction, mood) — they predict chronicity better than the MRI does"],"tx":["Acute nonspecific: stay ACTIVE (bed rest harms), reassurance with expected recovery (most improve substantially in 2-6 wks), superficial heat, NSAIDs scheduled short-course +/- non-benzodiazepine muscle relaxant a few days; acetaminophen alone underperforms; NO opioids, NO systemic steroids for nonspecific LBP","Radiculopathy: same activity advice + NSAIDs; most herniations improve by 6-12 wks without surgery; epidural steroid injection for severe refractory radicular pain (short-term relief only); surgical referral for progressive/severe motor deficit, cauda equina (emergent), or persistent disabling radicular pain >6 wks with concordant MRI (surgery speeds relief; 1-2 yr outcomes converge — SPORT)","Chronic LBP: exercise therapy is the backbone (any kind the patient will do — core, yoga, Pilates, walking), multidisciplinary rehab, CBT/pain neuroscience education, mindfulness; adjuncts: duloxetine (evidence-backed), NSAIDs; tramadol/opioids only as a rare, contracted last resort; spinal manipulation and acupuncture are reasonable adjuncts (ACP)","Return-to-work early with modified duties — prolonged work absence is itself a risk factor for never returning","Spinal stenosis phenotype (older, neurogenic claudication better leaning forward/shopping cart sign): structured exercise + activity modification first; decompression for progressive or lifestyle-limiting disease","Set the language: 'your back is strong, this is a sprain-equivalent, motion is the medicine' — nocebo phrases ('degeneration', 'bone on bone', 'slipped disc') measurably worsen disability"],"pearls":["Ask about urinary retention out loud — patients don't volunteer it, and cauda equina missed on Friday becomes a lawsuit and a wheelchair by Monday","The MRI report is the most iatrogenic document in back pain: if you didn't need it to act, you didn't need it at all","Night pain relieved by ibuprofen in a stiff 28-year-old is axSpA, not 'chronic strain' — a decade of delayed diagnosis is the specialty's shame","Fear-avoidance beliefs at visit one predict disability at year one — treat the fear as seriously as the pain"],"refs":[{"t":"ACP Noninvasive Treatments for Low Back Pain","u":"https://www.acponline.org"},{"t":"NASS / Choosing Wisely Imaging Guidance","u":"https://www.choosingwisely.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Low Back Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538173/"}]},{"id":"fm-knee-pain","name":"Knee Pain (OA, Meniscal, Patellofemoral)","sec":"fm","present":["Sort by age + mechanism + location: OA (older, insidious, medial > lateral, worse with use, stiffness <30 min), meniscal (twist injury or degenerative in >40s, joint-line pain, catching/locking, delayed effusion), patellofemoral pain (young/active, anterior 'theater sign' — worse with stairs, squatting, prolonged sitting)","Acute traumatic set: ACL (pivot + pop + immediate effusion), MCL/LCL (valgus/varus stress), patellar dislocation (medial tenderness, apprehension), fracture","Referred pain traps: hip OA presenting as knee pain (examine the hip in every knee visit, especially children — SCFE) and lumbar radiculopathy"],"dx":["Ottawa knee rules for radiographs after trauma: age >=55, isolated patellar tenderness, fibular head tenderness, cannot flex 90 degrees, cannot bear weight 4 steps","OA is clinical: age >=45 + activity-related pain + no/short morning stiffness (ACR/NICE) — radiographs (weight-bearing) confirm when needed but severity correlates poorly with symptoms; NO MRI for obvious OA","Exam battery: effusion, ROM, joint-line tenderness + McMurray (meniscus), Lachman (best ACL test), valgus/varus stress, patellar apprehension/grind; aspirate a hot monoarthritis — crystal vs septic arthritis is a lab call, not an eyeball call","MRI only when arthroscopy or reconstruction is genuinely in play: suspected ACL tear, locked knee, young-patient acute meniscal tear, or refractory undiagnosed pain — degenerative meniscal tears in >40s are near-universal MRI findings and mostly surgical red herrings"],"tx":["Knee OA core (AAOS/OARSI/ACR): exercise + strengthening (quad-focused) is the disease-modifying 'drug', weight loss >=5-10% (dose-dependent relief), land + aquatic PT, cane in the opposite hand, unloader brace for unicompartmental disease","OA meds: topical NSAIDs FIRST-LINE (especially >=75 or GI/CV risk); oral NSAIDs lowest-dose intermittent with GI/renal/CV screening +/- PPI; duloxetine for chronic widespread-pain phenotype; acetaminophen modest at best; AVOID opioids and routine glucosamine (ACR strong against); IA corticosteroid for flares (short-lived, weeks-months); IA hyaluronate weak/controversial","TKA referral when pain + function fail comprehensive nonoperative care — radiographic severity alone is not the ticket; optimize BMI, glycemia, smoking status pre-referral","Degenerative meniscal tear WITHOUT locking: treat as OA-spectrum — PT first (METEOR, ESCAPE: arthroscopic partial meniscectomy no better than PT for most); true mechanical locking or young traumatic tear -> ortho (repair preserves the meniscus that prevents future OA)","Patellofemoral pain: education + load management (relative rest from pain-provoking loads, not total rest), hip abductor/external rotator + quad strengthening (hip-and-knee programs beat knee-only), short-term taping/foot orthoses as adjuncts, NSAIDs briefly; imaging normal and unnecessary in classic cases; recovery in months — say so upfront","Acute ligament sprains: PEACE-and-LOVE style early management, early protected motion; isolated MCL heals nonoperatively; ACL tears -> shared decision (reconstruction for instability/cutting sports vs structured rehab)"],"pearls":["The degenerative meniscal tear on MRI is the appendix scar of the aging knee — present in half of asymptomatic 50-somethings; treat the patient, not the radiologist","Quad strength is the best knee drug nobody refills — write PT like you mean it and follow up on attendance","A child with 'knee pain' gets a hip exam and often a hip x-ray — missed SCFE is a career-defining regret","Anterior knee pain in a runner that flared when mileage doubled is a training-load diagnosis — fix the ramp, not just the kneecap"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Knee Osteoarthritis Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"ACR Osteoarthritis Management Guideline","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557657/"}]},{"id":"fm-shoulder","name":"Shoulder Pain (Rotator Cuff, Frozen Shoulder)","sec":"fm","present":["Rotator cuff spectrum (subacromial pain): lateral deltoid ache, painful arc 60-120 degrees, worse overhead and lying on that side; weakness suggests tear — acute traumatic weakness vs gradual degenerative","Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder): insidious global stiffness + pain, hallmark loss of PASSIVE ROM (external rotation first/most); phases — painful freezing (2-9 mo), frozen (4-12), thawing (12-24+); check the A1c: diabetes and thyroid disease are the classic associations","Other bins: glenohumeral OA (crepitus, global loss, older), AC joint (point tenderness on top, cross-body adduction pain), instability/labrum (young, dislocators, apprehension), calcific tendinitis (abrupt severe atraumatic pain), biceps tendinopathy (anterior); referred: neck (radicular pattern, Spurling), and the can't-miss extrinsics — cardiac ischemia (left shoulder + exertion) and diaphragmatic irritation"],"dx":["The single most discriminating move: passive vs active ROM — passive preserved + active painful/weak = cuff; passive AND active restricted = frozen shoulder or glenohumeral OA (x-ray splits them: OA has findings, frozen shoulder is radiographically normal by definition)","Cuff exam: painful arc, Jobe/empty-can (supraspinatus), resisted external rotation (infraspinatus), lift-off/belly-press (subscapularis), Hawkins/Neer (impingement, sensitive not specific); drop-arm suggests large tear","Imaging: x-ray first when structural disease suspected (AP/axillary — the axillary view is what catches the missed posterior dislocation); ultrasound ~ MRI for cuff tears — order advanced imaging when surgery is actually contemplated (acute traumatic full-thickness tear, young patient, failed 3 mo conservative), not for garden-variety subacromial pain","Red flags: post-seizure/electrocution posterior dislocation, unexplained mass, fever + hot joint (aspirate), progressive neuro deficit, rest/night pain with cancer history"],"tx":["Subacromial pain/cuff tendinopathy: relative rest from overhead aggravators (not immobilization — that breeds stiffness), progressive PT (cuff + scapular stabilizer loading — the evidence-backed core, 12-wk horizon), NSAIDs short course, one subacromial steroid injection as adjunct for pain blocking PT participation (short-term benefit; avoid repeated injections — tendon-toxic)","Cuff tears: degenerative/atraumatic partial or full-thickness in older adults -> PT first (most do well; surgery if failing 3-6 mo or functionally demanding); acute traumatic full-thickness in younger/active patients -> early surgical referral (repair within weeks preserves outcome)","Frozen shoulder: educate on the self-limited-but-long arc — cornerstone is intra-articular (glenohumeral, not subacromial) steroid injection EARLY in the painful phase (best evidence window) + gentle stretching PT within pain tolerance; aggressive PT in the freezing phase inflames; refractory >6-12 mo -> hydrodilatation, MUA, or arthroscopic capsular release; optimize the diabetes it rode in on","Glenohumeral OA: activity modification, analgesia, IA steroid sparingly, arthroplasty referral when function fails; AC joint: modification + targeted injection; calcific tendinitis: NSAIDs, PT, ultrasound-guided barbotage works well","Across all: keep the shoulder MOVING within tolerance — the shoulder's default failure mode is stiffness; slings are for fractures and fresh dislocations, briefly"],"pearls":["Passive ROM is the exam — the hand that moves their arm for them separates frozen shoulder from cuff disease in ten seconds","New frozen shoulder is a metabolic prompt: check the A1c and TSH, and warn diabetics it runs longer and meaner","An MRI 'cuff tear' in a 65-year-old is often wallpaper — asymptomatic tears live in a quarter of that age group; correlate before you refer","Bilateral shoulder girdle aching + morning stiffness in a >50-year-old with a screaming ESR is PMR — prednisone 15 mg is both test and treatment"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Rotator Cuff Injury Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Shoulder Pain Evaluation","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adhesive Capsulitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532955/"}]},{"id":"fm-oa","name":"Osteoarthritis Management","sec":"fm","present":["Activity-related joint pain with brief (<30 min) morning stiffness and gelling after inactivity; knees, hips, hands (DIP/PIP/first CMC), and spine dominate; bony enlargement (Heberden/Bouchard nodes), crepitus, reduced ROM","Inflammatory imposters to exclude by pattern: RA (MCP/wrist, prolonged stiffness, soft-tissue swelling), gout/CPPD (episodic hot joints — CPPD loves the OA knee), psoriatic (DIP + nails + psoriasis), hemochromatosis arthropathy (2nd-3rd MCP hooks in a younger patient)","Frame at diagnosis: whole-joint disease driven by load, muscle weakness, obesity, prior injury, and genetics — 'wear and repair', not 'bone on bone' fatalism"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis in typical presentations (age >=45, usage-related pain, stiffness <30 min — NICE): no labs, no imaging required to start treatment","When tests enter: x-rays (weight-bearing for knees) for surgical planning or diagnostic doubt; labs only to exclude mimics (CRP/ESR normal-ish in OA, RF/CCP if RA pattern, ferritin/transferrin sat for the young MCP arthropathy); aspirate any effusion that's warm or sudden — crystals and infection change everything","Radiographic-symptom discordance is the rule: severe films with mild pain and vice versa — treat function and pain, never the Kellgren-Lawrence grade","Track function with something repeatable (WOMAC-style questions, walk tolerance, stairs) so 'better or worse' is data, not vibes"],"tx":["Non-drug core for EVERY patient (ACR/OARSI strong): therapeutic exercise (strengthening + aerobic; land or aquatic — adherence beats modality), weight management (>=5% loss meaningful, >=10% better), self-efficacy/self-management programs, walking aids and braces as indicated (cane opposite hand, tibiofemoral unloader), first-CMC orthosis for thumb OA","Topical NSAIDs first-line for knee/hand OA (efficacy near oral, systemic risk near zero); oral NSAIDs next — lowest effective dose, intermittent, with GI (PPI/celecoxib choice), renal, CV risk triage; duloxetine 30-60 mg for chronic pain phenotype/multisite pain; acetaminophen minor benefit — adjunct at best","Injections: IA corticosteroid for flares (weeks-to-months relief; reasonable a few times/yr per joint); hyaluronic acid weakly supported (ACR conditionally against for knee, against for hip); PRP/stem cells — insufficient evidence, say so plainly","Do NOT: opioids (poor efficacy, real harm — ACR strong against tramadol-first thinking too), glucosamine/chondroitin (ACR strong against), arthroscopic 'clean-out' for OA knees (sham-equivalent), routine MRI","Hand OA specifics: education + hand therapy exercises + CMC orthosis, topical NSAIDs; erosive interphalangeal flares may warrant short oral NSAID courses","Arthroplasty referral: pain + functional limitation refractory to comprehensive conservative care — outcomes excellent for hip/knee; pre-hab, weight, A1c <8, smoking cessation improve results; young patients get the longevity-of-implant conversation, not automatic deferral"],"pearls":["Exercise IS the treatment, not the thing patients do alongside treatment — prescribe it with doses and follow-up like any drug","An OA knee that suddenly turns hot overnight grew crystals or bacteria — tap it before you blame a 'flare'","The phrase 'bone on bone' costs more function than the cartilage loss does — swap in 'the joint is sensitized and the muscles are the shock absorbers we can rebuild'","When every joint 'has OA' and everything hurts everywhere, reconsider: fibromyalgia, hypothyroidism, and depression routinely wear an OA costume"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR/Arthritis Foundation OA Guideline","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"OARSI Non-Surgical Management of Knee OA","u":"https://oarsi.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoarthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482326/"}]},{"id":"fm-osteoporosis","name":"Osteoporosis Screening & Treatment","sec":"fm","present":["Silent until fracture: screen women >=65 (USPSTF), postmenopausal <65 with elevated risk (FRAX-guided per 2025 update); men — no USPSTF recommendation, but screen with fragility fracture, long-term steroids, ADT, or high-risk conditions","Fragility fracture (fall from standing height: hip, vertebral, wrist, humerus) = clinical osteoporosis regardless of DXA — no further proof needed to treat","Hunt secondary causes and drug drivers: steroids >=2.5-5 mg prednisone >=3 mo (start protection early), aromatase inhibitors, ADT, PPIs, SSRIs, anticonvulsants, excess thyroid hormone, celiac, hyperparathyroidism, CKD, alcohol"],"dx":["DXA hip + spine: T-score <=-2.5 osteoporosis, -1 to -2.5 osteopenia (then run FRAX: treat if 10-yr hip fracture >=3% or major osteoporotic >=20%)","Vertebral fracture assessment/lateral imaging when height loss >=1.5 in, kyphosis, or steroid use — silent vertebral fractures reclassify to severe","Baseline labs before treating: CBC, CMP (Ca, Cr, LFTs), 25-OH vitamin D, +/- PTH, TSH, celiac serology, testosterone (men), SPEP/free light chains if fracture + anemia or suspicion; correct vitamin D BEFORE antiresorptives (hypocalcemia risk)","Repeat DXA q2y on treatment (same machine); stable/improved BMD = success — the goal is no fractures, not normalized T-scores"],"tx":["Universal base: calcium 1000-1200 mg/d (diet first, supplement the gap), vitamin D 800-1000 IU/d, weight-bearing + resistance exercise, fall-proof the home, taper the deprescribable culprits, no smoking, limit alcohol","First-line: oral bisphosphonate — alendronate 70 mg wkly or risedronate 35 mg wkly (empty stomach, full glass water, upright 30 min); zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly for GI intolerance/adherence/post-hip-fracture (HORIZON: mortality benefit); avoid oral if esophageal disease, CrCl <30-35","Denosumab 60 mg SC q6mo when bisphosphonates fail/contraindicated or advanced CKD (watch Ca) — but it is a commitment: missed doses trigger rebound vertebral fractures; never stop without transitioning to a bisphosphonate","Very-high-risk (T <=-3.0, recent/multiple fractures, fracture on therapy): anabolic FIRST then antiresorptive — teriparatide/abaloparatide SC daily x2 yr, or romosozumab monthly x1 yr (avoid with MI/stroke history); anabolic-then-antiresorptive beats the reverse sequence","Drug holiday after 5 yr oral / 3 yr IV bisphosphonate if risk no longer high (fracture-free, T >-2.5); reassess q2-3y; rare harms in perspective: ONJ and atypical femoral fracture are orders of magnitude rarer than the hip fractures prevented — new thigh/groin ache on long-term therapy gets femur imaging","Glucocorticoid-induced: treat at osteopenic thresholds per ACR risk stratification; don't wait for -2.5"],"pearls":["The wrist fracture at 62 is the hip fracture at 78 sending a warning letter — treat the first fragility fracture like the sentinel event it is","Denosumab has no exit without a bridge — if the patient can't guarantee q6mo forever, pick a different drug","Height loss >1.5 inches is a free vertebral fracture screen hiding in the vitals","Fix the vitamin D before the zoledronic acid, or the first infusion buys you a hypocalcemia phone call"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Osteoporosis Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"Bone Health & Osteoporosis Foundation Clinician Guide","u":"https://www.bonehealthandosteoporosis.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoporosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441901/"}]},{"id":"fm-gout","name":"Gout (Outpatient Prevention & ULT Titration)","sec":"fm","present":["Acute flare: abrupt (often overnight) monoarthritis — red, hot, exquisite; podagra (first MTP) classic; also midfoot, ankle, knee; later polyarticular and tophaceous","Triggers: alcohol (beer/spirits), purine loads, fructose, dehydration, diuretics, hospitalization/surgery, and STARTING urate-lowering therapy without prophylaxis","Between flares: quantify burden (flares/yr, tophi, erosions) and comorbidity cluster — HTN, CKD, obesity, metabolic syndrome travel with it"],"dx":["Gold standard: arthrocentesis — negatively birefringent needle-shaped MSU crystals; ALWAYS tap when septic arthritis is plausible (fever, prosthetic joint, ill) — gout and infection coexist and a crystal-positive tap does not exclude infection (send culture)","Clinical diagnosis acceptable for classic recurrent podagra with hyperuricemia; ultrasound double-contour sign or DECT urate when the tap is impractical","Serum urate can be NORMAL or low during a flare — recheck 2+ wks after; asymptomatic hyperuricemia alone is not a treatment indication (ACR)","Rule the mimics: septic joint, CPPD/pseudogout (rhomboid weakly-positive crystals, chondrocalcinosis, older knees/wrists), cellulitis, trauma"],"tx":["Flare (treat within 24 h, any of three): NSAID full-dose (naproxen 500 BID) x5-7 d; colchicine 1.2 mg then 0.6 mg 1 h later, then 0.6 daily-BID (only if within ~36 h of onset; dose-adjust CKD, drug interactions — clarithromycin, cyclosporine); prednisone 30-40 mg x5 d (the CKD/anticoagulated-friendly option); intra-articular steroid for one accessible joint","ULT indications (ACR strong): >=2 flares/yr, tophi, or radiographic damage; conditional: first flare with CKD >=3, urate >9, or urolithiasis","Allopurinol first-line INCLUDING CKD: start 100 mg/d (50 mg if CKD >=3), titrate q2-5 wks by urate — treat-to-target <6 mg/dL (<5 with tophi); doses often 300-600+ mg — the 300 mg plateau is the classic undertreatment; HLA-B*58:01 testing before starting in Southeast Asian and African American patients (SCAR risk)","Anti-inflammatory prophylaxis DURING titration 3-6 mo (colchicine 0.6 daily, low-dose NSAID, or low-dose prednisone) — starting ULT without it manufactures flares and destroys adherence; do NOT stop ULT during a flare","Febuxostat second-line (CV mortality signal in CARES — caution with ASCVD); probenecid rarely (needs CrCl, uric acid overexcreters excluded); pegloticase via rheum for refractory tophaceous disease","Lifestyle as adjunct not substitute: less alcohol/fructose/organ meat, weight loss, hydration; swap hydrochlorothiazide toward losartan (mild uricosuric) when BP allows"],"pearls":["The flare during ULT titration means the drug is WORKING (urate shifting) — pre-brief the patient or they'll quit the allopurinol that was about to cure them","Allopurinol 300 mg is a waystation, not a destination — titrate to the urate target like you titrate metformin to the A1c","A hot first MTP is gout; a hot knee with a fever is a needle — the tap you skip is the prosthetic-joint infection you miss","Urate <6 maintained for years shrinks tophi and ends flares — gout is the most curable arthritis we routinely undertreat"],"refs":[{"t":"ACR Guideline for the Management of Gout","u":"https://rheumatology.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Gout","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546606/"},{"t":"FDA Febuxostat Safety Communication","u":"https://www.fda.gov"}]},{"id":"fm-headache","name":"Headache in Clinic (Migraine Prophylaxis, Medication Overuse)","sec":"fm","present":["Classify primary first: migraine (POUND: pulsatile, one-day duration 4-72 h, unilateral, nausea, disabling — 4/5 highly predictive; +/- aura), tension-type (bilateral, pressing, non-disabling), cluster (strictly unilateral orbital, 15-180 min, autonomic features, restlessness — refer)","SNNOOP red flags: systemic signs/fever, neoplasm history, neuro deficit, onset thunderclap (peak <1 min -> ED/SAH pathway), older onset >50 (temporal arteritis — jaw claudication, ESR/CRP), pattern change, positional, precipitated by Valsalva/exertion, pregnancy/postpartum, papilledema, immunocompromise","Take a disability + medication-day census: headache days/mo, acute-med days/mo, caffeine, and functional impact (MIDAS-style) — this drives every decision"],"dx":["No neuroimaging for stable, recurrent migraine with normal neuro exam (Choosing Wisely); MRI (not CT) when red flags, atypical aura, or exam abnormalities","Medication-overuse headache: headache >=15 d/mo + acute meds >=10 d/mo (triptans, opioids, combos, butalbital) or >=15 d/mo (simple analgesics) — the chronic-daily-headache engine hiding in the refill history","Chronic migraine: >=15 headache d/mo x3 mo with >=8 migraine days; screen the amplifiers: sleep apnea, depression/anxiety, cervicogenic contribution, caffeine","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension check in the young obese patient with daily headache: fundoscopy or refer for papilledema before calling it chronic migraine"],"tx":["Acute migraine — treat EARLY at mild-moderate phase: NSAID (naproxen/ibuprofen) for mild; triptan for moderate-severe (sumatriptan 50-100 PO, 6 SC for rapid/vomiting; try 2-3 different triptans before abandoning class); combine triptan+naproxen (synergy); antiemetic adjunct (metoclopramide); gepants (ubrogepant/rimegepant) or lasmiditan when triptans fail/contraindicated (triptans avoided in CAD/uncontrolled HTN/hemiplegic aura); cap acute meds at <=2 d/wk — say the number out loud","NO butalbital, NO opioids for migraine — they are MOH factories with the fastest conversion rates","Prophylaxis when >=4 headache d/mo, disabling attacks, or MOH risk: first-line oral — propranolol/metoprolol, topiramate (teratogen — contraception conversation), amitriptyline/venlafaxine, candesartan; pick by comorbidity (HTN -> beta-blocker/candesartan; insomnia/depression -> amitriptyline; obesity -> topiramate); adequate trial = 8-12 wks at target dose; success = 50% reduction, tracked by diary","CGRP-era escalation: monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab monthly SC) or daily gepants after 2 failed oral classes (payer-typical); onabotulinumtoxinA for chronic migraine via neurology","MOH treatment: name it, taper/stop the culprit (abrupt for triptans/NSAIDs; taper butalbital/opioids), start prophylaxis simultaneously, bridge with naproxen or short steroid course, and warn about the 1-2 wk withdrawal worsening — otherwise they relapse to the bottle","Lifestyle scaffolding: regular sleep/meals/hydration, caffeine consistency then reduction, aerobic exercise, trigger diary with skepticism (many 'triggers' are prodrome cravings); magnesium/riboflavin/CoQ10 reasonable adjuncts"],"pearls":["Count medication days before adjusting anything — prophylaxis cannot outrun daily Excedrin","The 'sinus headache' with photophobia that recurs monthly is a migraine with real estate confusion — treat it as one and it finally responds","Topiramate in a woman of childbearing age without a contraception discussion is a documentation and safety failure","Thunderclap means the worst-headache clock started in seconds — that patient is leaving your clinic in an ambulance, not with a triptan"],"refs":[{"t":"AHS Consensus: Migraine Treatment","u":"https://americanheadachesociety.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Migraine Management","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Medication Overuse Headache","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538150/"}]},{"id":"fm-dizziness","name":"Dizziness & Vertigo (Office Evaluation)","sec":"fm","present":["Replace 'what do you mean by dizzy' (unreliable) with TiTrATE: Timing (episodic vs continuous), Triggers (positional, spontaneous, orthostatic), And Targeted Examination","Episodic-positional seconds-long spinning = BPPV; episodic-spontaneous minutes-hours with headache history = vestibular migraine, with aural fullness/hearing loss/tinnitus = Meniere; continuous days-long after viral illness = vestibular neuritis — VS the stroke that mimics it","Presyncope bucket (lightheaded on standing, graying vision): orthostatics, meds (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, TCAs), arrhythmia, anemia, dehydration; chronic nonspecific unsteadiness in the elderly is usually multifactorial (vision + neuropathy + vestibular + polypharmacy) — and PPPD after a vestibular insult"],"dx":["Dix-Hallpike for positional vertigo: upbeating-torsional nystagmus with latency and fatigability = posterior canal BPPV (diagnostic AND leads straight to treatment); supine roll test if Hallpike negative but story fits (horizontal canal)","Acute continuous vertigo (acute vestibular syndrome): HINTS beats early MRI when performed competently — peripheral pattern = abnormal head impulse (catch-up saccade), unidirectional horizontal nystagmus, no vertical skew; ANY central feature (normal head impulse with spontaneous nystagmus, direction-changing or vertical nystagmus, skew, new hearing loss with vascular risk, severe gait ataxia — can't sit unsupported) -> stroke pathway/ED (CT misses posterior fossa; MRI-DWI, and even it can be falsely negative <48 h)","Orthostatic vitals done right: supine 5 min then 1 and 3 min standing; drop >=20 SBP or >=10 DBP, or HR rise >=30 (POTS in the young)","No routine labs/imaging for classic BPPV or neuritis; audiometry for Meniere suspicion; medication autopsy in every elderly dizzy patient"],"tx":["BPPV: Epley maneuver in the room — 80%+ effective, repeat at follow-up if needed; teach home Epley/Brandt-Daroff; do NOT prescribe meclizine for BPPV (sedates, delays compensation, falls); recurrence is common — re-treat, and check vitamin D (supplementation reduces recurrence)","Vestibular neuritis: brief symptomatic control ONLY x24-72 h (antiemetic, short benzodiazepine or antihistamine), then STOP suppressants and mobilize — vestibular rehab (VRT) is the treatment; steroids optional early (evidence mixed); recovery over weeks","Vestibular migraine: treat as migraine — lifestyle + prophylaxis (propranolol, amitriptyline, topiramate); acute vestibular suppressants sparingly","Meniere: salt restriction, betahistine or diuretic trial, refer ENT (audiometry, intratympanic options); document hearing trajectory","Presyncope: fix the fixable — deprescribe/space antihypertensives, hydration/salt, compression, counter-maneuvers; ECG for all recurrent/exertional/cardiac-history presyncope (+ family hx sudden death) and low threshold for monitor","PPPD (persistent postural-perceptual dizziness: chronic swaying worse upright/visual motion after an insult): name it, VRT + SSRI/SNRI + CBT — reassurance without a diagnosis fails these patients"],"pearls":["The Epley you perform today prevents the meclizine prescription that causes next month's fall","HINTS only applies to the continuously vertiginous patient WITH nystagmus — applying it between episodes or without nystagmus is how it 'misses' strokes","A dizzy patient who cannot stand or sit unassisted is central until proven otherwise, whatever the head impulse shows","In the 80-year-old, dizziness is a syndrome, not a diagnosis — the med list, the eyes, the feet, and the vestibule each pay part of the bill"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS BPPV Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Dizziness Evaluation (TiTrATE)","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vertigo","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482356/"}]},{"id":"fm-depression","name":"Depression (Screening, SSRIs, Follow-Up)","sec":"fm","present":["Screen all adults (USPSTF B) and adolescents 12-18: PHQ-2 gateway -> PHQ-9; postpartum with EPDS; positive screen obligates same-visit severity assessment, suicide risk assessment, and a plan — screening without capacity to act is theater","MDD (DSM-5): >=5 symptoms x2 wks incl depressed mood or anhedonia (SIGECAPS); grade by PHQ-9: 5-9 mild, 10-14 moderate, 15-19 mod-severe, >=20 severe","Rule out the impostors and pivots: bipolar spectrum (screen mania/hypomania — MDQ — BEFORE starting an antidepressant), hypothyroidism, anemia, OSA, substance/alcohol use, medication-induced (steroids, isotretinoin), grief (can coexist), perimenopause"],"dx":["Suicide assessment every positive screen and every follow-up: ideation -> plan -> intent -> means -> protective factors (Columbia/C-SSRS framing); PHQ-9 item 9 positive requires direct conversation, not a checkbox","Active SI with plan/intent: same-day crisis evaluation (988, crisis team, ED) — arrange, don't just recommend; passive ideation: safety planning intervention (warning signs, coping, contacts, means restriction counseling) documented in the chart","Labs pragmatic at diagnosis: TSH, CBC, CMP, +/- B12/D; no biomarker diagnoses depression","Measurement-based care: repeat PHQ-9 every visit; response = 50% drop, remission = <5 — the score is the vital sign of this disease"],"tx":["Mild: psychotherapy first-line (CBT/behavioral activation/IPT), exercise prescription (real effect sizes), bibliotherapy/digital CBT; moderate-severe: antidepressant + therapy beats either alone","SSRI start (sertraline 50 -> 100-200; escitalopram 10 -> 20) chosen by side-effect profile, interactions, prior response, cost; alternatives by phenotype: bupropion (fatigue, weight/sexual concerns; avoid seizure/eating disorder), mirtazapine (insomnia, appetite loss), SNRI (comorbid pain), avoid paroxetine (anticholinergic, withdrawal, pregnancy)","Set the contract at the first script: 2-4 wks to begin working, 6-8 for full effect, common early side effects fade, do NOT stop abruptly, and follow-up in 2-4 wks (1-2 wks or sooner for adolescents/young adults — boxed warning window: monitor activation/SI early)","Inadequate response at 4-8 wks on adequate dose: optimize dose -> switch class or within class -> augment (add bupropion, or low-dose atypical — aripiprazole; lithium via psych); 2 adequate failures = treatment-resistant -> psychiatry referral (also refer: bipolar suspicion, psychosis, pregnancy complexity, SI)","Continue 6-12 mo after remission (first episode); >=2 episodes or severe -> maintenance years-to-indefinite; discontinue by slow taper (fluoxetine self-tapers; venlafaxine/paroxetine need the slowest) — distinguish discontinuation symptoms (rapid onset, dizziness/zaps, resolve on redosing) from relapse (weeks later, mood-predominant)","Treat the whole chart: alcohol/substance use, insomnia (CBT-I), pain, social drivers; perinatal — sertraline preferred, untreated depression is itself a fetal/maternal risk"],"pearls":["One manic episode missed = one SSRI-induced mixed state you own — the MDQ takes ninety seconds and belongs before the first prescription","The most common cause of 'SSRI failure' is 4 weeks of sertraline 50 with no follow-up visit — dose, duration, and adherence before switching","Ask about sexual side effects at follow-up; patients won't volunteer the number-one reason they quietly stopped","'Are you thinking about ending your life?' asked plainly does not plant the idea — it opens the only door that matters"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Depression & Suicide Risk Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"APA Practice Guideline: Major Depressive Disorder","u":"https://www.psychiatry.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Major Depressive Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559078/"}]},{"id":"fm-anxiety","name":"Anxiety Disorders (GAD, Panic)","sec":"fm","present":["Screen adults <65 (USPSTF 2023) and adolescents: GAD-2 -> GAD-7 (5-9 mild, 10-14 moderate, >=15 severe)","GAD: excessive, hard-to-control worry across domains >=6 mo + restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension, sleep disturbance; panic disorder: recurrent unexpected panic attacks + >=1 mo of anticipatory anxiety/avoidance; map the cousins — social anxiety, specific phobia, agoraphobia, PTSD, OCD (different first-line nuances)","Panic patients present somatically — chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, dizziness — often via urgent care and the ED; validate the symptoms as real while reframing the mechanism"],"dx":["Rule out mimics proportionate to the story: TSH for everyone, +/- CBC, glucose, ECG for palpitations/chest pain; caffeine/energy drinks, stimulants (prescribed and otherwise), albuterol overuse, decongestants, cannabis, alcohol/benzo withdrawal — the pharmacologic anxiety census","Rare zebras earn a story first: pheochromocytoma (paroxysms + hypertension + headache/diaphoresis), hyperthyroid (weight loss, tremor, tachycardia), arrhythmia (abrupt-offset palpitations -> monitor)","Screen the fellow travelers: depression (majority overlap), substance use as self-medication, suicide risk in severe/comorbid cases","Track with GAD-7 (and panic frequency diary) every visit — measurement-based care applies to anxiety too"],"tx":["First-line, both disorders: CBT (exposure-based for panic; worry-focused for GAD) and/or SSRI — sertraline 25 mg (yes, half the depression start — anxious patients are activation-prone) or escitalopram 5-10, titrate q2-4 wks; SNRIs (venlafaxine XR, duloxetine) equally first-line for GAD","The onboarding speech prevents the callback: early jitteriness possible week 1-2 (start low, temporary), benefit at 4-6 wks, full effect 8-12, and panic patients specifically fear the side effects — pre-brief or they stop at day 3","Response inadequate at 6-8 wks adequate dose: optimize -> switch SSRI/SNRI -> augment or alternatives: buspirone (GAD adjunct, no dependence, needs BID-TID adherence), hydroxyzine PRN-bridge (sedating, anticholinergic in elderly), pregabalin (GAD evidence, misuse potential), mirtazapine; propranolol for performance-type situational anxiety","Benzodiazepines: short-term bridge (2-4 wks) at SSRI initiation in severe panic at most — scheduled short-acting benzos long-term breed dependence, interdose rebound, falls, and block the exposure learning CBT depends on; avoid entirely with substance-use history, OSA, opioids; if inherited on chronic benzos, taper glacially (~5-10%/mo) with support","CBT specifics worth prescribing by name: interoceptive exposure for panic (recreate the feared sensations), worry-postponement and intolerance-of-uncertainty work for GAD; digital CBT and apps count when access is thin","Duration: continue medication 12 mo after response before slow taper; relapse -> restart and extend; lifestyle scaffolding — caffeine down, alcohol honesty, exercise (anxiolytic in trials), sleep regularity"],"pearls":["The panic patient's fifth normal troponin is not reassurance — the diagnosis and its treatment are; name panic disorder confidently and treatment can finally start","Start the SSRI at half-dose in anxiety or the medication will 'prove' the patient's fear that pills make them worse","PRN lorazepam for daily anxiety is a slow-motion dependency plan — if they need something daily, it should be something that treats the disorder","Ask what the anxiety stops them from doing — avoidance, not worry intensity, is the disability metric and the CBT target"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Anxiety Screening in Adults","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"ADAA Clinical Resources","u":"https://adaa.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Generalized Anxiety Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441870/"}]},{"id":"fm-adhd","name":"ADHD (Child & Adult, Primary Care Role)","sec":"fm","present":["Children: inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity impairing function in >=2 settings (home AND school), several symptoms before age 12; AAP supports primary-care diagnosis 4-18 with Vanderbilt/Conners scales from parent AND teacher","Adults: >=5 symptoms (DSM-5 adult threshold), childhood onset by history (report cards, family input help), current impairment — disorganization, time blindness, job/relationship churn; ASRS screener; new-onset 'ADHD' at 45 without any childhood trail warrants a broader differential","Differential and comorbidity are the work: anxiety, depression, learning disorders, sleep deprivation/OSA, substance use, trauma, thyroid, absence seizures (staring spells), hearing/vision — comorbidity is the rule (2/3 of kids)"],"dx":["No confirmatory test: diagnosis = DSM-5 criteria + multi-informant rating scales + functional impairment; neuropsych testing for diagnostic ambiguity or learning-disorder mapping, not routine","Baseline before stimulants: cardiac history (syncope, exertional symptoms, family sudden death <35, structural disease) + exam + BP/HR — ECG only if that screen is positive (AHA); document height/weight (kids), sleep, appetite, mood, substance use","School side: ADHD qualifies for 504 accommodations/IEP evaluation — the letter you write moves more than the milligrams sometimes","Adults: screen substance use honestly; stimulant misuse/diversion risk assessment is part of the visit, not an accusation"],"tx":["Preschool (4-5): parent training in behavior management (PTBM) FIRST (AAP); methylphenidate only if moderate-severe and behavioral therapy insufficient","6+: stimulant medication is the most effective intervention (effect sizes ~0.8-1.0) + behavioral/organizational skills therapy; methylphenidate-class vs amphetamine-class — either first in kids (MPH slightly favored young), amphetamines slightly favored adults; long-acting formulations preferred for adherence, coverage, and lower misuse potential","Titrate to best response q1-2 wks (school feedback loops); manage the predictable: appetite suppression (dose with/after breakfast, calorie-load evenings), sleep delay (dose timing, earlier release formulation), height/weight surveillance in kids (drug holidays debatable — decide by growth curve), BP/HR each visit","Nonstimulants when stimulants fail/intolerable/contraindicated or active SUD concern: atomoxetine (weeks to work, 24-h coverage), viloxazine, alpha-2 agonists (guanfacine XR/clonidine XR — good adjunct for hyperactivity/tics/sleep, taper don't stop)","Adults: same medication logic + coaching/CBT for ADHD, externalize the executive function (calendars, alarms, single-inbox systems); treat the comorbid mood/anxiety in parallel — sequence by severity","Controlled-substance housekeeping: state PDMP checks, one prescriber, scheduled follow-ups (monthly early, then q3mo), document response with scales — structure protects patient and prescriber"],"pearls":["A teacher scale that's clean while home is on fire means look for the family-system or anxiety story — impairment in ONE setting isn't ADHD","Stimulants don't cause substance abuse — untreated ADHD is the bigger SUD risk factor; treated kids do better, not worse","The college student who's 'always had it but never told anyone' during finals week gets a real evaluation, not a same-day Adderall script — and saying so kindly is a skill","'The medicine stopped working' in October is often a growth spurt, a new stressor, or missed doses — investigate before escalating milligrams"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP ADHD Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"CDC ADHD Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/adhd"},{"t":"StatPearls: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441838/"}]},{"id":"fm-insomnia","name":"Insomnia","sec":"fm","present":["Chronic insomnia: difficulty initiating/maintaining sleep or early waking with DAYTIME consequence, >=3 nights/wk >=3 mo despite adequate opportunity; short-term (<3 mo) usually stressor-linked","Sleep history beats sleep complaint: bed/wake times (and weekend drift), latency, awakenings, naps, screens, caffeine timing and dose, alcohol ('nightcap' = fragmentation), work schedule, bed partner report","Screen the masqueraders: OSA (snoring, witnessed apneas, unrefreshing sleep, STOP-BANG), restless legs (urge to move + evening worsening + relief with movement — check ferritin, treat if <75), circadian disorders (delayed phase in teens is not insomnia), depression/anxiety, GERD, nocturia, pain, meds (steroids, beta-agonists, SSRIs, stimulants, evening diuretics)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; 2-wk sleep diary (or wearable data interpreted with salt) beats recall; polysomnography ONLY for suspected OSA, PLMD, or parasomnias — not for insomnia itself","Actually quantify caffeine (energy drinks, pre-workout) and alcohol; ask about cannabis-for-sleep (rebound insomnia on stopping)","Distinguish sleep-state misperception (dramatic complaint, near-normal diary/partner report) — reassurance and CBT-I still work","Set the target as daytime function, not a number of hours — the 8-hour myth fuels the anxiety that maintains the insomnia"],"tx":["CBT-I is FIRST-LINE for chronic insomnia (ACP, AASM) — beats drugs long-term with no side-effect bill: stimulus control (bed = sleep/sex only; out of bed if awake ~20 min; fixed wake time 7 d/wk), sleep restriction/compression (match time-in-bed to actual sleep, then expand — the counterintuitive engine of the therapy), cognitive work on sleep catastrophizing, relaxation training; digital CBT-I (validated apps) when therapists are scarce","Sleep hygiene ALONE is not treatment — it's the warm-up everyone already failed; deliver it inside CBT-I, not instead of it","Short-term pharmacotherapy (ideally alongside CBT-I, lowest dose, shortest course, exit plan stated at the first script): DORAs (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant — maintenance insomnia, few next-day effects), low-dose doxepin 3-6 mg (maintenance, elderly-friendly), zolpidem/eszopiclone (sleep onset; complex sleep behaviors boxed warning; women/elderly lower doses; avoid with alcohol/opioids/OSA), ramelteon (onset, no abuse potential)","Avoid: chronic benzodiazepines for sleep, OTC antihistamines especially in the elderly (Beers: anticholinergic load, falls, next-day fog), routine trazodone-forever without reassessment; melatonin — modest onset benefit, more useful for circadian phase problems (low dose, hours before bed) than for maintenance insomnia; quetiapine-for-sleep is metabolic harm without insomnia evidence","Deprescribing inherited hypnotics: gradual taper with CBT-I support doubles success; expect and pre-brief rebound insomnia for a few nights so it doesn't 'prove' the drug was needed","Special populations: pregnancy — CBT-I, doxylamine acceptable; elderly — CBT-I works at every age, drugs cost more (falls, fractures, cognition); shift workers — light timing, anchor sleep, strategic caffeine/naps"],"pearls":["The patient sleeping 5 hours in a 9-hour bed window needs LESS time in bed, not a stronger pill — sleep restriction is the paradox that cures","Ask 'what do you do when you can't sleep?' — the answer (lying there fighting, phone in hand) IS the maintaining behavior CBT-I dismantles","An 'insomniac' who snores, naps involuntarily, and wakes unrefreshed has a sleep study to attend, not a zolpidem refill","Fixed wake time seven days a week is the single highest-yield instruction in sleep medicine — everything else negotiates around it"],"refs":[{"t":"ACP Guideline: Management of Chronic Insomnia","u":"https://www.acponline.org"},{"t":"AASM Clinical Practice Guidelines","u":"https://aasm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Insomnia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526136/"}]},{"id":"fm-sbirt","name":"Alcohol & Substance Use Screening (SBIRT) & Outpatient Management","sec":"fm","present":["Screen every adult annually: alcohol — single question ('How many times in the past year >=5 (men <=64) / >=4 (women, 65+) drinks in a day?') or AUDIT-C; drugs — direct question or single-item screen; USPSTF B for alcohol screening + brief intervention and for drug-use screening (adults)","Positive screen -> full assessment: quantity/frequency, DSM-5 use-disorder criteria count (2-3 mild, 4-5 moderate, >=6 severe), consequences, prior treatment, withdrawal history (seizures/DTs = high-risk), readiness to change","Look for the presentations that ARE the screen: uncontrolled HTN, insomnia, GERD, recurrent falls/injuries, transaminitis/macrocytosis, anxiety, chronic pain with escalating opioids"],"dx":["Brief intervention (the BI of SBIRT), 5-15 min motivational style: personalized feedback against norms, elicit pros/cons, advise clearly, negotiate a goal (cut down vs abstain), arrange follow-up — repeated briefly at visits; effective for risky drinking, weaker for severe AUD (that needs treatment, not advice)","Labs when indicated: LFTs/GGT, CBC (MCV), PEth (phosphatidylethanol — weeks-long alcohol biomarker, useful and honest to discuss openly), hep C/HIV screening for injection use, pregnancy test when relevant","Withdrawal risk stratification before any alcohol quit attempt: prior withdrawal seizures/DTs, heavy daily use, comorbidity -> plan ambulatory vs medically managed withdrawal; NEVER 'just stop' the high-risk drinker","Overdose risk assessment for anyone using opioids (prescribed or not): dose, benzo/alcohol co-use, prior overdose, post-incarceration/abstinence periods (tolerance loss)"],"tx":["AUD pharmacotherapy is scandalously underused — offer it in primary care: naltrexone 50 mg PO daily or 380 mg IM monthly FIRST-LINE (reduces heavy-drinking days; avoid with opioid use/anticipated need, check LFTs; works with a cut-down goal, not only abstinence); acamprosate 666 TID for maintenance of abstinence (renal-adjust); disulfiram only for motivated, supervised abstinence; topiramate/gabapentin second-line (gabapentin also helps withdrawal-spectrum insomnia/anxiety)","Ambulatory alcohol withdrawal for low-risk patients: gabapentin or symptom-triggered benzodiazepine protocol with daily contact, thiamine for everyone; ANY high-risk feature -> supervised/inpatient withdrawal","OUD: buprenorphine belongs in family medicine (no X-waiver anymore) — standard or low-dose initiation, maintenance dosing 16-24 mg, treat as chronic disease with routine visits/UDS as therapeutic tools not traps; methadone via OTP; naltrexone-XR only after full washout (precipitates withdrawal otherwise; adherence cliff)","Naloxone co-prescribed and taught for every OUD patient, every high-dose opioid script, and every household with risk — no controversy, just do it","Tobacco/vaping and cannabis get their own honest conversations (CUD is real: irritability/sleep disturbance on stopping; no approved pharmacotherapy — CBT/MI); stimulant use disorder: contingency management has the best evidence, refer where available","Referral to treatment (the RT): match severity to level (outpatient counseling, IOP, residential), warm handoffs beat pamphlets, mutual-help options plural (AA/SMART Recovery), and the door stays open after relapse — return to use is data for the plan, not a moral verdict"],"pearls":["'Do you ever drink?' invites a no — the validated single question with numbers in it is the screen that actually finds risky drinking","Naltrexone for AUD is as evidence-based as metformin for diabetes and prescribed a tenth as often — be the clinic that fixes that","The most dangerous week of opioid use disorder is the first week after abstinence (jail, detox, hospitalization) — that's when the naloxone conversation saves the life","Withdrawal kills alcoholics; opioid withdrawal kills almost no one but drives return to use — triage your fear accordingly"],"refs":[{"t":"USPSTF Unhealthy Alcohol & Drug Use Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"SAMHSA SBIRT & Treatment Resources","u":"https://www.samhsa.gov"},{"t":"ASAM Clinical Guidelines","u":"https://www.asam.org"}]},{"id":"fm-thyroid","name":"Thyroid Disorders (Outpatient: Nodules, Hypo/Hyper Titration)","sec":"fm","present":["Hypothyroid: fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, dry skin, menorrhagia, bradycardia — or (commonly) an abnormal TSH on nonspecific-symptom workup; Hashimoto's dominates","Hyperthyroid: weight loss, palpitations, heat intolerance, tremor, anxiety, AF in the elderly (sometimes 'apathetic' hyperthyroidism = just AF + weight loss); Graves (diffuse, +/- orbitopathy) vs toxic nodule(s) vs thyroiditis (transient)","Nodules: usually incidental (palpation or imaging); the questions are only two — is it functioning, and is it cancer (risk factors: childhood neck radiation, family hx MTC/MEN2, rapid growth, hoarseness, fixed/hard, lymphadenopathy)"],"dx":["TSH first, always; abnormal -> add free T4 (+ T3 if TSH suppressed): high TSH/low FT4 overt hypo; high TSH/normal FT4 subclinical hypo (confirm by REPEAT in 6-12 wks with TPO Ab — most 'abnormal' TSH normalizes); low TSH/high FT4-T3 overt hyper","Hyperthyroid etiology: TRAb (Graves — positive spares the uptake scan) or radioactive iodine uptake/scan (diffuse high = Graves, focal = toxic nodule, LOW uptake = thyroiditis/exogenous — critical fork: low-uptake disease gets NO antithyroid drugs); don't scan while pregnant","Nodules: TSH + dedicated thyroid ultrasound; suppressed TSH -> uptake scan (hot nodules are essentially never cancer — treat the hyperthyroidism, skip the needle); FNA by sonographic risk (TI-RADS/ATA): highly suspicious >=1 cm, intermediate >=1-1.5 cm, spongiform/cystic observe or >=2 cm; purely cystic — no FNA","Titration traps: check TSH 6-8 wks after ANY dose change (not sooner — it lags); amiodarone, lithium, checkpoint inhibitors, biotin (assay interference — hold 2 d), and pregnancy each rewrite the rules"],"tx":["Overt hypothyroid: levothyroxine full replacement ~1.6 mcg/kg/d for healthy adults; start 25-50 mcg and creep up in elderly/CAD; empty stomach, 30-60 min before breakfast (or consistent bedtime), 4 h from calcium/iron/PPIs; recheck TSH 6-8 wks, then annually when stable; brand-consistency beats brand-choice","Subclinical hypothyroid: treat if TSH >=10, positive TPO with symptoms, pregnancy/trying to conceive; otherwise monitor — most elderly with TSH 5-7 need nothing (age-shifted normal range), and overtreatment buys AF and fractures","Dose-change triggers to anticipate: pregnancy (increase ~30% immediately — often 2 extra doses/wk — and refer/consult; target trimester-specific TSH), significant weight change, starting estrogen, malabsorption/celiac, new interfering meds","Graves: methimazole first-line (PTU only first-trimester pregnancy/thyroid storm) — counsel agranulocytosis (fever + sore throat = STOP and CBC) and hepatotoxicity; beta-blocker (atenolol/propranolol) for symptoms; definitive options via endocrine: radioiodine (worsens orbitopathy risk — steroid cover/smoking cessation) or thyroidectomy; 12-18 mo ATD course then TRAb-guided withdrawal is legitimate","Thyroiditis (postpartum, subacute/painful, silent): beta-blockade through the toxic phase, NSAIDs/steroids for painful subacute, then WATCH for the hypothyroid phase (often transient — don't commit to lifelong levothyroxine reflexively); no antithyroid drugs (there's no overproduction)","Nodule follow-up: benign FNA -> surveillance ultrasound (interval by risk pattern); Bethesda III/IV -> molecular testing or repeat FNA/endocrine referral; growing/symptomatic/suspicious -> surgery referral; suppressive levothyroxine for nodules is obsolete"],"pearls":["Half of levothyroxine 'failures' are calcium, coffee, iron, or a PPI standing between the pill and the ileum — audit the morning routine before the dose","A tender thyroid after a viral illness with a suppressed TSH is thyroiditis — methimazole here treats a disease the patient doesn't have","The pregnant hypothyroid patient needed her dose increased the day the test turned positive — teach the two-extra-doses-a-week trick at the preconception visit","Fever and sore throat on methimazole is agranulocytosis until the CBC says otherwise — make sure the patient can recite this rule"],"refs":[{"t":"ATA Guidelines (Hypothyroidism, Hyperthyroidism, Nodules)","u":"https://www.thyroid.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Thyroid Disease Management","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hypothyroidism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519536/"}]},{"id":"fm-anemia","name":"Anemia (Outpatient Workup)","sec":"fm","present":["Often incidental on CBC; symptomatic when faster or deeper: fatigue, dyspnea on exertion, pica/ice craving (iron deficiency's tell), restless legs; exam — pallor, glossitis/koilonychia (iron), jaundice (hemolysis), neuro signs (B12)","The primary-care reframe: anemia is a FINDING, not a diagnosis — the workup names the cause, and in adults the cause may be a bleeding lesion","Ask the bleeding review: menstrual quantification (>=7 d, clots, flooding), GI symptoms/melena, NSAID/anticoagulant use, prior GI surgery, diet (vegan -> B12; tea-with-meals -> iron absorption), alcohol"],"dx":["Start with MCV + retic + smear: microcytic (iron deficiency, thalassemia trait, chronic disease, sideroblastic), normocytic (chronic disease/CKD, early iron deficiency, hemolysis, marrow), macrocytic (B12/folate, alcohol/liver, hypothyroid, MDS in the elderly, drugs — hydroxyurea, MTX, zidovudine)","Iron studies decoded: ferritin <30 = iron deficiency, full stop (<45 per AGA); ferritin 30-100 with inflammation — use TSAT <20% + clinical context; ferritin is an acute-phase liar in CRP-positive patients","Iron deficiency WITHOUT obvious explanation (all men, postmenopausal women, and premenopausal women without clearly attributable menstrual loss per judgment): bidirectional endoscopy (EGD + colonoscopy) — this is the guideline hill to die on; add celiac serology to the workup; negative scopes + refractory -> small bowel evaluation","Microcytosis with normal-ish RBC count and disproportionately low MCV, or family history: hemoglobin electrophoresis/thalassemia trait (Mentzer <13 hints) — do NOT iron-load a thalassemia trait based on MCV alone (check iron studies; the two coexist too); B12 borderline (150-400): confirm with methylmalonic acid; hemolysis screen when retics high: LDH, haptoglobin, indirect bili, Coombs"],"tx":["Iron deficiency: treat the deficiency AND find the source — oral iron (ferrous sulfate 325 mg) ONCE daily or every-OTHER-day (hepcidin physiology: alternate-day dosing absorbs as well or better with fewer GI effects); take with vitamin C/away from calcium, tea, PPIs; expect retic bump ~1 wk, Hgb +1-2 g/dL by 4 wks — check it; continue ~3 mo after Hgb normalizes to refill ferritin stores","IV iron (ferric carboxymaltose, iron sucrose) when: oral intolerance/failure, malabsorption (celiac, bypass), CKD on ESA, IBD, heavy ongoing loss, second/third-trimester pregnancy with significant deficiency, or preop optimization — stop treating IV iron like an exotic escalation; watch for post-FCM hypophosphatemia when repeating","B12 deficiency: 1000 mcg IM daily-weekly loading then monthly, OR high-dose oral 1000-2000 mcg daily (passive absorption works even in pernicious anemia for many) — neuro symptoms favor parenteral loading; check for pernicious anemia (anti-IF antibodies), replete K-watchfully in severe cases, and never treat 'borderline B12 with neuro symptoms' with folate alone (masks the count, misses the cord)","Folate: 1 mg daily (alcohol use, pregnancy, hemolysis, MTX) — but confirm B12 status first","Anemia of chronic disease/inflammation: treat the driver; CKD anemia — check/replete iron FIRST (TSAT/ferritin targets), then ESA discussion via nephrology for Hgb <10 (targets ~10-11.5, not normal — normalization increases CV events)","Refer heme: unexplained cytopenias/bicytopenia, suspected MDS (macrocytic elderly with failure to explain), hemolysis beyond simple cases, suspected myeloma (anemia + renal + bone pain + high protein gap -> SPEP/FLC first)"],"pearls":["Ferritin under 30 ends the 'is it iron deficiency' debate — the remaining question is 'why', and in a 60-year-old man the answer lives in his colon until proven otherwise","Every-other-day iron is the rare deprescription that improves efficacy — less pill, more absorption, fewer black stools","'Anemia of chronic disease' in a patient with a TSAT of 12% is iron deficiency wearing a disguise — inflammation hides iron, it doesn't replace it","The macrocytic anemia with a tingling gait is a B12 emergency-in-slow-motion — folate will fix the blood and forfeit the spinal cord"],"refs":[{"t":"AGA Guideline: Iron Deficiency Anemia Evaluation","u":"https://gastro.org"},{"t":"ASH Anemia Resources","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Iron Deficiency Anemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448065/"}]},{"id":"fm-ckd","name":"Chronic Kidney Disease (Primary Care Management & Referral Timing)","sec":"fm","present":["Silent until late — found by screening the right people: diabetes (annual), hypertension, CVD, family history, prior AKI; CKD = abnormal eGFR (<60) or markers of damage (ACR >=30, hematuria, structural) persisting >=3 MONTHS — one low eGFR is not CKD, repeat it","Stage by the KDIGO heat map: G-stage (eGFR: G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 <15) x A-stage (ACR: A1 <30, A2 30-300, A3 >300) — the ACR column changes risk and therapy as much as the eGFR row","History sweep: NSAID habit, herbal/supplement use, recurrent stones, obstruction/LUTS, autoimmune symptoms, family polycystic disease"],"dx":["Every CKD patient gets BOTH numbers: eGFR (creatinine-based; 2021 race-free CKD-EPI; add cystatin C when muscle mass distorts — amputee, bodybuilder, cachexia, borderline decisions) AND urine ACR — an eGFR without an ACR is half a diagnosis","Establish cause: UA with microscopy (dysmorphic RBCs/casts -> glomerular -> nephrology now), renal ultrasound (obstruction, size asymmetry, cysts), and cause-directed labs when the picture isn't diabetic/hypertensive (SPEP/FLC in the anemic elderly, ANA/complements, hepatitis/HIV serologies)","Trajectory beats snapshot: plot eGFR over time — rapid progression (>5 mL/min/yr sustained decline) is itself a referral trigger; use validated risk (Kidney Failure Risk Equation) to translate stage into 2/5-yr failure probability for patients and referral decisions","Complication surveillance from G3: Hgb (anemia), K/bicarb (acidosis), Ca/phos/PTH/25-OH-D (mineral-bone, mainly G4+), and med-list toxicology every visit"],"tx":["The four-pillar disease-modifying stack (deploy, don't defer): (1) ACEi/ARB titrated to max tolerated for HTN + albuminuria (A2-A3) — a <=30% creatinine rise is expected, not a stop signal; (2) SGLT2 inhibitor for eGFR >=20-25 with albuminuria or diabetes (DAPA-CKD, EMPA-KIDNEY — benefit with or without DM; continue as eGFR falls, expect the initial dip); (3) statin per KDIGO for CKD >=50 yrs (don't chase LDL targets on dialysis); (4) BP to <120-130 systolic as tolerated + finerenone for diabetic CKD with albuminuria despite ACEi/ARB (FIDELIO/FIGARO — watch K); GLP-1 RA (FLOW trial) joins the roster in diabetic CKD","Glycemic target individualized (~A1c 7-8% by hypoglycemia risk); metformin fine to eGFR >=30 (halve 30-45); dose-adjust the usual suspects: gabapentin, DOACs, baclofen, TMP-SMX, fluoroquinolones; insulin needs decline as GFR falls","Nephrotoxin hygiene taught explicitly: NO chronic NSAIDs, sick-day rules (hold ACEi/ARB, SGLT2i, diuretics, metformin during vomiting/diarrhea/dehydration), contrast risk-benefit honestly (don't withhold necessary imaging), bowel-prep phosphate avoidance","Complications: metabolic acidosis (bicarb <22) — sodium bicarbonate 650 mg BID-TID slows progression; anemia — iron first, ESA via nephrology <10; hyperkalemia — dietary counseling, diuretic optimization, potassium binders (patiromer/SZC) to KEEP the RAASi on board rather than reflexively stopping the drug that protects the kidney","Immunize (flu, COVID, pneumococcal, hep B when approaching dialysis), and dose the lifestyle levers: protein moderation (~0.8 g/kg, avoid very-high-protein fads), sodium <2 g, exercise, weight, smoking cessation","REFER nephrology: eGFR <30 (G4 — non-negotiable, enables modality education/access planning/transplant eval BEFORE crash dialysis), ACR >300 or nephrotic-range, rapid progression, resistant HTN on 4 agents, refractory K/acidosis/anemia, hereditary disease, unclear cause, glomerular hematuria; co-manage, don't hand off — the pillars stay yours"],"pearls":["Stopping the ACE inhibitor for a 25% creatinine rise fires the kidney's best bodyguard for doing its job — bank the rise and keep the drug","The SGLT2 inhibitor is renal medicine's statin moment and it's still under-prescribed in exactly the clinics reading this — eGFR 25 with albuminuria is a start signal, not a contraindication","Late referral (first nephrology visit within 3 months of dialysis) is an independent mortality risk — the G4 referral is about planning a good start, not surrendering the patient","Every CKD med review is a dosing audit: the gabapentin causing the confusion and the glyburide causing the hypoglycemia were both dosed for kidneys the patient no longer has"],"refs":[{"t":"KDIGO CKD Evaluation & Management Guideline","u":"https://kdigo.org"},{"t":"NKF Primary Care CKD Resources","u":"https://www.kidney.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Kidney Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535404/"}]},{"id":"fm-uti","name":"UTIs & Recurrent UTI Prevention (Outpatient)","sec":"fm","present":["Uncomplicated cystitis (healthy nonpregnant women): dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic discomfort, +/- hematuria — WITHOUT fever, flank pain, or systemic features; combination of classic symptoms without vaginal discharge carries very high pretest probability","Pyelonephritis: fever, chills, flank pain/CVA tenderness, nausea +/- cystitis symptoms; complicated territory: pregnancy, male sex, catheter, stones/obstruction, immunocompromise, urologic abnormality, recent instrumentation","Recurrent UTI: >=2 in 6 mo or >=3 in 12 mo, culture-proven — distinguish relapse (same organism, <2 wks) from reinfection; in postmenopausal women think GSM (genitourinary syndrome of menopause); differential for 'recurrent UTI' with negative cultures: overactive bladder, GSM, urethritis (STI), painful bladder syndrome, vulvovaginitis"],"dx":["Classic uncomplicated cystitis needs NO urinalysis or culture — treat empirically by phone/protocol; dipstick when uncertain (nitrites specific, LE sensitive)","CULTURE before treating: pyelonephritis, complicated features, pregnancy, men, recurrence/treatment failure, atypical symptoms — and always in recurrent-UTI workups (the pattern of organisms drives strategy)","ASYMPTOMATIC bacteriuria: screen and treat ONLY in pregnancy and before urologic procedures with mucosal breach — everywhere else (elderly, diabetics, catheters, 'smelly urine', falls-without-delirium workups) treating it causes resistance without benefit (IDSA); a positive culture in a nursing-home patient without genitourinary symptoms is colonization, not infection","Imaging (CT or ultrasound) for pyelonephritis only when: obstruction suspected (colic, stones history), no improvement at 48-72 h, recurrent pyelo, or severe illness; recurrent uncomplicated cystitis in women does NOT need cystoscopy/imaging absent red flags (hematuria persisting after treatment, obstruction, prior urologic surgery)"],"tx":["Uncomplicated cystitis first-line (IDSA/AUA): nitrofurantoin monohydrate 100 mg BID x5 d (avoid CrCl <30, not for pyelo — no tissue levels), TMP-SMX DS BID x3 d (if local E. coli resistance <20% and no recent use), fosfomycin 3 g x1; fluoroquinolones are NOT for cystitis (collateral damage, boxed warnings) — reserve for pyelonephritis/complicated; beta-lactams (cefdinir, amox-clav) acceptable alternates x5-7 d","Outpatient pyelonephritis (stable, tolerating PO): culture first, then ciprofloxacin 500 BID x7 d or levofloxacin 750 x5-7 d; where FQ resistance >10% or recent exposure — one IV dose (ceftriaxone 1 g) then oral; TMP-SMX x14 d if susceptible; recheck at 48-72 h — no improvement = image + reassess; toxic, pregnant, vomiting, obstructed -> ED/admission","Men with UTI: culture always, 7+ days minimum, think prostatitis when febrile/perineal pain (then 2-4+ wks of an FQ or TMP-SMX); a single culture-proven simple cystitis in an otherwise healthy man no longer mandates full urologic workup — recurrences and complicated features do","Recurrent UTI prevention ladder (AUA/SUFU): behavioral honesty (hydration to 2-3 L works — trial evidence; post-coital voiding traditional), then VAGINAL ESTROGEN for peri/postmenopausal women (cream/ring — dramatically effective, chronically underused, safe including most breast cancer survivors with oncology input), then antibiotic strategies: post-coital single-dose (nitrofurantoin 100 or TMP-SMX SS) when intercourse-pattern, self-start therapy for reliable patients (standing script + culture kit), or continuous low-dose prophylaxis 3-6-12 mo (nitrofurantoin 50-100 qhs — counsel rare pulmonary/hepatic toxicity with long-term use)","Adjuncts with honest labeling: cranberry products — modest evidence, reasonable to try; methenamine hippurate — decent trial data (ALTAR: noninferior to prophylactic antibiotics), needs acidic urine, avoid in significant CKD; D-mannose — evidence disappointing in recent trials; phenazopyridine 2 d max for dysuria (orange everything, not an antibiotic — say so)","Pregnancy: treat asymptomatic bacteriuria AND cystitis (nitrofurantoin/cephalexin/fosfomycin — avoid nitrofurantoin and TMP-SMX near term/first trimester respectively per usual caveats), test-of-cure, and pyelonephritis is an admission"],"pearls":["The nursing-home urine culture ordered for 'confusion' finds colonization, earns cipro, and delivers C. diff — delirium workups start elsewhere unless genitourinary symptoms exist","Vaginal estrogen is the single most effective and least prescribed recurrent-UTI intervention in the postmenopausal woman — it rebuilds the ecosystem antibiotics keep carpet-bombing","A 'UTI' that never grows anything is a different disease wearing the costume — stop the antibiotic cycle and work up GSM, OAB, and urethritis","Nitrofurantoin never treats pyelonephritis — the drug that owns the bladder never reaches the kidney"],"refs":[{"t":"AUA/SUFU Recurrent UTI Guideline","u":"https://www.auanet.org"},{"t":"IDSA Uncomplicated Cystitis & Pyelonephritis / ASB Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Tract Infection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470195/"}]},{"id":"fm-sti","name":"STI Screening & Treatment (Outpatient)","sec":"fm","present":["Screening is opt-out and history-driven (the 5 P's: partners, practices, protection, past STIs, pregnancy intention): annual chlamydia/gonorrhea for all sexually active women <25 (USPSTF/CDC) and older with risk; syphilis + HIV for anyone at increased risk (and HIV once for everyone 15-65); MSM — at least annual (q3-6mo if higher-risk) CT/GC at ALL anatomic sites (urethral, pharyngeal, rectal by exposure), syphilis, HIV; pregnancy panel at first visit (HIV, syphilis, HBV, +/- CT/GC) with third-trimester syphilis re-screen per rising congenital syphilis rates","Symptomatic syndromes: urethritis/cervicitis (discharge, dysuria), PID (lower abdominal pain + cervical motion/adnexal tenderness — low threshold to treat), genital ulcers (painful clustered = HSV; painless indurated = syphilis chancre), proctitis, epididymitis","Normalize the conversation and the exam; partner status and exposure sites determine what you swab — you cannot find pharyngeal gonorrhea in a urine cup"],"dx":["NAAT is king: first-catch urine or (better in women) vaginal swab — self-collected swabs are valid and preferred by patients; site-specific NAATs for pharynx/rectum; test-of-cure NOT routine for chlamydia/gonorrhea EXCEPT pharyngeal GC (7-14 d) and pregnancy — but DO re-test everyone at 3 months (reinfection is the rule)","Syphilis: reverse-sequence (treponemal EIA -> RPR) or traditional — either way you need the RPR TITER to stage activity and follow treatment (fourfold drop = response); stage clinically: primary (chancre), secondary (rash incl palms/soles, condyloma lata), latent early vs late (timing matters for dosing), neuro/ocular/otic symptoms at ANY stage -> LP/urgent ophtho and IV therapy","HSV: PCR of lesion (best), type-specific serology only for specific questions (discordant couples, recurrent undiagnosed symptoms) — not screening; trichomonas: NAAT (wet mount insensitive) — test women with discharge and consider in persistent 'BV'","HIV testing with every STI diagnosis (an STI is an HIV risk event by definition) + hepatitis B/C per risk; and every STI visit is a PrEP-eligibility visit — bacterial STI in the last 6-12 months is itself an indication conversation"],"tx":["Chlamydia: DOXYCYCLINE 100 mg BID x7 d (now beats azithromycin — superior for rectal disease which is common and often unrecognized in women too); azithromycin 1 g only when adherence/pregnancy demands (pregnancy: azithro); abstain 7 d","Gonorrhea: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM x1 (1 g if >=150 kg) — alone; add doxycycline x7 d only if chlamydia not excluded; pharyngeal needs the test-of-cure; cephalosporin allergy -> gentamicin 240 IM + azithro 2 g (and call ID for the tough ones); resistance is a moving target — check current CDC guidance","Syphilis: benzathine penicillin G 2.4 MU IM x1 (primary/secondary/early latent), weekly x3 (late latent/unknown); pregnancy = penicillin PERIOD (desensitize allergics); warn about Jarisch-Herxheimer; follow RPR at 6/12 mo","PID outpatient (mild-moderate): ceftriaxone 500 IM + doxycycline 100 BID x14 d + METRONIDAZOLE 500 BID x14 d (now standard triple); admit: pregnancy, TOA, severe illness, failed oral, can't exclude surgical abdomen; low diagnostic bar is intentional — the cost of undertreating is tubal factor infertility","HSV: first episode acyclovir 400 TID or valacyclovir 1 g BID x7-10 d; recurrences — patient-initiated short courses at prodrome; suppression (valacyclovir 500-1000 daily) for frequent recurrences or discordant couples (reduces transmission ~50%); trichomonas: metronidazole 500 BID x7 d for women (single 2 g dose inferior), 2 g x1 acceptable for men — treat partners","The public-health half of the visit: treat partners — expedited partner therapy (EPT) is legal in most states for CT/GC (not for MSM-GC where evaluation preferred); report notifiable infections; vaccinate the encounter (HPV through 26/shared-decision to 45, HBV, HAV, mpox where indicated); doxy-PEP (doxycycline 200 mg within 72 h of condomless sex) for MSM/transgender women with a bacterial STI in the past year — real CDC guidance, offer it"],"pearls":["Screening only urine in an MSM patient misses the majority of gonorrhea — the pharynx and rectum are where the epidemic hides","The 3-month retest isn't distrust, it's epidemiology — reinfection from an untreated partner is the most common 'treatment failure'","Every positive syphilis screen without a titer is an unfinished lab order — you cannot follow what you never measured","PID is a clinical diagnosis you make generously and treat completely — the ultrasound can be normal while the tubes scar"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC STI Treatment Guidelines","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/std"},{"t":"USPSTF STI Screening Recommendations","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"CDC PrEP & Doxy-PEP Guidance","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv"}]},{"id":"fm-contraception","name":"Contraception Counseling & Options","sec":"fm","present":["Frame around the patient's reproductive goals (One Key Question: 'Would you like to become pregnant in the next year?') — counseling is shared decision-making across efficacy, side effects, bleeding profile, reversibility, autonomy — not an efficacy lecture; screen for coercion","Tiered effectiveness with typical use: LARC (IUDs, implant <1% failure) >> injectable (~4-6%) > pill/patch/ring (~7-9%) > condoms/withdrawal/fertility-awareness (13-20%+); condoms remain the only STI protection — dual method messaging","Rule out pregnancy reasonably (criteria: <=7 d after LMP, no intercourse since LMP, correct consistent contraception use, etc.) and START TODAY (quick start) with 7 d backup — do not send anyone home to wait for a period"],"dx":["Medical eligibility runs through the US MEC (CDC app — category 1-4): the big estrogen exclusions for combined methods: migraine WITH aura, smoking >=15/day AND >=35 yo, hypertension (esp uncontrolled), VTE history/thrombophilia, <21 d postpartum, breast cancer, major surgery with immobilization — when estrogen is out, everything progestin-only and both IUDs usually remain","BP measurement is the only mandatory exam/test before combined hormonal contraception; NO pelvic exam or cytology gatekeeping for pills; STI screening per guidelines can happen the same day as IUD insertion (don't delay for results)","Drug interactions that matter: enzyme inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, topiramate >=200, St John's wort) degrade pills/patch/ring/implant — IUDs and DMPA immune; lamotrigine levels fall on estrogen","Postpartum mapping: immediate postpartum LARC is MEC-fine; combined methods wait >=21-42 d (VTE); lactation — progestin-anything anytime, combined methods after milk established per shared decision"],"tx":["LARC first-line offered to everyone including adolescents and nulliparous patients (ACOG/AAP): levonorgestrel IUDs 3-8 yrs (lighter/absent periods — a feature for heavy bleeders), copper IUD 10-12 yrs (hormone-free; heavier cramps/bleeding trade-off), etonogestrel implant 3-5 yrs (irregular bleeding is THE counseling point and the top removal reason — pre-brief it); pre-insertion NSAID, consider cervical preparation/anesthesia options per patient (pain counseling now standard of care)","Combined pills: pick a monophasic 20-35 mcg EE generic, continuous/extended cycling is legitimate (skip placebo weeks for fewer withdrawal bleeds, endometriosis, menstrual migraine WITHOUT aura); missed-pill rules simplified: one missed — take ASAP carry on; two+ — take latest, backup 7 d, EC if needed; patch (avoid >=90 kg efficacy question) and ring are adherence-friendly equivalents","Progestin-only options: norethindrone POP (strict-ish timing) vs drospirenone POP (24/4, forgiving window); DMPA q13wk (weight gain and bone-density-reversible warnings, delayed return to fertility up to 10-18 mo — counsel the timeline-sensitive)","Emergency contraception ladder: copper or LNG IUD (most effective, up to 5 d, and leaves with ongoing contraception) > ulipristal 30 mg (up to 5 d, better with obesity; delays ovulation later — hold hormonal restart 5 d) > levonorgestrel 1.5 mg (best <72 h, weight-attenuated) — advance-prescribe EC freely","Bleeding management on progestin methods (the retention skill): reassure trajectory, NSAID 5-7 d courses, +/- short combined-pill or estrogen bridge when acceptable — most discontinuation is bleeding-driven and preventable with counseling","Permanent options both directions (salpingectomy trends for ovarian-cancer reduction; vasectomy is safer/cheaper/underused — pitch it to the couple), and document refusal-free counseling: the patient who wants to stop contraception gets preconception care, not persuasion"],"pearls":["Migraine with aura + estrogen is the eligibility question you will actually face weekly — ask about aura specifically, and remember the progestin universe stays open","Quick start today beats perfect start next cycle — the pregnancy you prevent is the one during the 'waiting for my period' gap","The implant's irregular bleeding removed more implants than any complication ever did — the counseling you give at insertion is the continuation rate","Same-day LARC for the patient in front of you: every 'come back after your STI results/next period' visit has a no-show rate and a pregnancy rate"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC US Medical Eligibility Criteria (US MEC) & SPR","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/reproductivehealth"},{"t":"ACOG Contraception Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Contraception","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536949/"}]},{"id":"fm-menstrual","name":"Menstrual Disorders (AUB, Dysmenorrhea, PCOS)","sec":"fm","present":["AUB vocabulary: heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB — soaking hourly, clots >2.5 cm, flooding, iron deficiency), intermenstrual, irregular/infrequent; structure the differential with PALM-COEIN: structural (Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia) vs nonstructural (Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic — anticoagulants/contraceptives, Not classified)","Primary dysmenorrhea: crampy midline pain first 1-3 d of flow since adolescence, otherwise well; secondary flags: pain worsening over years, dyspareunia, non-cyclic pain, infertility -> endometriosis (average diagnostic delay ~7-10 yrs — take it seriously early)","PCOS: irregular cycles (>35 d/ <8/yr), hyperandrogenism (hirsutism — Ferriman-Gallwey, acne, androgenic alopecia), often weight struggles, acanthosis; also the teen whose 'irregular periods' were dismissed for a decade"],"dx":["AUB baseline: pregnancy test FIRST always, CBC + ferritin (HMB is the top cause of iron deficiency in young women), TSH; coagulopathy screen (von Willebrand panel) when HMB since menarche +/- family history/bleeding elsewhere; transvaginal ultrasound as first-line imaging (fibroid mapping, endometrial stripe, polyp suspicion -> saline-infusion sono or hysteroscopy)","ENDOMETRIAL SAMPLING (office biopsy) rules — the malignancy net: ANY postmenopausal bleeding (or stripe >4 mm; any recurrent PMB regardless of stripe), AUB >=45 yo, and <45 with unopposed-estrogen exposure (obesity, chronic anovulation/PCOS), failed medical management, or Lynch risk","PCOS (Rotterdam, 2 of 3 after excluding mimics): ovulatory dysfunction, clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenism, polycystic morphology (or elevated AMH per 2023 international guideline; ultrasound/AMH NOT for diagnosis within 8 yrs of menarche); exclude the imposters: TSH, prolactin, 17-OHP (nonclassic CAH), and screen for the story of Cushing/androgen-secreting tumor (rapid virilization = different pathway)","PCOS metabolic surveillance is lifelong primary care: OGTT or A1c q1-3y, lipids, BP, weight trajectory, OSA screening, mood/anxiety and disordered-eating screening — and endometrial protection accounting (how many months since a bleed?)"],"tx":["HMB medical ladder: levonorgestrel 52 mg IUD is the most effective medical option (~90% reduction — first-line, also treats dysmenorrhea/endometrial protection); combined hormonal (cyclic or continuous); tranexamic acid 1300 mg TID x5 d of menses (hormone-free, ~40-50% reduction) or NSAIDs (naproxen — ~20-30% + pain relief) for those avoiding hormones; treat the iron deficiency in parallel — the ferritin is part of the disease","Acute heavy bleeding (stable, outpatient): high-dose regimens (e.g., monophasic OCP taper or oral progestin — norethindrone/medroxyprogesterone taper) + TXA; unstable/pregnant/failed -> ED/gyn","Structural AUB: symptomatic fibroids — IUD/TXA trial, GnRH antagonist combos (elagolix/relugolix) as bridge, then gyn referral (myomectomy fertility-sparing vs hysterectomy vs embolization); polyps -> hysteroscopic removal; refractory HMB family-complete -> ablation vs hysterectomy discussion","Primary dysmenorrhea: NSAIDs STARTED AT ONSET (or day before) scheduled through day 2 (ibuprofen 400-600 q6, naproxen 500 then 250 q8) + heat (evidence-backed) + hormonal suppression (any combined method or LNG-IUD; continuous dosing for the worst); failure of 3-6 mo of adequate NSAID+hormonal therapy = presumed endometriosis -> empiric progestin/dienogest or GnRH pathway and gyn referral — laparoscopy is not required to treat","PCOS by the patient's current goal: (a) not seeking pregnancy — combined OCP first-line (cycle control + androgen suppression), metformin for metabolic phenotype, GLP-1-era weight management genuinely changes the disease, anti-androgen (spironolactone 50-100 BID — contraception required) for hirsutism/acne after 6 mo OCP, mechanical/eflornithine adjuncts; ENSURE endometrial protection if declining hormones (cyclic progestin or IUD — >=3-4 bleeds/yr minimum); (b) seeking pregnancy — letrozole first-line ovulation induction (beats clomiphene for live birth), preconception weight/metabolic optimization, timely fertility referral","Across all: validate the symptom burden — menstrual disorders are among the most minimized complaints in medicine; school/work accommodation letters and iron repletion are part of the prescription"],"pearls":["Postmenopausal bleeding is endometrial cancer until the biopsy says otherwise — 'probably atrophy' is a conclusion you earn with tissue, not assumption","The teenager with HMB since her very first period gets a von Willebrand panel — inherited bleeding disorders live undiagnosed in gynecology clinics","PCOS management without an endometrial-protection plan is a hyperplasia subscription — count the bleeds per year out loud","Letrozole replaced clomiphene as first-line PCOS ovulation induction years ago — the live-birth data already voted"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG AUB & PCOS Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"International Evidence-Based PCOS Guideline","u":"https://www.monash.edu"},{"t":"StatPearls: Abnormal Uterine Bleeding","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532913/"}]},{"id":"fm-menopause","name":"Menopause & Hormone Therapy","sec":"fm","present":["Menopause = 12 mo amenorrhea (median 51-52); perimenopause starts years earlier with cycle irregularity + vasomotor symptoms (VMS — hot flashes/night sweats, up to 80% of women, median duration ~7-10 yrs), sleep disruption, mood lability, brain-fog complaints, arthralgias","Genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM): vaginal dryness, dyspareunia, urinary urgency/recurrent UTIs — progressive WITHOUT treatment, unlike VMS which eventually wane; chronically under-asked and under-treated","No labs needed for diagnosis in the right age with the right story (FSH fluctuates wildly in perimenopause — a normal one excludes nothing); check FSH/estradiol only for premature (<40, = premature ovarian insufficiency — different disease, hormones until natural menopause age unless contraindicated) or diagnostic ambiguity (post-ablation, IUD amenorrhea); pregnancy remains possible in perimenopause — contraception until 12 mo amenorrhea (or age-based)"],"dx":["The 2002 WHI hangover is the counseling battleground: re-read for patients — risks were driven by older women far from menopause on oral CEE+MPA; modern framing (Menopause Society 2022): for healthy symptomatic women <60 or <10 yrs from menopause, benefits of HT generally OUTWEIGH risks — the timing hypothesis","Risk-stratify before HT: personal breast cancer, estrogen-dependent malignancy, unexplained vaginal bleeding, active/high-risk VTE or CVD, severe liver disease = contraindications; migraine with aura, controlled HTN, gallbladder disease = route-and-formulation nuance (transdermal favored)","Baseline: BP, breast/cervical screening current, individualized VTE and ASCVD risk; investigate ANY postmenopausal bleeding (biopsy pathway) including unscheduled bleeding beyond the first ~6 mo on HT","Symptom inventory beyond flashes: sleep, mood (perimenopause is a depression-vulnerability window — screen), sexual function/libido, GSM, and the long-game risks the visit should cover: bone (fracture risk assessment) and CV risk factors"],"tx":["VMS first-line for appropriate candidates: systemic HT — TRANSDERMAL estradiol (patch 0.025-0.1 mg; lower VTE/stroke signal than oral, better with obesity/triglycerides/migraine) or oral estradiol 0.5-1 mg; UTERUS PRESENT = progestogen required (micronized progesterone 100 mg qhs — sleep-friendly, favorable breast/CV profile — or LNG-IUD covering both contraception and protection in perimenopause); no uterus = estrogen alone; combined estradiol/bazedoxifene where progestogen intolerance","Dose lowest-effective, reassess annually — but NO arbitrary stop date or forced taper at 65: continuation is individualized risk-benefit; extended use for persistent VMS or bone protection is legitimate with informed consent","Nonhormonal VMS (breast cancer patients, HT-decliners, contraindications): fezolinetant (NK3 antagonist — first-in-class, LFT monitoring) and elinzanetant-era agents; SSRIs/SNRIs (paroxetine 7.5 — but NOT with tamoxifen (CYP2D6); venlafaxine/escitalopram/citalopram), gabapentin (nighttime-predominant symptoms), oxybutynin, CBT and clinical hypnosis (real trial evidence); lifestyle honestly framed (layers/triggers help coping; evidence for supplements — black cohosh, phytoestrogens — is weak; say so kindly)","GSM: LOW-DOSE VAGINAL ESTROGEN (cream, tablet, ring) — safe long-term, minimal absorption, NO progestogen needed, effective for dryness/dyspareunia/recurrent UTI; safe in most breast cancer survivors with oncology collaboration (especially non-AI regimens); alternatives: vaginal moisturizers (scheduled) + lubricants (with intercourse), vaginal DHEA (prasterone), ospemifene; systemic HT does NOT reliably fix GSM — treat locally even when treating systemically","Do NOT: compounded 'bioidentical' pellet mills (unregulated dosing, supraphysiologic levels — the Menopause Society is unambiguous), testosterone except for properly consented hypoactive sexual desire trials at female-physiologic doses, or HT for the sole purpose of preventing chronic disease","The parallel agenda: bone density/fracture assessment (HT prevents bone loss — a legitimate tiebreaker), lipids/BP/diabetes screening, weight-neutral exercise prescription (resistance training for the muscle-bone-metabolism triad), mood treatment when present, and sexual-health conversation the patient was waiting for you to open"],"pearls":["A decade of women were denied effective therapy by a headline — the eligible 52-year-old flushing through meetings deserves the modern risk conversation, not the 2002 one","Vaginal estrogen is to GSM what glasses are to presbyopia: local, safe, and it keeps working only while used — stopping it 'to see' restarts the atrophy","Unscheduled bleeding on HT past the settling-in window is a biopsy conversation, not a dose adjustment","Perimenopause is a mood-disorder vulnerability window — the 48-year-old with new insomnia, rage, and tears may need a PHQ-9 and possibly an SSRI alongside (or instead of) the estradiol conversation"],"refs":[{"t":"The Menopause Society Position Statements","u":"https://menopause.org"},{"t":"ACOG Menopause Management","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Menopause","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507826/"}]},{"id":"fm-prenatal","name":"Prenatal Care Essentials & Preconception Counseling","sec":"fm","present":["Preconception is a primary-care visit disguised as everything else: One Key Question routinely; optimize BEFORE conception — folic acid 400-800 mcg daily (>=1 mo prior; 4 mg with prior NTD or on anticonvulsants), A1c <6.5 in diabetes (malformation risk tracks first-trimester glucose), medication audit (swap ACEi/ARB, statins per nuance, warfarin, isotretinoin, valproate, methotrexate, high-dose topiramate), rubella/varicella immunity (vaccinate BEFORE, not during), treat thyroid disease, substance/alcohol/tobacco cessation, weight and BP groundwork, genetic carrier screening offer, IPV screening","Confirm and date: LMP + first-trimester ultrasound (most accurate dating window); establish care ideally by 8-10 wks; visit rhythm q4wk to 28, q2wk to 36, weekly to delivery (flex with risk and telehealth)","Family physicians deliver prenatal care and in many settings deliveries — know your scope and your consult map (OB, MFM) cold"],"dx":["Initial panel: blood type + antibody screen (Rh planning), CBC, rubella/varicella immunity, HBsAg, HIV, syphilis (repeat third trimester — congenital syphilis is surging), urine culture (treat asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy — the exception that proves the rule), chlamydia/gonorrhea per age/risk, pap if due, hemoglobinopathy screen per background, early A1c/glucose if risk factors; offer aneuploidy screening: cell-free DNA from 10 wks (best screen; it is a SCREEN — positives get diagnostic CVS/amnio counseling) or serum screening","Scheduled milestones: 18-22 wk anatomy ultrasound; 24-28 wk — 1-h 50 g glucose challenge (>=130-140 -> 3-h OGTT) for GDM, repeat CBC, antibody screen + RhoGAM 300 mcg at 28 wks for Rh-negative (and after any bleeding/trauma/procedure); 36-37 wk — GBS rectovaginal culture (intrapartum penicillin if positive); serial fundal heights, BP + urine protein attention EVERY visit (the preeclampsia radar), fetal movement counseling third trimester","Risk-stratify for preeclampsia EARLY: prior preeclampsia, CKD, chronic HTN, diabetes, autoimmune disease, multifetal — one high-risk or >=2 moderate (nulliparity, obesity, age >=35, family hx, sociodemographic risk) -> LOW-DOSE ASPIRIN 81-162 mg starting 12-16 wks (USPSTF/ACOG — the most underprescribed intervention in obstetrics)","Red flags by trimester drilled into every visit's safety-netting: first — bleeding + pain (ectopic until located: quantitative hCG + TVUS pathway), hyperemesis; second/third — preeclampsia symptoms (headache unrelieved, visual changes, RUQ pain, edema + BP >=140/90 -> same-day evaluation; >=160/110 = emergency), decreased fetal movement (kick-count -> same-day testing), preterm labor signs, PPROM, vaginal bleeding (previa/abruption — no digital exam until placenta located)"],"tx":["Universal prescriptions: prenatal vitamin with folate + iron (screen and treat anemia by trimester-specific cutoffs; IV iron for second/third-trimester significant deficiency), vitamin D per level, DHA reasonable; nausea ladder — pyridoxine 10-25 TID +/- doxylamine (first-line, effective, safe) -> ondansetron conversation with balanced counseling; heartburn/constipation managed aggressively (they're not trivial to the patient)","Vaccinate every pregnancy: influenza (any trimester), COVID per guidance, Tdap 27-36 wks (pertussis cocooning via transplacental antibody), RSV — abrysvo 32-36 wks (Sep-Jan) OR plan infant nirsevimab; NO live vaccines (MMR/varicella postpartum)","Medication management courage: TREAT depression (sertraline-anchored risk-benefit — untreated perinatal depression harms two patients), continue needed asthma/epilepsy/thyroid regimens with dose vigilance (levothyroxine UP ~30% immediately), labetalol/nifedipine for chronic HTN (target <140/90 per CHAP trial era), insulin or (per shared decision) metformin for GDM failing nutrition therapy — and glyburide has fallen from favor","GDM owned in primary care where scoped: medical nutrition therapy + glucose self-monitoring (fasting <95, 1-h post <140), escalate to pharmacotherapy for persistent exceedances, growth surveillance, 4-12 wk postpartum OGTT then LIFELONG diabetes screening q1-3y (GDM = the great T2DM prophecy — don't lose these patients after delivery)","Consult/refer (MFM or OB per local model): chronic disease pregnancies (pregestational diabetes, CKD, cardiac, lupus), prior preterm birth (progesterone/cerclage discussions), multiples, abnormal placentation, abnormal serum/cfDNA screening, growth restriction, cholestasis (pruritus + bile acids), any hypertensive disorder — co-manage with clear escalation lines","Fourth-trimester care is the family doctor's home turf: postpartum visit(s) within 3 wks + comprehensive by 12 wks — depression screening (EPDS), BP checks after hypertensive disorders (readmission window), contraception (immediate LARC access), lactation support, and translating pregnancy complications (preeclampsia, GDM) into the LIFELONG cardiovascular/metabolic risk register they truly are"],"pearls":["The aspirin decision happens at the FIRST prenatal visit or it happens too late — screen every intake against the preeclampsia risk list like it's an allergy check","Pregnancy is a stress test the chart remembers: preeclampsia and GDM are cardiology and endocrine referrals mailed 20 years into the future — put them on the problem list permanently","An anxious call about decreased fetal movement is never triaged to 'drink juice and call back' — it's same-day evaluation, every time","Third-trimester syphilis rescreening is the two-dollar test currently preventing a congenital-syphilis epidemic from being worse — don't skip it because the first one was negative"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Prenatal Care Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"USPSTF Aspirin for Preeclampsia Prevention","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"AAFP Maternity Care Resources","u":"https://www.aafp.org"}]},{"id":"fm-mens-health","name":"Erectile Dysfunction & Men's Health","sec":"fm","present":["ED: persistent inability to achieve/maintain erection sufficient for satisfactory activity — quantify (IIEF-5/SHIM), onset pattern (gradual organic vs abrupt/situational psychogenic — preserved morning erections point situational), and hear it as VASCULAR NEWS: ED precedes coronary events by ~3-5 yrs (shared endothelium, smaller arteries fail first)","The men's-health visit ED opens: cardiovascular risk inventory, diabetes/lipids/BP screening, testosterone-deficiency symptoms (libido loss, fatigue, depression — distinct from ED itself), LUTS, sleep apnea, alcohol/tobacco/anabolic steroid and recreational drug use, relationship/mental health, and the med list (SSRIs, thiazides, beta-blockers, finasteride, opioids — iatrogenic ED is common)","Screen the screening gaps men bring: many haven't seen a doctor in years — the ED visit is the USPSTF catch-up visit (BP, lipids, diabetes, colorectal, AAA in ever-smokers 65-75, depression)"],"dx":["Focused workup (AUA): morning TOTAL testosterone (repeat if low, add LH/prolactin if confirmed — secondary pattern gets pituitary thinking), A1c/glucose, lipids; exam — BP, BMI, genital exam, femoral/peripheral pulses; NO routine imaging or nocturnal tumescence testing","Cardiovascular triage before treating (Princeton framing): low-risk (most) — treat; intermediate — stress evaluation first; the exercise-capacity proxy: climbing 2 flights briskly without symptoms ~= sexual-activity METs","Testosterone deficiency diagnosis requires BOTH consistent symptoms AND unequivocally low morning levels x2 (<~300 ng/dL lab-dependent) — do not treat a number without symptoms or symptoms without a number; screen for secondary causes: obesity/OSA (the big reversible), opioids, hyperprolactinemia, hemochromatosis","Psychogenic/mixed contributors screened respectfully: performance anxiety, depression (bidirectional), relationship dynamics, porn-related expectations in younger men — abrupt-onset ED in a healthy 28-year-old is rarely arterial"],"tx":["Foundation that actually reverses trajectory: exercise (aerobic improves erectile scores comparably to PDE5 monotherapy in trials), weight loss, diabetes/BP/lipid optimization, smoking cessation, alcohol moderation, CPAP for OSA, med substitutions where feasible (thiazide/beta-blocker -> ARB/CCB conversations; SSRI -> bupropion when appropriate)","PDE5 inhibitors first-line: sildenafil 50 -> 100 mg (empty stomach, 30-60 min lead, effect ~4-6 h) or tadalafil 10 -> 20 mg PRN (36-h window; food-independent) or tadalafil 5 mg DAILY (spontaneity + treats coexisting LUTS/BPH — the twofer); counsel to try adequate dose >=6-8 attempts before calling failure; side effects: headache, flushing, dyspepsia, nasal congestion, rare priapism/vision (NAION warning)","ABSOLUTE contraindication: nitrates (any form, including PRN sublingual — the med-rec question that prevents the hypotensive disaster); caution stacking with alpha-blockers (dose-separate, prefer tamsulosin + tadalafil-daily patterns)","PDE5 failure pathway: verify use/timing/dose, address testosterone if truly low, then urology: vacuum erection devices, intracavernosal alprostadil injections (most effective medical therapy), intraurethral alprostadil, penile prosthesis (highest satisfaction of any ED treatment at the end of the road)","Testosterone therapy ONLY for confirmed hypogonadism: gels/injections/patches with monitoring (levels, hematocrit — the common stopping reason, PSA/DRE per shared decision >=40-50s); TRAVERSE reassured on CV events; testosterone does NOT treat ED with normal levels and SUPPRESSES fertility (the young man wanting children gets hCG/alternatives via endocrine-urology, never straight testosterone); anabolic-steroid history changes the whole axis conversation","Premature ejaculation (commonly comorbid, rarely volunteered): behavioral techniques + dapoxetine-style on-demand SSRIs where available/off-label daily paroxetine-sertraline, topical anesthetics; Peyronie's (curvature + pain): reassure early-phase, refer for progressive/severe — and ask, because he won't mention it"],"pearls":["New ED in a 55-year-old is a cardiology consult the patient booked without knowing it — calculate the ASCVD risk before writing the sildenafil","The nitrate question is asked out loud at EVERY PDE5 refill, not just the first — the NSTEMI he had last spring changed the answer","Tadalafil 5 mg daily quietly treats the ED and the nocturia in one script — the most elegant twofer in the formulary","Testosterone marketed as an energy supplement has created a generation of iatrogenic infertility — check what the 'clinic' he mentions actually prescribed"],"refs":[{"t":"AUA Erectile Dysfunction Guideline","u":"https://www.auanet.org"},{"t":"Endocrine Society Testosterone Therapy Guideline","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Erectile Dysfunction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562253/"}]},{"id":"fm-bph","name":"BPH & LUTS","sec":"fm","present":["LUTS split into storage (frequency, urgency, nocturia — bother lives here) and voiding (weak stream, hesitancy, straining, intermittency, incomplete emptying); quantify with IPSS/AUA-SI (0-7 mild, 8-19 moderate, 20-35 severe + the bother question that actually drives treatment)","Not all male LUTS is prostate: nocturia alone -> think nocturnal polyuria (evening fluids, caffeine/alcohol, diuretic timing, CHF, OSA, poorly controlled DM); storage-predominant -> overactive bladder; new + pain/hematuria -> different workups entirely","Red flags rerouting the visit: hematuria (gross -> urology, micro -> risk-stratified workup), recurrent UTIs, retention symptoms, incontinence, neuro disease (parkinsonism, MS, cord issues), prior urethral trauma/instrumentation (stricture), abnormal DRE"],"dx":["Core evaluation: history + IPSS + focused exam with DRE (size estimate, nodules, tone), urinalysis (hematuria/infection/glucose); PSA discussion when it changes management (10+ yr horizon, informs prostate volume/progression risk — and see the separate shared-decision screening conversation); frequency-volume chart / bladder diary for nocturia or storage-dominant pictures (the diary diagnoses nocturnal polyuria in minutes)","NOT routine: cystoscopy, urodynamics, upper-tract imaging — reserve for hematuria, retention, prior surgery, neuro suspicion, or presurgical planning; post-void residual when retention/overflow suspected or before/with antimuscarinic decisions","Medication autopsy: decongestants and anticholinergics precipitate retention; diuretic timing manufactures nocturia; opioids constipate the pelvis into dysfunction","Progression risk factors worth naming: larger prostate, higher PSA, older age — flags for who benefits most from 5-ARI strategy and who fails watchful waiting"],"tx":["Mild symptoms/low bother: watchful waiting + behavioral therapy that genuinely works — evening fluid restriction, caffeine/alcohol reduction, timed/double voiding, constipation treatment, diuretic timing shift to morning/mid-day, bladder retraining for urgency","Moderate-severe bother pharmacotherapy: alpha-blocker first-line for speed (days-weeks) — tamsulosin 0.4 mg qhs or silodosin (uroselective; ejaculatory dysfunction trade-off) vs alfuzosin (less ejaculatory effect) vs doxazosin/terazosin (BP-drop legacy agents — orthostasis counseling); THE cataract flag: intraoperative floppy iris syndrome — patients tell their eye surgeon about tamsulosin before surgery, forever","Add (or start) 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor for LARGER prostates (>30-40 g or PSA >1.5): finasteride/dutasteride — shrinks gland, reduces retention and surgery risk (MTOPS/CombAT for combination), but 6-12 mo to work, halves PSA (DOUBLE the on-treatment PSA for screening interpretation — write it in the chart), sexual side effects and persistent-symptom counseling honestly done","Storage-symptom overlays: add beta-3 agonist (mirabegron — BP check; vibegron) preferred over antimuscarinics in older men (anticholinergic burden/cognition; check PVR first either way); nocturnal polyuria proven on diary -> behavioral first, desmopressin only with sodium-monitoring caution in the appropriately young-ish; tadalafil 5 mg daily treats LUTS + ED together","Escalate to urology: retention/catheter dependence, refractory symptoms on combination therapy, recurrent UTIs/bladder stones, renal insufficiency from obstruction, hematuria, or patient preference for procedure — the menu now runs from office MISTs (UroLift, Rezum steam) through TURP (still the reference standard), HoLEP (big glands), aquablation, to prostate artery embolization: bother-driven shared decisions, sexual-function preservation profiles differ and patients care","Retention basics before the referral: catheterize, treat reversible contributors (meds, constipation, UTI), alpha-blocker >=3 d before trial of void"],"pearls":["The bladder diary is the highest-yield 'test' in LUTS — the man with nocturia x4 who makes 1.8 L overnight has a fluid-and-heart problem no prostate procedure fixes","Double the PSA of every man on finasteride before you interpret it — the 'reassuring 1.9' may be a very real 3.8","Tamsulosin plus tomorrow's cataract surgery equals floppy iris — the ophthalmology question belongs in your med-counseling script","An anticholinergic for overactive bladder in an 80-year-old with an unchecked post-void residual is a retention consult you scheduled yourself — PVR first, mirabegron preferred"],"refs":[{"t":"AUA BPH/LUTS Guideline","u":"https://www.auanet.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558920/"}]},{"id":"fm-skin","name":"Skin Complaints in Primary Care (Rashes, Lesion Triage, When to Biopsy/Refer)","sec":"fm","present":["Describe before you diagnose — primary morphology (macule, papule, plaque, vesicle, pustule, nodule), configuration, distribution, and evolution narrow most rashes fast; the primary-care top hits: dermatitis family (atopic, contact, seborrheic), tinea, acne/rosacea, psoriasis, urticaria, drug eruptions, viral exanthems, and 'is this spot cancer'","Lesion triage language: ABCDE for melanoma (Asymmetry, Border, Color variegation, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving) PLUS the ugly-duckling sign (the nevus that doesn't match its siblings) and the EFG of nodular melanoma (Elevated, Firm, Growing — the ABCDE-evading killer)","The red-flag rash list that leaves clinic tonight: febrile + petechiae/purpura, mucosal involvement + skin pain/dusky targets (SJS/TEN), rapidly progressive pain out of proportion (necrotizing infection), erythroderma, DRESS suspicion (rash + fever + facial edema + eosinophilia 2-8 wks after a new drug — classically anticonvulsants, allopurinol, sulfa)"],"dx":["Cheap bedside wins: KOH prep for scale (tinea/candida — SCRAPE AND PROVE before committing to months of therapy or any steroid), dermoscopy if trained (halves unnecessary excisions), skin scraping for scabies (burrows: web spaces, wrists, genitals — itch worse at night, treat empirically when story is classic and household itches)","Biopsy decision rules: shave for suspected BCC/SCC (pearly telangiectatic papule; hyperkeratotic tender papule on sun-damage) — but for ANY lesion where melanoma is in the differential: narrow-margin EXCISIONAL/full-thickness biopsy (never a superficial shave through a possible melanoma — Breslow depth IS the prognosis and the surgery plan; partial sampling only when lesion size/site demands, taking the thickest part)","Punch biopsy for inflammatory dermatoses you can't name (send in formalin; add a perilesional DIF specimen when blistering disease suspected); bacterial culture blisters/pustules that look impetiginized; viral PCR for atypical vesicles","When morphology and response don't match the label, re-scrape and rethink: 'eczema failing steroids' is tinea incognito, scabies, contact allergy to the treatment, CTCL (fixed patches in bathing-trunk distribution over years), or psoriasis all the time"],"tx":["Topical steroid literacy is the family-medicine superpower: match potency to site and disease (low — face/folds/genitals: hydrocortisone 2.5, desonide; medium — trunk/limbs: triamcinolone 0.1; high/superpotent — palms/soles/thick plaques/lichen simplex: clobetasol), ointments beat creams for penetration/dry lesions, TWO-week course then reassess, fingertip-unit dosing taught explicitly, steroid-sparing (tacrolimus/pimecrolimus, crisaborole) for face/folds/maintenance — and undertreatment (pea-sized dabs of hydrocortisone 1% for flaring atopic dermatitis) fails more patients than side effects ever will","The pattern prescriptions: atopic dermatitis — soak-and-seal moisturizing as DRUG not suggestion, appropriate-potency steroid bursts, bleach baths/mupirocin for the impetiginized, dupilumab-era referral for the uncontrolled; tinea corporis/pedis — topical azole/terbinafine 2-4 wks (NEVER combination steroid-antifungals — clotrimazole-betamethasone breeds tinea incognito); tinea capitis/onychomycosis — ORAL terbinafine (confirm first: KOH/culture/PAS nail clipping); acne — ladder from benzoyl peroxide + topical retinoid through doxycycline (always WITH topicals, 3-4 mo limit) to isotretinoin referral (and treat acne seriously — scarring is permanent, iPLEDGE for the childbearing-capable); psoriasis — potent topical + vitamin D analog, mind the joints (ask about inflammatory back/joint pain — PsA screening is the primary-care job), biologic-era referral for >3-5% BSA or QoL failure","Urticaria: second-gen antihistamine UP TO FOURFOLD dosing before anything exotic, hunt drug/infection triggers, NO oral steroids for routine chronic urticaria, epinephrine plan when any airway/anaphylaxis feature ever; drug eruptions — stop the culprit, document the allergy PROPERLY (reaction type and date, not just the drug name)","Lesion management in-scope: cryotherapy for actinic keratoses (field disease -> 5-FU/imiquimod courses) and warts; excision/ED&C for low-risk BCC/SCC per training and site (respect the H-zone face — Mohs referral territory: recurrent, ill-defined, >6 mm high-risk sites, aggressive histology); seborrheic keratoses reassured (sudden eruptive crops with weight loss = Leser-Trelat, a different conversation)","Refer dermatology: any lesion you'd call melanoma-possible without excisional capability TODAY (and expedite — melanoma referrals are tracked in weeks), diagnostic uncertainty after biopsy, blistering disease, erythroderma, severe drug reactions (with the ED), CTCL suspicion, biologic-candidacy psoriasis/HS/atopic disease, isotretinoin, pediatric derm puzzles","Sun counseling with numbers: daily SPF 30+ broad-spectrum, the tanning-bed conversation with every teen (class 1 carcinogen), and self-exam teaching for the high-risk (prior skin cancer, >50 nevi, family melanoma, immunosuppressed — transplant patients get formal derm surveillance)"],"pearls":["Scrape before you steroid — the KOH you skip is the tinea incognito you create, now larger, weirder, and steroid-fed","Never shave what might be melanoma: the biopsy that transects the base deletes the Breslow depth and with it the entire staging conversation","The 'spider bite' that recurs is MRSA, the 'eczema' on one shin for two years is stasis or Bowen's, and the 'stubborn dandruff' in an infant plus petechiae is a referral — pattern-breaks are the diagnosis","A fixed itchy patch that has ignored three steroid courses across two years has earned a punch biopsy, not a fourth cream"],"refs":[{"t":"AAD Clinical Guidelines","u":"https://www.aad.org"},{"t":"AAFP: Common Skin Conditions","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Skin Biopsy Techniques","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470457/"}]},{"id":"fm-geriatric","name":"Geriatric Assessment (Falls, Polypharmacy, Cognition, Driving)","sec":"fm","present":["The geriatric visit runs on function, not diagnoses: ADLs (bathing, dressing, toileting, transfers, continence, feeding) and IADLs (finances, meds, cooking, transport, phone) — IADL slippage is the early-warning system; the 5 M's frame: Mind, Mobility, Medications, Multicomplexity, what Matters most","Screen falls ANNUALLY (ask: fallen in the past year? unsteady? fear of falling?) — any yes triggers assessment; a fall is a syndrome-presenting-event: meds, orthostasis, vision, feet/footwear, strength/balance, home hazards, alcohol, cognition, and the cardiac/neuro causes hiding among them","Cognitive concern arrives sideways — missed bills, repeated questions, med errors, the worried daughter in the room; distinguish delirium (acute, fluctuating, inattention — workup NOW), depression (pseudodementia), and dementia trajectory; hearing loss masquerades as and accelerates cognitive decline — check it"],"dx":["Falls objective battery: Timed Up and Go (>12-15 s = risk), 30-second chair stand, orthostatic vitals done properly, visual acuity, feet examined, gait watched down an actual hallway; DEXA/fracture-risk pairing (the fall assessment and the osteoporosis assessment are the same conversation)","Cognition: Mini-Cog (3-word recall + clock) as the fast screen, MoCA when characterizing (adjust expectations for education), collateral history mandatory; positive screen workup: CBC, CMP, TSH, B12, +/- structural imaging per presentation, depression screen (GDS), sleep/OSA and alcohol honesty, med-list-as-suspect (anticholinergics, benzos, opioids, hypoglycemics)","Polypharmacy audit EVERY visit past 4-5 meds: Beers/STOPP-START screens — the recurring offenders: benzodiazepines/Z-drugs, anticholinergics (diphenhydramine, oxybutynin, TCAs — cumulative cognitive burden), sulfonylureas (glyburide), NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, PPIs-without-indication, duplicate anticoagulant-antiplatelet stacking, and the prescribing cascade (the amlodipine edema treated with furosemide treated with potassium)","Screen the silent load-bearers: hearing (whisper test/audiometry referral — hearing aids are cognition-protective per ACHIEVE-era evidence), vision, continence (never volunteered), nutrition/weight loss (>=5% = workup), mood, loneliness/elder mistreatment (ask alone), advance-care-planning status, and caregiver strain (the second patient in the room)"],"tx":["Falls prescription with actual evidence: strength + balance EXERCISE (PT referral, tai chi, Otago — the single best-proven intervention), medication deprescribing (especially psychotropics), cataract surgery when due, single-lens glasses for outdoor walkers, home safety evaluation (OT), footwear counseling, vitamin D if deficient/high-risk (routine supplementation alone no longer carries the day), and treat the orthostasis (rehydrate, deprescribe, slow position changes, compression)","Deprescribing as active therapy: one target per visit, taper what needs tapering (benzos glacially with support), align every med with a current indication and the patient's time-to-benefit horizon (the statin conversation differs at 92 with frailty vs 72 training for a 10K); loop in the pharmacist — med-reconciliation-plus-review visits are billable and effective","Dementia diagnosed: name it honestly (the diagnosis unlocks planning while capacity permits), structure and routine, treat reversible amplifiers (hearing aids, depression, OSA), cholinesterase inhibitor (donepezil — realistic-benefit framing, GI/bradycardia counseling) +/- memantine moderate-severe; behavioral symptoms — nonpharm FIRST always (identify triggers: pain, constipation, UTI-not-really, boredom, overstimulation); antipsychotics only for danger/distress refractory to everything, lowest-dose-shortest-time with the mortality boxed-warning conversation documented; anti-amyloid era (lecanemab/donanemab) = early-AD neurology referral pathway with eligibility and ARIA realism","Driving: state the concern directly, structured assessment (vision, cognition incl clock/trails, physical function), occupational-therapy driving evaluation as the neutral arbiter, know your state's reporting rules — and pair every retirement-from-driving conversation with a transportation plan (the isolation you cause is a morbidity too)","Care architecture: advance care planning EARLY and revisited (healthcare proxy, POLST when appropriate, what-matters conversations before the crisis), fall-risk-to-fracture-prevention bridge (treat the osteoporosis), immunization sweep (flu, COVID, RSV, pneumococcal, shingles, Tdap), and know the escalation menu — home health, adult day programs, PACE, respite, palliative care referral for symptom/goal complexity at ANY prognosis (not just hospice timing)","The annual wellness visit is the scaffold that pays for all of this — use its structured health-risk assessment to systematize what ad-hoc visits drop"],"pearls":["The medication list is the most modifiable organ in geriatrics — the best new prescription is often a deletion","'She's just getting old' is where delirium, depression, B12, hypothyroidism, and hearing loss go to hide — age explains wrinkles, not new confusion","Ask 'what does a typical day look like?' — it screens function, cognition, mood, and isolation in one open question no checklist matches","Every fall visit that ends without an exercise referral and a med review treated the bruise and reloaded the gun"],"refs":[{"t":"AGS Beers Criteria & Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.americangeriatrics.org"},{"t":"CDC STEADI Falls Prevention","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/steadi"},{"t":"StatPearls: Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559042/"}]},{"id":"fm-chronic-pain","name":"Chronic Pain Management & Opioid Stewardship in Primary Care","sec":"fm","present":["Chronic pain (>3 mo) reframed at the first visit: a disease of the pain system, not a 10-point score to zero out — assess with function-forward tools (PEG: Pain intensity, Enjoyment, General activity), pain phenotype (nociceptive vs neuropathic — burning/shooting/allodynia, DN4/painDETECT — vs nociplastic/central sensitization: widespread, sleep-wrecked, fatigue, fibromyalgia-pattern), and the amplifiers: depression, anxiety, PTSD, insomnia, catastrophizing, deconditioning, litigation/work context","Set goals in verbs: walk the dog, return to work modified, sleep through — 30% pain reduction + functional gain = success; the zero-pain promise is the setup for the opioid escalation spiral","Inherited-patient triage (the real-world Monday): the new patient on chronic opioids gets continuity, PDMP review, honest risk assessment, and a plan — not a reflexive refusal (abandonment/forced-taper harms are documented: withdrawal, illicit transition, suicide risk) and not autopilot refills either"],"dx":["Diagnose the generator when there is one (the OA, the radiculopathy, the neuropathy — see their entries) and name nociplastic pain when there isn't (fibromyalgia: widespread pain index + symptom severity, exam without synovitis, normal-ish labs — CBC, CMP, TSH, +/- CRP; do NOT serially image or ANA-fish without signals)","Risk-stratify BEFORE any opioid: ORT/DIRE-style assessment — personal/family SUD history, psychiatric comorbidity, age, benzodiazepine use, prior overdose; check the PDMP (mandated most states) and baseline UDS with the interpretation humility it demands (know what your immunoassay misses and false-flags — confirm surprises with GC-MS before any confrontation)","Sleep and mood are diagnostic targets, not footnotes: OSA screening (opioids + OSA is a respiratory-depression multiplier), PHQ-9/GAD-7 tracked — treating the depression is treating the pain","Reassess function serially (PEG each visit): 'no functional change on stable high-dose opioids for years' is itself a finding that argues the therapy isn't working"],"tx":["Multimodal foundation for every phenotype: movement therapy dosed like a drug (graded exercise, PT with pacing — the flare-then-quit cycle broken by pacing education), CBT for pain/ACT (real effect sizes; digital versions count), sleep repair (CBT-I), weight optimization when load-bearing joints, and treat the psychiatric comorbidity in parallel not sequence","Match pharmacology to phenotype: neuropathic — duloxetine/venlafaxine, gabapentin/pregabalin (titrate, renal-dose, misuse-awareness, taper-don't-stop), TCAs (nortriptyline over amitriptyline in the older), topicals (lidocaine 5%, capsaicin 8% referral) for focal; nociplastic/fibromyalgia — duloxetine, milnacipran, pregabalin, amitriptyline low-dose, and EXERCISE as the anchor (opioids actively contraindicated — worsen central sensitization); nociceptive — the OA/back-pain toolkits (topical then oral NSAIDs with risk triage, duloxetine)","Opioids (CDC 2022 — flexible clinical guideline, not the misapplied 2016 hard caps): for selected chronic non-cancer pain ONLY after nonopioid multimodal failure, with expected-benefits-realistic conversation; START low immediate-release, avoid initiating long-acting, avoid opioid+benzo co-prescribing, co-prescribe NALOXONE (>=50 MME, any co-sedative, SUD history, prior overdose — and honestly, offer broadly), agreement + PDMP + periodic UDS as safety structure (framed as monitoring like an INR, not suspicion), reassess within 1-4 wks of any start/escalation, and the >=50/>=90 MME thresholds are reassessment tripwires not cliffs","Tapering ONLY collaboratively and slowly (5-10%/month or slower for years-long therapy) when risks outweigh benefits or the patient opts in — with scheduled support, withdrawal management, mood surveillance, and NO patient abandonment; if opioid use disorder criteria emerge (loss of control, craving, use despite harm — distinguish from physical dependence which is expected pharmacology): pivot WITH the patient to buprenorphine — the same prescriber can and should","Escalation menu beyond the clinic: interventional (epidural steroid for radicular pain — short-term; medial branch blocks/RFA for facet-pattern axial pain; joint injections), pain psychology, multidisciplinary pain programs (best evidence, worst access — refer anyway), spinal cord stimulation via pain specialty for refractory neuropathic/CRPS phenotypes with honest-evidence counseling, and palliative-care co-management for serious-illness pain","Document like it matters (it does): indication, function scores, risk mitigation, PDMP checks, and the shared plan — good records protect good care in a scrutinized space"],"pearls":["Track function, not just pain scores — the patient whose PEG improves at unchanged pain intensity is succeeding, and the reverse is your signal to change course","Physical dependence is physiology; addiction is loss of control — confusing them either denies real OUD treatment or insults a stable patient into leaving care","The forced rapid taper is not stewardship, it's risk transfer — the same milligrams matter less than the alliance, the pace, and the destination","Fibromyalgia treated with escalating oxycodone gets predictably worse — naming nociplastic pain correctly is the treatment decision"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids (2022)","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/opioids"},{"t":"HHS Pain Management Best Practices","u":"https://www.hhs.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Pain Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553030/"}]},{"id":"fm-fatigue","name":"Fatigue & Unexplained Symptoms (Structured Workup)","sec":"fm","present":["Characterize before chasing: fatigue vs sleepiness (falls asleep — think sleep disorder) vs weakness (objective — think neuromuscular) vs dyspnea/exertional intolerance; duration (recent <1 mo, prolonged 1-6, chronic >6), pattern (unrefreshing sleep? post-exertional crash?), and the life context asked without prejudice (new baby, three jobs, caregiving, grief)","The history IS the test: sleep quantity/quality/apnea symptoms, mood (depression is the most common identifiable cause), medications (beta-blockers, antihistamines, benzos, statin-myalgia question, opioids), substances (alcohol quantified, cannabis, stimulant crash), red flags (weight loss, fever, night sweats, lymphadenopathy, bleeding, focal anything), and infection timeline (post-viral onset is a real and respectable history point)","Unexplained-symptom presentations broadly (fatigue, dizziness, pain, palpitations combos): common, disabling, and worsened by both under-workup (missed disease) and over-workup (cascade of incidentalomas + the message 'we found nothing' = 'you have nothing')"],"dx":["Tier-1 labs for prolonged fatigue (deliberately finite): CBC, CMP, TSH, glucose/A1c, ESR or CRP, ferritin (iron deficiency WITHOUT anemia causes fatigue — treat <30, consider <50 with symptoms), UA, pregnancy when applicable, +/- HIV and hepatitis C (screen-eligible anyway), B12, vitamin D (test-and-treat honesty: replete deficiency, don't promise miracles), celiac serology when GI/iron threads exist","Deploy the targeted tools, not the shotgun: Epworth + STOP-BANG -> sleep study (OSA is the most missed organic cause in the obese-snoring-hypertensive phenotype), PHQ-9/GAD-7 always (presented as routine vitals, not accusation), 2-week sleep/activity diary, orthostatic vitals + standing test when dizzy/tachycardic threads (POTS: HR rise >=30 within 10 min upright, symptoms, no hypotension — commonly post-viral)","NO tier-2 fishing without hooks: ANA, Lyme serology (without exposure/objective signs), cortisol, 'adrenal fatigue' panels (not a diagnosis), heavy metals, chronic EBV titers (everyone's positive) — each generates false-positive cascades; tier-2 is EARNED by specific findings (synovitis -> RF/CCP; proximal weakness -> CK; lymphadenopathy -> imaging/biopsy pathway)","ME/CFS recognized by criteria, not exclusion-exhaustion (IOM 2015): >=6 mo substantial function reduction + profound fatigue not relieved by rest + POST-EXERTIONAL MALAISE (the pathognomonic crash 12-48 h after exertion) + unrefreshing sleep, plus cognitive impairment or orthostatic intolerance — overlaps heavily with long COVID phenotypes; diagnose it, name it, and stop the annual re-fishing"],"tx":["Treat what tier-1 found (the satisfying minority): the OSA, the iron, the hypothyroidism, the depression, the alcohol, the beta-blocker dose; and treat the sleep even when it isn't 'the cause' — CBT-I improves fatigue across diagnoses","For unexplained/functional presentations, the consultation IS the intervention: validate explicitly ('your symptoms are real; normal tests mean we've ruled out the dangerous causes, not that nothing is happening'), give a positive explanatory model (nervous-system sensitization/dysregulation language), schedule REGULAR follow-up decoupled from new symptoms (kills the symptom-ticket dynamic), one clinician quarterbacking, and a firm gentle moratorium on serial re-imaging without new objective findings","Graded activity individualized WITH the crucial exception: in ME/CFS/post-exertional-malaise phenotypes, classic graded exercise therapy can HARM — teach PACING instead (energy envelope, activity diaries, pre-emptive rest), treat orthostatic intolerance (fluids, salt, compression, +/- propranolol/ivabradine-pattern referrals for POTS), symptomatic sleep and pain management, and honest prognosis partnership","Depression/anxiety treatment offered without requiring the patient to accept a psychological CAUSE ('treating this improves the symptom regardless of origin' framing preserves alliance); CBT and mindfulness-based approaches have evidence across functional syndromes — refer as symptom treatment, not verdict","Disability and accommodation paperwork done seriously and honestly — function documentation visits are clinical care for this population; and re-examine annually with fresh eyes: the 'chronic fatigue' patient can still develop the new anemia, the new murmur, the new lymph node (sensitivity stays on even when the fishing stops)","Refer purposefully, not reflexively: sleep medicine (study-positive or high suspicion), rheumatology (objective findings only), cardiology (POTS/exertional red flags), long-COVID/ME-CFS programs where they exist — serial specialty ping-pong without a quarterback is how these patients get lost"],"pearls":["Ferritin of 12 with a normal hemoglobin is a treatable cause of fatigue the CBC just vouched innocent — iron deficiency doesn't wait for anemia to cause symptoms","Ask 'what happens the day after you push yourself?' — post-exertional malaise is one question, and it's the fork between exercise prescription and pacing prescription","'All your tests are normal' lands as dismissal; 'we've ruled out the dangerous causes and here's what I think is happening' lands as care — same data, different medicine","The tier-2 shotgun panel doesn't reassure anyone: the weakly-positive ANA you didn't need becomes the rheumatology referral, the repeat panel, and the year of worry"],"refs":[{"t":"AAFP: Fatigue Evaluation","u":"https://www.aafp.org"},{"t":"CDC ME/CFS Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/me-cfs"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fatigue","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430685/"}]},{"id":"fm-care-transitions","name":"Care Transitions, Chronic Care Management & Social Determinants","sec":"fm","present":["The riskiest procedure in medicine is the handoff: post-discharge is a vulnerability window — ~20% of Medicare discharges readmit within 30 d, roughly half of adults experience a medication discrepancy at transition; the primary-care job is to close the loop the hospital opened","Transition intake reads the whole move: discharge summary obtained (fight for it), diagnosis changes, med changes WITH reasons, pending results flagged at discharge (the classic dropped ball), follow-up appointments actually scheduled, home services ordered vs delivered, functional change (the admission cost muscle and confidence), and the patient's own understanding via teach-back","Social determinants are clinical data: food insecurity (Hunger Vital Sign 2-question), housing instability, transportation (the top no-show driver), utilities, interpersonal safety, health literacy, insurance/pharmacy-cost reality — screen systematically because patients rarely volunteer and clinicians reliably misguess"],"dx":["The post-discharge visit protocol (within 7 d high-risk, 14 d moderate — TCM billing structure exists precisely for this, with the interactive contact within 2 business days): medication RECONCILIATION line-by-line against what's physically in the home (brown-bag or photo review — find the duplicated generics, the 'stopped' drug still being taken, the new drug never filled for cost), symptom trajectory vs discharge, red-flag review specific to the admission (the HF weight, the COPD action plan, the wound), pending-result chase, and advance-care-planning update while the hospitalization is fresh context","Risk-stratify the panel, not just the patient: registries for the chronic-disease populations (diabetes not-at-goal, HF, COPD, CKD), care gaps run proactively (the overdue A1c finds the patient before the ER does), and identify the high-utilizer/complex-needs patients for care-management enrollment (CCM for 2+ chronic conditions — structured between-visit work that Medicare pays for)","Screen SDOH with validated brevity (PRAPARE-style or the CMS accountable-health domains) and — the ethical rule — screen only what you can respond to: build the response map first (food bank contacts, 211, medical-legal partnership, transportation benefits, pharmacy assistance programs, community health workers)","Diagnose the system failures like pathology: the readmission root-caused (was it disease progression, med error, absent support, or an appointment that never existed?), the no-show pattern (transportation? cost? distrust?) — each has a different treatment"],"tx":["Deploy the transition toolkit with evidence behind it: structured med reconciliation + teach-back education, single point-of-contact follow-up, early visit, red-flag action plans in plain language (Coleman CTI and Project RED lineage — transitional-care interventions reduce readmissions when actually staffed); pharmacist co-management for the polypharmacy-heavy; home health ordered with specific goals not vibes","Chronic care management as infrastructure: care plans that live and update (problems, goals, meds, barriers, team members), between-visit protocols (BP cuff uploads, HF weight calls, diabetes titration by standing order), team-based care used to license-top (RN/pharmacist titration protocols, CHW outreach, behavioral health integration/collaborative care for the depression that sinks every other outcome), and the annual wellness visit as the panel-maintenance scaffold","SDOH interventions that are medicine: food-insecurity referral + produce-prescription programs where they exist, medical-legal partnership for the housing/benefits fights, transportation arrangement for the dialysis-and-oncology tier, cost-conscious prescribing as a DEFAULT (generic-first, 90-day fills, price-transparency tools, deprescribe the unaffordable duplicate — the unfilled prescription is a dosing error you caused), interpreter services always (never the grandchild for serious news), and documentation with Z-codes so the burden is visible and resourced","The warm handoff beats the cold referral universally: behavioral health introduced in-room, the specialist office called while the patient sits there, the community health worker met by name — completion rates follow relationships","Continuity itself is the intervention with the strongest evidence in this entire entry: sustained continuity with a personal physician associates with lower mortality, fewer admissions, and lower costs — protect panel continuity in scheduling like the therapeutic asset it is","And close every loop you open: referrals tracked to completed-note-received, results to patient-notified, the task list as patient-safety instrument — 'I assumed the specialist told them' is the transition error in its natural habitat"],"pearls":["The med list in the discharge summary, the pharmacy record, and the kitchen counter are three different documents — reconciliation means all three in one conversation","Ask 'what did they tell you was the reason you were in the hospital?' — the teach-back answer predicts the readmission better than most risk scores","An unaffordable prescription is a prescribing error with extra steps — ask the cost question at the point of care, not the pharmacy counter","Continuity is the quiet superpower of family medicine: the doctor who knows the patient prevents admissions no algorithm can — schedule like you believe it"],"refs":[{"t":"AHRQ Care Transitions Resources","u":"https://www.ahrq.gov"},{"t":"CMS Chronic Care & Transitional Care Management","u":"https://www.cms.gov"},{"t":"AAFP: Social Determinants of Health","u":"https://www.aafp.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-acute-ischemic-stroke","name":"Acute Ischemic Stroke - Thrombolysis & Thrombectomy","sec":"neuro","present":["Sudden focal neurologic deficit (face/arm weakness, speech disturbance - FAST) with a definable last-known-well time; the deficit maps to a vascular territory","Quantify severity with the NIHSS; time is brain - roughly 1.9 million neurons lost per minute of large-vessel occlusion","Screen for stroke mimics (seizure with Todd paralysis, hypoglycemia, migraine aura, functional) but treat time-critically - do not let mimic-hunting cause delay"],"dx":["Emergent noncontrast CT first to EXCLUDE HEMORRHAGE (the gate to thrombolysis); check glucose immediately (hypoglycemia mimics stroke)","CT angiography for large-vessel occlusion (LVO) and CT perfusion (or MRI DWI/PWI mismatch) to select extended-window thrombectomy candidates","Establish last-known-well precisely - it defines the treatment windows; wake-up strokes may qualify via imaging (DWI-FLAIR mismatch, WAKE-UP trial)"],"tx":["IV thrombolysis: alteplase within 4.5 h of onset (tenecteplase increasingly preferred - single bolus, non-inferior/superior in LVO) after excluding hemorrhage and contraindications; lower BP to <185/110 BEFORE lysing and keep <180/105 after","Mechanical thrombectomy for anterior-circulation LVO up to 24 h in selected patients by perfusion imaging (DAWN, DEFUSE-3) - the single biggest advance in stroke care; do NOT withhold lytic while arranging thrombectomy if eligible (bridge)","If not a lytic/thrombectomy candidate: aspirin within 24-48 h (dual antiplatelet for minor stroke - see secondary prevention), permissive hypertension unless treated, stroke-unit admission, and start the etiologic workup"],"pearls":["The noncontrast CT is to rule out blood, not to see the infarct - an early normal CT does not argue against stroke or against giving the lytic","Check a glucose before you call anything a stroke - hypoglycemia is the great mimic and is instantly reversible","Thrombectomy out to 24 h changed the field - a salvageable penumbra on perfusion imaging matters more than the clock alone","Bridge, do not choose: an LVO patient eligible for both gets the lytic AND goes to thrombectomy"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Acute Ischemic Stroke Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Stroke","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535369/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-tia","name":"TIA & Rapid Risk Stratification","sec":"neuro","present":["Transient focal neurologic deficit from ischemia WITHOUT infarction - the modern tissue-based definition (imaging-negative), replacing the old 24-h time rule; symptoms typically resolve within an hour","Common patterns: transient hemiparesis/aphasia (carotid territory), amaurosis fugax (retinal), or brainstem symptoms (posterior circulation)","A TIA is a warning shot - the highest stroke risk is in the first 48 h to days, so it is an URGENT, not routine, evaluation"],"dx":["Risk-stratify with ABCD2 (age, BP, clinical features, duration, diabetes) to triage urgency - but image everyone: MRI (DWI) to detect infarction (reclassifies to stroke) and vessel imaging (carotid/intracranial)","Urgent workup: vessel imaging (CTA/MRA/carotid duplex), cardiac evaluation (ECG, telemetry, echo), and labs (glucose, lipids) - the same etiologic search as ischemic stroke","Distinguish TIA mimics: seizure, migraine aura, syncope, hypoglycemia, functional"],"tx":["Start secondary prevention immediately - do not wait for the full workup: high-intensity statin, BP management, and antithrombotic therapy","High-risk TIA/minor stroke: short-course DUAL antiplatelet therapy (aspirin + clopidogrel for 21 days, then single agent - CHANCE/POINT) reduces early recurrent stroke; ticagrelor+aspirin (THALES) an alternative","Carotid revascularization referral for symptomatic significant stenosis (within 2 weeks); anticoagulate if atrial fibrillation is found"],"pearls":["TIA is defined by tissue now, not time - an hour of symptoms with a DWI lesion is a stroke, and that distinction changes prognosis","The first 48 hours carry the highest stroke risk - a TIA is an emergency workup, not a clinic follow-up","Start the statin and antiplatelet the day of the TIA - secondary prevention does not wait for the etiology","21 days of dual antiplatelet then mono - the CHANCE/POINT window, not indefinite dual therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA TIA/Secondary Prevention Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Transient Ischemic Attack","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459143/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-stroke-prevention","name":"Ischemic Stroke Etiologic Workup & Secondary Prevention","sec":"neuro","present":["After the acute phase, the job is to find WHY the stroke happened and prevent the next one - recurrence risk is highest early and etiology-dependent","Classify by TOAST: large-artery atherosclerosis, cardioembolism, small-vessel (lacunar), other determined cause (dissection, vasculitis, hypercoagulable), and undetermined (incl embolic stroke of undetermined source - ESUS)","The mechanism drives the prevention strategy - antiplatelet vs anticoagulation vs procedure"],"dx":["Vessel imaging (carotid/intracranial - CTA/MRA/duplex), cardiac workup (ECG, telemetry, transthoracic +/- transesophageal echo), and PROLONGED rhythm monitoring for occult atrial fibrillation (CRYSTAL-AF - AF is often paroxysmal and missed on a single ECG)","Labs: lipids, A1c/glucose; targeted hypercoagulable/vasculitis/genetic workup in young or cryptogenic stroke","Assess for a right-to-left shunt (PFO) in young ESUS - PFO closure benefits selected patients (RESPECT/CLOSE)"],"tx":["Non-cardioembolic: antiPLATELET (aspirin, clopidogrel, or aspirin-dipyridamole) + high-intensity statin (SPARCL) + BP control + lifestyle/diabetes management","Cardioembolic (atrial fibrillation): ANTICOAGULATION - DOAC preferred over warfarin for nonvalvular AF; timing after stroke balances hemorrhagic transformation vs recurrence","Symptomatic carotid stenosis: revascularization referral (endarterectomy/stenting); intracranial atherosclerosis: aggressive medical therapy (SAMMPRIS - stenting worse); do not anticoagulate ESUS routinely (NAVIGATE/RE-SPECT ESUS negative)"],"pearls":["The stroke subtype IS the prevention plan - anticoagulate the AF, antiplatelet the atherosclerosis, and do not mix them up","A single ECG misses paroxysmal AF - prolonged monitoring finds the cardioembolic source in cryptogenic stroke","ESUS is not an anticoagulation indication - the trials said DOACs did not beat aspirin without documented AF","High-intensity statin is secondary prevention even with a normal LDL - SPARCL treated the vessel, not just the number"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Secondary Stroke Prevention Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stroke Prevention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470234/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-cvst","name":"Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis","sec":"neuro","present":["Thrombosis of the dural venous sinuses/cortical veins - a stroke type that defies arterial territories; HEADACHE is the most common symptom (often progressive, sometimes thunderclap)","Also seizures, focal deficits, encephalopathy, and signs of raised ICP (papilledema, CN VI palsy); venous infarcts with hemorrhage that cross arterial boundaries are a clue","Risk factors: pregnancy/postpartum, oral contraceptives/estrogen, thrombophilia, dehydration, malignancy, infection (mastoiditis/sinusitis), and (rarely) vaccine-induced immune thrombotic thrombocytopenia"],"dx":["Neuroimaging with dedicated venography: MRV or CT venography demonstrates the clot/filling defect (the empty delta sign on contrast CT); a noncontrast CT may show a dense sinus/cord sign but is often normal","Elevated D-dimer supports but a normal value does not exclude CVST; check for hemorrhagic venous infarction","Workup for the underlying prothrombotic cause (thrombophilia, malignancy, pregnancy-related, drugs); LP if raised ICP threatens vision (also therapeutic)"],"tx":["ANTICOAGULATION is the treatment - and notably it is given even in the presence of associated intracranial HEMORRHAGE (the hemorrhage is venous congestion, and anticoagulation treats the cause)","Start with heparin (LMWH/UFH), then transition to oral anticoagulation (warfarin traditionally; DOACs supported by RE-SPECT CVT for non-cancer, non-pregnant patients) for months","Manage complications: seizures (AED for those who have seized), raised ICP/threatened vision (acetazolamide, LP, rarely CSF diversion or optic nerve sheath fenestration); endovascular thrombectomy for refractory deterioration (selected)"],"pearls":["A headache crossing into seizures, focal signs, or a hemorrhage that ignores arterial territories should trigger a venogram - CVST hides from routine CT","Anticoagulate even when there is hemorrhage - counterintuitive, but the venous congestion is driving the bleed","Postpartum and estrogen use are the classic settings - ask about them in any young person with a new progressive headache","Protect vision: raised ICP from CVST can blind - monitor fundi and treat the pressure"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Cerebral Venous Thrombosis Statement","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Venous Thrombosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459315/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-dissection","name":"Cervical Artery Dissection & Stroke in the Young","sec":"neuro","present":["Carotid or vertebral artery dissection is a leading cause of stroke in younger adults; classic triad (incomplete) of unilateral head/neck/face pain, partial Horner syndrome (carotid), and delayed cerebral/retinal ischemia","Often preceded by minor trauma, neck manipulation, or occurs spontaneously (connective tissue disease - Ehlers-Danlos, fibromuscular dysplasia); vertebral dissection can cause posterior-circulation strokes with neck pain","Consider in any young stroke, especially with neck pain, Horner syndrome, or pulsatile tinnitus"],"dx":["Vessel imaging: CTA or MRA (with fat-saturated T1 showing intramural hematoma/crescent) demonstrates the dissection; catheter angiography for equivocal cases","Broad YOUNG-STROKE workup: cardiac (PFO/shunt, echo), prolonged rhythm monitoring, hypercoagulable and vasculitis panels, toxicology (cocaine/amphetamines), and consider RCVS, CADASIL, and vasculopathies","Screen for connective tissue disease features and fibromuscular dysplasia"],"tx":["Antithrombotic therapy to prevent thromboembolic stroke - either antiplatelet OR anticoagulation (CADISS trial found no significant difference in stroke prevention); many favor antiplatelet, individualized by stroke burden and imaging","Acute ischemic stroke from dissection can still receive IV thrombolysis/thrombectomy per standard eligibility","Most dissections heal over 3-6 months - re-image and reassess antithrombotic duration; endovascular/surgical treatment reserved for refractory ischemia or pseudoaneurysm"],"pearls":["Neck pain plus a partial Horner syndrome plus later ischemia in a young person is carotid dissection until imaging says otherwise","CADISS found antiplatelet and anticoagulation roughly equivalent - the choice is individualized, not dogmatic","A young stroke deserves a wide net - PFO, drugs, dissection, hypercoagulable states, and vasculopathies all live here","Fat-saturated MRI showing the intramural hematoma is the imaging signature of dissection"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Stroke in the Young / Dissection Resources","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Artery Dissection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430835/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-first-seizure","name":"First Unprovoked Seizure & Epilepsy Diagnosis","sec":"neuro","present":["Distinguish PROVOKED/acute symptomatic seizures (from a reversible insult - hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, alcohol withdrawal, drugs, acute stroke/infection) from UNPROVOKED seizures (no immediate trigger)","A convincing history separates seizure from mimics: aura, tongue-lateral biting, postictal confusion, and gradual recovery favor seizure; abrupt recovery and situational triggers favor syncope","Epilepsy is a disease of recurrent unprovoked seizures - a first seizure is a diagnostic and risk-assessment problem, not automatically epilepsy"],"dx":["Epilepsy (ILAE definition) = >=2 unprovoked seizures >24 h apart, OR one unprovoked seizure with a >=60% recurrence risk (e.g., an epileptogenic lesion on MRI or clearly epileptiform EEG), OR an epilepsy syndrome","Workup of a first seizure: EEG (epileptiform discharges raise recurrence risk), brain MRI (structural lesion), and labs/toxicology to exclude provoked causes; consider LP if infection suspected","Characterize seizure type (focal vs generalized onset) and screen for a syndrome - it guides drug choice and prognosis"],"tx":["After a FIRST unprovoked seizure, starting an antiseizure medication reduces near-term recurrence but does not change long-term prognosis - shared decision based on recurrence risk (abnormal EEG/MRI, nocturnal, focal)","Treat provoked seizures by fixing the cause (correct sodium/glucose, manage withdrawal) - long-term ASMs usually unnecessary","Counsel on safety and DRIVING restrictions (state-specific reporting/lapse rules), seizure precautions, sleep, and alcohol; document the counseling"],"pearls":["One unprovoked seizure plus an epileptogenic MRI lesion or epileptiform EEG already meets the epilepsy definition - you do not always need two","Provoked seizures are treated by removing the provocation - the sodium and the glucose, not a lifelong ASM","The driving conversation is part of the visit and the chart - know your state's rules and document them","Lateral tongue biting and postictal confusion point to seizure; abrupt full recovery points to syncope"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/AES First Seizure Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Seizure","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430765/"},{"t":"ILAE Resources","u":"https://www.ilae.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-epilepsy-management","name":"Epilepsy - Antiseizure Medication Selection & Management","sec":"neuro","present":["Once epilepsy is diagnosed, drug choice is driven by SEIZURE TYPE/SYNDROME (focal vs generalized), comorbidities, sex/childbearing potential, interactions, and side-effect profile","Goal is seizure freedom with no/minimal side effects on monotherapy where possible; ~2/3 respond to the first or second appropriate drug","Recognize drug resistance early (failure of two appropriate, tolerated ASMs) - it triggers referral to an epilepsy center for surgical/device evaluation"],"dx":["Confirm seizure classification (video-EEG when uncertain) - misclassifying generalized as focal (or vice versa) leads to the wrong drug and worse control","Baseline and monitoring per drug: some need level or lab monitoring; HLA-B*15:02 before carbamazepine in at-risk ancestries (SJS/TEN risk)","Reassess adherence, triggers (sleep deprivation, alcohol), and interactions at every visit"],"tx":["Broad-spectrum agents (levetiracetam, lamotrigine, valproate, topiramate, zonisamide) cover most; focal epilepsy also treated with lamotrigine/levetiracetam/lacosamide/carbamazepine; absence epilepsy - ethosuximide/valproate (NOT sodium-channel blockers, which can worsen generalized seizures)","WOMEN of childbearing potential: AVOID VALPROATE (highest teratogenicity - neural tube defects, lower IQ) when possible; prefer lamotrigine/levetiracetam, give folic acid, and counsel on enzyme-inducer interactions with contraception","Counsel on SUDEP (sudden unexpected death in epilepsy) - adherence and seizure control reduce risk; taper/withdraw ASMs only after a sustained seizure-free interval with shared decision-making"],"pearls":["Sodium-channel blockers can worsen generalized (absence/myoclonic) epilepsy - classify the seizure before you pick the drug","Valproate is a last choice in women who could become pregnant - the teratogenic and neurodevelopmental risk is real and avoidable","Two failed appropriate drugs defines drug resistance - that is a referral, not a third random drug","Talk about SUDEP - patients deserve to know that adherence and control reduce a real risk"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/AES Epilepsy Treatment Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Antiseizure Medications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482269/"},{"t":"ILAE Resources","u":"https://www.ilae.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-status-epilepticus","name":"Status Epilepticus","sec":"neuro","present":["A neurologic emergency: >=5 minutes of continuous seizure OR recurrent seizures without recovery of consciousness between them (operational definition prompting treatment)","Convulsive status is obvious; NONCONVULSIVE status epilepticus presents as unexplained altered mental status/coma and requires EEG to detect - suspect it in the patient who does not wake up","Prolonged seizures cause neuronal injury and become harder to stop over time - treat fast and escalate on a clock"],"dx":["Simultaneous treatment and workup: fingerstick glucose, electrolytes (Na, Ca, Mg), toxicology, ASM levels, and consider infection/structural causes (CT, LP); thiamine before glucose in at-risk patients","Continuous EEG for refractory cases, to confirm cessation of electrographic seizures, and to detect nonconvulsive status","Identify and treat the precipitant (missed medications, alcohol withdrawal, infection, stroke, metabolic)"],"tx":["FIRST-LINE: a benzodiazepine at an adequate dose - IV lorazepam, or IM midazolam when no IV (RAMPART showed IM midazolam non-inferior/superior in the field); under-dosing benzodiazepines is the classic error","SECOND-LINE (if seizures persist): an IV ASM - levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate (ESETT found them roughly equivalent) - give a full loading dose","REFRACTORY status: intubation and continuous anesthetic infusion (midazolam, propofol, or pentobarbital) with continuous EEG titration; treat the underlying cause throughout"],"pearls":["The most common mistake is too little benzodiazepine, too late - give the full first-line dose promptly","A patient who will not wake up after a seizure may still be seizing electrically - get an EEG for nonconvulsive status","ESETT: levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, and valproate are essentially interchangeable second-line - pick one and load fully","Give thiamine before glucose in the malnourished/alcohol-using patient to avoid precipitating Wernicke"],"refs":[{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society Status Epilepticus Guideline","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Status Epilepticus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430686/"},{"t":"American Epilepsy Society","u":"https://www.aesnet.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-pnes","name":"Psychogenic Nonepileptic Events (Functional Seizures)","sec":"neuro","present":["Paroxysmal episodes that resemble epileptic seizures but arise from psychological mechanisms, WITHOUT epileptiform EEG correlate - a subtype of functional neurological disorder","Semiologic clues (not individually definitive): longer duration, waxing-waning/asynchronous limb movements, side-to-side head shaking, pelvic thrusting, ictal EYE CLOSURE, preserved awareness during bilateral motor activity, and rapid postictal reorientation with emotional distress","Often coexist with epilepsy and with a history of trauma, anxiety, depression, or prior medically unexplained symptoms; not consciously produced (distinct from malingering/factitious)"],"dx":["Video-EEG capturing a typical event is the GOLD STANDARD - a clinical event without ictal epileptiform activity (and normal ictal/postictal EEG in a convulsive-appearing event) confirms it","A normal interictal EEG does not diagnose it, and prolonged epileptic seizures can look nonepileptic - capture is key; a normal serum prolactin after a convulsive event is supportive but not definitive","Screen for comorbid epilepsy and psychiatric conditions"],"tx":["Delivery of the diagnosis is therapeutic and pivotal: explain it as a real, common, and treatable condition (not 'faking' and not 'nothing wrong'), validate the symptoms, and preserve rapport - a poor explanation drives patients away and to more testing","Taper unnecessary antiseizure medications (they do not help and cause harm) once epilepsy is excluded; treat comorbid psychiatric illness","Cognitive behavioral therapy is the evidence-based treatment; multidisciplinary care (neurology + psychiatry/psychology) improves outcomes"],"pearls":["Video-EEG capturing an event is the only way to be sure - semiology suggests but does not prove","How you deliver the diagnosis IS the first treatment - framing it as real and treatable, not fake, determines whether the patient engages","Functional seizures are not consciously produced - separate them from malingering in your language and your mind","Patients can have BOTH functional seizures and epilepsy - excluding one does not exclude the other"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / FND Society Resources","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Psychogenic Nonepileptic Seizures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544319/"},{"t":"FND Society","u":"https://www.fndsociety.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-migraine","name":"Migraine - Specialist Diagnosis & Management","sec":"neuro","present":["Recurrent moderate-severe headache (often unilateral, pulsating, aggravated by activity) with nausea and photo/phonophobia; +/- AURA (visual, sensory, or language, developing over minutes and lasting <60 min)","Chronic migraine: headache >=15 days/month for >3 months with migrainous features on >=8 - screen for medication-overuse headache and treatable contributors","Recognize migraine variants and mimics; a specialist lens focuses on refractory disease, chronic migraine, and preventive strategy"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis by ICHD criteria with a normal exam; neuroimaging only for red flags (SNNOOP10) or atypical/changing features","Track headache days, acute-medication days (overuse threshold), aura features, and disability (e.g., MIDAS) to guide preventive decisions","Screen for comorbidities that shape treatment: depression/anxiety, obesity, sleep apnea, and cardiovascular risk (affects triptan/ergot use)"],"tx":["Acute: NSAIDs for mild-moderate; triptans for moderate-severe (avoid in vascular disease); gepants (ubrogepant, rimegepant) and ditans (lasmiditan) when triptans fail/are contraindicated; treat early and limit acute drugs to <=2 days/week to prevent overuse headache","Preventive when frequent/disabling: pick by comorbidity - topiramate (avoid in pregnancy), beta-blockers, amitriptyline, candesartan, valproate (not in women of childbearing potential); CGRP monoclonal antibodies or gepants after oral failures; onabotulinumtoxinA for CHRONIC migraine","Treat medication-overuse headache by withdrawing the overused acute drug and starting prevention; lifestyle scaffolding (sleep, hydration, exercise, trigger management)"],"pearls":["Count acute-medication days - migraine that seems refractory is often medication-overuse headache in disguise","Match the preventive to the comorbidity: the hypertensive gets a beta-blocker/candesartan, the depressed-insomniac gets amitriptyline, the obese patient may get topiramate","CGRP therapies and botulinum toxin transformed refractory and chronic migraine - escalate rather than accept disability","Aura with vascular risk changes the acute toolkit - triptans and ergots are relatively contraindicated"],"refs":[{"t":"American Headache Society Migraine Guidance","u":"https://americanheadachesociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Migraine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560787/"},{"t":"ICHD-3 Classification","u":"https://ichd-3.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-cluster-tacs","name":"Cluster Headache & Trigeminal Autonomic Cephalalgias","sec":"neuro","present":["Trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias (TACs) pair strictly UNILATERAL head pain with ipsilateral cranial AUTONOMIC features (lacrimation, conjunctival injection, rhinorrhea, ptosis/miosis, eyelid edema)","CLUSTER headache: excruciating unilateral orbital/temporal pain, 15-180 min, up to several times daily in bouts lasting weeks-months with remissions, circadian/circannual periodicity, and marked RESTLESSNESS/agitation (unlike the still migraineur)","Other TACs by duration/frequency: paroxysmal hemicrania (shorter, more frequent), SUNCT/SUNA (very brief, many daily), and hemicrania continua (continuous)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis by ICHD criteria; MRI of the brain WITH pituitary/cavernous views at least once to exclude secondary causes (pituitary lesions, posterior fossa) - a first cluster-like headache warrants imaging","The INDOMETHACIN-responsive TACs (paroxysmal hemicrania and hemicrania continua) are defined by their absolute response to indomethacin - a therapeutic trial is diagnostic","Characterize attack duration, frequency, and periodicity to separate the TAC subtypes"],"tx":["Cluster ACUTE: high-flow 100% OXYGEN by non-rebreather and subcutaneous/intranasal sumatriptan (or zolmitriptan) - fast-acting routes because attacks are short and severe","Cluster PREVENTIVE: verapamil (first-line, ECG monitoring for heart block), a steroid bridge (prednisone taper) or greater occipital nerve block to cover the gap, and galcanezumab (CGRP mAb) for episodic cluster; refractory - lithium, topiramate, neuromodulation","Paroxysmal hemicrania and hemicrania continua: INDOMETHACIN (also diagnostic); SUNCT/SUNA - lamotrigine and IV lidocaine"],"pearls":["Restlessness distinguishes cluster from migraine - the cluster patient paces and rocks, the migraineur lies still in the dark","High-flow oxygen and subcutaneous sumatriptan are the acute cluster answers - oral drugs are too slow for a 60-minute attack","A trial of indomethacin is both test and treatment for hemicrania continua and paroxysmal hemicrania","Image at least once - a secondary lesion (especially pituitary) can masquerade as a TAC"],"refs":[{"t":"American Headache Society Cluster/TAC Guidance","u":"https://americanheadachesociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cluster Headache","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544241/"},{"t":"ICHD-3 Classification","u":"https://ichd-3.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-secondary-headache","name":"Secondary Headache & Thunderclap Red Flags","sec":"neuro","present":["The core skill is separating benign primary headache from dangerous SECONDARY causes using red flags (SNNOOP10): systemic symptoms/fever, neoplasm history, neurologic deficit, sudden/THUNDERCLAP onset, older age (>50) at onset, pattern change, positional headache, precipitated by Valsalva, papilledema, pregnancy/postpartum, and painful eye/immunocompromise","THUNDERCLAP headache (maximal within 60 seconds) is a differential of emergencies: subarachnoid hemorrhage, reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS), cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, arterial dissection, pituitary apoplexy, and spontaneous intracranial hypotension","New headache in an older adult raises giant cell arteritis and mass lesions"],"dx":["Thunderclap workup: urgent noncontrast CT (highly sensitive for SAH within 6 h) and, if negative with ongoing suspicion, lumbar puncture for xanthochromia; add CTA/MRA/MRV to evaluate vessels (aneurysm, RCVS, dissection, CVST)","Giant cell arteritis: ESR/CRP and temporal artery biopsy (or vascular ultrasound) in the >50 patient with new headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, or visual symptoms - do not wait to start steroids","Image and examine for the specific red-flag syndrome suspected (papilledema -> raised ICP workup; positional -> intracranial hypotension/hypertension)"],"tx":["Treat the identified cause: SAH (secure aneurysm - neurosurgery/neuro-IR), CVST (anticoagulation), dissection (antithrombotic), pituitary apoplexy (steroids +/- surgery), RCVS (remove triggers, nimodipine, avoid vasoconstrictors)","GIANT CELL ARTERITIS: start high-dose corticosteroids IMMEDIATELY when suspected (do not wait for biopsy) to prevent irreversible blindness; add tocilizumab as a steroid-sparing agent","Spontaneous intracranial hypotension (postural headache, CSF leak): conservative measures then epidural blood patch"],"pearls":["Thunderclap headache is SAH until proven otherwise - CT within 6 h, then LP for xanthochromia if CT is negative","In suspected giant cell arteritis, steroids come before the biopsy - vision lost is not recovered, and the biopsy stays positive for days","A first or worst headache after 50, or any new red flag, changes a headache from a diagnosis into a workup","Recurrent thunderclaps with vasoconstriction on angiography is RCVS - stop the vasoconstrictors and avoid steroids"],"refs":[{"t":"American Headache Society / AAN Red Flag Guidance","u":"https://americanheadachesociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thunderclap Headache","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560629/"},{"t":"ICHD-3 Classification","u":"https://ichd-3.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-tn-medical","name":"Trigeminal Neuralgia - Medical Management","sec":"neuro","present":["Paroxysms of brief, severe, ELECTRIC-SHOCK-like facial pain in trigeminal territory (usually V2/V3), triggered by light touch, chewing, or cold air, with refractory (pain-free) intervals - a neurology diagnosis before any surgical referral","Classic (idiopathic) TN typically from neurovascular compression; a normal interictal exam is expected","Red flags for a SECONDARY cause (younger patient, bilateral pain, sensory deficit, other cranial neuropathies) point to multiple sclerosis or a structural lesion"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis by ICHD criteria; MRI (including thin cuts through the trigeminal root and screening for MS plaques/tumors) is warranted, especially with any atypical or red-flag features","Distinguish from dental pathology (a frequent cause of misdiagnosis and unnecessary procedures), painful trigeminal neuropathy (constant pain + sensory loss), and TACs","A dramatic response to carbamazepine supports the diagnosis"],"tx":["First-line MEDICAL therapy: CARBAMAZEPINE (or oxcarbazepine) - often dramatically effective and part of the diagnostic picture; monitor for hyponatremia, and check HLA-B*15:02 in at-risk ancestries (SJS/TEN)","Add-on/alternatives for intolerance or partial response: lamotrigine, baclofen, gabapentin/pregabalin; multiple sclerosis-related TN may respond to these as well","Refer for a PROCEDURE (microvascular decompression, percutaneous rhizotomy, or stereotactic radiosurgery - the surgical lens, covered in neurosurgery) when pain is refractory to or intolerant of medications - MVD is the most durable in fit candidates"],"pearls":["Carbamazepine is both the first-line drug and a diagnostic clue - classic TN usually responds impressively","Young, bilateral, or with sensory loss means image for MS or a mass - do not assume idiopathic TN","Refractory or intolerant to medication is the trigger to refer for a procedure - do not leave the patient in agony on failing pills","Constant background pain with numbness is painful trigeminal neuropathy, not classic TN - a different entity and prognosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/EFNS Trigeminal Neuralgia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Trigeminal Neuralgia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554486/"},{"t":"ICHD-3 Classification","u":"https://ichd-3.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-headache-pregnancy","name":"Headache in Pregnancy","sec":"neuro","present":["Pregnancy shifts both the headache differential and the therapeutic options; most primary migraine IMPROVES in pregnancy (especially after the first trimester), so a new or worsening headache raises concern for a secondary cause","Dangerous pregnancy-specific causes: PREECLAMPSIA/eclampsia, posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES), cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (hypercoagulable state), pituitary apoplexy (Sheehan/adenoma), reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (often postpartum), and subarachnoid hemorrhage","The peripartum and postpartum periods carry elevated risk for CVST, RCVS, and PRES"],"dx":["Check BLOOD PRESSURE and urine protein in any pregnant patient with headache - preeclampsia is the can't-miss; assess for visual symptoms, hyperreflexia, and edema","Image when red flags are present: MRI (and MRV for venous thrombosis) is preferred and safe in pregnancy; noncontrast CT when needed for emergencies - do not withhold necessary imaging for fear of the fetus","Distinguish primary migraine (usually improving, prior similar history) from new secondary headache"],"tx":["Acute migraine in pregnancy: acetaminophen first-line; metoclopramide for nausea/pain; limited/selective triptan use (sumatriptan has the most reassuring data) per shared decision; avoid NSAIDs in the third trimester and ergots throughout","Preventive when needed: nonpharmacologic measures, and if a drug is required, choose the safest (avoid VALPROATE and TOPIRAMATE - teratogenic; propranolol or amitriptyline with counseling); optimize magnesium/lifestyle","Treat the secondary cause emergently (preeclampsia -> magnesium and delivery planning with OB; CVST -> anticoagulation; PRES -> BP control) - these are OB/neuro co-managed emergencies"],"pearls":["A new or worsening headache in pregnancy is secondary until excluded - primary migraine usually gets BETTER, not worse","Take the blood pressure and check the urine - preeclampsia is the headache you cannot miss in pregnancy","Do not withhold necessary MRI/MRV over fetal-safety fears - the missed CVST or preeclampsia is the bigger danger","Valproate and topiramate are off the table for prevention in pregnancy - reach for propranolol or amitriptyline if a drug is truly needed"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / AAN Headache in Pregnancy Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Headache in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430726/"},{"t":"American Headache Society","u":"https://americanheadachesociety.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-ms","name":"Multiple Sclerosis - Diagnosis & Disease-Modifying Therapy","sec":"neuro","present":["An immune-mediated demyelinating disease of the CNS; relapsing-remitting MS (most common) presents with subacute attacks - optic neuritis, myelitis, brainstem/cerebellar syndromes - that partially recover, disseminated in space and time","Progressive forms (primary and secondary progressive) accrue disability without discrete relapses; typical onset in young adults, more common in women","Distinguish a true relapse (new/worsening deficit >24 h, no fever/infection) from a PSEUDORELAPSE (recrudescence of old symptoms with heat or infection - Uhthoff phenomenon)"],"dx":["Diagnosis by the McDonald criteria integrating clinical attacks with MRI dissemination in SPACE (lesions in >=2 of periventricular, juxtacortical/cortical, infratentorial, spinal cord) and in TIME (new lesions over time, or simultaneous enhancing + non-enhancing lesions)","CSF oligoclonal bands (not in serum) support the diagnosis and can substitute for dissemination in time; exclude mimics (NMOSD/MOGAD antibodies, B12, vasculitis, sarcoid, infection)","MRI brain and spinal cord with gadolinium is the workhorse for diagnosis and monitoring"],"tx":["Acute relapse: high-dose corticosteroids (IV or oral methylprednisolone) speed recovery; plasma exchange for steroid-refractory severe relapses","Disease-modifying therapy to reduce relapses and disability accrual, ranging from platform agents to HIGH-EFFICACY drugs (anti-CD20 - ocrelizumab/ofatumumab, natalizumab, S1P modulators, cladribine); early high-efficacy treatment is increasingly favored - individualize by activity, risk, and monitoring (e.g., JCV antibody and PML risk with natalizumab)","Symptomatic management (spasticity, fatigue, bladder, neuropathic pain, mood) and vitamin D/lifestyle; treat pseudorelapses by treating the trigger, not escalating DMT"],"pearls":["Dissemination in space AND time is the diagnostic backbone - one enhancing plus one non-enhancing lesion can satisfy time on a single scan","A fever or infection unmasking old deficits is a pseudorelapse - cool and treat the infection, do not give steroids or change the DMT","Oligoclonal bands in CSF but not serum support MS and can stand in for dissemination in time","Check the NMOSD/MOGAD antibodies before committing to MS - some MS drugs worsen NMOSD"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Multiple Sclerosis Guidelines","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Multiple Sclerosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499849/"},{"t":"National MS Society Professional Resources","u":"https://www.nationalmssociety.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-nmosd-mogad","name":"NMOSD & MOGAD","sec":"neuro","present":["Antibody-mediated demyelinating diseases distinct from MS. NMOSD (aquaporin-4 IgG): severe optic neuritis (often bilateral/sequential), LONGITUDINALLY EXTENSIVE transverse myelitis (>=3 vertebral segments), and area postrema syndrome (intractable nausea/vomiting/hiccups)","MOGAD (MOG-IgG): ADEM (especially in children), optic neuritis (often bilateral, anterior with disc swelling), and myelitis; frequently monophasic but can relapse","Attacks are often MORE SEVERE than MS relapses, making correct classification and prompt treatment critical"],"dx":["Serum AQP4-IgG (cell-based assay) for NMOSD and serum MOG-IgG for MOGAD - the defining tests; distinguish from MS clinically and radiographically (longitudinally extensive cord lesions, area postrema, bilateral ON favor NMOSD/MOGAD)","MRI brain and spine, and CSF (NMOSD often lacks the MS-type oligoclonal bands); check antibody titers with attention to assay quality and timing (treat with high suspicion even if pending)","Recognizing the pattern prevents giving MS therapies that worsen NMOSD"],"tx":["Acute attacks: high-dose IV corticosteroids, with early PLASMA EXCHANGE for severe or steroid-refractory attacks (lower threshold than in MS given attack severity)","NMOSD maintenance: relapse-prevention immunotherapy - rituximab and approved agents (eculizumab, inebilizumab, satralizumab); AVOID typical MS disease-modifying therapies (interferons, natalizumab, fingolimod) which can WORSEN NMOSD","MOGAD: treat acute attacks; maintenance immunotherapy for relapsing disease (steroids, IVIG, rituximab), individualized since many are monophasic"],"pearls":["Longitudinally extensive myelitis, bilateral optic neuritis, or intractable vomiting (area postrema) should prompt AQP4/MOG testing, not an MS label","MS drugs can worsen NMOSD - getting the antibody right changes the whole treatment plan","NMOSD/MOGAD attacks are severe - lower your threshold for plasma exchange","MOGAD is often monophasic and steroid-responsive - do not over-commit to lifelong immunosuppression without relapses"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / International NMOSD-MOGAD Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neuromyelitis Optica","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572115/"},{"t":"Sumaira Foundation / Professional Resources","u":"https://www.sumairafoundation.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-optic-neuritis","name":"Optic Neuritis","sec":"neuro","present":["Subacute MONOCULAR vision loss over hours-days with PAIN ON EYE MOVEMENT, dyschromatopsia (red desaturation), and a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD - the objective sign)","Typical (MS-associated) optic neuritis: unilateral, mild-moderate, retrobulbar (normal disc in ~2/3); ATYPICAL features (bilateral, severe, marked disc swelling, poor recovery, no pain) suggest NMOSD/MOGAD or other causes","A common first presentation of multiple sclerosis"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (RAPD, visual field defect, color loss); MRI of brain and orbits with contrast assesses MS risk (white-matter lesions - Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial) and the optic nerve","Test AQP4-IgG and MOG-IgG when features are atypical (bilateral, severe, poor recovery) to catch NMOSD/MOGAD; exclude infectious/inflammatory and compressive/ischemic mimics","Visual field testing and OCT to document and follow; look for other demyelinating lesions"],"tx":["IV corticosteroids (high-dose methylprednisolone) SPEED recovery but do not change final visual acuity (ONTT); oral prednisone at standard dose ALONE was associated with more recurrences in ONTT - avoid low-dose oral steroid monotherapy","Severe or NMOSD/MOGAD-related optic neuritis: consider plasma exchange and start disease-appropriate maintenance immunotherapy","If MRI shows demyelinating lesions, discuss MS-preventive disease-modifying therapy; treat the underlying antibody-mediated disease when present"],"pearls":["The RAPD is the objective sign - a patient reporting unilateral vision loss without an afferent defect makes optic neuritis unlikely","Steroids speed recovery but do not improve the final acuity - and low-dose oral steroid alone raised recurrence in the ONTT","Atypical features (bilateral, severe, no pain, poor recovery) mean test AQP4/MOG - it is not just MS","The brain MRI at presentation stratifies MS risk and guides the disease-modifying conversation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAN Optic Neuritis (ONTT) Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Optic Neuritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557853/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-autoimmune-encephalitis","name":"Autoimmune & Paraneoplastic Encephalitis","sec":"neuro","present":["Subacute (days-weeks) onset of memory loss, psychiatric/behavioral change, seizures, and altered consciousness - a treatable cause of encephalopathy that mimics psychiatric illness or rapidly progressive dementia","Syndromic clues: anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (young women, prominent psychiatric onset, orofacial DYSKINESIAS, autonomic instability, ovarian TERATOMA); anti-LGI1 (faciobrachial dystonic seizures, HYPONATREMIA, limbic encephalitis in older adults)","Paraneoplastic syndromes (anti-Hu, anti-Ma2, anti-CV2, etc.) may present with limbic encephalitis, cerebellar degeneration, or brainstem/neuropathy syndromes and can precede the cancer diagnosis"],"dx":["Send SERUM AND CSF autoantibody panels (cell-surface antibodies - NMDAR, LGI1, CASPR2, GABA-B - and intracellular/paraneoplastic - Hu, Ma2, Yo); CSF may show lymphocytic pleocytosis/oligoclonal bands","MRI (medial temporal T2/FLAIR signal in limbic encephalitis, though often normal in NMDAR) and EEG (extreme delta brush in NMDAR; seizures/slowing); exclude infection (HSV PCR - it can even trigger post-HSV NMDAR encephalitis)","TUMOR SEARCH is mandatory when a paraneoplastic/associated antibody is found (CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, pelvic ultrasound/MRI for teratoma, PET if occult) - the cancer drives treatment and prognosis"],"tx":["Prompt IMMUNOTHERAPY: first-line high-dose steroids, IVIG, and/or plasma exchange; second-line rituximab and cyclophosphamide for refractory cases (cell-surface antibody syndromes respond well and can recover substantially)","REMOVE the tumor when present (e.g., ovarian teratoma in NMDAR encephalitis) - it is central to recovery; treat the underlying malignancy for paraneoplastic syndromes","Supportive/ICU care (status epilepticus, autonomic instability, dyskinesias); intracellular-antigen paraneoplastic syndromes respond less to immunotherapy - prognosis tied to the cancer"],"pearls":["Subacute psychiatric change with seizures, dyskinesias, and autonomic instability in a young woman is anti-NMDAR encephalitis until proven otherwise - look for the teratoma","Faciobrachial dystonic seizures with hyponatremia point to anti-LGI1 - a very treatable limbic encephalitis","Send BOTH serum and CSF - some antibodies are detected in one and missed in the other","Finding the antibody obligates a tumor hunt - the cancer is part of the disease and its removal is part of the cure"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Autoimmune Encephalitis Resources","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Autoimmune Encephalitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578203/"},{"t":"Encephalitis International","u":"https://www.encephalitis.info"}]},{"id":"neuro-cns-vasculitis-sarcoid","name":"CNS Vasculitis & Neurosarcoidosis","sec":"neuro","present":["Primary angiitis of the CNS (PACNS): inflammation of CNS vessels causing headache, cognitive decline, and strokes in MULTIPLE vascular territories over time in the absence of systemic vasculitis","Secondary CNS vasculitis from systemic disease (ANCA-associated, lupus, infection, drugs); mimics include reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS - thunderclap headaches, reversible over weeks) and atherosclerosis/emboli","Neurosarcoidosis: granulomatous involvement of the nervous system - cranial neuropathies (especially FACIAL nerve, often bilateral), basal/leptomeningeal disease, hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction, myelopathy, and neuropathy"],"dx":["CNS vasculitis: MRI (multifocal infarcts/enhancement), vessel imaging (angiography may show beading - nonspecific), CSF (inflammatory), and often BRAIN/leptomeningeal BIOPSY for definitive diagnosis; exclude RCVS (which is not treated with immunosuppression) and infection","Neurosarcoidosis: MRI with contrast (leptomeningeal/dural enhancement, cranial nerve/hypothalamic involvement), CSF (lymphocytic, high protein, sometimes low glucose), and evidence of systemic sarcoid (chest imaging, tissue biopsy - serum/CSF ACE is nonspecific and insensitive)","Tissue confirmation (systemic node/lung when possible, or CNS) strengthens both diagnoses and guides therapy"],"tx":["CNS vasculitis: high-dose corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide (or rituximab) for induction, then maintenance immunosuppression; treat the underlying systemic cause in secondary vasculitis","Neurosarcoidosis: corticosteroids are first-line; steroid-sparing agents (methotrexate, mycophenolate, azathioprine) and anti-TNF agents (infliximab) for refractory/severe disease","Distinguish and NOT immunosuppress the mimics - RCVS is managed by removing vasoconstrictors and supportive care, and infection must be excluded before immunosuppression"],"pearls":["Strokes in multiple territories in a young or atypical patient, with headache and cognitive decline, should raise CNS vasculitis - but rule out RCVS first (thunderclaps, reversible, do NOT immunosuppress)","Bilateral facial nerve palsy plus basal meningitis is neurosarcoidosis until proven otherwise","Serum and CSF ACE are neither sensitive nor specific - do not hang the neurosarcoid diagnosis on them","Biopsy matters - immunosuppressing a vasculitis mimic (infection, RCVS, atherosclerosis) can be catastrophic"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / Vasculitis Foundation Resources","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Primary CNS Vasculitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430880/"},{"t":"Foundation for Sarcoidosis Research","u":"https://www.stopsarcoidosis.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-peripheral-neuropathy","name":"Peripheral Neuropathy - Diagnostic Approach","sec":"neuro","present":["Symptoms of nerve dysfunction - numbness, tingling, burning pain, weakness - whose CAUSE is found by defining the PATTERN before ordering tests","Localize by distribution: length-dependent symmetric polyneuropathy (stocking-glove - metabolic/toxic, most common), mononeuropathy (entrapment), MONONEURITIS MULTIPLEX (stepwise, multiple named nerves - think vasculitis), and polyradiculopathy","Characterize fiber type (large-fiber - proprioception/vibration/reflexes; small-fiber - pain/temperature/autonomic) and tempo (acute vs chronic)"],"dx":["Nerve conduction studies/EMG classify AXONAL vs DEMYELINATING and confirm the pattern - the key branch point (demyelinating -> CIDP/hereditary/paraprotein; axonal -> metabolic/toxic)","Tiered labs for the common causes: glucose/A1c (diabetes is the leading cause), B12/methylmalonic acid, TSH, CBC/CMP, SPEP/immunofixation (paraprotein); targeted testing (heavy metals, HIV, autoimmune, genetic CMT panel) by pattern","Small-fiber neuropathy with normal NCS may need skin biopsy (intraepidermal nerve fiber density) or autonomic testing"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE: optimize glycemia, replace B12, remove the toxin/offending drug, treat the paraprotein/inflammatory neuropathy; immunotherapy for treatable immune neuropathies (CIDP, vasculitic)","Symptomatic neuropathic pain control (see neuropathic pain entry): gabapentinoids, duloxetine, TCAs, topical agents","Foot care/protection for insensate feet, fall prevention, and address autonomic features"],"pearls":["Find the pattern first - length-dependent, mononeuritis multiplex, or demyelinating each points to a different cause and workup","Mononeuritis multiplex is vasculitis until proven otherwise - it is an urgent, treatable pattern, not a routine neuropathy","NCS/EMG splitting axonal from demyelinating is the pivot that reorganizes the entire differential","Normal NCS with burning feet and autonomic symptoms may be small-fiber neuropathy - the standard study misses it"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Neuropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542220/"},{"t":"Foundation for Peripheral Neuropathy","u":"https://www.foundationforpn.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-gbs","name":"Guillain-Barre Syndrome","sec":"neuro","present":["Acute immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy: rapidly progressive, usually ASCENDING, symmetric weakness with early AREFLEXIA, evolving over days to <4 weeks; often 1-3 weeks after a gastrointestinal (Campylobacter jejuni) or respiratory infection","Sensory symptoms/pain and autonomic instability (arrhythmias, BP swings) are common; the feared course is RESPIRATORY failure from diaphragmatic weakness","Variants: AIDP (demyelinating, most common in the West), AMAN (axonal), and Miller Fisher syndrome (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia - anti-GQ1b)"],"dx":["Clinical recognition drives early treatment; CSF shows ALBUMINOCYTOLOGIC DISSOCIATION (high protein with normal cell count - may be normal in the first week)","Nerve conduction studies confirm and subtype (demyelinating vs axonal); anti-GQ1b for Miller Fisher; exclude mimics (cord compression, transverse myelitis, tick paralysis, botulism, hypokalemia)","Serial bedside RESPIRATORY monitoring - forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force (NIF) - to anticipate respiratory failure BEFORE hypoxia (the 20/30/40 rule as a trigger for ICU/intubation)"],"tx":["IVIG or PLASMA EXCHANGE - equally effective; do NOT combine them and do NOT give corticosteroids (steroids are ineffective/harmful in GBS, unlike CIDP)","ICU-level monitoring for respiratory and autonomic instability; intubate for declining FVC/NIF or bulbar failure - do not wait for hypoxia; treat pain and dysautonomia","Supportive care (DVT prophylaxis, physiotherapy) and rehabilitation; most improve over weeks-months, though some have residual deficits"],"pearls":["Ascending weakness with early areflexia after a diarrheal illness is GBS - the reflexes disappear before the CSF protein rises","Watch the FVC/NIF, not the oxygen saturation - respiratory failure in GBS is mechanical and the sat drops late","Steroids do NOT work in GBS (they do in CIDP) - give IVIG or plasma exchange, not both, and skip the steroids","Autonomic instability can kill - monitor rhythm and blood pressure, not just strength"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / GBS Management Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Guillain-Barre Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532254/"},{"t":"GBS/CIDP Foundation International","u":"https://www.gbs-cidp.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-cidp","name":"Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy (CIDP)","sec":"neuro","present":["An acquired, immune-mediated demyelinating polyneuropathy that is the CHRONIC counterpart of GBS - progressive or relapsing over AT LEAST 8 WEEKS (vs the <4-week nadir of GBS)","Typically SYMMETRIC PROXIMAL AND DISTAL weakness with sensory loss and areflexia; a treatable cause of chronic weakness that is frequently missed or misdiagnosed","Variants exist (multifocal, distal, sensory-predominant); overdiagnosis also occurs, so objective demyelination matters"],"dx":["Nerve conduction studies demonstrating DEMYELINATION (conduction block, temporal dispersion, prolonged latencies) are central to the diagnosis (EFNS/PNS criteria); CSF shows albuminocytologic dissociation (elevated protein)","MRI may show nerve root/plexus enlargement/enhancement; nerve biopsy occasionally; exclude mimics (hereditary neuropathies, paraproteinemic/anti-MAG neuropathy, POEMS - check SPEP/immunofixation)","Distinguish from GBS by the TIME COURSE (>=8 weeks) and from diabetic and hereditary neuropathies"],"tx":["Unlike GBS, CIDP responds to CORTICOSTEROIDS - first-line options are steroids, IVIG, or plasma exchange (all effective); choose by comorbidity and access","Maintenance therapy is usually needed (periodic IVIG - including subcutaneous formulations, steroids, or steroid-sparing immunosuppressants); reassess the diagnosis if there is no response (misdiagnosis is common)","Monitor for treatment response objectively and taper to the lowest effective maintenance"],"pearls":["The 8-week rule separates CIDP from GBS - a demyelinating polyneuropathy that keeps progressing past two months is CIDP","Steroids WORK in CIDP (and do not in GBS) - the treatments diverge even though the diseases are cousins","CIDP is both under- and over-diagnosed - insist on electrophysiologic demyelination before committing to lifelong immunotherapy","No response to first-line therapy should prompt rethinking the diagnosis (anti-MAG, POEMS, hereditary), not just escalating"],"refs":[{"t":"EFNS/PNS CIDP Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563249/"},{"t":"GBS/CIDP Foundation International","u":"https://www.gbs-cidp.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-myasthenia","name":"Myasthenia Gravis","sec":"neuro","present":["A disorder of the neuromuscular junction causing FATIGABLE weakness that worsens with activity and improves with rest; fluctuating and often worse later in the day","OCULAR onset is common (ptosis, binocular diplopia) and may generalize; generalized disease affects bulbar (dysarthria, dysphagia, chewing fatigue), neck, limb, and respiratory muscles - reflexes and sensation are normal","MYASTHENIC CRISIS (respiratory failure from weakness) is the emergency; distinguish precipitants (infection, surgery, certain drugs)"],"dx":["Antibodies: acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies (most patients) and MuSK antibodies (a distinct subgroup - more bulbar/respiratory, poorer response to cholinesterase inhibitors)","Electrodiagnostics: repetitive nerve stimulation showing a DECREMENTAL response, and single-fiber EMG (most sensitive - increased jitter); bedside ice-pack test for ptosis supports ocular MG","CT/MRI of the CHEST to evaluate for THYMOMA (thymic pathology is common and alters management); screen for associated autoimmune disease (thyroid)"],"tx":["Symptomatic: pyridostigmine (acetylcholinesterase inhibitor); immunotherapy for generalized disease - corticosteroids (start low/titrate to avoid transient worsening) and steroid-sparing agents (azathioprine, mycophenolate), with newer targeted therapies (complement inhibitors, FcRn antagonists, rituximab for MuSK)","THYMECTOMY improves outcomes in AChR-positive generalized MG (and is indicated for thymoma at any age)","CRISIS: airway protection and IVIG or plasma exchange (rapid-acting); AVOID crisis-triggering drugs (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, beta-blockers, magnesium, certain others) and monitor respiratory function"],"pearls":["Fatigable, fluctuating weakness with normal reflexes and sensation is myasthenia - the ptosis worsens as the day goes on","Always image the chest for a thymoma - it changes management and may indicate thymectomy","In crisis, use IVIG or plasma exchange for speed - steroids can transiently worsen weakness at initiation","Know the drug list that triggers crisis (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium, beta-blockers) - the wrong antibiotic can intubate your patient"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / MGFA Myasthenia Gravis Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Myasthenia Gravis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559331/"},{"t":"Myasthenia Gravis Foundation of America","u":"https://myasthenia.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-als","name":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis & Motor Neuron Disease","sec":"neuro","present":["A progressive degeneration of motor neurons producing combined UPPER and LOWER motor neuron signs WITHOUT sensory loss - the diagnostic hallmark","Upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski) coexist with lower motor neuron signs (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) across regions; bulbar onset (dysarthria, dysphagia) or limb onset, spreading over time","Cognitive/behavioral change (FTD spectrum) in a subset; respiratory muscle failure is the usual cause of death"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis supported by EMG showing active and chronic DENERVATION (fibrillations, fasciculations, reinnervation) across multiple regions; criteria (El Escorial / Gold Coast) integrate UMN+LMN involvement","MRI (brain/spine) and labs to EXCLUDE MIMICS - cervical spondylotic myelopathy, multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (treatable!), myasthenia, inclusion body myositis, and metabolic causes","There is no confirmatory test - the diagnosis rests on the pattern and the exclusion of mimics"],"tx":["Disease-modifying options modestly slow progression: RILUZOLE (extends survival) and edaravone; newer agents per evolving evidence - counsel realistically","MULTIDISCIPLINARY clinic care improves survival and quality of life: respiratory support (noninvasive ventilation for hypoventilation), nutrition (PEG for dysphagia/weight loss), secretion and spasticity management, communication aids, and mobility","Early PALLIATIVE care and advance-care planning; treat pseudobulbar affect (dextromethorphan-quinidine) and symptoms proactively"],"pearls":["Combined upper and lower motor neuron signs WITHOUT sensory loss is the ALS signature - sensory symptoms should make you doubt the diagnosis","Always exclude the treatable mimics - multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block and cervical myelopathy can masquerade as ALS","Multidisciplinary ALS clinics and noninvasive ventilation prolong survival - the referral is a therapeutic act","Riluzole and edaravone slow, not stop - honest counseling and early palliative involvement are part of good care"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN ALS Practice Parameters","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556151/"},{"t":"ALS Association Professional Resources","u":"https://www.als.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-myopathy","name":"Inflammatory & Metabolic Myopathies","sec":"neuro","present":["Muscle disease presenting with PROXIMAL, symmetric weakness (difficulty rising from chairs, climbing stairs, lifting overhead) - usually painless; distinguish from neuropathy (distal, sensory) and NMJ disease (fatigable)","Inflammatory: dermatomyositis (rash - heliotrope, Gottron papules; malignancy association), polymyositis, immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (often STATIN/HMGCR- or SRP-associated, high CK), and inclusion body myositis (older men, DISTAL and ASYMMETRIC finger flexor/quadriceps weakness, refractory to immunotherapy)","Metabolic/genetic myopathies (e.g., McArdle disease) present with exercise intolerance, cramps, and rhabdomyolysis; toxic myopathies from drugs"],"dx":["Elevated CK (very high in necrotizing myopathy; may be normal in IBM), EMG showing a MYOPATHIC pattern, and MRI of muscle to identify edema/targets for biopsy","MYOSITIS-SPECIFIC ANTIBODIES (e.g., anti-Jo-1/antisynthetase, anti-Mi-2, anti-MDA5 with interstitial lung disease, anti-HMGCR) refine subtype and systemic risk; muscle biopsy characterizes the pattern","MALIGNANCY screening in dermatomyositis (age-appropriate cancer workup); assess for interstitial lung disease (antisynthetase/MDA5) and dysphagia"],"tx":["Inflammatory myopathies (except IBM): CORTICOSTEROIDS first-line plus a steroid-sparing agent (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, IVIG - especially dermatomyositis); treat associated ILD and screen/treat malignancy","Inclusion body myositis is largely REFRACTORY to immunotherapy - avoid prolonged steroids; focus on exercise, dysphagia management, and supportive care","Metabolic myopathies: activity modification, avoid triggers, and specific therapies where available; stop the offending drug in toxic/necrotizing statin-associated myopathy (HMGCR-associated may still need immunotherapy)"],"pearls":["Proximal painless weakness with a high CK is inflammatory myopathy until proven otherwise - but a normal CK does not exclude inclusion body myositis","Distal, asymmetric weakness (finger flexors, quadriceps) in an older man that fails steroids is inclusion body myositis - stop flogging it with immunosuppression","Dermatomyositis demands a cancer search - the rash can be the herald of an occult malignancy","Anti-MDA5 and antisynthetase antibodies flag interstitial lung disease - the lungs, not the muscles, may be the threat"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / Myositis Association Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Inflammatory Myopathies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542120/"},{"t":"The Myositis Association","u":"https://www.myositis.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-bell-palsy","name":"Bell Palsy & Facial Neuropathy","sec":"neuro","present":["Acute PERIPHERAL (lower motor neuron) facial weakness affecting the WHOLE hemiface including the FOREHEAD - distinguishing it from a central (UMN) lesion where the forehead is spared","Bell palsy is the idiopathic (often post-viral, HSV-associated) form: unilateral facial droop over hours-days, +/- ipsilateral loss of taste (anterior 2/3 tongue), hyperacusis, retroauricular pain, and impaired eye closure","Red flags for a NON-Bell cause: bilateral palsy (Lyme, GBS/Miller Fisher, sarcoid), vesicles in the ear/palate (Ramsay Hunt - herpes zoster oticus), slowly progressive or recurrent weakness (tumor), and other cranial neuropathies or long-tract signs"],"dx":["Bell palsy is a clinical diagnosis in a typical, isolated, acute peripheral facial palsy - no routine imaging or labs needed","Investigate atypical features: Lyme serology in endemic areas/bilateral palsy, MRI for progressive/recurrent/mass suspicion, and look for zoster vesicles (Ramsay Hunt); consider GBS if bilateral with areflexia","Grade severity (House-Brackmann) and specifically assess EYE closure/corneal protection"],"tx":["Early CORTICOSTEROIDS (prednisone/prednisolone within 72 h) improve recovery and are the mainstay for Bell palsy","Adding an antiviral (valacyclovir) may offer a small benefit in severe palsy and is standard for Ramsay Hunt (steroids + antiviral)","EYE PROTECTION is essential when closure is impaired: lubricating drops, ointment, and taping/moisture chamber at night to prevent exposure keratopathy"],"pearls":["Forehead involvement is the whole game - a spared forehead means a central lesion (stroke), not Bell palsy","Start steroids within 72 hours - the earlier the better for recovery","Look in the ear and at the palate - vesicles make it Ramsay Hunt, which needs an antiviral and has a worse prognosis","Bilateral facial palsy is not Bell - think Lyme, GBS, or sarcoid and work it up"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Bell Palsy Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bell Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482290/"},{"t":"American Academy of Otolaryngology","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-sma","name":"Spinal Muscular Atrophy & Hereditary Motor Neuron Disease","sec":"neuro","present":["Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA): autosomal recessive degeneration of anterior horn cells from SMN1 loss - pure LOWER motor neuron weakness (hypotonia, areflexia, fasciculations - classically tongue) WITHOUT sensory loss or upper motor neuron signs","Phenotypic spectrum by onset/severity (type 1 infantile through type 4 adult), modified by SMN2 copy number; proximal, symmetric weakness with preserved cognition","Hereditary motor neuron/neuropathy disorders (e.g., distal hereditary motor neuropathies, Kennedy disease - X-linked bulbospinal atrophy with gynecomastia) are important pattern relatives"],"dx":["Genetic testing for homozygous SMN1 deletion/mutation confirms SMA (and SMN2 copy number informs prognosis) - it has replaced EMG/biopsy as the diagnostic standard","EMG shows a neurogenic (denervation/reinnervation) pattern with normal sensory studies; consider Kennedy disease (androgen receptor CAG repeat) in adult men with bulbar involvement and gynecomastia","NEWBORN SCREENING for SMA now enables presymptomatic diagnosis and early treatment"],"tx":["Disease-MODIFYING therapies have transformed SMA: SMN2 splicing modifiers (nusinersen intrathecal, risdiplam oral) and SMN1 GENE REPLACEMENT (onasemnogene abeparvovec) - earliest (presymptomatic) treatment yields the best outcomes","Multidisciplinary supportive care: respiratory support, nutrition, physical/occupational therapy, orthopedic management (scoliosis, contractures)","Genetic counseling for families; early referral for the specific disease-modifying pathway"],"pearls":["Pure lower motor neuron weakness with a normal sensory exam and areflexia in a child points to SMA - confirm with SMN1 genetics, not a biopsy","Newborn screening plus disease-modifying drugs mean early diagnosis now changes the trajectory - time matters","An adult man with slowly progressive bulbar and limb weakness plus gynecomastia may have Kennedy disease - test the androgen receptor CAG repeat","SMN2 copy number is the prognostic dial - it modifies the severity of the same SMN1 loss"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / SMA Care Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Muscular Atrophy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560687/"},{"t":"Cure SMA Professional Resources","u":"https://www.curesma.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-radiculopathy-plexopathy","name":"Radiculopathy & Plexopathy - Electrodiagnostic Approach","sec":"neuro","present":["Radiculopathy: nerve ROOT dysfunction (compression/inflammation) causing DERMATOMAL sensory symptoms and MYOTOMAL weakness with pain in a root distribution - cervical (C6/C7) and lumbosacral (L5/S1) most common","Plexopathy: brachial or lumbosacral PLEXUS lesions producing weakness/sensory loss spanning MULTIPLE roots and peripheral nerves - causes include trauma, tumor infiltration, radiation, diabetes (lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy/amyotrophy), and idiopathic neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner)","The clinical task is localizing the lesion to root vs plexus vs peripheral nerve"],"dx":["ELECTRODIAGNOSTICS localize precisely: in RADICULOPATHY, sensory nerve action potentials are PRESERVED (lesion is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion) with EMG denervation in a myotomal (multi-nerve, single-root) distribution including paraspinals; in PLEXOPATHY, sensory responses are REDUCED and EMG changes span multiple roots/nerves but spare paraspinals","MRI of the spine (radiculopathy) or plexus (plexopathy - to find tumor/radiation change); paraspinal muscle involvement on EMG points to a root lesion","Consider malignant infiltration vs radiation plexopathy (myokymia on EMG favors radiation) and neuralgic amyotrophy (acute pain then patchy weakness)"],"tx":["Compressive radiculopathy: most improve with conservative care (see the neurosurgical radiculopathy entries for surgical thresholds) - analgesia, physical therapy, and treat progressive/severe deficits","Plexopathy: treat the cause - oncologic therapy for tumor infiltration, supportive care and time for neuralgic amyotrophy (often improves over months), glycemic and supportive care for diabetic radiculoplexus neuropathy","Neuropathic pain management and rehabilitation across both; refer for surgical decompression or oncologic management as indicated"],"pearls":["The sensory nerve action potential is the localizer: preserved in radiculopathy (lesion proximal to the ganglion), reduced in plexopathy","Paraspinal denervation on EMG points to the root - the plexus does not innervate paraspinals","Acute severe shoulder pain followed by patchy arm weakness is neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner) - it usually recovers with time","Myokymia on EMG in a cancer survivor favors radiation plexopathy over tumor recurrence - a crucial and reassuring distinction"],"refs":[{"t":"AANEM Electrodiagnostic Guidance","u":"https://www.aanem.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Radiculopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430837/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-parkinson","name":"Parkinson Disease - Diagnosis & Management","sec":"neuro","present":["A neurodegenerative synucleinopathy with the cardinal motor features: bradykinesia (required) plus resting TREMOR (4-6 Hz, pill-rolling) and/or RIGIDITY (cogwheel); postural instability appears later","ASYMMETRIC onset is characteristic; supportive features include a good levodopa response and hyposmia; nonmotor features (REM sleep behavior disorder, constipation, hyposmia, depression) often PRECEDE motor signs by years","Distinguish from essential tremor (action vs rest), drug-induced parkinsonism, and atypical parkinsonism"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (MDS criteria): bradykinesia plus rest tremor or rigidity, with supportive features and the absence of red flags for atypical parkinsonism (early falls, symmetric onset, poor levodopa response, early autonomic/cognitive failure, gaze palsy)","Neuroimaging is usually normal in PD; DaT-SPECT can confirm a dopaminergic deficit and separate PD/atypical parkinsonism from essential tremor or drug-induced tremor when uncertain (it does NOT distinguish PD from atypical parkinsonism)","A robust and sustained LEVODOPA RESPONSE supports the diagnosis"],"tx":["LEVODOPA (with carbidopa) is the most effective symptomatic therapy; dopamine agonists and MAO-B inhibitors are alternatives/adjuncts - in younger patients agonists may delay levodopa, but weigh impulse-control disorder risk","Manage MOTOR FLUCTUATIONS and dyskinesias over time (adjust dosing, add COMT/MAO-B inhibitors, amantadine for dyskinesia); DEEP BRAIN STIMULATION for levodopa-responsive motor complications refractory to medication (the surgical lens, covered in neurosurgery)","Treat NONMOTOR symptoms deliberately: constipation, orthostatic hypotension, depression/anxiety, psychosis (pimavanserin/quetiapine - AVOID typical antipsychotics and high-potency agents), REM sleep behavior disorder, and cognitive impairment; physical therapy and exercise slow functional decline"],"pearls":["Asymmetric rest tremor, bradykinesia, and a clear levodopa response is Parkinson disease - symmetric onset with early falls or poor levodopa response is a red flag for atypical parkinsonism","REM sleep behavior disorder and hyposmia are prodromal - they can precede the tremor by a decade","Do not block dopamine in these patients - typical antipsychotics and metoclopramide worsen parkinsonism; use quetiapine or pimavanserin for psychosis","Nonmotor symptoms drive quality of life as much as tremor - treat the constipation, orthostasis, and mood on purpose"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / MDS Parkinson Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Parkinson Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470193/"},{"t":"Michael J. Fox Foundation Professional Resources","u":"https://www.michaeljfox.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-atypical-parkinsonism","name":"Atypical Parkinsonism (PSP, MSA, CBD)","sec":"neuro","present":["Parkinson-plus syndromes that mimic PD early but declare themselves with red flags: SYMMETRIC onset, EARLY FALLS, poor/absent levodopa response, and early autonomic, cognitive, or oculomotor failure - faster progression than PD","Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP): early falls (often BACKWARD), vertical SUPRANUCLEAR GAZE PALSY (impaired downgaze), axial rigidity, and a surprised facial expression","Multiple system atrophy (MSA): parkinsonism or cerebellar ataxia PLUS prominent early AUTONOMIC failure (orthostatic hypotension, urogenital); corticobasal degeneration (CBD): markedly ASYMMETRIC rigidity/apraxia, dystonia, myoclonus, and alien-limb phenomenon"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis using consensus criteria (MDS-PSP, MSA criteria); the pattern of red flags separates the subtypes and PD","MRI clues: PSP midbrain atrophy ('hummingbird'/'penguin' sign), MSA 'hot cross bun' pontine sign and putaminal changes; autonomic testing for MSA; DaT-SPECT confirms dopaminergic deficit but does NOT distinguish among the atypical syndromes","A poor and non-sustained levodopa response supports atypical parkinsonism over PD"],"tx":["Largely symptomatic and supportive - these syndromes respond POORLY to levodopa (a trial is still warranted, sometimes modest benefit in MSA)","Aggressive supportive care: falls prevention and physical/occupational therapy (PSP falls are dangerous), autonomic management in MSA (midodrine/fludrocortisone, avoid nocturnal hypertension), speech/swallow therapy, and treat the nonmotor burden","Early goals-of-care and palliative planning given the faster, more disabling course; avoid dopamine-blocking agents"],"pearls":["Early falls, symmetric onset, and a poor levodopa response are the trio that says atypical parkinsonism, not PD","Impaired downgaze (vertical supranuclear gaze palsy) with backward falls is PSP until proven otherwise","Prominent early autonomic failure with parkinsonism or ataxia is MSA - the orthostatic hypotension is the tell","Marked asymmetry with apraxia, myoclonus, and an alien limb points to corticobasal degeneration"],"refs":[{"t":"MDS Atypical Parkinsonism Criteria","u":"https://www.movementdisorders.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Progressive Supranuclear Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526098/"},{"t":"CurePSP Professional Resources","u":"https://www.psp.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-essential-tremor","name":"Essential Tremor","sec":"neuro","present":["The most common movement disorder: a bilateral, largely symmetric ACTION tremor (postural and kinetic) of the hands/forearms, often with head or voice tremor - worsened by activity, stress, and caffeine","Contrast with Parkinson tremor (REST tremor, asymmetric, with bradykinesia/rigidity): essential tremor appears with sustained posture and movement (holding a cup, writing, pouring) and IMPROVES at rest","Frequently a family history (autosomal dominant); classic partial improvement with ALCOHOL is a supportive clue"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: isolated action tremor without parkinsonism or other neurologic signs, typically >=3 years' duration; assess head/voice involvement and functional impact (writing, drinking, eating)","Exclude enhanced physiologic tremor (hyperthyroidism, drugs/stimulants, caffeine, anxiety - check TSH and the medication list), Parkinson disease, dystonic tremor, and cerebellar tremor","DaT-SPECT only when separating essential tremor from parkinsonian tremor is genuinely unclear"],"tx":["First-line: PROPRANOLOL or PRIMIDONE (roughly equally effective; combine if partial response); second-line agents include topiramate and gabapentin","Treat when the tremor is functionally or socially disabling; address exacerbating factors (caffeine, stimulant drugs, anxiety)","Refractory disabling tremor: refer for procedures - deep brain stimulation (VIM thalamus) or MR-guided focused ultrasound thalamotomy (the surgical/procedural lens)"],"pearls":["Action tremor that improves at rest and with a drink of alcohol, often familial, is essential tremor - the opposite pattern of Parkinson rest tremor","Check the TSH and the medication list - enhanced physiologic tremor from thyrotoxicosis or stimulants masquerades as essential tremor","Propranolol and primidone are the workhorses - combine them for partial responders before calling it refractory","Disabling, medication-refractory tremor is a referral for DBS or focused ultrasound, not a life sentence"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Essential Tremor Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Essential Tremor","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499986/"},{"t":"International Essential Tremor Foundation","u":"https://www.essentialtremor.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-dystonia","name":"Dystonia","sec":"neuro","present":["Sustained or intermittent muscle contractions causing abnormal, often TWISTING/PATTERNED postures and repetitive movements; frequently task-specific and improved by a SENSORY TRICK (geste antagoniste - e.g., touching the chin relieves cervical dystonia)","Focal dystonias are common in adults: cervical dystonia (torticollis), blepharospasm, laryngeal (spasmodic dysphonia), and task-specific (writer's cramp, musician's dystonia); generalized/childhood-onset dystonias are often genetic","Distinguish primary (isolated) dystonia from secondary/acquired (structural, drug-induced, metabolic) and dystonia-plus syndromes"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis by phenomenology; classify by age of onset, body distribution, and whether isolated or combined with other features","Investigate secondary causes when there are red flags (abrupt onset, hemidystonia, additional neurologic signs, early age): MRI, and EXCLUDE WILSON DISEASE in any young patient with dystonia (ceruloplasmin, urinary copper, slit-lamp); consider DOPA-RESPONSIVE DYSTONIA in childhood-onset cases","Genetic testing for early-onset/generalized dystonia (e.g., DYT-TOR1A)"],"tx":["BOTULINUM TOXIN injection is first-line for most FOCAL dystonias (cervical, blepharospasm, laryngeal, limb) - targeted chemodenervation of overactive muscles","Oral agents (anticholinergics like trihexyphenidyl, baclofen, benzodiazepines) for generalized/segmental dystonia; ALWAYS trial LEVODOPA in childhood-onset dystonia to catch dopa-responsive dystonia (dramatic, sustained response)","Deep brain stimulation (GPi) for refractory generalized/segmental dystonia (the surgical lens); treat any identified secondary cause (Wilson disease, offending drug)"],"pearls":["A sensory trick that relieves the abnormal posture is a hallmark of dystonia - ask the patient to show you","Every young patient with dystonia gets a Wilson disease workup - it is treatable and missing it is catastrophic","Trial levodopa in childhood-onset dystonia - dopa-responsive dystonia can be dramatically and durably reversed","Botulinum toxin is the first-line answer for focal dystonias - targeted, effective, and repeatable"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / MDS Dystonia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dystonia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448144/"},{"t":"Dystonia Medical Research Foundation","u":"https://dystonia-foundation.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-huntington","name":"Huntington Disease & Chorea","sec":"neuro","present":["CHOREA is brief, irregular, unpredictable, flowing involuntary movements that flit from body part to body part; the differential is broad (Huntington, drug-induced, autoimmune, Sydenham, vascular, metabolic, pregnancy-related chorea gravidarum)","Huntington disease: autosomal dominant, adult-onset triad of a MOVEMENT disorder (chorea, later dystonia/parkinsonism), progressive COGNITIVE decline, and PSYCHIATRIC symptoms (depression, irritability, apathy, high suicide risk)","Family history is often present; juvenile HD (Westphal variant) presents with rigidity/bradykinesia rather than chorea"],"dx":["Huntington disease is confirmed by GENETIC testing for the HTT CAG trinucleotide REPEAT EXPANSION (>=40 fully penetrant); pre-test genetic counseling is essential given the predictive, incurable nature","Work up non-HD chorea by context: medication/drug review (dopaminergic agents, neuroleptics causing tardive, cocaine), autoimmune/paraneoplastic panels, ASO titers and rheumatic history (Sydenham), thyroid, pregnancy, structural lesions, and consider other genetic choreas","Assess cognition, mood, and SUICIDE RISK at diagnosis and follow-up"],"tx":["Symptomatic only - no disease-modifying therapy yet: chorea is treated (when disabling) with VMAT2 inhibitors (deutetrabenazine, valbenazine, tetrabenazine - monitor for depression/suicidality) or antipsychotics (which also help behavior)","Treat the substantial PSYCHIATRIC burden aggressively (depression, irritability, psychosis) and monitor suicide risk; multidisciplinary care (neurology, psychiatry, PT/OT/speech, genetic counseling, social work)","Treat non-HD chorea by cause (stop the offending drug, immunotherapy for autoimmune/Sydenham, correct metabolic derangements)"],"pearls":["Adult-onset chorea with cognitive decline, psychiatric symptoms, and a family history is Huntington disease - confirm with the CAG repeat, with counseling first","The psychiatric burden, not the chorea, often causes the most harm - screen for depression and suicide at every visit","VMAT2 inhibitors for chorea can worsen depression - weigh the trade-off in a population already at suicide risk","Not all chorea is Huntington - review the drug list and consider autoimmune, Sydenham, and metabolic causes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Huntington Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Huntington Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559166/"},{"t":"Huntington's Disease Society of America","u":"https://hdsa.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-rls","name":"Restless Legs Syndrome & Periodic Limb Movements","sec":"neuro","present":["Restless legs syndrome (RLS/Willis-Ekbom disease): an URGE to move the legs, usually with uncomfortable sensations, that begins/worsens at REST and in the EVENING/night and is RELIEVED BY MOVEMENT - the four diagnostic essentials","The circadian, evening-predominant pattern disrupts sleep initiation; periodic limb movements of sleep (PLMS) are stereotyped leg jerks during sleep, often coexisting and noted by a bed partner","Secondary/exacerbating factors: IRON DEFICIENCY, pregnancy, renal failure, neuropathy, and drugs (antihistamines, dopamine antagonists, many antidepressants)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the four essential features; polysomnography is not required (used to quantify PLMS or evaluate comorbid sleep disorders)","Check IRON studies in everyone - a FERRITIN <=75 ng/mL (or low transferrin saturation) is a treatable driver and central to management; review medications that worsen RLS","Screen for and address contributors (renal disease, neuropathy, pregnancy)"],"tx":["Correct IRON first: oral (or IV) iron repletion when ferritin is low often improves or resolves symptoms - the single most important intervention","First-line pharmacotherapy for moderate-severe RLS is now the ALPHA-2-DELTA LIGANDS (gabapentin enacarbil, pregabalin, gabapentin) - preferred over dopamine agonists because of AUGMENTATION (dopaminergic therapy paradoxically worsening/advancing symptoms over time)","Dopamine agonists are used cautiously given augmentation risk; treat comorbid conditions, remove offending drugs, and counsel on sleep hygiene and evening activity"],"pearls":["Urge to move, worse at rest and in the evening, relieved by movement - the four features make the diagnosis without a sleep study","Check the ferritin in every RLS patient - iron repletion (target ferritin above ~75-100) can be curative","Alpha-2-delta ligands have overtaken dopamine agonists as first-line - augmentation is the reason to avoid leaning on dopaminergics","Scan the medication list - antihistamines, dopamine blockers, and many antidepressants provoke RLS"],"refs":[{"t":"AASM / AAN Restless Legs Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://aasm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Restless Legs Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430878/"},{"t":"Restless Legs Syndrome Foundation","u":"https://www.rls.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-tics-tourette","name":"Tics & Tourette Syndrome","sec":"neuro","present":["Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic movements (motor) or vocalizations (phonic) preceded by a PREMONITORY URGE and TEMPORARILY SUPPRESSIBLE (at the cost of rising inner tension) - features that distinguish them from other hyperkinetic movements","Tourette syndrome: onset in childhood with BOTH multiple motor and >=1 phonic tic persisting >1 year; tics wax and wane, shift in character, and often peak in early adolescence then improve","Coprolalia is uncommon and NOT required; comorbid ADHD and OCD are the rule and often cause more impairment than the tics themselves"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the history and observation (or home video); no routine imaging/labs for typical Tourette syndrome","Screen for and characterize the comorbidities (ADHD, OCD, anxiety, learning issues) - they frequently drive the functional impairment; consider secondary tic causes if atypical (adult onset, abrupt, focal, other neurologic signs)","Assess impact (physical discomfort, social, academic) to decide whether treatment is warranted"],"tx":["Not all tics need treatment - education and reassurance suffice for many; treat when tics cause pain, functional impairment, or social difficulty","First-line behavioral therapy: COMPREHENSIVE BEHAVIORAL INTERVENTION FOR TICS (CBIT), which includes habit reversal training - as effective as medication for many and without drug side effects","Pharmacotherapy when needed: alpha-2 agonists (guanfacine, clonidine - especially with comorbid ADHD), VMAT2 inhibitors, or antipsychotics (reserved, monitor side effects); TREAT the comorbid ADHD/OCD, which often improves overall function more than tic suppression"],"pearls":["A premonitory urge and temporary suppressibility distinguish tics from chorea, myoclonus, and dystonia","Coprolalia is rare and not required for Tourette syndrome - the media stereotype misleads families and clinicians","CBIT is first-line and rivals medication - behavioral therapy before pills for many patients","Treat the ADHD and OCD - the comorbidities usually impair the child more than the tics do, and stimulants for ADHD are generally safe despite old fears"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Tourette Syndrome/Tics Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tourette Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448200/"},{"t":"Tourette Association of America","u":"https://tourette.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-drug-induced-movement","name":"Drug-Induced Movement Disorders","sec":"neuro","present":["Iatrogenic movement disorders, most from DOPAMINE-RECEPTOR-BLOCKING agents (antipsychotics AND antiemetics - metoclopramide, prochlorperazine): the key is a temporal link to the offending drug","Acute: dystonic reactions (hours-days - oculogyric crisis, torticollis, laryngospasm) and AKATHISIA (an inner restlessness/urge to move, often mistaken for anxiety/agitation); subacute: drug-induced PARKINSONISM (symmetric, unlike PD)","Delayed: TARDIVE DYSKINESIA (repetitive orofacial/lingual and limb movements after months-years of exposure - often persistent even after stopping the drug); neuroleptic malignant syndrome is the hyperthermic emergency"],"dx":["History is diagnostic - link the movement to the drug and its timeline; drug-induced parkinsonism is typically SYMMETRIC and DaT-SPECT-normal (dopaminergic system intact), separating it from Parkinson disease","Recognize akathisia (frequently under-recognized and misattributed to worsening psychiatric illness) and distinguish tardive dyskinesia from other hyperkinetic disorders","Review ALL dopamine-blocking exposures including antiemetics; assess for neuroleptic malignant syndrome (fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, elevated CK) as an emergency"],"tx":["Acute dystonic reaction: anticholinergics (benztropine, diphenhydramine) - rapidly effective; STOP or switch the offending agent when possible","Akathisia: reduce/switch the drug; propranolol (beta-blocker) is first-line, with benzodiazepines/mirtazapine as options; drug-induced parkinsonism: withdraw/switch the agent (may take months to resolve), anticholinergics if needed","TARDIVE DYSKINESIA: VMAT2 INHIBITORS (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine) are the evidence-based treatment; minimize/switch the causative antipsychotic cautiously (abrupt withdrawal can transiently worsen it); neuroleptic malignant syndrome - stop the drug, supportive/cooling, dantrolene/bromocriptine"],"pearls":["Antiemetics count - metoclopramide and prochlorperazine cause the same parkinsonism, akathisia, and tardive dyskinesia as antipsychotics","Akathisia is the great mimic of psychiatric agitation - do not escalate the antipsychotic for restlessness the antipsychotic is causing","Drug-induced parkinsonism is symmetric and DaT-normal - that separates it from Parkinson disease, though the two can coexist","VMAT2 inhibitors are the answer for tardive dyskinesia - and simply stopping the drug can transiently worsen it before it improves"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Tardive Syndromes Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Drug-Induced Movement Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560755/"},{"t":"Movement Disorder Society","u":"https://www.movementdisorders.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-alzheimer","name":"Alzheimer Disease & Mild Cognitive Impairment","sec":"neuro","present":["The leading cause of dementia: insidious, progressive AMNESTIC decline (impaired encoding of new memories) with later language, visuospatial, and executive dysfunction and eventual functional dependence","Mild cognitive impairment (MCI): objective cognitive decline beyond normal aging WITHOUT loss of functional independence - an at-risk state that may or may not progress to dementia","Distinguish from normal aging, depression (pseudodementia), delirium, and reversible causes; atypical presentations (posterior cortical atrophy, logopenic aphasia) exist"],"dx":["Cognitive assessment (MoCA/MMSE) plus a collateral history of functional decline; rule out REVERSIBLE contributors (B12, TSH, depression, medications, sleep apnea) and obtain structural imaging (MRI - hippocampal/medial temporal atrophy, exclude other pathology)","Biomarkers increasingly define Alzheimer pathology: amyloid PET, and CSF (or emerging plasma) amyloid-beta 42/40 and phosphorylated tau - useful when diagnosis is uncertain or disease-modifying therapy is considered","Stage severity and screen for behavioral/psychological symptoms and safety issues (driving, finances, wandering)"],"tx":["Symptomatic: cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) for mild-moderate and memantine for moderate-severe (combined in advanced disease) - modest benefit; counsel realistically","Anti-amyloid MONOCLONAL ANTIBODIES (lecanemab, donanemab) modestly slow decline in early symptomatic, amyloid-confirmed disease - require confirmed amyloid, careful selection, and MRI monitoring for ARIA (amyloid-related imaging abnormalities - edema/hemorrhage), higher risk in APOE4 carriers","Nonpharmacologic care is central: caregiver support and education, structure/routine, treat behavioral symptoms nonpharmacologically first, manage vascular risk factors and comorbid depression/sleep, advance-care planning, and safety"],"pearls":["Screen for reversible causes before labeling dementia - B12, thyroid, depression, sleep apnea, and medications are the treatable mimics","MCI is a risk state, not a sentence - many do not progress; monitor and modify vascular risk","Anti-amyloid antibodies require confirmed amyloid and ARIA monitoring - APOE4 carriers carry higher hemorrhage/edema risk","Caregiver support is a prescription - it delays institutionalization and reduces crises as much as any drug"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Dementia / Alzheimer Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Alzheimer Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499922/"},{"t":"Alzheimer's Association Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.alz.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-ftd","name":"Frontotemporal Dementia","sec":"neuro","present":["A group of younger-onset dementias (often 50s-60s) targeting the frontal and temporal lobes, with MEMORY relatively PRESERVED early - the opposite emphasis from Alzheimer disease","Behavioral variant (bvFTD): early personality/behavior change - disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsive/ritualistic behavior, dietary changes/hyperorality - often mistaken for a primary psychiatric disorder","Primary progressive aphasia variants: semantic (loss of word/object meaning) and nonfluent/agrammatic (effortful, agrammatic speech); overlap with ALS and with atypical parkinsonism (PSP/CBD) occurs"],"dx":["Clinical criteria plus MRI/FDG-PET showing FRONTAL and/or TEMPORAL atrophy/hypometabolism (asymmetric in language variants); neuropsychological testing highlights executive/behavioral or language deficits with spared early memory","Screen for the ALS overlap (motor neuron signs) and a family history (a substantial minority are genetic - C9orf72, MAPT, GRN); exclude primary psychiatric illness and other dementias","Amyloid biomarkers are typically NEGATIVE (helping separate from atypical Alzheimer presentations)"],"tx":["No disease-modifying therapy - management is symptomatic and supportive; cholinesterase inhibitors are NOT helpful and may worsen behavior (unlike in Alzheimer)","Behavioral symptoms: nonpharmacologic strategies first; SSRIs may help disinhibition/compulsions; use antipsychotics sparingly and cautiously (extrapyramidal sensitivity, especially with parkinsonian overlap)","Intensive CAREGIVER support and safety planning (financial vulnerability, impulsivity), speech therapy for aphasia, genetic counseling when familial, and early advance-care planning"],"pearls":["Early personality and behavior change with preserved memory in a 55-year-old is behavioral-variant FTD, not depression or Alzheimer","Cholinesterase inhibitors do not help FTD and can worsen behavior - it is not treated like Alzheimer","Look for the ALS overlap and the family history - C9orf72 links FTD and motor neuron disease","The financial and social vulnerability from disinhibition is a safety emergency families need help managing early"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Frontotemporal Dementia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Frontotemporal Dementia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559286/"},{"t":"Association for Frontotemporal Degeneration","u":"https://www.theaftd.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-dlb-pdd","name":"Dementia with Lewy Bodies & Parkinson Disease Dementia","sec":"neuro","present":["Lewy body dementias share alpha-synuclein pathology; core features: FLUCTUATING cognition/attention, recurrent well-formed VISUAL HALLUCINATIONS, spontaneous PARKINSONISM, and REM SLEEP BEHAVIOR DISORDER (dream enactment, often predating dementia by years)","The distinction from Parkinson disease dementia is by TIMING (the '1-year rule'): dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) when cognitive decline begins before or within ~1 year of parkinsonism; Parkinson disease dementia (PDD) when dementia develops in established Parkinson disease","Prominent autonomic dysfunction, falls, and SEVERE NEUROLEPTIC SENSITIVITY are characteristic"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (DLB consensus criteria) from the core features; supportive biomarkers include reduced DAT uptake on DaT-SPECT and REM-sleep-without-atonia on polysomnography","A careful history for RBD (bed-partner report), cognitive fluctuations, and visual hallucinations; distinguish from Alzheimer disease (visual hallucinations and parkinsonism are early here) and delirium","Screen autonomic function and fall risk"],"tx":["Cholinesterase inhibitors (rivastigmine, donepezil) are notably EFFECTIVE for cognition and hallucinations in Lewy body dementia; treat parkinsonism cautiously with levodopa (can worsen hallucinations - use the lowest effective dose)","AVOID typical and high-potency antipsychotics - severe neuroleptic sensitivity can cause life-threatening worsening; if an antipsychotic is essential, use quetiapine or pimavanserin cautiously","Treat RBD (melatonin, clonazepam) with bedroom safety measures; manage autonomic symptoms, depression, and falls"],"pearls":["Fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, and dream enactment is Lewy body dementia - and the timing versus parkinsonism separates DLB from PDD","Do NOT give typical antipsychotics - neuroleptic sensitivity in Lewy body dementia can be fatal; reach for quetiapine or pimavanserin only if unavoidable","REM sleep behavior disorder can precede the dementia by years - it is an early synucleinopathy marker","Cholinesterase inhibitors work well here for both cognition and hallucinations - more so than in most dementias"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / DLB Consortium Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dementia with Lewy Bodies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482441/"},{"t":"Lewy Body Dementia Association","u":"https://www.lbda.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-vascular-cognitive","name":"Vascular Cognitive Impairment","sec":"neuro","present":["Cognitive impairment/dementia caused by cerebrovascular disease - ranging from strategic single infarcts to multi-infarct and small-vessel (subcortical) disease; often EXECUTIVE and processing-speed deficits with slowed gait, rather than a pure amnestic picture","A STEPWISE decline temporally linked to strokes, or gradual decline from progressive small-vessel disease; focal neurologic signs and vascular risk factors support it","MIXED pathology (vascular + Alzheimer) is very common in older adults - the two coexist more often than either occurs alone"],"dx":["MRI (or CT) demonstrating relevant vascular lesions: cortical/subcortical infarcts, extensive white-matter hyperintensities (small-vessel disease), lacunes, and microbleeds; correlate the burden and location with the cognitive profile","Assess and quantify VASCULAR RISK FACTORS (hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, atrial fibrillation, smoking); screen for coexisting Alzheimer pathology when relevant","Consider hereditary small-vessel disease (CADASIL) in younger patients with migraine, strokes, and a family history"],"tx":["The cornerstone is aggressive VASCULAR RISK FACTOR control and stroke PREVENTION (BP, lipids, glucose, antithrombotic per stroke etiology, smoking cessation) - to slow progression, since there is no specific disease-modifying drug","Cholinesterase inhibitors/memantine offer modest benefit mainly when there is coexisting Alzheimer pathology; treat depression and manage the cognitive/behavioral symptoms","Multidisciplinary support, physical activity, and management of gait/falls and functional needs"],"pearls":["Executive dysfunction and slowed gait with a stepwise course and vascular risk factors point to vascular cognitive impairment rather than a pure amnestic dementia","Controlling blood pressure and the other vascular risks is the disease-modifying therapy - prevention is the treatment","Mixed dementia is the rule in the elderly - vascular and Alzheimer pathology usually travel together","Young patient with migraines, lacunar strokes, and a family history? Think CADASIL"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Vascular Cognitive Impairment Statement","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vascular Dementia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430817/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-rpd","name":"Rapidly Progressive Dementia (incl Creutzfeldt-Jakob)","sec":"neuro","present":["Cognitive decline to dementia over WEEKS TO MONTHS (not years) - a distinct, urgent category because a meaningful fraction of causes are TREATABLE and must not be missed","The mnemonic-worthy differential (VITAMINS): Vascular, Infectious, Toxic-metabolic, Autoimmune (limbic/autoimmune encephalitis), Metastatic/neoplastic (incl paraneoplastic, CNS lymphoma), Iatrogenic, Neurodegenerative (prion), and Systemic/Seizure","Prion disease (Creutzfeldt-Jakob) classically presents with rapidly progressive dementia PLUS MYOCLONUS and ataxia, evolving to akinetic mutism and death over months"],"dx":["Broad, PARALLEL workup because time matters: MRI with DWI (CJD shows cortical ribboning and basal ganglia restricted diffusion), EEG (periodic sharp-wave complexes in CJD), and a wide CSF/serum panel - autoimmune/paraneoplastic antibodies, infectious studies, and CJD markers (RT-QuIC - highly specific, plus 14-3-3/total tau)","Aggressively pursue the TREATABLE causes first (autoimmune encephalitis, CNS infection, vasculitis, toxic-metabolic, neoplastic) - do not anchor on a neurodegenerative/prion diagnosis prematurely","Consider paraneoplastic causes with a tumor search; CJD remains a diagnosis supported by imaging/CSF (definitive is pathologic)"],"tx":["Treat the identified reversible cause promptly: immunotherapy for autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis, antimicrobials for infection, correction of toxic-metabolic derangements, oncologic therapy - the entire point of the urgent workup","Prion disease has NO effective treatment - care is supportive and palliative; institute infection-control/prion precautions and mandatory reporting","Symptom management (myoclonus, seizures, agitation) and family/goals-of-care support"],"pearls":["Dementia over weeks-to-months is a medical emergency workup - the reversible autoimmune and infectious causes are the ones you cannot afford to miss","Rapidly progressive dementia with myoclonus and cortical ribboning/basal ganglia restriction on DWI is Creutzfeldt-Jakob until the treatable mimics are excluded","RT-QuIC has made CJD diagnosis far more specific - but rule out autoimmune encephalitis first, because that one is treatable","Run the workup in parallel, not in sequence - the clock is the enemy in rapidly progressive dementia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Rapidly Progressive Dementia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507860/"},{"t":"CDC Prion Diseases","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/prions"}]},{"id":"neuro-bacterial-meningitis","name":"Bacterial Meningitis","sec":"neuro","present":["A neurologic and infectious EMERGENCY: fever, headache, neck stiffness, and altered mental status; the classic triad is often incomplete, so a lower threshold is needed, especially at extremes of age and in the immunocompromised","Meningismus (nuchal rigidity, Kernig/Brudzinski), photophobia, and rapidly progressive encephalopathy; petechial/purpuric rash suggests meningococcemia","Common pathogens vary by age/host: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis in adults, add Listeria at extremes of age/immunocompromise, and consider healthcare-associated organisms post-neurosurgery"],"dx":["Do NOT delay antibiotics for the workup: obtain blood cultures and START empiric antibiotics + dexamethasone immediately, then perform LUMBAR PUNCTURE - get a HEAD CT before LP only if focal deficit, papilledema, immunocompromise, seizure, or decreased consciousness (herniation risk)","CSF in bacterial meningitis: elevated opening pressure, NEUTROPHILIC pleocytosis, LOW glucose (CSF:serum ratio low), HIGH protein, positive Gram stain/culture; PCR/multiplex panels aid pathogen identification","Distinguish from viral (lymphocytic, normal glucose) and other meningitides"],"tx":["EMPIRIC therapy without delay: vancomycin + a third-generation cephalosporin (ceftriaxone/cefotaxime), ADD AMPICILLIN when Listeria is a concern (age >50, immunocompromised, pregnant, neonate); adjust for post-neurosurgical/penetrating settings","ADJUNCTIVE DEXAMETHASONE before or with the first antibiotic dose reduces morbidity/mortality in pneumococcal meningitis - give it early or not at all","Droplet precautions and CHEMOPROPHYLAXIS for close contacts of meningococcal disease; manage complications (raised ICP, seizures, hydrocephalus, cerebritis) and report notifiable disease"],"pearls":["Antibiotics do not wait for the LP - blood cultures, empiric antibiotics + dexamethasone, then tap; a normal CT does not make an LP safe, but the listed red flags require CT first","Add ampicillin for Listeria at the extremes of age, in pregnancy, and in the immunocompromised - the cephalosporin misses it","Dexamethasone must go in early (with or before the first antibiotic) to help in pneumococcal meningitis - late steroids do not work","A purpuric rash with meningitis is meningococcemia - isolate, treat, and give contacts chemoprophylaxis"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Bacterial Meningitis Guideline","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bacterial Meningitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470351/"},{"t":"CDC Meningitis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/meningitis"}]},{"id":"neuro-encephalitis","name":"Viral & HSV Encephalitis","sec":"neuro","present":["Inflammation of the brain PARENCHYMA (distinct from meningitis) presenting with altered mental status/encephalopathy PLUS fever, seizures, focal deficits, and psychiatric/behavioral change","HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS (HSV-1) encephalitis is the treatable can't-miss: temporal-lobe predilection causing memory disturbance, aphasia, personality change, and focal seizures - untreated it is often fatal or devastating","Broad viral differential (arboviruses/West Nile, enteroviruses, VZV) and the key overlap with AUTOIMMUNE encephalitis (which HSV can even trigger post-infectiously)"],"dx":["Neuroimaging (MRI - HSV shows TEMPORAL LOBE edema/enhancement, sometimes hemorrhagic) and EEG (focal temporal slowing/epileptiform - PLEDs); LP with CSF showing LYMPHOCYTIC pleocytosis, elevated protein, often some RBCs","CSF HSV PCR is the diagnostic test (may be falsely NEGATIVE in the first ~72 h - repeat if suspicion is high and do not stop empiric therapy on an early negative); broaden viral PCR panel and send autoimmune encephalitis antibodies given the overlap","Do not delay treatment for confirmation"],"tx":["START empiric IV ACYCLOVIR IMMEDIATELY in any suspected encephalitis - do not wait for PCR; early treatment is the single biggest determinant of outcome in HSV encephalitis","Continue acyclovir for confirmed HSV encephalitis (typically ~14-21 days) with attention to renal function/hydration; manage seizures, raised ICP, and supportive/ICU care","If a patient improves then RELAPSES with new psychiatric/movement symptoms after HSV encephalitis, consider post-HSV ANTI-NMDAR autoimmune encephalitis and treat with immunotherapy"],"pearls":["Empiric acyclovir goes in the moment encephalitis is suspected - the drug is safe and the delay is what causes the devastating outcomes","An early negative CSF HSV PCR does not exclude it - repeat the PCR and keep treating if the picture fits","Temporal lobe involvement on MRI/EEG in an encephalopathic febrile patient is HSV until proven otherwise","Relapse after HSV encephalitis with psychiatric/movement symptoms is often anti-NMDAR autoimmune encephalitis - a second, immunotherapy-responsive disease"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Encephalitis Guideline","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Herpes Simplex Encephalitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560862/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-neuro-infections-systemic","name":"Neurologic Complications of Systemic Infection (Neurosyphilis, HIV, Lyme)","sec":"neuro","present":["Systemic infections with protean NEUROLOGIC manifestations that must be actively considered - they are treatable great mimics","NEUROSYPHILIS: can occur at any stage - early (meningitis, ocular/otic, meningovascular stroke in a young patient) or late (tabes dorsalis - sensory ataxia/lightning pains; general paresis - dementia/psychiatric); Argyll Robertson pupils are classic","HIV: aseptic meningitis at seroconversion, HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder, and opportunistic CNS disease with advanced immunosuppression (toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis, PML, CNS lymphoma, CMV). LYME (neuroborreliosis): facial palsy (often BILATERAL), lymphocytic meningitis, and painful radiculitis"],"dx":["Neurosyphilis: serum treponemal + nontreponemal (RPR) tests, then LP with CSF-VDRL (specific) and CSF pleocytosis/protein; test/treat and screen for HIV co-infection","HIV: HIV testing in undiagnosed patients; in known HIV, CD4-stratified workup for opportunistic infections (CSF cryptococcal antigen, Toxoplasma serology/imaging with ring-enhancing lesions, JC virus PCR for PML, CMV PCR) and neuroimaging","Lyme neuroborreliosis: serologic testing (ELISA with confirmatory immunoblot) plus CSF studies in endemic exposure; consider these infections in unexplained neurologic syndromes"],"tx":["Neurosyphilis: IV PENICILLIN G (aqueous crystalline) for 10-14 days - the standard regimen; follow CSF/serologic response and treat partners; watch for Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction","HIV: antiretroviral therapy plus PATHOGEN-SPECIFIC treatment (pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine for toxoplasmosis, induction amphotericin B + flucytosine for cryptococcal meningitis with ICP management, etc.); immune reconstitution considerations","Lyme neuroborreliosis: appropriate antibiotics (IV ceftriaxone or oral doxycycline per manifestation/guidelines); most neurologic Lyme responds well to therapy"],"pearls":["Consider neurosyphilis in any unexplained young stroke, dementia, or ocular inflammation - and always co-test for HIV","A ring-enhancing brain lesion in advanced HIV is toxoplasmosis vs CNS lymphoma - CD4 count, serology, and response to empiric therapy guide you","Bilateral facial palsy in an endemic area is Lyme neuroborreliosis until proven otherwise","These infectious mimics are treatable - the error is failing to think of them in a puzzling neurologic presentation"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA / CDC STI & Lyme Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neurosyphilis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK540979/"},{"t":"CDC HIV / Lyme / Syphilis Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"neuro-myelopathy","name":"Acute Myelopathy & Transverse Myelitis","sec":"neuro","present":["Spinal cord dysfunction producing a SENSORY LEVEL, para-/quadriparesis, and bladder/bowel involvement - a neurologic emergency because the first, urgent job is to exclude COMPRESSION","Transverse myelitis: inflammatory, non-compressive cord lesion with subacute weakness, sensory level, and sphincter dysfunction; causes include demyelinating disease (MS - short-segment; NMOSD/MOGAD - LONGITUDINALLY EXTENSIVE), postinfectious, systemic autoimmune, and paraneoplastic","Distinguish from vascular (spinal cord infarction - hyperacute, anterior cord pattern), compressive (tumor, abscess, disc), and metabolic/nutritional myelopathies"],"dx":["URGENT MRI of the spine WITH CONTRAST first to EXCLUDE A COMPRESSIVE lesion (surgical emergency); it also characterizes inflammatory lesions (short vs longitudinally extensive - the latter suggests NMOSD/MOGAD)","If non-compressive: LP (inflammatory CSF, oligoclonal bands), brain MRI (MS lesions), and antibody testing (AQP4-IgG, MOG-IgG) plus a workup for systemic autoimmune/infectious/paraneoplastic causes","Consider B12/copper deficiency (subacute combined degeneration - dorsal column pattern) and dural AV fistula in progressive myelopathy"],"tx":["COMPRESSIVE myelopathy -> emergent decompression/treat the cause (surgery/radiation - the surgical lens); do not miss it","Inflammatory transverse myelitis: high-dose IV corticosteroids, with PLASMA EXCHANGE for severe or steroid-refractory cases; treat the underlying disease (MS/NMOSD/MOGAD-directed maintenance, immunotherapy for systemic autoimmune)","Spinal cord infarction: supportive/risk-factor management; nutritional myelopathy - replace B12/copper; rehabilitation and bladder management across causes"],"pearls":["Rule out compression FIRST with urgent contrast MRI - a compressive myelopathy is a surgical emergency masquerading as transverse myelitis","Longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (>=3 segments) points to NMOSD/MOGAD, not typical MS - send the antibodies","A dorsal-column sensory myelopathy with a macrocytic anemia is B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration) - and folate alone will not fix it","A progressive myelopathy with cord edema and dorsal flow voids may be a spinal dural AV fistula - treatable if recognized"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Transverse Myelitis Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Transverse Myelitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559302/"},{"t":"Siegel Rare Neuroimmune Association","u":"https://wearesrna.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-diplopia-cn-palsies","name":"Diplopia & Ocular Motor Cranial Nerve Palsies (III, IV, VI)","sec":"neuro","present":["BINOCULAR diplopia (resolves when either eye is covered) localizes to the ocular motor system (CN III, IV, VI, the neuromuscular junction, or muscle); MONOCULAR diplopia (persists with one eye covered) is usually an ocular/refractive problem","CN III palsy: ptosis and a 'down-and-out' eye; the PUPIL is the key - a painful, pupil-INVOLVING third-nerve palsy is a POSTERIOR COMMUNICATING ARTERY ANEURYSM until proven otherwise (compressive lesions hit the peripheral pupillary fibers)","CN IV (vertical diplopia worse on downgaze/reading, head tilt) and CN VI (horizontal diplopia, esotropia, impaired abduction - a nonspecific false-localizing sign of raised ICP)"],"dx":["The pupil rule in CN III palsy drives urgency: PUPIL-INVOLVING (or painful) third-nerve palsy -> urgent vascular imaging (CTA/MRA, catheter angiography) for a compressive ANEURYSM; pupil-SPARING isolated III in a vasculopath is often ischemic (microvascular) and observed","Consider myasthenia gravis (fatigable, pupil-spared, variable - can mimic any pattern) and thyroid eye disease; MRI for atypical, multiple, or progressive cranial neuropathies and for suspected brainstem/cavernous sinus lesions","Screen giant cell arteritis in older patients with diplopia/ischemic palsy (ESR/CRP)"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE: secure an aneurysm causing a third-nerve palsy (neurosurgery/neuro-IR - emergency); microvascular ischemic palsies typically resolve over weeks-months with vascular risk-factor management","Treat myasthenia (see MG entry), thyroid eye disease, and giant cell arteritis (urgent steroids) as indicated","Symptomatic relief of diplopia (patching, prisms) while awaiting recovery; refer to neuro-ophthalmology for complex/incomplete recovery"],"pearls":["Binocular vs monocular is the first fork - binocular diplopia is a neurologic/ocular-motor problem, monocular is an eyeball/refraction problem","The pupil makes the third-nerve-palsy decision: pupil-involving or painful means image urgently for a posterior communicating artery aneurysm","A sixth-nerve palsy can be a false localizing sign of raised intracranial pressure - look for papilledema and a cause","Fatigable, pupil-sparing, variable ophthalmoplegia that crosses single-nerve boundaries is myasthenia gravis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAN Neuro-Ophthalmology Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Oculomotor Nerve Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430919/"},{"t":"North American Neuro-Ophthalmology Society","u":"https://www.nanosweb.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-vertigo-central-peripheral","name":"Central vs Peripheral Vertigo (Neurology Lens)","sec":"neuro","present":["The central task is separating benign PERIPHERAL vestibular causes (BPPV, vestibular neuritis, Meniere) from dangerous CENTRAL causes (posterior-circulation stroke, especially in the acute vestibular syndrome)","Peripheral clues: severe spinning with nausea, unidirectional horizontal nystagmus that suppresses with fixation, and a positional or brief time course; central clues: prominent gait/truncal ataxia out of proportion, direction-changing or vertical nystagmus, and associated brainstem signs","An isolated acute vestibular syndrome can be a cerebellar/brainstem STROKE masquerading as vestibular neuritis"],"dx":["For the acute continuous vestibular syndrome with nystagmus, the HINTS exam (Head Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew) outperforms early MRI: a PERIPHERAL pattern = abnormal head impulse (corrective saccade) + unidirectional nystagmus + no skew; ANY central feature (normal head impulse, direction-changing/vertical nystagmus, or skew deviation) points to stroke","BPPV: Dix-Hallpike elicits upbeating-torsional, fatigable positional nystagmus; a normal head impulse WITH spontaneous nystagmus in acute vestibular syndrome is worrisome for central cause","Early MRI (with DWI) can be falsely negative in small posterior-fossa strokes within the first 48 h - HINTS done correctly is more sensitive acutely; image and admit when central features exist"],"tx":["BPPV: canalith repositioning (Epley) - definitive and drug-free; avoid chronic vestibular suppressants","Vestibular neuritis: brief symptomatic vestibular suppressants (24-72 h only) then vestibular rehabilitation; steroids sometimes used; Meniere - salt restriction, diuretics, and ENT referral","CENTRAL cause (stroke): admit and manage as a posterior-circulation stroke (see stroke entries); do not discharge a 'benign vertigo' with central red flags"],"pearls":["HINTS beats MRI acutely for the vestibular syndrome - but only when done correctly and only in the continuously vertiginous patient WITH nystagmus","A NORMAL head impulse test in a patient with acute spontaneous vertigo and nystagmus is a red flag for stroke, not reassurance","Gait ataxia out of proportion to the vertigo (cannot stand/walk) is central until proven otherwise","An early negative MRI does not exclude a posterior-fossa stroke in the first 48 hours - trust a worrisome exam over an early scan"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / Barany Society Vertigo Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vertigo (Central vs Peripheral)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482356/"},{"t":"American Academy of Otolaryngology","u":"https://www.entnet.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-coma","name":"Coma & Approach to Altered Mental Status","sec":"neuro","present":["Coma is a state of unarousable unresponsiveness; the approach is a rapid, structured localization + resuscitation problem - is this a diffuse/metabolic-toxic process or a structural lesion (and does it threaten herniation)?","Consciousness requires the brainstem ascending reticular activating system PLUS at least one cerebral hemisphere - so coma implies bilateral hemispheric dysfunction (usually toxic-metabolic) or a brainstem/reticular lesion","Assess level (GCS), and the brainstem: pupils, extraocular/oculocephalic reflexes, corneal, gag, and motor responses/posturing"],"dx":["Simultaneous stabilization and workup: ABCs, immediate GLUCOSE, and empiric reversal where indicated (thiamine before glucose, naloxone for opioid toxidrome); labs (electrolytes, ammonia, LFTs, tox screen, ABG), and consider infection","The brainstem exam LOCALIZES: reactive pupils with preserved reflexes favor a metabolic cause; asymmetric/fixed pupils, gaze deviation, or focal posturing favor a structural lesion -> urgent CT; LP if meningitis/encephalitis suspected (after CT when needed)","EEG to detect NONCONVULSIVE STATUS EPILEPTICUS (a treatable cause of unexplained coma); MRI for posterior fossa/brainstem or when CT is unrevealing"],"tx":["Treat the reversible causes fast: glucose (with thiamine), naloxone, correct severe electrolyte/metabolic derangements, treat infection, and reverse toxins/overdose per toxidrome","Structural lesions with mass effect/herniation -> neurosurgical/ICP management (see the neurosurgery ICP entry); nonconvulsive status -> antiseizure treatment","Supportive neurocritical care and serial exams; determine prognosis/goals with families once the cause and trajectory are clearer (avoid premature prognostication, especially post-arrest before adequate observation)"],"pearls":["Coma is bilateral hemispheric or brainstem/reticular dysfunction - the brainstem exam tells you structural versus metabolic in minutes","Give glucose (with thiamine first in the at-risk) and consider naloxone before anything fancy - the fastest reversibles come first","Unexplained coma may be nonconvulsive status epilepticus - get an EEG, because it is treatable and invisible without one","Reactive pupils and intact brainstem reflexes in a comatose patient point toward a metabolic-toxic cause - a structural lesion usually breaks the symmetry"],"refs":[{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society / AAN Coma Guidance","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Coma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430722/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-toxic-metabolic","name":"Toxic-Metabolic Encephalopathy & Wernicke","sec":"neuro","present":["Diffuse cerebral dysfunction (altered attention/consciousness, confusion, ASTERIXIS, myoclonus, often waxing-waning) from a systemic derangement rather than a focal brain lesion - the exam is usually NON-focal","Broad causes: hepatic and uremic encephalopathy, hyper/hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, hypo/hyperglycemia, hypercapnia, thyroid disease, sepsis-associated encephalopathy, and DRUGS/toxins (including serotonin syndrome, medication effects, alcohol/withdrawal)","WERNICKE ENCEPHALOPATHY (thiamine deficiency): the classic triad of CONFUSION, OPHTHALMOPLEGIA/nystagmus, and ATAXIA - though the full triad is often absent, so treat on suspicion"],"dx":["Targeted metabolic workup: electrolytes (Na, Ca, glucose), ammonia, renal/hepatic function, ABG (CO2), TSH, infection screen, medication/tox review; EEG may show diffuse slowing (and triphasic waves in metabolic encephalopathies) and excludes nonconvulsive status","Neuroimaging to exclude a structural cause when focal signs, trauma, or diagnostic uncertainty exist; Wernicke is a CLINICAL diagnosis (MRI may show mammillary body/periaqueductal changes but is not required)","Identify the specific derangement so it can be corrected"],"tx":["Correct the underlying metabolic derangement (lactulose/rifaximin for hepatic encephalopathy, careful sodium correction, dialysis for uremia, treat infection, stop/adjust offending drugs)","WERNICKE: give high-dose PARENTERAL THIAMINE promptly and BEFORE glucose in at-risk patients (glucose loading can precipitate/worsen Wernicke) - undertreatment risks irreversible Korsakoff amnestic syndrome; treat empirically when suspected","Supportive care and prevention of secondary injury; monitor for improvement as the cause is corrected"],"pearls":["A confused patient with asterixis and a non-focal exam has a metabolic-toxic encephalopathy until proven otherwise - find and fix the derangement","Give thiamine before glucose in anyone at nutritional risk - glucose first can precipitate Wernicke","Treat Wernicke on suspicion with high-dose parenteral thiamine - the full triad is usually incomplete, and waiting risks permanent Korsakoff amnesia","Triphasic waves on EEG suggest a metabolic encephalopathy (e.g., hepatic) but are not specific - and always exclude nonconvulsive status"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / EFNS Wernicke Encephalopathy Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Wernicke Encephalopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430729/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-wilson-metabolic","name":"Wilson Disease & Inherited Metabolic Neurology","sec":"neuro","present":["Wilson disease: an autosomal recessive disorder of copper transport (ATP7B) causing copper accumulation - a TREATABLE cause of neurologic disease that must not be missed in a young patient","Neurologic/psychiatric presentation (often 2nd-3rd decade): movement disorder (tremor - classically 'wing-beating', dystonia, parkinsonism, dysarthria, drooling), and psychiatric/behavioral change; hepatic presentation (hepatitis, cirrhosis, acute liver failure) may precede or coexist","Broader category: consider inherited metabolic disorders (leukodystrophies, lysosomal/mitochondrial disease, urea cycle defects, Fabry) in young patients with unexplained progressive neurologic disease and systemic clues"],"dx":["Wilson workup: LOW serum CERULOPLASMIN, elevated 24-hour URINARY COPPER, and slit-lamp exam for KAYSER-FLEISCHER RINGS (copper in Descemet membrane - present in most with neurologic disease); confirm with liver copper/genetic testing as needed","Brain MRI may show basal ganglia changes (the 'face of the giant panda' midbrain sign); assess hepatic involvement","Screen SIBLINGS (autosomal recessive) - presymptomatic treatment prevents disease; a low threshold to test any young person with an unexplained movement disorder, dystonia, or psychiatric change"],"tx":["Wilson disease is TREATABLE with lifelong therapy: copper CHELATION (penicillamine or trientine) and/or ZINC (blocks intestinal copper absorption), plus a low-copper diet; neurologic worsening can occur early with chelation - monitor closely","Liver transplantation for acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis; treat symptomatically and rehabilitate","For other inherited metabolic disorders: enzyme replacement/substrate reduction where available (e.g., Fabry), supportive/multidisciplinary care, and genetic counseling"],"pearls":["Any young patient with a movement disorder, dystonia, or new psychiatric symptoms - especially with liver disease - gets a Wilson workup, because it is treatable and missing it is tragic","Low ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper, and Kayser-Fleischer rings make the diagnosis; the rings are almost always present when there is neurologic disease","Screen the siblings - presymptomatic treatment of Wilson disease prevents the whole disease","Chelation can transiently worsen neurologic symptoms - start carefully and monitor"],"refs":[{"t":"AASLD Wilson Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.aasld.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Wilson Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441990/"},{"t":"Wilson Disease Association","u":"https://www.wilsondisease.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-mitochondrial","name":"Mitochondrial Disease","sec":"neuro","present":["Multisystem disorders of oxidative phosphorylation that preferentially affect HIGH-ENERGY tissues (brain, muscle, eye, heart, endocrine) - suspect when otherwise-unrelated organ problems cluster in one patient","Recognizable neurologic syndromes: MELAS (mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, STROKE-LIKE episodes that cross vascular territories), MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibers), Leigh syndrome (infantile), CPEO/Kearns-Sayre (progressive external ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, pigmentary retinopathy, cardiac conduction block), and Leber hereditary optic neuropathy","Clues: maternal inheritance pattern (mtDNA), exercise intolerance, short stature, sensorineural hearing loss, diabetes, cardiomyopathy, and lactic acidosis"],"dx":["Screening: elevated lactate (serum/CSF), and pattern recognition of the multisystem clustering; MRI (stroke-like lesions crossing territories in MELAS, basal ganglia/brainstem in Leigh); MR spectroscopy lactate peak","Definitive workup: GENETIC testing (mtDNA for classic maternally inherited syndromes - e.g., m.3243A>G in MELAS - and nuclear gene panels, since many mitochondrial disorders are nuclear-encoded); muscle biopsy (ragged-red fibers, COX-negative fibers) and respiratory-chain enzymology when genetics are unrevealing","Assess ALL organs (audiology, ophthalmology, cardiac conduction/echo, endocrine) - the disease is systemic"],"tx":["Largely SUPPORTIVE and multisystem - there is no cure: treat seizures, manage cardiac conduction disease (pacemaker in Kearns-Sayre), diabetes, hearing loss, and provide rehabilitation","Avoid mitochondrial TOXINS/precipitants: certain drugs (valproate can be hepatotoxic/deadly in POLG-related disease; aminoglycosides worsen hearing; some antiretrovirals; metformin/lactic-acidosis caution) and physiologic stress (fasting, dehydration, illness)","'Mito cocktails' (coenzyme Q10, riboflavin, etc.) have limited evidence but are commonly used; L-arginine is used for acute MELAS stroke-like episodes; genetic counseling given inheritance complexity (mtDNA heteroplasmy)"],"pearls":["Unexplained multisystem disease (brain + muscle + eye + heart + endocrine) with lactic acidosis and maternal inheritance is mitochondrial until proven otherwise","Stroke-like episodes that do NOT respect vascular territories are the MELAS signature - not embolic stroke","Avoid valproate in suspected POLG-related mitochondrial disease - it can precipitate fatal liver failure","Think mitochondrial when ptosis and progressive ophthalmoplegia pair with a cardiac conduction block (Kearns-Sayre) - that patient needs cardiology and possibly a pacemaker"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / Mitochondrial Medicine Society Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mitochondrial Myopathies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565602/"},{"t":"United Mitochondrial Disease Foundation","u":"https://www.umdf.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-neuro-oncology","name":"Neuro-Oncology - Medical Management & Neurologic Complications","sec":"neuro","present":["The neurologist's role in cancer: managing the NEUROLOGIC COMPLICATIONS of tumors and their treatment, distinct from the surgical resection (the neurosurgery lens)","Common problems: peritumoral EDEMA and raised ICP, tumor-related SEIZURES, brain/leptomeningeal metastases, and epidural spinal cord compression (an oncologic emergency)","Treatment-related neurotoxicity is increasingly important: chemotherapy neuropathy, radiation necrosis/leukoencephalopathy, and IMMUNOTHERAPY-related neurologic events (immune checkpoint inhibitor encephalitis, myasthenia-like syndromes, neuropathy, and CAR-T neurotoxicity/ICANS)"],"dx":["MRI with contrast for parenchymal/leptomeningeal disease; LP with cytology (often repeated) for LEPTOMENINGEAL metastasis; EEG for seizures; distinguish RADIATION NECROSIS from tumor progression (perfusion/spectroscopy, sometimes biopsy)","For neurologic decline in a cancer patient, generate a broad differential: metastasis/progression, paraneoplastic syndrome (antibody testing), treatment neurotoxicity, infection (immunosuppressed), metabolic, and vascular","Recognize immunotherapy neurotoxicity syndromes early (checkpoint-inhibitor encephalitis/neuropathy; CAR-T ICANS with the ICE score) - they are treatable and time-sensitive"],"tx":["Peritumoral edema/raised ICP: CORTICOSTEROIDS (dexamethasone) - dramatic symptom relief; taper to lowest effective dose; seizures: antiseizure medication for those who have seized (routine prophylaxis is NOT recommended), preferring non-enzyme-inducing agents (levetiracetam) to avoid chemo interactions","Epidural spinal cord compression: steroids + urgent radiation/surgery (see the neurosurgery entry); brain metastases: coordinate SRS/WBRT/surgery and systemic therapy; leptomeningeal disease: intrathecal/systemic therapy and radiation, poor prognosis","Immunotherapy neurotoxicity: hold the offending agent and give corticosteroids (and IVIG/PLEX or further immunosuppression for severe checkpoint-inhibitor syndromes); ICANS - supportive care and steroids per protocol; treat chemo-induced neuropathy symptomatically and adjust dosing"],"pearls":["Dexamethasone is the fast fix for symptomatic peritumoral edema - but do not give routine seizure prophylaxis to brain tumor patients who have never seized","Prefer levetiracetam over enzyme-inducing antiseizure drugs in cancer patients to avoid gutting chemotherapy levels","New neurologic symptoms on immunotherapy are a treatable emergency - checkpoint-inhibitor encephalitis and CAR-T ICANS need prompt recognition and steroids","Radiation necrosis can perfectly mimic tumor progression on MRI - perfusion imaging or biopsy may be needed to tell them apart"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN CNS / Immunotherapy Toxicity Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Paraneoplastic & Neuro-Oncology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430743/"},{"t":"Society for Neuro-Oncology","u":"https://www.soc-neuro-onc.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-fnd","name":"Functional Neurological Disorder","sec":"neuro","present":["Genuine neurologic symptoms (weakness, movement disorders, sensory loss, functional seizures, gait disorder) arising from altered nervous-system FUNCTIONING rather than structural disease - real, common, and not feigned","A RULE-IN diagnosis based on positive clinical signs of internal inconsistency and incongruence with recognized disease - NOT a diagnosis of exclusion or 'nothing found'","Frequently coexists with structural neurologic and psychiatric conditions; onset may follow physical injury, illness, or stress (though a psychological trigger is not required)"],"dx":["POSITIVE signs make the diagnosis: HOOVER SIGN (functional leg weakness normalizes with contralateral hip flexion), a positive drift without pronation, tremor ENTRAINMENT (functional tremor takes on the frequency of a tasked movement), the tremor-distractibility/variability, and the collapsing/give-way weakness pattern","Functional seizures are confirmed by video-EEG (see the PNES entry); limit unnecessary investigations once positive signs are present to avoid iatrogenic harm and reinforcement","Screen for and treat comorbid structural disease and psychiatric conditions - they coexist and do not exclude FND"],"tx":["The EXPLANATION of the diagnosis is the pivotal first treatment: deliver it clearly and non-stigmatizing (real, common, treatable, and how the positive signs demonstrate it) - a good explanation improves engagement and outcomes; showing the patient their own Hoover sign can be therapeutic","Multidisciplinary, symptom-tailored rehabilitation: PHYSIOTHERAPY (functional motor/gait), occupational therapy, and psychological therapy (CBT) - especially for functional seizures; specialist FND programs when available","Treat comorbid depression/anxiety; avoid unnecessary tests, medications, and procedures that reinforce illness"],"pearls":["FND is a rule-IN diagnosis from positive signs (Hoover, entrainment, distractibility) - not a label for 'we found nothing'","How you explain it IS the treatment - a validating, mechanistic explanation (not 'it is all in your head') determines whether the patient engages","Demonstrating the Hoover sign to the patient can be both diagnostic and therapeutic","FND coexists with real structural disease - finding one does not exclude the other, and over-investigating causes harm"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Functional Neurological Disorder Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Functional Neurologic Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551567/"},{"t":"FND Society","u":"https://www.fndsociety.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-sleep","name":"Sleep Neurology (Narcolepsy, REM Sleep Behavior Disorder)","sec":"neuro","present":["NARCOLEPSY: chronic excessive daytime sleepiness with irresistible sleep attacks; type 1 adds CATAPLEXY (sudden emotion-triggered loss of muscle tone with preserved consciousness) from loss of hypocretin/orexin neurons; also sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations (the classic tetrad)","REM SLEEP BEHAVIOR DISORDER (RBD): loss of normal REM atonia leading to DREAM ENACTMENT (punching, kicking, vocalizing) with injury risk - a strong PRODROMAL marker of synucleinopathies (Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia, MSA)","Distinguish primary sleep disorders from sleep-deprivation and from obstructive sleep apnea (the most common cause of daytime sleepiness)"],"dx":["Narcolepsy: overnight polysomnography followed by the MULTIPLE SLEEP LATENCY TEST (short sleep latency and >=2 sleep-onset REM periods); low CSF hypocretin-1 confirms type 1; exclude other causes of sleepiness (OSA, insufficient sleep, medications)","RBD: polysomnography demonstrating REM sleep WITHOUT ATONIA plus the clinical history of dream enactment; screen for the underlying/emerging synucleinopathy","Epworth Sleepiness Scale and a sleep history/diary frame the complaint"],"tx":["Narcolepsy: scheduled naps and sleep hygiene plus wake-promoting agents (modafinil/armodafinil, solriamfetol, pitolisant, stimulants); cataplexy responds to sodium oxybate, pitolisant, or antidepressants (SNRIs/venlafaxine) that suppress REM","RBD: bedroom SAFETY measures (remove hazards, protect the bed partner) and melatonin (first-line, well tolerated) or clonazepam; counsel about the long-term neurodegeneration risk with appropriate sensitivity","Treat comorbid OSA and optimize sleep; refer to sleep medicine for testing and management"],"pearls":["Cataplexy - emotion-triggered loss of tone with preserved awareness - is nearly specific for narcolepsy type 1 and reflects hypocretin loss","REM sleep behavior disorder is an early synucleinopathy flag - many patients later develop Parkinson disease or Lewy body dementia, so counsel and follow","Prioritize bedroom safety in RBD - the injuries (to patient and bed partner) are the immediate danger; melatonin is a gentle first-line","Rule out obstructive sleep apnea first - it is the most common cause of the daytime sleepiness that prompts a narcolepsy workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AASM Narcolepsy & RBD Guidelines","u":"https://aasm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Narcolepsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459236/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-ataxia","name":"Hereditary & Acquired Ataxias","sec":"neuro","present":["Ataxia is incoordination from cerebellar (or sensory/vestibular) dysfunction: gait unsteadiness, limb dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, intention tremor, dysarthria (scanning speech), and nystagmus","Localize: CEREBELLAR ataxia (worsens with eyes open or closed, with cerebellar signs) vs SENSORY ataxia (proprioceptive loss - positive Romberg, worse in the dark) vs vestibular","Sort by tempo/cause: acute (stroke, toxin/alcohol, Miller Fisher, Wernicke), subacute (paraneoplastic, immune, structural), and chronic/progressive (hereditary - Friedreich ataxia, spinocerebellar ataxias - and acquired degenerative such as MSA-C)"],"dx":["Characterize onset and associated features; MRI (cerebellar/brainstem atrophy or lesion) is central; the TEMPO drives the workup","Acquired/reversible causes to pursue: alcohol/nutritional (thiamine, vitamin E), toxins/drugs (phenytoin, lithium), autoimmune/paraneoplastic (antibody panels, tumor search), hypothyroidism, celiac (gluten ataxia), and structural lesions","Hereditary workup for chronic progressive ataxia: genetic testing (Friedreich ataxia - GAA repeat; spinocerebellar ataxia repeat panels), family history, and age of onset"],"tx":["Treat the TREATABLE/acquired causes: replace vitamins (thiamine, E), remove the toxin/offending drug, immunotherapy for autoimmune/paraneoplastic ataxia (and treat the tumor), gluten-free diet for gluten ataxia, thyroid replacement","Hereditary ataxias are largely supportive - physical/occupational/speech therapy, mobility aids, and management of associated features (Friedreich: cardiomyopathy, diabetes, scoliosis surveillance); some emerging disease-specific therapies","Genetic counseling and multidisciplinary rehabilitation across chronic ataxias"],"pearls":["Separate cerebellar from sensory ataxia at the bedside - a positive Romberg (much worse with eyes closed) points to proprioceptive/sensory loss, not the cerebellum","The tempo tells the story - acute ataxia hunts for stroke, toxin, or Wernicke; chronic progressive ataxia hunts for a genetic cause","Do not miss the reversible ataxias - alcohol/thiamine, vitamin E, celiac (gluten ataxia), hypothyroidism, and paraneoplastic are treatable","Friedreich ataxia is more than ataxia - screen for the cardiomyopathy and diabetes that drive its morbidity"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Ataxia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ataxia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562284/"},{"t":"National Ataxia Foundation","u":"https://www.ataxia.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-autonomic","name":"Autonomic Disorders - Orthostatic Hypotension & POTS","sec":"neuro","present":["Autonomic dysfunction presenting with orthostatic intolerance: lightheadedness, presyncope, fatigue, and cognitive fog on standing, sometimes with sweating/GI/bladder disturbance","Neurogenic ORTHOSTATIC HYPOTENSION: a sustained fall in BP on standing (>=20 mmHg systolic or >=10 mmHg diastolic) WITHOUT an appropriate compensatory heart-rate rise - seen in synucleinopathies (Parkinson disease, MSA, pure autonomic failure), diabetic/amyloid autonomic neuropathy, and medications","POSTURAL ORTHOSTATIC TACHYCARDIA SYNDROME (POTS): a sustained HEART-RATE rise >=30 bpm (>=40 in adolescents) on standing WITHOUT orthostatic hypotension, with orthostatic symptoms - typically young women, often post-viral/deconditioning"],"dx":["Orthostatic vital signs done properly (supine then 1 and 3 min standing) are the bedside test; tilt-table testing and formal autonomic function testing quantify and characterize; distinguish neurogenic OH (no HR compensation) from volume/medication-related OH","For OH, evaluate the underlying cause: synucleinopathy features, diabetic/amyloid neuropathy, medications (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, TCAs, diuretics), and volume status; for POTS, exclude other causes of tachycardia (anemia, thyroid, dehydration, cardiac)","Screen for the broader autonomic footprint (GI dysmotility, bladder, sudomotor, sexual dysfunction)"],"tx":["NONpharmacologic first for both: liberalize salt and FLUIDS (unless contraindicated), compression garments (waist-high/abdominal), gradual position changes, physical counter-maneuvers, and for POTS a structured reconditioning EXERCISE program (the cornerstone) plus removing offending drugs","Neurogenic OH pharmacotherapy when needed: midodrine, droxidopa, or fludrocortisone - and manage the classic SUPINE HYPERTENSION trade-off (avoid dosing at bedtime, elevate the head of the bed)","POTS pharmacotherapy as adjunct: beta-blockers (low dose) or ivabradine for tachycardia, midodrine, and fludrocortisone; treat comorbidities (many have hypermobility/EDS, migraine, GI issues)"],"pearls":["The heart-rate response separates the two: a BP drop WITHOUT a compensatory HR rise is neurogenic orthostatic hypotension; a HR rise WITHOUT a BP drop is POTS","Check the medication list and volume status before diagnosing a primary autonomic disorder - drugs and dehydration are the common culprits","Exercise reconditioning is the cornerstone of POTS treatment - drugs are adjuncts to it, not substitutes","In neurogenic OH, treating the standing pressure risks supine hypertension - avoid bedtime pressors and elevate the head of the bed"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / Autonomic Disorders Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541074/"},{"t":"Dysautonomia International","u":"https://www.dysautonomiainternational.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-phakomatoses","name":"Neurocutaneous Syndromes (Phakomatoses)","sec":"neuro","present":["Inherited disorders with combined NEUROLOGIC and CUTANEOUS features and a predisposition to tumors - the skin is the window to the nervous-system disease","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1): cafe-au-lait macules, axillary/inguinal freckling, neurofibromas, Lisch nodules, optic pathway gliomas; NF2: bilateral VESTIBULAR SCHWANNOMAS, meningiomas, ependymomas (few skin findings)","Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC): facial angiofibromas, ash-leaf/hypomelanotic macules, shagreen patch, cortical tubers/SEGAs, epilepsy (infantile spasms), and rhabdomyomas/angiomyolipomas; Sturge-Weber (facial port-wine stain in V1, leptomeningeal angioma, seizures, glaucoma); von Hippel-Lindau (hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnostic criteria plus a careful skin exam and family history; NF1/NF2/TSC/VHL have defined criteria and genetic testing (NF1, NF2, TSC1/TSC2, VHL genes)","Surveillance imaging and screening tailored to each syndrome (e.g., NF1 optic pathway glioma monitoring; NF2 brain/spine MRI and audiology; TSC brain/renal imaging and EEG; VHL abdominal/CNS imaging and catecholamines for pheochromocytoma)","Multisystem evaluation (ophthalmology, dermatology, audiology, nephrology, cardiology) per syndrome"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary surveillance and complication-directed treatment: manage epilepsy (vigabatrin for TSC infantile spasms), tumors (surgery/observation), and organ-specific disease","Targeted therapies where available: mTOR inhibitors (everolimus/sirolimus) for TSC (SEGAs, angiomyolipomas, seizures) and MEK inhibitors (selumetinib) for symptomatic/inoperable NF1 plexiform neurofibromas","Genetic counseling; screen at-risk relatives; treat pheochromocytoma (VHL) and monitor for the syndrome-specific malignancies"],"pearls":["The skin exam is the screening test - cafe-au-lait macules, ash-leaf spots, angiofibromas, and port-wine stains point to specific neurocutaneous syndromes","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas define NF2 - a different disease from the cafe-au-lait NF1","TSC infantile spasms respond to vigabatrin, and mTOR inhibitors now treat several TSC manifestations - the syndrome has targeted therapy","Von Hippel-Lindau carries pheochromocytoma and renal cell carcinoma - screen catecholamines and image the abdomen, not just the CNS"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / TSC & NF Consensus Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neurocutaneous Syndromes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430837/"},{"t":"Children's Tumor Foundation","u":"https://www.ctf.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-neuropathic-pain","name":"Neuropathic Pain Management","sec":"neuro","present":["Pain arising from a lesion or disease of the SOMATOSENSORY system - described as burning, shooting, electric, or lancinating, with allodynia (pain from non-painful stimuli) and hyperalgesia","Common syndromes: painful diabetic/other polyneuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia, radiculopathy/sciatica, trigeminal neuralgia (see its own entry), central pain (post-stroke, MS), and complex regional pain syndrome","Distinguish from nociceptive pain - the mechanism dictates a different pharmacology (opioids are relatively ineffective and not first-line)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the character and distribution of pain plus evidence of a somatosensory lesion; screening tools (e.g., DN4) help; identify and treat the UNDERLYING cause (glycemic control, nerve compression, herpes zoster)","Examine for the neurologic deficit and localize; electrodiagnostics/skin biopsy when a neuropathy needs characterization (see the peripheral neuropathy entry)","Set realistic goals - meaningful pain REDUCTION and improved function, not complete abolition"],"tx":["First-line agents (evidence-based): gabapentinoids (gabapentin, pregabalin), SNRIs (DULOXETINE, venlafaxine), and tricyclic antidepressants (nortriptyline/amitriptyline - anticholinergic caution in the elderly); titrate to effect and tolerance","Topical options for localized pain (lidocaine 5%, high-concentration capsaicin - especially postherpetic neuralgia); combination therapy for partial responders; treat the underlying disease","AVOID opioids as first-line (limited efficacy, dependence risk) - reserve for select refractory cases; multidisciplinary/pain-psychology and physical therapy for chronic and complex regional pain syndromes; carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia specifically"],"pearls":["Neuropathic pain does not respond well to opioids - gabapentinoids, duloxetine, and TCAs are the evidence-based first line","Treat the cause alongside the symptom - controlling the diabetes or decompressing the root does more than any analgesic","Set expectations for partial relief and better function - promising zero pain sets up failure and escalation","Topical lidocaine and capsaicin shine for localized pain like postherpetic neuralgia - systemic side effects are avoided"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / NeuPSIG Neuropathic Pain Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neuropathic Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554604/"},{"t":"International Association for the Study of Pain","u":"https://www.iasp-pain.org"}]},{"id":"neuro-syncope-vs-seizure","name":"Syncope vs Seizure - Differentiating Transient Events","sec":"neuro","present":["Transient loss of consciousness is a common, high-stakes triage problem - the core task is separating SYNCOPE (transient global cerebral hypoperfusion) from a SEIZURE (abnormal cortical activity), and both from psychogenic events","Syncope clues: prodrome of lightheadedness/nausea/warmth/tunnel vision, provoking triggers (standing, pain, micturition, prolonged standing), rapid recovery, and pallor; brief myoclonic jerks (CONVULSIVE SYNCOPE) can occur and are misread as seizures","Seizure clues: aura, LATERAL TONGUE biting, sustained rhythmic convulsion, head-turning, cyanosis, and prolonged POSTICTAL confusion; consider cardiac syncope (exertional, palpitations, family history of sudden death) as the dangerous subset"],"dx":["The HISTORY (including a witness account) is the most powerful tool: postictal confusion and lateral tongue biting favor seizure; identifiable triggers, rapid orientation, and pallor favor syncope","Syncope workup centers on the HEART: ECG for everyone (look for arrhythmia, long QT, Brugada, conduction disease, ischemia), orthostatic vitals, and cardiac evaluation for red flags (exertional, palpitations, structural disease, family history of sudden death)","Seizure workup: EEG and brain imaging (MRI) for a suspected first seizure; consider video-EEG when spells are recurrent and the diagnosis is unclear (also captures psychogenic events)"],"tx":["Treat the mechanism: reflex/vasovagal syncope - education, trigger avoidance, hydration/salt, counter-maneuvers; orthostatic - see autonomic entry; CARDIAC syncope - urgent cardiology (pacemaker/ICD/antiarrhythmic as indicated) because it carries mortality risk","Seizure - manage per the seizure/epilepsy entries (treat provoked causes, ASM decisions for unprovoked)","Do NOT reflexively start antiseizure medication for convulsive syncope - the jerks are hypoxic myoclonus, and the real problem may be a treatable cardiac cause"],"pearls":["The witness history decides it - postictal confusion and lateral tongue biting mean seizure; a clear trigger with rapid recovery and pallor means syncope","Convulsive syncope (brief jerks from hypoxia) is the classic seizure mimic - do not label it epilepsy and miss a cardiac cause","Every syncope gets an ECG - the dangerous causes (arrhythmia, long QT, Brugada, structural disease) hide there","Exertional loss of consciousness or a family history of sudden death is cardiac until proven otherwise - that is the one that kills"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ACC/HRS Syncope Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Syncope","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442006/"},{"t":"American Academy of Neurology","u":"https://www.aan.com"}]},{"id":"neuro-post-concussion","name":"Post-Concussion Syndrome","sec":"neuro","present":["Persistent symptoms beyond the expected recovery window (weeks to months) after a concussion/mild TBI - the medical-neurology follow-up to the acute injury triage (the acute/surgical assessment is covered in neurosurgery)","Symptom clusters: PHYSICAL (persistent headache - often migrainous or tension-type, dizziness, fatigue, light/noise sensitivity), COGNITIVE (attention, memory, processing-speed complaints, brain fog), and EMOTIONAL/SLEEP (irritability, anxiety, depression, insomnia)","Risk factors for persistence: prior migraine/mental-health history, greater initial symptom burden, and secondary factors (poor sleep, mood, deconditioning) that PERPETUATE symptoms"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; neuroimaging is typically NORMAL and not routinely needed absent red flags (imaging is for the acute injury or new focal signs) - avoid over-testing that reinforces illness","Identify the TREATABLE symptom drivers: post-traumatic headache type (migrainous vs tension vs medication-overuse), vestibular/oculomotor dysfunction, cervicogenic contribution, sleep disruption, and mood/anxiety","Assess for medication-overuse headache (a common, reversible perpetuator) and screen mood/sleep"],"tx":["Early education and reassurance about expected recovery, plus GRADED return to activity - the old 'rest until symptom-free' advice is outdated; relative rest for ~24-48 h then gradual, sub-symptom-threshold reintroduction of cognitive and physical activity (and sub-symptom aerobic exercise aids recovery)","Targeted, symptom-specific therapy: treat post-traumatic HEADACHE by its phenotype (migraine-directed therapy, avoid medication overuse), vestibular/oculomotor rehabilitation for dizziness, cervical physiotherapy, and sleep and MOOD treatment (CBT) - the mood/sleep/pain triad drives persistence","Multidisciplinary concussion clinic for refractory cases; graduated, medically supervised return-to-sport and return-to-work/school plans"],"pearls":["Prolonged strict rest is outdated - early sub-symptom-threshold activity and graded return speed recovery, and prolonged cocooning worsens it","Treat the headache by its type - much post-concussion headache is migrainous and responds to migraine therapy (while medication overuse quietly perpetuates it)","Mood, sleep, and pain are the perpetuating triad - treating depression, insomnia, and headache does more than any 'brain rest'","Normal imaging is expected - resist repeated scans that validate illness rather than recovery"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN / CDC Concussion & Post-Concussion Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postconcussion Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534786/"},{"t":"CDC Traumatic Brain Injury","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/traumaticbraininjury"}]},{"id":"nsg-tbi-severe","name":"Severe Traumatic Brain Injury & ICP-Directed Management","sec":"nsg","present":["GCS <=8 after resuscitation defines severe TBI; the enemy is SECONDARY injury (hypoxia, hypotension, raised ICP) layered on the primary impact","Monro-Kellie doctrine: fixed skull volume (brain + blood + CSF) - once compensatory CSF/venous displacement is exhausted, small volume adds spike ICP catastrophically","Screen the whole trauma patient: hypotension (SBP <90, or <110 per BTF age bands) and hypoxia each independently double mortality - fix them before the head"],"dx":["Noncontrast head CT immediately; grade with Marshall/Rotterdam; repeat CT at 6 h or with any neuro decline (progressive hemorrhagic injury is the rule, not exception)","ICP monitoring (BTF) for salvageable severe TBI with abnormal CT, or normal CT plus >=2 of: age >40, motor posturing, SBP <90","Targets: ICP <22 mmHg, CPP 60-70 (CPP = MAP - ICP); avoid CPP <50 (ischemia) and aggressive CPP >70 with fluids/pressors (ARDS risk)","Multimodal adjuncts where available: brain tissue oxygen (PbtO2 >20), TCD; c-spine cleared/immobilized until proven"],"tx":["Tiered therapy: (0) head of bed 30 deg, midline neck, normothermia, normocapnia (PaCO2 35-45), sedation/analgesia, treat seizures; (1) CSF drainage via EVD, osmotherapy - hypertonic saline (3% or 23.4% bolus) or mannitol 0.25-1 g/kg (watch volume status, osmolar gap); (2) neuromuscular blockade, mild hypocapnia 30-35 as bridge; (3) barbiturate coma, decompressive craniectomy, hypothermia (rescue, not prophylactic - Eurotherm/POLAR negative)","Hyperventilation is a TEMPORIZING bridge to the OR only - prophylactic hyperventilation worsens outcome (vasoconstriction-induced ischemia)","Seizure prophylaxis: levetiracetam or phenytoin x7 days reduces EARLY post-traumatic seizures (BTF) - does not prevent late epilepsy","Correct coagulopathy aggressively; reverse anticoagulants; maintain Na, glucose, temperature; no steroids (CRASH trial - increased mortality)"],"tech":["ICP monitor: intraparenchymal bolt (Kocher point) or ventriculostomy (EVD - both measures AND treats via CSF drainage)","Decompressive craniectomy when medically refractory: large fronto-temporo-parietal flap (AP diameter >=12 cm) to avoid venous strangulation at bone edges; open/expand dura (duraplasty), leave bone out","RESCUEicp: last-tier craniectomy increases survival but with more severe disability/vegetative survivors - consent conversation matters; DECRA (early, ICP 20-25) worsened outcomes - not for early modest elevations"],"after":["Neuro ICU: continuous ICP/CPP, sedation weaning trials, VTE prophylaxis started ~24-72 h once hemorrhage stable, early enteral nutrition, glucose/Na control, temperature management","Wean osmotherapy gradually (rebound); serial CT; treat post-op complications (hygroma, hydrocephalus, infection)","Cranioplasty typically 6-12 wks (up to 3 mo) after swelling resolves; watch syndrome of the trephined (sinking skin flap - neuro decline improved by cranioplasty)","Prognostication with humility (IMPACT/CRASH calculators inform, don't dictate); early rehab referral, family communication"],"pearls":["Hypotension and hypoxia are the two secondary insults you can actually prevent - one episode of SBP <90 measurably worsens outcome","Prophylactic hyperventilation and steroids both kill in TBI - the reflexes to unlearn","A blown pupil in a deteriorating TBI is going to the OR, not to a longer medical ladder","The repeat CT at 6 h exists because contusions blossom - the first scan is a snapshot of a moving target"],"refs":[{"t":"Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://www.braintrauma.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Traumatic Brain Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459300/"},{"t":"AANS: Traumatic Brain Injury","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-edh","name":"Epidural Hematoma","sec":"nsg","present":["Blood between skull and dura - biconvex/lens-shaped on CT, does NOT cross suture lines (dura adheres at sutures)","Classic middle meningeal ARTERY tear from temporal bone fracture; the textbook LUCID INTERVAL (brief LOC, then lucid, then rapid deterioration) as the clot expands","Rapid uncal herniation possible: ipsilateral blown pupil (CN III), contralateral hemiparesis - a true surgical emergency"],"dx":["Noncontrast CT: hyperdense biconvex collection; assess volume, thickness, midline shift; a 'swirl sign' (hypodense active bleeding) predicts expansion","Correlate GCS trajectory - EDH deteriorates fast; low threshold for repeat imaging if any change","Identify associated skull fracture and rule out coincident intradural injury"],"tx":["Surgical evacuation (craniotomy) for: volume >30 cm3 regardless of GCS, OR acute EDH with GCS <9 and anisocoria - urgent (BTF surgical guidelines)","Nonoperative observation reasonable for small (<30 cm3, <15 mm thickness, <5 mm shift) EDH in a neurologically intact patient - with serial exams and repeat CT","Reverse coagulopathy, control ICP as a bridge to OR"],"tech":["Craniotomy centered over the clot; evacuate hematoma, achieve hemostasis of the bleeding source (coagulate/pack the middle meningeal artery, foramen spinosum bone wax)","Dural tack-up (hitch) sutures to the bone edges and central tenting to obliterate the epidural space and prevent re-accumulation","Consider leaving a subgaleal/epidural drain; replace bone flap (evacuation, not craniectomy, in most)"],"after":["Post-op CT to confirm evacuation; ICU neuro checks; watch for re-accumulation","If evacuated early before secondary injury, prognosis is EXCELLENT - EDH is the 'talk and die/talk and recover' lesion where speed determines everything","Standard TBI care otherwise: VTE prophylaxis timing, seizure prophylaxis per associated injury"],"pearls":["Lens-shaped, doesn't cross sutures, arterial, fast - EDH is the lesion where minutes of door-to-OR time equal neurologic outcome","The lucid interval is a trap: a well-appearing patient after temporal trauma can crash in the CT waiting room","An intact young patient with a large EDH still often needs surgery - volume/thickness criteria, not just GCS","Fix the foramen spinosum bleeder with bone wax or you will be back in the OR for re-accumulation"],"refs":[{"t":"BTF/CNS Surgical Management of TBI","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epidural Hematoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518982/"},{"t":"AANS: Head Injury","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-sdh","name":"Acute & Chronic Subdural Hematoma","sec":"nsg","present":["Blood between dura and arachnoid - CRESCENT-shaped, CROSSES suture lines (but not midline dural reflections); source is bridging veins","Acute SDH: high-energy trauma (young) or trivial trauma in atrophic/anticoagulated elderly; hyperdense crescent, often with worse underlying brain injury than EDH","Chronic SDH: weeks after minor/forgotten trauma in elderly/alcohol/atrophy; hypodense (or mixed) crescent, insidious - headache, confusion, gait decline, focal deficit; membranes and recurrent microhemorrhage drive growth"],"dx":["Noncontrast CT defines density (acute hyperdense, subacute isodense - easy to miss, look for effaced sulci/shift, chronic hypodense) and mass effect (thickness, midline shift)","Acute isodense SDH in anemic/subacute phase can hide - use MRI or contrast if suspicion high with a 'too-smooth' hemisphere","Anticoagulation/antiplatelet and coagulopathy status central to decisions"],"tx":["Acute SDH surgical criteria (BTF): thickness >10 mm OR midline shift >5 mm regardless of GCS; or smaller with GCS drop >=2, ICP >20, or pupillary changes - craniotomy (often with craniectomy if swollen brain)","Chronic SDH: surgery (burr-hole drainage) for symptomatic or large lesions; small asymptomatic can be observed with imaging; correct/hold anticoagulation and reassess restart timing","Middle meningeal artery (MMA) embolization: emerging adjunct/alternative reducing chronic SDH recurrence (EMBOLISE/MAGIC-MT era) - devascularizes the membranes"],"tech":["Acute: large craniotomy to evacuate clot and control cortical bleeding; craniectomy + duraplasty if brain swells; treat underlying contusion","Chronic: one or two burr holes, irrigate subdural space until clear, place a subdural drain (reduces recurrence); twist-drill or mini-craniotomy for organized/membranous collections","MMA embolization via endovascular access to the middle meningeal artery, particles/liquid embolic"],"after":["Subdural drain typically 24-48 h; keep patient relatively FLAT to promote brain re-expansion (obliterates the space); encourage hydration","Recurrence is the chronic-SDH signature (~10-20%) - counsel and re-image for symptom return; time anticoagulation restart against rebleed vs thrombosis risk individually","Acute SDH: full neuro-ICU TBI care, VTE prophylaxis timing, seizure prophylaxis for cortical involvement"],"pearls":["Crescent that crosses sutures = subdural; the venous source means it can be slower than EDH but the brain underneath is often sicker","The isodense subacute SDH is the classic miss - look for asymmetry, effaced sulci, and shift rather than obvious blood","Chronic SDH is a disease of atrophy and anticoagulation - fix the reversible bleeding driver or you'll drain it twice","MMA embolization is changing chronic SDH management - know it exists when counseling the recurrence-prone elderly patient"],"refs":[{"t":"BTF/CNS Surgical Management of TBI","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Subdural Hematoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532970/"},{"t":"AANS: Subdural Hematoma","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-contusion","name":"Cerebral Contusion & Traumatic Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage","sec":"nsg","present":["Bruising of brain parenchyma, classically inferior frontal and anterior temporal lobes (coup-contrecoup against bony ridges); may be single or multiple ('salt and pepper' petechial hemorrhage + edema)","Delayed deterioration is characteristic: contusions BLOSSOM (progressive hemorrhagic injury) over 24-72 h, and temporal contusions can herniate with modest volume","Presentation ranges from headache/confusion to focal deficit to rapid decline as edema/mass effect evolves"],"dx":["Noncontrast CT: mixed-density parenchymal lesion; REPEAT CT at ~6 h and with any change - the second scan often looks much worse than the first","Watch temporal contusions especially: less volume tolerated before uncal herniation given proximity to the tentorial edge and brainstem","ICP monitoring per BTF criteria when GCS <=8 or when clinical exam is unavailable (sedated/paralyzed)"],"tx":["Medical management for most: ICP-directed tiered therapy, seizure prophylaxis (levetiracetam/phenytoin x7 d), coagulopathy correction, serial imaging","Surgical evacuation for: progressive neuro deterioration attributable to the lesion, refractory ICP, or significant mass effect - frontal/temporal contusions with volume/shift and clinical correlation (BTF surgical guidance)","Decompressive craniectomy when diffuse swelling accompanies contusions and ICP is refractory"],"tech":["Craniotomy over the contusion; gentle subpial resection/evacuation of necrotic hemorrhagic brain and clot; meticulous hemostasis","Temporal contusion with herniation: evacuation +/- anterior temporal lobectomy and decompressive craniectomy to relieve brainstem pressure","Duraplasty and bone-out if brain is swollen"],"after":["ICU with serial neuro exams and repeat CT (blossoming continues); wean sedation to assess","VTE prophylaxis delayed until hemorrhage demonstrably stable on imaging; continue AED for 7 days (longer only if seizures occur)","Rehabilitation for frontal/temporal syndromes (executive dysfunction, disinhibition, memory) - counsel families on the cognitive/behavioral sequelae that outlast the CT findings"],"pearls":["The contusion you observe today is the craniotomy you may do tomorrow - the 6-hour repeat scan is not optional","Temporal lobe contusions punch above their volume - low threshold to act because the brainstem is right there","Coagulopathy turns a stable contusion into an expanding one - reverse it early and aggressively","Behavioral and executive deficits from frontal contusions are the disability families aren't warned about - say it out loud"],"refs":[{"t":"Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://www.braintrauma.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Contusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526090/"},{"t":"CNS: Surgical Management of TBI","u":"https://www.cns.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-skull-fracture","name":"Depressed Skull Fracture & Penetrating Brain Injury","sec":"nsg","present":["Depressed skull fracture: inward displacement of a bone segment; OPEN (scalp laceration/communication, dural tear risk, CSF leak) vs CLOSED; depth greater than the skull table thickness raises dural violation and seizure risk","Penetrating injury (GSW, stab, impalement): trajectory determines vascular, ventricular, and eloquent-tissue involvement; GSW crossing midline/multilobar/ventricular has poor prognosis","Assess for CSF leak (rhinorrhea/otorrhea), cranial nerve injury, and vascular injury clues along the trajectory"],"dx":["CT (bone and brain windows) for fracture depth, intracranial fragments, pneumocephalus, underlying hematoma/contusion; CTA when trajectory approaches major vessels/dural sinuses (traumatic aneurysm, dissection, dural sinus injury)","Beta-2 transferrin to confirm suspected CSF leak; document neuro and cranial nerve exam","Tetanus status; wound contamination assessment for open injuries"],"tx":["Elevation surgery for depressed fractures: open depressed fractures usually operated for debridement and to reduce infection; elevate for depression greater than table thickness, dural penetration, significant underlying hematoma, gross contamination, frontal sinus involvement, or cosmetic deformity; simple closed non-significant depressions may be managed nonoperatively","Antibiotics for open injuries; broad coverage and neurosurgical debridement; anticonvulsant prophylaxis (penetrating injury and depressed fractures carry high early seizure risk)","Penetrating: resuscitate, reverse coagulopathy; surgery for accessible mass lesions, debridement of devitalized tissue and accessible fragments - do NOT chase deep/retained fragments near vessels or eloquent tissue"],"tech":["Depressed fracture: incorporate the laceration into the flap, elevate depressed fragments, debride, repair dura (primary or graft) to seal CSF, reconstruct/replace bone if not grossly contaminated (otherwise delayed cranioplasty)","Penetrating/GSW: wide debridement of nonviable scalp/bone/brain, hemostasis, watertight dural closure; remove readily accessible bone/metallic fragments but accept retained deep fragments to avoid catastrophic vascular injury","Address dural venous sinus injury with packing/repair; frontal sinus cranialization when involved"],"after":["Antibiotics and close infection surveillance (abscess, meningitis, osteomyelitis); AED continued; monitor for delayed CSF leak","CTA/DSA follow-up when trajectory risked vessels - traumatic pseudoaneurysms can present with delayed hemorrhage","Delayed cranioplasty for contaminated/removed bone; rehab and cranial nerve follow-up"],"pearls":["Open depressed fracture = infection risk - debridement and a watertight dura are the operation's real goals","Don't chase the bullet: retained deep fragments beat an iatrogenic carotid or sinus injury","Any trajectory near a vessel or sinus earns a CTA - traumatic aneurysms bleed days later","Frontal sinus involvement changes the operation (cranialization) and the leak risk - look for it"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS/CNS Penetrating Brain Injury Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Depressed Skull Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538219/"},{"t":"AANS: Skull Fracture","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-icp-herniation","name":"Elevated ICP, Herniation Syndromes & Decompressive Craniectomy","sec":"nsg","present":["Raised ICP presents as headache, vomiting, decreased consciousness, papilledema (subacute), and Cushing triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respiration - a LATE brainstem sign)","Herniation syndromes: uncal (ipsilateral CN III palsy - blown pupil, contralateral hemiparesis, then coma), central (rostrocaudal deterioration), subfalcine (cingulate under falx - ACA compromise), tonsillar (cerebellar tonsils through foramen magnum - apnea/cardiovascular collapse), and upward (posterior fossa mass)","Normal ICP 5-15 mmHg; sustained >22 mmHg is treated; the pressure-volume curve is exponential - late compensation fails suddenly"],"dx":["CT for the cause (mass, hemorrhage, edema, hydrocephalus) and herniation signs (effaced basal cisterns, midline shift, uncal/tonsillar descent)","Invasive ICP monitoring (EVD or parenchymal) per BTF; EVD preferred when hydrocephalus present (measures and treats)","Serial exams - a new anisocoria or motor asymmetry is herniation until proven otherwise"],"tx":["Emergency measures: elevate head 30 deg, midline neck, treat pain/agitation/seizure, normocarbia, normothermia; hyperosmolar bolus (hypertonic saline 3%/23.4% or mannitol 1 g/kg); brief hyperventilation (PaCO2 30-35) as a BRIDGE only","Definitive: remove the cause - evacuate mass/hematoma, CSF diversion for hydrocephalus, decompressive craniectomy for refractory swelling","Tiered escalation to sedation, neuromuscular blockade, barbiturate coma; hypothermia only as rescue"],"tech":["Decompressive craniectomy: large flap (>=12 cm AP) - hemicraniectomy for unilateral swelling, bifrontal for diffuse; expansile duraplasty; the bone is banked (subcutaneous pocket or frozen) or discarded","For malignant MCA infarction: early hemicraniectomy within 48 h (DESTINY/DECIMAL/HAMLET) reduces mortality - benefit clearest <60 yo; consent for survival-with-disability","Ensure the flap is large enough - a small craniectomy strangulates herniating brain at the bone edges (venous infarction)"],"after":["Neuro-ICU with ICP/CPP goals; wean osmotherapy gradually to avoid rebound; monitor for hygroma, hydrocephalus, infection, and the sinking flap","Syndrome of the trephined (motor/cognitive decline with a concave flap, worse upright) - improved by cranioplasty","Cranioplasty at ~6-12 wks (later if infection/contamination); autologous bone or custom implant"],"pearls":["Cushing triad is a late, ominous sign - by the time you see it the herniation clock is nearly out","A too-small craniectomy is worse than none - the bone edge becomes a tourniquet on swollen brain","Osmotherapy buys minutes to hours; only removing the cause or decompressing buys outcome","Malignant MCA stroke in a young patient is a hemicraniectomy conversation - within 48 h, before the pupil blows"],"refs":[{"t":"Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines","u":"https://www.braintrauma.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Herniation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542246/"},{"t":"AANS: Intracranial Pressure Monitoring","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-evd","name":"External Ventricular Drain & Intracranial Pressure Monitoring","sec":"nsg","present":["EVD indications: acute hydrocephalus (IVH, aSAH, posterior fossa mass, tumor obstructing CSF), ICP monitoring plus therapeutic CSF drainage, and CSF sampling/access","ICP monitor types: intraparenchymal 'bolt' (monitoring only) vs ventricular catheter/EVD (monitors AND drains); choose EVD when CSF diversion is therapeutic","The device both diagnoses (ICP number, waveform) and treats (CSF drainage lowers ICP) - unique among monitors"],"dx":["ICP waveform literacy: P1 (percussion) > P2 (tidal) > P3 (dicrotic) normally; P2 rising above P1 signals poor compliance; Lundberg A (plateau) waves are ominous","Correct transducer leveling to a fixed reference (tragus/external auditory meatus ~ foramen of Monro) - a mis-leveled drain gives false numbers and mis-drains CSF","CSF studies (cell count, glucose, protein, culture) to diagnose ventriculostomy-related infection/ventriculitis - trend the cell-count-to-RBC ratio in bloody CSF"],"tx":["Set drainage against the reference height (e.g., 10-15 cm H2O above the tragus) to drain CSF above that pressure; alternate monitoring vs draining per protocol","Manage over-drainage (collapse, subdural hygroma/hemorrhage, upward herniation with posterior fossa mass) and under-drainage (rising ICP)","Antibiotic/silver-impregnated catheters and strict sterile technique reduce infection; minimize CSF sampling frequency"],"tech":["Freehand bedside placement at Kocher point: ~11 cm posterior to nasion, ~3 cm lateral to midline (mid-pupillary line), aim toward the ipsilateral medial canthus and external auditory meatus; target the frontal horn of the lateral ventricle at ~6-7 cm depth","Confirm CSF egress; tunnel the catheter subcutaneously to reduce infection; secure and connect to the leveled drainage/transducer system","Post-placement CT confirms tip position; parenchymal bolt placed via a cranial access bolt when only monitoring is needed"],"after":["Daily neuro checks + ICP/waveform trends; monitor drain output (volume/color), keep the system leveled, and clamp/level for transport with care","Wean by raising the drain and/or clamp trials with clinical + imaging surveillance; failed wean -> consider permanent shunt (VP shunt)","Surveillance for ventriculitis and catheter tract hemorrhage; remove as early as safe - infection risk rises with dwell time"],"pearls":["A leveled transducer is the whole game - a drain referenced to the wrong point either lies about the ICP or siphons the ventricles flat","Over-drainage in a posterior fossa mass can cause upward herniation - don't open the drain wide reflexively","Rising P2 above P1 warns of failing compliance before the ICP number screams","The longer the EVD dwells, the higher the ventriculitis risk - wean or convert with intent, don't let it linger"],"refs":[{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society EVD Guidelines","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: External Ventricular Drain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459224/"},{"t":"AANS: Hydrocephalus","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-brain-death","name":"Brain Death Determination & Neurocritical Care Essentials","sec":"nsg","present":["Brain death = irreversible cessation of ALL brain function including brainstem; a clinical diagnosis with strict prerequisites, not a coma of uncertain depth","Context: catastrophic brain injury (massive TBI, aSAH, anoxic injury, herniation) with a known, irreversible, proximate cause","The determination has profound medicolegal and donation implications - it must be rigorous and reproducible"],"dx":["Prerequisites first (AAN 2023 update): established irreversible cause; EXCLUDE confounders - core temp >=36 C, SBP/MAP adequate, no severe electrolyte/acid-base/endocrine derangement, no CNS depressant drugs/paralytics (wait appropriate clearance), no severe neuromuscular condition","Clinical exam: coma (no response to noxious stimuli), absent brainstem reflexes (pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic/oculovestibular cold-caloric, gag, cough), no motor response (spinal reflexes may persist)","Apnea test: pre-oxygenate, disconnect, observe for respiratory effort while CO2 rises - positive (confirms) when PaCO2 >=60 (or >=20 above baseline) with no respiratory drive; abort for instability","Ancillary tests (nuclear medicine cerebral blood flow, EEG, TCD, or CTA per protocol) when apnea test cannot be completed or exam confounded - not a substitute for a valid clinical exam when one is possible"],"tx":["There is no 'treatment' of brain death - the pivot is to family communication and, per wishes/law, organ donation coordination","Before determination, neurocritical care aims to prevent secondary injury and preserve the option of recovery/donation: normotension, normoxia, normocarbia, normothermia, Na/glucose control, seizure control","Notify the organ procurement organization (OPO) per protocol; the treating team does not approach for donation (separation of roles)"],"tech":["Apnea test procedure: correct baseline ABG, 100% preoxygenation, provide oxygen during disconnection (insufflation catheter or T-piece), monitor for chest/abdominal movement, draw ABG at ~8-10 min, reconnect; document rise in PaCO2 and absence of respiratory effort","Standardized checklist and qualified examiner(s) per institutional/state policy; single vs two exams per local law and patient age (pediatrics differ)"],"after":["Time of death is the time the determination is completed and documented; clear, compassionate family communication throughout","Donor physiologic management (if proceeding): treat neurogenic hemodynamic instability, diabetes insipidus (desmopressin), and endocrine derangements to preserve organs","Meticulous documentation of prerequisites, exam, apnea test/ancillary results, and examiners"],"pearls":["Prerequisites are the whole exam - a hypothermic, drug-confounded, or hypotensive patient cannot be declared, period","Spinal reflexes (triple flexion, Lazarus sign) can persist and confuse families and staff - they are not brain function","The apnea test proves absent respiratory drive at a CO2 that should maximally stimulate breathing - do it correctly or use an ancillary test","Keep the donation conversation separate from the treating team - the roles must not blur"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Brain Death/Death by Neurologic Criteria Guideline (2023)","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society Resources","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Death","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545144/"}]},{"id":"nsg-concussion","name":"Concussion & Mild TBI","sec":"nsg","present":["Mild TBI: GCS 13-15 with brief or no LOC, +/- amnesia, confusion; concussion is the clinical syndrome (headache, dizziness, fogginess, photophobia, sleep and mood change) without a structural lesion required","The neurosurgical role: identify the minority with surgically relevant lesions (small SDH, EDH, traumatic SAH, contusion) and manage anticoagulated patients and repeat imaging decisions","Beware the deteriorating 'mild' TBI - anticoagulation, age, and mechanism raise the stakes"],"dx":["Apply validated CT decision rules to spare unnecessary scans: Canadian CT Head Rule (GCS <15 at 2 h, suspected open/depressed fracture, basilar skull fracture signs, >=2 vomiting episodes, age >=65, and for medium risk amnesia/mechanism) or New Orleans Criteria","Anticoagulant/antiplatelet use lowers the imaging threshold and mandates a period of observation +/- repeat CT even with an initially normal scan","Document a baseline neuro exam and symptom inventory; grade recovery over time rather than a single snapshot"],"tx":["Most concussions: no CT needed, no surgery - the treatment is education, brief relative rest, then graded return to activity","Traumatic hemorrhage on CT: neurosurgical observation with serial exams and repeat imaging; small stable lesions in intact patients are managed nonoperatively; reverse anticoagulation for any significant bleed","Symptomatic management: acetaminophen (limit rebound-prone analgesics), sleep hygiene, gradual reintroduction of cognitive and physical load; refer persistent post-concussive symptoms to concussion/rehab pathways"],"tech":["Typically nonoperative - the 'procedure' is a disciplined observation protocol: serial GCS/neuro checks and a defined repeat-CT trigger for anticoagulated patients or any decline","Anticoagulation reversal when a bleed is present (PCC/vitamin K for warfarin, specific reversal agents for DOACs, platelet strategies case-by-case)"],"after":["Graded return-to-activity/return-to-play: stepwise increase in cognitive and physical demand, advancing only if symptom-free at each stage; no same-day return to sport after concussion","Second-impact syndrome counseling: a second head injury before recovery can cause catastrophic diffuse swelling - the reason for staged return","Clear discharge instructions and safety-netting (worsening headache, repeated vomiting, drowsiness, focal deficit -> return); follow-up for persistent symptoms"],"pearls":["A validated CT rule prevents both the missed bleed and the needless scan - know one and apply it","'Mild' plus anticoagulation is not mild - observe, often re-image, and reverse if anything shows","No athlete returns to play the same day - staged, symptom-guided progression prevents second-impact catastrophe","A normal first CT in an anticoagulated patient does not end the story - delayed hemorrhage happens"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Concussion/mTBI Clinical Guidance","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/traumaticbraininjury"},{"t":"StatPearls: Concussion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537017/"},{"t":"AANS: Concussion","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-asah","name":"Aneurysmal Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","sec":"nsg","present":["Sudden 'worst headache of life' / thunderclap headache (peak within seconds), +/- transient LOC, meningismus, photophobia, vomiting; sentinel (warning) headaches may precede rupture","Grade clinically (Hunt-Hess or WFNS by GCS +/- deficit) and radiographically (modified Fisher by cistern/ventricular blood - predicts vasospasm)","A ruptured aneurysm is a time-critical emergency: rebleeding is deadly and front-loaded in the first 24-72 h"],"dx":["Noncontrast CT is highly sensitive within 6 h of onset; if negative and suspicion persists, LP for xanthochromia (RBCs and bilirubin) - do not anchor on a normal late CT","CTA (and DSA - the gold standard) to identify aneurysm location, morphology, and plan clipping vs coiling; look for multiple aneurysms","Baseline labs, ECG (neurogenic stunned myocardium/troponin), and serial Na (cerebral salt wasting/SIADH)"],"tx":["Secure the aneurysm EARLY (<24-72 h) to prevent rebleeding - clip or coil per aneurysm/patient factors","Pre-securing: strict BP control (limit rebleed - target SBP typically <140-160 while maintaining perfusion), reverse coagulopathy, analgesia, quiet environment, antiemetics","Nimodipine 60 mg q4h x21 days improves NEUROLOGIC OUTCOME (not angiographic vasospasm) - start early; EVD for acute hydrocephalus/IVH; manage Na, seizures, and cardiopulmonary complications"],"tech":["Acute neurosurgical procedures here center on EVD placement for hydrocephalus (see EVD entry) and, when clipping is chosen, pterional craniotomy with microsurgical clip application (detailed in the clip-vs-coil entry)","Endovascular coiling in the angio suite as the alternative/preferred route for many aneurysms"],"after":["Neuro-ICU 14+ days: vasospasm/DCI surveillance (serial exams, TCD, CTA/CTP) peaking days 4-14, euvolemia and induced hypertension for DCI, endovascular rescue for refractory spasm","Sodium management (CSW vs SIADH - the distinction is volume status; avoid fluid restriction if hypovolemic), seizure and hydrocephalus management, VTE prophylaxis after the aneurysm is secured","Screen for chronic hydrocephalus (shunt), and counsel smoking cessation/BP control and screening of first-degree relatives in familial/multiple aneurysm settings"],"pearls":["CT is sensitive early but not late - a compelling thunderclap with a negative CT still needs an LP for xanthochromia","Rebleeding kills in the first hours - BP control and early securing are the whole early game","Nimodipine helps the brain, not the artery - it improves outcome without preventing angiographic spasm","Hyponatremia after SAH is usually salt WASTING (hypovolemic) - restricting fluids can worsen DCI"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Aneurysmal SAH Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society SAH Management","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441958/"}]},{"id":"nsg-aneurysm","name":"Cerebral Aneurysm - Clipping vs Endovascular Coiling","sec":"nsg","present":["Saccular aneurysms cluster at circle-of-Willis branch points (AComm, PComm, MCA bifurcation, basilar apex); present ruptured (SAH) or unruptured (incidental, mass effect - e.g., PComm aneurysm with painful CN III palsy)","Unruptured natural history guides prophylactic treatment: size, location (posterior circulation higher risk), prior SAH, growth, family history","The core decision for every aneurysm: treat or observe, and if treating, clip or coil"],"dx":["DSA is the gold standard for morphology (neck, dome-to-neck ratio, branch involvement); CTA/MRA for screening and planning","Risk-stratify unruptured aneurysms with PHASES (Population, Hypertension, Age, Size, Earlier SAH, Site) and ELAPSS for growth; weigh against treatment risk","ISUIA informed natural-history estimates (small anterior-circulation aneurysms have low rupture rates); individualize"],"tx":["Ruptured: secure early to prevent rebleed; ISAT showed better 1-year independent survival with COILING for aneurysms suitable for either - at the cost of higher retreatment and small late rebleed rates, so durable follow-up matters","Selection factors: MCA and wide-neck/branch-incorporating aneurysms often favor CLIPPING; posterior circulation and elderly/high-surgical-risk favor COILING; wide-neck/giant/fusiform -> flow diversion or adjuncts","Unruptured: shared decision weighing PHASES risk vs treatment morbidity; treat growth, symptomatic mass effect, or high-risk features"],"tech":["Microsurgical clipping: pterional (or orbitozygomatic/interhemispheric per location) craniotomy, proximal control, careful dissection of the neck, apply clip across the neck sparing parent/perforating vessels; intraoperative ICG angiography/Doppler confirms occlusion and patency","Endovascular: transfemoral/transradial access, microcatheter into the sac, detachable coils +/- balloon/stent assist; flow diverters (e.g., pipeline-type) reconstruct the parent vessel for wide-neck/giant aneurysms; intrasaccular devices (WEB) for bifurcation aneurysms","Complication awareness: intraoperative rupture, perforator occlusion (clip); thromboembolism, coil migration, perforation (endovascular)"],"after":["Ruptured: full SAH neuro-ICU care (vasospasm/DCI, Na, hydrocephalus, nimodipine)","Endovascular patients on flow diverters/stents need dual antiplatelet therapy - plan and monitor; angiographic follow-up to detect recurrence/retreatment need (higher after coiling)","Clipped aneurysms are generally durable with less imaging follow-up; screen for de novo aneurysms in familial/multiple cases and enforce BP control and smoking cessation"],"pearls":["ISAT nudged suitable ruptured aneurysms toward coiling for better early outcome - but the retreatment and follow-up burden is the trade","MCA aneurysms are the classic 'clip' aneurysm; basilar apex the classic 'coil' - anatomy drives the tool","PComm aneurysm with a painful third-nerve palsy is a warning of impending rupture - treat urgently","Flow diverters buy durability for hard aneurysms but commit the patient to dual antiplatelet therapy - factor that into ruptured cases"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Unruptured Intracranial Aneurysm Guidance","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Aneurysm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430927/"},{"t":"AANS: Cerebral Aneurysm","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-avm","name":"Arteriovenous Malformation (AVM)","sec":"nsg","present":["A nidus of abnormal arteriovenous shunting without intervening capillaries - high-flow feeding arteries and arterialized draining veins","Presents with hemorrhage (most common and most feared), seizures, headache, or focal deficit; can be an incidental finding","Annual hemorrhage risk roughly 2-4% overall, higher with prior rupture, deep location, deep venous drainage, and associated aneurysms"],"dx":["MRI/MRA localizes and shows the nidus and flow voids; DSA is the gold standard defining feeders, nidus, and venous drainage for grading and treatment planning","Grade with Spetzler-Martin (size, eloquence of adjacent brain, deep venous drainage) +/- supplementary Lawton-Young factors to predict surgical risk","Identify flow-related and intranidal aneurysms (added bleeding risk)"],"tx":["Ruptured AVMs are generally treated (definitive obliteration reduces re-rupture); unruptured management is controversial after ARUBA (medical management had fewer strokes/deaths at interim vs intervention) - individualize with a multidisciplinary team","Modalities, often multimodal: microsurgical resection (definitive, immediate cure for accessible low-grade), stereotactic radiosurgery (small/deep/eloquent - obliteration over a 2-3 year latency), and endovascular embolization (usually adjunctive to reduce flow before surgery/SRS, occasionally curative for small AVMs)","Low Spetzler-Martin grades (I-II) favor surgery; high grades (IV-V) often observed or managed conservatively given prohibitive risk"],"tech":["Microsurgical resection: wide exposure, circumferential dissection securing feeding arteries FIRST and preserving the main draining vein until the end (premature venous occlusion causes catastrophic nidus engorgement and rupture); resect the nidus en bloc","Pre-operative embolization of deep/inaccessible feeders reduces intraoperative blood loss and normal-perfusion-pressure breakthrough","Radiosurgery delivers a conformal dose to the nidus; obliteration is delayed and confirmed angiographically"],"after":["Post-resection angiography to confirm complete obliteration (residual nidus re-bleeds); manage normal perfusion pressure breakthrough with strict BP control","SRS patients require the latency-period counseling (bleeding risk persists until obliteration) and delayed angiographic confirmation at 2-3 years; monitor for radiation-induced changes","Seizure management and neuro-rehab as needed"],"pearls":["Take the arteries first and the vein last - occluding the draining vein early turns an AVM into an emergency on the table","ARUBA made unruptured AVM management a genuine shared decision - obliteration prevents future bleeds but carries upfront risk","Spetzler-Martin grade is the shorthand for surgical risk - low grades cure well, high grades often shouldn't be touched","Radiosurgery does not protect until it obliterates - the 2-3 year latency still carries hemorrhage risk"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Brain AVM Management Statement","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Arteriovenous Malformation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531479/"},{"t":"AANS: Arteriovenous Malformation","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-ich","name":"Spontaneous Intracerebral Hemorrhage - Surgical Indications","sec":"nsg","present":["Nontraumatic parenchymal hemorrhage: hypertensive (deep - basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, cerebellum) most common; lobar in the elderly suggests cerebral amyloid angiopathy; also vascular malformation, tumor, coagulopathy, drugs","Presents with sudden focal deficit, headache, decreased consciousness, and signs of raised ICP; deterioration from hematoma expansion and edema","Cerebellar and brainstem locations carry unique surgical urgency due to brainstem compression and hydrocephalus"],"dx":["Noncontrast CT quantifies location/volume (ABC/2) and IVH; CTA 'spot sign' predicts expansion; consider CTA/MRI to exclude underlying lesion (aneurysm, AVM, tumor) especially in lobar/atypical/young patients","Prognosticate with the ICH score (GCS, volume, IVH, infratentorial origin, age) - useful but not a self-fulfilling ceiling on care","Coagulation studies and medication history (anticoagulants/antiplatelets) central to management"],"tx":["Medical core: aggressive BP lowering (target SBP ~140, INTERACT2/ATACH-2 - avoid overly aggressive drops with large hematomas), reverse anticoagulation immediately (PCC/vitamin K, DOAC-specific reversal), manage ICP and glucose, seizure treatment (not routine prophylaxis)","Supratentorial ICH: routine early surgical evacuation has NOT shown clear benefit (STICH, STICH II) - reserve for deteriorating patients or as life-saving decompression; minimally invasive evacuation (MISTIE III) reduced mortality but did not improve functional outcome (best results with good clot reduction)","CEREBELLAR ICH is the clear surgical indication: evacuate if >3 cm, or with brainstem compression or hydrocephalus - do NOT just place an EVD and wait (upward herniation risk)"],"tech":["Cerebellar hemorrhage: suboccipital craniectomy/craniotomy with hematoma evacuation +/- EVD for hydrocephalus","Supratentorial life-saving: craniotomy evacuation +/- decompressive craniectomy for refractory mass effect; minimally invasive (stereotactic/endoscopic aspiration +/- thrombolytic) where protocols exist","IVH with hydrocephalus: EVD; intraventricular thrombolysis (CLEAR III) cleared clot and reduced mortality without improving mRS"],"after":["Neuro-ICU: continued BP control, ICP management, Na/glucose, VTE prophylaxis (mechanical early, pharmacologic once stable), and repeat imaging for expansion","Rehabilitation is the backbone of recovery; treat the CAUSE (BP optimization for hypertensive; definitive treatment for identified AVM/aneurysm/tumor; amyloid counseling on recurrence and anticoagulation avoidance)","Goals-of-care conversations informed by, not dictated by, prognostic scores; avoid early self-fulfilling DNR/withdrawal"],"pearls":["Cerebellar ICH is the location where surgery clearly saves lives - >3 cm or brainstem/hydrocephalus means OR, not observation","Supratentorial ICH surgery is mostly life-saving decompression, not routine - STICH taught restraint","Reverse the anticoagulant now - hematoma expansion is the enemy and the clock is short","An early DNR based on a grim ICH score can become a self-fulfilling prophecy - give the brain and the family time"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Spontaneous ICH Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Intracerebral Hemorrhage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553103/"},{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society ICH Resources","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-cavernoma","name":"Cavernous Malformation","sec":"nsg","present":["Angiographically OCCULT low-flow vascular lesion of dilated sinusoidal channels; classic 'popcorn' or mulberry appearance with a hemosiderin rim","Presents with seizures (supratentorial), focal deficits, or hemorrhage (lower-morbidity bleeds than AVM but repetitive in some); often incidental","Familial form (multiple lesions, CCM gene mutations - KRIT1/CCM1) and radiation-induced forms exist; brainstem cavernomas behave more aggressively symptom-wise"],"dx":["MRI is diagnostic: T2* gradient-echo and SWI are most sensitive (blooming from hemosiderin), showing the popcorn core and rim; catheter angiography is negative (occult)","Look for an associated developmental venous anomaly (DVA) - must be preserved at surgery to avoid venous infarction","Screen for multiplicity/family history to identify familial CCM"],"tx":["Observation for asymptomatic/incidental lesions in non-eloquent, surgically difficult areas - many never cause trouble; track with imaging","Surgical resection for: symptomatic hemorrhage (especially repeat), medically refractory epilepsy attributable to the lesion, or progressive deficit - accessible lesions","Radiosurgery is controversial and reserved for select surgically inaccessible symptomatic lesions; anticonvulsants for seizure control"],"tech":["Microsurgical resection: navigation-guided approach, enter through the shortest non-eloquent corridor; remove the cavernoma completely (residual re-bleeds) while PRESERVING the associated DVA","Brainstem cavernomas: operate only when the lesion reaches a pial/ependymal surface, using safe entry zones and neuromonitoring - the 'two-hemorrhage' threshold and symptomatic accessible lesions guide timing","For epilepsy, resect the hemosiderin-stained gliotic rim when it can be done safely to improve seizure freedom"],"after":["Post-op MRI to confirm complete resection; seizure-outcome follow-up for epilepsy cases","Familial CCM: surveillance imaging and genetic counseling; counsel on hemorrhage risk of remaining lesions","Brainstem cases: intensive rehab for cranial nerve/long-tract deficits, many of which improve over time"],"pearls":["MRI popcorn with a hemosiderin ring and a negative angiogram is a cavernoma - the occult lesion","Preserve the developmental venous anomaly - it is the normal drainage of that brain, not part of the lesion","Brainstem cavernomas earn surgery when they reach a surface and have bled - safe entry zones and patience","Multiple cavernomas mean think familial - image the family and counsel on genetics"],"refs":[{"t":"Angioma Alliance / Synopsis of CCM Management","u":"https://www.angioma.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Cavernous Malformation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538144/"},{"t":"AANS: Cavernous Malformation","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-davf","name":"Dural Arteriovenous Fistula","sec":"nsg","present":["Acquired abnormal shunt within the dura between meningeal arteries and a dural venous sinus/cortical vein; often related to prior sinus thrombosis","Presents with pulsatile tinnitus, headache, bruit, or - when cortical venous reflux is present - hemorrhage, seizures, or a progressive neurologic/dementia-like syndrome (venous hypertension)","Cavernous-sinus dAVFs present with a red eye, chemosis, proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia"],"dx":["DSA is the gold standard: defines arterial feeders, the fistulous point, and - crucially - the VENOUS drainage pattern","Classify by Borden (I-III) and Cognard (I-V) - the key risk factor is CORTICAL VENOUS REFLUX/drainage, which predicts hemorrhage and neurologic deficit","MRI/MRA/CTA may show engorged vessels, venous congestion, or hemorrhage and prompt the angiogram"],"tx":["Benign low-grade fistulas without cortical venous reflux (Borden I) may be observed or treated for symptom relief (e.g., intolerable tinnitus)","Any fistula WITH cortical venous reflux (Borden II-III / higher Cognard) should be treated to eliminate hemorrhage/deficit risk - disconnect the cortical venous drainage","Endovascular therapy is usually first-line; surgery or radiosurgery for selected/refractory cases"],"tech":["Endovascular: transvenous coil/embolic occlusion of the diseased sinus segment, or transarterial liquid embolic (e.g., Onyx-type) penetration to the fistulous point and proximal draining vein","Surgical disconnection: craniotomy to interrupt the arterialized cortical draining vein at its dural exit (simple, durable for many cortical-vein fistulas)","Radiosurgery as an adjunct/alternative for low-flow lesions with delayed obliteration"],"after":["Post-treatment angiographic confirmation of cure (residual cortical venous reflux keeps the risk); surveillance for recurrence","Symptom follow-up (tinnitus, ophthalmic signs in cavernous fistulas); manage venous-hypertension sequelae","Investigate/treat an underlying hypercoagulable/sinus-thrombosis cause when identified"],"pearls":["The venous drainage pattern - not the arterial supply - decides danger: cortical venous reflux is the treat-now feature","Pulsatile tinnitus with a dural bruit deserves an angiogram, not reassurance, if any red flags exist","A red, proptotic, chemotic eye with ophthalmoplegia can be a carotid-cavernous/dural cavernous fistula - image the venous drainage","Disconnect the cortical vein and the lesion is cured - leave reflux behind and the hemorrhage risk remains"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Cerebrovascular Statements","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dural Arteriovenous Fistula","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560731/"},{"t":"AANS: Dural AV Fistula","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-vasospasm","name":"Cerebral Vasospasm & Delayed Cerebral Ischemia","sec":"nsg","present":["After aneurysmal SAH, arterial narrowing (angiographic vasospasm) peaks days 4-14; delayed cerebral ischemia (DCI) is the clinical syndrome of new deficit/decline not explained by other causes","Higher clot burden (modified Fisher) predicts risk; DCI is a leading cause of morbidity in survivors of the initial bleed","Vigilance window is roughly the first two weeks in the neuro-ICU"],"dx":["Serial neuro exams are the primary monitor; transcranial Doppler (rising mean velocities, elevated Lindegaard ratio) screens for large-vessel spasm","CT angiography/CT perfusion confirms vessel narrowing and hypoperfusion; DSA is definitive and therapeutic","Exclude mimics of decline: hydrocephalus, rebleed, seizure, hyponatremia, fever/infection, sedation"],"tx":["Prophylaxis/outcome: oral nimodipine x21 days (improves outcome); maintain EUVOLEMIA - modern practice abandons prophylactic hypervolemia/triple-H (harmful fluid overload)","For symptomatic DCI: induced HYPERTENSION (raise MAP with vasopressors, titrate to neuro exam) as first-line hemodynamic augmentation once the aneurysm is secured","Refractory DCI: endovascular rescue - intra-arterial vasodilators (e.g., verapamil/nicardipine) and balloon angioplasty for accessible proximal spasm"],"tech":["Endovascular rescue: DSA with intra-arterial calcium-channel-blocker infusion into spastic territories and transluminal balloon angioplasty for focal proximal large-vessel spasm","Ensure the aneurysm is secured before aggressive induced hypertension; optimize cardiac output as an adjunct in refractory cases"],"after":["Continue exam-driven surveillance through the risk window; wean hemodynamic augmentation as spasm resolves","Manage complications of therapy (pressor-related, fluid balance, catheter-related) and treat hyponatremia by restoring volume (CSW) rather than fluid restriction","Rehabilitation for established infarcts; the goal is preventing DCI-related infarction while the vessels recover"],"pearls":["Angiographic spasm is common; clinical DCI is what harms - treat the patient's exam, not just the Doppler number","Triple-H is out - euvolemia plus induced hypertension is the modern hemodynamic strategy","Nimodipine improves outcome without reversing the narrowing - keep it going even when vessels look tight","New deficit after SAH day 4 is DCI until you have excluded rebleed, hydrocephalus, seizure, and sodium"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Aneurysmal SAH Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"Neurocritical Care Society SAH/DCI Guidance","u":"https://www.neurocriticalcare.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Vasospasm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507831/"}]},{"id":"nsg-carotid","name":"Carotid Stenosis & Carotid Endarterectomy","sec":"nsg","present":["Atherosclerotic narrowing of the cervical internal carotid; SYMPTOMATIC (ipsilateral TIA, amaurosis fugax, or minor stroke) vs ASYMPTOMATIC (incidental bruit/imaging)","Symptomatic disease carries a high early re-stroke risk - urgency is the theme","Distinguish carotid-territory events from posterior-circulation and cardioembolic sources"],"dx":["Duplex ultrasound screens and grades stenosis; corroborate with CTA/MRA (or DSA) before intervention to confirm degree and anatomy","Symptomatic thresholds from NASCET/ECST: >=70% strong benefit from revascularization, 50-69% moderate benefit (patient-selection dependent); <50% - medical therapy","Asymptomatic (ACAS/ACST): modest absolute benefit from surgery over older medical therapy - reassess in the era of intensive modern medical management"],"tx":["Best medical therapy for all: high-intensity statin, antiplatelet, BP control, glycemic control, smoking cessation - the foundation that has narrowed the surgical benefit for asymptomatic disease","Revascularize SYMPTOMATIC significant stenosis EARLY - ideally within 2 weeks of the event (benefit falls with delay); CEA is the reference standard","CEA vs carotid artery stenting (CAS): CREST showed similar composite outcomes but MORE periprocedural stroke with CAS and more MI with CEA; younger patients tolerate CAS better, older patients (>70) do better with CEA; anatomy (high lesion, hostile neck, restenosis) influences choice"],"tech":["CEA: cervical incision along the SCM, expose common/internal/external carotid, systemic heparin, clamp with cerebral protection (selective SHUNT based on stump pressure/EEG/awake exam), arteriotomy, endarterectomy of plaque, close with a PATCH (reduces restenosis) or eversion technique","CAS: endovascular access, embolic protection device, angioplasty and stent across the lesion","Cranial-nerve awareness (hypoglossal, vagus, marginal mandibular, superior laryngeal) during dissection"],"after":["Strict post-op BP and neuro monitoring: watch for hyperperfusion syndrome (headache, seizure, hemorrhage after relieving a tight stenosis - control BP), neck hematoma (airway emergency), and cranial nerve deficits","Continue antiplatelet + statin + risk-factor control lifelong; CAS requires dual antiplatelet therapy periprocedurally","Duplex surveillance for restenosis; reinforce stroke-prevention lifestyle and medication adherence"],"pearls":["Symptomatic tight carotid stenosis is a within-2-weeks problem - delay forfeits the benefit","CEA vs CAS is a stroke-versus-MI and young-versus-old trade - CREST in one sentence","Post-CEA hyperperfusion syndrome turns a technical success into a hemorrhage - control the blood pressure","A tense neck hematoma after CEA is an airway emergency - open at the bedside if needed"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Carotid/Extracranial Disease Guideline","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Carotid Endarterectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470582/"},{"t":"AANS: Carotid Stenosis","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-moyamoya","name":"Moyamoya Disease & Cerebral Revascularization","sec":"nsg","present":["Progressive stenosis/occlusion of the distal ICAs and proximal circle of Willis with a compensatory fragile collateral network ('puff of smoke' - moyamoya - on angiography)","Bimodal presentation: ISCHEMIA (TIA/stroke, often precipitated by hyperventilation/crying in CHILDREN) and HEMORRHAGE (from fragile collaterals, more in ADULTS)","Idiopathic moyamoya disease vs moyamoya syndrome (associated with sickle cell, NF1, radiation, Down syndrome)"],"dx":["DSA is definitive - grades disease (Suzuki stages) and maps collaterals; MRI/MRA shows infarcts, flow voids, and the basal collateral network","Perfusion imaging and cerebrovascular reserve testing (acetazolamide challenge) identify hemodynamically compromised territories that benefit from bypass","Evaluate for underlying associated conditions (moyamoya syndrome)"],"tx":["Surgical revascularization is the mainstay to reduce future ischemic and hemorrhagic events - medical therapy alone does not halt progression","Antiplatelet therapy for ischemic symptoms; meticulous perioperative management to avoid stroke (avoid hypotension, hypovolemia, and hypocapnia)","Direct vs indirect bypass chosen by age and vessel caliber (children often indirect; adults tolerate direct)"],"tech":["Direct bypass: superficial temporal artery-to-middle cerebral artery (STA-MCA) microanastomosis for immediate flow augmentation","Indirect bypass: lay vascularized tissue on the brain surface to induce neoangiogenesis over months - EDAS (encephalo-duro-arterio-synangiosis), EMS, pial synangiosis (favored in children)","Combined direct+indirect procedures in selected adults"],"after":["Strict perioperative normotension, euvolemia, and NORMOCAPNIA - hyperventilation-induced vasoconstriction can precipitate perioperative stroke; good pain control (crying/hyperventilation risk in kids)","Antiplatelet continuation; surveillance angiography/perfusion to confirm bypass patency and neoangiogenesis (indirect takes months to mature)","Long-term follow-up for disease progression on the contralateral side"],"pearls":["Puff of smoke on angiography with a child who strokes when crying is moyamoya - hyperventilation is the trigger","Avoid hypocapnia perioperatively - blowing off CO2 constricts already-marginal vessels into infarction","Bypass, not pills, changes the natural history - medical therapy alone doesn't stop progression","Indirect synangiosis works on a delay - the neovascular supply matures over months, so counsel accordingly"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Moyamoya Statements","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Moyamoya Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430964/"},{"t":"AANS: Moyamoya Disease","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-glioma","name":"Diffuse Glioma (Low- and High-Grade)","sec":"nsg","present":["Infiltrative intra-axial tumors presenting with new-onset seizures, progressive focal deficit, headache, or cognitive/personality change","Range from lower-grade (younger patients, slower) to glioblastoma (older, aggressive); imaging spectrum from non-enhancing infiltrative to ring-enhancing necrotic masses","The WHO 2021 classification is MOLECULAR, not just histologic - it reframes diagnosis and prognosis"],"dx":["MRI with and without contrast (and advanced sequences - perfusion, spectroscopy) characterizes the lesion; the tissue diagnosis integrates molecular markers","Key molecular features: IDH mutation (better prognosis), 1p/19q codeletion (defines oligodendroglioma), and in glioblastoma MGMT promoter methylation (predicts temozolomide benefit) and CDKN2A/B status","IDH-wildtype astrocytomas with certain molecular features are classified as glioblastoma even without classic histology"],"tx":["MAXIMAL SAFE RESECTION improves survival across grades - extent of resection is a modifiable prognostic factor - balanced against preserving neurologic function","Glioblastoma: the Stupp protocol - maximal resection then concurrent radiotherapy + temozolomide, followed by adjuvant temozolomide (+/- tumor-treating fields); MGMT methylation predicts chemo responsiveness","Lower-grade gliomas: resection followed by radiation +/- PCV or temozolomide in higher-risk patients; seizure control with antiepileptics and steroids for symptomatic edema"],"tech":["Navigation-guided craniotomy with adjuncts to maximize safe resection: 5-ALA fluorescence (high-grade tumor glows pink), intraoperative MRI/ultrasound, and functional mapping (awake craniotomy or motor mapping) for eloquent-area tumors","Frameless stereotactic biopsy when the lesion is deep/eloquent/multifocal and resection is not safe - to obtain molecular diagnosis","Cortical/subcortical stimulation mapping to define and preserve language and motor tracts"],"after":["Post-op MRI within 24-48 h to quantify residual enhancing tumor (extent of resection); steroids tapered as tolerated; AED continued for seizures","Coordinate neuro-oncology and radiation oncology for adjuvant therapy; surveillance MRI to distinguish progression from pseudoprogression","Supportive care, rehabilitation, and honest prognostic and goals-of-care discussions - glioblastoma remains incurable"],"pearls":["Molecular markers now define the diagnosis - IDH, 1p/19q, and MGMT change both prognosis and treatment, so tissue must be characterized","Extent of resection is one of the few prognostic factors the surgeon controls - safe maximal resection matters","5-ALA makes high-grade tumor fluoresce - a practical tool for pushing resection to its safe limit","MGMT methylation predicts temozolomide benefit - it is prognostic and predictive, not a footnote"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN CNS Cancers Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"WHO Classification of CNS Tumors (2021) overview","u":"https://www.iarc.who.int"},{"t":"StatPearls: Glioblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441964/"}]},{"id":"nsg-meningioma","name":"Meningioma","sec":"nsg","present":["Extra-axial, dural-based tumors arising from arachnoid cap cells; a broad dural attachment with a 'dural tail' on contrast MRI is characteristic","Often incidental; symptomatic lesions cause seizures, focal deficits, or headache by mass effect and peritumoral edema; location dictates syndrome (parasagittal leg weakness, sphenoid wing/orbital, olfactory groove anosmia, CPA)","Mostly benign (WHO grade 1) but grade 2 (atypical) and grade 3 (anaplastic) recur aggressively"],"dx":["Contrast MRI: homogeneously enhancing dural-based mass with dural tail; CT shows calcification and hyperostosis of adjacent bone","Grade by WHO histology (mitoses, brain invasion, features); higher grade -> higher recurrence and consideration of radiation","Angiography/embolization planning for hypervascular tumors; assess venous sinus involvement (parasagittal)"],"tx":["Small, asymptomatic meningiomas (especially in older patients) - OBSERVE with serial imaging; many grow slowly or not at all","Symptomatic or growing tumors: surgical resection is first-line and often curative for accessible grade 1 lesions; extent of dural/bone removal predicts recurrence","Stereotactic radiosurgery for small tumors, surgically difficult locations (skull base, cavernous sinus), or residual/recurrent disease; adjuvant fractionated RT for grade 2-3 or subtotal resection"],"tech":["Craniotomy tailored to location; devascularize the dural base early, debulk, then dissect the arachnoid plane off brain/vessels/cranial nerves; resect involved dura and hyperostotic bone","Grade the resection by SIMPSON criteria (I complete incl dura/bone -> V decompression only) - lower Simpson grade correlates with lower recurrence","Preoperative embolization for very vascular tumors; sinus-invading parasagittal tumors require judgment about resecting vs preserving a patent sinus"],"after":["Post-op MRI baseline; surveillance imaging cadence by grade and resection completeness; watch for recurrence at the dural margin","Grade 2-3 or subtotal resections -> radiation oncology referral; manage residual edema (steroid taper) and seizures","Long-term follow-up - even grade 1 tumors can recur years later, especially with incomplete dural/bone removal"],"pearls":["Dural tail plus homogeneous enhancement plus hyperostosis equals meningioma - an extra-axial diagnosis","Simpson grade encodes the recurrence risk - taking the dura and bone, not just the tumor, is the durable operation","An asymptomatic small meningioma in an elderly patient usually wants watching, not surgery","The cavernous sinus and optic apparatus turn resection into radiosurgery territory - know when to stop and radiate"],"refs":[{"t":"EANO Meningioma Guideline","u":"https://www.eano.eu"},{"t":"NCCN CNS Cancers Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Meningioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560538/"}]},{"id":"nsg-brain-mets","name":"Brain Metastases","sec":"nsg","present":["The most common intracranial tumor overall; often MULTIPLE, at the gray-white junction and in a watershed/hematogenous distribution","Primaries: lung (most common), breast, melanoma, renal, and colorectal; presents with headache, seizure, focal deficit, or as a known-cancer surveillance finding","May be the first sign of an undiagnosed systemic cancer"],"dx":["Contrast MRI: multiple enhancing lesions with disproportionate vasogenic edema at the gray-white junction; hemorrhagic mets suggest melanoma, renal, thyroid, choriocarcinoma","Systemic staging (CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, PET) to find the primary and assess extracranial disease burden - which drives prognosis and strategy","When a solitary lesion appears without known primary, tissue diagnosis (resection/biopsy) may be needed to exclude primary brain tumor/abscess"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary and prognosis-driven (systemic disease control and performance status matter): options are surgery, stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS), whole-brain RT (WBRT), and systemic/targeted therapy","Surgery for a large/symptomatic or dominant lesion, a solitary accessible met (especially with controlled systemic disease), or when diagnosis is unknown; SRS for limited numbers of small lesions - spares cognition compared with WBRT","WBRT reserved for numerous lesions or leptomeningeal disease (QUARTZ questioned WBRT benefit in poor-prognosis NSCLC); dexamethasone for symptomatic edema, AED for seizures (not routine prophylaxis)"],"tech":["Craniotomy with navigation for the dominant/symptomatic lesion; en bloc resection where feasible (may reduce leptomeningeal seeding vs piecemeal); functional mapping for eloquent locations","SRS delivers a high conformal dose to small lesions/resection cavity in one or a few fractions","Consider post-resection cavity SRS to reduce local recurrence"],"after":["Post-op MRI; adjuvant SRS to the resection cavity reduces local recurrence (vs observation) while limiting the cognitive cost of WBRT","Coordinate systemic/targeted/immunotherapy with medical oncology (some CNS-penetrant agents change the calculus); surveillance MRI for new/distant brain disease","Steroid taper, rehabilitation, and goals-of-care aligned with systemic disease trajectory"],"pearls":["Multiple enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction in a cancer patient are mets until proven otherwise - but a solitary lesion may still need tissue","SRS spares cognition; WBRT is retreating from routine use - reserve whole-brain radiation for diffuse or leptomeningeal disease","Systemic disease control and performance status drive brain-met strategy more than the number of lesions alone","Cavity SRS after resection buys local control without the neurocognitive toll of whole-brain radiation"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCO/SNO/ASTRO Brain Metastases Guideline","u":"https://www.asco.org"},{"t":"NCCN CNS Cancers Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Metastasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470246/"}]},{"id":"nsg-pituitary","name":"Pituitary Adenoma & Transsphenoidal Surgery","sec":"nsg","present":["Sellar masses that present by ENDOCRINE excess (functioning) or MASS EFFECT (nonfunctioning): bitemporal hemianopsia from optic chiasm compression, headache, and hypopituitarism","Functioning subtypes: prolactinoma (galactorrhea, amenorrhea, low libido), GH-secreting (acromegaly/gigantism), ACTH-secreting (Cushing disease); microadenoma <10 mm vs macroadenoma >=10 mm","Pituitary APOPLEXY - sudden headache, vision loss, ophthalmoplegia, and hypopituitarism from hemorrhage/infarction - is an emergency"],"dx":["Dedicated pituitary MRI (thin sellar cuts, dynamic contrast) characterizes size, cavernous sinus invasion, and chiasm compression","Full hormonal workup: prolactin (beware the hook effect in giant prolactinomas - dilute the sample), IGF-1/GH suppression for acromegaly, cortisol axis testing for Cushing, and anterior pituitary panel for hypopituitarism","Formal visual field testing when the chiasm is involved"],"tx":["PROLACTINOMA is the exception - medical therapy FIRST: dopamine agonists (cabergoline) shrink most prolactinomas and restore function; surgery reserved for intolerance, resistance, or apoplexy","Nonfunctioning macroadenomas with mass effect/visual loss, GH- and ACTH-secreting tumors -> transsphenoidal surgery is first-line; medical adjuncts (somatostatin analogs for acromegaly, steroidogenesis inhibitors for Cushing) as needed","Apoplexy with visual/neurologic compromise -> urgent surgical decompression and stress-dose steroids; hormone replacement for deficiencies"],"tech":["Endoscopic endonasal transsphenoidal approach: through the nostril and sphenoid sinus to the sella, open the floor, resect the adenoma while preserving the normal gland and respecting the diaphragma/arachnoid","Intraoperative CSF leak repair (fat/fascia graft, nasoseptal flap) when the arachnoid is breached; navigation and Doppler to avoid the carotids","Extended endoscopic approaches for suprasellar/invasive tumors; craniotomy rarely for large lesions unsuitable for the transsphenoidal corridor"],"after":["Monitor sodium and urine output closely for diabetes insipidus (early, polyuria/hypernatremia) and delayed SIADH (~day 7 hyponatremia) - the classic post-transsphenoidal fluid/electrolyte swings","Watch for CSF rhinorrhea (meningitis risk), check post-op cortisol axis and replace as needed, and reassess hormone hypersecretion (remission) and visual fields","ENT nasal care; endocrine follow-up for long-term replacement and biochemical surveillance"],"pearls":["Prolactinoma is the pituitary tumor you treat with a pill first - dopamine agonists, not the OR, unless apoplexy or failure","Check a prolactin before operating on any sellar mass - and dilute it in giant tumors to defeat the hook effect","Post-transsphenoidal sodium is a rollercoaster - DI early, SIADH around day 7 - watch it deliberately","Sudden headache with vision loss and ophthalmoplegia in a known/undiagnosed adenoma is apoplexy - steroids and possible urgent decompression"],"refs":[{"t":"Endocrine Society Pituitary Guidelines","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pituitary Adenoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441967/"},{"t":"AANS: Pituitary Tumors","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-vestibular-schwannoma","name":"Vestibular Schwannoma & Cerebellopontine Angle Tumors","sec":"nsg","present":["Benign Schwann-cell tumor of the vestibular division of CN VIII, growing in the internal auditory canal/cerebellopontine angle (CPA)","Classic triad evolves: unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, then imbalance; large tumors add trigeminal (facial numbness, absent corneal reflex), facial nerve, and brainstem/hydrocephalus signs","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas define neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2); CPA differential includes meningioma and epidermoid"],"dx":["Gadolinium MRI of the IACs: enhancing IAC/CPA mass (an 'ice cream cone' filling and extending from the canal); audiometry documents the sensorineural loss","Assess hearing (word recognition) and tumor size/brainstem compression to guide management; screen for NF2 in young/bilateral cases","Baseline facial nerve function and, when relevant, hydrocephalus"],"tx":["Three strategies by size, hearing, age, and symptoms: OBSERVE (small, minimally symptomatic - serial MRI, many grow slowly), STEREOTACTIC RADIOSURGERY (small-to-medium, good tumor control with hearing/facial-nerve preservation goals), or MICROSURGERY (larger tumors, brainstem compression, or failed other options)","Facial nerve preservation is the paramount functional goal; hearing preservation attempted when serviceable hearing remains and the approach allows","Manage hydrocephalus and trigeminal/brainstem symptoms as needed"],"tech":["Approaches: retrosigmoid (posterior fossa - can attempt hearing preservation, good for most sizes), translabyrinthine (sacrifices hearing, excellent facial nerve/IAC access for larger tumors), and middle fossa (small intracanalicular tumors with hearing preservation)","Continuous facial nerve (and cochlear nerve) intraoperative monitoring; microsurgical dissection of the tumor off the facial/cochlear nerves and brainstem","Subtotal resection with residual observation/radiosurgery when facial nerve is at risk - function over completeness"],"after":["Facial nerve outcome (House-Brackmann grade) and eye protection if facial weakness (lubrication, tarsorrhaphy) - corneal exposure with a numb cornea is a real risk","Watch for CSF leak (rhinorrhea via mastoid air cells/wound), headache, and balance rehabilitation (vestibular compensation over weeks)","Serial MRI surveillance for residual/recurrence; audiology follow-up; NF2 patients need lifelong multidisciplinary care"],"pearls":["Unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus earns an IAC MRI - the ice-cream-cone lesion","Facial nerve function trumps gross-total resection - leaving residual to spare CN VII is a good decision, not a failure","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas mean NF2 until proven otherwise - a different disease and a lifelong plan","A numb cornea plus a weak face is a blindness risk - protect the eye aggressively after surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"Congress of Neurological Surgeons Vestibular Schwannoma Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acoustic Neuroma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562312/"},{"t":"AANS: Acoustic Neuroma","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-skull-base","name":"Skull Base Tumors","sec":"nsg","present":["A heterogeneous group at the cranial base: chordoma and chondrosarcoma (clivus/petroclival), esthesioneuroblastoma (cribriform plate), skull base meningioma, sinonasal malignancies with intracranial extension, and juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","Present insidiously with cranial neuropathies (diplopia, facial numbness, dysphagia, hearing loss), nasal obstruction/epistaxis, headache, or endocrine dysfunction depending on location","The theme is critical neurovascular structures (carotids, cranial nerves, brainstem, optic apparatus) in a confined corridor"],"dx":["MRI (soft tissue, dural/cavernous sinus, brainstem) plus CT (bony anatomy, erosion, calcification) are complementary; CTA/MRA and sometimes angiography define vascular relationships and feeders","Chordoma (notochord remnant, midline clival, T2-bright) vs chondrosarcoma (off-midline petroclival) distinction; biopsy for malignant/sinonasal lesions","Baseline cranial nerve, visual, audiologic, and endocrine assessment to plan and to follow"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary skull-base team (neurosurgery + ENT/otology + endocrine + radiation/medical oncology); goal is maximal safe resection with cranial nerve and vascular preservation","Chordoma/chondrosarcoma: maximal safe resection followed by high-dose radiation (proton/particle therapy) - locally aggressive, recurrence-prone; esthesioneuroblastoma: resection (often endoscopic) + radiation +/- chemotherapy by stage (Kadish)","Benign lesions (some meningiomas, angiofibromas) may be resected or, for residual/high-risk locations, treated/adjuncted with radiosurgery; preoperative embolization for hypervascular tumors (angiofibroma)"],"tech":["Endoscopic endonasal skull-base approaches for midline ventral lesions (clivus, cribriform, sella/parasellar) - the workhorse for many chordomas, esthesioneuroblastomas, and sinonasal tumors","Open/lateral cranial-base approaches (petrosal, orbitozygomatic, far-lateral, infratemporal fossa) for lateral or extensive tumors; combined cranionasal approaches for large lesions","Vascularized reconstruction (nasoseptal flap, pericranial flap) to seal the skull base; neuronavigation, Doppler, and cranial-nerve monitoring throughout"],"after":["CSF LEAK is the dominant early risk after skull-base resection - vascularized reconstruction and sometimes a lumbar drain mitigate it; monitor for rhinorrhea and meningitis","Cranial nerve deficits (swallow/airway safety for lower cranial nerves, eye protection for a numb/weak face) drive early rehab; endocrine replacement when the axis is involved","Coordinate adjuvant radiation (proton therapy for chordoma) and long-term surveillance imaging; these tumors recur and need durable follow-up"],"pearls":["Skull-base tumors are a team sport - neurosurgery, ENT/otology, endocrine, and radiation oncology plan together or the patient loses function","Midline clival T2-bright lesion is chordoma; off-midline petroclival is chondrosarcoma - and both want maximal resection plus proton radiation","CSF leak is the reconstruction's report card - the nasoseptal flap transformed endoscopic skull-base outcomes","Map the carotids and cranial nerves before you operate in this corridor - the margin for error is millimeters"],"refs":[{"t":"North American Skull Base Society Resources","u":"https://www.nasbs.org"},{"t":"NCCN Head & Neck / CNS Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chordoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430846/"}]},{"id":"nsg-csf-leak","name":"CSF Leak & Rhinorrhea Repair","sec":"nsg","present":["CSF escaping through a dural/skull-base defect: rhinorrhea (anterior skull base) or otorrhea (temporal bone); clear, salty, unilateral watery drainage, positional (worse leaning forward), sometimes with a salty postnasal taste","Causes: traumatic (skull base fracture), iatrogenic (post-sinus/skull-base/pituitary surgery), and SPONTANEOUS - the latter strongly associated with obesity and RAISED intracranial pressure (idiopathic intracranial hypertension physiology)","The danger is ascending MENINGITIS through the defect"],"dx":["Confirm the fluid is CSF with BETA-2 TRANSFERRIN (highly specific) - the key test before extensive imaging","Localize the defect: high-resolution CT of the skull base for bony dehiscence, MRI/MR cisternography for meningoencephalocele/active leak, and CT cisternography or intrathecal fluorescein at surgery for elusive leaks","Assess for raised ICP (fundoscopy, opening pressure) especially in spontaneous leaks - it drives recurrence"],"tx":["Many traumatic leaks close with CONSERVATIVE measures: bed rest, head elevation, stool softeners/avoid straining, and lumbar drainage to divert CSF and let the dura heal","Persistent, spontaneous, or high-flow leaks and any meningoencephalocele -> surgical repair (endoscopic endonasal for anterior skull base is the workhorse)","Treat the UNDERLYING raised ICP in spontaneous leaks (weight loss, acetazolamide, and sometimes CSF diversion) or the repair will fail; meningitis prophylaxis is not routine but vaccinate and treat infection promptly"],"tech":["Endoscopic endonasal repair: identify the defect, prepare the margins, and reconstruct in layers (fascia/fat/bone graft +/- vascularized NASOSEPTAL FLAP for larger defects); intrathecal fluorescein aids localization","Temporal bone/otologic leaks repaired via mastoid/middle fossa approaches; open craniotomy for large or multiple anterior fossa defects","Perioperative lumbar drain in high-flow/high-ICP repairs to protect the reconstruction"],"after":["Lumbar drain management (avoid over-drainage/pneumocephalus), activity and straining restrictions, and head-of-bed elevation during healing","Treat the underlying ICP (acetazolamide, weight loss, shunt/ETV if needed) to prevent recurrence in spontaneous cases","Monitor for meningitis and recurrent rhinorrhea; nasal care after endoscopic repair"],"pearls":["Beta-2 transferrin proves it is CSF before you image the whole skull base - the anchoring test","Spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea is often a raised-ICP disease in disguise - fix the pressure or the repair leaks again","Clear unilateral watery rhinorrhea after skull-base trauma is CSF until proven otherwise - meningitis is the stake","The nasoseptal flap turned high-flow skull-base leaks from a persistent problem into a reliably repairable one"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO-HNS/Skull Base Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.entnet.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: CSF Rhinorrhea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470175/"},{"t":"AANS: CSF Leak","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-intraventricular","name":"Intraventricular & Pineal Region Tumors","sec":"nsg","present":["Tumors within the ventricles or pineal region present chiefly by OBSTRUCTIVE HYDROCEPHALUS (headache, vomiting, papilledema) as they block CSF pathways","Lateral/third-ventricle lesions: central neurocytoma (young adults, foramen of Monro), ependymoma/subependymoma, choroid plexus papilloma (CSF overproduction in children), meningioma (atrium)","Pineal region: germ-cell tumors (germinoma most common) and pineal parenchymal tumors (pineocytoma/pineoblastoma) - PARINAUD syndrome (upgaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, light-near dissociation) from tectal compression"],"dx":["MRI with contrast defines location, attachment, and hydrocephalus; CT shows calcification","Pineal region: send serum AND CSF tumor markers - AFP and beta-hCG (elevated markers indicate a nongerminomatous/secreting germ cell tumor and can obviate biopsy; pure germinoma may have mildly elevated beta-hCG)","CSF cytology and spine MRI for tumors that seed (germinoma, pineoblastoma, ependymoma); tissue diagnosis via endoscopic or stereotactic biopsy when markers are negative"],"tx":["Relieve hydrocephalus first when symptomatic: endoscopic third ventriculostomy (often combined with endoscopic biopsy of pineal tumors) or EVD","Marker-positive germ cell tumors and germinomas are exquisitely radiosensitive/chemosensitive - treat medically/radiation rather than resect; secreting NGGCTs get chemo + RT +/- resection of residual","Resect marker-negative pineal parenchymal tumors, neurocytomas, ependymomas, and choroid plexus tumors; adjuvant therapy by pathology/grade"],"tech":["Lateral/third ventricle approaches: transcortical (through a gyrus) or transcallosal (anterior interhemispheric, splitting a short segment of corpus callosum) to the ventricle; navigation and careful venous (internal cerebral veins) preservation","Pineal region: supracerebellar-infratentorial (sitting/park-bench) or occipital transtentorial approach - deep corridor among deep venous structures (vein of Galen, internal cerebral veins) demands meticulous technique","Endoscopic approaches for intraventricular biopsy, cyst fenestration, and simultaneous ETV"],"after":["Watch for post-op hydrocephalus (may still need a shunt), memory issues (fornix injury with transcallosal/transforaminal routes), and venous infarction","Coordinate oncology/radiation for germ cell and malignant parenchymal tumors; CSF and spine surveillance for seeding tumors","Ophthalmology follow-up for Parinaud-related gaze deficits (often partially recover)"],"pearls":["Pineal region markers can make the diagnosis without a knife - always send serum AND CSF AFP/beta-hCG before planning surgery","A germinoma is treated with radiation/chemo, not resection - do not do a morbid operation on a radiosensitive tumor","Intraventricular tumors kill by hydrocephalus - securing CSF flow (ETV/EVD) is the first priority","Deep venous anatomy owns the pineal region - respect the vein of Galen complex or the corridor becomes catastrophic"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN CNS Cancers Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pineal Gland Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499796/"},{"t":"AANS: Brain Tumors","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-peds-posterior-fossa","name":"Pediatric Posterior Fossa Tumors","sec":"nsg","present":["The posterior fossa is the most common location for pediatric brain tumors; classic presentation is morning headache, vomiting, and ataxia from obstructive hydrocephalus and cerebellar/brainstem involvement","Main entities: medulloblastoma (midline vermian, embryonal, seeds CSF), pilocytic astrocytoma (cystic with enhancing mural nodule, excellent prognosis), ependymoma (fourth ventricle, extends through foramina), and diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG - cranial neuropathies, poor prognosis)","Look for head tilt, cranial nerve palsies, and signs of raised ICP; infants may show macrocephaly and sunsetting eyes"],"dx":["MRI brain with contrast characterizes the mass, hydrocephalus, and brainstem involvement; MRI of the entire SPINE plus CSF cytology to stage CSF dissemination (essential for medulloblastoma/ependymoma) - ideally before or well after surgery to avoid artifact","DIPG is often a radiographic diagnosis (diffuse pontine expansion) - biopsy increasingly done for molecular targets (H3K27M)","Molecular subgrouping (e.g., medulloblastoma WNT/SHH/group 3/4) refines prognosis and therapy"],"tx":["Manage hydrocephalus (many resolve after tumor removal; EVD or ETV perioperatively as needed - avoid routine pre-emptive shunt when resection will relieve obstruction)","Maximal safe resection for medulloblastoma, ependymoma, and pilocytic astrocytoma (gross-total resection of pilocytic astrocytoma is often curative); risk-adapted craniospinal radiation (age-limited) + chemotherapy for medulloblastoma; ependymoma needs maximal resection + focal RT","DIPG: focal radiation is palliative standard; resection is not appropriate - the pons cannot be resected"],"tech":["Suboccipital craniotomy (midline for vermian/fourth-ventricle tumors, paramedian/telovelar approach to the fourth ventricle to spare the vermis and reduce cerebellar mutism), prone or concorde positioning, microsurgical resection off the brainstem floor","EVD/ETV for hydrocephalus; intraoperative neuromonitoring; avoid aggressive dissection of tumor densely adherent to the brainstem","Telovelar approach (through the cerebellomedullary fissure) preferred over vermian splitting to reduce posterior fossa syndrome"],"after":["POSTERIOR FOSSA SYNDROME / cerebellar mutism (delayed mutism, ataxia, emotional lability days after midline resection) - counsel families; usually improves over weeks-months but can leave deficits","Watch for persistent hydrocephalus needing a shunt, CSF leak/pseudomeningocele, and cranial nerve/swallowing deficits","Multidisciplinary pediatric neuro-oncology for adjuvant therapy; long-term surveillance for recurrence and treatment (radiation) late effects on the developing brain"],"pearls":["Morning headache with vomiting and ataxia in a child is a posterior fossa tumor until imaging says otherwise","Stage the whole neuraxis for medulloblastoma and ependymoma - the spine MRI and CSF cytology change the treatment plan","The telovelar approach spares the vermis and lowers the risk of cerebellar mutism versus splitting the vermis","A pilocytic astrocytoma with a mural nodule can be cured by complete resection - not all pediatric tumors are grim"],"refs":[{"t":"Children's Oncology Group / NCCN Pediatric CNS","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Medulloblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538197/"},{"t":"AANS: Pediatric Brain Tumors","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-awake-craniotomy","name":"Awake Craniotomy & Intraoperative Brain Mapping","sec":"nsg","present":["Used to resect lesions in or near ELOQUENT cortex/white-matter tracts (language, primary motor, and key subcortical pathways) while continuously testing function - most often for gliomas near speech/motor areas and some epilepsy surgery","Goal: maximize extent of resection while preserving neurologic function by mapping the individual patient's functional anatomy (which varies and can be reorganized by tumor)","Patient selection requires cooperation, tolerance of the awake phase, and appropriate anatomy"],"dx":["Preoperative functional MRI and diffusion-tensor tractography map eloquent cortex and tracts to plan the corridor and counsel risk","Neuropsychological/speech baseline and task rehearsal; anesthesia and neuropsychology co-planning","Confirm the lesion abuts eloquent territory such that intraoperative mapping changes the resection boundary"],"tx":["Intraoperative direct electrical stimulation (DES) is the gold standard: positive language/motor sites define no-go boundaries; resection proceeds to functional (not just anatomic) margins","Balance oncologic goal (supramaximal safe resection improves survival in glioma) against real-time function; stop at eloquent boundaries","Manage intraoperative seizures provoked by stimulation with cold saline irrigation and short-acting agents"],"tech":["Asleep-awake-asleep (or monitored anesthesia care) technique: sedation/airway control for opening and closing, awake for mapping and resection; scalp blocks for analgesia","Cortical mapping (number counting/naming for speech, movement for motor) then subcortical stimulation as resection deepens to protect tracts (e.g., arcuate fasciculus, corticospinal tract)","Continuous task performance by the patient with a neuropsychologist; navigation and, where available, intraoperative imaging"],"after":["Expect a transient post-op deficit (supplementary motor area syndrome, mild dysphasia) that often recovers with rehabilitation - counsel in advance","Standard tumor post-op care: MRI for extent of resection, steroid taper, AEDs; speech/OT/PT rehabilitation","Document mapping findings; monitor for seizures and delayed neurologic change"],"pearls":["Mapping resects to FUNCTIONAL margins, not anatomic ones - the patient's job on the table is the safety system","Cold saline is the first move for a stimulation-induced seizure during mapping","Supplementary motor area resections cause a dramatic but usually recoverable transient deficit - warn the family","fMRI/tractography plans the approach, but intraoperative stimulation is the truth - functional anatomy is individual"],"refs":[{"t":"Congress of Neurological Surgeons Tumor Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Awake Craniotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572053/"},{"t":"AANS: Brain Mapping","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-srs","name":"Stereotactic Radiosurgery (Gamma Knife) Essentials","sec":"nsg","present":["Delivery of a single (or few) highly conformal, high-dose radiation session to a precisely defined intracranial target using stereotactic localization - not open surgery","Common indications: brain metastases (limited number), acoustic neuroma/meningioma (small or residual/inaccessible), AVMs, trigeminal neuralgia, pituitary adenomas, and functional targets","Platforms: frame-based Gamma Knife (cobalt sources) and frameless linac-based systems"],"dx":["High-resolution stereotactic MRI/CT for target definition and dose planning; the target must be well-circumscribed and of a size amenable to a safe single-session dose","Multidisciplinary selection (neurosurgery + radiation oncology + physics); confirm diagnosis, because SRS treats without tissue","Assess prior radiation and proximity to critical structures (optic apparatus, brainstem) that limit dose"],"tx":["Dose by indication (illustrative): metastases and AVMs receive higher marginal doses; vestibular schwannoma/meningioma use lower doses (~12-13 Gy) to preserve cranial nerve function; trigeminal neuralgia targets the nerve root","Radiobiology: high single-dose ablation with a steep dose falloff spares surrounding brain; effect on benign tumors/AVMs is DELAYED (months to years - AVM obliteration over 2-3 years)","Fractionated stereotactic radiotherapy for larger lesions or those near critical structures when single-session dose would be unsafe"],"tech":["Frame-based: rigid stereotactic head frame under local anesthetic for sub-millimeter accuracy, imaging, computerized dose planning (multiple isocenters shape the dose to the target), then treatment; frame removed same day","Frameless (mask-based) linac SRS allows fractionation and comfort; cavity SRS after metastasis resection","Precise immobilization and image guidance are the core - the whole technique is accuracy"],"after":["Typically outpatient with rapid recovery; monitor for delayed effects - peritumoral edema, adverse radiation effect/radionecrosis (may need steroids or bevacizumab), and cranial neuropathy","Serial MRI surveillance to assess response (transient post-SRS enlargement/pseudoprogression is common in schwannomas), AVM obliteration (confirm by angiography at 2-3 years), and new/distant disease","Counsel that benign-target and AVM effects are delayed - protection is not immediate"],"pearls":["SRS is radiation, not resection - it needs a diagnosis and a well-defined, appropriately small target","Benefit is delayed for benign tumors and AVMs - the AVM is not protected until it obliterates at 2-3 years","Dose is capped by the optic apparatus and brainstem - proximity, not just size, limits candidacy","Post-SRS transient enlargement of a vestibular schwannoma is usually pseudoprogression, not failure - watch before re-treating"],"refs":[{"t":"ASTRO Stereotactic Radiosurgery Guidance","u":"https://www.astro.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stereotactic Radiosurgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526097/"},{"t":"AANS: Stereotactic Radiosurgery","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-cervical-radiculopathy","name":"Cervical Radiculopathy & Anterior Cervical Discectomy and Fusion (ACDF)","sec":"nsg","present":["Compressive cervical nerve-root disease (herniated disc or spondylotic foraminal stenosis) causing dermatomal arm pain, paresthesia, and myotomal weakness; C6 and C7 roots most common","Neck pain radiating to the arm reproduced/worsened by Spurling maneuver (extension + ipsilateral rotation + axial load); relieved by the shoulder-abduction (hand-on-head) sign","Distinguish from myelopathy (long-tract signs), peripheral entrapment, and shoulder pathology"],"dx":["MRI cervical spine is the study of choice to correlate the symptomatic root with disc/osteophyte compression; CT for bony detail; EMG/NCS when the level is unclear or to exclude peripheral neuropathy","Match the clinical root level to imaging - operate on concordant findings, not incidental MRI changes","Red flags (myelopathy, infection, tumor) change the pathway"],"tx":["MOST cervical radiculopathy resolves with nonoperative care over weeks-months: NSAIDs, activity modification, physical therapy, short courses of oral steroids, and selective transforaminal epidural injections","Surgery for: persistent/disabling radicular pain despite adequate conservative care (typically 6-12 weeks), progressive motor deficit, or clinically significant weakness","ACDF is the workhorse; posterior cervical foraminotomy is an option for posterolateral soft-disc/foraminal compression; cervical disc arthroplasty preserves motion in selected single/two-level disease"],"tech":["ACDF: transverse anterior neck incision, blunt plane medial to the carotid sheath and lateral to the trachea/esophagus, discectomy and removal of osteophytes/posterior longitudinal ligament to decompress the root/cord, interbody graft/cage, and anterior plate fixation","Cervical disc arthroplasty replaces the disc with a motion-preserving implant to reduce adjacent-segment stress","Posterior foraminotomy: keyhole decompression of the foramen without fusion"],"after":["Watch early for dysphagia (retraction), hematoma (airway emergency - a tense expanding neck needs immediate evacuation), and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy (hoarseness)","Soft collar as needed; radicular pain often improves quickly; monitor fusion and counsel on ADJACENT-SEGMENT disease over years","Return to activity progressively; smoking cessation improves fusion"],"pearls":["Spurling reproduces the arm pain, shoulder abduction relieves it - the bedside signs that localize the root","Most cervical radiculopathy is a nonoperative disease that gets better - operate for failed conservative care or motor loss, not for the MRI","A tense, expanding neck after ACDF is an airway emergency - open the wound at the bedside if needed","Arthroplasty preserves motion and may spare the adjacent segment - a reasonable ACDF alternative in the right single-level patient"],"refs":[{"t":"NASS Cervical Radiculopathy Guideline","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Radiculopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441828/"},{"t":"AANS: Cervical Spine Surgery","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-cervical-myelopathy","name":"Cervical Spondylotic Myelopathy","sec":"nsg","present":["Chronic cord compression from cervical spondylosis (disc/osteophyte, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, OPLL) - the most common cause of spinal cord dysfunction in adults over 55","Insidious: loss of hand dexterity (buttoning, handwriting - myelopathy hands), gait imbalance, and later bladder change; upper-motor-neuron signs BELOW the level and lower-motor-neuron signs AT the level","Signs: hyperreflexia, Hoffmann sign, Babinski, clonus, wide-based gait, and finger-escape/grip-release difficulty"],"dx":["MRI cervical spine shows cord compression +/- T2 signal change (myelomalacia - a marker of severity/prognosis); CT/CT-myelogram for bony detail and OPLL","Grade severity with the modified Japanese Orthopaedic Association (mJOA) score to guide decisions and track progression","Differentiate from ALS, MS, B12 deficiency/subacute combined degeneration, and peripheral neuropathy"],"tx":["Surgical decompression is the mainstay for moderate-to-severe myelopathy and for progressive disease - it halts progression and often improves function (the natural history of significant myelopathy is stepwise decline)","Mild, stable myelopathy may be observed closely with serial exams/mJOA, but progression warrants surgery; nonoperative care does not reverse cord compression","Choose approach by compression location, number of levels, alignment, and OPLL"],"tech":["Anterior (ACDF or corpectomy with strut/cage and plate) for focal ventral compression over 1-2 levels or with kyphosis; posterior (laminoplasty, or laminectomy with instrumented fusion) for multilevel disease with preserved lordosis","Laminoplasty preserves motion and avoids fusion in appropriate multilevel cases; alignment must be favorable (kyphosis limits posterior-only approaches)","Intraoperative neuromonitoring (MEP/SSEP) to protect the already-compromised cord"],"after":["Monitor neurologic status and for C5 palsy (delayed deltoid/biceps weakness after decompression - usually recovers), dysphagia (anterior), and wound/CSF issues","Rehabilitation for gait/hand function; counsel that surgery aims to STOP progression - established deficits may only partially recover","Fusion surveillance and adjacent-segment counseling; smoking cessation"],"pearls":["Clumsy hands and a wobbly gait in an older adult with brisk reflexes is cervical myelopathy - check the Hoffmann sign","Surgery's promise is to stop the decline; recovery of long-standing deficits is a bonus, not a guarantee - counsel early","T2 cord signal change signals a more compromised cord and less complete recovery - it argues against waiting","C5 palsy after decompression is common and usually transient - warn the patient so it does not read as a surgical error"],"refs":[{"t":"AOSpine / WFNS Cervical Myelopathy Guidelines","u":"https://www.aospine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Spondylotic Myelopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482312/"},{"t":"AANS: Cervical Myelopathy","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-lumbar-disc","name":"Lumbar Disc Herniation & Microdiscectomy","sec":"nsg","present":["Herniated nucleus pulposus compressing a lumbar nerve root -> radicular leg pain (sciatica) in a dermatomal pattern, often worse than the back pain; L4-5 and L5-S1 most common (L5 and S1 roots)","Positive straight-leg raise (and crossed SLR - more specific); myotomal weakness (foot drop with L5, plantarflexion/ankle jerk loss with S1), sensory changes","Red flags: bilateral symptoms, saddle anesthesia, or bladder/bowel dysfunction -> evaluate for cauda equina emergently"],"dx":["MRI lumbar spine correlates the herniation with the symptomatic root; reserve early imaging for red flags or persistent/progressive deficit (most sciatica improves before imaging matters)","Concordance between the clinical root and the imaged level is essential - asymptomatic disc herniations are common on MRI","EMG occasionally to clarify the level or chronicity"],"tx":["MOST lumbar disc herniations improve with nonoperative care over 6-12 weeks: activity modification (not bed rest), NSAIDs, physical therapy, and selective transforaminal epidural steroid injection for pain control","Surgery for: persistent/disabling radicular pain after adequate conservative care, progressive/significant motor deficit, or cauda equina (emergent) - SPORT showed faster relief with surgery, with outcomes converging over 1-2 years","Shared decision: surgery speeds recovery; conservative care often reaches a similar endpoint for those who can wait"],"tech":["Microdiscectomy: small midline incision, hemilaminotomy/flavectomy, gentle retraction of the traversing root, and removal of the herniated fragment decompressing the root (minimally invasive tubular techniques are common)","Preserve the facet joint to avoid iatrogenic instability; achieve hemostasis; avoid dural tears","Endoscopic discectomy is an emerging minimally invasive option"],"after":["Rapid relief of leg pain is typical; watch for recurrent herniation (same-level re-herniation ~5-10%), incidental durotomy/CSF leak, and rare discitis","Early mobilization, core rehabilitation, avoid heavy lifting/bending early; smoking cessation and weight management reduce recurrence","Counsel that back pain may persist even when leg pain resolves"],"pearls":["Sciatica is mostly a self-limited disease - operate for failed conservative care or a real motor deficit, not for the MRI","SPORT in a sentence: surgery relieves leg pain faster, but non-surgical patients often catch up by a year or two","A crossed straight-leg raise is more specific than the ipsilateral one for a significant herniation","New saddle anesthesia or urinary retention converts sciatica into a cauda equina emergency - ask every time"],"refs":[{"t":"NASS Lumbar Disc Herniation Guideline","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lumbar Disc Herniation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560878/"},{"t":"AANS: Herniated Disc","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-lumbar-stenosis","name":"Lumbar Spinal Stenosis & Decompression","sec":"nsg","present":["Degenerative narrowing of the central canal/lateral recess/foramina compressing the cauda equina and roots - the leading reason for spine surgery in older adults","NEUROGENIC CLAUDICATION: bilateral buttock/leg pain, heaviness, and paresthesia brought on by standing/walking and relieved by SITTING or LEANING FORWARD (flexion opens the canal - the shopping-cart sign)","Distinguish from vascular claudication (relieved by standing still, absent pulses, calf-predominant)"],"dx":["MRI lumbar spine demonstrates central/lateral recess/foraminal stenosis; standing/flexion-extension films assess dynamic instability/spondylolisthesis (which changes the operation)","Correlate imaging with the positional, flexion-relieved symptom pattern - degenerative stenosis is common on imaging without symptoms","Ankle-brachial index/vascular studies when vascular claudication is in the differential"],"tx":["Nonoperative first for most: physical therapy (flexion-based), NSAIDs, activity modification, and epidural steroid injections for symptom control - stenosis is not usually an emergency","Surgery (decompression) for persistent, function-limiting neurogenic claudication despite conservative care, or progressive deficit; SPORT supported surgical benefit for symptomatic stenosis","Add fusion when there is associated instability/spondylolisthesis - not for uncomplicated stenosis"],"tech":["Decompressive laminectomy (or bilateral laminotomy/foraminotomy) to widen the canal and recesses, preserving the facets and pars to avoid destabilization","Minimally invasive/unilateral approaches for bilateral decompression preserve midline structures","Fusion (instrumented) added only when decompression would destabilize a listhetic/unstable segment"],"after":["Neurogenic claudication typically improves; early mobilization and reconditioning; watch for durotomy/CSF leak and, if extensive facet resection, iatrogenic instability","Counsel on gradual recovery and the possibility of restenosis or adjacent-level disease over years","Weight management and core strengthening for durable benefit"],"pearls":["Better leaning on the cart, worse walking upright - the shopping-cart sign is neurogenic claudication in one image","Decompression alone suffices for uncomplicated stenosis; add fusion only for real instability/spondylolisthesis","Stenosis is rarely urgent - the exception is a progressive deficit or cauda equina, which changes the timeline","Preserve the pars and facets during decompression or you convert stenosis surgery into an instability problem"],"refs":[{"t":"NASS Lumbar Spinal Stenosis Guideline","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lumbar Spinal Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448076/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Stenosis","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-spondylolisthesis","name":"Degenerative Spondylolisthesis & Lumbar Fusion","sec":"nsg","present":["Anterior slippage of one vertebra on another from degenerative facet/disc changes (most common at L4-5); often with stenosis symptoms - neurogenic claudication and/or radiculopathy, +/- mechanical back pain","Different from isthmic spondylolisthesis (pars defect, younger patients); degenerative type is a disease of older adults and facet arthropathy","Symptoms driven by the associated canal/recess stenosis and any dynamic instability"],"dx":["Standing lateral and FLEXION-EXTENSION radiographs are key - they reveal the slip and any DYNAMIC instability (motion between flexion and extension) that plain supine MRI can miss","Grade the slip by Meyerding (I-IV); MRI defines neural compression","Instability + neural compression is the combination that argues for decompression plus fusion"],"tx":["Nonoperative care for symptom control first (PT, NSAIDs, injections) when tolerable","Surgery for refractory symptoms: decompression of the stenosis, and FUSION when instability is present - SLIP trial showed decompression + fusion improved outcomes over decompression alone in degenerative spondylolisthesis with symptomatic stenosis (though some stable slips do well with decompression alone - SPORT)","Individualize: stable, minimal-slip stenosis may be decompressed alone; mobile/unstable slips get instrumented fusion"],"tech":["Decompression (laminectomy) of the stenotic level plus instrumented posterolateral or interbody fusion (TLIF/PLIF/ALIF) with pedicle screws to stabilize and restore alignment","Interbody fusion adds anterior column support and indirect foraminal decompression; reduction of the slip as anatomy allows","Minimally invasive fusion options reduce muscle disruption"],"after":["Fusion healing over months - smoking cessation, avoid NSAIDs early per surgeon preference (fusion), and bracing per protocol","Watch for adjacent-segment degeneration over years, pseudarthrosis, and hardware issues; rehabilitation for core strength","Monitor for durotomy/CSF leak and neurologic change post-op"],"pearls":["Flexion-extension films are the test that catches the instability a supine MRI hides - order them before deciding on fusion","SLIP tilted practice toward decompression + fusion for symptomatic degenerative spondylolisthesis, but stable slips can still do well decompressed alone","Degenerative (facet) and isthmic (pars defect) spondylolisthesis are different diseases in different ages - name which one you have","Fusing an unstable segment restores stability but exports stress to the neighbors - counsel on adjacent-segment disease"],"refs":[{"t":"NASS Degenerative Spondylolisthesis Guideline","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spondylolisthesis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430767/"},{"t":"AANS: Spondylolisthesis","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-cauda-equina","name":"Cauda Equina Syndrome","sec":"nsg","present":["A surgical EMERGENCY from compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots (most often a massive central disc herniation; also tumor, hematoma, abscess, trauma)","Red-flag cluster: new urinary RETENTION (with overflow incontinence), SADDLE (perineal) anesthesia, bilateral leg pain/weakness/sensory loss, and reduced anal tone/sexual dysfunction","Urinary retention is the most consistent finding; painless retention with overflow is a late, ominous sign"],"dx":["URGENT MRI of the lumbosacral spine (whole spine if level uncertain) - do not delay; CT myelogram if MRI contraindicated","Assess post-void residual (bladder scan/catheterization), perineal sensation, and anal tone/reflex on exam","Time is neural tissue - imaging is expedited, not scheduled"],"tx":["Emergent surgical DECOMPRESSION - the evidence favors decompression as soon as feasible; earlier surgery (commonly targeted within 24-48 h of onset, and sooner when possible) improves the odds of recovering bladder/bowel and motor function","Do not wait for business hours for an established, progressing cauda equina - it is treated like the emergency it is","Reverse contributing coagulopathy (epidural hematoma) and treat infection (abscess) as the cause dictates"],"tech":["Wide laminectomy and decompression at the involved level(s) with careful removal of the compressive lesion (large disc fragment, hematoma evacuation, abscess drainage) - gentle handling of swollen, compressed roots","Central massive disc: adequate bony exposure to safely mobilize and decompress without further root injury","Durotomy/tumor-specific technique when the cause is intradural"],"after":["Bladder management (catheterization, urology involvement) and monitoring for recovery - autonomic and bladder function may lag or remain incomplete depending on preoperative severity/duration","Rehabilitation (mobility, bowel/bladder programs); counsel realistically that recovery correlates with the severity and duration of preoperative deficits","Treat the underlying cause definitively (antibiotics for abscess, oncologic care for tumor)"],"pearls":["Ask about urinary retention and check saddle sensation in every acute severe back-and-leg presentation - the diagnosis is missed by not asking","Painless retention with overflow is late cauda equina - do not be reassured by the absence of pain","This is a same-night operation, not a Monday operation - earlier decompression protects bladder and bowel","Preoperative severity and duration predict recovery - which is exactly why speed matters"],"refs":[{"t":"NASS / Spine Emergency Resources","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cauda Equina Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537200/"},{"t":"AANS: Cauda Equina Syndrome","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-sci","name":"Acute Spinal Cord Injury Management","sec":"nsg","present":["Traumatic cord injury with motor/sensory deficit below the level; define the neurologic level and completeness with the ASIA exam (ASIA Impairment Scale A complete -> E normal)","Recognize incomplete syndromes: central cord (upper > lower extremity weakness, hyperextension in the elderly with spondylosis - the most common), Brown-Sequard (hemisection - ipsilateral motor/proprioception, contralateral pain/temp), anterior cord (motor + pain/temp lost, proprioception spared - worst prognosis), posterior cord","Distinguish SPINAL shock (transient areflexia/flaccidity, return of bulbocavernosus reflex marks its end) from NEUROGENIC shock (hypotension + bradycardia from sympathetic disruption in injuries above ~T6)"],"dx":["CT of the spine for bony injury (trauma protocol); MRI to assess cord signal (edema/hemorrhage - prognostic), ligamentous injury, disc/hematoma compression","Serial standardized ASIA exams to track level/completeness and detect deterioration; assess the whole spine (non-contiguous injuries are common)","Maintain full spinal precautions until cleared"],"tx":["Prevent secondary injury: maintain MAP goals (commonly 85-90 mmHg for ~5-7 days) to support cord perfusion; treat neurogenic shock (fluids, vasopressors +/- atropine/chronotrope for bradycardia); ensure oxygenation/ventilation (high cervical injuries threaten the diaphragm - C3-5)","High-dose methylprednisolone is CONTROVERSIAL and NOT routinely recommended (NASCIS harms - infection, GI bleed - outweigh marginal benefit in most guidelines); individualized at most","Early surgical decompression (commonly within 24 h - STASCIS suggested benefit) and stabilization for cord compression/instability improves outcomes in appropriate patients"],"tech":["Decompression and stabilization tailored to the injury: anterior (corpectomy/discectomy + plate) and/or posterior (laminectomy + instrumented fusion) to relieve cord compression and restore alignment/stability","Closed reduction of cervical facet dislocations (awake, with traction) can rapidly decompress before definitive fixation in selected patients","Intraoperative neuromonitoring; goal is a decompressed cord and a stable spine"],"after":["Comprehensive ICU/spine-unit care: hemodynamic goals, respiratory support (high cervical), DVT prophylaxis (high thrombosis risk), skin/pressure-injury prevention, neurogenic bladder/bowel programs, and autonomic dysreflexia awareness (injuries >=T6)","Early rehabilitation and psychological support; prognosis correlates with initial ASIA grade and completeness (incomplete injuries recover more)","Screen for and manage complications (pneumonia, VTE, pressure injury, autonomic dysreflexia)"],"pearls":["Neurogenic shock is hypotension WITH bradycardia - if the injured patient is tachycardic and hypotensive, look for hemorrhage first","Central cord syndrome in an older person after a hyperextension fall is the classic incomplete injury - hands worse than legs","High-dose steroids for SCI are largely out - the complications outweigh the marginal, contested benefit","MAP support and early decompression are the interventions that actually protect the cord - perfuse it and unpinch it"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS/CNS Acute Spinal Cord Injury Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Cord Injuries","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560721/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Cord Injury","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-cspine-fractures","name":"Cervical Spine Fractures & Instability","sec":"nsg","present":["Result from high-energy trauma (and low-energy falls in the ankylosed/osteoporotic spine); neck pain, tenderness, torticollis, +/- neurologic deficit","Key patterns: atlas (Jefferson burst - C1 ring), odontoid (Anderson-D'Alonzo types I-III; type II through the base is unstable and nonunion-prone), hangman's fracture (C2 traumatic spondylolisthesis), atlanto-occipital/atlantoaxial dislocation, and subaxial (C3-7) injuries","Ankylosing spondylitis/DISH spines fracture through fused bone from trivial trauma and are highly unstable - a special high-alert group"],"dx":["Apply validated clearance rules to low-risk patients (NEXUS, Canadian C-Spine Rule); CT is the imaging standard for bony injury in at-risk patients; MRI for suspected ligamentous injury, cord signal, or unexplained deficit","Classify subaxial injuries with the SLIC (Subaxial Injury Classification) and craniocervical injuries appropriately to guide operative vs nonoperative care","In ankylosing spine trauma, CT the ENTIRE spine and have a very low threshold - these fractures are easily missed and catastrophic"],"tx":["Stable fractures: external immobilization (rigid collar; halo vest for selected unstable C1-2 patterns) with follow-up imaging to confirm healing","Unstable fractures, dislocations, or those with cord compression/deficit: surgical stabilization +/- decompression; type II odontoid in older patients often fixed (or halo, with higher nonunion) - individualized","Reduce dislocations (closed traction for facet dislocations in the awake patient) and stabilize"],"tech":["Posterior approaches: occipitocervical fusion for craniocervical instability, C1-2 fixation (Goel-Harms segmental screws) for atlantoaxial instability/odontoid, lateral mass/pedicle screw constructs for subaxial injuries","Anterior approaches: odontoid screw for selected type II fractures (preserves rotation), anterior cervical decompression and fusion for disc/body injuries with cord compression","Halo vest for select C1-2 and pediatric injuries"],"after":["Neurologic monitoring, immobilization compliance, and imaging to confirm alignment/fusion; watch for hardware failure and nonunion (odontoid)","Ankylosing spine patients need meticulous handling, avoidance of forced positioning, and awareness of epidural hematoma risk","Rehabilitation and, for elderly odontoid fractures, attention to overall morbidity/mortality (these carry significant risk)"],"pearls":["The ankylosed (AS/DISH) spine fractures like a long bone from minor trauma and is grossly unstable - image the whole spine and treat every neck complaint seriously","Type II odontoid fractures love nonunion - management is individualized and often surgical, especially in the elderly","NEXUS and the Canadian C-Spine Rule clear the low-risk patient without imaging - know the criteria cold","A facet dislocation can be reduced with awake traction to rapidly decompress before definitive fixation"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS/CNS Cervical Spine Injury Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Spine Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448124/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Trauma","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-tl-fractures","name":"Thoracolumbar Fractures","sec":"nsg","present":["Common at the thoracolumbar junction (T10-L2) where the rigid thoracic spine meets the mobile lumbar; from high-energy trauma or osteoporotic/pathologic low-energy fractures","Patterns: compression (anterior column), burst (anterior + middle column, retropulsion into canal), flexion-distraction/CHANCE (seatbelt injury - posterior ligamentous/bony disruption, associated intra-abdominal injury), and fracture-dislocation (highly unstable)","Assess for neurologic deficit (conus/cauda level), the posterior ligamentous complex, and associated injuries (calcaneus, abdominal in Chance)"],"dx":["CT defines bony injury, canal compromise, and column involvement; MRI evaluates the POSTERIOR LIGAMENTOUS COMPLEX (PLC) integrity and cord/conus - PLC status is pivotal for stability","Use the TLICS (Thoracolumbar Injury Classification and Severity) score: injury morphology + PLC integrity + neurologic status -> guides operative vs nonoperative (score >=5 favors surgery, <=3 nonoperative, 4 equivocal)","Screen for the seatbelt sign and abdominal injury with Chance fractures"],"tx":["Stable injuries (most compression fractures, many burst fractures with intact PLC and no deficit): nonoperative with bracing (TLSO) and mobilization, with follow-up imaging","Surgery for unstable injuries (disrupted PLC, fracture-dislocation), progressive deformity, or neurologic deficit with canal compromise: decompression (if deficit) and stabilization","Chance fractures with bony/ligamentous disruption often require fixation; treat associated abdominal injuries"],"tech":["Posterior instrumented fusion (pedicle screws) spanning the injury, with reduction of deformity; decompression (laminectomy/transpedicular) when there is neural compression from retropulsion","Anterior or combined approaches for significant anterior column deficiency/reconstruction (corpectomy + cage)","Percutaneous/minimally invasive fixation for select injuries; cement augmentation in osteoporotic bone"],"after":["Bracing per plan, early mobilization, and imaging to confirm alignment/fusion; DVT prophylaxis; neurologic monitoring","Rehabilitation; counsel on deformity/pain and hardware issues; manage osteoporosis when relevant","Watch for progressive kyphosis in conservatively managed injuries"],"pearls":["TLICS turns the stability question into a score - morphology, PLC, and neurology decide operate versus brace","The posterior ligamentous complex is the hinge of stability - an MRI showing PLC disruption changes the plan","A Chance (seatbelt) fracture comes with an abdomen to examine - look for the associated hollow-viscus injury","A burst fracture with an intact PLC and no deficit often does well in a brace - not every canal compromise needs surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"AOSpine Thoracolumbar Injury Classification","u":"https://www.aospine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracolumbar Burst Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499985/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Fractures","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-spinal-tumors","name":"Metastatic Epidural Spinal Cord Compression & Spinal Tumors","sec":"nsg","present":["Metastatic epidural spinal cord compression (MESCC) is an ONCOLOGIC EMERGENCY: progressive back pain (often the first and earliest symptom, worse at night/recumbent), then radiculopathy, weakness, sensory level, and bladder/bowel dysfunction","Common primaries: breast, lung, prostate, renal, myeloma; the spine is the most common site of skeletal metastasis","Categorize spinal tumors: extradural (metastases - most common), intradural-extramedullary (meningioma, schwannoma), and intramedullary (ependymoma, astrocytoma)"],"dx":["URGENT whole-spine MRI with contrast for any cancer patient with new/progressive back pain or neurologic signs - do not wait for weakness (pain precedes deficit and outcome tracks pre-treatment function)","Grade cord compression (Bilsky/ESCC scale) and assess mechanical stability with the SINS (Spinal Instability Neoplastic Score); tissue diagnosis if primary unknown","Staging and multidisciplinary (radiation/medical oncology) input on tumor radiosensitivity and prognosis"],"tx":["Start dexamethasone immediately for symptomatic MESCC to reduce edema and preserve function while arranging definitive treatment","Patchell trial paradigm: direct decompressive SURGERY followed by radiation improved ambulation over radiation alone for suitable patients with cord compression - select by prognosis, stability, and radiosensitivity","Radiosensitive tumors (myeloma, lymphoma, some breast/prostate) may respond to radiation alone; the modern approach is often SEPARATION SURGERY (decompress the cord, create margin) + stereotactic body radiotherapy for durable local control"],"tech":["Decompression (posterior laminectomy +/- transpedicular/costotransversectomy) to relieve the cord, with instrumented stabilization for mechanical instability (high SINS)","Separation surgery: circumferential epidural decompression to create a safe margin from the cord, enabling high-dose stereotactic radiation without over-resecting tumor","Percutaneous stabilization/cement augmentation for pain from pathologic fractures without cord compression"],"after":["Continue steroids (taper), oncologic therapy coordination, and radiation planning; early mobilization and rehabilitation","Prognosis and goals-of-care central - MESCC often signals advanced disease; ambulation status at treatment predicts post-treatment ambulation","VTE prophylaxis, pain control, and surveillance for further spinal disease"],"pearls":["New or worsening back pain in a cancer patient is cord compression until an urgent MRI says otherwise - pain precedes paralysis","Pre-treatment ambulatory status is the strongest predictor of walking afterward - which is why you image and act before the deficit","Patchell: decompressive surgery THEN radiation beat radiation alone for the right compression patients","SINS scores the mechanical stability question - a stable radiosensitive tumor may be radiation alone; an unstable one needs stabilization"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN CNS/Bone Metastasis Guidance","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Metastatic Spinal Cord Compression","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441891/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Tumors","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-spinal-abscess","name":"Spinal Epidural Abscess","sec":"nsg","present":["Pus in the spinal epidural space; the classic triad is back pain + fever + neurologic deficit (though all three are present in a minority - fever may be absent)","Risk factors: diabetes, IV drug use, immunosuppression, recent spinal procedure/instrumentation, bacteremia, and indwelling catheters; Staphylococcus aureus (incl MRSA) is the leading organism","Progression can be rapid from local pain to catastrophic paralysis - a high-index-of-suspicion diagnosis"],"dx":["URGENT contrast-enhanced MRI of the spine (image the whole spine - skip lesions occur) is the study of choice","Labs: elevated ESR/CRP and WBC; BLOOD CULTURES before antibiotics; identify the organism (blood cultures or image-guided/operative sampling) - crucial for tailored therapy","Look for the source (endocarditis, skin/soft tissue, discitis-osteomyelitis) and comorbid infection"],"tx":["Neurosurgical/spine emergency: surgical DECOMPRESSION AND DRAINAGE for neurologic deficit, progression, or significant compression - combined with prolonged culture-directed IV antibiotics (typically 6+ weeks)","Empiric antibiotics after cultures: cover MRSA (vancomycin) plus gram-negatives (e.g., a third/fourth-gen cephalosporin) pending results - but do not let antibiotics delay surgery in the deteriorating patient","Nonoperative management (antibiotics alone with very close monitoring) only in selected neurologically intact patients with reliable follow-up and identified organism - deterioration mandates surgery"],"tech":["Laminectomy over the involved level(s) with evacuation of the epidural pus and copious irrigation; obtain operative cultures; stabilize if osteomyelitis/instability coexists","Address concurrent discitis/vertebral osteomyelitis and drain paraspinal/psoas collections as needed","Multilevel or skip abscesses may require decompression at multiple sites or catheter irrigation"],"after":["Prolonged culture-directed IV antibiotics with infectious-disease co-management; monitor inflammatory markers and neurologic recovery","Rehabilitation; recovery correlates strongly with preoperative neurologic status and duration of deficit - delays cost function","Surveillance for recurrence, treat the underlying source, and address risk factors (glycemic control, IVDU support, catheter care)"],"pearls":["The classic triad is often incomplete - back pain plus a risk factor (IVDU, diabetes) should trigger an urgent MRI even without fever","Image the WHOLE spine - epidural abscesses skip levels and the second site is the one that paralyzes","Blood cultures before antibiotics - identifying the organism steers 6 weeks of therapy","Preoperative neurologic status is the prognosis - a walking patient who deteriorates while you 'observe' is a preventable tragedy"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA Native Vertebral Osteomyelitis Guideline","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Epidural Abscess","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441890/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal Infections","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-spinal-avm","name":"Spinal Vascular Malformations (Spinal AVM/dAVF)","sec":"nsg","present":["Spinal DURAL arteriovenous fistula (type I) is the most common - typically older men with a PROGRESSIVE myelopathy (ascending sensorimotor loss, gait difficulty, sphincter dysfunction) from venous congestion/hypertension (historically Foix-Alajouanine syndrome)","True spinal AVMs (intramedullary/glomus - type II, juvenile - type III, perimedullary - type IV) present younger, may hemorrhage (subarachnoid or intramedullary) or cause progressive deficits","The dural fistula is under-recognized and treatable - a myelopathy that is easily mistaken for degenerative or demyelinating disease"],"dx":["MRI shows cord T2 hyperintensity (edema) with dilated perimedullary flow voids (serpentine dorsal veins) - the clue that prompts angiography","Spinal DSA is the gold standard - localizes the fistula/nidus, feeding radicular arteries, and (critically) the artery of Adamkiewicz to plan safe treatment","Consider the diagnosis in any unexplained progressive myelopathy, especially in older men - it is curable if found"],"tx":["Spinal dural AVF: definitive treatment by DISCONNECTION - either surgical ligation of the intradural draining vein at the fistula or endovascular embolization (durable embolic must reach the fistula/proximal vein); untreated, myelopathy progresses","Intramedullary/complex AVMs: multidisciplinary, often multimodal (embolization +/- microsurgery +/- radiosurgery), weighing risk given eloquent cord","Treat early - venous congestive myelopathy recovery depends on duration/severity of the deficit"],"tech":["Surgical: laminectomy, intradural identification of the arterialized draining vein, and coagulation/division at the dural fistula point - simple and durable for type I fistulas","Endovascular: superselective embolization of the fistula with liquid embolic reaching the proximal draining vein; must avoid the anterior spinal artery/artery of Adamkiewicz to prevent cord infarction","Intraoperative angiography/indocyanine green to confirm obliteration"],"after":["Post-treatment imaging/angiography to confirm cure (residual shunt continues the congestion); monitor neurologic recovery, which is often gradual and partial","Rehabilitation for gait/bladder function; treat earlier for better recovery in future patients (teaching point for the referral chain)","Surveillance for recurrence, particularly after embolization"],"pearls":["A progressive myelopathy in an older man with cord edema and dorsal flow voids is a spinal dural AVF until angiography proves otherwise - and it is curable","The treatment is disconnecting the draining vein - surgery is simple and durable for the common type I fistula","Spare the artery of Adamkiewicz - embolizing into the anterior spinal supply infarcts the cord","Recovery tracks duration of symptoms - the fistula found and fixed early does far better"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Spinal Vascular Malformation Resources","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Arteriovenous Malformation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560731/"},{"t":"AANS: Spinal AVM","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-vertebral-augmentation","name":"Vertebral Augmentation (Kyphoplasty/Vertebroplasty)","sec":"nsg","present":["Percutaneous cement stabilization of PAINFUL vertebral compression fractures - osteoporotic (most common), or pathologic (myeloma, metastasis)","Presents with focal, activity-related midline back pain over the fractured level, often after minimal trauma in osteoporotic/elderly patients; point tenderness at the level","Distinguish the acute painful fracture (the augmentation target) from old, healed, or asymptomatic fractures"],"dx":["Radiographs identify the fracture; MRI (STIR/marrow edema) or bone scan confirms the fracture is ACUTE/active (edema) and pain-generating - the key to selecting responders","Rule out fractures needing different management: those with retropulsion/canal compromise, instability, or neurologic deficit are not simple augmentation candidates","Work up pathologic fractures (biopsy at augmentation when malignancy possible) and treat underlying osteoporosis"],"tx":["Consider augmentation for acute/subacute painful osteoporotic fractures with inadequate response to conservative care (analgesia, bracing, activity) - evidence is mixed (early sham-controlled trials VERTOS II positive vs INVEST/Buchbinder negative; VAPOUR positive for acute severe pain), so patient selection (acute, edema-positive, focally tender) matters","Kyphoplasty (balloon cavity + cement) vs vertebroplasty (direct cement injection) - kyphoplasty may restore some height and lower cement-leak pressure","Always pair with osteoporosis pharmacotherapy and fall prevention - augmentation treats the pain, not the disease"],"tech":["Fluoroscopically guided transpedicular (or extrapedicular) needle placement into the vertebral body; kyphoplasty inflates a balloon to create a cavity and partially reduce the fracture, then injects PMMA cement under low pressure; vertebroplasty injects cement directly","Meticulous imaging to avoid CEMENT LEAKAGE (into the canal, foramina, or veins - can cause radiculopathy, cord compression, or pulmonary embolism)","Biopsy through the same access when malignancy is suspected"],"after":["Rapid pain relief and mobilization are typical when patient selection is right; monitor for cement-leak complications and new ADJACENT-LEVEL fractures (a known risk - the stiff augmented level loads its neighbors)","Start/optimize osteoporosis therapy (bisphosphonate/anabolic), calcium/vitamin D, and fall prevention to prevent the next fracture","Physical therapy and pain follow-up"],"pearls":["MRI marrow edema separates the painful acute fracture (responds) from the incidental old one (won't) - it is the selection test","The augmentation trials disagree because they enrolled different patients - acute, focally tender, edema-positive fractures are the responders","Cement leakage is the feared complication - low-pressure injection under live fluoroscopy is the safeguard","Augmentation treats a symptom - without osteoporosis therapy the patient returns with the adjacent-level fracture"],"refs":[{"t":"Society of Interventional Radiology / Spine Guidance","u":"https://www.sirweb.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vertebroplasty and Kyphoplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551702/"},{"t":"AANS: Vertebral Compression Fractures","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-hydrocephalus-shunt","name":"Hydrocephalus & Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt","sec":"nsg","present":["Accumulation of CSF with ventricular enlargement from overproduction (rare - choroid plexus tumor), obstruction (OBSTRUCTIVE/noncommunicating - aqueductal stenosis, tumor, IVH), or impaired absorption (COMMUNICATING - post-hemorrhagic, post-meningitic)","Acute raised-ICP presentation (headache, vomiting, decreased consciousness, papilledema); infants show macrocephaly, bulging fontanelle, sunsetting eyes, and split sutures","Chronic/normal-pressure forms present more insidiously (see iNPH entry)"],"dx":["CT/MRI shows ventriculomegaly disproportionate to sulci; identify the obstructing lesion and level; MRI (including cine flow) characterizes aqueductal patency and cause","Determine communicating vs obstructive - it drives the choice between shunt and endoscopic third ventriculostomy","Assess for the treatable cause (tumor removal may relieve obstruction) before committing to lifelong hardware"],"tx":["Treat the cause when possible; for persistent hydrocephalus, CSF diversion - ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt is the workhorse (alternatives: ventriculoatrial, ventriculopleural)","Endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV) is preferred for suitable OBSTRUCTIVE hydrocephalus (e.g., aqueductal stenosis) to avoid hardware (see ETV entry)","Programmable (adjustable) valves allow noninvasive pressure tuning to balance under- vs over-drainage"],"tech":["VP shunt: ventricular catheter (frontal Kocher or occipital Keen point) -> valve (fixed or programmable, +/- antisiphon/gravitational device) -> distal catheter tunneled subcutaneously to the peritoneal cavity","Confirm ventricular entry (CSF flow) and distal placement (often laparoscopic-assisted peritoneal insertion); antibiotic-impregnated catheters and meticulous sterile technique reduce infection","Set/confirm valve pressure and document the programmable setting"],"after":["Educate patient/family on shunt malfunction and infection warning signs (headache, vomiting, decreased consciousness, fever) - lifelong vigilance","Note that MRI can reprogram some adjustable valves - RECHECK/RESET the setting after any MRI","Baseline post-op imaging (shunt series/CT) to document catheter position and ventricular size for future comparison"],"pearls":["Communicating vs obstructive is the fork in the road - obstructive hydrocephalus may be cured by ETV without any hardware","A programmable valve can be altered by MRI - always confirm and reset the setting afterward","Fix the cause first when you can - removing an obstructing tumor may spare the patient a lifelong shunt","Document the baseline ventricle size and valve setting - future malfunction is diagnosed by comparison"],"refs":[{"t":"Hydrocephalus Association Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.hydroassoc.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hydrocephalus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560875/"},{"t":"AANS: Hydrocephalus","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-shunt-malfunction","name":"Shunt Malfunction & Shunt Infection","sec":"nsg","present":["Shunt MALFUNCTION (obstruction is most common - proximal catheter, valve, or distal) presents with recurrent hydrocephalus symptoms: headache, vomiting, lethargy, and (in children) irritability, bulging fontanelle, upgaze difficulty - often mimicking the patient's original presentation","Shunt INFECTION usually occurs within weeks-months of surgery: fever, shunt-tract erythema/tenderness, meningitis, abdominal pain/peritonitis (distal), and malfunction; Staphylococcus epidermidis and S. aureus predominate","Over-drainage presents differently: postural headaches, slit ventricles, or subdural collections"],"dx":["Imaging: CT/MRI (compare ventricle size to the patient's baseline - enlargement suggests malfunction; unchanged small ventricles do not exclude it) and a SHUNT SERIES (plain radiographs of the whole shunt) for disconnection/kinking/migration","Shunt TAP (sterile) to assess flow/opening pressure and obtain CSF for cell count, glucose, protein, Gram stain, and CULTURE when infection is suspected - the definitive infection test","Nuclear/patency studies (shuntogram) when function is uncertain; assess distal end (abdominal ultrasound for pseudocyst)"],"tx":["Malfunction: surgical REVISION of the obstructed/disconnected component (or the whole system); emergent for acute decompensation/raised ICP (temporize with a tap or EVD if needed)","Infection: EXTERNALIZE or REMOVE the entire shunt, place an EVD for temporary CSF diversion, and give culture-directed IV antibiotics (often intraventricular for ventriculitis) until CSF sterilizes, then REIMPLANT a new shunt - retaining infected hardware fails","Manage over-drainage (raise valve pressure/programmable adjustment, antisiphon device; treat subdurals)"],"tech":["Revision: identify the failed component (proximal catheter often occluded by choroid/debris - replace; distal obstruction/pseudocyst - revise distal end, consider new peritoneal or alternative site)","Infection: remove all hardware, EVD placement, and staged reimplantation after CSF clears; consider antibiotic-impregnated catheters at reimplantation","Endoscopic assistance for stuck/adherent ventricular catheters"],"after":["Post-revision imaging and symptom resolution confirm success; educate again on warning signs - shunted patients have lifelong malfunction/infection risk","After infection: confirm CSF sterility before reimplantation; ID co-management for antibiotic course; monitor for re-infection","Counsel families that a shunt is a lifelong device requiring vigilance and periodic revision"],"pearls":["A shunted patient with their original symptoms has a shunt malfunction until proven otherwise - compare ventricles to their own baseline, not to normal","Normal-looking (small) ventricles do NOT exclude malfunction, especially with non-compliant/slit ventricles - use the whole picture and tap if unsure","Infected hardware must come out - you cannot antibiotic your way to cure with the shunt in place","Reset the programmable valve after MRI, and remember over-drainage (postural headache, subdurals) is malfunction too"],"refs":[{"t":"Hydrocephalus Association / Shunt Resources","u":"https://www.hydroassoc.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459351/"},{"t":"AANS: Hydrocephalus","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-etv","name":"Endoscopic Third Ventriculostomy","sec":"nsg","present":["An endoscopic procedure creating a stoma in the floor of the third ventricle to divert CSF into the basal cisterns - bypassing an obstruction and treating OBSTRUCTIVE hydrocephalus WITHOUT implanting hardware","Best candidates: noncommunicating hydrocephalus from aqueductal stenosis, tectal/posterior fossa tumors, and some cases of shunt failure - where the block is downstream of the third ventricle","Poor for communicating hydrocephalus (absorption problem) and very young infants (lower success)"],"dx":["MRI (including cine phase-contrast flow) confirms obstructive pattern, aqueductal stenosis, and adequate third-ventricle floor anatomy and prepontine cistern space for a safe stoma","Estimate likelihood of success with the ETV Success Score (age, cause of hydrocephalus, previous shunt) - older children/adults with aqueductal stenosis do best","Confirm the anatomy (position of basilar artery below the floor) to plan a safe fenestration"],"tx":["ETV for appropriately selected obstructive hydrocephalus - avoids shunt hardware and its lifelong malfunction/infection burden","Combine with endoscopic biopsy for intraventricular/pineal tumors causing the obstruction","When ETV is not suitable or fails, revert to shunting"],"tech":["Rigid endoscope via a frontal burr hole (Kocher point) into the lateral then third ventricle; identify landmarks (mammillary bodies, infundibular recess) and perforate the floor in the midline anterior to the basilar artery (between mammillary bodies and infundibulum)","Enlarge the stoma bluntly (balloon/forceps) and inspect the underlying membrane of Liliequist; confirm flow","Meticulous avoidance of the basilar artery and perforators - catastrophic hemorrhage is the dreaded complication"],"after":["Monitor for early failure (persistent/recurrent hydrocephalus - the stoma can close, especially in infants); post-op MRI flow void through the stoma confirms patency","Watch for complications: hemorrhage, transient memory/hypothalamic disturbance, CSF leak, and infection (lower than shunt infection)","Educate on symptom recurrence - late ETV closure can occur; some patients ultimately need a shunt"],"pearls":["ETV cures obstructive hydrocephalus without hardware - the right patient trades a lifelong shunt for a stoma","The ETV Success Score predicts who benefits - aqueductal stenosis in an older child/adult is the sweet spot","The basilar artery sits just under the third-ventricle floor - fenestrate in the midline, anterior, and gently","ETV can fail late by closing - counsel on recurrent symptoms rather than assuming permanent cure"],"refs":[{"t":"Hydrocephalus Association Clinical Resources","u":"https://www.hydroassoc.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endoscopic Third Ventriculostomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555996/"},{"t":"AANS: Hydrocephalus Treatment","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-chiari","name":"Chiari I Malformation","sec":"nsg","present":["Caudal descent of the cerebellar TONSILS below the foramen magnum (conventionally >5 mm); crowding at the craniocervical junction impairs CSF flow","Classic symptom: occipital/suboccipital headache triggered by VALSALVA (cough, strain, laughter); also neck pain, dizziness, and lower cranial nerve/brainstem symptoms","SYRINGOMYELIA (a cord syrinx) in a substantial minority -> central-cord-pattern signs: dissociated (cape-like) sensory loss, hand wasting, scoliosis (especially in children)"],"dx":["MRI brain and cervical spine: measure tonsillar descent and, importantly, look for peg-like tonsils, crowding, and a SYRINX; cine phase-contrast MRI assesses CSF flow at the foramen magnum","Distinguish symptomatic Chiari I from an incidental radiographic finding (common) - treatment is for symptoms/syrinx, not the millimeters alone","Exclude secondary tonsillar descent (raised ICP, intracranial hypotension/CSF leak) which is managed differently"],"tx":["Asymptomatic Chiari I without syrinx: OBSERVE with clinical/imaging follow-up - the measurement alone does not warrant surgery","Surgical decompression for symptomatic patients (disabling Valsalva headaches, brainstem symptoms) or a syrinx - to restore CSF flow at the craniocervical junction","Treat any secondary cause (e.g., a CSF leak causing sagging) rather than decompressing"],"tech":["Posterior fossa (foramen magnum) decompression: suboccipital craniectomy + C1 laminectomy; duraplasty (opening and patch-grafting the dura) is added in most symptomatic/syrinx cases to expand the cisterna magna (bony decompression alone is an option in select pediatric cases)","Optional tonsillar reduction/coagulation and arachnoid dissection when needed to open CSF pathways","Avoid over-decompression (cerebellar ptosis) and ensure watertight dural closure to limit CSF leak/pseudomeningocele"],"after":["Monitor for CSF leak/pseudomeningocele and aseptic meningitis; headaches often improve; the SYRINX typically shrinks over months once flow is restored (follow with imaging)","Counsel that not all symptoms fully resolve and that the goal is halting progression and relieving the CSF block","Rehabilitation for established deficits; long-term imaging follow-up of the syrinx"],"pearls":["The cough/Valsalva occipital headache is the Chiari I symptom that predicts response to decompression - millimeters alone do not","Find the syrinx - a cord cavity changes an incidental Chiari into a surgical one and explains the hand wasting and scoliosis","Rule out a CSF leak causing SECONDARY tonsillar sag before decompressing - the treatment is opposite","The syrinx resolves after you fix the flow, not by draining it directly - decompress the foramen magnum"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Chiari Malformation Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chiari Malformation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431076/"},{"t":"Column / Bobby Jones CSF Chiari Resources","u":"https://www.bobbyjonescsf.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-inph","name":"Idiopathic Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus","sec":"nsg","present":["A potentially REVERSIBLE cause of dementia in older adults; the classic Hakim triad (wet, wobbly, wacky): gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment","GAIT is typically first and most responsive - magnetic, wide-based, shuffling, short steps with turning difficulty ('glue-footed'); cognitive slowing is subcortical (executive/attention), unlike Alzheimer memory loss","Communicating hydrocephalus with normal opening pressure - hence the name"],"dx":["MRI: ventriculomegaly out of proportion to atrophy (Evans index >0.3), a tight high convexity, and the DESH pattern (disproportionately enlarged subarachnoid-space hydrocephalus - widened Sylvian fissures with tight vertex)","CSF drainage trials PREDICT shunt response and are the crux of selection: high-volume (30-50 mL) lumbar tap test with pre/post gait (and cognitive) assessment, or extended lumbar drain trial (higher sensitivity)","Exclude/quantify comorbid neurodegeneration and vascular disease (which lower the response ceiling)"],"tx":["VP shunting (typically a programmable valve) is the treatment - gait improvement is the most reliable outcome; incontinence and cognition respond less consistently","Select rigorously by CSF-drainage response - shunting non-responders exposes frail elderly to hardware risk without benefit","Optimize comorbid contributors (vascular risk, deconditioning) in parallel"],"tech":["VP shunt placement with a programmable valve (+/- antisiphon/gravitational device) to titrate drainage and manage over-drainage subdurals - a real risk in the atrophic elderly brain","Start at a conservative (higher) pressure and step down while watching for subdural collections","Careful catheter placement and technique as for any shunt"],"after":["Serial gait/cognitive assessment to document benefit; adjust the programmable valve to balance symptom control against over-drainage","Monitor for SUBDURAL hematoma/hygroma (common in this population - a reason to start conservative and adjust); manage with valve adjustment or drainage as needed","Ongoing rehabilitation (physical therapy for gait) amplifies the shunt's benefit"],"pearls":["Gait is the first to go and the first to respond - a big tap-test gait improvement is the best predictor of shunt benefit","NPH is the dementia you can sometimes reverse - screen the wet, wobbly, wacky older patient with a drainage trial","The subcortical, executive slowing of NPH is unlike the amnestic pattern of Alzheimer disease - the cognitive profile is a clue","Program conservatively and watch for subdurals - the atrophic brain over-drains easily"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS/CNS iNPH Guidelines","u":"https://www.cns.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558925/"},{"t":"Hydrocephalus Association: NPH","u":"https://www.hydroassoc.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-colloid-cyst","name":"Colloid Cyst","sec":"nsg","present":["A benign mucin-filled cyst classically at the roof of the THIRD VENTRICLE near the foramen of Monro - positioned to intermittently obstruct CSF flow","Can cause POSITIONAL headaches, and intermittent obstruction can produce acute hydrocephalus; the feared presentation is sudden deterioration or SUDDEN DEATH from acute obstruction","Often incidental on imaging; symptoms correlate with size and ventricular obstruction"],"dx":["CT: classically a hyperdense rounded lesion at the foramen of Monro; MRI variable signal (often T1-bright from proteinaceous content) - defines size and hydrocephalus","Assess ventricular size and obstruction; larger cysts (roughly >=1 cm) and those with hydrocephalus or symptoms carry higher risk","Distinguish from other third-ventricle lesions"],"tx":["Symptomatic cysts and those causing hydrocephalus -> surgical removal; larger/obstructing cysts even if minimally symptomatic are often treated given the sudden-deterioration risk","Small, asymptomatic, non-obstructing cysts may be observed with serial imaging - individualized by size, ventricular status, and patient factors","Relieve acute hydrocephalus (EVD) as a bridge if the patient presents obstructed"],"tech":["Endoscopic resection/aspiration via a frontal burr hole (minimally invasive, increasingly first-line) - fenestrate and remove cyst contents and wall as feasible","Microsurgical transcallosal (or transcortical) approach for complete removal, especially for firm/adherent cysts or when endoscopy is insufficient - lower recurrence but more invasive","Address hydrocephalus; protect the fornices (memory) during any transforaminal manipulation"],"after":["Watch for persistent hydrocephalus (may still need a shunt), MEMORY disturbance from fornix manipulation, and recurrence (higher after incomplete/endoscopic removal - surveillance imaging)","Confirm relief of obstruction; counsel on recurrence surveillance","Rehabilitation if memory affected (often improves)"],"pearls":["A hyperdense rounded lesion at the foramen of Monro is a colloid cyst - positioned exactly to block CSF and, rarely, kill suddenly","The fornices flank the surgical corridor - protect them or trade the cyst for a memory deficit","Endoscopy is elegant and minimally invasive but has higher recurrence than complete transcallosal removal - counsel on surveillance","An obstructing colloid cyst can decompensate fast - relieve hydrocephalus promptly and do not dismiss the incidental large one"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Colloid Cyst Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Colloid Cyst","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470314/"},{"t":"CNS: Intraventricular Tumors","u":"https://www.cns.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-iih","name":"Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension","sec":"nsg","present":["Raised ICP without a mass or hydrocephalus, classically in OBESE women of childbearing age; headache (often daily), pulsatile tinnitus, transient visual obscurations, and diplopia (CN VI palsy - a false localizing sign)","PAPILLEDEMA is the cardinal sign and the reason vision is at stake - untreated IIH threatens permanent visual loss","Associations/mimics: venous sinus thrombosis, certain drugs (tetracyclines, vitamin A/retinoids, growth hormone), and secondary causes must be excluded"],"dx":["Modified Dandy criteria: signs/symptoms of raised ICP, no localizing findings except CN VI, normal neuroimaging (no mass/hydrocephalus - often slit ventricles, empty sella, flattened globe, transverse venous sinus stenosis on MRV), and elevated LP OPENING PRESSURE with normal CSF composition","MRI + MR VENOGRAPHY to exclude venous sinus thrombosis and identify sinus stenosis; LP after imaging (opening pressure typically >25 cm H2O)","Formal, serial OPHTHALMOLOGIC assessment (visual fields, OCT) - vision is the outcome that matters and drives urgency"],"tx":["WEIGHT LOSS is disease-modifying (even modest loss helps; bariatric surgery for severe obesity); acetazolamide is first-line medical therapy (IIHTT - improved visual field outcomes) + weight management; topiramate as an alternative/adjunct","Serial visual monitoring guides escalation; symptomatic LPs are temporizing, not definitive","Surgery/procedures for FULMINANT disease or progressive visual loss despite medical therapy - do not wait while vision declines"],"tech":["CSF diversion (VP or lumboperitoneal shunt) for medically refractory raised ICP/headache with visual threat - programmable valve to manage over-drainage","Optic nerve sheath fenestration when visual loss dominates (protects vision, less effect on headache)","Venous sinus stenting for selected patients with a significant transverse sinus stenosis and pressure gradient - an increasingly used option"],"after":["Close ophthalmologic follow-up (fields/OCT) to confirm papilledema resolution and protect vision; reinforce weight management as the durable fix","Manage shunt complications (over-drainage, obstruction - LP shunts fail often) or fenestration/stent-related issues","Screen for and address secondary causes/offending drugs; counsel on recurrence with weight regain"],"pearls":["Vision, not headache, is the emergency in IIH - serial visual fields and OCT drive the urgency, and papilledema is the sign to respect","Always get an MRV - venous sinus thrombosis is the great mimic you cannot miss","Weight loss is genuinely disease-modifying - it is treatment, not lifestyle advice, in IIH","Fulminant IIH with worsening vision goes to a procedure (fenestration/shunt/stent) now - do not keep titrating pills while the optic nerve dies"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/NORDIC IIH Resources","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448143/"},{"t":"AANS: Pseudotumor Cerebri","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-myelomeningocele","name":"Myelomeningocele & Spinal Dysraphism (Pediatric)","sec":"nsg","present":["Neural tube defects from failed neurulation: OPEN dysraphism (myelomeningocele - exposed neural placode in a sac, CSF leak, motor/sensory and bowel/bladder deficits below the level) vs CLOSED/occult dysraphism (lipomyelomeningocele, dermal sinus, split cord, filum lipoma - often with a cutaneous stigma: tuft of hair, dimple, lipoma, hemangioma)","Myelomeningocele is nearly always associated with the Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus; occult lesions may present later with tethered cord (see tethered cord entry)","Level of the lesion predicts function (ambulation, continence)"],"dx":["Prenatal detection: elevated maternal serum AFP and fetal ultrasound/MRI (Chiari II, ventriculomegaly, the lemon and banana signs); postnatal exam of the defect and neurologic level","MRI of the whole neuraxis for the cord, associated anomalies (syrinx, split cord, tethering), and the Chiari II hindbrain; head imaging for hydrocephalus","Screen for a cutaneous marker in any suspected occult dysraphism"],"tx":["Prevention is paramount: periconceptional FOLIC ACID markedly reduces neural tube defects - the public-health headline","Open myelomeningocele: closure to protect the placode and prevent infection - traditionally postnatal within ~48-72 h; PRENATAL (fetal) repair (MOMS trial) reduces the need for shunting and improves motor outcomes in selected cases (at some maternal/fetal risk)","Manage associated hydrocephalus (shunt or ETV) and symptomatic Chiari II; occult dysraphism with tethering/neurologic change -> surgical release"],"tech":["Myelomeningocele closure: reconstitute the neural placode into a tube, close dura, and achieve layered watertight skin/soft-tissue coverage (flaps for large defects) to prevent CSF leak and infection","Fetal repair via open or fetoscopic techniques in specialized centers (MOMS criteria)","Untethering, lipoma debulking, and split-cord (diastematomyelia) bony spur removal for the relevant occult lesions"],"after":["Multidisciplinary spina bifida care for life: neurosurgery (shunt/Chiari/tethering surveillance), urology (neurogenic bladder - protect kidneys), orthopedics (deformity, ambulation), and rehabilitation","Monitor for shunt malfunction, symptomatic Chiari II (stridor, apnea, swallowing - can be life-threatening in infants), and later tethered cord as the child grows","Latex allergy precautions (high prevalence); developmental and bowel/bladder programs"],"pearls":["Folic acid before and during early pregnancy is the single biggest lever against neural tube defects - the prevention headline","Every myelomeningocele comes with a Chiari II and usually hydrocephalus - anticipate the shunt and the hindbrain symptoms","MOMS shifted selected cases to prenatal repair - fewer shunts and better motor function, at a cost in maternal/fetal risk","A dimple, hairy patch, or lipoma over the spine is a closed dysraphism clue - image before it tethers and declares itself"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Spina Bifida Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Myelomeningocele","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546696/"},{"t":"Spina Bifida Association Clinical Care Guidelines","u":"https://www.spinabifidaassociation.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-tethered-cord","name":"Tethered Cord Syndrome","sec":"nsg","present":["A stretch-induced dysfunction of the caudal cord from an abnormally anchored, low-lying conus - the conus normally ends at ~L1-2; tethering restricts its normal ascent","Causes: tight/fatty filum terminale, lipomyelomeningocele and other occult dysraphism, postsurgical scarring (retethering after myelomeningocele repair), split cord malformation, dermal sinus tract","Presentation: children with a cutaneous stigma (dimple, hairy patch, lipoma, hemangioma), progressive leg weakness/sensory change, gait or foot deformity, scoliosis, back/leg pain, and bladder/bowel dysfunction; adults may present later with pain and progressive urologic decline (often after a stretch event)"],"dx":["MRI of the lumbosacral spine: low-lying conus (below L2), thickened/fatty filum, associated lipoma/split cord/dermal sinus; the whole neuraxis when dysraphism is present","Urodynamic studies to document and follow neurogenic bladder - often the earliest objective sign and a key indication/outcome measure","Correlate imaging with progressive neurologic/urologic findings - an incidentally low conus without symptoms is managed differently than a symptomatic, progressing one"],"tx":["Surgical UNTETHERING for symptomatic/progressive tethered cord to halt decline (established deficits may not fully reverse) - the goal is to stop progression","Prophylactic untethering of asymptomatic occult lesions (e.g., some lipomas) is debated - individualized by lesion type and risk of progression","Manage the neurogenic bladder (urology) and orthopedic deformities in parallel"],"tech":["Laminectomy/laminotomy at the relevant level; microsurgical release of the tethering element - divide a tight/fatty filum terminale, debulk a lipoma off the placode, and dissect scar (with neuromonitoring - EMG/stimulation to identify functional roots vs filum)","Split cord malformation: remove the bony/fibrous median septum (diastematomyelia); excise a dermal sinus tract in its entirety to prevent infection","Aim for a mobile cord and watertight dural closure to reduce CSF leak and retethering"],"after":["Flat positioning after surgery per protocol; watch for CSF leak/pseudomeningocele; monitor neurologic and urologic status for improvement/stabilization","RETETHERING can occur (especially after lipoma/complex repairs and as children grow) - counsel on symptom recurrence and maintain surveillance","Continued multidisciplinary spina bifida/urology follow-up; urodynamics to track bladder function"],"pearls":["A midline cutaneous stigma over the lower spine is a tethered-cord clue - image before progressive weakness and bladder loss declare it","Untethering stops progression; it does not reliably reverse established deficits - operate before the deficit, not after","Urodynamics are the sensitive tracker here - bladder change is often the earliest and most reversible sign","Retethering as the child grows is a real risk - recurrent symptoms after a good repair are not a failure of diagnosis but a call for re-imaging"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Tethered Spinal Cord Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tethered Cord Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560534/"},{"t":"Spina Bifida Association Clinical Guidelines","u":"https://www.spinabifidaassociation.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-craniosynostosis","name":"Craniosynostosis (Pediatric)","sec":"nsg","present":["Premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, restricting growth perpendicular to the suture and producing a characteristic head shape (Virchow's law)","Single-suture patterns: sagittal (scaphocephaly - long narrow head, most common), metopic (trigonocephaly - triangular forehead, hypotelorism), unilateral coronal (anterior plagiocephaly - forehead flattening, harlequin orbit), lambdoid (rare, posterior plagiocephaly)","Syndromic multi-suture synostosis (Apert, Crouzon, Pfeiffer - FGFR/TWIST mutations) with midface hypoplasia and raised-ICP risk; distinguish true synostosis from POSITIONAL plagiocephaly (deformational, open sutures - treated nonsurgically)"],"dx":["Clinical exam of head shape, suture ridging, and fontanelles; the key distinction from positional plagiocephaly is patent sutures and the ear/forehead relationship (parallelogram in positional vs trapezoid in lambdoid synostosis)","CT with 3D reconstruction confirms the fused suture(s) and assesses for signs of raised ICP; genetics for suspected syndromic cases","Ophthalmologic exam (papilledema) and developmental assessment, particularly in multi-suture/syndromic disease"],"tx":["Positional plagiocephaly: NONsurgical - repositioning, tummy time, and helmet orthosis; it is not craniosynostosis","True craniosynostosis: surgery to release the fused suture and reshape/expand the skull, protecting brain growth and correcting deformity; timing depends on technique","Syndromic/multi-suture disease: staged reconstruction with a craniofacial team; monitor and treat raised ICP (multi-suture cases carry real ICP risk) and address midface/airway issues"],"tech":["Endoscopic strip craniectomy (minimally invasive, ideally EARLY at ~2-4 months) followed by postoperative helmet molding - less blood loss, shorter stay, for suitable single-suture cases","Open cranial vault remodeling / fronto-orbital advancement (typically later, ~6-12 months) for definitive reshaping, coronal/metopic frontal-orbital deformities, and syndromic reconstruction","Posterior vault distraction osteogenesis to expand volume in multi-suture/syndromic cases; careful attention to blood loss (major consideration in infants)"],"after":["Helmet therapy adherence after endoscopic repair is essential to the result; monitor head growth and shape","Watch for blood loss/transfusion needs, and in syndromic cases for RAISED ICP, hydrocephalus, airway compromise (midface hypoplasia/OSA), and Chiari - long-term craniofacial follow-up","Developmental surveillance; multidisciplinary care (neurosurgery, plastics/craniofacial, genetics, ophthalmology)"],"pearls":["Separate positional plagiocephaly (open sutures, repositioning/helmet) from true synostosis (fused suture, surgery) - the ear/forehead geometry tells them apart","Endoscopic strip craniectomy works only if done EARLY and paired with helmeting - timing is the whole strategy","Multi-suture and syndromic synostosis threaten ICP and the airway - these need a craniofacial team, not a single suture release","Blood loss is the infant's real intraoperative danger in open vault remodeling - plan transfusion and monitor closely"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Craniosynostosis Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Craniosynostosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544366/"},{"t":"AAP Craniofacial Care Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-trigeminal-neuralgia","name":"Trigeminal Neuralgia & Microvascular Decompression","sec":"nsg","present":["Paroxysms of severe, brief, ELECTRIC-SHOCK-like facial pain in trigeminal territory (usually V2/V3), triggered by light touch, chewing, talking, brushing teeth, or cold air - with pain-free intervals between attacks","Classic (type 1) trigeminal neuralgia is most often caused by neurovascular compression of the trigeminal root (commonly the superior cerebellar artery); the exam is typically normal between attacks","Red flags for SECONDARY causes (sensory loss, bilateral pain, other cranial neuropathies, young patient) point to multiple sclerosis (demyelination at the root entry zone) or a tumor (e.g., CPA) - image accordingly"],"dx":["Diagnosis is clinical (International Classification of Headache Disorders criteria); high-resolution MRI (including thin cuts through the root entry zone / FIESTA/CISS) evaluates for a compressing vessel and excludes MS plaques and tumors","Normal interictal neurologic exam supports classic TN; sensory deficit or additional cranial nerve signs prompt a secondary workup","Distinguish from dental pathology (a common cause of misdiagnosis and unnecessary dental procedures), cluster headache, and painful trigeminal neuropathy"],"tx":["First-line is MEDICAL: carbamazepine (or oxcarbazepine) is the drug of choice and often dramatically effective; baclofen, lamotrigine, and gabapentin are alternatives/adjuncts","For medically refractory or intolerant patients, procedural options: MICROVASCULAR DECOMPRESSION (MVD) is the most durable and the only treatment addressing the cause (in classic TN with a compressing vessel), best for good surgical candidates","Ablative/less-invasive options for poor surgical candidates or MS-related TN: percutaneous rhizotomy (radiofrequency, glycerol, or balloon compression) and stereotactic radiosurgery (Gamma Knife) - effective but with a higher recurrence and a risk of facial numbness"],"tech":["MVD: retrosigmoid craniotomy, microsurgical exposure of the trigeminal root at the brainstem, identify the offending vessel, and interpose a cushion (e.g., Teflon felt) between vessel and nerve - decompressing without lesioning the nerve","Percutaneous procedures via the foramen ovale to the Gasserian ganglion: radiofrequency thermocoagulation, glycerol injection, or balloon compression - selectively injure pain fibers","Radiosurgery targets the trigeminal root with a focused dose (delayed effect)"],"after":["MVD outcomes: high initial pain-free rates with the best long-term durability among procedures; monitor for CSF leak, hearing loss, facial numbness/weakness, and rare cerebellar/brainstem complications","Ablative procedures trade some sensory loss for pain relief and have higher recurrence - counsel on numbness (and rare anesthesia dolorosa) and the possibility of repeat treatment","Taper medications as pain resolves; follow for recurrence and re-treat as needed"],"pearls":["Brief electric-shock facial pain triggered by light touch with a normal exam is classic trigeminal neuralgia - and carbamazepine is both treatment and a diagnostic clue (it usually works)","Young, bilateral, or with sensory loss? Think MS or tumor and image the root entry zone - do not assume classic TN","MVD treats the cause and lasts the longest - it is the procedure of choice for the fit patient with a compressing vessel","Ablative procedures relieve pain by injuring the nerve - more numbness, more recurrence; reserve for poor surgical candidates or MS-related pain"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/EFNS Trigeminal Neuralgia Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Trigeminal Neuralgia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554486/"},{"t":"AANS: Trigeminal Neuralgia","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-dbs","name":"Deep Brain Stimulation for Movement Disorders","sec":"nsg","present":["Implanted electrodes delivering adjustable high-frequency stimulation to deep targets to modulate dysfunctional motor circuits - a reversible, non-lesional therapy","Established indications: Parkinson disease (motor fluctuations, dyskinesia, tremor), essential tremor, and dystonia; also used for select refractory OCD and epilepsy","Candidacy centers on a good but medication-limited response (levodopa-responsive PD with fluctuations/dyskinesia or disabling tremor) rather than end-stage or atypical disease"],"dx":["Multidisciplinary evaluation (movement-disorder neurology + neurosurgery + neuropsychology): confirm diagnosis, document levodopa responsiveness (on/off testing in PD), and screen cognition/mood (dementia and untreated psychiatric illness are relative contraindications)","MRI for targeting and to exclude structural contraindications; the response to levodopa predicts DBS motor benefit in PD (tremor and dyskinesia respond well; axial/non-levodopa-responsive symptoms less so)","Target selection by symptom: STN or GPi for PD, VIM thalamus for tremor, GPi for dystonia"],"tx":["DBS is an adjunct, not a cure - it reduces motor fluctuations and can allow medication reduction (STN) or directly control dyskinesia (GPi) in PD, and markedly reduces tremor (VIM) and dystonia (GPi)","Programming (adjusting contacts, amplitude, frequency, pulse width) over weeks-months tailors benefit and minimizes side effects - the therapy is delivered as much in clinic as in the OR","Continue/optimize medications alongside stimulation"],"tech":["Stereotactic implantation of the lead(s) to the target using frame-based or frameless navigation with image guidance; intraoperative microelectrode recording and/or awake test stimulation (or asleep MRI/CT-verified placement) to confirm target and screen for side effects","Leads connected via extensions to an implantable pulse generator (like a pacemaker) placed subcutaneously in the chest/abdomen","Meticulous targeting - millimeters determine benefit vs stimulation-induced side effects (dysarthria, paresthesia, mood/eye deviation)"],"after":["Initial programming after a settling period, then iterative adjustments; counsel on realistic expectations (motor benefit, not disease modification) and MRI-conditional device precautions","Monitor for surgical complications (hemorrhage, infection, lead migration/fracture) and stimulation side effects; manage battery replacement over years (or rechargeable systems)","Ongoing multidisciplinary follow-up; watch mood/cognition (especially with STN stimulation and medication reduction)"],"pearls":["Levodopa responsiveness predicts DBS benefit in Parkinson disease - symptoms that respond to levodopa respond to DBS (tremor and dyskinesia especially); levodopa-resistant axial symptoms usually won't","DBS is reversible and adjustable - its advantage over lesioning is that the therapy is programmed, not permanent","Screen cognition and psychiatric status first - dementia and untreated depression are why good candidates become poor ones","STN often lets you cut medication; GPi often directly tames dyskinesia - target choice follows the goal"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN Deep Brain Stimulation Guidance","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Deep Brain Stimulation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557847/"},{"t":"AANS: Deep Brain Stimulation","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-epilepsy-surgery","name":"Epilepsy Surgery Essentials","sec":"nsg","present":["For DRUG-RESISTANT epilepsy - defined as failure to achieve sustained seizure freedom after adequate trials of two appropriate, tolerated antiseizure medications - surgery can be the most effective path to seizure freedom and should be considered early, not as a last resort","The commonest resectable substrate is mesial TEMPORAL LOBE epilepsy with hippocampal sclerosis; also focal cortical dysplasia, tumors (DNET, ganglioglioma), cavernomas, and other lesional epilepsy","Recognize surgically remediable syndromes and refer to an epilepsy center - years are often lost before referral"],"dx":["Presurgical workup localizes the epileptogenic zone: video-EEG monitoring (capture seizures), high-resolution epilepsy-protocol MRI, and neuropsychological testing; PET/SPECT, MEG, and functional MRI as needed","When noninvasive data are discordant or the zone is near eloquent cortex, INVASIVE monitoring (subdural grids or stereo-EEG depth electrodes) maps seizure onset and functional cortex","The Wada test or functional MRI lateralizes language/memory before temporal resection to predict deficits"],"tx":["Resective surgery aims for seizure freedom by removing the epileptogenic zone: anterior temporal lobectomy/amygdalohippocampectomy for mesial temporal lobe epilepsy (a randomized trial showed surgery far superior to continued medical therapy for seizure freedom), or lesionectomy for a discrete lesion","When resection is not safe/feasible: disconnection (corpus callosotomy for drop attacks, hemispherotomy for hemispheric syndromes) or NEUROMODULATION - vagus nerve stimulation, responsive neurostimulation (RNS), or DBS (anterior thalamic nucleus)","Laser interstitial thermal therapy (LITT) offers a minimally invasive ablation for select targets (e.g., mesial temporal, hypothalamic hamartoma)"],"tech":["Anterior temporal lobectomy with amygdalohippocampectomy; tailored lesionectomy with electrocorticography; awake mapping when near eloquent cortex","Stereo-EEG: minimally invasive depth-electrode implantation to record from deep/multiple regions and define the network; hemispherotomy disconnects rather than removes a hemisphere","LITT: stereotactic laser fiber with real-time MRI thermometry to ablate the target through a small burr hole"],"after":["Track seizure outcome (Engel/ILAE classes); many mesial temporal patients achieve seizure freedom - continue medications initially and taper cautiously over time under neurology guidance","Monitor for expected/neuropsychological effects (e.g., verbal memory or naming changes after dominant temporal resection, visual field cut - superior quadrantanopia), and surgical complications","Comprehensive epilepsy-center follow-up; neuromodulation devices require ongoing programming"],"pearls":["Two failed appropriate medications defines drug resistance - that is the referral trigger for surgical evaluation, and waiting longer just costs seizure-free years","Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy with hippocampal sclerosis is the classic surgically curable epilepsy - a trial proved surgery beats continued pills for seizure freedom","Wada/fMRI before dominant temporal resection protects language and memory - map before you cut","When you cannot resect safely, you can disconnect or neuromodulate - callosotomy, hemispherotomy, RNS/VNS/DBS, and LITT expand the options"],"refs":[{"t":"ILAE / AAN Epilepsy Surgery Guidance","u":"https://www.ilae.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epilepsy Surgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534794/"},{"t":"AANS: Epilepsy Surgery","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-baclofen-pump","name":"Spasticity & Intrathecal Baclofen Pump","sec":"nsg","present":["Severe, disabling spasticity (velocity-dependent hypertonia from upper-motor-neuron injury) - cerebral palsy, spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, stroke, and traumatic brain injury","Consider intrathecal baclofen (ITB) when spasticity is generalized (especially lower-extremity/truncal), impairs function, care, or comfort, and is inadequately controlled or intolerably side-effected by oral agents (baclofen, tizanidine, dantrolene, benzodiazepines)","Goals vary: improve function/gait, ease caregiving and positioning, reduce pain, and prevent contractures - clarify the goal before implanting"],"dx":["Multidisciplinary spasticity assessment (physiatry, neurosurgery, PT/OT): grade tone (modified Ashworth), map the pattern (focal vs generalized), and distinguish spasticity from fixed contracture (which a pump won't fix)","A screening intrathecal baclofen TRIAL (test-dose via lumbar puncture with objective tone assessment) predicts response before committing to an implanted pump","Match therapy to distribution: focal spasticity may be better served by botulinum toxin or selective procedures; generalized lower-body spasticity favors ITB"],"tx":["Intrathecal baclofen delivers drug directly to the spinal cord at a tiny fraction of the oral dose, achieving control with far fewer systemic side effects (sedation) - titratable and reversible","Alternatives/adjuncts by pattern: oral agents, botulinum toxin for focal spasticity, and selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR) - especially in ambulatory spastic-diplegia cerebral palsy - as a permanent option","Continue rehabilitation - the pump enables therapy; it does not replace it"],"tech":["Implant a programmable pump in a subcutaneous abdominal pocket connected to an intrathecal catheter tunneled to the lumbar subarachnoid space; program the dose/rate and refill percutaneously at intervals","Selective dorsal rhizotomy: laminotomy/laminectomy with intraoperative EMG-guided selective sectioning of abnormal dorsal (sensory) rootlets to reduce the reflex arc driving spasticity","Catheter and pump handling with strict sterile technique (infection/meningitis risk)"],"after":["CRITICAL safety point: abrupt ITB WITHDRAWAL (pump malfunction, empty reservoir, catheter failure, missed refill) is a life-threatening emergency - high fever, rigidity, altered mental status, rhabdomyolysis (mimics but is worse than neuroleptic malignant syndrome/sepsis) - treat urgently and restore baclofen; OVERDOSE causes drowsiness, respiratory depression, and coma","Scheduled refills and device surveillance are mandatory; troubleshoot dosing changes and catheter problems promptly","Ongoing PT/OT to translate reduced tone into function and to prevent contractures; monitor for infection and CSF leak"],"pearls":["Do the intrathecal test dose before implanting - the trial predicts who benefits and prevents an implanted disappointment","A pump treats spasticity, not fixed contracture - examine for the difference or you will implant the wrong solution","Abrupt intrathecal baclofen withdrawal is a killer - fever, rigidity, and altered mentation in a pump patient is withdrawal until proven otherwise; restore the drug fast","Selective dorsal rhizotomy is the permanent alternative for the right ambulatory spastic-diplegia child - not everyone needs a lifelong pump"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Spasticity/ITB Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Intrathecal Baclofen","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526038/"},{"t":"AAP/AACPDM Cerebral Palsy Spasticity Care","u":"https://www.aacpdm.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-peripheral-nerve","name":"Peripheral Nerve Entrapment & Traumatic Nerve Injury","sec":"nsg","present":["Entrapment neuropathies: carpal tunnel (median at the wrist - nocturnal hand paresthesia, thenar wasting, Phalen/Tinel), cubital tunnel (ulnar at the elbow - ring/little finger numbness, intrinsic weakness, Froment sign), and others (radial, peroneal at the fibular head, meralgia paresthetica)","Traumatic nerve injury classified by severity - Seddon (neurapraxia, axonotmesis, neurotmesis) / Sunderland (I-V): neurapraxia recovers, neurotmesis (transection) needs repair; brachial plexus injuries and sharp lacerations are important subsets","Presentation: focal weakness/atrophy, sensory loss in the nerve's distribution, and pain; a Tinel sign that advances distally over time signals regeneration"],"dx":["Electrodiagnostics (EMG/nerve conduction studies) localize and grade the lesion and distinguish demyelinating conduction block (better prognosis) from axonal loss; timing matters (findings evolve over ~2-4 weeks)","Imaging: high-resolution ultrasound and MR neurography visualize the nerve, entrapment, neuroma, or discontinuity; useful in trauma and atypical cases","For trauma, determine whether the nerve is in continuity - this drives observe-vs-explore decisions"],"tx":["Entrapment - conservative first for mild/moderate: splinting (neutral wrist for carpal tunnel; elbow extension/padding for cubital tunnel), activity modification, and corticosteroid injection (carpal tunnel); surgical decompression (release) for severe, progressive, or refractory cases (denervation/atrophy is a strong operate signal)","Closed traumatic injuries in continuity (neurapraxia/axonotmesis): observe with serial exams/EMG for spontaneous recovery (axons regenerate ~1 mm/day) - explore if no recovery by the expected interval (commonly ~3 months)","Sharp transections: early primary repair; more extensive gaps/injuries need grafting or nerve transfers - the timing and technique define outcome"],"tech":["Decompression: carpal tunnel release (open or endoscopic - divide the transverse carpal ligament); cubital tunnel - in situ decompression vs anterior transposition of the ulnar nerve","Nerve repair/reconstruction: tension-free primary microsurgical coaptation for clean transections; interposition nerve grafting (e.g., sural graft) for gaps; NERVE TRANSFERS (reroute an expendable donor fascicle to a critical target - e.g., in brachial plexus injury) restore function when proximal repair is impractical","Neurolysis for scarred nerves in continuity; intraoperative nerve stimulation/monitoring to assess conduction"],"after":["Hand therapy/rehabilitation to maintain range of motion and retrain, protect insensate skin, and prevent contracture while awaiting regeneration; splinting as needed","Track recovery with advancing Tinel sign and serial EMG; counsel that regeneration is SLOW (~1 mm/day) and outcome depends on injury severity, level, and time to repair","Manage neuropathic pain (gabapentinoids, duloxetine) and painful neuromas (may need revision/surgery)"],"pearls":["Thenar wasting or advancing denervation in carpal tunnel is a decompress-now sign - do not keep splinting an atrophying hand","A Tinel sign that marches distally over weeks is regeneration in progress - a reason to keep observing a closed injury in continuity","Sharp cut = early repair; blunt/closed in continuity = observe and re-image/EMG, exploring if no recovery by ~3 months","Nerve transfers changed reconstruction - a nearby expendable donor can restore a critical function when proximal repair would fail (brachial plexus especially)"],"refs":[{"t":"AANS Peripheral Nerve Resources","u":"https://www.aans.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Nerve Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436035/"},{"t":"AAOS: Nerve Injuries","u":"https://www.aaos.org"}]},{"id":"nsg-brain-abscess","name":"Brain Abscess & Surgical CNS Infection","sec":"nsg","present":["A focal intraparenchymal infection evolving from cerebritis to an encapsulated abscess; presents subacutely with headache (most common), focal deficit, seizure, and often only low-grade or absent fever - the classic triad (headache, fever, focal deficit) is present in a minority","Sources: contiguous spread (otitis/mastoiditis, sinusitis, dental), hematogenous seeding (endocarditis, lung, right-to-left shunt), and direct inoculation (trauma, neurosurgery); immunocompromise broadens the organisms (Toxoplasma, Nocardia, fungi)","Empyema (subdural/epidural) is a related surgical emergency, often from sinusitis, that can deteriorate rapidly"],"dx":["Contrast MRI is the study of choice: a ring-enhancing lesion with central restricted diffusion on DWI (helps distinguish abscess from necrotic tumor, which usually does not restrict); assess number, size, and location","Identify the source and organism: blood cultures, echocardiography, dental/ENT and chest evaluation; but STEREOTACTIC ASPIRATION for Gram stain/culture is often needed for microbiologic diagnosis and to guide therapy","AVOID lumbar puncture when there is a mass lesion with raised ICP (herniation risk) - it rarely helps and can harm"],"tx":["Combine surgery and prolonged antibiotics: aspiration (stereotactic) or excision for abscesses that are large (commonly >2-2.5 cm), causing significant mass effect, near the ventricle (risk of rupture -> ventriculitis, high mortality), or failing medical therapy","Empiric IV antibiotics to cover streptococci, anaerobes, and gram-negatives (e.g., a third-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole; add vancomycin for staph/post-procedure), then tailor to cultures; typically 6-8 weeks IV","Small abscesses/cerebritis or surgically inaccessible lesions may be treated medically with close imaging follow-up; treat the primary source (mastoidectomy, sinus drainage, dental)"],"tech":["Stereotactic/navigated needle aspiration through a burr hole - diagnostic (culture) and therapeutic (decompress), often repeatable, and preferred for deep/eloquent locations","Craniotomy for excision when abscesses are superficial, multiloculated, fungal, contain foreign material, or recur - and for subdural empyema, which usually needs prompt surgical drainage (craniotomy/craniectomy)","Address the contiguous source in the same setting when feasible"],"after":["Serial imaging to confirm shrinkage; continue and adjust culture-directed IV antibiotics (ID co-management); manage seizures (common - AEDs) and raised ICP","Watch for abscess re-accumulation, ventricular rupture (a catastrophic complication near periventricular abscesses), and hydrocephalus; steroids used judiciously for significant edema/mass effect","Treat the underlying source and predisposing condition (endocarditis, immunodeficiency, right-to-left shunt) to prevent recurrence"],"pearls":["Ring enhancement with central DWI restriction points to abscess over necrotic tumor - the diffusion sequence earns its keep here","Do not LP the patient with a mass and raised ICP - it rarely helps and can herniate them; get tissue by aspiration instead","A periventricular abscess that ruptures into the ventricle (ventriculitis) is a mortality event - decompress the at-risk lesion before it blows","Fever is often absent - subacute headache with a new focal deficit and a source (sinus, ear, teeth, heart) is an abscess until imaging says otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"IDSA / Brain Abscess Management Resources","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Abscess","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441841/"},{"t":"AANS: Brain Abscess","u":"https://www.aans.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-normal-labor","name":"Normal Labor & Delivery (Stages & Management)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Labor is regular contractions producing progressive cervical change; the first stage (onset to full dilation) has a latent phase (slow, to ~6 cm) and an active phase (accelerated, from ~6 cm - the contemporary Zhang labor curve inflection, replacing the older 4 cm anchor)","Second stage (full dilation to delivery) and third stage (delivery of placenta); assess presentation, position, station, and fetal status on admission","Distinguish true labor from Braxton-Hicks/false labor by progressive cervical change, not contraction perception alone"],"dx":["Serial cervical exams for dilation, effacement, and station (relative to ischial spines); confirm cephalic presentation and position (occiput anterior favorable) by exam +/- ultrasound","Contemporary norms: active phase arrest requires >=6 cm dilation, ruptured membranes, and either >=4 h of adequate contractions without change or >=6 h with inadequate contractions (oxytocin); second-stage duration allowances are longer with epidural and parity","Continuous vs intermittent fetal monitoring per risk; assess adequacy of contractions (palpation or IUPC/Montevideo units)"],"tx":["Support labor progress: ambulation/position changes, hydration, analgesia (including neuraxial), and amniotomy/oxytocin for protraction/arrest per contemporary criteria - avoid diagnosing failed labor before the active phase truly stalls","Active management of the THIRD STAGE reduces postpartum hemorrhage: prophylactic OXYTOCIN with delivery of the anterior shoulder, gentle cord traction, and uterine massage","DELAYED CORD CLAMPING (30-60 s) benefits the neonate (iron stores) in vigorous term and preterm infants"],"tech":["Spontaneous vaginal delivery: control the fetal head with a modified Ritgen maneuver and perineal support, deliver the anterior then posterior shoulder, and manage the cord; RESTRICTIVE (not routine) episiotomy","Deliver and inspect the placenta for completeness and vessels; repair perineal lacerations by degree (first through fourth) in anatomic layers","Encourage immediate skin-to-skin and early breastfeeding"],"after":["Immediate postpartum monitoring of uterine tone and bleeding (the highest-risk window for hemorrhage), vital signs, and perineal repair; quantify blood loss","Support breastfeeding/skin-to-skin, bladder function, and analgesia; assess laceration and perineal care","Routine postpartum observation and the fourth-trimester care plan (mood, contraception, follow-up)"],"pearls":["The active phase now starts at ~6 cm, not 4 - do not call arrest of labor before the labor curve says the patient is actually in active-phase arrest","Active management of the third stage (prophylactic oxytocin) is the simplest, highest-yield hemorrhage prevention","The first hour postpartum is when atony bleeds - watch the fundus, not just the vitals","Restrictive episiotomy beats routine - cutting for every delivery causes more severe lacerations, not fewer"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / SMFM Labor Management","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Normal Labor and Delivery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544290/"},{"t":"WHO Intrapartum Care Recommendations","u":"https://www.who.int"}]},{"id":"obgyn-fhr-monitoring","name":"Intrapartum Fetal Heart Rate Monitoring","sec":"obgyn","present":["Continuous electronic fetal monitoring assesses fetal oxygenation during labor; interpret the tracing systematically: baseline rate, VARIABILITY, accelerations, decelerations, and contractions","Decelerations by pattern: EARLY (mirror contractions - head compression, benign), VARIABLE (abrupt, variable timing - cord compression), and LATE (delayed nadir after the contraction peak - uteroplacental insufficiency, the worrisome pattern)","Moderate variability and accelerations are the strongest reassurance of a well-oxygenated fetus"],"dx":["NICHD three-tier system: Category I (normal - baseline 110-160, moderate variability, no late/variable decelerations - continue), Category III (abnormal - absent variability with recurrent lates/variables or bradycardia, or a sinusoidal pattern - urgent), Category II (indeterminate - everything in between, requires evaluation and management)","Assess baseline (bradycardia <110, tachycardia >160), variability (absent/minimal/moderate/marked), and decelerations (recurrent if with >=50% of contractions); watch for TACHYSYSTOLE (>5 contractions/10 min)","Category II is the vast majority - manage it with a stepwise algorithm rather than reflexive delivery"],"tx":["Intrauterine RESUSCITATION for concerning tracings: maternal repositioning (left lateral), IV fluid bolus, oxygen (judicious), reduce/stop oxytocin, treat hypotension (especially post-epidural - ephedrine/phenylephrine), and consider a tocolytic for tachysystole; amnioinfusion for recurrent variables from cord compression","Category III or persistent non-reassuring Category II unresponsive to resuscitation -> expedite delivery (operative vaginal or cesarean)","Correlate with the clinical picture (abruption, cord prolapse, uterine rupture) rather than treating the tracing in isolation"],"tech":["Escalate monitoring when the external tracing is inadequate: fetal scalp electrode (FSE) for a direct fetal ECG signal and an intrauterine pressure catheter (IUPC) to quantify contraction strength (Montevideo units)","Perform intrauterine resuscitation maneuvers; amnioinfusion via IUPC for recurrent variable decelerations","Prepare for expedited delivery when indicated"],"after":["Document the tracing interpretation, interventions, and response; ensure neonatal team readiness for a non-reassuring pattern at delivery","Cord gases at delivery for significantly abnormal tracings to document acid-base status","Debrief and review for quality when an emergent delivery results"],"pearls":["Moderate variability is the single most reassuring feature - a fetus with moderate variability is very unlikely to be acidemic","Late decelerations are the ominous pattern (uteroplacental insufficiency); early decelerations are benign head compression","Category II is not a delivery diagnosis - it is a 'work the algorithm' diagnosis: reposition, fluids, reduce oxytocin, reassess","A sinusoidal pattern suggests fetal anemia (e.g., abruption, alloimmunization, vasa previa) - a red flag, not a resuscitation problem"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Intrapartum Fetal Heart Rate Monitoring","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fetal Heart Monitoring","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557393/"},{"t":"NICHD Consensus Nomenclature","u":"https://www.nichd.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"obgyn-induction","name":"Induction & Augmentation of Labor","sec":"obgyn","present":["Induction initiates labor before spontaneous onset; augmentation accelerates a labor that has slowed - both used for maternal/fetal indications (postterm, preeclampsia, GDM, PPROM, growth restriction, oligohydramnios) or by shared decision at term (ARRIVE trial supported elective induction at 39 weeks in low-risk nulliparas)","Success and method depend heavily on the CERVIX: an unfavorable (unripe) cervix needs ripening first","Contraindications to induction/vaginal delivery (prior classical cesarean, placenta previa, transverse lie, active genital herpes) must be excluded"],"dx":["Assess cervical favorability with the BISHOP SCORE (dilation, effacement, station, consistency, position); a low score predicts a longer induction and higher cesarean risk and indicates cervical ripening","Confirm indication, gestational age, presentation, and fetal status; document the plan and consent","Monitor for tachysystole and category changes throughout"],"tx":["Cervical ripening for the unfavorable cervix: mechanical (transcervical FOLEY balloon) and/or pharmacologic PROSTAGLANDINS (misoprostol - PGE1, or dinoprostone - PGE2); combination methods (Foley + oxytocin or misoprostol) shorten time to delivery","OXYTOCIN infusion (titrated) for induction of a favorable cervix and for augmentation; AMNIOTOMY augments and allows internal monitoring","AVOID prostaglandins (misoprostol) for induction in patients with a PRIOR CESAREAN (uterine rupture risk) - use mechanical methods/oxytocin and counsel"],"tech":["Transcervical Foley/balloon catheter placement above the internal os, inflated for mechanical dilation; misoprostol dosing (low-dose vaginal/oral) or dinoprostone insert per protocol","Oxytocin titration to adequate contractions (goal ~200-250 Montevideo units) while avoiding tachysystole; amniotomy when the presenting part is applied","Continuous fetal monitoring during ripening/oxytocin"],"after":["Reassess cervical progress and fetal status; manage tachysystole (reduce/stop oxytocin, tocolytic) and category changes","Proceed to delivery; document induction indication, agents, and course","Postpartum care as for the delivery route achieved"],"pearls":["The Bishop score decides the method - an unfavorable cervix needs ripening before oxytocin, or you buy a failed induction and a cesarean","ARRIVE made elective 39-week induction in low-risk nulliparas a reasonable, cesarean-neutral option - counsel accordingly","No misoprostol with a prior cesarean - the uterine-rupture risk makes mechanical ripening the safer path","Tachysystole with a category change is an oxytocin/prostaglandin problem - turn it down and resuscitate before anything else"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Induction of Labor Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Induction of Labor","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537136/"},{"t":"ARRIVE Trial (NICHD)","u":"https://www.nichd.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"obgyn-operative-vaginal","name":"Operative Vaginal Delivery (Forceps & Vacuum)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Assisted vaginal delivery with vacuum or forceps to expedite delivery in the SECOND STAGE for: a non-reassuring fetal tracing, prolonged second stage, or maternal indication (exhaustion, need to avoid Valsalva - e.g., cardiac disease)","Requires strict PREREQUISITES and an experienced operator; an alternative to cesarean when conditions are favorable","Classified by station and rotation (outlet, low, mid) - higher stations carry more risk"],"dx":["Confirm the ABCDEFGH prerequisites before attempting: fully dilated cervix, ruptured membranes, ENGAGED head with known POSITION and station (at least +2, no higher than low/outlet for most), adequate pelvis, empty bladder, analgesia, consent, and a backup plan for failure","Accurately determine fetal head position (exam +/- ultrasound) - malposition drives failure and injury","Do not attempt with an unengaged head, incomplete dilation, or uncertain position"],"tx":["Choose vacuum or forceps by operator skill and clinical situation; vacuum has fewer maternal lacerations but more cephalohematoma/subgaleal risk; forceps has more maternal trauma but a firmer grip and rotational capability","Apply during contractions with maternal effort; abandon for a failed attempt (no descent over pulls, or vacuum pop-offs) and move to cesarean - do NOT persist or switch instruments indiscriminately","Anticipate and prepare for SHOULDER DYSTOCIA (increased risk) and postpartum hemorrhage/lacerations"],"tech":["Vacuum: apply the cup over the flexion point (~3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle), check for no maternal tissue, and apply traction along the pelvic curve during contractions; limit pulls, pop-offs, and total time","Forceps: correct application (biparietal, bimalar - the blades cephalic and symmetric), then traction along the axis; classify by station/rotation","Have a clear failure threshold and immediate cesarean capability"],"after":["Careful inspection for maternal LACERATIONS (including higher-order and sulcal tears) and repair; assess for postpartum hemorrhage","Neonatal assessment for scalp injury (cephalohematoma, subgaleal hemorrhage - monitor for expansion/anemia), facial/brachial plexus injury, and hyperbilirubinemia","Document indication, prerequisites, application, and outcome"],"pearls":["Know the position before you pull - the commonest cause of failed/complicated operative delivery is a misjudged fetal head position","Have a failure threshold and honor it - persistent traction or switching instruments after failure causes the worst injuries","Vacuum trades maternal lacerations for neonatal scalp injury; forceps trades scalp injury for maternal trauma - choose by situation and skill","Anticipate shoulder dystocia after every operative vaginal delivery - the risk is elevated"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Operative Vaginal Birth","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Operative Vaginal Delivery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526069/"},{"t":"Royal College (RCOG) Assisted Vaginal Birth","u":"https://www.rcog.org.uk"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cesarean","name":"Cesarean Delivery","sec":"obgyn","present":["Surgical delivery through abdominal and uterine incisions; the most common major surgery in many settings - indications include labor dystocia/arrest, non-reassuring fetal status, malpresentation, placenta previa/accreta, prior classical incision, and maternal indications","Scheduled (e.g., previa, prior classical, breech) vs unscheduled/emergent (arrest, non-reassuring tracing, cord prolapse)","Counsel on risks (hemorrhage, infection, VTE, organ injury, future accreta/placentation risk) and implications for future deliveries"],"dx":["Confirm the indication and rule out reversible causes of the tracing/arrest; assess placental location and fetal presentation; ensure appropriate anesthesia (usually neuraxial)","Categorize urgency to set the decision-to-delivery interval (emergent for cord prolapse, prolonged bradycardia, uterine rupture, abruption)","Type and screen/crossmatch and anticipate hemorrhage risk (previa/accreta)"],"tx":["Preoperative preparation: PROPHYLACTIC ANTIBIOTICS BEFORE INCISION (reduces endometritis/wound infection), VTE prophylaxis (mechanical +/- pharmacologic), and neuraxial anesthesia where possible","LOW TRANSVERSE hysterotomy is standard (allows future TOLAC); classical (vertical) incision for specific situations (very preterm poorly formed lower segment, some malpresentations, anterior previa/accreta) - and it precludes future labor","Manage intraoperative complications (atony - uterotonics, extension/injury, hemorrhage)"],"tech":["Abdominal entry (Pfannenstiel or Joel-Cohen); enter the peritoneum, develop the bladder flap, and make a low transverse uterine incision; deliver the fetus and placenta, then close the uterus (commonly double-layer) and abdominal wall in layers","Antibiotics before skin incision; careful hemostasis; identify and protect the bladder and ureters","For accreta/hemorrhage: be prepared for cesarean hysterectomy and massive transfusion"],"after":["Post-op care: monitor uterine tone/bleeding, early mobilization and VTE prophylaxis, multimodal analgesia, early feeding, and bladder management; watch for endometritis and wound complications","Support breastfeeding; counsel on future delivery route (TOLAC candidacy depends on incision type) and interpregnancy interval","Wound care and follow-up; screen for postpartum mood disorders"],"pearls":["Antibiotics go in BEFORE skin incision, not after cord clamp - the timing change reduced post-cesarean infection","The uterine incision type dictates the future: low transverse allows TOLAC; classical/vertical mandates repeat cesarean and raises rupture risk","Every prior cesarean raises the future accreta risk, especially with an overlying previa - counsel and plan placental imaging","Early mobilization, multimodal analgesia, and VTE prophylaxis are the ERAS pillars that speed recovery and prevent the deadly clot"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Cesarean Delivery Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cesarean Section","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546707/"},{"t":"ERAS Society Cesarean Guidelines","u":"https://erassociety.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-vbac","name":"Trial of Labor After Cesarean (TOLAC/VBAC)","sec":"obgyn","present":["A planned attempt at vaginal birth after a prior cesarean (TOLAC); success (VBAC) rates are high (~60-80%) in appropriate candidates and avoid repeat-surgery morbidity","The central risk is UTERINE RUPTURE (~0.5-0.9% with one prior LOW TRANSVERSE incision) - catastrophic for mother and fetus, so TOLAC requires facilities able to perform emergency cesarean","Shared decision-making weighing success likelihood against rupture risk"],"dx":["Candidacy: prior LOW TRANSVERSE cesarean(s), no contraindicated uterine scar or prior rupture, and an adequate setting; use validated VBAC success calculators (favorable: prior vaginal birth, spontaneous labor, favorable cervix; less favorable: recurrent indication, obesity, advanced maternal age)","CONTRAINDICATIONS: prior CLASSICAL or T-incision, prior uterine rupture, extensive transfundal surgery, or inability to perform emergency cesarean","Continuous fetal monitoring during TOLAC - a change in the tracing (often the first sign of rupture) or new pain/bleeding/loss of station prompts urgent evaluation"],"tx":["Support spontaneous labor; oxytocin augmentation is permissible with caution; AVOID prostaglandins (misoprostol) for cervical ripening - they raise rupture risk (mechanical ripening is preferred if needed)","Maintain readiness for emergency cesarean and hemorrhage throughout","Repeat cesarean for those who decline or are ineligible, or if TOLAC fails/complications arise"],"tech":["Conduct as a monitored vaginal delivery with immediate cesarean capability; if uterine rupture is suspected (fetal bradycardia, acute pain, loss of station, hemorrhage) -> emergent laparotomy","Cautious oxytocin titration; no misoprostol","At delivery, be prepared for uterine repair/hysterectomy if rupture occurs"],"after":["Post-VBAC: routine postpartum care; post-rupture: surgical repair vs hysterectomy, transfusion, and neonatal resuscitation","Counsel on future delivery planning based on the outcome and any intraoperative findings","Document candidacy, counseling, course, and outcome"],"pearls":["Low transverse scar = a TOLAC candidate; classical/T scar or prior rupture = a mandatory repeat cesarean","The first sign of uterine rupture is often a fetal heart rate abnormality (bradycardia), not pain - continuous monitoring is non-negotiable","No misoprostol for TOLAC ripening - the rupture risk is unacceptable","TOLAC belongs where an emergency cesarean can happen immediately - the setting is part of the safety"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG VBAC Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vaginal Birth After Cesarean","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507844/"},{"t":"MFMU VBAC Calculator","u":"https://www.mfmunetwork.bsc.gwu.edu"}]},{"id":"obgyn-pph","name":"Postpartum Hemorrhage","sec":"obgyn","present":["Cumulative blood loss >=1000 mL, or blood loss with signs/symptoms of hypovolemia, within 24 h of birth (primary PPH); a leading cause of maternal mortality and often preventable","The causes are the FOUR T's: TONE (uterine atony - the most common, ~70-80%), TRAUMA (lacerations, hematoma, uterine inversion/rupture), TISSUE (retained placenta/products, accreta), and THROMBIN (coagulopathy - DIC, inherited)","Recognize early with QUANTITATIVE blood loss (not visual estimation) and vital-sign trends; risk-stratify on admission"],"dx":["Identify the cause rapidly: palpate for a boggy atonic uterus (tone), inspect for lacerations/hematoma (trauma), examine the placenta and uterus for retained tissue (tissue), and check coagulation/fibrinogen (thrombin - fibrinogen falls early in obstetric hemorrhage)","Quantify blood loss and monitor vitals; activate a MASSIVE TRANSFUSION protocol for ongoing severe hemorrhage","Ultrasound for retained products; consider uterine inversion (a smooth mass at the introitus with a boggy fundus) and rupture"],"tx":["Simultaneous resuscitation and cause-directed therapy: large-bore IV access, fluids/BLOOD products (balanced transfusion), and treat the cause","ATONY: uterine massage + UTEROTONICS - oxytocin first-line, then methylergonovine (AVOID in hypertension), carboprost/PGF2alpha (AVOID in asthma), and misoprostol; give TRANEXAMIC ACID early (WOMAN trial - reduces death from bleeding when given within 3 h)","Trauma - repair lacerations/evacuate hematoma/reduce inversion; tissue - remove retained products (manual/curettage); thrombin - correct coagulopathy (fibrinogen/cryoprecipitate, targeted factors)"],"tech":["Escalating mechanical/surgical control for refractory atony: bimanual uterine compression, intrauterine balloon TAMPONADE (Bakri) or uterine packing, uterine compression sutures (B-LYNCH), uterine/utero-ovarian and internal iliac artery ligation, and pelvic artery EMBOLIZATION (if stable)","HYSTERECTOMY as the definitive life-saving procedure when conservative measures fail (and up front for accreta)","For inversion: manual replacement (sometimes with uterine relaxation/tocolysis) then uterotonics"],"after":["Post-hemorrhage ICU/step-down monitoring, ongoing transfusion and coagulation correction, and surveillance for Sheehan syndrome and recurrence","Debrief and document; treat anemia (iron, transfusion) and support recovery and breastfeeding","Counsel on future pregnancy risk and any surgical outcomes (e.g., hysterectomy)"],"pearls":["Atony causes most PPH - massage and stack the uterotonics early, and give tranexamic acid within 3 hours","Quantify blood loss - visual estimation dramatically under-counts, and delay is what kills","Match the uterotonic to comorbidity: no methylergonovine in hypertension, no carboprost in asthma","Have the escalation ladder ready (balloon -> compression sutures -> artery ligation/embolization -> hysterectomy) - do not get stuck repeating a failed step"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Postpartum Hemorrhage Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postpartum Hemorrhage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499988/"},{"t":"WOMAN Trial / WHO PPH Recommendations","u":"https://www.who.int"}]},{"id":"obgyn-shoulder-dystocia","name":"Shoulder Dystocia","sec":"obgyn","present":["An obstetric EMERGENCY where, after delivery of the head, the anterior shoulder impacts behind the pubic symphysis and fails to deliver with routine traction - the TURTLE SIGN (head retracts against the perineum)","Unpredictable and often unpreventable; risk factors (macrosomia, diabetes, prior dystocia, operative vaginal delivery) have poor predictive value","The danger is neonatal hypoxia (cord compression) and BRACHIAL PLEXUS injury / clavicular fracture, plus maternal trauma"],"dx":["Recognize immediately when gentle downward traction fails to deliver the shoulder after the head delivers (turtle sign) - announce the emergency and call for help, note the time","Do NOT apply fundal pressure (worsens impaction) and avoid excessive lateral traction (brachial plexus injury)","Assess for and document maneuvers and neonatal/maternal outcomes"],"tx":["Work through a maneuver sequence promptly and calmly (HELPERR): call for Help, evaluate for Episiotomy (for room to maneuver), McRoberts (legs hyperflexed onto abdomen) with suprapubic Pressure - these two resolve most cases","Then internal rotational maneuvers and delivery of the posterior arm; last-resort maneuvers if needed","Avoid fundal pressure throughout; maintain axial (not excessive lateral) traction"],"tech":["McRoberts + suprapubic pressure first; then Rubin (push the anterior shoulder toward the fetal chest) and Woods corkscrew (rotate the posterior shoulder) rotational maneuvers; DELIVERY OF THE POSTERIOR ARM is highly effective","Last resorts: Gaskin (all-fours) position, deliberate clavicular fracture, and the Zavanelli maneuver (cephalic replacement + cesarean) or symphysiotomy in extremis","Document the sequence, timing, and which shoulder was anterior"],"after":["Neonatal assessment for BRACHIAL PLEXUS palsy (Erb - waiter's tip), clavicular/humeral fracture, and hypoxic injury; involve neonatology","Maternal assessment for postpartum hemorrhage and severe (third/fourth-degree) lacerations","Careful, detailed DOCUMENTATION (maneuvers, timing, personnel, anterior shoulder, neonatal exam) - medicolegally and clinically important; debrief"],"pearls":["McRoberts plus suprapubic pressure relieves most shoulder dystocias - start there, and never use fundal pressure","Delivery of the posterior arm is one of the most effective maneuvers when the first steps fail - reach for it","Excess lateral traction causes the brachial plexus injury - keep traction axial and gentle","Meticulous documentation of the timed maneuver sequence is part of the standard of care"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Shoulder Dystocia Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Shoulder Dystocia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470427/"},{"t":"RCOG Shoulder Dystocia Guideline","u":"https://www.rcog.org.uk"}]},{"id":"obgyn-preeclampsia","name":"Preeclampsia, Eclampsia & HELLP Syndrome","sec":"obgyn","present":["A hypertensive disorder of pregnancy after 20 weeks: new-onset HYPERTENSION (>=140/90) with PROTEINURIA or with severe-feature end-organ involvement even without proteinuria","SEVERE FEATURES: BP >=160/110, thrombocytopenia (<100k), elevated transaminases/RUQ pain, renal insufficiency (Cr >1.1 or doubling), pulmonary edema, and new cerebral/visual symptoms (headache, scotomata)","ECLAMPSIA = seizures; HELLP = Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets - a severe variant that can present with epigastric/RUQ pain and may occur without marked hypertension"],"dx":["BP measurement (two elevated readings) plus labs: CBC (platelets, hemolysis - smear/LDH/haptoglobin), LFTs, creatinine, and urine protein (protein:creatinine ratio or 24-h); assess for symptoms of severe features","Distinguish from chronic hypertension, gestational hypertension (no proteinuria/severe features), and superimposed preeclampsia; HELLP labs even with only mild hypertension","Fetal assessment (growth, fluid, Doppler, monitoring) - uteroplacental insufficiency is common"],"tx":["MAGNESIUM SULFATE for seizure prophylaxis (preeclampsia with severe features) and treatment (eclampsia) - monitor for toxicity (loss of reflexes, respiratory depression; calcium gluconate is the antidote)","ACUTE severe-range hypertension (>=160/110) treated urgently with IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, or oral immediate-release nifedipine to prevent stroke","DELIVERY is the definitive treatment: deliver at >=37 weeks for preeclampsia without severe features, and at >=34 weeks (or immediately if unstable) with severe features/eclampsia/HELLP; give antenatal steroids if preterm and time allows"],"tech":["Deliver by obstetric indication (vaginal delivery is appropriate when feasible; cesarean for the usual indications); continue magnesium through delivery and ~24 h postpartum","Manage the delivery with attention to BP control, magnesium, and hemorrhage risk (thrombocytopenia)","HELLP: supportive care, delivery, and management of coagulopathy/bleeding"],"after":["Postpartum monitoring is critical - preeclampsia can PRESENT OR WORSEN POSTPARTUM (continue BP surveillance and treat; late postpartum preeclampsia/eclampsia occurs); continue magnesium ~24 h","Counsel on the LONG-TERM cardiovascular risk (preeclampsia is a lifelong ASCVD risk marker) and on low-dose aspirin for prevention in future pregnancies","Arrange close postpartum BP follow-up (readmission window) and interval CV risk management"],"pearls":["Severe-range BP (>=160/110) is a stroke risk - treat it within an hour with IV labetalol/hydralazine or oral nifedipine","Magnesium prevents and treats eclamptic seizures - it is not an antihypertensive; give both","Preeclampsia does not always resolve at delivery - it can worsen postpartum, so BP surveillance continues after discharge","HELLP can present with epigastric/RUQ pain and near-normal blood pressure - check the platelets and LFTs, not just the cuff"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Hypertension in Pregnancy Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Preeclampsia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570611/"},{"t":"SMFM Preeclampsia Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-htn-pregnancy","name":"Chronic & Gestational Hypertension in Pregnancy","sec":"obgyn","present":["CHRONIC hypertension: BP >=140/90 predating pregnancy or diagnosed before 20 weeks; GESTATIONAL hypertension: new hypertension after 20 weeks WITHOUT proteinuria or severe features (a diagnosis that may progress to preeclampsia)","Both increase the risk of superimposed preeclampsia, fetal growth restriction, abruption, and preterm birth - so they are surveillance-heavy conditions","Distinguish from preeclampsia (which adds proteinuria/end-organ involvement)"],"dx":["Confirm and classify by timing and the presence/absence of proteinuria and severe features; baseline labs in chronic hypertension (renal function, urine protein) to detect later superimposed preeclampsia","Serial monitoring for progression to preeclampsia (BP, symptoms, labs, urine protein) and fetal growth surveillance (serial ultrasounds, antenatal testing)","Evaluate for secondary hypertension when atypical/severe/early"],"tx":["TREAT to a target of <140/90 in pregnancy (CHAP trial showed treating mild chronic hypertension to <140/90 improved outcomes without harming fetal growth) - pregnancy-safe agents: labetalol, extended-release nifedipine, and methyldopa; AVOID ACE inhibitors/ARBs (fetal toxicity)","LOW-DOSE ASPIRIN starting 12-16 weeks for preeclampsia prevention in those at risk (chronic hypertension qualifies)","Delivery timing individualized: gestational/chronic hypertension typically delivered around 37-39 weeks; earlier if it becomes preeclampsia with severe features"],"tech":["Antihypertensive management and BP monitoring (including home BP); deliver by obstetric indication at the planned gestational age","Fetal surveillance (growth ultrasounds, antenatal testing) throughout","Manage progression to preeclampsia per that pathway"],"after":["Postpartum BP surveillance and medication adjustment (BP can rise postpartum); chronic hypertension continues to need management","Counsel on cardiovascular risk and preconception optimization for future pregnancies (BP control, aspirin plan)","Transition to primary care for ongoing hypertension management"],"pearls":["CHAP changed practice - treat mild chronic hypertension in pregnancy to <140/90; the old permissive threshold is gone","ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated in pregnancy - switch to labetalol, nifedipine, or methyldopa preconception or at diagnosis","Gestational hypertension is a moving target - it can become preeclampsia, so surveillance is the management","Low-dose aspirin from 12-16 weeks is the evidence-based preeclampsia prevention for at-risk patients, including chronic hypertension"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Chronic Hypertension in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"CHAP Trial (NHLBI)","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertension in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430839/"}]},{"id":"obgyn-abruption","name":"Placental Abruption","sec":"obgyn","present":["Premature separation of a normally implanted placenta - classically PAINFUL vaginal bleeding with uterine tenderness and frequent, tetanic contractions (tachysystole/hypertonus); a leading cause of antepartum hemorrhage and fetal compromise","Bleeding may be REVEALED (external) or CONCEALED (retroplacental - external bleeding underestimates the true loss); severity ranges from mild to fetal death with maternal DIC","Risk factors: hypertension/preeclampsia, prior abruption, trauma, cocaine/smoking, PPROM, polyhydramnios/multiples with rapid decompression"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis - ultrasound is INSENSITIVE for abruption (a normal scan does not exclude it); the picture is bleeding + pain/uterine irritability + a non-reassuring fetal tracing","Assess maternal hemodynamics and fetal status; monitor for DIC (fibrinogen falls - the most useful early marker, plus platelets, PT/PTT, D-dimer)","Distinguish from previa (painless bleeding) and other causes; a Kleihauer-Betke may be checked but does not rule it out"],"tx":["Resuscitate the mother (IV access, blood products, correct coagulopathy) and continuously monitor the fetus; the management hinges on maternal/fetal stability and gestational age","Unstable mother or non-reassuring fetal status -> urgent DELIVERY (usually cesarean; vaginal if delivery is imminent and both are stable); stable, remote from term, mild abruption -> expectant management with close monitoring and steroids if preterm","Anticipate and treat postpartum hemorrhage and DIC aggressively (fibrinogen/cryoprecipitate, massive transfusion protocol)"],"tech":["Deliver by the route dictated by stability and fetal status; at cesarean, a COUVELAIRE uterus (blood extravasated into the myometrium) may be seen but is not itself an indication for hysterectomy","Manage coagulopathy in the operating room; be prepared for atony and hemorrhage","Neonatal team present for a compromised fetus"],"after":["Postpartum monitoring for hemorrhage and DIC resolution; transfusion support and anemia management","Counsel on recurrence risk (elevated in future pregnancies) and modifiable factors (blood pressure control, smoking/cocaine cessation)","Rh immunoglobulin if indicated; debrief and support"],"pearls":["Ultrasound cannot rule out abruption - it is a clinical diagnosis of painful bleeding with uterine irritability and a worrisome tracing","Concealed abruption underestimates blood loss - watch the fibrinogen and vitals, not just the pad","Fibrinogen is the early DIC marker in obstetric hemorrhage - it drops before the classic coagulation times","Cocaine and uncontrolled hypertension are classic triggers - ask and address them"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Antepartum Hemorrhage / Abruption","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Abruptio Placentae","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482335/"},{"t":"SMFM Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-previa-accreta","name":"Placenta Previa & Placenta Accreta Spectrum","sec":"obgyn","present":["Placenta PREVIA: the placenta covers or lies near the internal cervical os - classically PAINLESS vaginal bleeding in the second/third trimester; delivery is by cesarean","Placenta ACCRETA SPECTRUM (accreta/increta/percreta): abnormally adherent/invasive placenta that fails to separate, causing catastrophic hemorrhage - the strongest risk factor is a PRIOR CESAREAN with an overlying previa (risk rises with each prior cesarean)","Suspect accreta antenatally to plan - unrecognized accreta at delivery is a hemorrhage disaster"],"dx":["Ultrasound diagnoses previa (transvaginal is safe and accurate) and screens for accreta (loss of the clear zone, lacunae, bladder-wall interruption, increased vascularity); MRI is adjunctive for depth/topography, especially posterior or percreta","Do NOT perform a digital cervical exam with a known/suspected previa (can provoke hemorrhage); avoid intercourse","Plan placental imaging in any patient with prior cesarean(s) and an anterior low-lying placenta/previa"],"tx":["Previa: pelvic rest, manage bleeding episodes (some require hospitalization/transfusion), and schedule CESAREAN delivery (typically late preterm ~36-37 weeks, earlier for recurrent bleeding); steroids if preterm delivery likely","Accreta spectrum: PLANNED, scheduled delivery at a center with a multidisciplinary team (MFM, gyn-onc/surgery, anesthesia, blood bank, urology, ICU); typically PLANNED CESAREAN HYSTERECTOMY with the placenta LEFT IN SITU (do not attempt to remove it)","Massive transfusion readiness; consider ureteral stents and interventional radiology (balloon occlusion) per protocol"],"tech":["Previa cesarean: anticipate anterior placenta and hemorrhage; classical incision may be needed to avoid the placenta","Accreta: cesarean hysterectomy leaving the placenta attached (removal triggers hemorrhage); meticulous surgical dissection, ureteral protection, and massive transfusion; some centers use conservative/expectant management in selected cases","Coordinate the multidisciplinary team and blood products in advance"],"after":["ICU-level postoperative care for accreta (hemorrhage, transfusion, organ injury - bladder/ureter); monitor for DIC and reoperation","Counsel on the surgical outcome (hysterectomy/fertility), and on the markedly elevated recurrence/accreta risk in any future pregnancy after cesarean","Support recovery and the psychological impact"],"pearls":["Painless third-trimester bleeding is previa until proven otherwise - and no digital exam until previa is excluded","Prior cesarean plus an anterior previa is the accreta setup - image the placenta deliberately and plan","Accreta is a planned, team, scheduled operation - the deaths come from unrecognized accreta discovered at delivery","Leave the placenta in situ in accreta and proceed to hysterectomy - trying to remove it unleashes the hemorrhage"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / SMFM Placenta Accreta Spectrum","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Placenta Previa","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539818/"},{"t":"SMFM Accreta Care Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cord-prolapse","name":"Umbilical Cord Prolapse","sec":"obgyn","present":["An obstetric EMERGENCY where the umbilical cord descends alongside (occult) or past (overt) the presenting part after membrane rupture, causing cord compression and acute fetal compromise","Presents as sudden, severe fetal BRADYCARDIA or prolonged variable decelerations immediately after rupture of membranes, or a palpable/visible pulsating cord on exam","Risk factors: malpresentation, unengaged presenting part, polyhydramnios, prematurity, multiple gestation, and iatrogenic (amniotomy with a high presenting part)"],"dx":["Recognize immediately: acute fetal heart rate deceleration/bradycardia after ROM, or palpation of the cord on cervical exam - this is a clinical, hands-on diagnosis requiring instant action","Do not delay for imaging; call for help and prepare for immediate delivery","Assess dilation and delivery feasibility"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE manual ELEVATION of the presenting part off the cord (keep the examining hand in the vagina lifting the fetal head) to relieve compression - maintain until delivery","Reposition the mother (knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg), stop oxytocin, consider bladder filling and a tocolytic to buy time, and minimize cord handling (avoid vasospasm)","EMERGENT delivery - usually cesarean (unless vaginal delivery is truly imminent) - is the definitive treatment"],"tech":["Continuous manual elevation of the presenting part while moving to the operating room; knee-chest/Trendelenburg positioning; bladder instillation (~500 mL) as an adjunct to elevate the head","Emergent cesarean delivery; operative vaginal or spontaneous delivery only if fully dilated and imminent","Neonatal resuscitation team ready"],"after":["Neonatal assessment (hypoxic injury) and standard postpartum/post-cesarean care","Cord gases to document acid-base status; debrief and document the timeline","Support and counsel the family"],"pearls":["Keep your hand in - manual elevation of the presenting part off the cord is the intervention that buys the fetus time to delivery","Cord prolapse is a decision-to-delivery race - call it, elevate, position, and move to the OR","Beware amniotomy with a high, unengaged presenting part - it is a classic iatrogenic cause","Minimize cord handling - manipulation causes vasospasm and worsens the compression"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / RCOG Cord Prolapse Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Umbilical Cord Prolapse","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542241/"},{"t":"RCOG Umbilical Cord Prolapse Guideline","u":"https://www.rcog.org.uk"}]},{"id":"obgyn-preterm-labor","name":"Preterm Labor & Tocolysis","sec":"obgyn","present":["Regular contractions with cervical change before 37 weeks; preterm birth is the leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, so the goal is to gain time for fetal maturation interventions","Assess for the cause/associated conditions (infection, PPROM, abruption, cervical insufficiency, multiple gestation) and contraindications to prolonging pregnancy","Symptoms (contractions, pressure, discharge) are nonspecific - objective assessment prevents over-treatment"],"dx":["Confirm with cervical change on serial exams; TRANSVAGINAL cervical LENGTH (short cervix raises risk) and fetal FIBRONECTIN (a negative test has a strong NEGATIVE predictive value - reassuring) help triage who is truly in preterm labor","Evaluate for infection (including GBS status/urine), PPROM (sterile speculum), and fetal well-being/presentation","Rule out contraindications to tocolysis (chorioamnionitis, severe abruption, non-reassuring fetal status, lethal anomaly)"],"tx":["The interventions that actually help the baby: ANTENATAL CORTICOSTEROIDS (betamethasone/dexamethasone) for fetal lung maturity (24-34 weeks, and up to ~36-37 in selected late-preterm), MAGNESIUM SULFATE for NEUROPROTECTION (<32 weeks - reduces cerebral palsy), and GBS prophylaxis in labor","TOCOLYSIS buys ~48 h to complete steroids and transfer to a higher-level center - it does NOT meaningfully prolong pregnancy: first-line agents are nifedipine (calcium channel blocker) or indomethacin (NSAID, preferred <32 weeks - watch ductal/oligohydramnios effects); terbutaline for short-term use","Do not tocolyze when prolonging pregnancy is contraindicated (infection, severe abruption, non-reassuring status)"],"tech":["Administer the steroid course, magnesium neuroprotection, and tocolytic per protocol; arrange maternal transfer to an appropriate-level facility if needed","GBS prophylaxis (penicillin) in labor per status","Prepare for preterm delivery and neonatal resuscitation"],"after":["If delivery is averted, continue surveillance; if preterm birth proceeds, ensure neonatology involvement and appropriate-level nursery care","Address the underlying cause and counsel on recurrence risk and future prevention (progesterone, cervical surveillance)","Support and follow-up"],"pearls":["Tocolysis buys 48 hours for steroids and transport - it does not prolong pregnancy, so use it for that purpose, not as a cure","The steroid course and magnesium neuroprotection (<32 weeks) are the interventions that change the baby's outcome","A negative fetal fibronectin with a normal cervical length is strongly reassuring - it helps avoid unnecessary admission and tocolysis","Do not tocolyze through chorioamnionitis or a severe abruption - prolonging that pregnancy harms mother and fetus"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Preterm Labor & Tocolysis Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Preterm Labor","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544170/"},{"t":"SMFM Preterm Birth Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-pprom","name":"Preterm Premature Rupture of Membranes (PPROM)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Rupture of the fetal membranes BEFORE the onset of labor AND before 37 weeks (PPROM) - distinct from term PROM; presents with a gush or continuous leakage of fluid","The key tensions are latency (time to delivery) vs the risks of prolonging (infection - chorioamnionitis, cord prolapse, abruption) - management balances prematurity against infection","Gestational age drives management (previable, 24 to <34 weeks, and 34-37 weeks)"],"dx":["Confirm with a STERILE SPECULUM exam: POOLING of fluid in the vagina, NITRAZINE (alkaline pH turns blue), and FERNING on microscopy; commercial tests (PAMG-1/AFP) if uncertain - AVOID DIGITAL exam (introduces infection and shortens latency)","Ultrasound (oligohydramnios supports it), assess fetal presentation and well-being, and evaluate for infection and labor","Establish gestational age precisely - it dictates the plan"],"tx":["Expectant management (to gain fetal maturity) vs delivery depends on gestational age and complications: deliver for chorioamnionitis, non-reassuring fetal status, abruption, or labor at any age","Preterm (roughly 24 to <34 weeks) without infection: EXPECTANT management with LATENCY ANTIBIOTICS (ampicillin/amoxicillin + erythromycin/azithromycin - prolong latency and reduce infection), antenatal CORTICOSTEROIDS, magnesium neuroprotection <32 weeks, and GBS prophylaxis; at ~34 weeks, shared decision on delivery vs continued expectant management","Avoid tocolysis for prolonged use; monitor closely for infection"],"tech":["Inpatient surveillance (temperature, WBC/CRP trends, fetal monitoring) during expectant management; deliver by obstetric indication when triggered","GBS prophylaxis and neonatal preparation","Sterile technique - minimize exams"],"after":["Neonatal care for prematurity; maternal monitoring for postpartum endometritis","Counsel on recurrence risk and future preterm-birth prevention","Document the latency course and interventions"],"pearls":["No digital exam in PPROM - it shortens latency and imports infection; confirm with sterile speculum (pooling, nitrazine, ferning)","Latency antibiotics prolong the pregnancy and reduce infection in preterm PPROM - a core evidence-based intervention","Deliver for chorioamnionitis, fetal compromise, or abruption regardless of gestational age - infection changes everything","Give steroids and magnesium (<32 weeks) - the maturity interventions still apply after membranes rupture"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Prelabor Rupture of Membranes","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Premature Rupture of Membranes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532888/"},{"t":"SMFM Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-chorioamnionitis","name":"Chorioamnionitis & Maternal Sepsis","sec":"obgyn","present":["Intra-amniotic infection/inflammation (chorioamnionitis, now often termed 'intraamniotic infection' or Triple I): maternal FEVER plus supporting signs (maternal/fetal tachycardia, uterine tenderness, purulent/foul amniotic fluid, maternal leukocytosis)","Risk rises with prolonged rupture of membranes, prolonged labor, multiple exams, and PPROM; it endangers both mother (endometritis, sepsis, hemorrhage from atony) and neonate (sepsis)","MATERNAL SEPSIS is the feared escalation - obstetric patients can decompensate with subtle early signs; sepsis remains a leading cause of maternal death"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: fever (>=39.0 C once, or 38.0-38.9 C with additional criteria) plus signs; supportive labs (CBC, cultures); amniocentesis (glucose, Gram stain, culture) rarely needed","Recognize maternal sepsis early - the usual scores underperform in pregnancy (physiologic tachycardia, lower BP); watch lactate, end-organ perfusion, and a low threshold for escalation (obstetric-modified warning scores)","Identify the source (intraamniotic vs pyelonephritis, pneumonia, etc.)"],"tx":["Broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS promptly (ampicillin + gentamicin, with anaerobic coverage - clindamycin/metronidazole - if cesarean) and antipyretics; treat intrapartum, do not wait until after delivery","DELIVERY is part of the treatment (the infected uterus is the source) - chorioamnionitis is not by itself an indication for immediate cesarean, but expedite delivery and proceed by obstetric indication","Maternal sepsis: the sepsis bundle adapted for pregnancy - cultures, early antibiotics, IV fluids, lactate, and source control; escalate to ICU as needed"],"tech":["Deliver by the appropriate route; anticipate uterine ATONY and postpartum hemorrhage (an infected uterus contracts poorly) and treat aggressively","Source control (delivery; drainage of an abscess/retained products if present)","Post-cesarean, add anaerobic coverage and continue antibiotics per response"],"after":["Continue antibiotics postpartum per clinical course (often until afebrile and improving); monitor for postpartum endometritis and hemorrhage","Neonatal evaluation for sepsis (notify pediatrics); support recovery","Debrief; counsel on future risk and modifiable factors"],"pearls":["Fever plus fetal/maternal tachycardia or uterine tenderness in labor is intraamniotic infection - start antibiotics intrapartum and expedite delivery","An infected uterus is an atonic uterus - anticipate and pre-empt postpartum hemorrhage","Standard sepsis scores under-call obstetric sepsis because of pregnancy physiology - keep a low threshold and trust lactate/perfusion","Delivery is source control - chorioamnionitis alone does not mandate cesarean, but it does mandate getting the baby out"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Intraamniotic Infection Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chorioamnionitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448076/"},{"t":"SMFM Maternal Sepsis Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-afe","name":"Amniotic Fluid Embolism","sec":"obgyn","present":["A rare, catastrophic obstetric emergency of sudden CARDIORESPIRATORY collapse and coagulopathy during labor, delivery, or immediately postpartum - thought to be an anaphylactoid response to amniotic fluid/fetal antigens entering the maternal circulation","Classic sequence: abrupt HYPOXIA and HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often with seizures), followed by DIC with severe hemorrhage; high maternal and fetal mortality","Occurs suddenly and unpredictably - risk factors (advanced age, multiparity, operative delivery, placental abnormalities) are weak"],"dx":["A clinical diagnosis of EXCLUSION made in real time - there is no confirmatory test; recognize the triad of acute hypoxia, hemodynamic collapse, and coagulopathy without another explanation","Exclude mimics rapidly (pulmonary embolism, eclampsia, anaphylaxis, hemorrhage/high spinal, sepsis, MI) while resuscitating","Send DIC labs (fibrinogen, platelets, coagulation) - the coagulopathy is often profound"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE high-quality resuscitation: airway/oxygenation/intubation, hemodynamic support (vasopressors, inotropes, avoid excess fluid), and if arrest occurs, high-quality CPR with LEFT UTERINE DISPLACEMENT and PERIMORTEM CESAREAN within ~4-5 minutes to aid maternal resuscitation and save the fetus","Aggressive management of DIC/hemorrhage: massive transfusion protocol, fibrinogen/cryoprecipitate, and obstetric hemorrhage measures","Supportive ICU care; some centers use an emerging 'A-OK' protocol (atropine, ondansetron, ketorolac) - adjunctive and not definitive"],"tech":["Resuscitation-focused: intubation, central access, invasive monitoring, and consideration of advanced support (ECMO) in refractory cases at capable centers","Perimortem cesarean delivery during maternal arrest; surgical/interventional control of postpartum hemorrhage","Multidisciplinary crisis response (obstetrics, anesthesia, critical care, hematology)"],"after":["ICU care for multi-organ support, ongoing coagulopathy/hemorrhage management, and neurologic assessment after hypoxia","Neonatal intensive care; debriefing and psychological support for the family and team","Counsel survivors on the low but real recurrence uncertainty in future pregnancies"],"pearls":["Sudden hypoxia, cardiovascular collapse, and DIC around delivery with no other explanation is amniotic fluid embolism - it is a real-time clinical diagnosis, not a lab one","The coagulopathy is a defining feature - be ready for massive transfusion and profound DIC","If arrest occurs, perimortem cesarean within ~4-5 minutes aids maternal resuscitation (relieves aortocaval compression) as much as it saves the fetus","There is no specific cure - outcomes hinge on immediate, high-quality resuscitation and hemorrhage control"],"refs":[{"t":"SMFM Amniotic Fluid Embolism Guidance","u":"https://www.smfm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amniotic Fluid Embolism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470224/"},{"t":"ACOG Obstetric Emergencies Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-vte-pregnancy","name":"Venous Thromboembolism in Pregnancy","sec":"obgyn","present":["Pregnancy and the postpartum period are HYPERCOAGULABLE (Virchow's triad - stasis, hypercoagulability, endothelial injury), making VTE a leading cause of maternal death; risk is HIGHEST POSTPARTUM","Deep vein thrombosis (often LEFT leg, iliofemoral) and pulmonary embolism (dyspnea, chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxia); symptoms overlap with normal pregnancy, so maintain suspicion","Assess for thrombophilia and prior VTE, which drive prophylaxis decisions"],"dx":["Suspected DVT: compression ultrasound of the leg (repeat/iliac imaging if negative but suspicious); suspected PE: chest imaging - CT pulmonary angiography or V/Q scan (both acceptable in pregnancy; choose by chest x-ray and institutional practice), with attention to minimizing but not withholding necessary imaging","D-dimer is less useful (physiologically elevated in pregnancy); do not withhold imaging for a 'normal' D-dimer","Evaluate for thrombophilia selectively (timing matters - some tests are unreliable in acute clot/pregnancy)"],"tx":["LOW-MOLECULAR-WEIGHT HEPARIN (LMWH) is the anticoagulant of choice in pregnancy - it does not cross the placenta; AVOID WARFARIN (teratogenic/fetal effects) and DOACs (insufficient safety data, cross placenta/breast milk)","Treat acute VTE with therapeutic LMWH for the remainder of pregnancy and at least 6 weeks postpartum (minimum ~3 months total); plan PERIPARTUM management to avoid neuraxial/delivery bleeding (transition/hold around delivery, or switch to unfractionated heparin near term)","Thromboprophylaxis (LMWH) for high-risk patients antepartum and/or postpartum based on risk factors (prior VTE, high-risk thrombophilia, cesarean plus risk factors)"],"tech":["Manage anticoagulation around delivery: hold LMWH appropriately before neuraxial anesthesia/delivery (timing rules) and resume postpartum; consider an IVC filter only in specific situations (acute VTE near delivery with contraindication to anticoagulation)","Coordinate with anesthesia for neuraxial safety windows","Postpartum, warfarin and LMWH are compatible with breastfeeding (DOACs are not preferred)"],"after":["Continue anticoagulation for the full course (at least 6 weeks postpartum and >=3 months total); the postpartum period is the highest-risk window, so prophylaxis decisions extend past delivery","Counsel on future-pregnancy prophylaxis and thrombophilia implications","Monitor for bleeding and recurrence"],"pearls":["LMWH is the pregnancy anticoagulant - warfarin is teratogenic and DOACs lack safety data, so both are avoided","The postpartum weeks are the peak VTE risk - do not stop thinking about prophylaxis at delivery","Do not withhold CT-PA or V/Q for a suspected PE over radiation fears - a missed PE is the bigger danger, and D-dimer is unreliable in pregnancy","Plan the peripartum anticoagulation window with anesthesia to keep neuraxial analgesia safe"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Thromboembolism in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: VTE in Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538279/"},{"t":"ASH Venous Thromboembolism Guidelines","u":"https://www.hematology.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ectopic","name":"Ectopic Pregnancy","sec":"obgyn","present":["Implantation outside the endometrial cavity - most commonly in the fallopian TUBE (ampulla); presents with first-trimester abdominal/pelvic pain and vaginal bleeding with a positive pregnancy test, and can RUPTURE into life-threatening hemorrhage","A leading cause of first-trimester maternal death; risk factors include prior ectopic, tubal surgery/damage, PID, IUD in place, and assisted reproduction","Any pregnant patient with pain/bleeding and hemodynamic instability is a ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise"],"dx":["Quantitative hCG + transvaginal ULTRASOUND: an intrauterine pregnancy on ultrasound essentially excludes ectopic (except heterotopic, rare); above the DISCRIMINATORY ZONE (hCG ~3500) without an intrauterine pregnancy suggests ectopic","Serial hCG trends in a pregnancy of unknown location (a normal intrauterine pregnancy rises appropriately; an abnormal plateau/slow rise or fall suggests ectopic or failing pregnancy) - do not act on a single value","Rh typing (give Rh immunoglobulin if Rh-negative); assess hemodynamic stability"],"tx":["UNSTABLE / ruptured -> emergent SURGERY and resuscitation; do not delay for confirmation","Stable, appropriate candidate -> METHOTREXATE (medical management) with strict criteria (low/plateauing hCG, small unruptured mass, no fetal cardiac activity, reliable follow-up, no contraindications) and protocolized hCG follow-up to zero","Stable but not a methotrexate candidate or medical management fails -> laparoscopic surgery; expectant management only for select declining hCG cases"],"tech":["Surgical: LAPAROSCOPY preferred - SALPINGECTOMY (remove the tube - for rupture, significant damage, or completed childbearing) vs SALPINGOSTOMY (tube-sparing - selected cases, with risk of persistent trophoblast requiring hCG follow-up)","Methotrexate protocols (single- or multi-dose) with day-4/7 hCG checks; counsel on precautions (avoid folic acid, NSAIDs, alcohol; sun; delay conception)","Give Rh immunoglobulin if indicated"],"after":["Follow hCG to ZERO after methotrexate or salpingostomy to confirm resolution (persistent trophoblast can rupture); monitor for treatment failure","Counsel on the recurrence risk and future fertility (and heterotopic risk with ART); provide contraception counseling and preconception planning","Emotional support for pregnancy loss"],"pearls":["An intrauterine pregnancy on ultrasound essentially rules out ectopic - the exception, heterotopic pregnancy, matters mainly after assisted reproduction","Do not act on a single hCG - the trend and the discriminatory zone with ultrasound guide the diagnosis","Instability equals surgery now - a ruptured ectopic is a resuscitate-and-operate emergency, not a workup","Methotrexate demands strict criteria and hCG follow-up to zero - persistent trophoblast can still rupture"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Tubal Ectopic Pregnancy Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ectopic Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539860/"},{"t":"SMFM / ASRM Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cesarean-scar-ectopic","name":"Cesarean Scar Niche & Cesarean Scar Ectopic","sec":"obgyn","present":["Complications of the cesarean scar: a NICHE (isthmocele) is a myometrial defect at the prior hysterotomy that can cause postmenstrual spotting, pelvic pain, and secondary infertility; a CESAREAN SCAR ECTOPIC is implantation of a pregnancy within the scar defect - a dangerous, accreta-spectrum-related condition","Cesarean scar ectopic presents in early pregnancy (often asymptomatic on early scan, or with bleeding/pain) and can progress to uterine rupture, catastrophic hemorrhage, or morbidly adherent placenta","Incidence is rising with rising cesarean rates"],"dx":["Transvaginal ULTRASOUND is key: for scar ectopic - a gestational sac implanted in the anterior lower uterine segment at the scar, empty uterine cavity and cervical canal, thin/absent myometrium between the sac and bladder, and prominent vascularity; MRI adjunctive","Distinguish scar ectopic from a cervical ectopic and a low intrauterine pregnancy/miscarriage in progress - misdiagnosis leads to dangerous D&C","Niche/isthmocele is imaged by ultrasound/saline sonohysterography in the non-pregnant patient with abnormal bleeding/infertility"],"tx":["Cesarean scar ectopic requires EARLY, definitive treatment - do NOT perform a blind D&C (high hemorrhage/rupture risk); options include methotrexate (systemic and/or local injection), surgical excision (often with niche repair), and adjunctive uterine artery embolization","Individualize by hemodynamic stability, gestational age, and fertility desires at a center experienced with the condition; continued pregnancy carries high accreta/rupture risk and is counseled carefully","Niche/isthmocele causing symptoms: hysteroscopic or laparoscopic niche resection/repair for bleeding or infertility"],"tech":["Scar ectopic: local/systemic methotrexate, hysteroscopic or laparoscopic/transvaginal excision of the implantation with scar repair, and uterine artery embolization to reduce bleeding; avoid instrumentation that provokes hemorrhage","Niche repair: hysteroscopic resection of the defect edges, or laparoscopic/vaginal excision and re-approximation of the myometrium","Prepare for hemorrhage and possible hysterectomy in scar ectopic"],"after":["Follow hCG to resolution after scar ectopic treatment; monitor for hemorrhage and retained tissue","Counsel on the high recurrence risk of scar ectopic and accreta in future pregnancies; discuss interpregnancy interval and delivery planning","For niche repair, reassess bleeding/fertility outcomes"],"pearls":["A gestational sac in the anterior lower segment at the cesarean scar with thin overlying myometrium is a scar ectopic - never treat it with a blind D&C","Cesarean scar ectopic is on the accreta spectrum - it threatens rupture and massive hemorrhage and needs early, definitive, center-based care","A niche (isthmocele) explains postmenstrual spotting and secondary infertility after cesarean - image it with sonohysterography","Rising cesarean rates are driving both conditions - keep them in the differential of early-pregnancy bleeding and post-cesarean abnormal bleeding"],"refs":[{"t":"SMFM Cesarean Scar Pregnancy Guidance","u":"https://www.smfm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cesarean Scar Ectopic Pregnancy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560743/"},{"t":"ACOG Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-early-pregnancy-loss","name":"Early Pregnancy Loss & Recurrent Pregnancy Loss","sec":"obgyn","present":["Early pregnancy loss (miscarriage): a nonviable intrauterine pregnancy before 13 weeks; presentations include threatened (bleeding, closed os, viable), inevitable (bleeding, open os), incomplete (retained products), missed (no cardiac activity, closed os), and complete loss","Most sporadic losses are due to embryonic ANEUPLOIDY; presents with vaginal bleeding and/or cramping, or is found on ultrasound","RECURRENT pregnancy loss (>=2-3 losses) is a distinct entity warranting evaluation"],"dx":["Transvaginal ULTRASOUND criteria for a definitively nonviable pregnancy: CRL >=7 mm with NO cardiac activity, or mean sac diameter >=25 mm with NO embryo (and other guideline criteria) - avoid acting prematurely on indeterminate scans (repeat to confirm)","Serial hCG in a pregnancy of unknown location to distinguish viable IUP, ectopic, and failing pregnancy; assess hemodynamic stability and Rh status","Recurrent loss workup: antiphospholipid syndrome (the treatable one - lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-glycoprotein), uterine cavity/anatomy (septum), parental karyotype/balanced translocation, and thyroid/prolactin; many remain unexplained"],"tx":["Options for confirmed early loss (share the decision): EXPECTANT management, MEDICAL management (MIFEPRISTONE followed by MISOPROSTOL - more effective than misoprostol alone), or SURGICAL uterine aspiration (D&C/manual vacuum aspiration) - urgent surgery for hemorrhage or infection","Give Rh IMMUNOGLOBULIN to Rh-negative patients; provide analgesia and clear return precautions","Recurrent loss: treat the identified cause - antiphospholipid syndrome with low-dose aspirin + heparin, septum resection, thyroid optimization; supportive care for unexplained loss (many subsequently succeed)"],"tech":["Uterine aspiration (manual vacuum aspiration or suction curettage) for surgical management or hemorrhage/infection; ensure complete evacuation and send products for pathology (and cytogenetics in recurrent loss)","Medical protocol: mifepristone then misoprostol with follow-up to confirm completion","Hysteroscopic septum resection for a uterine septum in recurrent loss"],"after":["Confirm completion (exam/ultrasound/hCG) and monitor for hemorrhage/infection (retained products, endometritis); Rh immunoglobulin as needed","EMOTIONAL support and counseling - pregnancy loss is a grief event; discuss timing of future conception and contraception","Recurrent loss: individualized plan and early support in the next pregnancy"],"pearls":["Confirm nonviability by strict ultrasound criteria (CRL >=7 mm without cardiac activity, or MSD >=25 mm without an embryo) - do not act on an indeterminate early scan","Mifepristone plus misoprostol beats misoprostol alone for medical management of early pregnancy loss","Antiphospholipid syndrome is the treatable cause of recurrent loss - aspirin plus heparin - so it is a must-test","Give Rh immunoglobulin and provide grief support - the medical and the human care both matter"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Early Pregnancy Loss Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Early Pregnancy Loss","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532992/"},{"t":"ASRM Recurrent Pregnancy Loss Guidance","u":"https://www.asrm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-gtd","name":"Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","sec":"obgyn","present":["Abnormal proliferation of placental/trophoblastic tissue - the benign spectrum is the HYDATIDIFORM MOLE (complete mole: 46,XX/XY entirely paternal, no fetus, diffuse vesicles; partial mole: triploid, some fetal parts)","Presents with first-trimester bleeding, a uterus LARGE FOR DATES, markedly ELEVATED hCG, hyperemesis, and (classically) early-onset preeclampsia or hyperthyroidism (hCG cross-reactivity) before 20 weeks","Distinguish molar pregnancy (benign but with malignant potential) from gestational trophoblastic NEOPLASIA (the malignant sequelae - covered separately)"],"dx":["Ultrasound: a complete mole shows a 'SNOWSTORM'/cluster-of-grapes pattern with no fetus; markedly elevated quantitative hCG (often very high); partial moles are subtler (fetal parts, abnormal placenta)","Confirm by histopathology after evacuation; check baseline labs (thyroid, preeclampsia labs) and chest imaging if concerned for metastasis","Rh typing"],"tx":["Uterine EVACUATION by SUCTION dilation and curettage is the treatment; oxytocin after evacuation and Rh immunoglobulin if Rh-negative; hysterectomy is an option in older patients with completed childbearing","The essential follow-up is SERIAL hCG monitoring to zero to detect post-molar gestational trophoblastic NEOPLASIA (persistent/rising hCG)","CONTRACEPTION during surveillance (to interpret hCG) - reliable contraception for the monitoring period; avoid pregnancy until hCG normalizes and surveillance completes"],"tech":["Suction curettage with careful attention to hemorrhage (large, vascular uterus); send tissue for pathology and ploidy; anticipate the need for uterotonics and transfusion","Manage associated hyperthyroidism/preeclampsia perioperatively","Hysterectomy as an alternative definitive option"],"after":["SERIAL quantitative hCG surveillance (weekly until normal, then monthly) to detect neoplasia - the cornerstone of post-molar care; effective contraception throughout so a new pregnancy does not confound the hCG","Refer to gyn-oncology for rising/plateauing hCG (post-molar GTN, which is highly chemo-responsive)","Counsel on the recurrence risk in future pregnancies (early ultrasound next time)"],"pearls":["A snowstorm uterus with a sky-high hCG and a uterus large for dates is a complete mole - evacuate and then watch the hCG","Early preeclampsia or hyperthyroidism before 20 weeks is a molar-pregnancy clue (hCG cross-reactivity)","The hCG trend to zero, with reliable contraception during surveillance, is how you catch post-molar neoplasia early","Rising or plateauing hCG after evacuation is gestational trophoblastic neoplasia - refer to gyn-onc; it is highly curable"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / FIGO Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hydatidiform Mole","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459155/"},{"t":"NCCN Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-hyperemesis","name":"Hyperemesis Gravidarum","sec":"obgyn","present":["Severe nausea and vomiting of pregnancy at the extreme end of the spectrum: persistent vomiting with WEIGHT LOSS (>5%), DEHYDRATION, ketosis, and electrolyte disturbance - distinct from common (milder) nausea and vomiting of pregnancy","Typically peaks in the first trimester; can cause hospitalization, and (rarely) Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency and Mallory-Weiss tears","Consider contributors/mimics: molar or multiple gestation (very high hCG), and non-pregnancy causes (thyroid, GI, urinary, CNS)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; assess dehydration, weight change, and labs (electrolytes, ketones, renal function, and consider thyroid - hCG-mediated transient hyperthyroidism); urinalysis for ketones/infection","Ultrasound to evaluate for MULTIPLE gestation and molar pregnancy (both raise hCG and worsen symptoms)","Exclude other causes when features are atypical (later onset, abdominal pain, fever, neurologic signs)"],"tx":["Stepwise therapy: start with PYRIDOXINE (vitamin B6) +/- DOXYLAMINE (first-line), then add antiemetics (antihistamines - dimenhydrinate/metoclopramide/promethazine, then ondansetron - counsel and time appropriately)","For dehydration/inability to tolerate oral intake: IV FLUID resuscitation with electrolyte correction, and THIAMINE before dextrose-containing fluids (prevent Wernicke); antiemetics IV","Refractory cases: corticosteroids (after the first trimester) and nutritional support (enteral, rarely parenteral)"],"tech":["IV rehydration and electrolyte/thiamine repletion; scheduled antiemetics; nutritional support (NG/enteral feeding, TPN only if refractory) in severe cases","Monitor weight, ketones, and electrolytes during treatment","Address any identified contributor (molar evacuation, etc.)"],"after":["Transition to oral intake and outpatient management as tolerated; ongoing antiemetics and nutritional follow-up; monitor fetal growth","Counsel on the high recurrence risk in future pregnancies and early treatment; provide support (the condition is debilitating and under-recognized)","Watch for complications resolving (electrolytes, weight recovery)"],"pearls":["Hyperemesis is more than morning sickness - it requires weight loss, dehydration, and ketosis/electrolyte derangement, not just vomiting","Give thiamine before dextrose in the dehydrated hyperemetic patient to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","Very severe symptoms should prompt an ultrasound for molar or multiple gestation (both drive hCG up)","Start with pyridoxine and doxylamine and escalate - do not jump straight to the strongest antiemetic"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Nausea & Vomiting of Pregnancy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hyperemesis Gravidarum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532917/"},{"t":"SMFM Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-gdm","name":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus","sec":"obgyn","present":["Glucose intolerance first recognized in pregnancy (typically the second half), driven by placental insulin resistance; usually asymptomatic and found by screening","Untreated GDM raises risks of MACROSOMIA (and shoulder dystocia/birth trauma), neonatal hypoglycemia, preeclampsia, cesarean, and polyhydramnios; it also predicts future maternal type 2 diabetes","Distinguish from pregestational (overt) diabetes (higher anomaly risk) - screen early if risk factors present"],"dx":["Universal screening at 24-28 weeks: the two-step approach (50 g glucose challenge, then a 100 g 3-hour OGTT if abnormal) or the one-step 75 g 2-hour OGTT; early screening for high-risk patients (obesity, prior GDM, strong family history)","Diagnose by established glucose thresholds; assess for other risk factors and comorbidities","Fetal growth surveillance (macrosomia, polyhydramnios) as pregnancy advances"],"tx":["First-line is medical NUTRITION therapy and exercise with SELF-MONITORING of blood glucose (fasting and postprandial targets); most achieve control with lifestyle","When glucose targets are not met, add pharmacotherapy - INSULIN is the preferred agent in pregnancy (does not cross the placenta); metformin/glyburide are alternatives with counseling about placental transfer","Fetal surveillance and delivery planning: antenatal testing for medication-treated/poorly controlled GDM, and delivery timing individualized (well-controlled diet-only GDM can approach term; earlier for poor control/macrosomia); consider cesarean counseling for estimated fetal weight >=4500 g"],"tech":["Glucose management with monitoring and insulin titration; intrapartum glucose control; deliver by obstetric indication with attention to shoulder dystocia risk in macrosomia","Neonatal preparation for hypoglycemia monitoring","Coordinate nutrition/diabetes education"],"after":["POSTPARTUM glucose testing (a 75 g 2-hour OGTT at ~4-12 weeks) to detect persistent diabetes/impaired glucose tolerance - GDM confers a high lifetime type 2 diabetes risk that needs ongoing screening","Counsel on lifestyle, weight, breastfeeding (protective), and recurrence risk; plan preconception optimization for future pregnancies","Transition to primary care for long-term diabetes surveillance"],"pearls":["Insulin is the preferred pharmacotherapy for GDM - it does not cross the placenta, unlike the oral agents","Most GDM is controlled with diet and exercise - reserve medication for those who miss glucose targets","Do not forget the postpartum OGTT at 4-12 weeks - GDM is a powerful predictor of future type 2 diabetes","Macrosomia drives the shoulder dystocia and cesarean counseling - follow fetal growth and individualize delivery"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Gestational Diabetes Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Gestational Diabetes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545196/"},{"t":"ADA Standards of Care - Pregnancy","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cervical-insufficiency","name":"Cervical Insufficiency & Cerclage","sec":"obgyn","present":["Cervical insufficiency: painless cervical dilation leading to second-trimester pregnancy loss or extreme preterm birth, classically WITHOUT contractions - a distinct mechanism from preterm labor","Presents as a history of recurrent painless mid-trimester losses, or as an incidental finding of a dilated cervix/exposed membranes on exam or a SHORT CERVIX on ultrasound","Distinguish from preterm labor (contractions) and infection/abruption"],"dx":["Diagnosis is largely by HISTORY (prior painless second-trimester losses) and by transvaginal cervical LENGTH surveillance (a short cervix, especially with a prior preterm birth) or physical-exam finding of premature dilation","Exclude infection, labor, and abruption when the cervix is found dilated; ultrasound for cervical length in at-risk patients","Evaluate for the appropriate cerclage indication category"],"tx":["Vaginal PROGESTERONE reduces preterm birth in patients with a short cervix; cervical CERCLAGE (a suture reinforcing the cervix) is indicated for specific scenarios","Cerclage TYPES by indication: HISTORY-indicated (prior classic cervical insufficiency, placed ~12-14 weeks), ULTRASOUND-indicated (short cervix with a prior preterm birth), and EXAM-indicated/rescue (dilated cervix on exam)","Transabdominal cerclage for failed transvaginal cerclage or absent/very short cervix; activity modification is of limited proven benefit"],"tech":["Transvaginal cerclage placement (McDONALD - simple purse-string; SHIRODKAR - submucosal), typically under regional anesthesia; transABDOMINAL cerclage (open or laparoscopic) for select cases (delivery then requires cesarean)","Rule out infection/labor before rescue cerclage; place with attention to membrane integrity","Progesterone as indicated alongside"],"after":["Monitor for complications (rupture of membranes, infection, preterm labor); REMOVE the transvaginal cerclage at ~36-37 weeks or with labor/rupture of membranes (an abdominal cerclage stays and mandates cesarean)","Counsel on recurrence and the plan for future pregnancies (history-indicated cerclage, early surveillance)","Ongoing preterm-birth prevention (progesterone, cervical length monitoring)"],"pearls":["Cervical insufficiency is PAINLESS second-trimester dilation - the absence of contractions is what distinguishes it from preterm labor","Match the cerclage to the indication: history-indicated, ultrasound-indicated (short cervix + prior preterm birth), or exam-indicated rescue","Remove a transvaginal cerclage at 36-37 weeks or when labor/rupture occurs - a retained cerclage in labor can lacerate the cervix","Rule out infection and labor before a rescue cerclage - placing one into chorioamnionitis is harmful"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Cerclage / Cervical Insufficiency Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Insufficiency","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525954/"},{"t":"SMFM Preterm Birth Prevention Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-multiple-gestation","name":"Multiple Gestation","sec":"obgyn","present":["Twins and higher-order multiples carry markedly higher risk of preterm birth, fetal growth restriction, preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, malpresentation, and postpartum hemorrhage","CHORIONICITY (and amnionicity) drives risk far more than zygosity: monochorionic pregnancies share a placenta and are prone to unique complications (TTTS, TAPS, selective FGR, TRAP), while dichorionic twins behave more like two singletons","Monoamniotic and conjoined twins are the highest-risk subsets"],"dx":["Determine CHORIONICITY in the FIRST TRIMESTER (most accurate then): dichorionic shows the lambda/twin-peak sign and a thick dividing membrane; monochorionic shows the T-sign and a thin membrane - this determines the entire surveillance plan","Serial growth ultrasounds and, for monochorionic pairs, more frequent surveillance (every ~2 weeks from 16 weeks for TTTS/TAPS with fluid and Doppler assessment)","Screen for preeclampsia and GDM; assess presentation as delivery approaches"],"tx":["Intensified antenatal surveillance tailored to chorionicity; consider aspirin for preeclampsia prevention; nutrition and growth monitoring","Timing of delivery by type: uncomplicated dichorionic-diamniotic ~38 weeks, monochorionic-diamniotic ~36-37 weeks, monochorionic-monoamniotic earlier (~32-34 weeks, often inpatient) given cord-entanglement risk","Manage the complications specific to monochorionic twins (see TTTS entry) at a fetal-care center"],"tech":["Delivery route by presentation: di-di or di vertex-vertex twins may deliver vaginally with expertise (with capability for internal podalic version/breech extraction of twin B); cesarean for non-vertex first twin, monoamniotic twins, and higher-order multiples","Continuous monitoring of both fetuses in labor; anesthesia and neonatal teams present","Be ready for postpartum hemorrhage (overdistended uterus)"],"after":["Heightened postpartum HEMORRHAGE vigilance (uterine overdistension causes atony) with active third-stage management and uterotonics ready","Neonatal care for prematurity and growth issues; support for the practical/psychological load of multiples","Counsel on recurrence and future pregnancy planning"],"pearls":["Chorionicity is the master variable - establish it in the first trimester, because monochorionic pregnancies need far more surveillance","The T-sign means monochorionic (shared placenta, TTTS risk); the lambda/twin-peak sign means dichorionic","An overdistended twin uterus is an atony/hemorrhage setup - have uterotonics and a plan ready at delivery","Deliver by chorionicity and presentation - monoamniotic twins are delivered early by cesarean for cord entanglement"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / SMFM Multifetal Gestation Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Multiple Gestation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564402/"},{"t":"SMFM Twin Pregnancy Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ttts","name":"Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome & Complicated Monochorionic Twins","sec":"obgyn","present":["Complications unique to MONOCHORIONIC (shared-placenta) twins from unbalanced inter-twin placental vascular anastomoses","TWIN-TWIN TRANSFUSION SYNDROME (TTTS): net blood flow from a DONOR twin (oligohydramnios, 'stuck twin', growth-restricted) to a RECIPIENT twin (polyhydramnios, volume overload, hydrops) - a leading cause of loss in monochorionic pregnancies","Related conditions: twin anemia-polycythemia sequence (TAPS - Hb discordance without big fluid differences), selective fetal growth restriction (sFGR), and twin reversed arterial perfusion (TRAP) sequence"],"dx":["Serial ultrasound surveillance of monochorionic-diamniotic twins for the amniotic-fluid discordance defining TTTS (recipient polyhydramnios + donor oligohydramnios); stage by the QUINTERO system (I-V, incorporating bladder visualization, Dopplers, hydrops, and demise)","TAPS diagnosed by discordant middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocities (anemia in one, polycythemia in the other) without the fluid discordance; assess Dopplers and growth for sFGR","Refer promptly to a FETAL-CARE center for staging and treatment"],"tx":["TTTS (stage II and above, or progressive): FETOSCOPIC LASER PHOTOCOAGULATION of the placental anastomoses is the treatment of choice - it addresses the underlying vascular connections and improves survival versus amnioreduction","Expectant surveillance for stage I in some cases; amnioreduction or septostomy are secondary options; selective reduction in severe/discordant-anomaly situations","Manage TAPS (intrauterine transfusion, laser, or delivery by severity), sFGR, and TRAP (radiofrequency ablation/cord occlusion of the acardiac twin) individually"],"tech":["Fetoscopic laser: percutaneous fetoscopic identification and laser coagulation of the crossing placental anastomoses (ideally the Solomon technique - coagulating the whole vascular equator) to functionally separate the circulations","Amnioreduction for symptomatic polyhydramnios; radiofrequency ablation/cord occlusion for TRAP or selective reduction","Post-procedure fetal surveillance"],"after":["Post-laser ultrasound surveillance for recurrence, TAPS, and fetal well-being; monitor for preterm labor/PPROM (procedure risks)","Neonatal care for both twins (neurologic and hematologic surveillance); counsel on outcomes","Plan delivery timing at the fetal-care center"],"pearls":["TTTS is a monochorionic problem - the donor is oligo/stuck, the recipient is poly/overloaded, and only a shared placenta makes it possible","Quintero staging drives management, and fetoscopic laser (not just amnioreduction) is the treatment of choice for advanced TTTS because it fixes the anastomoses","Discordant MCA velocities without fluid discordance is TAPS - a different monochorionic complication","Any monochorionic pregnancy needs a fetal-care center on speed dial - these complications move fast"],"refs":[{"t":"SMFM Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://www.smfm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537176/"},{"t":"NAFTNet Fetal Therapy Resources","u":"https://www.naftnet.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-fgr","name":"Fetal Growth Restriction & Antenatal Fetal Surveillance","sec":"obgyn","present":["Fetal growth restriction (FGR): estimated fetal weight (or abdominal circumference) below the 10th percentile, reflecting a fetus that has not met its growth potential - associated with stillbirth, perinatal morbidity, and later health effects","Distinguish a constitutionally SMALL but healthy fetus from pathologic FGR (usually placental insufficiency); early-onset (<32 weeks, often more severe/placental) vs late-onset FGR","Causes: placental (most common - insufficiency, preeclampsia), fetal (aneuploidy, infection - the TORCH group), and maternal (hypertension, substance use, malnutrition)"],"dx":["Serial growth ultrasounds with UMBILICAL ARTERY DOPPLER as the key tool: increasing resistance -> ABSENT then REVERSED end-diastolic flow signals worsening placental insufficiency and higher risk","Add middle cerebral artery and ductus venosus Doppler and the biophysical profile/NST for surveillance intensity; evaluate for a cause (anatomy scan for anomalies, consider infection/genetic workup, especially in early/severe FGR)","Assess amniotic fluid (oligohydramnios) and maternal preeclampsia"],"tx":["No treatment reverses established FGR - management is SURVEILLANCE and optimally TIMED DELIVERY balancing prematurity against stillbirth risk","Escalate surveillance (Dopplers, BPP, NST) as findings worsen; give antenatal corticosteroids and magnesium neuroprotection if preterm delivery is anticipated","Treat/optimize maternal contributors (blood pressure, smoking cessation)"],"tech":["Doppler-guided surveillance and delivery: absent/reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow and abnormal ductus venosus drive earlier delivery; deliver by obstetric indication (often cesarean for severe FGR with abnormal Dopplers and a non-reassuring status)","Coordinate neonatology for a growth-restricted, often preterm infant","Send the placenta for pathology"],"after":["Neonatal care for the growth-restricted infant (hypoglycemia, thermoregulation, polycythemia); placental pathology to clarify etiology","Counsel on recurrence risk and future-pregnancy prevention (aspirin for placental-mediated disease, early surveillance)","Postpartum maternal follow-up (preeclampsia/CV risk)"],"pearls":["Umbilical artery Doppler is the workhorse - absent or reversed end-diastolic flow marks deteriorating placental function and drives delivery timing","Separate the constitutionally small fetus from pathologic FGR - serial growth and Dopplers, not a single percentile, make the call","Early-onset, severe FGR should prompt a search for aneuploidy and infection, not just placental insufficiency","There is no fix for FGR in utero - the intervention is well-timed delivery, informed by surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / SMFM Fetal Growth Restriction","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fetal Growth Restriction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562135/"},{"t":"SMFM FGR Consult Series","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-fetal-anomalies","name":"Fetal Anomalies & Obstetric Ultrasound","sec":"obgyn","present":["The mid-trimester (18-22 week) fetal anatomy survey systematically screens for structural anomalies; abnormal findings range from isolated and manageable to lethal, and drive counseling, delivery planning, and fetal/neonatal care","Common/high-yield anomalies: neural tube defects (anencephaly, spina bifida), congenital heart disease (the most common anomaly, often missed), abdominal wall defects (GASTROSCHISIS - free-floating bowel, no covering membrane vs OMPHALOCELE - membrane-covered, midline, associated with aneuploidy/syndromes), diaphragmatic hernia, and hydrops fetalis","Soft markers may indicate increased aneuploidy risk and prompt further evaluation"],"dx":["Standardized anatomy ultrasound; a detailed (targeted) ultrasound, fetal ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY (for cardiac anomalies/risk factors), and sometimes fetal MRI when an anomaly is found or suspected","Offer diagnostic testing (amniocentesis for karyotype/microarray) and infection workup as indicated; refer to MFM/genetics for counseling","Correlate findings and assess for associated anomalies/syndromes"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary counseling and care planning: some anomalies benefit from FETAL THERAPY (e.g., prenatal myelomeningocele repair - the MOMS trial improved outcomes; fetoscopic tracheal occlusion for severe CDH in trials), many require planned delivery at a center with pediatric surgery/neonatology","Delivery location, timing, and mode are tailored to the anomaly (e.g., gastroschisis needs neonatal surgical readiness); some lethal anomalies are managed with palliative/comfort planning","Support informed decision-making including continuation and pregnancy options per patient values and local law"],"tech":["Detailed sonographic assessment, fetal echocardiography, and fetal MRI to characterize anomalies; diagnostic amniocentesis; referral for fetal intervention when indicated","Plan the delivery setting (level III/IV center) and the immediate neonatal surgical/medical plan (e.g., EXIT procedure for airway-obstructing masses)","Coordinate the maternal-fetal team"],"after":["Deliver at the planned center with neonatology/pediatric surgery ready; execute the neonatal management plan (e.g., gastroschisis silo/closure)","Genetic follow-up and recurrence counseling; bereavement support for lethal anomalies","Postpartum maternal care and future-pregnancy planning (e.g., folic acid for NTD prevention)"],"pearls":["Distinguish gastroschisis (no covering membrane, right of midline, usually isolated) from omphalocele (membrane-covered, midline, strongly linked to aneuploidy/syndromes) - the workup and prognosis differ","Congenital heart disease is the most common and most commonly missed anomaly - a low threshold for fetal echocardiography matters","Prenatal myelomeningocele repair (MOMS trial) improved neurologic outcomes - a fetal anomaly can be a fetal-surgery referral","Finding one anomaly obligates a search for others and a genetics referral - anomalies cluster into syndromes"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / AIUM Fetal Ultrasound Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prenatal Ultrasound","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563296/"},{"t":"NAFTNet Fetal Therapy Resources","u":"https://www.naftnet.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-rh-alloimmunization","name":"Rh Alloimmunization & Isoimmunization","sec":"obgyn","present":["Maternal red-cell alloantibodies (classically anti-D in an Rh-negative mother carrying an Rh-positive fetus, but also anti-Kell, anti-c, and others) cross the placenta and hemolyze fetal red cells - HEMOLYTIC DISEASE OF THE FETUS AND NEWBORN","Sensitization occurs from fetomaternal hemorrhage (delivery, miscarriage, trauma, procedures, or a prior pregnancy); the FIRST affected pregnancy is often mild, with SUBSEQUENT pregnancies more severe (anamnestic response)","Severe disease causes fetal anemia, high-output failure, and HYDROPS FETALIS"],"dx":["Antibody SCREEN (indirect Coombs) at the first visit; if a clinically significant antibody is present, follow TITERS and identify paternal/fetal antigen status (cell-free fetal DNA can determine fetal Rh)","Above a CRITICAL TITER, monitor for fetal anemia with serial middle cerebral artery PEAK SYSTOLIC VELOCITY Doppler (elevated MCA-PSV predicts fetal anemia noninvasively, replacing serial amniocentesis)","Assess for hydrops on ultrasound"],"tx":["PREVENTION with Rh(D) IMMUNE GLOBULIN (RhoGAM) in Rh-negative unsensitized patients: routinely at ~28 weeks and within 72 h postpartum if the neonate is Rh-positive, and after any sensitizing event (miscarriage, ectopic, CVS/amnio, trauma, external version, antepartum bleeding); dose per fetomaternal hemorrhage quantification (Kleihauer-Betke/flow)","Already sensitized: MCA-PSV surveillance and, for fetal anemia, intrauterine transfusion (IUT) at a fetal-care center; time delivery based on severity","RhoGAM does NOT help once alloimmunization has occurred (it is prevention, not treatment)"],"tech":["Intrauterine transfusion of the fetus (ultrasound-guided, usually into the umbilical vein) for significant fetal anemia; serial transfusions may be needed","Deliver at a center with neonatal transfusion/exchange capability; MCA Doppler-guided timing","Quantify fetomaternal hemorrhage to dose RhoGAM correctly"],"after":["Neonatal management of hemolytic disease: hyperbilirubinemia (phototherapy, exchange transfusion), anemia (transfusion), and monitoring; involve neonatology","Counsel that FUTURE pregnancies are typically MORE severely affected - plan early surveillance and specialist care; document the antibody","Ensure appropriate postpartum RhoGAM in unsensitized Rh-negative patients"],"pearls":["RhoGAM is prevention, not treatment - give it at 28 weeks, postpartum (if baby Rh-positive), and after every sensitizing event; it does nothing once antibodies exist","MCA peak systolic velocity Doppler noninvasively predicts fetal anemia and has replaced serial amniocentesis for surveillance","Sensitized pregnancies get worse with each subsequent pregnancy - the first is often the mildest","Quantify fetomaternal hemorrhage (Kleihauer-Betke) after large bleeds/trauma to give an adequate RhoGAM dose"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Prevention of Rh Alloimmunization","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hemolytic Disease of the Fetus and Newborn","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557477/"},{"t":"SMFM Alloimmunization Resources","u":"https://www.smfm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-prenatal-screening","name":"Prenatal Genetic Screening & Diagnosis","sec":"obgyn","present":["All pregnant patients are OFFERED both aneuploidy SCREENING and the OPTION of diagnostic testing, regardless of age - the old age-based cutoff is obsolete","Screening estimates RISK (does not diagnose); diagnostic testing gives a definitive answer with a small procedure-related risk - patients choose based on values after counseling","Also includes carrier screening for inherited conditions and screening for structural anomalies (anatomy scan) and neural tube defects"],"dx":["Aneuploidy SCREENING options: cell-free DNA (cfDNA/NIPT - high sensitivity/specificity for trisomy 21/18/13, best positive predictive value, from ~10 weeks), first-trimester combined (nuchal translucency + PAPP-A/hCG), and second-trimester quad screen; maternal serum AFP/anatomy scan for neural tube defects","DIAGNOSTIC testing: chorionic villus sampling (CVS, ~10-13 weeks - placental tissue) and AMNIOCENTESIS (>=15 weeks - amniotic fluid), with karyotype/chromosomal microarray; small procedure-related loss risk","Offer carrier screening (expanded panels or ethnicity-based) ideally preconception"],"tx":["Nondirective genetic COUNSELING is central: explain that a positive screen requires diagnostic confirmation (cfDNA is a screen, not a diagnosis - false positives occur, especially at low prior risk and from confined placental mosaicism/maternal factors)","Facilitate diagnostic testing for those who want certainty or have a positive screen/anomaly; provide results with options and support","Coordinate MFM/genetics referral for abnormal results and anomaly management"],"tech":["CVS: transabdominal or transcervical sampling of placental villi (~10-13 weeks); AMNIOCENTESIS: ultrasound-guided transabdominal aspiration of amniotic fluid (>=15 weeks) for karyotype/microarray/other testing","Rh immunoglobulin for Rh-negative patients undergoing invasive procedures","Anatomy ultrasound and fetal echo for structural screening"],"after":["Deliver results with genetic counseling and nondirective options; arrange specialist follow-up for confirmed abnormalities","Document informed choices; support decision-making per patient values and local law","Plan pregnancy management/delivery based on findings"],"pearls":["Offer screening AND the option of diagnostic testing to everyone - age-based offering is outdated","cfDNA is the best-performing SCREEN but is not diagnostic - a positive result needs CVS or amniocentesis to confirm, because false positives happen","CVS is earlier (10-13 weeks, placenta); amniocentesis is later (>=15 weeks, fluid) - choose by gestational age and indication","Counseling is nondirective - the clinician informs and supports; the patient decides"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Prenatal Genetic Screening & Diagnostic Testing","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prenatal Genetic Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557702/"},{"t":"ACMG / SMFM Resources","u":"https://www.acmg.net"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ppd","name":"Postpartum Depression & Peripartum Mood Disorders","sec":"obgyn","present":["A spectrum in the peripartum period: transient 'BABY BLUES' (mild, self-limited, resolving within ~2 weeks) vs POSTPARTUM DEPRESSION (persistent depressive symptoms with functional impairment, often within the first months) vs POSTPARTUM PSYCHOSIS (a psychiatric EMERGENCY)","Perinatal mood and anxiety disorders are the most common complications of childbearing and a leading contributor to maternal mortality (suicide); anxiety and OCD-spectrum symptoms also occur","Postpartum psychosis (rapid onset of confusion, delusions, hallucinations, mood lability - often linked to bipolar disorder) carries risk of suicide and infanticide"],"dx":["Universal SCREENING with a validated tool (Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale or PHQ-9) during pregnancy and postpartum; assess severity, function, and SAFETY (suicidal ideation, thoughts of harming the infant)","Distinguish baby blues (mild, <2 weeks) from postpartum depression (persistent, impairing) and recognize postpartum psychosis as an emergency requiring immediate psychiatric evaluation/hospitalization","Screen for bipolar disorder before starting an antidepressant (to avoid precipitating mania), and evaluate thyroid/anemia as contributors"],"tx":["Postpartum depression: psychotherapy (CBT/IPT) for mild-moderate, and SSRIs (sertraline is often first-line and breastfeeding-compatible) for moderate-severe; newer neuroactive-steroid options (brexanolone IV, zuranolone oral) target postpartum depression specifically","Ensure SAFETY planning and mobilize support; treat comorbid anxiety; most antidepressants are compatible with breastfeeding (discuss risk/benefit)","POSTPARTUM PSYCHOSIS: emergency psychiatric admission, mood stabilization/antipsychotics, and safety for mother and infant"],"tech":["Screening-and-treatment pathway: validated screen -> severity/safety assessment -> stepped care (therapy, pharmacotherapy, or emergency psychiatric referral); coordinate with psychiatry/behavioral health","Brexanolone requires a monitored infusion setting; arrange lactation-informed medication choices","Establish a safety plan and follow-up interval"],"after":["Close FOLLOW-UP to assess response and safety; continue therapy/medication for an adequate course; coordinate the fourth-trimester care team (obstetrics, primary care, psychiatry, pediatrics)","Counsel on recurrence risk in future pregnancies and preventive planning; support the mother-infant dyad and partner","Reassess suicide/infant-safety risk at each contact"],"pearls":["Screen every peripartum patient with a validated tool - perinatal mood disorders are the most common complication of childbirth and a leading cause of maternal death","Baby blues resolve within ~2 weeks; persistent, impairing symptoms are postpartum depression and need treatment","Postpartum psychosis is an emergency - rapid onset with confusion/delusions demands immediate psychiatric admission for infanticide/suicide risk","Screen for bipolar disorder before prescribing an antidepressant - unopposed antidepressants can trigger mania"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Perinatal Mental Health Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postpartum Depression","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519070/"},{"t":"MGH Center for Women's Mental Health","u":"https://womensmentalhealth.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-aub-surgical","name":"Abnormal Uterine Bleeding - Surgical Evaluation & Management","sec":"obgyn","present":["Abnormal uterine bleeding (heavy, prolonged, intermenstrual, or irregular) in the non-pregnant patient - the specialist/surgical evaluation extends beyond first-line primary-care management to structural diagnosis and procedural treatment","Framework: the PALM-COEIN system separates STRUCTURAL causes (Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia) from NON-structural (Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not otherwise classified)","ACUTE heavy bleeding can cause hemodynamic instability and requires urgent control"],"dx":["Always exclude PREGNANCY; then transvaginal ultrasound (+/- SALINE INFUSION SONOHYSTEROGRAPHY to define intracavitary lesions - polyps, submucosal fibroids) and hysteroscopy as needed","ENDOMETRIAL SAMPLING (biopsy) to exclude hyperplasia/cancer in at-risk patients: age >=45, or younger with unopposed-estrogen risk (obesity, PCOS, chronic anovulation) or failed medical therapy","Assess for coagulopathy (especially in adolescents with heavy bleeding from menarche - von Willebrand disease) and check CBC/ferritin"],"tx":["Acute heavy bleeding: hemodynamic stabilization, high-dose estrogen or IV conjugated estrogen, tranexamic acid, and hormonal therapy; surgical control (D&C, uterine tamponade) if refractory","Chronic AUB medical options: the LEVONORGESTREL IUD (highly effective for heavy menstrual bleeding), combined hormonals, progestins, and tranexamic acid","SURGICAL options when medical therapy fails or a structural lesion is present: hysteroscopic polypectomy/myomectomy, ENDOMETRIAL ABLATION (for those who have completed childbearing and have a normal cavity/no hyperplasia), and HYSTERECTOMY (definitive)"],"tech":["Operative HYSTEROSCOPY to resect polyps/submucosal fibroids; endometrial ablation (various energy modalities) after excluding hyperplasia/cancer and completing childbearing; dilation and curettage for tissue/acute control","Hysterectomy for definitive management; uterine artery embolization for fibroid-related bleeding","Ensure the endometrium is sampled before ablation"],"after":["Follow response and treat iron-deficiency anemia; counsel that ablation is not contraception and pregnancy after ablation is high-risk (contraception needed)","Surveillance for recurrence; pathology review guides further management (hyperplasia -> progestin/hysterectomy)","Fertility-sparing choices for those desiring future pregnancy"],"pearls":["Rule out pregnancy first, then use PALM-COEIN to organize the workup into structural vs non-structural causes","Sample the endometrium in AUB with cancer risk - age >=45, or younger with unopposed-estrogen states - before calling it benign","The levonorgestrel IUD is a first-line, highly effective treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding - often avoiding surgery","Endometrial ablation requires a benign endometrium and completed childbearing - never ablate an unsampled or hyperplastic cavity, and it is not contraception"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Abnormal Uterine Bleeding Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Abnormal Uterine Bleeding","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532913/"},{"t":"FIGO PALM-COEIN System","u":"https://www.figo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-leiomyoma","name":"Uterine Leiomyoma (Fibroids)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Benign monoclonal smooth-muscle tumors of the myometrium - the most common pelvic tumor in reproductive-age women; frequently asymptomatic but can cause heavy menstrual bleeding, bulk symptoms (pressure, urinary frequency, constipation), pain, and infertility/pregnancy complications","Classified by location (FIGO 0-8): SUBMUCOSAL (distort the cavity - most bleeding/fertility impact), intramural, and subserosal/pedunculated (bulk symptoms)","In pregnancy they may undergo painful red (carneous) degeneration; malignant leiomyosarcoma is rare"],"dx":["Pelvic examination and transvaginal ULTRASOUND (first-line); saline sonohysterography or hysteroscopy to define submucosal/intracavitary fibroids; MRI for mapping before myomectomy/UAE or when the diagnosis/adenomyosis is unclear","Evaluate anemia (CBC/ferritin) with heavy bleeding; exclude other causes of bleeding (endometrial sampling per AUB criteria)","Assess symptom burden and reproductive goals (they drive treatment)"],"tx":["Asymptomatic fibroids generally need no treatment (surveillance); treat based on symptoms and fertility desires","MEDICAL: tranexamic acid and hormonal therapies (levonorgestrel IUD, combined hormonals, progestins) for bleeding; GnRH AGONISTS/ANTAGONISTS shrink fibroids and treat bleeding (often as a bridge to surgery or to correct anemia - limited duration due to bone loss, antagonist combos mitigate this)","SURGICAL/procedural: MYOMECTOMY (uterine-sparing - hysteroscopic for submucosal, laparoscopic/open for others - the fertility-preserving option), UTERINE ARTERY EMBOLIZATION (UAE - avoid if fertility desired), and HYSTERECTOMY (definitive)"],"tech":["Hysteroscopic myomectomy for submucosal fibroids; laparoscopic/robotic or abdominal myomectomy for intramural/subserosal fibroids (counsel on morcellation concerns given rare occult sarcoma); UAE by interventional radiology","Hysterectomy for definitive treatment; MRI-guided focused ultrasound in select centers","Correct anemia preoperatively (iron, GnRH bridge)"],"after":["Post-myomectomy: counsel on RECURRENCE (fibroids can regrow) and, depending on cavity entry, possible cesarean delivery and uterine-rupture considerations in future pregnancy","Monitor symptom relief and anemia recovery; UAE patients may have post-embolization pain/syndrome","Fertility planning per procedure"],"pearls":["Location predicts symptoms - submucosal fibroids drive bleeding and infertility; subserosal ones cause bulk pressure","Match treatment to fertility goals - myomectomy preserves the uterus; UAE and hysterectomy do not preserve/optimize fertility","GnRH agonists/antagonists shrink fibroids and fix anemia as a surgical bridge, but bone loss limits duration","Rapid growth (especially postmenopausal) raises the rare concern for leiomyosarcoma - and morcellation of an occult sarcoma is why tissue-containment matters"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Uterine Leiomyoma Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Uterine Fibroids","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546680/"},{"t":"FIGO Leiomyoma Classification","u":"https://www.figo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-endometriosis","name":"Endometriosis","sec":"obgyn","present":["Endometrial-like tissue outside the uterus (pelvic peritoneum, ovaries - ENDOMETRIOMAS/'chocolate cysts', rectovaginal septum) causing a chronic inflammatory, estrogen-dependent condition","Classic symptoms: cyclic then chronic pelvic pain, DYSMENORRHEA, DEEP DYSPAREUNIA, dyschezia, and INFERTILITY; symptom severity correlates poorly with disease extent","A leading cause of chronic pelvic pain and infertility, often with a long diagnostic delay"],"dx":["Clinical suspicion from the symptom pattern and exam (tender nodularity, fixed retroverted uterus, adnexal mass); transvaginal ultrasound detects endometriomas and deep disease; MRI for mapping","LAPAROSCOPY with histology is the traditional diagnostic gold standard, but empiric medical treatment based on clinical diagnosis is now appropriate before surgery in many cases","Evaluate infertility and rule out other causes of pelvic pain"],"tx":["First-line MEDICAL therapy for pain: NSAIDs plus hormonal SUPPRESSION - combined hormonal contraceptives or PROGESTINS (continuous), the levonorgestrel IUD; second-line GnRH agonists/ANTAGONISTS (with add-back to limit bone loss) and aromatase inhibitors","SURGICAL treatment (laparoscopic EXCISION or ablation of lesions, endometrioma cystectomy) for refractory pain or when fertility is desired; balance endometrioma surgery against ovarian reserve loss","INFERTILITY: surgery can improve fertility in some; assisted reproductive technology (IVF) is often the effective path - hormonal suppression does not treat infertility"],"tech":["Laparoscopic excision/ablation of endometriotic implants and adhesiolysis; ovarian endometrioma cystectomy (careful technique to preserve ovarian reserve); excision of deep infiltrating disease (may involve bowel/urologic surgery)","Definitive surgery (hysterectomy +/- oophorectomy) for those with completed childbearing and refractory disease","Fertility-focused planning with REI"],"after":["Endometriosis is CHRONIC and RECURS - plan long-term hormonal suppression after surgery to delay recurrence (unless pursuing pregnancy)","Multidisciplinary pain management for persistent symptoms; monitor ovarian reserve after endometrioma surgery","Fertility counseling and timely REI referral"],"pearls":["Symptom severity does not track with disease extent - a patient with minimal disease can have severe pain and vice versa","You no longer need laparoscopy to start treatment - empiric hormonal suppression for clinically diagnosed endometriosis is appropriate","Endometrioma cystectomy costs ovarian reserve - weigh surgery against future fertility, especially before IVF","Endometriosis recurs - post-surgical hormonal suppression delays recurrence unless the patient is trying to conceive"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Endometriosis Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endometriosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567777/"},{"t":"ASRM / ESHRE Endometriosis Guidelines","u":"https://www.asrm.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-adenomyosis","name":"Adenomyosis","sec":"obgyn","present":["Endometrial glands and stroma within the MYOMETRIUM, causing a diffusely enlarged, boggy, tender uterus; classically presents with HEAVY MENSTRUAL BLEEDING and progressively worsening DYSMENORRHEA","Often coexists with fibroids and endometriosis and overlaps their symptoms; typically in multiparous women in their 40s but increasingly recognized earlier with imaging","A frequently under-diagnosed cause of AUB and pelvic pain"],"dx":["Transvaginal ULTRASOUND (asymmetric myometrial thickening, myometrial cysts, heterogeneity, indistinct endometrial-myometrial junction) and MRI (a widened JUNCTIONAL ZONE is characteristic) support the diagnosis noninvasively","Historically diagnosed only on hysterectomy pathology; imaging now allows a clinical diagnosis","Exclude/assess coexisting fibroids, endometriosis, and endometrial pathology (sample if AUB criteria met)"],"tx":["MEDICAL first-line: the LEVONORGESTREL IUD is highly effective for the bleeding and pain; combined hormonals, continuous progestins, tranexamic acid, and GnRH agonists/antagonists are options","Uterine-sparing procedural options (uterine artery embolization, adenomyomectomy, focused ultrasound) for selected patients wishing to avoid hysterectomy","HYSTERECTOMY is the DEFINITIVE treatment for those who have completed childbearing with refractory symptoms"],"tech":["Hysterectomy for definitive management; uterine-sparing surgery (adenomyomectomy) is technically difficult and reserved for select fertility-desiring patients; UAE by interventional radiology","Levonorgestrel IUD placement as the mainstay conservative therapy","Preoperative anemia correction"],"after":["Monitor symptom relief and treat iron-deficiency anemia; counsel on fertility implications (adenomyosis is associated with subfertility and pregnancy complications)","For uterine-sparing options, counsel on recurrence and pregnancy considerations","Follow-up for symptom control"],"pearls":["A diffusely enlarged, boggy, tender uterus with heavy painful periods in a multiparous 40-something is the classic adenomyosis picture","MRI showing a widened junctional zone is the imaging signature - you no longer need a hysterectomy specimen to diagnose it","The levonorgestrel IUD is the effective first-line conservative treatment for both the bleeding and the pain","Hysterectomy is the only definitive cure - uterine-sparing options are limited and less durable"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Adenomyosis / AUB Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adenomyosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539868/"},{"t":"RCOG Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Resources","u":"https://www.rcog.org.uk"}]},{"id":"obgyn-adnexal-masses","name":"Adnexal Masses & Ovarian Cysts","sec":"obgyn","present":["Adnexal masses span functional cysts (follicular, corpus luteum), benign neoplasms (dermoid/mature teratoma, serous/mucinous cystadenoma, endometrioma), and malignancy; also non-ovarian (tubal, paratubal, ectopic, hydrosalpinx)","Most reproductive-age cysts are functional and RESOLVE; the clinical task is risk-stratifying for malignancy and identifying masses at risk of torsion/rupture","Presentation ranges from incidental (asymptomatic) to pain, pressure, or acute complications"],"dx":["Transvaginal ULTRASOUND characterizes the mass; reassuring simple cysts vs concerning features (solid components, thick septations, papillary projections, ascites, increased vascularity) - use standardized systems (O-RADS, IOTA) to stratify","TUMOR MARKERS by context and age: CA-125 (more specific in POSTmenopausal women; nonspecific premenopausally - elevated by endometriosis, fibroids, PID), and germ-cell markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) in young patients with complex/solid masses","POSTMENOPAUSAL complex masses and rising markers raise malignancy concern -> gyn-oncology referral"],"tx":["Expectant management with follow-up ultrasound for simple/functional cysts (most resolve); no intervention for asymptomatic simple cysts within size guidelines","SURGERY for persistent, large, symptomatic, or suspicious masses: cystectomy (ovary-sparing) vs oophorectomy depending on age/features; refer suspicious masses to GYN-ONCOLOGY for appropriate staging/surgery","Acute complications (torsion, rupture with hemorrhage) require urgent surgery"],"tech":["Laparoscopic ovarian CYSTECTOMY (fertility-preserving) or salpingo-oophorectomy; avoid cyst spillage for possibly malignant masses (upstaging risk) - suspicious masses go to gyn-onc for proper surgical staging","Frozen section intraoperatively when malignancy is possible","Manage torsion/rupture emergently"],"after":["Pathology review guides follow-up; surveillance imaging for indeterminate masses managed expectantly","Counsel on fertility (ovary-sparing where possible) and recurrence; postmenopausal or malignant findings need oncologic follow-up","Symptom and complication monitoring"],"pearls":["Most reproductive-age cysts are functional and resolve - image, stratify, and often just follow rather than operate","CA-125 is specific enough to help in postmenopausal women but is nonspecific premenopausally (endometriosis, fibroids, PID all raise it)","A complex or solid mass in a postmenopausal woman, or rising markers, means gyn-oncology referral and proper staging - do not spill a possibly malignant cyst","Germ-cell tumor markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) belong in the workup of a young patient with a solid adnexal mass"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Adnexal Mass / Ovarian Cyst Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adnexal Mass","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560751/"},{"t":"O-RADS / ACR Ultrasound Resources","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ovarian-torsion","name":"Ovarian Torsion","sec":"obgyn","present":["Rotation of the ovary (and often the tube) on its vascular pedicle, compromising blood supply - a gynecologic surgical EMERGENCY that threatens ovarian viability","Sudden-onset severe, often unilateral pelvic/adnexal pain, frequently with nausea and vomiting; may be intermittent (torsion/detorsion); usually associated with an ovarian MASS/cyst that acts as a lead point (dermoids classic)","More common with enlarged ovaries (masses, ovulation induction/OHSS); can occur in pregnancy"],"dx":["Clinical suspicion is paramount; transvaginal/pelvic ULTRASOUND with DOPPLER shows an enlarged ovary +/- a mass and a 'whirlpool' sign - but PRESERVED Doppler flow does NOT exclude torsion (dual blood supply and intermittent torsion)","Do not let a normal Doppler falsely reassure you - the diagnosis is often clinical and confirmed at surgery","Assess for the lead-point mass and rule out other causes of acute pelvic pain (ectopic, appendicitis, ruptured cyst, TOA)"],"tx":["Prompt surgical exploration (usually LAPAROSCOPY) with DETORSION to restore blood flow - ovary-sparing management is now standard even when the ovary looks dusky/necrotic (it frequently recovers), especially in young/fertility-desiring patients","Remove or drain the causative cyst (cystectomy) to reduce recurrence; oophorectomy reserved for clearly necrotic tissue or malignancy concern (especially postmenopausal)","Time is ovary - delay increases the chance of irreversible loss"],"tech":["Laparoscopic detorsion (untwist the pedicle) and ovarian cystectomy of the lead-point mass; consider oophoropexy for recurrent torsion or absent ovary on the other side","Assess viability after detorsion; salpingo-oophorectomy only for frankly necrotic/malignant tissue","Handle gently to preserve the ovary"],"after":["Monitor recovery; counsel on recurrence risk and future fertility (ovary-sparing preserves function); pathology of any removed cyst","For recurrent torsion, discuss oophoropexy","Follow-up for symptom resolution"],"pearls":["A normal Doppler does NOT rule out torsion - the diagnosis is clinical, and preserved flow (dual supply, intermittent torsion) is a classic trap","Detorse and spare the ovary even when it looks necrotic - a dusky ovary often recovers, and reflexive oophorectomy costs fertility","Look for the lead-point cyst (dermoids are classic) and remove it to prevent recurrence","Time is ovary - torsion is a get-to-the-OR emergency, not a watch-and-wait"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Adnexal Torsion Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ovarian Torsion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560675/"},{"t":"Society of Gynecologic Surgeons","u":"https://www.sgsonline.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-pid-toa","name":"Pelvic Inflammatory Disease & Tubo-Ovarian Abscess","sec":"obgyn","present":["Ascending infection of the upper female genital tract (endometritis, salpingitis, tubo-ovarian abscess, pelvic peritonitis), usually POLYMICROBIAL and often involving gonorrhea/chlamydia; the surgical/specialist counterpart to primary-care STI screening","Presents with lower abdominal/pelvic pain, and on exam CERVICAL MOTION, uterine, or adnexal TENDERNESS; may have fever, discharge, or abnormal bleeding - the presentation is often subtle, so a LOW threshold to treat prevents sequelae","TUBO-OVARIAN ABSCESS (TOA) is a walled-off collection - a more severe, potentially rupturing complication"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: treat empirically for PID in an at-risk patient with pelvic/lower-abdominal pain and cervical motion/uterine/adnexal tenderness with no other cause - do not wait for confirmation","Test for gonorrhea/chlamydia (NAAT), HIV, and syphilis; pelvic ULTRASOUND for suspected TOA (complex adnexal/multiloculated mass); pregnancy test (exclude ectopic)","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis - RUQ pain) is an associated finding"],"tx":["Empiric broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS covering gonorrhea, chlamydia, and anaerobes: outpatient regimens (ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole) for mild-moderate disease; INPATIENT IV therapy (e.g., cefotetan/cefoxitin + doxycycline, or clindamycin + gentamicin) for severe disease, TOA, pregnancy, failed outpatient therapy, or inability to tolerate oral","TUBO-OVARIAN ABSCESS: IV antibiotics with anaerobic coverage; DRAINAGE (image-guided or surgical) for large abscesses (often >7 cm), rupture, or failure to improve in ~48-72 h","Treat sexual PARTNERS and counsel on STI prevention"],"tech":["Image-guided (interventional radiology) or surgical (laparoscopic) drainage of a tubo-ovarian abscess; surgery for a ruptured TOA (a surgical emergency with sepsis risk)","Laparoscopy can be diagnostic/therapeutic in uncertain or refractory cases","Remove/drain the source"],"after":["Ensure clinical improvement within 72 h (reassess/escalate if not); complete the antibiotic course and re-test/treat as indicated","Counsel on the LONG-TERM sequelae: infertility, ECTOPIC pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain from tubal damage - and on prevention/partner treatment","Repeat imaging for TOA resolution"],"pearls":["Keep a low threshold to treat PID - the minimum criteria (pelvic pain + cervical motion/uterine/adnexal tenderness) are enough, because missed PID causes infertility and ectopics","Always cover anaerobes when TOA is a possibility, and add metronidazole to outpatient regimens","A tubo-ovarian abscess that is large, ruptured, or not improving in 48-72 h needs drainage, not just more antibiotics","Treat the partner and screen for other STIs - PID is a sexually transmitted infection with lifelong reproductive consequences"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC STI Treatment Guidelines - PID","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/std"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499959/"},{"t":"ACOG PID Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-chronic-pelvic-pain","name":"Chronic Pelvic Pain","sec":"obgyn","present":["Noncyclic pelvic pain lasting >=6 months, severe enough to cause functional impairment - a complex, often MULTIFACTORIAL condition rather than a single diagnosis","Contributing/overlapping sources: gynecologic (endometriosis, adenomyosis, adhesions, pelvic congestion), urologic (interstitial cystitis/bladder pain syndrome), gastrointestinal (irritable bowel syndrome), MUSCULOSKELETAL/myofascial (pelvic floor dysfunction), and neuropathic/central sensitization, with frequent psychological comorbidity","Central sensitization can amplify and perpetuate pain independent of a peripheral cause"],"dx":["Comprehensive, biopsychosocial evaluation: detailed history (cyclicity, dyspareunia, urinary/bowel symptoms, prior surgery, trauma/mood), and a systematic exam INCLUDING the PELVIC FLOOR (trigger points), abdominal wall (Carnett sign), and bladder/bowel","Targeted testing: pelvic ultrasound, STI testing, urinalysis; laparoscopy when endometriosis/adhesions are suspected (but a negative laparoscopy does not exclude pain - avoid repeated surgeries)","Screen for depression/anxiety and prior trauma"],"tx":["MULTIMODAL, multidisciplinary management targeting the identified contributors: treat endometriosis/adenomyosis (hormonal suppression), interstitial cystitis and IBS on their own pathways, and PELVIC FLOOR PHYSICAL THERAPY for myofascial dysfunction","Pain-modulating medications (NSAIDs, neuropathic agents - gabapentinoids, TCAs/SNRIs), trigger-point injections, and cognitive behavioral therapy/pain psychology for central sensitization","Surgery only for a clear surgical target (e.g., endometriosis excision) - avoid repeated operations that do not help and can worsen central pain"],"tech":["Diagnostic/therapeutic laparoscopy for suspected endometriosis/adhesions; trigger-point and nerve blocks; pelvic floor rehabilitation","Avoid low-yield repeated surgery; coordinate multidisciplinary care (gynecology, urology, GI, PT, pain, behavioral health)","Individualized procedural options by cause"],"after":["Longitudinal, patient-centered follow-up with realistic goals (function and pain reduction, not always cure); reassess and adjust the multimodal plan","Address mood, sleep, and quality of life; avoid escalating opioids (limited benefit, dependence risk)","Support and continuity of care"],"pearls":["Chronic pelvic pain is usually multifactorial - examine the pelvic floor and abdominal wall, not just the uterus and ovaries","A negative laparoscopy does not mean there is no pain - resist the pull toward repeated, unhelpful surgery","Central sensitization perpetuates pain beyond any peripheral trigger - pain psychology and neuromodulators are part of treatment, not an afterthought","Treat every contributing system (gyn, bladder, bowel, muscle, mood) - fixing one alone rarely resolves multifactorial pain"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Chronic Pelvic Pain Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Pelvic Pain in Women","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554585/"},{"t":"International Pelvic Pain Society","u":"https://www.pelvicpain.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-vulvar-vaginal-benign","name":"Benign Vulvar & Vaginal Disorders","sec":"obgyn","present":["Common benign conditions of the vulva/vagina: BARTHOLIN gland cyst/abscess (a painful posterolateral introital mass), LICHEN SCLEROSUS (thin, white, atrophic 'cigarette-paper' skin with itching, dyspareunia, and architectural loss - figure-of-eight distribution), lichen planus, lichen simplex chronicus, and contact/irritant dermatitis","Vaginitis (bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis, trichomoniasis) and atrophic/genitourinary syndrome of menopause also present with vulvovaginal symptoms","The key is distinguishing benign dermatoses from premalignant/malignant lesions"],"dx":["Careful vulvar EXAMINATION; BIOPSY any suspicious, non-resolving, or atypical lesion - lichen sclerosus and lichen planus carry a risk of vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia/SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA, so persistent or ulcerated lesions must be sampled","Diagnose vaginitis with pH, wet mount/microscopy, and NAAT as appropriate; assess for atrophy in postmenopausal patients","Bartholin abscess is a clinical diagnosis; consider biopsy/excision of a Bartholin mass in older patients (rare gland carcinoma)"],"tx":["Bartholin abscess: incision and drainage with placement of a WORD CATHETER (to maintain a drainage tract), or MARSUPIALIZATION for recurrence; antibiotics if cellulitis/systemic signs","Lichen sclerosus: high-potency topical CORTICOSTEROIDS (clobetasol) - effective and symptom-relieving, with maintenance therapy and long-term SURVEILLANCE for malignant change; treat lichen planus similarly (topical/other immunomodulators)","Treat vaginitis by cause; genitourinary syndrome of menopause with vaginal moisturizers/estrogen"],"tech":["Bartholin: Word catheter placement or marsupialization (suturing the cyst wall open); excision for recurrent/complex cases","Vulvar BIOPSY (punch biopsy) of suspicious lesions for histologic diagnosis","Excisional procedures for premalignant lesions (see cervical/vulvar dysplasia pathways)"],"after":["Lichen sclerosus needs LONG-TERM follow-up and maintenance steroids with periodic exam for malignant transformation; counsel on the small but real cancer risk","Bartholin: monitor healing and recurrence; recurrent disease may need marsupialization/excision","Reassess symptom control and adherence"],"pearls":["Biopsy the vulvar lesion that does not resolve or looks atypical - lichen sclerosus and lichen planus can harbor or progress to squamous cell carcinoma","Clobetasol is the workhorse for lichen sclerosus, with maintenance therapy and lifelong surveillance","A Bartholin abscess is treated with a Word catheter or marsupialization, not just simple incision (which recurs)","A new solid Bartholin-area mass in an older woman warrants biopsy/excision to exclude rare gland carcinoma"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Vulvar / Lichen Sclerosus Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lichen Sclerosus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538246/"},{"t":"International Society for the Study of Vulvovaginal Disease","u":"https://www.issvd.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-sexual-dysfunction","name":"Female Sexual Dysfunction & Vulvodynia","sec":"obgyn","present":["Female sexual dysfunction spans disorders of DESIRE (hypoactive sexual desire disorder), AROUSAL, ORGASM, and sexual PAIN - when persistent and causing personal DISTRESS","VULVODYNIA: chronic vulvar pain (>=3 months) without an identifiable cause - classified as localized (e.g., provoked vestibulodynia - pain on touch/penetration) or generalized, and provoked vs spontaneous","Genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM) and dyspareunia from atrophy are common, treatable contributors"],"dx":["A biopsychosocial assessment: sexual, medical, psychological, relationship, and medication history (SSRIs, hormonal contraceptives), plus a focused exam; for vulvodynia, the cotton-swab (Q-tip) test maps localized tenderness (provoked vestibulodynia)","Exclude/treat specific causes: infection, dermatoses (lichen sclerosus/planus), atrophy/GSM, pelvic floor dysfunction, and endometriosis","Screen for depression, anxiety, and history of trauma"],"tx":["Address contributors first (treat atrophy/GSM with vaginal moisturizers/estrogen, dermatoses, infections; adjust offending medications) and provide education/counseling","Multimodal, patient-centered care: PELVIC FLOOR PHYSICAL THERAPY (central for provoked vestibulodynia and pelvic floor dysfunction), topical agents (lidocaine), neuromodulators (TCAs, gabapentinoids) for vulvodynia, and psychological/sex therapy (CBT)","For hypoactive sexual desire disorder in select patients: flibanserin or bremelanotide, after addressing reversible factors"],"tech":["Cotton-swab mapping and pelvic floor assessment guide therapy; pelvic floor physical therapy and biofeedback; vestibulectomy is a rare last-resort surgical option for refractory provoked vestibulodynia","Topical/injectable therapies for localized pain; multidisciplinary coordination (PT, psychology, sexual medicine)","Individualized treatment plans"],"after":["Longitudinal follow-up with realistic goals and multidisciplinary support; reassess and adjust; address relationship and psychological dimensions","Reinforce that these are common, legitimate, and treatable conditions","Continuity and coordination of care"],"pearls":["Sexual dysfunction requires personal distress to be a disorder - and it is common, legitimate, and treatable, not something to dismiss","The cotton-swab test localizes provoked vestibulodynia - a specific, treatable form of vulvodynia","Pelvic floor physical therapy is a cornerstone for vulvodynia and pelvic floor dysfunction - not just an afterthought","Look for reversible contributors first - atrophy/GSM, medications (SSRIs, some contraceptives), and dermatoses are common and fixable"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Female Sexual Dysfunction Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vulvodynia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559099/"},{"t":"International Society for the Study of Women's Sexual Health","u":"https://www.isswsh.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-benign-breast","name":"Benign Breast Disease","sec":"obgyn","present":["Common benign breast conditions the gynecologist encounters: FIBROADENOMA (a mobile, rubbery, well-circumscribed mass in young women), simple CYSTS, fibrocystic changes (lumpy, tender, cyclic), mastalgia, and lactational MASTITIS/abscess","NIPPLE DISCHARGE - distinguish physiologic (bilateral, multiductal, non-spontaneous) from PATHOLOGIC (spontaneous, unilateral, single-duct, bloody/serous - raises concern for intraductal papilloma or malignancy)","The essential task is to reassure benign findings while not missing breast cancer"],"dx":["TRIPLE ASSESSMENT for a breast mass: clinical exam + IMAGING (ULTRASOUND first in women <30 and in pregnancy/lactation; MAMMOGRAM +/- ultrasound in women >=30) + tissue sampling (CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY) for anything suspicious or indeterminate","Aspirate symptomatic/complex cysts; evaluate pathologic nipple discharge (imaging +/- ductography/MRI); mastitis/abscess diagnosed clinically (+/- ultrasound for abscess)","Refer suspicious findings to a breast specialist"],"tx":["Fibroadenoma: observation (with confirmed benign biopsy) or excision if large/growing/symptomatic; simple cysts - aspirate if symptomatic; fibrocystic changes/mastalgia - supportive care, well-fitting bra, and reassurance","LACTATIONAL MASTITIS: continue breastfeeding/milk removal, supportive care, and ANTIBIOTICS (cover staph) if not improving; breast ABSCESS - needle aspiration or incision and drainage plus antibiotics (continue breastfeeding)","Pathologic nipple discharge/intraductal papilloma - surgical duct excision as indicated"],"tech":["Ultrasound-guided cyst aspiration and core needle biopsy; incision and drainage or ultrasound-guided aspiration of a breast abscess; duct excision for pathologic discharge/papilloma","Coordinate imaging and biopsy through breast imaging/surgery","Excisional procedures for select benign lesions"],"after":["Ensure imaging-pathology CONCORDANCE and appropriate follow-up; benign biopsy with concordant imaging can be surveilled; discordant results need re-biopsy/excision","Counsel that most benign breast disease does not raise cancer risk (some proliferative lesions with atypia do - risk-stratify and follow)","Support ongoing breastfeeding through mastitis/abscess treatment"],"pearls":["Use triple assessment (exam + imaging + biopsy) for any breast mass - and choose ultrasound first in the young and in pregnancy/lactation","Pathologic nipple discharge is spontaneous, unilateral, single-duct, and bloody/serous - that is the one to work up, not the bilateral multiductal discharge","Do not stop breastfeeding for mastitis or abscess - continued milk removal is part of the treatment","Imaging-pathology concordance decides follow-up - a benign biopsy that does not fit the imaging needs re-evaluation"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Benign Breast Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Benign Breast Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556033/"},{"t":"American College of Radiology BI-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-hysterectomy","name":"Hysterectomy - Indications & Approaches","sec":"obgyn","present":["Surgical removal of the uterus - one of the most common gynecologic operations; indications include symptomatic fibroids, abnormal uterine bleeding, adenomyosis, endometriosis, prolapse, chronic pain, and gynecologic MALIGNANCY (and obstetric emergencies)","Types: TOTAL (uterus + cervix) vs SUPRACERVICAL (cervix retained - requires continued cervical cancer screening); with or without removal of tubes/ovaries","The decision integrates the indication, benignity vs malignancy, patient preference, and surgical candidacy"],"dx":["Confirm the indication and rule out reversible/medical alternatives; appropriate preoperative workup (endometrial sampling for AUB, cervical screening up to date, imaging for masses)","Assess for malignancy (which changes the operation and often mandates gyn-oncology and staging); evaluate surgical risk and anatomy","Counsel thoroughly (irreversible loss of fertility, alternatives, oophorectomy decision)"],"tx":["Choose the least invasive appropriate ROUTE: VAGINAL hysterectomy is preferred when feasible (best outcomes/recovery), then laparoscopic/robotic (minimally invasive), with ABDOMINAL (open) reserved for large uteri, malignancy needing staging, or extensive adhesions/anatomy","OPHORECTOMY decision individualized: retain ovaries in premenopausal women without indication for removal (preserve hormonal function/reduce mortality), while OPPORTUNISTIC SALPINGECTOMY (removing the tubes) is recommended to reduce future ovarian/tubal cancer risk","Perioperative VTE prophylaxis and antibiotic prophylaxis"],"tech":["Vaginal, laparoscopic/robotic, or abdominal hysterectomy per route; secure the vascular pedicles (uterine, utero-ovarian/infundibulopelvic), identify and PROTECT the URETERS and bladder, and close the vaginal cuff","Opportunistic bilateral salpingectomy; morcellation avoided/contained given occult malignancy concerns","Meticulous ureteral/bladder/bowel identification to prevent injury"],"after":["Post-op recovery per route (faster with minimally invasive/vaginal); early mobilization, VTE prophylaxis, and pain control; monitor for injury (ureteral/bladder - hematuria, urinoma; bowel), infection, and vaginal cuff DEHISCENCE (activity/coitus restrictions)","If oophorectomy in a premenopausal patient, discuss hormone therapy for surgical menopause; retained cervix (supracervical) still needs screening","Follow-up for cuff healing and symptom resolution"],"pearls":["Vaginal hysterectomy has the best outcomes when feasible - choose the least invasive route the anatomy and indication allow","Retain the ovaries in premenopausal women without an indication to remove them, but do opportunistic salpingectomy to cut future ovarian/tubal cancer risk","The ureter is the structure to fear - deliberate identification and protection prevents the classic hysterectomy injury","A supracervical hysterectomy leaves the cervix - the patient still needs cervical cancer screening, and may have ongoing cyclic spotting"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Choosing the Route of Hysterectomy","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hysterectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564366/"},{"t":"AAGL Minimally Invasive Gynecology","u":"https://www.aagl.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-migs","name":"Minimally Invasive Gynecologic Surgery (Laparoscopy & Hysteroscopy)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Minimally invasive approaches are the backbone of modern gynecologic surgery: LAPAROSCOPY (and robotics) for intra-abdominal/pelvic procedures and HYSTEROSCOPY for intrauterine (cavity) procedures - offering faster recovery and less morbidity than open surgery","Laparoscopy: adnexal surgery, ectopic, endometriosis, myomectomy, hysterectomy, sterilization; hysteroscopy: polypectomy, submucosal myomectomy, adhesiolysis, septum resection, and endometrial evaluation","Understanding the unique complications of each is essential to resident practice"],"dx":["Patient selection and preoperative assessment (imaging, anatomy, prior surgery/adhesions, cardiopulmonary tolerance of pneumoperitoneum/positioning)","Match the approach to the pathology (intracavitary lesions -> hysteroscopy; pelvic/adnexal disease -> laparoscopy)","Consent for approach-specific risks and possible conversion to open surgery"],"tx":["LAPAROSCOPY: establish pneumoperitoneum and place trocars (entry - Veress/open/optical - is the highest-risk step for vascular/bowel injury); manage CO2 insufflation physiology (be mindful in cardiopulmonary disease); Trendelenburg positioning","HYSTEROSCOPY: uterine distension with media - be vigilant for FLUID ABSORPTION/OVERLOAD; hypotonic (glycine/sorbitol) media risk HYPONATREMIA and fluid overload, so monitor fluid deficit strictly and use isotonic media with bipolar systems where possible","Enhanced recovery protocols; convert to laparotomy when safety requires"],"tech":["Laparoscopic entry (Veress needle, direct/optical, or open Hasson), trocar placement avoiding the epigastric vessels, and instrument use; robotic platforms for complex dissection","Hysteroscopic resection with monitored distension media and a strict FLUID DEFICIT limit to prevent overload/hyponatremia; electrosurgical safety","Meticulous hemostasis and organ identification"],"after":["Recovery is typically rapid (outpatient/short stay); monitor for entry-related injuries (delayed bowel injury presenting as post-op fever/pain/ileus), port-site issues, and, after hysteroscopy, fluid-overload/hyponatremia and uterine perforation","Shoulder-tip pain from residual CO2 is common and self-limited; activity guidance per procedure","Follow-up for the specific procedure performed"],"pearls":["Laparoscopic ENTRY is the most dangerous moment - major vascular and bowel injuries happen at trocar/Veress placement","Hysteroscopic fluid overload is the silent danger - track the fluid deficit relentlessly, especially with hypotonic media (hyponatremia risk)","A delayed post-op presentation of fever, pain, and ileus after laparoscopy is a bowel injury until proven otherwise","Use isotonic distension media with bipolar hysteroscopic instruments to reduce the hyponatremia risk"],"refs":[{"t":"AAGL Laparoscopic & Hysteroscopic Guidance","u":"https://www.aagl.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hysteroscopy / Laparoscopy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564345/"},{"t":"ACOG Gynecologic Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-sterilization","name":"Female Sterilization & Contraceptive Procedures","sec":"obgyn","present":["The procedural side of contraception (distinct from primary-care contraceptive counseling): permanent STERILIZATION and the placement of long-acting reversible contraception (LARC)","Sterilization options: interval or postpartum tubal surgery, now most often bilateral SALPINGECTOMY (complete tube removal) - which also reduces future OVARIAN/tubal cancer risk - performed laparoscopically or at cesarean/postpartum","LARC (intrauterine devices - hormonal/copper, and the etonogestrel implant) are the most effective REVERSIBLE methods and are placed as office procedures"],"dx":["Counsel thoroughly on PERMANENCE and REGRET risk (higher in younger patients and those making the decision peripartum) - sterilization should be considered irreversible; offer highly effective reversible alternatives (LARC is as effective as sterilization without permanence)","Rule out pregnancy before LARC placement/sterilization; assess candidacy and contraindications","Shared decision-making respecting reproductive autonomy"],"tx":["Sterilization: laparoscopic or postpartum bilateral SALPINGECTOMY (preferred over tubal occlusion for efficacy and ovarian cancer risk reduction); counsel that failure, though rare, carries a higher likelihood of ECTOPIC pregnancy","LARC: IUD (levonorgestrel or copper) and the subdermal implant - place in the office; copper IUD also serves as the most effective emergency contraception","Provide immediate postpartum/post-abortion LARC and sterilization options for access and efficacy"],"tech":["Laparoscopic bilateral salpingectomy (or postpartum minilaparotomy salpingectomy); at the time of cesarean, salpingectomy/tubal interruption","IUD insertion (sound the uterus, place through the cervix) and subdermal implant insertion (subcutaneous, inner upper arm) with sterile technique","Confirm placement (IUD strings/ultrasound as needed)"],"after":["Sterilization is immediately/near-immediately effective (confirm per method); counsel that pregnancy after sterilization, though rare, has elevated ectopic risk - evaluate any missed period with a pregnancy test","LARC follow-up for strings/placement and side effects; manage expulsion/malposition; both IUD types and the implant are highly satisfactory long-term","Reinforce STI protection (these methods do not prevent STIs)"],"pearls":["Bilateral salpingectomy is now the preferred sterilization - it is highly effective and lowers future ovarian/tubal cancer risk","LARC is as effective as sterilization but reversible - always offer it, especially to younger patients where sterilization regret is higher","After sterilization, a pregnancy is more likely to be ectopic - test and evaluate any missed period promptly","The copper IUD is the most effective emergency contraception and provides ongoing contraception - a two-for-one option"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG Sterilization & LARC Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tubal Sterilization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549873/"},{"t":"CDC US Medical Eligibility Criteria (US MEC)","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/reproductivehealth"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cervical-dysplasia","name":"Cervical Dysplasia & Colposcopy","sec":"obgyn","present":["The management of abnormal cervical cancer SCREENING results - the precancer-detection pathway that prevents invasive cervical cancer, driven by persistent high-risk HPV infection","Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN 1 - low-grade/usually regresses; CIN 2/3 - high-grade precancer) and cytologic abnormalities (ASC-US, LSIL, ASC-H, HSIL, AGC) are stratified by RISK, not managed by a single result in isolation","HPV VACCINATION is the primary prevention that is reshaping incidence"],"dx":["RISK-BASED management (ASCCP guidelines) integrates current results WITH history (prior HPV/cytology) to decide between surveillance, colposcopy, or treatment - the same current result can lead to different actions based on prior risk","COLPOSCOPY with directed BIOPSY (and endocervical sampling) evaluates the cervix when the risk threshold is met (application of acetic acid/Lugol; identify acetowhite, mosaicism, punctation, atypical vessels)","Correlate cytology, HPV status, and histology; special populations (pregnancy, immunocompromised) have modified pathways"],"tx":["CIN 1 (low-grade): usually OBSERVATION with surveillance (most regress); treat persistent disease","CIN 2/3 (high-grade precancer): EXCISION - loop electrosurgical excision procedure (LEEP) or cold-knife cone (which also provides a specimen to exclude invasion) - or ablation in select cases; treat per risk and fertility considerations","HPV vaccination (primary prevention) and appropriate post-treatment surveillance"],"tech":["Colposcopy with acetic acid, directed cervical biopsies, and endocervical curettage; EXCISIONAL treatment via LEEP (office, loop excision of the transformation zone) or cold-knife conization (operative, for glandular disease/suspected invasion/margin needs)","Balance adequate excision against cervical length (see cervical insufficiency)","Pathology review of margins/invasion"],"after":["Post-treatment SURVEILLANCE with HPV-based testing (high risk of recurrence/residual disease) per guidelines; excisional treatment increases future PRETERM BIRTH and cervical insufficiency risk - counsel and monitor in subsequent pregnancies","Reinforce ongoing screening (precancer treatment does not end the need for surveillance) and HPV vaccination","Manage margins/glandular disease appropriately (further excision/hysterectomy if indicated)"],"pearls":["Management is RISK-BASED, not result-based - the same Pap/HPV result is handled differently depending on the patient's screening history","CIN 1 usually regresses (observe); CIN 2/3 is precancer and is treated, typically by excision (LEEP/cone)","Excisional treatment raises future preterm birth and cervical insufficiency risk - excise adequately but not excessively, and monitor future pregnancies","HPV vaccination is the primary prevention that is dropping high-grade disease and cancer incidence - promote it"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCCP Risk-Based Management Guidelines","u":"https://www.asccp.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544371/"},{"t":"ACOG Cervical Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-pop","name":"Pelvic Organ Prolapse","sec":"obgyn","present":["Descent of the pelvic organs into/through the vagina from pelvic floor support failure: anterior wall (CYSTOCELE - bladder), posterior wall (RECTOCELE - rectum), apical (uterine or vaginal vault), and enterocele","Symptoms: a vaginal BULGE/pressure (the most specific symptom), and associated urinary, defecatory, or sexual dysfunction; often related to vaginal childbirth, aging/menopause, and raised intra-abdominal pressure","Severity ranges from asymptomatic to organs protruding beyond the introitus"],"dx":["Pelvic examination with the POP-Q (Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification) system to stage the compartments and degree; reproduce the prolapse (standing/Valsalva) to assess maximal descent","Evaluate associated bladder/bowel function (including OCCULT stress incontinence unmasked by reduction of the prolapse) and post-void residual","Correlate symptoms with exam - asymptomatic prolapse generally does not require treatment"],"tx":["Conservative first-line: observation for asymptomatic prolapse, PELVIC FLOOR muscle training, and a vaginal PESSARY (effective, nonsurgical - fitted and maintained) - especially for those who decline/are unfit for surgery or desire future childbearing","SURGICAL repair for symptomatic prolapse: reconstructive (native-tissue vaginal repair, apical suspension - sacrospinous/uterosacral ligament suspension, or abdominal/laparoscopic SACROCOLPOPEXY) or OBLITERATIVE (colpocleisis) for those not desiring vaginal function","Address apical support (the keystone) and any concurrent incontinence; counsel on transvaginal MESH cautions"],"tech":["Pessary fitting and maintenance; reconstructive surgery (apical suspension is key to durable repair) - sacrocolpopexy (mesh-augmented, abdominal/laparoscopic/robotic) or native-tissue vaginal repairs; colpocleisis for obliterative approach","Concomitant anti-incontinence procedure when indicated; ureteral/bladder protection","Individualize by anatomy, activity, and goals"],"after":["Post-op activity restrictions to protect the repair; monitor for RECURRENCE (common over time), mesh complications (exposure/erosion with mesh procedures), and new/unmasked stress incontinence","Pessary users need periodic removal/cleaning and mucosal surveillance (erosion)","Follow-up for symptom relief and function"],"pearls":["The vaginal bulge is the most specific symptom - and asymptomatic prolapse generally needs no treatment","A pessary is an effective, durable nonsurgical option - offer it before or instead of surgery, especially for poor surgical candidates or future childbearing","Apical support is the keystone - a durable repair must address the top of the vagina, not just the anterior/posterior walls","Reducing the prolapse can unmask occult stress incontinence - test for it so the repair does not create a new problem"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / AUGS Pelvic Organ Prolapse Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pelvic Organ Prolapse","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563229/"},{"t":"American Urogynecologic Society","u":"https://www.augs.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-incontinence","name":"Urinary Incontinence (Stress & Urgency)","sec":"obgyn","present":["Involuntary urine leakage; the two main types: STRESS incontinence (leakage with cough/laugh/exertion - from urethral hypermobility or intrinsic sphincter deficiency) and URGENCY incontinence (leakage preceded by a strong urge - overactive bladder), with MIXED being common","Distinguishing the type by history is the essential first step because treatment diverges","Common, underreported, and treatable; overflow and functional incontinence are other patterns to recognize"],"dx":["History (symptom pattern, a voiding DIARY), examination (COUGH STRESS TEST demonstrating leakage with hypermobility), urinalysis (exclude infection), and POST-VOID RESIDUAL (exclude retention/overflow)","Urodynamic testing is reserved for complex, mixed, refractory, or pre-surgical cases - not routine for uncomplicated stress or urgency incontinence","Assess for reversible/contributing factors (medications, diuretics, atrophy, constipation)"],"tx":["STRESS incontinence: first-line PELVIC FLOOR muscle training (Kegels), weight loss, and pessary/continence devices; SURGERY - the midurethral SLING (mesh) is the standard effective procedure (also urethral bulking, Burch colposuspension)","URGENCY/overactive bladder: BEHAVIORAL therapy (bladder training, fluid management) first, then medications (antimuscarinics - oxybutynin, or the beta-3 agonist mirabegron), and third-line onabotulinumtoxinA bladder injection, percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation (PTNS), or sacral neuromodulation","Treat contributors (atrophy with vaginal estrogen); mixed incontinence - target the predominant/most bothersome component"],"tech":["Midurethral sling placement (retropubic or transobturator) or urethral bulking for stress incontinence; intravesical onabotulinumtoxinA injection, sacral neuromodulation implant, or PTNS for refractory urgency","Pessary/continence device fitting; cystoscopy as needed","Individualize the procedure to the type and severity"],"after":["Post-sling monitoring for voiding dysfunction/retention, mesh exposure, and persistent/recurrent leakage; Botox patients need PVR monitoring (retention risk) and may need repeat injections","Reinforce behavioral measures and follow response; manage side effects (antimuscarinic anticholinergic burden - caution in older adults, prefer mirabegron)","Follow-up for symptom control and quality of life"],"pearls":["Separate stress from urgency incontinence by history first - the whole treatment pathway diverges from there","Pelvic floor training and behavioral therapy are first-line for both types - start conservative before slings or drugs","The midurethral sling is the standard effective surgery for stress incontinence; Botox, PTNS, and sacral neuromodulation are the escalation for refractory urgency","Watch anticholinergic burden in older adults with overactive-bladder drugs - mirabegron is often the safer choice"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / AUGS Urinary Incontinence Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Incontinence","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559095/"},{"t":"American Urogynecologic Society","u":"https://www.augs.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-cervical-cancer","name":"Cervical Cancer","sec":"obgyn","present":["A largely PREVENTABLE cancer caused by persistent high-risk HPV infection (HPV 16/18 most common); screening and HPV vaccination have dramatically reduced incidence where implemented","Presentation: often asymptomatic/screen-detected early; symptomatic disease causes POSTCOITAL or abnormal vaginal BLEEDING, discharge, and (advanced) pelvic pain, urinary/bowel symptoms, or leg swelling","Predominantly squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma"],"dx":["Diagnosis by cervical BIOPSY (of a visible lesion, or via colposcopy from abnormal screening); do not rely on a Pap smear to diagnose invasive cancer - biopsy the visible/suspicious lesion","FIGO STAGING incorporates clinical exam and now imaging (MRI for local extent, PET/CT for nodal/distant disease) and pathology; assess tumor size, depth, parametrial/nodal involvement","Multidisciplinary gyn-oncology evaluation"],"tx":["EARLY-stage disease: surgery - RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY with pelvic lymphadenectomy (open approach preferred after the LACC trial showed worse outcomes with minimally invasive radical hysterectomy), or fertility-sparing radical TRACHELECTOMY for selected small tumors; simple hysterectomy/conization for microinvasive disease","LOCALLY ADVANCED disease: concurrent CHEMORADIATION (cisplatin-based chemotherapy with external beam radiation + brachytherapy) is the standard - not primary surgery","Advanced/recurrent: systemic therapy (chemotherapy + bevacizumab, and immunotherapy - checkpoint inhibitors for PD-L1-positive disease); prevention via HPV vaccination and screening"],"tech":["Radical hysterectomy (removal of uterus, parametria, upper vagina) with lymph node assessment (sentinel node mapping in select early disease) - OPEN approach preferred for radical hysterectomy per LACC; radical trachelectomy for fertility preservation","Chemoradiation with brachytherapy for locally advanced disease (brachytherapy is essential to outcomes)","Gyn-oncology surgical staging/management"],"after":["Surveillance for recurrence (exam, symptom review, imaging as indicated); manage treatment sequelae - lymphedema, radiation effects (bowel/bladder/vaginal stenosis), and menopausal symptoms/sexual health","Fertility and pregnancy counseling after trachelectomy (higher preterm/obstetric risk); psychosocial support","Reinforce prevention (HPV vaccination, screening) for the population"],"pearls":["Biopsy the visible cervical lesion - a Pap is a screening test and can be falsely negative with a frank cancer present","After the LACC trial, radical hysterectomy for cervical cancer is done OPEN - minimally invasive radical hysterectomy had worse survival","Locally advanced cervical cancer is treated with chemoradiation plus brachytherapy, NOT primary surgery - and brachytherapy is essential to cure","It is a preventable cancer - HPV vaccination and screening are the real victory; trachelectomy can preserve fertility in carefully selected early disease"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Cervical Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431093/"},{"t":"SGO / FIGO Cervical Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.sgo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-endometrial-cancer","name":"Endometrial Hyperplasia & Endometrial Cancer","sec":"obgyn","present":["The most common gynecologic malignancy in high-income countries; the cardinal symptom is POSTMENOPAUSAL BLEEDING - which is endometrial cancer until proven otherwise - and abnormal/intermenstrual bleeding in premenopausal at-risk patients","Driven largely by UNOPPOSED ESTROGEN: obesity, chronic anovulation/PCOS, nulliparity, early menarche/late menopause, tamoxifen, and estrogen-only therapy; LYNCH syndrome is the key hereditary cause","Endometrial hyperplasia is the precursor - hyperplasia WITHOUT atypia vs ATYPICAL hyperplasia / endometrial intraepithelial neoplasia (EIN), which has a high rate of concurrent/progression to carcinoma"],"dx":["ENDOMETRIAL SAMPLING (office biopsy) is the diagnostic test for postmenopausal or at-risk abnormal bleeding; transvaginal ultrasound endometrial thickness (a thin stripe <=4 mm in postmenopausal bleeding is reassuring) helps triage, but persistent bleeding warrants sampling/hysteroscopy","Histology distinguishes hyperplasia (with/without atypia) from carcinoma and its type - type I (endometrioid, estrogen-driven, better prognosis) vs type II (serous/clear cell, more aggressive)","Surgical FIGO staging; screen for Lynch syndrome (universal tumor testing/mismatch repair)"],"tx":["Hyperplasia WITHOUT atypia: progestin therapy (oral or levonorgestrel IUD) and address the estrogen source (weight, anovulation); atypical hyperplasia/EIN: HYSTERECTOMY (high concurrent cancer risk), or high-dose progestin (LNG-IUS) for fertility preservation with close surveillance","Endometrial CANCER: surgical staging - total HYSTERECTOMY + BILATERAL SALPINGO-OOPHORECTOMY with lymph node assessment (sentinel node mapping); adjuvant radiation/chemotherapy by stage/risk","Fertility-sparing progestin therapy for select early, low-grade cancer in young patients desiring pregnancy (with strict surveillance and eventual hysterectomy)"],"tech":["Minimally invasive hysterectomy + BSO with SENTINEL LYMPH NODE mapping is standard for staging (unlike cervical cancer, MIS is appropriate here); omental/peritoneal assessment for high-grade/serous histology","Office endometrial biopsy or hysteroscopy with D&C for diagnosis","Adjuvant therapy planning with gyn-oncology"],"after":["Surveillance for recurrence (symptom-directed, exam); manage surgical menopause (hormone therapy generally avoided after estrogen-driven cancer - individualize) and treatment sequelae","Lynch syndrome implications for the patient and family (colorectal and other cancer screening, genetic counseling)","Address obesity and metabolic risk (drives both recurrence and comorbidity)"],"pearls":["Postmenopausal bleeding is endometrial cancer until proven otherwise - sample the endometrium, do not just reassure","Atypical hyperplasia (EIN) carries a high rate of concurrent carcinoma - it is usually a hysterectomy, not just progestins, unless preserving fertility with close surveillance","Unlike cervical cancer, minimally invasive hysterectomy with sentinel node mapping is standard for endometrial cancer staging","Universal Lynch screening on endometrial tumors catches a hereditary syndrome with major implications for the patient and family"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Uterine Neoplasms Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endometrial Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525981/"},{"t":"SGO Endometrial Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.sgo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ovarian-cancer","name":"Ovarian & Fallopian Tube Cancer","sec":"obgyn","present":["The most LETHAL gynecologic malignancy - largely because it presents LATE with vague symptoms (bloating, early satiety, pelvic/abdominal pain, urinary frequency) and a pelvic/adnexal mass with ascites; there is NO effective screening","High-grade SEROUS carcinoma predominates and largely originates in the FALLOPIAN TUBE (serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma, STIC) - reframing 'ovarian' cancer and motivating opportunistic salpingectomy for prevention","Hereditary drivers: BRCA1/2 and other homologous-recombination genes, and Lynch syndrome; germ-cell and sex-cord stromal tumors occur in younger patients"],"dx":["Evaluate an adnexal mass with ultrasound and tumor markers (CA-125 - useful especially postmenopausally; germ-cell markers AFP/hCG/LDH in the young); imaging (CT) for spread/ascites","Diagnosis and staging are SURGICAL (do not biopsy a resectable suspicious mass percutaneously - upstaging/spillage risk); refer suspicious masses to GYN-ONCOLOGY","GENETIC testing (BRCA/HRD and Lynch) for all epithelial ovarian cancers - it guides treatment (PARP inhibitors) and family screening"],"tx":["Primary treatment is CYTOREDUCTIVE (debulking) SURGERY to no visible residual disease PLUS platinum/taxane CHEMOTHERAPY (carboplatin + paclitaxel); neoadjuvant chemotherapy with interval debulking when upfront optimal cytoreduction is not feasible","MAINTENANCE therapy has transformed outcomes: PARP INHIBITORS (especially for BRCA-mutated/HRD-positive disease) and bevacizumab","Germ-cell/sex-cord tumors: often fertility-sparing surgery +/- chemotherapy (highly curable germ-cell tumors)"],"tech":["Surgical cytoreduction: hysterectomy + BSO, omentectomy, peritoneal debulking, and resection of metastatic disease to achieve minimal residual disease (residual disease is the key prognostic factor)","Risk-reducing bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy for BRCA carriers (prevention); opportunistic salpingectomy at other pelvic surgery","Gyn-oncology surgical and systemic management"],"after":["Surveillance (exam, CA-125 trends, imaging as indicated); most respond initially but RECURRENCE is common - manage as a chronic relapsing disease with further chemotherapy/maintenance","Genetics-driven family cascade testing and risk-reducing surgery for carriers; supportive/palliative care integration","Fertility and hormonal counseling in young patients with fertility-sparing treatment"],"pearls":["There is no effective screening for ovarian cancer - it presents late with vague bloating/satiety, so a new persistent constellation plus a mass and ascites is a red flag","Most high-grade serous cancer starts in the fallopian tube (STIC) - which is why opportunistic salpingectomy is a prevention strategy","Do not percutaneously biopsy a resectable suspicious ovarian mass - it can upstage; refer to gyn-onc for surgical staging","Test every epithelial ovarian cancer for BRCA/HRD - it opens PARP-inhibitor maintenance and protects the family"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Ovarian Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ovarian Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567760/"},{"t":"SGO Ovarian Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.sgo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-vulvar-vaginal-cancer","name":"Vulvar & Vaginal Cancer","sec":"obgyn","present":["Vulvar cancer (mostly SQUAMOUS cell carcinoma) presents as a vulvar LESION, ulcer, plaque, or persistent PRURITUS - often in older women; two pathways: HPV-related (via usual-type VIN, younger) and HPV-independent (arising in LICHEN SCLEROSUS via differentiated VIN, older)","Vaginal cancer is rare (also mostly squamous, HPV-related); clear cell adenocarcinoma has a historical link to in-utero DES exposure","Delayed diagnosis is common because symptoms are attributed to benign dermatoses - biopsy persistent lesions"],"dx":["BIOPSY any suspicious, persistent, or non-resolving vulvar/vaginal lesion (do not treat presumptively for months) - histology confirms and grades; map extent","Assess for inguinofemoral LYMPH NODE involvement (the key prognostic factor in vulvar cancer) clinically and with imaging; stage (FIGO)","Evaluate for precursor lesions (VIN) and coexisting lichen sclerosus"],"tx":["Vulvar cancer: WIDE LOCAL EXCISION (or vulvectomy) with adequate margins PLUS inguinofemoral lymph node assessment - SENTINEL LYMPH NODE biopsy for early, unifocal small tumors (reduces the morbidity of full lymphadenectomy)","Radiation +/- chemotherapy for advanced disease, positive nodes, or to preserve structures; individualized multimodal therapy","Vaginal cancer: often chemoradiation (surgery for select early lesions); treat VIN precursors to prevent progression"],"tech":["Wide local excision/vulvectomy with margin control; SENTINEL LYMPH NODE mapping (inguinofemoral) for eligible early tumors, or inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy; reconstruction as needed","Brachytherapy/external radiation for vaginal cancer","Gyn-oncology multimodal management"],"after":["Monitor wound healing and manage LYMPHEDEMA (a major morbidity after inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy) and lymphocele; surveillance for recurrence (exam-based)","Address sexual function, body image, and psychosocial impact; treat the underlying lichen sclerosus and surveil for new lesions","Ongoing dermatologic/gynecologic follow-up"],"pearls":["Biopsy the persistent vulvar lesion or itch - attributing it to a benign dermatosis for months is how vulvar cancer is missed","Inguinofemoral nodal status is the dominant prognostic factor in vulvar cancer - and sentinel node biopsy spares many the morbidity of full lymphadenectomy","Two roads to vulvar cancer: HPV (younger, usual VIN) and lichen sclerosus (older, differentiated VIN) - surveil lichen sclerosus for change","Lymphedema after groin dissection is a major, lasting morbidity - sentinel node mapping and careful technique matter"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Vulvar Cancer Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vulvar Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567798/"},{"t":"SGO Vulvar/Vaginal Cancer Resources","u":"https://www.sgo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-gtn","name":"Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia","sec":"obgyn","present":["The MALIGNANT end of the gestational trophoblastic spectrum: invasive mole, CHORIOCARCINOMA, placental-site trophoblastic tumor (PSTT), and epithelioid trophoblastic tumor - arising after a molar pregnancy, miscarriage, ectopic, or a normal term pregnancy","Suspected by a RISING or PLATEAUING hCG after evacuation of a mole, or new symptoms (abnormal bleeding, or metastatic disease - lung nodules with hemoptysis, vaginal, brain, liver) after any pregnancy","Highly CURABLE even when metastatic - among the most chemosensitive of cancers"],"dx":["Diagnosis is largely by the hCG pattern (FIGO criteria for post-molar GTN: plateau, rise, or persistence of hCG) rather than biopsy (biopsy of a vascular lesion risks hemorrhage); a NEW pregnancy must be excluded as the cause of rising hCG","Stage and RISK-SCORE with the FIGO/WHO system (age, antecedent pregnancy, hCG level, tumor size, metastatic sites/number, prior chemotherapy) to classify LOW vs HIGH risk - which determines single- vs multi-agent chemotherapy","Imaging for metastases (chest, and brain/liver if indicated); PSTT/ETT behave differently (less chemosensitive, more surgical)"],"tx":["LOW-RISK GTN: SINGLE-AGENT chemotherapy (methotrexate or actinomycin-D) - high cure rates","HIGH-RISK GTN: MULTI-AGENT chemotherapy (EMA-CO regimen) with excellent survival; treat metastatic disease accordingly","PSTT/ETT: primarily SURGICAL (hysterectomy) as they are relatively chemoresistant; hCG surveillance to remission"],"tech":["Chemotherapy is the mainstay (single- or multi-agent by risk); hysterectomy for PSTT/ETT or to control hemorrhage/chemoresistant local disease","Manage hemorrhage from vascular metastatic lesions (embolization) as needed","Gyn-oncology management with hCG-guided therapy"],"after":["hCG SURVEILLANCE to confirmed remission and for a defined period afterward, with reliable CONTRACEPTION throughout (a new pregnancy would confound hCG monitoring)","Counsel that future pregnancies are usually normal, with early ultrasound and post-pregnancy hCG check; excellent long-term prognosis","Psychosocial support"],"pearls":["A rising or plateauing hCG after evacuation (or new metastatic symptoms after any pregnancy) is GTN - and it is diagnosed by the hCG pattern, not a risky biopsy","GTN is one of the most curable cancers - FIGO/WHO risk scoring sorts patients into single-agent (low-risk) vs multi-agent EMA-CO (high-risk) therapy","Placental-site and epithelioid trophoblastic tumors are the chemoresistant exceptions - they are treated surgically","Exclude a new pregnancy before attributing a rising hCG to GTN, and keep patients on reliable contraception during hCG surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia Guidelines","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499907/"},{"t":"FIGO GTN Staging & Scoring","u":"https://www.figo.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-infertility","name":"Infertility Evaluation & Assisted Reproductive Technology","sec":"obgyn","present":["Infertility: failure to conceive after 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse (or 6 months if the patient is >=35, or immediately with known risk factors) - evaluate BOTH partners","Major categories: OVULATORY dysfunction (PCOS, hypothalamic, POI), TUBAL/pelvic factor (prior PID, endometriosis), UTERINE/cervical factor, MALE factor (a major contributor - always test), and unexplained","Female age-related decline in oocyte quantity/quality is the dominant prognostic factor - counsel on the time-sensitivity"],"dx":["Female workup: confirm OVULATION (history, midluteal progesterone), assess OVARIAN RESERVE (AMH, antral follicle count - predicts response, not necessarily natural fertility), and evaluate TUBAL patency and the uterine cavity (HYSTEROSALPINGOGRAM or sonohysterography)","Male workup: SEMEN ANALYSIS early (simple, high-yield, and male factor is common)","Targeted testing (TSH, prolactin, and for PCOS/hyperandrogenism) and evaluation for endometriosis"],"tx":["Treat the identified cause: OVULATION INDUCTION with LETROZOLE (first-line, especially in PCOS - superior to clomiphene for live birth in PCOS) or clomiphene +/- intrauterine insemination (IUI); gonadotropins for select cases","IN VITRO FERTILIZATION (IVF) for tubal factor, severe male factor (with ICSI), diminished reserve/age, endometriosis, and failed simpler treatments; surgical correction of tubal/uterine factors where appropriate","Lifestyle optimization and counseling on success rates by age/diagnosis"],"tech":["Hysterosalpingogram for tubal patency; IUI (timed intrauterine insemination); IVF cycle - controlled ovarian stimulation, transvaginal ultrasound-guided OOCYTE RETRIEVAL, fertilization (+/- ICSI), and EMBRYO TRANSFER; preimplantation genetic testing when indicated","Surgical correction (hysteroscopic septum/polyp/adhesion, laparoscopic endometriosis)","Monitor stimulation to prevent OHSS (see that entry)"],"after":["Early pregnancy monitoring after ART (higher ectopic and MULTIPLE-gestation risk - single-embryo transfer reduces this); watch for OHSS after stimulation","Emotional support (infertility and its treatment are stressful) and counseling on cumulative success and next steps","Address the age/time factor and future planning"],"pearls":["Evaluate both partners and get a semen analysis early - male factor is common and the test is simple","Letrozole is first-line ovulation induction, and it beats clomiphene for live birth in PCOS","Ovarian reserve testing (AMH/AFC) predicts IVF response - it is not a good test of natural fertility or a reason to panic in a young ovulatory patient","Age is the dominant variable - counsel on time-sensitivity and use single-embryo transfer to avoid high-risk multiples"],"refs":[{"t":"ASRM Infertility & ART Guidelines","u":"https://www.asrm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Infertility","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556033/"},{"t":"ACOG Infertility Resources","u":"https://www.acog.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-ohss","name":"Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome","sec":"obgyn","present":["An IATROGENIC complication of ovarian stimulation for assisted reproduction: an exaggerated response to gonadotropins with ovarian enlargement and a systemic capillary-leak/fluid shift","Ranges from mild (bloating, discomfort) to SEVERE/critical: tense ASCITES, pleural effusions, HEMOCONCENTRATION, oliguria, electrolyte derangement, THROMBOEMBOLISM, and (rarely) ARDS or renal failure","Triggered particularly by the hCG trigger and by pregnancy (endogenous hCG); high-risk features: PCOS, young age, high antral follicle count/AMH, and a large number of follicles/high estradiol - EARLY (post-trigger) vs LATE (pregnancy-related) OHSS"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis in the context of recent ovarian stimulation: abdominal distension/pain, enlarged ovaries on ultrasound, ascites/effusions, and labs showing HEMOCONCENTRATION (elevated hematocrit), leukocytosis, electrolyte abnormalities (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia), and renal/hepatic changes","Grade severity (mild/moderate/severe/critical) to guide setting of care; assess for thrombosis and respiratory compromise","Exclude other causes of an acute abdomen (ovarian torsion, ectopic, hemorrhage)"],"tx":["PREVENTION is key: use GnRH-ANTAGONIST protocols with a GnRH-AGONIST TRIGGER (instead of hCG) in high responders, a FREEZE-ALL strategy (defer embryo transfer to avoid pregnancy-driven late OHSS), cabergoline, and individualized dosing","Mild-moderate: supportive outpatient care (oral hydration, analgesia, activity/monitoring) with clear escalation criteria","SEVERE/critical: hospitalize - judicious IV fluids/albumin, VTE PROPHYLAXIS (real thrombosis risk), paracentesis for tense ascites/respiratory compromise, and correction of derangements"],"tech":["Therapeutic PARACENTESIS (ultrasound-guided) for tense, symptomatic ascites or respiratory compromise; thoracentesis for large effusions","Preventive laboratory/ultrasound monitoring during stimulation and trigger-strategy selection","Critical care support for the rare severe cases"],"after":["Monitor to resolution (OHSS is self-limited but can worsen if pregnancy occurs - late OHSS); continue VTE prophylaxis given the elevated thrombosis risk","Counsel on the freeze-all/embryo-transfer plan and future cycle modifications to prevent recurrence","Follow labs and symptoms until normalized"],"pearls":["OHSS is iatrogenic and largely PREVENTABLE - antagonist protocols with an agonist trigger and freeze-all strategies protect high responders (PCOS, high AMH)","Hemoconcentration and thrombosis are the dangers - give VTE prophylaxis and watch the hematocrit, not just the ascites","Pregnancy (endogenous hCG) drives late OHSS - a freeze-all cycle sidesteps it","Tense ascites with respiratory compromise is drained (paracentesis) - and fluids are given judiciously to avoid worsening third-spacing"],"refs":[{"t":"ASRM Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://www.asrm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519032/"},{"t":"ESHRE OHSS Resources","u":"https://www.eshre.eu"}]},{"id":"obgyn-fertility-preservation","name":"Fertility Preservation","sec":"obgyn","present":["Preserving reproductive potential before GONADOTOXIC therapy (chemotherapy - especially alkylating agents, pelvic radiation) or gonadectomy - a time-sensitive ONCOFERTILITY need that requires URGENT referral before treatment starts","Also used for medical conditions threatening ovarian function (e.g., endometriosis surgery, Turner syndrome, autoimmune/genetic risks) and for anticipated AGE-related fertility decline (planned/elective)","The window is narrow - counseling and referral must happen before cancer treatment begins"],"dx":["Assess the patient's diagnosis, treatment timeline (how much time before gonadotoxic therapy), age/ovarian reserve, and pubertal status; multidisciplinary coordination with oncology to avoid delaying cancer treatment","Counsel on the gonadotoxicity of the planned regimen and realistic success rates of preservation options","Rapid REI referral is the key action"],"tx":["Established options for females: OOCYTE or EMBRYO CRYOPRESERVATION (requires ~2 weeks of ovarian stimulation - random-start protocols allow quick initiation), and OVARIAN TISSUE CRYOPRESERVATION with later reimplantation (no longer experimental - the only option for prepubertal girls and when there is no time to stimulate)","Adjuncts/other: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION (oophoropexy) out of the radiation field before pelvic RT, GnRH agonists during chemotherapy (adjunctive ovarian protection - imperfect), and fertility-sparing surgery for select gynecologic cancers","Prioritize the option that fits the available time and does not delay life-saving treatment"],"tech":["Controlled ovarian stimulation with transvaginal OOCYTE RETRIEVAL and vitrification of oocytes/embryos; laparoscopic OVARIAN TISSUE harvest and cryopreservation; ovarian transposition before pelvic radiation","Random-start stimulation to shorten the timeline; coordinate with the oncology schedule","Later thaw/reimplantation or ART use of stored gametes/tissue"],"after":["Long-term storage and coordination for future use (ART with cryopreserved oocytes/embryos, or reimplantation of ovarian tissue - which can also restore endocrine function)","Counsel on realistic future success, and on monitoring for ovarian function recovery after treatment; psychological support","Reassess reproductive goals after cancer treatment/recovery"],"pearls":["Fertility preservation is TIME-SENSITIVE - refer to REI before gonadotoxic therapy starts, not after; random-start stimulation buys speed","Ovarian tissue cryopreservation is now standard (not experimental) and is the option for prepubertal girls and when there is no time to stimulate","Ovarian transposition moves the ovaries out of a pelvic radiation field - a simple, effective adjunct","Coordinate with oncology so preservation does not delay life-saving cancer treatment - the cancer comes first, but the conversation must happen up front"],"refs":[{"t":"ASCO / ASRM Fertility Preservation Guidance","u":"https://www.asrm.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fertility Preservation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564376/"},{"t":"Oncofertility Consortium","u":"https://www.oncofertility.msu.edu"}]},{"id":"obgyn-amenorrhea","name":"Amenorrhea & Ovulatory Dysfunction","sec":"obgyn","present":["PRIMARY amenorrhea: no menarche by age 15 with normal secondary sexual characteristics, or by 13 with none; SECONDARY amenorrhea: absence of menses for >=3 months (previously regular) or >=6 months (previously irregular)","Ovulatory dysfunction (oligo-/anovulation) causes irregular or absent menses and is a common infertility cause; organize the differential by COMPARTMENT: outflow tract/uterus, ovary, pituitary, and hypothalamus","The FIRST step is always to exclude PREGNANCY"],"dx":["After a negative pregnancy test: check TSH, PROLACTIN (prolactinoma/medications), FSH/LH and estradiol, and androgens if hyperandrogenic; a progestin withdrawal bleed and history/exam localize the problem","Compartmental causes: hypothalamic (FUNCTIONAL hypothalamic amenorrhea from stress/weight loss/excessive exercise/eating disorders - low FSH/LH), pituitary (prolactinoma - high prolactin), ovarian (PCOS - the most common ovulatory disorder; PRIMARY OVARIAN INSUFFICIENCY - high FSH), and outflow (Asherman syndrome, imperforate hymen, Müllerian agenesis - primary amenorrhea with normal secondary characteristics)","Imaging (pelvic ultrasound/MRI for anatomy, brain MRI for pituitary), karyotype for primary amenorrhea as indicated"],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause: functional hypothalamic amenorrhea - restore energy balance/reduce stress (nutrition, decreased excessive exercise, address eating disorders) and support BONE HEALTH; prolactinoma - dopamine agonists; PCOS - lifestyle, hormonal regulation, and ovulation induction (letrozole) if pregnancy desired","Restore/replace estrogen to protect BONE and cardiovascular health when hypoestrogenic (e.g., POI, prolonged hypothalamic amenorrhea)","Outflow/anatomic causes: surgical correction (hysteroscopic lysis for Asherman, hymenectomy, Müllerian anomaly management)"],"tech":["Hysteroscopic adhesiolysis for Asherman syndrome; surgical management of outflow obstruction/Müllerian anomalies; imaging-guided workup","Ovulation induction for those desiring pregnancy (see infertility entry)","Endocrine/anatomic evaluation as indicated"],"after":["Address the downstream consequences of hypoestrogenism (bone density, cardiovascular) and fertility goals; longitudinal follow-up of the underlying condition","For functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, monitor recovery of menses with restored energy availability; multidisciplinary care for eating disorders","Fertility and bone-health counseling"],"pearls":["Always rule out pregnancy first - it is the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea","Organize the workup by compartment (outflow, ovary, pituitary, hypothalamus) with TSH, prolactin, FSH/LH, and estradiol - it makes a broad differential tractable","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea (low FSH/LH from energy deficit/stress) needs restored energy balance and bone protection, not just a pill to induce bleeding","Primary amenorrhea with normal breasts but absent uterus points to Müllerian agenesis or androgen insensitivity - karyotype and imaging sort them out"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / ASRM Amenorrhea Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amenorrhea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482168/"},{"t":"Endocrine Society Resources","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-poi","name":"Primary Ovarian Insufficiency & Disorders of Pubertal Development","sec":"obgyn","present":["Primary ovarian insufficiency (POI): loss of normal ovarian function BEFORE age 40 - amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea with HYPOESTROGENISM and elevated gonadotropins (menopausal-range FSH), often with vasomotor symptoms and infertility (distinct from, and earlier than, natural menopause)","Causes: genetic (TURNER syndrome, FMR1 premutation - fragile X-associated), autoimmune, iatrogenic (chemo/radiation/surgery), and idiopathic","Disorders of pubertal development: DELAYED puberty (no breast development by ~13) vs PRECOCIOUS puberty (before ~8), and disorders of sex development (DSD) - each needing structured evaluation"],"dx":["POI: confirm with elevated FSH (menopausal range on repeat) and low estradiol in a patient <40 with menstrual disturbance; then find the cause - KARYOTYPE (Turner/mosaicism), FMR1 premutation testing, adrenal/thyroid autoantibodies, and assess for associated autoimmune disease","Delayed puberty: distinguish hypogonadotropic (low FSH/LH - constitutional delay, hypothalamic/pituitary) from hypergonadotropic (high FSH/LH - gonadal failure, e.g., Turner); bone age, imaging, and karyotype","Precocious puberty: central (GnRH-dependent) vs peripheral; DSD requires multidisciplinary evaluation"],"tx":["POI: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY (estrogen + progestin) until the average age of natural menopause (~51) to protect BONE and CARDIOVASCULAR health and relieve symptoms - this is replacement of a deficiency, a different risk/benefit calculus than postmenopausal HRT","Fertility: spontaneous pregnancy is uncommon but possible; DONOR OOCYTE IVF is the effective path for those desiring pregnancy; screen for associated conditions (Turner - cardiac/aortic, thyroid; FMR1 - genetic counseling)","Pubertal disorders: treat the cause - hormone induction for hypogonadism, GnRH agonists to halt central precocious puberty, and multidisciplinary DSD care"],"tech":["Largely medical/hormonal management; karyotype and genetic testing; multidisciplinary coordination (endocrinology, genetics, cardiology for Turner, psychology)","Assisted reproduction (donor oocyte) for fertility; pubertal induction protocols","Imaging as indicated"],"after":["Long-term HRT and surveillance of bone density and cardiovascular/associated conditions (e.g., Turner aortic monitoring, thyroid/adrenal autoimmunity); PSYCHOLOGICAL support (POI at a young age is a significant emotional diagnosis)","Fertility counseling and family planning (donor oocyte); genetic counseling (FMR1 premutation family implications)","Ongoing multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["POI is not early menopause to be left untreated - give hormone replacement until ~51 to protect bone and cardiovascular health; the risk/benefit differs from postmenopausal HRT","Work up the cause: karyotype (Turner) and FMR1 premutation (fragile X) testing plus autoimmune screening are core","Fertility in POI is usually via donor oocyte, though rare spontaneous pregnancy occurs - counsel accordingly","Separate hypogonadotropic (low FSH/LH) from hypergonadotropic (high FSH/LH) causes in delayed puberty - it splits constitutional delay from gonadal failure"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / ASRM Primary Ovarian Insufficiency Guidance","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Primary Ovarian Insufficiency","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499830/"},{"t":"Endocrine Society Pubertal Disorders","u":"https://www.endocrine.org"}]},{"id":"obgyn-peds-adolescent-gyn","name":"Pediatric & Adolescent Gynecology","sec":"obgyn","present":["Gynecologic care tailored to children and adolescents: prepubertal VULVOVAGINITIS (the most common pediatric gynecologic complaint - usually nonspecific, hygiene-related), LABIAL ADHESIONS, and vulvar complaints","Adolescent MENSTRUAL disorders: heavy menstrual bleeding (often anovulatory in early gynecologic years, but screen for BLEEDING DISORDERS - von Willebrand disease - especially with heavy menses from menarche), dysmenorrhea, and irregular cycles; the menstrual cycle as a 'vital sign'","OBSTRUCTIVE Müllerian anomalies (imperforate hymen, transverse vaginal septum) presenting with primary amenorrhea + cyclic pain + a pelvic mass (hematocolpos), and MRKH (Müllerian agenesis)"],"dx":["AGE-APPROPRIATE, minimally invasive evaluation: external/visual exam (no speculum in prepubertal girls; exam under anesthesia if needed), and history from child and caregiver with attention to development and safeguarding","Prepubertal vulvovaginitis: assess hygiene, foreign body (persistent bloody/foul discharge), and specific infections; adolescent heavy bleeding: CBC/ferritin and a bleeding-disorder workup (von Willebrand panel) when heavy from menarche","Obstructive anomalies: imaging (ultrasound/MRI) for hematocolpos/Müllerian anatomy; be alert to signs of sexual abuse and mandated reporting"],"tx":["Prepubertal vulvovaginitis: hygiene measures and reassurance (most is nonspecific); treat specific infections and remove foreign bodies; labial adhesions - topical estrogen (or observation if asymptomatic)","Adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding: hormonal management (combined hormonal contraceptives, progestins) and tranexamic acid, treat iron deficiency, and manage any identified bleeding disorder; dysmenorrhea - NSAIDs and hormonal therapy","Obstructive anomalies: SURGICAL correction (hymenectomy for imperforate hymen, septum resection); MRKH - multidisciplinary care (vaginal dilation/creation, psychological support, fertility counseling)"],"tech":["Examination under anesthesia when indicated; hymenectomy/incision for imperforate hymen; transverse vaginal septum resection; vaginoscopy for a suspected foreign body","Ultrasound/MRI for Müllerian anatomy; minimally invasive, developmentally sensitive approaches","Multidisciplinary surgical planning for complex anomalies"],"after":["Follow-up for symptom resolution and adherence; psychosocial support (menstrual disorders and anomalies affect adolescent well-being and body image); ensure CONFIDENTIALITY within legal frameworks while involving caregivers appropriately","Bleeding-disorder and anemia follow-up; long-term care for Müllerian anomalies (fertility, sexual function)","Reinforce vaccination (HPV), contraception counseling for sexually active adolescents, and safeguarding"],"pearls":["Prepubertal vulvovaginitis is usually nonspecific and hygiene-related - but persistent bloody/foul discharge suggests a foreign body","Heavy menstrual bleeding from menarche should trigger a bleeding-disorder workup - von Willebrand disease commonly presents this way in adolescents","Primary amenorrhea with cyclic pelvic pain and a mass is an obstructive anomaly (imperforate hymen/transverse septum with hematocolpos) - image and treat surgically","Adapt the exam to the child - no speculum in prepubertal girls, exam under anesthesia when needed, and always keep safeguarding and confidentiality in mind"],"refs":[{"t":"ACOG / NASPAG Pediatric & Adolescent Gynecology","u":"https://www.acog.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric and Adolescent Gynecology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559113/"},{"t":"North American Society for Pediatric and Adolescent Gynecology","u":"https://www.naspag.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-acute-angle-closure","name":"Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma","sec":"ophtho","present":["An ophthalmic EMERGENCY: acute obstruction of aqueous outflow at the trabecular meshwork by the iris, causing a rapid, severe IOP spike","Classic presentation: sudden severe eye PAIN, blurred vision with HALOS around lights, a red eye, a fixed MID-DILATED pupil, a hazy/edematous cornea, and often headache, nausea, and VOMITING (can mimic an acute abdomen or migraine)","Risk factors: hyperopia (short eye, shallow anterior chamber), older age, female, East Asian ancestry, and precipitants (dim light, mydriatic/anticholinergic drugs, stress)"],"dx":["Markedly ELEVATED intraocular pressure (often 40-80 mmHg) by tonometry; slit-lamp shows a shallow anterior chamber, corneal edema, and a mid-dilated poorly reactive pupil","GONIOSCOPY confirms a closed angle (and evaluate the fellow eye, which is anatomically at risk); examine for the underlying mechanism (pupillary block most common)","Distinguish from other causes of acute red painful eye (uveitis, keratitis, endophthalmitis)"],"tx":["URGENTLY lower the IOP: topical agents (timolol, apraclonidine/brimonidine, and once the iris responds, PILOCARPINE to break pupillary block) plus systemic ACETAZOLAMIDE and, if refractory, IV MANNITOL; topical steroid to reduce inflammation","Corneal edema may need clearing (glycerin) to enable laser; reassess IOP frequently","DEFINITIVE treatment is laser PERIPHERAL IRIDOTOMY once the eye is quiet - and PROPHYLACTIC iridotomy of the fellow (at-risk) eye"],"tech":["Laser peripheral IRIDOTOMY (LPI) creates an alternate aqueous pathway, relieving pupillary block; performed in both the affected and the fellow eye","Lens extraction (cataract/clear lens) is increasingly used to deepen the chamber and definitively open the angle in appropriate cases","Corneal indentation/paracentesis in refractory acute cases to abort the attack"],"after":["Monitor IOP and the angle after iridotomy; some eyes develop chronic angle closure/glaucoma and need ongoing therapy","Ensure the FELLOW eye receives prophylactic LPI (it shares the anatomy and is at high risk)","Counsel on precipitating medications (avoid anticholinergics/sympathomimetics that dilate) and warning symptoms"],"pearls":["A fixed MID-DILATED pupil with a rock-hard painful red eye and halos is acute angle closure until proven otherwise - check the IOP immediately","Nausea and vomiting can dominate and send the patient to the ED for an 'abdomen' or 'migraine' - always ask about the eye","Definitive cure is laser iridotomy - and the fellow eye needs prophylactic iridotomy because it has the same at-risk anatomy","Avoid dilating and anticholinergic drugs in anatomically narrow angles - they can precipitate an attack"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Primary Angle Closure Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430857/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Angle Closure","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-crao","name":"Central Retinal Artery Occlusion","sec":"ophtho","present":["SUDDEN, PAINLESS, severe monocular vision loss - the ocular equivalent of a STROKE (retinal infarction from central retinal artery obstruction)","Exam: a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), retinal WHITENING (edema) with a CHERRY-RED SPOT at the fovea, arteriolar narrowing, and box-carring of blood; a branch occlusion causes a sectoral field defect","Usually embolic (carotid atherosclerosis, cardiac) or thrombotic; GIANT CELL ARTERITIS must be excluded in older patients"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the fundus picture and RAPD; OCT shows inner retinal edema; fluorescein angiography confirms delayed filling","URGENTLY exclude giant cell arteritis (ESR/CRP, symptoms) in patients >50; the fellow eye and life are at risk","Treat as an acute ischemic stroke event - emergent STROKE workup (carotid imaging, cardiac evaluation) because of high concurrent/subsequent stroke risk"],"tx":["Time-critical and, unfortunately, with limited proven ocular therapy: acute maneuvers (ocular massage, IOP lowering - topical/acetazolamide, anterior chamber PARACENTESIS) are often attempted to dislodge the embolus in the hyperacute window","Consider thrombolysis only within a hyperacute window at a stroke center (evidence evolving/controversial) - the priority is treating it as a stroke","If GCA-related: immediate high-dose corticosteroids to protect the fellow eye"],"tech":["Ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, and IOP-lowering maneuvers in the acute setting","Coordinate emergent neurology/stroke pathway (imaging, thrombolysis consideration)","Panretinal photocoagulation/anti-VEGF later if neovascularization develops"],"after":["Complete the stroke SECONDARY PREVENTION workup and management (carotid, cardiac, vascular risk factors) - CRAO is a warning of systemic vascular disease","Monitor for ocular neovascularization (a later complication) and treat; visual prognosis is often poor","Manage GCA with steroid taper and biopsy if applicable"],"pearls":["CRAO is a stroke - the eye may not recover, but the workup and secondary prevention can save the brain and the fellow eye","A cherry-red spot with retinal whitening and an RAPD in sudden painless vision loss is CRAO","Always exclude giant cell arteritis in the older patient - it is the treatable, fellow-eye-threatening cause","Ocular therapies are low-yield; the highest-value action is treating it as an acute cerebrovascular event"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA & AAO CRAO Statement","u":"https://www.ahajournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Central Retinal Artery Occlusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470354/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: CRAO","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-chemical-burns","name":"Chemical (Alkali/Acid) Ocular Burns","sec":"ophtho","present":["A true ocular EMERGENCY where treatment (irrigation) precedes full assessment; ALKALI burns (lye, ammonia, cement/lime) are worse than acid - they cause LIQUEFACTIVE necrosis and penetrate deeply, whereas ACIDS cause coagulative necrosis that somewhat limits penetration","Presents with pain, redness, tearing, blepharospasm, and reduced vision; severity ranges from mild epithelial injury to LIMBAL ISCHEMIA (blanching - 'a white eye' is ominous) and corneal opacification","Household and industrial exposures; assess mechanism and agent"],"dx":["Do NOT delay for a full exam - IRRIGATE FIRST; then check pH (irrigate until neutral ~7), evert the lids to remove particulate matter, and assess extent","GRADE severity by LIMBAL ISCHEMIA and corneal/conjunctival involvement (Roper-Hall or Dua classification) - the degree of limbal stem-cell loss predicts prognosis","Measure IOP (can be elevated) and assess the epithelial defect with fluorescein"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE, copious IRRIGATION with saline/water (liters, sustained) until the pH normalizes - this is the single most important intervention and time is vision; remove particulate lime/cement","Then: topical antibiotic, cycloplegic, topical corticosteroid (control inflammation early), ascorbate and citrate (support healing/reduce ulceration), and IOP control","Severe burns: amniotic membrane, aggressive lubrication, and specialist management for the ocular surface"],"tech":["Copious irrigation (Morgan lens or IV tubing), lid eversion and sweeping of fornices for particulate matter","Amniotic membrane transplantation for significant surface injury; later limbal stem cell transplantation for stem-cell deficiency","Manage secondary glaucoma and surface reconstruction as needed"],"after":["Monitor for LIMBAL STEM CELL DEFICIENCY (conjunctivalization, persistent epithelial defect), symblepharon, corneal melt/perforation, and secondary glaucoma","Prolonged ocular-surface rehabilitation; the white, ischemic limbus predicts a poor outcome","Serial follow-up and specialist (cornea) care"],"pearls":["Irrigate before you examine - copious immediate irrigation to a neutral pH is the treatment that saves the eye; do not delay for history or imaging","Alkali is worse than acid - it liquefies and penetrates, so cement, lye, and ammonia burns are the dangerous ones","A WHITE eye (limbal ischemia/blanching) is worse than a red one - blanching means the limbal stem cells are dying","Remove particulate lime/cement from the fornices - retained particles keep burning"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Chemical Burn Management","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chemical Ocular Burns","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459331/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Chemical Injury","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-open-globe","name":"Open Globe / Ruptured Globe Injury","sec":"ophtho","present":["A full-thickness wound of the cornea and/or sclera from penetrating/perforating trauma or blunt rupture - a surgical EMERGENCY threatening the eye","Signs: a peaked/teardrop PUPIL, uveal tissue prolapse (dark tissue at the wound), a positive SEIDEL test (aqueous streaming through the wound), shallow or flat anterior chamber, LOW IOP, 360-degree bullous subconjunctival hemorrhage, and reduced vision","Suspect with any high-velocity injury (hammering metal-on-metal - intraocular foreign body), sharp trauma, or significant blunt trauma"],"dx":["High clinical suspicion; do NOT manipulate or apply pressure to the eye; gentle exam only - defer tonometry and any maneuver that could express intraocular contents","Imaging: CT orbits (thin cuts) to identify the rupture and intraocular FOREIGN BODY - AVOID MRI if a metallic foreign body is possible; ultrasound is generally avoided (pressure)","Assess visual acuity/light perception and the mechanism"],"tx":["Protect the eye with a rigid SHIELD (no patch/pressure), keep the patient NPO for surgery, control pain/nausea (avoid Valsalva), give IV broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS and TETANUS prophylaxis, and avoid topical drops/ointments","URGENT surgical exploration and primary repair by ophthalmology; remove intraocular foreign bodies","Endophthalmitis prophylaxis and antiemetics to prevent expulsion of contents"],"tech":["Primary surgical globe repair (corneal/scleral wound closure), reposition or excise prolapsed uvea, and remove intraocular foreign body; secondary vitreoretinal surgery as needed","Intravitreal/systemic antibiotics for endophthalmitis risk","Enucleation only for a non-salvageable eye (and to consider sympathetic ophthalmia risk)"],"after":["Monitor for ENDOPHTHALMITIS (a major risk) and treat aggressively; assess for retinal detachment and other posterior injury","Counsel on SYMPATHETIC OPHTHALMIA (rare bilateral autoimmune uveitis after penetrating injury threatening the fellow eye) - a reason to consider timely repair/enucleation of a blind, disorganized eye","Visual rehabilitation and protective eyewear counseling"],"pearls":["Shield, don't press - a rigid shield, NPO, antibiotics, tetanus, and no drops; any pressure can extrude intraocular contents","A peaked pupil, positive Seidel, and a soft eye after trauma is an open globe - image with CT, and never order MRI if a metallic foreign body is possible","Ask about hammering metal on metal - it is the classic history for an occult intraocular foreign body","Remember sympathetic ophthalmia - a penetrating injury can later threaten the healthy fellow eye"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Open Globe Injury Management","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Globe Rupture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551637/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Open Globe","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-orbital-cellulitis","name":"Orbital Cellulitis (& Preseptal Cellulitis)","sec":"ophtho","present":["The critical distinction is PRESEPTAL (anterior to the orbital septum - eyelid) vs ORBITAL (postseptal) cellulitis; ORBITAL cellulitis is a sight- and life-threatening EMERGENCY","PRESEPTAL: eyelid swelling, erythema, and tenderness WITHOUT proptosis, without pain/restriction of eye movements, and with NORMAL vision and pupils","ORBITAL: the danger signs - PROPTOSIS, PAINFUL and RESTRICTED extraocular movements (ophthalmoplegia), decreased vision, an RAPD, and chemosis; most often from adjacent ETHMOID SINUSITIS (especially in children)"],"dx":["Examine specifically for proptosis, EOM pain/restriction, vision, color vision, and pupils (RAPD) - these separate orbital from preseptal disease","CT of the ORBITS and SINUSES with contrast for suspected orbital cellulitis to identify subperiosteal/orbital ABSCESS and sinus source; blood cultures and labs","Watch for complications: cavernous sinus thrombosis, intracranial extension/abscess, and vision loss"],"tx":["PRESEPTAL: oral antibiotics with close outpatient follow-up (admit young children/toxic/uncertain cases)","ORBITAL cellulitis: ADMIT for IV broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS (cover Staph incl MRSA, Strep, and anaerobes), ENT/ophthalmology co-management, and serial vision checks","SURGICAL drainage of a significant abscess or if vision worsens/no improvement on antibiotics; treat the sinus source"],"tech":["Surgical drainage of subperiosteal/orbital abscess and endoscopic sinus surgery (ENT) for the source","Serial visual acuity, color vision, pupil, and proptosis checks to detect optic nerve compromise","Manage intracranial/cavernous sinus complications with neurosurgery/ID"],"after":["Monitor vision closely during treatment (optic neuropathy from pressure/ischemia); complete the antibiotic course and confirm resolution","Identify and treat the underlying sinusitis; watch for and manage complications","Follow-up imaging for abscess resolution"],"pearls":["Proptosis, painful/restricted eye movements, and vision change mean ORBITAL (postseptal) cellulitis - an emergency; their ABSENCE with just lid swelling is preseptal","Orbital cellulitis usually comes from ethmoid sinusitis, especially in children - image the orbits AND sinuses","Serial vision and pupil checks are the early-warning system for optic nerve compromise - deterioration means drainage","Beware the deadly extensions: cavernous sinus thrombosis and intracranial abscess"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Orbital Cellulitis Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Orbital Cellulitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507901/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Orbital Cellulitis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-endophthalmitis","name":"Endophthalmitis","sec":"ophtho","present":["A vision-threatening intraocular INFECTION (usually bacterial) - an EMERGENCY; contexts: acute POST-CATARACT surgery (most common), post-intravitreal injection, post-glaucoma bleb, post-trauma, and ENDOGENOUS (hematogenous seeding, e.g., Candida, in ill/immunocompromised/IV drug users)","Presents with rapidly worsening PAIN and VISION LOSS, redness, HYPOPYON (layered pus in the anterior chamber), and VITRITIS (cells obscuring the fundus)","Acute postoperative endophthalmitis typically appears within days of surgery/injection"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis in the right context (recent surgery/injection/trauma + pain, hypopyon, vitritis, decreasing vision); B-scan ultrasound assesses the vitreous when the view is poor and looks for retinal detachment","Obtain AQUEOUS and VITREOUS samples for Gram stain and CULTURE - ideally at the time of intravitreal treatment (a 'tap and inject')","Endogenous cases need a systemic infection workup (blood cultures, echocardiography, imaging)"],"tx":["URGENT intravitreal ANTIBIOTICS (vancomycin for gram-positives + ceftazidime or amikacin for gram-negatives) - the mainstay; +/- intravitreal steroids per practice","PARS PLANA VITRECTOMY for severe disease: the Endophthalmitis Vitrectomy Study (EVS) supported immediate vitrectomy for post-cataract endophthalmitis presenting with LIGHT-PERCEPTION-only vision","Endogenous/fungal endophthalmitis: systemic antifungals/antibiotics plus intravitreal therapy; remove the source"],"tech":["Vitreous 'tap and inject' (sample + intravitreal antibiotics); pars plana vitrectomy for severe or vision-at-light-perception cases","Repeat intravitreal injections based on response","Manage the primary source (bleb, wound, systemic infection)"],"after":["Close monitoring and repeat treatment as needed; guarded prognosis, especially with virulent organisms (streptococcal, gram-negative) or delayed treatment","Watch for retinal detachment and other complications; visual rehabilitation","For endogenous cases, treat and follow the systemic infection"],"pearls":["Pain and dropping vision with a hypopyon days after cataract surgery or an intravitreal injection is endophthalmitis - tap and inject urgently","The EVS taught us: immediate vitrectomy benefits post-cataract endophthalmitis that presents at light-perception vision","Intravitreal vancomycin plus ceftazidime is the empiric backbone - do not rely on topical/systemic antibiotics alone","Think endogenous (often Candida) in the septic, immunocompromised, or IV-drug-using patient with eye findings"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Endophthalmitis Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endophthalmitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559079/"},{"t":"Endophthalmitis Vitrectomy Study (EVS)","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-retrobulbar-hemorrhage","name":"Retrobulbar Hemorrhage & Orbital Compartment Syndrome","sec":"ophtho","present":["Bleeding into the retrobulbar space (after trauma, a retrobulbar/peribulbar block, or orbital/sinus surgery) creates an ORBITAL COMPARTMENT SYNDROME - a vision-threatening EMERGENCY","Rising orbital pressure causes PROPTOSIS, a TIGHT/tense orbit, markedly ELEVATED IOP, decreasing VISION, an RAPD, pain, and restricted eye movements - vision loss from optic nerve compression and/or central retinal artery occlusion","Time-critical: irreversible optic nerve/retinal ischemia can occur within an hour"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis - do NOT wait for imaging when vision is threatened: the triad of proptosis, elevated IOP, and decreasing vision with an RAPD after the relevant event","Check IOP (often very high) and vision/pupils; imaging (CT) is confirmatory but should not delay treatment","Recognize the at-risk settings (facial trauma, orbital surgery, retrobulbar anesthesia)"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE surgical decompression: lateral CANTHOTOMY and CANTHOLYSIS (cut the lateral canthal tendon's inferior +/- superior crus) at the bedside to release orbital pressure - do this on clinical grounds, do not wait for imaging or ophthalmology if vision is being lost","Adjunctive IOP-lowering (acetazolamide, mannitol, topical) while arranging definitive care; treat the cause (reverse coagulopathy)","Further orbital decompression if canthotomy is insufficient"],"tech":["Lateral CANTHOTOMY and inferior (+/- superior) CANTHOLYSIS - the sight-saving bedside procedure that decompresses the orbit","Surgical orbital decompression and hematoma evacuation if needed","Manage the underlying bleeding source/coagulopathy"],"after":["Recheck vision, IOP, and pupils immediately after canthotomy to confirm decompression; escalate if not improved","Monitor for optic nerve recovery and manage residual injury; treat the cause","Wound care of the canthotomy site"],"pearls":["Proptosis + high IOP + falling vision after trauma or a retrobulbar block is orbital compartment syndrome - do the lateral canthotomy/cantholysis NOW, before imaging","This is one of the few ophthalmic procedures a non-ophthalmologist may need to perform emergently to save vision","Minutes matter - the optic nerve and retina tolerate the pressure/ischemia for only a short time","After cutting, reassess vision and IOP - if still tight, release the other crus or decompress further"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Orbital Compartment Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retrobulbar Hemorrhage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551628/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Orbital Compartment Syndrome","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-hyphema","name":"Traumatic Hyphema","sec":"ophtho","present":["Blood in the ANTERIOR CHAMBER, usually after blunt ocular trauma (tearing of iris/ciliary body vessels); layers inferiorly and is graded by the height of the blood","Presents with pain, blurred vision, and a visible blood level; a total ('eight-ball') hyphema is severe","The dangers are secondary REBLEEDING (typically days 3-5), elevated IOP, and corneal BLOOD STAINING"],"dx":["Slit-lamp exam to grade the hyphema and measure IOP; rule out an OPEN GLOBE and other trauma (dilated exam when safe, gonioscopy deferred to avoid rebleed)","SICKLE CELL screening (trait/disease) - sickled cells clog the trabecular meshwork and cause dangerous IOP rises even with small hyphemas, and tolerate elevated IOP poorly","Assess for associated injuries (angle recession, lens, retina)"],"tx":["Conservative management: activity restriction/REST with head-of-bed ELEVATION (lets blood settle inferiorly, away from the visual axis and to speed clearance), a rigid eye SHIELD, and a topical cycloplegic and corticosteroid","Control IOP (topical agents; use carbonic anhydrase inhibitors CAUTIOUSLY in sickle cell disease - they can worsen sickling); AVOID antiplatelet/NSAID agents (rebleed risk); aminocaproic/tranexamic acid in selected cases","SURGICAL washout for uncontrolled IOP (to prevent optic nerve damage/staining), corneal blood staining, or a large non-clearing hyphema"],"tech":["Anterior chamber washout/clot evacuation for refractory high IOP or corneal staining","IOP-lowering procedures if needed; manage associated angle/lens/retinal injury","Careful, staged examination to avoid provoking rebleed"],"after":["Watch closely for REBLEEDING (worse, often on days 3-5) and IOP spikes; serial IOP and exams","Long-term surveillance for ANGLE-RECESSION GLAUCOMA (delayed, from the original trauma) and cataract","Counsel on protective eyewear and avoiding strenuous activity/antiplatelet drugs during recovery"],"pearls":["Elevate the head and shield the eye - settling the blood inferiorly protects the visual axis and clears it faster","Screen for sickle cell - even a small hyphema can cause a dangerous IOP spike, and CAIs can worsen sickling","The feared complication is rebleeding around days 3-5 - avoid antiplatelets/NSAIDs and restrict activity","Years later, watch for angle-recession glaucoma from the original blunt injury"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Hyphema Management","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hyphema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507802/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Traumatic Hyphema","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-gca-aion","name":"Giant Cell Arteritis & Arteritic Anterior Ischemic Optic Neuropathy","sec":"ophtho","present":["Giant cell arteritis (GCA) is a medium/large-vessel VASCULITIS in patients >50 that causes ARTERITIC anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AAION) - sudden, often severe, PAINLESS vision loss from optic disc infarction","Systemic clues: new HEADACHE, JAW CLAUDICATION (highly specific), scalp tenderness, temporal artery tenderness/beading, and polymyalgia rheumatica (proximal aching); may have transient vision loss (amaurosis) or diplopia preceding it","An OPHTHALMIC EMERGENCY - the FELLOW eye is at imminent risk without treatment (this content overlaps the neurology secondary-headache entry)"],"dx":["Inflammatory markers: markedly elevated ESR and CRP (and thrombocytosis) support GCA; the fundus shows a PALE, swollen optic disc (pallid disc edema - distinguishing arteritic from non-arteritic AION)","TEMPORAL ARTERY BIOPSY confirms the diagnosis (do not delay steroids for it; the biopsy remains positive for days-weeks); temporal artery ultrasound (halo sign) is an adjunct","Distinguish from non-arteritic AION (younger, no systemic symptoms, normal ESR/CRP, hyperemic disc edema, crowded 'disc at risk')"],"tx":["START HIGH-DOSE CORTICOSTEROIDS IMMEDIATELY on clinical suspicion - do NOT wait for the biopsy - to protect the fellow eye and prevent further loss (IV methylprednisolone for visual involvement, then high-dose oral)","Temporal artery biopsy within about 1-2 weeks to confirm; add TOCILIZUMAB as a steroid-sparing agent (GiACTA trial)","Rheumatology co-management for the prolonged steroid taper and disease monitoring"],"tech":["Temporal artery biopsy (adequate length, watch for skip lesions); temporal/axillary artery ultrasound as an adjunct","No ocular procedure reverses the optic infarct - treatment is medical and preventive","Monitor for large-vessel involvement (aortitis) over time"],"after":["Slow corticosteroid TAPER over months-years with rheumatology; monitor ESR/CRP and symptoms for relapse and for steroid side effects (bone, glucose, etc.)","Vigilant protection/monitoring of the FELLOW eye; screen for aortic aneurysm/large-vessel disease long-term","Counsel that vision already lost usually does not recover - the goal is preventing further loss"],"pearls":["Steroids come BEFORE the biopsy - in suspected GCA, treat immediately to save the fellow eye; the biopsy stays positive for days","Jaw claudication and a markedly elevated ESR/CRP in a patient over 50 with vision loss is GCA until proven otherwise","PALE (pallid) disc edema suggests arteritic AION; a hyperemic, crowded disc in a younger patient suggests non-arteritic AION","The fellow eye is the eye you are trying to save - the affected eye's vision rarely recovers"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / ACR Giant Cell Arteritis Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Giant Cell Arteritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459376/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Arteritic AION","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-red-eye","name":"Approach to the Red Eye","sec":"ophtho","present":["The 'red eye' is one of the most common ophthalmic presentations - the core skill is triaging BENIGN, self-limited causes from SIGHT-THREATENING emergencies","Benign/common: conjunctivitis, subconjunctival hemorrhage, dry eye, blepharitis, episcleritis; sight-threatening: keratitis/corneal ulcer, anterior uveitis (iritis), acute angle-closure glaucoma, scleritis, and endophthalmitis","RED FLAGS that demand urgent evaluation: significant PAIN, PHOTOPHOBIA, decreased VISION, a corneal opacity/infiltrate, ciliary flush (perilimbal redness), a fixed/abnormal pupil, or high IOP"],"dx":["A structured exam: visual ACUITY (the vital sign of the eye), pupils (size/reactivity/RAPD), pattern of redness (diffuse vs ciliary flush), the CORNEA with FLUORESCEIN (staining, infiltrate), the anterior chamber (cells/flare, hypopyon), and IOP","Key discriminators: itching -> allergic; discharge -> conjunctivitis; pain/photophobia/decreased vision -> keratitis/uveitis/angle closure; contact lens wear -> keratitis until proven otherwise","Evert the lids (foreign body) and check for herpetic dendrites"],"tx":["Treat the specific cause (see the dedicated entries); benign conditions get supportive/targeted therapy, while red-flag conditions need urgent ophthalmology referral","AVOID reflexive topical steroids and NEVER prescribe topical anesthetics for home use (corneal toxicity/melt) or patch a contact-lens-related abrasion","When in doubt about a red flag, refer same-day"],"tech":["Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein staining; measure IOP; lid eversion for foreign body; document visual acuity","Corneal scraping/cultures if an infiltrate suggests infectious keratitis","Referral pathways for sight-threatening causes"],"after":["Set return precautions (worsening pain, vision, photophobia); arrange appropriate follow-up by diagnosis","Contact-lens hygiene counseling for CL wearers; avoid steroid/anesthetic misuse","Escalate promptly if red flags emerge"],"pearls":["Check the VISION first - a red eye with reduced vision is a red flag until proven otherwise","Pain, photophobia, ciliary flush, corneal opacity, an abnormal pupil, or high IOP separate the dangerous red eye from the benign one","Never give a patient topical anesthetic to take home and never patch a contact-lens abrasion - both cause harm","A contact-lens wearer with a red painful eye has microbial keratitis until proven otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Conjunctivitis / Red Eye Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Red Eye Evaluation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448045/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Red Eye","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-conjunctivitis","name":"Conjunctivitis (Bacterial, Viral, Allergic)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inflammation of the conjunctiva - a very common red eye, usually with PRESERVED vision and no significant pain (features that reassure against a sight-threatening cause)","VIRAL (most common, adenoviral): watery discharge, follicular reaction, a tender PREAURICULAR node, often starts in one eye then the other, highly CONTAGIOUS (epidemic keratoconjunctivitis); BACTERIAL: purulent discharge with lids stuck together; ALLERGIC: bilateral ITCHING, chemosis, papillae, and a personal/family atopy history","Hyperacute purulent conjunctivitis suggests GONOCOCCAL infection - an emergency (corneal perforation risk); neonatal conjunctivitis has its own differential"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the discharge type, itching, laterality, and lymph node; culture/Gram stain for HYPERACUTE (gonococcal), neonatal, or severe/refractory cases","Look for red flags that mean it is NOT simple conjunctivitis: pain, photophobia, decreased vision, ciliary flush, or corneal involvement","Consider chlamydial conjunctivitis (chronic follicular) in sexually active adults and neonates"],"tx":["VIRAL: supportive (cool compresses, lubricants) and strict HYGIENE/contagion precautions - it is self-limited but very contagious; avoid steroids (can prolong adenoviral shedding, mask herpes)","BACTERIAL: topical antibiotics (often self-limited but antibiotics speed resolution); ALLERGIC: topical antihistamine/MAST-CELL stabilizers, cool compresses, allergen avoidance","GONOCOCCAL: systemic ceftriaxone (+ treat chlamydia) and saline irrigation - urgent; chlamydial: systemic azithromycin/doxycycline and partner treatment"],"tech":["Conjunctival culture/Gram stain and NAAT for gonococcal/chlamydial disease; saline irrigation for copious purulent discharge","No surgical procedure for routine conjunctivitis","Manage complications (membranes in severe adenoviral disease)"],"after":["Counsel on the CONTAGIOUS period (adenoviral - hand hygiene, avoid sharing towels, work/school precautions) and return precautions for red flags","Allergic disease - ongoing avoidance and maintenance therapy; treat partners for STIs (gonococcal/chlamydial)","Follow-up for non-resolving or worsening cases"],"pearls":["Preserved vision, no true pain, and itching or discharge point to conjunctivitis - pain, photophobia, and reduced vision point away from it","Watery discharge with a preauricular node is viral (adenoviral) and highly contagious - hygiene is the treatment","Hyperacute copious purulent discharge is gonococcal until proven otherwise - it can perforate the cornea and needs systemic ceftriaxone","Avoid steroids in an undifferentiated red eye - they worsen herpes and prolong adenoviral shedding"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Conjunctivitis Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Conjunctivitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541034/"},{"t":"CDC Conjunctivitis Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/conjunctivitis"}]},{"id":"ophtho-scleritis-episcleritis","name":"Scleritis & Episcleritis","sec":"ophtho","present":["Two distinct inflammations of the outer eye wall. EPISCLERITIS: mild, often sectoral redness with minimal/no pain, benign and self-limited, common in young adults","SCLERITIS: severe, deep, BORING PAIN (often waking the patient) with a violaceous hue, tenderness, and potential vision threat; subtypes include diffuse, nodular, and the worst - NECROTIZING scleritis (with or without inflammation - scleromalacia perforans)","SCLERITIS carries a strong (~50%) association with SYSTEMIC disease (rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis/other vasculitides, relapsing polychondritis, infection)"],"dx":["The PHENYLEPHRINE (2.5%) test helps: episcleral vessels BLANCH with phenylephrine (episcleritis), whereas the deeper scleral vessels do NOT blanch (scleritis)","Assess pain severity (severe boring pain favors scleritis), and for scleritis pursue a SYSTEMIC workup (autoimmune serologies - RF, ANCA, ANA; infectious causes) and screen for posterior scleritis (B-scan - 'T-sign')","Distinguish from conjunctivitis and other red eyes"],"tx":["EPISCLERITIS: reassurance, lubricants, and oral NSAIDs if symptomatic - it is self-limited","SCLERITIS: oral NSAIDs for mild disease, then systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS and immunosuppression/biologics for moderate-severe or necrotizing disease; TREAT the underlying SYSTEMIC disease (rheumatology co-management)","Infectious scleritis needs targeted antimicrobials; avoid isolated topical steroids for necrotizing disease"],"tech":["Phenylephrine test and B-scan (posterior scleritis); systemic workup laboratory panel","Rarely surgical (scleral patch graft) for impending/actual perforation in necrotizing disease","Immunosuppression managed with rheumatology"],"after":["Scleritis: monitor for progression to necrotizing disease and perforation; ongoing systemic disease surveillance and immunosuppression","Episcleritis: reassure about the benign, recurrent nature","Follow-up for treatment response and steroid-sparing management"],"pearls":["Deep BORING pain that wakes the patient means scleritis, not episcleritis - and it can threaten the eye","Phenylephrine blanches the redness of episcleritis but not scleritis - a simple bedside discriminator","Half of scleritis is linked to systemic disease - workup for RA, ANCA-associated vasculitis, and relapsing polychondritis is mandatory","Necrotizing scleritis is an ophthalmic and systemic emergency - it signals aggressive vasculitis and risks perforation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Scleritis / Episcleritis Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Scleritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499944/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Scleritis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-bacterial-keratitis","name":"Infectious (Bacterial) Keratitis / Corneal Ulcer","sec":"ophtho","present":["A microbial infection of the cornea - a sight-threatening EMERGENCY presenting with pain, photophobia, redness, discharge, and reduced vision","Exam: an epithelial DEFECT overlying a white stromal INFILTRATE (the ulcer), with anterior chamber reaction and possibly a HYPOPYON; larger/central ulcers threaten vision and can perforate","The dominant risk factor is CONTACT LENS wear (especially overnight/poor hygiene - Pseudomonas, and Acanthamoeba with water exposure); also trauma, ocular surface disease, and prior herpetic disease"],"dx":["Slit-lamp characterization of the infiltrate (size, depth, location); for significant/central/vision-threatening ulcers, CORNEAL SCRAPING for Gram stain, culture, and sensitivities BEFORE starting broad therapy","Consider the organism by history: Pseudomonas (contact lens - rapid, mucopurulent), fungal (vegetative/organic trauma - feathery margins), Acanthamoeba (water exposure, severe pain out of proportion, ring infiltrate), herpetic","Assess for perforation risk and involve cornea specialists for severe cases"],"tx":["Empiric intensive topical ANTIBIOTICS: fortified vancomycin + tobramycin/ceftazidime, or a broad-spectrum fluoroquinolone, dosed HOURLY (including overnight) initially, then tapered by response and cultures","AVOID topical corticosteroids initially (can worsen infection, especially fungal/Acanthamoeba) - steroids are used cautiously only later under specialist guidance","Fungal - topical natamycin/voriconazole; Acanthamoeba - biguanides/diamidines; discontinue contact lens wear and treat surface disease"],"tech":["Corneal scraping for microbiology; corneal cross-linking (PACK-CXL) in select infectious ulcers; therapeutic KERATOPLASTY for impending/actual perforation","Amniotic membrane/tissue adhesive for melt; manage secondary complications","Specialist cornea management for severe/refractory cases"],"after":["DAILY monitoring initially to confirm response (infiltrate size, epithelial healing, AC reaction); adjust therapy by cultures","Counsel intensively on CONTACT LENS hygiene (no sleeping in lenses, no water exposure, replacement schedules) to prevent recurrence","Manage residual corneal SCARRING and visual rehabilitation (rigid lenses, transplant if central scar)"],"pearls":["A contact-lens wearer with a painful red eye and a corneal infiltrate has microbial keratitis until proven otherwise - scrape and treat, do not just prescribe drops","Culture the significant ulcer BEFORE broad antibiotics, and dose fortified antibiotics hourly at first","Do NOT start topical steroids on an undiagnosed ulcer - they worsen fungal and Acanthamoeba infections","Pain out of proportion with a ring infiltrate and water exposure is Acanthamoeba - a notoriously difficult, vision-threatening diagnosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Bacterial Keratitis Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bacterial Keratitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441908/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Infectious Keratitis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-herpes-keratitis","name":"Herpes Simplex Keratitis & Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus","sec":"ophtho","present":["HERPES SIMPLEX (HSV) keratitis: recurrent corneal infection with several forms - EPITHELIAL (a branching DENDRITE with terminal bulbs, staining with fluorescein), STROMAL (immune-mediated, vision-threatening), and endothelial/disciform keratitis; often unilateral, recurrent, with reduced corneal sensation","HERPES ZOSTER OPHTHALMICUS (HZO): reactivation of varicella-zoster in the V1 (ophthalmic) trigeminal division - a painful vesicular dermatomal rash, with HUTCHINSON SIGN (nasal tip/side involvement = nasociliary nerve) predicting ocular involvement (keratitis, uveitis, scleritis)","Both can cause chronic/recurrent disease, scarring, and neurotrophic keratopathy"],"dx":["HSV epithelial keratitis is a clinical diagnosis (fluorescein dendrites with terminal bulbs - distinct from the pseudodendrites of zoster); reduced corneal sensation supports herpetic disease; PCR of corneal swabs if uncertain","HZO: clinical (dermatomal V1 rash, Hutchinson sign) with a full eye exam for keratitis, uveitis, elevated IOP, and retinitis (acute retinal necrosis)","Distinguish epithelial (infectious, steroid-contraindicated) from stromal (immune, needs steroid + antiviral cover)"],"tx":["HSV EPITHELIAL keratitis: topical (ganciclovir gel/trifluridine) or ORAL antivirals (acyclovir/valacyclovir); do NOT use topical steroids alone on epithelial disease (worsens it)","HSV STROMAL/disciform (immune-mediated): topical CORTICOSTEROIDS WITH antiviral coverage; ORAL antiviral PROPHYLAXIS reduces recurrences (Herpetic Eye Disease Study, HEDS)","HZO: oral antivirals (acyclovir/valacyclovir/famciclovir), ideally within 72 h, plus management of ocular complications (topical steroids for keratitis/uveitis under supervision) and pain/PHN"],"tech":["Debridement of dendritic epithelium in select cases; manage neurotrophic ulcers (lubrication, amniotic membrane, tarsorrhaphy); keratoplasty for scarring","Manage elevated IOP and uveitis; treat acute retinal necrosis aggressively (IV/intravitreal antivirals)","Long-term antiviral prophylaxis regimens"],"after":["ORAL ANTIVIRAL PROPHYLAXIS for recurrent HSV disease (HEDS-supported) to reduce recurrences and stromal disease/scarring","Monitor for complications: corneal scarring, neurotrophic keratopathy, secondary glaucoma, and (HZO) postherpetic neuralgia and delayed ocular inflammation","Vaccination counseling for zoster prevention in eligible patients"],"pearls":["A true dendrite with terminal bulbs and reduced corneal sensation is HSV epithelial keratitis - and it must NOT be treated with topical steroids alone","Stromal (immune) HSV keratitis needs steroids WITH antiviral cover - the opposite rule from epithelial disease","Hutchinson sign (lesions on the nose tip) in zoster predicts eye involvement via the nasociliary nerve - examine the eye carefully","Oral antiviral prophylaxis (HEDS) reduces HSV recurrences - a key long-term strategy for recurrent disease"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Herpetic Keratitis / HZO Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Herpes Simplex Keratitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431102/"},{"t":"Herpetic Eye Disease Study (HEDS)","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-corneal-abrasion-fb","name":"Corneal Abrasion & Foreign Body","sec":"ophtho","present":["A corneal epithelial defect (abrasion) or a lodged corneal/conjunctival FOREIGN BODY - very common, presenting with acute pain, FOREIGN BODY SENSATION, photophobia, tearing, and blepharospasm","History of trauma, contact lens use, or a projectile (grinding/hammering - raises concern for a penetrating/intraocular foreign body)","A subtarsal foreign body causes vertical, linear corneal abrasions ('ice-rink' tracks)"],"dx":["FLUORESCEIN staining reveals the epithelial defect; EVERT the upper lid to find a hidden subtarsal foreign body and sweep the fornices","Rule out an OPEN GLOBE / intraocular foreign body when the mechanism is high-velocity (Seidel test, examine for entry wound; CT if suspicious) - do not miss a penetrating injury","Note metallic foreign bodies (may leave a RUST RING) and assess depth"],"tx":["Uncomplicated abrasion: topical ANTIBIOTIC (prophylaxis), a cycloplegic for pain relief if significant, and oral analgesia; most heal within 24-48 h","REMOVE corneal/conjunctival foreign bodies (needle/burr under slit-lamp) and any RUST RING; do NOT patch a CONTACT-LENS-related abrasion (Pseudomonas risk) and do NOT prescribe TOPICAL ANESTHETICS for home use (toxic, delays healing, causes melt)","Contact-lens wearers get antipseudomonal coverage and closer follow-up"],"tech":["Slit-lamp foreign body removal (25-27g needle/spud) and rust-ring removal with an ophthalmic burr; lid eversion for subtarsal foreign bodies","Bandage contact lens for select large/painful abrasions (with antibiotic cover)","CT and surgical exploration if an intraocular foreign body is suspected"],"after":["Recheck if not healing in 24-48 h; watch for infection (infiltrate) and, with recurrent symptoms, RECURRENT CORNEAL EROSION (treat with lubrication/hyperosmotics, and consider procedures)","Contact-lens hygiene counseling; protective eyewear for occupational injuries","Follow-up for larger abrasions and any foreign body with residual rust/scar"],"pearls":["Always evert the upper lid - a hidden subtarsal foreign body causes recurring vertical abrasions until it is removed","Never send a patient home with topical anesthetic and never patch a contact-lens abrasion - both cause real harm","A grinding/hammering history means rule out an intraocular foreign body - fluorescein an eye, but also think Seidel and CT","Recurrent pain on waking after an abrasion suggests recurrent corneal erosion - a distinct, treatable sequela"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Corneal Abrasion / Foreign Body Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Corneal Foreign Body","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536978/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Corneal Abrasion","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-dry-eye","name":"Dry Eye Disease (Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca)","sec":"ophtho","present":["A multifactorial ocular-surface disease from tear-film instability - AQUEOUS-DEFICIENT (reduced production - lacrimal dysfunction, Sjogren) and/or EVAPORATIVE (meibomian gland dysfunction - the most common) types","Symptoms: grittiness/foreign-body sensation, burning, redness, FLUCTUATING/blurred vision that clears with blinking, and paradoxical REFLEX TEARING; worse with screens (reduced blink), wind, and low humidity","Very common, chronic, and frequently under-treated; associations include age, medications (antihistamines, antidepressants), autoimmune disease, and contact lens wear"],"dx":["Assess the tear film and surface: reduced tear breakup time (TBUT), corneal/conjunctival STAINING (fluorescein/lissamine green), SCHIRMER test (aqueous production), and meibomian gland evaluation","Screen for SJOGREN syndrome and other systemic autoimmune disease when dry eye is severe or with dry mouth/arthralgia","Distinguish evaporative (MGD, lid disease) from aqueous-deficient to target therapy"],"tx":["Foundation: artificial TEARS/lubricants (preservative-free for frequent use) and environmental/behavioral measures (blink breaks, humidity, omega-3s, review offending medications)","MGD/evaporative: warm compresses, lid hygiene, and sometimes topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline/azithromycin for anti-inflammatory effect)","Moderate-severe: topical anti-inflammatories (CYCLOSPORINE, lifitegrast, short-course steroids), PUNCTAL PLUGS/occlusion, autologous serum tears, and treat the underlying systemic disease"],"tech":["Punctal plug insertion or punctal cautery (occlude tear drainage to retain tears); meibomian gland expression/thermal treatments for MGD","Autologous serum tear preparation for severe surface disease; amniotic membrane for persistent defects","Manage lid disease contributing to evaporative dry eye"],"after":["Chronic, stepwise management with realistic expectations (control, not cure); reassess and escalate by severity","Systemic workup/referral (rheumatology) for suspected Sjogren; monitor for surface complications (filamentary keratitis, corneal ulceration in severe cases)","Optimize before any refractive/cataract surgery (dry eye worsens outcomes)"],"pearls":["Fluctuating vision that clears with blinking is a classic dry-eye clue - the tear film, not the retina, is the problem","Most dry eye is evaporative (meibomian gland dysfunction) - treat the lids (warm compresses, hygiene), not just the tears","Reflex tearing does not rule out dry eye - a poor-quality tear film triggers paradoxical watering","Severe dry eye with dry mouth and arthralgia should prompt a Sjogren workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Dry Eye Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dry Eye Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470411/"},{"t":"TFOS DEWS II Report","u":"https://www.tearfilm.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-blepharitis","name":"Blepharitis & Meibomian Gland Dysfunction","sec":"ophtho","present":["Chronic inflammation of the eyelid margins - ANTERIOR (lash-based: staphylococcal with collarettes/crusting, or seborrheic) and POSTERIOR (MEIBOMIAN GLAND DYSFUNCTION - MGD, the most common cause of evaporative dry eye)","Symptoms: burning, itching, grittiness, crusting/mattering (worse in the morning), red lid margins, and recurrent styes/chalazia; often chronic and relapsing","Frequently associated with ROSACEA (facial telangiectasia, flushing) and seborrheic dermatitis"],"dx":["Slit-lamp exam of the lid margins: collarettes/crusting at the lashes (anterior), telangiectasia and capped/inspissated meibomian gland orifices with turbid/toothpaste-like expression (posterior/MGD), and tear-film instability","Assess for associated dry eye and ocular surface staining; note rosacea/seborrhea","Consider Demodex (cylindrical dandruff) and, in unilateral/atypical chronic blepharitis, sebaceous carcinoma (biopsy)"],"tx":["Cornerstone lid HYGIENE: WARM COMPRESSES, lid massage, and lid-margin cleansing - daily and ongoing (this is a chronic, maintenance condition)","MGD/rosacea-associated disease: oral tetracyclines (DOXYCYCLINE) or azithromycin for their anti-inflammatory/meibomian effects; topical antibiotic/steroid combinations for flares","Treat associated dry eye and rosacea; manage chalazia (warm compresses, then incision & curettage if persistent)"],"tech":["Meibomian gland expression and thermal pulsation treatments; chalazion incision and curettage or intralesional steroid for persistent lesions","Lid-margin debridement; Demodex-directed therapy (tea tree oil) when relevant","Biopsy for suspicious unilateral chronic lid disease"],"after":["Emphasize LONG-TERM maintenance lid hygiene - blepharitis is controlled, not cured, and lapses cause recurrence","Manage associated rosacea and dry eye; monitor for recurrent chalazia and complications","Reassess adherence and escalate for refractory disease"],"pearls":["Blepharitis is a chronic maintenance disease - warm compresses and lid hygiene are the durable treatment, and stopping them brings it back","Posterior blepharitis (MGD) is the leading cause of evaporative dry eye - treat the glands, not just the surface","Rosacea and blepharitis travel together - doxycycline treats both","A unilateral, chronic, atypical blepharitis that does not respond should be biopsied to exclude sebaceous carcinoma"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Blepharitis Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Blepharitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459305/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Meibomian Gland Dysfunction","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-keratoconus","name":"Keratoconus & Corneal Ectasia","sec":"ophtho","present":["A progressive corneal ECTASIA: thinning and cone-shaped protrusion of the cornea causing irregular myopic ASTIGMATISM and progressive visual distortion, typically beginning in adolescence/early adulthood","Associations/risks: EYE RUBBING (a major modifiable driver), atopy/allergic eye disease, Down syndrome, and connective tissue disorders","Signs: scissoring red reflex on retinoscopy, Vogt striae, a Fleischer ring, Munson sign (lower lid bulge on downgaze), and, acutely, corneal HYDROPS (sudden edema/pain from a Descemet break)"],"dx":["Corneal TOPOGRAPHY/TOMOGRAPHY is diagnostic and monitors progression (asymmetric steepening, inferior thinning, elevation maps); refraction shows increasing irregular astigmatism","Detect subclinical/forme fruste keratoconus before refractive surgery (a key screening role - LASIK is contraindicated in ectasia-prone corneas)","Assess corneal thickness (pachymetry) and rule out post-refractive ectasia"],"tx":["Early/mild: glasses, then RIGID GAS-PERMEABLE or scleral contact lenses to correct the irregular astigmatism","Halt PROGRESSION with corneal COLLAGEN CROSS-LINKING (CXL - riboflavin + UV-A stiffens the cornea) in documented progressive disease - a disease-modifying intervention","Advanced disease/scarring/failed contact lenses: keratoplasty (deep anterior lamellar - DALK, or penetrating - PK); intracorneal ring segments for select cases; and crucially, STOP EYE RUBBING"],"tech":["Corneal collagen CROSS-LINKING to arrest progression; intrastromal corneal ring segment implantation; DALK or PK for advanced disease/scarring","Contact lens fitting (rigid gas-permeable/scleral); manage acute hydrops (supportive, then possible intervention)","Topography-guided planning"],"after":["Monitor progression with serial topography (especially in young patients, who progress fastest); reinforce NO EYE RUBBING and treat atopy","Post-cross-linking follow-up for stabilization; post-transplant graft surveillance","Optimize contact-lens correction and visual rehabilitation"],"pearls":["Increasing irregular astigmatism in a young patient with scissoring retinoscopy is keratoconus - confirm with topography","Eye rubbing drives progression - counseling to stop rubbing (and treating the atopy that causes it) is real disease management","Cross-linking halts progression - catch and treat progressive disease before it needs a transplant","Screen for subclinical keratoconus before refractive surgery - LASIK on an ectatic cornea causes ectasia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Keratoconus / Corneal Ectasia Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Keratoconus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470435/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Keratoconus","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-corneal-dystrophies","name":"Corneal Dystrophies & Keratoplasty","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inherited, usually bilateral corneal opacities classified by layer: EPITHELIAL/basement membrane (map-dot-fingerprint - causes RECURRENT EROSIONS), STROMAL (lattice, granular, macular - progressive opacity), and ENDOTHELIAL (FUCHS dystrophy - the most clinically important)","FUCHS endothelial dystrophy: progressive endothelial cell loss -> corneal EDEMA with morning blur that clears through the day (as the cornea dehydrates), guttae on exam, and eventually bullous keratopathy with pain","Corneal transplantation (keratoplasty) is the definitive treatment when vision/comfort is lost"],"dx":["Slit-lamp diagnosis of the dystrophy type (guttae and corneal thickening in Fuchs; characteristic stromal deposits); specular/confocal microscopy and pachymetry quantify endothelial status in Fuchs","Recurrent erosion syndrome from epithelial dystrophy diagnosed by history (recurrent pain on waking) and exam","Assess visual significance and corneal edema to time surgery"],"tx":["Symptomatic/medical measures first: hypertonic saline (5% NaCl) to dehydrate the cornea in Fuchs edema, lubrication, and bandage lenses/erosion treatment for epithelial dystrophies","KERATOPLASTY when vision or comfort declines - modern ENDOTHELIAL keratoplasty (DSEK/DMEK) selectively replaces the diseased endothelium for Fuchs/bullous keratopathy (faster recovery than full-thickness); DALK for anterior stromal disease; PK for full-thickness disease","Combined cataract + endothelial keratoplasty is common in older Fuchs patients"],"tech":["DMEK/DSEK (endothelial keratoplasty - transplant only Descemet/endothelium, air/gas tamponade to attach the graft); DALK (anterior lamellar); PK (penetrating, full-thickness)","Manage graft attachment (positioning), and phototherapeutic keratectomy for select anterior dystrophies/erosions","Cataract surgery timing/combination in Fuchs"],"after":["Graft REJECTION surveillance (a rejection line, keratic precipitates, new edema - patients must report redness/pain/vision change urgently) and LONG-TERM topical corticosteroids; DMEK has lower rejection than PK","Manage graft detachment (re-bubbling), IOP (steroid response), and suture-related issues (PK)","Visual rehabilitation and refractive management post-transplant"],"pearls":["Fuchs dystrophy causes morning blur that clears during the day - the cornea is edematous overnight and dehydrates while awake","Modern endothelial keratoplasty (DMEK/DSEK) replaces just the diseased layer for Fuchs - faster recovery and less rejection than a full-thickness transplant","Map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy is a common cause of recurrent corneal erosions - treat the erosions, not just the pain","After any keratoplasty, teach the rejection warning signs (redness, sensitivity, vision drop) - early treatment can save the graft"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Corneal Dystrophies / Keratoplasty Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fuchs Endothelial Dystrophy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430886/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Corneal Dystrophies","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-poag","name":"Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma","sec":"ophtho","present":["A chronic, progressive optic NEUROPATHY with characteristic optic disc CUPPING and corresponding VISUAL FIELD loss, with an OPEN drainage angle - typically ASYMPTOMATIC until advanced","Elevated intraocular pressure is the major risk factor, but NORMAL-TENSION glaucoma occurs; peripheral field is lost first (central vision preserved until late), so patients often present with advanced disease","Risk factors: elevated IOP, older age, family history, African ancestry, thin central cornea, and myopia; a leading cause of irreversible blindness worldwide"],"dx":["Comprehensive assessment: IOP (tonometry), GONIOSCOPY (confirms an open angle), optic disc/RNFL evaluation (increased cup-to-disc ratio, rim thinning, disc hemorrhages) with OCT, and VISUAL FIELD testing (perimetry) - the functional measure of damage","Pachymetry (thin cornea is a risk factor and affects IOP measurement); the diagnosis integrates structure (disc/OCT) and function (fields), not IOP alone","Screen at-risk populations (asymptomatic disease)"],"tx":["Lower IOP (the only modifiable factor proven to slow progression) to a target: topical PROSTAGLANDIN analogues are first-line (once daily, effective), plus beta-blockers, alpha-2 agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as needed","Laser TRABECULOPLASTY (SLT) is effective and increasingly used as a FIRST-LINE option (the LiGHT trial supported primary SLT)","SURGERY (see the surgical entry) when medical/laser therapy fails to control progression"],"tech":["Selective laser trabeculoplasty (SLT) to lower IOP; incisional/MIGS surgery for refractory disease","Serial OCT/RNFL and visual field testing to track progression","Individualized target-IOP setting"],"after":["LIFELONG monitoring of IOP, optic nerve, and visual fields to detect progression; adjust therapy to hit target IOP and slow field loss","Emphasize ADHERENCE (asymptomatic disease -> poor compliance) and correct drop technique; screen at-risk family members","Low-vision rehabilitation for advanced disease"],"pearls":["Glaucoma is silent until it is advanced - peripheral vision goes first, so screening and adherence (not symptoms) drive care","Lowering IOP is the only proven way to slow glaucoma - even in normal-tension glaucoma, reducing IOP helps","Primary SLT (LiGHT trial) is a legitimate first-line alternative to daily drops","The diagnosis integrates disc/OCT structure and visual-field function - a 'normal' IOP does not exclude glaucoma"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Open-Angle Glaucoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441887/"},{"t":"LiGHT Trial / AAO EyeWiki: POAG","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-chronic-angle-closure","name":"Chronic Primary Angle-Closure Disease","sec":"ophtho","present":["A spectrum of anatomically narrow angles distinct from the acute attack: primary angle-closure SUSPECT (appositional narrow angle, normal IOP/nerve) -> primary angle CLOSURE (synechiae or raised IOP) -> primary angle-closure GLAUCOMA (with optic neuropathy)","Often ASYMPTOMATIC (chronic, creeping angle closure) rather than the dramatic acute attack; may have intermittent subacute episodes (transient blur/halos/brow ache in dim light that resolve)","Risk factors mirror acute angle closure: hyperopia, older age, female sex, East Asian ancestry, and a shallow anterior chamber"],"dx":["GONIOSCOPY is essential - it identifies the narrow/closed angle and peripheral anterior synechiae and distinguishes appositional from synechial closure; anterior-segment OCT/ultrasound biomicroscopy supplement","Assess IOP, optic nerve, and visual fields for glaucomatous damage; identify the mechanism (pupillary block, plateau iris, phacomorphic/lens-related)","Screen the fellow eye and at-risk anatomy"],"tx":["Laser PERIPHERAL IRIDOTOMY relieves pupillary block and is the traditional first step for anatomically narrow/closed angles","LENS EXTRACTION (clear-lens or cataract surgery) deepens the anterior chamber and opens the angle - the EAGLE trial supported clear lens extraction as an effective option in primary angle closure/glaucoma","Add IOP-lowering medical therapy and glaucoma surgery as needed for established glaucoma; treat plateau iris (iridoplasty) when present"],"tech":["Laser peripheral iridotomy; LENS EXTRACTION (phacoemulsification) to deepen the chamber; laser iridoplasty for plateau iris; goniosynechialysis in select cases","Glaucoma surgery for uncontrolled disease","Serial gonioscopy and nerve/field monitoring"],"after":["Ongoing IOP, optic nerve, and visual-field surveillance (this is chronic glaucoma once damage occurs); treat both eyes' anatomy","Counsel on angle-closure-precipitating medications and symptoms; monitor after lens extraction","Adherence support and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Not all angle closure is dramatic - chronic 'creeping' angle closure is asymptomatic and found only on gonioscopy","Gonioscopy is the indispensable test - you cannot manage angle closure without grading the angle and finding synechiae","Lens extraction (EAGLE trial) can be a definitive treatment - removing the bulky lens deepens the chamber and opens the angle","Iridotomy fixes pupillary block but not plateau iris or a phacomorphic mechanism - identify the mechanism to pick the right procedure"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Primary Angle Closure Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"EAGLE Trial / StatPearls: Angle-Closure Glaucoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430857/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Chronic Angle Closure","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-secondary-glaucoma","name":"Secondary Glaucomas","sec":"ophtho","present":["Glaucoma from an identifiable secondary cause raising IOP; recognizing the mechanism is essential because treatment targets the cause","NEOVASCULAR glaucoma (from retinal ISCHEMIA - proliferative diabetic retinopathy, ischemic CRVO, ocular ischemic syndrome - with iris/angle neovascularization/rubeosis - a painful, aggressive glaucoma), PSEUDOEXFOLIATION (flaky material, weak zonules), PIGMENTARY (pigment dispersion in myopic young men), STEROID-induced, UVEITIC, angle-RECESSION (post-trauma), and phacomorphic/lens-related","Presentation varies from asymptomatic elevated IOP to acute pain (neovascular, lens-related)"],"dx":["GONIOSCOPY and slit-lamp identify the cause: rubeosis/neovascular vessels in the angle, exfoliation material on the lens/pupil margin, a Krukenberg spindle/transillumination defects (pigmentary), synechiae (uveitic), or a recessed angle (trauma)","History of steroid use, trauma, uveitis, diabetes/vein occlusion, or high myopia points to the mechanism","Assess the optic nerve and fields for glaucomatous damage; evaluate the retina for ischemia in neovascular glaucoma"],"tx":["Treat the underlying CAUSE plus lower IOP: NEOVASCULAR glaucoma - urgent PANRETINAL PHOTOCOAGULATION and/or anti-VEGF to eliminate the ischemic drive, plus IOP control/glaucoma surgery; STEROID-induced - stop/taper the steroid; UVEITIC - control inflammation (steroids) while managing IOP","Pseudoexfoliation/pigmentary - standard IOP-lowering (medical/SLT) with attention to their surgical implications (weak zonules in pseudoexfoliation increase cataract surgery risk)","Lens-related (phacomorphic/phacolytic) - cataract extraction; escalate to glaucoma surgery for uncontrolled IOP"],"tech":["Panretinal photocoagulation and anti-VEGF for neovascular glaucoma; glaucoma drainage surgery (tubes often preferred in neovascular/uveitic glaucoma); cataract extraction for lens-related mechanisms","SLT/medical therapy for pseudoexfoliative/pigmentary glaucoma","Cause-specific interventions"],"after":["Manage the primary disease (retinal ischemia, inflammation, steroid exposure) to prevent recurrence; monitor IOP, nerve, and fields","Pseudoexfoliation patients need careful cataract-surgery planning (zonular weakness); neovascular glaucoma has a guarded prognosis","Long-term glaucoma surveillance"],"pearls":["Neovascular glaucoma signals severe retinal ischemia (diabetes, ischemic CRVO) - treat the ischemia (PRP/anti-VEGF), not just the pressure","Always ask about steroids - steroid-induced glaucoma is common and reversible if caught (stop the steroid)","Pseudoexfoliation means weak zonules - it raises the risk of complications during cataract surgery, so plan ahead","Gonioscopy reveals the mechanism (rubeosis, recession, synechiae, exfoliation) - the cause dictates the treatment"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Secondary Glaucoma Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neovascular Glaucoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560862/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Secondary Glaucomas","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-glaucoma-surgery","name":"Glaucoma Surgical Management","sec":"ophtho","present":["Surgery is indicated when medical therapy and laser fail to control IOP or halt glaucomatous progression, or when adherence/access make drops impractical","The classic filtration operation is TRABECULECTOMY (a guarded fistula creating a subconjunctival bleb); TUBE SHUNTS (Ahmed/Baerveldt drainage devices) are used for refractory, neovascular, uveitic, or failed-trabeculectomy cases","MINIMALLY INVASIVE glaucoma surgery (MIGS) offers a safer but generally less potent IOP reduction, often combined with cataract surgery"],"dx":["Establish progression despite maximal tolerated medical/laser therapy (worsening fields/OCT, uncontrolled IOP); select the procedure by target IOP, prior surgery, conjunctival health, and glaucoma type","Assess the angle and cause (neovascular/uveitic favor tubes); evaluate the ocular surface (affects bleb success)","Set the target IOP the surgery must achieve"],"tx":["TRABECULECTOMY with antifibrotic agents (mitomycin-C) for greater IOP lowering; TUBE SHUNT for refractory/complex cases (the Tube versus Trabeculectomy study informs selection)","MIGS (trabecular bypass stents, goniotomy/canal procedures) for mild-moderate glaucoma, often at the time of cataract surgery - safer, modest effect; cyclophotocoagulation (reduce aqueous production) for refractory eyes/limited visual potential","Continue individualized IOP-lowering; manage the underlying secondary cause"],"tech":["Trabeculectomy (partial-thickness scleral flap + sclerostomy + peripheral iridectomy, with mitomycin-C and a titratable bleb); glaucoma DRAINAGE DEVICE implantation (tube into the anterior chamber, plate under the conjunctiva); MIGS (iStent/Hydrus, goniotomy); cyclophotocoagulation","Bleb needling/revision for scarring; suture lysis to titrate flow","Combine with phacoemulsification when indicated"],"after":["Trabeculectomy needs intensive early management: titrate flow (suture lysis), watch for HYPOTONY, bleb LEAKS, and long-term BLEB-related INFECTION/blebitis-endophthalmitis (lifelong risk - patients must report a red, painful, or discharging eye urgently)","Tube shunts: monitor for tube erosion, diplopia, and corneal decompensation; MIGS has faster recovery","Lifelong IOP, nerve, and field surveillance; manage IOP spikes/failure"],"pearls":["Trabeculectomy with mitomycin-C gives the biggest IOP drop but demands careful bleb management and carries lifelong endophthalmitis risk","Tubes are favored for neovascular, uveitic, and failed-trabeculectomy eyes (Tube versus Trabeculectomy study)","MIGS trades potency for safety - great for mild-moderate glaucoma combined with cataract surgery","Teach bleb patients to report a red, painful eye immediately - blebitis can progress to endophthalmitis years later"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Glaucoma Surgery Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"Tube Versus Trabeculectomy Study","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441887/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Glaucoma Surgery / MIGS","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-cataract","name":"Cataract & Cataract Surgery","sec":"ophtho","present":["Opacification of the crystalline LENS - the leading cause of reversible blindness worldwide; presents with painless, progressive blurred vision, GLARE/halos (especially night driving), reduced contrast, and a MYOPIC SHIFT (with nuclear sclerosis)","Morphologic types: NUCLEAR sclerotic (yellowing, myopic shift), CORTICAL (spoke-like), and POSTERIOR SUBCAPSULAR (glare, near-vision difficulty, faster - associated with steroids/diabetes/youth)","Mostly age-related; also metabolic (diabetes), traumatic, steroid-induced, and congenital"],"dx":["Slit-lamp grading of the cataract and its visual significance; measure best-corrected acuity and assess glare disability; evaluate the rest of the eye (macula, optic nerve) to set realistic expectations","BIOMETRY (axial length, keratometry) to calculate the intraocular lens power; assess for comorbidity affecting outcome (Fuchs, macular disease, pseudoexfoliation/zonular weakness)","Determine when surgery is warranted (visual/functional impairment)"],"tx":["Cataract SURGERY when the cataract impairs function/vision (or for a medical indication) - modern PHACOEMULSIFICATION with intraocular LENS (IOL) implantation is highly successful and usually outpatient","Refractive planning: monofocal, toric (astigmatism), or multifocal/EDOF IOLs based on the patient's goals; manage comorbid disease around surgery","Optimize the ocular surface (dry eye) preoperatively for accurate biometry and outcomes"],"tech":["Phacoemulsification: capsulorhexis, ultrasonic lens emulsification/aspiration, and IOL implantation in the capsular bag (femtosecond-laser-assisted in some settings); manage complications intraoperatively (posterior capsule rupture, dropped nucleus, zonular dialysis)","YAG laser posterior CAPSULOTOMY for later posterior capsular opacification","Pupil expansion/capsular support devices for complex cases (pseudoexfoliation, small pupil)"],"after":["Postoperative topical antibiotic/steroid drops and follow-up; watch for ENDOPHTHALMITIS (pain, dropping vision, hypopyon - urgent), intraocular pressure spikes, and CYSTOID MACULAR EDEMA","POSTERIOR CAPSULAR OPACIFICATION (the most common late issue - gradual blur months-years later) is treated with a quick YAG capsulotomy","Refractive follow-up (glasses for residual error/near); manage a dislocated IOL/late complications"],"pearls":["A nuclear cataract causes a myopic shift - the patient who suddenly reads without glasses ('second sight') may be developing one","Set expectations by examining the macula and optic nerve first - cataract surgery only helps if the rest of the eye can see","Gradual blur months to years after successful cataract surgery is usually posterior capsular opacification - a 5-minute YAG capsulotomy fixes it","Pain and dropping vision with a hypopyon after cataract surgery is endophthalmitis - an emergency, not normal recovery"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Cataract Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cataract","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539699/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Cataract Surgery","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-anterior-uveitis","name":"Anterior Uveitis (Iritis)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inflammation of the anterior uvea (iris/ciliary body): a painful red eye with PHOTOPHOBIA, CILIARY FLUSH (perilimbal redness), tearing, and often decreased vision - a sight-threatening cause of red eye","Hallmark exam findings: anterior chamber CELLS and FLARE, keratic precipitates (KPs) on the corneal endothelium, a small/poorly reactive pupil (miosis), posterior SYNECHIAE, and sometimes a HYPOPYON","May be acute/recurrent (often HLA-B27-associated) or chronic/granulomatous; can be unilateral or bilateral"],"dx":["Slit-lamp diagnosis of anterior chamber cells/flare and KPs; classify granulomatous (large 'mutton-fat' KPs - sarcoid, TB, syphilis, herpetic) vs non-granulomatous, and measure IOP (can be high or low)","Targeted WORKUP for recurrent, bilateral, granulomatous, or severe cases: HLA-B27 and associated conditions (ankylosing spondylitis, IBD, reactive/psoriatic arthritis), sarcoidosis (chest imaging/ACE), syphilis, TB, and herpetic causes","Dilated exam to assess for posterior involvement and complications"],"tx":["Topical CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g., prednisolone acetate, tapered by response) to control inflammation, plus a CYCLOPLEGIC (cyclopentolate/homatropine/atropine) for pain relief and to prevent/break posterior synechiae","Monitor and manage IOP (steroid response or trabeculitis); treat the underlying CAUSE (herpetic uveitis needs antivirals; infectious causes need targeted therapy; systemic disease needs systemic management)","Recurrent/severe/posterior disease may need periocular/systemic steroids or immunomodulatory therapy"],"tech":["Largely medical; periocular/intravitreal steroid injections or systemic immunosuppression for severe/recurrent disease","Manage complications (glaucoma surgery, cataract surgery once quiet, synechiae)","Rheumatology/uveitis specialist co-management for systemic disease"],"after":["TAPER steroids slowly to avoid rebound; monitor for and manage complications: secondary GLAUCOMA, CATARACT (from inflammation and steroids), cystoid macular edema, and band keratopathy","Investigate and treat the underlying systemic disease for recurrent cases; counsel on recurrence and prompt re-presentation","Long-term follow-up for chronic/recurrent uveitis"],"pearls":["Pain, photophobia, ciliary flush, and anterior chamber cells make iritis - a sight-threatening red eye that needs steroids and cycloplegia, not antibiotics","Cycloplegia does double duty - it relieves the ciliary-spasm pain and prevents/breaks posterior synechiae","Recurrent, bilateral, or granulomatous uveitis earns a systemic workup (HLA-B27 disease, sarcoid, syphilis, TB, herpes)","Watch the IOP and lens - uveitis and its steroid treatment both cause glaucoma and cataract over time"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Uveitis Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anterior Uveitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441909/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Anterior Uveitis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-posterior-uveitis","name":"Intermediate, Posterior & Panuveitis","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inflammation of the posterior segment: INTERMEDIATE uveitis (vitreous - vitritis, 'snowballs' and pars plana 'snowbanks', e.g., pars planitis - associated with MS and sarcoidosis), POSTERIOR uveitis (retina/choroid - retinitis/choroiditis), and PANUVEITIS (all segments)","Symptoms: floaters and blurred/decreased vision (less pain/redness than anterior uveitis); posterior/panuveitis can be vision-threatening via macular involvement","Important syndromes: sarcoidosis, Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (bilateral panuveitis + neuro/skin/auditory), Behcet, birdshot chorioretinopathy, and SYMPATHETIC OPHTHALMIA (after penetrating injury/surgery to the fellow eye)"],"dx":["Dilated fundus exam (vitritis, chorioretinal lesions), OCT (macular edema), and fluorescein/indocyanine-green ANGIOGRAPHY to characterize vascular leakage and choroidal lesions","Directed systemic WORKUP for non-infectious causes (sarcoid - chest imaging/ACE, HLA-A29 for birdshot, syphilis/TB always in the differential); rule out INFECTIOUS and masquerade (lymphoma) causes before immunosuppressing","Vitreous biopsy in atypical/refractory cases (exclude infection and intraocular lymphoma)"],"tx":["NON-infectious uveitis: corticosteroids (topical are inadequate for posterior disease - use periocular/intravitreal/systemic steroids) and IMMUNOMODULATORY therapy/biologics (methotrexate, mycophenolate, adalimumab) for chronic/severe disease to spare steroids","Treat the SPECIFIC entity (e.g., VKH and sympathetic ophthalmia need prompt aggressive immunosuppression to preserve vision)","Always EXCLUDE and treat infectious causes first (see the infectious uveitis entry) - steroids alone can worsen infection"],"tech":["Periocular/intravitreal corticosteroid injections and sustained-release steroid implants (fluocinolone/dexamethasone); diagnostic/therapeutic vitrectomy and vitreous biopsy","Manage complications surgically (cataract, glaucoma, epiretinal membrane)","Uveitis-specialist and rheumatology co-management for systemic immunosuppression"],"after":["Monitor and treat the vision-limiting complications: cystoid MACULAR EDEMA, cataract, glaucoma, epiretinal membrane, and choroidal neovascularization","Steroid-sparing immunomodulation with lab monitoring for chronic disease; treat the underlying systemic condition","Long-term surveillance for recurrence and drug toxicity"],"pearls":["Floaters and decreasing vision with a quiet-looking front of the eye can be posterior uveitis - examine the vitreous and retina","Topical steroids do not reach the back of the eye - posterior/panuveitis needs periocular, intravitreal, or systemic therapy","Always exclude infection and masquerade (lymphoma) before immunosuppressing - a vitreous biopsy is worthwhile in atypical cases","VKH and sympathetic ophthalmia are bilateral, vision-threatening panuveitides needing prompt aggressive immunosuppression"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Uveitis Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Posterior Uveitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK580525/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Intermediate/Posterior Uveitis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-infectious-uveitis","name":"Infectious Uveitis & Ocular Manifestations","sec":"ophtho","present":["Infectious causes of uveitis/retinitis that must be identified because treatment is ANTIMICROBIAL (and steroids alone can be harmful)","TOXOPLASMOSIS: the most common infectious posterior uveitis - a focal necrotizing retinochoroiditis, classically a new lesion beside an old pigmented scar with overlying vitritis ('headlight in the fog'); CMV RETINITIS: in the immunocompromised (AIDS with CD4<50, transplant) - hemorrhagic 'pizza-pie' retinitis","SYPHILIS ('the great masquerader' - can cause any uveitis, do an RPR/treponemal test in ALL uveitis), TUBERCULOSIS, and herpetic ACUTE RETINAL NECROSIS (HSV/VZV - rapidly progressive peripheral necrotizing retinitis)"],"dx":["Clinical pattern recognition plus targeted testing: toxoplasma serology, and aqueous/vitreous PCR for CMV/HSV/VZV/toxoplasma when needed; SYPHILIS and HIV testing in essentially all uveitis, and TB screening","Recognize the immune status (CMV and other opportunistic infections in the immunocompromised); ARN can occur in immunocompetent hosts","Fundus exam/imaging to characterize the retinitis and its progression"],"tx":["Pathogen-SPECIFIC therapy: TOXOPLASMOSIS - pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine (+ folinic acid) or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (treat vision-threatening/severe lesions; steroids only WITH antimicrobial cover); CMV retinitis - systemic/intravitreal ganciclovir/valganciclovir/foscarnet PLUS immune recovery (antiretroviral therapy)","SYPHILIS - treat as NEUROSYPHILIS (IV penicillin); TB - antitubercular therapy; ARN - prompt systemic/intravitreal ANTIVIRALS (acyclovir/valacyclovir) to protect the fellow eye","Add corticosteroids only under antimicrobial cover for inflammation"],"tech":["Aqueous/vitreous tap for PCR; intravitreal antiviral injections (CMV, ARN); laser/surgery for associated retinal detachment (ARN often detaches)","Manage immune reconstitution and systemic infection","ID co-management"],"after":["Toxoplasmosis: watch for RECURRENCE (new lesions at old scars); consider prophylaxis in recurrent/vision-threatening disease; CMV: continue therapy and restore immunity, monitor for immune recovery uveitis","ARN: high risk of retinal detachment - close surveillance; treat/monitor the fellow eye","Treat and follow the underlying systemic infection (HIV, syphilis, TB)"],"pearls":["Test for syphilis and HIV in ALL uveitis - syphilis masquerades as any pattern and is eminently treatable","A new retinochoroiditis lesion next to an old pigmented scar with vitritis ('headlight in fog') is toxoplasmosis","CMV retinitis means profound immunosuppression (CD4<50) - treat the eye and restore immunity","Do not give steroids alone for an infectious retinitis - cover with the appropriate antimicrobial first"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Infectious Uveitis Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ocular Toxoplasmosis / CMV Retinitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545285/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Infectious Uveitis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-diabetic-retinopathy","name":"Diabetic Retinopathy","sec":"ophtho","present":["A leading cause of vision loss in working-age adults, from chronic hyperglycemic microvascular damage; often ASYMPTOMATIC until advanced, which is why SCREENING is essential","NONPROLIFERATIVE (NPDR): microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots, and venous beading; PROLIFERATIVE (PDR): retinal/disc NEOVASCULARIZATION that bleeds (vitreous hemorrhage), scars, and causes tractional retinal detachment and neovascular glaucoma","MACULAR EDEMA (see separate entry) is the most common cause of vision loss and can occur at any stage"],"dx":["DILATED fundus exam (or validated retinal photography) with SCREENING intervals: type 1 - within ~5 years of diagnosis; type 2 - at diagnosis; and in PREGNANCY (can progress rapidly)","OCT (macular edema) and fluorescein angiography/wide-field imaging (ischemia, neovascularization); grade severity to set follow-up and treatment","Correlate with systemic control (A1c, BP, lipids, renal disease)"],"tx":["SYSTEMIC control is foundational: glycemia, blood pressure, and lipids slow progression - the retinopathy reflects overall vascular disease","PROLIFERATIVE disease: PANRETINAL PHOTOCOAGULATION (PRP) and/or anti-VEGF to induce regression of neovascularization; anti-VEGF also treats coexistent macular edema","VITRECTOMY for non-clearing vitreous hemorrhage or tractional retinal detachment; treat neovascular glaucoma (PRP + anti-VEGF + IOP control)"],"tech":["Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP); intravitreal anti-VEGF injections; pars plana VITRECTOMY (with endolaser/membrane peel) for vitreous hemorrhage/tractional detachment","Focal/grid laser and anti-VEGF for macular edema (see that entry)","Manage neovascular glaucoma"],"after":["LIFELONG surveillance at intervals set by severity; reinforce systemic control (the single biggest lever on progression)","Anti-VEGF requires ongoing repeat injections and monitoring; watch for progression to neovascular complications","Coordinate diabetes care (endocrinology/primary care) - eye disease predicts nephropathy/cardiovascular risk"],"pearls":["Diabetic retinopathy is asymptomatic until advanced - screening at the right intervals (and in pregnancy) catches treatable disease","Proliferative disease means neovascularization that bleeds and detaches - PRP and/or anti-VEGF induce regression","Systemic control (glucose, BP, lipids) is the foundation - the retina mirrors the vasculature everywhere","Diabetic retinopathy predicts systemic vascular disease (nephropathy, cardiovascular) - the eye is a window on the whole patient"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Diabetic Retinopathy Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Diabetic Retinopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560805/"},{"t":"DRCR Retina Network","u":"https://public.jaeb.org/drcrnet"}]},{"id":"ophtho-dme","name":"Diabetic Macular Edema","sec":"ophtho","present":["Retinal thickening/fluid at the MACULA from leaking diabetic microvasculature - the MOST COMMON cause of vision loss in diabetic retinopathy, and it can occur at any stage of retinopathy","Symptoms: central blurring, metamorphopsia (distortion), and reduced acuity; may be subtle early","Classified by OCT as center-INVOLVING (threatens acuity) vs non-center-involving"],"dx":["OCT is the key test - it detects and quantifies macular thickening/intraretinal fluid and guides treatment/monitoring; fluorescein angiography identifies leakage and ischemia","Determine center involvement and acuity to decide on treatment","Assess overall retinopathy severity and systemic control"],"tx":["Anti-VEGF INTRAVITREAL injections are first-line for center-involving DME with vision loss (DRCR protocols - aflibercept/ranibizumab/bevacizumab), improving vision in many","FOCAL/GRID LASER for non-center-involving or as adjunct; intravitreal CORTICOSTEROID implants (dexamethasone/fluocinolone) for select/refractory or pseudophakic eyes (watch IOP/cataract)","Optimize systemic control (glucose, BP, lipids) alongside"],"tech":["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections (repeated per treat-and-extend/PRN protocols); focal/grid laser photocoagulation; intravitreal steroid implant placement","OCT-guided retreatment decisions","Manage steroid-related IOP rise/cataract"],"after":["REPEATED injections and OCT monitoring over the long term (DME is a chronic, relapsing condition); set expectations for ongoing visits","Reinforce systemic control; monitor the fellow eye and overall retinopathy","Manage treatment-related issues (injection risks, steroid response)"],"pearls":["OCT is the workhorse for DME - it defines center involvement, guides anti-VEGF, and tracks response","Anti-VEGF is first-line for center-involving DME and can improve (not just stabilize) vision","DME can occur at ANY level of retinopathy - do not wait for proliferative disease to check the macula","Steroid implants help refractory/pseudophakic eyes but cost you IOP rises and cataract"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Diabetic Macular Edema Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Diabetic Macular Edema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK580523/"},{"t":"DRCR Retina Network Protocols","u":"https://public.jaeb.org/drcrnet"}]},{"id":"ophtho-amd","name":"Age-Related Macular Degeneration (Dry & Wet)","sec":"ophtho","present":["The leading cause of central vision loss in older adults; affects the MACULA (central vision), sparing the peripheral field","DRY (non-neovascular, ~90%): DRUSEN and retinal pigment epithelial changes, progressing in some to GEOGRAPHIC ATROPHY with gradual central vision loss; WET (neovascular, ~10% but most severe vision loss): choroidal NEOVASCULARIZATION with fluid/hemorrhage causing SUDDEN central distortion (metamorphopsia) and vision loss","Risk factors: age, smoking (major, modifiable), genetics, and cardiovascular risk"],"dx":["Dilated exam (drusen, pigmentary change, hemorrhage/fluid) with OCT (the key test - detects intraretinal/subretinal fluid of wet AMD and atrophy); OCT-angiography/fluorescein angiography confirm neovascularization","AMSLER GRID for self-monitoring of metamorphopsia/new distortion (prompts urgent evaluation for conversion to wet AMD)","Stage severity to guide supplements and monitoring"],"tx":["DRY AMD: AREDS2 vitamin SUPPLEMENTS (for intermediate/advanced disease) reduce progression risk; smoking CESSATION and lifestyle; monitor for conversion to wet - newer complement inhibitors (intravitreal) can slow geographic atrophy progression","WET AMD: intravitreal ANTI-VEGF injections (aflibercept/ranibizumab/bevacizumab/faricimab) are the mainstay - they preserve and often improve vision; prompt treatment matters","Low-vision rehabilitation for established central loss"],"tech":["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections (treat-and-extend/PRN) for wet AMD; intravitreal complement inhibitors for geographic atrophy","OCT-guided retreatment; photodynamic therapy in select cases","Low-vision aids and rehabilitation"],"after":["Wet AMD: ongoing repeat injections with OCT monitoring; the fellow eye is at risk (monitor with Amsler/OCT)","Dry AMD: periodic monitoring, AREDS2 supplements, and smoking cessation; watch for conversion to wet","Low-vision support and driving/functional counseling for central vision loss"],"pearls":["New central distortion (metamorphopsia) on the Amsler grid is conversion to WET AMD until proven otherwise - urgent OCT and anti-VEGF","Anti-VEGF transformed wet AMD from blinding to treatable - but it requires ongoing injections","AREDS2 supplements slow progression of intermediate/advanced DRY AMD (not a treatment for wet)","Smoking is the biggest modifiable risk factor - cessation is real AMD prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Age-Related Macular Degeneration Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Age-Related Macular Degeneration","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559253/"},{"t":"AREDS2 / AAO EyeWiki: AMD","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-retinal-detachment","name":"Retinal Detachment","sec":"ophtho","present":["Separation of the neurosensory retina from the underlying retinal pigment epithelium - a vision-threatening condition (urgent/emergent when the macula is still attached)","Three types: RHEGMATOGENOUS (most common - a retinal BREAK/tear lets fluid under the retina; presents with FLASHES, new FLOATERS, and a progressing 'CURTAIN' or field defect), TRACTIONAL (fibrovascular traction - proliferative diabetic retinopathy), and EXUDATIVE (fluid without a break - tumor, inflammation, malignant hypertension)","'MACULA-ON' (central vision intact) is a surgical emergency to preserve central vision; 'macula-off' is urgent but less time-critical for central acuity"],"dx":["DILATED fundus exam with SCLERAL DEPRESSION to find the detachment and the causative break(s); B-scan ULTRASOUND when the view is obscured (vitreous hemorrhage)","Determine type and MACULA status (on vs off) - this dictates urgency; identify all retinal breaks","Look for the cause in tractional (diabetes) and exudative (tumor/inflammation) detachments"],"tx":["Retinal TEARS without detachment: seal with LASER retinopexy or cryotherapy to prevent progression","RHEGMATOGENOUS detachment: SURGICAL repair - pneumatic RETINOPEXY (gas bubble + retinopexy for select superior detachments), SCLERAL BUCKLE, and/or pars plana VITRECTOMY (often with gas/oil tamponade); mac-on detachments go urgently","TRACTIONAL - vitrectomy to relieve traction (treat the diabetic disease); EXUDATIVE - treat the underlying cause (usually NOT primarily surgical)"],"tech":["Laser retinopexy/cryopexy for tears; pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckle, and pars plana vitrectomy (with endolaser and gas/silicone-oil tamponade) for detachment repair","Combined approaches for complex/PVR detachments","Silicone oil removal as a later procedure"],"after":["Strict POSITIONING (face-down or per bubble location) after gas/pneumatic procedures; avoid air travel/altitude with an intraocular gas bubble (dangerous IOP rise)","Monitor for RE-DETACHMENT and PROLIFERATIVE VITREORETINOPATHY (the main cause of surgical failure); visual recovery depends heavily on preoperative macula status","Fellow-eye and long-term surveillance"],"pearls":["Flashes, a shower of new floaters, and a progressing curtain over the vision is a retinal detachment - dilate and examine urgently","Macula-ON detachment is the emergency - repair before the central macula detaches to preserve reading vision","Never let a patient with an intraocular gas bubble fly or go to altitude - the bubble expands and spikes the IOP catastrophically","Seal retinal tears with laser/cryo before they detach - a cheap, quick, sight-saving step"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Retinal Detachment Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retinal Detachment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551502/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Retinal Detachment","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-rvo","name":"Retinal Vein Occlusion (CRVO & BRVO)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Obstruction of retinal venous outflow causing hemorrhage, edema, and ischemia; the second most common retinal vascular disease after diabetic retinopathy","CENTRAL RVO: painless vision loss with diffuse '4-quadrant' retinal hemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins, and disc edema ('blood and thunder' fundus); classified ISCHEMIC vs non-ischemic (ischemic CRVO risks neovascular glaucoma). BRANCH RVO: sectoral hemorrhages along a vein, typically at an arteriovenous crossing","Risk factors: HYPERTENSION, hyperlipidemia, diabetes, glaucoma, and (in the young) hypercoagulable states/inflammation"],"dx":["Dilated exam of the hemorrhage distribution; OCT for MACULAR EDEMA (the main cause of vision loss) and fluorescein angiography to assess ISCHEMIA and neovascularization","Determine ischemic vs non-ischemic (guides neovascular risk/monitoring)","Evaluate and treat systemic risk factors (BP, lipids, glucose); hypercoagulable/inflammatory workup in young/bilateral cases"],"tx":["MACULAR EDEMA: intravitreal ANTI-VEGF (first-line) and/or steroid implants to improve vision","NEOVASCULARIZATION (ischemic disease/neovascular glaucoma): PANRETINAL/sectoral PHOTOCOAGULATION plus anti-VEGF; manage neovascular glaucoma IOP","Control systemic risk factors (the underlying vascular disease); no proven benefit from routine anticoagulation"],"tech":["Intravitreal anti-VEGF/steroid injections for edema; panretinal or sectoral laser photocoagulation for neovascularization; manage neovascular glaucoma","OCT-guided retreatment","Systemic risk-factor management"],"after":["MONITOR ischemic CRVO closely for anterior-segment NEOVASCULARIZATION and NEOVASCULAR GLAUCOMA (the '90-day glaucoma') and treat promptly; ongoing anti-VEGF for recurrent edema","Manage cardiovascular risk factors (RVO is a marker of systemic vascular disease and stroke risk)","Fellow-eye and long-term surveillance"],"pearls":["A 'blood and thunder' fundus with 4 quadrants of hemorrhage is a central retinal vein occlusion","Macular edema (treated with anti-VEGF) is the main cause of vision loss; ischemia is the main cause of neovascular glaucoma","Watch ischemic CRVO for neovascular glaucoma - the classic '90-day glaucoma' - and treat with PRP/anti-VEGF","RVO is a vascular-disease marker - manage the blood pressure, lipids, and glucose, not just the eye"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Retinal Vein Occlusion Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retinal Vein Occlusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525985/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Retinal Vein Occlusion","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-pvd-tears","name":"Posterior Vitreous Detachment, Retinal Tears & Flashes/Floaters","sec":"ophtho","present":["Posterior VITREOUS detachment (PVD): age-related separation of the vitreous gel from the retina - extremely common and usually benign, presenting with new FLOATERS (a Weiss ring), and FLASHES (photopsia) from vitreoretinal traction","The critical concern is that the traction of a PVD can cause a RETINAL TEAR (and subsequent detachment) - so acute-onset flashes/floaters require a dilated exam to exclude a tear","WARNING signs of progression to detachment: a shower of many floaters, a curtain/shadow in the peripheral field, or vision loss"],"dx":["DILATED fundus exam with SCLERAL DEPRESSION to examine the peripheral retina for tears/holes; B-scan if the view is poor (vitreous hemorrhage - which raises tear likelihood)","Assess for pigment or hemorrhage in the vitreous (tobacco dust/Shafer sign suggests a retinal break)","Re-examine when symptoms change/worsen"],"tx":["Uncomplicated PVD (no tear): observation and reassurance - floaters usually diminish/become less noticeable over time","RETINAL TEAR: prompt LASER retinopexy or cryotherapy to wall off the break and prevent detachment","Persistent, visually significant floaters: rarely, vitrectomy or laser vitreolysis in selected cases"],"tech":["Laser retinopexy or cryopexy to treat retinal tears; vitrectomy for select disabling floaters or non-clearing hemorrhage","Scleral depression exam technique to detect peripheral breaks","Repeat examination for evolving symptoms"],"after":["RETURN PRECAUTIONS are essential: teach the patient to return immediately for a sudden increase in floaters/flashes or a curtain in the vision (signs of a tear/detachment)","Re-examine at an interval (a tear can appear days-weeks after PVD onset), especially with vitreous hemorrhage or pigment","Fellow-eye counseling (PVD often becomes bilateral)"],"pearls":["Acute flashes and new floaters need a dilated exam with scleral depression - to catch the retinal tear before it detaches","Vitreous pigment/hemorrhage (Shafer sign) with PVD markedly raises the odds of a retinal break - hunt for it","Uncomplicated PVD is benign and reassurance suffices - but a tear must be lasered promptly","Teach the warning signs (a curtain, a shower of floaters) - a tear can appear after the first exam is normal"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Posterior Vitreous Detachment / Retinal Tear PPP","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Posterior Vitreous Detachment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK580524/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: PVD & Retinal Breaks","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-hypertensive-retinopathy","name":"Hypertensive Retinopathy","sec":"ophtho","present":["Retinal microvascular changes from systemic HYPERTENSION - a window on the severity and chronicity of blood-pressure disease (this content overlaps the internal-medicine hypertension framing)","Chronic changes: arteriolar NARROWING, arteriovenous NICKING (at crossings), copper/silver wiring, and (with more severe disease) flame HEMORRHAGES, COTTON-WOOL spots, hard exudates, and a MACULAR STAR","MALIGNANT hypertension: bilateral optic disc EDEMA with retinal hemorrhages/exudates - a hypertensive EMERGENCY with end-organ damage"],"dx":["FUNDOSCOPY reveals the changes; grade severity (e.g., Keith-Wagener-Barker/modified) - though the key clinical branch point is distinguishing chronic changes from malignant hypertension (disc edema)","Correlate with blood pressure and assess for other end-organ damage (renal, cardiac, neurologic)","Distinguish from diabetic retinopathy and other causes of retinal hemorrhages/cotton-wool spots"],"tx":["Treat the systemic HYPERTENSION - the retinopathy improves as blood pressure is controlled; MALIGNANT hypertension with disc edema is an EMERGENCY needing controlled BP lowering and evaluation for end-organ damage","Manage associated retinal vascular complications (vein/artery occlusions, macular edema, ischemic optic neuropathy) if they occur","Cardiovascular risk-factor management overall"],"tech":["No primary ocular procedure - management is systemic; treat any secondary retinal vascular complication (anti-VEGF/laser for occlusion-related edema)","Fundus photography for documentation/monitoring","Coordinate with medicine for BP control"],"after":["Ongoing BP control and cardiovascular risk management (primary care/medicine); the retinal findings track with systemic disease control","Monitor for and treat complications (occlusions, macular edema); repeat exams as indicated","Patient education on hypertension as a systemic, sight- and life-threatening disease"],"pearls":["AV nicking and arteriolar narrowing signal chronic hypertension - the retina records the blood-pressure history","Bilateral disc edema with hemorrhages and exudates is malignant hypertension - a hypertensive emergency, not just a retinopathy","Treat the blood pressure and the retinopathy follows - the eye is a barometer of systemic control","Hypertension also causes vein/artery occlusions and ischemic optic neuropathy - the retinopathy is one of several ocular consequences"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Hypertensive Retinopathy Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertensive Retinopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525980/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Hypertensive Retinopathy","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-hereditary-retinal","name":"Hereditary Retinal Degenerations & Macular Dystrophies","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inherited photoreceptor/retinal degenerations. RETINITIS PIGMENTOSA (RP): the prototypical rod-cone dystrophy - NIGHT BLINDNESS (nyctalopia) and progressive PERIPHERAL field loss ('tunnel vision'), with a classic triad of BONE-SPICULE pigmentation, attenuated retinal vessels, and a waxy pale disc","Macular DYSTROPHIES: STARGARDT disease (the most common juvenile macular dystrophy - central vision loss with retinal flecks and a 'dark/silent choroid'), Best vitelliform dystrophy, and cone/cone-rod dystrophies","May be isolated or syndromic (e.g., Usher - RP + hearing loss; Bardet-Biedl)"],"dx":["ELECTRORETINOGRAM (ERG) documents photoreceptor dysfunction (reduced/extinguished responses - rod-cone pattern in RP); fundus autofluorescence and OCT characterize the degeneration","GENETIC testing identifies the mutation (crucial for prognosis, inheritance counseling, and gene-therapy eligibility); assess for syndromic features (hearing, systemic)","Visual field testing tracks peripheral loss in RP"],"tx":["Largely SUPPORTIVE historically - LOW-VISION rehabilitation, and GENETIC counseling; caution with high-dose vitamin A supplementation (specific genotypes may worsen)","GENE THERAPY has arrived: voretigene neparvovec (subretinal AAV) for RPE65-mediated inherited retinal dystrophy (Leber congenital amaurosis/RP) - the first approved ocular gene therapy","Manage associated complications (cataract, cystoid macular edema in RP) and syndromic features"],"tech":["Subretinal GENE THERAPY (voretigene) for RPE65 mutations; retinal-prosthesis/emerging therapies in trials","Cataract surgery and CME management in RP","Genetic testing and counseling pathways"],"after":["LOW-VISION rehabilitation and support services; GENETIC counseling for the patient and family (inheritance, family screening)","Long-term surveillance for complications and disease progression; connect to clinical trials/registries","Psychosocial support for progressive vision loss"],"pearls":["Night blindness with progressive tunnel vision and bone-spicule pigmentation is retinitis pigmentosa - confirm with ERG and genetic testing","Genetic testing is now actionable - RPE65 disease has an approved gene therapy (voretigene)","Stargardt is the common juvenile macular dystrophy - central vision loss with flecks and a dark choroid on angiography","Look for syndromes - RP plus hearing loss is Usher; genetic counseling matters for the whole family"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Inherited Retinal Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retinitis Pigmentosa","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519518/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Inherited Retinal Degenerations","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-optic-neuritis","name":"Optic Neuritis","sec":"ophtho","present":["Inflammatory demyelination of the optic nerve (this content shares with the neurology entry, framed for the ophthalmology reader): SUBACUTE monocular vision loss over hours-days with PAIN ON EYE MOVEMENT, red DESATURATION (dyschromatopsia), and a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)","Typical (MS-associated) optic neuritis is unilateral, retrobulbar (a NORMAL disc in ~2/3, papillitis with a swollen disc in the rest), and mild-moderate; a common first presentation of multiple sclerosis","ATYPICAL features (bilateral, severe, marked disc swelling, poor recovery, no pain, older/pediatric) suggest NMOSD, MOGAD, or other causes"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (RAPD, central field defect, color loss); MRI of the brain and orbits with contrast to assess MS risk (white-matter lesions - the Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial) and the optic nerve","Test AQP4-IgG and MOG-IgG for atypical presentations (bilateral, severe, poor recovery) to catch NMOSD/MOGAD; exclude infectious/inflammatory and compressive/ischemic causes","Visual fields and OCT to document and follow"],"tx":["IV corticosteroids (high-dose methylprednisolone) SPEED recovery but do not change final acuity (ONTT); AVOID low-dose oral prednisone MONOTHERAPY (associated with more recurrences in the ONTT)","Atypical/NMOSD/MOGAD-related disease: consider plasma exchange for severe cases and start disease-appropriate maintenance immunotherapy","If the MRI shows demyelinating lesions, discuss MS disease-modifying therapy with neurology"],"tech":["No ocular procedure - management is medical (IV steroids, plasma exchange for severe atypical cases)","Neurology/neuro-ophthalmology co-management","Serial OCT/fields to monitor"],"after":["MS RISK counseling and neurology referral for disease-modifying therapy when the MRI is abnormal; most typical cases recover substantial vision over weeks","Monitor for recurrence and for evolution to NMOSD/MOGAD (which are more severe and treated differently)","Manage residual deficits and any relapses"],"pearls":["Painful vision loss with an RAPD and red desaturation in a young adult is optic neuritis - and often the first sign of MS","Steroids speed recovery but do not change the final acuity - and low-dose oral steroid ALONE raised recurrence in the ONTT","Atypical features (bilateral, severe, poor recovery) mean test AQP4/MOG - it may be NMOSD or MOGAD, not MS","The brain MRI stratifies MS risk and drives the disease-modifying-therapy conversation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAN Optic Neuritis (ONTT) Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Optic Neuritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557853/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Optic Neuritis","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-papilledema","name":"Papilledema & Optic Disc Edema (Raised Intracranial Pressure)","sec":"ophtho","present":["PAPILLEDEMA specifically means optic disc swelling from RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (by definition bilateral) - it must be distinguished from other causes of a swollen disc (papillitis, AION, malignant hypertension, infiltration)","Symptoms of raised ICP: headache (worse lying down/morning), TRANSIENT VISUAL OBSCURATIONS (brief graying with position change), pulsatile tinnitus, diplopia (CN VI palsy - a false-localizing sign), and (late) vision loss; an ENLARGED BLIND SPOT on fields","Causes: intracranial mass/hemorrhage, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, and IDIOPATHIC INTRACRANIAL HYPERTENSION (IIH - typically young, obese women)"],"dx":["Recognize bilateral disc edema (blurred margins, hyperemia, obscured vessels, hemorrhages) and distinguish PSEUDOpapilledema (optic disc drusen) with OCT/ultrasound/autofluorescence","URGENT NEUROIMAGING first (MRI + MR VENOGRAPHY to exclude a mass and venous sinus thrombosis), THEN lumbar puncture with an OPENING PRESSURE and CSF analysis if imaging is normal (do not LP before imaging)","Formal VISUAL FIELDS to quantify and monitor optic nerve function (the key measure of threat to vision)"],"tx":["Treat the underlying CAUSE (tumor, venous thrombosis, infection); IIH - WEIGHT LOSS and ACETAZOLAMIDE (reduces CSF production), with escalation for vision loss","VISION-THREATENING/progressive IIH: surgical intervention - OPTIC NERVE SHEATH FENESTRATION (protects the nerve) or CSF diversion (VP/LP shunt) and venous sinus stenting in selected cases (this overlaps the neurosurgical management)","Serial monitoring of vision/fields drives escalation"],"tech":["Optic nerve sheath fenestration for progressive vision loss; CSF shunting and venous sinus stenting (neurosurgery/neuro-IR) for refractory raised ICP","Lumbar puncture (diagnostic/therapeutic) with opening pressure","Serial perimetry and OCT to track the optic nerve"],"after":["Serial VISUAL FIELD and OCT surveillance is essential - vision loss from papilledema can be insidious and preventable; escalate promptly if fields worsen","Manage the underlying condition long-term (weight management in IIH, treat the mass/thrombosis)","Fellow-eye and ongoing neurologic follow-up"],"pearls":["Papilledema = disc swelling from raised ICP (bilateral) - do not use the term for other causes of disc edema","Image (MRI + MRV) BEFORE the lumbar puncture - you must exclude a mass and venous sinus thrombosis first","Transient visual obscurations and an enlarged blind spot are early clues - serial fields catch the vision-threatening progression","Distinguish true papilledema from optic disc drusen (pseudopapilledema) with OCT/ultrasound before triggering a big workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / NANOS Papilledema Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Papilledema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430879/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Papilledema","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-naion","name":"Non-Arteritic Anterior Ischemic Optic Neuropathy (NAION)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Infarction of the anterior optic nerve head from small-vessel hypoperfusion (this content shares with the neurology framing): SUDDEN, PAINLESS, usually monocular vision loss, classically with an ALTITUDINAL (superior or inferior) visual field defect and an RAPD","The disc shows HYPEREMIC (not pallid) edema, and the fellow eye typically has a small, crowded, cupless disc - the 'DISC AT RISK'","Vasculopathic risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, sleep apnea, nocturnal hypotension); a well-recognized association with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from the sudden painless loss, altitudinal field defect, RAPD, and hyperemic disc edema; examine the fellow eye for the crowded 'disc at risk'","CRITICALLY, exclude ARTERITIC AION (giant cell arteritis) in patients >50 - ESR/CRP and GCA symptoms - because that is the treatable, fellow-eye-threatening mimic","Assess and manage vasculopathic risk factors (including sleep apnea)"],"tx":["No proven effective acute treatment reverses NAION - management is RISK-FACTOR modification (control hypertension/diabetes/lipids, treat sleep apnea, AVOID nocturnal hypotension by not dosing antihypertensives at bedtime)","Do NOT withhold GCA treatment while distinguishing arteritic disease - if any doubt in an older patient, treat as arteritic until excluded","Counsel on the risk to the fellow eye and modifiable factors"],"tech":["No surgical treatment (the Ischemic Optic Neuropathy Decompression Trial showed optic nerve sheath decompression is NOT beneficial and may be harmful in NAION)","Risk-factor management and sleep study as indicated","Neuro-ophthalmology follow-up"],"after":["Modify vascular risk factors to reduce fellow-eye risk (the fellow eye is at elevated risk over years); manage sleep apnea","Counsel that visual recovery is limited/variable and that the goal is protecting the other eye","Monitor both eyes long-term"],"pearls":["Sudden painless vision loss with an altitudinal field defect and a HYPEREMIC (not pale) swollen disc is NAION","Always exclude arteritic AION (GCA) in the older patient first - it is the treatable, fellow-eye-threatening cause","Do not dose blood-pressure meds at bedtime in these patients - nocturnal hypotension is a precipitant","Optic nerve sheath decompression does not help NAION (IONDT) - there is no surgical fix, only risk-factor control"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / NANOS Ischemic Optic Neuropathy Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anterior Ischemic Optic Neuropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430893/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: NAION","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-diplopia-cn-palsies","name":"Diplopia & Ocular Motor Cranial Nerve Palsies (III, IV, VI)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Double vision (this content shares with the neurology entry, framed for the ophthalmology reader): BINOCULAR diplopia (resolves when either eye is covered) localizes to the ocular motor system; MONOCULAR diplopia (persists with one eye covered) is an ocular/refractive problem (dry eye, cataract, astigmatism)","CN III palsy: ptosis and a 'DOWN-AND-OUT' eye - the PUPIL is the alarm (a painful, pupil-INVOLVING third-nerve palsy is a POSTERIOR COMMUNICATING ARTERY ANEURYSM until proven otherwise)","CN IV (vertical diplopia worse on downgaze/reading, compensatory head tilt) and CN VI (horizontal diplopia, esotropia, impaired abduction - a false-localizing sign of raised ICP)"],"dx":["Confirm binocular vs monocular; assess motility, alignment, lid, and PUPIL; the pupil rule in CN III palsy drives urgency (pupil-involving/painful -> urgent vascular imaging for aneurysm; pupil-sparing isolated III in a vasculopath is often ischemic and observed)","Consider MYASTHENIA GRAVIS (fatigable, pupil-sparing, variable - can mimic any pattern) and thyroid eye disease (restrictive); neuroimaging for multiple/atypical/progressive palsies and any pupil-involving CN III","Screen giant cell arteritis in older patients with an ischemic palsy"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE: secure an aneurysm causing a third-nerve palsy (emergency - neurosurgery/neuro-IR); microvascular ischemic palsies typically recover over weeks-months with vascular risk-factor management","Symptomatic relief while awaiting recovery: occlusion (patch) or PRISMS for diplopia; treat myasthenia/thyroid eye disease as indicated","STRABISMUS surgery for stable, non-resolving misalignment"],"tech":["Prisms and occlusion for symptomatic relief; STRABISMUS (muscle) surgery for stable deviations that do not resolve","Neuroimaging/angiography workup for compressive causes","Neuro-ophthalmology co-management"],"after":["Monitor microvascular palsies for the expected RECOVERY over ~3 months; re-image if it does not recover as expected or new signs appear","Defer strabismus surgery until the deviation is STABLE (usually ~6 months); manage diplopia in the interim","Manage the underlying cause and vascular risk factors"],"pearls":["Binocular vs monocular is the first fork - binocular is neurologic/ocular-motor, monocular is an eyeball/refraction problem","The pupil makes the third-nerve decision - pupil-involving or painful means image urgently for a posterior communicating artery aneurysm","A sixth-nerve palsy can falsely localize raised ICP - look for papilledema and a cause","Fatigable, variable, pupil-sparing ophthalmoplegia that crosses single-nerve boundaries is myasthenia gravis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / NANOS Diplopia & Ocular Motor Palsy Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Oculomotor Nerve Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430919/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Cranial Nerve Palsies","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-pupillary-abnormalities","name":"Pupillary Abnormalities (Anisocoria, RAPD, Horner, Adie)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Abnormal pupils localize disease along the afferent (vision-in) or efferent (motor) pathways - a high-yield bedside examination","ANISOCORIA (unequal pupils): determine whether the LARGER or SMALLER pupil is abnormal by comparing the difference in LIGHT vs DARK - a defect greater in DARK points to the small pupil (sympathetic - HORNER); greater in LIGHT points to the large pupil (parasympathetic - CN III palsy, ADIE tonic pupil, or pharmacologic)","RAPD (Marcus Gunn pupil): an AFFERENT defect from optic nerve or extensive retinal disease - not a cause of anisocoria"],"dx":["Examine pupils in LIGHT and DARK and check the light reflex and near response; HORNER syndrome (miosis + ptosis + anhidrosis) is confirmed pharmacologically (apraclonidine reversal) and requires localization (carotid dissection - painful Horner is an emergency; apical lung tumor; neuroblastoma in children)","ADIE tonic pupil: a dilated pupil with light-near DISSOCIATION, sectoral iris palsy, and cholinergic DENERVATION SUPERSENSITIVITY (constricts to dilute pilocarpine); pharmacologic mydriasis does NOT constrict to pilocarpine","Argyll Robertson pupils (small, light-near dissociation) suggest neurosyphilis"],"tx":["Treat the underlying CAUSE - the pupil is a clue, not usually the disease: HORNER -> urgent workup for carotid dissection (with pain), apical lung mass, or (in a child) neuroblastoma; CN III palsy -> aneurysm workup if pupil-involving","Adie tonic pupil is benign (often with absent reflexes - Adie syndrome) - reassurance +/- symptomatic dilute pilocarpine for photophobia","Pharmacologic and physiologic anisocoria need no treatment once identified"],"tech":["Pharmacologic pupil testing (apraclonidine/cocaine for Horner; dilute pilocarpine for Adie); imaging directed by the localization (CTA/MRA for Horner/aneurysm)","No primary ocular procedure - workup is diagnostic","Neuro-ophthalmology/relevant specialist referral"],"after":["Complete the cause-specific workup (carotid imaging for painful Horner, chest imaging for a Horner with a smoking history, aneurysm evaluation for pupil-involving CN III)","Reassure for benign entities (Adie, physiologic anisocoria); follow as indicated","Manage the identified underlying disease"],"pearls":["Compare anisocoria in light and dark - worse in dark implicates the small pupil (Horner); worse in light implicates the large pupil (III palsy/Adie/pharmacologic)","A PAINFUL Horner syndrome is a carotid dissection until proven otherwise - image urgently","Adie tonic pupil shows light-near dissociation and constricts to DILUTE pilocarpine (denervation supersensitivity) - pharmacologic dilation does not","An RAPD is an afferent (optic nerve/retina) sign and does not cause unequal pupil size"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / NANOS Pupillary Disorders Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anisocoria / Horner Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430887/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Pupillary Abnormalities","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-strabismus","name":"Strabismus (Esotropia & Exotropia)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Ocular MISALIGNMENT: ESOtropia (inward), EXOtropia (outward), and hyper/hypotropia (vertical); may be constant or intermittent, and comitant (equal in all gazes) or incomitant (varies - suggests a nerve palsy/restriction)","Childhood types: infantile (congenital) esotropia, ACCOMMODATIVE esotropia (from uncorrected hyperopia - crossing when focusing), and intermittent exotropia; adult strabismus may be decompensated childhood disease or from a new palsy/restriction","Key consequences in children: AMBLYOPIA and loss of binocularity/stereopsis; distinguish true strabismus from PSEUDOSTRABISMUS (wide epicanthal folds simulating esotropia)"],"dx":["Measure alignment: the CORNEAL LIGHT REFLEX (Hirschberg) and the COVER/UNCOVER and alternate cover tests to detect and quantify tropia/phoria; assess motility for incomitance (palsy/restriction)","CYCLOPLEGIC REFRACTION is essential in children (uncovers hyperopia driving accommodative esotropia); assess for amblyopia and rule out an organic cause (e.g., retinoblastoma presenting with strabismus - always examine the fundus)","New/incomitant strabismus in adults prompts a workup for the underlying cause"],"tx":["Correct the refractive error first - ACCOMMODATIVE esotropia often resolves with the full hyperopic GLASSES correction (a non-surgical cure)","Treat AMBLYOPIA (patching/atropine) before or alongside alignment; prisms for small/adult deviations","STRABISMUS SURGERY (recess/resect the extraocular muscles) for significant residual/non-accommodative deviation to restore alignment and, in children, binocular potential"],"tech":["Strabismus (extraocular muscle) surgery - recession (weaken) and resection (strengthen) procedures, adjustable sutures in adults; botulinum toxin to muscles in select cases","Glasses/prisms for accommodative and small deviations","Cycloplegic refraction and sensorimotor evaluation"],"after":["Continue AMBLYOPIA treatment and monitor alignment/binocularity; strabismus can recur or over/undercorrect, sometimes needing repeat surgery","Ongoing refraction and follow-up through visual maturation in children","Adult patients: monitor for diplopia and functional/psychosocial outcomes"],"pearls":["Always do a cycloplegic refraction in childhood esotropia - accommodative esotropia can be cured with glasses alone","Strabismus in a child mandates a fundus exam - do not miss retinoblastoma or another organic cause presenting as an eye turn","Treat the amblyopia, not just the alignment - surgery straightens the eyes but the vision needs patching/penalization","Pseudostrabismus (epicanthal folds) is common and benign - the corneal light reflex is centered, unlike true strabismus"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAPOS Strabismus Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Strabismus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560782/"},{"t":"AAPOS Strabismus Resources","u":"https://www.aapos.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-amblyopia","name":"Amblyopia","sec":"ophtho","present":["Reduced best-corrected vision in one (or both) eyes from ABNORMAL visual development in childhood WITHOUT a structural cause commensurate with the loss - the leading cause of monocular vision loss in children/young adults, and TREATABLE if caught within the critical period","Mechanisms: STRABISMIC (constant eye turn), REFRACTIVE (anisometropic - unequal refractive error, or high bilateral ametropic error), and DEPRIVATION (a media opacity - congenital cataract, ptosis covering the pupil - the most severe/urgent)","The developing brain suppresses the input from the poorer eye"],"dx":["Age-appropriate VISION testing showing reduced acuity not explained by structural disease; cover test and CYCLOPLEGIC REFRACTION to find strabismus and refractive causes","CRUCIALLY, rule out an ORGANIC cause for the reduced vision (retinoblastoma, optic nerve/retinal disease) before attributing it to amblyopia","Detect deprivation causes (cataract, ptosis) that need urgent removal"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE first: correct the refractive error (glasses), and REMOVE deprivation urgently (congenital cataract surgery, ptosis repair) within the critical window","Then PENALIZE the better eye to force use of the amblyopic eye: PATCHING or ATROPINE penalization (blurs the good eye) - the mainstay of amblyopia therapy","Earlier treatment yields better outcomes; some improvement is possible into later childhood"],"tech":["Occlusion (patching) and pharmacologic (atropine) penalization; treat the underlying cause procedurally when needed (cataract extraction, ptosis repair)","Refractive correction and adherence support","Monitoring visits to titrate therapy"],"after":["Monitor for treatment response and for REVERSE amblyopia (over-patching harming the good eye); maintain therapy and watch for RECURRENCE after stopping","Continue through visual maturation; reinforce adherence (the hardest part of amblyopia care)","Long-term follow-up of the treated eye"],"pearls":["Amblyopia is treatable if caught early - the critical period is why pediatric vision screening matters","Rule out an organic cause before labeling reduced vision as amblyopia - retinoblastoma can hide behind a 'lazy eye'","Fix the cause first (glasses, cataract removal, ptosis repair), THEN penalize the good eye (patch/atropine)","Watch for reverse amblyopia and recurrence - patching can over-treat, and gains can regress after stopping"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAPOS Amblyopia Preferred Practice Pattern","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amblyopia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430890/"},{"t":"PEDIG Amblyopia Studies","u":"https://public.jaeb.org/pedig"}]},{"id":"ophtho-rop","name":"Retinopathy of Prematurity","sec":"ophtho","present":["A vasoproliferative retinopathy of PREMATURE, LOW-BIRTH-WEIGHT infants: incomplete retinal vascularization at birth followed by abnormal NEOVASCULARIZATION that can progress to fibrosis, tractional retinal DETACHMENT, and blindness","Risk factors: low gestational age and birth weight, and supplemental OXYGEN exposure; a leading cause of preventable childhood blindness","Described by ZONE (I-III, how far vascularization has progressed), STAGE (1-5, severity of the vascular response), and PLUS disease (dilation/tortuosity of posterior vessels - a marker of activity/severity)"],"dx":["Protocol-based SCREENING dilated exams (by an ophthalmologist) for at-risk infants (e.g., birth weight <=1500 g or gestational age <=30-31 weeks, or per criteria), beginning at defined postnatal/postmenstrual ages and repeated at intervals set by findings","Document zone, stage, and plus disease; identify 'type 1' (treatment-warranted) disease","Wide-field imaging assists documentation/telemedicine screening"],"tx":["Treatment-warranted (type 1) ROP: LASER photocoagulation of the avascular peripheral retina (ablates the ischemic drive) - the standard; intravitreal ANTI-VEGF is an alternative/adjunct (especially aggressive posterior/zone I disease), with attention to systemic and recurrence considerations","Advanced disease with detachment: vitreoretinal SURGERY (vitrectomy/scleral buckle) - outcomes are guarded","Careful, monitored oxygen use in the nursery is preventive"],"tech":["Peripheral retinal LASER photocoagulation; intravitreal anti-VEGF injection; vitrectomy/scleral buckle for detachment","Serial screening exams with scleral depression/imaging","Neonatal-team coordination for oxygen management"],"after":["Continue SCREENING until retinal vascularization is complete/mature or disease regresses; ANTI-VEGF-treated eyes need PROLONGED follow-up (late reactivation/recurrence)","Long-term ophthalmic surveillance - survivors have higher rates of myopia, strabismus, amblyopia, and later retinal detachment","Family counseling and developmental follow-up"],"pearls":["ROP is described by zone, stage, and plus disease - and 'plus' disease (posterior vessel dilation/tortuosity) signals the disease needing treatment","Protocol-driven screening of premature infants is what prevents blindness - the exam must happen on schedule","Laser ablation of the avascular retina is standard; anti-VEGF is an option but needs prolonged follow-up for recurrence","ROP survivors need lifelong eye care - myopia, strabismus, amblyopia, and late detachment are common"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAP / AAPOS ROP Screening Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retinopathy of Prematurity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562319/"},{"t":"AAP Policy on ROP Screening","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-pediatric-cataract-leukocoria","name":"Pediatric Cataract & Leukocoria","sec":"ophtho","present":["LEUKOCORIA (a white pupillary reflex - an abnormal/absent red reflex) is a critical pediatric sign with a can't-miss differential led by RETINOBLASTOMA (a life-threatening malignancy)","Differential of leukocoria: RETINOBLASTOMA, congenital CATARACT, retinopathy of prematurity, Coats disease, persistent fetal vasculature (PHPV), and toxocariasis","Congenital/pediatric CATARACT causes deprivation AMBLYOPIA and, if dense/central, must be removed URGENTLY (within weeks) to allow visual development"],"dx":["RED REFLEX screening (Bruckner test) in every newborn/child - an abnormal, asymmetric, or white reflex demands prompt referral; dilated exam and often EXAM UNDER ANESTHESIA","Ultrasound/MRI to characterize a mass (retinoblastoma - calcification) and exclude malignancy; do NOT biopsy a suspected retinoblastoma (seeding risk)","Workup for the cause of congenital cataract (metabolic, infectious - TORCH, genetic) and assess for bilaterality/family history (retinoblastoma - RB1)"],"tx":["RETINOBLASTOMA: urgent referral to ocular oncology - treatment (chemoreduction, focal therapy, intra-arterial/intravitreal chemotherapy, enucleation for advanced) prioritizes LIFE, then eye, then vision","Congenital CATARACT: timely lensectomy with aphakic correction (contact lens/glasses, or IOL in older children) and aggressive AMBLYOPIA therapy - the surgery is only half the battle","Treat other causes (Coats - laser/anti-VEGF; ROP - see that entry)"],"tech":["Lensectomy (+/- IOL) for cataract with subsequent optical correction; ocular-oncology procedures for retinoblastoma (intra-arterial/intravitreal chemo, laser/cryo, plaque, enucleation)","Exam under anesthesia for diagnosis/monitoring","Amblyopia management post-cataract surgery"],"after":["Intensive AMBLYOPIA therapy and optical correction after cataract surgery (deprivation amblyopia is severe); monitor for glaucoma (a common post-pediatric-cataract complication)","RETINOBLASTOMA: genetic counseling (RB1 - heritable/bilateral, second-cancer risk), systemic surveillance, and long-term oncology follow-up","Lifelong ophthalmic follow-up"],"pearls":["An absent or white red reflex (leukocoria) is retinoblastoma until proven otherwise - refer urgently and do not biopsy","Congenital cataract is a time-critical emergency for VISION - dense central cataracts must come out within weeks to prevent deprivation amblyopia","After pediatric cataract surgery, the amblyopia therapy and glaucoma surveillance are as important as the operation","Bilateral retinoblastoma is heritable (RB1) - genetics counseling and second-cancer surveillance are essential"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAPOS Pediatric Cataract & Leukocoria Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Leukocoria / Retinoblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544197/"},{"t":"AAPOS Pediatric Ophthalmology Resources","u":"https://www.aapos.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-congenital-pediatric","name":"Congenital & Pediatric Conditions (NLD Obstruction, Congenital Glaucoma, Ptosis)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Common pediatric conditions the resident must recognize. Congenital NASOLACRIMAL DUCT OBSTRUCTION (NLDO): chronic tearing (epiphora) and mucoid discharge/matting from a blocked tear duct - very common and usually SELF-RESOLVING by 12 months","Primary congenital GLAUCOMA: the classic triad of EPIPHORA, PHOTOPHOBIA, and blepharospasm, with BUPHTHALMOS (enlarged eye), corneal clouding/edema, and Haab striae - a surgical emergency for vision","Congenital PTOSIS: a drooping lid that is AMBLYOGENIC if it covers the visual axis or induces astigmatism/a chin-up head posture"],"dx":["NLDO is clinical (tearing/discharge with a patent-appearing eye; dye disappearance test); distinguish from the sight-threatening causes of a tearing infant (congenital glaucoma)","Congenital glaucoma: EXAM UNDER ANESTHESIA with IOP measurement, corneal diameter (megalocornea/buphthalmos), and gonioscopy/optic nerve assessment - tearing + photophobia + a big cloudy cornea is glaucoma until proven otherwise","Ptosis: measure the margin-reflex distance and levator function; assess for amblyopia and associated syndromes (e.g., Marcus Gunn jaw-winking, CN III palsy, Horner)"],"tx":["NLDO: conservative - lacrimal sac MASSAGE (Crigler) and hygiene; most resolve by age 1; PROBING (+/- irrigation, intubation, balloon) for persistent obstruction beyond ~12 months or recurrent dacryocystitis","Congenital GLAUCOMA: SURGICAL (angle surgery - goniotomy or trabeculotomy) is the primary treatment; medical therapy is temporizing","Amblyogenic PTOSIS: repair (levator advancement, or FRONTALIS SLING for poor levator function) - urgency depends on the amblyopia risk; treat amblyopia"],"tech":["Lacrimal probing/intubation/balloon dacryoplasty for persistent NLDO; angle surgery (goniotomy/trabeculotomy) for congenital glaucoma; ptosis repair (levator advancement or frontalis sling)","Exam under anesthesia for diagnosis/procedures in infants","Amblyopia management alongside"],"after":["Congenital glaucoma needs LIFELONG IOP and optic nerve monitoring (high recurrence/complexity) and amblyopia therapy; NLDO - reassess after probing for resolution","Ptosis: monitor for amblyopia and alignment; counsel on recurrence/revision (especially sling)","Long-term pediatric ophthalmology follow-up"],"pearls":["A tearing infant is usually benign nasolacrimal duct obstruction - but tearing WITH photophobia and a big cloudy cornea is congenital glaucoma, a surgical emergency","Most congenital NLD obstruction resolves by age 1 with massage - reserve probing for persistent/recurrent cases","Congenital glaucoma is treated with angle surgery (goniotomy/trabeculotomy), not primarily drops","Ptosis that covers the pupil or induces astigmatism is amblyogenic - repair on the amblyopia timeline, not just cosmesis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / AAPOS Pediatric Ophthalmology Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Congenital Glaucoma / Nasolacrimal Duct Obstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551504/"},{"t":"AAPOS Resources","u":"https://www.aapos.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-eyelid-malpositions","name":"Eyelid Malpositions (Entropion, Ectropion, Ptosis)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Common eyelid position disorders. ENTROPION: the lid margin turns IN, so lashes rub the cornea (trichiasis) causing irritation, foreign-body sensation, and corneal abrasion/ulcer risk (usually involutional/age-related, or cicatricial)","ECTROPION: the lid margin turns OUT, causing exposure, tearing (epiphora), and conjunctival keratinization (involutional, paralytic - facial nerve palsy, or cicatricial)","PTOSIS: drooping of the upper lid - most commonly APONEUROTIC (involutional, levator dehiscence); other causes are myogenic, mechanical, and NEUROGENIC (which must be excluded - CN III palsy, HORNER, myasthenia)"],"dx":["Clinical exam of lid position, margin, and eyelash direction; assess corneal exposure/staining (entropion/ectropion) and lid tone (snap-back test)","PTOSIS workup: measure the margin-reflex distance and LEVATOR FUNCTION; critically, EXCLUDE dangerous neurogenic causes - a pupil-involving CN III palsy (aneurysm), Horner syndrome (miosis/anhidrosis), and fatigable myasthenia","Identify cicatricial causes (scarring, ocular cicatricial pemphigoid) that change the surgical approach"],"tx":["Protect the cornea in the interim (lubrication, taping, bandage lens for exposure/trichiasis)","SURGICAL correction: entropion/ectropion repair (tighten/reposition the lid - lateral tarsal strip and retractor repair); PTOSIS repair (levator advancement, conjunctivo-Mullerectomy, or a FRONTALIS SLING when levator function is poor)","Treat the underlying cause first for neurogenic ptosis (do not just operate on a myasthenic or CN III lid)"],"tech":["Entropion repair (lateral tarsal strip + lower-lid retractor reinsertion); ectropion repair (horizontal lid tightening/medial spindle); ptosis repair (levator advancement, Muller-muscle-conjunctival resection, frontalis sling)","Epilation/electrolysis/cryo for trichiasis","Correct the causative pathology (cicatricial disease)"],"after":["Monitor corneal health and symptom relief; watch for over/undercorrection, recurrence, and (post-ptosis repair) LAGOPHTHALMOS/exposure keratopathy - lubricate to protect the cornea","Manage the underlying disease (facial palsy, cicatricial conditions)","Follow-up for revision as needed"],"pearls":["Entropion turns lashes IN (corneal abrasion risk) and ectropion turns the lid OUT (exposure/tearing) - protect the cornea while awaiting surgical repair","Before repairing ptosis, exclude the dangerous neurogenic causes - CN III palsy (aneurysm), Horner, and fatigable myasthenia","Poor levator function ptosis needs a frontalis sling, not a levator advancement - measure levator function to plan the operation","After ptosis repair, watch for lagophthalmos and exposure keratopathy - a raised lid that no longer closes fully"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Eyelid Malposition Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Entropion / Ectropion / Ptosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459324/"},{"t":"ASOPRS Oculoplastics Resources","u":"https://www.asoprs.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-eyelid-tumors","name":"Eyelid & Periocular Tumors","sec":"ophtho","present":["Eyelid tumors span benign lesions and malignancies; the periocular skin is a common site for skin cancer (this content overlaps the dermatology framing, with an eyelid-specific lens)","BASAL CELL CARCINOMA (most common - typically LOWER lid/medial canthus, a pearly nodule with telangiectasia, central ulceration, and MADAROSIS - loss of lashes); SQUAMOUS cell carcinoma; and SEBACEOUS GLAND CARCINOMA - an aggressive tumor that MASQUERADES as recurrent chalazion or chronic unilateral blepharitis (with pagetoid spread)","Features suggesting malignancy: ulceration, induration, irregular/pearly borders, telangiectasia, lash loss (madarosis), and destruction of normal architecture"],"dx":["BIOPSY any suspicious or non-resolving eyelid lesion - and specifically biopsy a RECURRENT 'chalazion' or a chronic unilateral blepharitis to exclude SEBACEOUS carcinoma (a notorious masquerader)","Map extent (including medial canthal/orbital involvement) and, for aggressive tumors (sebaceous, melanoma), assess regional nodes/systemic spread","Dermatologic/oculoplastic and pathology correlation"],"tx":["Complete surgical EXCISION with MARGIN CONTROL - MOHS micrographic surgery or excision with frozen-section margins is preferred for eyelid malignancies to spare tissue while clearing margins, followed by eyelid RECONSTRUCTION","Sebaceous carcinoma and melanoma need wider/more aggressive management and staging (map biopsies for pagetoid spread in sebaceous carcinoma); radiation/systemic therapy for advanced/unresectable disease","Benign lesions - excise if symptomatic/uncertain"],"tech":["Mohs/margin-controlled excision and eyelid reconstruction (tarsoconjunctival flaps, grafts); sentinel node/staging for high-risk tumors","Map biopsies for suspected sebaceous carcinoma spread","Reconstruction to restore lid function and protect the cornea"],"after":["Surveillance for RECURRENCE and (sebaceous carcinoma/melanoma) regional/systemic metastasis; monitor reconstruction for lid function and corneal protection","Dermatologic follow-up and sun-protection counseling; genetic/systemic evaluation for aggressive tumors","Long-term periodic exams"],"pearls":["A recurrent 'chalazion' or chronic unilateral blepharitis that will not resolve must be biopsied - sebaceous carcinoma masquerades as both and is deadly if missed","Basal cell carcinoma favors the lower lid/medial canthus with a pearly, telangiectatic, ulcerated look and lash loss (madarosis)","Margin-controlled excision (Mohs) plus reconstruction is the standard for eyelid malignancy - clear the tumor and protect the eye","Lash loss, ulceration, and destruction of the lid margin are red flags for malignancy over a benign lesion"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Eyelid Tumors Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Eyelid Malignancies / Sebaceous Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563142/"},{"t":"ASOPRS Oculoplastics Resources","u":"https://www.asoprs.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-thyroid-eye-disease","name":"Thyroid Eye Disease (Graves Orbitopathy)","sec":"ophtho","present":["An autoimmune inflammatory ORBITOPATHY (this content overlaps the endocrine/Graves framing) - the most common cause of proptosis in adults, usually with Graves hyperthyroidism (but can occur euthyroid/hypothyroid)","Signs: eyelid RETRACTION and lag, PROPTOSIS (exophthalmos), restrictive strabismus/DIPLOPIA (enlarged extraocular muscles), conjunctival injection/chemosis, and EXPOSURE keratopathy; the sight-threatening complication is COMPRESSIVE OPTIC NEUROPATHY (apical muscle crowding)","SMOKING strongly worsens it; the disease has an ACTIVE (inflammatory) phase and then a stable/fibrotic phase"],"dx":["Clinical exam (lid position, proptosis by exophthalmometry, motility, cornea) plus a CLINICAL ACTIVITY SCORE to gauge inflammation; CT/MRI of the orbits shows fusiform EXTRAOCULAR MUSCLE enlargement (SPARING the tendons) and apical crowding","CRITICAL: screen for COMPRESSIVE OPTIC NEUROPATHY - check vision, COLOR vision, pupils (RAPD), and fields (it can occur without dramatic proptosis)","Thyroid function and TSH-receptor antibodies (TRAb); assess smoking status"],"tx":["Foundational: SMOKING CESSATION, restore/maintain EUthyroid status, and ocular surface protection (lubrication) for exposure","ACTIVE moderate-severe disease: IV corticosteroids, TEPROTUMUMAB (an IGF-1R antibody that reduces proptosis/diplopia), and/or orbital radiotherapy/immunomodulation","SIGHT-THREATENING compressive optic neuropathy or severe exposure: urgent high-dose steroids and orbital DECOMPRESSION"],"tech":["Orbital DECOMPRESSION (for compressive optic neuropathy/severe proptosis); then, in the stable phase and in order, STRABISMUS surgery (diplopia) and finally EYELID surgery (retraction) - the standard staged sequence","Teprotumumab infusions; orbital radiotherapy","Corneal protection procedures for exposure"],"after":["Distinguish and time interventions by phase - REHABILITATIVE surgery is deferred to the STABLE phase (decompression -> strabismus -> lids in that order); monitor for optic neuropathy throughout the active phase","Ongoing thyroid management and smoking-cessation support; monitor teprotumumab side effects (hearing, hyperglycemia)","Long-term follow-up for recurrence and residual deformity/diplopia"],"pearls":["Thyroid eye disease is the most common cause of adult proptosis - and smoking cessation is one of the most effective things you can offer","Do not miss compressive optic neuropathy - check color vision, pupils, and fields; it threatens sight even without dramatic proptosis and needs urgent steroids/decompression","CT shows enlarged muscles that SPARE the tendons - the classic imaging feature that distinguishes it from other orbital disease","Rehabilitative surgery follows a set order in the STABLE phase: decompression, then strabismus, then eyelids"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO / EUGOGO Thyroid Eye Disease Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroid Eye Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK582135/"},{"t":"ASOPRS / EUGOGO Resources","u":"https://www.asoprs.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-ocular-oncology","name":"Ocular Oncology (Choroidal Melanoma, Retinoblastoma, Metastases)","sec":"ophtho","present":["Intraocular tumors, benign and malignant. CHOROIDAL MELANOMA: the most common PRIMARY intraocular malignancy in ADULTS - a pigmented dome/collar-button subretinal mass, often asymptomatic or with vision changes/field loss; metastasizes hematogenously, classically to the LIVER","RETINOBLASTOMA: the most common intraocular malignancy in CHILDREN - presents with LEUKOCORIA/strabismus, driven by RB1 mutations (heritable/bilateral forms), and is LIFE-THREATENING","Choroidal METASTASES (from breast, lung) are actually the most common intraocular malignancy overall; also ocular surface tumors and intraocular lymphoma"],"dx":["CHOROIDAL MELANOMA: dilated exam and ULTRASOUND (B-scan - a dome/collar-button with low internal reflectivity/acoustic hollowing); risk features for growth/malignancy (thickness, subretinal fluid, orange pigment, symptoms, margin) and systemic staging (LIVER imaging); AVOID incisional biopsy generally (use fine-needle for prognostication)","RETINOBLASTOMA: exam under anesthesia and imaging (calcification on ultrasound/CT, MRI for extension) - do NOT biopsy (seeding); genetic RB1 testing","Choroidal metastasis: typically creamy/plateau lesions, often multifocal/bilateral - search for the primary"],"tx":["CHOROIDAL MELANOMA: PLAQUE BRACHYTHERAPY (globe-conserving, standard for many - Collaborative Ocular Melanoma Study), proton-beam radiation, or ENUCLEATION for large tumors; treat/surveil for metastases","RETINOBLASTOMA: multimodal ocular-oncology care - systemic/intra-arterial/intravitreal CHEMOTHERAPY, focal therapy (laser/cryo), plaque, and ENUCLEATION for advanced disease (priority: life > eye > vision)","Choroidal METASTASES: treat the systemic cancer +/- radiation/systemic therapy"],"tech":["Plaque brachytherapy and proton-beam radiotherapy; enucleation; intra-arterial and intravitreal chemotherapy for retinoblastoma; fine-needle aspiration biopsy for melanoma prognostication (gene-expression profiling)","Ocular-oncology-specialist procedures","Systemic oncology coordination"],"after":["CHOROIDAL MELANOMA: LIFELONG systemic surveillance for METASTASIS (especially liver); gene-expression profiling stratifies metastatic risk","RETINOBLASTOMA: genetic counseling (RB1 - heritable/bilateral, risk of second cancers, family screening) and long-term oncologic/ophthalmic follow-up","Monitor treated eyes for radiation retinopathy/complications and tumor recurrence"],"pearls":["A pigmented dome or collar-button choroidal mass with low internal reflectivity on B-scan is a melanoma - and it metastasizes to the LIVER, so systemic surveillance is lifelong","Do not biopsy a suspected retinoblastoma (or generally a melanoma incisionally) - seeding risk; imaging and specialist referral instead","Retinoblastoma priorities are life, then eye, then vision - and RB1 status drives genetics counseling for the whole family","Choroidal metastases (breast/lung) are the most common intraocular malignancy overall - a new choroidal lesion can be the first sign of systemic cancer"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Ocular Oncology Guidance","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Choroidal Melanoma / Retinoblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544332/"},{"t":"COMS / AAO EyeWiki: Ocular Oncology","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ophtho-refractive","name":"Refractive Error & Refractive Surgery","sec":"ophtho","present":["Refractive errors are the most common cause of correctable vision impairment: MYOPIA (nearsightedness - image focuses in front of the retina, usually a long/axial eye), HYPEROPIA (farsightedness), ASTIGMATISM (irregular corneal/lens curvature), and PRESBYOPIA (age-related loss of accommodation, near-vision difficulty from ~40s)","Myopia prevalence is rising globally ('myopia epidemic'); high myopia raises the risk of retinal detachment, myopic maculopathy, and glaucoma","Refractive surgery aims to reduce spectacle/contact-lens dependence"],"dx":["Manifest and cycloplegic REFRACTION to quantify the error; before refractive surgery, screen with corneal TOPOGRAPHY/tomography and PACHYMETRY to EXCLUDE keratoconus/ectasia-prone thin corneas and evaluate DRY EYE (a major cause of dissatisfaction)","Assess candidacy (stable refraction, adequate corneal thickness, realistic expectations, pupil size)","Distinguish and manage pathologic causes of refractive change (cataract - myopic shift, diabetes-related fluctuation)"],"tx":["Non-surgical: GLASSES and CONTACT LENSES; myopia CONTROL in children (low-dose atropine, orthokeratology, specialized lenses) to slow progression","CORNEAL refractive surgery: LASIK (flap + excimer ablation), PRK (surface ablation - for thinner corneas/certain occupations), and SMILE (lenticule extraction)","LENS-based options: phakic intraocular lenses (high myopia) and refractive lens exchange/presbyopia-correcting IOLs (older patients); presbyopia - reading glasses, monovision, multifocal correction"],"tech":["LASIK (femtosecond flap + excimer laser), PRK (epithelial removal + excimer), SMILE (femtosecond lenticule extraction); phakic IOL implantation and refractive lens exchange","Wavefront/topography-guided ablation planning","Corneal cross-linking if ectasia risk"],"after":["Manage post-refractive DRY EYE (common, usually temporary) and monitor for ECTASIA (progressive steepening - the reason for careful preoperative screening); night-vision symptoms (halos/glare) and refractive enhancements","PRK has a longer visual recovery/more discomfort than LASIK but avoids flap complications; counsel on realistic outcomes and presbyopia (surgery does not stop age-related reading changes)","Long-term monitoring, especially in high myopia (retina/glaucoma surveillance)"],"pearls":["Screen every refractive-surgery candidate with topography and pachymetry - operating on an ectasia-prone cornea causes post-LASIK ectasia","Dry eye is the leading cause of refractive-surgery dissatisfaction - evaluate and optimize it before and after surgery","Myopia control (low-dose atropine, ortho-K) can slow progression in children - high myopia is not just a glasses problem, it raises detachment/glaucoma risk","Refractive surgery does not cure presbyopia - the 45-year-old will still need help for near vision"],"refs":[{"t":"AAO Refractive Errors & Refractive Surgery PPP","u":"https://www.aao.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Refractive Surgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565884/"},{"t":"AAO EyeWiki: Refractive Surgery","u":"https://eyewiki.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-compartment-syndrome","name":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","sec":"ortho","present":["A limb-threatening EMERGENCY: rising pressure within a closed osteofascial compartment compromises perfusion, causing ischemic muscle/nerve injury - a missed diagnosis is a classic source of catastrophic outcomes and litigation","Most common after TIBIAL SHAFT fractures, high-energy forearm injuries, crush, reperfusion, and tight casts/dressings; can occur WITHOUT a fracture","The earliest and most reliable sign is PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to the injury and PAIN ON PASSIVE STRETCH of the compartment muscles - the classic '5 Ps' (pulselessness, pallor, paresthesia, paralysis) are LATE and unreliable"],"dx":["Primarily a CLINICAL diagnosis - serial exams for escalating pain, pain on passive stretch, a tense/swollen compartment, and later paresthesia; a high index of suspicion is essential (obtunded/regional-block patients cannot report pain - measure pressures)","COMPARTMENT PRESSURE measurement when the exam is unreliable or equivocal: an absolute pressure or, better, a DELTA PRESSURE (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) of <=30 mmHg supports the diagnosis","Do not wait for pulselessness (a late/end-stage finding) - and remember pulses are often present"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE decompression: remove/split casts and circumferential dressings, keep the limb at HEART LEVEL (not elevated - elevation reduces perfusion pressure), correct hypotension, and give oxygen/analgesia","Definitive treatment is emergent surgical FASCIOTOMY of ALL involved compartments - time-critical (irreversible damage within hours); do not delay","Treat rhabdomyolysis/reperfusion (fluids, monitor potassium/renal function)"],"tech":["Emergent FASCIOTOMY releasing all compartments (e.g., two-incision four-compartment leg fasciotomy); leave wounds open, debride nonviable muscle","Serial washouts and delayed closure/skin grafting once swelling resolves","Stabilize the associated fracture"],"after":["Return to the OR for repeat debridement and delayed wound closure/grafting; monitor for and manage RHABDOMYOLYSIS and renal injury","Rehabilitation for residual weakness/contracture (Volkmann ischemic contracture if missed); functional recovery depends on how quickly decompression occurred","Late reconstruction for established contractures"],"pearls":["Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the early, reliable signs - the '5 Ps' are late; do not wait for pulselessness","Keep the limb at heart level, not elevated - elevation lowers perfusion pressure and worsens ischemia","When in doubt (obtunded/blocked patient, equivocal exam), measure compartment pressures - a delta pressure <=30 mmHg is decompression territory","A tibial shaft fracture is the classic setting - and compartment syndrome can occur with pulses present and without a fracture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Acute Compartment Syndrome","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Compartment Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448124/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-open-fractures","name":"Open Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["A fracture communicating with the external environment through a wound - an orthopedic URGENCY with high risk of INFECTION and soft-tissue compromise","Graded by the GUSTILO-ANDERSON classification (I: <1 cm clean; II: 1-10 cm; III: extensive soft-tissue injury/contamination, subdivided IIIA adequate coverage, IIIB needs flap coverage, IIIC arterial injury requiring repair)","Common with high-energy trauma; the tibia is the most frequent open long-bone fracture (subcutaneous location)"],"dx":["Assess the wound, soft-tissue envelope, NEUROVASCULAR status, and contamination; classify (Gustilo-Anderson) - though the final grade is often determined intraoperatively","Radiographs of the whole bone (joint above and below); evaluate for compartment syndrome and associated injuries","Document tetanus status and vascular exam"],"tx":["EARLY IV ANTIBIOTICS (the single most important infection-prevention step - give as soon as possible, ideally within hours) - a first-generation cephalosporin, adding gram-negative/anaerobic coverage for higher grades/contamination; TETANUS prophylaxis","Remove gross contamination, provisional reduction/splinting, and sterile dressing in the ED; urgent surgical IRRIGATION AND DEBRIDEMENT with fracture stabilization","Timing of debridement is prompt (urgent, not necessarily within a rigid 6-hour window per modern evidence, but antibiotics are time-critical)"],"tech":["Operative irrigation and DEBRIDEMENT of nonviable tissue, fracture stabilization (external fixation for damage control, or internal fixation when appropriate), and repeat washouts","Soft-tissue coverage (flap for IIIB) - timely coverage reduces infection; vascular repair for IIIC","Bone grafting/reconstruction for bone loss at later stages"],"after":["Serial debridements and definitive soft-tissue coverage; monitor for INFECTION, nonunion, and compartment syndrome","Continue antibiotics per protocol; rehabilitation and staged reconstruction of bone/soft-tissue defects","Long-term follow-up for union and function"],"pearls":["Early IV antibiotics are the top priority - give them fast; they matter more for infection prevention than a rigid debridement time window","Gustilo-Anderson grade drives antibiotics and coverage - IIIB needs a flap, IIIC has an arterial injury needing repair","Do not forget tetanus prophylaxis and a documented neurovascular exam","The tibia is the classic open long-bone fracture - and open fractures raise the risk of compartment syndrome too"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Open Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Open Fracture Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448086/"},{"t":"OTA Open Fracture Guidelines","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-septic-arthritis","name":"Septic Arthritis (Native Joint)","sec":"ortho","present":["Bacterial infection within a joint - an EMERGENCY because purulent enzymes destroy articular CARTILAGE within days, causing permanent joint damage","Presents with an acutely painful, swollen, warm, erythematous joint with severely restricted/painful motion; the patient resists any movement; often febrile (the knee is most common)","Routes: hematogenous (most common - Staph aureus predominates; gonococcal in young sexually active adults), direct inoculation, or contiguous spread; higher risk with prosthetic joints, immunosuppression, diabetes, IVDU, and pre-existing joint disease"],"dx":["URGENT joint ASPIRATION (arthrocentesis) before antibiotics when feasible: synovial fluid for cell count (typically WBC >50,000 with neutrophil predominance, though lower does not exclude), GRAM STAIN, CULTURE, and crystals (to assess coexisting gout/pseudogout)","Blood cultures, inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP), and imaging; a Kocher-criteria-type assessment aids pediatric hip evaluation","Do not let a normal-appearing radiograph or absent fever reassure you - the aspirate is the test"],"tx":["Surgical DRAINAGE/washout (arthroscopic or open) to evacuate the purulent, cartilage-destroying material - this is the definitive treatment, often requiring repeated washouts","IV ANTIBIOTICS after cultures are obtained (empiric anti-staphylococcal coverage, tailored to culture/Gram stain and risk factors), then a prolonged course","Do NOT rely on antibiotics alone for a native septic joint - it needs source control (drainage)"],"tech":["Arthroscopic or open surgical irrigation and debridement/drainage of the joint; repeat washouts as needed; the hip and shoulder often warrant urgent open/arthroscopic drainage","Prosthetic joint infection follows a different surgical algorithm (see arthroplasty complications)","Serial synovial sampling to confirm clearance"],"after":["Complete the prolonged IV/oral antibiotic course (ID co-management); early mobilization/physiotherapy once the infection is controlled to preserve joint function","Monitor for recurrence and for residual joint damage/secondary osteoarthritis","Treat the underlying source and risk factors"],"pearls":["A hot, swollen, exquisitely painful joint that resists all movement is septic arthritis until proven otherwise - aspirate urgently, cartilage dies in days","Aspirate before antibiotics when possible - the synovial Gram stain and culture direct therapy, and crystals do not exclude infection (they can coexist)","Septic arthritis needs surgical drainage plus antibiotics - antibiotics alone do not clear a native septic joint","Staph aureus is the most common organism; think gonococcal in a young sexually active patient with migratory/polyarticular symptoms"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Septic Arthritis","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Septic Arthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538176/"},{"t":"IDSA Joint Infection Resources","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-necrotizing-infection","name":"Necrotizing Soft Tissue Infection (Fasciitis)","sec":"ortho","present":["A rapidly progressive, LIFE-THREATENING infection of fascia and soft tissue with tissue necrosis - a surgical EMERGENCY where delay dramatically increases mortality","PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to exam findings is the hallmark early clue; later signs include rapidly spreading erythema, tense edema/bullae, skin discoloration/necrosis, crepitus (gas), and systemic TOXICITY (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, altered mentation)","Types: polymicrobial (type I - diabetics/immunocompromised), Group A Streptococcus/Clostridium (type II - can affect healthy hosts), and others; risk factors include diabetes, immunosuppression, and skin breaches"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis driven by suspicion - do NOT delay surgery for imaging; pain out of proportion, systemic toxicity, and rapid progression should trigger immediate surgical exploration","Adjuncts (should not delay the OR): the LRINEC score, labs (leukocytosis, elevated lactate/CRP, renal dysfunction), and imaging (gas on plain film/CT) support but do not rule out the diagnosis; surgical exploration ('finger test' - lack of resistance, dishwater fluid, non-bleeding tissue) confirms","Blood/tissue cultures to tailor antibiotics"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE, aggressive surgical DEBRIDEMENT of all necrotic tissue (the decisive intervention) - early and repeated; time to surgery is the key survival determinant","Broad-spectrum IV ANTIBIOTICS (cover gram-positive incl MRSA, gram-negative, and anaerobes) PLUS CLINDAMYCIN (anti-toxin effect for strep/clostridial); aggressive hemodynamic resuscitation/ICU care","IVIG in selected streptococcal toxic shock cases; amputation if limb non-salvageable"],"tech":["Radical surgical debridement back to healthy, bleeding tissue with repeated re-explorations every 12-24 h until clean; amputation for non-salvageable limbs","Delayed reconstruction/coverage (grafts/flaps) after source control","Multidisciplinary critical care and ID management"],"after":["Serial debridements, ICU-level resuscitation, and eventual soft-tissue reconstruction; high morbidity/mortality and prolonged recovery","Rehabilitation and psychological support; manage the underlying condition (diabetes, immunosuppression)","Monitor for recurrent/ongoing infection"],"pearls":["Pain out of proportion to the visible findings plus systemic toxicity is necrotizing infection until proven otherwise - and the treatment is the OR, not more imaging","Time to surgical debridement is the single biggest driver of survival - do not delay for a scan or the LRINEC score","Add clindamycin to broad-spectrum antibiotics for its anti-toxin effect in strep/clostridial disease","'Dishwater' fluid, non-bleeding gray tissue, and easy finger dissection along the fascia confirm it at exploration"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Necrotizing Fasciitis","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Necrotizing Soft Tissue Infections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430756/"},{"t":"IDSA Skin & Soft Tissue Infection Guidelines","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-cauda-equina","name":"Cauda Equina Syndrome","sec":"ortho","present":["Compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus - a surgical EMERGENCY (this content shares with the neurosurgery entry, framed for the orthopedic reader)","Red-flag features: bilateral sciatica/leg pain and weakness, SADDLE ANESTHESIA (perineal/perianal numbness), and BLADDER/BOWEL dysfunction (urinary RETENTION with overflow incontinence is the most consistent) plus reduced anal tone/sexual dysfunction","Most often from a large central LUMBAR DISC HERNIATION; also tumor, epidural abscess/hematoma, and trauma"],"dx":["Urgent clinical assessment including a rectal exam (tone/sensation) and post-void RESIDUAL bladder volume (urinary retention is a key objective sign); do not dismiss new bilateral radicular symptoms with bladder change","EMERGENT MRI of the lumbosacral spine to identify and localize the compressive lesion","Distinguish complete (retention/overflow - worse prognosis) from incomplete cauda equina"],"tx":["Emergent surgical DECOMPRESSION (e.g., wide laminectomy/discectomy) - timing matters, and earlier decompression is associated with better recovery of bladder/bowel and neurologic function","Treat the specific cause (discectomy for disc; drainage/antibiotics for abscess; oncologic management for tumor)","This is time-critical - do not delay decompression for a compressive lesion causing progressive deficits"],"tech":["Surgical decompression (laminectomy and discectomy/mass removal) to relieve the cauda equina compression","Cause-specific surgery (abscess drainage, tumor resection/stabilization)","Neuromonitoring as indicated"],"after":["Monitor and rehabilitate neurologic, bladder, bowel, and sexual function - recovery can be incomplete, especially if decompression was delayed or the syndrome was complete at presentation","Bladder management (catheterization) and pelvic-floor/continence rehabilitation; physical therapy for residual weakness","Long-term follow-up and support"],"pearls":["Bilateral sciatica with saddle anesthesia and urinary retention is cauda equina syndrome - get an emergent MRI and surgical decompression","Urinary retention (check a post-void residual) and reduced anal tone are the objective red flags - do not rely on symptoms alone","Earlier decompression gives better bladder/bowel recovery - it is a get-to-the-OR emergency, not a next-day problem","A large central lumbar disc herniation is the classic cause, but always consider abscess, hematoma, and tumor"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Cauda Equina Syndrome","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cauda Equina Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537200/"},{"t":"NASS Spine Care Resources","u":"https://www.spine.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-fat-embolism","name":"Fat Embolism Syndrome","sec":"ortho","present":["A systemic reaction to fat/marrow embolization, classically after LONG-BONE (femur/tibia) or PELVIC fractures (and intramedullary reaming) - typically appearing 24-72 hours after injury","The classic TRIAD: RESPIRATORY distress/hypoxemia (most common/earliest), NEUROLOGIC changes (confusion, agitation, decreased consciousness), and a PETECHIAL RASH (often on the conjunctivae, axillae, chest - a late but specific sign)","Higher risk with multiple long-bone fractures and delayed fixation; can range from subclinical to fatal (ARDS)"],"dx":["A largely CLINICAL diagnosis of exclusion in the right setting (recent long-bone/pelvic fracture + hypoxemia + neurologic change +/- petechiae); supportive criteria (e.g., Gurd) help","Investigations show hypoxemia (ABG), and may show anemia/thrombocytopenia; imaging (chest) can show ARDS-pattern infiltrates; exclude PE, pneumonia, and other causes","Fundoscopy may reveal retinal fat emboli/hemorrhages"],"tx":["Primarily SUPPORTIVE: oxygenation and respiratory support (up to mechanical ventilation for ARDS), hemodynamic support, and good ICU care - most cases resolve with support","PREVENTION is key: EARLY fracture STABILIZATION reduces the incidence; careful surgical technique","No specific proven pharmacologic therapy (steroids studied for prevention but not standard treatment)"],"tech":["No specific procedure - management is supportive/critical care; early operative fracture stabilization is the preventive 'intervention'","Respiratory support (ventilation) for severe hypoxemia/ARDS","Monitoring in a higher-acuity setting"],"after":["Supportive care until resolution (most recover fully over days); monitor respiratory and neurologic recovery","Proceed with definitive fracture care as the patient stabilizes; watch for ARDS complications","Rehabilitation as needed"],"pearls":["Hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae 24-72 hours after a femur/pelvis fracture is fat embolism syndrome","Respiratory compromise is usually the earliest feature; the petechial rash is specific but late","Early fracture stabilization is the best prevention - delayed fixation raises the risk","It is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion and treatment is supportive - rule out PE and pneumonia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Fat Embolism Syndrome","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fat Embolism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499885/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-knee-dislocation","name":"Knee Dislocation & Popliteal Vascular Injury","sec":"ortho","present":["Tibiofemoral DISLOCATION - a true orthopedic EMERGENCY because of the high risk of POPLITEAL ARTERY injury (the artery is tethered at the adductor hiatus and soleal arch) and multiligament disruption","High-energy (MVA) or, importantly, LOW-energy in obese patients; many dislocations SPONTANEOUSLY REDUCE before evaluation, so a multiligamentous knee injury implies a possible occult dislocation with vascular risk","Associated common peroneal NERVE injury (foot drop) is frequent"],"dx":["Emergent REDUCTION and then a meticulous, SERIAL vascular assessment - popliteal injury can be present with palpable pulses initially and declare later (intimal injury/thrombosis)","ANKLE-BRACHIAL INDEX (ABI): <0.9 mandates CT angiography/vascular consult; hard signs of vascular injury go straight to the OR; serial exams even if initially normal","Assess neurologic status (peroneal nerve), compartments, and ligamentous injury (usually delayed MRI); radiographs pre/post reduction"],"tx":["IMMEDIATE closed REDUCTION and splinting; URGENT vascular repair for any arterial injury (revascularization within ~6-8 hours to avoid limb loss) - vascular surgery involvement is critical","Provisional stabilization (external fixation for instability/vascular repair); staged multiligament reconstruction later","Serial neurovascular and COMPARTMENT monitoring (high compartment syndrome risk after revascularization)"],"tech":["Closed reduction; external fixation for gross instability or to protect a vascular repair; delayed multiligament ligamentous reconstruction","Vascular repair/bypass (vascular surgery) for popliteal injury; fasciotomy for/with reperfusion","MRI-guided surgical planning for ligament reconstruction"],"after":["Close monitoring for delayed vascular compromise and compartment syndrome after reduction/revascularization; peroneal nerve deficits often need bracing (foot drop) and may not recover","Staged ligament reconstruction and prolonged rehabilitation; stiffness is common","Long-term functional follow-up"],"pearls":["A knee dislocation is a vascular emergency - the popliteal artery is tethered and injured even when it looks reduced","Pulses can be present initially and fail later - use serial exams and the ABI (<0.9 -> CT angiography)","A multiligament knee injury may be a spontaneously reduced dislocation - treat it with the same vascular vigilance","Watch for peroneal nerve palsy (foot drop) and post-revascularization compartment syndrome"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Knee Dislocation","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Knee Dislocation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470595/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-clavicle-fractures","name":"Clavicle Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["One of the most common fractures, usually from a fall onto the shoulder or outstretched hand; presents with pain, deformity/tenting of the skin, and a droopy shoulder","Classified by location: MIDSHAFT (most common, ~80%), distal/lateral (relationship to the coracoclavicular ligaments matters for stability), and medial","Assess for skin compromise, neurovascular injury (subclavian vessels/brachial plexus), and associated injuries (rib fractures, pneumothorax) in high-energy cases"],"dx":["Radiographs (dedicated clavicle views) to classify and assess displacement, shortening, and comminution; examine skin integrity and neurovascular status","Evaluate for open injury/skin tenting (risk of skin necrosis) and associated thoracic injury","CT for medial/sternoclavicular injuries (posterior displacement can be dangerous)"],"tx":["MOST midshaft clavicle fractures heal well NONOPERATIVELY (sling/immobilization) with good functional outcomes","SURGICAL fixation (plate or intramedullary) is considered for open fractures, skin compromise, neurovascular injury, significantly displaced/shortened fractures, and select athletes/bilateral/polytrauma - surgery lowers nonunion/symptomatic malunion but adds surgical risk","Distal fractures that are unstable (CC ligament disruption) more often need fixation"],"tech":["Open reduction internal FIXATION with a plate (superior/anteroinferior) or intramedullary device for operative cases; CC ligament reconstruction for unstable distal fractures","Nonoperative: sling immobilization with early range-of-motion progression","Address skin/soft-tissue and associated injuries"],"after":["Nonoperative: sling for comfort with progressive range of motion and return to activity as pain/union allow; counsel on a possible bump (malunion) that is usually well tolerated","Watch for NONUNION (higher with displacement/shortening/comminution) and symptomatic malunion; hardware irritation can prompt later plate removal","Return to sport/work guided by union and strength"],"pearls":["Most midshaft clavicle fractures do well without surgery - a sling and time is the default","Operate for open fractures, skin tenting/compromise, neurovascular injury, and marked displacement/shortening","Posteriorly displaced medial (sternoclavicular) injuries can compress mediastinal structures - get a CT and involve appropriate teams","Counsel patients that a visible bump after nonoperative healing is common and usually asymptomatic"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Clavicle Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Clavicle Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507892/"},{"t":"OTA Fracture Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-proximal-humerus","name":"Proximal Humerus Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Common fractures, especially FRAGILITY fractures in older osteoporotic patients (fall onto an outstretched hand) and higher-energy injuries in the young; present with shoulder pain, swelling, and ecchymosis","Described by the NEER classification based on displacement of the four 'parts' (articular surface/head, greater tuberosity, lesser tuberosity, shaft)","Assess AXILLARY NERVE function (deltoid/lateral shoulder sensation) and vascular status; the head's blood supply is at risk in some patterns (osteonecrosis)"],"dx":["Shoulder radiographs (AP, scapular-Y, axillary views) to classify part displacement; CT for surgical planning in complex/comminuted fractures","Examine the axillary nerve and vascularity; assess for dislocation (fracture-dislocation)","Evaluate bone quality/osteoporosis (fragility fracture workup)"],"tx":["MOST proximal humerus fractures are minimally displaced and treated NONOPERATIVELY (sling + early motion) with good outcomes","SURGICAL options for displaced/unstable patterns: open reduction internal fixation (locking plate), intramedullary nailing, or ARTHROPLASTY - reverse total shoulder arthroplasty is favored for complex fractures in older patients (and hemiarthroplasty in selected cases)","Individualize by fracture pattern, bone quality, age, and function; the PROFHER-type evidence supports nonoperative care for many displaced fractures in older adults"],"tech":["Locking-plate ORIF or intramedullary nailing for reconstructable fractures; hemiarthroplasty or REVERSE total shoulder arthroplasty for complex/head-split/osteoporotic fractures in older patients","Nonoperative: sling with a structured early range-of-motion protocol","Fragility fracture bone-health referral"],"after":["Early, progressive shoulder range of motion is important to avoid STIFFNESS (a common problem); structured physiotherapy","Monitor for osteonecrosis of the humeral head, malunion/nonunion, and (post-fixation) screw penetration; manage stiffness","Osteoporosis treatment for fragility fractures; functional recovery over months"],"pearls":["Most proximal humerus fractures are minimally displaced and do well with a sling and early motion","Always check the axillary nerve (deltoid/lateral shoulder sensation) - it is the commonly injured nerve here","Reverse total shoulder arthroplasty is increasingly the choice for complex fractures in older, osteoporotic patients","A proximal humerus fragility fracture is a bone-health flag - work up and treat osteoporosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Proximal Humerus Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Proximal Humerus Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537083/"},{"t":"OTA Fracture Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-humeral-shaft","name":"Humeral Shaft Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Fractures of the humeral diaphysis from direct trauma, falls, or (spiral distal-third fractures) twisting injuries; present with arm pain, deformity, and swelling","The signature associated injury is RADIAL NERVE palsy (WRIST DROP, loss of finger/thumb extension, and dorsal hand sensory loss) - the radial nerve runs in the spiral groove and is at risk especially with mid/distal-third (Holstein-Lewis) fractures","Most are amenable to nonoperative care due to the forgiving soft-tissue envelope and shoulder/elbow motion compensating for angulation"],"dx":["Radiographs of the humerus (full length, joint above and below); document RADIAL NERVE function carefully before and after any manipulation","Assess skin/soft tissue and vascular status; classify pattern (transverse, spiral, comminuted)","CT rarely needed unless intra-articular extension"],"tx":["MOST heal NONOPERATIVELY in a functional (Sarmiento) BRACE - the humerus tolerates considerable angulation with good functional outcome","SURGICAL fixation (plate ORIF or intramedullary nail) for open fractures, vascular injury, polytrauma/floating elbow, inability to maintain alignment, pathologic fractures, or certain nerve situations","Radial nerve palsy is usually a NEUROPRAXIA that RECOVERS spontaneously - observe (most recover over ~3-4 months); explore if it develops after reduction or in open fractures"],"tech":["Functional bracing (Sarmiento) for nonoperative care; plate ORIF (open reduction with compression/bridge plating) or intramedullary nailing for operative cases","Radial nerve exploration/repair in select cases (open fracture, palsy after manipulation, no recovery)","Address associated injuries (floating elbow)"],"after":["Serial radiographs to confirm alignment/union in a brace; monitor RADIAL NERVE recovery (test wrist/finger extension) - most recover, follow with a wrist splint in the interim","Watch for nonunion (mid-shaft transverse patterns) and stiffness; shoulder/elbow rehabilitation","Return to activity as union progresses"],"pearls":["Check for wrist drop - radial nerve palsy is the classic association of a humeral shaft (especially distal-third Holstein-Lewis) fracture","Most humeral shaft fractures heal in a functional brace - the arm tolerates angulation well","A radial nerve palsy present at injury is usually a neuropraxia that recovers - observe; a palsy that appears AFTER reduction warrants exploration","Support the wrist with a splint while awaiting radial nerve recovery to maintain function"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Humeral Shaft Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Humeral Shaft Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448074/"},{"t":"OTA Fracture Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-distal-radius","name":"Distal Radius Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["The most common upper-extremity fracture, with a BIMODAL distribution: FRAGILITY fractures in older (osteoporotic) patients from a fall on an outstretched hand, and high-energy fractures in the young","Eponyms describe patterns: COLLES (dorsally angulated/displaced - 'dinner-fork' deformity, the most common), SMITH (volar/reverse Colles), BARTON (intra-articular shear), and Chauffeur (radial styloid)","Assess for MEDIAN NERVE dysfunction (acute carpal tunnel) and associated injuries (distal radioulnar joint - DRUJ, scaphoid)"],"dx":["Wrist radiographs (PA, lateral) to assess radial height/inclination, VOLAR TILT, articular step-off, and comminution; CT for intra-articular planning","Examine MEDIAN NERVE function (acute carpal tunnel syndrome - progressive numbness) and DRUJ stability","Evaluate bone quality (fragility fracture) and associated carpal injuries"],"tx":["NONOPERATIVE: closed REDUCTION and casting for stable/acceptably-reduced fractures (especially lower-demand older patients - functional outcomes are often good even with some deformity)","OPERATIVE when reduction is unacceptable/unstable: acceptable-alignment parameters include adequate radial height/inclination, articular step-off <2 mm, and dorsal tilt within limits - failing these, fix (usually VOLAR LOCKING PLATE, or external fixation/pinning)","Acute carpal tunnel (progressive median symptoms) warrants urgent carpal tunnel release +/- fixation"],"tech":["Closed reduction and cast/splint immobilization; open reduction internal fixation with a VOLAR LOCKING PLATE (most common operative technique), or K-wire fixation/external fixation for select patterns","Carpal tunnel release for acute median nerve compression; DRUJ stabilization if unstable","Fragility fracture bone-health referral"],"after":["Cast/splint for ~6 weeks (nonoperative) or early motion after rigid volar-plate fixation; hand therapy for range of motion, grip strength, and stiffness prevention","Monitor for malunion, median nerve symptoms, tendon irritation/rupture (EPL - extensor pollicis longus, and flexor tendons over prominent hardware), and complex regional pain syndrome","Osteoporosis evaluation for fragility fractures; return to activity by union/function"],"pearls":["The dorsally displaced 'dinner-fork' deformity is a Colles fracture - the most common distal radius pattern","Watch for acute carpal tunnel syndrome - progressive median nerve numbness after a distal radius fracture can need urgent release","Older, lower-demand patients often do well even with some residual deformity - individualize the decision to operate","A distal radius fragility fracture is a sentinel osteoporosis event - order the bone-health workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Distal Radius Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Distal Radius Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536974/"},{"t":"ASSH Hand Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-scaphoid","name":"Scaphoid Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["The most common CARPAL bone fracture, typically from a fall on an outstretched hand in a young adult; presents with radial-sided wrist pain and ANATOMIC SNUFFBOX tenderness","Clinically important because of a RETROGRADE blood supply (entering distally), which puts the PROXIMAL pole at risk of AVASCULAR NECROSIS and NONUNION - especially with proximal or displaced fractures","Missed/untreated fractures can progress to scaphoid nonunion advanced collapse (SNAC) arthritis"],"dx":["Snuffbox tenderness with a normal initial radiograph should be treated as an OCCULT scaphoid fracture (X-rays are often falsely negative early) - immobilize and re-image, or obtain MRI/CT for early definitive diagnosis","Scaphoid-view radiographs; CT for fracture displacement/union and MRI for occult fracture/vascularity","Localize the fracture (proximal pole worst prognosis)"],"tx":["NONDISPLACED fractures: cast IMMOBILIZATION (thumb-spica) - waist fractures usually heal, though healing is slow (weeks-months)","DISPLACED, proximal pole, or nonunion fractures: surgical FIXATION (percutaneous/open compression screw) - also offered to some nondisplaced fractures for faster return; bone grafting for nonunion/AVN","A high index of suspicion prevents the missed fracture that becomes a nonunion"],"tech":["Percutaneous or open compression SCREW fixation (e.g., Herbert screw); vascularized/non-vascularized bone grafting for nonunion or avascular necrosis; salvage procedures for SNAC arthritis","Thumb-spica cast for nondisplaced waist fractures","CT to confirm union"],"after":["Prolonged immobilization/follow-up with CT to CONFIRM UNION (clinical and radiographic healing lag); hand therapy for stiffness after immobilization","Monitor for NONUNION and AVASCULAR NECROSIS (proximal pole) - low threshold to reassess persistent pain","Return to activity only after confirmed union"],"pearls":["Snuffbox tenderness with a normal X-ray is a scaphoid fracture until proven otherwise - immobilize and re-image or get an MRI","The retrograde blood supply puts the proximal pole at risk of avascular necrosis and nonunion - proximal/displaced fractures often need surgery","Healing is slow and radiographs lag - use CT to confirm union before clearing return to activity","A missed scaphoid fracture becomes a nonunion and, eventually, SNAC wrist arthritis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Scaphoid Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Scaphoid Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536907/"},{"t":"ASSH Hand Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-elbow-fractures","name":"Elbow Fractures (Olecranon, Radial Head, Distal Humerus, Terrible Triad)","sec":"ortho","present":["Adult elbow injuries spanning OLECRANON fractures (disrupt the extensor mechanism - inability to actively extend), RADIAL HEAD fractures (the most common elbow fracture - lateral pain, limited rotation), DISTAL HUMERUS fractures (often complex intra-articular), and the 'TERRIBLE TRIAD' (elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture - highly unstable)","Present with pain, swelling, limited motion, and (with instability) recurrent dislocation; assess for open injury and neurovascular status (ulnar nerve, brachial artery)","The elbow is prone to post-injury STIFFNESS, so restoring stable motion early is a theme"],"dx":["Elbow radiographs (AP, lateral - look for a posterior FAT PAD sign indicating an occult intra-articular fracture, often a radial head fracture); CT for intra-articular/comminuted patterns and surgical planning","Assess the extensor mechanism (olecranon), forearm rotation (radial head), stability (terrible triad), and neurovascular status (ulnar nerve)","Evaluate for associated forearm/wrist injuries (Essex-Lopresti - radial head fracture with DRUJ disruption)"],"tx":["OLECRANON: displaced fractures need fixation (tension-band wiring for simple, plate for comminuted) to restore the extensor mechanism; nondisplaced can be splinted","RADIAL HEAD: nondisplaced/stable - early motion (nonoperative); displaced/blocking rotation - ORIF or radial head arthroplasty (excision alone avoided if the elbow needs the radial head for stability)","DISTAL HUMERUS - ORIF (dual plating) or elbow arthroplasty in select elderly; TERRIBLE TRIAD - restore stability (radial head, coronoid/ligament repair) to allow early motion"],"tech":["Tension-band wiring or plate fixation (olecranon); ORIF or radial head arthroplasty (radial head); dual-column plating or total elbow arthroplasty (distal humerus); staged stabilization of the terrible triad (radial head, coronoid, LUCL repair)","Ulnar nerve handling/transposition as indicated","Early motion protocols"],"after":["EARLY, protected range of motion is critical - the elbow stiffens easily; structured therapy after stable fixation","Monitor for stiffness/contracture, heterotopic ossification, ulnar neuropathy, hardware irritation, and instability; nonunion is uncommon but possible","Return to activity guided by stability and union"],"pearls":["A posterior fat pad sign on elbow X-ray means an occult intra-articular fracture - often a radial head fracture - even if you cannot see a fracture line","Do not just excise a radial head that the elbow relies on for stability (e.g., terrible triad) - fix or replace it","Restore the extensor mechanism in olecranon fractures - inability to extend against gravity is the functional deficit","The elbow stiffens fast - restore stability and start early motion to protect function"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Elbow Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Radial Head / Olecranon Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556011/"},{"t":"OTA Fracture Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-hip-fractures","name":"Hip Fractures (Femoral Neck & Intertrochanteric)","sec":"ortho","present":["A common, high-morbidity FRAGILITY injury in older adults (low-energy fall) - hip fractures carry substantial 1-year MORTALITY and functional decline, making them a systemic as well as an orthopedic event","Anatomic types with different implications: FEMORAL NECK (intracapsular - risk to the femoral head blood supply, so displaced fractures often need arthroplasty) vs INTERTROCHANTERIC (extracapsular - preserved vascularity, fixed with a device)","Present with a shortened, externally rotated leg and inability to bear weight; occult fractures need MRI"],"dx":["Hip/pelvis radiographs; if clinical suspicion is high with normal X-rays, MRI (or CT) for an OCCULT fracture","Classify (Garden for femoral neck displacement; stable vs unstable intertrochanteric); medical optimization workup (these patients are often frail with comorbidities)","Evaluate the cause of the fall and bone health (fragility fracture)"],"tx":["EARLY SURGERY (ideally within ~24-48 hours) improves outcomes and mortality - combined with medical optimization and multidisciplinary (ortho-geriatric) care","FEMORAL NECK: nondisplaced/young - fixation (screws); DISPLACED in older patients - ARTHROPLASTY (hemiarthroplasty, or total hip arthroplasty for active patients); young displaced neck fractures - urgent anatomic reduction/fixation (attempt to save the head)","INTERTROCHANTERIC: internal fixation (sliding hip screw or cephalomedullary/intramedullary nail)"],"tech":["Cannulated screw fixation (nondisplaced neck); hemiarthroplasty or total hip arthroplasty (displaced neck in older patients); sliding hip screw or cephalomedullary NAIL (intertrochanteric)","Multidisciplinary perioperative (ortho-geriatric) optimization","Fragility fracture bone-health pathway"],"after":["EARLY MOBILIZATION/weight-bearing (usually as tolerated) and multidisciplinary rehabilitation to combat deconditioning, delirium, VTE, and pressure injury; ortho-geriatric co-management","OSTEOPOROSIS treatment and FALLS prevention (secondary fracture prevention is essential); watch for AVN/nonunion (neck fixation) and implant issues","Address goals of care and functional recovery; high 1-year mortality warrants realistic counseling"],"pearls":["A hip fracture in an older adult is a life-threatening event - early surgery (within ~24-48h) and ortho-geriatric co-management improve survival","Displaced intracapsular (femoral neck) fractures in older patients get arthroplasty because the head's blood supply is compromised; extracapsular (intertrochanteric) fractures get fixed","A shortened, externally rotated leg is the classic exam - but a normal X-ray with ongoing suspicion needs an MRI for an occult fracture","Do not stop at the fracture - treat the osteoporosis and prevent the next fall"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Hip Fracture Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hip Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557514/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Hip Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-femoral-shaft","name":"Femoral Shaft Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Fractures of the femoral diaphysis - usually HIGH-ENERGY (MVA, fall from height) in younger patients (consider pathologic/fragility in low-energy elderly fractures); associated with significant blood loss and polytrauma","Present with thigh pain, deformity, swelling, and inability to bear weight; substantial hemorrhage can occur into the thigh","A femoral shaft fracture should prompt evaluation for an associated FEMORAL NECK fracture (ipsilateral, easily missed) and other injuries"],"dx":["Femur radiographs (full length including hip and knee) - and dedicated hip imaging/CT to exclude an associated FEMORAL NECK fracture; trauma survey for associated injuries","Assess neurovascular status, compartments, and skin (open fracture); estimate blood loss/hemodynamics","Consider pathologic fracture in low-energy/atypical cases"],"tx":["Initial: resuscitation, pain control, and provisional stabilization (traction/splint); definitive treatment is intramedullary NAILING (the standard) - typically within 24-48 h when the patient is stable","DAMAGE-CONTROL orthopedics (temporary external fixation) for the unstable/polytrauma patient, converting to definitive nailing when physiology allows (see the damage-control entry)","Address the associated femoral neck fracture and other injuries"],"tech":["Intramedullary NAILING (antegrade or retrograde) - the gold standard for femoral shaft fractures; external fixation for damage control; plating in select cases","Reaming considerations (fat embolism/lung injury in the multiply injured)","Manage associated injuries surgically"],"after":["Early mobilization and weight-bearing (as fracture pattern/fixation allow); rehabilitation for strength and range of motion; VTE prophylaxis","Monitor for malrotation/leg-length discrepancy, nonunion/delayed union, and (with reaming in polytrauma) pulmonary complications","Return to function over months; screen for the missed ipsilateral neck fracture"],"pearls":["Intramedullary nailing is the standard for femoral shaft fractures - and most are high-energy injuries","Always look for an associated ipsilateral femoral NECK fracture - it is commonly missed and changes management","A femur fracture can hide significant blood loss - resuscitate and consider it in the trauma picture","In the unstable polytrauma patient, use damage-control external fixation first and nail later"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Femoral Shaft Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Femoral Shaft Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556057/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-distal-femur-tibial-plateau","name":"Distal Femur & Tibial Plateau Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Periarticular fractures around the knee. DISTAL FEMUR fractures occur in a bimodal pattern (high-energy young; low-energy osteoporotic elderly, including PERIPROSTHETIC fractures above a knee replacement); TIBIAL PLATEAU fractures result from axial load +/- varus/valgus force (e.g., 'bumper' injuries)","Present with knee pain, swelling (hemarthrosis), deformity, and inability to bear weight; often intra-articular with joint-surface depression/split","High risk of associated SOFT-TISSUE injury and COMPARTMENT SYNDROME (especially high-energy tibial plateau), and neurovascular injury"],"dx":["Knee/femur/tibia radiographs; CT is essential for intra-articular fractures (articular depression, split, comminution) and surgical planning; assess the Schatzker pattern (tibial plateau)","Vigilant NEUROVASCULAR and COMPARTMENT assessment (high-energy plateau fractures - popliteal vessels, peroneal nerve); evaluate the soft-tissue envelope (timing of surgery depends on swelling/blisters)","MRI/exam for associated ligament/meniscal injury"],"tx":["Restore the articular surface and axial alignment: most displaced intra-articular fractures need ORIF (plates/screws) to reconstruct the joint and mechanical axis; nondisplaced/stable patterns may be nonoperative","Staged management for high-energy injuries with soft-tissue compromise: spanning EXTERNAL FIXATION first, definitive fixation once swelling resolves","Periprosthetic distal femur fractures: fixation (locking plate/retrograde nail) or revision; manage bone loss/comminution"],"tech":["Open reduction internal fixation with periarticular locking plates (and retrograde nailing for select distal femur); articular reduction with bone grafting/void filler for depressed plateau segments","Spanning external fixation for temporary stabilization; arthroscopically assisted reduction in select plateau fractures","Address associated ligament/meniscus injuries"],"after":["Typically NON-weight-bearing or limited weight-bearing for a period (protect the articular reconstruction) with early knee RANGE OF MOTION to prevent stiffness; structured rehabilitation","Monitor for compartment syndrome (early), post-traumatic ARTHRITIS, malalignment/nonunion, stiffness, and infection","Gradual return to weight-bearing/function over months"],"pearls":["CT is essential for these intra-articular fractures - it reveals the depression/split that plain films underestimate and guides fixation","High-energy tibial plateau fractures are compartment-syndrome and neurovascular-injury magnets - examine and re-examine","Stage high-energy periarticular fractures - external fixation first, definitive fixation after the soft tissues recover, to avoid wound disasters","Restore the joint surface and mechanical axis to reduce post-traumatic arthritis; start early motion to prevent stiffness"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Tibial Plateau & Distal Femur Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tibial Plateau Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499851/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-tibial-shaft","name":"Tibial Shaft Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["The most common long-bone fracture, and the most common OPEN long-bone fracture (the subcutaneous anteromedial tibia has a thin soft-tissue envelope); from direct trauma, torsion (sports), or high-energy mechanisms","Present with leg pain, deformity, swelling, and inability to bear weight; frequently associated with fibula fractures","THE key associated emergency is acute COMPARTMENT SYNDROME - tibial shaft fractures are the leading cause"],"dx":["Tibia/fibula radiographs (full length, knee and ankle); assess the SOFT-TISSUE envelope, open-fracture grade, and neurovascular status","RELENTLESS monitoring for COMPARTMENT SYNDROME (serial exams; measure pressures if the exam is unreliable)","CT for intra-articular extension (plateau/plafond)"],"tx":["DISPLACED/unstable diaphyseal fractures: intramedullary NAILING is the standard (good union, early weight-bearing); certain patterns/locations use plating; low-energy stable fractures may be treated in a cast/functional brace with acceptable alignment","OPEN fractures: early antibiotics, debridement, stabilization, and soft-tissue coverage (high infection/nonunion risk)","Maintain a low threshold for FASCIOTOMY if compartment syndrome develops"],"tech":["Intramedullary nailing (reamed) for most displaced tibial shaft fractures; plate or external fixation for select/proximal/distal or open patterns; fasciotomy for compartment syndrome","Soft-tissue coverage (flaps) for open injuries; bone grafting for defects/nonunion","Cast/functional bracing for stable low-energy fractures"],"after":["Progressive weight-bearing (early after nailing); serial radiographs to monitor union - the tibia has a notable NONUNION/delayed-union rate (poor vascularity distally, high-energy/open injuries)","Continued compartment-syndrome vigilance early; rehabilitation for knee/ankle motion and strength","Manage nonunion (dynamization, exchange nailing, grafting) if union stalls"],"pearls":["Tibial shaft fractures are the number-one cause of acute compartment syndrome - examine and re-examine the leg","The subcutaneous tibia makes open fractures common - grade them, give early antibiotics, and plan soft-tissue coverage","Intramedullary nailing is the workhorse for displaced tibial shaft fractures and allows early weight-bearing","Watch for nonunion/delayed union - the distal tibia's tenuous blood supply and high-energy/open patterns are prone to it"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Tibial Shaft Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tibial Shaft Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448180/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-ankle-fractures","name":"Ankle Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Very common fractures of the malleoli (lateral/fibula, medial, posterior) from rotational/twisting injuries; present with ankle pain, swelling, ecchymosis, deformity, and difficulty bearing weight","The key concept is ankle STABILITY: the talus must sit congruently in the mortise - unstable fractures (bimalleolar/trimalleolar, or a fracture with deltoid/syndesmosis disruption) shift the talus and need anatomic restoration","Assess the SYNDESMOSIS (high fibular fractures - Maisonneuve - with medial injury), skin (fracture blisters), and neurovascular status"],"dx":["Ankle radiographs (AP, lateral, MORTISE view) to assess malleolar fractures, talar shift, and mortise congruity/medial clear space; the Weber (A/B/C by fibula level relative to syndesmosis) and Lauge-Hansen systems classify","Assess STABILITY (stress views/weight-bearing films for isolated lateral malleolus fractures) and the syndesmosis; palpate the proximal fibula (Maisonneuve)","CT for posterior malleolus/comminution; the OTTAWA ANKLE RULES guide who needs imaging acutely"],"tx":["STABLE fractures (e.g., isolated nondisplaced lateral malleolus with intact mortise): NONOPERATIVE (cast/boot, protected weight-bearing)","UNSTABLE fractures (talar shift, bimalleolar/trimalleolar, syndesmotic injury): ORIF to restore the mortise and stability; syndesmosis stabilization (screw/suture-button) when disrupted","Manage soft-tissue swelling/blisters (timing of surgery); urgent reduction of dislocated/deformed ankles to protect skin/neurovascular structures"],"tech":["Open reduction internal fixation (lateral malleolus plate, medial malleolus screws, posterior malleolus fixation) to restore the mortise; syndesmotic screw or suture-button fixation for syndesmotic disruption","Closed reduction and immobilization for stable fractures; urgent reduction of fracture-dislocations","CT-guided planning for complex patterns"],"after":["Protected immobilization and a period of restricted weight-bearing (pattern-dependent), progressing to range of motion and weight-bearing; physiotherapy for stiffness and strength","Monitor mortise congruity, wound healing (soft-tissue complications are common), and post-traumatic arthritis; syndesmotic fixation management per protocol","Return to activity as union/stability allow"],"pearls":["Stability, not just the fracture line, decides treatment - talar shift or a bimalleolar-equivalent injury means the ankle is unstable and usually needs fixation","Feel the proximal fibula - a high fibular fracture with medial-sided injury is a Maisonneuve pattern with syndesmotic disruption","Restore the mortise anatomically - even small talar shift accelerates post-traumatic arthritis","Reduce a dislocated/deformed ankle urgently to protect the skin and neurovascular structures, then image"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Ankle Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ankle Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470337/"},{"t":"OTA Fracture Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-foot-fractures","name":"Calcaneus & Foot Fractures (Lisfranc, Talus)","sec":"ortho","present":["High-yield foot injuries. CALCANEUS fractures: usually axial-load (fall from height) - look for associated SPINE and contralateral limb injuries and compartment syndrome of the foot; TALUS fractures (talar neck): high-energy, with AVASCULAR NECROSIS risk from tenuous blood supply","LISFRANC (tarsometatarsal) injuries: often missed - midfoot pain/swelling, plantar ECCHYMOSIS, and inability to bear weight after a twisting/axial injury; a subtle malalignment signals a significant ligamentous/bony disruption","Present with foot pain, swelling, deformity, and difficulty/inability to bear weight"],"dx":["Foot radiographs (including WEIGHT-BEARING or stress views for Lisfranc - a widened gap between the 1st/2nd metatarsal bases or a 'fleck sign' indicates injury); CT for calcaneus/talus/Lisfranc detail and surgical planning","Calcaneus: assess Bohler's angle and evaluate for the associated axial-load injuries (spine!); talus: classify (Hawkins) and assess vascularity risk","Maintain suspicion for foot COMPARTMENT SYNDROME in crush/high-energy injuries"],"tx":["CALCANEUS: nonoperative for many (soft-tissue-dependent); ORIF for displaced intra-articular fractures to restore the subtalar joint (controversial, soft-tissue timing critical)","TALAR NECK: often urgent anatomic reduction and fixation to reduce AVN risk; LISFRANC: anatomic reduction with fixation (ORIF or primary arthrodesis) - even subtle instability warrants surgery to prevent midfoot collapse/arthritis","Respect the soft-tissue envelope (delay surgery for swelling/blisters), especially in the calcaneus"],"tech":["ORIF of displaced intra-articular calcaneus fractures (respecting soft tissues); anatomic reduction and screw fixation of talar neck fractures; Lisfranc ORIF or primary arthrodesis with anatomic realignment","Foot fasciotomy for compartment syndrome; staged fixation for compromised soft tissues","CT-guided operative planning"],"after":["Prolonged NON-weight-bearing (foot fractures are slow) with staged progression; monitor wound healing (calcaneus has notorious wound complications) and soft-tissue status","Watch for AVASCULAR NECROSIS (talus - the Hawkins sign on X-ray indicates preserved vascularity), post-traumatic subtalar/midfoot ARTHRITIS, and malunion; physiotherapy","Return to weight-bearing/activity slowly over months"],"pearls":["A calcaneus fracture from a fall means look for spine and contralateral limb injuries too - and foot compartment syndrome","Lisfranc injuries are commonly missed - plantar ecchymosis and a subtle 1st-2nd metatarsal gap (weight-bearing views) are the clues, and instability needs surgery","Talar neck fractures threaten the talus's blood supply - anatomic reduction reduces the avascular necrosis risk (watch for the Hawkins sign)","Respect the soft tissues - calcaneus and high-energy foot fractures have high wound-complication rates, so time surgery to the swelling"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Calcaneus, Talus & Lisfranc Injuries","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Calcaneus / Lisfranc Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544332/"},{"t":"AOFAS Foot & Ankle Resources","u":"https://www.aofas.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-pelvic-acetabular","name":"Pelvic & Acetabular Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["High-energy PELVIC RING injuries can be LIFE-THREATENING due to HEMORRHAGE (venous plexus/arterial bleeding, large retroperitoneal space) and associated visceral/genitourinary injuries; ACETABULAR fractures are intra-articular hip-socket injuries threatening the joint","Pelvic ring patterns (Young-Burgess: anteroposterior compression/'open-book', lateral compression, vertical shear) predict instability and bleeding; present with pelvic pain, instability, deformity, and (in unstable injuries) hemodynamic compromise","Also seen as LOW-energy fragility fractures in the elderly (usually stable)"],"dx":["In high-energy trauma: ATLS approach - a pelvic X-ray in the trauma bay, then CT; assess hemodynamics and for associated injuries (bladder/urethra - blood at the meatus, high-riding prostate; rectal/vaginal injury; neurovascular)","Classify ring stability (Young-Burgess/Tile) and acetabular pattern (Letournel columns/walls) with CT","Do NOT repeatedly 'spring' the pelvis (can dislodge clot); one gentle assessment"],"tx":["UNSTABLE/bleeding pelvis: RESUSCITATION with a PELVIC BINDER (closes the ring, tamponades bleeding), transfusion protocol, and hemorrhage control - ANGIOEMBOLIZATION (arterial) and/or preperitoneal PACKING and external fixation; multidisciplinary trauma care","Definitive fixation of unstable ring injuries (external fixation, percutaneous/open fixation) once stabilized","ACETABULAR fractures: displaced/incongruent joints usually need ORIF to restore the articular surface (or acute total hip arthroplasty in select older patients)"],"tech":["Pelvic binder and external fixation for provisional stabilization; angioembolization/preperitoneal packing for hemorrhage; definitive percutaneous (iliosacral screws) or open reduction internal fixation of the ring; ORIF of acetabular columns/walls","Damage-control approach in the unstable patient","Total hip arthroplasty for select acetabular fractures"],"after":["ICU-level care for the polytrauma patient; VTE prophylaxis (high risk); staged definitive fixation; protected weight-bearing per fixation","Monitor for post-traumatic hip ARTHRITIS (acetabular), malunion/nonunion, nerve injury (lumbosacral plexus/sciatic), and genitourinary/sexual dysfunction; rehabilitation","Long-term functional follow-up; fragility pelvic fractures need bone-health and mobility support"],"pearls":["An unstable pelvic ring injury is a hemorrhage emergency - a pelvic binder plus resuscitation and hemorrhage control (embolization/packing) come before definitive fixation","Look for the associated injuries - bladder/urethral (blood at the meatus), rectal/vaginal, and lumbosacral nerve injuries travel with pelvic fractures","Do not repeatedly rock the pelvis - one gentle exam; excessive manipulation can dislodge clot and worsen bleeding","Acetabular fractures are intra-articular - restoring a congruent hip joint (usually ORIF) is key to avoiding rapid arthritis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Pelvic & Acetabular Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pelvic Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430734/"},{"t":"OTA Trauma Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-peds-physeal","name":"Pediatric Physeal (Salter-Harris) & Supracondylar Humerus Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Children's bones differ: the GROWTH PLATE (physis) is a weak point, so PHYSEAL fractures are common and can disturb growth; the SALTER-HARRIS classification (I-V) grades physeal involvement and prognosis (higher grades and type V crush have more growth-arrest risk)","The SUPRACONDYLAR HUMERUS fracture is the classic, high-stakes pediatric elbow fracture (fall on outstretched hand) - prone to NEUROVASCULAR injury (anterior interosseous/median nerve, brachial artery) and, if malreduced, cubitus varus deformity","Children also get plastic deformation, buckle (torus), and greenstick fractures; remodeling potential is high near growing physes"],"dx":["Radiographs of the injured region (comparison views of the other side can help in children); assess the physis and classify (Salter-Harris)","SUPRACONDYLAR: assess displacement (Gartland I-III), and meticulously document NEUROVASCULAR status (radial pulse, hand perfusion, and median/anterior interosseous, radial, and ulnar nerve function - test the 'OK' sign)","Watch for compartment syndrome and the pulseless hand"],"tx":["Many physeal and buckle fractures are stable and treated with IMMOBILIZATION; displaced physeal fractures need gentle anatomic REDUCTION (minimize repeated attempts - iatrogenic physeal injury)","SUPRACONDYLAR: nondisplaced (Gartland I) - casting; displaced (II-III) - CLOSED REDUCTION and percutaneous PINNING; a pulseless/poorly perfused hand or open injury is urgent (possible vascular exploration)","Counsel on growth-arrest risk for higher-grade/type V physeal injuries"],"tech":["Closed reduction and percutaneous PINNING (K-wires) for displaced supracondylar and unstable physeal fractures; open reduction for irreducible/open/vascular cases","Gentle single-attempt reductions to protect the physis; cast immobilization for stable fractures","Vascular exploration for the persistently pulseless/poorly perfused hand"],"after":["Cast/pin care with follow-up radiographs; remove pins at healing; monitor NEUROVASCULAR recovery (most nerve palsies are neuropraxias that recover) and range of motion","Long-term surveillance for GROWTH DISTURBANCE/angular deformity (physeal arrest) and cubitus varus (supracondylar malunion); most children remodel and recover well","Return to activity after healing"],"pearls":["Salter-Harris grade predicts growth-arrest risk - higher grades and the type V crush injury are the worrisome ones","The supracondylar fracture is a neurovascular fracture - document median/AIN, radial, ulnar nerve function and hand perfusion before and after reduction","Displaced supracondylar fractures are reduced and pinned; a pulseless, poorly perfused hand is a surgical emergency","Minimize repeated reduction attempts across a physis - the manipulation itself can injure the growth plate"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Supracondylar & Physeal Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Salter-Harris / Supracondylar Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470518/"},{"t":"POSNA Pediatric Orthopaedic Resources","u":"https://posna.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-ddh","name":"Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH)","sec":"ortho","present":["A spectrum of abnormal hip development, from a shallow/dysplastic acetabulum to a dislocatable or frankly dislocated hip - present at or developing after birth; early detection is critical to prevent long-term dysfunction and early arthritis","Risk factors: FEMALE sex, BREECH position, FAMILY history, firstborn, and oligohydramnios ('the 4 Fs' variably); often the LEFT hip","Infant signs: hip INSTABILITY (Ortolani/Barlow), asymmetric skin folds, limited abduction, and (walking age) a limp/Trendelenburg gait or leg-length difference"],"dx":["Newborn clinical screening with the ORTOLANI (reduces a dislocated hip - a 'clunk') and BARLOW (dislocates a reducible hip) maneuvers; asymmetric abduction is a later sign as the hip becomes fixed","Imaging: ULTRASOUND is the study of choice under ~4-6 months (before the femoral head ossifies); RADIOGRAPHS after ~4-6 months (assess acetabular index, Shenton's line)","Selective ultrasound screening for risk factors (breech, family history) and abnormal/equivocal exams"],"tx":["YOUNGER infants (typically <6 months) with a reducible hip: a PAVLIK HARNESS (holds the hip flexed/abducted to promote reduction and acetabular development) with monitoring","Older infants/failed harness: closed reduction and SPICA CASTING (+/- adductor tenotomy, arthrogram); OLDER children may need OPEN reduction and femoral/pelvic OSTEOTOMY","Earlier treatment yields better outcomes - a missed DDH means a harder operation and worse hips"],"tech":["Pavlik harness (dynamic flexion-abduction); closed reduction with spica casting under anesthesia (arthrogram-guided); open reduction with femoral shortening/pelvic (acetabular) osteotomy for older/failed cases","Ultrasound/arthrogram-guided reduction confirmation","Adductor tenotomy as an adjunct"],"after":["Serial exam and ultrasound/radiographic MONITORING of reduction and acetabular development; harness/cast care; watch for AVASCULAR NECROSIS of the femoral head (a treatment complication) and residual dysplasia","Long-term follow-up to skeletal maturity for residual dysplasia (which predisposes to early arthritis); some need later osteotomy","Family counseling on screening of future siblings"],"pearls":["Screen every newborn hip with Ortolani/Barlow - and remember the risk factors (female, breech, family history), especially the left hip","Ultrasound is the imaging test under ~4-6 months (before ossification); radiographs take over after that","Earlier treatment is easier and better - a Pavlik harness in infancy versus open surgery/osteotomy if missed","Avascular necrosis of the femoral head is the feared complication of DDH treatment - reductions must be gentle and monitored"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA: Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563157/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: DDH","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-scfe","name":"Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE)","sec":"ortho","present":["Displacement of the femoral epiphysis (head) relative to the metaphysis through the growth plate - the femoral head 'slips' posteriorly/inferiorly - typically in OVERWEIGHT ADOLESCENTS around puberty (a Salter-Harris type I equivalent through the physis)","Presents with hip, groin, thigh, or KNEE pain (referred - a classic pitfall) and a limp; the leg is often externally rotated, with obligatory external rotation on hip flexion (Drehmann sign)","An orthopedic URGENCY - progression and unstable slips risk avascular necrosis and severe deformity; consider ENDOCRINE causes (hypothyroidism, etc.) in atypical ages"],"dx":["AP and FROG-LEG LATERAL pelvis radiographs (BILATERAL - the other hip is at risk) - the lateral view best shows the slip; Klein's line fails to intersect the epiphysis","Classify STABLE (able to bear weight, better prognosis) vs UNSTABLE (unable to bear weight - high AVN risk); grade severity","Evaluate for endocrine/metabolic causes in atypical presentations"],"tx":["Make the patient NON-WEIGHT-BEARING immediately and obtain urgent surgical stabilization - do not let the slip progress","Surgical IN SITU PINNING (percutaneous screw fixation across the physis) is the standard to stabilize the slip and prevent progression; unstable/severe slips are higher-risk and managed carefully (avoid forceful reduction - increases AVN)","Consider PROPHYLACTIC fixation of the contralateral hip in high-risk patients (young age, endocrinopathy)"],"tech":["Percutaneous in situ SCREW fixation across the physis; complex/severe deformity may need osteotomy or a modified Dunn procedure (higher AVN risk); prophylactic contralateral pinning in selected cases","Avoid aggressive reduction maneuvers (AVN risk)","Endocrine workup/treatment when indicated"],"after":["Protected weight-bearing and follow-up; monitor for AVASCULAR NECROSIS (especially unstable slips) and CHONDROLYSIS, and for a CONTRALATERAL slip (the other hip commonly slips later - counsel to return for new hip/knee pain)","Watch for residual deformity/femoroacetabular impingement and early arthritis; activity progression per healing","Long-term hip surveillance"],"pearls":["An overweight adolescent with hip, thigh, or KNEE pain and a limp is SCFE until proven otherwise - knee pain referred from the hip is the classic miss","Get a frog-leg lateral and image BOTH hips - and make the patient non-weight-bearing immediately","In situ pinning stabilizes the slip - avoid forceful reduction, which raises the avascular necrosis risk","The other hip often slips later - counsel the family and consider prophylactic fixation in high-risk patients"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA: Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538302/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: SCFE","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-perthes","name":"Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease","sec":"ortho","present":["Idiopathic AVASCULAR NECROSIS (osteonecrosis) of the femoral head in CHILDREN (typically ages ~4-8, more common in boys) - the femoral head loses its blood supply, undergoes necrosis, then fragments and reossifies over time","Presents with an insidious LIMP and hip/groin/thigh/KNEE pain (referred), often painless or mild, with limited hip abduction and internal rotation","The disease self-limits through stages (necrosis -> fragmentation -> reossification -> remodeling); outcome depends on preserving femoral head SPHERICITY and CONTAINMENT within the acetabulum"],"dx":["Radiographs stage the disease (early - smaller/sclerotic epiphysis, subchondral fracture - the crescent sign; then fragmentation and reossification); MRI/bone scan detect early disease before X-ray changes","Assess the extent of head involvement and hip range of motion (loss of abduction/internal rotation); classify (Herring lateral pillar) for prognosis","Rule out other causes of a limping child (septic arthritis, SCFE, transient synovitis)"],"tx":["Goals: maintain hip MOTION and CONTAINMENT of the femoral head within the acetabulum so it remodels spherically","YOUNGER children with less involvement often do well with NONOPERATIVE care (activity modification, physiotherapy for range of motion, +/- bracing); OLDER children/more severe involvement may benefit from CONTAINMENT SURGERY (femoral or pelvic OSTEOTOMY)","Symptom control and preserving motion during the active phases"],"tech":["Containment surgery - femoral varus osteotomy or pelvic (acetabular) osteotomy - to keep the head covered during remodeling; physiotherapy and bracing as nonoperative containment","Range-of-motion restoration procedures for stiffness","Serial imaging to guide management"],"after":["Long-term follow-up through the disease stages to skeletal maturity, monitoring femoral head shape/containment and hip motion","Outcome hinges on residual head sphericity/congruity - a deformed head (aspherical) predisposes to early hip ARTHRITIS and impingement in adulthood","Activity guidance and management of residual deformity"],"pearls":["A young child (4-8) with a painless or mild limp and referred knee/thigh pain and lost hip abduction is Perthes - it is femoral head avascular necrosis of childhood","The whole treatment philosophy is 'containment' - keep the head covered and mobile so it remodels round","Younger, less-involved children generally do well nonoperatively; older/severely involved hips may need osteotomy","Prognosis depends on head sphericity at maturity - an aspherical head means early arthritis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA: Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448185/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Perthes Disease","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-clubfoot","name":"Clubfoot & Pediatric Foot Deformities","sec":"ortho","present":["Congenital talipes equinovarus (CLUBFOOT): a common congenital foot deformity with a fixed combination of hindfoot EQUINUS and VARUS, midfoot CAVUS, and forefoot ADDUCTUS ('CAVE') - the foot points down and in","May be idiopathic or associated with neuromuscular/syndromic conditions (assess for spina bifida, arthrogryposis); usually diagnosed at birth (or prenatally)","Other pediatric foot issues to recognize: flexible flatfoot (usually physiologic/benign), metatarsus adductus (often resolves), tarsal coalition (rigid painful flatfoot in older children), and cavus foot (consider a neurologic cause)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis of the rigid deformity at birth (distinguish from positional/flexible deformities that correct passively); assess rigidity and associated anomalies (spine, neuromuscular)","Radiographs are less useful in the newborn (unossified) but help in older children/coalition; MRI/CT for tarsal coalition","Screen for syndromic/neuromuscular associations"],"tx":["CLUBFOOT: the PONSETI METHOD is the standard - serial manipulation and CASTING to gradually correct the deformity, usually with a percutaneous ACHILLES TENOTOMY to correct residual equinus, followed by a foot-abduction BRACE (boots and bar) to maintain correction","BRACE ADHERENCE is the key to preventing relapse; surgery (more extensive releases) is reserved for resistant/relapsed/syndromic cases","Flexible flatfoot/metatarsus adductus - usually observation; tarsal coalition/symptomatic rigid deformities - orthotics or surgery"],"tech":["Ponseti serial casting with percutaneous Achilles tenotomy; foot-abduction orthosis (boots and bar) for maintenance; surgical release/tendon transfer for resistant/relapsed or neuromuscular clubfoot","Coalition resection or arthrodesis for symptomatic tarsal coalition","Bracing/orthotics for select deformities"],"after":["Strict BRACE compliance (boots and bar, often for years) to prevent RELAPSE - the biggest determinant of success; regular follow-up for recurrence","Monitor foot growth, function, and gait; manage relapse promptly (repeat casting/tenotomy or surgery)","Long-term follow-up, especially for neuromuscular/syndromic cases"],"pearls":["Clubfoot is the CAVE deformity (cavus, adductus, varus, equinus) - and the Ponseti casting method (with an Achilles tenotomy) is the standard treatment, largely replacing extensive surgery","Brace adherence (boots and bar) is what prevents relapse - counsel families relentlessly","Distinguish rigid clubfoot from flexible/positional deformities that correct passively and often need only observation","A rigid, painful flatfoot in an older child suggests a tarsal coalition; a cavus foot warrants a neurologic workup"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA: Clubfoot (Ponseti Method)","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Clubfoot","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546706/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Clubfoot & Pediatric Foot","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-peds-scoliosis","name":"Pediatric & Adolescent Scoliosis / Spinal Deformity","sec":"ortho","present":["A three-dimensional spinal deformity (lateral curvature WITH vertebral rotation); ADOLESCENT IDIOPATHIC scoliosis is the most common (typically girls, a right thoracic curve), but also congenital (vertebral anomalies), neuromuscular (cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy), and syndromic causes","Usually PAINLESS and detected on screening; findings include a rib HUMP on forward bending, shoulder/waist asymmetry, and truncal shift","RED FLAGS that suggest a non-idiopathic (e.g., intraspinal) cause and warrant MRI: significant PAIN, a LEFT thoracic curve, rapid progression, neurologic signs, or atypical age"],"dx":["Screening with the ADAMS FORWARD-BEND test and a scoliometer; standing full-spine RADIOGRAPHS measure the COBB ANGLE (the standard for curve magnitude) and skeletal maturity (Risser sign)","Assess PROGRESSION RISK by remaining growth (skeletal maturity, menarchal status) and curve magnitude - immature patients with larger curves progress","MRI for red flags (pain, left thoracic curve, neurologic findings, early onset)"],"tx":["OBSERVATION for small curves (roughly <25 degrees) with serial monitoring during growth","BRACING for moderate curves (roughly 25-45 degrees) in SKELETALLY IMMATURE patients - it does not correct the curve but slows/halts PROGRESSION (BrAIST trial showed bracing reduces progression to surgical threshold)","SURGERY (posterior spinal fusion with instrumentation) for large curves (roughly >45-50 degrees) or progressive deformity - to correct and stabilize"],"tech":["Thoracolumbosacral orthosis (brace) for immature moderate curves; posterior spinal instrumentation and FUSION (pedicle screws/rods) for large/progressive curves; vertebral body tethering (growth-modulating) in select cases","Growing constructs for early-onset scoliosis","Neuromonitoring during deformity surgery"],"after":["Serial monitoring of curve progression through skeletal maturity; brace-compliance support (hours worn predicts benefit); post-fusion rehabilitation and activity progression","Monitor for curve progression above/below fusion, hardware issues, and (severe/neuromuscular) pulmonary compromise","Long-term follow-up for deformity and function"],"pearls":["Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is usually a painless right thoracic curve in a girl - pain or a LEFT thoracic curve is a red flag for an intraspinal cause (get an MRI)","The Cobb angle plus remaining growth drives management: observe small curves, brace moderate curves in the immature, fuse large ones","Bracing does not straighten the spine - it prevents progression to a surgical curve (BrAIST), and compliance matters","Screen with the Adams forward-bend test - the rib hump reveals the rotational component"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA / SRS: Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499908/"},{"t":"Scoliosis Research Society","u":"https://www.srs.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-acl","name":"ACL Injury","sec":"ortho","present":["Anterior cruciate ligament tears - a common sports injury from a non-contact PIVOT/deceleration/landing (often a 'POP' felt), followed by rapid HEMARTHROSIS (swelling within hours) and a sense of INSTABILITY/giving way","The ACL resists anterior tibial translation and rotation; tears frequently accompany meniscal and MCL injuries (the 'unhappy triad')","More common in female athletes; recurrent instability episodes can damage the meniscus/cartilage over time"],"dx":["Examination: the LACHMAN test (most sensitive), anterior drawer, and PIVOT-SHIFT (rotational instability); an acute tense hemarthrosis after a pivoting injury strongly suggests ACL rupture","MRI confirms the tear and identifies associated meniscal/chondral/ligament injuries and bone bruises","Assess for the locked knee (bucket-handle meniscus) and other injuries"],"tx":["NONOPERATIVE (rehabilitation focused on quadriceps/hamstring strength and neuromuscular control) is reasonable for lower-demand patients or those willing to modify activity","ACL RECONSTRUCTION (arthroscopic, with autograft - hamstring/BTB/quadriceps - or allograft) for young/active patients, those with recurrent instability, or high-demand pivoting sports; repair addresses associated meniscal injury","Prevention programs (neuromuscular training) reduce injury risk, especially in female athletes"],"tech":["Arthroscopic ACL RECONSTRUCTION with graft (autograft or allograft); concomitant meniscal repair; lateral extra-articular tenodesis in select high-risk cases","Bracing and structured rehabilitation for nonoperative management","Address meniscal/chondral pathology at surgery"],"after":["Structured, progressive REHABILITATION is central; return to pivoting sport is typically ~9-12 months with criteria-based (not just time-based) clearance to reduce re-injury","Monitor for graft failure/re-tear (higher in young athletes returning early), contralateral ACL injury, and long-term osteoarthritis risk","Emphasize neuromuscular retraining and return-to-sport testing"],"pearls":["A pop with immediate hemarthrosis after a non-contact pivot is an ACL tear - the Lachman test is your most sensitive exam","Reconstruction is favored for young, active, pivoting athletes and recurrent instability; rehab alone can suffice for lower-demand patients","Return to sport is criteria-based (~9-12 months) - returning too early raises re-tear and contralateral-injury risk","Neuromuscular prevention programs genuinely reduce ACL injuries, especially in female athletes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: ACL Injury","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499848/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-meniscus","name":"Meniscal Injury","sec":"ortho","present":["Tears of the fibrocartilaginous menisci (which cushion and stabilize the knee): TRAUMATIC tears in younger patients (a twisting/squatting injury) versus DEGENERATIVE tears in older patients (often with coexisting osteoarthritis)","Symptoms: joint-line pain, effusion, and MECHANICAL symptoms (catching, clicking, or LOCKING - a displaced BUCKET-HANDLE fragment can block extension, a semi-urgent problem)","The meniscus has a poor blood supply except peripherally ('red zone'), which determines healing/repair potential"],"dx":["Exam: joint-LINE tenderness, effusion, and provocative tests (McMurray, Thessaly); a LOCKED knee that cannot fully extend suggests a bucket-handle tear","MRI confirms and characterizes the tear (location, pattern, red vs white zone) and coexisting osteoarthritis","Distinguish traumatic (repairable) from degenerative tears"],"tx":["REPAIRABLE tears (peripheral, vascular 'red zone', younger patients, acute) - arthroscopic meniscal REPAIR to preserve the meniscus (protects against arthritis)","NON-repairable/degenerative tears - partial meniscectomy for mechanical symptoms, but for DEGENERATIVE tears with osteoarthritis, PHYSICAL THERAPY is first-line (arthroscopic meniscectomy is NOT superior to PT for degenerative tears without mechanical locking)","A locked bucket-handle tear warrants prompt surgery to reduce/repair"],"tech":["Arthroscopic meniscal REPAIR (preferred when feasible - inside-out, all-inside, outside-in) or partial meniscectomy; meniscal transplantation in select young post-meniscectomy patients","Reduce and repair a bucket-handle tear promptly","Address concomitant ligament injury (ACL)"],"after":["REPAIR requires protected rehabilitation (restricted weight-bearing/flexion for weeks to protect healing); meniscectomy allows faster return but accelerates osteoarthritis (removing meniscus loses cushioning)","Monitor for re-tear (repair) and progression to osteoarthritis; rehabilitation for strength and motion","Counsel that meniscus preservation protects the knee long-term"],"pearls":["Preserve the meniscus when you can - repair (not removal) protects against future osteoarthritis, especially in the young","For a DEGENERATIVE meniscal tear with arthritis and no locking, physical therapy is first-line - arthroscopy is not better","A truly locked knee (bucket-handle tear blocking extension) is a reason to operate sooner","Joint-line tenderness with mechanical catching/locking and a positive McMurray points to a meniscal tear"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Meniscal Injury","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Meniscus Injuries","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431067/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-multiligament-knee","name":"PCL / MCL / LCL & Multiligament Knee Injury","sec":"ortho","present":["The other knee ligaments. PCL (posterior cruciate): from a dashboard injury or hyperflexion/fall on a flexed knee (posterior tibial force); MCL (medial collateral - the MOST common knee ligament injury): from a VALGUS force; LCL/posterolateral corner (PLC): from a VARUS/rotational force","MULTILIGAMENT injuries (two or more, e.g., ACL+PCL+PLC) imply a high-energy injury or a spontaneously reduced KNEE DISLOCATION - with the associated VASCULAR and nerve risks","Present with instability, effusion, and pain; isolated MCL/PCL injuries are often subtle"],"dx":["Exam: POSTERIOR DRAWER and posterior tibial SAG (PCL), VALGUS stress (MCL), VARUS stress and dial/external rotation tests (LCL/PLC); grade laxity","MRI characterizes the ligament injuries; for suspected multiligament injury/dislocation, ASSESS the VASCULAR status (popliteal artery) and peroneal nerve","Radiographs (avulsions, alignment); recognize the multiligament pattern as a reduced dislocation"],"tx":["Isolated MCL: usually NONOPERATIVE (hinged brace, rehabilitation - it heals well); isolated PCL (lower grade): often nonoperative (quadriceps rehab)","LCL/POSTEROLATERAL CORNER injuries and MULTILIGAMENT injuries typically need SURGICAL repair/reconstruction (PLC injuries do poorly untreated and cause failure of cruciate reconstructions)","Multiligament/dislocation: staged reconstruction after ensuring vascular integrity (see knee dislocation)"],"tech":["Hinged bracing and rehabilitation (MCL, low-grade PCL); surgical reconstruction/repair of PLC, high-grade PCL, and multiligament injuries (staged)","Vascular assessment/management for multiligament/dislocation patterns","Address all injured structures to restore stability"],"after":["Bracing and progressive rehabilitation; multiligament reconstructions have prolonged recovery and a tendency to STIFFNESS - balance early motion with protection","Monitor for residual instability, stiffness (arthrofibrosis), and (multiligament) neurovascular issues","Criteria-based return to activity"],"pearls":["The MCL is the most commonly injured knee ligament and usually heals with a brace and rehab - not surgery","Do not miss the posterolateral corner - untreated PLC injury causes chronic instability and dooms ACL/PCL reconstructions","A multiligament knee injury is a spontaneously reduced dislocation until proven otherwise - check the vessels and nerve","Posterior sag and a positive posterior drawer indicate a PCL injury, which is often managed nonoperatively when isolated"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: PCL / PLC / Multiligament Knee","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Posterior Cruciate Ligament Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430726/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-patellofemoral","name":"Patellofemoral Disorders & Patellar Instability","sec":"ortho","present":["Two overlapping problems. PATELLOFEMORAL PAIN SYNDROME (PFPS): anterior knee pain worsened by stairs, squatting, and prolonged sitting ('theater sign') - common in young, active people (often women), from maltracking/overuse without structural instability","PATELLAR INSTABILITY/DISLOCATION: the patella dislocates LATERALLY (disrupting the medial patellofemoral ligament - MPFL), with apprehension and recurrent episodes; predisposing anatomy includes trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, and an increased TT-TG distance","A first dislocation may reduce spontaneously (with knee extension) and can shear off an osteochondral fragment"],"dx":["PFPS: clinical - anterior knee pain, patellar tenderness/crepitus, and pain with the noted activities; assess lower-limb alignment and hip/core weakness","INSTABILITY: apprehension test, a positive J-SIGN (lateral patellar tracking), and imaging for predisposing anatomy (radiographs, and CT/MRI for trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, TT-TG distance, and osteochondral fragments after dislocation)","Exclude other causes of anterior knee pain"],"tx":["PFPS: PHYSICAL THERAPY is the mainstay (quadriceps/VMO and especially HIP/core strengthening), activity modification, and taping/orthotics - most improve without surgery","PATELLAR INSTABILITY: first-time dislocation - rehabilitation (unless an osteochondral fragment needs fixation); RECURRENT instability - MPFL RECONSTRUCTION, with bony procedures (tibial tubercle osteotomy, trochleoplasty) to address underlying anatomy","Remove/fix loose osteochondral fragments after dislocation"],"tech":["MPFL reconstruction for recurrent instability; tibial tubercle osteotomy (to correct an elevated TT-TG or patella alta) and trochleoplasty for dysplasia; osteochondral fragment fixation/removal","Physical therapy and bracing for PFPS and first-time dislocations","Address the specific anatomic risk factors"],"after":["PFPS: adherence to a hip/quadriceps rehabilitation program is the key to recovery; instability surgery - protected rehab and progressive return","Monitor for recurrent dislocation (higher with untreated anatomic risk factors and in the young) and for cartilage damage/arthritis","Criteria-based return to activity"],"pearls":["Patellofemoral pain is treated with rehab - and the hip/core, not just the quad, is where the fix often lies","Patellar dislocations go laterally and tear the MPFL - recurrent instability is where MPFL reconstruction (plus addressing bony risk factors) comes in","After a patellar dislocation, look for a sheared osteochondral fragment that may need fixation","Trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, and a high TT-TG distance predict recurrence - correct them for durable results"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Patellofemoral Instability & Pain","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Patellar Dislocation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538278/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-cartilage-lesions","name":"Cartilage & Osteochondral Lesions","sec":"ortho","present":["Focal defects of articular CARTILAGE (and the underlying bone in osteochondral lesions) - a challenge because hyaline cartilage has poor intrinsic healing; common in the knee, talus, and elbow (capitellum)","OSTEOCHONDRITIS DISSECANS (OCD): a subchondral bone lesion (often in adolescents/young adults) that can separate and form a loose body; osteochondral fractures follow acute trauma/dislocation","Present with activity-related pain, effusion, and MECHANICAL symptoms (catching/locking from unstable fragments/loose bodies)"],"dx":["Radiographs may show the OCD lesion/loose body; MRI is the key study to assess cartilage, subchondral bone, and lesion STABILITY (fluid beneath the fragment suggests instability)","Assess lesion size, depth, containment, and the patient's age/skeletal maturity (juvenile OCD has better healing potential)","Identify loose bodies causing mechanical symptoms"],"tx":["Stable/juvenile OCD lesions: nonoperative (activity modification/offloading) can allow healing; unstable/loose fragments need surgery","Cartilage restoration options by size/location: MICROFRACTURE (marrow stimulation - forms fibrocartilage, good for small lesions), OSTEOCHONDRAL AUTOGRAFT/allograft transfer (OATS/OCA - for larger/osteochondral defects), and autologous chondrocyte implantation (ACI/MACI); OCD FIXATION when the fragment is salvageable","Address malalignment/instability that overloads the cartilage"],"tech":["Arthroscopic microfracture (marrow stimulation); osteochondral autograft/allograft transplantation (OATS/OCA); autologous chondrocyte implantation (ACI/MACI); OCD fragment fixation or loose-body removal","Realignment osteotomy to offload the lesion","MRI-guided treatment selection by lesion size/depth"],"after":["PROLONGED protected rehabilitation (restricted weight-bearing, often continuous passive motion) to protect the healing cartilage - recovery is slow and return to sport is delayed","Monitor for graft/repair failure and progression to osteoarthritis; staged rehabilitation","Manage underlying alignment/instability to protect the result"],"pearls":["Cartilage does not heal well on its own - treatment matches the lesion: microfracture for small defects, osteochondral grafts/ACI for larger ones","MRI assesses OCD stability - fluid beneath the fragment means it is unstable and likely needs surgery","Juvenile OCD (open physes) has real healing potential with offloading - adults do worse","Fix or offload the underlying alignment/instability, or the cartilage repair will fail"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Cartilage Injury & OCD","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteochondritis Dissecans","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526091/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-rotator-cuff","name":"Rotator Cuff Disease","sec":"ortho","present":["A spectrum from tendinopathy/impingement to partial- and full-thickness TEARS of the rotator cuff (supraspinatus most commonly) - DEGENERATIVE tears are common with age, while TRAUMATIC tears occur in younger patients after a fall/injury","Present with lateral shoulder/deltoid pain (worse at NIGHT and with OVERHEAD activity), weakness, and loss of function; chronic massive tears can cause pseudoparalysis and cuff-tear arthropathy","Subacromial impingement and biceps pathology often coexist"],"dx":["Examination: painful arc, empty-can/JOBE test (supraspinatus), external-rotation lag/weakness (infraspinatus), lift-off/belly-press (subscapularis), DROP-ARM (large tear), and impingement signs (Neer, Hawkins)","Imaging: radiographs (proximal humeral migration in massive chronic tears); ULTRASOUND or MRI to confirm and size the tear","Distinguish degenerative from acute traumatic tears (affects urgency of repair)"],"tx":["NONOPERATIVE (physical therapy for the cuff and scapular stabilizers, activity modification, +/- subacromial corticosteroid injection) is effective for many degenerative/partial tears and impingement","SURGICAL REPAIR for acute traumatic full-thickness tears (repair earlier), younger patients, and those who fail nonoperative care - usually arthroscopic","IRREPARABLE/massive tears or cuff-tear arthropathy: options include superior capsular reconstruction, tendon transfer, or REVERSE total shoulder arthroplasty"],"tech":["Arthroscopic rotator cuff REPAIR (suture anchors); subacromial decompression as indicated; reverse total shoulder ARTHROPLASTY for cuff-tear arthropathy/irreparable tears; tendon transfer/superior capsular reconstruction in select cases","Corticosteroid injection and structured physiotherapy for nonoperative management","Address concomitant biceps/AC pathology"],"after":["After repair: a period of SLING immobilization then a STAGED rehabilitation protocol (protect the repair before loading) - recovery is months-long","Monitor for RE-TEAR (higher with larger tears, older/degenerative tissue, poor compliance) and stiffness","Set expectations - degenerative cuff disease is common and not all tears need repair"],"pearls":["Night pain and painful overhead weakness with a positive Jobe/drop-arm test point to a rotator cuff tear","Repair acute traumatic full-thickness tears earlier; many degenerative tears and impingement do well with therapy and injection","Massive irreparable tears with arthritis (cuff-tear arthropathy) are an indication for reverse total shoulder arthroplasty","Protect the repair - staged rehab matters, and re-tears are common with large, degenerative tears"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Rotator Cuff Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rotator Cuff Tear","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547664/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Rotator Cuff Disease","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-shoulder-instability","name":"Shoulder Instability & Dislocation","sec":"ortho","present":["The glenohumeral joint is the most mobile - and most commonly DISLOCATED - joint. ANTERIOR dislocation (>90%) from an abduction/external-rotation force, POSTERIOR dislocation from seizures/electrocution/dashboard trauma (easily missed - a 'lightbulb sign' on X-ray)","Anterior dislocation typically tears the anteroinferior labrum (BANKART lesion) and can dent the humeral head (HILL-SACHS lesion); present with a squared-off shoulder, an arm held in slight abduction/external rotation, and severe pain","RECURRENCE is high, especially in YOUNG (contact) athletes; assess the axillary nerve"],"dx":["Radiographs (AP, axillary/scapular-Y) BEFORE and AFTER reduction to confirm direction and detect fractures (a posterior dislocation may look subtle - get the axillary view); assess AXILLARY NERVE (deltoid/lateral shoulder sensation)","After reduction, MRI/MR-arthrogram for labral (Bankart) and bony (glenoid/Hill-Sachs) lesions in recurrent/young patients; CT for bone loss quantification","Apprehension/relocation tests for chronic instability"],"tx":["Acute: prompt closed REDUCTION and a period of immobilization, then rehabilitation","YOUNG athletes and RECURRENT instability: arthroscopic BANKART REPAIR (labral repair); significant GLENOID BONE LOSS or engaging Hill-Sachs lesions need a bony procedure (LATARJET coracoid transfer)","First-time dislocation in older patients often does well nonoperatively (but higher rotator cuff tear risk in older patients - evaluate the cuff)"],"tech":["Closed reduction (various techniques); arthroscopic Bankart (labral) repair; LATARJET/coracoid transfer or bone-block procedures for glenoid bone loss; remplissage for engaging Hill-Sachs lesions","Rehabilitation and bracing","Address associated cuff/nerve injury"],"after":["Immobilization then progressive rehabilitation focusing on rotator cuff/scapular control; counsel young athletes on HIGH RECURRENCE risk without stabilization","Monitor for recurrent instability, and (older patients) associated rotator cuff tears; return to sport is criteria-based","Bone-loss cases need appropriate surgical planning to avoid failure"],"pearls":["Anterior dislocation is by far the most common; a posterior dislocation (seizure, electrocution) is the one that gets missed - get the axillary view","Young contact athletes have very high recurrence after a first dislocation - arthroscopic Bankart repair is often warranted","Significant glenoid bone loss or an engaging Hill-Sachs lesion means a soft-tissue-only repair will fail - do a Latarjet","Always check the axillary nerve, and in older patients evaluate for an associated rotator cuff tear"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Shoulder Instability","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Shoulder Dislocation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459125/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-shoulder-other","name":"Adhesive Capsulitis, AC Joint & Biceps Disorders","sec":"ortho","present":["A cluster of common shoulder-pain sources. ADHESIVE CAPSULITIS ('frozen shoulder'): painful, progressive GLOBAL loss of shoulder motion (especially EXTERNAL ROTATION) from capsular inflammation/fibrosis - strongly associated with DIABETES; it evolves through freezing, frozen, and thawing phases and is usually self-limited (but slow, over 1-3 years)","ACROMIOCLAVICULAR (AC) JOINT: separations (fall onto the shoulder - graded I-VI) and AC osteoarthritis (top-of-shoulder pain, cross-body adduction pain)","BICEPS: long-head biceps tendinopathy/instability and PROXIMAL rupture (a 'Popeye' deformity - often well tolerated)"],"dx":["Adhesive capsulitis: clinical - loss of both ACTIVE and PASSIVE motion (especially external rotation) with otherwise normal imaging; screen for diabetes/thyroid disease","AC joint: exam (localized AC tenderness, cross-body adduction), radiographs grade separations (Rockwood); biceps: Speed/Yergason tests, exam for Popeye deformity","Exclude rotator cuff and cervical causes"],"tx":["ADHESIVE CAPSULITIS: reassurance (self-limited), physical therapy/stretching, analgesia, and intra-articular CORTICOSTEROID injection (helps pain and recovery); refractory cases - hydrodilatation, manipulation under anesthesia, or arthroscopic capsular release; optimize diabetes","AC SEPARATIONS: most (grades I-III) are NONOPERATIVE (sling, rehab); high grades (IV-VI) or symptomatic AC OA - surgery (reconstruction/distal clavicle excision)","BICEPS: proximal ruptures usually nonoperative; tenodesis/tenotomy for symptomatic tendinopathy/instability"],"tech":["Arthroscopic capsular release/manipulation under anesthesia (refractory frozen shoulder); AC/coracoclavicular reconstruction or distal clavicle excision (AC pathology); biceps tenodesis/tenotomy","Injections (glenohumeral for capsulitis, AC joint) and structured physiotherapy","Address associated cuff disease"],"after":["Adhesive capsulitis: reassure about the long but generally favorable natural history; maintain a stretching program; monitor motion recovery","AC/biceps: rehabilitation and return to activity; counsel that a Popeye deformity is usually cosmetic","Optimize diabetes (drives frozen shoulder recurrence/severity)"],"pearls":["Loss of PASSIVE external rotation (not just active) is the hallmark of adhesive capsulitis - and diabetes is the classic association","Frozen shoulder is self-limited but slow (1-3 years) - therapy and a steroid injection help; release is for the refractory few","Most AC separations (grades I-III) are treated nonoperatively - reserve surgery for high grades or symptomatic AC arthritis","A proximal biceps ('Popeye') rupture is usually well tolerated and treated nonoperatively"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Adhesive Capsulitis, AC Joint & Biceps","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adhesive Capsulitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532955/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-carpal-tunnel","name":"Carpal Tunnel & Compressive Neuropathies","sec":"ortho","present":["Peripheral nerve compression syndromes. CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME (median nerve at the wrist - the most common): numbness/tingling in the THUMB, index, middle, and radial ring finger, worse at NIGHT and with driving/phone use, with thenar weakness/atrophy in advanced cases","CUBITAL TUNNEL SYNDROME (ulnar nerve at the elbow - the second most common): numbness in the SMALL and ulnar ring finger, worse with elbow flexion, and intrinsic hand weakness/clawing when advanced","Risk factors: repetitive use, pregnancy, obesity, diabetes, hypothyroidism, and rheumatoid arthritis"],"dx":["Clinical: provocative tests (Phalen and Tinel at the wrist for carpal tunnel; elbow flexion and Tinel at the cubital tunnel for ulnar), sensory mapping, and assessment of thenar/intrinsic muscle wasting","ELECTRODIAGNOSTIC studies (nerve conduction/EMG) confirm the diagnosis, localize and grade severity, and guide surgery","Screen for contributing systemic conditions"],"tx":["MILD-MODERATE carpal tunnel: night SPLINTING (neutral wrist), activity modification, and corticosteroid injection; cubital tunnel: activity modification, night elbow splinting (avoid prolonged flexion), padding","SURGICAL decompression for severe/persistent symptoms or motor deficit: carpal tunnel RELEASE (transverse carpal ligament) and ulnar nerve decompression +/- TRANSPOSITION at the elbow","Treat underlying contributors"],"tech":["Carpal tunnel release (open or endoscopic - dividing the transverse carpal ligament); cubital tunnel decompression, medial epicondylectomy, or ulnar nerve transposition","Splinting, injection, and activity modification for nonoperative care","Nerve studies to guide timing"],"after":["Symptom relief is often rapid for sensory symptoms; established motor deficit/atrophy may not fully recover (a reason not to delay surgery in severe cases)","Hand therapy for grip/strength; monitor for recurrence and scar/pillar pain after release","Manage contributing conditions"],"pearls":["Night-time numbness of the thumb-to-radial-ring fingers is carpal tunnel; small-and-ring-finger numbness worse with elbow flexion is cubital tunnel","Thenar (median) or intrinsic (ulnar) wasting is a late sign - do not delay decompression once there is motor involvement","Nerve conduction studies confirm, localize, and grade - useful before surgery","Look for the treatable systemic contributors: pregnancy, diabetes, hypothyroidism, and rheumatoid arthritis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Carpal Tunnel Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448179/"},{"t":"ASSH Hand Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-hand-infections-tendon","name":"Hand Infections & Flexor Tendon Injuries","sec":"ortho","present":["Hand infections are urgent because of the tight compartments and functional stakes: FELON (pulp-space abscess of the fingertip - tense, throbbing), PARONYCHIA (nail-fold infection), and pyogenic FLEXOR TENOSYNOVITIS (infection within the flexor sheath - a surgical emergency)","FIGHT BITE (a laceration over the MCP joint from a punch to a mouth - Eikenella/oral flora, often with joint/tendon involvement) is notorious for being underestimated","FLEXOR TENDON lacerations disrupt active finger flexion; zone II ('no man's land') repairs are technically demanding"],"dx":["Recognize pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis by the KANAVEL SIGNS: a fusiform (sausage) swollen digit, held in FLEXION, tenderness along the flexor sheath, and PAIN ON PASSIVE EXTENSION - the earliest and most reliable sign","Examine tendon function individually (FDP and FDS) for lacerations; assess neurovascular status and, for fight bites, joint penetration (radiographs for tooth fragments/air/fracture)","Culture and image as needed; a low threshold to explore"],"tx":["FELON/PARONYCHIA: incision and drainage plus antibiotics; FLEXOR TENOSYNOVITIS: urgent surgical DRAINAGE/irrigation of the sheath plus IV antibiotics (delay risks tendon necrosis/adhesions)","FIGHT BITE: do NOT close primarily - irrigation, debridement, antibiotics covering Eikenella/oral flora, and joint exploration if the MCP is involved","FLEXOR TENDON lacerations: surgical REPAIR (ideally early) followed by a structured therapy protocol"],"tech":["Incision and drainage of felon/paronychia/deep-space infection; surgical sheath irrigation for flexor tenosynovitis; irrigation/debridement (not primary closure) for fight bites; flexor tendon repair (core + epitendinous sutures)","Joint exploration/washout for septic MCP (fight bite)","Hand therapy protocols post tendon repair"],"after":["Antibiotics and wound care; HAND THERAPY after tendon repair (early controlled motion protocols) to prevent adhesions/stiffness while protecting the repair","Monitor for stiffness, tendon RE-RUPTURE, recurrent/ongoing infection, and functional recovery","Elevation and edema control"],"pearls":["The Kanavel signs - fusiform swelling, a flexed digit, tenderness along the sheath, and pain on passive extension - flag pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis, a surgical emergency","Never primarily close a fight bite - treat it as a contaminated joint injury with Eikenella coverage and exploration","Zone II ('no man's land') flexor tendon repairs are unforgiving - and all tendon repairs need a careful hand-therapy protocol","Drain a felon or flexor sheath infection - antibiotics alone do not clear a closed-space hand infection"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Hand Infections & Flexor Tendon Injury","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Flexor Tenosynovitis / Hand Infections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526074/"},{"t":"ASSH Hand Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-hand-conditions","name":"Dupuytren, Trigger Finger & Hand Osteoarthritis","sec":"ortho","present":["Common non-traumatic hand conditions. DUPUYTREN CONTRACTURE: progressive fibrosis of the PALMAR fascia forming nodules and CORDS that flex the fingers (ring and small most common) - familial, Northern European descent, associated with diabetes/alcohol","TRIGGER FINGER (stenosing tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley): CATCHING/LOCKING of the finger with painful clicking, worse in the morning; associated with diabetes","HAND OSTEOARTHRITIS: commonly the thumb CMC (basal) joint (pain with pinch/grip) and the finger DIP (Heberden nodes) and PIP (Bouchard nodes) joints"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: Dupuytren - palpable palmar cords/nodules with a positive tabletop test (cannot flatten the hand); trigger finger - a palpable A1-pulley nodule with triggering; hand OA - joint-specific tenderness/deformity (grind test for CMC OA)","Radiographs for hand OA (joint space loss, osteophytes) and to plan surgery","Assess functional impairment (the driver of treatment)"],"tx":["TRIGGER FINGER: corticosteroid INJECTION (often curative) first; A1 PULLEY RELEASE (percutaneous/open) for refractory cases (release earlier in diabetics who respond less to injection)","DUPUYTREN: intervene when contracture impairs function (positive tabletop test) - collagenase injection, needle aponeurotomy, or fasciectomy","HAND OA: splinting, activity modification, NSAIDs, and injections first; surgery (CMC arthroplasty/suspensionplasty, joint fusion/arthroplasty) for refractory disease"],"tech":["A1 pulley release (trigger finger); needle aponeurotomy, collagenase injection, or fasciectomy (Dupuytren); CMC arthroplasty/suspensionplasty, arthrodesis, or joint replacement (hand OA)","Corticosteroid injections and splinting for nonoperative management","Hand therapy alongside"],"after":["Hand THERAPY and splinting after surgery (especially Dupuytren - to maintain correction and prevent RECURRENCE, which is common); trigger finger release usually recovers quickly","Monitor for recurrence (Dupuytren), stiffness, and functional outcomes","Manage associated diabetes"],"pearls":["Dupuytren is a palmar-fascia disease (cords/nodules) - operate on function (a positive tabletop test), and expect a recurrence rate","Trigger finger often resolves with a corticosteroid injection - reserve A1 pulley release for refractory cases (and release diabetics sooner)","Thumb CMC (basal joint) arthritis is a very common, treatable cause of hand pain with pinch - start with splinting and injection","Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes mark finger osteoarthritis - largely managed conservatively"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Dupuytren, Trigger Finger & Hand OA","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Trigger Finger / Dupuytren Contracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526096/"},{"t":"ASSH Hand Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-ankle-sprains","name":"Ankle Sprains & Chronic Instability","sec":"ortho","present":["The most common musculoskeletal injury: LATERAL ankle sprains from an INVERSION mechanism, injuring the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL, most common) +/- calcaneofibular ligament","HIGH ANKLE (syndesmotic) sprains from external rotation cause pain above the ankle joint and a longer recovery; medial (deltoid) sprains are less common","Present with lateral ankle pain, swelling, and difficulty bearing weight; most heal, but a subset develops CHRONIC instability (recurrent giving way)"],"dx":["Apply the OTTAWA ANKLE RULES to decide who needs radiographs (bony tenderness at the malleoli/navicular/5th metatarsal base, or inability to bear weight) - to exclude fracture","Exam: anterior DRAWER and talar tilt (ligamentous laxity), and the squeeze/external-rotation test for syndesmotic (high ankle) injury; grade the sprain","MRI/stress views for chronic instability or when a fracture/osteochondral lesion is suspected"],"tx":["FUNCTIONAL rehabilitation is the mainstay (NOT prolonged immobilization): the PRICE/protection principles early, then early weight-bearing, bracing, and PROPRIOCEPTIVE/strengthening exercises","High ankle (syndesmotic) sprains need longer protection and, if unstable/diastatic, surgical stabilization","CHRONIC lateral instability that fails rehabilitation: ligament reconstruction/repair (BROSTROM procedure)"],"tech":["Functional bracing and proprioceptive rehabilitation; Brostrom (Brostrom-Gould) lateral ligament repair for chronic instability; syndesmotic fixation (screw/suture-button) for unstable high ankle sprains","Address associated osteochondral lesions","Physiotherapy program"],"after":["Progressive return to activity with a focus on PROPRIOCEPTION and strengthening to prevent RECURRENCE; bracing during return to sport","Monitor for chronic instability, persistent pain (consider osteochondral lesion, peroneal tendon injury, or occult fracture), and recurrent sprains","Criteria-based return to sport"],"pearls":["Use the Ottawa ankle rules to spare unnecessary X-rays while not missing fractures","Functional rehabilitation with early weight-bearing and proprioceptive training beats prolonged immobilization for lateral sprains","A high ankle (syndesmotic) sprain hurts above the joint, recovers slowly, and may need surgical stabilization if unstable","Persistent pain after a 'simple sprain' should prompt a look for an osteochondral lesion, peroneal tendon injury, or occult fracture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Ankle Sprains","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ankle Sprain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459212/"},{"t":"AOFAS Foot & Ankle Resources","u":"https://www.aofas.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-achilles","name":"Achilles Tendon Rupture & Tendinopathy","sec":"ortho","present":["ACHILLES RUPTURE: a sudden injury classically in the middle-aged 'weekend warrior' during push-off/acceleration - a sensation of being kicked/struck in the back of the ankle, a POP, and weak plantarflexion (difficulty with toe-off/stairs)","ACHILLES TENDINOPATHY: overuse-related pain and thickening, either MID-SUBSTANCE (2-6 cm above insertion) or INSERTIONAL (at the calcaneus, sometimes with a Haglund deformity)","Fluoroquinolone antibiotics and corticosteroids increase rupture/tendinopathy risk"],"dx":["Rupture: a palpable GAP in the tendon and a positive THOMPSON test (squeezing the calf produces NO plantarflexion when the tendon is ruptured); ultrasound/MRI if uncertain","Tendinopathy: localized tenderness/thickening, pain with activity; imaging (ultrasound/MRI) for refractory cases","Assess the mechanism and risk factors (fluoroquinolones, steroids)"],"tx":["RUPTURE: both NONOPERATIVE (functional bracing/early rehabilitation in equinus, progressively dorsiflexing) and SURGICAL repair yield good outcomes - modern FUNCTIONAL rehab has narrowed the difference; surgery lowers re-rupture risk but adds wound/infection risk (shared decision by activity/patient factors)","TENDINOPATHY: ECCENTRIC loading exercise is the evidence-based mainstay, plus activity modification; avoid corticosteroid injection into the tendon (rupture risk); surgery for refractory cases","Early functional rehabilitation is emphasized for ruptures regardless of operative choice"],"tech":["Achilles tendon repair (open or percutaneous) for select ruptures; debridement/reconstruction (+/- Haglund resection, FHL transfer) for refractory tendinopathy","Functional bracing/boot with a graduated protocol for nonoperative rupture management","Eccentric exercise programs for tendinopathy"],"after":["Rupture: a graduated FUNCTIONAL rehabilitation protocol in a boot (progressive dorsiflexion and weight-bearing) whether treated operatively or not; return to sport over months","Monitor for RE-RUPTURE (higher with nonoperative treatment), wound complications (operative), and calf weakness","Tendinopathy: adherence to eccentric loading; slow recovery"],"pearls":["A positive Thompson test (calf squeeze produces no plantarflexion) diagnoses an Achilles rupture - and patients can still weakly plantarflex through other muscles, so do not be fooled","Both surgery and functional bracing work for rupture - modern functional rehab narrowed the gap; surgery lowers re-rupture but adds wound risk","Eccentric loading is the evidence-based treatment for Achilles tendinopathy - and do not inject steroid into the tendon","Ask about fluoroquinolones and steroids - they predispose to rupture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Achilles Tendon Rupture","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Achilles Tendon Rupture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430844/"},{"t":"AOFAS Foot & Ankle Resources","u":"https://www.aofas.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-foot-disorders","name":"Hallux Valgus, Plantar Fasciitis & Diabetic Foot","sec":"ortho","present":["Common foot conditions. HALLUX VALGUS (bunion): lateral deviation of the great toe with a medial eminence and first-MTP pain - multifactorial (genetics, footwear); PLANTAR FASCIITIS: the most common cause of heel pain - sharp inferior heel pain worst with the FIRST STEPS in the morning and after rest","DIABETIC FOOT: the dangerous combination of NEUROPATHY (loss of protective sensation), peripheral VASCULAR disease, and impaired healing/immunity leading to ULCERS, deep infection, CHARCOT arthropathy, and amputation","These range from nuisance (bunion, fasciitis) to limb-threatening (diabetic foot)"],"dx":["Hallux valgus: clinical and weight-bearing radiographs (hallux valgus and intermetatarsal angles); plantar fasciitis: clinical (point tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, positive windlass test) - imaging usually unnecessary","DIABETIC FOOT: examine for loss of protective sensation (10 g monofilament), pulses/perfusion, deformity, and ulcers; probe-to-bone and imaging (X-ray/MRI) for osteomyelitis; distinguish Charcot (warm, swollen, deformed neuropathic foot) from infection","Assess vascular status and infection depth"],"tx":["HALLUX VALGUS: conservative first (wide footwear, spacers, orthotics); surgical OSTEOTOMY/realignment (+/- fusion) for pain/deformity failing conservative care - not for cosmesis alone; PLANTAR FASCIITIS: stretching (calf/plantar fascia), orthotics/heel cups, activity modification - it is largely SELF-LIMITED (most resolve within a year); injections/other for refractory cases","DIABETIC FOOT: multidisciplinary care - glycemic control, OFFLOADING (total contact cast for ulcers), infection control (antibiotics, debridement), revascularization when needed, and prevention (foot care, protective footwear); Charcot - immobilization/offloading and specialist management","Amputation for non-salvageable infection/ischemia"],"tech":["Bunion correction (distal/proximal metatarsal osteotomy, Lapidus fusion); operative debridement, offloading (total contact cast), and amputation for diabetic foot; plantar fascia procedures for refractory fasciitis","Revascularization (vascular surgery) for the ischemic diabetic foot","Charcot reconstruction in select cases"],"after":["Hallux valgus: protected weight-bearing/footwear after osteotomy, recurrence possible; plantar fasciitis: reassurance about the favorable natural history and adherence to stretching","DIABETIC FOOT: relentless PREVENTION (daily foot inspection, footwear, glycemic control, regular podiatric/foot care) - recurrence and contralateral disease are common, and the goal is limb preservation","Long-term surveillance for the diabetic/Charcot foot"],"pearls":["First-step-in-the-morning heel pain with medial calcaneal tenderness is plantar fasciitis - mostly self-limited, treated with stretching and orthotics","Operate on a bunion for pain/function, not cosmesis - and counsel on recurrence","The diabetic foot is a limb-threat trifecta: neuropathy, ischemia, and impaired healing - offloading, infection control, and prevention preserve limbs","Distinguish Charcot arthropathy (warm, swollen, deformed neuropathic foot) from infection - and probe-to-bone/MRI for osteomyelitis in a diabetic ulcer"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Hallux Valgus, Plantar Fasciitis & Diabetic Foot","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Plantar Fasciitis / Diabetic Foot","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431073/"},{"t":"AOFAS Foot & Ankle Resources","u":"https://www.aofas.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-hip-oa-tha","name":"Hip Osteoarthritis & Total Hip Arthroplasty","sec":"ortho","present":["Degenerative hip joint disease (this content overlaps the family-medicine framing, with an operative lens): GROIN pain (may refer to the thigh/knee/buttock), stiffness, and reduced range of motion (loss of internal rotation is often the earliest), worse with weight-bearing/activity","Primary (age-related) or secondary (dysplasia, prior trauma, avascular necrosis, femoroacetabular impingement); a leading cause of disability in older adults","Exam: antalgic gait, restricted/painful hip motion (especially internal rotation and flexion), and reproduction of groin pain"],"dx":["Weight-bearing hip/pelvis RADIOGRAPHS: joint-space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis/cysts, and osteophytes; correlate with symptoms and function","Exclude referred pain (spine) and other hip pathology; assess for secondary causes (dysplasia, prior AVN)","Functional/pain assessment drives the decision to operate"],"tx":["CONSERVATIVE first: weight management, activity modification, physical therapy, analgesia (acetaminophen/NSAIDs), assistive devices, and intra-articular injections","TOTAL HIP ARTHROPLASTY (THA) for end-stage symptomatic arthritis failing conservative care - one of the most SUCCESSFUL and cost-effective operations in medicine, reliably relieving pain and restoring function","Choice of surgical approach and implant fixation individualized; manage comorbidities perioperatively"],"tech":["Total hip arthroplasty (replacement of femoral head and acetabulum) via anterior/posterior/lateral approaches; hip resurfacing in select younger patients; manage bone stock/deformity","Multidisciplinary perioperative optimization and VTE prophylaxis","Injections/PT for nonoperative management"],"after":["Early mobilization and rehabilitation; VTE prophylaxis; dislocation PRECAUTIONS depend on the surgical approach (higher early dislocation risk with posterior approach) - counsel accordingly","Monitor for the key complications: DISLOCATION, periprosthetic INFECTION, aseptic loosening/wear (long-term), leg-length discrepancy, and periprosthetic fracture; implants are durable for many years","Long-term follow-up for implant survivorship"],"pearls":["Loss of hip internal rotation with groin pain is early hip osteoarthritis - and hip pain often refers to the knee","Total hip arthroplasty is among the most successful operations in medicine - reserve it for end-stage disease that failed conservative care","Dislocation precautions and risk depend on the surgical approach - tailor the counseling","Always exclude referred spinal pain and secondary causes (dysplasia, AVN) before attributing hip pain to primary OA"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Hip Osteoarthritis Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hip Osteoarthritis / Arthroplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519067/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: THA","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-knee-oa-tka","name":"Knee Osteoarthritis & Total Knee Arthroplasty","sec":"ortho","present":["Degenerative knee arthritis (overlaps the family-medicine framing, operative lens): activity-related knee PAIN, stiffness (especially after rest - gelling), CREPITUS, effusions, and progressive VARUS (bow-legged) or valgus deformity","Primary or secondary (post-traumatic, post-meniscectomy, inflammatory); may be compartment-specific (medial most common)","A very common cause of disability; exam shows crepitus, effusion, reduced motion, and malalignment"],"dx":["WEIGHT-BEARING knee radiographs (standing) reveal joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, sclerosis, and alignment (weight-bearing is essential - non-weight-bearing films underestimate)","Correlate radiographic severity with symptoms/function; assess ligamentous stability and deformity","Exclude inflammatory/crystal arthritis and referred pain"],"tx":["CONSERVATIVE first-line: weight loss, exercise/physical therapy (strong evidence), NSAIDs/analgesia, and intra-articular corticosteroid injections; the evidence does NOT strongly support viscosupplementation, PRP, or arthroscopy for degenerative arthritis","TOTAL KNEE ARTHROPLASTY (TKA) for end-stage symptomatic disease failing conservative care (highly successful for pain relief); UNICOMPARTMENTAL knee arthroplasty for isolated single-compartment disease; osteotomy in select younger, active patients with unicompartmental disease/malalignment","Manage comorbidities and optimize perioperatively (weight, glycemia, smoking)"],"tech":["Total knee arthroplasty (resurfacing femur/tibia +/- patella); unicompartmental knee arthroplasty for single-compartment disease; high tibial osteotomy for younger active patients with malalignment","Perioperative optimization and VTE prophylaxis","Injections/PT for nonoperative care"],"after":["EARLY range of motion and rehabilitation are critical (STIFFNESS/arthrofibrosis is the notable TKA complication) alongside weight-bearing as tolerated; VTE prophylaxis","Monitor for the key complications: STIFFNESS, periprosthetic INFECTION (PJI), instability, patellar issues, aseptic loosening, and periprosthetic fracture; durable long-term results","Long-term implant surveillance and function follow-up"],"pearls":["Order WEIGHT-BEARING knee films - non-weight-bearing views hide the joint-space loss of osteoarthritis","Exercise and weight loss are genuinely effective first-line therapy - and arthroscopy/viscosupplementation are not supported for degenerative knee arthritis","Total knee arthroplasty reliably relieves pain in end-stage disease; unicompartmental replacement fits isolated single-compartment arthritis","Early motion after TKA is essential - stiffness (arthrofibrosis) is the characteristic complication"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS Knee Osteoarthritis Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Knee Osteoarthritis / Arthroplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507884/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: TKA","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-osteonecrosis","name":"Osteonecrosis (Avascular Necrosis)","sec":"ortho","present":["Death of bone from impaired blood supply, most commonly the FEMORAL HEAD (but also humeral head, knee, talus, scaphoid, lunate); untreated, the necrotic segment COLLAPSES and causes secondary arthritis","Risk factors: CORTICOSTEROIDS and ALCOHOL (the most common atraumatic causes), TRAUMA (femoral neck fracture/dislocation), SICKLE CELL disease, SLE, caisson disease, and idiopathic","Presents with insidious, activity-related joint (groin) pain; often bilateral in atraumatic disease"],"dx":["MRI is the most SENSITIVE and specific test - it detects EARLY disease before radiographic changes (radiographs are normal early; the CRESCENT SIGN of subchondral collapse and then flattening appear later)","STAGE the disease (Ficat-Arlet or ARCO) - pre-collapse vs post-collapse is the critical branch point for treatment","Screen the contralateral hip (often bilateral) and identify risk factors"],"tx":["PRE-COLLAPSE (early) disease: JOINT-PRESERVING options - CORE DECOMPRESSION (+/- bone grafting/biologics) to relieve pressure and promote revascularization; modify risk factors; bisphosphonates and protected weight-bearing are adjuncts of debated benefit","POST-COLLAPSE disease/secondary arthritis: total hip (or relevant joint) ARTHROPLASTY - the reliable option once the head has collapsed","Individualize by stage, lesion size/location, and joint"],"tech":["Core decompression (+/- vascularized/non-vascularized bone grafting) for pre-collapse hips; total hip arthroplasty for collapsed/arthritic joints; joint-specific procedures for other sites","Address the underlying risk factors (steroid/alcohol, sickle cell)","MRI-guided staging"],"after":["Monitor for progression to COLLAPSE (the disease can advance despite joint-preserving surgery); protected weight-bearing and follow-up imaging","Manage risk factors to protect the contralateral joint; arthroplasty patients follow standard post-replacement care","Long-term surveillance"],"pearls":["MRI catches osteonecrosis before X-rays do - order it early in an at-risk patient with joint pain and normal radiographs","Steroids and alcohol are the classic atraumatic causes - and atraumatic disease is often bilateral (image both hips)","Pre-collapse disease gets joint-preserving core decompression; once the head collapses, it is arthroplasty","The crescent sign heralds subchondral collapse - the turning point from preservation toward replacement"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Osteonecrosis","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Avascular Necrosis / Osteonecrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537007/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: AVN of the Femoral Head","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-pji-arthroplasty-complications","name":"Periprosthetic Joint Infection & Arthroplasty Complications","sec":"ortho","present":["Complications of joint replacement. PERIPROSTHETIC JOINT INFECTION (PJI): a devastating complication - ACUTE (early post-op or acute hematogenous - pain, wound drainage, warmth, fever) or CHRONIC (persistent pain, a draining sinus, early loosening)","Other complications: aseptic LOOSENING (the most common long-term reason for revision), INSTABILITY/dislocation, PERIPROSTHETIC FRACTURE, polyethylene wear/osteolysis, and stiffness","PJI must be distinguished from aseptic failure because the treatment is completely different"],"dx":["Work up suspected PJI systematically: inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP - and D-dimer), then JOINT ASPIRATION for synovial WBC count/differential, culture, and biomarkers (ALPHA-DEFENSIN/leukocyte esterase); use standardized (MSIS/ICM) CRITERIA","Radiographs for loosening/fracture/wear; hold antibiotics before culture when feasible to maximize yield; identify the organism and biofilm considerations","Assess implant fixation and timing (acute vs chronic) to plan surgery"],"tx":["ACUTE PJI (early/acute hematogenous, well-fixed implant): DEBRIDEMENT, Antibiotics, and Implant Retention (DAIR) with exchange of modular parts, plus prolonged antibiotics","CHRONIC PJI: implant REMOVAL and staged reconstruction - the TWO-STAGE revision (resection + antibiotic SPACER, then reimplantation) is the gold standard in many settings (single-stage in selected cases); prolonged organism-directed antibiotics with ID","ASEPTIC complications: revision arthroplasty for loosening/instability/wear; fixation or revision for periprosthetic fracture (Vancouver/relevant classification)"],"tech":["DAIR (debridement + modular exchange) for acute PJI; one- or TWO-STAGE revision with an antibiotic-loaded spacer for chronic PJI; revision arthroplasty for aseptic loosening/instability; ORIF or revision for periprosthetic fracture","Antibiotic cement/spacers and prolonged targeted antibiotics (ID co-management)","Bone-loss reconstruction techniques"],"after":["PROLONGED organism-directed ANTIBIOTICS and staged reconstruction with ID co-management; monitor for infection clearance (repeat markers/aspiration) before reimplantation","Rehabilitation and function restoration; monitor for recurrence/reinfection and mechanical complications","Long-term surveillance of the revised joint"],"pearls":["Every painful joint replacement is infected until proven otherwise - work up PJI (ESR/CRP then aspiration) before blaming aseptic loosening","Hold antibiotics before aspiration/cultures when you can - it protects the diagnostic yield you need to treat correctly","Acute PJI with a well-fixed implant can be treated with DAIR; chronic PJI usually needs implant removal and (often two-stage) revision","Aseptic loosening is the most common long-term reason for revision - but always rule out infection first"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / MSIS Periprosthetic Joint Infection Guidance","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prosthetic Joint Infection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448131/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: PJI & Arthroplasty Complications","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-lumbar-disc-degenerative","name":"Lumbar Disc Herniation, Radiculopathy & Degenerative Spine","sec":"ortho","present":["Degenerative lumbar spine disease (this content shares with the neurosurgery entry, framed for the orthopedic reader): a herniated disc compressing a nerve root causes RADICULOPATHY - dermatomal leg pain (SCIATICA), often with numbness/weakness, typically worse than the back pain","Common levels: L4-L5 and L5-S1 (foot dorsiflexion/great-toe extension weakness, and plantarflexion/ankle jerk changes respectively); degenerative disc disease and facet arthropathy cause mechanical axial back pain","RED FLAGS mandate urgent evaluation: cauda equina (saddle anesthesia, retention), infection, malignancy, or major/progressive neurologic deficit"],"dx":["Clinical: dermatomal pain/sensory loss, myotomal weakness, reflex changes, and a positive STRAIGHT-LEG RAISE (tension sign); localize the level","MRI is the imaging test of choice when symptoms are persistent/severe or red flags are present (but incidental disc findings are common - correlate with symptoms)","Screen for red flags (cauda equina, infection, tumor)"],"tx":["MOST radiculopathy from disc herniation IMPROVES with NONOPERATIVE care over 6-12 weeks: activity modification, analgesia/NSAIDs, physical therapy, and EPIDURAL steroid injections for symptom control","SURGERY (microdiscectomy) for persistent/disabling radicular pain failing conservative care, or for progressive/significant neurologic deficit; decompression (+/- FUSION) for stenosis/instability","CAUDA EQUINA or a progressive major motor deficit is an EMERGENCY (urgent decompression)"],"tech":["Microdiscectomy for refractory disc-related radiculopathy; laminectomy/decompression for stenosis; instrumented fusion for instability/spondylolisthesis/deformity","Epidural steroid injections and physical therapy for nonoperative management","Emergent decompression for cauda equina"],"after":["Rehabilitation and gradual return to activity after nonoperative recovery or microdiscectomy; counsel that most improve and that surgery speeds relief of leg pain more than back pain","Monitor for recurrent herniation, persistent deficits, and (fusion) adjacent-segment issues","Address ergonomics, core strengthening, and risk factors"],"pearls":["Lumbar radiculopathy is usually a self-limited disc problem - most improve in 6-12 weeks without surgery","Surgery (microdiscectomy) relieves LEG pain more reliably than back pain - set expectations accordingly","Correlate MRI with the exam - disc bulges are common incidental findings and do not always explain the pain","Cauda equina (saddle anesthesia, urinary retention) or a progressive motor deficit is the emergency exception to conservative care"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / NASS: Lumbar Disc Herniation & Degenerative Spine","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lumbar Disc Herniation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560878/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Lumbar Disc Herniation","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-spinal-trauma-deformity","name":"Spinal Trauma & Adult Spinal Deformity","sec":"ortho","present":["Two spine-surgeon domains (this content shares with the neurosurgery framing, ortho lens). SPINAL TRAUMA: vertebral fractures from high-energy injury (or low-energy in osteoporosis) - COMPRESSION (anterior column), BURST (retropulsion into the canal), flexion-distraction (CHANCE - seatbelt, associated abdominal injury), and fracture-DISLOCATION (highly unstable, often with spinal cord injury)","ADULT SPINAL DEFORMITY: degenerative scoliosis and sagittal-plane imbalance (loss of lumbar lordosis) causing back pain, stooped posture, and difficulty standing upright","Present with back pain, deformity, and (with neural compromise) neurologic deficit"],"dx":["TRAUMA: assess stability and neurologic status; CT defines the bony injury and MRI the neural elements/ligaments; classification systems (TLICS - thoracolumbar injury classification) integrate morphology, neurology, and the posterior ligamentous complex to guide operative vs nonoperative care","DEFORMITY: standing full-length spine radiographs measure curves and SAGITTAL BALANCE parameters (pelvic incidence, lumbar lordosis mismatch) that correlate with disability","Screen for spinal cord injury and associated injuries in trauma"],"tx":["TRAUMA: stable fractures (many compression/some burst without deficit) - BRACING and observation; unstable fractures, significant canal compromise with deficit, or fracture-dislocations - surgical DECOMPRESSION and STABILIZATION/fusion; manage spinal cord injury (see SCI care)","DEFORMITY: nonoperative (PT, injections, pain management) for many; reconstructive surgery (decompression, instrumented fusion, osteotomies) to restore sagittal alignment for disabling deformity/deficit","Osteoporotic vertebral fractures - pain management, bracing, and vertebral augmentation in select cases"],"tech":["Posterior (and/or anterior) instrumented fusion and decompression for unstable fractures; deformity correction with osteotomies (e.g., pedicle subtraction) and long-segment instrumentation; vertebral augmentation (kyphoplasty) for select osteoporotic fractures","Bracing for stable injuries","Neuromonitoring during deformity/trauma surgery"],"after":["Rehabilitation (and SPINAL CORD INJURY care where present); monitor neurologic status, alignment maintenance, and hardware; deformity surgery has significant complication rates (pseudarthrosis, proximal junctional issues)","Bracing follow-up for nonoperative fractures; bone-health treatment for osteoporotic fractures","Long-term follow-up for alignment and function"],"pearls":["Assess stability and the neurologic exam first in spinal trauma - TLICS integrates fracture morphology, neurology, and the posterior ligamentous complex to decide on surgery","A Chance (flexion-distraction) fracture from a seatbelt is associated with intra-abdominal injuries - look for them","Burst fractures with retropulsion and deficit need decompression/stabilization; many compression fractures are braced","Adult spinal deformity is about SAGITTAL balance - restoring lumbar lordosis (not just curve size) drives outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / AOSpine: Spinal Trauma & Deformity","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracolumbar Spine Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448158/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Spine Trauma & Adult Deformity","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-bone-tumors","name":"Bone Tumors (Benign & Malignant)","sec":"ortho","present":["Primary bone tumors span benign and malignant. BENIGN: osteochondroma (the most common - a cartilage-capped bony projection), enchondroma, giant cell tumor (epiphyseal, locally aggressive), OSTEOID OSTEOMA (NIGHT PAIN classically relieved by NSAIDs/aspirin), aneurysmal bone cyst, fibrous dysplasia, and non-ossifying fibroma","MALIGNANT: OSTEOSARCOMA (adolescents/young adults, metaphysis around the knee - a sunburst/Codman-triangle periosteal reaction), EWING SARCOMA (children/young - diaphysis, an 'onion-skin' reaction, small round blue cells), and CHONDROSARCOMA (older adults)","RED FLAGS: deep, unremitting or NIGHT pain, a growing mass, a PATHOLOGIC fracture, and systemic symptoms"],"dx":["RADIOGRAPHS first - assess aggressiveness (a wide zone of transition, cortical destruction, periosteal reaction, and soft-tissue mass suggest malignancy; well-defined sclerotic margins suggest benign); MRI for local extent and staging imaging (chest CT, bone scan/PET)","CRUCIAL: if a primary bone sarcoma is possible, REFER to a specialized sarcoma center BEFORE biopsy - an improperly placed biopsy tract can compromise limb salvage; the biopsy should be done by the treating team","Correlate age, location, and radiographic pattern (each tumor has a predilection)"],"tx":["BENIGN: observation for many (asymptomatic osteochondroma/NOF); curettage/excision for symptomatic or aggressive lesions (e.g., giant cell tumor - curettage + adjuvant, +/- denosumab); radiofrequency ablation for osteoid osteoma","MALIGNANT: multidisciplinary sarcoma care - OSTEOSARCOMA and EWING are treated with NEOADJUVANT CHEMOTHERAPY, then wide resection (LIMB SALVAGE where possible) with reconstruction, then further chemotherapy; Ewing is also radiosensitive; chondrosarcoma is largely surgical (chemo/radiation-resistant)","Manage/stabilize pathologic fractures within the oncologic plan"],"tech":["Biopsy (at the treating sarcoma center); curettage +/- adjuvant for benign/locally aggressive lesions; wide/limb-salvage resection with endoprosthetic or biologic reconstruction (or amputation when necessary) for malignant tumors","Radiofrequency ablation (osteoid osteoma)","Multidisciplinary oncologic surgery"],"after":["ONCOLOGIC surveillance for recurrence and metastasis (lung); reconstruction follow-up (implant/graft longevity, function) and rehabilitation","Continue systemic therapy per oncology; monitor for treatment effects and complications","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["Do NOT biopsy a possible bone sarcoma before referral - a poorly placed biopsy tract can cost the patient a limb-salvage option; send them to a sarcoma center","Night pain relieved by NSAIDs/aspirin is the classic osteoid osteoma story","Osteosarcoma favors the metaphysis around the knee in adolescents (sunburst/Codman triangle); Ewing favors the diaphysis with an onion-skin reaction","Aggressive radiographic features (wide zone of transition, cortical destruction, soft-tissue mass) flag malignancy - image and refer"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / MSTS: Bone Tumors","u":"https://www.msts.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bone Tumors / Osteosarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544239/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Bone Tumors","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-soft-tissue-mets","name":"Soft Tissue Sarcoma & Metastatic Bone Disease","sec":"ortho","present":["SOFT TISSUE SARCOMA: malignant tumors of mesenchymal soft tissues - the WARNING features are a mass that is DEEP (to fascia), LARGE (>5 cm), ENLARGING, or painful (any such mass is a sarcoma until proven otherwise, even if painless)","METASTATIC BONE DISEASE: far more common than primary bone cancer in adults - metastases from BREAST, PROSTATE, LUNG, KIDNEY, and THYROID cancers cause bone pain and PATHOLOGIC or impending fractures; in an adult >40 with a destructive bone lesion, metastasis/myeloma is the leading diagnosis","Present with a soft-tissue mass or with bone pain/pathologic fracture"],"dx":["SOFT TISSUE MASS: MRI characterizes it; a deep/large/enlarging mass warrants REFERRAL to a sarcoma center and a properly planned biopsy (do not 'shell out' an unknown mass)","METASTATIC DISEASE: imaging of the lesion and a systematic search for the PRIMARY (history/exam, CT chest-abdomen-pelvis, bone scan, labs incl PSA, and myeloma workup - SPEP/UPEP); assess FRACTURE RISK (the MIRELS score for impending fracture)","Biopsy to confirm; stage the disease"],"tx":["SOFT TISSUE SARCOMA: multidisciplinary care - WIDE surgical excision (limb salvage) often with radiotherapy (+/- chemotherapy by subtype) at a sarcoma center","METASTATIC BONE DISEASE: PROPHYLACTIC fixation of IMPENDING pathologic fractures (high Mirels score) and stabilization/arthroplasty of actual pathologic fractures, plus radiotherapy and systemic/oncologic therapy; treat the primary and support bone health (bisphosphonates/denosumab)","Palliative and supportive care integration"],"tech":["Wide excision (+/- radiotherapy) for soft tissue sarcoma; prophylactic intramedullary nailing/plating or endoprosthetic replacement for impending/actual pathologic fractures; stabilization tailored to expected survival/lesion","Biopsy at the treating center; radiotherapy planning","Multidisciplinary oncologic management"],"after":["Oncologic surveillance and systemic therapy; rehabilitation and functional support; monitor fixation/reconstruction","Bone-health agents and pain/palliative management for metastatic disease; treat the primary","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up"],"pearls":["A deep, large (>5 cm), or enlarging soft-tissue mass is a sarcoma until proven otherwise - even if painless; refer before biopsy","In an adult over 40 with a destructive bone lesion, think metastasis or myeloma first - and find the primary (breast, prostate, lung, kidney, thyroid) plus a myeloma workup","Fix impending pathologic fractures prophylactically (high Mirels score) - it is easier and better than fixing after they break","Do not shell out an unknown soft-tissue mass - an unplanned excision compromises definitive sarcoma treatment"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / MSTS: Soft Tissue Sarcoma & Metastatic Bone Disease","u":"https://www.msts.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Metastatic Bone Disease / Soft Tissue Sarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430685/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Metastatic Bone Disease","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-osteomyelitis","name":"Osteomyelitis (Acute & Chronic)","sec":"ortho","present":["Infection of BONE. HEMATOGENOUS osteomyelitis: seeding via blood - in CHILDREN it favors the long-bone METAPHYSIS (rich blood supply), and in adults the VERTEBRAE (vertebral osteomyelitis/discitis); CONTIGUOUS/direct osteomyelitis follows open fracture, surgery, or a diabetic foot ulcer","STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS is the most common organism (consider Salmonella in sickle cell, Pseudomonas in IVDU/puncture wounds); ACUTE vs CHRONIC (with dead bone - SEQUESTRUM, new bone - involucrum, and draining SINUS tracts)","Presents with localized bone pain, warmth, and (acute) systemic signs; chronic disease smolders with sinus drainage"],"dx":["Labs (WBC, ESR, CRP - useful to trend), blood cultures; MRI is the most sensitive/specific imaging (marrow edema early - plain films lag by ~2 weeks); a positive PROBE-TO-BONE and MRI help in diabetic foot","BONE BIOPSY/culture (the gold standard) to identify the organism and direct therapy - obtain before antibiotics when the patient is stable","Identify the source and vascular status (diabetic foot); vertebral osteomyelitis - image the whole spine, assess for epidural abscess"],"tx":["Organism-directed, PROLONGED ANTIBIOTICS (typically several weeks, initially IV) - empiric anti-staphylococcal coverage adjusted to cultures; hemodynamically stable patients benefit from culture-directed therapy (biopsy first)","SURGICAL DEBRIDEMENT for chronic osteomyelitis (remove sequestrum/dead bone), abscess drainage, dead-space management, and stabilization; acute hematogenous osteomyelitis (especially in children) may respond to antibiotics alone if caught early (drain abscesses)","Address the source (revascularization/offloading in diabetic foot; hardware decisions)"],"tech":["Surgical debridement of necrotic/infected bone and dead-space management (antibiotic beads/spacers, flaps); stabilization of associated fractures; drainage of subperiosteal/epidural abscess","CT/MRI-guided or open bone biopsy for culture","Reconstruction (bone transport/grafting) for defects"],"after":["Complete the PROLONGED antibiotic course with ID co-management and marker (ESR/CRP) trending; staged reconstruction of bone/soft-tissue defects","Monitor for RECURRENCE (chronic osteomyelitis can relapse), nonunion, and functional outcome; manage the underlying condition (diabetes, vascular disease)","Long-term surveillance"],"pearls":["Staph aureus is the usual culprit - hematogenous osteomyelitis hits the metaphysis in children and the vertebrae in adults","MRI is the sensitive early test - plain films lag about two weeks behind","Get a bone biopsy/culture before antibiotics when the patient is stable - chronic osteomyelitis needs organism-directed therapy AND debridement of dead bone","Think Salmonella osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease and Pseudomonas after a nail-through-the-shoe puncture"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / IDSA: Osteomyelitis","u":"https://www.idsociety.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteomyelitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532250/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Osteomyelitis","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-osteoporosis-fragility","name":"Osteoporosis, Fragility Fractures & Metabolic Bone Disease","sec":"ortho","present":["OSTEOPOROSIS: reduced bone mass and microarchitectural deterioration causing FRAGILITY FRACTURES (from a fall from standing height or less) - classically the HIP, VERTEBRAE (often silent, causing height loss/kyphosis), and distal radius (this content shares with the family-medicine framing, from the fracture-care lens)","A fragility fracture is a SENTINEL event - it markedly raises the risk of the next fracture, yet the osteoporosis is frequently untreated ('the osteoporosis care gap')","Also recognize OSTEOMALACIA (defective mineralization - vitamin D deficiency), PAGET disease of bone, hyperparathyroidism, and renal osteodystrophy as metabolic bone diseases"],"dx":["Diagnose osteoporosis by a fragility fracture OR by DXA (T-score <=-2.5); FRAX estimates 10-year fracture risk to guide treatment; a fragility fracture itself is diagnostic regardless of DXA","Work up SECONDARY causes and metabolic contributors (vitamin D/calcium, renal/hepatic function, thyroid, PTH, and testosterone/other as indicated) - especially in younger patients or severe disease","Assess falls risk"],"tx":["After a fragility fracture: TREAT the fracture AND the bone - ensure calcium/vitamin D, start pharmacotherapy (ANTIRESORPTIVES - bisphosphonates or denosumab; ANABOLICS - teriparatide/abaloparatide or romosozumab for high-risk/severe disease), and institute FALLS PREVENTION","A FRACTURE LIAISON SERVICE (systematic identification, workup, and treatment of fragility-fracture patients) closes the care gap and prevents subsequent fractures","Treat the specific metabolic disease (vitamin D for osteomalacia, bisphosphonates for symptomatic Paget, address hyperparathyroidism/renal disease)"],"tech":["Fracture fixation/arthroplasty for the fragility fracture itself; vertebral augmentation (kyphoplasty/vertebroplasty) for select painful osteoporotic vertebral fractures","Pharmacologic bone therapy and a fracture-liaison pathway","Falls-prevention and rehabilitation programs"],"after":["SECONDARY FRACTURE PREVENTION is the priority - initiate and MAINTAIN bone therapy (adherence is poor), monitor with DXA/markers, and reassess (e.g., bisphosphonate 'drug holiday' considerations, denosumab continuation to avoid rebound)","Falls prevention, vitamin D/calcium sufficiency, and rehabilitation to restore function and mobility","Ongoing multidisciplinary (ortho-geriatric/endocrine) follow-up"],"pearls":["A fragility fracture is a sentinel event - it must trigger osteoporosis workup and treatment, yet this care gap is huge; a fracture liaison service closes it","Treat the bone, not just the break - calcium/vitamin D plus an antiresorptive or anabolic prevents the next fracture","Vertebral fractures are often silent - unexplained height loss and kyphosis are clues","Do not stop denosumab abruptly (rebound fractures), and consider anabolics first in very high-risk/severe osteoporosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / AACE: Osteoporosis & Fragility Fractures","u":"https://www.aaos.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoporosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441901/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Osteoporosis & Metabolic Bone Disease","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-fracture-principles","name":"Fracture Healing Principles, Closed Reduction & Casting","sec":"ortho","present":["The foundational concepts underpinning all fracture care. Bone heals by two pathways: PRIMARY (direct) healing under absolute rigid fixation (no callus, cutting cones) and SECONDARY (indirect) healing under relative stability (CALLUS formation through inflammatory -> soft callus -> hard callus -> remodeling stages)","Describing a fracture systematically: location, PATTERN (transverse, oblique, spiral, comminuted, segmental), displacement, ANGULATION, translation, shortening/rotation, intra-articular extension, and open vs closed","Fracture management balances STABILITY and BIOLOGY (blood supply) - the two requirements for union"],"dx":["Orthogonal RADIOGRAPHS including the joint ABOVE AND BELOW; describe the fracture precisely to communicate and plan; CT for intra-articular/complex patterns","Identify factors that impair healing: patient (SMOKING, diabetes, poor nutrition, NSAIDs/steroids, advanced age), fracture (open, comminuted, bone loss, poor blood supply), and INFECTION","Assess soft tissues, neurovascular status, and open-wound status"],"tx":["CLOSED REDUCTION principles: recreate then reverse the injury mechanism, apply traction, and correct the deformity; adequate analgesia/relaxation; confirm reduction radiographically","IMMOBILIZATION: splints acutely (accommodate swelling), then CASTS - immobilize the joint above and below, use THREE-POINT molding to maintain reduction; account for expected settling/angulation","Optimize biology (smoking cessation, nutrition) and choose relative vs absolute stability by fracture and goals; operative fixation when closed methods cannot maintain acceptable alignment"],"tech":["Closed reduction and splinting/casting (three-point molding, appropriate padding); traction techniques; conversion to operative fixation (relative stability - bridge plating/nailing; absolute stability - lag screw/compression plating) when indicated","Well-molded cast application avoiding a circumferential tight cast acutely","Serial radiographic checks of reduction"],"after":["CAST CARE and precautions: watch for COMPARTMENT SYNDROME (a tight cast - split/bivalve it), pressure sores, and neurovascular compromise; elevate and monitor; return precautions for increasing pain/numbness","SERIAL RADIOGRAPHS to confirm maintained reduction and progressive union; adjust/re-reduce or escalate to surgery if alignment is lost","Progressive rehabilitation and weight-bearing per healing; address non-healing risk factors"],"pearls":["Union needs both stability and biology (blood supply) - every fixation decision balances the two","Absolute stability heals by primary (no callus) healing; relative stability heals by secondary healing with callus - do not expect callus across a rigidly compressed fracture","Splint acutely and cast later - a circumferential tight cast on a fresh, swelling injury risks compartment syndrome","Image the joint above and below, describe the fracture precisely, and re-check reduction radiographically after casting"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Fracture Healing & Principles","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fracture Healing / Reduction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551678/"},{"t":"OTA Basic Fracture Management","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-polytrauma","name":"Polytrauma & Damage-Control Orthopedics","sec":"ortho","present":["The severely injured (POLYTRAUMA) patient with multiple system injuries - orthopedic care is integrated into the ATLS-based, physiology-driven approach, because fracture surgery adds a 'SECOND HIT' inflammatory insult that a marginal patient may not tolerate","Long-bone/pelvic fractures contribute to hemorrhage and, if unstabilized, to pulmonary complications; timing and extent of orthopedic surgery are matched to the patient's physiology","Present in the trauma bay with multiple injuries, hemodynamic and metabolic derangement, and competing surgical priorities"],"dx":["ATLS primary/secondary survey; identify all injuries and stratify the patient's physiologic status (STABLE, BORDERLINE, unstable, in extremis) using markers of resuscitation (lactate, base deficit, pH, temperature, coagulation)","Prioritize life-threatening injuries; identify limb-threatening orthopedic emergencies (open fractures, mangled limbs, vascular injury, compartment syndrome, unstable pelvis)","Reassess physiology continuously to time definitive surgery"],"tx":["DAMAGE-CONTROL ORTHOPEDICS (DCO) for the unstable/borderline patient: rapid TEMPORARY stabilization (external fixation), hemorrhage control, and débridement - minimizing the surgical second hit while the patient is resuscitated in the ICU","EARLY TOTAL CARE (ETC - definitive fixation up front) for the physiologically STABLE patient (definitive long-bone fixation reduces pulmonary complications)","Convert to definitive fixation once physiology normalizes (lactate cleared, resuscitated); treat the whole patient (multidisciplinary trauma care)"],"tech":["External fixation for rapid provisional stabilization (damage control); staged conversion to definitive internal fixation (nailing/plating) when physiology permits; early definitive fixation in stable patients","Hemorrhage control (pelvic binder/packing/embolization) and débridement","ICU resuscitation and reassessment"],"after":["Staged DEFINITIVE fixation timed to physiologic recovery; ICU-level resuscitation, VTE prophylaxis, and management of the systemic inflammatory response/complications (ARDS, multi-organ dysfunction)","Rehabilitation and reconstruction of residual injuries; monitor for missed injuries (tertiary survey)","Long-term functional recovery"],"pearls":["Match the orthopedic plan to the patient's physiology - damage control (external fixation) for the unstable/borderline patient, early total care for the stable one","Fracture surgery is a 'second hit' - a marginal, under-resuscitated patient can be tipped into ARDS/organ failure by prolonged definitive surgery done too early","Stabilize long bones and the pelvis early (even temporarily) - it controls hemorrhage and reduces pulmonary complications","Resuscitation markers (lactate, base deficit) - not the clock - tell you when to convert to definitive fixation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OTA: Damage Control Orthopaedics","u":"https://ota.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Polytrauma / Damage Control Orthopedics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553148/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Polytrauma","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-nonunion-malunion","name":"Nonunion & Malunion","sec":"ortho","present":["Complications of fracture healing. NONUNION: failure of a fracture to unite in the expected time (or arrested healing) - classified as HYPERTROPHIC (abundant callus but instability - good biology, needs stability), ATROPHIC (poor callus/biology - needs biology), or oligotrophic; INFECTED nonunion is a distinct, difficult category","MALUNION: a fracture that has healed in an unacceptable position (angular, rotational, or shortening deformity) causing dysfunction, deformity, or accelerated arthritis","Present with persistent pain/motion at the fracture site (nonunion) or deformity/functional impairment (malunion)"],"dx":["Radiographs (+/- CT to confirm nonunion and assess the fracture line/callus); classify the nonunion type to determine what it lacks (stability vs biology)","ALWAYS rule out INFECTION as a cause of nonunion (ESR/CRP, and intraoperative cultures) - infected nonunion changes the entire strategy","Assess alignment/deformity (malunion) and its functional/arthritic consequences"],"tx":["HYPERTROPHIC nonunion (good biology, poor stability): improve STABILITY (revision fixation/compression); ATROPHIC nonunion (poor biology): add BIOLOGY (bone GRAFT, BMP) plus stability; INFECTED nonunion: eradicate infection (débridement, antibiotics, staged reconstruction) then achieve union","MALUNION: corrective OSTEOTOMY to realign, with fixation; address resulting joint problems","Optimize patient factors (smoking cessation, nutrition, metabolic/vitamin D)"],"tech":["Revision internal fixation (compression) for hypertrophic nonunion; bone grafting (autograft/allograft) +/- BMP and stable fixation for atrophic nonunion; staged débridement/antibiotic management then reconstruction for infected nonunion; corrective osteotomy for malunion (bone transport for defects)","Address the deficient element (stability vs biology)","Metabolic/patient optimization"],"after":["Monitor for UNION (serial radiographs) after revision; continued optimization of biology/patient factors; infected nonunion needs prolonged antibiotics and staged care","Rehabilitation for function; manage residual deformity/arthritis","Long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Classify the nonunion to know what it needs: hypertrophic nonunion lacks STABILITY, atrophic nonunion lacks BIOLOGY - give the missing ingredient","Always exclude infection as a cause of nonunion before revising - an infected nonunion treated as aseptic will fail","Smoking is a major, modifiable cause of nonunion - address it","Malunion is a healed-but-crooked fracture - corrective osteotomy realigns it and can forestall arthritis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Nonunion & Malunion","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Nonunion / Fracture Nonunion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554385/"},{"t":"OTA Nonunion Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-stress-fractures","name":"Stress Fractures","sec":"ortho","present":["Overuse injuries where repetitive submaximal loading exceeds the bone's remodeling capacity (FATIGUE fractures in normal bone from abnormal stress - athletes, military recruits; INSUFFICIENCY fractures in weak bone from normal stress - osteoporosis)","Present with insidious, ACTIVITY-RELATED pain that improves with rest and worsens with continued activity; localized bony tenderness","Some are HIGH-RISK (tension-side or poorly vascularized locations prone to nonunion/completion): the superior/tension side of the FEMORAL NECK, the anterior TIBIA ('dreaded black line'), the 5th metatarsal base (Jones), the tarsal NAVICULAR, and the talus - versus LOW-RISK sites"],"dx":["RADIOGRAPHS are often NORMAL early (changes lag by weeks - periosteal reaction, a lucent line); MRI (or bone scan) is sensitive for early diagnosis and grading","CLASSIFY as high-risk vs low-risk by location (drives aggressiveness of treatment)","Evaluate for underlying causes: the FEMALE ATHLETE TRIAD / RELATIVE ENERGY DEFICIENCY IN SPORT (RED-S) - low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone density - and other bone-health issues"],"tx":["LOW-RISK stress fractures: relative REST and activity modification with graduated return, addressing training errors and biomechanics","HIGH-RISK stress fractures: protected/non-weight-bearing or SURGICAL fixation (e.g., tension-side femoral neck, anterior tibia, Jones fractures) because of the nonunion/completion risk","Correct the underlying contributors (RED-S/energy availability, calcium/vitamin D, training load, footwear)"],"tech":["Surgical fixation (e.g., screw fixation of a femoral neck or Jones fracture; intramedullary screw for the 5th metatarsal) for high-risk stress fractures; protected weight-bearing/immobilization for others","Address biomechanics/orthotics","Bone-health optimization"],"after":["GRADUATED return to activity with load progression once healed; monitor high-risk sites for completion/nonunion; manage recurrence","Address the RED-S/bone-health contributors and training errors to prevent recurrence (multidisciplinary - sports medicine, nutrition)","Follow-up imaging as indicated"],"pearls":["Activity-related pain that eases with rest and a normal early X-ray is a stress fracture - get an MRI","Location decides aggressiveness: high-risk sites (tension-side femoral neck, anterior tibia 'dreaded black line', Jones, navicular, talus) risk nonunion/completion and often need fixation","A stress fracture is a red flag for RED-S / the female athlete triad - screen for low energy availability and bone health","Return to activity is graduated - rushing back re-injures the bone"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Stress Fractures","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stress Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554538/"},{"t":"AOSSM / Female Athlete Triad Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-amputations","name":"Amputations","sec":"ortho","present":["Surgical removal of a limb segment when the limb is non-salvageable or a burden; the leading indication is DYSVASCULAR disease (diabetes/peripheral arterial disease), followed by TRAUMA (mangled/non-reconstructable limb), severe INFECTION, tumor, and congenital deficiency","Level selection balances removing all non-viable/diseased tissue against preserving LENGTH and JOINTS for function - the more PROXIMAL the amputation, the higher the ENERGY expenditure of walking and the poorer the functional outcome","Below-knee (transtibial) amputation is generally preferred over above-knee (transfemoral) when healing allows, because knee preservation greatly improves prosthetic function"],"dx":["Assess limb viability, VASCULAR status/healing potential (perfusion, nutrition, tissue oxygenation) to select a level that will HEAL, and the soft-tissue envelope for durable coverage","For trauma, weigh salvage vs amputation (mangled extremity - and remember salvage is not always better functionally); assess infection/tumor margins","Optimize the patient (glycemia, nutrition, perfusion, smoking) for healing"],"tx":["Choose the most DISTAL level that will reliably HEAL while achieving the surgical goal (clear infection/tumor, viable tissue), preserving length/joints and a good soft-tissue/muscle envelope over the bone end","GUILLOTINE (open) amputation first for uncontrolled infection/sepsis, with delayed definitive closure; definitive amputation with a durable myodesis/myoplasty flap otherwise","Manage nerves to reduce symptomatic neuroma (traction neurectomy or TARGETED MUSCLE REINNERVATION); multidisciplinary rehabilitation planning from the start"],"tech":["Amputation at the selected level with stable soft-tissue coverage and myodesis; guillotine amputation for source control in sepsis, staged to definitive closure; nerve management (traction neurectomy, targeted muscle reinnervation) to prevent neuroma/phantom pain","Rigid dressing/shaping for prosthetic preparation","Revision for wound/level problems"],"after":["Residual limb care, shaping, and desensitization; PROSTHETIC fitting and gait rehabilitation with a multidisciplinary team (a major determinant of functional outcome)","Manage PHANTOM LIMB pain and neuroma, contractures (prevent knee/hip flexion contractures), wound healing, and skin breakdown","Long-term care of the residual limb and the contralateral (at-risk) limb, plus psychosocial support"],"pearls":["Preserve length and joints - the more proximal the amputation, the higher the energy cost of walking and the worse the function; a below-knee level beats above-knee when it will heal","Pick the most distal level that will reliably HEAL - a level that fails to heal forces a higher revision","Do a guillotine amputation first for uncontrolled infection/sepsis, then close definitively once the patient is stable","Plan for phantom pain and neuroma - nerve handling (targeted muscle reinnervation) and early rehab/prosthetics improve outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Amputations","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amputation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546594/"},{"t":"OTA / Limb Salvage Resources","u":"https://ota.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-fai-labral","name":"Femoroacetabular Impingement & Hip Labral Tears","sec":"ortho","present":["Femoroacetabular impingement (FAI): abnormal contact between the proximal femur and acetabular rim during motion, causing labral and chondral damage and a recognized pathway to EARLY hip OSTEOARTHRITIS","Morphologic types: CAM (an aspherical femoral head-neck junction - typically young athletic males), PINCER (acetabular over-coverage/rim over-hang), and MIXED (most common)","Present with activity-/flexion-related GROIN pain (the 'C-SIGN' - patient cups the hand around the hip), pain with prolonged sitting/pivoting, often in young active adults; labral tears cause catching/clicking"],"dx":["Exam: a positive anterior IMPINGEMENT test (FADIR - flexion/adduction/internal rotation reproduces groin pain) and limited hip internal rotation in flexion","Radiographs assess morphology (increased ALPHA ANGLE for cam; crossover/rim signs for pincer) and early arthritis; MR ARTHROGRAPHY detects labral tears and chondral damage","Distinguish from other groin pain sources; assess joint-space (established arthritis limits joint-preserving options)"],"tx":["FIRST-LINE nonoperative: activity modification, physical therapy (core/hip stability, avoiding impinging positions), and NSAIDs +/- intra-articular injection (also diagnostic)","SURGICAL for refractory symptoms with correctable morphology and preserved joint space: hip ARTHROSCOPY with femoral/acetabular OSTEOCHONDROPLASTY (reshape the impinging bone) and LABRAL repair/reconstruction","Address the bony morphology - treating the labral tear without correcting the impingement risks failure; established arthritis is better served by arthroplasty"],"tech":["Hip arthroscopy with osteochondroplasty (cam/pincer resection) and labral repair/reconstruction; periacetabular or other osteotomy for select dysplasia/deformity; arthroplasty if the joint is already arthritic","Diagnostic/therapeutic intra-articular injection","Physiotherapy program"],"after":["Structured post-arthroscopy REHABILITATION with graduated return to sport; outcomes are best with preserved cartilage and corrected morphology","Monitor for progression to osteoarthritis (FAI is a risk factor) and for residual symptoms/failure (often from under-corrected bone or unrecognized arthritis)","Activity modification long-term"],"pearls":["Groin pain with a positive FADIR/impingement test and limited internal rotation in flexion in a young active adult is FAI","Cam impingement (aspherical head-neck, high alpha angle) is the classic young-athletic-male pattern; pincer is over-coverage - most are mixed","Fix the bone, not just the labrum - repairing a labral tear without correcting the impinging morphology invites failure","FAI is a pathway to early hip osteoarthritis - but once significant arthritis is present, arthroscopy disappoints and arthroplasty is the answer"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Femoroacetabular Impingement","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Femoroacetabular Impingement","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547726/"},{"t":"AOSSM Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.sportsmed.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-peripheral-nerve-injury","name":"Peripheral Nerve Injuries","sec":"ortho","present":["Injuries to peripheral nerves from laceration, TRACTION (stretch), or COMPRESSION - graded by severity (SEDDON: NEURAPRAXIA - conduction block, intact axon, full recovery; AXONOTMESIS - axon disrupted, sheath intact, recovers by regeneration; NEUROTMESIS - complete transection, needs repair; and the finer SUNDERLAND I-V)","Classic fracture associations: RADIAL nerve with humeral shaft fractures, common PERONEAL nerve with knee dislocation/fibular neck, AXILLARY nerve with shoulder dislocation/proximal humerus, and sciatic with hip dislocation/posterior acetabulum","Present with motor weakness and sensory loss in the nerve's distribution"],"dx":["Careful motor/sensory mapping to localize the nerve and severity; an advancing TINEL SIGN tracks regeneration; distinguish complete from incomplete lesions","ELECTRODIAGNOSTIC studies (EMG/nerve conduction) - most informative at ~3-4 weeks (after Wallerian degeneration declares) - to grade the injury and follow reinnervation; imaging (ultrasound/MRI) for structural lesions","Regeneration proceeds at roughly 1 mm/day - set the timeline for expected recovery"],"tx":["CLOSED/traction injuries and neurapraxia: OBSERVE - most recover spontaneously (support with splinting/therapy to prevent contractures while awaiting recovery)","SHARP LACERATIONS (neurotmesis): early primary REPAIR (or nerve grafting for gaps); nerve injuries not recovering by the expected time warrant exploration/repair/grafting or NERVE TRANSFERS","For irrecoverable deficits: TENDON TRANSFERS to restore function; manage neuropathic pain"],"tech":["Primary nerve repair or grafting for transections/gaps; nerve transfers for proximal/irrecoverable injuries; tendon transfers for permanent functional loss; neurolysis for compression","Splinting to maintain position and prevent contracture during recovery","EMG-guided timing of intervention"],"after":["Follow recovery with serial exam (advancing Tinel), function, and EMG; hand/physical THERAPY and splinting to preserve joint motion and prevent contractures while the nerve regenerates","Set realistic timelines (regeneration is slow, ~1 mm/day); intervene if recovery stalls at the expected milestone","Manage neuropathic pain and residual deficits (transfers/orthotics)"],"pearls":["Grade the injury - neurapraxia and most closed traction injuries recover with observation; a sharp laceration (neurotmesis) needs repair","Know the fracture-nerve pairings: radial/humeral shaft, peroneal/knee, axillary/shoulder, sciatic/hip","Get EMG at ~3-4 weeks, not day one - it needs time for Wallerian degeneration to declare","Regeneration is slow (~1 mm/day) - splint to prevent contractures while waiting, and reassess if it stalls"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / OrthoBullets: Peripheral Nerve Injury","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Nerve Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK540956/"},{"t":"ASSH Nerve Injury Resources","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"ortho-cp-neuromuscular","name":"Cerebral Palsy & Neuromuscular Orthopedics","sec":"ortho","present":["Orthopedic management of neuromuscular conditions. CEREBRAL PALSY (CP): a NON-progressive brain injury causing a PROGRESSIVE musculoskeletal picture - SPASTICITY leads to muscle contractures, joint deformity, HIP subluxation/dislocation, scoliosis, and gait abnormalities (toe-walking/equinus, crouch gait); function is graded by the GMFCS","Other neuromuscular disorders: DUCHENNE muscular dystrophy (progressive weakness, Gowers sign, scoliosis, calf pseudohypertrophy), spina bifida (myelomeningocele), and spinal muscular atrophy","Goals differ from typical orthopedics - maximizing FUNCTION, positioning, ease of care, and preventing deformity, rather than 'normalizing'"],"dx":["Assess tone/spasticity, contractures, joint stability (especially HIP - surveillance for subluxation/dislocation), spine (scoliosis), and gait (instrumented gait analysis in ambulators); classify function (GMFCS)","HIP SURVEILLANCE with serial radiographs (migration percentage) is essential in CP - hips silently subluxate/dislocate","Coordinate multidisciplinary evaluation (neurology, PM&R, therapy)"],"tx":["MULTIDISCIPLINARY, function-focused care: physical/occupational therapy, bracing/orthoses, and TONE management (oral agents, BOTULINUM toxin, intrathecal baclofen, selective dorsal rhizotomy)","SURGERY: soft-tissue lengthenings (e.g., for equinus/contractures - often multilevel single-event surgery in ambulators), bony osteotomies and HIP RECONSTRUCTION for subluxation/dislocation, and SCOLIOSIS fusion for progressive neuromuscular curves","Duchenne/other dystrophies: manage scoliosis (surgery), contractures, and the systemic disease (cardiopulmonary)"],"tech":["Soft-tissue lengthening/tendon transfers (often multilevel single-event surgery), femoral/pelvic osteotomies and hip reconstruction, and posterior spinal fusion for neuromuscular scoliosis; tone procedures (selective dorsal rhizotomy, baclofen pump)","Bracing/orthotics and seating for positioning","Gait-analysis-guided planning in ambulators"],"after":["Long-term MULTIDISCIPLINARY follow-up and continued HIP/SPINE surveillance; rehabilitation and equipment/seating optimization; recurrence of deformity as the child grows","Manage the systemic aspects (respiratory/cardiac in dystrophies) and caregiver support","Function- and care-focused goals of treatment"],"pearls":["In cerebral palsy the brain lesion is static but the orthopedic deformity is progressive - spasticity begets contractures, hip displacement, and scoliosis over time","Hip surveillance (migration percentage on serial films) catches silent subluxation before dislocation - a core, easily forgotten duty in CP care","Goals are function, positioning, and ease of care - not making the child 'normal'; tone management and well-timed multilevel surgery serve those goals","Neuromuscular scoliosis (CP, Duchenne) progresses and often needs fusion - and the systemic disease (cardiopulmonary) shapes the plan"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / POSNA: Cerebral Palsy & Neuromuscular Orthopedics","u":"https://posna.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538147/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Neuromuscular Disorders","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-cervical-degenerative-myelopathy","name":"Cervical Spine Degenerative Disease & Myelopathy","sec":"ortho","present":["Degenerative cervical spine disease (this content shares with the neurosurgery framing, ortho lens). CERVICAL RADICULOPATHY: nerve-root compression (disc/spondylosis) causing DERMATOMAL arm pain, numbness, and weakness; reproduced by the SPURLING maneuver","CERVICAL SPONDYLOTIC MYELOPATHY (CSM) - the most important entity: spinal CORD compression causing insidious HAND CLUMSINESS (loss of fine motor control/'myelopathic hands'), GAIT imbalance, and UPPER-motor-neuron signs (hyperreflexia, HOFFMANN sign, Babinski) - it is PROGRESSIVE and the leading cause of spinal cord dysfunction in older adults","Present with neck pain, arm symptoms (radiculopathy), and/or the subtle hand/gait dysfunction of myelopathy"],"dx":["Examine for radicular (dermatomal, Spurling) vs myelopathic findings (hyperreflexia, Hoffmann, Babinski, clonus, gait/balance testing, hand dexterity - the grip-and-release test)","MRI is the imaging test of choice - it shows the disc/spondylotic compression and cord signal change in myelopathy","Grade myelopathy severity (e.g., mJOA) and correlate imaging with the exam"],"tx":["RADICULOPATHY: most improves with NONOPERATIVE care (activity modification, therapy, medications, +/- injections); surgery for refractory pain or significant/progressive deficit","CERVICAL MYELOPATHY: SURGICAL DECOMPRESSION is generally recommended for moderate-severe or PROGRESSIVE myelopathy to halt progression (recovery of lost function is variable) - anterior (ACDF/corpectomy), posterior (laminoplasty/laminectomy and fusion), or combined, based on the pathology and alignment","Do not simply observe progressive myelopathy - the goal is to stop the decline"],"tech":["Anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF)/corpectomy, cervical disc arthroplasty, or posterior laminoplasty/laminectomy and fusion for decompression; approach chosen by number of levels, alignment, and pathology","Nonoperative measures (therapy, injections) for radiculopathy","Neuromonitoring during surgery"],"after":["Rehabilitation; monitor neurologic status and (myelopathy) whether progression has halted - early surgery gives better outcomes, and long-standing deficits may only partially recover","Monitor for adjacent-segment degeneration (fusion), fusion/hardware issues, and residual symptoms","Long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Hand clumsiness plus gait imbalance with hyperreflexia, a positive Hoffmann sign, and Babinski is cervical spondylotic myelopathy - a progressive cord problem, not just 'arthritis'","Cervical radiculopathy mostly improves without surgery; myelopathy that is moderate-severe or progressive is a surgical decompression to halt decline","MRI cord signal change supports myelopathy - correlate it with the upper-motor-neuron exam","Recovery of established myelopathic deficits is variable - the main goal of surgery is to stop progression, so do not just watch it worsen"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / AOSpine: Cervical Spondylotic Myelopathy","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Myelopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482312/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Cervical Myelopathy & Radiculopathy","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"ortho-stenosis-spondylolisthesis","name":"Spinal Stenosis & Spondylolisthesis","sec":"ortho","present":["Two common degenerative lumbar conditions (this content shares with the neurosurgery framing, ortho lens). LUMBAR SPINAL STENOSIS: narrowing of the canal/foramina (degenerative, older adults) causing NEUROGENIC CLAUDICATION - buttock/leg pain, heaviness, and numbness with WALKING and STANDING (spinal extension), RELIEVED by SITTING/leaning forward (flexion - the 'shopping-cart sign')","SPONDYLOLISTHESIS: forward slippage of one vertebra on another - ISTHMIC (from a pars defect/spondylolysis, often in young athletes with extension activities) and DEGENERATIVE (older adults, typically L4-L5, with stenosis)","Present with back pain, claudication-type leg symptoms, and (spondylolisthesis) a step-off/hamstring tightness"],"dx":["Distinguish NEUROGENIC from VASCULAR claudication (neurogenic is positional - relieved by flexion/sitting, pulses intact; vascular is relieved by rest alone); exam for radicular/neurologic findings","MRI shows canal/foraminal stenosis and neural compression; radiographs (including FLEXION-EXTENSION views) assess spondylolisthesis and INSTABILITY; oblique views/CT show the pars defect (spondylolysis)","Grade the slip (Meyerding) and assess stability"],"tx":["NONOPERATIVE first for most: physical therapy (flexion-based), activity modification, medications, and EPIDURAL steroid injections","SURGERY for refractory symptoms or progressive deficit: DECOMPRESSION (laminectomy) for stenosis; add FUSION when there is INSTABILITY or a (degenerative/higher-grade) spondylolisthesis - decompression alone can destabilize an unstable slip","Young athletes with symptomatic spondylolysis: activity modification/bracing, with surgery for refractory cases"],"tech":["Laminectomy/decompression for stenosis; decompression WITH instrumented fusion for unstable/spondylolisthetic segments; pars repair in select young spondylolysis; interbody fusion techniques","Epidural injections and physical therapy for nonoperative management","Flexion-extension imaging to determine the need for fusion"],"after":["Rehabilitation and gradual return to activity; counsel that decompression reliably relieves LEG/claudication symptoms more than back pain","Monitor for recurrent stenosis, slip progression, ADJACENT-SEGMENT degeneration (after fusion), and pseudarthrosis","Long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Neurogenic claudication is positional - leg symptoms with walking/standing that ease when the patient sits or leans on a shopping cart (flexion); vascular claudication eases with rest alone","Add a fusion when there is instability or a degenerative spondylolisthesis - decompressing an unstable slip alone can worsen it","Isthmic spondylolisthesis comes from a pars defect (spondylolysis) in young athletes doing extension sports; degenerative spondylolisthesis is an older-adult L4-L5 problem with stenosis","Decompression relieves leg/claudication symptoms better than axial back pain - set expectations accordingly"],"refs":[{"t":"AAOS / NASS: Lumbar Stenosis & Spondylolisthesis","u":"https://www.spine.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Stenosis / Spondylolisthesis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441989/"},{"t":"OrthoBullets: Spinal Stenosis & Spondylolisthesis","u":"https://www.orthobullets.com"}]},{"id":"path-cell-injury-necrosis","name":"Cell Injury, Necrosis & Apoptosis","sec":"path","present":["Cell injury results from HYPOXIA/ISCHEMIA (the most common cause), toxins, infections, immune reactions, physical/chemical agents, and nutritional imbalance - manifesting grossly as infarcts, gangrene, and organ dysfunction","REVERSIBLE injury (cellular swelling/hydropic change, fatty change) precedes IRREVERSIBLE injury (membrane damage, mitochondrial dysfunction, massive calcium influx)","The two death pathways are NECROSIS (pathologic, with inflammation) and APOPTOSIS (programmed, without inflammation)"],"dx":["NECROSIS PATTERNS: COAGULATIVE (ischemic infarct of most solid organs - preserved 'ghost' architecture), LIQUEFACTIVE (brain infarct, abscess), CASEOUS (TB/fungal - 'cheese-like', granulomatous), FAT (pancreatitis - saponification/chalky), FIBRINOID (vessel walls in malignant hypertension/vasculitis - immune complexes), and GANGRENOUS (limbs - dry vs wet)","APOPTOSIS: single-cell death with cell shrinkage, chromatin condensation (pyknosis/karyorrhexis), apoptotic bodies, and NO surrounding inflammation - executed by CASPASES via the INTRINSIC (mitochondrial, Bcl-2 family) or EXTRINSIC (Fas/death-receptor) pathway","Reversible injury shows cellular swelling and fatty change; irreversible injury shows membrane defects, mitochondrial vacuolization, and nuclear changes (pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis)"],"tx":["The morphology localizes the process and cause - coagulative necrosis points to ischemia/infarction; fat necrosis to pancreatitis; caseous to infection; fibrinoid to immune/vascular injury","Necrosis releases intracellular contents (troponin, transaminases) that serve as clinical injury markers and triggers inflammation; apoptosis dysregulation underlies cancer, autoimmunity, and degeneration","Recognizing the pattern guides the clinical differential and the search for the underlying insult"],"pearls":["Coagulative necrosis preserves cell outlines ('ghost cells') and is the hallmark of ischemic infarction in most organs - EXCEPT the brain, which undergoes liquefactive necrosis","Caseous necrosis means TB or fungal infection until proven otherwise; fat necrosis with saponification points to pancreatitis; fibrinoid necrosis to malignant hypertension/vasculitis","Apoptosis is caspase-mediated, affects single cells, and provokes NO inflammation - distinguishing it from necrosis","Cellular (hydropic) swelling is the earliest sign of reversible injury; membrane damage and calcium influx mark the transition to irreversibility"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cell Injury / Necrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537297/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Necrosis","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-inflammation","name":"Acute & Chronic Inflammation","sec":"path","present":["ACUTE inflammation is the rapid, short-lived response to injury/infection, dominated by NEUTROPHILS, with the cardinal signs rubor (redness), tumor (swelling), calor (heat), dolor (pain), and functio laesa (loss of function)","CHRONIC inflammation is prolonged, dominated by MONONUCLEAR cells (MACROPHAGES, lymphocytes, plasma cells), with concurrent tissue destruction, angiogenesis, and FIBROSIS/repair","Chronic inflammation arises from persistent infection, autoimmune disease, prolonged toxin exposure, or unresolved acute inflammation"],"dx":["ACUTE: vascular changes (vasodilation and increased permeability producing a protein-rich EXUDATE) and neutrophil emigration; morphologic patterns include SEROUS (effusion), FIBRINOUS (serosal surfaces), SUPPURATIVE/purulent (pus, abscess - often pyogenic bacteria), and ULCERATIVE","CHRONIC: infiltrate of macrophages, lymphocytes, and PLASMA CELLS with fibroblast proliferation and neovascularization; GRANULOMATOUS inflammation is a distinct chronic subtype","Key mediators: histamine, prostaglandins/leukotrienes, complement, and cytokines (TNF, IL-1, IL-6); distinguish an EXUDATE (high protein/cellular, inflammatory) from a TRANSUDATE (low protein, hydrostatic/oncotic)"],"tx":["Determine infectious vs sterile cause; the pattern predicts outcomes - resolution, abscess formation, chronic progression, or scarring/fibrosis","Neutrophil predominance signals acute (often bacterial) processes; plasma cells/lymphocytes signal chronicity - guiding the clinical differential and workup","Mediators are therapeutic targets (NSAIDs, corticosteroids, anti-TNF biologics)"],"pearls":["Neutrophils = acute; lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages = chronic inflammation","Suppurative (purulent) inflammation means pus - neutrophils plus liquefactive necrosis - and suggests pyogenic bacteria","Exudate (protein-rich, inflammatory - Light's criteria) versus transudate (protein-poor, from hydrostatic/oncotic imbalance) is a core effusion distinction","Chronic inflammation simultaneously destroys tissue and drives repair (fibrosis and angiogenesis)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute & Chronic Inflammation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556083/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Inflammation","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-granulomatous-inflammation","name":"Granulomatous Inflammation","sec":"path","present":["A distinctive form of chronic inflammation defined by the GRANULOMA - an organized collection of activated macrophages (EPITHELIOID histiocytes), often with multinucleated GIANT CELLS and a rim of lymphocytes","It represents the immune response to a persistent, poorly degradable, or antigenically potent stimulus (a T-cell/macrophage, often type-IV hypersensitivity, reaction)","Clinically it underlies TB, sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, fungal infections, and foreign-body reactions"],"dx":["The central distinction is CASEATING (central caseous necrosis - classically TUBERCULOSIS and some fungi) versus NON-CASEATING (SARCOIDOSIS, Crohn disease, foreign body, berylliosis, cat-scratch disease)","Giant cell types: LANGHANS (peripheral horseshoe nuclei - infectious granulomas) and foreign-body type; sarcoid granulomas are non-necrotizing/'naked' and may show asteroid or Schaumann bodies (nonspecific)","ALWAYS perform special stains/cultures to exclude infection: acid-fast (AFB) for mycobacteria and GMS/PAS for fungi"],"tx":["Excluding INFECTION (with stains and cultures) before attributing a granuloma to a non-infectious cause is essential - the treatments diverge completely (anti-tuberculous therapy vs immunosuppression/corticosteroids)","Sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of EXCLUSION (non-necrotizing granulomas after infection and other causes are ruled out)","The clinical context and stain/culture results direct definitive management"],"pearls":["Caseating granuloma = think TB or fungal infection until proven otherwise; always order AFB and fungal (GMS/PAS) stains","Non-caseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis (exclusion diagnosis), Crohn disease, or foreign body - but infection must still be excluded","Langhans giant cells (horseshoe of peripheral nuclei) are seen in infectious granulomas; asteroid and Schaumann bodies in sarcoid are nonspecific","Never call it sarcoidosis until infection is excluded - a missed mycobacterial/fungal infection treated with steroids can be catastrophic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Granuloma / Granulomatous Inflammation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544218/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Granulomatous Inflammation","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-neoplasia-grading-staging","name":"Neoplasia — Grading, Staging & Nomenclature","sec":"path","present":["Neoplasms are classified as BENIGN or MALIGNANT; NOMENCLATURE generally reflects the tissue of origin plus a suffix: '-oma' for benign, 'CARCINOMA' for malignant epithelial, and 'SARCOMA' for malignant mesenchymal tumors","Important EXCEPTIONS that sound benign ('-oma') but are malignant: lymphoma, melanoma, seminoma, mesothelioma, and glioma/glioblastoma","Malignant tumors invade and metastasize; benign tumors are localized and encapsulated"],"dx":["Features of MALIGNANCY: ANAPLASIA (loss of differentiation), pleomorphism, high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, hyperchromasia, prominent nucleoli, numerous and ATYPICAL MITOSES, loss of polarity, INVASION through the basement membrane, and metastasis","GRADE reflects DIFFERENTIATION (how closely the tumor resembles its normal tissue - well/moderately/poorly differentiated); STAGE reflects EXTENT of spread via the TNM system (Tumor size/depth, regional Nodes, distant Metastasis)","Carcinoma IN SITU has malignant cytology but has NOT breached the basement membrane; desmoplasia (reactive stroma) often accompanies invasive carcinoma"],"tx":["STAGE is generally the strongest determinant of prognosis and treatment (more so than grade for most solid tumors); grade informs expected behavior","Margin status, lymphovascular invasion, and TNM stage directly guide surgical, radiation, and systemic therapy decisions","Accurate nomenclature and staging drive the entire oncologic management plan"],"pearls":["'-carcinoma' = epithelial malignancy, '-sarcoma' = mesenchymal malignancy - but beware the malignant '-omas' (lymphoma, melanoma, seminoma, mesothelioma, glioma)","Stage (extent of spread) usually predicts outcome better than grade (degree of differentiation)","Carcinoma in situ has not invaded the basement membrane - crossing it defines invasive carcinoma","Anaplasia (undifferentiated cells with atypical mitoses and pleomorphism) is the histologic hallmark of malignancy"],"refs":[{"t":"AJCC Cancer Staging","u":"https://www.cancerstaging.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cancer / Neoplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554462/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Grading & Staging","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-ihc-molecular-markers","name":"Diagnostic Workup — Immunohistochemistry, Molecular & Tumor Markers","sec":"path","present":["Ancillary studies classify tumors, determine the site of origin (especially for metastases/carcinoma of unknown primary), and predict response to targeted therapy","IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY (IHC) uses antibodies against lineage-specific proteins; MOLECULAR testing detects diagnostic/predictive DNA-RNA alterations; serum TUMOR MARKERS aid monitoring","These tools convert an 'undifferentiated malignancy' into an actionable, specific diagnosis"],"dx":["LINEAGE IHC: cytokeratins (epithelial/carcinoma), CD45/LCA (lymphoma), S100/SOX10/HMB-45/Melan-A (melanoma), desmin/myogenin (muscle), chromogranin/synaptophysin (neuroendocrine); SITE-OF-ORIGIN markers: TTF-1 (lung/thyroid), CDX2 (GI), PAX8 (renal/Mullerian/thyroid), GATA3 (breast/urothelial), and ER/PR/HER2 (breast)","MOLECULAR/PREDICTIVE: FISH (HER2 amplification, ALK rearrangement, sarcoma translocations), PCR/NGS for driver mutations (EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS), and MSI/mismatch-repair status (Lynch, immunotherapy)","SERUM MARKERS (PSA, AFP, CEA, CA-125, CA 19-9, beta-hCG) are best for monitoring treatment/recurrence rather than screening"],"tx":["IHC/molecular results establish the primary in metastatic disease, confirm difficult diagnoses, and determine eligibility for targeted/immuno-therapy (e.g., HER2-directed therapy, EGFR/ALK inhibitors, PD-L1-based immunotherapy)","Predictive biomarkers increasingly dictate first-line systemic therapy","Correct panel selection avoids misdiagnosis and unnecessary tissue exhaustion"],"pearls":["An 'undifferentiated tumor' panel of cytokeratin + CD45 (LCA) + S100 sorts the big three: carcinoma vs lymphoma vs melanoma","For carcinoma of unknown primary, site markers (TTF-1, CDX2, PAX8, GATA3) plus keratin subsets point to the origin","Serum tumor markers are for monitoring, not screening (few exceptions) - a normal marker does not exclude cancer","HER2, ER/PR (breast), EGFR/ALK (lung), and MSI/MMR (immunotherapy/Lynch) are the classic predictive biomarkers to know"],"refs":[{"t":"College of American Pathologists (CAP)","u":"https://www.cap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Immunohistochemistry","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560637/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Stains & Molecular","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-acute-leukemias","name":"Acute Leukemias (AML & ALL)","sec":"path","present":["Acute leukemias are aggressive clonal proliferations of immature BLASTS that replace bone marrow, causing PANCYTOPENIA - fatigue/pallor (anemia), infections (neutropenia), and bleeding/petechiae (thrombocytopenia) - over days to weeks","ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA (ALL) predominates in CHILDREN (may present with lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, bone pain, and CNS/testicular involvement); ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA (AML) predominates in ADULTS","Presentation can be a medical emergency (leukostasis, tumor lysis, DIC - especially acute promyelocytic leukemia)"],"dx":["Diagnosis requires >=20% BLASTS in blood/marrow; AML blasts may show AUER RODS (MPO-positive), while lymphoblasts are TdT-positive; flow cytometry immunophenotyping distinguishes lineage (myeloid: CD13/CD33/MPO; B-ALL: CD19/CD10; T-ALL: CD3)","CYTOGENETICS/molecular define subtype and prognosis: APL t(15;17) PML-RARA (good, treat with ATRA), t(8;21) and inv(16) (favorable AML), FLT3-ITD (adverse); ALL - Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) BCR-ABL (adverse but targetable), hyperdiploidy/ETV6-RUNX1 (favorable, pediatric)","WHO classification integrates morphology, immunophenotype, cytogenetics, and molecular findings"],"tx":["Blast percentage, lineage, and genetics determine therapy and prognosis; APL (t(15;17)) is a can't-miss - ATRA/arsenic is highly curative but DIC is life-threatening if untreated","Philadelphia-positive ALL is treated with tyrosine kinase inhibitors added to chemotherapy; risk stratification guides transplant","Rapid diagnosis matters - acute leukemia can present with leukostasis, tumor lysis syndrome, and DIC requiring emergent management"],"pearls":[">=20% blasts defines acute leukemia; Auer rods and MPO positivity indicate AML, while TdT positivity indicates lymphoblasts (ALL)","Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL, t(15;17) PML-RARA) is a hematologic emergency (DIC) that is highly curable with ATRA - recognize it fast","ALL is the most common childhood cancer; AML is more common in adults","t(9;22)/BCR-ABL (Philadelphia) in ALL is adverse but targetable with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Leukemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459163/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Acute Leukemia","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-cll-cml","name":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia & Chronic Myeloid Leukemia","sec":"path","present":["CHRONIC LYMPHOCYTIC LEUKEMIA (CLL): the most common adult leukemia in the Western world - typically an OLDER adult with lymphocytosis found incidentally, or with lymphadenopathy, fatigue, and cytopenias; it is the same disease as small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL)","CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA (CML): a myeloproliferative neoplasm with marked leukocytosis and a LEFT-SHIFTED granulocytic series, splenomegaly, and fatigue; may progress through accelerated phase to BLAST CRISIS","Both are chronic, but their biology and treatment differ completely"],"dx":["CLL: peripheral smear shows mature small lymphocytes with 'SMUDGE CELLS'; flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population co-expressing CD5, CD19, CD20 (dim), and CD23; prognostic markers include IGHV mutation status, del(17p)/TP53, and del(13q)","CML: peripheral leukocytosis with all granulocyte stages and BASOPHILIA; the defining lesion is the PHILADELPHIA CHROMOSOME t(9;22) producing the BCR-ABL1 fusion (constitutively active tyrosine kinase) - confirmed by FISH/PCR","Low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase supports CML over a leukemoid reaction"],"tx":["CML is the paradigm of targeted therapy - BCR-ABL TYROSINE KINASE INHIBITORS (imatinib and successors) produce durable remissions and near-normal survival; monitor BCR-ABL transcripts","CLL management ranges from observation of early disease to targeted agents (BTK inhibitors, BCL-2 inhibitors); del(17p)/TP53 predicts poor response to chemoimmunotherapy","CLL can transform to aggressive lymphoma (Richter transformation) and carries autoimmune cytopenia risk"],"pearls":["Smudge cells on the smear and a CD5+/CD23+ clonal B-cell population define CLL (= SLL)","The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22)/BCR-ABL1 defines CML - and makes it exquisitely treatable with tyrosine kinase inhibitors","CML basophilia and low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase help distinguish it from a leukemoid reaction","Richter transformation is the shift of CLL into an aggressive large B-cell lymphoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: CLL / CML","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470433/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Chronic Leukemia","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-mpn-mds","name":"Myeloproliferative Neoplasms & Myelodysplastic Syndromes","sec":"path","present":["MYELOPROLIFERATIVE NEOPLASMS (MPN) are clonal proliferations of mature myeloid cells: POLYCYTHEMIA VERA (PV - erythrocytosis, aquagenic pruritus, thrombosis, ruddy face), ESSENTIAL THROMBOCYTHEMIA (ET - thrombocytosis, thrombosis/bleeding), and PRIMARY MYELOFIBROSIS (PMF - marrow fibrosis, massive splenomegaly, teardrop cells)","MYELODYSPLASTIC SYNDROMES (MDS) are clonal disorders of INEFFECTIVE hematopoiesis in older adults - cytopenias with a hypercellular, DYSPLASTIC marrow and a risk of progression to AML","Both groups can transform to acute leukemia"],"dx":["MPN driver mutations: JAK2 V617F (nearly all PV, ~half of ET/PMF), CALR, and MPL; PV shows increased red cell mass with low erythropoietin; PMF shows marrow fibrosis with a leukoerythroblastic smear and TEARDROP cells (dacrocytes)","MDS: peripheral cytopenias with morphologic DYSPLASIA (e.g., hypolobated/Pelger-Huet neutrophils, ring sideroblasts, hypogranular cells), <20% blasts, and characteristic cytogenetics (del(5q), -7, complex); risk-stratified by IPSS-R","CML is excluded from this 'BCR-ABL-negative MPN' group by its Philadelphia chromosome"],"tx":["PV: phlebotomy plus low-dose aspirin (and cytoreduction for high risk); ET/PMF risk-adapted therapy; thrombosis is the major MPN complication","MDS: supportive care/transfusion, hypomethylating agents, lenalidomide (del(5q)), and transplant for higher-risk disease; blast percentage and cytogenetics drive prognosis","Both MPN and MDS carry a risk of leukemic transformation that shapes surveillance"],"pearls":["JAK2 V617F underlies almost all polycythemia vera and about half of essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis","Teardrop cells (dacrocytes) and a leukoerythroblastic smear with marrow fibrosis point to primary myelofibrosis","MDS is ineffective, dysplastic hematopoiesis with <20% blasts - crossing 20% blasts means transformation to AML","A low erythropoietin with high red cell mass supports polycythemia vera over secondary erythrocytosis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: MPN / MDS","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459311/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: MPN & MDS","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-hodgkin-lymphoma","name":"Hodgkin Lymphoma","sec":"path","present":["A lymphoma with a characteristic BIMODAL age distribution (young adults and older adults) that typically arises in LYMPH NODES and spreads in a CONTIGUOUS, orderly fashion between nodal groups","Presents with painless lymphadenopathy (often cervical/mediastinal), and B SYMPTOMS (fever, drenching night sweats, weight loss); classic (but uncommon) pain in involved nodes after alcohol","Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is distinct from nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma"],"dx":["The diagnostic cell is the REED-STERNBERG cell - a large binucleate ('owl-eye') cell in a background of reactive lymphocytes, eosinophils, and plasma cells (the malignant cells are the minority)","Classic Hodgkin RS cells are CD15+ and CD30+ and CD45-negative (and usually CD20-negative); subtypes include nodular sclerosis (most common, mediastinal, young women, lacunar cells), mixed cellularity (EBV-associated), lymphocyte-rich, and lymphocyte-depleted","Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma has 'popcorn' (LP) cells that are CD20+ and CD45+ but CD15/CD30-negative"],"tx":["Staging (Ann Arbor) and B symptoms guide combined chemotherapy +/- radiation; classic Hodgkin lymphoma has a HIGH CURE rate","CD30 expression enables targeted therapy (anti-CD30 antibody-drug conjugate); contiguous spread makes radiation fields rational","Late effects of therapy (secondary malignancy, cardiac) are a long-term survivorship concern"],"pearls":["The Reed-Sternberg cell (binucleate 'owl-eye') is the hallmark - CD15+/CD30+ and CD45-negative in classic Hodgkin lymphoma","Nodular sclerosis is the most common subtype (young women, mediastinal mass, lacunar cells); mixed cellularity is EBV-associated","Hodgkin spreads contiguously between adjacent nodal groups (unlike the more scattered spread of non-Hodgkin lymphoma)","Nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL has CD20+/CD45+ 'popcorn' cells - a different immunophenotype and course"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499969/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-non-hodgkin-lymphoma","name":"Non-Hodgkin Lymphomas (DLBCL, Follicular, Burkitt, Mantle, MALT)","sec":"path","present":["A heterogeneous group of lymphoid malignancies (mostly B-cell) that can be NODAL or EXTRANODAL and often present with more widespread, non-contiguous disease than Hodgkin lymphoma","Behavior spans AGGRESSIVE (diffuse large B-cell lymphoma - DLBCL, Burkitt - a rapidly enlarging mass) to INDOLENT (follicular lymphoma, MALT, small lymphocytic lymphoma)","Presentation ranges from painless lymphadenopathy to organ-based masses, cytopenias, and B symptoms"],"dx":["DLBCL: the most common NHL - sheets of large B cells (CD20+); a subset arises from transformation of follicular lymphoma; cell-of-origin and MYC/BCL2/BCL6 rearrangements ('double-hit') refine prognosis","FOLLICULAR lymphoma: nodular growth of CD10+/BCL6+ B cells with t(14;18) BCL2 overexpression (indolent); MANTLE CELL: CD5+ B cells with t(11;14) CYCLIN D1 (aggressive despite small cells); BURKITT: 'STARRY-SKY' pattern, near-100% Ki-67, t(8;14) MYC (very aggressive, tumor lysis risk)","MALT (marginal zone) lymphoma: extranodal, arises in chronic inflammation (gastric MALT from HELICOBACTER PYLORI, salivary in Sjogren, thyroid in Hashimoto)"],"tx":["Aggressive lymphomas (DLBCL, Burkitt) are treated with intent to cure by intensive chemoimmunotherapy (anti-CD20 based); Burkitt requires tumor lysis prophylaxis","Indolent lymphomas (follicular, MALT) may be observed or treated when symptomatic; gastric MALT often REGRESSES with H. pylori ERADICATION","Immunophenotype and cytogenetics define the entity, prognosis, and targeted options"],"pearls":["DLBCL is the most common NHL and is aggressive but often curable; 'double-hit' (MYC + BCL2/BCL6) confers worse prognosis","Translocations to know: follicular t(14;18)/BCL2, mantle cell t(11;14)/cyclin D1 (CD5+), Burkitt t(8;14)/MYC (starry-sky, Ki-67 ~100%)","Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by H. pylori and can regress with antibiotic eradication","Mantle cell lymphoma is CD5-positive like CLL but is aggressive and cyclin D1-positive"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559328/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: B-Cell Lymphomas","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-plasma-cell-myeloma","name":"Plasma Cell Myeloma & Plasma Cell Dyscrasias","sec":"path","present":["MULTIPLE MYELOMA is a malignant clonal proliferation of PLASMA CELLS in the bone marrow producing a monoclonal immunoglobulin (M protein)","The classic 'CRAB' features: hyperCALCEMIA, RENAL insufficiency, ANEMIA, and lytic BONE lesions/pain - plus recurrent infections","It exists on a spectrum with MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) and smoldering myeloma"],"dx":["Marrow shows increased clonal plasma cells (often with atypical/immature forms); serum/urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation reveals an M SPIKE (usually IgG or IgA); serum FREE LIGHT CHAINS with an abnormal ratio; urine BENCE-JONES protein (free light chains)","Imaging shows LYTIC ('punched-out') bone lesions (skeletal survey/MRI/PET); the peripheral smear may show ROULEAUX; light-chain deposition can cause cast nephropathy (myeloma kidney) and AL amyloidosis","WHO/IMWG criteria integrate clonal plasma cells, M protein, and end-organ (CRAB) or biomarker criteria to distinguish myeloma from MGUS/smoldering disease"],"tx":["CRAB features (or defining biomarkers) distinguish treatment-requiring myeloma from MGUS/smoldering disease that are observed - MGUS carries a small annual risk of progression","Management centers on proteasome inhibitors, immunomodulators, anti-CD38 antibodies, and autologous stem cell transplant; monitor with M protein/free light chains","Complications (hypercalcemia, renal failure, cord compression, infection) require prompt recognition"],"pearls":["CRAB = hyperCalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lesions - the end-organ damage that defines symptomatic myeloma","Rouleaux on the smear and an M spike on electrophoresis suggest a plasma cell dyscrasia; free light chains cause cast nephropathy","MGUS is common, asymptomatic, and observed - but progresses to myeloma at roughly 1% per year","Myeloma causes purely LYTIC bone lesions (bone scans, which detect osteoblastic activity, can be falsely negative)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Multiple Myeloma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534764/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Multiple Myeloma","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Plasma Cell Neoplasms","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-anemias-smear","name":"Anemias — Morphologic Classification & Peripheral Blood Smear","sec":"path","present":["Anemia (reduced hemoglobin/hematocrit) presents with fatigue, pallor, dyspnea, and tachycardia; the pathologic approach classifies it by red cell SIZE (MCV) and the peripheral smear","MICROCYTIC (low MCV): iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease, sideroblastic; MACROCYTIC (high MCV): B12/folate deficiency (megaloblastic), and non-megaloblastic (liver disease, alcohol, MDS); NORMOCYTIC: hemolysis, acute blood loss, marrow failure, chronic disease","The reticulocyte count separates hypoproliferative (low retic) from hemolytic/blood-loss (high retic) anemias"],"dx":["IRON DEFICIENCY: microcytic hypochromic cells with high RDW, low ferritin/iron/transferrin saturation, high TIBC; THALASSEMIA: microcytosis with target cells and a near-normal RDW (electrophoresis confirms)","MEGALOBLASTIC (B12/folate): macro-ovalocytes and HYPERSEGMENTED neutrophils; HEMOLYSIS: high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, reticulocytosis - with smear clues (SCHISTOCYTES in microangiopathy, SPHEROCYTES in hereditary spherocytosis/warm AIHA, SICKLE cells, BITE cells in G6PD)","The peripheral smear is the key diagnostic tool - morphology directs the workup"],"tx":["Establish the mechanism before treating - iron deficiency mandates a source search (GI blood loss/malignancy in adults), and treating B12 deficiency as folate deficiency risks neurologic harm","Hemolysis workup (direct antiglobulin/Coombs test, LDH, haptoglobin) distinguishes immune from non-immune causes; schistocytes demand urgent evaluation for a microangiopathy (TTP/HUS/DIC)","Reticulocyte response guides whether the marrow is compensating"],"pearls":["Classify anemia by MCV (micro/normo/macro) and use the reticulocyte count to separate hypoproliferative from hemolytic/blood-loss anemia","Hypersegmented neutrophils = megaloblastic (B12/folate); schistocytes = microangiopathic hemolysis (TTP/HUS/DIC); bite cells = G6PD deficiency","Iron deficiency has a HIGH RDW and low ferritin; thalassemia has a near-normal RDW with target cells - do not just give iron","New iron-deficiency anemia in an adult requires a search for a GI source (including colorectal cancer)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Anemia / Peripheral Smear","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499994/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Anemia","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-coagulation-disorders","name":"Coagulation Disorders & Testing (incl. DIC)","sec":"path","present":["Coagulation disorders cause abnormal BLEEDING (factor deficiencies) or, in consumptive states, both bleeding and thrombosis; the lab evaluation centers on the PT and aPTT plus platelet count and fibrinogen","INHERITED: HEMOPHILIA A (factor VIII) and B (factor IX) - X-linked, causing hemarthroses and deep bleeds with an isolated prolonged aPTT; VON WILLEBRAND DISEASE - the most common inherited bleeding disorder, mucocutaneous bleeding","ACQUIRED: vitamin K deficiency/warfarin, liver disease, and DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC)"],"dx":["The PT assesses the EXTRINSIC/common pathway (factor VII - sensitive to warfarin/vitamin K/liver disease); the aPTT assesses the INTRINSIC/common pathway (factors VIII, IX, XI, XII); a MIXING STUDY distinguishes factor deficiency (corrects) from an inhibitor (does not correct)","DIC: widespread activation of coagulation consuming factors and platelets - PROLONGED PT AND aPTT, LOW fibrinogen and platelets, and elevated D-DIMER, with SCHISTOCYTES on the smear; triggered by sepsis, malignancy, obstetric emergencies, and trauma","von Willebrand disease shows a prolonged bleeding time/PFA with variable aPTT (VWF stabilizes factor VIII)"],"tx":["Localizing the defect to the PT, aPTT, or both directs the diagnosis; DIC is a consumptive emergency - treat the underlying trigger and support with products (fibrinogen/cryoprecipitate, platelets, FFP)","Hemophilia is treated with factor replacement; von Willebrand disease with desmopressin or VWF concentrate","An isolated prolonged aPTT that does not correct on mixing suggests an inhibitor (factor inhibitor or lupus anticoagulant)"],"pearls":["PT = extrinsic (factor VII, warfarin/vitamin K/liver); aPTT = intrinsic (VIII, IX, XI, XII); a mixing study that corrects means deficiency, one that does not means an inhibitor","DIC prolongs BOTH PT and aPTT with low fibrinogen and platelets, high D-dimer, and schistocytes - find and treat the trigger","Hemophilia A/B are X-linked with an isolated prolonged aPTT and deep bleeding (hemarthroses); von Willebrand disease causes mucocutaneous bleeding","Vitamin K deficiency/warfarin prolongs the PT first (factor VII has the shortest half-life)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Coagulation / DIC","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441834/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Coagulation","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-thrombophilia","name":"Thrombophilia & Hypercoagulable States","sec":"path","present":["Hypercoagulable states predispose to venous (and sometimes arterial) THROMBOSIS - presenting as DVT, pulmonary embolism, or thrombosis at unusual sites (cerebral, splanchnic)","INHERITED: FACTOR V LEIDEN (the most common - activated protein C resistance), prothrombin G20210A, protein C/S deficiency, and antithrombin deficiency; ACQUIRED: ANTIPHOSPHOLIPID SYNDROME, malignancy, pregnancy/estrogen, immobility, and nephrotic syndrome","Red flags for a workup: thrombosis that is unprovoked, recurrent, at a young age, at unusual sites, or with a strong family history"],"dx":["Testing includes activated protein C resistance/factor V Leiden, prothrombin gene mutation, protein C, protein S, and antithrombin activity, and antiphospholipid antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I)","ANTIPHOSPHOLIPID SYNDROME paradoxically PROLONGS the aPTT in vitro (lupus anticoagulant) while causing thrombosis in vivo; it is a clinical + laboratory diagnosis requiring persistent antibodies","Timing matters - acute thrombosis and anticoagulation alter many assays, so confirmatory testing is often deferred"],"tx":["The result influences anticoagulation duration and intensity and family counseling; provoked, transient risk factors differ from a strong inherited/acquired thrombophilia","Antiphospholipid syndrome typically warrants warfarin (direct oral anticoagulants are less effective, especially triple-positive disease)","Protein C/S deficiency underlies warfarin-induced skin necrosis (transient hypercoagulability at initiation)"],"pearls":["Factor V Leiden (activated protein C resistance) is the most common inherited thrombophilia","Antiphospholipid syndrome prolongs the aPTT in the test tube but causes thrombosis in the patient - and usually needs warfarin rather than a DOAC","Unprovoked, recurrent, young-age, or unusual-site thrombosis with a family history should prompt a thrombophilia evaluation","Test after the acute event and off interfering anticoagulation - acute clot and heparin/warfarin distort the assays"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thrombophilia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538501/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Hypercoagulable States","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-platelet-disorders","name":"Platelet Disorders (ITP, TTP/HUS, HIT)","sec":"path","present":["Thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction cause MUCOCUTANEOUS bleeding (petechiae, purpura, epistaxis, gum bleeding) - in contrast to the deep bleeding of coagulation factor disorders","IMMUNE THROMBOCYTOPENIA (ITP): isolated thrombocytopenia from antiplatelet antibodies (often post-viral in children, chronic in adults); THROMBOTIC MICROANGIOPATHIES: TTP and HUS combine thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolysis","HEPARIN-INDUCED THROMBOCYTOPENIA (HIT): a paradoxical prothrombotic drop in platelets after heparin exposure"],"dx":["ITP is a diagnosis of EXCLUSION - isolated low platelets with a normal smear (aside from large platelets) and no other cause; the marrow shows normal/increased megakaryocytes","TTP: the pentad (thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with SCHISTOCYTES, neurologic changes, renal dysfunction, fever) from ADAMTS13 deficiency; HUS: similar with prominent renal failure, classically after Shiga-toxin E. coli O157:H7 in children","HIT: platelet fall (~30-50%) typically 5-10 days after heparin, with THROMBOSIS; confirmed by anti-PF4/heparin antibodies and a functional (serotonin release) assay; use the 4T score"],"tx":["The mechanism dictates opposite treatments - TTP is a hematologic EMERGENCY requiring urgent PLASMA EXCHANGE (do NOT give platelets, which can worsen thrombosis); HIT requires STOPPING heparin and starting a non-heparin anticoagulant (platelet transfusion also avoided)","ITP is treated (when bleeding/very low counts) with corticosteroids, IVIG, and thrombopoietin agonists; many children recover spontaneously","Recognizing schistocytes on the smear is the trigger to evaluate for a life-threatening microangiopathy"],"pearls":["Platelet-type bleeding is mucocutaneous (petechiae, purpura); factor-type bleeding is deep (hemarthroses, hematomas)","TTP is a plasma-exchange emergency - and platelet transfusions are contraindicated in both TTP and HIT (they fuel thrombosis)","HIT is prothrombotic: platelets fall 5-10 days after heparin WITH clotting - stop all heparin and use a non-heparin anticoagulant","ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion - isolated thrombocytopenia with an otherwise normal smear"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thrombocytopenia (ITP/TTP/HIT)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430830/"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Platelet Disorders","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-transfusion-medicine","name":"Transfusion Medicine — Products, Compatibility & Reactions","sec":"path","present":["Transfusion medicine covers blood PRODUCTS (packed red cells, platelets, fresh frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate), compatibility testing, and the recognition/management of transfusion REACTIONS","The ABO and Rh(D) systems are central: type O is the universal RED CELL donor, type AB the universal recipient; naturally occurring anti-A/anti-B antibodies make ABO-incompatible red cell transfusion catastrophic","Reactions range from mild febrile/allergic events to fatal acute hemolysis"],"dx":["Pretransfusion testing: ABO/Rh TYPE, antibody SCREEN, and CROSSMATCH; the direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test detects antibody-coated red cells (in hemolytic reactions)","ACUTE HEMOLYTIC reaction (usually ABO mismatch/clerical error): fever, flank pain, hypotension, hemoglobinuria - intravascular hemolysis; FEBRILE NON-HEMOLYTIC (common, cytokines/leukocytes); ALLERGIC/anaphylactic (IgA-deficient patients); TRALI (respiratory distress/non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema - a leading cause of transfusion death); TACO (volume overload); delayed hemolytic (anamnestic antibody)","Bacterial contamination (notably platelets, stored at room temperature) causes sepsis"],"tx":["STOP the transfusion immediately for any significant reaction, maintain IV access/support, and send samples (clerical check, repeat type/crossmatch, DAT) - acute hemolytic reactions require aggressive supportive care","Product selection matters: irradiated components prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease; leukoreduction reduces febrile reactions/CMV; washed products for severe allergic/IgA-deficient patients","Massive transfusion protocols balance red cells, plasma, and platelets"],"pearls":["ABO-incompatible red cells cause acute intravascular hemolysis - most often from a clerical/identification error; verify patient identity meticulously","O is the universal red cell donor; AB is the universal plasma donor (the reverse of red cells)","TRALI (non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema) is a leading cause of transfusion-related death; TACO is circulatory overload - both cause post-transfusion dyspnea","Platelets are stored at room temperature and carry the highest bacterial-contamination/sepsis risk"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Transfusion Reactions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482202/"},{"t":"AABB Transfusion Resources","u":"https://www.aabb.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Transfusion Medicine","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-microbiology-sepsis","name":"Clinical Microbiology & Sepsis Diagnostics","sec":"path","present":["Clinical microbiology identifies infectious organisms and their susceptibilities to guide therapy; SEPSIS - life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection - depends on timely, accurate diagnostics","Core methods: GRAM STAIN and culture, antigen/serology, and increasingly rapid MOLECULAR (PCR/multiplex/NGS) and MALDI-TOF identification","Blood cultures, plus source-specific specimens, anchor the workup of the septic patient"],"dx":["The GRAM STAIN rapidly sorts organisms: gram-positive COCCI in clusters (Staphylococcus) vs chains/pairs (Streptococcus/Enterococcus); gram-negative rods (Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas); the stain guides empiric therapy before culture results return","Antimicrobial SUSCEPTIBILITY testing (MIC/breakpoints) directs targeted therapy and detects resistance (MRSA, ESBL, carbapenem-resistant organisms); special stains/cultures for mycobacteria (AFB) and fungi (GMS/KOH)","Sepsis biomarkers (lactate; procalcitonin) support assessment, but obtaining cultures BEFORE antibiotics is critical"],"tx":["The Gram stain and rapid molecular results allow early appropriate empiric therapy; de-escalate to targeted agents once susceptibilities return (antimicrobial stewardship)","In sepsis, early source control, cultures before antibiotics, and prompt broad-spectrum therapy improve outcomes","Resistance detection changes therapy (e.g., vancomycin for MRSA, carbapenems for ESBL)"],"pearls":["Draw blood cultures BEFORE starting antibiotics whenever feasible - and the Gram stain guides empiric therapy hours before culture growth","Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus; in chains = Streptococcus/Enterococcus; catalase and coagulase tests further separate them","Procalcitonin and lactate support (but do not replace) clinical sepsis judgment and cultures","Detecting resistance patterns (MRSA, ESBL, CRE) is what turns a culture result into the right antibiotic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Sepsis / Microbiology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537054/"},{"t":"CDC Antimicrobial Resistance","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/antimicrobial-resistance"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Microbiology","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-breast-carcinoma","name":"Breast Carcinoma (DCIS & Invasive; Receptor Status)","sec":"path","present":["Breast cancer commonly presents as a mass, mammographic abnormality (calcifications/density), or nipple change; it spans in-situ and invasive disease","DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU (DCIS): malignant cells confined by the basement membrane (a non-obligate precursor, often detected as calcifications); INVASIVE ductal carcinoma (no special type) is the most common invasive cancer","INVASIVE LOBULAR carcinoma classically shows single-file infiltration and can be mammographically subtle and bilateral/multifocal"],"dx":["Core-needle BIOPSY establishes histology and grade; invasion through the basement membrane distinguishes invasive carcinoma from DCIS; lobular carcinoma characteristically LOSES E-CADHERIN (single-file cells)","RECEPTOR/biomarker profiling is mandatory: ESTROGEN and PROGESTERONE receptors (ER/PR) and HER2 (IHC +/- FISH) define subtypes; 'TRIPLE-NEGATIVE' (ER-/PR-/HER2-) is more aggressive; Ki-67 and gene-expression assays refine risk","Sentinel lymph node status and tumor size/grade drive staging"],"tx":["Receptor status directs therapy - ER/PR positivity enables endocrine therapy; HER2 amplification enables HER2-targeted therapy; triple-negative disease relies on chemotherapy +/- immunotherapy","DCIS is treated to prevent progression (excision +/- radiation/endocrine therapy); invasive cancer combines surgery, radiation, and receptor-guided systemic therapy","The biomarker panel is one of the clearest examples of pathology directly determining treatment"],"pearls":["DCIS is confined by the basement membrane; invasion through it defines invasive carcinoma and the ability to metastasize","Invasive lobular carcinoma loses E-cadherin (single-file growth) and is often subtle on imaging and bilateral/multifocal","Always report ER, PR, and HER2 - they define subtype and directly determine endocrine, anti-HER2, and chemotherapy decisions","Triple-negative (ER-/PR-/HER2-) breast cancer is more aggressive and is associated with BRCA mutations"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Breast Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482286/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Breast Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Breast Carcinoma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-benign-breast-disease","name":"Benign Breast Disease & Fibroadenoma","sec":"path","present":["Benign breast conditions are common causes of masses, pain, and imaging findings; the key clinical task is separating benign disease from carcinoma and stratifying cancer RISK","FIBROADENOMA: the most common benign breast tumor in young women - a well-circumscribed, mobile, rubbery mass; FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES cause lumpiness and cyclical pain","Other entities: intraductal papilloma (nipple discharge), fat necrosis (after trauma/surgery, can mimic cancer), and phyllodes tumor (can be borderline/malignant)"],"dx":["FIBROADENOMA: a biphasic proliferation of stroma and glands, well-circumscribed; PHYLLODES tumor resembles a fibroadenoma but has a hypercellular 'leaf-like' stroma and a spectrum from benign to malignant","Proliferative lesions stratify cancer RISK: non-proliferative changes (minimal risk), proliferative without atypia (slightly increased), and ATYPICAL HYPERPLASIA (ductal/lobular - markedly increased risk, a marker and non-obligate precursor)","Fat necrosis shows fat with lipid-laden macrophages and can produce a spiculated, cancer-mimicking mass/calcifications"],"tx":["Benign lesions are managed by reassurance/observation or excision depending on size, symptoms, and risk; ATYPICAL hyperplasia warrants excision and heightened surveillance/risk-reduction","Phyllodes tumors require wide EXCISION (they recur locally, and malignant variants can metastasize)","Distinguishing a fibroadenoma from a phyllodes tumor and identifying atypia are the decisions that change management"],"pearls":["Fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumor in young women - well-circumscribed, mobile, and rubbery","A 'fibroadenoma' that is large, rapidly growing, or has cellular leaf-like stroma may be a phyllodes tumor - which needs wide excision","Atypical ductal/lobular hyperplasia markedly increases future breast cancer risk in BOTH breasts (a risk marker, not just a local lesion)","Fat necrosis after trauma/surgery can mimic carcinoma clinically and radiographically - biopsy resolves the question"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Fibroadenoma / Benign Breast Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535345/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Benign Breast","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"American Cancer Society: Breast Conditions","u":"https://www.cancer.org"}]},{"id":"path-barrett-esophageal-carcinoma","name":"Barrett Esophagus & Esophageal Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["Chronic gastroesophageal reflux damages the distal esophagus; BARRETT ESOPHAGUS is the metaplastic replacement of squamous mucosa by intestinal-type COLUMNAR epithelium - a precursor to adenocarcinoma","ESOPHAGEAL ADENOCARCINOMA (arising in Barrett/reflux, distal esophagus - rising in incidence in Western countries) vs SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (mid-esophagus, linked to smoking and alcohol)","Both typically present late with progressive DYSPHAGIA (solids then liquids) and weight loss"],"dx":["BARRETT: endoscopic salmon-colored mucosa with biopsy showing intestinal metaplasia (GOBLET CELLS); dysplasia is graded (negative, indefinite, low-grade, high-grade) and predicts progression to adenocarcinoma","ADENOCARCINOMA: glandular malignancy of the distal esophagus/GE junction; SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA: keratinizing malignancy of the mid/upper esophagus","Staging (endoscopic ultrasound, CT/PET) assesses depth and nodes"],"tx":["Barrett esophagus is managed with reflux control and endoscopic SURVEILLANCE; dysplasia is treated with endoscopic eradication (resection/ablation) to prevent cancer","Invasive carcinoma requires multimodal therapy (surgery, chemoradiation) based on stage; the metaplasia-dysplasia-carcinoma sequence is the rationale for surveillance","Detecting and grading dysplasia is the pivotal pathology decision"],"pearls":["Barrett esophagus = intestinal metaplasia with GOBLET CELLS replacing squamous mucosa - the precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma","Adenocarcinoma is distal (reflux/Barrett, Western); squamous cell carcinoma is mid-esophageal (smoking/alcohol)","Dysplasia grade in Barrett drives management - high-grade dysplasia warrants endoscopic eradication","Progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids with weight loss is esophageal cancer until proven otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Barrett Esophagus / Esophageal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459355/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Esophageal Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Esophagus","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-gastritis-gastric-carcinoma","name":"Gastritis, H. pylori & Gastric Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["Gastritis is inflammation of the gastric mucosa: ACUTE (erosive - NSAIDs, alcohol, stress) and CHRONIC (most commonly HELICOBACTER PYLORI, or autoimmune)","H. PYLORI chronic gastritis (antral-predominant) predisposes to peptic ulcers, gastric ADENOCARCINOMA, and MALT lymphoma; AUTOIMMUNE (body-predominant) gastritis destroys parietal cells causing pernicious anemia (B12 deficiency)","GASTRIC CARCINOMA often presents late with weight loss, early satiety, and epigastric pain"],"dx":["H. PYLORI: curved gram-negative rods on the mucosal surface (highlighted on biopsy special stains), with chronic active gastritis and lymphoid follicles; testing includes urea breath test, stool antigen, and biopsy","GASTRIC ADENOCARCINOMA subtypes: INTESTINAL type (glandular, related to H. pylori/intestinal metaplasia, forms a mass) and DIFFUSE type (poorly cohesive SIGNET-RING cells, LINITIS PLASTICA - a rigid 'leather-bottle' stomach); hereditary diffuse gastric cancer is linked to CDH1/E-cadherin","Autoimmune gastritis shows body/fundic atrophy with anti-parietal/anti-intrinsic-factor antibodies and can lead to neuroendocrine (ECL-cell) tumors"],"tx":["H. pylori ERADICATION heals gastritis/ulcers, reduces cancer risk, and can cause regression of gastric MALT lymphoma","Gastric carcinoma is staged and treated with surgery +/- chemotherapy/chemoradiation; HER2 testing identifies candidates for HER2-targeted therapy","Recognizing intestinal metaplasia/dysplasia enables surveillance before invasive cancer develops"],"pearls":["H. pylori chronic gastritis links to peptic ulcers, gastric adenocarcinoma, AND MALT lymphoma - and eradication can reverse the MALT lymphoma","Diffuse gastric cancer shows signet-ring cells and linitis plastica ('leather-bottle' stomach); hereditary cases carry CDH1/E-cadherin mutations","Autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells (body/fundus) -> achlorhydria, B12 deficiency/pernicious anemia, and ECL-cell hyperplasia/carcinoids","Intestinal-type gastric cancer follows H. pylori -> chronic gastritis -> intestinal metaplasia -> dysplasia -> carcinoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gastritis / Gastric Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544250/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Gastric Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Stomach","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-colorectal-carcinoma","name":"Colorectal Polyps & Carcinoma (Adenoma-Carcinoma Sequence)","sec":"path","present":["Colorectal cancer usually arises from precursor POLYPS over years - presenting with occult/overt GI bleeding, iron-deficiency anemia, change in bowel habits, or obstruction (left-sided) versus anemia/fatigue (right-sided)","ADENOMATOUS polyps (tubular, tubulovillous, VILLOUS - highest malignant potential) are the classic precursors; SESSILE SERRATED lesions are an alternative pathway","Screening (colonoscopy) interrupts the sequence by removing precursor polyps"],"dx":["The ADENOMA-CARCINOMA sequence: normal mucosa -> APC mutation (adenoma) -> KRAS -> TP53 -> invasive adenocarcinoma; villous histology, larger size, and high-grade dysplasia raise risk","Colorectal adenocarcinoma is a gland-forming malignancy; MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY/mismatch-repair (MMR) status is tested for LYNCH syndrome and immunotherapy/prognosis; RAS/BRAF status guides targeted therapy","Hereditary syndromes: FAP (APC - hundreds of polyps, near-certain cancer) and LYNCH/HNPCC (MMR deficiency, MSI-high, right-sided)"],"tx":["Polypectomy prevents cancer; invasive cancer is staged (TNM) and treated with surgery +/- chemotherapy; molecular markers (MMR/MSI, RAS, BRAF) guide systemic and immunotherapy","MMR/MSI testing screens for Lynch syndrome (with implications for the family) and predicts immunotherapy response","New iron-deficiency anemia in an older adult mandates colon evaluation"],"pearls":["The adenoma-carcinoma sequence runs APC -> KRAS -> TP53; villous and larger adenomas have the highest malignant potential","Left-sided cancers obstruct and bleed (change in caliber); right-sided cancers present with iron-deficiency anemia and fatigue","Test every colorectal cancer for mismatch-repair/MSI status - it screens for Lynch syndrome and guides immunotherapy","FAP (APC) causes hundreds of polyps with near-inevitable cancer; Lynch (MMR) causes MSI-high, often right-sided cancers"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Colorectal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470380/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Colon Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Colon Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-ibd","name":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn vs Ulcerative Colitis)","sec":"path","present":["Chronic idiopathic inflammatory bowel disease presenting with diarrhea (often bloody in ulcerative colitis), abdominal pain, weight loss, and extraintestinal manifestations (arthritis, uveitis, skin, primary sclerosing cholangitis)","CROHN DISEASE: can affect ANY part of the GI tract (mouth to anus), classically the terminal ileum, with SKIP lesions; ULCERATIVE COLITIS: CONTINUOUS involvement limited to the COLON, beginning in the rectum","Both increase colorectal cancer risk with long-standing colonic disease"],"dx":["CROHN: TRANSMURAL inflammation with NON-CASEATING GRANULOMAS, 'cobblestone' mucosa, creeping fat, strictures, and FISTULAE; skip lesions on endoscopy/histology","ULCERATIVE COLITIS: MUCOSAL/submucosal inflammation with CRYPT ABSCESSES, continuous involvement from the rectum proximally, pseudopolyps, and (in severe disease) toxic megacolon","Chronic architectural distortion (crypt branching/atrophy) distinguishes IBD from acute self-limited colitis; surveillance biopsies assess DYSPLASIA"],"tx":["Distinguishing Crohn (transmural, granulomas, fistulizing, any site) from ulcerative colitis (mucosal, continuous, colon-only) guides medical therapy and surgery - colectomy is curative for ulcerative colitis but not Crohn","Long-standing colitis requires dysplasia SURVEILLANCE (colorectal cancer risk); primary sclerosing cholangitis (associated with UC) further raises risk","Granulomas favor Crohn but their absence does not exclude it"],"pearls":["Crohn = transmural, skip lesions, non-caseating granulomas, fistulae, mouth-to-anus (often terminal ileum); UC = mucosal, continuous, rectum-to-proximal, crypt abscesses, colon only","Colectomy cures ulcerative colitis but NOT Crohn disease (which recurs)","Chronic crypt architectural distortion separates IBD from acute self-limited colitis","Long-standing colitis raises colorectal cancer risk - surveillance biopsies look for dysplasia (UC + primary sclerosing cholangitis is especially high risk)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Inflammatory Bowel Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470312/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: IBD","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"Crohn's & Colitis Foundation","u":"https://www.crohnscolitisfoundation.org"}]},{"id":"path-celiac-malabsorption","name":"Celiac Disease & Malabsorption","sec":"path","present":["Malabsorption presents with diarrhea/steatorrhea, weight loss, bloating, and nutritional deficiencies (iron, folate, fat-soluble vitamins)","CELIAC DISEASE is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by GLUTEN in genetically susceptible people (HLA-DQ2/DQ8); it may present classically or subtly (iron-deficiency anemia, osteoporosis, dermatitis herpetiformis)","Other malabsorption causes include pancreatic insufficiency, bacterial overgrowth, and Whipple disease"],"dx":["CELIAC serology: anti-TISSUE TRANSGLUTAMINASE (tTG) IgA (with total IgA); DUODENAL BIOPSY (while on gluten) shows VILLOUS ATROPHY, crypt hyperplasia, and INTRAEPITHELIAL LYMPHOCYTOSIS (Marsh grading), predominantly in the proximal small bowel","The changes are not fully specific - correlate with serology and clinical response to a gluten-free diet; HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing has a high negative predictive value","Whipple disease shows PAS-positive macrophages (Tropheryma whipplei)"],"tx":["A lifelong GLUTEN-FREE diet reverses the enteropathy and symptoms and is the treatment for celiac disease; monitor serology and nutritional status","Refractory or non-responsive celiac disease and long-standing disease carry a risk of enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","Recognizing celiac as a cause of unexplained iron-deficiency anemia or osteoporosis is a common clinical pearl"],"pearls":["Celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis in the proximal small bowel - and improves on a gluten-free diet","Confirm with tTG-IgA (check total IgA to avoid a false negative in IgA deficiency) and biopsy while the patient is still eating gluten","Consider celiac in unexplained iron-deficiency anemia, osteoporosis, or dermatitis herpetiformis - it is often not the classic diarrhea","Long-standing/refractory celiac disease risks enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Celiac Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441900/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Celiac Disease","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"NIDDK: Celiac Disease","u":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"path-hepatitis-cirrhosis","name":"Viral Hepatitis & Cirrhosis","sec":"path","present":["Hepatitis is liver inflammation; VIRAL hepatitis (A, B, C, D, E) and other insults (alcohol, metabolic/fatty liver, autoimmune, drugs) can progress to chronic injury and CIRRHOSIS","Presentation ranges from asymptomatic elevated transaminases to jaundice, and - in cirrhosis - the stigmata of portal hypertension and liver failure (ascites, varices, encephalopathy, coagulopathy)","HEPATITIS B and C are the major causes of chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma worldwide"],"dx":["Acute hepatitis shows hepatocyte injury with lobular inflammation and apoptotic (Councilman) bodies; CHRONIC hepatitis shows portal inflammation with interface activity, graded/staged for necroinflammation and FIBROSIS; hepatitis B can show 'ground-glass' hepatocytes","CIRRHOSIS is diffuse bridging FIBROSIS with REGENERATIVE NODULES replacing normal architecture - the common end stage of chronic liver injury; serology/viral load and (increasingly) non-invasive fibrosis assessment guide diagnosis","Alcoholic/steatohepatitis shows steatosis, ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils; autoimmune hepatitis shows plasma-cell-rich interface hepatitis"],"tx":["Etiology guides therapy - hepatitis C is now curable with direct-acting antivirals, and hepatitis B is controlled with antivirals; abstinence and metabolic control address alcohol/fatty liver disease","Cirrhosis mandates surveillance for HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA and management of portal hypertension complications; it is largely irreversible once established","Staging fibrosis and identifying the cause change both prognosis and treatment"],"pearls":["Cirrhosis = bridging fibrosis + regenerative nodules replacing normal architecture - the common end stage of chronic liver injury","Hepatitis B and C are the leading causes of chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma; hepatitis C is now curable with direct-acting antivirals","Ground-glass hepatocytes suggest chronic hepatitis B; Mallory-Denk bodies and neutrophils suggest steatohepatitis; plasma-cell interface hepatitis suggests autoimmune hepatitis","Cirrhosis requires lifelong hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Viral Hepatitis / Cirrhosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554549/"},{"t":"CDC Viral Hepatitis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/hepatitis"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Liver","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-hepatocellular-carcinoma","name":"Hepatocellular Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["The most common primary liver malignancy - arising overwhelmingly in the setting of CIRRHOSIS (hepatitis B and C, alcohol, metabolic/fatty liver disease, and aflatoxin exposure)","Presents with right-upper-quadrant pain, weight loss, worsening liver decompensation, or is detected on surveillance in a cirrhotic patient; serum ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) is often elevated","The FIBROLAMELLAR variant occurs in YOUNG patients WITHOUT cirrhosis and does not elevate AFP"],"dx":["Grossly often multifocal/nodular in a cirrhotic liver and may be bile-stained (green); histology shows thickened trabeculae/pseudoacinar growth of malignant hepatocytes with BILE production and a tendency for VASCULAR (portal vein) invasion","IHC: HepPar-1, arginase-1, glypican-3, and a canalicular pCEA/CD10 pattern confirm hepatocellular origin; in the right context, characteristic imaging (LI-RADS) can be diagnostic WITHOUT biopsy","Fibrolamellar HCC shows large cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and lamellar fibrosis"],"tx":["Cirrhotic patients undergo SURVEILLANCE (ultrasound +/- AFP); treatment is stage-based - resection, TRANSPLANTATION (Milan criteria), ablation, transarterial chemoembolization, and systemic therapy","AFP trends track treatment response and recurrence","Preventing the underlying liver disease (hepatitis vaccination/treatment) is the upstream strategy"],"pearls":["Hepatocellular carcinoma arises in cirrhosis and characteristically invades the portal vein - AFP is often elevated","HepPar-1, arginase-1, and glypican-3 confirm hepatocellular origin; imaging (LI-RADS) can diagnose HCC in a cirrhotic liver without biopsy","Fibrolamellar carcinoma is the exception: young patients, no cirrhosis, normal AFP","Surveillance of cirrhotic patients is what makes HCC potentially curable (transplant/resection/ablation)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hepatocellular Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559177/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Hepatobiliary Cancers","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Liver Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-pancreatic-carcinoma-pancreatitis","name":"Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma & Pancreatitis","sec":"path","present":["PANCREATITIS: acute (most often GALLSTONES or ALCOHOL) with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back and elevated lipase; chronic pancreatitis leads to fibrosis, calcifications, and exocrine/endocrine insufficiency","PANCREATIC DUCTAL ADENOCARCINOMA (PDAC): typically presents LATE with weight loss and (head-of-pancreas tumors) painless obstructive JAUNDICE with a palpable non-tender gallbladder (COURVOISIER sign)","Paraneoplastic migratory thrombophlebitis (TROUSSEAU sign) and new-onset diabetes can herald PDAC"],"dx":["ACUTE pancreatitis shows fat necrosis with SAPONIFICATION (calcium soaps, can cause hypocalcemia), edema, and hemorrhage; chronic pancreatitis shows fibrosis, ductal dilation/calcifications, and acinar atrophy","PDAC: an infiltrating ductal ADENOCARCINOMA with abundant DESMOPLASTIC stroma and prominent PERINEURAL/vascular invasion, arising from PanIN precursors; KRAS is mutated in ~90%, with CDKN2A, TP53, and SMAD4 alterations","CA 19-9 supports diagnosis and monitors PDAC"],"tx":["Acute pancreatitis is managed supportively with treatment of the cause (gallstones/alcohol); chronic pancreatitis addresses pain and enzyme/insulin insufficiency","PDAC has a poor prognosis - only a minority are resectable (Whipple procedure for head lesions) - with chemotherapy central to treatment; CA 19-9 monitors response","The late presentation and aggressive biology make PDAC one of the deadliest cancers"],"pearls":["Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma carries a KRAS mutation in ~90% and shows desmoplasia with perineural invasion","Painless obstructive jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier) and migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau) point to pancreatic head cancer","Acute pancreatitis causes fat saponification - which can drop serum calcium","CA 19-9 is a monitoring marker for PDAC (not a screening test)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pancreatic Cancer / Pancreatitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518996/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Pancreatic Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Pancreas","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-prostate-adenocarcinoma","name":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma (Gleason Grading)","sec":"path","present":["The most common visceral cancer in men - often asymptomatic and detected via an elevated PSA or an abnormal digital rectal exam; it typically arises in the PERIPHERAL zone","Advanced disease may cause lower urinary tract symptoms or present with OSTEOBLASTIC bone metastases (bone pain, elevated alkaline phosphatase)","Most are acinar adenocarcinomas, graded by the Gleason system"],"dx":["Histology shows small, crowded, infiltrative glands lined by a SINGLE cell layer (LOSS of the basal cell layer) with prominent NUCLEOLI; loss of basal cells is confirmed by NEGATIVE p63/high-molecular-weight cytokeratin, and tumor cells are POSITIVE for AMACR (racemase)","GLEASON grading assigns patterns by architecture; the sum of the two most prevalent patterns yields a score that maps to GRADE GROUPS 1-5, which drive prognosis; perineural invasion is common","PSA supports diagnosis and monitoring (bone metastases are characteristically osteoblastic)"],"tx":["Risk stratification (PSA, Grade Group, stage) guides ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE for low-risk disease versus surgery/radiation, and androgen-deprivation therapy for advanced disease","PSA is used for monitoring after treatment","The Grade Group is one of the clearest examples of grade directing clinical management"],"pearls":["Prostate cancer glands lose their basal cell layer - negative p63/HMWCK and positive AMACR (racemase) confirm it","The Gleason score (sum of the two dominant patterns) maps to Grade Groups 1-5 that drive management, including active surveillance for low-risk disease","Prostate cancer favors the peripheral zone and metastasizes to bone as OSTEOBLASTIC lesions","Prominent nucleoli and small crowded infiltrative glands are diagnostic cytologic clues"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470550/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Prostate Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Prostate","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-renal-cell-carcinoma","name":"Renal Cell Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["The most common malignant kidney tumor in adults; the classic triad of HEMATURIA, flank pain, and a palpable mass is now uncommon - most are found incidentally on imaging","Risk factors include SMOKING, obesity, and hereditary syndromes (VON HIPPEL-LINDAU); it is notorious for PARANEOPLASTIC syndromes","It arises from renal tubular epithelium and characteristically invades the RENAL VEIN/IVC"],"dx":["CLEAR CELL RCC is the most common subtype - polygonal cells with clear (lipid/glycogen) cytoplasm and a delicate 'chicken-wire' vasculature, associated with VHL gene (chromosome 3p) alterations; papillary and chromophobe are other subtypes","IHC: PAX8 and CD10 support renal origin; assess for renal vein/IVC extension and grade (nucleolar/ISUP grade)","Paraneoplastic effects: erythropoietin (POLYCYTHEMIA), PTHrP (hypercalcemia), and Stauffer syndrome (hepatic dysfunction without metastasis)"],"tx":["Localized disease is treated surgically (partial or radical nephrectomy); RCC is classically RESISTANT to conventional chemotherapy and radiation","Advanced disease is treated with anti-angiogenic tyrosine kinase inhibitors, mTOR inhibitors, and immunotherapy","Recognizing paraneoplastic syndromes can be the presenting clue"],"pearls":["Clear cell RCC (the most common subtype) is linked to VHL/3p and invades the renal vein - it is resistant to conventional chemotherapy and radiation","Think paraneoplastic: EPO -> polycythemia, PTHrP -> hypercalcemia, and Stauffer syndrome (reversible hepatic dysfunction without liver metastasis)","PAX8 and CD10 mark renal origin","Most RCCs today are incidental imaging findings, not the classic triad"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470336/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Kidney Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Kidney Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-bladder-urothelial-carcinoma","name":"Bladder Urothelial Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["The most common bladder malignancy - classically presents with PAINLESS gross HEMATURIA in an older adult","Major risk factors: cigarette SMOKING (the biggest), occupational AROMATIC AMINES/aniline dyes, and cyclophosphamide; SCHISTOSOMA HAEMATOBIUM infection predisposes instead to squamous cell carcinoma","It tends to be multifocal and recurrent (a 'field' effect across the urothelium)"],"dx":["UROTHELIAL (transitional cell) carcinoma occurs as PAPILLARY tumors (often low-grade, recurring) and FLAT lesions/CARCINOMA IN SITU (high-grade); the critical prognostic divide is NON-muscle-invasive versus MUSCLE-INVASIVE disease (depth of invasion into the detrusor)","Urine cytology is sensitive for high-grade tumors and CIS; cystoscopy with biopsy/TURBT establishes grade and stage","Squamous cell carcinoma (Schistosoma, chronic irritation) and adenocarcinoma (urachal) are less common"],"tx":["Non-muscle-invasive disease is managed with transurethral resection (TURBT) and intravesical therapy (BCG for high-risk); muscle-invasive disease requires radical CYSTECTOMY +/- chemotherapy","Lifelong SURVEILLANCE is needed because of frequent recurrence","Depth of muscle invasion is the pathology finding that most changes management"],"pearls":["Painless gross hematuria in an adult is bladder cancer until proven otherwise - smoking and aromatic amines are the key risks","Muscle (detrusor) invasion is the pivotal distinction - non-muscle-invasive is managed endoscopically +/- intravesical BCG, muscle-invasive needs cystectomy","Papillary tumors are often low-grade and recur; flat carcinoma in situ is high-grade","Schistosoma haematobium predisposes to SQUAMOUS cell carcinoma of the bladder (not urothelial)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536923/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Bladder Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Bladder","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-testicular-germ-cell-tumors","name":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","sec":"path","present":["The most common solid malignancy in YOUNG MEN (roughly 15-35 years) - typically a PAINLESS testicular mass or enlargement","CRYPTORCHIDISM (undescended testis) is a major risk factor (for both testes)","Germ cell tumors are broadly SEMINOMATOUS or NON-SEMINOMATOUS, a distinction that drives markers and treatment"],"dx":["SEMINOMA (most common): sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm ('fried-egg') and a lymphocytic infiltrate; radiosensitive; may elevate hCG but AFP is NEVER elevated in pure seminoma","NON-SEMINOMATOUS: embryonal carcinoma, YOLK SAC tumor (AFP; Schiller-Duval bodies - the most common childhood testicular tumor), CHORIOCARCINOMA (hCG; hemorrhagic), and teratoma; serum markers AFP, beta-hCG, and LDH aid diagnosis/monitoring; IHC includes OCT3/4, SALL4, PLAP","Diagnosis is by radical INGUINAL ORCHIECTOMY - transscrotal biopsy is contraindicated (risks tumor seeding)"],"tx":["Radical inguinal orchiectomy is both diagnostic and therapeutic; SEMINOMA is highly radiosensitive; non-seminomatous tumors are treated with cisplatin-based CHEMOTHERAPY - germ cell tumors are highly CURABLE even when metastatic","Serum markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) monitor treatment response and recurrence","An elevated AFP indicates a non-seminomatous component regardless of morphology"],"pearls":["A painless testicular mass in a young man is a germ cell tumor until proven otherwise - work up with markers and do a radical INGUINAL orchiectomy (never transscrotal biopsy)","AFP is NEVER elevated in pure seminoma - an elevated AFP means a non-seminomatous element is present","Yolk sac tumor = AFP and Schiller-Duval bodies; choriocarcinoma = hCG and hematogenous spread","Germ cell tumors are among the most curable cancers, even metastatic (cisplatin-based chemotherapy)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Testicular Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448073/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Testicular Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Testis","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-cervical-carcinoma-hpv","name":"Cervical Dysplasia, HPV & Cervical Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["Cervical cancer and its precursors are caused by persistent infection with high-risk HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS (especially types 16 and 18)","Precancerous dysplasia is usually asymptomatic and SCREEN-detected; invasive cancer may cause postcoital/abnormal bleeding and discharge","Screening (Pap cytology and HPV testing) and HPV vaccination have dramatically reduced incidence"],"dx":["High-risk HPV integrates and expresses E6 (degrades p53) and E7 (inactivates Rb) oncoproteins; the cytopathic effect produces KOILOCYTES; dysplasia is graded as CIN 1/2/3 (or low- vs high-grade SIL), arising at the TRANSFORMATION ZONE","Most cervical cancers are SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (adenocarcinoma is less common); p16 IHC is a surrogate marker for high-risk HPV","Pap smear samples the transformation zone; colposcopy/biopsy confirms high-grade lesions"],"tx":["PREVENTION dominates: HPV vaccination and screening detect/treat high-grade dysplasia (LEEP/cone excision) before invasion","Invasive cancer is treated by stage with surgery and/or chemoradiation","Interrupting the dysplasia-to-carcinoma sequence via screening is a public-health success story"],"pearls":["High-risk HPV (16/18) drives cervical cancer via E6 (p53) and E7 (Rb) - koilocytes are the cytologic footprint","Dysplasia (CIN/SIL) begins at the transformation zone and progresses to squamous cell carcinoma; p16 is an HPV surrogate marker","Screening plus HPV vaccination prevents most cervical cancer - treating high-grade dysplasia interrupts the sequence","Most cervical cancer is squamous cell carcinoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Cancer / CIN","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431093/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Cervical Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Cervix","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-endometrial-carcinoma","name":"Endometrial Hyperplasia & Carcinoma","sec":"path","present":["Endometrial carcinoma is the most common gynecologic malignancy in developed countries - the cardinal presentation is POSTMENOPAUSAL BLEEDING (which must be evaluated to exclude cancer)","UNOPPOSED ESTROGEN is the central risk factor for the common (endometrioid) type: obesity, PCOS, nulliparity, early menarche/late menopause, tamoxifen, and estrogen-only hormone therapy","Endometrial hyperplasia is the precursor lesion"],"dx":["Endometrial HYPERPLASIA is classified by the presence of ATYPIA - atypical (endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia) hyperplasia is the true precursor with significant progression risk","TYPE I (endometrioid) carcinoma is estrogen-driven, associated with PTEN/mismatch-repair alterations, lower grade, and better prognosis; TYPE II (serous, clear cell) is NON-estrogen-driven, associated with TP53, occurs in older women, and is aggressive","Diagnosis is by endometrial biopsy/curettage; LYNCH syndrome is an important hereditary association"],"tx":["Atypical hyperplasia is treated with progestins or hysterectomy; carcinoma is treated with hysterectomy/surgical staging +/- adjuvant therapy","Type distinction (endometrioid vs serous) predicts behavior and management","Evaluating postmenopausal bleeding promptly is the key clinical action"],"pearls":["Postmenopausal bleeding is endometrial cancer until proven otherwise - biopsy it","Unopposed estrogen (obesity, PCOS, nulliparity, tamoxifen) drives Type I endometrioid carcinoma; atypical hyperplasia is the precursor","Type I (endometrioid, PTEN, estrogen-related) is favorable; Type II (serous, p53, older women) is aggressive","Endometrial cancer is a sentinel cancer for Lynch syndrome"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Endometrial Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525981/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Uterine Neoplasms","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Uterus","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-ovarian-tumors","name":"Ovarian Tumors (Epithelial, Germ Cell, Sex Cord-Stromal)","sec":"path","present":["Ovarian tumors often present LATE with vague symptoms (bloating, pelvic pressure, a mass, ascites) - contributing to a poor prognosis for malignant epithelial disease","Risk factors for epithelial cancer include BRCA1/2 mutations, family history, and nulliparity; the three major categories are EPITHELIAL, GERM CELL, and SEX CORD-STROMAL","A metastatic tumor to the ovary (Krukenberg) must always be considered"],"dx":["EPITHELIAL tumors (most common and the leading cause of gynecologic cancer death): SEROUS (high-grade serous linked to TP53/BRCA, with PSAMMOMA bodies; often from the fallopian tube), mucinous, and endometrioid; serum CA-125 is used for monitoring","GERM CELL tumors (younger patients): mature cystic TERATOMA (dermoid - the most common, benign), dysgerminoma, and yolk sac tumor (AFP); SEX CORD-STROMAL: GRANULOSA cell (estrogen, CALL-EXNER bodies, inhibin), Sertoli-Leydig (androgen), and fibroma (MEIGS syndrome - fibroma + ascites + pleural effusion)","KRUKENBERG tumor = bilateral metastatic SIGNET-RING adenocarcinoma (classically from the stomach)"],"tx":["Epithelial ovarian cancer is treated with surgical debulking and platinum-based chemotherapy; germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumors are managed per type","Markers guide monitoring: CA-125 (epithelial), AFP/hCG (germ cell), inhibin (granulosa)","BRCA status informs treatment (PARP inhibitors) and family screening"],"pearls":["Epithelial tumors are the most common ovarian cancers and cause the most deaths - high-grade serous carries TP53/BRCA and shows psammoma bodies","Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid) is the most common germ cell tumor and is benign","Granulosa cell tumors make estrogen (Call-Exner bodies, inhibin); a fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion is Meigs syndrome","A Krukenberg tumor is bilateral metastatic signet-ring carcinoma to the ovaries (usually gastric)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ovarian Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526091/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Ovarian Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Ovary","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-gestational-trophoblastic-disease","name":"Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","sec":"path","present":["A spectrum of trophoblastic proliferations; HYDATIDIFORM MOLE presents with vaginal bleeding, a uterus LARGE for dates, hyperemesis, markedly elevated beta-hCG, and a 'SNOWSTORM' pattern on ultrasound with grape-like vesicles","Early preeclampsia and hyperthyroidism can occur from very high hCG","CHORIOCARCINOMA is a malignant trophoblastic tumor that can follow a molar pregnancy, abortion, or normal pregnancy and metastasizes (classically to the lung)"],"dx":["COMPLETE MOLE: entirely PATERNAL genome (usually 46,XX), NO fetal parts, with diffuse hydropic villi and circumferential trophoblast HYPERPLASIA - higher malignant potential (~a small percent progress to choriocarcinoma); p57 IHC is NEGATIVE","PARTIAL MOLE: TRIPLOID (69 chromosomes) with some fetal parts and focal changes; p57 is positive; lower malignant potential","CHORIOCARCINOMA: sheets of malignant cytotrophoblast/syncytiotrophoblast with hemorrhage and NO chorionic villi; hematogenous spread and very high hCG"],"tx":["Molar pregnancy is treated with uterine EVACUATION (D&C) and serial beta-hCG monitoring to zero (surveillance for persistent/malignant disease)","Choriocarcinoma is highly CHEMOSENSITIVE (methotrexate-based), even when metastatic","Following hCG to resolution is the essential post-molar management step"],"pearls":["Complete mole = all-paternal (46,XX), no fetus, 'snowstorm'/grape-like vesicles, very high hCG, and p57-negative; partial mole = triploid with fetal parts and p57-positive","Complete moles carry a higher risk of progressing to choriocarcinoma than partial moles","Choriocarcinoma has NO villi, spreads hematogenously (lung), and is very chemosensitive (methotrexate)","Monitor beta-hCG to zero after evacuating a mole to catch persistent trophoblastic disease"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537001/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Molar Pregnancy / Choriocarcinoma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"path-thyroid-pathology","name":"Thyroid — Thyroiditis & Neoplasms","sec":"path","present":["Thyroid disease presents as a nodule/goiter or as hypo-/hyperthyroidism; THYROIDITIS includes HASHIMOTO (autoimmune hypothyroidism), GRAVES (autoimmune hyperthyroidism), and subacute/de Quervain (painful, post-viral)","Thyroid NEOPLASMS present as nodules and are stratified by fine-needle aspiration","The task is separating benign thyroiditis/nodules from the several distinct carcinomas"],"dx":["HASHIMOTO: lymphocytic infiltrate with germinal centers and HURTHLE cells; anti-TPO antibodies; leads to hypothyroidism and carries a marginal risk of thyroid lymphoma. GRAVES: diffuse hyperplasia with scalloped colloid; TSI antibodies","CARCINOMAS: PAPILLARY (most common; diagnosed by NUCLEAR features - 'Orphan-Annie-eye' clearing, grooves, pseudoinclusions - plus PSAMMOMA bodies; BRAF; spreads via LYMPHATICS; excellent prognosis); FOLLICULAR (requires CAPSULAR/VASCULAR invasion for diagnosis - not diagnosable on FNA; hematogenous spread); MEDULLARY (C-cell origin, CALCITONIN, AMYLOID stroma, MEN2/RET); ANAPLASTIC (elderly, undifferentiated, rapidly fatal)","FNA is reported by the Bethesda system to guide surgery"],"tx":["Thyroiditis is managed with hormone replacement (Hashimoto) or antithyroid therapy (Graves); carcinomas are treated with thyroidectomy +/- radioactive iodine (differentiated types)","Medullary carcinoma prompts genetic (RET/MEN2) evaluation; anaplastic carcinoma is treated palliatively/aggressively but has a dismal prognosis","Follicular lesions on FNA require surgical excision to assess for invasion"],"pearls":["Papillary carcinoma is diagnosed by NUCLEAR features (Orphan-Annie clearing, grooves, pseudoinclusions) and psammoma bodies - it spreads via lymphatics and has the best prognosis","Follicular carcinoma cannot be diagnosed on FNA - it requires capsular or vascular invasion seen on the excised specimen","Medullary carcinoma makes calcitonin, has amyloid stroma, and is associated with MEN2/RET","Anaplastic carcinoma is a rapidly fatal tumor of the elderly; Hashimoto causes hypothyroidism and a small lymphoma risk"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroid Cancer / Thyroiditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459299/"},{"t":"ATA Thyroid Guidelines","u":"https://www.thyroid.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Thyroid","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-diabetes-islet-pathology","name":"Diabetes Mellitus & Pancreatic Islet Pathology","sec":"path","present":["Diabetes mellitus is chronic HYPERGLYCEMIA from defects in insulin secretion, action, or both; TYPE 1 (autoimmune beta-cell destruction, younger onset, prone to ketoacidosis) and TYPE 2 (insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency, associated with obesity - the most common form)","Presentation ranges from polyuria/polydipsia to incidental hyperglycemia to acute crises (DKA, hyperosmolar state)","The morbidity is driven by chronic vascular COMPLICATIONS"],"dx":["TYPE 1: INSULITIS (lymphocytic islet inflammation) with islet autoantibodies (e.g., anti-GAD) and HLA associations; TYPE 2: islet AMYLOID (IAPP/amylin) deposition with insulin resistance","COMPLICATION pathology - MICROVASCULAR: diabetic NEPHROPATHY (basement membrane thickening and nodular KIMMELSTIEL-WILSON glomerulosclerosis), retinopathy, and neuropathy; MACROVASCULAR: accelerated ATHEROSCLEROSIS; mechanisms include nonenzymatic glycation (advanced glycation end-products)","Diagnosis uses fasting glucose, HbA1c, and glucose tolerance testing; HbA1c reflects chronic control"],"tx":["Glycemic control (insulin for type 1; lifestyle and agents for type 2) prevents/delays microvascular complications; blood-pressure and lipid control address macrovascular risk","Recognizing Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules and basement membrane changes links the systemic disease to organ damage","Islet cell tumors (insulinoma, gastrinoma) are separate neoplasms of the endocrine pancreas"],"pearls":["Type 1 diabetes = autoimmune insulitis with islet autoantibodies; type 2 = insulin resistance with islet amyloid (IAPP) deposition","Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodular glomerulosclerosis is the classic renal lesion of diabetic nephropathy","Advanced glycation end-products and basement membrane thickening underlie the microvascular complications","HbA1c reflects average glycemia over ~3 months and tracks long-term control"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Diabetes Mellitus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551501/"},{"t":"American Diabetes Association Standards of Care","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Pancreas (Endocrine)","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-lung-carcinoma","name":"Lung Carcinoma (Small Cell vs Non-Small Cell)","sec":"path","present":["A leading cause of cancer death - presenting with cough, hemoptysis, weight loss, or (often) late-stage disease; strongly linked to SMOKING (with some adenocarcinomas in never-smokers)","The fundamental divide is SMALL CELL lung carcinoma (SCLC) versus NON-SMALL CELL lung carcinoma (NSCLC), which differ in biology and treatment","Various PARANEOPLASTIC syndromes and local complications (Pancoast tumor, SVC syndrome) may be the presentation"],"dx":["SMALL CELL: a central, high-grade NEUROENDOCRINE carcinoma ('oat cells', chromogranin/synaptophysin) that is very aggressive with early metastasis; associated paraneoplastic SIADH, ectopic ACTH, and Lambert-Eaton syndrome - almost always in smokers, generally NOT surgical","NSCLC - ADENOCARCINOMA (the most common overall; peripheral; more common in women/never-smokers; glandular/mucin; TTF-1-positive; targetable EGFR/ALK/KRAS alterations); SQUAMOUS CELL (central; keratin pearls/intercellular bridges; cavitation; PTHrP -> hypercalcemia); and large cell","Molecular and PD-L1 testing of NSCLC is now standard"],"tx":["SCLC is treated with chemotherapy +/- radiation (usually not surgery, given early spread); NSCLC is resected when localized, with molecularly TARGETED therapy (EGFR/ALK inhibitors) and IMMUNOTHERAPY (PD-L1-guided) for advanced disease","Distinguishing SCLC from NSCLC, and subtyping/molecular profiling NSCLC, directly determines therapy","Paraneoplastic recognition can lead to the diagnosis"],"pearls":["Small cell carcinoma is a central neuroendocrine cancer with paraneoplastic SIADH/ACTH/Lambert-Eaton - aggressive, early-metastasizing, and non-surgical","Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer, is peripheral and TTF-1-positive, and carries targetable EGFR/ALK alterations","Squamous cell carcinoma is central and cavitary with keratin pearls and can secrete PTHrP (hypercalcemia)","Every advanced NSCLC needs molecular (EGFR/ALK) and PD-L1 testing to guide therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482357/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: NSCLC / SCLC","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Lung Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-granulomatous-lung-disease","name":"Granulomatous & Infectious Lung Disease","sec":"path","present":["Infectious and granulomatous lung diseases include TUBERCULOSIS (cough, hemoptysis, night sweats, weight loss), SARCOIDOSIS (often asymptomatic bilateral hilar adenopathy in a young adult), and PNEUMONIA (fever, productive cough, consolidation)","Fungal infections (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis) and other organisms also produce granulomatous inflammation","Distinguishing infection from non-infectious granulomatous disease is the central diagnostic problem"],"dx":["TUBERCULOSIS: CASEATING granulomas with acid-fast bacilli (AFB stain); the GHON complex (parenchymal focus + hilar nodes) in primary disease and upper-lobe cavitary reactivation; SARCOIDOSIS: NON-CASEATING granulomas, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, elevated ACE, and hypercalcemia - a diagnosis of EXCLUSION","PNEUMONIA: lobar (consolidation) versus bronchopneumonia (patchy); identify the organism with Gram stain/culture and special stains (AFB for mycobacteria, GMS for fungi)","Always stain granulomas for organisms before attributing them to sarcoidosis"],"tx":["Tuberculosis requires multidrug anti-tuberculous therapy; sarcoidosis is observed or treated with corticosteroids; pneumonia is treated with appropriate antimicrobials","The stain/culture results dictate completely different management (anti-infective vs immunosuppression)","Missing an infection and treating it as sarcoid with steroids can be dangerous"],"pearls":["Caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli = tuberculosis (Ghon complex; upper-lobe cavitary reactivation)","Sarcoidosis shows NON-caseating granulomas, bilateral hilar adenopathy, elevated ACE, and hypercalcemia - and is a diagnosis of exclusion","Always stain a lung granuloma for organisms (AFB, fungal) before calling it sarcoid","Lobar consolidation versus patchy bronchopneumonia are the two classic bacterial pneumonia patterns"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tuberculosis / Sarcoidosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441916/"},{"t":"CDC Tuberculosis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/tb"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Lung Nontumor","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-glomerular-disease","name":"Glomerular Disease (Nephritic vs Nephrotic)","sec":"path","present":["Glomerular diseases divide into two clinical syndromes: NEPHROTIC (heavy proteinuria >3.5 g/day, edema, hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia) and NEPHRITIC (hematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria, mild proteinuria, and azotemia)","The distinction directs the differential, workup, and treatment","Renal biopsy with light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy classifies the specific entity"],"dx":["NEPHROTIC: MINIMAL CHANGE disease (children; normal light microscopy with foot-process EFFACEMENT on EM; steroid-responsive), FOCAL SEGMENTAL GLOMERULOSCLEROSIS (adults, HIV, obesity), MEMBRANOUS nephropathy (adults; subepithelial 'spikes'; anti-PLA2R), diabetic nephropathy, and amyloidosis","NEPHRITIC: IgA nephropathy (mesangial IgA; the most common; synpharyngitic hematuria), POST-INFECTIOUS glomerulonephritis (subepithelial 'humps'; granular staining), RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE/crescentic GN (anti-GBM/Goodpasture - LINEAR staining; ANCA-associated; immune-complex), and lupus nephritis","Immunofluorescence pattern (linear, granular, mesangial, pauci-immune) and EM deposit location are key discriminators"],"tx":["Treatment is entity-specific: corticosteroids for minimal change disease, immunosuppression for many nephritic/proliferative diseases, and treatment of the underlying cause (diabetes, lupus, infection)","Crescents on biopsy signal rapidly progressive GN - a nephrologic emergency requiring prompt immunosuppression","The nephrotic/nephritic framework plus biopsy findings guides therapy"],"pearls":["Nephrotic = proteinuria/edema/hypoalbuminemia/hyperlipidemia; nephritic = hematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, and azotemia","Minimal change disease (children, foot-process effacement, steroid-responsive) and membranous nephropathy (anti-PLA2R, subepithelial spikes) are classic nephrotic causes","IgA nephropathy (mesangial IgA) is the most common glomerulonephritis; post-infectious GN shows subepithelial 'humps'","Crescents mean rapidly progressive GN - linear IF = anti-GBM/Goodpasture, pauci-immune = ANCA-associated"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Glomerulonephritis / Nephrotic Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560644/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Kidney (Medical Renal)","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"National Kidney Foundation","u":"https://www.kidney.org"}]},{"id":"path-atherosclerosis-ihd","name":"Atherosclerosis & Ischemic Heart Disease","sec":"path","present":["ATHEROSCLEROSIS is the intimal accumulation of lipid and fibrous tissue in large and medium arteries - the basis of ISCHEMIC HEART DISEASE, stroke, and peripheral artery disease","Manifestations include stable angina, acute coronary syndromes/myocardial infarction, sudden cardiac death, and claudication","Risk factors: hyperlipidemia (LDL), smoking, hypertension, diabetes, and age"],"dx":["The lesion evolves from a FATTY STREAK (lipid-laden 'FOAM CELL' macrophages) to a fibrofatty ATHEROMATOUS PLAQUE (a lipid/necrotic core beneath a fibrous cap); the abdominal aorta, coronary, popliteal, and carotid arteries are most affected","Complications: progressive STENOSIS, plaque RUPTURE with superimposed THROMBOSIS (the trigger of acute coronary events), aneurysm, and embolization; a 'VULNERABLE' plaque has a thin fibrous cap and large lipid core","Ischemic heart disease results from coronary atherosclerosis limiting myocardial perfusion"],"tx":["Management targets risk factors (statins, blood pressure/glucose control, smoking cessation, antiplatelet therapy) and, for significant disease, revascularization","Understanding that plaque RUPTURE (not just gradual stenosis) causes acute thrombosis explains the presentation of acute coronary syndromes","Plaque stabilization is a central therapeutic goal"],"pearls":["Atherosclerosis progresses from fatty streaks (foam cells) to fibrofatty plaques with a lipid core and fibrous cap","Acute coronary events are usually caused by plaque RUPTURE and thrombosis - not merely by the degree of stenosis","A 'vulnerable' plaque has a thin fibrous cap over a large lipid core","Most-affected vessels: abdominal aorta, then coronary, popliteal, and carotid arteries"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atherosclerosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507799/"},{"t":"American Heart Association","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Heart & Vessels","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-myocardial-infarction-evolution","name":"Myocardial Infarction — Pathologic Evolution","sec":"path","present":["Myocardial infarction is ischemic myocardial necrosis, usually from acute coronary thrombosis on a ruptured atherosclerotic plaque - presenting with chest pain and a rise in cardiac TROPONIN","The histologic appearance and the risk of specific COMPLICATIONS evolve predictably over time","Knowing the timeline explains why particular complications occur at particular days"],"dx":["TIME COURSE: first hours - minimal light-microscopic change (wavy fibers); 4-24 hours - early COAGULATIVE necrosis (and contraction band necrosis with reperfusion); 1-3 days - coagulative necrosis with NEUTROPHIL infiltration; 3-7 days - MACROPHAGES remove dead myocytes (the wall is WEAKEST here); 1-2 weeks - GRANULATION tissue; and by ~2 months - a dense collagenous SCAR","Gross changes lag histology (pallor, then a yellow softened center, then a red-gray depressed scar)","Cardiac enzymes (troponin) confirm the clinical event"],"tx":["COMPLICATIONS by time: early ARRHYTHMIA (leading early cause of death); 3-7 days - myocardial RUPTURE (free wall -> tamponade; septum -> VSD; papillary muscle -> acute mitral regurgitation) and fibrinous PERICARDITIS; later - ventricular ANEURYSM, mural thrombus/embolism, and Dressler (autoimmune) pericarditis","Reperfusion therapy limits infarct size (with contraction-band necrosis as a histologic footprint)","Anticipating time-specific complications guides monitoring"],"pearls":["MI histology timeline: neutrophils ~1-3 days, macrophages ~3-7 days (wall is weakest -> rupture risk), granulation tissue ~1-2 weeks, and collagenous scar by ~2 months","Myocardial free-wall rupture (tamponade), septal rupture (VSD), and papillary muscle rupture (mitral regurgitation) cluster around days 3-7","Contraction band necrosis is the histologic signature of reperfusion","Dressler syndrome is an autoimmune pericarditis appearing weeks after the infarct"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Myocardial Infarction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537076/"},{"t":"American Heart Association","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Myocardial Infarction","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-melanoma","name":"Melanoma (Breslow Depth & Staging)","sec":"path","present":["A malignant tumor of MELANOCYTES - the most lethal common skin cancer; risk factors include UV/sun exposure, fair skin, many/dysplastic nevi, and family history","Clinical warning signs follow the ABCDEs: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter >6 mm, and Evolution/change","Melanoma can also arise at non-cutaneous sites (mucosal, acral, uveal)"],"dx":["Subtypes include superficial spreading (most common), nodular, lentigo maligna, and acral lentiginous; the RADIAL growth phase precedes the VERTICAL growth phase (which confers metastatic potential)","The single most important PROGNOSTIC factor is BRESLOW DEPTH (tumor thickness in mm); ULCERATION and mitotic activity also matter; IHC markers include S100, SOX10, HMB-45, and Melan-A","BRAF V600E mutation is common and therapeutically targetable; sentinel lymph node biopsy assesses regional spread"],"tx":["Wide local EXCISION with margins based on Breslow depth, plus sentinel node biopsy for staging; advanced disease is treated with IMMUNOTHERAPY (checkpoint inhibitors) and BRAF/MEK-targeted therapy","Breslow depth directly determines margins, sentinel node decisions, and prognosis","Immunotherapy has dramatically improved outcomes in advanced melanoma"],"pearls":["Breslow depth (thickness) is the most important prognostic factor in melanoma; ulceration is an adverse feature","The vertical growth phase confers metastatic potential (the radial phase does not)","S100/SOX10/HMB-45/Melan-A confirm melanocytic origin; BRAF V600E is targetable","Use the ABCDEs to flag suspicious lesions - and remember melanoma can be amelanotic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Melanoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470409/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Melanoma","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Melanoma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-non-melanoma-skin-cancer","name":"Non-Melanoma Skin Cancer (BCC & SCC)","sec":"path","present":["Keratinocyte-derived skin cancers on sun-exposed skin (this content shares with the dermatology framing, pathology lens) - the most common cancers overall","BASAL CELL CARCINOMA: a slow-growing PEARLY papule with TELANGIECTASIAS and a rolled border, often on the face - locally destructive but rarely metastasizing; SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA: a scaly, ulcerated, or nodular lesion arising from ACTINIC KERATOSIS, with real metastatic potential","Chronic UV exposure is the dominant risk; immunosuppression markedly increases SCC"],"dx":["BASAL CELL CARCINOMA: nests of basaloid cells with PERIPHERAL PALISADING and a retraction (clefting) artifact; driven by the HEDGEHOG pathway (PTCH mutations); rarely metastasizes","SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA: atypical keratinocytes with KERATIN PEARLS and intercellular bridges, arising from actinic keratosis/in-situ disease (Bowen); can metastasize, especially at high-risk sites or in immunosuppressed patients","Biopsy confirms the diagnosis and depth/differentiation"],"tx":["Treatment is complete EXCISION (MOHS surgery for the face/high-risk sites), with destructive/topical options for superficial lesions and radiation as an alternative","High-risk SCC features (size, depth, perineural invasion, immunosuppression) drive nodal assessment and adjuvant therapy","Sun protection and surveillance address the field of UV damage"],"pearls":["Basal cell carcinoma is the most common human cancer - pearly with telangiectasias, palisading basaloid nests, hedgehog/PTCH-driven, and rarely metastasizes","Squamous cell carcinoma arises from actinic keratosis, shows keratin pearls, and CAN metastasize (especially in immunosuppressed patients/high-risk sites)","Mohs surgery is preferred on the face for margin control with tissue preservation","Chronic UV exposure is the common driver of both"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Basal Cell / Squamous Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482439/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Skin Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Skin Nonmelanocytic Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-bone-tumors","name":"Bone Tumors (Osteosarcoma, Ewing, Chondrosarcoma, Giant Cell)","sec":"path","present":["Primary bone tumors present with pain, a mass, or PATHOLOGIC FRACTURE; patient AGE and tumor LOCATION within the bone are the most useful clinical clues","Metastatic carcinoma (breast, prostate, lung) is actually the most common malignancy involving bone overall","The classic primary tumors each have a signature age, site, and radiographic pattern"],"dx":["OSTEOSARCOMA (most common primary bone malignancy): adolescents, the METAPHYSIS around the KNEE (distal femur/proximal tibia), with malignant OSTEOID production and a CODMAN triangle/sunburst periosteal reaction; associated with Rb/TP53 (Li-Fraumeni)","EWING SARCOMA: children/adolescents, the DIAPHYSIS, SMALL ROUND BLUE cells, 'ONION-SKIN' periosteal reaction, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1, and CD99; CHONDROSARCOMA: older adults, cartilage-producing; GIANT CELL TUMOR: young adults, the EPIPHYSIS of long bones, 'soap-bubble' lysis, osteoclast-like giant cells (RANKL), locally aggressive but usually benign","Osteochondroma is the most common benign bone tumor"],"tx":["Osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma are treated with chemotherapy plus surgical resection; chondrosarcoma is primarily surgical; giant cell tumor is treated surgically (with denosumab/RANKL options)","Age, location, and matrix (osteoid vs cartilage) plus characteristic genetics establish the diagnosis","Distinguishing primary tumors from far-more-common metastases is a key first step"],"pearls":["Osteosarcoma: adolescents, metaphysis around the knee, osteoid production, Codman triangle/sunburst, Li-Fraumeni association","Ewing sarcoma: children, diaphysis, small round blue cells, onion-skin periosteum, t(11;22), CD99-positive","Giant cell tumor: young adults, epiphysis, soap-bubble lysis, osteoclast-like giant cells - locally aggressive but usually benign","Metastatic carcinoma (breast/prostate/lung) is the most common malignancy in bone overall"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bone Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559239/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Bone Cancer","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Bone Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-soft-tissue-sarcomas","name":"Soft Tissue Sarcomas — Overview","sec":"path","present":["Malignant tumors of mesenchymal (connective) tissue - presenting as an enlarging, often PAINLESS, DEEP soft-tissue mass (the extremities are the most common site)","They are rare and heterogeneous; risk factors include prior radiation and certain genetic syndromes","A deep or enlarging soft-tissue mass should raise suspicion for sarcoma rather than a benign lipoma/cyst"],"dx":["Subtypes include LIPOSARCOMA (MDM2 amplification), leiomyosarcoma, undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, SYNOVIAL sarcoma (young adults; t(X;18) SS18-SSX), RHABDOMYOSARCOMA (children; embryonal/alveolar; desmin/myogenin-positive), and GASTROINTESTINAL STROMAL TUMOR (GIST; KIT/CD117 or PDGFRA)","GRADE (differentiation, mitotic rate, and necrosis) is the main prognostic driver; many sarcomas carry characteristic TRANSLOCATIONS that are diagnostic; they metastasize HEMATOGENOUSLY (classically to the LUNG)","Core-needle/incisional biopsy (with careful planning to avoid compromising later resection) establishes the diagnosis"],"tx":["Treatment centers on wide surgical EXCISION with negative margins, often with radiation +/- chemotherapy; GIST responds to KIT-targeted therapy (imatinib)","Grade and margin status guide adjuvant therapy and prognosis","Biopsy technique matters - a poorly planned biopsy can compromise limb-sparing surgery"],"pearls":["A deep, enlarging soft-tissue mass is a sarcoma until proven otherwise - and sarcomas spread hematogenously to the lung","Characteristic translocations are diagnostic: synovial sarcoma t(X;18); and rhabdomyosarcoma (the most common pediatric soft-tissue sarcoma) is desmin/myogenin-positive","GIST is defined by KIT/CD117 (or PDGFRA) and responds to imatinib","Grade (differentiation, mitoses, necrosis) is the dominant prognostic factor"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Soft Tissue Sarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538330/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Soft Tissue Sarcoma","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Soft Tissue","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-cns-tumors","name":"CNS Tumors (Glioblastoma, Meningioma, Metastasis)","sec":"path","present":["Central nervous system tumors present with headache, SEIZURES, focal neurologic deficits, or signs of raised intracranial pressure; in adults they are often supratentorial, while pediatric primary tumors are frequently infratentorial","METASTASES are the most common intracranial tumors overall","Grading and, increasingly, MOLECULAR classification determine prognosis and therapy"],"dx":["GLIOBLASTOMA (the most common malignant primary brain tumor in adults): an astrocytic tumor with PSEUDOPALISADING NECROSIS and MICROVASCULAR PROLIFERATION, GFAP-positive, often crossing the corpus callosum ('BUTTERFLY' lesion), typically IDH-wildtype, with a poor prognosis","MENINGIOMA (the most common primary CNS tumor overall): an extra-axial, DURAL-BASED mass, more common in women, with whorls and PSAMMOMA bodies, EMA-positive, usually benign; METASTASES: multiple lesions at the GRAY-WHITE junction (lung, breast, melanoma, renal, GI)","Other tumors: schwannoma (S100), oligodendroglioma (1p/19q co-deletion, 'fried-egg' cells), and pediatric medulloblastoma/pilocytic astrocytoma; WHO grading integrates molecular markers (IDH, 1p/19q)"],"tx":["Treatment combines surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy (temozolomide for glioblastoma); meningiomas are often resected/observed; brain metastases are managed with surgery/radiosurgery and systemic therapy","Molecular markers (IDH status, 1p/19q co-deletion) now define glioma diagnosis and prognosis","Identifying a metastasis prompts a search for the primary"],"pearls":["Glioblastoma shows pseudopalisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation, is GFAP-positive, and may form a butterfly lesion across the corpus callosum - poor prognosis","Meningioma is dural-based and extra-axial with whorls and psammoma bodies (EMA-positive) and is usually benign; it is more common in women","Metastases are the most common intracranial tumors overall - multiple lesions at the gray-white junction","Molecular markers (IDH, 1p/19q co-deletion) now drive glioma classification"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Tumors / Glioblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470275/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: CNS Cancers","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: CNS Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-amyloidosis","name":"Amyloidosis","sec":"path","present":["Amyloidosis is the extracellular deposition of MISFOLDED proteins as insoluble fibrils, causing progressive organ dysfunction","Clinical presentations are protean: nephrotic-range proteinuria/renal failure, restrictive CARDIOMYOPATHY/heart failure, hepatomegaly, peripheral neuropathy, MACROGLOSSIA, and malabsorption","It may be systemic or localized, and primary or secondary to another disease"],"dx":["Amyloid deposits form a BETA-PLEATED SHEET structure and stain with CONGO RED, showing pathognomonic APPLE-GREEN BIREFRINGENCE under polarized light","TYPES: AL (primary - immunoglobulin LIGHT CHAINS from a plasma cell dyscrasia/myeloma), AA (secondary - serum amyloid A from CHRONIC INFLAMMATION such as rheumatoid arthritis or chronic infection), ATTR (TRANSTHYRETIN - hereditary or wild-type/senile cardiac), beta-2-microglobulin (long-term dialysis), and Abeta (Alzheimer disease)","Typing is confirmed by mass spectrometry (or immunohistochemistry); tissue is obtained by fat-pad aspirate or organ biopsy"],"tx":["Treatment targets the underlying process: chemotherapy/plasma-cell-directed therapy for AL, control of inflammation for AA, and transthyretin stabilizers/silencers for ATTR","Organ support (cardiac, renal) is important; accurate TYPING is essential because treatments differ completely","Recognizing restrictive cardiomyopathy or nephrotic syndrome with amyloid changes prompts the workup"],"pearls":["Congo red staining with apple-green birefringence under polarized light is the pathognomonic finding","AL amyloid = immunoglobulin light chains (plasma cell dyscrasia/myeloma); AA amyloid = serum amyloid A from chronic inflammation","ATTR (transthyretin) amyloid causes hereditary and senile (wild-type) cardiac amyloidosis","Amyloid can cause restrictive cardiomyopathy, nephrotic syndrome, and macroglossia - type it by mass spectrometry because treatment depends on the protein"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Amyloidosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470285/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Amyloidosis","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"Amyloidosis Foundation","u":"https://amyloidosis.org"}]},{"id":"path-cytopathology-fna","name":"Cytopathology & FNA Principles","sec":"path","present":["Cytopathology diagnoses disease from individual cells and small clusters - via EXFOLIATIVE specimens (Pap smears, effusions, urine) and FINE-NEEDLE ASPIRATION (FNA) of masses (thyroid, lymph node, breast, salivary gland)","It is minimally invasive and central to cancer SCREENING (the Pap smear) and rapid triage of masses","The trade-off is that cytology assesses cells but not tissue architecture"],"dx":["The PAP SMEAR samples the cervical transformation zone for cervical cancer screening, reported by the BETHESDA system (detecting dysplasia and HPV effect - koilocytes); THYROID FNA is reported by its own Bethesda system (categories I-VI) with associated malignancy risks that guide surgery","A key LIMITATION: FNA gives cytologic detail but cannot assess INVASION/architecture - so it cannot distinguish a follicular adenoma from a follicular carcinoma (which requires capsular/vascular invasion on excision)","CELL BLOCKS allow immunohistochemistry; rapid on-site evaluation (ROSE) confirms specimen adequacy; effusion cytology detects malignant cells"],"tx":["Cytology TRIAGES patients to surgery, core biopsy, or clinical management; the Pap smear interrupts cervical cancer by detecting treatable dysplasia","Standardized reporting (Bethesda systems) links cytologic categories to malignancy risk and next steps","Ancillary testing on cell blocks (IHC/molecular) extends what a small sample can diagnose"],"pearls":["The Pap smear (plus HPV testing) is one of medicine's great screening successes - Bethesda reporting standardizes cervical and thyroid cytology","FNA yields cytology (cells) but not architecture - it cannot distinguish follicular adenoma from carcinoma (invasion is needed)","Cell blocks enable immunohistochemistry on cytology specimens; ROSE checks adequacy at the bedside","Effusion cytology can reveal malignancy (e.g., adenocarcinoma cells in a pleural effusion)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Fine Needle Aspiration / Cytology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559273/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Cytopathology","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"College of American Pathologists (CAP)","u":"https://www.cap.org"}]},{"id":"path-autopsy-forensic","name":"Autopsy & Forensic Pathology Principles","sec":"path","present":["The AUTOPSY (postmortem examination) determines the cause and circumstances of death, serves quality assurance and education, and supports public-health surveillance","HOSPITAL/clinical autopsies (with family consent) differ from FORENSIC/medicolegal autopsies (performed by a medical examiner/coroner for unnatural, unexpected, or unattended deaths)","It remains a gold standard for discovering missed diagnoses"],"dx":["Distinguish CAUSE of death (the disease/injury initiating the lethal sequence), MECHANISM (the physiologic derangement, e.g., exsanguination), and MANNER of death - one of five categories: NATURAL, ACCIDENT, SUICIDE, HOMICIDE, or UNDETERMINED","POSTMORTEM changes help estimate the time of death: LIVOR mortis (dependent pooling), RIGOR mortis (stiffening), and ALGOR mortis (body cooling)","Examination combines external inspection, internal gross dissection, histology, and TOXICOLOGY; medical examiner jurisdiction covers violent, suspicious, or unattended deaths"],"tx":["Findings inform families (closure, hereditary risk), the legal system (forensic cases), public health, and hospital quality/education","Correctly assigning cause and manner of death is the core deliverable","The autopsy continues to reveal clinically missed diagnoses, supporting quality improvement"],"pearls":["Distinguish cause (the lethal disease/injury) from mechanism (the physiologic derangement) from manner of death (natural, accident, suicide, homicide, undetermined)","Postmortem changes - livor, rigor, and algor mortis - help estimate the time of death","Medical examiner/coroner jurisdiction covers violent, suspicious, unexpected, or unattended deaths","The autopsy remains a gold standard for quality assurance and for uncovering missed diagnoses"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Autopsy / Forensic Pathology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551617/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Forensic Pathology","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"CDC National Vital Statistics System","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss"}]},{"id":"path-head-neck-oral-scc","name":"Head & Neck / Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma & Premalignancy","sec":"path","present":["Squamous cell carcinoma is the dominant malignancy of the oral cavity, oropharynx, larynx, and pharynx (this content shares with the otolaryngology framing, pathology lens)","Two major risk pathways: TOBACCO plus ALCOHOL (synergistic, classic keratinizing SCC) and high-risk HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS (oropharyngeal/tonsil - younger patients, better prognosis)","PREMALIGNANT lesions (leukoplakia, erythroplakia) precede many oral cancers; presentations include a non-healing ulcer/mass, neck node, referred otalgia, and dysphagia"],"dx":["Histology shows keratinizing SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (keratin pearls, intercellular bridges; graded by differentiation) with assessment of PERINEURAL/lymphovascular invasion and nodal metastases","PREMALIGNANCY: LEUKOPLAKIA (a white plaque - hyperkeratosis/dysplasia) and ERYTHROPLAKIA (a red patch with HIGHER dysplasia/carcinoma-in-situ risk); biopsy is required to grade dysplasia","HPV/p16 testing of oropharyngeal SCC identifies the favorable-prognosis, separately staged HPV-associated disease; FIELD CANCERIZATION predisposes to second primaries"],"tx":["Premalignant lesions are excised/surveilled with tobacco-alcohol cessation; invasive cancer is treated with surgery + neck dissection and/or (chemo)radiation, with reconstruction for larger defects","HPV status changes staging, prognosis, and de-escalation eligibility (oropharynx)","Field cancerization mandates lifelong surveillance for second primaries"],"pearls":["Tobacco and alcohol act synergistically for head-and-neck SCC; erythroplakia carries a higher dysplasia/cancer risk than leukoplakia","HPV-positive (p16+) oropharyngeal SCC affects younger patients and has a markedly better prognosis - and is staged separately","Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges are the hallmark of keratinizing SCC; perineural invasion is an adverse feature","Field cancerization means these patients need lifelong surveillance for second primaries (shares content with the ENT entries)"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Head & Neck Cancers","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Oral Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563268/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Oral Cavity / Oropharynx","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-salivary-gland-tumors","name":"Salivary Gland Tumors","sec":"path","present":["Neoplasms of the major (parotid, submandibular, sublingual) and minor salivary glands (this content shares with the otolaryngology framing, pathology lens)","The PAROTID is the most common site and most parotid tumors are BENIGN, but the SMALLER the gland, the HIGHER the probability of malignancy","FACIAL NERVE involvement, pain, or rapid growth in a parotid mass suggests malignancy"],"dx":["BENIGN: PLEOMORPHIC ADENOMA (the most common salivary tumor - a biphasic mix of epithelial cells and myxoid/CHONDROID stroma; recurs if incompletely excised and can undergo malignant transformation) and WARTHIN tumor (older male smokers, can be bilateral; oncocytic epithelium over a LYMPHOID stroma)","MALIGNANT: MUCOEPIDERMOID CARCINOMA (the most common salivary malignancy - mucous + squamous/intermediate cells; MAML2 fusion) and ADENOID CYSTIC CARCINOMA (cribriform 'Swiss-cheese' pattern with prominent PERINEURAL invasion and characteristic LATE recurrence)","FNA and MRI characterize the mass; facial nerve status is assessed for parotid tumors"],"tx":["Pleomorphic adenoma is removed with a cuff of normal tissue (parotidectomy) - never enucleated (recurrence/transformation risk); malignant tumors get resection + neck dissection (high-grade/nodal) + adjuvant radiation, preserving the facial nerve when possible","Benign vs malignant distinction and facial nerve assessment guide the operation","Adenoid cystic carcinoma requires long-term follow-up for late recurrence/metastasis"],"pearls":["The smaller the salivary gland, the more likely a tumor is malignant; pleomorphic adenoma (chondromyxoid stroma) is the most common overall and must not be enucleated","Warthin tumor: older male smokers, often bilateral, with a lymphoid stroma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common salivary malignancy; adenoid cystic carcinoma spreads perineurally and recurs late","Facial nerve weakness in a parotid mass strongly suggests malignancy (shares content with the ENT entries)"],"refs":[{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Head & Neck Cancers","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Salivary Gland Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538279/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Salivary Glands","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-vasculitis","name":"Vasculitis — Classification & Pathology","sec":"path","present":["Inflammation of blood vessel walls causing ischemia, infarction, and aneurysm - presenting with systemic features (fever, weight loss, fatigue) plus organ-specific damage","Vasculitides are classified by the SIZE of vessel predominantly involved (large, medium, small)","Recognition matters because some forms (giant cell arteritis) threaten vision or organs if untreated"],"dx":["LARGE-vessel: GIANT CELL (temporal) ARTERITIS - elderly, headache, JAW CLAUDICATION, vision loss, high ESR, granulomatous inflammation with SKIP lesions (temporal artery biopsy; associated with polymyalgia rheumatica); Takayasu arteritis - young women, aortic arch, 'pulseless'","MEDIUM-vessel: POLYARTERITIS NODOSA (segmental transmural necrotizing inflammation, HBV-associated, SPARES the lungs, 'rosary-bead' microaneurysms, renal/GI involvement, ANCA-negative); Kawasaki disease (children, coronary artery aneurysms). SMALL-vessel ANCA-associated: GPA (c-ANCA/PR3; upper + lower respiratory + kidney; necrotizing granulomas), microscopic polyangiitis (p-ANCA/MPO; no granulomas), and EGPA (asthma, eosinophilia, p-ANCA)","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein) - children, palpable purpura, post-URI; FIBRINOID NECROSIS is a common histologic feature"],"tx":["Most vasculitides are treated with corticosteroids and immunosuppression; GIANT CELL ARTERITIS is a semi-emergency - start high-dose steroids promptly (do not wait for biopsy) to prevent blindness","ANCA serology and vessel size guide the specific diagnosis and therapy","Organ-threatening disease requires prompt aggressive immunosuppression"],"pearls":["Classify vasculitis by vessel size; giant cell arteritis (elderly, jaw claudication, vision loss, high ESR) needs urgent steroids - biopsy shows granulomas with skip lesions","Polyarteritis nodosa is HBV-associated, spares the lungs, causes renal/GI disease, and is ANCA-negative","GPA is c-ANCA/PR3 with necrotizing granulomas of the respiratory tract and kidney; EGPA features asthma and eosinophilia","Henoch-Schonlein (IgA vasculitis) causes palpable purpura in children after an upper respiratory infection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vasculitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545186/"},{"t":"American College of Rheumatology","u":"https://www.rheumatology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Vasculitis","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-autoimmune-ctd","name":"Autoimmune Connective Tissue Disease Pathology (SLE, Scleroderma, Sjögren)","sec":"path","present":["Systemic autoimmune connective tissue diseases cause multi-organ injury through autoantibodies and immune-complex deposition","SYSTEMIC LUPUS ERYTHEMATOSUS (SLE): young women with malar rash, photosensitivity, arthritis, serositis, nephritis, and cytopenias; SYSTEMIC SCLEROSIS (scleroderma): skin thickening, Raynaud phenomenon, and GI/pulmonary/renal fibrosis; SJOGREN syndrome: dry eyes and mouth (sicca)","These diseases overlap and are defined by characteristic autoantibody and tissue patterns"],"dx":["SLE: ANA is sensitive (screening), while anti-dsDNA and anti-SMITH are specific; immune-complex deposition drives LUPUS NEPHRITIS (classes I-VI; 'WIRE-LOOP' lesions and 'full-house' immunofluorescence) and Libman-Sacks (non-bacterial) endocarditis","SYSTEMIC SCLEROSIS: anti-SCL-70 (anti-topoisomerase) in diffuse disease and ANTI-CENTROMERE in limited disease (CREST); histology shows dermal/organ COLLAGEN deposition and an 'onion-skin' renal vascular change","SJOGREN: anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB with lymphocytic infiltration of salivary/lacrimal glands - and an increased risk of MALT LYMPHOMA"],"tx":["Management is immunosuppression tailored to organ involvement (e.g., lupus nephritis) with disease-specific monitoring","Autoantibody profiles both diagnose and predict organ patterns/prognosis","Recognizing the increased malignancy risk (Sjogren -> MALT lymphoma) is an important surveillance point"],"pearls":["SLE: ANA screens (sensitive); anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific; lupus nephritis (classes I-VI, wire-loop lesions, full-house IF) drives morbidity; Libman-Sacks endocarditis is classic","Scleroderma: anti-Scl-70 (diffuse) versus anti-centromere (limited/CREST); diffuse collagen deposition and onion-skin renal vessels","Sjogren: anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB, sicca symptoms, and a heightened MALT lymphoma risk","These are immune-complex/autoantibody-mediated multisystem diseases with overlapping features"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: SLE / Connective Tissue Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535405/"},{"t":"American College of Rheumatology","u":"https://www.rheumatology.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Autoimmune / Kidney (Lupus)","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-neuroendocrine-tumors","name":"Neuroendocrine Tumors (Carcinoid, GEP-NETs)","sec":"path","present":["Neuroendocrine tumors arise from neuroendocrine cells throughout the body - most commonly in the gastrointestinal tract and pancreas (GEP-NETs) and the lung","Many are non-functional; FUNCTIONAL tumors secrete hormones (insulinoma, gastrinoma/Zollinger-Ellison, glucagonoma, VIPoma)","CARCINOID SYNDROME (flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right-sided heart disease) occurs when a midgut carcinoid metastasizes to the LIVER and releases serotonin into the systemic circulation"],"dx":["Histology shows uniform cells with 'SALT-AND-PEPPER' chromatin in organoid/nested/trabecular patterns; CHROMOGRANIN and SYNAPTOPHYSIN are positive","GRADE is determined by the Ki-67 proliferation index and mitotic rate (well-differentiated NET G1-G3 vs poorly differentiated neuroendocrine CARCINOMA); serotonin/urinary 5-HIAA supports carcinoid","Common GI sites include the small bowel, appendix, and rectum; MEN1 is an important hereditary association (pancreatic NETs)"],"tx":["Localized tumors are resected; somatostatin analogs (octreotide) control symptoms and growth, with peptide receptor radionuclide therapy and other systemic options for advanced disease","Grade (Ki-67) drives prognosis and treatment intensity","Carcinoid syndrome implies hepatic metastases (the liver otherwise clears serotonin)"],"pearls":["Chromogranin and synaptophysin positivity with 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin define neuroendocrine tumors; Ki-67 grades them","Carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhea, wheezing) requires liver metastases - and is worked up with urinary 5-HIAA","Functional pancreatic NETs include insulinoma (hypoglycemia) and gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison); MEN1 is a key association","Well-differentiated NET and poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinoma are very different in behavior and treatment"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Neuroendocrine Tumors / Carcinoid","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448101/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Neuroendocrine Tumors","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Neuroendocrine Tumors","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-mesothelioma-asbestos","name":"Mesothelioma & Asbestos-Related Disease","sec":"path","present":["Malignant mesothelioma is an aggressive tumor of the serosal (mesothelial) lining - most often PLEURAL - strongly linked to ASBESTOS exposure with a LONG latency (typically 20-40 years)","Presents with chest pain, dyspnea, and a (often bloody) PLEURAL EFFUSION, with tumor encasing the lung","Asbestos also causes ASBESTOSIS (interstitial fibrosis), benign PLEURAL PLAQUES, and - synergistically with smoking - lung carcinoma"],"dx":["Mesothelioma has epithelioid, sarcomatoid, and biphasic patterns; the key task is distinguishing it from metastatic ADENOCARCINOMA by IHC - mesothelioma is POSITIVE for CALRETININ, WT1, D2-40, and cytokeratin 5/6, whereas adenocarcinoma is positive for markers like TTF-1, claudin-4, and BerEP4","ASBESTOS (ferruginous) BODIES may be identified in lung tissue; benign pleural plaques (often calcified) are markers of exposure, not malignancy","Pleural fluid cytology and biopsy establish the diagnosis"],"tx":["Malignant mesothelioma has a poor prognosis and is managed with multimodal therapy (surgery, chemotherapy, radiation) in selected patients","Correct IHC distinction from adenocarcinoma is essential (very different treatment and prognosis)","Asbestos-related benign disease (plaques) needs recognition without over-treatment"],"pearls":["Mesothelioma is asbestos-related with a long latency and is confirmed by calretinin/WT1/D2-40/CK5-6 positivity (adenocarcinoma is TTF-1/BerEP4-positive)","Pleural plaques are benign markers of asbestos exposure - not mesothelioma","Asbestos plus smoking act synergistically to raise LUNG cancer risk; notably, smoking does NOT increase mesothelioma risk","Ferruginous (asbestos) bodies in tissue indicate significant asbestos exposure"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mesothelioma / Asbestos","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534557/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Mesothelioma","u":"https://www.nccn.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Mesothelioma","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"path-placental-perinatal","name":"Placental & Perinatal Pathology","sec":"path","present":["Placental examination explains pregnancy complications and adverse fetal/neonatal outcomes and informs recurrence risk - a core component of stillbirth, prematurity, and growth-restriction workups","Perinatal pathology addresses the fetus/neonate around the time of birth (stillbirth, congenital anomalies, prematurity)","The placenta is often the key to understanding an adverse outcome"],"dx":["ACUTE CHORIOAMNIONITIS: ascending infection with a MATERNAL inflammatory response (neutrophils in the membranes/chorionic plate) and a FETAL response (funisitis/chorionic vessel inflammation) - associated with preterm labor; VILLITIS reflects infection/immune injury","MATERNAL VASCULAR MALPERFUSION (infarcts, accelerated villous maturation) underlies preeclampsia and fetal GROWTH RESTRICTION; ABRUPTION shows a retroplacental hematoma; abnormal implantation is the PLACENTA ACCRETA spectrum (accreta/increta/percreta - hemorrhage risk)","Umbilical cord findings (velamentous insertion, a SINGLE umbilical artery - associated with anomalies) and monochorionic twin complications (twin-twin transfusion) are assessed; meconium and TORCH infections are noted"],"tx":["Findings guide neonatal care, maternal follow-up, and counseling on recurrence risk in future pregnancies","The placental diagnosis often provides the explanation in a stillbirth or growth-restriction evaluation","Recognizing accreta spectrum informs delivery planning (hemorrhage risk)"],"pearls":["Acute chorioamnionitis is an ascending infection - neutrophils are the maternal response, funisitis/chorionic vessel inflammation the fetal response","Maternal vascular malperfusion (infarcts, accelerated maturation) underlies preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction","Placenta accreta spectrum (abnormal invasion) carries a major postpartum hemorrhage risk","A single umbilical artery is associated with congenital anomalies - and placental exam is central to any stillbirth workup"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chorioamnionitis / Placental Pathology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559163/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Placenta","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"CDC Reproductive Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/reproductivehealth"}]},{"id":"path-lymphadenopathy","name":"Reactive vs Neoplastic Lymphadenopathy & Infectious Lymphadenitis","sec":"path","present":["Enlarged lymph nodes are extremely common - the central task is distinguishing benign REACTIVE/infectious lymphadenopathy from NEOPLASTIC disease (lymphoma or metastatic carcinoma)","Worrisome features: persistent, hard/fixed, or supraclavicular nodes, older age, and B symptoms","Node architecture on biopsy is the key discriminator"],"dx":["REACTIVE hyperplasia PRESERVES nodal architecture with a POLYMORPHOUS population: FOLLICULAR hyperplasia (reactive germinal centers with TINGIBLE-BODY macrophages, BCL2-NEGATIVE germinal centers) - versus FOLLICULAR LYMPHOMA (architectural EFFACEMENT, monomorphous, BCL2-POSITIVE); paracortical hyperplasia (viral) and sinus histiocytosis are other reactive patterns","INFECTIOUS lymphadenitis: granulomatous (TB, sarcoid) and necrotizing (cat-scratch - stellate microabscesses); NEOPLASTIC: lymphoma (effacement, clonality on flow cytometry) or METASTATIC carcinoma (which seeds the SUBCAPSULAR SINUS first)","EXCISIONAL biopsy is preferred when lymphoma is suspected (architecture is needed); flow cytometry assesses clonality"],"tx":["Treat infectious causes; biopsy persistent or suspicious nodes; manage lymphoma/metastatic disease per the specific diagnosis","Preserved architecture with a polymorphous population reassures; effacement with a monomorphous/monoclonal population signals malignancy","Choosing excisional over needle biopsy for suspected lymphoma preserves the architecture needed for diagnosis"],"pearls":["Preserved architecture + polymorphous cells = reactive; effaced architecture + monomorphous/monoclonal cells = lymphoma","Reactive follicular hyperplasia has BCL2-negative germinal centers with tingible-body macrophages; follicular lymphoma is BCL2-positive","Metastatic carcinoma first seeds the subcapsular sinus of the node","Persistent, hard, fixed, or supraclavicular nodes warrant biopsy - and excisional biopsy is preferred for suspected lymphoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lymphadenopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513250/"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Lymph Nodes","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"},{"t":"American Society of Hematology","u":"https://www.hematology.org"}]},{"id":"path-predictive-biomarkers","name":"Predictive Molecular Biomarkers (EGFR, ALK, BRAF, HER2, MSI/MMR)","sec":"path","present":["Predictive biomarkers are molecular alterations that forecast RESPONSE (or resistance) to specific targeted and immuno-therapies - the foundation of precision oncology","They are distinct from prognostic markers (which predict outcome regardless of therapy)","Testing is now standard of care across many solid tumors and drives drug selection"],"dx":["LUNG: EGFR mutations and ALK/ROS1 rearrangements predict response to the corresponding tyrosine kinase inhibitors; BRAF V600E (MELANOMA, thyroid, colorectal) predicts BRAF/MEK inhibitor response; KRAS mutation predicts RESISTANCE to anti-EGFR therapy in colorectal cancer (KRAS G12C is now targetable)","HER2 (ERBB2) amplification (breast, gastric) predicts anti-HER2 therapy response; MSI-HIGH/mismatch-repair DEFICIENCY (Lynch syndrome) and PD-L1 expression predict immunotherapy response; BRCA1/2 predicts PARP-inhibitor benefit; NTRK fusions are tumor-agnostic targets","Methods span IHC, FISH, PCR, and next-generation SEQUENCING (NGS); many are FDA-approved companion diagnostics"],"tx":["Biomarker results directly determine eligibility for targeted agents and immunotherapy - and often the first-line regimen","Comprehensive NGS panels increasingly replace single-gene testing in advanced cancer","Accurate testing (and interpretation) is essential to matching patients to effective therapy"],"pearls":["Lung: EGFR/ALK/ROS1 drive TKI selection; melanoma/thyroid/colorectal BRAF V600E enables BRAF/MEK inhibitors","Colorectal cancer must be KRAS/NRAS wild-type for anti-EGFR therapy to work; HER2 amplification (breast/gastric) enables anti-HER2 therapy","MSI-high/mismatch-repair deficiency flags Lynch syndrome AND predicts immunotherapy response; PD-L1 also guides immunotherapy","NTRK fusions are a tumor-agnostic target - testing is increasingly done with NGS panels/companion diagnostics"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Molecular / Predictive Biomarkers","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576418/"},{"t":"College of American Pathologists (CAP)","u":"https://www.cap.org"},{"t":"NCCN Biomarkers Compendium","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"path-frozen-section-intraop","name":"Frozen Section & Intraoperative Consultation Principles","sec":"path","present":["The FROZEN SECTION provides a rapid intraoperative diagnosis (typically ~15-20 minutes) to guide the surgeon in real time while the patient is still in the operating room","It answers focused questions - not a substitute for the definitive permanent-section diagnosis","Intraoperative consultation combines frozen sections with gross examination and touch-prep cytology"],"dx":["Common uses: MARGIN assessment (is tumor present at the resection edge?), confirming that DIAGNOSTIC/lesional tissue is present (e.g., adequate for permanent workup), IDENTIFYING tissue (parathyroid vs thyroid vs lymph node), and assessing SPREAD (lymph nodes, peritoneal implants)","LIMITATIONS: freezing ARTIFACT, sampling error, and lower resolution than permanent sections - frozen section is poorly suited to fatty/calcified tissue and to diagnoses that require architecture (e.g., FOLLICULAR thyroid lesions, lymphoma), which should be DEFERRED to permanent sections","Touch-prep/scrape cytology is a useful adjunct at the time of frozen section"],"tx":["Results guide the extent of resection, the need for further surgery, and node dissection decisions during the same operation","Clear communication with the surgeon and appropriate DEFERRAL of ambiguous cases to permanent sections are essential to avoid intraoperative errors","The permanent section remains the definitive diagnosis"],"pearls":["Frozen section (~15-20 min) guides surgery in real time - for margins, tissue identification, and node/spread assessment","It has real limitations (freezing artifact, sampling) and is poorly suited to fat, calcified tissue, follicular thyroid lesions, and lymphoma - defer these to permanent sections","Touch-prep cytology is a helpful adjunct during intraoperative consultation","When in doubt, defer to permanent sections and communicate clearly with the surgeon"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Frozen Section / Intraoperative Consultation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574531/"},{"t":"College of American Pathologists (CAP)","u":"https://www.cap.org"},{"t":"Pathology Outlines: Frozen Section","u":"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-resuscitation","name":"Neonatal Resuscitation & Delivery-Room Care (APGAR)","sec":"peds","present":["At birth, most newborns need only warming, drying, and stimulation to transition; a minority need active support, and a smaller number need positive-pressure ventilation or more","The APGAR score (Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration; each 0-2) is assigned at 1 and 5 minutes to describe status and response - but it does NOT guide resuscitation (act, do not wait for a score)","Neonatal collapse is usually respiratory in origin"],"dx":["Assess HEART RATE (the single most important indicator), respiratory effort, and tone in the first moments; the Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP) algorithm structures the response","A term, vigorous, breathing infant with good tone stays with the mother (delayed cord clamping); apnea/gasping or heart rate <100 signals the need for intervention","Bradycardia in the newborn almost always reflects inadequate ventilation, not primary cardiac disease"],"tx":["Initial steps: warm, dry, stimulate, position, and clear the airway; if apneic/gasping or HR <100 -> effective POSITIVE-PRESSURE VENTILATION (the key intervention)","If HR <60 despite effective ventilation -> add CHEST COMPRESSIONS and epinephrine; ventilation quality is corrected first (the MR SOPA steps)","Delayed cord clamping is standard for vigorous infants"],"pearls":["Neonatal bradycardia is almost always RESPIRATORY - effective positive-pressure ventilation is the key first step, not chest compressions","Heart rate is the most important indicator guiding resuscitation","APGAR describes status and prognosis but does NOT direct resuscitation - act on assessment, don't wait for the score","Only add compressions/epinephrine when HR stays <60 despite effective ventilation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Neonatal Resuscitation Program","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Resuscitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568756/"}]},{"id":"peds-prematurity","name":"Prematurity & Its Complications","sec":"peds","present":["Preterm birth (<37 weeks) leaves immature organ systems vulnerable - the lower the gestational age, the higher the risk of complications","Complications span the lungs, brain, gut, eyes, and circulation and drive neonatal ICU care","Antenatal corticosteroids given before anticipated preterm birth reduce several complications"],"dx":["RESPIRATORY DISTRESS SYNDROME (surfactant deficiency - diffuse 'ground-glass'/reticulogranular lungs with air bronchograms); BRONCHOPULMONARY DYSPLASIA (chronic lung disease - oxygen dependence at 36 weeks corrected)","INTRAVENTRICULAR HEMORRHAGE (germinal matrix bleed, first days of life, graded I-IV - screened by cranial ultrasound); NECROTIZING ENTEROCOLITIS (feeding intolerance, bloody stools, PNEUMATOSIS INTESTINALIS on imaging); RETINOPATHY OF PREMATURITY (abnormal retinal vascularization - requires screening to prevent blindness)","Apnea of prematurity and patent ductus arteriosus are common"],"tx":["Antenatal steroids and postnatal SURFACTANT for RDS; respiratory support (CPAP/ventilation); NEC is managed with bowel rest/antibiotics and surgery for perforation","ROP screening with laser/anti-VEGF as needed; developmental follow-up","Preventing and monitoring for each complication is the core of neonatal care"],"pearls":["RDS is surfactant deficiency (ground-glass lungs, air bronchograms) - prevented by antenatal steroids and treated with surfactant","NEC presents with feeding intolerance and bloody stools; pneumatosis intestinalis is the radiographic hallmark (surgery if perforation)","Intraventricular hemorrhage arises from the germinal matrix in the first days of life","Retinopathy of prematurity requires screening - it is a preventable cause of blindness; BPD = oxygen need at 36 weeks"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Preterm Infant Care","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prematurity / Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568728/"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-jaundice","name":"Neonatal Jaundice & Hyperbilirubinemia","sec":"peds","present":["Jaundice (visible when bilirubin exceeds ~5 mg/dL) is extremely common in newborns; most is PHYSIOLOGIC unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia peaking around days 3-5","The feared consequence of very high UNCONJUGATED bilirubin is KERNICTERUS (bilirubin encephalopathy - basal ganglia injury)","Distinguishing benign physiologic jaundice from pathologic causes is the central task"],"dx":["RED FLAGS for pathologic jaundice: onset in the FIRST 24 HOURS (always pathologic - hemolysis from ABO/Rh incompatibility or G6PD deficiency), a rapid rise or very high level, and CONJUGATED (direct) hyperbilirubinemia (always pathologic)","Workup: total and direct bilirubin, blood type/Coombs, CBC/smear, and G6PD; plot on the Bhutani nomogram; unconjugated causes include physiologic, breastfeeding/breast-milk jaundice, hemolysis, and Gilbert/Crigler-Najjar","CONJUGATED hyperbilirubinemia demands urgent evaluation for BILIARY ATRESIA (time-sensitive) and infection/metabolic disease"],"tx":["PHOTOTHERAPY converts bilirubin to excretable forms; exchange transfusion for severe/rapidly rising levels; ensure adequate feeding/hydration","Treat the underlying cause; biliary atresia requires timely surgery (Kasai portoenterostomy) - delays worsen outcomes","Use the nomogram and risk factors to time treatment"],"pearls":["Jaundice in the first 24 hours of life is ALWAYS pathologic - think hemolysis (ABO/Rh, G6PD)","Unconjugated bilirubin is neurotoxic and causes kernicterus (choreoathetoid cerebral palsy, hearing loss)","CONJUGATED (direct) hyperbilirubinemia is always pathologic - biliary atresia is time-sensitive (Kasai procedure)","Phototherapy treats unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia; use the Bhutani nomogram to guide treatment"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Hyperbilirubinemia Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Jaundice","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532930/"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-sepsis-torch","name":"Neonatal Sepsis & TORCH Infections","sec":"peds","present":["Neonatal sepsis presents NONSPECIFICALLY - temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy, respiratory distress, and apnea - so a low threshold for evaluation is essential","EARLY-onset sepsis (<72 hours; maternal/intrapartum source - GROUP B STREP, E. coli, Listeria) vs LATE-onset (nosocomial/community)","Congenital TORCH infections cause characteristic multi-organ findings"],"dx":["Sepsis workup: blood, urine, and CSF cultures with CBC/CRP; GROUP B STREP is the leading early-onset pathogen (intrapartum prophylaxis reduces it)","TORCH: Toxoplasma (diffuse intracranial calcifications, chorioretinitis), Other (syphilis, varicella, parvovirus, Zika), Rubella (cataracts, PDA, deafness), CYTOMEGALOVIRUS (the most common congenital infection - PERIVENTRICULAR calcifications, sensorineural hearing loss), and HSV (skin/eye/mouth, CNS, or disseminated)","Congenital infection clues: IUGR, hepatosplenomegaly, rash, jaundice, and chorioretinitis"],"tx":["Start empiric antibiotics PROMPTLY - AMPICILLIN + GENTAMICIN for early-onset sepsis; add ACYCLOVIR if HSV is a concern (ill neonate, vesicles, seizures, maternal history)","Intrapartum GBS prophylaxis prevents early-onset GBS disease","Treat specific congenital infections and provide supportive care/follow-up (hearing, development)"],"pearls":["Neonatal sepsis is nonspecific - keep a low threshold for a full workup and empiric ampicillin + gentamicin","Group B Strep is the leading early-onset pathogen (intrapartum prophylaxis prevents it); Listeria coverage is why ampicillin is included","CMV is the most common congenital infection (periventricular calcifications, SNHL); toxoplasmosis causes diffuse intracranial calcifications","Consider and cover HSV (acyclovir) in an ill, vesicular, or seizing neonate"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Neonatal Sepsis / GBS Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Sepsis / TORCH","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531478/"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-respiratory-distress","name":"Neonatal Respiratory Distress (RDS, TTN, Meconium Aspiration)","sec":"peds","present":["Respiratory distress soon after birth presents with tachypnea, GRUNTING, retractions, nasal flaring, and cyanosis - a common neonatal emergency with several causes","The main differentials are respiratory distress syndrome, transient tachypnea, and meconium aspiration (plus pneumonia, pneumothorax, and congenital anomalies)","Gestational age and delivery history point to the likely cause"],"dx":["RDS: PRETERM infants; surfactant deficiency with diffuse 'ground-glass'/reticulogranular opacities and air bronchograms; worsens then improves with surfactant","TRANSIENT TACHYPNEA OF THE NEWBORN (TTN): term/late-preterm, often after CESAREAN delivery; delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid ('wet lungs', fluid in fissures) that self-resolves within 24-72 hours; MECONIUM ASPIRATION: term/POST-TERM with meconium-stained fluid, patchy infiltrates/hyperinflation, and a risk of persistent pulmonary hypertension (PPHN)","Chest radiograph patterns help distinguish the causes"],"tx":["RDS: CPAP/surfactant (and antenatal steroids for prevention); TTN: supportive oxygen (self-limited)","Meconium aspiration: respiratory support (avoid routine tracheal suctioning of vigorous infants); manage PPHN","Supportive care and cause-specific management"],"pearls":["RDS = preterm + surfactant deficiency (ground-glass lungs, air bronchograms); prevent with antenatal steroids, treat with surfactant","TTN = term/cesarean, retained lung fluid, resolves in 1-3 days (supportive)","Meconium aspiration = term/post-term with a risk of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Antenatal corticosteroids are the key prevention for RDS"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Newborn Respiratory Care","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Respiratory Distress","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560549/"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-hypoglycemia-idm","name":"Neonatal Hypoglycemia & Infant of a Diabetic Mother","sec":"peds","present":["Neonatal hypoglycemia is common and often ASYMPTOMATIC, but can cause jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, apnea, and seizures - and neurologic injury if severe/persistent","At-risk groups: the INFANT OF A DIABETIC MOTHER (IDM), large- or small-for-gestational-age infants, preterm/late-preterm, and stressed/ill newborns","IDM results from maternal hyperglycemia driving fetal hyperinsulinism"],"dx":["Screen at-risk infants with glucose monitoring using age-based thresholds; symptoms plus a low glucose confirm the problem","IDM: fetal hyperinsulinism causes MACROSOMIA and, after birth (when the maternal glucose supply stops), HYPOGLYCEMIA - along with polycythemia, hypocalcemia, RDS (delayed surfactant maturation), and congenital anomalies (cardiac, caudal regression)","Persistent hypoglycemia prompts a workup for hyperinsulinism, inborn errors, and endocrine causes"],"tx":["Feed early; give IV DEXTROSE for symptomatic or significantly low glucose; treat the underlying cause","IDM infants need glucose monitoring and support until feeding is established","Persistent/refractory hypoglycemia warrants an endocrine/metabolic evaluation"],"pearls":["The infant of a diabetic mother has fetal hyperinsulinism -> macrosomia plus neonatal hypoglycemia once the maternal glucose supply stops","IDM also brings polycythemia, hypocalcemia, RDS (delayed surfactant), and cardiac anomalies/caudal regression","Feed early and give IV dextrose for symptomatic/low glucose; screen at-risk infants","Persistent hypoglycemia needs a workup (hyperinsulinism, inborn errors of metabolism)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Neonatal Hypoglycemia Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Hypoglycemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537105/"}]},{"id":"peds-newborn-screening-iem","name":"Newborn Screening & Inborn Errors of Metabolism","sec":"peds","present":["Universal NEWBORN SCREENING (a heel-stick panel) detects treatable disorders BEFORE symptoms develop, preventing death and disability","INBORN ERRORS OF METABOLISM (IEM) often present in the newborn/infant with lethargy, poor feeding, vomiting, encephalopathy, and metabolic derangements - classically after feeding/protein or during intercurrent illness","Early recognition is life-saving and disability-preventing"],"dx":["Newborn screening covers PKU, congenital hypothyroidism, GALACTOSEMIA, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, fatty-acid oxidation defects (MCAD), hemoglobinopathies (sickle cell), cystic fibrosis, and SCID, among others","IEM categories: aminoacidopathies (PKU), UREA CYCLE defects (HYPERAMMONEMIA without acidosis), ORGANIC ACIDEMIAS (anion-gap metabolic acidosis), fatty-acid oxidation defects (HYPOKETOTIC hypoglycemia - MCAD), and galactosemia (E. coli sepsis, cataracts)","Acute workup of a decompensating neonate: AMMONIA, glucose, blood gas, lactate, and ketones"],"tx":["Disorder-specific management (dietary restriction - e.g., phenylalanine in PKU, galactose in galactosemia - and cofactors); acute crises: stop feeds, give IV dextrose (D10), and treat hyperammonemia urgently","Early detection via screening prevents irreversible injury","Metabolic/genetics specialist involvement is essential"],"pearls":["Newborn screening detects treatable disorders before symptoms (PKU, congenital hypothyroidism, galactosemia, CAH, MCAD, sickle cell, CF, SCID)","Suspect an IEM in a decompensating neonate with lethargy/vomiting/acidosis - check ammonia, glucose, and a blood gas","Galactosemia predisposes to E. coli sepsis and cataracts; PKU requires phenylalanine restriction; MCAD requires avoiding fasting","Hyperammonemia without acidosis points to a urea cycle defect; anion-gap acidosis to an organic acidemia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / ACMG Newborn Screening","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Inborn Errors of Metabolism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459183/"}]},{"id":"peds-developmental-milestones","name":"Developmental Milestones & Developmental Delay","sec":"peds","present":["Development spans GROSS MOTOR, FINE MOTOR, LANGUAGE, and SOCIAL domains; surveillance at well-child visits tracks progress","Developmental DELAY (failing to reach milestones) and especially REGRESSION (loss of skills) are red flags","Early identification enables early intervention"],"dx":["Key milestones: social smile ~2 months, rolls ~4-6 months, sits ~6 months, stranger anxiety ~9 months, pincer grasp/pulls to stand ~9-12 months, first words/walks ~12-15 months, and 2-word phrases ~2 years","RED FLAGS: no social smile by 3 months, no babble/gesture by 12 months, no words by 16 months, and ANY loss of previously acquired skills (regression - always abnormal)","Standardized screening (ASQ; M-CHAT for autism at 18 and 24 months); global developmental delay affects multiple domains"],"tx":["Refer promptly to EARLY INTERVENTION services; evaluate delay with hearing and vision testing first, then targeted (genetic/metabolic) workup","Early therapy (speech, occupational, physical) improves outcomes","Ongoing developmental surveillance and family support"],"pearls":["Loss of previously acquired skills (REGRESSION) is always abnormal and warrants evaluation","No words by 16 months or no babble/gesture by 12 months - check hearing first, then evaluate further","Screen for autism with the M-CHAT at 18 and 24 months","Early intervention improves developmental outcomes - refer without delay"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Developmental Milestones","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/actearly"},{"t":"AAP Developmental Surveillance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-failure-to-thrive","name":"Failure to Thrive & Growth Assessment","sec":"peds","present":["Failure to thrive (FTT) is inadequate weight gain/growth for age - typically weight dropping across growth-chart percentiles","It reflects an imbalance of caloric intake, absorption, and expenditure; WEIGHT is affected before length and head circumference","Most cases are non-organic (inadequate intake), often related to feeding or psychosocial factors"],"dx":["Plot serial WEIGHT, LENGTH, and HEAD CIRCUMFERENCE on appropriate growth charts; the pattern is informative - weight falls first (caloric), then length, with head circumference relatively PRESERVED unless the problem is severe or congenital","Categorize as inadequate INTAKE (most common), inadequate absorption/increased losses, or increased metabolic demand; a careful feeding and psychosocial history plus exam usually guides the (limited) workup","Avoid shotgun laboratory testing - history and exam target it"],"tx":["NUTRITIONAL rehabilitation with catch-up calories; address feeding technique and psychosocial contributors; treat any organic cause","A multidisciplinary approach (nutrition, social work, feeding therapy) helps","Monitor catch-up growth over time"],"pearls":["Most failure to thrive is inadequate caloric intake (often feeding/psychosocial), not organic disease","Weight drops before length, and head circumference is relatively preserved except in severe or congenital cases","Use growth charts and a thorough feeding/social history before extensive labs","Be alert to neglect as a contributor"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Failure to Thrive Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Failure to Thrive","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459287/"}]},{"id":"peds-well-child-care","name":"Well-Child Care & Anticipatory Guidance","sec":"peds","present":["Well-child visits (frequent in infancy, then periodic through adolescence) bundle growth/development monitoring, SCREENING, IMMUNIZATIONS, and ANTICIPATORY GUIDANCE","They are the backbone of pediatric preventive care and injury prevention","Anticipatory guidance is tailored to the child's developmental stage"],"dx":["Screening: newborn (metabolic, hearing, critical congenital heart disease pulse-ox, bilirubin), LEAD and ANEMIA, vision/hearing, autism (M-CHAT at 18/24 months), adolescent depression, and development; plot growth/BMI","Anticipatory guidance by age: SAFE SLEEP, car seats, nutrition, injury prevention, and screen-time","Track immunization status and catch up as needed"],"tx":["Deliver immunizations on schedule and provide SAFETY counseling: back-to-sleep (SIDS reduction), REAR-FACING car seats, water/pool safety, firearm safety, and poison prevention","Nutrition and dental guidance; developmental/behavioral support","Reinforce the medical home and continuity"],"pearls":["Well-child care combines growth/development surveillance, screening, immunizations, and anticipatory guidance","Safe-sleep practices (back to sleep, firm surface, no soft bedding) reduce SIDS; car seats are rear-facing in early childhood","Screen for lead and anemia, vision/hearing, autism (M-CHAT at 18/24 months), and adolescent depression","Anticipatory guidance is stage-specific injury prevention and health promotion"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Bright Futures / Periodicity","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"CDC Child Development / Safety","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/childdevelopment"}]},{"id":"peds-immunizations","name":"Immunizations & Vaccine-Preventable Diseases","sec":"peds","present":["Routine childhood immunization prevents once-common serious diseases and provides herd immunity","The schedule is age-structured, and understanding LIVE vs inactivated vaccines and true contraindications is essential","Vaccine hesitancy is addressed with clear, evidence-based counseling"],"dx":["Schedule highlights: HepB at birth; a 2/4/6-month series (DTaP, IPV, Hib, PCV, rotavirus); MMR and VARICELLA at ~12 months; HepA; annual influenza; and HPV, Tdap, and meningococcal (MenACWY) at 11-12 years","LIVE vaccines (MMR, varicella, rotavirus, intranasal influenza) require caution in significant IMMUNOCOMPROMISE and PREGNANCY; MMR/varicella are not given before 12 months (maternal antibody interference)","TRUE contraindication = anaphylaxis to a vaccine component; mild illness and most egg allergy are NOT contraindications"],"tx":["Follow the routine and CATCH-UP schedules; use every clinical encounter to update immunizations","Counsel families with accurate, empathetic, evidence-based information","Document and reconcile immunization records"],"pearls":["Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, rotavirus) are avoided in significant immunocompromise/pregnancy; MMR/varicella are given at/after 12 months","A true contraindication is anaphylaxis to a component - mild illness and most egg allergy are NOT contraindications","HPV, Tdap, and meningococcal vaccines are given at 11-12 years","Use every visit to catch up missed vaccines"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Immunization Schedules","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/schedules"},{"t":"AAP Immunization Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-autism-adhd","name":"Autism Spectrum Disorder & ADHD","sec":"peds","present":["AUTISM SPECTRUM DISORDER (ASD): persistent deficits in SOCIAL COMMUNICATION plus RESTRICTED/REPETITIVE behaviors, with onset in early childhood","ATTENTION-DEFICIT/HYPERACTIVITY DISORDER (ADHD): inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that is impairing, present for >=6 months, in MULTIPLE settings, with onset before age 12","Both are common neurodevelopmental disorders that benefit from early identification"],"dx":["ASD: screen with the M-CHAT at 18 and 24 months; diagnosis is clinical (DSM-5); early signs include lack of joint attention, no response to name, and language regression; evaluate hearing and consider fragile X/genetics","ADHD: apply DSM-5 criteria using information from >=2 settings (parent AND teacher rating scales, e.g., Vanderbilt); exclude mimics (hearing/vision problems, sleep disorders, learning disability, anxiety)","Comorbidities are common in both"],"tx":["ASD: early BEHAVIORAL intervention (e.g., applied behavior analysis), speech and occupational therapy, and individualized educational support","ADHD: behavioral therapy (first-line in preschoolers), STIMULANTS (methylphenidate/amphetamine - effective) with school accommodations for older children","Multidisciplinary, family-centered management"],"pearls":["ASD = social-communication deficits + restricted/repetitive behaviors - screen with the M-CHAT at 18 and 24 months and check hearing","Early behavioral intervention improves autism outcomes; consider fragile X in the workup","ADHD requires impairing symptoms in >=2 settings before age 12 - use parent and teacher rating scales","Stimulants are effective for ADHD; behavioral therapy is first-line in preschool-aged children"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP ADHD / Autism Guidelines","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"CDC Autism (ASD) Resources","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/autism"}]},{"id":"peds-febrile-infant","name":"Febrile Infant / Fever Without a Source","sec":"peds","present":["Fever (>=38 C) in a young infant WITHOUT a localizing source raises concern for a SERIOUS BACTERIAL INFECTION (UTI, bacteremia, meningitis)","Because young infants cannot localize infection and have immature immunity, AGE drives the evaluation","Rapid, age-appropriate assessment prevents missed life-threatening infection"],"dx":["NEONATE (<28 days) with fever: FULL sepsis workup (blood, urine, and CSF cultures) + empiric antibiotics + ADMISSION (high SBI risk, cannot be reliably risk-stratified); consider HSV","29-60 days: risk-STRATIFY with inflammatory markers, urinalysis/culture, and +/- lumbar puncture per validated protocols; >60 days/toddlers: source-directed evaluation","UTI is the most common serious bacterial infection in this setting; obtain a proper urine specimen"],"tx":["Neonates: empiric AMPICILLIN + GENTAMICIN (add ACYCLOVIR if HSV is a concern) and admit; older infants: manage per risk stratification (observation vs empiric therapy)","Ensure appropriate cultures before antibiotics when feasible","Close follow-up and clear return precautions"],"pearls":["Fever in a neonate (<28 days) mandates a full sepsis workup (blood, urine, CSF) plus empiric antibiotics and admission","UTI is the most common serious bacterial infection in febrile young infants - get a proper (cath/clean-catch) urine","Consider HSV in an ill, vesicular, or seizing neonate and start acyclovir","Use validated risk-stratification for 29-60 day infants rather than treating all identically"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Febrile Infant Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fever Without a Source (Pediatric)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441947/"}]},{"id":"peds-bronchiolitis","name":"Bronchiolitis (RSV)","sec":"peds","present":["A lower-respiratory viral illness in children under 2 (RESPIRATORY SYNCYTIAL VIRUS is the most common cause), peaking in winter","Begins as a URI prodrome, then cough, WHEEZE, crackles, tachypnea, increased work of breathing, and feeding difficulty; young infants may have APNEA","Most cases are self-limited but some infants need support"],"dx":["Bronchiolitis is a CLINICAL diagnosis - routine viral testing and chest imaging are NOT needed","Assess work of breathing, hydration, and oxygenation; identify risk factors for severe disease (prematurity, young age, cardiopulmonary disease)","Distinguish from asthma/foreign body in older or atypical cases"],"tx":["SUPPORTIVE care is the mainstay: nasal suctioning, hydration, and oxygen as needed; BRONCHODILATORS, corticosteroids, and antibiotics are NOT routinely recommended (they do not help)","Prevention: RSV immunoprophylaxis (nirsevimab; palivizumab for high-risk) and hand hygiene","Admit for hypoxia, dehydration, apnea, or significant distress"],"pearls":["Bronchiolitis is a clinical diagnosis - manage with supportive care (suction, hydration, oxygen)","Bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics are NOT routinely helpful in bronchiolitis","RSV is the most common cause, and young infants can present with apnea","RSV immunoprophylaxis (nirsevimab) is now used for prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Bronchiolitis Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bronchiolitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441959/"}]},{"id":"peds-croup","name":"Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis)","sec":"peds","present":["A viral upper-airway illness (usually PARAINFLUENZA) in children ~6 months to 3 years, with subglottic inflammation","Classic features: a BARKING/seal-like cough, inspiratory STRIDOR, and hoarseness, often worse at NIGHT, with a low-grade fever","Most cases are mild and self-limited"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis; if imaging is obtained, the frontal neck radiograph may show a subglottic 'STEEPLE SIGN' (not required)","Assess severity by stridor: stridor AT REST indicates more severe disease","Distinguish from EPIGLOTTITIS (toxic, drooling, no barky cough) and foreign body"],"tx":["CORTICOSTEROIDS (dexamethasone) for essentially all severities; NEBULIZED EPINEPHRINE for moderate-severe disease/stridor at rest (observe afterward for rebound)","Supportive care (calm the child, humidified air); most children are managed at home","Admit/observe for stridor at rest and after epinephrine"],"pearls":["Barking cough + inspiratory stridor + hoarseness = croup (parainfluenza)","Give dexamethasone to essentially all croup; add nebulized epinephrine for stridor at rest (observe for rebound)","The steeple sign supports the diagnosis but is not required","Distinguish from epiglottitis (toxic, drooling, no barky cough) and foreign body aspiration"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Croup Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Croup","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431070/"}]},{"id":"peds-pneumonia","name":"Pediatric Community-Acquired Pneumonia","sec":"peds","present":["Pneumonia presents with fever, cough, TACHYPNEA (the most sensitive sign), increased work of breathing, and sometimes focal exam findings","Etiology varies by AGE, and viral causes predominate in young children","Severity ranges from outpatient illness to hypoxic respiratory failure"],"dx":["By age: neonates (GBS, E. coli), VIRAL most common in young children (RSV and others), and bacterial - STREPTOCOCCUS PNEUMONIAE (the most common typical bacterial cause), MYCOPLASMA (school-age 'walking pneumonia'), and Staphylococcus","Chest radiograph is NOT routine for uncomplicated outpatient pneumonia (reserve for uncertainty, severe, or complicated cases); tachypnea is the key clinical sign","Assess oxygenation and hydration"],"tx":["Viral pneumonia is supportive; typical bacterial CAP is treated with AMOXICILLIN (first-line outpatient); a MACROLIDE covers atypical/Mycoplasma disease","Admit for hypoxia, significant distress, dehydration, or young infants","Reassess if not improving (effusion/empyema)"],"pearls":["Tachypnea is the most sensitive sign of pneumonia in children","Amoxicillin is first-line for typical bacterial CAP (Streptococcus pneumoniae)","Mycoplasma ('walking pneumonia') affects school-age children - treat with a macrolide","Chest imaging is not routine for uncomplicated outpatient pneumonia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / PIDS Pediatric CAP Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Pneumonia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536940/"}]},{"id":"peds-otitis-pharyngitis","name":"Acute Otitis Media & Pharyngitis","sec":"peds","present":["Two of the most common pediatric infections. ACUTE OTITIS MEDIA (AOM): ear pain/tugging, fever, and irritability after a URI (shares content with the ENT framing)","PHARYNGITIS: sore throat that is usually VIRAL, with GROUP A STREPTOCOCCAL (GABHS) infection as the key bacterial cause to identify and treat","Distinguishing bacterial from viral disease guides antibiotic use"],"dx":["AOM: a BULGING, erythematous, immobile tympanic membrane on pneumatic otoscopy (an effusion alone is otitis media with effusion, not AOM)","GABHS pharyngitis: fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, and absence of cough (Centor/McIsaac) - CONFIRM with rapid antigen test/culture before antibiotics; treat strep to prevent rheumatic fever","Recognize infectious MONONUCLEOSIS (EBV) - avoid amoxicillin (causes a rash)"],"tx":["AOM: analgesia plus high-dose AMOXICILLIN (or watchful waiting in older children with mild disease)","GABHS pharyngitis: penicillin/amoxicillin (prevents acute rheumatic fever); viral pharyngitis is supportive","Antibiotic stewardship - test before treating pharyngitis"],"pearls":["AOM = a bulging, immobile tympanic membrane - treat with high-dose amoxicillin (or watchful waiting in older/mild cases)","Confirm strep pharyngitis with a test before antibiotics - treat it to prevent rheumatic fever","Avoid amoxicillin if mononucleosis is possible (it causes a rash)","Most sore throats are viral and need no antibiotics"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP AOM / Pharyngitis Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Otitis Media / Pharyngitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470332/"}]},{"id":"peds-uti","name":"Pediatric Urinary Tract Infection","sec":"peds","present":["UTI is a common and important pediatric infection - in INFANTS it presents NONSPECIFICALLY (fever without a source, poor feeding, irritability)","Older children have classic symptoms (dysuria, frequency, urgency, abdominal/flank pain)","It is a leading serious bacterial infection in febrile young children"],"dx":["Obtain urine properly - catheterization or clean-catch, NOT a bag specimen for diagnosis; urinalysis (leukocyte esterase, nitrites, pyuria) plus URINE CULTURE (the gold standard)","E. COLI is the most common pathogen; consider imaging - renal ULTRASOUND after a first febrile UTI, and VCUG for recurrent/atypical infections to evaluate VESICOURETERAL REFLUX","Fever and systemic signs suggest pyelonephritis"],"tx":["Empiric antibiotics tailored to local resistance, then narrowed by culture; ensure follow-up","Evaluate for anatomic abnormalities (vesicoureteral reflux) in selected children to prevent scarring","Adequate hydration and prompt treatment reduce renal scarring"],"pearls":["UTI is a leading cause of fever without a source in infants - obtain a proper (cath/clean-catch) urine culture, not a bag specimen","E. coli is the most common pathogen","Renal ultrasound after a first febrile UTI; VCUG for recurrent/atypical infection (vesicoureteral reflux)","Prompt treatment reduces the risk of renal scarring"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP UTI Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric UTI","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534915/"}]},{"id":"peds-meningitis-sepsis","name":"Pediatric Meningitis & Sepsis","sec":"peds","present":["Bacterial meningitis/sepsis is a pediatric emergency; NEONATES/INFANTS present nonspecifically (fever, lethargy, poor feeding, irritability, a bulging fontanelle, seizures)","Older children show fever, headache, NECK STIFFNESS, photophobia, and altered mental status; PETECHIAE/PURPURA with shock suggests MENINGOCOCCEMIA","Rapid recognition and treatment save lives and prevent disability"],"dx":["LUMBAR PUNCTURE analyzes CSF (BACTERIAL: high WBC/neutrophils, high protein, LOW glucose; VIRAL: lymphocytic, normal glucose); obtain blood cultures","Pathogens by age: NEONATES - GBS, E. coli, LISTERIA; older children - S. PNEUMONIAE and N. MENINGITIDIS","Do NOT delay antibiotics waiting for LP or CT"],"tx":["Start EMPIRIC antibiotics IMMEDIATELY (age-appropriate - e.g., ceftriaxone + vancomycin for older children; add AMPICILLIN for Listeria in neonates); consider dexamethasone (Hib)","Droplet precautions and PROPHYLAXIS for close contacts of meningococcal disease; ICU-level support for sepsis/shock","Aggressive resuscitation for septic shock"],"pearls":["Start empiric antibiotics IMMEDIATELY for suspected bacterial meningitis - do not delay for LP or CT","Neonatal pathogens are GBS/E. coli/Listeria (cover Listeria with ampicillin); older children get pneumococcus/meningococcus","Petechiae/purpura with shock = meningococcemia - an emergency requiring contact prophylaxis","CSF pattern (neutrophilic with low glucose vs lymphocytic with normal glucose) distinguishes bacterial from viral"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / Red Book: Meningitis","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Bacterial Meningitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470351/"}]},{"id":"peds-viral-exanthems","name":"Viral Exanthems","sec":"peds","present":["Fever with a RASH is a common pediatric presentation; several classic exanthems have recognizable patterns and implications","Distinguishing them guides isolation, treatment, and identification of at-risk contacts (pregnancy, immunocompromise)","Vaccination has reduced measles and varicella where uptake is high"],"dx":["MEASLES (rubeola): high fever with the '3 Cs' (Cough, Coryza, Conjunctivitis), KOPLIK spots, then a cephalocaudal maculopapular rash; VARICELLA (chickenpox): pruritic vesicles in DIFFERENT STAGES simultaneously ('dewdrop on a rose petal')","ROSEOLA (HHV-6): high fever for 3-5 days, then a rash appearing AS the fever breaks (well-appearing; febrile seizures); FIFTH DISEASE (parvovirus B19): 'SLAPPED-CHEEK' then lacy rash (aplastic crisis in sickle cell; hydrops risk in pregnancy)","HAND-FOOT-MOUTH (coxsackievirus): oral ulcers with vesicles on hands/feet; SCARLET FEVER (GABHS): sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, Pastia lines"],"tx":["Most are SUPPORTIVE; measles - vitamin A and isolation; scarlet fever - penicillin (it is a strep infection)","Counsel about contagious periods and at-risk contacts (parvovirus in pregnancy/sickle cell)","Prevention via MMR and varicella vaccination"],"pearls":["Measles = the 3 Cs (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) plus Koplik spots BEFORE the rash","Roseola = high fever THEN rash as the fever breaks (HHV-6; associated with febrile seizures)","Fifth disease = slapped-cheek (parvovirus B19) - dangerous in pregnancy and causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease","Varicella lesions appear in different stages at once; scarlet fever (strawberry tongue, sandpaper rash) is treated as strep"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Red Book: Exanthems","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"CDC Measles / Varicella","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/measles"}]},{"id":"peds-kawasaki","name":"Kawasaki Disease","sec":"peds","present":["A medium-vessel VASCULITIS of young children (typically <5 years) - the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries (shares content with the pathology vasculitis framing)","The critical danger is CORONARY ARTERY ANEURYSMS","Presents with prolonged high fever and characteristic mucocutaneous features"],"dx":["Classic criteria: FEVER for >=5 days PLUS >=4 of 5 features - remember 'CRASH': CONJUNCTIVITIS (bilateral, non-exudative), RASH (polymorphous), ADENOPATHY (cervical, often unilateral), STRAWBERRY tongue/mucosal changes (cracked lips), and HAND/FOOT changes (edema/erythema, later periungual desquamation)","Supportive labs: elevated ESR/CRP, thrombocytosis (later), and sterile pyuria; ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY assesses the coronary arteries","INCOMPLETE Kawasaki (fewer criteria) is common in infants - keep a high index of suspicion"],"tx":["IVIG plus ASPIRIN, ideally within 10 days of fever onset, reduces the risk of coronary aneurysms","Echocardiographic surveillance for coronary involvement","Kawasaki disease is one of the few pediatric indications for aspirin"],"pearls":["Kawasaki = fever >=5 days + CRASH (Conjunctivitis, Rash, Adenopathy, Strawberry tongue, Hands/feet) - the threat is coronary artery aneurysms","Treat with IVIG + aspirin within 10 days and obtain an echocardiogram","It is one of the few pediatric indications for aspirin","Consider incomplete Kawasaki in infants with prolonged unexplained fever"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA / AAP Kawasaki Disease Guideline","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Kawasaki Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537163/"}]},{"id":"peds-asthma","name":"Pediatric Asthma","sec":"peds","present":["A chronic inflammatory airway disease with reversible obstruction - the most common chronic disease of childhood","Presents with recurrent WHEEZE, cough (especially at night/with exercise), dyspnea, and chest tightness; triggers include viral infections, allergens, and exercise","Atopy and family history are common"],"dx":["Clinical pattern of recurrent reversible symptoms; SPIROMETRY (in older children) shows reversible obstruction; assess CONTROL and severity and classify (intermittent vs persistent)","During exacerbations, gauge severity by work of breathing, oxygenation, and air movement; the differential includes bronchiolitis (<2 years) and foreign body","A 'silent chest'/poor air movement signals severe obstruction"],"tx":["SHORT-ACTING BETA-AGONIST (albuterol) for quick relief; INHALED CORTICOSTEROIDS are the controller mainstay for persistent asthma, with step-up/step-down therapy","Exacerbations: SABA plus systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS and oxygen as needed; provide an action plan, trigger avoidance, and spacer/technique teaching","Reassess control and adjust therapy"],"pearls":["Inhaled corticosteroids are the controller mainstay for persistent asthma; albuterol is for acute relief","Treat exacerbations with SABA plus systemic steroids and oxygen; assess and step therapy by control","A silent chest or poor air movement is ominous (severe obstruction)","Consider a foreign body in a young child with sudden or focal wheeze"],"refs":[{"t":"NHLBI / NAEPP Asthma Guidelines","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Asthma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551631/"}]},{"id":"peds-cystic-fibrosis","name":"Cystic Fibrosis","sec":"peds","present":["An autosomal recessive disease caused by CFTR mutations (a defective chloride channel) affecting the lungs, pancreas, GI tract, and reproductive system","Manifestations: recurrent sinopulmonary infections with BRONCHIECTASIS, PANCREATIC INSUFFICIENCY (steatorrhea, failure to thrive, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency), MECONIUM ILEUS in the neonate, salty-tasting sweat, nasal polyps, and male infertility","It is often detected on newborn screening"],"dx":["Newborn screening (immunoreactive trypsinogen) prompts confirmatory SWEAT CHLORIDE testing (elevated is diagnostic) plus CFTR genetic testing","Chronic airway colonization (Staphylococcus, then PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA) drives progressive lung disease","Assess pancreatic, hepatic, and nutritional status"],"tx":["Airway clearance and inhaled therapies (dornase alfa, hypertonic saline), antibiotics for exacerbations, PANCREATIC ENZYME replacement plus fat-soluble (ADEK) vitamins, and aggressive nutrition","CFTR MODULATOR therapy (for eligible mutations) has transformed outcomes; care at a multidisciplinary CF center","Ongoing surveillance for complications (CF-related diabetes, liver disease)"],"pearls":["CF is autosomal recessive CFTR dysfunction -> recurrent pulmonary infections (Pseudomonas), pancreatic insufficiency, and meconium ileus","Diagnose with sweat chloride testing plus genetics (newborn screening flags it via immunoreactive trypsinogen)","Treat pancreatic insufficiency with enzymes and fat-soluble (ADEK) vitamins","CFTR modulators have transformed care for eligible patients; salty-tasting sweat is a classic clue"],"refs":[{"t":"Cystic Fibrosis Foundation","u":"https://www.cff.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cystic Fibrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493206/"}]},{"id":"peds-congenital-heart-disease","name":"Congenital Heart Disease (Cyanotic vs Acyanotic)","sec":"peds","present":["Congenital heart disease presents with a murmur, CYANOSIS, heart failure (poor feeding, tachypnea, diaphoresis, failure to thrive), or via pulse-oximetry screening","The timing of presentation and the presence of cyanosis narrow the differential","Some lesions are DUCTAL-DEPENDENT and decompensate as the ductus arteriosus closes"],"dx":["ACYANOTIC (left-to-right shunts): VSD (the most common CHD; holosystolic murmur), ASD (fixed split S2), PDA (continuous 'machinery' murmur), and COARCTATION (differential upper/lower extremity pulses and blood pressures)","CYANOTIC (right-to-left; the '5 Ts'): TETRALOGY OF FALLOT (the most common cyanotic lesion overall; 'boot-shaped' heart; 'tet spells'), TRANSPOSITION of the great arteries (the most common cyanotic lesion presenting in the NEWBORN), truncus arteriosus, TAPVR, and tricuspid atresia","Pulse-oximetry (CCHD) screening and echocardiography establish the diagnosis"],"tx":["PROSTAGLANDIN E1 keeps the ductus arteriosus open in ductal-dependent lesions (a life-saving temporizing measure); definitive surgical or catheter-based repair follows","Manage heart failure and 'tet spells' (knee-to-chest positioning, oxygen)","Coordinate with pediatric cardiology/cardiac surgery"],"pearls":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect overall; Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic lesion (boot-shaped heart, tet spells)","Transposition of the great arteries presents with cyanosis in the newborn","Keep the ductus open with prostaglandin E1 in ductal-dependent lesions","Coarctation shows differential upper/lower extremity pulses and blood pressures - use CCHD pulse-ox screening"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA / AAP Congenital Heart Disease","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Congenital Heart Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441893/"}]},{"id":"peds-gastroenteritis-dehydration","name":"Acute Gastroenteritis & Dehydration","sec":"peds","present":["Acute gastroenteritis (vomiting and diarrhea, most often VIRAL - rotavirus/norovirus) is very common in children","The main danger is DEHYDRATION, so hydration assessment drives management","Bacterial and parasitic causes are less common but important (bloody diarrhea)"],"dx":["Assess hydration CLINICALLY (mild/moderate/severe) using mucous membranes, capillary refill, tears, urine output, mental status, and skin turgor","Most disease is viral; BLOODY diarrhea suggests bacterial causes (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, Shiga-toxin E. coli - HUS risk); stool studies only for bloody, prolonged, or severe illness","Watch for HEMOLYTIC UREMIC SYNDROME after Shiga-toxin E. coli"],"tx":["ORAL REHYDRATION therapy is first-line for mild-moderate dehydration; IV fluids for severe dehydration or failed oral rehydration; continue/early refeeding","ONDANSETRON can reduce vomiting to aid oral rehydration; AVOID antibiotics in suspected Shiga-toxin E. coli (increases HUS risk) and avoid antidiarrheals in young children","Rotavirus vaccination prevents severe disease"],"pearls":["Oral rehydration therapy is first-line for mild-moderate dehydration; assess hydration clinically (cap refill, mucous membranes, urine output, mental status)","Most gastroenteritis is viral and needs no antibiotics","Avoid antibiotics in suspected Shiga-toxin E. coli (they increase the risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome)","Ondansetron helps vomiting; the rotavirus vaccine prevents severe disease"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / CDC Acute Gastroenteritis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Gastroenteritis / Dehydration","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499994/"}]},{"id":"peds-pyloric-stenosis","name":"Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis","sec":"peds","present":["Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle causing gastric outlet obstruction - classically in a firstborn MALE at 2-8 weeks of age","NON-BILIOUS PROJECTILE vomiting after feeds in a HUNGRY infant ('hungry vomiter'), with a palpable 'OLIVE' epigastric mass, visible peristalsis, and progressive dehydration/weight loss","The vomiting is non-bilious because the obstruction is proximal to the ampulla"],"dx":["The classic metabolic picture is a HYPOCHLOREMIC, HYPOKALEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (from vomiting gastric HCl)","ULTRASOUND is diagnostic (an elongated, thickened pylorus - 'target'/donut sign); the palpable olive confirms it clinically","Assess hydration and electrolytes"],"tx":["CORRECT the fluid, electrolyte, and ALKALOSIS derangements FIRST - the metabolic disturbance is the emergency, not the operation","Definitive treatment is PYLOROMYOTOMY (Ramstedt), which is NOT an emergency and is deferred until the infant is resuscitated","Resume feeds after surgery"],"pearls":["Non-bilious projectile vomiting in a hungry 3-6 week-old with an 'olive' mass is pyloric stenosis","The classic lab finding is a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis","Correct the electrolytes and alkalosis BEFORE surgery - pyloromyotomy is not emergent, but the metabolic derangement is","Ultrasound is diagnostic; firstborn males are classic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pyloric Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430850/"},{"t":"AAP Pediatric Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-intussusception","name":"Intussusception","sec":"peds","present":["Telescoping of one segment of bowel into another (usually ILEOCOLIC), most common between 6 months and 3 years","Classic triad: intermittent COLICKY abdominal pain (the child draws up the legs), a palpable SAUSAGE-shaped mass, and 'CURRANT-JELLY' stool (a late finding of blood and mucus); LETHARGY between episodes can be prominent","Most are idiopathic (post-viral lymphoid hyperplasia); a pathologic LEAD POINT (Meckel diverticulum, HSP, lymphoma) is more likely in older/atypical cases"],"dx":["ULTRASOUND is the diagnostic test of choice (a 'target'/donut sign)","Assess for a lead point in older children or recurrent cases","Lethargy may dominate and mislead toward a neurologic cause"],"tx":["An AIR (or contrast) ENEMA is both DIAGNOSTIC and THERAPEUTIC (hydrostatic/pneumatic reduction)","Surgery is reserved for failed enema reduction, perforation, or peritonitis","Fluid resuscitation and monitoring for recurrence"],"pearls":["Intermittent colicky pain + currant-jelly stool + a sausage-shaped mass = intussusception (ileocolic, 6 months-3 years)","Ultrasound shows the target sign; an air/contrast enema both diagnoses and reduces it","Lethargy can be the dominant feature and mislead the diagnosis","Look for a pathologic lead point (Meckel, HSP) in older or recurrent cases; surgery if the enema fails"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intussusception","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431078/"},{"t":"AAP Pediatric Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-constipation-hirschsprung","name":"Constipation & Hirschsprung Disease","sec":"peds","present":["FUNCTIONAL constipation is by far the most common - often triggered by a dietary change or toilet training, with painful stools, withholding behavior, and overflow ENCOPRESIS","HIRSCHSPRUNG DISEASE (congenital colonic AGANGLIONOSIS) presents in the newborn with FAILURE TO PASS MECONIUM in the first 48 hours, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting","Distinguishing the two prevents missing a congenital surgical cause"],"dx":["Functional constipation is a clinical diagnosis without red flags; HIRSCHSPRUNG RED FLAGS: delayed meconium passage, ribbon-like stools, failure to thrive, and onset at birth - with a tight empty rectum and explosive stool on withdrawal of the examining finger","Hirschsprung workup: contrast enema (a transition zone), anorectal manometry, and RECTAL BIOPSY showing ABSENT GANGLION cells (the gold standard)","Hirschsprung-associated ENTEROCOLITIS is a dangerous complication"],"tx":["Functional constipation: disimpaction then MAINTENANCE with osmotic laxatives (polyethylene glycol) plus behavioral and dietary measures","Hirschsprung disease: surgical pull-through procedure","Recognize and urgently treat Hirschsprung enterocolitis"],"pearls":["Functional constipation is far more common (withholding, painful stools, overflow encopresis) - treat with PEG plus behavioral measures","Failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours, then obstruction, suggests Hirschsprung disease","Rectal biopsy showing absent ganglion cells is the gold standard for Hirschsprung","Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis is dangerous - watch for it"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hirschsprung Disease / Constipation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526015/"},{"t":"AAP / NASPGHAN Constipation Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-ingestion-aspiration","name":"Foreign Body / Caustic Ingestion & Aspiration","sec":"peds","present":["Young children explore by mouth, leading to foreign-body ASPIRATION (choking, cough, wheeze, unilateral decreased breath sounds) and INGESTION (drooling, dysphagia, food refusal) - shares content with the ENT framing","Two ingestions are true EMERGENCIES: the BUTTON BATTERY and multiple high-powered MAGNETS","Caustic (alkali/acid) ingestions cause esophageal injury"],"dx":["A BUTTON BATTERY lodged in the esophagus causes rapid caustic/liquefactive injury (a 'double-ring' sign on X-ray) - an emergency; MULTIPLE MAGNETS can trap bowel between them and cause necrosis/perforation","CAUSTIC ingestion: ALKALI causes liquefactive (deeper) injury and ACID causes coagulative injury - endoscopy assesses esophageal damage; airway foreign bodies are often radiolucent (rigid bronchoscopy is diagnostic/therapeutic)","Coins are radiopaque and often benign if in the stomach"],"tx":["An esophageal BUTTON BATTERY requires IMMEDIATE removal; airway foreign bodies are removed by RIGID BRONCHOSCOPY","Caustic ingestion: do NOT induce vomiting or attempt neutralization; provide supportive care and endoscopy for injury","Asymptomatic coins in the stomach can often be observed; counsel on prevention"],"pearls":["An esophageal button battery is an emergency - it causes caustic injury within hours (double-ring sign) and must be removed immediately","Multiple magnets can cause bowel necrosis/perforation by trapping tissue between them","For caustic ingestions, do NOT induce vomiting or neutralize - use supportive care and endoscopy","A persistent unilateral wheeze suggests an airway foreign body (rigid bronchoscopy)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Foreign Body / Caustic Ingestion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459193/"},{"t":"AAP / Poison Control Resources","u":"https://www.poison.org"}]},{"id":"peds-t1dm-dka","name":"Type 1 Diabetes & Diabetic Ketoacidosis","sec":"peds","present":["Type 1 diabetes (autoimmune beta-cell destruction) commonly presents in childhood with POLYURIA, POLYDIPSIA, and WEIGHT LOSS - and frequently first presents in DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA)","DKA: vomiting, abdominal pain, KUSSMAUL breathing, 'fruity' breath, dehydration, and altered mental status","New-onset diabetes may be misattributed to gastroenteritis until DKA is recognized"],"dx":["DKA is defined by HYPERGLYCEMIA + KETOSIS + ANION-GAP metabolic ACIDOSIS; check glucose, ketones, blood gas, and electrolytes","Islet autoantibodies confirm type 1; note that TOTAL-BODY POTASSIUM is depleted even when the serum potassium is normal/high","Assess degree of dehydration and acidosis"],"tx":["DKA management: FLUIDS first, then an INSULIN infusion and careful POTASSIUM replacement, with close monitoring","The feared complication is CEREBRAL EDEMA (the leading cause of death in pediatric DKA) - avoid overly rapid correction of glucose/osmolality/fluids","Long-term: insulin therapy and comprehensive diabetes education"],"pearls":["New-onset type 1 diabetes often presents in DKA - polyuria/polydipsia/weight loss followed by vomiting, Kussmaul breathing, and fruity breath","DKA = hyperglycemia + ketosis + anion-gap acidosis; treat with fluids, insulin, and potassium (total-body potassium is depleted)","Cerebral edema is the feared, often fatal complication - avoid too-rapid correction","Do not mistake DKA vomiting/abdominal pain for gastroenteritis"],"refs":[{"t":"ISPAD / ADA Pediatric DKA Guidelines","u":"https://diabetesjournals.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric DKA","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551551/"}]},{"id":"peds-congenital-hypothyroidism","name":"Congenital Hypothyroidism & Thyroid Disorders","sec":"peds","present":["CONGENITAL HYPOTHYROIDISM is usually ASYMPTOMATIC at birth (maternal thyroid hormone crosses the placenta) - which is why NEWBORN SCREENING is critical to prevent irreversible intellectual disability","Late/untreated signs: prolonged jaundice, poor feeding, constipation, hypotonia, a large fontanelle, umbilical hernia, and macroglossia","ACQUIRED thyroid disease includes HASHIMOTO thyroiditis (the most common acquired cause, with a goiter) and Graves disease"],"dx":["NEWBORN SCREENING (TSH/T4) is the key test - thyroid DYSGENESIS is the most common congenital cause","Acquired disease: TSH/free T4, anti-TPO antibodies (Hashimoto), and TSI (Graves)","Confirm and identify the cause promptly"],"tx":["Congenital hypothyroidism: start LEVOTHYROXINE EARLY - treatment is time-critical to prevent permanent intellectual disability","Acquired hypothyroidism: levothyroxine; Graves disease: antithyroid therapy","Monitor growth and development"],"pearls":["Congenital hypothyroidism is detected by newborn screening and must be treated EARLY with levothyroxine - delay causes irreversible intellectual disability","It is usually asymptomatic at birth because maternal T4 crosses the placenta (thyroid dysgenesis is the most common cause)","Prolonged neonatal jaundice can be a clue to congenital hypothyroidism","Hashimoto thyroiditis (anti-TPO, goiter) is the most common acquired cause in children"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Congenital Hypothyroidism Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Congenital Hypothyroidism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558913/"}]},{"id":"peds-short-stature-puberty","name":"Short Stature & Disorders of Puberty","sec":"peds","present":["SHORT STATURE (height below the 3rd percentile or crossing downward) and abnormal puberty timing are common endocrine concerns","PRECOCIOUS puberty (before 8 in girls, 9 in boys) and DELAYED puberty (no signs by 13 in girls, 14 in boys)","Growth VELOCITY and bone age are the key evaluative tools"],"dx":["SHORT STATURE: FAMILIAL (normal velocity, normal bone age) and CONSTITUTIONAL DELAY ('late bloomer' - delayed bone age, normal velocity, family history) are normal variants; a LOW growth velocity is the red flag for pathology (growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, TURNER syndrome in girls, chronic disease, Cushing)","Evaluate with growth charts, growth velocity, and BONE AGE; check a karyotype in a short girl (Turner)","PRECOCIOUS puberty: central (GnRH-dependent) vs peripheral; DELAYED: constitutional (most common) vs hypogonadism"],"tx":["Constitutional delay: reassurance; growth hormone for GH deficiency/Turner; treat underlying causes","Central precocious puberty: GnRH agonist therapy","Monitor growth, puberty, and psychosocial impact"],"pearls":["A normal growth VELOCITY is reassuring - constitutional delay ('late bloomer') has a delayed bone age, normal velocity, and a family history","A LOW growth velocity is the red flag - think GH deficiency, hypothyroidism, or Turner syndrome (check a karyotype in short girls)","Bone age helps distinguish normal variants from pathology","Central precocious puberty is treated with a GnRH agonist"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / Pediatric Endocrine Society Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Short Stature / Puberty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563251/"}]},{"id":"peds-down-syndrome","name":"Down Syndrome & Common Chromosomal Disorders","sec":"peds","present":["TRISOMY 21 (Down syndrome) is the most common chromosomal disorder - featuring hypotonia, characteristic facies (flat facial profile, upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds), a single transverse palmar crease, and intellectual disability","Associated conditions: congenital heart disease (AV canal defect), duodenal atresia, Hirschsprung disease, hypothyroidism, an increased leukemia risk, and early Alzheimer disease","Other trisomies (Edwards/18, Patau/13) are severe with early mortality"],"dx":["Prenatal screening/diagnosis and postnatal karyotype confirm the diagnosis; maternal age increases risk","Systematic evaluation for associated anomalies: ECHOCARDIOGRAM, thyroid studies, hearing/vision, and cervical spine (atlantoaxial instability)","Recognize the pattern of dysmorphology"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary care with SCREENING and management of associated conditions (cardiac, thyroid, hearing/vision, leukemia surveillance)","Early intervention and developmental support","Family counseling and support"],"pearls":["Down syndrome (trisomy 21): hypotonia, characteristic facies, AV canal defect, duodenal atresia ('double bubble'), hypothyroidism, and increased leukemia risk","Screen with echocardiography, thyroid studies, and hearing/vision testing","Edwards (18) and Patau (13) syndromes are severe with early mortality","Advanced maternal age increases the risk of trisomies"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Down Syndrome Health Supervision","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Down Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526016/"}]},{"id":"peds-genetic-syndromes","name":"Common Genetic Syndromes & Dysmorphology","sec":"peds","present":["Recognizing patterns of malformation ('dysmorphology') enables diagnosis of common genetic syndromes and guides targeted testing and management","A dysmorphic child with growth, developmental, or organ-specific findings warrants a systematic genetic approach","Several syndromes have high-yield, recognizable phenotypes"],"dx":["TURNER (45,X): short stature, webbed neck, coarctation/bicuspid aortic valve, gonadal dysgenesis/amenorrhea, and lymphedema; KLINEFELTER (47,XXY): tall stature, hypogonadism, gynecomastia, infertility","FRAGILE X (FMR1 trinucleotide expansion): the most common INHERITED intellectual disability, with autism, a long face/large ears, and macroorchidism; DIGEORGE (22q11 deletion): 'CATCH-22' - Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia (immunodeficiency), Cleft palate, HYPOCALCEMIA","PRADER-WILLI vs ANGELMAN (chromosome 15 imprinting); Williams syndrome (supravalvular aortic stenosis, hypercalcemia, 'friendly' personality); Marfan (FBN1)"],"tx":["Genetic testing and counseling; manage syndrome-specific complications (e.g., cardiac in Turner/Williams, immune/calcium in DiGeorge)","Multidisciplinary care and family support","Surveillance per syndrome"],"pearls":["Turner syndrome (45,X): short stature, webbed neck, coarctation, and streak gonads - karyotype any short girl with amenorrhea","Fragile X (FMR1) is the most common inherited intellectual disability and is associated with autism","DiGeorge (22q11): CATCH-22 - cardiac defects, hypocalcemia, immunodeficiency (thymic hypoplasia), and cleft palate","Prader-Willi (hyperphagia/obesity) and Angelman ('happy puppet') result from chromosome 15 imprinting; Williams = supravalvular AS + hypercalcemia + friendly personality"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Genetic Syndromes / Dysmorphology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557782/"},{"t":"AAP Genetics Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-sickle-cell","name":"Sickle Cell Disease","sec":"peds","present":["An autosomal recessive hemoglobinopathy (HbS, from a glutamate-to-valine substitution in beta-globin) causing VASO-OCCLUSION and chronic HEMOLYSIS","Manifestations: painful vaso-occlusive crises, DACTYLITIS (often the first sign in infants), ACUTE CHEST SYNDROME, splenic sequestration, stroke, priapism, and infections","FUNCTIONAL ASPLENIA predisposes to overwhelming infection with encapsulated organisms"],"dx":["Detected on NEWBORN SCREENING; hemoglobin ELECTROPHORESIS confirms HbS; the smear shows sickled cells and Howell-Jolly bodies (asplenia)","ACUTE CHEST SYNDROME (fever, chest pain, hypoxia, new infiltrate) is a leading cause of death; APLASTIC crisis follows parvovirus B19 infection","Screen stroke risk with transcranial Doppler"],"tx":["Vaso-occlusive crisis: hydration, analgesia, and oxygen; acute chest syndrome is an emergency (antibiotics, transfusion); PENICILLIN PROPHYLAXIS plus vaccination against encapsulated organisms is life-saving","HYDROXYUREA (raises fetal hemoglobin) reduces crises; transfusion/exchange and TCD-guided stroke prevention; folate; curative options include transplant and gene therapy","Comprehensive preventive care and family education"],"pearls":["Functional asplenia means a risk of encapsulated-organism sepsis - penicillin prophylaxis and vaccines are life-saving","Acute chest syndrome is a leading cause of death (treat as an emergency)","Hydroxyurea (raises fetal hemoglobin) reduces the frequency of crises","Dactylitis is often the first sign in infants; aplastic crisis is triggered by parvovirus B19; TCD screening guides stroke prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"NHLBI Sickle Cell Disease Guidelines","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sickle Cell Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482164/"}]},{"id":"peds-anemias","name":"Iron-Deficiency & Pediatric Anemias","sec":"peds","present":["Anemia presents with pallor, fatigue, and sometimes PICA; IRON DEFICIENCY is the most common cause in children","A classic pediatric scenario is the toddler with EXCESSIVE COW'S MILK intake (which displaces iron-rich foods and can cause GI blood loss)","Anemia is classified by red cell size and the peripheral smear"],"dx":["MICROCYTIC differential: IRON DEFICIENCY (low ferritin/iron, HIGH RDW - dietary/cow's-milk in toddlers), THALASSEMIA (target cells, near-NORMAL RDW - confirm by electrophoresis), and LEAD poisoning (BASOPHILIC STIPPLING)","Check CBC, smear, and ferritin; screen for anemia around 12 months; recognize the physiologic nadir of infancy","Also consider hemolytic (hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD) and marrow failure causes"],"tx":["Iron deficiency: oral IRON supplementation, LIMIT cow's milk, and dietary counseling; lead poisoning: remove the source (+/- chelation)","Treat the specific underlying cause","Recheck response to therapy"],"pearls":["Excessive cow's milk in toddlers is a classic cause of iron-deficiency anemia - limit milk and give oral iron","Microcytic differential: iron deficiency (high RDW, low ferritin), thalassemia (normal RDW, target cells), and lead (basophilic stippling)","Screen for anemia at ~12 months of age","Distinguish iron deficiency from thalassemia trait to avoid unnecessary iron and identify carriers"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Iron Deficiency / Anemia Screening","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Iron Deficiency Anemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448065/"}]},{"id":"peds-all","name":"Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia","sec":"peds","present":["The MOST COMMON childhood cancer (peak age 2-5 years) - a proliferation of lymphoblasts that replaces marrow (shares content with the pathology hematopathology framing)","Presents with PANCYTOPENIA (fatigue/pallor, infections, bruising/bleeding/petechiae), BONE PAIN/limp, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and fever; T-cell ALL may have a mediastinal mass","It can present acutely with cytopenias and marrow failure"],"dx":["Diagnosis requires >=20% lymphoBLASTS in the marrow; blasts are TdT-positive, with flow cytometry defining B-ALL (CD19/CD10) or T-ALL (CD3)","Cytogenetics stratify risk: hyperdiploidy/ETV6-RUNX1 are FAVORABLE (pediatric), while the PHILADELPHIA chromosome t(9;22) is adverse (but targetable); the CNS and testes are sanctuary sites","Assess for tumor lysis syndrome at presentation"],"tx":["Multi-agent CHEMOTHERAPY over ~2-3 years (induction, consolidation, maintenance) with CNS PROPHYLAXIS (intrathecal therapy); tyrosine kinase inhibitors for Philadelphia-positive disease","Childhood ALL has a generally EXCELLENT prognosis/cure rate","Tumor lysis prophylaxis and supportive care"],"pearls":["ALL is the most common childhood cancer (peak 2-5 years) - pancytopenia plus bone pain/limp and organomegaly",">=20% blasts (TdT-positive) define it; childhood ALL is generally highly curable","CNS prophylaxis is essential (the CNS is a sanctuary site)","The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) is adverse but targetable with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor; watch for tumor lysis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459149/"},{"t":"NCCN Guidelines: Pediatric ALL","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"peds-solid-tumors","name":"Pediatric Solid Tumors (Wilms, Neuroblastoma, Retinoblastoma)","sec":"peds","present":["Common pediatric solid tumors that frequently present as an ABDOMINAL MASS or an eye finding (shares content with the pathology and ophthalmology framings)","WILMS tumor and NEUROBLASTOMA are the classic abdominal masses; RETINOBLASTOMA presents in the eye","Distinguishing them by features guides urgent, specific management"],"dx":["WILMS TUMOR (nephroblastoma): the most common childhood renal tumor (~2-5 years) - a smooth abdominal mass that does NOT cross the midline, with hematuria/hypertension; associated with WT1 (WAGR, Beckwith-Wiedemann) - avoid vigorous palpation","NEUROBLASTOMA: the most common extracranial solid tumor of infancy (adrenal/sympathetic chain) - a firm mass that CROSSES the midline and may be calcified, with elevated urinary catecholamines (VMA/HVA), N-MYC amplification (adverse), 'raccoon eyes,' and opsoclonus-myoclonus (stage 4S can spontaneously regress)","RETINOBLASTOMA: LEUKOCORIA (a white pupillary reflex) and strabismus, caused by RB1 mutation (bilateral disease is hereditary)"],"tx":["Wilms tumor: nephrectomy plus chemotherapy (generally good prognosis); neuroblastoma: risk-stratified multimodal therapy; retinoblastoma: eye-preserving therapy or enucleation","Genetic counseling for heritable forms (RB1, WT1)","Coordinate with pediatric oncology"],"pearls":["Wilms tumor is an abdominal mass that does NOT cross the midline (~2-5 years, WT1/Beckwith-Wiedemann) - avoid vigorous palpation","Neuroblastoma CROSSES the midline, is often calcified, secretes catecholamines (VMA/HVA), and carries N-myc; stage 4S can regress","Retinoblastoma presents with LEUKOCORIA and strabismus (RB1; bilateral = hereditary)","An abdominal mass crossing vs not crossing the midline helps separate neuroblastoma from Wilms"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Wilms Tumor / Neuroblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442009/"},{"t":"NCCN / COG Pediatric Oncology Resources","u":"https://www.nccn.org"}]},{"id":"peds-nephrotic-syndrome","name":"Nephrotic Syndrome (Minimal Change Disease)","sec":"peds","present":["Nephrotic syndrome presents with EDEMA (periorbital first, then generalized), heavy PROTEINURIA, HYPOALBUMINEMIA, and hyperlipidemia (shares content with the pathology glomerular framing)","MINIMAL CHANGE DISEASE is the most common cause in children (typically 2-6 years), often following a viral illness","It is usually steroid-responsive"],"dx":["Heavy proteinuria with hypoalbuminemia and edema; MINIMAL CHANGE disease shows NORMAL light microscopy with foot-process EFFACEMENT on electron microscopy and is STEROID-RESPONSIVE (so biopsy is often deferred in typical children)","Complications: INFECTION (loss of immunoglobulins - encapsulated organisms) and THROMBOSIS (loss of antithrombin)","Biopsy is reserved for steroid-resistant or atypical presentations"],"tx":["Empiric CORTICOSTEROIDS are first-line in children (most respond) - typically without an initial biopsy; manage salt/fluid and complications","Steroid-resistant or atypical disease warrants biopsy and further evaluation (e.g., FSGS)","Monitor for infection and thrombosis"],"pearls":["Minimal change disease is the most common childhood nephrotic syndrome and is steroid-responsive - treat empirically with steroids without biopsy","Electron microscopy shows foot-process effacement (normal light microscopy)","Watch for infection (loss of immunoglobulins) and thrombosis (loss of antithrombin)","Periorbital edema is often the earliest sign; biopsy only steroid-resistant/atypical cases"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Minimal Change Disease / Nephrotic Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500028/"},{"t":"AAP / KDIGO Nephrotic Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-hematuria-nephritis","name":"Hematuria, Post-Strep Glomerulonephritis & HSP","sec":"peds","present":["Nephritic presentations in children (shares content with the pathology framing). POST-STREPTOCOCCAL GLOMERULONEPHRITIS: gross HEMATURIA ('tea/cola-colored' urine), edema, hypertension, and oliguria 1-3 weeks AFTER strep pharyngitis or impetigo","IgA nephropathy: hematuria concurrent with a URI (synpharyngitic); HENOCH-SCHONLEIN PURPURA (IgA vasculitis): PALPABLE PURPURA on the legs/buttocks plus arthralgia, abdominal pain, and renal involvement - the most common childhood vasculitis","Distinguishing glomerular from other bleeding directs the workup"],"dx":["POST-STREP GN: LOW complement (C3) with elevated ASO/anti-DNase B, and subepithelial 'humps' on biopsy; HSP: a clinical diagnosis (palpable purpura + the tetrad) with IgA deposition","Urinalysis: RBC CASTS and dysmorphic RBCs indicate a GLOMERULAR source","Assess renal function and blood pressure"],"tx":["Post-strep GN: SUPPORTIVE care (it is usually self-limited with a good prognosis in children) with blood-pressure/fluid management","HSP: supportive care with monitoring of renal involvement; corticosteroids for severe GI/renal disease; watch for intussusception","Follow renal recovery"],"pearls":["Post-strep GN is nephritic (tea-colored urine, edema, hypertension) 1-3 weeks after strep, with LOW C3 and elevated ASO - supportive care, good prognosis in children","HSP (IgA vasculitis) is the most common childhood vasculitis: palpable purpura (legs/buttocks) + arthritis + abdominal pain + nephritis","RBC casts indicate glomerular bleeding","Watch for intussusception and renal involvement in HSP"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Post-Streptococcal GN / HSP","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538255/"},{"t":"AAP Nephrology Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-febrile-seizures-epilepsy","name":"Febrile Seizures & Pediatric Epilepsy","sec":"peds","present":["FEBRILE SEIZURES (6 months-5 years, occurring WITH fever and WITHOUT a CNS infection or metabolic cause) are the most common seizures in childhood","SIMPLE febrile seizures (generalized, <15 minutes, once in 24 hours) are benign; COMPLEX febrile seizures (focal, prolonged >15 minutes, or recurrent within 24 hours) warrant more attention","EPILEPSY (recurrent unprovoked seizures) has distinct pediatric syndromes"],"dx":["A SIMPLE febrile seizure is a clinical diagnosis - NO routine lumbar puncture, EEG, or imaging (evaluate the source of the FEVER); complex/atypical features or concern for meningitis prompt further workup","Epilepsy syndromes to recognize: ABSENCE (3-Hz spike-and-wave, staring spells), benign rolandic, INFANTILE SPASMS (hypsarrhythmia - West syndrome), and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Rule out meningitis when clinical features raise concern"],"tx":["Febrile seizures: REASSURANCE (they do not typically cause epilepsy or brain damage; recurrence risk exists), antipyretics for comfort (they do NOT prevent recurrence), and treat the fever source","Epilepsy: antiseizure medication; STATUS EPILEPTICUS - benzodiazepines first","INFANTILE SPASMS require urgent recognition and treatment"],"pearls":["Simple febrile seizures are benign - reassure, and do NOT routinely do LP/EEG/imaging; focus on the fever source (rule out meningitis if concerning)","Antipyretics do not prevent febrile seizure recurrence","Absence epilepsy = 3-Hz spike-and-wave with staring (treat with ethosuximide); infantile spasms = hypsarrhythmia (West syndrome) needing urgent treatment","Status epilepticus is treated with benzodiazepines first"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Febrile Seizure Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Febrile Seizures / Pediatric Epilepsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448123/"}]},{"id":"peds-cerebral-palsy","name":"Cerebral Palsy","sec":"peds","present":["A NON-PROGRESSIVE disorder of movement and posture resulting from early (often prenatal) brain injury or malformation - risk factors include PREMATURITY, hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, infection, and kernicterus","Presents with delayed motor milestones, abnormal TONE (SPASTICITY is most common), persistent primitive reflexes, and early HAND PREFERENCE (before age 1)","Common comorbidities: epilepsy, intellectual disability, vision/hearing impairment, and feeding difficulty"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (motor delay plus abnormal tone/exam that is NON-progressive - regression suggests an alternative diagnosis); MRI helps identify the etiology","Classify by type (SPASTIC - diplegia/hemiplegia/quadriplegia, dyskinetic, ataxic) and distribution","Screen for the associated comorbidities"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary management: physical/occupational therapy, SPASTICITY treatment (baclofen, botulinum toxin, orthopedic surgery), and management of comorbidities","It is not curable - care focuses on maximizing function and quality of life","Nutrition and communication support as needed"],"pearls":["Cerebral palsy is a NON-progressive motor disorder from early brain injury - regression suggests a different (progressive) diagnosis","Spastic type is most common; early hand preference (before age 1) is a red flag","Prematurity is a major risk factor","Comorbidities (epilepsy, intellectual disability, feeding/vision/hearing) are common and need screening"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Cerebral Palsy Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538147/"}]},{"id":"peds-hypotonia-floppy-infant","name":"Hypotonia / The Floppy Infant","sec":"peds","present":["The 'FLOPPY INFANT' has decreased muscle tone - a frog-leg posture, head lag, and slipping through the examiner's hands","The key task is localizing the problem to a CENTRAL (brain) cause (most common) versus a PERIPHERAL (motor unit) cause","Peripheral causes typically pair hypotonia with true WEAKNESS"],"dx":["CENTRAL hypotonia: tone reduced MORE than strength, with relatively preserved reflexes and often dysmorphism/seizures (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, Down syndrome, PRADER-WILLI, metabolic disease)","PERIPHERAL hypotonia: prominent WEAKNESS with reduced reflexes - SPINAL MUSCULAR ATROPHY (SMN1; tongue fasciculations with an alert face and areflexia), congenital myopathies, myotonic dystrophy, and neuromuscular-junction disorders (infant BOTULISM, myasthenia)","Workup: exam, genetic testing (SMA, Prader-Willi), CK, and metabolic studies"],"tx":["Cause-specific management; SPINAL MUSCULAR ATROPHY now has disease-modifying therapy (gene therapy/antisense) where EARLY treatment is critical","Supportive care (respiratory, feeding) and early referral","Infant botulism is supportive with botulism immune globulin"],"pearls":["Distinguish central hypotonia (tone down but strength/reflexes relatively preserved, +/- dysmorphism/seizures - e.g., Down, Prader-Willi, HIE) from peripheral (weakness + reduced reflexes)","SMA (SMN1): tongue fasciculations, an alert face, and areflexia - now treatable, and early treatment is critical","Infant botulism: constipation then descending weakness (classically after honey exposure)","Send genetic testing early in an unexplained floppy infant"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypotonia / Floppy Infant","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562209/"},{"t":"AAP Neurology Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-jia","name":"Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis","sec":"peds","present":["Chronic ARTHRITIS (lasting >6 weeks) with onset before age 16 - the most common chronic rheumatic disease of childhood","Subtypes: OLIGOARTICULAR (the most common - a few large joints in young girls, with a risk of UVEITIS), POLYARTICULAR (RF-positive resembles adult rheumatoid arthritis), and SYSTEMIC (Still disease)","Systemic JIA features quotidian fever, a salmon-colored rash, hepatosplenomegaly, and serositis"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (arthritis >6 weeks with exclusion of other causes); ANA positivity flags UVEITIS risk (needs ophthalmology screening); rheumatoid factor identifies the RA-like subtype","SYSTEMIC JIA: high ferritin, and exclusion of infection/malignancy; watch for MACROPHAGE ACTIVATION SYNDROME (a life-threatening complication)","Imaging/labs support the diagnosis and monitor disease"],"tx":["NSAIDs, intra-articular/systemic corticosteroids, DMARDs (methotrexate), and biologics; regular OPHTHALMOLOGY screening for asymptomatic uveitis","Physical therapy to preserve joint function","Monitor for macrophage activation syndrome in systemic JIA"],"pearls":["Oligoarticular JIA (young girls, ANA-positive) carries a high risk of asymptomatic UVEITIS - it needs regular ophthalmology screening to prevent blindness","Systemic JIA (Still disease) = quotidian fever + salmon rash + arthritis; watch for macrophage activation syndrome","Polyarticular RF-positive JIA resembles adult rheumatoid arthritis","The arthritis must persist >6 weeks (and other causes be excluded) to diagnose JIA"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554605/"},{"t":"American College of Rheumatology","u":"https://www.rheumatology.org"}]},{"id":"peds-orthopedic-conditions","name":"Pediatric Orthopedic Conditions (DDH, SCFE, Perthes, Scoliosis)","sec":"peds","present":["Common pediatric musculoskeletal problems presenting as a limp, hip/knee pain, or a screening abnormality (shares content with the orthopedics framing)","Age is the key clue: hip dysplasia in newborns, Legg-Calve-Perthes in young children, and SCFE in adolescents","A limping or painful child needs a systematic, age-based approach"],"dx":["DEVELOPMENTAL DYSPLASIA OF THE HIP (DDH): newborn BARLOW/ORTOLANI maneuvers and asymmetric folds (risk: breech, female, family history) - ULTRASOUND before ~6 months, radiographs after; LEGG-CALVE-PERTHES: avascular necrosis of the femoral head (boys 4-8 years, a painless limp)","SLIPPED CAPITAL FEMORAL EPIPHYSIS (SCFE): an OBESE ADOLESCENT with hip OR KNEE pain and a limp (out-toeing) - an urgent diagnosis with bilateral risk; NURSEMAID'S ELBOW (radial head subluxation after an arm pull in a toddler); SCOLIOSIS (adolescent - the Adam's forward-bend test, Cobb angle)","Remember that hip pathology can refer pain to the KNEE"],"tx":["DDH: Pavlik harness; Perthes: observation/containment; SCFE: URGENT surgical pinning (non-weight-bearing until fixed); nursemaid's elbow: simple reduction; scoliosis: observe/brace/surgery by Cobb angle","Early diagnosis prevents long-term deformity/arthritis","Age-appropriate imaging and referral"],"pearls":["DDH: Barlow/Ortolani in newborns; ultrasound before 6 months (risk: breech, female, family history)","SCFE: an obese adolescent with hip OR KNEE pain and a limp - urgent pinning, keep them non-weight-bearing (bilateral risk)","Legg-Calve-Perthes is avascular necrosis of the femoral head (boys 4-8, painless limp)","Knee pain can be referred from the hip - always examine the hip in a limping child"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP DDH / Orthopedic Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: DDH / SCFE / Perthes","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499949/"}]},{"id":"peds-adolescent-medicine","name":"Adolescent Medicine & Confidentiality","sec":"peds","present":["Adolescent care addresses puberty, psychosocial development, and risk behaviors - with CONFIDENTIALITY central to building trust and eliciting honest history","The leading causes of adolescent mortality (unintentional injury, homicide, suicide) are largely preventable","A developmentally appropriate, nonjudgmental approach is essential"],"dx":["Use the HEEADSSS psychosocial interview (Home, Education/Employment, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide/depression, Safety), conducting part of the visit with the adolescent ALONE","Provide CONFIDENTIAL care for sensitive topics (sexual health, mental health, substance use) - protections and minor-consent laws vary by jurisdiction, with LIMITS (imminent harm to self/others, abuse)","Screen for depression and suicidality"],"tx":["Deliver confidential, developmentally appropriate care; screen and counsel on risk behaviors; involve family when appropriate and permitted","Breach confidentiality only for safety concerns (self-harm, abuse, harm to others)","Connect to mental health and preventive services"],"pearls":["Confidentiality (with limits) is essential to adolescent care - interview part of the visit with the teen alone","Use HEEADSSS to screen psychosocial risk","Leading causes of adolescent death (injury, homicide, suicide) are preventable - screen for depression and suicidality","Confidentiality is breached for safety (self-harm, abuse); minor-consent laws vary by state"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Adolescent Health / Bright Futures","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adolescent Confidentiality","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564383/"}]},{"id":"peds-eating-disorders-mental-health","name":"Eating Disorders & Adolescent Mental Health","sec":"peds","present":["Eating disorders (anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa, avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder, binge-eating disorder) commonly begin in adolescence and carry the HIGHEST mortality of any psychiatric illness","They frequently co-occur with depression, anxiety, and suicidality, so mental-health screening is essential","This is a clinical/educational overview for recognition and medical management - not lifestyle or diet guidance"],"dx":["Screen for warning signs and MEDICAL complications rather than relying on weight alone: bradycardia, orthostasis/hypotension, ELECTROLYTE abnormalities (e.g., hypokalemia with purging), amenorrhea, dental erosion, and Russell's sign","A normal body weight does NOT exclude a serious eating disorder; assess vital-sign and hemodynamic stability","Screen broadly for depression, anxiety, and self-harm"],"tx":["MULTIDISCIPLINARY care (medical, nutritional, and mental-health); FAMILY-BASED therapy is first-line for adolescent anorexia nervosa; treat comorbid depression/anxiety/suicidality","Medical stabilization/hospitalization for instability (severe bradycardia, electrolyte derangement, hemodynamic compromise); monitor for REFEEDING SYNDROME (hypophosphatemia) when nutrition is restored","Connect families to appropriate specialty support and resources"],"pearls":["Eating disorders have the highest mortality of psychiatric illnesses - screen actively and do not be reassured by a 'normal' weight","Look for bradycardia, orthostasis, hypokalemia (purging), and amenorrhea as markers of medical instability","Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia) is a danger when nutrition is restored - monitor electrolytes closely","Family-based therapy is first-line for adolescent anorexia; always screen for and treat comorbid depression/suicidality"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Eating Disorders Clinical Report","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Eating Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567805/"}]},{"id":"peds-adolescent-substance-use","name":"Adolescent Substance Use","sec":"peds","present":["Adolescent substance use ranges from experimentation to a substance use disorder - alcohol, cannabis, NICOTINE/VAPING, opioids, and stimulants","Vaping/e-cigarette use has risen sharply; overdose (opioids) and motor-vehicle crashes are major risks","A confidential, nonjudgmental approach improves disclosure and engagement"],"dx":["SCREEN with a validated tool (e.g., CRAFFT) and assess severity and functional impact; look for behavioral/school changes","Recognize co-occurring mental-health conditions (which are common)","Assess overdose and injury risk"],"tx":["Brief intervention and MOTIVATIONAL INTERVIEWING; referral to treatment for a substance use disorder; HARM REDUCTION (naloxone for opioid-overdose risk)","Confidential care and treatment of comorbid mental-health conditions","Counsel on driving/overdose safety"],"pearls":["Screen adolescents for substance use with a validated tool such as CRAFFT","Vaping/e-cigarette use is a major and growing concern","Use a confidential, nonjudgmental approach with motivational interviewing","Provide naloxone for opioid-overdose risk; substance use commonly co-occurs with mental-health conditions"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Substance Use Screening (SBIRT)","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adolescent Substance Use","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574499/"}]},{"id":"peds-child-abuse","name":"Child Abuse & Non-Accidental Trauma","sec":"peds","present":["Child maltreatment (physical abuse, neglect, sexual abuse, emotional abuse) is a can't-miss diagnosis with serious morbidity and mortality","Clues include injuries INCONSISTENT with the history or developmental stage, delayed presentation, and a changing story","Recognition and mandated reporting protect the child"],"dx":["RED FLAGS: injury inconsistent with the mechanism/developmental stage, patterned injuries, and BRUISES in a NON-MOBILE infant ('those who don't cruise rarely bruise'); specific fractures (metaphyseal corner/'bucket-handle', posterior rib, multiple fractures of different ages)","ABUSIVE HEAD TRAUMA ('shaken baby'): subdural hemorrhages with RETINAL hemorrhages; burns with patterned/immersion distributions","Evaluation: SKELETAL SURVEY (children <2), dilated retinal exam, and studies to exclude medical mimics (coagulopathy, metabolic bone disease); document carefully"],"tx":["Ensure the child's SAFETY, treat injuries, and file a MANDATORY REPORT (clinicians are mandated reporters - report reasonable suspicion, do not wait for proof)","Multidisciplinary involvement (child-protection team, social work); specialized evaluation for suspected sexual abuse","Coordinate with child protective services"],"pearls":["Bruises in a non-mobile infant ('those who don't cruise rarely bruise') are a red flag for abuse","Classic abusive fractures: metaphyseal corner, posterior rib, and fractures of different ages; abusive head trauma pairs subdural with retinal hemorrhages","Obtain a skeletal survey in children under 2 with suspected abuse","Clinicians are mandatory reporters - report reasonable suspicion and ensure the child's safety"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Child Abuse & Neglect Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Child Abuse / Non-Accidental Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459146/"}]},{"id":"peds-emergencies","name":"Pediatric Emergencies (PALS, Shock, SIDS/BRUE, Poisoning)","sec":"peds","present":["Recognizing the critically ill child - impending arrest, shock, altered infant events, and ingestions - is a core pediatric skill","Unlike adults, pediatric arrest is usually RESPIRATORY in origin (respiratory failure/shock precede cardiac arrest)","Early recognition and support prevent deterioration to arrest"],"dx":["The Pediatric Assessment Triangle (Appearance, Work of breathing, Circulation) rapidly identifies the sick child; SHOCK types - HYPOVOLEMIC (most common, from dehydration), distributive/septic, and cardiogenic","SIDS (sudden unexplained infant death during sleep) versus BRUE (a Brief Resolved Unexplained Event - risk-stratified into low vs high risk); POISONING - recognize TOXIDROMES and specific antidotes","Assess airway/breathing/circulation first"],"tx":["PALS algorithms with EARLY respiratory support; FLUID resuscitation (isotonic boluses) for shock (cautious in cardiogenic shock)","Poisoning: ABCs, decontamination, and ANTIDOTES (N-acetylcysteine for acetaminophen, naloxone for opioids) with poison-control guidance; SIDS prevention through SAFE SLEEP (back to sleep, firm surface, no soft bedding)","Risk-stratify BRUE (most are low-risk)"],"pearls":["Pediatric arrest is usually RESPIRATORY in origin - support breathing early (unlike the primary-cardiac adult pattern)","Hypovolemic shock (dehydration) is the most common pediatric shock - treat with isotonic fluid boluses","Safe sleep (back to sleep, firm surface, no soft bedding) prevents SIDS; most BRUEs are low-risk","Know key antidotes (N-acetylcysteine for acetaminophen, naloxone for opioids) and call poison control"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA Pediatric Advanced Life Support (PALS)","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"AAP BRUE / Safe Sleep Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-neonatal-abstinence","name":"Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome","sec":"peds","present":["Withdrawal in a newborn from in-utero substance exposure - most commonly OPIOIDS (also benzodiazepines and others)","Onset within hours to days after birth: IRRITABILITY, a HIGH-PITCHED CRY, tremors, hypertonia, poor feeding, sweating, sneezing, diarrhea, and (severe) seizures","Timing of onset depends on the substance's half-life"],"dx":["Maternal history and toxicology; standardized withdrawal scoring (e.g., Finnegan) or a function-based assessment guides care","Onset is earlier with short-acting opioids and later with methadone/buprenorphine","Assess feeding, weight, and severity"],"tx":["NON-PHARMACOLOGIC care first: the 'EAT, SLEEP, CONSOLE' approach - rooming-in, swaddling, a low-stimulation environment, and responsive feeding","PHARMACOLOGIC therapy (an opioid such as morphine/methadone) for severe or refractory symptoms; support feeding and weight gain","Breastfeeding is generally encouraged for mothers on medication-assisted treatment (not illicit use)"],"pearls":["Neonatal abstinence syndrome from in-utero opioid exposure: irritability, high-pitched cry, tremors, poor feeding, and GI symptoms","Non-pharmacologic care comes first (Eat-Sleep-Console, rooming-in, swaddling, low stimulation)","Add an opioid (morphine) for severe/refractory symptoms","Onset timing depends on the drug's half-life (later for methadone/buprenorphine)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551498/"}]},{"id":"peds-malrotation-volvulus","name":"Malrotation & Volvulus / Neonatal Surgical Abdomen","sec":"peds","present":["BILIOUS VOMITING in a neonate or infant is a surgical EMERGENCY until proven otherwise - the feared cause is MALROTATION with MIDGUT VOLVULUS, which can infarct the entire midgut within hours","Other neonatal surgical abdomens: DUODENAL ATRESIA ('double bubble', associated with Down syndrome), jejunoileal atresia, NEC, imperforate anus, and meconium ileus","Rapid recognition preserves bowel and life"],"dx":["Bilious vomiting mandates URGENT evaluation; the UPPER GI SERIES is the gold standard for malrotation (abnormal position of the duodenojejunal junction/ligament of Treitz; a 'CORKSCREW' appearance with volvulus)","Ladd bands cause obstruction; duodenal atresia shows a 'double bubble' (and prompts a Down syndrome evaluation)","Assess for peritonitis/ischemia"],"tx":["Malrotation with volvulus requires EMERGENT surgery (LADD procedure); NPO, fluid resuscitation, and gastric decompression meanwhile","Time is bowel - do not delay operative management","Cause-specific surgery for other neonatal obstructions"],"pearls":["Bilious vomiting in a neonate is malrotation with midgut volvulus until proven otherwise - a surgical emergency (the midgut can infarct in hours)","Upper GI series is the gold standard (corkscrew sign, abnormal ligament of Treitz)","The Ladd procedure is the operative treatment - do not delay","Duodenal atresia = double bubble and is associated with Down syndrome"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Malrotation / Volvulus","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537152/"},{"t":"AAP Pediatric Surgery Resources","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-testicular-torsion","name":"Testicular Torsion & the Acute Scrotum","sec":"peds","present":["SUDDEN, severe testicular/scrotal pain with swelling and nausea/vomiting - a urologic EMERGENCY with a narrow window (testicular salvage falls sharply after ~6 hours)","Bimodal incidence: neonates and peripubertal/adolescent boys","The acute scrotum differential includes torsion, epididymitis, and torsion of the appendix testis"],"dx":["TORSION: a high-riding testis with a horizontal lie, an ABSENT CREMASTERIC reflex, and a negative Prehn sign; Doppler ultrasound shows decreased/absent flow - but do NOT delay surgery for imaging if the presentation is classic","EPIDIDYMITIS: more gradual, with dysuria, a POSITIVE Prehn sign (relief with elevation), and a preserved cremasteric reflex; torsion of the appendix testis shows a 'BLUE DOT' sign","Time from onset is critical to viability"],"tx":["EMERGENT surgical exploration with detorsion and bilateral ORCHIOPEXY (fix BOTH testes); manual detorsion can temporize","Salvage is time-dependent - operative management should not wait for imaging in classic cases","Treat epididymitis medically once torsion is excluded"],"pearls":["Testicular torsion is a surgical emergency - salvage drops sharply after ~6 hours, so don't delay surgery for imaging in classic cases","Torsion: absent cremasteric reflex, high-riding horizontal testis; epididymitis: positive Prehn sign and preserved cremasteric reflex","Orchiopexy fixes BOTH testes (the anatomic predisposition is bilateral)","A 'blue dot' sign suggests torsion of the appendix testis (less emergent)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Testicular Torsion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441987/"},{"t":"AAP / AUA Acute Scrotum Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-anaphylaxis-food-allergy","name":"Anaphylaxis & Food Allergy","sec":"peds","present":["ANAPHYLAXIS is an acute, potentially fatal systemic IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reaction - urticaria/angioedema plus RESPIRATORY (wheeze/stridor), GI, and/or CARDIOVASCULAR (hypotension) involvement","FOOD is the most common pediatric trigger (peanut, tree nuts, milk, egg, shellfish)","Rapid onset after allergen exposure is characteristic"],"dx":["A CLINICAL diagnosis: acute onset with skin/mucosal involvement PLUS respiratory compromise OR hypotension, or typical features after a known allergen exposure","Food allergy: history plus skin-prick/specific-IgE testing; oral food challenge is the gold standard","Do not wait for laboratory confirmation to treat anaphylaxis"],"tx":["INTRAMUSCULAR EPINEPHRINE (anterolateral thigh) is FIRST-LINE and given IMMEDIATELY - there is no absolute contraindication, and delay increases mortality; antihistamines, corticosteroids, and bronchodilators are ADJUNCTS only","Observe for a BIPHASIC reaction; discharge with an epinephrine autoinjector and an allergy action plan, and counsel on avoidance","EARLY allergen introduction (e.g., peanut) reduces the development of food allergy"],"pearls":["Intramuscular epinephrine (anterolateral thigh) is first-line for anaphylaxis - give it immediately; antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts only","Anaphylaxis = skin/mucosa plus respiratory compromise or hypotension after an allergen","Watch for biphasic reactions and prescribe autoinjectors with an action plan","Early peanut introduction reduces the risk of peanut allergy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAAAI / AAP Anaphylaxis & Food Allergy","u":"https://www.aaaai.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Anaphylaxis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482124/"}]},{"id":"peds-enuresis-encopresis","name":"Enuresis & Encopresis","sec":"peds","present":["ENURESIS is involuntary voiding (usually NOCTURNAL) in a child at least 5 years old - PRIMARY (never consistently dry) is the most common","ENCOPRESIS is fecal soiling (age >=4) usually from chronic CONSTIPATION with overflow","Both are common and usually benign, but red flags warrant evaluation"],"dx":["PRIMARY nocturnal enuresis is developmental (family history, deep sleep) and benign - exclude SECONDARY causes (UTI, diabetes, constipation, psychosocial stress, obstructive sleep apnea), especially with daytime symptoms or new onset (secondary)","ENCOPRESIS is usually RETENTIVE (constipation with overflow soiling) - history and exam confirm stool retention","Urinalysis and a constipation assessment guide the workup"],"tx":["Enuresis: reassurance and motivational/behavioral measures; the ENURESIS ALARM is the most effective long-term therapy, with desmopressin for short-term needs (e.g., sleepovers)","Encopresis: treat the underlying CONSTIPATION (disimpaction then PEG maintenance) plus a behavioral toileting routine","Address contributing factors"],"pearls":["Primary nocturnal enuresis is common and usually benign (developmental, familial) - reassure","The enuresis alarm is the most effective long-term treatment; desmopressin is for short-term use","Secondary enuresis or daytime symptoms warrant a workup (UTI, diabetes, constipation, OSA)","Encopresis is usually overflow from constipation - treat the constipation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Enuresis / Constipation Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Enuresis / Encopresis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545181/"}]},{"id":"peds-hypertension","name":"Pediatric Hypertension","sec":"peds","present":["Pediatric hypertension is blood pressure at or above the 95th PERCENTILE for age, sex, and height on repeated measurements","PRIMARY hypertension (increasingly common, driven by OBESITY) predominates in older children/adolescents; SECONDARY hypertension is more likely in YOUNGER children","It is often asymptomatic and found on routine measurement"],"dx":["Use the correct cuff and technique and CONFIRM on repeated visits; a YOUNGER child or severe hypertension prompts a SECONDARY workup - RENAL disease is the most common cause in children (also renovascular, COARCTATION, and endocrine causes: pheochromocytoma, CAH, hyperaldosteronism, Cushing)","Check FOUR-EXTREMITY blood pressures for coarctation; evaluate for END-ORGAN damage (echocardiography for LVH, renal and retinal assessment)","Distinguish primary (obesity, older, family history) from secondary"],"tx":["Primary hypertension: LIFESTYLE modification (weight, diet, exercise); treat the specific cause of secondary hypertension","Antihypertensive medication (e.g., ACE inhibitor) for persistent, symptomatic, or end-organ disease","Monitor blood pressure and end-organ status"],"pearls":["Pediatric hypertension is BP >=95th percentile (for age/sex/height), confirmed on repeat measurement","Younger children are more likely to have SECONDARY hypertension - renal disease is the most common cause (work them up)","Obesity drives primary hypertension in adolescents","Check four-extremity blood pressures for coarctation and assess for end-organ damage (LVH)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP Pediatric Hypertension Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Hypertension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537298/"}]},{"id":"peds-immunodeficiency","name":"Primary Immunodeficiency (Recurrent-Infection Workup)","sec":"peds","present":["Primary immunodeficiencies present with recurrent, SEVERE, PERSISTENT, or UNUSUAL infections - the classic warning signs (plus failure to thrive and a family history)","The PATTERN of infection points to the arm of the immune system involved","Early recognition is life-saving, especially for severe combined immunodeficiency"],"dx":["ANTIBODY/B-CELL defects (recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria): X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton), common variable immunodeficiency, and IgA deficiency (the most common); T-CELL/COMBINED defects (opportunistic, viral, fungal infections): SEVERE COMBINED IMMUNODEFICIENCY (SCID - a medical emergency, now on newborn screening via TREC)","PHAGOCYTE defects: CHRONIC GRANULOMATOUS DISEASE (catalase-positive organisms, abscesses; abnormal DHR/nitroblue-tetrazolium test); COMPLEMENT defects (terminal C5-9 -> NEISSERIA infections; CH50)","Workup: immunoglobulin levels, vaccine titers, lymphocyte subsets, and specific functional tests"],"tx":["Cause-specific: IVIG for antibody deficiencies, prophylactic antibiotics, and HEMATOPOIETIC STEM CELL TRANSPLANT for SCID (urgent/curative)","AVOID LIVE vaccines in significant immunodeficiency, and use irradiated/CMV-safe blood products for SCID","Early referral to immunology"],"pearls":["Warning signs of primary immunodeficiency: recurrent, severe, unusual, or persistent infections plus a family history","SCID is a medical emergency (now on newborn screening via TREC) - urgent stem cell transplant; avoid live vaccines and use irradiated/CMV-safe blood","Antibody deficiencies cause encapsulated sinopulmonary infections (treat with IVIG); chronic granulomatous disease involves catalase-positive organisms (DHR test)","Terminal complement deficiencies predispose to Neisseria infections"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Primary Immunodeficiency","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442022/"},{"t":"AAP / Immune Deficiency Foundation","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-septic-arthritis-osteomyelitis","name":"Pediatric Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis / The Limping Child","sec":"peds","present":["Musculoskeletal infection presents with a LIMP or refusal to bear weight, joint/bone pain and swelling, and fever (shares content with the orthopedics framing)","SEPTIC ARTHRITIS (joint infection - an emergency risking joint destruction) and OSTEOMYELITIS (bone infection); STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS is most common (Kingella in young children)","The limping child requires a systematic, urgent approach to avoid missing infection"],"dx":["SEPTIC ARTHRITIS: the KOCHER criteria (fever, non-weight-bearing, elevated ESR, elevated WBC) raise suspicion; JOINT ASPIRATION is the gold standard (purulent fluid, high WBC, culture)","OSTEOMYELITIS: favors the METAPHYSIS of long bones - MRI detects it early (radiographs are late), with elevated ESR/CRP and blood cultures","Distinguish from TRANSIENT SYNOVITIS (post-viral, well-appearing, self-limited, lower inflammatory markers)"],"tx":["Septic arthritis is a surgical EMERGENCY - urgent DRAINAGE/washout plus IV antibiotics (delay causes joint destruction, especially the hip)","Osteomyelitis: IV then oral antibiotics (empiric anti-staphylococcal) +/- drainage","Prompt diagnosis and treatment preserve the joint/bone"],"pearls":["Septic arthritis is a surgical emergency - joint aspiration is the gold standard, and delay destroys the joint (especially the hip)","Kocher criteria help distinguish septic arthritis from transient synovitis","Staphylococcus aureus is most common (Kingella in young children); osteomyelitis favors the metaphysis and is seen early on MRI","A limping or non-weight-bearing febrile child needs urgent evaluation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Septic Arthritis / Osteomyelitis (Pediatric)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538176/"},{"t":"AAP Musculoskeletal Infection Guidance","u":"https://www.aap.org"}]},{"id":"peds-cleft-lip-palate","name":"Cleft Lip & Palate and Craniofacial","sec":"peds","present":["Cleft lip and/or palate are the most common congenital craniofacial anomalies (shares content with the otolaryngology framing)","Consequences: FEEDING difficulty (cleft palate), speech problems (velopharyngeal insufficiency), recurrent otitis media/effusion (eustachian tube dysfunction), and dental/facial-growth issues","May be isolated or syndromic (e.g., Pierre Robin sequence)"],"dx":["Diagnosis is clinical (cleft lip is often seen on prenatal ultrasound); examine the palate - including for a SUBMUCOUS cleft (bifid uvula, notched hard palate) - and assess feeding, AIRWAY (Pierre Robin - micrognathia, glossoptosis), and hearing/middle ear","Evaluate for associated syndromes (genetics)","Serial hearing assessment (effusion is expected)"],"tx":["STAGED, team-based repair: cleft LIP repair around ~3-6 months and cleft PALATE repair around ~9-12 months (before speech develops); specialized feeding support meanwhile","TYMPANOSTOMY TUBES for the near-universal middle-ear disease; later speech therapy/velopharyngeal surgery, orthodontics, and alveolar bone grafting","Coordinated multidisciplinary CLEFT TEAM care"],"pearls":["Cleft palate brings near-universal eustachian tube dysfunction - expect chronic ear effusions/hearing loss and use tympanostomy tubes","Repair is staged: lip around 3-6 months, palate around 9-12 months (before speech develops)","Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, cleft palate) can obstruct the neonatal airway","Look for a submucous cleft (bifid uvula) in hypernasal speech (shares content with the ENT entry)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / ACPA Cleft & Craniofacial Care","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cleft Lip and Palate","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547629/"}]},{"id":"peds-strabismus-amblyopia","name":"Strabismus, Amblyopia & Leukocoria/ROP","sec":"peds","present":["Pediatric vision red flags (shares content with the ophthalmology framing). STRABISMUS (ocular misalignment), AMBLYOPIA ('lazy eye' - reduced vision from disuse during a critical period, the leading cause of childhood vision loss and treatable if caught early)","LEUKOCORIA (a WHITE pupillary reflex) is urgent - it can signal RETINOBLASTOMA or a congenital cataract; RETINOPATHY OF PREMATURITY threatens preterm infants","Early detection preserves vision"],"dx":["The RED REFLEX exam is key - an asymmetric or WHITE reflex (leukocoria) requires urgent ophthalmology (retinoblastoma, cataract); the corneal light reflex and cover test detect strabismus","Constant strabismus (or any misalignment persisting beyond ~4 months) is abnormal (intermittent misalignment is common in early infancy); amblyopia is detected by vision screening","ROP screening is performed in at-risk preterm infants"],"tx":["AMBLYOPIA: correct refractive error and PATCH/penalize the better eye - treatment is time-critical (the critical period); STRABISMUS: glasses, patching, and/or surgery","LEUKOCORIA: urgent ophthalmology referral (retinoblastoma is life-threatening); ROP: screening with laser/anti-VEGF as needed","Screen the red reflex at every well-child visit"],"pearls":["Leukocoria (a white pupillary reflex/absent red reflex) is urgent - it can mean retinoblastoma or a cataract","Amblyopia is the leading cause of childhood vision loss and is treatable during the critical period (patch the better eye)","Constant strabismus, or misalignment persisting beyond ~4 months, is abnormal and warrants referral","Screen the red reflex at every well-child visit; screen preterm infants for ROP (shares content with the ophthalmology entries)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAP / AAPOS Pediatric Eye Evaluation","u":"https://www.aap.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amblyopia / Strabismus / Leukocoria","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430890/"}]},{"id":"plastics-wound-healing-scars","name":"Wound Healing, Keloids & Hypertrophic Scars","sec":"plastics","present":["Wound healing proceeds in overlapping phases: HEMOSTASIS/INFLAMMATION (day 0-3), PROLIFERATION (fibroblasts, granulation, epithelialization, angiogenesis; day 3-3wk), and REMODELING (collagen III->I, months-1yr) - the reconstructive question is always which phase the wound is in","HYPERTROPHIC scars stay within the original wound borders, often improve over time, and follow tension/burns; KELOIDS extend BEYOND the original wound, do not regress, recur after excision, and favor darker skin, earlobes, sternum, shoulders, and the deltoid","Chronic/non-healing wounds signal an impediment: ischemia, infection, pressure, diabetes/neuropathy, venous stasis, radiation, malnutrition, or malignant degeneration (Marjolin ulcer in a chronic wound/burn scar)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; distinguish keloid vs hypertrophic scar by whether the scar extends beyond the wound margin, as this changes the recurrence risk and treatment","Optimize host factors before elective reconstruction: glycemic control, nutrition (albumin/prealbumin, protein), smoking cessation (nicotine is a potent vasoconstrictor that raises flap/graft loss), and treat infection","Biopsy any chronic wound with heaped edges or non-healing over months to exclude Marjolin ulcer (SCC)"],"tx":["Scar prevention/first-line: tension-off closure, silicone sheeting/gel, sun protection, and time; hypertrophic scars often respond to silicone + intralesional TRIAMCINOLONE","KELOIDS: intralesional steroid (+/- 5-FU), pressure/silicone, and if excised, close primarily and give adjuvant therapy (steroid injection, and for high-risk keloids, low-dose RADIATION within 24-48h) because excision alone recurs","Optimize the wound bed (debride, control bioburden/moisture balance - TIME framework), offload/compress by etiology, and reconstruct up the ladder once the bed is ready"],"tech":["Layered tension-free closure with dermal (buried) sutures to offload the epidermis; eversion of skin edges; place incisions in relaxed skin tension lines (RSTLs)","Intralesional triamcinolone injection into the scar substance (not the surrounding fat, to avoid atrophy); serial injections q4-6wk","Keloid excision with immediate adjuvant (intralesional steroid at closure +/- postoperative radiation for refractory keloids)"],"after":["Silicone therapy and scar massage for months; strict photoprotection of the maturing scar; set expectations that remodeling takes up to a year","Serial follow-up for keloid recurrence; repeat steroid injections as needed and watch for skin atrophy/telangiectasia from over-injection","Address the underlying cause in chronic wounds (offloading, compression, revascularization) so the reconstruction does not fail"],"pearls":["Keloid extends BEYOND the wound and recurs; hypertrophic scar stays within it and often improves - the border is the board answer","Never excise a keloid and simply close it - excision alone recurs; always pair with adjuvant (steroid +/- radiation)","A chronic non-healing wound or burn scar that suddenly changes is Marjolin ulcer until biopsy proves otherwise","Nicotine and uncontrolled glucose are the quiet killers of flaps and grafts - optimize the host before you reconstruct"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Wound Healing Phases","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470443/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-skin-grafts","name":"Skin Grafts (STSG & FTSG)","sec":"plastics","present":["A skin graft is the FIRST rung of usable coverage on the reconstructive ladder once primary closure and healing by secondary intention are inadequate; it needs a vascularized, non-infected, non-irradiated bed","SPLIT-THICKNESS (STSG: epidermis + partial dermis) covers large areas and takes more reliably but contracts more, is thinner/shinier, and matches color poorly; FULL-THICKNESS (FTSG: epidermis + full dermis) contracts less and looks better but needs a better bed and leaves a donor that must be closed primarily","Graft take fails from the classic quartet: HEMATOMA/seroma (mechanical barrier), INFECTION (>10^5 organisms, esp. beta-hemolytic strep), SHEAR (movement), and a POOR BED (bone/tendon without periosteum/paratenon, or irradiated tissue)"],"dx":["Assess the recipient bed: granulating wounds, muscle, fascia, and dermis take grafts; bare cortical bone, bare tendon, and exposed hardware do NOT and need a flap instead","Quantitatively/clinically clear infection first; beta-hemolytic Streptococcus is a specific contraindication to grafting","Choose STSG vs FTSG by size, contracture tolerance (avoid STSG over joints/face where possible), and cosmesis"],"tx":["Graft take occurs in stages: IMBIBITION (plasmatic, 24-48h), INOSCULATION (48-72h), and REVASCULARIZATION/neovascularization (~4-7d) - everything in aftercare protects these fragile early days","MESHING an STSG allows expansion and lets fluid escape (prevents hematoma/seroma) at the cost of a cobblestone appearance; sheet grafts are used on the face/hands for cosmesis","When a bed cannot support a graft (exposed bone/tendon/hardware), move up the ladder to a flap"],"tech":["STSG harvested with a dermatome (typically 0.012-0.018 inch) from thigh/buttock; mesh 1:1.5 to 1:3; secure with staples/sutures/glue and a bolster/tie-over or negative-pressure dressing to eliminate dead space and shear","FTSG harvested (e.g., preauricular, supraclavicular, groin) with the donor closed primarily; defat the graft, fenestrate to allow drainage, and bolster","STSG donor site heals by re-epithelialization from adnexal remnants (like a partial-thickness burn)"],"after":["Immobilize and pad the graft; first take-down ~day 5 (earlier if infection suspected); avoid shear and hematoma; negative-pressure or bolster dressings improve take on irregular/mobile beds","Long-term: moisturize, sun-protect, and expect secondary contracture (worse with STSG) - splint grafts over joints; the donor site is often more painful than the graft","Delayed/incomplete take is managed with wound care and regrafting once the bed re-granulates"],"pearls":["Grafts survive by imbibition then inosculation then revascularization - so hematoma, shear, and infection (the take-killers) all act in the first 3-5 days","Exposed bone without periosteum, tendon without paratenon, and irradiated beds will NOT take a graft - go to a flap","STSG contracts MORE (secondary contracture) but takes more reliably; FTSG contracts less and looks better but is more demanding - pick by site","Beta-hemolytic strep in the wound is a hard contraindication to grafting"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Skin Grafting","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532874/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Skin Graft","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"plastics-local-flaps","name":"Local Flaps & Flap Design","sec":"plastics","present":["A flap carries its own blood supply, so it can cover beds a graft cannot (exposed bone, tendon, hardware, irradiated tissue) and is the workhorse for facial/functional reconstruction where contour and durability matter","LOCAL flaps recruit adjacent tissue by ADVANCEMENT (straight sliding: V-Y, rectangular), ROTATION (pivoting an arc), or TRANSPOSITION (rhombic/Limberg, bilobed, Z-plasty) - matched to defect size, laxity, and where the scars will fall","Z-PLASTY lengthens and reorients a scar/contracture: a 60-degree Z-plasty gains ~75% length and rotates the central limb by ~90 degrees along RSTLs"],"dx":["Analyze the defect (size, depth, exposed structures, subunit) and the surrounding tissue reservoir/laxity, then choose the flap that borrows laxity where it exists and hides scars in RSTLs and subunit borders","Classify by blood supply: RANDOM-pattern flaps rely on the subdermal plexus (length limited by width, historically ~3:1 on the face); AXIAL flaps carry a named vessel and can be longer/narrower","Respect facial aesthetic subunits and free margins (lid, ala, lip) - a flap that distorts a free margin is the wrong flap"],"tx":["Choose the simplest flap that reliably closes the defect while respecting subunits and free margins; delay a random flap (surgical delay) if a longer flap is needed on marginal perfusion","Z-plasty for linear contractures crossing joints or webs (and to break up straight-line scars); rhombic/bilobed for cheek, temple, and nasal defects","If local tissue is insufficient or irradiated, escalate to regional/pedicled or free flaps"],"tech":["Advancement (V-Y, single/bipedicle), rotation (curvilinear arc with a back-cut/Burow triangle to release), and transposition (rhombic/Limberg ~60-degree, bilobed for convex nasal tip, Z-plasty 60-degree limbs for length gain)","Undermine in the correct plane to mobilize without devascularizing the subdermal plexus; keep flaps well-perfused, tension-off, and inset without kinking the pedicle","Design so the tension vector and final scars sit in RSTLs / subunit junction lines"],"after":["Monitor flap color, capillary refill, and warmth; avoid tension and hematoma; venous congestion (dusky, brisk refill) is the early failure mode of local flaps","Support with light dressings; nicotine cessation is essential to survival; revise/thin the flap electively at a later stage if bulky (e.g., bilobed dog-ears)","Massage and scar care once healed; sun protection to optimize final color match"],"pearls":["A 60-degree Z-plasty gains about 75% length - the go-to for releasing linear contractures and webs across joints","Flaps beat grafts when bone, tendon, or hardware is exposed, or the bed is irradiated - they bring their own blood supply","Design flaps to respect facial subunits and never tent a free margin (eyelid/ala/lip); borrow laxity from where it exists","Venous congestion, not arterial insufficiency, is the usual reason a local flap dies - watch for a dusky flap with brisk refill"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Local Flaps","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551503/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-pedicled-regional-flaps","name":"Pedicled & Regional Flaps (Angiosomes)","sec":"plastics","present":["A pedicled/regional flap transfers tissue on a named vascular pedicle to a nearby defect without dividing the blood supply - the reliable, no-microsurgery option for chest, back, head/neck, perineum, and extremity coverage","The ANGIOSOME concept (Taylor): the body is a mosaic of vascular territories fed by a source artery; flaps survive their own angiosome plus one adjacent territory across choke vessels - this predicts safe flap length","Workhorses: PECTORALIS MAJOR and latissimus (chest/head-neck), GLUTEUS/gracilis (perineum, pressure sores), gastrocnemius (knee/proximal-third leg), soleus (middle-third leg), and the reverse sural for the distal-third leg/heel"],"dx":["Match the defect location to a regional flap whose arc of rotation reaches it: proximal-third leg -> gastrocnemius, middle-third -> soleus, distal-third/ankle -> free flap or reverse sural","Classify muscle flaps by Mathes-Nahai vascular pattern (I-V), which predicts how much muscle survives on a given pedicle and where the flap can be based","Confirm the pedicle is intact (not injured by prior trauma/surgery/radiation) with exam +/- imaging before committing"],"tx":["Use a pedicled flap when a robust local option exists and microsurgery is unnecessary or unsafe; it is faster and needs no anastomosis but has a limited arc of rotation","Muscle/myocutaneous flaps fill dead space and fight infection (good for osteomyelitis, sternal wounds, pressure sores); fasciocutaneous flaps give thin, pliable, sensate-capable coverage","When no pedicled flap reaches (e.g., distal-third leg, large composite head/neck defects), convert to free tissue transfer"],"tech":["Elevate on the dominant pedicle per Mathes-Nahai; e.g., pectoralis major on the thoracoacromial pedicle tunneled to the neck; latissimus on the thoracodorsal; gastrocnemius medial/lateral heads for the knee","Preserve the pedicle during elevation, avoid tension/kinking at the pivot point, and inset without compressing the vessels; skin-graft the muscle surface if a myocutaneous paddle is not used","Reverse sural flap is raised distally based on retrograde perfusion via peroneal perforators for hindfoot/ankle coverage"],"after":["Position to avoid pedicle compression; monitor the flap and any skin paddle; offload/immobilize (e.g., knee immobilizer after gastrocnemius, non-weight-bearing for leg flaps)","Manage donor morbidity (functional loss is usually modest for gastrocnemius/latissimus); graft the donor or muscle surface as planned","Escalate promptly for congestion or pedicle kinking; late thinning/revision for bulky myocutaneous paddles"],"pearls":["Leg thirds map to flaps: proximal = gastrocnemius, middle = soleus, distal = free flap (or reverse sural) - a classic board reflex","Angiosomes explain why a flap survives its own territory plus one adjacent zone across choke vessels - design length accordingly","Muscle flaps obliterate dead space and bring antibiotics to infected wounds (sternum, pressure sores, osteomyelitis)","Pedicled flaps trade a wider arc of rotation for the simplicity of never dividing the blood supply"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Muscle Flaps","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563292/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-free-flap-microsurgery","name":"Free Tissue Transfer & Microsurgery Principles","sec":"plastics","present":["A FREE flap detaches tissue with its artery and vein(s) and re-anastomoses them to recipient vessels at a distant site - used when no local/regional option reaches or suffices (distal-third leg, composite head/neck, autologous breast, large oncologic defects)","Common free flaps: ANTEROLATERAL THIGH (ALT, the versatile fasciocutaneous workhorse), RADIAL FOREARM (thin/pliable, intraoral), FIBULA (vascularized bone for mandible/long-bone), LATISSIMUS (large muscle), and DIEP (abdominal skin/fat for breast)","The threat is anastomotic FAILURE: arterial thrombosis (pale, cool, no refill, no bleeding on pinprick) or the more common VENOUS thrombosis (blue/dusky, brisk refill, dark rapid bleeding) - both demand emergent take-back"],"dx":["Select recipient vessels of adequate caliber and inflow outside any zone of injury/radiation; image perforators (CTA) when needed (e.g., DIEP, fibula) and confirm a patent pedal arch (Allen/imaging) before harvesting a fibula or radial forearm","Continuous flap monitoring (clinical color/turgor/refill/temperature + Doppler, and implantable/optical monitors for buried flaps) to catch compromise EARLY, when salvage rates are highest","Distinguish arterial vs venous compromise clinically because it changes the operative fix"],"tx":["Meticulous microvascular anastomosis under the microscope; the FIRST 24-72h carry the highest thrombosis risk, so early recognition and take-back drive salvage","Treat compromise emergently: return to OR, evacuate hematoma, revise the anastomosis/thrombectomy, and consider thrombolysis; LEECHES can temporize pure venous congestion","Optimize perfusion: keep the patient warm, euvolemic, and normotensive; avoid pressors that clamp the pedicle when possible; treat the underlying compression/kink"],"tech":["Harvest on a defined perforator/pedicle (e.g., ALT on descending branch of lateral circumflex femoral; DIEP on deep inferior epigastric perforators; fibula on the peroneal vessels), then end-to-end or end-to-side anastomosis of artery and vein under magnification, often with a venous COUPLER","Preserve pedicle geometry (no kinking/twist/tension), flush with heparinized saline, and confirm patency (milking/flow) before inset","Perforator dissection (DIEP/ALT) spares the underlying muscle to reduce donor morbidity"],"after":["Flap checks q1h initially (color, temperature, capillary refill, Doppler signal); keep warm/hydrated, avoid pedicle compression (positioning, dressings, neck ties for head/neck flaps), and no nicotine","A congested (venous) flap turns blue with brisk refill; a white flap with no refill is arterial - either way, back to the OR; the first 72h are decisive","Donor-site care and rehab (e.g., splint/therapy after radial forearm; protected weight-bearing after fibula); staged debulking/revision later for contour"],"pearls":["Venous thrombosis is more common than arterial and looks BLUE with brisk refill; arterial failure looks WHITE with no refill and no pinprick bleeding","The first 24-72 hours are when free flaps fail - relentless monitoring and a low threshold to take back drive salvage","Check the pedal arch before harvesting a radial forearm (Allen test) or fibula - you cannot sacrifice the hand's or foot's dominant supply","Leeches buy time for a venously congested flap but do not fix the underlying anastomotic problem - and cover for Aeromonas"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Free Tissue Transfer / Flaps","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563243/"},{"t":"American Society for Reconstructive Microsurgery","u":"https://www.microsurg.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-tissue-expansion","name":"Tissue Expansion","sec":"plastics","present":["Tissue expansion generates extra skin of matched color, texture, thickness, and sensation by placing a silicone balloon under adjacent tissue and inflating it serially - ideal for scalp (burn alopecia, giant nevi), breast, and staged reconstruction where 'like replaces like'","Best where a stable platform underlies the expander (calvarium, ribs, ilium) and skin is not scarred/irradiated; poor over the lower leg and irradiated beds","Trades a multi-week, multi-visit course and a temporary deformity for superior tissue match versus a graft or distant flap"],"dx":["Plan the base over a firm bony platform, incisions along future flap edges/RSTLs, and expander size/port location (remote vs integrated) before implantation","Assess skin quality: avoid heavily scarred, infected, or irradiated skin, which necroses or extrudes under expansion","Estimate required advancement; expanded skin gains true new surface area plus mechanical creep/stretch"],"tx":["Serial saline injections (weekly, to patient tolerance/skin blanching) over 6-12 weeks until enough tissue is generated, then a second stage removes the expander and advances/transposes the flap","Complications: infection, EXTRUSION/exposure, deflation, and skin necrosis; a threatened expander is deflated and salvaged or removed","Not for emergent coverage - it is a planned, staged technique"],"tech":["Insert the expander through a remote incision into a precise subcutaneous/subgaleal (scalp) or submuscular (breast) pocket; place the fill port where it can be palpated and accessed safely away from the balloon","Inflate serially with saline via the port using aseptic technique until the skin blanches or the patient reports tightness; a capsule forms around the device","Second stage: capsulectomy/capsulotomy as needed, expander removal, and flap advancement to resurface the defect"],"after":["Weekly clinic inflations with wound/skin checks; teach the patient to watch for erythema, pain, or exposure (signs of infection/extrusion)","Protect the expanded site from trauma; the temporary bulge is expected and reversed at the second stage","After exchange, standard flap monitoring and scar care; some recoil of expanded tissue occurs, so slightly over-expand"],"pearls":["Tissue expansion is the gold standard for burn-scar ALOPECIA and giant congenital nevi of the scalp - it recreates hair-bearing skin no graft can match","Never expand irradiated or heavily scarred skin - it extrudes and necroses","A firm bony platform beneath the expander (skull, ribs) makes expansion efficient; soft mobile beds (leg) do not expand well","It is a two-stage, planned operation - useless for acute wound coverage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tissue Expansion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576390/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-cleft-lip","name":"Cleft Lip","sec":"plastics","present":["Cleft lip (+/- cleft palate) is a common congenital anomaly from failure of fusion of the medial nasal and maxillary prominences; it ranges from a microform/forme fruste notch to a complete cleft into the nostril floor, and may be unilateral (more often LEFT) or bilateral","Associated nasal deformity is the rule: on the cleft side the ala is flattened/displaced, the columella deviates, and the alveolus/philtrum are disrupted; feeding difficulty with an intact palate is usually manageable, unlike isolated cleft palate","Screen for a syndrome (e.g., Van der Woude - lip pits; 22q11 deletion) and associated anomalies; cleft LIP is more common in males, cleft PALATE alone in females"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (often on prenatal ultrasound); classify unilateral vs bilateral, complete vs incomplete, and document the associated nasal deformity and alveolar involvement","Evaluate feeding, weight gain, and airway; assess for associated anomalies and refer to genetics if syndromic features (Pierre Robin sequence, Van der Woude, 22q11)","Multidisciplinary cleft-team planning (surgery, speech, orthodontics, ENT/audiology, genetics)"],"tx":["Optimize feeding/growth first (specialized cleft nipples/bottles); consider PRESURGICAL NASOALVEOLAR MOLDING (NAM) or lip taping to narrow a wide/bilateral cleft before repair","Primary lip repair by the classic 'RULE OF 10s' guideline (~10 weeks old, ~10 lb, hemoglobin ~10) using a tension-reducing, philtrum-restoring design","Correct the nasal deformity primarily and stage alveolar bone grafting later; coordinate with palate repair timing"],"tech":["Rotation-advancement repair (Millard) reconstructs the philtral column and Cupid's bow; alternatives include Fisher anatomic subunit and, for wide clefts, Mohler modifications","Reapproximate the orbicularis oris muscle across the cleft (functional muscle continuity), restore the white roll/vermilion, and address the nasal cartilage (primary rhinoplasty/McComb suspension)","Bilateral repairs reconstruct a philtrum and columella (often with a banked fork flap or primary columellar lengthening)"],"after":["Protect the repair: arm/elbow immobilizers, no pacifiers/objects to the mouth, meticulous suture-line care, and resumed careful feeding; nasal stents/conformers maintain the corrected nostril shape","Cleft-team follow-up for speech, hearing (recurrent otitis media/effusions are common - many need tympanostomy tubes), dental/orthodontic care, and secondary revisions","Later stages: alveolar bone grafting (~mixed dentition, age 7-9), definitive rhinoplasty near skeletal maturity, and lip/scar revisions as needed"],"pearls":["Cleft lip is more common in boys and on the LEFT; isolated cleft palate is more common in girls - a favorite exam distinction","Reapproximating the orbicularis oris is the functional heart of the repair - skin closure alone leaves a dynamic deformity","Always look for a syndrome: lip pits = Van der Woude; think 22q11 (velocardiofacial) with cardiac/immune features","Cleft ear disease is universal - eustachian dysfunction means chronic effusions and the need for tubes and audiology"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cleft Lip and Palate","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547743/"},{"t":"CDC: Facts about Cleft Lip and Cleft Palate","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/about/cleft-lip-cleft-palate.html"}]},{"id":"plastics-cleft-palate-vpi","name":"Cleft Palate & Velopharyngeal Insufficiency","sec":"plastics","present":["Cleft palate results from failure of fusion of the palatal shelves; it disrupts the levator veli palatini sling, causing feeding difficulty (nasal regurgitation, poor suck), eustachian tube dysfunction with chronic middle-ear effusions, and, if unrepaired, HYPERNASAL speech","VELOPHARYNGEAL INSUFFICIENCY (VPI) is incomplete closure of the velopharyngeal port during speech -> hypernasal resonance, nasal air emission, and compensatory misarticulations; it may persist or arise AFTER palate repair (or after adenoidectomy)","Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia + glossoptosis + U-shaped cleft palate) presents with airway obstruction that dominates early management"],"dx":["Diagnose the cleft clinically; assess feeding, growth, and airway (especially Pierre Robin); audiology and otologic exam for effusions","Assess VPI with perceptual speech evaluation plus NASENDOSCOPY and/or videofluoroscopy to see the closure pattern and gap size (guides the operation)","Cleft-team, longitudinal follow-up for speech, hearing, dentition, and psychosocial needs"],"tx":["Repair the palate around 9-12 MONTHS (before meaningful speech develops) to restore the levator sling and separate oral/nasal cavities; balance early repair (better speech) against maxillary growth restriction","Manage ear disease aggressively (tympanostomy tubes, audiology) because effusions are near-universal and threaten hearing/speech","Treat persistent VPI after repair with speech therapy first, then a secondary VPI operation tailored to the gap/closure pattern"],"tech":["Palatoplasty reconstructs the levator sling: intravelar veloplasty, Furlow DOUBLE-OPPOSING Z-PLASTY (lengthens the palate and reorients the muscle), or two-flap (Bardach) / Von Langenbeck techniques","Secondary VPI surgery: PHARYNGEAL FLAP (for a large central gap with good lateral wall motion) or sphincter pharyngoplasty (for poor lateral wall motion); posterior wall augmentation for small gaps","Manage Pierre Robin airway before elective palate work (positioning, NPA, tongue-lip adhesion, or mandibular distraction)"],"after":["Protect the repair (soft diet, no straws/hard objects, arm restraints in infants); watch for oronasal FISTULA and bleeding; feeding support","Ongoing speech therapy, serial audiology, and orthodontic/dental care; monitor midface growth (repair can restrict maxillary growth -> later Le Fort I)","After pharyngeal flap, watch for obstructive sleep apnea and hyponasality (over-correction)"],"pearls":["Time palate repair ~9-12 months to catch speech development, accepting some maxillary growth restriction as the trade-off","The Furlow double-opposing Z-plasty both lengthens the palate and realigns the levator - a favorite for the muscular sling and VPI","VPI can appear AFTER adenoidectomy in a child with a submucous cleft - always examine for a bifid uvula and notched hard palate first","Pharyngeal flap fixes VPI but can cause obstructive sleep apnea - match the operation to the closure pattern seen on nasendoscopy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cleft Palate","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499970/"},{"t":"American Cleft Palate Craniofacial Association","u":"https://acpacares.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-craniosynostosis","name":"Craniosynostosis","sec":"plastics","present":["Premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures restricts skull growth PERPENDICULAR to the fused suture and drives compensatory growth parallel to it (Virchow's law), producing a predictable head shape","Single-suture patterns: SAGITTAL -> scaphocephaly (long, narrow); unilateral CORONAL -> anterior plagiocephaly (with the classic 'harlequin' orbit); METOPIC -> trigonocephaly (triangular forehead, hypotelorism); bilateral coronal -> brachycephaly; lambdoid (rare) -> posterior plagiocephaly","Distinguish from POSITIONAL plagiocephaly (deformational, from back-sleeping) which has a parallelogram shape with the ipsilateral ear pushed FORWARD and improves with repositioning/helmet - no fused suture"],"dx":["Physical exam of head shape, palpable ridging over the suture, and fontanelle; measure cephalic index; positional plagiocephaly is the key benign mimic","Confirm with CT/3D reconstruction (fused suture, sometimes low-dose or ultrasound in infancy); assess for raised intracranial pressure (especially multisuture/syndromic)","Screen for syndromes when multiple sutures fuse or with limb anomalies (Apert, Crouzon, Pfeiffer, Muenke - FGFR/TWIST mutations)"],"tx":["Surgical correction of true synostosis to relieve/prevent raised ICP and normalize shape; positional plagiocephaly is treated conservatively (repositioning, physiotherapy for torticollis, helmet)","Early (<3-6 months): minimally invasive ENDOSCOPIC strip craniectomy + postoperative helmet molding; later or complex cases: open CRANIAL VAULT REMODELING / fronto-orbital advancement","Multisuture/syndromic synostosis may need staged vault expansion, distraction, and midface advancement"],"tech":["Endoscopic suturectomy with wide strip craniectomy over the fused suture (minimal blood loss, short stay) followed by custom helmet therapy to guide growth","Open fronto-orbital advancement and cranial vault remodeling reshape the forehead/orbits and expand the vault; posterior vault DISTRACTION osteogenesis for large expansions","Meticulous hemostasis and blood-loss management are central because infants have low circulating volume"],"after":["Helmet therapy after endoscopic repair (months); serial head-shape and growth monitoring; watch for raised ICP (developmental regression, papilledema) especially in syndromic cases","Transfusion planning and close hemodynamic monitoring perioperatively; neurodevelopmental follow-up","Long-term craniofacial-team surveillance for reossification, relapse, and staged midface work in syndromic patients"],"pearls":["Positional plagiocephaly = parallelogram with the ear pushed FORWARD and no ridge; lambdoid synostosis = trapezoid with the ear pushed BACKWARD - do not operate on positional molding","Virchow's law: the skull can't grow across a fused suture, so it grows parallel to it - this predicts every deformity shape","Metopic synostosis gives a triangular forehead with hypotelorism (trigonocephaly); unilateral coronal gives the 'harlequin' orbit on X-ray","Multisuture or syndromic synostosis raises intracranial pressure - the operation is for the brain, not just the shape"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Craniosynostosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544366/"},{"t":"American Society of Maxillofacial Surgeons","u":"https://www.maxface.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-craniofacial-syndromes","name":"Craniofacial Syndromes & Microsomia","sec":"plastics","present":["Syndromic craniosynostoses share midface hypoplasia, exorbitism, and often airway compromise: CROUZON (FGFR2; no limb anomaly), APERT (FGFR2; symmetric syndactyly 'mitten hands'), PFEIFFER (broad thumbs/great toes), and MUENKE (FGFR3; unicoronal)","TREACHER COLLINS (TCOF1; mandibulofacial dysostosis) shows bilateral, symmetric malar/mandibular hypoplasia, down-slanting palpebral fissures, lower-lid colobomas, and microtia; PIERRE ROBIN SEQUENCE is micrognathia + glossoptosis + cleft palate with airway obstruction","CRANIOFACIAL MICROSOMIA (hemifacial microsomia / Goldenhar) is typically UNILATERAL underdevelopment of the ear, mandible, and soft tissues (+/- epibulbar dermoids, vertebral anomalies)"],"dx":["Recognize the pattern clinically (symmetric vs unilateral, limb findings, ear anomalies), confirm with genetics (FGFR1/2/3, TWIST1, TCOF1) and CT for the skeletal deformity","Prioritize AIRWAY and vision: exorbitism risks corneal exposure; midface hypoplasia and micrognathia cause obstructive apnea; assess hearing (atresia/ossicular anomalies)","Grade mandibular deficiency in microsomia (Pruzansky/Kaban) to plan reconstruction; multidisciplinary craniofacial team throughout"],"tx":["Sequence by function then form: secure airway and protect the cornea first; stage vault expansion, fronto-orbital advancement, and MIDFACE ADVANCEMENT (Le Fort III / monobloc, often by distraction) for exorbitism and airway","Treacher Collins: protect eyes (lid colobomas), address airway, then staged malar/orbital reconstruction (bone grafts) and microtia/ear work","Craniofacial microsomia: mandibular DISTRACTION or costochondral graft for the deficient ramus, occlusal correction, soft-tissue augmentation (fat grafting/free flap), and ear reconstruction"],"tech":["Le Fort III / monobloc advancement, often via DISTRACTION OSTEOGENESIS, moves the hypoplastic midface forward to correct exorbitism and expand the airway","Mandibular distraction osteogenesis lengthens the deficient ramus in microsomia/Pierre Robin; costochondral rib graft reconstructs the absent condyle/ramus","Autologous (rib) or prosthetic ear reconstruction; orbital/zygomatic bone grafting in Treacher Collins"],"after":["Airway surveillance (many need tracheostomy or benefit from distraction to avoid it); corneal lubrication/protection; hearing habilitation (bone-conduction aids, atresia repair)","Serial craniofacial-team follow-up through growth with staged osteotomies, orthognathic surgery near maturity, and psychosocial support","Distraction protocols (latency, activation, consolidation) with device care and imaging"],"pearls":["Apert = SYNDACTYLY (mitten hands) with FGFR2; Crouzon has the same midface but NORMAL limbs - the hands separate them on the exam","In syndromic synostosis the enemy is the airway and the exposed cornea from exorbitism - midface advancement (Le Fort III) treats both","Treacher Collins is bilateral and SYMMETRIC; craniofacial (hemifacial) microsomia is UNILATERAL - symmetry is the first branch point","Pierre Robin is a SEQUENCE (micrognathia -> glossoptosis -> cleft palate/airway obstruction), not a single-gene syndrome - manage the airway first"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Craniofacial Syndromes / Crouzon","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518992/"},{"t":"MedlinePlus: Craniofacial Anomalies","u":"https://medlineplus.gov"}]},{"id":"plastics-mandible-fractures","name":"Mandible Fractures","sec":"plastics","present":["The mandible is a ring, so it often fractures in TWO places; the most common sites are the CONDYLE, ANGLE (associated with third molars), body, and parasymphysis","Signs: malocclusion (the most sensitive clinical sign), trismus, a step-off/mobility, mental-nerve numbness (V3), a sublingual hematoma (Coleman sign), and a broken/loose tooth in the fracture line","A displaced/bilateral (esp. parasymphyseal 'flail') mandible fracture can cause airway obstruction as the tongue base loses support"],"dx":["Test occlusion and look for step-offs, mobility, gingival lacerations, and V3 sensory loss; check for associated cervical spine and other facial injuries","Imaging: CT face (panoramic radiograph if CT unavailable) to map all fractures (remember the second break)","Classify by site, favorable vs unfavorable (muscle pull), displacement, and whether teeth are in the line"],"tx":["Establish premorbid occlusion as the reconstructive goal; nondisplaced/favorable fractures may be treated closed with MAXILLOMANDIBULAR FIXATION (MMF); displaced/unfavorable and angle/body fractures usually need ORIF","Prophylactic antibiotics for open (tooth-bearing) fractures; manage the tooth in the line (extract if fractured/mobile/infected, otherwise often retained)","Most condylar fractures are treated CLOSED with a short period of MMF/guiding elastics and early mobilization; open reduction for specific indications (displacement into the fossa, foreign body, bilateral condyle with loss of height, inability to obtain occlusion)"],"tech":["ORIF via intraoral (or external) approach with load-bearing/load-sharing plates: Champy technique uses a monocortical miniplate along the external oblique ridge (tension band) for angle fractures; larger plates for comminuted/atrophic mandibles","Reestablish occlusion first with MMF (arch bars/screws) to set the reduction, then plate; avoid tooth roots and the inferior alveolar nerve","Comminuted/atrophic edentulous fractures need a load-BEARING reconstruction plate"],"after":["Soft/liquid diet, oral hygiene, and elastics/MMF as indicated; follow occlusion at intervals; watch for infection, nonunion, malunion, and hardware exposure","Monitor mental-nerve recovery; jaw physiotherapy after closed condylar treatment to prevent ankylosis and restore range of motion","In children, condylar fractures are usually treated closed with early mobilization to avoid growth disturbance/ankylosis"],"pearls":["Malocclusion is the most sensitive sign of a mandible fracture - the patient tells you their bite feels off","The mandible is a ring: find the second fracture, classically a condyle opposite an angle/body break","Most condylar fractures are treated CLOSED with early mobilization - open reduction risks the facial nerve and is reserved for specific indications","A bilateral parasymphyseal fracture can drop the tongue base and obstruct the airway - watch the airway first"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mandibular Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507705/"},{"t":"AO Surgery Reference (CMF)","u":"https://surgeryreference.aofoundation.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-midface-fractures","name":"Midface Fractures (Le Fort & ZMC)","sec":"plastics","present":["LE FORT fractures separate the midface from the skull base along predictable planes, ALL crossing the PTERYGOID plates: I (transverse, palate-bearing segment), II (pyramidal, through the nasofrontal region and orbital floor), III (craniofacial disjunction, through the zygomaticofrontal sutures and orbits)","The ZYGOMATICOMAXILLARY COMPLEX (ZMC / 'tripod') fracture disrupts the zygoma at its arch, infraorbital rim/floor, and zygomaticofrontal suture -> malar flattening, a palpable rim step, trismus (impingement on the coronoid), and V2 (infraorbital) numbness","High-energy midface trauma threatens the airway and comes with brisk bleeding and frequent concomitant intracranial/cervical/ocular injury"],"dx":["Test midface mobility (grasp the palate and rock), palpate rim step-offs, check V2 sensation, extraocular movements, visual acuity, and for CSF rhinorrhea (Le Fort II/III)","CT face with fine cuts and 3D reconstruction defines the pattern; a formal ophthalmology exam is mandatory with orbital involvement","Look for the classic ZMC quartet: malar flattening, infraorbital step, V2 hypesthesia, and trismus"],"tx":["Secure the airway and control hemorrhage first (anterior/posterior packing, reduction; angiography/embolization for refractory bleeding); repair after life threats are addressed","Restore the vertical and horizontal facial BUTTRESSES and reestablish occlusion; nondisplaced ZMC may be observed, displaced ZMC needs reduction and fixation at 1-3 points","Repair an associated orbital floor defect (see orbital fractures) and manage entrapment urgently"],"tech":["ORIF along the facial buttresses (zygomaticomaxillary, nasomaxillary, zygomaticofrontal, and pterygomaxillary) with miniplates via gingivobuccal, subciliary/transconjunctival, and lateral brow/upper-lid approaches; reestablish MMF for Le Fort patterns with occlusal involvement","Reduce the ZMC (e.g., Gillies temporal or intraoral approach) and fixate at the ZF suture, infraorbital rim, and zygomaticomaxillary buttress as needed; isolated arch fractures often need only reduction","Reconstruct comminuted buttresses with bone graft; confirm reduction/occlusion intraoperatively"],"after":["Sinus precautions (no nose-blowing) and antibiotics for sinus-involving fractures; monitor vision (retrobulbar hematoma is a surgical emergency), occlusion, and V2 recovery","Soft diet/oral hygiene with occlusal fixation; follow for malunion, enophthalmos, malar asymmetry, and infraorbital numbness","Watch for CSF leak resolution in Le Fort II/III and treat persistent leaks"],"pearls":["Every Le Fort fracture crosses the PTERYGOID plates - that is the unifying rule (I palate, II pyramidal, III craniofacial disjunction)","The ZMC 'tripod' gives malar flattening + infraorbital rim step + V2 numbness + trismus - reconstruct the buttresses, not just the cosmetic bump","Airway and hemorrhage come before fixation in high-energy midface trauma; posterior epistaxis can be exsanguinating","New visual loss with proptosis and a tense orbit after midface trauma is a retrobulbar hematoma - lateral canthotomy/cantholysis now"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Le Fort Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526060/"},{"t":"AO Surgery Reference (CMF)","u":"https://surgeryreference.aofoundation.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-orbital-fractures","name":"Orbital Fractures","sec":"plastics","present":["A BLOWOUT fracture occurs when a sudden rise in intraorbital pressure fractures the thin orbital FLOOR (into the maxillary sinus) and/or MEDIAL WALL (lamina papyracea), potentially herniating fat and the inferior rectus","Signs: periorbital ecchymosis/edema, ENOPHTHALMOS (may be masked early by swelling), infraorbital (V2) numbness, and diplopia; entrapment causes restricted UPWARD gaze","The pediatric 'WHITE-EYED BLOWOUT' (trapdoor fracture with true muscle entrapment) can have minimal bruising but marked restriction, pain, and the OCULOCARDIAC REFLEX (bradycardia/nausea) - a surgical emergency"],"dx":["Full eye exam: visual acuity, pupils, extraocular movements, and a FORCED DUCTION test to distinguish true entrapment from edema; assess for enophthalmos/hypoglobus and globe injury/rupture (an absolute exclusion for orbital manipulation until cleared)","CT orbits (coronal cuts) sizes the defect, shows herniated contents, and reveals muscle entrapment; ophthalmology consult is mandatory","Rule out associated globe rupture, retrobulbar hematoma, and traumatic optic neuropathy"],"tx":["Emergent repair for muscle entrapment (especially the pediatric trapdoor with oculocardiac reflex) to prevent muscle ischemia and permanent diplopia","Non-urgent repair (within ~2 weeks, after swelling settles) for large defects (>50% floor), significant enophthalmos, or persistent symptomatic diplopia; small asymptomatic fractures are observed","Sinus precautions and treat associated injuries; a tense proptotic orbit with vision loss (retrobulbar hematoma) needs immediate lateral canthotomy/cantholysis"],"tech":["Approach the floor via transconjunctival (+/- lateral canthotomy) or subciliary incision; release herniated contents, reconstruct the floor/medial wall with a thin titanium mesh/porous polyethylene or bone graft, and confirm free ductions","Intraoperative forced duction before and after implant placement confirms release of entrapment; navigation/intraoperative imaging helps position large-defect implants","Avoid over/under-projection of the implant to prevent iatrogenic enophthalmos or optic-nerve compression"],"after":["Sinus precautions (no nose-blowing), head elevation, and vision checks (post-op retrobulbar hematoma is an emergency); monitor diplopia, enophthalmos, and infraorbital sensation","Ophthalmology follow-up; residual diplopia often improves over weeks as edema resolves; persistent diplopia may need prisms or strabismus surgery","Watch for implant infection/migration and lower-lid malposition (ectropion/entropion) from the approach"],"pearls":["The pediatric WHITE-EYED BLOWOUT hides a trapped muscle behind a quiet-looking eye - restriction + pain + bradycardia (oculocardiac reflex) is an operative emergency, don't wait for swelling to settle","Forced duction testing separates true entrapment from mere edema and guides urgency","Always exclude globe rupture, retrobulbar hematoma, and optic neuropathy before touching the orbit - vision trumps cosmesis","Enophthalmos may be masked by early swelling - re-examine after edema resolves before deciding against repair"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Orbital Floor (Blowout) Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK599521/"},{"t":"AO Surgery Reference (CMF)","u":"https://surgeryreference.aofoundation.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nasal-noe-frontal-fractures","name":"Nasal, NOE & Frontal Sinus Fractures","sec":"plastics","present":["NASAL bone fractures are the most common facial fracture: deformity, epistaxis, and swelling; the must-not-miss is a SEPTAL HEMATOMA (boggy bluish septal swelling) which, if undrained, causes cartilage necrosis and a saddle-nose deformity or septal abscess","NASO-ORBITO-ETHMOID (NOE) fractures disrupt the medial canthal attachment -> TELECANTHUS (widened intercanthal distance), a flattened/saddled nasal dorsum, and often CSF rhinorrhea; the bowstring test detects a detached medial canthal tendon","FRONTAL SINUS fractures involve the anterior and/or posterior table; posterior-table/dural involvement risks CSF leak, meningitis, and late mucocele"],"dx":["Always examine the nasal septum for a hematoma; assess for CSF rhinorrhea (halo/ring sign, beta-2-transferrin), and check medial canthal position (intercanthal distance ~ half the interpupillary distance)","CT face/sinuses defines NOE and frontal sinus patterns, posterior-table displacement, and nasofrontal outflow tract (duct) involvement","Bimanual/bowstring exam tests medial canthal tendon integrity in suspected NOE injury"],"tx":["DRAIN a septal hematoma immediately (incision + packing) to save the cartilage; reduce simple nasal fractures closed within ~1-2 weeks (before healing sets), with septorhinoplasty later for residual deformity","NOE: ORIF with medial CANTHOPEXY/transnasal canthopexy to restore intercanthal width and nasal projection (often needs a dorsal bone graft)","Frontal sinus: isolated/nondisplaced anterior-table -> observe/repair for contour; nasofrontal duct injury or displaced posterior table -> sinus obliteration or CRANIALIZATION with repair of any dural tear/CSF leak"],"tech":["Closed nasal reduction with elevators and external splinting; septal hematoma drainage with a small incision, evacuation, and quilting/packing to prevent reaccumulation","NOE ORIF with plating of the fragments and transnasal wiring/canthopexy to reposition the medial canthal tendons; dorsal (calvarial/rib) bone graft for nasal support","Frontal sinus cranialization (remove the posterior table, exenterate mucosa, repair dura) or obliteration (remove all mucosa, plug the duct, fill the sinus); anterior-table ORIF for contour"],"after":["Reassess for reaccumulated septal hematoma; nasal splint 1-2 weeks; sinus precautions and antibiotics where indicated; monitor for persistent CSF leak","NOE/frontal follow-up for telecanthus correction, nasal projection, and LATE mucocele/mucopyocele (can present years later) after frontal sinus injury","Counsel that nasal deformity/obstruction may need later septorhinoplasty once healed"],"pearls":["Never discharge a nasal fracture without inspecting for a SEPTAL HEMATOMA - miss it and the cartilage necroses into a saddle-nose or abscess","NOE fractures widen the intercanthal distance (telecanthus) - the medial canthal tendon is the key structure to reattach","A displaced posterior-table frontal sinus fracture or duct injury means CSF leak risk and late mucocele - obliterate or cranialize, don't just plate the front","CSF rhinorrhea after midface/NOE/frontal trauma is confirmed by beta-2-transferrin, not the halo sign alone"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nasal Fracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537265/"},{"t":"AO Surgery Reference (CMF)","u":"https://surgeryreference.aofoundation.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-microtia-ear-reconstruction","name":"Microtia & Ear Reconstruction / Otoplasty","sec":"plastics","present":["MICROTIA is congenital underdevelopment of the auricle (graded I-IV, IV = anotia), usually unilateral and right-sided, often with aural atresia and conductive hearing loss; it may be isolated or syndromic (craniofacial microsomia, Treacher Collins)","Acquired ear deformities follow trauma, burns, or tumor resection; an untreated auricular HEMATOMA organizes into a 'CAULIFLOWER EAR'","PROMINENT ('bat') EAR results from an absent antihelical fold and/or a deep/overdeveloped concha - a normal-size but protruding ear addressed by otoplasty"],"dx":["Grade microtia and assess the contralateral ear as a template; audiology (bone- vs air-conduction) and CT temporal bone for atresia/ossicular status; screen for associated syndromes","For prominent ear, define the deformity (lack of antihelical fold vs conchal excess vs both) to plan the correction","Coordinate hearing habilitation with ENT/audiology before or alongside auricular reconstruction"],"tx":["Autologous microtia reconstruction (costal cartilage framework, Brent/Nagata staged technique) is typically begun around AGE 6-10 when the rib cartilage is adequate and the contralateral ear near adult size; alternatives are a porous polyethylene (Medpor) framework or a prosthetic ear","Address hearing separately (bone-conduction device; atresiaplasty in selected cases) - reconstruct the auricle before atresia surgery so incisions/blood supply are preserved","Drain an auricular hematoma promptly with a bolster/quilting to prevent cauliflower ear"],"tech":["Fabricate a 3D cartilage framework from costal cartilage carved to mirror the normal ear, implant it in a skin pocket, then stage lobule transposition, tragus construction, and elevation with a temporoparietal fascia flap + skin graft","Otoplasty: Mustarde horizontal mattress sutures create the antihelical fold, Furnas conchomastoid sutures set back an overprojected concha, +/- conchal cartilage excision - cartilage-sparing suture techniques are favored","Prosthetic reconstruction uses osseointegrated implants to anchor a custom auricular prosthesis"],"after":["Protect the framework from pressure/trauma; staged operations months apart; sports/contact precautions; long-term monitoring of the cartilage graft and skin coverage","After otoplasty: a supportive head dressing then a headband at night for weeks; watch for hematoma, suture extrusion, chondritis, and recurrence/over-correction (telephone-ear deformity)","Continue audiologic support; counsel families on the multi-stage timeline for microtia"],"pearls":["Time autologous microtia repair to when costal cartilage is sufficient (~age 6-10) and reconstruct the AURICLE before any atresia/hearing surgery to protect the skin envelope","Drain an auricular hematoma and bolster it - an undrained one becomes a permanent cauliflower ear","Otoplasty recreates the missing ANTIHELICAL FOLD (Mustarde sutures) and sets back an excess concha (Furnas) - it addresses the deformity, not the ear size","Microtia is usually right-sided and unilateral and demands a syndrome screen (craniofacial microsomia, Treacher Collins) plus audiology"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Microtia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562327/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-facial-nerve-reanimation","name":"Facial Nerve Palsy & Facial Reanimation","sec":"plastics","present":["Facial paralysis (CN VII) causes brow ptosis, incomplete eye closure (LAGOPHTHALMOS with corneal exposure), midface droop, and oral incompetence; etiologies include Bell palsy, trauma, tumor (parotid/skull base), iatrogenic, and congenital (Mobius)","The reconstructive strategy hinges on TIME and NERVE availability: recent injury with a viable distal nerve/muscle (<~12-18 months) favors nerve procedures; long-standing paralysis with muscle atrophy needs new muscle (free functional transfer) or static support","The EYE is the priority - corneal exposure threatens vision and must be protected immediately regardless of the reanimation plan"],"dx":["Localize and grade (House-Brackmann); distinguish upper-motor-neuron (forehead spared) from lower-motor-neuron (forehead involved) palsy; assess completeness, duration, and cause","Electrodiagnostics (EMG/NCS/ENoG) gauge the viability of the native musculature and prognosis; imaging for tumor/trauma along the nerve course","Determine whether the proximal facial nerve and distal muscles are still usable - this drives the entire algorithm"],"tx":["Protect the eye first: lubrication, taping, and procedures for lagophthalmos - upper-lid GOLD/PLATINUM WEIGHT, lower-lid tightening (canthoplasty/tarsal strip), and brow lift","Restore a smile: with a recent viable proximal stump and distal targets, do direct repair/nerve graft or NERVE TRANSFERS (masseteric-to-facial, cross-facial nerve graft); for chronic paralysis with dead muscle, use FREE FUNCTIONAL MUSCLE TRANSFER (gracilis) powered by the masseteric nerve or a cross-facial graft","Static procedures (fascia lata slings, static suspension) reposition resting tone when dynamic reanimation is not feasible; temporalis tendon transfer offers a regional dynamic option"],"tech":["Cross-facial nerve grafting (sural graft from the contralateral VII) can power a delayed free gracilis for a spontaneous, emotion-linked smile; the masseteric (V3) nerve gives strong, reliable excursion but a learned smile","Free gracilis transfer: inset the muscle from the oral commissure to the zygomatic arch, anastomose vessels, and coapt the motor nerve (masseteric or cross-facial graft)","Static slings (fascia lata/tendon) suspend the commissure; temporalis tendon transfer (lengthening myoplasty) reroutes muscle to the oral commissure"],"after":["Ongoing corneal protection until eye closure is restored; facial neuromuscular RETRAINING/biofeedback is central to outcomes after nerve/muscle procedures (reinnervation takes many months)","Serial follow-up for smile excursion, symmetry, and synkinesis; targeted chemodenervation (botulinum toxin) balances the face and treats synkinesis","Counsel on the long timeline (months for reinnervation) and staged nature of cross-facial + free-muscle approaches"],"pearls":["Forehead SPARED = central (UMN) lesion; forehead INVOLVED = peripheral (VII) lesion - the first fork in facial palsy","Protect the cornea before anything else - a gold-weight upper lid and lower-lid tightening prevent exposure keratopathy that can blind","Masseteric nerve gives powerful, reliable smile excursion; a cross-facial graft gives a spontaneous, emotional smile but is weaker - trade power for spontaneity","After ~12-18 months the native facial muscles are gone - you must import new muscle (free gracilis), not just reroute a nerve"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Facial Nerve Palsy / Reanimation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549815/"},{"t":"American Academy of Facial Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery","u":"https://www.aafprs.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-head-neck-reconstruction","name":"Head & Neck Oncologic Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["After ablative resection of head/neck cancer, reconstruction restores form and the intertwined FUNCTIONS of speech, swallowing, mastication, and airway; the defect (mucosal, composite bone, through-and-through) and prior/planned radiation dictate the method","Free flaps are the modern workhorse: RADIAL FOREARM (thin, pliable - floor of mouth, tongue, pharynx), ANTEROLATERAL THIGH (bulkier soft tissue), FIBULA (segmental mandible/maxilla with dental implants), and scapula (composite bone + skin)","Reconstruction must anticipate adjuvant radiation, which stiffens tissue and imperils healing - so bring in well-vascularized, non-irradiated tissue"],"dx":["Define the defect in three dimensions (mucosa, bone, skin) and the functional units lost; assess recipient vessels (prior neck dissection/radiation reduces options) and donor sites (Allen test before radial forearm; pedal arch before fibula)","Plan with the ablative team and consider dental rehabilitation (osseointegrated implants) when using fibula/scapula for the jaw","Nutritional and airway planning (many need a temporary tracheostomy and feeding access)"],"tx":["Reconstruct 'like with like': soft-tissue mucosal defects with fasciocutaneous free flaps, segmental mandible/maxilla with vascularized bone (fibula) to allow dental implants and withstand radiation","Preserve/restore the swallowing conduit and oral competence; use flap bulk judiciously (too much impairs tongue mobility)","Coordinate timing with adjuvant therapy; well-vascularized flaps tolerate postoperative radiation far better than local tissue"],"tech":["Osteotomize the fibula to contour the neomandible along preplanned (often virtual-surgical-planning/3D-printed cutting-guide) angles, plate it, and anastomose the peroneal pedicle; inset the skin paddle for mucosal lining","Radial forearm/ALT free flaps line intraoral/pharyngeal defects; two flaps or chimeric flaps address through-and-through composite defects","Microvascular anastomosis to neck vessels (facial, superior thyroid, external carotid branches; internal jugular/branches for veins)"],"after":["Free-flap monitoring, tracheostomy and feeding-tube care, and early speech/swallow therapy; expect and manage the effects of adjuvant radiation on the reconstruction","Dental rehabilitation with implants in vascularized bone; long-term surveillance for tumor recurrence and functional outcomes","Manage fistula, flap compromise, and donor-site morbidity (splint/therapy for radial forearm; protected weight-bearing for fibula)"],"pearls":["Vascularized FIBULA is the standard for segmental mandible reconstruction because it takes dental implants and tolerates radiation - non-vascularized bone grafts fail in irradiated fields","Check the pedal arch (fibula) and do an Allen test (radial forearm) BEFORE harvest - never sacrifice the dominant supply to a limb","Reconstruction is judged by SPEECH and SWALLOWING, not just wound closure - flap choice balances lining, bulk, and tongue mobility","Plan for radiation from the start: bring in healthy, well-perfused tissue because irradiated local tissue heals poorly"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Free Flaps in Head and Neck Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576418/"},{"t":"American Society for Reconstructive Microsurgery","u":"https://www.microsurg.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nasal-reconstruction","name":"Nasal Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["Nasal defects (usually after Mohs excision of skin cancer, or trauma/burns) are reconstructed by the AESTHETIC SUBUNIT principle - the nose divides into dorsum, sidewalls, tip, alae, soft-triangle, and columella, and replacing a whole subunit gives better scars than patching part of one","The nose has THREE layers - lining, structural support (cartilage/bone), and skin cover - and a durable result must restore all three; missing support leads to collapse, notching, and airway obstruction","Skin quality varies by zone: thick sebaceous skin over the tip/ala vs thin mobile skin over the dorsum/sidewall guides the reconstructive choice"],"dx":["Analyze the defect by subunit(s) involved, depth (which of the three layers are missing), and size; confirm clear oncologic margins before definitive reconstruction","Choose the reconstruction to match skin color/texture and to rebuild support (cartilage grafts) and lining as needed","Plan staging (forehead flap is multi-stage) and set patient expectations"],"tx":["Small superficial defects: secondary intention (concave surfaces), primary closure, skin graft, or a local flap (bilobed for the lower third, dorsal nasal/rotation for the upper two-thirds)","Larger/full-thickness defects, especially of the tip/ala: the PARAMEDIAN FOREHEAD FLAP (axial, on the supratrochlear artery) provides ideal color/texture match, combined with cartilage grafts for support and a lining flap for the inner layer","Rebuild support with septal, conchal, or costal cartilage grafts even when native alar cartilage was uninvolved (alar rim grafts prevent notching/collapse)"],"tech":["Bilobed transposition flap for convex tip defects; paramedian forehead flap raised on the supratrochlear vessels, transposed and later divided/inset at a second (and sometimes third thinning) stage 3 weeks apart","Reconstruct lining with turn-in flaps, mucosal flaps, or a folded forehead flap; place cartilage batten/alar rim grafts for structural support","Restore subunit boundaries so incisions fall in the natural creases (alar groove, nasofacial sulcus)"],"after":["Multi-stage forehead flap care: protect the pedicle, division at ~3 weeks, and staged thinning/contouring; the forehead donor closes primarily and heals with a favorable vertical scar","Watch for flap congestion, cartilage-graft warping/exposure, and airway patency; dermabrasion/revision refines the final contour and color match","Sun protection and scar care; counsel on the multi-visit timeline"],"pearls":["Reconstruct by SUBUNIT: if more than ~50% of a convex subunit (tip/ala) is missing, resurface the whole subunit so the scars hide in the borders","Restore all THREE layers - lining, cartilage support, and cover - or the reconstructed nose collapses and obstructs","The paramedian FOREHEAD FLAP (supratrochlear artery) is the gold standard for large tip/alar defects for its unmatched color/texture match","Add alar rim/batten cartilage grafts even when the native cartilage was intact - they prevent late notching and valve collapse"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nasal Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576417/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-lip-cheek-eyelid-reconstruction","name":"Lip, Cheek & Eyelid Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["LIP reconstruction must restore oral competence, the vermilion/white-roll, and the sphincter; defects are sized as a fraction of lip width because that determines the technique, and the commissure and philtrum are key landmarks","EYELID reconstruction protects the globe and tear film; the lid has an ANTERIOR lamella (skin/orbicularis) and POSTERIOR lamella (tarsus/conjunctiva), and at least one lamella must be vascularized - you cannot graft both layers over each other","CHEEK defects are resurfaced by aesthetic zones (medial/suborbital, preauricular/lateral, buccomandibular) using the abundant local laxity, most often after skin-cancer excision"],"dx":["Lip: measure the horizontal defect as a percentage of lip length (guides primary closure vs local lip-switch vs regional flap) and note vermilion/commissure involvement","Eyelid: define full- vs partial-thickness and which lamella is missing; assess the canthal tendons, lacrimal system, and lower-lid laxity","Cheek: match the reconstruction to the zone's skin color/texture and available laxity; confirm oncologic margins first"],"tx":["Lip: defects up to ~1/3 (upper) to ~1/2 (lower) close primarily as a wedge; larger defects use lip-switch flaps (ABBE for philtrum, ESTLANDER for commissure) or advancement (Karapandzic keeps innervation/sphincter, Bernard-Burow for very large defects)","Eyelid: small full-thickness lid margin defects close directly (+/- lateral cantholysis); larger defects reconstruct each lamella (e.g., a TARSOCONJUNCTIVAL flap - Hughes - for the posterior lamella plus a skin graft/flap anteriorly), keeping one lamella vascularized","Cheek: primary closure/local flaps (cervicofacial advancement-rotation flap for large defects) matching subunit boundaries"],"tech":["Abbe (lip-switch) flap pedicled on the labial artery to reconstruct the philtrum, divided at ~2-3 weeks; Karapandzic circumoral advancement preserves neurovascular supply and the sphincter; Estlander flap rebuilds the commissure (may need later commissuroplasty)","Hughes tarsoconjunctival flap from the upper lid reconstructs the lower-lid posterior lamella (a two-stage, lid-sharing procedure) with an anterior skin graft/flap; Tenzel semicircular flap for moderate lid-margin defects","Cervicofacial flap elevated in a subcutaneous/sub-SMAS plane for large cheek defects; place incisions in the preauricular crease and nasofacial sulcus"],"after":["Lip: soft diet, oral hygiene, staged flap division (Abbe/Estlander) at 2-3 weeks; monitor competence, drooling, and microstomia; later commissuroplasty/revision","Eyelid: protect the cornea; the Hughes flap temporarily occludes vision until division (avoid in monocular patients/young children who risk amblyopia); watch for lid retraction, ectropion, and lash loss","Cheek: monitor flap perfusion, hematoma, and lower-lid position (cervicofacial flaps can pull the lower lid into ectropion) - support with canthopexy if needed"],"pearls":["Lip defects are sized as a FRACTION of lip width: up to ~1/3-1/2 close primarily; bigger ones need Abbe (philtrum), Estlander (commissure), or Karapandzic (preserves the sphincter and its nerves)","Eyelid rule: reconstruct one lamella with a graft and the OTHER with a vascularized flap - you cannot stack two grafts and expect survival","The Hughes tarsoconjunctival flap temporarily blocks the eye - avoid it in a one-eyed adult or in young children (amblyopia risk)","Cervicofacial cheek flaps notoriously pull the lower lid into ectropion - support the lid (canthopexy) at the time of reconstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lip Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585123/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-scalp-calvarial-reconstruction","name":"Scalp & Calvarial Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["Scalp defects follow tumor excision (SCC/BCC/melanoma), trauma, burns, and radiation; the scalp is inelastic (galea limits stretch) yet richly vascularized, so small defects need local rearrangement while large or radiated defects need flaps","The reconstructive question is whether the PERICRANIUM is intact (a graftable bed) or bone/hardware/dura is exposed (needs a flap); calvarial defects additionally need bony coverage of the brain","Radiated scalp and exposed calvarium/dura are the difficult scenarios that push reconstruction to regional or free flaps"],"dx":["Assess defect size, whether pericranium is present, exposure of bone/dura/hardware, prior radiation, and oncologic margins; image the calvarium/dura for full-thickness (bone) defects","Determine if a skin graft will take (needs pericranium or vascularized bed) or a flap is required (exposed bone without pericranium, dura, or irradiated bed)","Plan bony reconstruction (cranioplasty) separately from soft-tissue cover for full-thickness defects"],"tx":["Small defects: primary closure with galeal scoring, or local scalp rotation/advancement/transposition flaps (orticochea/pinwheel) that recruit the scalp's laxity","Bed with pericranium: SKIN GRAFT is a reasonable simple option; exposed bone with pericranium removed can be burred to bleeding diploe to granulate before grafting","Large/composite/irradiated defects: regional (temporoparietal, trapezius) or FREE FLAPS (latissimus dorsi + skin graft, ALT) for durable coverage; reconstruct calvarial bone with autologous bone or alloplastic cranioplasty (titanium/PEEK) - staged after infection/radiation risk is addressed"],"tech":["Galeal scoring (parallel incisions in the galea perpendicular to the tension vector) gains length; multiple rotation/transposition flaps (pinwheel) close larger central defects","Free latissimus dorsi flap with an overlying skin graft covers extensive scalp defects and irradiated wounds; tissue expansion recreates hair-bearing scalp for burn alopecia","Cranioplasty with split calvarial bone graft or custom alloplastic implant (titanium mesh/PEEK) restores calvarial contour and protection"],"after":["Flap/graft monitoring; drains to prevent subgaleal hematoma; watch for infection over hardware/cranioplasty and CSF leak with dural involvement","Delay or stage cranioplasty when infection/radiation risk is high; long-term surveillance for tumor recurrence and implant complications (exposure, infection)","Hair-bearing reconstruction (expansion) for alopecia; scar and sun care"],"pearls":["If the pericranium is intact, a skin graft usually works; if bone/dura/hardware is exposed or the bed is irradiated, you need a flap","Galeal scoring buys length in the inelastic scalp - the galea, not the skin, is what limits closure","The free LATISSIMUS + skin graft is the workhorse for large or irradiated scalp defects","Reconstruct soft tissue and bone as separate problems - a cranioplasty under a poorly vascularized or infected cover will fail"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Scalp Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526114/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-flexor-tendon-injuries","name":"Flexor Tendon Injuries & Rehab","sec":"plastics","present":["Flexor tendon lacerations (usually volar hand/finger wounds) present with loss of active flexion; test FDP (isolated DIP flexion) and FDS (isolated PIP flexion with the other fingers held extended) separately for each digit","ZONE II (Bunnell's 'no man's land', from the A1 pulley to the FDS insertion) is the classic problem zone where FDP and FDS run together in the tight fibro-osseous sheath - adhesions and re-rupture are common","Partial lacerations can still glide; a flexor tendon that has retracted requires retrieval, and a lacerated tendon at the wrist may implicate the median/ulnar nerve and artery"],"dx":["Examine resting cascade (a lacerated tendon breaks the normal flexion cascade), test FDS and FDP independently, and assess digital nerve (two-point discrimination) and vascular status distal to the wound","Explore the wound in a bloodless field; the tendon ends may retract far from the skin laceration (finger position at injury matters)","Classify by zone (I-V) - zone determines repair strategy, pulley management, and rehab"],"tx":["Primary repair (ideally within days, up to ~2-3 weeks) with a core suture technique; PRESERVE the A2 and A4 PULLEYS to prevent bowstringing (venting/partial release of other pulleys is acceptable to allow gliding)","A strong core suture (4- to 6-strand) plus an epitendinous suture allows EARLY controlled MOTION rehab, which reduces adhesions","Zone I FDP avulsion ('jersey finger') is repaired to bone (suture anchor/pullout); delayed presentation or a segmental gap may need staged reconstruction with a tendon graft (Hunter rod)"],"tech":["Multi-strand core suture (e.g., modified Kessler/cruciate, 4-6 strands with a 3-0/4-0 suture) that crosses the repair, buttressed by a running epitendinous 6-0 suture for a smooth gliding surface and added strength","Retrieve retracted proximal ends gently (avoid the 'multiple grasping' that scars the tendon); repair FDP and, when feasible, FDS; preserve A2/A4","For jersey finger, reattach FDP to the distal phalanx (bone anchor or button/pullout suture)"],"after":["EARLY controlled mobilization protocols (place-and-hold or early active motion in a dorsal blocking splint) balance gliding against re-rupture; strong multi-strand repairs enable early active motion","Hand therapy is essential; monitor for the two main failures - RE-RUPTURE (too much load early) and ADHESIONS/stiffness (too little motion)","Tenolysis is considered months later for adhesions that limit gliding after the repair has healed and therapy has plateaued"],"pearls":["Zone II is 'no man's land' - FDP and FDS crowd the sheath, so adhesions and re-rupture are the enemies; a strong repair plus early controlled motion is the modern answer","PRESERVE the A2 and A4 pulleys - lose them and the tendon bowstrings, robbing flexion power and arc","Jersey finger = FDP avulsion (often the ring finger grabbing a jersey) - repair to bone; the retracted stump may sit in the palm","Test FDS by holding the other fingers extended (blocking FDP) - isolated PIP flexion confirms an intact FDS"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Flexor Tendon Injuries","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526009/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-extensor-tendon-injuries","name":"Extensor Tendon Injuries (Mallet & Boutonniere)","sec":"plastics","present":["Extensor injuries are grouped into 9 ZONES (odd zones over joints); the classic patterns are MALLET (zone I, terminal tendon at the DIP) and BOUTONNIERE (zone III, central slip at the PIP)","MALLET FINGER: forced flexion of an extended DIP disrupts the terminal tendon -> DIP droop and loss of active DIP extension; may be tendinous or a bony avulsion","BOUTONNIERE: central slip rupture lets the lateral bands slip volarly, buttonholing the PIP into flexion with DIP hyperextension - the deformity may be delayed, so a fresh PIP dorsal injury with weak active PIP extension is guilty until proven otherwise"],"dx":["Examine active extension at each joint; the Elson test detects an acute central slip injury (weak PIP extension with fixed DIP hyperextension when the PIP is flexed over a table edge)","Radiographs assess bony mallet (avulsion fragment, DIP subluxation) and other fractures","Zone and open-vs-closed status determine splinting vs surgery"],"tx":["Most CLOSED mallet fingers: continuous DIP EXTENSION SPLINTING for 6-8 weeks (the DIP must not flex during this time, or the clock restarts); surgery for large bony fragments with joint subluxation or open injuries","Acute closed boutonniere/central slip injury: splint the PIP in EXTENSION (leaving DIP free for active flexion) for ~4-6 weeks; open or displaced injuries need repair","Open extensor lacerations are repaired (extensors are thin/flat - use a core + epitendinous or figure-of-eight technique) followed by protected motion"],"tech":["Extensor repair with figure-of-eight or Kessler-type core suture appropriate to the flat tendon; central slip reattachment to the middle phalanx base (suture anchor) for zone III injuries","Bony mallet with subluxation: extension-block pinning or ORIF of the fragment; K-wire DIP fixation as an alternative to external splinting","Sagittal band repair for zone V extensor subluxation ('boxer's knuckle')"],"after":["Mallet: uninterrupted DIP extension splinting 6-8 weeks then night splinting; boutonniere: PIP extension splint with active DIP flexion to keep lateral bands mobile","Hand therapy with relative-motion splinting for repaired extensors allows early protected motion; educate that a single episode of DIP flexion in a mallet resets healing","Watch for extensor lag, PIP stiffness, and swan-neck/boutonniere progression; late reconstruction for chronic deformities"],"pearls":["Closed mallet finger is treated by 6-8 weeks of UNINTERRUPTED DIP extension splinting - if the patient flexes the DIP even once, the count starts over","A dorsal PIP injury with weak active PIP extension is a central slip (boutonniere) rupture until proven otherwise - splint the PIP in extension early to prevent a fixed deformity","In boutonniere splinting, keep the DIP FREE for active flexion to draw the lateral bands back dorsally","Extensor tendons are thin and flat - repairs are weaker and prone to lag, so relative-motion splinting and therapy matter"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mallet Finger","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562234/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-hand-fractures","name":"Metacarpal & Phalangeal Fractures","sec":"plastics","present":["Hand fractures are common; the key clinical checks are ROTATIONAL MALALIGNMENT (fingers should point toward the scaphoid on flexion - any scissoring/overlap is malrotation) and angulation, which are far more important than a millimeter of shortening","BOXER'S FRACTURE (5th metacarpal neck) tolerates significant volar angulation because of MCP mobility; more radial metacarpals tolerate less","Intra-articular fractures (e.g., Bennett/Rolando at the thumb CMC base, condylar phalangeal fractures) need anatomic joint restoration"],"dx":["Assess rotation with the fingers flexed (look for scissoring), angulation, shortening, and any open wound (a tooth wound over the MCP is a FIGHT-BITE until proven otherwise - a surgical emergency)","Radiographs (PA, lateral, oblique) define the pattern; note intra-articular involvement and joint subluxation (Bennett)","Neurovascular and tendon exam distal to the fracture"],"tx":["Many closed, non-rotated, acceptably angulated fractures are treated non-operatively (buddy taping, functional bracing, or a well-molded splint) with early motion to avoid stiffness","Operative indications: any ROTATIONAL deformity, unacceptable angulation/shortening, intra-articular displacement, multiple/unstable fractures, and open fractures - fix with K-wires, screws, or plates","Bennett/Rolando thumb base fractures need reduction and fixation (CMC joint stability); fight-bite wounds require urgent washout and antibiotics"],"tech":["Closed reduction and percutaneous K-wire fixation for many metacarpal/phalangeal fractures; lag screws or plates for oblique/spiral, intra-articular, or unstable patterns","Correct rotation intraoperatively by checking the cascade/scissoring; the 90-90 intraosseous wiring or mini-fragment plates for select phalangeal fractures","Bennett fracture: closed reduction with thumb traction/pronation and percutaneous pinning of the CMC (or ORIF for large fragments)"],"after":["EARLY protected motion is the theme - the hand stiffens fast, so buddy taping/removable splints and hand therapy beat prolonged rigid immobilization","Monitor for malrotation, stiffness, nonunion/malunion, and extensor lag; remove percutaneous K-wires at ~3-4 weeks","Fight-bite: admit for IV antibiotics covering Eikenella and oral flora, serial washouts, never close primarily"],"pearls":["ROTATION is the deformity you must not accept - even a few degrees causes finger scissoring; check the cascade with the fingers flexed, not just the X-ray","Boxer's (5th MC neck) fracture tolerates a lot of volar angulation; index/long metacarpals tolerate very little because they are less mobile","A laceration over the MCP from a punch is a FIGHT-BITE (human tooth) - washout, antibiotics for Eikenella, and never close it","The hand's enemy is stiffness - favor early protected motion over prolonged immobilization whenever the fracture is stable"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Metacarpal Fractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536960/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-fingertip-nailbed-injuries","name":"Fingertip & Nail Bed Injuries","sec":"plastics","present":["Fingertip injuries are the most common hand injuries; management depends on the geometry of tissue loss (volar vs dorsal oblique), exposed bone, and the status of the NAIL BED and germinal matrix","A subungual HEMATOMA under >~50% of the nail suggests a nail bed laceration; a nail plate avulsed from its fold and a fracture of the distal phalanx (a Seymour fracture in children is an OPEN physeal fracture with the nail bed interposed) change management","Amputations distal to the FDP insertion and lunula can often be reconstructed with attention to preserving length and sensate, durable pulp"],"dx":["Characterize the defect: size, exposed bone, volar vs dorsal tissue loss, and nail bed/matrix involvement; radiograph for distal phalanx fracture (and Seymour fracture in kids)","Assess sensation and perfusion of the remaining pulp; in children beware the Seymour fracture (open, needs washout and matrix repair)","Determine whether tissue can heal by secondary intention vs needs a flap or graft"],"tx":["Small defects (<~1-1.5 cm) without exposed bone heal excellently by SECONDARY INTENTION with dressing changes, preserving sensation and contour","NAIL BED LACERATIONS: repair with fine absorbable suture (or tissue adhesive) after removing the nail plate; REPLACE the nail plate or a spacer under the eponychial fold to keep it open and mold the new nail","Exposed bone/larger defects: local flaps (V-Y advancement - Atasoy volar, Kutler lateral; homodigital/cross-finger or thenar flaps for volar defects) to provide durable, sensate pulp coverage; revision amputation when reconstruction is not warranted"],"tech":["Meticulous nail bed repair under magnification (7-0 absorbable) with the nail plate/spacer reinserted into the fold; trephinate an intact nail for a large painful subungual hematoma","V-Y (Atasoy/Kutler) advancement, cross-finger, or thenar flaps for pulp coverage; the germinal matrix must be repaired precisely (its scarring causes split/absent nails)","Seymour fracture: remove interposed nail bed, irrigate, reduce, and repair the matrix (treat as open)"],"after":["Dressing changes and therapy; the replaced nail plate protects the repair and guides new nail growth (a new nail takes months and may be ridged); educate on transient cold intolerance and hypersensitivity","Monitor flap perfusion; watch for nail deformity (split, hook 'parrot-beak' nail if the bony support/pulp was not preserved) and stiffness after cross-finger flaps","Return of protective sensation is expected; late nail-bed reconstruction (matrix graft) for symptomatic deformities"],"pearls":["Fingertip defects without exposed bone heal beautifully by SECONDARY INTENTION - don't overtreat; you preserve better sensation than any graft","Always REPLACE the nail plate (or a spacer) after a nail bed repair to stent the eponychial fold open and template the new nail","A child's fingertip injury with a nail avulsion may be a SEYMOUR fracture - an open physeal injury with the nail bed trapped in the fracture; washout and matrix repair","Preserve pulp and length with sensate flaps (V-Y, cross-finger) - a durable, sensate fingertip beats a shortened numb one"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Fingertip Injuries","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441987/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-replantation-revascularization","name":"Replantation & Revascularization","sec":"plastics","present":["Replantation reattaches a completely amputated part; revascularization restores flow to an incompletely amputated (still-connected) part - both are microsurgical emergencies where ischemia time drives salvageability","STRONG indications: thumb, multiple digits, any amputation in a CHILD, hand/wrist/forearm through-palm, and sharp/clean amputations; RELATIVE contraindications: single digit in zone II (proximal to FDS insertion is often not replanted in adults), severe crush/avulsion, prolonged warm ischemia, gross contamination, and significant comorbidity/multi-trauma","Ischemia tolerance: digits (little muscle) tolerate ~12h warm / ~24h cold; parts with muscle (hand/forearm) tolerate far less (~6h warm) before reperfusion injury and myonecrosis"],"dx":["Assess the mechanism (sharp vs crush/avulsion), level, ischemia time, and the patient's overall status; cool the amputated part properly during transport","Radiograph both the stump and the part; evaluate for concomitant life-threatening injuries first (life before limb)","Decide replant vs revision amputation weighing indications, contamination, and expected function"],"tx":["Transport the part correctly: wrap in saline-moistened gauze, seal in a bag, and place ON ICE - NEVER directly on ice or submerged in fluid (frostbite/maceration ruins it)","Operative sequence (classic order): bone shortening/fixation, extensor then flexor tendon repair, ARTERY, nerve, then VEIN, then skin - reestablish inflow and outflow with vein grafts as needed","After revascularization, watch for and treat reperfusion; anticoagulation/antithrombotic protocols and leeches for venous congestion support survival"],"tech":["Microvascular anastomosis of digital arteries and veins under the microscope (often vein grafts for gaps); rigid skeletal fixation first to create a stable platform, then tendon and nerve coaptation","Fasciotomy for proximal (muscle-containing) replants to prevent compartment syndrome after reperfusion; shorten bone to allow tension-free vessel/nerve repair","Leech therapy (with Aeromonas antibiotic coverage) or heparin dressings for venous insufficiency when adequate venous outflow can't be established"],"after":["Warm room, no caffeine/nicotine, adequate hydration, and antithrombotic therapy; hourly monitoring of color, temperature, turgor, and Doppler; venous congestion (blue, brisk refill) is the common early failure","Early hand therapy once stable; expect prolonged sensory/motor recovery and cold intolerance; secondary procedures (tenolysis, nerve grafting) are common","Salvage attempts (re-exploration, leeches) for compromise; counsel on realistic function and the possibility of eventual amputation if the part fails"],"pearls":["Transport the amputated part in saline-moist gauze in a sealed bag ON ICE - direct ice contact or submersion destroys it","Absolute replant indications: THUMB, multiple digits, any part in a CHILD, and hand/forearm-level amputations; a single adult zone-II digit is often NOT replanted","Repair order after bone: extensor, flexor, ARTERY, nerve, VEIN, skin - inflow and outflow are the make-or-break steps","Muscle-containing (proximal) parts have short ischemia windows and need fasciotomies - reperfusion of dead muscle causes systemic injury"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Replantation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557587/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nerve-injury-repair","name":"Peripheral Nerve Injury & Repair","sec":"plastics","present":["Peripheral nerve injury presents with motor weakness, sensory loss, and sometimes neuropathic pain in the nerve's distribution; mechanism (sharp laceration, crush/traction, avulsion) predicts prognosis and repair strategy","SEDDON (neurapraxia/axonotmesis/neurotmesis) and SUNDERLAND (I-V) grade severity: neurapraxia (I - conduction block, recovers), axonotmesis (II-IV - axon disruption with variable recovery), neurotmesis (V - complete transection, needs surgery)","A painful TINEL sign that advances distally over weeks signals regenerating axons (~1 mm/day)"],"dx":["Detailed motor/sensory exam and serial follow-up; TINEL sign progression tracks regeneration; electrodiagnostics (EMG/NCS) at ~3-4 weeks distinguish neurapraxia from axonal loss and establish a baseline","Sharp/clean transections are explored and repaired early; closed injuries in continuity are observed for recovery, with imaging (ultrasound/MRI) and repeat EDX guiding surgery if no recovery by ~3 months","Assess for associated vascular/tendon injury and the zone of injury (contamination, tissue loss)"],"tx":["SHARP transection -> early primary TENSION-FREE epineurial coaptation (ideally within days-weeks); crush/avulsion with a gap -> nerve GRAFT (autograft - sural nerve) or a conduit/processed allograft for short gaps","Timing matters - motor endplates degrade; repair/reconstruct before irreversible muscle atrophy (generally within ~12-18 months for motor targets)","NERVE TRANSFERS (coapting an expendable donor fascicle close to the target muscle) restore function faster for proximal/high injuries by shortening reinnervation distance"],"tech":["Microsurgical epineurial (or grouped fascicular) coaptation under the microscope, tension-free, with fine sutures +/- fibrin glue; align fascicles anatomically","Interposition nerve autograft (sural, medial antebrachial cutaneous) or processed allograft/conduit to bridge gaps without tension; distal nerve transfers (e.g., for high ulnar/radial injuries) to restore function sooner","Protect the repair and address the soft-tissue bed (well-vascularized coverage aids regeneration)"],"after":["Protective splinting to offload the repair, then progressive motion; sensory RE-EDUCATION and motor rehab; monitor an advancing Tinel and returning function over months","Repeat EDX to confirm reinnervation; if a repair/transfer fails to reinnervate a muscle within the window, consider tendon transfer or free functioning muscle transfer","Manage neuropathic pain and neuroma (see below); counsel that recovery is slow and often incomplete"],"pearls":["Sharp clean cuts get early tension-free primary repair; gaps and messy injuries need grafts or nerve transfers - never repair under tension","Nerve regenerates at ~1 mm/day - a distally advancing Tinel is the bedside sign of recovery","Beat the clock on motor targets - denervated muscle endplates degrade by ~12-18 months, after which reinnervation fails","Nerve transfers restore function faster for high/proximal injuries by moving the repair close to the target muscle"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Nerve Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556203/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nerve-compression-syndromes","name":"Nerve Compression Syndromes (Carpal, Cubital, Radial Tunnel)","sec":"plastics","present":["Chronic entrapment of a peripheral nerve at an anatomic tunnel causes sensory then motor symptoms. CARPAL TUNNEL (median at the wrist): the most common - night-time numbness/tingling of the thumb-index-middle-radial ring, worse with driving/phone, and thenar weakness/atrophy when advanced","CUBITAL TUNNEL (ulnar at the elbow): numbness of the small/ulnar-ring finger, weak grip/pinch, and elbow-flexion-provoked symptoms; RADIAL TUNNEL: lateral forearm pain without motor loss (vs posterior interosseous syndrome, which weakens finger/thumb extension)","Risk factors: repetitive use, pregnancy, diabetes, hypothyroidism, and inflammatory arthritis"],"dx":["Provocative tests (Phalen, Tinel, Durkan carpal compression for CTS; elbow flexion/Tinel for cubital tunnel) and a focused motor/sensory exam; note thenar (median) or first-dorsal-interosseous/hypothenar (ulnar) atrophy","ELECTRODIAGNOSTICS (NCS/EMG) confirm the site and severity and guide surgery, especially when the diagnosis is uncertain or motor loss is present","Distinguish radial tunnel (pain, no motor deficit) from posterior interosseous nerve syndrome (motor weakness)"],"tx":["Mild/early: activity modification, night SPLINTING (neutral wrist for CTS; elbow extension splint for cubital tunnel), and a corticosteroid injection for CTS (diagnostic and often therapeutic)","SURGICAL DECOMPRESSION for persistent symptoms, motor deficit/atrophy, or failed conservative care: carpal tunnel RELEASE (transverse carpal ligament), cubital tunnel release (+/- ulnar nerve transposition), radial tunnel release","Address contributing systemic factors (thyroid, diabetes, inflammatory disease)"],"tech":["Carpal tunnel release - open or endoscopic division of the transverse carpal ligament; cubital tunnel - in situ decompression vs anterior transposition (subcutaneous/submuscular) of the ulnar nerve; radial tunnel decompression of the arcade of Frohse","Protect the recurrent motor (median) and palmar cutaneous branches during CTS release; avoid incomplete distal release","Confirm complete decompression of all compressive structures"],"after":["Early gentle motion after release; expect night symptoms/tingling to improve first, with strength and any atrophy recovering more slowly (or incompletely if severe/chronic)","Persistent symptoms warrant reassessment (incomplete release, wrong diagnosis, double crush, or advanced axonal loss); scar/pillar tenderness is common early after CTS release","Ergonomic modification and management of systemic contributors"],"pearls":["Night numbness in the median distribution relieved by shaking the hand is classic carpal tunnel; thenar atrophy means advanced disease - decompress","Cubital tunnel gives ulnar-sided numbness and weak pinch (Froment sign) - splint in extension first, decompress if it fails or motor loss appears","Radial tunnel is PAIN without motor loss; posterior interosseous syndrome is WEAKNESS - do not confuse them","EDX localizes and grades the entrapment and is invaluable when the picture is atypical or motor loss is present"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448179/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-tendon-transfers","name":"Tendon Transfers (Radial, Median & Ulnar Palsy)","sec":"plastics","present":["A tendon transfer re-routes a functioning, expendable muscle-tendon unit to restore lost motor function after irreversible nerve injury, tendon loss, or spasticity - a reconstruction when nerve repair is no longer possible","Common indications: RADIAL nerve palsy (loss of wrist/finger/thumb extension - wrist drop), high/low MEDIAN palsy (loss of thumb opposition and thumb/finger flexion), and ULNAR palsy (clawing, weak pinch/grip)","Restores balance and grasp/pinch/release when the muscle target itself cannot be reinnervated"],"dx":["Confirm the deficit is fixed (adequate time/failed nerve recovery) and that donor muscles are strong (MRC grade), expendable, and have adequate excursion; joints must be SUPPLE (treat contractures first)","Plan by the principles: one tendon-one function, synergism, adequate excursion/strength, straight line of pull, and a stable soft-tissue bed","Assess sensation and overall hand function/goals"],"tx":["RADIAL palsy - classic set: pronator teres -> ECRB (wrist extension), FCR (or FCU) -> EDC (finger extension), palmaris longus -> EPL (thumb extension)","LOW MEDIAN palsy - OPPONENSPLASTY (e.g., FDS ring, EIP, or abductor digiti minimi) to restore thumb opposition; HIGH median adds transfers for thumb/finger flexion","ULNAR palsy - anti-CLAW procedures (e.g., FDS lasso, transfers to improve MCP flexion/IP extension) and transfers to restore pinch"],"tech":["Harvest the donor tendon, route it through a subcutaneous/interosseous path in a straight line, and set tension with the wrist/hand in the appropriate position (tenodesis check); weave (Pulvertaft) the coaptation","Ensure the recipient joints move freely and the transfer's excursion matches the required task","Balance grasp and release - avoid over-tensioning"],"after":["Immobilize the transfer for ~3-4 weeks in a protected position, then supervised MOTOR RE-EDUCATION (retraining the donor to perform its new action) and progressive strengthening","Watch for adhesions, rupture, and over/under-correction; therapy is essential to a good outcome","Long-term functional assessment; secondary adjustments if needed"],"pearls":["Prerequisites first: supple joints, strong expendable donors, adequate excursion, and a stable bed - correct contractures before transferring","One tendon, one function, straight line of pull, and synergistic donors are the guiding principles","Radial palsy has a reliable, classic transfer set (PT->ECRB, FCR->EDC, PL->EPL) worth memorizing","Post-op motor re-education makes or breaks the result - the patient must learn to fire the donor for its new job"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tendon Transfers of the Hand","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551677/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-dupuytren","name":"Dupuytren Disease","sec":"plastics","present":["A benign fibroproliferative disease of the PALMAR FASCIA - myofibroblast-driven nodules and CORDS cause progressive flexion CONTRACTURE of the fingers (ring and small most common), impairing hand opening","Presents with palpable palmar nodules and pretendinous cords, then MCP and PIP flexion contractures; usually painless","Risk factors ('Dupuytren diathesis' - aggressive form): northern European ancestry, male, family history, and ectopic disease (Garrod knuckle pads, Ledderhose plantar, Peyronie)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis from cords/nodules and the pattern of contracture; the HUESTON TABLETOP test (cannot place the hand flat) signals treatment-worthy contracture","Measure MCP and PIP contracture angles (PIP contractures are harder to correct and recur more); document diathesis features","Distinguish from trigger finger, stenosing tenosynovitis, and camptodactyly"],"tx":["Intervene for functional impairment (positive tabletop test, MCP contracture >=~30 degrees, or any significant PIP contracture) - not for nodules alone","Options: collagenase CLOSTRIDIUM HISTOLYTICUM injection (enzymatic cord lysis then manipulation), needle APONEUROTOMY (percutaneous cord division), and surgical FASCIECTOMY (regional excision of diseased fascia)","MCP contractures respond well to all methods; PIP contractures are more stubborn and recur"],"tech":["Regional (selective) fasciectomy excises the diseased cords through Brunner/zigzag incisions, protecting the neurovascular bundles (which can be displaced by cords); dermofasciectomy with skin graft for severe/recurrent diathesis","Collagenase injection into the cord followed by finger extension manipulation to rupture it; needle aponeurotomy divides the cord percutaneously","Meticulous bundle protection - cords can spiral the digital nerve superficially/centrally"],"after":["Post-procedure EXTENSION splinting (especially night) and hand therapy to maintain correction and range; manage skin tears (common after collagenase/needle) and wound healing","Counsel on RECURRENCE - Dupuytren is not cured; recurrence is common, higher with diathesis and less invasive techniques, and PIP correction is the least durable","Monitor for complications (nerve injury, CRPS, flare)"],"pearls":["Treat the contracture, not the nodule - the tabletop test and functional loss define the indication","The digital nerve can be spiraled superficially by a cord - meticulous dissection prevents the classic iatrogenic nerve injury","MCP contractures correct well; PIP contractures are stubborn and recur - set expectations accordingly","Dupuytren recurs by nature - diathesis features predict aggressive, recurrent disease"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Dupuytren Contracture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526074/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-hand-infections","name":"Hand Infections (Flexor Tenosynovitis, Deep Spaces, Bites)","sec":"plastics","present":["Hand infections range from superficial (paronychia, felon, cellulitis) to deep, surgical emergencies; delay causes tendon/joint destruction and stiffness","PYOGENIC FLEXOR TENOSYNOVITIS is the can't-miss - the four KANAVEL SIGNS: fusiform (sausage) digit swelling, semiflexed resting posture, tenderness along the flexor sheath, and PAIN ON PASSIVE EXTENSION (the most sensitive)","BITE wounds (human 'fight bite' over the MCP - Eikenella; animal bites - Pasteurella) and DEEP SPACE (thenar, midpalmar) infections and septic arthritis are surgical"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis with the Kanavel signs for flexor tenosynovitis; examine for a clenched-fist tooth injury over the MCP (fight bite - assume joint/extensor tendon involvement)","Radiographs for foreign body, gas, osteomyelitis, or a retained tooth; labs and cultures; aspirate a suspected septic joint","Note host factors (diabetes, immunosuppression, IVDU) that worsen severity"],"tx":["Early/superficial: incision and drainage of a felon or paronychia, and IV/oral antibiotics for cellulitis; elevation and splinting","FLEXOR TENOSYNOVITIS, deep space infection, septic arthritis, and fight bites -> urgent surgical IRRIGATION AND DEBRIDEMENT plus IV empiric antibiotics (cover Staph/Strep incl MRSA; add anaerobic/gram-negative coverage for bites - amoxicillin-clavulanate/ampicillin-sulbactam)","NEVER simply close and send home a fight bite - it needs exploration/washout and antibiotics"],"tech":["Open sheath irrigation (proximal and distal incisions with catheter irrigation) for flexor tenosynovitis; formal drainage of thenar/midpalmar spaces along their anatomic boundaries; arthrotomy/washout for septic joints","Fight bite - extend the wound, explore the extensor tendon and MCP joint (injury occurs with the finger flexed, so the damage retracts proximally when extended), debride, leave open","Debride nonviable tissue; delayed closure/healing by secondary intention"],"after":["Repeat washouts as needed, IV then targeted oral antibiotics by culture, elevation, and EARLY hand therapy to prevent stiffness (the main long-term morbidity)","Monitor for spread, osteomyelitis, and tendon/joint sequelae; update tetanus and consider rabies for animal bites","Rehabilitation for motion and strength"],"pearls":["Pain on passive extension of a fusiform, semiflexed, sheath-tender digit is pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis - a surgical emergency","A wound over the MCP from a punch is a fight bite (Eikenella) until proven otherwise - explore the joint/tendon, never just close it","Cover bite infections for polymicrobial flora with amoxicillin-clavulanate (Pasteurella for animals, Eikenella for humans)","Elevate, splint, drain early, and start therapy early - stiffness is the enemy after any hand infection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hand Infection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560873/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-hand-wrist-tumors","name":"Hand & Wrist Tumors (Ganglion, Giant Cell, Enchondroma)","sec":"plastics","present":["Most hand masses are BENIGN; the task is to recognize the common benign lesions and flag the rare malignancy. GANGLION CYST is the most common hand/wrist mass - a firm, transilluminating, mucin-filled cyst (dorsal wrist from the scapholunate joint, volar wrist, or a retinacular/mucous cyst at the DIP)","GIANT CELL TUMOR OF THE TENDON SHEATH (the most common SOLID tumor) - a firm, non-transilluminating nodule; ENCHONDROMA - the most common primary BONE tumor of the hand (often found as a pathologic fracture through a lytic phalangeal lesion)","Red flags for sarcoma: rapid growth, size >~3-5 cm, deep/fixed, pain, and destructive imaging"],"dx":["Clinical exam (transillumination for ganglion), and imaging: radiographs for bone lesions (enchondroma - lytic, expansile, with stippled calcification), ultrasound (cystic vs solid), and MRI for solid/deep/atypical masses","Aspiration can confirm a ganglion (gelatinous mucin); BIOPSY any solid, enlarging, or radiographically aggressive mass - do not shell out an unknown mass without a plan","Have a low threshold to refer suspected sarcoma to a tumor center (biopsy through a plane that can be excised later)"],"tx":["GANGLION: reassurance/observation (many resolve), aspiration (high recurrence), or excision of the cyst WITH ITS STALK/origin for symptomatic/recurrent cysts","GIANT CELL TUMOR of tendon sheath: marginal excision (recurs if incompletely removed); ENCHONDROMA: observe if asymptomatic, curettage +/- bone graft for pain/pathologic fracture (fix the fracture first, then treat the lesion)","Malignant tumors -> oncologic wide excision/staging at a sarcoma center"],"tech":["Ganglion excision tracing the stalk to its joint/sheath origin (dorsal wrist -> scapholunate capsule) to reduce recurrence; giant cell tumor - meticulous marginal excision of all lobules","Enchondroma - curettage and bone grafting after the pathologic fracture heals; send all specimens to pathology","Plan biopsy incisions so they can be excised with a subsequent oncologic resection"],"after":["Counsel on RECURRENCE (ganglion and giant cell tumor recur, especially after incomplete excision/aspiration); early motion to limit stiffness","Confirm benign histology; surveillance imaging for enchondroma stability; refer confirmed malignancy for definitive management","Hand therapy for motion/strength"],"pearls":["A transilluminating dorsal wrist mass is almost always a ganglion - excise the stalk to the joint origin, not just the cyst, to cut recurrence","Giant cell tumor of the tendon sheath is the most common solid hand tumor and recurs if any lobule is left behind","A lytic phalanx with a pathologic fracture is usually an enchondroma - let the fracture heal, then curette and graft","Rapid growth, size, fixation, or destructive imaging means biopsy and sarcoma-center referral - do not blindly excise"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ganglion Cyst","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470168/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-congenital-hand","name":"Congenital Hand Differences (Syndactyly, Polydactyly, Thumb Hypoplasia)","sec":"plastics","present":["Congenital upper-limb differences classified by the modern OMT (Oberg-Manske-Tonkin)/updated system; key entities: SYNDACTYLY (fused digits - simple/complex, complete/incomplete), POLYDACTYLY (extra digit - radial/thumb, central, or ulnar/small-finger), and THUMB HYPOPLASIA","May be isolated or SYNDROMIC/associated (e.g., radial polydactyly, thumb hypoplasia in VACTERL, Holt-Oram, Fanconi anemia, TAR) - a workup can uncover systemic disease","Function and growth guide timing; the thumb's opposition is critical to hand function"],"dx":["Clinical classification and radiographs to define bony anatomy (shared phalanges/bifid metacarpal, presence of a functional CMC joint in thumb hypoplasia)","SCREEN for associated syndromes/anomalies (cardiac - Holt-Oram; hematologic - Fanconi anemia/TAR; VACTERL) especially with radial-sided anomalies - a normal-looking thumb difference can flag a systemic disease","Assess function, opposition, and pinch"],"tx":["Timing individualized (often ~1 year, or earlier for border/tight syndactyly to prevent deformity from differential growth); goal is function then appearance","SYNDACTYLY: surgical separation with local flaps and skin grafts, releasing border digits earlier; POLYDACTYLY: excise the least functional duplicate and reconstruct (radial/thumb duplication often needs bony/ligament/tendon reconstruction, not simple amputation)","THUMB HYPOPLASIA: reconstruct (opponensplasty, first-web release, ligament/joint stabilization) for a stable thumb with a usable CMC joint; POLLICIZATION (converting the index finger to a thumb) when the thumb/CMC is absent or nonfunctional (Blauth IIIB-V)"],"tech":["Syndactyly release with a dorsal advancement flap for the web commissure and full-thickness grafts for the digital defects; stage adjacent web releases to protect vascularity","Thumb duplication reconstruction (e.g., Bilhaut or combination) preserving the best components; POLLICIZATION rotates/shortens the index on its neurovascular pedicle into a thumb position","Preserve neurovascular bundles throughout"],"after":["Splinting/therapy and web-space maintenance; monitor for web CREEP and scar contracture as the child grows (may need revision)","Long-term functional follow-up (opposition, pinch, growth); genetics follow-up for syndromic cases","Family counseling and developmental support"],"pearls":["A radial-sided anomaly (thumb hypoplasia, radial polydactyly) is a cue to screen for Fanconi anemia, Holt-Oram, and VACTERL - the hand can reveal systemic disease","Release border-digit syndactyly (thumb-index, ring-small) earlier because differential growth causes deformity","Thumb polydactyly is rarely a simple amputation - reconstruct to build one good, stable, opposable thumb","Pollicization builds a thumb from the index finger when none is reconstructable - opposition is what makes a hand work"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Congenital Hand Anomalies / Syndactyly","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559200/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-thumb-reconstruction","name":"Thumb Reconstruction & Toe-to-Thumb Transfer","sec":"plastics","present":["The thumb provides ~40% of hand function through opposition/pinch, so its loss (traumatic amputation, congenital absence, tumor resection) is a major functional deficit warranting reconstruction","Reconstruction is guided by the LEVEL of loss (distal, through the metacarpophalangeal region, or proximal with loss of the basal joint/thenar muscles) and by patient needs","Goals: adequate LENGTH, stable and mobile joints, sensate durable pulp, opposition, and acceptable appearance"],"dx":["Assess the level and quality of remaining thumb, the CMC joint and thenar function, and the available donor options; evaluate the recipient vessels/nerves and the foot for a potential toe transfer","Determine whether length-preserving options (deepening the first web, distraction lengthening, osteoplastic reconstruction) suffice or a new digit is needed","Consider hand dominance, occupation, and patient priorities"],"tx":["Minor distal loss - local flaps and length preservation; moderate loss - first-web DEEPENING/opposition transfer, distraction lengthening, or POLLICIZATION (using an adjacent finger, e.g., an injured index)","Amputation with an inadequate stump - microvascular TOE-TO-THUMB transfer (great toe or second toe) reconstructs a sensate, mobile, growing (in children) thumb","Match the reconstruction to functional needs and donor-site tolerance"],"tech":["POLLICIZATION transposes a finger on its neurovascular pedicle to the thumb position with appropriate shortening/rotation and muscle rebalancing","TOE-TO-THUMB free transfer: harvest the great or second toe on the first dorsal metatarsal/plantar system, transfer to the hand, and perform microvascular artery/vein anastomoses plus nerve coaptation and tendon/bone fixation","Preserve first-web and opposition mechanics; ensure stable basal joint"],"after":["Free-flap/toe-transfer monitoring for perfusion; protected mobilization, then sensory re-education and opposition/pinch retraining with hand therapy","Manage the DONOR foot (preserve weight-bearing/push-off - great-toe harvest techniques minimize this); counsel on appearance and gait","Long-term functional follow-up; growth in pediatric toe transfers"],"pearls":["The thumb is ~40% of hand function - reconstructing opposition/pinch is worth major effort","Match the method to the level of loss: length-preserving options for distal loss, pollicization or toe transfer for major loss","Toe-to-thumb transfer gives a sensate, mobile, and (in children) growing thumb - the closest to a like-for-like replacement","Protect the donor foot's push-off - modern great/second-toe harvest limits gait morbidity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thumb Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560644/"},{"t":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand","u":"https://www.assh.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-implant-breast-reconstruction","name":"Implant-Based Breast Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["The most common method of post-mastectomy breast reconstruction - restoring breast shape with a tissue expander and/or permanent IMPLANT, offered immediately or in a delayed fashion","Timing/technique depends on mastectomy skin/nipple viability, need for radiation, and body habitus; ACELLULAR DERMAL MATRIX (ADM) supports the implant and defines the pocket","PREPECTORAL (implant over the muscle, with ADM) placement is increasingly favored over subpectoral to avoid animation deformity and muscle pain"],"dx":["Assess mastectomy flap perfusion (clinical +/- indocyanine-green angiography), skin envelope, contralateral symmetry, and RADIATION status/plan (radiation markedly raises implant complication/failure rates)","Evaluate patient goals, comorbidities (smoking, diabetes, obesity raise complications), and candidacy for one-stage (direct-to-implant) vs two-stage (expander-then-implant)","Plan oncologic coordination (nipple-sparing eligibility, adjuvant therapy timing)"],"tx":["TWO-STAGE: place a tissue EXPANDER at mastectomy, serially inflate over weeks, then exchange for a permanent implant; ONE-STAGE (direct-to-implant) in select patients with good flaps and the right envelope","PREPECTORAL vs subpectoral pocket with ADM; manage the reconstruction around any planned RADIATION (radiation before/after affects timing and may push toward autologous reconstruction)","Perioperative antibiotics and meticulous pocket/implant handling to reduce infection/biofilm"],"tech":["Tissue expander/implant placement in a prepectoral (ADM-wrapped) or dual-plane subpectoral pocket; serial expansion via an integrated port; second-stage exchange with pocket adjustment and symmetry work","Nipple reconstruction/tattoo and contralateral symmetrizing procedures as later stages","Aseptic 'no-touch' implant technique, pocket irrigation, and drain management to lower infection/capsular contracture"],"after":["Monitor for early complications: mastectomy-flap NECROSIS, infection/implant exposure (may require explantation), seroma, and hematoma; and late issues: CAPSULAR CONTRACTURE, malposition, rippling, and implant failure","RADIATION worsens outcomes (contracture, exposure) - counsel and consider autologous conversion; routine implant surveillance and awareness of BIA-ALCL (see breast augmentation entry)","Staged symmetry/nipple procedures and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Radiation is the dominant risk factor for implant reconstruction failure - it drives timing decisions and often favors autologous tissue","Prepectoral placement (with ADM) avoids the animation deformity and pain of subpectoral pockets and is increasingly standard","Mastectomy-flap perfusion decides direct-to-implant candidacy - ICG angiography helps avoid necrosis over the implant","Sterile technique, pocket irrigation, and no-touch handling reduce infection and capsular contracture"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Breast Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556097/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-autologous-breast-reconstruction","name":"Autologous Breast Reconstruction (DIEP/TRAM/LD)","sec":"plastics","present":["Reconstruction using the patient's OWN tissue - the abdomen is the workhorse donor; produces a natural, durable breast that ages and tolerates radiation better than implants","ABDOMINAL flaps: DIEP (deep inferior epigastric perforator - muscle-SPARING free flap, the gold standard), muscle-sparing TRAM, free/pedicled TRAM, and SIEA; other donors: LATISSIMUS DORSI (often with an implant), PAP (profunda), TUG/gracilis, and SGAP/IGAP (gluteal)","Best for patients wanting to avoid implants, those with prior/planned radiation, or with adequate donor tissue"],"dx":["Assess donor-site adequacy (abdominal pannus, prior abdominal surgery/scars, hernias) and perforator anatomy (CT/MR angiography maps perforators for DIEP planning)","Evaluate comorbidities affecting free-flap success (smoking - a relative contraindication, obesity, vascular disease) and recipient vessels (internal mammary vessels commonly used)","Coordinate with oncology (radiation timing) and set expectations on donor morbidity and operative length"],"tx":["Free DIEP is preferred to preserve the rectus muscle and minimize abdominal-wall morbidity (bulge/hernia) versus TRAM; pedicled TRAM or latissimus (+/- implant) when free-tissue transfer is not ideal","Immediate or delayed; unilateral or bilateral; combine with contralateral symmetry procedures","Microsurgical transfer with vigilant flap monitoring (the flap's survival hinges on the anastomoses)"],"tech":["Harvest the abdominal flap on the deep inferior epigastric perforators (DIEP - dissecting the perforator through/around the rectus without taking muscle), transfer to the chest, and anastomose to the internal mammary (or thoracodorsal) vessels; inset and shape","Close the abdominal donor like an abdominoplasty (+/- mesh for fascia); latissimus flap tunneled to the chest for the LD option","Confirm perfusion; buried-flap monitoring (implantable Doppler/skin paddle)"],"after":["Intensive early FLAP MONITORING (color, refill, temperature, Doppler) with immediate re-exploration for venous congestion/arterial compromise; manage the abdominal donor (bulge/hernia risk, seroma, healing)","Later revisions for symmetry, fat grafting for contour, and nipple reconstruction/tattoo; the reconstructed breast tolerates radiation better than an implant","Long-term durable result that changes with body weight; donor-site follow-up"],"pearls":["DIEP is the gold standard - it spares the rectus muscle and reduces abdominal-wall morbidity compared with TRAM","Autologous tissue tolerates radiation and ages naturally - it is often the better choice when radiation is in the picture","Free-flap survival depends on the microvascular anastomosis - the first 48-72 h of monitoring are critical","Preoperative CT/MR angiography maps the perforators and streamlines DIEP dissection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: DIEP Flap / Autologous Breast Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574512/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-reduction-mammaplasty","name":"Reduction Mammaplasty","sec":"plastics","present":["Surgical reduction of breast volume and reshaping/elevation of the nipple-areola complex (NAC) for symptomatic MACROMASTIA - a FUNCTIONAL operation relieving neck/back/shoulder pain, shoulder grooving, intertrigo, and postural strain","Presents in patients with disproportionately large, ptotic breasts and the classic symptom cluster; distinct from purely cosmetic augmentation/lift","Often reconstructive/functional (may be covered) rather than cosmetic"],"dx":["History of macromastia symptoms, exam of breast volume/ptosis and NAC position, and assessment of skin quality; document functional complaints and prior conservative measures","Screening MAMMOGRAPHY per age guidelines (and send resected tissue to pathology - occult carcinoma is occasionally found); assess comorbidities/smoking","Plan the estimated resection weight and pedicle based on anatomy"],"tx":["Reduction with a chosen NAC PEDICLE (inferior pedicle is common and reliable; superomedial and others used) that preserves NAC blood supply and sensation, plus a skin-resection pattern (WISE/inverted-T or vertical/short-scar)","Very large/ptotic breasts with a long nipple-to-notch distance may require a FREE NIPPLE GRAFT (when the pedicle would be too long to perfuse the NAC)","Balance volume reduction, shape, symmetry, and scar burden"],"tech":["Mark the new NAC position and skin pattern (Wise or vertical), de-epithelialize the pedicle, resect the excess breast tissue, and inset the NAC on its pedicle; close in layers over the reshaped mound","Inferior/superomedial pedicle for NAC perfusion; free nipple graft for extreme hypertrophy/long pedicles","Send all resected tissue to pathology"],"after":["Monitor NAC viability (venous congestion/necrosis - especially with long pedicles), wound healing at the inverted-T junction ('T-point' breakdown), hematoma, and fat necrosis","Counsel on altered NIPPLE SENSATION and possible reduced BREASTFEEDING ability; support bra and activity restriction; scar maturation over months","Symmetry assessment and rare revision"],"pearls":["Reduction mammaplasty is a functional operation - it reliably relieves the neck/back/shoulder symptoms of macromastia","Protect the NAC pedicle - a nipple-to-notch distance too long to perfuse the pedicle is an indication for a free nipple graft","Always send resected breast tissue to pathology - occult carcinoma is occasionally discovered","Counsel up front about changes to nipple sensation and breastfeeding"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Reduction Mammaplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551618/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-breast-augmentation-alcl","name":"Breast Augmentation & BIA-ALCL","sec":"plastics","present":["Augmentation increases breast size/shape with silicone or saline IMPLANTS (or autologous fat grafting) - one of the most common aesthetic operations; also used in reconstruction","Implant pocket (subglandular, subpectoral/dual-plane) and incision (inframammary, periareolar, transaxillary) are chosen by anatomy and patient factors","Two important implant-associated conditions the resident must know: BIA-ALCL and breast implant illness"],"dx":["Assess breast/chest anatomy, skin/soft-tissue coverage, ptosis (a ptotic breast may need a lift WITH augmentation), and symmetry; discuss implant type/size and pocket","Counsel on implant longevity (not lifetime devices), rupture surveillance (silicone rupture is often 'silent' - MRI/ultrasound surveillance per guidance), and the risks below","Set realistic expectations and shared decision-making"],"tx":["Implant selection (cohesive silicone vs saline; smooth vs textured - TEXTURED surfaces are linked to BIA-ALCL) and pocket placement; fat grafting as an autologous alternative/adjunct","BIA-ALCL (breast implant-associated ANAPLASTIC LARGE CELL LYMPHOMA - a T-cell lymphoma of the peri-implant capsule, associated with TEXTURED implants): presents as a LATE (years after) periprosthetic SEROMA or mass","Manage complications and reoperations over the device's lifetime"],"tech":["Implant placement via inframammary/periareolar/transaxillary approach into a subpectoral (dual-plane) or subglandular pocket; augmentation-mastopexy when ptosis coexists","BIA-ALCL work-up: aspirate the late seroma and send fluid for CD30/cytology (do not just drain and dismiss); treatment is EN BLOC capsulectomy with implant removal (excellent prognosis when localized)","Aseptic technique, pocket irrigation, and Keller-funnel/no-touch insertion to reduce contamination"],"after":["Monitor for CAPSULAR CONTRACTURE, rupture, malposition, rippling, infection, and hematoma; silicone rupture surveillance imaging per guidance","A LATE (>1 year) spontaneous seroma or mass around a (textured) implant must be worked up for BIA-ALCL - aspirate and test for CD30, do not ignore","Counsel that implants require lifelong follow-up and likely future revision; address 'breast implant illness' concerns with informed, patient-centered discussion"],"pearls":["A late periprosthetic seroma (years out, especially with textured implants) is BIA-ALCL until proven otherwise - aspirate and send for CD30/cytology","Localized BIA-ALCL is treated with en bloc capsulectomy and implant removal and has an excellent prognosis when caught early","Silicone implant rupture is often silent - surveillance imaging (MRI/ultrasound) detects it","A ptotic breast needs a lift, not just an implant - augmentation alone worsens ptosis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Breast Implant-Associated ALCL","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564349/"},{"t":"FDA: Breast Implants","u":"https://www.fda.gov"}]},{"id":"plastics-mastopexy","name":"Mastopexy & Augmentation-Mastopexy","sec":"plastics","present":["Mastopexy (breast LIFT) repositions the nipple-areola complex and reshapes a PTOTIC breast WITHOUT significantly changing volume - correcting sagging from involution, weight loss, or post-pregnancy changes","PTOSIS is graded (Regnault) by the nipple position relative to the inframammary fold: grade I (at the fold), II (below the fold but above the lower breast), III (below and at the lowest point), and pseudoptosis/glandular ptosis","Often combined with augmentation (lift for shape + implant for volume) or performed after massive weight loss"],"dx":["Assess ptosis grade, nipple-to-fold and nipple-to-notch distances, skin quality/elasticity, breast volume, and symmetry; distinguish true ptosis from glandular ptosis/pseudoptosis (which may only need volume)","Decide lift alone vs augmentation-mastopexy vs reduction based on volume desires and tissue","Age-appropriate mammographic screening"],"tx":["Choose the skin-resection pattern by ptosis severity: periareolar (mild), vertical/short-scar (moderate), or WISE/inverted-T (severe) - more ptosis needs more skin removal and more scar","AUGMENTATION-MASTOPEXY combines an implant (volume) with a lift (shape) - a technically demanding, higher-revision-rate operation because the two maneuvers work against each other (implant adds weight/expands; lift tightens)","Preserve NAC perfusion on its pedicle"],"tech":["De-epithelialize the pedicle, elevate/reposition the NAC, remove redundant skin per the chosen pattern, and reshape/close; add an implant in the appropriate pocket for augmentation-mastopexy","Match scar burden to ptosis; stage the augmentation and lift in high-risk patients to reduce complications","Protect the NAC blood supply and set nipple position accurately"],"after":["Monitor NAC viability, wound healing (T-junction), and symmetry; counsel on scars, altered nipple sensation, and RECURRENT ptosis over time (gravity/tissue quality)","Augmentation-mastopexy has a higher REVISION rate - counsel accordingly; supportive bra and activity limits","Scar maturation and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Mastopexy fixes shape/position, not volume - a deflated, ptotic breast wanting more fullness needs an implant too","Match the scar to the ptosis: periareolar for mild, vertical for moderate, inverted-T for severe","Augmentation-mastopexy fights itself (implant expands, lift tightens) - it is higher-risk and higher-revision, sometimes best staged","Protect the NAC pedicle - nipple necrosis is the feared complication of any lift"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mastopexy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564408/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-gynecomastia","name":"Gynecomastia","sec":"plastics","present":["Benign proliferation of male breast glandular tissue from an estrogen-to-androgen imbalance - presents as a palpable, often tender, concentric subareolar disc; must be distinguished from PSEUDOGYNECOMASTIA (fatty enlargement without glandular tissue) and, rarely, male breast cancer","Causes: physiologic (neonatal, pubertal - usually resolves, senile), drugs (spironolactone, anti-androgens, cimetidine, anabolic steroids, some antipsychotics, marijuana), and pathologic (hypogonadism, hyperthyroidism, liver/renal disease, testicular/adrenal tumors)","Bilateral and symmetric favors benign; a hard, eccentric, fixed mass with skin change or nipple discharge raises cancer concern"],"dx":["History (onset, drugs, systemic symptoms) and exam (concentric glandular disc vs fat; red flags for malignancy); most physiologic/pubertal cases need only reassurance and observation","Work up new/persistent/atypical cases: testosterone/LH/estradiol, hCG, prolactin, thyroid, and liver/renal function; mammography/ultrasound and biopsy for a suspicious unilateral mass","Identify and stop offending medications; treat underlying disease"],"tx":["Treat the CAUSE first (discontinue causative drugs, treat endocrine disease) - many resolve; reassure/observe pubertal gynecomastia (usually regresses within ~1-2 years)","Medical therapy (e.g., tamoxifen) may help early/painful glandular gynecomastia; established, fibrotic, or long-standing disease is unlikely to regress and is treated SURGICALLY","SURGERY: liposuction (fatty component/pseudogynecomastia) plus direct glandular EXCISION (subcutaneous mastectomy) of the disc; skin excision for significant excess after weight loss"],"tech":["Liposuction of the fatty component combined with subareolar glandular excision (pull-through or periareolar incision), preserving a thin subareolar disc to avoid a saucer/crater deformity; skin-reduction patterns for major excess/skin redundancy","Meticulous hemostasis and drains/compression to prevent hematoma/seroma","Send excised tissue to pathology"],"after":["Compression garment and monitoring for hematoma/seroma, contour irregularity (over-resection crater or residual fullness), and nipple sensation changes","Counsel on scar and recurrence (especially if an underlying cause/steroid use continues); psychological benefit is often significant","Follow the underlying condition"],"pearls":["Separate true gynecomastia (glandular disc) from pseudogynecomastia (fat) and from the rare male breast cancer (hard, eccentric, fixed, skin/nipple change)","Find and stop the cause - drugs and endocrine disease drive many cases, and pubertal gynecomastia usually resolves on its own","Established fibrotic gynecomastia will not regress medically - it needs surgery (liposuction + glandular excision)","Leave a thin subareolar disc during excision to avoid a tethered saucer/crater deformity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gynecomastia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430812/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-congenital-breast","name":"Congenital Breast Disorders (Poland Syndrome, Tuberous Breast)","sec":"plastics","present":["Developmental breast anomalies the plastic surgeon reconstructs. POLAND SYNDROME: unilateral absence/hypoplasia of the PECTORALIS MAJOR (sternal head) with variable breast/NAC hypoplasia or absence, and sometimes ipsilateral hand anomalies (symbrachydactyly) and chest-wall/rib deficiency","TUBEROUS (constricted) BREAST: a constricting fibrous ring limits breast base and lower-pole development, causing a narrow base, herniation of tissue into an enlarged areola, and a high inframammary fold","Also amastia/athelia, accessory nipples (polythelia along the milk line), and asymmetry"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis; assess the degree of muscle/breast/NAC deficiency (Poland) and the constriction/lower-pole deficiency and areolar herniation (tuberous breast); document asymmetry and chest-wall involvement","Imaging (and hand evaluation in Poland) to define anatomy; assess skeletal maturity (defer definitive surgery until development completes where possible)","Screen for associated anomalies"],"tx":["Individualized reconstruction after breast maturity: correct volume and shape and achieve symmetry with the contralateral breast","POLAND: restore chest/breast contour with implants/expanders, autologous tissue (latissimus - though often hypoplastic - or free flaps), fat grafting, and custom implants for the chest wall; address the NAC","TUBEROUS BREAST: release the constricting ring and expand the lower pole (scoring/radial release), reduce areolar herniation (periareolar/Benelli), and add volume (implant/fat) as needed"],"tech":["Poland reconstruction: expander/implant or autologous flap for volume, fat grafting for contour, and correction of the anterior axillary fold/NAC; staged as needed","Tuberous correction: radial scoring/release of the constricted lower pole to redistribute tissue, periareolar reduction of the herniated areola, and augmentation to build the deficient lower pole","Symmetry procedures on the contralateral breast"],"after":["Staged revisions for symmetry and contour (fat grafting, implant exchange); monitor implant/flap complications; long-term follow-up as the patient/breast changes","Significant PSYCHOSOCIAL benefit - address body image and expectations; counsel on the multi-stage nature","Contralateral surveillance/symmetry maintenance"],"pearls":["Poland syndrome is absence of the pectoralis (sternal head) with variable breast/NAC and hand anomalies - reconstruct both the chest contour and the breast","Tuberous breast has a constricted base and lower pole with areolar herniation - the fix is releasing the ring and expanding the lower pole, not just adding an implant","Time definitive reconstruction to breast maturity where possible, and expect staged procedures","These are high-impact psychosocial diagnoses - reconstruction addresses real functional and body-image needs"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Poland Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430793/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-chest-wall-reconstruction","name":"Chest Wall & Sternal Wound Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["Reconstruction of chest-wall defects from STERNAL WOUND infection/dehiscence after cardiac surgery, tumor resection, radiation necrosis, or trauma - often involving exposed bone, hardware, mediastinum, or lung","POST-STERNOTOMY mediastinitis/deep sternal wound infection presents with sternal instability, drainage, and sepsis after cardiac surgery (risk: diabetes, obesity, bilateral internal mammary harvest, prolonged surgery)","Large oncologic resections create defects needing both skeletal STABILITY (to prevent flail/paradoxical motion) and soft-tissue coverage"],"dx":["Recognize deep sternal wound infection (instability, drainage, fever, leukocytosis) - CT to assess collection/retrosternal involvement; assess sternal viability and hardware","For oncologic/traumatic defects, determine size and whether skeletal reconstruction is needed (generally defects >~5 cm or >4 ribs risk flail)","Assess available flap donors (pectoralis, rectus, omentum, latissimus) and prior vessel harvest (IMA use limits some options)"],"tx":["STERNAL WOUND infection: aggressive DEBRIDEMENT of necrotic sternum/cartilage/hardware, drainage, culture-directed antibiotics, and negative-pressure therapy, then vascularized FLAP obliteration of the dead space","Muscle/omental flaps fill the mediastinal dead space and bring vascularity to an infected/irradiated field (PECTORALIS MAJOR is the workhorse; rectus abdominis; OMENTUM for large/infected mediastinal defects)","Oncologic defects: SKELETAL reconstruction (mesh/methyl-methacrylate or biologic) for stability PLUS soft-tissue flap coverage"],"tech":["Serial debridement and NPWT; pectoralis major advancement/turnover flaps (based on thoracoacromial or IMA perforators) to fill the mediastinum; rectus abdominis or omental transposition for lower/large defects","Skeletal reconstruction with mesh (+/- methyl methacrylate) or rib-graft for large resections to prevent flail, then flap coverage","Preserve/assess IMA pedicles (prior CABG harvest affects flap choice)"],"after":["Monitor for recurrent infection, flap viability, and respiratory mechanics (paradoxical motion if skeletal reconstruction is inadequate); wean ventilation as stability allows","Culture-directed antibiotics, nutrition, and glycemic control; pulmonary rehabilitation","Long-term surveillance (oncologic recurrence; hardware/mesh issues)"],"pearls":["Deep sternal wound infection = debride the dead bone/cartilage/hardware, then obliterate the space with a vascularized flap (pectoralis, rectus, or omentum) - drainage alone fails","Big skeletal defects (>~4 ribs or >5 cm) need rigid reconstruction to prevent flail chest, plus soft-tissue coverage","The omentum is a powerful option for large, infected, or irradiated mediastinal defects","Prior internal mammary artery harvest (CABG) changes which flaps are available - know the vascular history"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chest Wall Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560923/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-abdominal-wall-reconstruction","name":"Abdominal Wall Reconstruction (Components Separation)","sec":"plastics","present":["Reconstruction of complex abdominal-wall defects: large/recurrent VENTRAL HERNIAS, loss of domain, enterocutaneous fistula fields, trauma, tumor resection, and open abdomens - restoring a functional, dynamic, tension-free myofascial wall","Goals: durable fascial closure, restoration of the linea alba/midline, dead-space/soft-tissue coverage, and control of infection/contamination","Complex when there is a wide defect, prior mesh infection, contamination, or loss of domain"],"dx":["Assess defect width, prior repairs/mesh, contamination (CDC wound class), stoma/fistula, and comorbidities (obesity, diabetes, smoking - optimize before elective repair); CT to define the defect and muscle","Classify the hernia/defect and plan the technique and mesh type/plane (contaminated fields favor biologic or specific synthetic strategies)","Coordinate with general surgery for concurrent bowel work"],"tx":["Reapproximate the midline with COMPONENTS SEPARATION to advance the rectus complexes without tension: ANTERIOR (Ramirez - release the external oblique aponeurosis) or POSTERIOR (transversus abdominis release, TAR) component separation","Reinforce with MESH in the appropriate plane (retromuscular/sublay - Rives-Stoppa is durable; biologic/absorbable in contaminated fields); avoid tension","Manage contamination/fistula and optimize the host (glycemia, nutrition, smoking cessation, weight)"],"tech":["Anterior component separation (external oblique release +/- perforator-sparing) or posterior TAR to gain myofascial advancement and a retromuscular space for sublay mesh (Rives-Stoppa/TAR)","Wide soft-tissue undermining risks skin-flap necrosis - perforator-sparing/minimally invasive releases reduce this; flap coverage (e.g., ALT, tensor fascia lata) for massive soft-tissue loss","Mesh placement in the retrorectus/preperitoneal plane; drains and closure"],"after":["Monitor for skin-flap necrosis, seroma, surgical-site infection, mesh complications, and hernia RECURRENCE; abdominal binder and activity restriction","Manage the physiology of restored domain (respiratory mechanics, intra-abdominal pressure - watch for compartment issues after large closures)","Long-term recurrence surveillance and comorbidity management"],"pearls":["Components separation advances the rectus muscles to a tension-free midline - anterior (external oblique) or posterior (transversus abdominis release, TAR)","Retromuscular (Rives-Stoppa/TAR sublay) mesh reinforcement is durable and keeps mesh off the bowel","Contaminated fields change the mesh choice (biologic/absorbable or specific synthetics) and staging","Optimize the host first - obesity, uncontrolled diabetes, and smoking sharply raise recurrence and wound failure"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Abdominal Wall Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470431/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-acute-burn-management","name":"Acute Burn Management & Resuscitation","sec":"plastics","present":["Thermal, scald, chemical, or electrical injury to skin/soft tissue; severity depends on DEPTH (superficial, superficial/deep partial-thickness, full-thickness) and %TBSA - and airway/inhalation injury drives early mortality","Assess for INHALATION injury (facial burns, singed nasal hair, carbonaceous sputum, stridor, closed-space fire) and carbon monoxide/cyanide exposure; a swelling airway can obstruct - intubate EARLY if suspected","Circumferential full-thickness burns of a limb/torso threaten perfusion/ventilation (need escharotomy)"],"dx":["Estimate %TBSA (Rule of Nines/Lund-Browder; palm ~1%) counting PARTIAL and full-thickness burns (not simple erythema) and assess depth; primary/secondary trauma survey","Airway assessment for inhalation injury (low threshold to intubate before edema obstructs); carboxyhemoglobin and consider cyanide in enclosed-space fires (give high-flow O2, hydroxocobalamin for cyanide)","Identify circumferential burns compromising perfusion/ventilation"],"tx":["FLUID resuscitation for large burns (generally >~20% TBSA adults) using a formula (e.g., PARKLAND ~4 mL x kg x %TBSA lactated Ringer, half in the first 8 h from the time of injury) TITRATED to URINE OUTPUT (~0.5 mL/kg/h adults; more in children/electrical) - avoid over-resuscitation ('fluid creep')","Early intubation for airway/inhalation injury; ESCHAROTOMY for circumferential full-thickness burns compromising perfusion or chest excursion","Analgesia, warming, tetanus, wound care; transfer to a BURN CENTER per referral criteria"],"tech":["Escharotomy: full-thickness incisions through the constricting eschar (mid-medial/lateral limb lines, chest wall) to restore perfusion/ventilation; fasciotomy for electrical/deep injury with compartment syndrome","Airway management and inhalation-injury care; initial burn wound cleansing/dressing","Foley to monitor resuscitation; NG for large burns"],"after":["Continuous titration of resuscitation to urine output (avoid both under- and over-resuscitation); monitor for compartment/abdominal compartment syndrome, AKI, and rhabdomyolysis (electrical burns)","Nutritional support (burns are hypermetabolic - early enteral feeding), infection surveillance, and staged wound management (see excision/grafting)","Transfer to burn center per ABA criteria; multidisciplinary care"],"pearls":["Airway first - a low threshold to intubate early in inhalation injury, before edema makes it impossible","Parkland is a STARTING point - titrate to urine output and avoid fluid creep (over-resuscitation causes compartment syndrome and ARDS)","Count only partial- and full-thickness burns in %TBSA (not simple erythema); palm ~1%","Circumferential full-thickness burns compromising perfusion/ventilation need escharotomy - and consider cyanide/CO in enclosed-space fires"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Burns Evaluation and Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430741/"},{"t":"American Burn Association","u":"https://ameriburn.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-burn-excision-grafting","name":"Burn Wound Excision & Grafting","sec":"plastics","present":["Definitive management of deep partial- and full-thickness burns that will not heal in time is EARLY EXCISION of the burned tissue and skin grafting - reducing infection, inflammation, and length of stay versus delayed spontaneous healing","Deep partial- and full-thickness burns lack the dermal appendages to re-epithelialize adequately and heal with hypertrophic scar/contracture if left - they need excision and coverage","Timing: early tangential/fascial excision (within days) once the patient is resuscitated/stable"],"dx":["Assess burn DEPTH and healing potential: superficial partial-thickness (blanches, painful, heals ~2 weeks - manage conservatively) vs DEEP partial/FULL-thickness (poor healing - needs excision/grafting)","Estimate excision blood loss and plan staged coverage for large TBSA (limited donor sites); assess hemodynamic/nutritional readiness","Serial reassessment as burns can convert/deepen"],"tx":["EARLY EXCISION (tangential - shave to viable, bleeding dermis; or fascial for very deep/large burns) followed by autografting; for extensive burns with limited donors, use MESHED autograft, temporary coverage (allograft, xenograft, biosynthetic), and dermal substitutes","CULTURED epithelial autografts and dermal templates (e.g., dermal regeneration matrix) for massive burns with inadequate donor sites","Perioperative blood/warmth management (excision is bloody) and infection control"],"tech":["Tangential excision with a dermatome/knife to a viable bleeding bed, or fascial excision for full-thickness/large burns; hemostasis (tourniquet, epinephrine tumescence, topical agents)","Autograft (sheet graft for face/hands/functional areas; meshed for large areas), staged with temporary biologic coverage and dermal substitutes when donors are scarce","Secure grafts and immobilize; harvest donor sites for re-cropping in extensive burns"],"after":["Graft care (immobilize, prevent shear/infection - beta-hemolytic strep melts grafts), donor-site healing, and staged re-grafting for large TBSA; nutrition for the hypermetabolic state","Begin scar/contracture prevention early (positioning, splinting, ranging) - functional areas and joints are priorities","Long-term scar management and reconstruction (see contractures entry)"],"pearls":["Early excision and grafting of deep burns beats waiting - less infection, less scarring, shorter stay","Superficial partial-thickness burns heal on their own in ~2 weeks; deep partial and full-thickness burns need excision and grafting","Excision is bloody and burns are hypermetabolic - plan for blood loss, warmth, and aggressive nutrition","In massive burns with few donor sites, use meshed autograft, temporary allograft/xenograft, and dermal substitutes to buy coverage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Burn Excision and Grafting","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430795/"},{"t":"American Burn Association","u":"https://ameriburn.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-burn-reconstruction-contractures","name":"Burn Reconstruction & Contractures","sec":"plastics","present":["Late/reconstructive phase of burn care addressing the sequelae of healed deep burns: hypertrophic scarring, CONTRACTURES across joints (limiting function), unstable scars, and disfigurement (face, hands, neck, axilla)","Burn scar CONTRACTURES tether and limit motion (neck extension, elbow/axilla, hand); the neck and hand are functional priorities","Unstable/chronic scars carry a long-term risk of MARJOLIN ULCER (squamous cell carcinoma in old burn scars)"],"dx":["Assess the contracture band(s), joint range, scar maturity/stability, and functional deficit; wait for scar MATURATION (often ~6-12 months) before elective reconstruction unless function is severely threatened","Prioritize function (contracture release restoring motion) over appearance; identify unstable scars/chronic ulcers (biopsy to exclude Marjolin)","Plan the reconstructive method by defect after release"],"tx":["Nonoperative scar management continues throughout: PRESSURE garments, SILICONE, massage, splinting/positioning, and range-of-motion therapy to prevent/limit contracture (start early)","Contracture RELEASE plus resurfacing of the resulting defect: Z-PLASTY/local flaps for linear bands, skin grafts (full-thickness preferred for less recontracture on functional areas), tissue expansion, and regional/free flaps or dermal templates for larger releases","Laser and steroid injection for hypertrophic scars; staged reconstruction for complex disfigurement"],"tech":["Z-plasty/multiple Z-plasties or local flaps to lengthen linear contracture bands; incisional release with full-thickness graft or flap coverage of the defect; tissue expansion to generate like tissue (scalp/face)","Free flaps/dermal regeneration templates for extensive resurfacing; laser (pulsed-dye/fractional) for scar remodeling","Aggressive post-release splinting/positioning to hold the correction"],"after":["Relentless post-release SPLINTING, positioning, and therapy to maintain the gain and prevent RECURRENT contracture; continue pressure/silicone/laser for scar maturation","Long-term surveillance of unstable/chronic scars for MALIGNANT change (Marjolin ulcer - biopsy non-healing or changing burn scars); psychosocial support","Staged revisions over time"],"pearls":["Prevention is treatment - early pressure, silicone, splinting, and ranging limit the contractures you would otherwise have to release","Release plus resurfacing: Z-plasty for linear bands, full-thickness graft/flap for broad releases; then splint hard to hold it","Wait for scar maturation for elective reconstruction, but do not delay when a contracture threatens critical function (neck/hand)","A non-healing or changing chronic burn scar is a Marjolin ulcer (SCC) until biopsy proves otherwise"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Burn Scar Contractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519545/"},{"t":"American Burn Association","u":"https://ameriburn.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-electrical-chemical-cold-injury","name":"Electrical, Chemical & Cold Injury (Frostbite)","sec":"plastics","present":["Special burn mechanisms with pitfalls beyond thermal burns. ELECTRICAL injury: current travels through deep tissue so the SKIN wound UNDERSTATES the damage - expect deep muscle necrosis, RHABDOMYOLYSIS, compartment syndrome, cardiac ARRHYTHMIA, and hidden injury between entry/exit points (high vs low voltage)","CHEMICAL burns: severity depends on agent, concentration, and CONTACT TIME - ALKALIS (liquefactive, penetrate deeply) are worse than acids; hydrofluoric acid causes systemic HYPOCALCEMIA","COLD injury/FROSTBITE: tissue freezing with ice-crystal and microvascular injury; graded by depth, with reperfusion determining final tissue loss"],"dx":["ELECTRICAL: cardiac monitoring/ECG (arrhythmia), check CK and urine for MYOGLOBINURIA, and serial compartment exams (deep muscle injury with a small skin wound); assess entry/exit and associated trauma (falls, tetany fractures)","CHEMICAL: identify the agent; for HYDROFLUORIC ACID, monitor calcium/magnesium/potassium and ECG (life-threatening hypocalcemia/hyperkalemia)","FROSTBITE: assess depth after rewarming; imaging (bone scan/angiography) later predicts tissue viability and demarcation"],"tx":["ELECTRICAL: aggressive fluids to maintain urine output and clear myoglobin (protect kidneys), treat hyperkalemia/arrhythmia, and FASCIOTOMY/debridement for compartment syndrome/necrotic muscle (skin injury underestimates fluid and tissue needs)","CHEMICAL: copious IRRIGATION (brush off dry powder first); HYDROFLUORIC ACID - topical/injected/intra-arterial CALCIUM GLUCONATE and correct systemic hypocalcemia","FROSTBITE: rapid REWARMING in warm water (37-39 C), analgesia, avoid refreezing; consider THROMBOLYSIS (tPA) for severe deep frostbite within a window to salvage tissue; delay amputation until demarcation ('frostbite in January, amputate in July')"],"tech":["Escharotomy/FASCIOTOMY and serial debridement for electrical deep injury/compartment syndrome; irrigation and agent-specific neutralization for chemical burns; calcium gluconate for HF","Frostbite: warm-water rewarming, wound care/blister management, and thrombolysis protocols for severe cases; delayed amputation at demarcation","Grafting/flap coverage of resulting defects"],"after":["ELECTRICAL: monitor renal function/myoglobin, delayed cardiac/neurologic effects (including delayed neuropathy/cataracts), and evolving deep tissue necrosis needing repeat debridement","FROSTBITE: allow demarcation before definitive amputation/reconstruction; long-term cold sensitivity/neuropathy; HF - continued electrolyte monitoring","Rehabilitation and reconstruction of the definitive defects"],"pearls":["Electrical injury: the small skin wound hides massive deep muscle damage - watch for rhabdomyolysis, compartment syndrome, and arrhythmia; resuscitate to urine output and fasciotomize","Alkali burns are worse than acid (liquefactive, deep) - and hydrofluoric acid causes lethal hypocalcemia treated with calcium gluconate","Frostbite: rewarm rapidly, never refreeze, consider tPA early for severe cases, and delay amputation until demarcation","Chemical burns depend on contact time - copious irrigation is the universal first step (brush off dry powders first)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Electrical Burns / Frostbite","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519001/"},{"t":"American Burn Association","u":"https://ameriburn.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-pressure-injuries","name":"Pressure Injuries & Flap Coverage","sec":"plastics","present":["Localized tissue damage over a BONY PROMINENCE from sustained PRESSURE (+/- shear/moisture) - sacrum, ischium, greater trochanter, and heel are classic sites; common in immobile, spinal-cord-injured, and debilitated patients","STAGED (NPUAP): stage 1 (non-blanchable erythema, intact skin), 2 (partial-thickness/blister), 3 (full-thickness into subcutaneous fat), 4 (exposed bone/muscle/tendon), plus unstageable and deep-tissue injury","Underlying OSTEOMYELITIS commonly complicates stage 3-4 wounds"],"dx":["Stage the wound and assess depth/undermining, infection, and osteomyelitis (probe-to-bone, imaging, bone biopsy/culture is the standard for diagnosing osteomyelitis)","Identify and address CAUSATIVE and host factors: pressure/shear, immobility, spasticity, incontinence/moisture, malnutrition (albumin/prealbumin), and anemia - surgery fails without these controlled","Assess the patient's overall status and ability to offload post-op (a non-adherent offloading plan dooms flap repair)"],"tx":["PREVENTION and conservative care are foundational: pressure REDISTRIBUTION (turning, specialty mattresses/cushions), moisture/incontinence control, nutrition optimization, spasticity management, and debridement of necrotic tissue","Stage 1-2 and many stage 3 wounds heal with wound care/offloading; deep stage 3-4 wounds with dead space/exposed bone often need surgical DEBRIDEMENT and FLAP coverage","Treat osteomyelitis (debride bone, culture-directed antibiotics) before/with definitive flap closure"],"tech":["Excise the wound/bursa and involved bone (ostectomy of the prominence), then obliterate dead space with a regional MUSCLE or FASCIOCUTANEOUS FLAP (e.g., gluteal rotation/V-Y for sacrum, hamstring/gluteal for ischium, tensor fascia lata for trochanter)","Design flaps to be re-advanceable for the high recurrence setting; drains and tension-free closure","Debride to healthy, bleeding tissue and clear infection first"],"after":["Strict post-op OFFLOADING (air-fluidized bed, no sitting/pressure on the flap for weeks) and a durable pressure-prevention plan - RECURRENCE is very high without behavioral/mechanical change","Nutrition, spasticity, incontinence, and mobility management long-term; monitor for dehiscence/recurrence","Multidisciplinary care (nursing, PT/OT, nutrition, SCI medicine)"],"pearls":["The operation is the easy part - pressure injuries recur relentlessly unless offloading, nutrition, moisture, and spasticity are fixed","Deep stage 3-4 wounds with dead space or exposed bone need debridement plus a flap, not just dressings; treat underlying osteomyelitis first","Choose flaps by site (gluteal for sacrum/ischium, tensor fascia lata for trochanter) and design them to be re-advanced for recurrences","Prevention (repositioning, support surfaces, skin/moisture care) beats any reconstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pressure Injury / Pressure Ulcer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553107/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-lower-extremity-reconstruction","name":"Lower Extremity Reconstruction (Gustilo, Flap Ladder)","sec":"plastics","present":["Reconstruction of leg/foot soft-tissue defects from open (often GUSTILO IIIB) tibial fractures, trauma, tumor resection, chronic wounds, and diabetic/vascular ulcers - the subcutaneous tibia and poor distal-third local options make this challenging","Goals: stable skeletal fixation, INFECTION control, and durable, well-vascularized SOFT-TISSUE COVERAGE of exposed bone/hardware/tendon to achieve union and a functional, salvageable limb","Some severe injuries (Gustilo IIIC with unreconstructable nerve/vascular loss) are better served by amputation - a limb-salvage-vs-amputation decision"],"dx":["Assess the wound, fracture (Gustilo grade), NEUROVASCULAR status (posterior tibial nerve/plantar sensation, distal pulses/angiography), and zone of injury for recipient vessels","Determine reconstructive needs by defect location: local/regional options for proximal/middle thirds, usually FREE FLAP for the distal third; consider the salvage-vs-amputation decision (severe IIIC, insensate foot, multi-level injury)","Plan timing - early definitive coverage (historically within ~72 h to 1 week) lowers infection"],"tx":["Reconstructive ladder by location: proximal-third defects - GASTROCNEMIUS; middle-third - SOLEUS; DISTAL-third and foot - free tissue transfer (ALT, gracilis, latissimus) or select local perforator/reverse-flow flaps","Early I&D, skeletal stabilization, and prompt vascularized coverage of exposed bone/hardware to prevent infection and achieve union; treat osteomyelitis/nonunion (may need bone grafting/transport)","Optimize perfusion (revascularize an ischemic limb before flap) and host factors (glycemia, smoking, nutrition)"],"tech":["Gastrocnemius/soleus rotation flaps (with skin graft) for proximal/middle thirds; FREE flap (ALT/gracilis/latissimus) to recipient vessels (posterior tibial/anterior tibial) for the distal third and foot; perforator/propeller and reverse sural flaps in selected cases","Coordinate skeletal fixation and, if needed, vascular repair; skin-graft muscle flaps","Confirm recipient vessels are outside the zone of injury"],"after":["Flap monitoring (free flaps), non-weight-bearing/elevation protocols, and staged rehabilitation toward weight-bearing and union; manage nonunion/osteomyelitis and hardware issues","Long-term functional recovery (gait, sensation) and surveillance; revision/debulking as needed","Reassess if salvage fails - delayed amputation is sometimes the more functional outcome"],"pearls":["Leg reconstruction by thirds: gastrocnemius (proximal), soleus (middle), free flap (distal) - a foundational algorithm","Gustilo IIIB needs a flap for coverage; early vascularized coverage of exposed bone/hardware prevents infection and drives union","Confirm recipient vessels are outside the zone of injury (angiography) before a free flap in trauma","Limb salvage is not always right - an insensate foot or unreconstructable IIIC may do better with amputation; weigh function honestly"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lower Extremity Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564325/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nsti-complex-wounds","name":"Necrotizing Soft Tissue Infections & Complex Wounds","sec":"plastics","present":["NECROTIZING soft tissue infection (necrotizing fasciitis, Fournier gangrene of the perineum) - a rapidly progressive, LIMB/LIFE-THREATENING infection along fascial planes; a surgical emergency where delay to debridement drives mortality","Presents with PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to skin findings, rapid progression, systemic toxicity/sepsis, and later skin changes (dusky/bullae, crepitus, 'dishwater' fluid, anesthesia of overlying skin); type I polymicrobial vs type II (group A Strep, +/- Staph)","Complex/chronic wounds (diabetic, venous, ischemic, radiation, dehisced surgical) are a related plastic-surgery domain requiring wound-bed optimization and coverage"],"dx":["Primarily CLINICAL and time-critical - do not delay surgery for imaging when suspicion is high; the LRINEC score and labs support but do not exclude; CT may show fascial gas/edema but a normal scan does not rule it out","The diagnosis is confirmed at SURGERY (gray necrotic fascia, lack of bleeding/resistance, dishwater pus, easy finger dissection along the plane)","For complex wounds: assess perfusion (ABI/vascular), infection/osteomyelitis, offloading, and host factors"],"tx":["NSTI: IMMEDIATE, aggressive surgical DEBRIDEMENT of all necrotic tissue (repeat every 24-48 h until clean), broad-spectrum ANTIBIOTICS (including CLINDAMYCIN for antitoxin effect against group A Strep, plus gram-negative/anaerobic coverage - e.g., vancomycin + piperacillin-tazobactam), and ICU resuscitation","Source control is everything - antibiotics alone fail; hyperbaric oxygen is adjunctive at best and must not delay debridement","Complex WOUNDS: debride, control bioburden, negative-pressure therapy, revascularize/offload, optimize nutrition/glycemia, then close/cover (graft/flap) up the reconstructive ladder"],"tech":["Wide serial surgical debridement to healthy bleeding tissue (do not spare - survival depends on complete excision); diverting colostomy for Fournier as needed; NPWT between debridements","Staged RECONSTRUCTION once infection is cleared and the wound is stable: skin graft, local/regional flap, or free flap for the resulting defect","Wound-bed preparation (debridement, moisture, bioburden, NPWT) before definitive closure of complex wounds"],"after":["Repeat debridements until clean, ongoing culture-directed antibiotics and critical-care support; then reconstruct the (often large) defect; high mortality with delay","Complex-wound follow-up: maintain offloading/compression/revascularization and host optimization to keep the wound healed; recurrence surveillance","Rehabilitation and psychosocial support for large defects/reconstruction"],"pearls":["Pain out of proportion, rapid progression, and systemic toxicity mean necrotizing infection - get to the OR; do not wait for imaging or a 'better' exam","Source control (early, complete, repeated debridement) saves lives - antibiotics alone do not; add clindamycin for the antitoxin effect against group A Strep","A normal CT does not exclude NSTI - the diagnosis is clinical and confirmed surgically (dishwater fluid, gray fascia, easy finger dissection)","Reconstruct only after the infection is fully controlled; complex wounds need a prepared, vascularized bed before coverage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Necrotizing Fasciitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430756/"},{"t":"CDC: Necrotizing Fasciitis","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"plastics-lymphedema","name":"Lymphedema & Surgical Management (LVA, VLNT)","sec":"plastics","present":["Chronic, progressive limb (or other region) swelling from impaired LYMPHATIC drainage; in developed countries most commonly SECONDARY (after cancer treatment - axillary/inguinal lymph node dissection and/or radiation, classically breast-cancer-related arm lymphedema); primary lymphedema is congenital/hereditary","Progresses through stages: early pitting/reversible edema -> nonpitting fibrosis and fatty deposition -> elephantiasis; complications include recurrent CELLULITIS and (rarely) lymphangiosarcoma","A positive STEMMER sign (cannot pinch a skin fold at the toe/finger base) supports the diagnosis"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (history of nodal surgery/radiation, distribution, Stemmer sign, staging) with limb volume/circumference measurement; LYMPHOSCINTIGRAPHY is the standard confirmatory study, and ICG lymphography maps functional lymphatics for surgical planning","Exclude other edema causes (venous, cardiac/renal/hepatic, hypoalbuminemia, DVT); assess for infection","Stage severity to guide therapy"],"tx":["Foundational nonoperative therapy: COMPLETE DECONGESTIVE THERAPY (manual lymphatic drainage, multilayer COMPRESSION bandaging/garments, exercise, and meticulous skin care) - the mainstay for most patients, plus prompt antibiotics for cellulitis","PHYSIOLOGIC surgery for early/fluid-predominant disease with functional lymphatics: LYMPHOVENOUS ANASTOMOSIS (LVA - connect lymphatics to venules to bypass the block) and VASCULARIZED LYMPH NODE TRANSFER (VLNT)","EXCISIONAL/reductive surgery (suction-assisted lipectomy for fatty-predominant limbs; staged skin/soft-tissue excision for advanced disease) when fibrofatty tissue dominates"],"tech":["LVA: supermicrosurgical anastomosis of subdermal lymphatic channels to nearby venules (ICG-guided); VLNT: free transfer of a vascularized lymph node flap to the affected basin to restore drainage/lymphangiogenesis","Liposuction (SAPL) for the fatty component; staged excisional procedures (e.g., Charles procedure) for end-stage limbs","Combine physiologic and reductive approaches by disease stage; prophylactic LVA at the time of node dissection is emerging"],"after":["LIFELONG compression/skin care and cellulitis prevention continue even after surgery (surgery is adjunct, not cure); monitor limb volume response and function","Manage recurrent cellulitis promptly (and consider prophylaxis for frequent episodes); ongoing decongestive therapy","Long-term follow-up and realistic expectations (control/reduction, not full normalization)"],"pearls":["Complete decongestive therapy (compression, manual drainage, skin care, exercise) is the mainstay - surgery is an adjunct, and compression continues afterward","Physiologic surgery (LVA, VLNT) helps EARLIER fluid-predominant disease with working lymphatics; liposuction/excision addresses the fatty-fibrotic limb","A positive Stemmer sign and a history of nodal surgery/radiation point to lymphedema - confirm with lymphoscintigraphy, map with ICG","Treat cellulitis promptly and prevent it - infections accelerate lymphedema and threaten the limb"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lymphedema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537239/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-melanoma","name":"Melanoma (Margins, SLNB & Reconstruction)","sec":"plastics","present":["Malignant tumor of melanocytes - the most lethal common skin cancer; the plastic surgeon's role is definitive WIDE EXCISION with appropriate margins, sentinel node staging, and reconstruction of the defect","Screen pigmented lesions with the ABCDE criteria (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter >6 mm, Evolution) and the 'ugly duckling' sign; subtypes: superficial spreading (most common), nodular (aggressive), lentigo maligna (sun-damaged elderly skin), and acral lentiginous (palms/soles/nail - the type most common in darker skin)","BRESLOW depth (tumor thickness) is the key prognostic factor and drives margins and sentinel node decisions"],"dx":["Diagnose by EXCISIONAL (narrow-margin) biopsy of the full lesion for accurate Breslow depth - avoid shave/partial biopsy that under-samples thickness; histology reports depth, ulceration, and mitoses","STAGE (AJCC) using Breslow thickness, ulceration, and nodal/metastatic status; imaging for higher-stage disease; test for actionable mutations (BRAF) in advanced disease","Assess regional nodes clinically; plan sentinel node biopsy by thickness/features"],"tx":["WIDE LOCAL EXCISION with margins by Breslow depth: in situ ~0.5-1 cm; <=1 mm -> 1 cm; >1-2 mm -> 1-2 cm; >2 mm -> 2 cm (down to fascia)","SENTINEL LYMPH NODE BIOPSY for staging/prognosis, generally offered for tumors >0.8 mm (or thinner with high-risk features); completion lymphadenectomy is now often replaced by nodal surveillance (MSLT-II)","Advanced/metastatic melanoma: IMMUNOTHERAPY (checkpoint inhibitors - anti-PD-1/CTLA-4) and TARGETED therapy (BRAF/MEK inhibitors) have transformed survival"],"tech":["Wide local excision to the appropriate margin and depth (to fascia), with SENTINEL NODE mapping (lymphoscintigraphy + blue dye/gamma probe) performed BEFORE the wide excision disrupts lymphatics","Reconstruction of the resulting defect up the ladder (primary closure, graft, or flap) - but confirm clear margins before complex flap reconstruction that rearranges tissue","Nail-unit/acral and facial lesions need specialized excision/reconstruction"],"after":["Surveillance for recurrence and new primaries (regular skin exams; imaging for higher stage); patient self-exam and sun protection; melanoma patients are at risk for additional melanomas","Manage nodal basins (surveillance ultrasound after positive SLNB per MSLT-II) and coordinate medical/surgical oncology for adjuvant therapy","Reconstruction revisions and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Do an excisional (not shave) biopsy for a suspected melanoma - Breslow depth drives everything and a shave can transect it","Margins scale with thickness (in situ 0.5-1 cm; up to 2 cm for thick tumors); sentinel node biopsy stages tumors generally >0.8 mm","Map the sentinel node BEFORE wide excision - the wide excision disrupts the lymphatic drainage you need to trace","Immunotherapy and BRAF/MEK-targeted therapy have dramatically improved advanced-melanoma survival - test for BRAF"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Melanoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470409/"},{"t":"NCCN / American Academy of Dermatology","u":"https://www.aad.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-nonmelanoma-skin-cancer","name":"Non-Melanoma Skin Cancer & Mohs Defect Reconstruction","sec":"plastics","present":["BASAL CELL CARCINOMA (BCC - the most common human cancer; locally invasive, rarely metastasizes; pearly telangiectatic papule with rolled borders/central ulceration) and SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA (SCC - a scaly/keratotic nodule or non-healing ulcer; can metastasize, especially high-risk sites/immunosuppressed)","Driven by cumulative UV exposure (and immunosuppression - transplant patients get aggressive SCCs); the face is a common, cosmetically/functionally sensitive site","Precursors/related: actinic keratosis (SCC precursor), Bowen disease (SCC in situ), keratoacanthoma"],"dx":["Diagnose by BIOPSY (shave/punch) to confirm type and identify high-risk histology (infiltrative/morpheaform BCC; poorly differentiated, perineural, or deeply invasive SCC)","Assess high-risk features (size, location on the face/'mask' zones, recurrence, immunosuppression, perineural invasion) that favor Mohs and mandate closer follow-up","Evaluate regional nodes for high-risk SCC"],"tx":["Excision with clear margins, or MOHS MICROGRAPHIC SURGERY (tissue-sparing, margin-controlled) for high-risk/recurrent tumors and cosmetically sensitive areas (face, H-zone) - Mohs gives the highest cure with maximal tissue preservation","Non-surgical options for select superficial/low-risk lesions (topical 5-FU/imiquimod, cryotherapy, curettage, radiation for non-surgical candidates); advanced/unresectable disease - hedgehog inhibitors (BCC) and immunotherapy (cemiplimab for advanced SCC)","RECONSTRUCT the Mohs defect once margins are clear"],"tech":["Reconstruct facial Mohs defects by SUBUNIT and up the reconstructive ladder: primary closure/secondary intention for small defects, local FLAPS (rhombic, bilobed, nasolabial, forehead) and full-thickness skin GRAFTS by site","Reconstruct only AFTER margin clearance (Mohs or permanent-section confirmation) - do not rearrange tissue over an unconfirmed margin","Match tissue color/contour and hide scars in subunit borders/RSTLs"],"after":["Surveillance for RECURRENCE and NEW primaries (field cancerization - these patients get more skin cancers), sun protection, and skin self-exam; closer follow-up for high-risk SCC and transplant patients","Manage perineural/nodal disease with oncology/radiation as indicated; monitor reconstruction healing and revise as needed","Dermatology co-management for ongoing screening"],"pearls":["BCC is the most common cancer and rarely metastasizes; SCC can metastasize - especially large, recurrent, perineural, or immunosuppressed-patient tumors","Mohs gives the highest cure and best tissue preservation for high-risk and facial tumors - reconstruct only after margins are clear","Reconstruct facial defects by subunit, hiding scars in borders and relaxed skin tension lines","These patients field-cancerize - lifelong surveillance and sun protection matter, and transplant patients get aggressive SCCs"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Basal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482439/"},{"t":"American Academy of Dermatology","u":"https://www.aad.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-vascular-anomalies","name":"Vascular Anomalies (Hemangiomas vs Malformations, ISSVA)","sec":"plastics","present":["The ISSVA classification splits vascular anomalies into TUMORS (proliferative - most importantly INFANTILE HEMANGIOMA) and MALFORMATIONS (structural vessel errors - capillary, venous, lymphatic, arteriovenous, and combined) - a distinction that dictates entirely different management","INFANTILE HEMANGIOMA: absent/small at birth, PROLIFERATES over the first months, then INVOLUTES over years; GLUT1-positive; complications when it ulcerates, obstructs (airway/eye - amblyopia), or signals PHACE syndrome","MALFORMATIONS: present at birth, GROW WITH the child, never involute; low-flow (capillary 'port-wine stain', venous, lymphatic) vs high-flow (arteriovenous - can be dangerous)"],"dx":["History (timing of onset and growth pattern) usually distinguishes hemangioma (appears/proliferates postnatally then regresses) from a malformation (present at birth, grows proportionately, persists)","Imaging (ultrasound with Doppler for flow; MRI/MRA for extent and flow characterization) confirms and defines malformations; biopsy/GLUT1 staining if uncertain","Recognize syndromic associations (PHACE with large facial hemangiomas; Sturge-Weber with V1 port-wine stain; Klippel-Trenaunay; CLOVES)"],"tx":["INFANTILE HEMANGIOMA: most need only observation (they involute), but treat problematic ones (ulceration, airway/periocular/functional threat, disfigurement) with ORAL PROPRANOLOL (first-line; timolol topical for small superficial lesions); surgery for residual deformity after involution","CAPILLARY malformation (port-wine stain): pulsed-dye LASER; VENOUS/LYMPHATIC malformations: sclerotherapy +/- resection, sirolimus for extensive/complicated lesions","ARTERIOVENOUS malformation (high-flow): the hardest - embolization plus complete resection (partial treatment/proximal ligation causes recruitment and worsening); multidisciplinary vascular-anomalies team"],"tech":["Propranolol therapy (with appropriate cardiac/glucose monitoring) for infantile hemangioma; pulsed-dye laser for port-wine stains; image-guided SCLEROTHERAPY for venous/lymphatic malformations","AVM: catheter EMBOLIZATION followed by complete surgical resection (avoid proximal feeder ligation, which triggers collateral recruitment); reconstruction of resulting defects","Multidisciplinary planning (interventional radiology, plastic surgery, dermatology, hematology-oncology)"],"after":["Hemangioma: monitor involution and treat residual skin/fibrofatty deformity later; malformations RECUR/persist and need long-term multidisciplinary follow-up","Surveillance for AVM recurrence (they recruit and re-expand); manage complications (bleeding, ulceration, functional/psychosocial impact)","Genetics/syndrome follow-up where relevant"],"pearls":["The first question is tumor vs malformation (ISSVA): a hemangioma appears after birth, proliferates, then involutes (GLUT1+); a malformation is present at birth, grows with the child, and never involutes","Oral propranolol is first-line for problematic infantile hemangiomas - most others just need observation as they involute","Never just ligate the proximal feeder of an AVM - it recruits collaterals and worsens; embolize then resect completely","Large facial hemangiomas (PHACE) and V1 port-wine stains (Sturge-Weber) flag syndromes - image and screen"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vascular Malformations / Hemangioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499851/"},{"t":"ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","u":"https://www.issva.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-rhinoplasty","name":"Rhinoplasty","sec":"plastics","present":["Surgery to reshape the nose for AESTHETIC and/or FUNCTIONAL goals; functional rhinoplasty/septoplasty relieves nasal obstruction (deviated septum, internal/external valve collapse), while aesthetic rhinoplasty refines the dorsum, tip, and proportions","The nose is analyzed by subunit and by the interplay of skin-soft-tissue envelope, bony-cartilaginous framework, and internal lining - changes to one affect the others","Combined functional-aesthetic goals are common (e.g., correcting a deviated septum while refining the dorsal hump)"],"dx":["Systematic analysis: dorsal profile/hump, tip projection/rotation/definition, radix, nasal length, deviation, and the airway (septum, turbinates, internal valve - Cottle/modified maneuvers, external valve collapse)","Assess skin thickness (thick skin hides refinement/heals with more scar; thin skin shows every irregularity), prior surgery, and realistic goals; photographic analysis","Screen for body-dysmorphic concerns and set expectations"],"tx":["Preserve or reconstruct nasal SUPPORT while reshaping - over-resection (of the dorsum, tip cartilage, or septum) causes late deformity and airway collapse; maintain an adequate dorsal septal L-STRUT (~10 mm) during septoplasty to prevent saddle-nose collapse","Techniques: dorsal reduction or PRESERVATION rhinoplasty, tip refinement with suture/graft techniques, osteotomies for the bony vault, and structural GRAFTS (spreader grafts for the internal valve/middle vault, columellar strut/septal extension for tip support, alar batten grafts)","Address the airway (septoplasty, turbinate reduction, valve support) alongside aesthetic changes"],"tech":["OPEN (transcolumellar incision - direct exposure/structural grafting) vs CLOSED (endonasal) approach; harvest cartilage grafts (septal preferred, then ear/rib) for support and contour; osteotomies to narrow/straighten the bony vault","Spreader grafts to open the internal nasal valve and reconstruct the middle vault; tip sutures/grafts for definition and support; preserve key support structures","Meticulous framework work - the long-term result depends on structural support, not just removal"],"after":["Splint/cast ~1 week, expect prolonged EDEMA (especially the tip, and in thick skin - final result can take 12+ months); avoid trauma/glasses on the dorsum during bony healing","Monitor for airway function, and late issues (dorsal irregularities, tip asymmetry, polly-beak, saddle deformity, valve collapse) that may need revision","Revision rhinoplasty is common and technically harder - set expectations"],"pearls":["Preserve support - over-resection of the dorsum/tip/septum causes late collapse and airway obstruction; keep an adequate L-strut (~10 mm) in septoplasty","Spreader grafts open the internal valve and rebuild the middle vault - a key functional-aesthetic maneuver","Thick skin hides refinements and swells long; thin skin reveals every irregularity - plan and counsel accordingly","The final result takes a year (tip edema is last to resolve); revision rhinoplasty is common and more difficult"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rhinoplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544290/"},{"t":"American Academy of Facial Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery","u":"https://www.aafprs.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-facelift-necklift","name":"Facelift (Rhytidectomy) & Neck Lift","sec":"plastics","present":["Rhytidectomy addresses facial and neck AGING - jowls, midface descent, deep nasolabial/marionette folds, and neck laxity (platysmal banding, submental fullness) - by repositioning descended soft tissue and redraping skin","Facial aging combines soft-tissue descent, volume loss, and skin changes; a lift repositions/tightens, while volume (fat grafting/fillers) and skin-quality treatments are complementary","The SMAS (superficial musculoaponeurotic system) is the key structural layer that gives durable results"],"dx":["Analyze the aging face by layer (skin, fat, SMAS/muscle, bone) and region (midface, jowl, neck); assess skin quality, laxity, volume loss, platysmal bands, and submental fat/anatomy","Evaluate comorbidities/smoking (a major risk for skin-flap necrosis - many surgeons require cessation), and set realistic goals; screen for body-dysmorphic concerns","Photographic analysis and individualized planning"],"tx":["SMAS-based rhytidectomy repositions deep tissue (SMAS plication, imbrication, or deep-plane techniques) with conservative skin redraping - tightening the SMAS, not the skin, avoids the 'wind-swept' over-pulled look and lasts longer","NECK: platysmaplasty (corset/midline plication) and submental liposuction/defatting for bands and fullness; combine with volume restoration (fat grafting) and skin resurfacing as needed","Manage tension on the skin closure to protect the flap and hide scars"],"tech":["Incisions around the ear (pre/postauricular, hairline) with skin-flap elevation and SMAS manipulation (plication vs deep-plane sub-SMAS dissection); platysmaplasty and submental work for the neck; meticulous hemostasis","Preserve the facial nerve branches (frontal/temporal and marginal mandibular are most vulnerable) and the great auricular nerve during dissection","Redrape and inset skin without tension"],"after":["Watch for the two classic complications: HEMATOMA (the most common early complication - a expanding/painful hematoma needs urgent evacuation; men and hypertension raise the risk) and skin-FLAP NECROSIS (higher with smoking/tension, classically postauricular)","Facial NERVE injury (usually neurapraxia/temporary) and great auricular nerve numbness; scar care and swelling resolution over weeks-months","Long-term: aging continues; results are refreshed, not permanent"],"pearls":["Tighten the SMAS, not the skin - deep-layer support gives a natural, durable result and avoids the over-pulled look","Expanding, painful hematoma is the most common early complication - control blood pressure and evacuate urgently; men/HTN are higher risk","Smoking is a major cause of skin-flap necrosis - insist on cessation before a facelift","Know where the facial nerve branches run (temporal and marginal mandibular are most at risk) and dissect to protect them"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rhytidectomy (Facelift)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564376/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-blepharoplasty-browlift","name":"Blepharoplasty & Brow Lift","sec":"plastics","present":["Periorbital rejuvenation. BLEPHAROPLASTY addresses UPPER-lid dermatochalasis (excess skin +/- fat causing hooding and, when severe, superior visual-field obstruction - a functional indication) and LOWER-lid bags/fat herniation and skin","BROW LIFT elevates a ptotic brow (brow descent contributes to upper-lid hooding - lifting the brow may be needed before/with upper blepharoplasty, or excess skin will recur)","Assess as a unit - brow position, upper lid, lower lid, and midface all interact"],"dx":["Evaluate brow position (true brow ptosis vs pseudo-excess of lid skin), levator function/eyelid PTOSIS (a droopy lid margin is different from excess skin and needs ptosis repair, not just skin excision), lower-lid TONE (snap-back/distraction test), and dry-eye status","Document visual-field obstruction for functional upper blepharoplasty; assess fat compartments and tear-trough/midface","Screen for thyroid eye disease and dry eye (both change the plan and risk)"],"tx":["UPPER blepharoplasty: conservative skin (and selective fat) excision, leaving enough skin for lid closure; correct coexisting brow ptosis (brow lift) and true lid ptosis (levator surgery) as indicated","LOWER blepharoplasty: transconjunctival (fat only, no skin scar) or transcutaneous approaches, with fat repositioning/removal and conservative skin management; support lax lower lids (canthopexy/canthoplasty) to prevent malposition","BROW LIFT techniques: endoscopic, direct, or temporal, by brow position/hairline"],"tech":["Upper lid: mark and excise a conservative skin ellipse (+/- muscle/fat), preserving enough skin to close; lower lid: transconjunctival fat repositioning/removal or transcutaneous skin-muscle flap with lateral canthal support","Endoscopic or open brow lift to reposition the brow; protect the supraorbital/supratrochlear and facial nerve (frontal branch)","Conservative resection - over-resection causes lagophthalmos (upper) and ectropion/retraction (lower)"],"after":["Watch for the sight-threatening complication of RETROBULBAR HEMORRHAGE (severe pain, proptosis, vision loss - an emergency needing suture release/lateral canthotomy and decompression); ice, head elevation, and avoid over-resection","LOWER-lid MALPOSITION (ectropion/scleral show/retraction) from over-resection or poor lid support, LAGOPHTHALMOS/dry eye (upper), and asymmetry; scar and swelling care","Long-term monitoring and revision as needed"],"pearls":["Separate the diagnoses: brow ptosis, lid skin excess (dermatochalasis), and lid-margin ptosis are different problems needing brow lift, skin excision, and levator surgery respectively","Lift the ptotic brow before/with upper blepharoplasty or the hooding recurs","Support the lax lower lid (canthopexy) during lower blepharoplasty to prevent ectropion/scleral show","Retrobulbar hemorrhage is the sight-threatening emergency - severe pain, proptosis, and vision loss demand immediate decompression"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Blepharoplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534273/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-neurotoxins-fillers","name":"Neurotoxins & Soft-Tissue Fillers","sec":"plastics","present":["Non-surgical facial rejuvenation. BOTULINUM TOXIN (neuromodulator) temporarily weakens targeted muscles to soften DYNAMIC wrinkles (glabellar frown, forehead, crow's feet) - effect onset in days, lasting ~3-4 months","Soft-tissue FILLERS (most commonly HYALURONIC ACID, which is reversible with hyaluronidase; also calcium hydroxylapatite, poly-L-lactic acid, and permanent options) restore VOLUME and fill static folds/lips/cheeks/tear troughs","Widely used, office-based, but with rare serious vascular complications"],"dx":["Analyze dynamic (movement) vs static (at rest) rhytids and volume deficits; select neuromodulator for dynamic lines and filler for volume/static folds; know the facial danger zones (glabella, nasal dorsum/tip, nasolabial) with high vascular-occlusion risk","Detailed facial vascular ANATOMY is essential - the glabella (supratrochlear) and nose (dorsal nasal/angular) communicate with the ophthalmic circulation (blindness risk)","Assess prior treatments, allergies, and realistic goals; screen for body-dysmorphic concerns"],"tx":["Neuromodulator injection into target muscles at appropriate doses; conservative dosing near the brow/lids to avoid ptosis; effect is temporary and titratable over repeated sessions","Filler placement in the correct plane/depth with techniques to reduce intravascular injection (aspiration, small aliquots, low pressure, moving-needle/cannula, and knowing the anatomy)","Have HYALURONIDASE immediately available to dissolve hyaluronic-acid filler for vascular occlusion or unwanted result"],"tech":["Precise intramuscular neuromodulator injections by facial-muscle target; filler injection with needle or blunt CANNULA in the correct plane, aspirating and using low-pressure small-aliquot technique to minimize intravascular injection","Recognize and immediately treat VASCULAR OCCLUSION - blanching, disproportionate pain, and dusky/livedo skin (skin necrosis), or vision change/pain (retinal artery occlusion) - with high-dose hyaluronidase (for HA), warmth, and urgent ophthalmology referral for visual symptoms","Aseptic technique to prevent infection/biofilm"],"after":["Neuromodulator: counsel on onset (days), duration (~3-4 months), and complications (brow/lid ptosis, asymmetry, unintended muscle weakness - all temporary); avoid manipulation that spreads toxin","Filler: watch for bruising, nodules/biofilm, the Tyndall effect (bluish HA too superficial), and the feared VASCULAR complications (skin necrosis, and rarely BLINDNESS from retrograde embolization) - early recognition and hyaluronidase are critical","Follow-up for touch-ups and complication management"],"pearls":["Neuromodulators treat dynamic lines (movement); fillers treat volume/static folds - matching the tool to the problem is the core skill","Know the facial vascular danger zones - the glabella and nose connect to the ophthalmic circulation, and intravascular filler can cause skin necrosis or blindness","Keep hyaluronidase on hand - it dissolves HA filler and is the treatment for vascular occlusion; blanching plus disproportionate pain is occlusion until proven otherwise","Vision change during/after facial filler is an emergency (retinal artery occlusion) - urgent ophthalmology referral"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Botulinum Toxin / Soft Tissue Fillers","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560944/"},{"t":"FDA: Dermal Fillers & Botulinum Toxin","u":"https://www.fda.gov"}]},{"id":"plastics-liposuction-fat-grafting","name":"Liposuction & Fat Grafting","sec":"plastics","present":["LIPOSUCTION removes localized subcutaneous fat deposits to reshape body contour (flanks, abdomen, thighs, submental) - a CONTOURING procedure, NOT a weight-loss or obesity treatment, best for the fit patient with diet-resistant deposits","FAT GRAFTING (autologous fat transfer) harvests fat by liposuction, processes it, and re-injects it to restore volume (face, breast, buttock, contour defects) - two sides of the same fat-handling coin","Tumescent technique (dilute local anesthetic + epinephrine) is standard and improves safety/hemostasis"],"dx":["Select good candidates: near-ideal weight with localized deposits and good skin ELASTICITY (poor skin tone will not retract and may need skin excision instead); assess skin quality, deposits, and asymmetry","Set expectations that this is contouring, not weight loss; screen comorbidities and body-dysmorphic concerns","For fat grafting, assess the recipient site and required volume (accounting for partial resorption)"],"tx":["Tumescent liposuction with appropriate cannulas, respecting SAFE aspiration volumes (large-volume >~5 L raises fluid-shift/complication risk) and leaving a smooth even layer to avoid contour irregularities/dermal injury","Fat grafting: atraumatic harvest, processing (decant/centrifuge/filter), and injection in SMALL ALIQUOTS across multiple tunnels/planes to maximize graft survival (each parcel needs a vascular bed); OVER-correct modestly for expected partial RESORPTION","Combine liposuction (contour) with fat grafting (volume) as needed"],"tech":["Suction-assisted (or power/ultrasound/laser-assisted) lipoplasty via small incisions with tumescent infiltration; even, cross-tunneling passes in the deep and superficial planes to prevent irregularities","Fat grafting with small-aliquot, multi-plane, low-pressure injection; for GLUTEAL (BBL) fat grafting, inject ONLY in the SUBCUTANEOUS plane, never intramuscular (the deep gluteal veins cause fatal fat EMBOLISM) - use ultrasound guidance and a rigid cannula","Monitor tumescent lidocaine dosing to avoid toxicity"],"after":["Compression garments, monitoring for contour irregularities, seroma, fluid shifts (large-volume), and skin changes; fat grafts partially resorb (final volume settles over months) and may need repeat sessions","Counsel on realistic, staged results and skin-retraction limits; weight stability preserves results","Long-term follow-up for symmetry/contour"],"pearls":["Liposuction is contouring for fit patients with localized fat and good skin tone - it is not a treatment for obesity, and poor skin elasticity needs excision, not just suction","Gluteal fat grafting (BBL) must stay SUBCUTANEOUS - intramuscular injection causes fatal fat embolism; use a rigid cannula and ultrasound guidance","Fat grafts survive best in small aliquots across many planes (each parcel needs a blood supply) - and over-correct for partial resorption","Respect tumescent lidocaine limits and large-volume fluid shifts - both are real safety issues"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Liposuction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563135/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-abdominoplasty-body-contouring","name":"Abdominoplasty & Post-Bariatric Body Contouring","sec":"plastics","present":["ABDOMINOPLASTY ('tummy tuck') removes excess lower-abdominal skin/fat and repairs RECTUS DIASTASIS (plication of separated rectus muscles) - for post-pregnancy or post-weight-loss laxity that liposuction alone cannot address","POST-BARIATRIC/massive-weight-loss body contouring treats the large skin redundancy left after major weight loss - abdominoplasty/panniculectomy, lower body lift (belt lipectomy), brachioplasty, thighplasty, and mastopexy/upper-body lift","Panniculectomy (removal of an overhanging pannus, often functional/hygienic) vs cosmetic abdominoplasty (adds muscle plication and umbilical transposition)"],"dx":["Assess skin laxity/redundancy, rectus diastasis, hernias, prior scars, and residual fat; confirm WEIGHT STABILITY (and, after bariatric surgery, adequate time and nutritional optimization - protein/vitamin deficiencies impair healing)","Evaluate comorbidities and VTE risk (body-contouring/abdominoplasty carries notable venous thromboembolism risk); smoking cessation is essential (skin-flap necrosis)","Plan staging for multiple areas and set expectations about scars"],"tx":["Abdominoplasty: excise the lower-abdominal skin/fat flap, PLICATE the rectus diastasis (restoring the abdominal wall/waist), transpose the umbilicus, and close over drains; miniabdominoplasty for limited lower-abdominal laxity; combine with liposuction (lipoabdominoplasty) judiciously to preserve flap perfusion","Post-bariatric: staged lower body lift/belt lipectomy, brachioplasty, thighplasty, breast/upper-body procedures; repair concurrent hernias","VTE PROPHYLAXIS (risk stratification, chemoprophylaxis for higher-risk cases) and nutritional optimization"],"tech":["Low transverse incision, flap elevation to the costal margin, rectus plication (diastasis repair), umbilical transposition, and tension-managed closure with drains; preserve flap perfusion (limit aggressive concurrent liposuction of the flap)","Belt lipectomy/lower body lift circumferentially for massive weight loss; brachioplasty/thighplasty for extremity redundancy","Meticulous hemostasis, dead-space management (progressive tension sutures reduce seroma), and drains"],"after":["Watch for SEROMA (the most common complication - drains/progressive tension sutures reduce it), skin-flap NECROSIS (higher with smoking/tension/aggressive liposuction), hematoma, and VTE (early ambulation + prophylaxis)","Flexed positioning early to protect the closure, compression garment, and activity restriction; scar maturation over months and possible revision","Nutritional follow-up (post-bariatric) and staged completion of other areas"],"pearls":["Abdominoplasty fixes what liposuction cannot - skin redundancy and rectus diastasis; plication restores the abdominal wall and waist","VTE is a real risk in abdominoplasty/body contouring - risk-stratify and use prophylaxis, and mobilize early","Smoking is a major cause of skin-flap necrosis; preserve flap perfusion by limiting aggressive concurrent flap liposuction","Post-bariatric contouring is staged and needs weight stability plus nutritional optimization - protein/vitamin deficiency impairs healing"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Abdominoplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431058/"},{"t":"American Society of Plastic Surgeons","u":"https://www.plasticsurgery.org"}]},{"id":"plastics-gender-affirming-surgery","name":"Gender-Affirming Surgery","sec":"plastics","present":["Surgical care for gender dysphoria, one component of multidisciplinary gender-affirming care (with mental health support and hormone therapy) - individualized to the patient's goals; not every patient wants every procedure","CHEST/'TOP' surgery: masculinizing mastectomy/chest contouring (double-incision with free nipple graft, or periareolar/keyhole for smaller chests) and feminizing breast augmentation. FACIAL feminization/masculinization and body contouring also fall here","GENITAL/'BOTTOM' surgery: masculinizing (metoidioplasty, phalloplasty with urethral lengthening) and feminizing (vaginoplasty - most commonly penile-inversion, or intestinal); complex, staged reconstructions"],"dx":["Multidisciplinary assessment per WPATH Standards of Care (informed consent, persistent well-documented gender dysphoria, capacity, and management of coexisting conditions); confirm goals and readiness","For genital surgery, address hormone therapy, fertility counseling (procedures may affect fertility - offer preservation), and hair removal for donor/graft sites (e.g., neovaginal lining, urethral flaps)","Assess comorbidities, smoking (flap risk), and social support"],"tx":["Masculinizing chest: mastectomy with chest contouring and nipple-areola repositioning (double-incision free-nipple-graft for larger chests; periareolar/keyhole for smaller); feminizing chest: augmentation (often with hormone-induced development first)","Feminizing genital: VAGINOPLASTY (penile-inversion is standard; intestinal for depth/revision) creating a neovagina, clitoroplasty, labiaplasty; masculinizing genital: METOIDIOPLASTY (uses hormonally enlarged clitoris) or PHALLOPLASTY (free/pedicled flap - e.g., radial forearm, ALT - with staged urethral lengthening and later erectile/testicular implants)","Facial feminization/masculinization and adjunct procedures as desired"],"tech":["Chest masculinization: mastectomy, chest-wall contouring, and free nipple grafting/repositioning; vaginoplasty: penile-inversion neovaginal canal creation with dilation regimen; phalloplasty: free/pedicled flap phallus with microsurgery, staged urethroplasty, and later penile/testicular prostheses","Preoperative hair removal of flap/lining donor sites; meticulous urethral reconstruction (the source of most phalloplasty complications - strictures/fistulae)","Staged approach for complex genital reconstruction"],"after":["Vaginoplasty requires a lifelong neovaginal DILATION regimen to maintain depth/caliber (loss of depth without it); monitor for the common complications - urethral STRICTURE/FISTULA (phalloplasty/metoidioplasty), wound healing/flap issues, and neovaginal stenosis","Ongoing multidisciplinary care (mental health, hormones, primary care - including any needed cancer screening of residual tissue); revisions are common in genital surgery","Psychosocial support and long-term follow-up"],"pearls":["Gender-affirming surgery is individualized and multidisciplinary (WPATH) - not every patient wants or needs every procedure","Vaginoplasty needs lifelong dilation to preserve depth and caliber - counsel and ensure adherence","Urethral complications (strictures, fistulae) are the most common problems after phalloplasty/metoidioplasty - meticulous urethroplasty and staging matter","Address fertility preservation and donor-site hair removal BEFORE surgery - both are time-sensitive planning steps"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gender Affirmation Surgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563196/"},{"t":"WPATH Standards of Care","u":"https://www.wpath.org"}]},{"id":"pmr-rehab-principles","name":"Rehabilitation Principles & Functional Assessment","sec":"pmr","present":["Rehabilitation restores FUNCTION, independence, and quality of life after disabling injury or illness - delivered by an INTERDISCIPLINARY team (physiatrist, PT, OT, speech-language pathology, rehab nursing, psychology, social work)","The modern framework is the WHO ICF model: IMPAIRMENT (body structure/function) -> ACTIVITY limitation -> PARTICIPATION restriction","Rehabilitation focuses on maximizing what a person can do, not only the underlying disease"],"dx":["FUNCTIONAL ASSESSMENT centers on ADLs (basic self-care: feeding, bathing, dressing, toileting, transfers, continence) vs IADLs (community tasks: cooking, finances, medications, transportation)","The FUNCTIONAL INDEPENDENCE MEASURE (FIM) grades the level of assistance/burden of care; set SMART, individualized rehab goals","Assess REHAB CANDIDACY - medical stability, ability to participate, and potential to improve"],"tx":["Interdisciplinary, goal-directed care matched to the appropriate LEVEL: acute INPATIENT rehab (able to tolerate ~3 hours/day of therapy), skilled nursing facility, home health, or outpatient therapy","Team communication and family involvement are central","Re-assess function and adjust goals over time"],"pearls":["Rehabilitation uses the ICF model: impairment -> activity limitation -> participation restriction","The FIM measures functional independence and burden of care; distinguish ADLs (self-care) from IADLs (community tasks)","Acute inpatient rehab generally requires the ability to tolerate ~3 hours/day of therapy and potential to improve","Rehabilitation is interdisciplinary and function-focused, not disease-focused alone"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Knowledge / Rehab Principles","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rehabilitation / ICF","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553142/"}]},{"id":"pmr-gait-analysis","name":"Gait Analysis & Abnormal Gait Patterns","sec":"pmr","present":["Gait deviations impair mobility and safety; recognizing a PATTERN helps localize the underlying cause","The gait cycle divides into STANCE (~60%) and SWING (~40%) phases","Distinct pathologic gaits map to specific neurologic or musculoskeletal problems"],"dx":["ANTALGIC (painful) gait: shortened stance on the affected side; TRENDELENBURG gait: hip ABDUCTOR (gluteus medius) weakness (or L5) - the pelvis DROPS toward the contralateral (swing) side; STEPPAGE/foot-drop gait: ankle dorsiflexor weakness (common peroneal/L5) with high-stepping","HEMIPLEGIC gait: circumduction of a spastic leg (stroke); PARKINSONIAN gait: shuffling, festinating, reduced arm swing; ATAXIC gait: wide-based (cerebellar)","WADDLING gait: proximal (pelvic girdle) myopathy causing bilateral Trendelenburg"],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause; an ANKLE-FOOT ORTHOSIS (AFO) addresses foot drop; gait training and appropriate assistive devices improve safety","Strengthening (e.g., hip abductors for Trendelenburg) and balance training as indicated","Match the intervention to the identified deficit"],"pearls":["Trendelenburg gait = hip abductor (gluteus medius) weakness - the pelvis drops to the contralateral swing side","Steppage gait (foot drop) = dorsiflexor weakness (common peroneal/L5) - treat with an AFO","Antalgic gait shortens stance on the painful side; parkinsonian gait is shuffling/festinating with reduced arm swing","A waddling gait suggests bilateral hip-girdle (proximal myopathy) weakness"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Gait Analysis Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Gait Analysis / Abnormal Gait","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554493/"}]},{"id":"pmr-exercise-modalities","name":"Therapeutic Exercise, Modalities & Manual Medicine","sec":"pmr","present":["Therapeutic exercise and physical modalities are core physiatric treatment tools prescribed by the rehabilitation team","Exercise types span range-of-motion, strengthening, endurance, stretching, and balance/proprioception","Modalities (heat, cold, electrotherapy) are adjuncts to active therapy"],"dx":["EXERCISE: range-of-motion (passive/active-assisted/active), STRENGTHENING (isometric = no joint movement; isotonic = concentric/ECCENTRIC = lengthening; isokinetic), aerobic/endurance, and stretching","MODALITIES: HEAT increases blood flow for subacute/chronic conditions (superficial hot packs; ULTRASOUND is a DEEP heating modality) - avoid over acute injury, malignancy, bleeding, or insensate/ischemic areas; COLD (cryotherapy) for acute injury/inflammation (vasoconstriction)","Electrotherapy (TENS for pain via gate control; e-stim), traction, and manual techniques (mobilization, manipulation, massage)"],"tx":["Match the modality/exercise to the phase of injury: RICE and cold for acute injury; heat and active exercise for subacute/chronic","Respect contraindications (heat over malignancy/insensate tissue; caution with anticoagulation)","Emphasize active exercise over passive modalities for durable benefit"],"pearls":["Heat increases blood flow (subacute/chronic) - avoid it over acute injury, malignancy, bleeding, or insensate/ischemic areas; cold is for acute injury (vasoconstriction)","Therapeutic ultrasound is a DEEP heating modality","Isometric = no joint movement; eccentric = a lengthening contraction (key for tendinopathy rehab)","Active therapeutic exercise, not passive modalities, drives lasting functional gains"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Modalities / Therapeutic Exercise","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Therapeutic Modalities","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576372/"}]},{"id":"pmr-stroke-rehab","name":"Stroke Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Stroke is a leading cause of long-term disability; deficits depend on the vascular territory - motor, sensory, language, cognition, swallowing, vision, and NEGLECT (shares content with the neurology framing)","Rehabilitation begins EARLY and is interdisciplinary","Motor recovery follows characteristic patterns"],"dx":["Assess hemiparesis (recovery proceeds PROXIMAL-to-DISTAL and follows synergy patterns), spasticity, APHASIA (dominant hemisphere), NEGLECT (non-dominant hemisphere - worse rehab prognosis), DYSPHAGIA, hemianopia, shoulder subluxation, and bladder function","Brunnstrom stages describe motor recovery; screen for post-stroke depression and cognitive impairment","Identify complications early (shoulder subluxation, contracture, pressure injury, DVT)"],"tx":["Early mobilization, task-specific/repetitive training, CONSTRAINT-INDUCED movement therapy, spasticity management, and DYSPHAGIA screening BEFORE oral feeding; DVT prophylaxis","Prevent complications (support the subluxed shoulder, prevent contractures/pressure injuries), treat depression, and address secondary prevention","Interdisciplinary, goal-directed rehabilitation"],"pearls":["Motor recovery proceeds proximal-to-distal and follows synergy patterns; constraint-induced therapy and task-specific repetition drive gains","Screen SWALLOWING before oral feeding (aspiration risk)","Non-dominant strokes cause neglect (a worse rehab prognosis); shoulder subluxation is common in flaccid hemiplegia - support the arm","Screen for and treat post-stroke depression"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/ASA Stroke Rehabilitation Guidelines","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stroke Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470422/"}]},{"id":"pmr-tbi-rehab","name":"Traumatic Brain Injury Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Traumatic brain injury (TBI) causes physical, COGNITIVE, and BEHAVIORAL sequelae (shares content with the neurology/neurosurgery framing); severity is graded by the Glasgow Coma Scale (mild 13-15, moderate 9-12, severe <=8)","Rehabilitation spans the continuum from acute care to community reintegration","Cognitive and behavioral recovery evolve over time"],"dx":["POST-TRAUMATIC AMNESIA (PTA) duration is a key PROGNOSTIC indicator; the RANCHO LOS AMIGOS scale tracks cognitive recovery (levels I-VIII)","Assess cognition, AGITATION/behavior, physical deficits, dysautonomia (paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity), and heterotopic ossification risk","Monitor for post-traumatic seizures and endocrine dysfunction"],"tx":["Manage AGITATION with ENVIRONMENTAL measures first (structure, reduce stimulation) - minimize sedation and restraints; provide cognitive rehabilitation and a structured environment","Prevent/treat complications (heterotopic ossification, spasticity, seizures, dysautonomia, DVT); family education and support","Rancho-level-guided, interdisciplinary care"],"pearls":["Post-traumatic amnesia (PTA) duration is a key prognostic indicator in TBI","The Rancho Los Amigos scale tracks cognitive recovery (levels I-VIII)","Manage agitation with environmental measures first - avoid oversedation and restraints","Watch for paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity (dysautonomia) and heterotopic ossification"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Brain Injury Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: TBI Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562179/"}]},{"id":"pmr-sci-classification","name":"Spinal Cord Injury — Classification (ASIA) & Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Spinal cord injury (SCI) level and completeness determine functional outcome (shares content with the neurosurgery framing); traumatic SCI classically affects young males (MVCs, falls)","The neurologic LEVEL and whether the injury is COMPLETE vs INCOMPLETE drive prognosis and the rehab plan","Recognized incomplete SYNDROMES have characteristic patterns"],"dx":["The ASIA/ISNCSCI exam defines motor and sensory levels and the ASIA IMPAIRMENT SCALE (A = complete, no motor/sensory function in the lowest sacral segments; B = sensory incomplete; C/D = motor incomplete; E = normal); SACRAL SPARING defines an incomplete injury","INCOMPLETE SYNDROMES: CENTRAL CORD (older, hyperextension - UPPER > lower extremity weakness, the most common), BROWN-SEQUARD (hemisection - ipsilateral motor/proprioception loss, contralateral pain/temperature loss), ANTERIOR CORD (motor and pain/temperature lost, proprioception spared - worst prognosis), and cauda equina","Functional outcomes map to level (e.g., C6 tenodesis grasp, C7 triceps/transfer independence)"],"tx":["Acute spine stabilization and prevention of secondary injury, then interdisciplinary rehab (mobility, ADLs, transfers, bladder/bowel, skin care)","PREVENT complications: autonomic dysreflexia, pressure injury, DVT, orthostatic hypotension, spasticity, heterotopic ossification, and respiratory issues (high cervical injuries)","Level-specific functional goals and equipment"],"pearls":["The ASIA exam classifies level and completeness - sacral sparing means the injury is incomplete; ASIA A is complete","Central cord syndrome (upper > lower extremity weakness, older patients, hyperextension) is the most common incomplete syndrome","Anterior cord syndrome has the worst prognosis; Brown-Sequard = ipsilateral motor/contralateral pain-temperature loss","Functional milestones follow the level (C6 tenodesis grasp, C7 independent transfers)"],"refs":[{"t":"ASIA / ISNCSCI Standards","u":"https://asia-spinalinjury.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Cord Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560721/"}]},{"id":"pmr-autonomic-dysreflexia","name":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","sec":"pmr","present":["A potentially life-threatening HYPERTENSIVE emergency in spinal cord injury at or above T6 - a NOXIOUS stimulus BELOW the injury triggers a massive unopposed sympathetic response","Symptoms: a pounding HEADACHE, flushing and sweating ABOVE the lesion, reflex BRADYCARDIA, nasal congestion, and anxiety","Untreated, it can cause seizure, stroke, or death"],"dx":["Suspect it in SCI at/above T6 with a sudden severe blood-pressure rise (often >20-40 mmHg above the patient's baseline) and the classic symptoms","Immediately search for the NOXIOUS TRIGGER - BLADDER distension (a blocked/kinked catheter) is the MOST COMMON, followed by bowel impaction, and then skin/pressure sources (tight clothing, ingrown nail, pressure injury)","It is a clinical emergency, not a diagnosis to work up slowly"],"tx":["EMERGENCY management: SIT THE PATIENT UP (to drop blood pressure orthostatically), LOOSEN constrictive clothing/devices, and FIND and REMOVE the trigger - check/drain the BLADDER first (unkink or replace the catheter), then relieve bowel impaction (with topical anesthetic)","Give a rapid-acting antihypertensive (e.g., nitroglycerin, nifedipine) if the blood pressure remains dangerously high","Monitor closely until resolved and educate the patient/caregivers"],"pearls":["Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in SCI at/above T6 - a noxious stimulus below the lesion causes a hypertensive emergency with headache and reflex bradycardia","Bladder distension is the most common trigger (then bowel impaction)","Treat by SITTING THE PATIENT UP, loosening clothing, and REMOVING the trigger (drain the bladder first) - add a short-acting antihypertensive if needed","It can cause stroke or death - treat it as an emergency"],"refs":[{"t":"PVA Autonomic Dysreflexia Clinical Practice Guideline","u":"https://pva.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Autonomic Dysreflexia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482434/"}]},{"id":"pmr-neurogenic-bladder-bowel","name":"Neurogenic Bladder & Bowel","sec":"pmr","present":["Loss of normal bladder and bowel control after neurologic injury (SCI, MS, spina bifida, stroke) - causing incontinence, retention, and (critically) risk to the KIDNEYS","Complications include UTIs, upper-tract (renal) damage from high pressures, and skin breakdown","Management protects renal function and promotes continence/independence"],"dx":["UPPER MOTOR NEURON / SPASTIC (reflexic) bladder (injury above the conus): a small, spastic bladder with DETRUSOR-SPHINCTER DYSSYNERGIA -> high storage pressures and reflux/renal risk; LOWER MOTOR NEURON / FLACCID (areflexic) bladder (conus/cauda): a large, atonic bladder with overflow and retention","URODYNAMICS characterize pressures and dyssynergia; the bowel is similarly UMN (reflexic) vs LMN (areflexic)","Monitor for UTIs and upper-tract changes"],"tx":["Bladder: CLEAN INTERMITTENT CATHETERIZATION is the mainstay for many; anticholinergics/timed voiding and management of dyssynergia to keep storage pressures LOW (protecting the kidneys)","Bowel: establish a scheduled BOWEL PROGRAM (fiber/fluids; reflex stimulation/suppositories for UMN, manual techniques for LMN)","Prevent and treat UTIs; surveillance of the upper tracts"],"pearls":["Protecting the UPPER urinary tracts (kidneys) is the priority - high detrusor pressures and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia threaten the kidneys","Clean intermittent catheterization is the mainstay for many neurogenic bladders","UMN (spastic/reflexic) bladder occurs above the conus; LMN (flaccid/areflexic) at the conus/cauda","Establish a scheduled bowel program (reflexic vs areflexic technique); neurogenic bladder predisposes to UTIs"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Neurogenic Bladder/Bowel Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Neurogenic Bladder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560617/"}]},{"id":"pmr-spasticity","name":"Spasticity Evaluation & Management","sec":"pmr","present":["Spasticity is a VELOCITY-DEPENDENT increase in muscle tone from upper-motor-neuron lesions (stroke, SCI, MS, cerebral palsy, TBI)","It can impair function, hygiene, and positioning and cause pain and CONTRACTURE - but is sometimes functionally useful (e.g., extensor tone for standing)","Treatment is indicated only when spasticity interferes with function, care, or comfort"],"dx":["Grade with the MODIFIED ASHWORTH SCALE (resistance to passive movement); the Tardieu scale adds a velocity component","Identify AGGRAVATING factors - NOXIOUS stimuli (UTI, pressure injury, ingrown toenail, constipation, pain) worsen spasticity","Assess whether the spasticity is helping or hindering function"],"tx":["STEPWISE approach: first REMOVE noxious triggers and optimize stretching/range-of-motion/positioning/therapy; then ORAL agents (baclofen, tizanidine, DANTROLENE - acts peripherally on muscle, benzodiazepines)","FOCAL spasticity: BOTULINUM TOXIN injections (targeted muscles) and nerve/motor-point blocks; severe generalized (especially lower-limb/spinal) spasticity: INTRATHECAL BACLOFEN pump; surgery (tendon lengthening, rhizotomy) for refractory cases","Treat only when spasticity is problematic"],"pearls":["Spasticity is velocity-dependent (graded by the Modified Ashworth scale)","Noxious stimuli (UTI, pressure injury, ingrown nail, constipation) worsen spasticity - look for and treat triggers first","Stepwise: therapy/stretching -> oral agents -> focal botulinum toxin -> intrathecal baclofen for severe disease","Dantrolene acts peripherally (on muscle); treat spasticity only when it impairs function, hygiene, or comfort"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Spasticity Management","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spasticity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507869/"}]},{"id":"pmr-ms-rehab","name":"Multiple Sclerosis Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Multiple sclerosis (a demyelinating CNS disease) causes FATIGUE, spasticity, weakness, ataxia, bladder dysfunction, cognitive changes, and HEAT sensitivity (shares content with the neurology framing)","Rehabilitation targets symptoms and function (disease-modifying therapy is managed separately by neurology)","FATIGUE is the most common disabling symptom"],"dx":["Assess rehab-amenable impairments: FATIGUE (the leading disabling symptom), spasticity, gait/balance, bladder, and cognition/depression","Note HEAT sensitivity (UHTHOFF phenomenon - transient worsening with elevated body temperature); the EDSS grades disability","Identify safety and mobility limitations"],"tx":["EXERCISE is safe and beneficial (avoid OVERHEATING); ENERGY-CONSERVATION strategies for fatigue; spasticity management; gait aids/AFO; bladder programs; and treatment of depression","Multidisciplinary, symptom- and function-focused rehabilitation","Cooling strategies during activity"],"pearls":["Fatigue is the most common disabling symptom of MS - use energy-conservation strategies","Heat worsens symptoms (Uhthoff phenomenon) - avoid overheating during exercise (which is otherwise beneficial)","Rehab is symptom- and function-focused (disease-modifying therapy is separate)","Address spasticity, bladder, gait/balance, and cognition/depression"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / National MS Society Rehab","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Multiple Sclerosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499849/"}]},{"id":"pmr-parkinson-rehab","name":"Parkinson Disease & Movement Disorder Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Parkinson disease causes bradykinesia, rigidity, tremor, and postural instability (shares content with the neurology framing) - producing gait impairment (festination, FREEZING), falls, hypophonia, and dysphagia","Rehabilitation complements medication and targets mobility, safety, speech, and swallowing","Falls from postural instability are a major concern"],"dx":["Assess gait/balance, FREEZING episodes, fall risk, speech (hypophonia), and swallowing; Hoehn & Yahr staging describes disease progression","Identify medication 'on/off' fluctuations to time therapy","Evaluate cognition and mood"],"tx":["AMPLITUDE-based training (LSVT BIG) and external CUEING (visual floor lines, auditory metronome) improve gait and freezing; LSVT LOUD for hypophonia; aerobic/resistance exercise (beneficial and possibly neuroprotective)","FALL PREVENTION and dysphagia management; time therapy to medication 'on' periods","Multidisciplinary, ongoing rehabilitation"],"pearls":["Amplitude-based training (LSVT BIG) and external cueing (visual lines, auditory metronome) help parkinsonian gait and FREEZING","LSVT LOUD improves soft speech (hypophonia)","Fall prevention is central (postural instability); exercise is beneficial and possibly neuroprotective","Time therapy sessions to medication 'on' periods for best participation"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / Parkinson Foundation Rehab","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Parkinson Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470193/"}]},{"id":"pmr-neuromuscular-rehab","name":"Neuromuscular Disease Rehabilitation (GBS, Myopathy, ALS)","sec":"pmr","present":["Weakness from disorders of the motor neuron, nerve, neuromuscular junction, or muscle (shares content with the neurology framing) - GUILLAIN-BARRE syndrome (acute, ascending, usually recovers), ALS (progressive), and myopathies","Rehabilitation is tailored to the trajectory (recovering vs progressive)","A key principle is avoiding OVERWORK weakness"],"dx":["Characterize the pattern and course; GBS requires acute monitoring of RESPIRATORY and autonomic status before the recovery/rehab phase; ALS is progressive and multidisciplinary","Assess strength, function, respiratory status, swallowing, and communication","Recognize that excessive/eccentric exercise can WORSEN weakness in some neuromuscular diseases"],"tx":["GBS: rehabilitate as strength returns (gentle, avoiding OVERWORK/fatigue), retrain gait/ADLs, and prevent contractures, pressure injuries, and DVT during the paralytic phase","ALS: focus on MAINTAINING function and independence - assistive devices, augmentative communication (AAC), respiratory support (non-invasive ventilation), and nutrition (PEG) - with palliative, multidisciplinary care","Energy conservation across neuromuscular diseases"],"pearls":["Avoid OVERWORK weakness in neuromuscular disease - excessive or eccentric exercise can worsen weakness","GBS generally recovers - rehab gains occur as reinnervation happens; prevent contractures/DVT during the paralytic phase","ALS is progressive - focus on maintaining function, communication (AAC), respiratory support (NIV), and nutrition (PEG)","Neuromuscular rehab emphasizes energy conservation and multidisciplinary care"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Neuromuscular Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Guillain-Barre / ALS","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532254/"}]},{"id":"pmr-dysphagia-rehab","name":"Dysphagia & Swallowing Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Swallowing impairment (from stroke, TBI, neuromuscular disease, head-and-neck cancer, or aging) risks ASPIRATION (pneumonia) and malnutrition (shares content with the neurology/ENT framings)","Speech-language pathology leads evaluation and therapy","Silent aspiration makes bedside assessment unreliable in isolation"],"dx":["Bedside swallow screening plus INSTRUMENTAL assessment: the MODIFIED BARIUM SWALLOW (videofluoroscopy) and FIBEROPTIC ENDOSCOPIC EVALUATION OF SWALLOWING (FEES) detect penetration/aspiration, including SILENT aspiration (no cough)","Characterize the impaired phase (oral vs pharyngeal)","Assess nutritional status and pneumonia risk"],"tx":["Diet/texture modification (thickened liquids), compensatory strategies (CHIN-TUCK, postural techniques), and swallow-strengthening exercises; a feeding tube if the swallow is unsafe","Aspiration precautions and ORAL CARE (reduces aspiration pneumonia)","SLP-directed, individualized therapy"],"pearls":["SILENT aspiration (no cough) is dangerous and missed on bedside exam - use instrumental assessment (MBS/FEES)","Chin-tuck posture and texture modification (thickened liquids) are compensatory strategies","Screen swallowing before oral feeding after stroke","Good oral hygiene reduces aspiration pneumonia"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Dysphagia Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dysphagia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559174/"}]},{"id":"pmr-aphasia-cognitive","name":"Aphasia & Cognitive-Communication Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Language and communication deficits after brain injury/stroke (dominant hemisphere) - APHASIA - plus cognitive-communication problems after TBI (shares content with the neurology framing)","Recognizing the type of communication deficit guides rehabilitation","Speech-language pathology directs therapy"],"dx":["APHASIA types: BROCA (non-fluent, effortful speech with PRESERVED comprehension - the patient is aware/frustrated; frontal) vs WERNICKE (FLUENT but nonsensical speech with POOR comprehension - the patient is unaware; temporal); global and conduction (poor repetition) aphasias","Distinguish APHASIA (language) from DYSARTHRIA (motor speech, language intact) and apraxia of speech; cognitive-communication deficits (attention, memory, executive function, pragmatics) are common after TBI","Formal SLP assessment characterizes the deficit"],"tx":["SLP-directed aphasia therapy and AUGMENTATIVE/ALTERNATIVE COMMUNICATION (AAC); cognitive rehabilitation with compensatory strategies (memory aids, structured routines)","Family/caregiver training in communication strategies","Individualized, goal-based therapy"],"pearls":["Broca aphasia = non-fluent, effortful speech with preserved comprehension (the patient is aware/frustrated)","Wernicke aphasia = fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension (the patient is unaware)","Distinguish aphasia (language) from dysarthria (motor speech)","Cognitive-communication deficits (attention/memory/pragmatics) are common after TBI - use compensatory strategies and AAC"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / ASHA Aphasia Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Aphasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559315/"}]},{"id":"pmr-low-back-pain","name":"Low Back Pain & Lumbar Radiculopathy","sec":"pmr","present":["Low back pain is extremely common - most is nonspecific/MECHANICAL and self-limited (shares content with the orthopedics/neurology framings); LUMBAR RADICULOPATHY causes dermatomal LEG pain from nerve-root compression (disc herniation, stenosis)","The key task is separating benign back pain from serious causes","RED FLAGS point to dangerous underlying disease"],"dx":["RED FLAGS: saddle anesthesia and bowel/bladder dysfunction (CAUDA EQUINA - emergency), fever/IV drug use (infection), weight loss/cancer history (malignancy), and major trauma/osteoporosis (fracture), or progressive neurologic deficit","The straight-leg-raise test suggests L5/S1 radiculopathy; localize the root - L4 (knee/quadriceps, patellar reflex), L5 (foot drop/dorsiflexion, great toe), S1 (plantarflexion, ankle reflex)","Most low back pain needs NO early imaging (image only for red flags or persistent/progressive symptoms)"],"tx":["Stay ACTIVE (avoid bed rest), analgesia (NSAIDs; AVOID opioids), physical therapy/exercise, and reassurance/education","Radiculopathy usually improves - options include epidural steroid injection and surgery for progressive deficit or cauda equina","Address biomechanics and function"],"pearls":["Most acute low back pain is nonspecific and self-limited - stay active, avoid bed rest, and don't image early (image only with red flags)","Cauda equina (saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction, bilateral leg symptoms) is a surgical EMERGENCY","Localize radiculopathy: L5 (foot drop, great toe), S1 (plantarflexion, ankle reflex)","Avoid opioids for low back pain"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / ACP Low Back Pain Guidance","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Low Back Pain / Radiculopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538173/"}]},{"id":"pmr-cervical-radiculopathy","name":"Cervical Radiculopathy & Neck Pain","sec":"pmr","present":["Neck pain with or without radiating ARM pain (shares content with the orthopedics/neurology framings); cervical RADICULOPATHY arises from disc herniation or spondylosis compressing a nerve root","The critical distinction is from cervical MYELOPATHY (spinal cord compression) - a surgical concern","Most radiculopathy improves with conservative care"],"dx":["The SPURLING test reproduces radicular pain; localize the root - C5 (deltoid/shoulder abduction, biceps reflex), C6 (wrist extension/biceps, thumb), C7 (triceps/wrist flexion, middle finger, triceps reflex), C8 (finger flexion/intrinsics)","MYELOPATHY red flags: gait imbalance, hand clumsiness, HYPERREFLEXIA, and positive HOFFMANN/Babinski signs - prompt MRI and surgical referral","EMG/imaging as needed for atypical or refractory cases"],"tx":["Radiculopathy: conservative care (physical therapy, NSAIDs, traction) with epidural steroid injection or surgery for refractory pain/progressive deficit","MYELOPATHY: surgical evaluation (it does not resolve with conservative care)","Address posture and biomechanics"],"pearls":["The Spurling test reproduces cervical radicular pain","Localize: C6 (wrist extension, thumb), C7 (triceps, middle finger)","Watch for MYELOPATHY (gait imbalance, hand clumsiness, hyperreflexia, Hoffmann/Babinski) - a surgical red flag, not simple radiculopathy","Most cervical radiculopathy improves conservatively"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Cervical Spine Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Radiculopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441828/"}]},{"id":"pmr-shoulder-disorders","name":"Shoulder Disorders (Rotator Cuff, Adhesive Capsulitis)","sec":"pmr","present":["Shoulder pain is common (shares content with the orthopedics framing) - ROTATOR CUFF disease (supraspinatus most affected: pain with overhead activity, weakness, night pain), subacromial IMPINGEMENT, and ADHESIVE CAPSULITIS ('frozen shoulder')","Other causes include biceps tendinopathy, AC-joint disease, and instability","Adhesive capsulitis is associated with DIABETES"],"dx":["Exam: impingement signs (NEER, Hawkins), rotator cuff testing (empty-can/drop-arm for supraspinatus; resisted external rotation for infraspinatus), and ADHESIVE CAPSULITIS (loss of BOTH passive and active range - especially external rotation)","Imaging (ultrasound/MRI) evaluates for tears when weakness or refractory symptoms are present","Distinguish impingement from a full-thickness tear"],"tx":["Rotator cuff/impingement: physical therapy (scapular and cuff strengthening), NSAIDs, and subacromial corticosteroid injection; surgery for full-thickness tears or refractory disease","Adhesive capsulitis: physical therapy/stretching and intra-articular injection - it is self-limited over months (freezing -> frozen -> thawing)","PT is first-line for most shoulder pain"],"pearls":["Rotator cuff (supraspinatus) disease: painful arc, weakness, and a positive empty-can/drop-arm test","Adhesive capsulitis loses PASSIVE range (especially external rotation) and is associated with diabetes - it is self-limited over months","Physical therapy is first-line for most shoulder pain","Weakness or a failed conservative course suggests a full-thickness tear (imaging)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Shoulder Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rotator Cuff / Adhesive Capsulitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547664/"}]},{"id":"pmr-knee-oa","name":"Knee Osteoarthritis & Pain","sec":"pmr","present":["Knee pain is a common presentation (shares content with the orthopedics framing); OSTEOARTHRITIS causes activity-related pain, brief morning stiffness (<30 minutes), and crepitus, especially in older/obese patients","Other causes: patellofemoral pain, meniscal, and ligamentous injury","The rehab approach emphasizes exercise and load management"],"dx":["Exam and WEIGHT-BEARING radiographs (joint-space narrowing, osteophytes) for OA; patellofemoral pain (anterior pain, worse on stairs); meniscal tears (McMurray, mechanical locking)","Imaging severity correlates poorly with symptoms","Assess alignment, strength, and function"],"tx":["OA: EXERCISE (QUADRICEPS strengthening) and WEIGHT LOSS are the most effective conservative measures; activity modification, topical/oral NSAIDs, corticosteroid injection (short-term), and bracing; total knee replacement for severe/refractory disease","Patellofemoral pain: quadriceps/hip strengthening","Avoid overreliance on imaging"],"pearls":["Exercise (quadriceps strengthening) and weight loss are the most effective conservative treatments for knee OA","Corticosteroid injections give only short-term relief; imaging severity correlates poorly with symptoms","Patellofemoral pain (anterior, worse on stairs) responds to quadriceps/hip strengthening","Total knee replacement is reserved for severe, refractory OA"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / OARSI Knee OA Guidance","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Knee Osteoarthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507884/"}]},{"id":"pmr-hip-pain","name":"Hip & Greater Trochanteric Pain","sec":"pmr","present":["Hip pain (shares content with the orthopedics framing); the LOCATION localizes the source - GROIN pain suggests intra-articular disease, LATERAL pain suggests greater trochanteric pain syndrome","Buttock/posterior pain is often referred (lumbar spine, SI joint, piriformis)","Accurate localization directs treatment"],"dx":["GROIN pain = intra-articular (OSTEOARTHRITIS, labral tear, AVASCULAR NECROSIS); LATERAL pain = GREATER TROCHANTERIC PAIN SYNDROME (gluteal tendinopathy/bursitis - tenderness over the trochanter)","Provocative tests (FABER/FADIR for intra-articular/labral), Trendelenburg (abductor weakness); radiographs for OA/AVN; consider MRI for AVN with a normal X-ray","Remember hip pathology can refer to the KNEE"],"tx":["OA: as for the knee (exercise, weight loss, injection, total hip replacement for end-stage); GREATER TROCHANTERIC PAIN SYNDROME: load management, GLUTEAL strengthening, physical therapy, and injection","Do not miss avascular necrosis (steroid/alcohol use) - obtain MRI if suspected with a normal X-ray","Address biomechanics"],"pearls":["Localize hip pain: GROIN = intra-articular (OA, labral tear, avascular necrosis); LATERAL = greater trochanteric pain syndrome (gluteal tendinopathy)","Treat greater trochanteric pain syndrome with gluteal strengthening and load management (not just 'bursitis' injections)","Consider avascular necrosis (steroid/alcohol use) with groin pain and a normal X-ray - get MRI","Hip pathology can refer pain to the knee - always examine the hip in knee pain"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Hip Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Greater Trochanteric Pain Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557433/"}]},{"id":"pmr-tendinopathies","name":"Epicondylitis & Other Tendinopathies","sec":"pmr","present":["Overuse tendinopathies (shares content with the orthopedics framing): LATERAL epicondylitis ('tennis elbow' - extensor origin, lateral elbow pain with gripping/wrist extension) and MEDIAL epicondylitis ('golfer's elbow' - flexor-pronator origin)","Other common sites: de Quervain tenosynovitis (thumb), Achilles, and patellar tendinopathy","Tendinopathy is DEGENERATIVE (tendinosis), not primarily inflammatory"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: focal tenderness and pain with resisted testing (resisted wrist EXTENSION for lateral epicondylitis; FINKELSTEIN test for de Quervain)","Imaging is usually unnecessary","Identify aggravating repetitive activities"],"tx":["Activity/LOAD modification and ECCENTRIC exercise (the mainstay of tendinopathy rehab), counterforce bracing, and topical/oral NSAIDs; most are self-limited","Corticosteroid injections give short-term relief but may WORSEN long-term outcomes","Correct biomechanics and technique"],"pearls":["Tendinopathy is degenerative (tendinosis), not inflammatory - so ECCENTRIC loading exercise is the mainstay (not just rest/NSAIDs)","Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow): pain with resisted wrist extension; de Quervain: positive Finkelstein test","Corticosteroid injections help short-term but may worsen long-term outcomes","Load management and correcting technique prevent recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Tendinopathy Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lateral Epicondylitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431092/"}]},{"id":"pmr-oa-rehab","name":"Osteoarthritis Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Osteoarthritis is the most common joint disease (shares content with the orthopedics framing) - a degenerative process producing activity-related pain, brief stiffness, and functional loss across weight-bearing and hand joints","The physiatric approach emphasizes function and active self-management over passive treatments","It affects the knees, hips, hands, and spine most"],"dx":["Clinical features: activity-related pain, morning stiffness LASTING <30 MINUTES, crepitus, and bony enlargement; radiographs show joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis/cysts","HEBERDEN (DIP) and BOUCHARD (PIP) nodes characterize hand OA","Distinguish from inflammatory arthritis (longer stiffness, systemic features)"],"tx":["Core non-pharmacologic care: EXERCISE (aerobic + strengthening), WEIGHT LOSS, education/self-management, and ASSISTIVE DEVICES/bracing (a cane in the OPPOSITE hand offloads the joint)","Pharmacologic: topical/oral NSAIDs and intra-articular corticosteroid; joint REPLACEMENT for end-stage disease","Emphasize function over passive modalities"],"pearls":["Exercise and weight loss are the cornerstone of OA management across joints - more effective than most passive modalities","Morning stiffness <30 minutes distinguishes OA from inflammatory arthritis","A cane in the OPPOSITE hand offloads the affected joint","Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes indicate hand OA; joint replacement is for end-stage disease"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / OARSI OA Guidance","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoarthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482326/"}]},{"id":"pmr-inflammatory-arthritis-rehab","name":"Inflammatory Arthritis Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Rheumatoid arthritis and other inflammatory arthritides (shares content with the internal medicine/rheumatology framing) cause joint inflammation, deformity, and functional loss","The rehab role is joint PROTECTION, deformity prevention, and maintaining function (medical/DMARD therapy is rheumatology-directed)","Distinguishing inflammatory from mechanical (OA) disease guides care"],"dx":["Inflammatory pattern: prolonged MORNING STIFFNESS (>1 hour), SYMMETRIC small-joint involvement (RA), warmth/swelling, and systemic features - versus OA's <30-minute stiffness and mechanical pain","Assess active vs quiescent disease and functional limitations","Medical diagnosis/management (DMARDs) is by rheumatology"],"tx":["JOINT PROTECTION techniques, ENERGY CONSERVATION, splinting, and range-of-motion/strengthening; balance rest during acute FLARES with exercise during quiescent periods","Assistive devices and adaptive equipment; patient education","Exercise is safe and beneficial even in RA"],"pearls":["Inflammatory arthritis has prolonged morning stiffness (>1 hour) and symmetric small-joint involvement (vs OA's <30 minutes, mechanical pattern)","Rehab focuses on joint protection, energy conservation, splinting, and maintaining ROM/strength","Exercise is safe and beneficial even in RA (balance rest during acute flares)","DMARDs are the medical mainstay (rheumatology-directed)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / ACR Rehabilitation Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rheumatoid Arthritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441999/"}]},{"id":"pmr-sports-concussion","name":"Sports Concussion","sec":"pmr","present":["A concussion is a mild traumatic brain injury from a biomechanical force (shares content with the neurology framing) - with headache, dizziness, confusion/fogginess, balance problems, and cognitive, emotional, and sleep symptoms","Loss of consciousness is NOT required for diagnosis","Recognition on the sideline prevents further injury"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (aided by standardized tools such as the SCAT); NO same-day return to play if a concussion is suspected ('when in doubt, sit them out')","Imaging is typically NORMAL and not needed unless red flags appear (focal deficit, worsening/prolonged symptoms, suspected structural injury)","Be aware of the rare, catastrophic SECOND-IMPACT syndrome (a second hit before recovery)"],"tx":["Brief RELATIVE rest, then a GRADUATED, symptom-limited RETURN-TO-PLAY protocol with medical clearance BEFORE returning to contact; RETURN TO LEARN precedes return to play","Most recover within weeks; persistent symptoms warrant targeted rehab (vestibular, cervical, cognitive)","Avoid prolonged strict rest"],"pearls":["'When in doubt, sit them out' - no same-day return to play after a suspected concussion","Use a graduated, symptom-limited return-to-play protocol with medical clearance before contact; return to LEARN comes first","Brief relative rest then gradual activity (not prolonged strict rest) is preferred","Second-impact syndrome is rare but catastrophic; imaging is usually not needed"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Concussion Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sports Concussion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537017/"}]},{"id":"pmr-overuse-sports-injuries","name":"Overuse & Sports Injuries","sec":"pmr","present":["Overuse injuries result from repetitive microtrauma (shares content with the orthopedics framing) - tendinopathy, STRESS FRACTURES, apophysitis (in growing athletes), and medial tibial stress syndrome ('shin splints')","Training errors and biomechanics are the usual drivers","Recognition prevents progression to complete fracture or chronic injury"],"dx":["Identify TRAINING ERRORS (too much, too soon) and biomechanical factors; STRESS FRACTURE presents with focal bony tenderness and activity-related pain - the early X-ray is often NORMAL (use MRI or bone scan)","HIGH-RISK stress fractures (femoral neck, anterior tibia, navicular, 5th metatarsal) require careful management; apophysitis (Osgood-Schlatter, Sever) affects growing athletes","Consider RED-S/female athlete triad in stress fractures"],"tx":["Relative REST/activity modification, correction of training errors and biomechanics, and gradual return; stress fractures need protected weight-bearing and attention to BONE HEALTH","Eccentric loading for tendinopathy; address the FEMALE ATHLETE TRIAD/RED-S (energy deficiency, menstrual dysfunction, low bone density)","Graduated return-to-sport progression"],"pearls":["Overuse injuries come from training errors (too much, too fast) - correct load and biomechanics","Stress fractures may have a normal early X-ray - use MRI or bone scan","High-risk stress fractures (femoral neck, anterior tibia, navicular, 5th metatarsal) need careful management","Consider RED-S/female athlete triad (energy deficiency, menstrual dysfunction, low bone density) in stress fractures"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Sports Medicine Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Stress Fractures / Overuse Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554538/"}]},{"id":"pmr-osteoporosis-falls","name":"Osteoporosis, Falls & Fragility-Fracture Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Osteoporosis (low bone mass) leads to FRAGILITY FRACTURES (hip, vertebral, wrist) - with FALLS as the major mechanism (shares content with the internal medicine/orthopedics framings)","Hip fractures carry major morbidity and mortality","Fall prevention and bone health are the twin rehab priorities"],"dx":["DXA (T-score <= -2.5 = osteoporosis) and FRAX risk assessment; a FRAGILITY FRACTURE defines osteoporosis REGARDLESS of the DXA score","FALLS assessment: gait/balance, medications, vision, orthostatic vitals, and home hazards","Check vitamin D status"],"tx":["FALL PREVENTION is multifactorial: strength/BALANCE exercise (e.g., Tai Chi), medication review, vision correction, and HOME-hazard reduction; bone health (calcium/vitamin D, bisphosphonates/anabolics)","POST-FRACTURE rehab: EARLY MOBILIZATION after hip fracture reduces mortality/complications; hip protectors in select patients","A multifactorial falls intervention is most effective"],"pearls":["A fragility fracture defines osteoporosis regardless of the DXA score - and warrants treatment","Fall prevention is multifactorial: strength/balance exercise (Tai Chi), medication review, vision, and home-hazard reduction","Early mobilization after hip fracture reduces mortality and complications","Check vitamin D and orthostatic vitals; DXA T-score <= -2.5 defines osteoporosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / AGS Falls & Osteoporosis","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoporosis / Falls","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441901/"}]},{"id":"pmr-degenerative-spine","name":"Degenerative Spine & Spinal Stenosis Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Degenerative spondylosis of the spine (shares content with the orthopedics/neurology framings); LUMBAR SPINAL STENOSIS classically affects older adults with NEUROGENIC CLAUDICATION","Neurogenic claudication: leg pain/heaviness with walking/standing, RELIEVED by FLEXION (sitting, leaning forward - the 'shopping-cart sign')","Cervical spondylosis can cause radiculopathy or myelopathy"],"dx":["Distinguish NEUROGENIC claudication (worse with extension/standing/walking, better with flexion/sitting) from VASCULAR claudication (worse with any exertion regardless of posture, relieved by standing/rest, diminished pulses)","MRI shows canal narrowing; assess for spondylolisthesis","Correlate imaging with symptoms (degenerative changes are common and often asymptomatic)"],"tx":["CONSERVATIVE care first: FLEXION-based exercise, activity modification, NSAIDs, and epidural steroid injection","Surgery (decompression +/- fusion) for refractory or progressive disease and function-limiting neurogenic claudication","Address function and walking tolerance"],"pearls":["Lumbar spinal stenosis causes NEUROGENIC claudication - leg symptoms worse with extension/walking and relieved by FLEXION (sitting, the shopping-cart sign)","Distinguish it from vascular claudication (posture-independent, relieved by rest, diminished pulses)","Flexion-based exercise helps stenosis","Surgery (decompression) is for refractory/progressive, function-limiting disease"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Spine Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lumbar Spinal Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430872/"}]},{"id":"pmr-electrodiagnostics","name":"Electrodiagnostics (EMG/NCS) Principles","sec":"pmr","present":["Electrodiagnostic studies (nerve conduction studies and needle EMG) extend the neurologic exam to localize and characterize disorders of nerve and muscle (shares content with the neurology framing)","They distinguish radiculopathy, plexopathy, neuropathy, myopathy, neuromuscular-junction disease, and motor neuron disease","Timing affects the findings"],"dx":["NERVE CONDUCTION STUDIES: reduced AMPLITUDE indicates AXON loss, while prolonged LATENCY/slowed VELOCITY indicates DEMYELINATION (motor and sensory nerves are studied)","NEEDLE EMG: spontaneous activity (FIBRILLATIONS/positive sharp waves) indicates ACTIVE denervation (appearing ~2-3 weeks after injury); large, polyphasic motor units indicate CHRONIC reinnervation; recruitment patterns add information","Findings LOCALIZE the lesion (root vs plexus vs nerve) and date it (acute vs chronic); paraspinal denervation points to radiculopathy"],"tx":["The study guides diagnosis, prognosis, and management - it is not itself a treatment","Timing matters: studies too early (before ~2-3 weeks) may miss denervation","Correlate electrodiagnostic findings with the clinical picture"],"pearls":["Nerve conduction studies distinguish axonal loss (low amplitude) from demyelination (slowed conduction/prolonged latency)","Needle EMG shows active denervation (fibrillations/positive sharp waves) ~2-3 weeks after injury; large polyphasic units indicate chronic reinnervation","EMG localizes the lesion (root vs plexus vs peripheral nerve) and dates it (acute vs chronic)","Paraspinal muscle denervation points to a radiculopathy"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / AANEM Electrodiagnostics","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Electrodiagnostic Studies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563236/"}]},{"id":"pmr-carpal-tunnel-entrapment","name":"Carpal Tunnel & Entrapment Neuropathies","sec":"pmr","present":["Focal compression neuropathies. CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME (median nerve at the wrist) is the MOST COMMON - numbness/tingling in the thumb, index, middle, and radial ring fingers, worse at NIGHT, with late thenar atrophy/weakness","Other entrapments: ULNAR at the elbow (cubital tunnel - 4th/5th digits, hand intrinsics), radial, and COMMON PERONEAL at the fibular head (foot drop)","Repetitive use and certain conditions predispose"],"dx":["Exam: provocative tests (PHALEN, TINEL) and the sensory distribution; EMG/NCS confirm and grade severity","Carpal tunnel associations: pregnancy, hypothyroidism, diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, and repetitive activity","Localize by the affected sensory/motor territory"],"tx":["Carpal tunnel: NIGHTTIME neutral-wrist SPLINTING (first-line), activity modification, corticosteroid injection, and surgical RELEASE for severe/refractory disease or thenar atrophy","Ulnar (cubital tunnel): elbow padding/positioning, then surgery; common peroneal: avoid compression, AFO for foot drop","Address underlying/contributing conditions"],"pearls":["Carpal tunnel syndrome (median nerve at the wrist) is the most common entrapment - night symptoms in the thumb/index/middle fingers with positive Phalen/Tinel","Nighttime neutral-wrist splinting is first-line; surgery for thenar atrophy or severe/refractory disease","Ulnar entrapment at the elbow (cubital tunnel) affects the 4th/5th digits; common peroneal compression at the fibular head causes foot drop","EMG/NCS confirm the diagnosis and grade severity"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Entrapment Neuropathy Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448179/"}]},{"id":"pmr-peripheral-neuropathy","name":"Peripheral Neuropathy Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Peripheral neuropathy (most commonly length-dependent) - DIABETES is the leading cause (also alcohol, B12 deficiency, chemotherapy, hereditary)","Presents with 'STOCKING-GLOVE' sensory loss, burning/numbness, distal weakness, and autonomic features - creating FALL and FOOT-ULCER risk","The rehab focus is safety, foot protection, and function"],"dx":["Characterize the pattern (distal symmetric polyneuropathy vs mononeuritis multiplex vs demyelinating - CIDP) and screen for causes (glucose/A1c, B12, TSH)","EMG/NCS distinguish AXONAL (most) from DEMYELINATING (CIDP - treatable); test PROTECTIVE sensation (monofilament)","Assess balance, gait, and foot skin integrity"],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause; FOOT care/protection prevents ulcers in insensate feet; FALL PREVENTION and balance training for sensory ataxia; gait aids/AFO/orthotics","NEUROPATHIC pain agents (gabapentinoids, SNRIs, TCAs) - not typical analgesics","Correct reversible causes (B12, thyroid)"],"pearls":["Diabetic distal symmetric polyneuropathy is the most common - insensate feet risk ulcers, so foot protection/care is critical","Fall prevention and balance training address sensory ataxia","Neuropathic pain responds to gabapentinoids/SNRIs/TCAs (not typical analgesics)","Check reversible causes (B12, thyroid); distinguish axonal (most) from demyelinating (CIDP, treatable)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Peripheral Neuropathy Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Neuropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542220/"}]},{"id":"pmr-plexopathy","name":"Brachial & Lumbosacral Plexopathy","sec":"pmr","present":["Plexus lesions produce deficits spanning MULTIPLE nerves/roots in one limb - not fitting a single nerve or root","BRACHIAL plexopathy: trauma/stretch, birth injury (ERB C5-6 / KLUMPKE C8-T1), radiation, neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner), and Pancoast tumor; LUMBOSACRAL plexopathy: diabetic amyotrophy, retroperitoneal hematoma/tumor, childbirth, and radiation","Localization and cause guide management"],"dx":["A deficit spanning multiple roots/nerves in one limb suggests plexopathy; EMG/NCS localize - the SENSORY nerve action potentials (SNAPs) are ABNORMAL in plexopathy but NORMAL in radiculopathy (the lesion is distal to the dorsal root ganglion) - a key distinction","MRI evaluates for structural causes (tumor, hematoma); identify inflammatory (Parsonage-Turner) and radiation etiologies","Erb palsy ('waiter's tip' posture) vs Klumpke (hand intrinsics)"],"tx":["Treat the underlying cause; rehabilitate with ROM (prevent contracture), strengthening as recovery occurs, bracing, and pain management","Neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner) often recovers over months; tumor/radiation causes need specific management","Monitor recovery and function"],"pearls":["Plexopathy causes deficits spanning multiple roots/nerves in one limb","SENSORY nerve action potentials are ABNORMAL in plexopathy but NORMAL in radiculopathy (the lesion is distal to the dorsal root ganglion) - a key EMG distinction","Erb palsy (C5-6, 'waiter's tip') vs Klumpke (C8-T1, hand intrinsics)","Parsonage-Turner (neuralgic amyotrophy): acute shoulder pain then weakness, often recovering; consider Pancoast tumor for lower brachial plexus"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Plexopathy Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Brachial Plexopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534249/"}]},{"id":"pmr-chronic-pain","name":"Chronic Pain — Multimodal Management","sec":"pmr","present":["Chronic pain (persisting >3 months, often beyond tissue healing) is best understood through a BIOPSYCHOSOCIAL lens - nociceptive, neuropathic, and NOCIPLASTIC (central sensitization) mechanisms","It impairs function, mood, and sleep and can drive disability","Management centers on function, not just pain scores"],"dx":["Characterize the pain TYPE (nociceptive vs neuropathic vs nociplastic) and the psychosocial context; assess FUNCTION","Screen for depression/anxiety, sleep disturbance, and opioid risk","Set functional goals"],"tx":["MULTIMODAL, biopsychosocial care: active EXERCISE/physical therapy, COGNITIVE-BEHAVIORAL therapy, non-opioid pharmacology (topicals, NSAIDs, neuropathic agents), and interventional procedures as ADJUNCTS","Address SLEEP and mood; MINIMIZE opioids (limited benefit and significant harm in chronic non-cancer pain); emphasize self-management","Target FUNCTION rather than pain scores alone"],"pearls":["Chronic pain needs a multimodal, biopsychosocial approach - active exercise, CBT, and non-opioid pharmacology, with function as the goal","Opioids have limited benefit and significant harm in chronic non-cancer pain","Address sleep, mood, and central sensitization (nociplastic pain)","Avoid the passive/procedure-only trap - active self-management drives outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Pain Management Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553030/"}]},{"id":"pmr-crps","name":"Complex Regional Pain Syndrome","sec":"pmr","present":["CRPS is regional pain DISPROPORTIONATE to the inciting injury/surgery (often a limb) - burning pain and ALLODYNIA with autonomic and trophic changes","Features: swelling, temperature/color changes, sweating, and hair/nail/skin changes, plus motor changes (weakness, tremor)","Type I (no identifiable nerve injury) vs Type II (causalgia - with nerve injury)"],"dx":["CLINICAL diagnosis using the BUDAPEST CRITERIA (sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor/edema, and motor/trophic categories) - a diagnosis of exclusion","No single test confirms it (imaging/bone scan are supportive at best)","Recognize it EARLY - delay worsens outcomes"],"tx":["EARLY mobilization and physical/occupational therapy (functional restoration, DESENSITIZATION, MIRROR therapy, graded motor imagery) are central - immobilization worsens CRPS","Adjuncts: neuropathic pain agents, bisphosphonates, and sympathetic blocks; multidisciplinary care","Early, function-focused treatment improves outcomes"],"pearls":["CRPS is regional pain out of proportion to the injury with autonomic/trophic changes (Budapest criteria, a clinical diagnosis)","EARLY mobilization and therapy (functional restoration, desensitization, mirror therapy) are central - immobilization worsens it","Type I (no nerve lesion) vs Type II (causalgia, with nerve injury)","Use a multidisciplinary approach and treat early"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R CRPS Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Complex Regional Pain Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430719/"}]},{"id":"pmr-myofascial-fibromyalgia","name":"Myofascial Pain & Fibromyalgia","sec":"pmr","present":["MYOFASCIAL pain is REGIONAL - taut muscle bands with TRIGGER POINTS that refer pain in characteristic patterns","FIBROMYALGIA is WIDESPREAD pain with fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, and cognitive difficulty ('fibro fog') - a central sensitization (nociplastic) disorder","Distinguishing regional from widespread pain guides treatment"],"dx":["MYOFASCIAL pain: palpable trigger points in taut bands with a reproducible referred pattern; FIBROMYALGIA: widespread pain and symptom-severity criteria (ACR) with NORMAL labs and no inflammation","Exclude mimics (hypothyroidism, inflammatory/autoimmune disease) when appropriate","Assess sleep, mood, and function"],"tx":["MYOFASCIAL pain: trigger-point release/injection or dry needling, stretching, posture correction, and treating perpetuating factors","FIBROMYALGIA: graded AEROBIC EXERCISE (best evidence), education, sleep optimization, and CBT, plus agents such as duloxetine, milnacipran, pregabalin, or amitriptyline - AVOID opioids","Multimodal, active management"],"pearls":["Fibromyalgia is a central sensitization (nociplastic) disorder - widespread pain, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, and 'fibro fog' with normal labs","Graded aerobic exercise has the best evidence, plus CBT/sleep/education and agents like duloxetine or pregabalin (avoid opioids)","Myofascial pain has focal trigger points in taut bands with referred pain (treat with trigger-point techniques/stretching)","Exclude mimics (thyroid, inflammatory disease)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / ACR Fibromyalgia Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Fibromyalgia / Myofascial Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK540974/"}]},{"id":"pmr-interventional-pain","name":"Interventional Pain Procedures","sec":"pmr","present":["Image-guided injections and procedures serve as ADJUNCTS within a comprehensive, function-focused pain plan (shares content with the anesthesiology pain framing)","They are not standalone cures - they facilitate participation in active rehabilitation","Patient selection is key"],"dx":["Common procedures: EPIDURAL STEROID injections (radicular pain from disc/stenosis - transient relief), FACET/MEDIAL BRANCH blocks and RADIOFREQUENCY ABLATION (axial facet-mediated pain), sacroiliac joint and intra-articular injections, SYMPATHETIC blocks (CRPS), and trigger-point injections","SPINAL CORD STIMULATION for refractory neuropathic/failed-back-surgery pain","Match the procedure to the pain generator"],"tx":["Select patients carefully, use image guidance, and COMBINE procedures with active rehabilitation and function goals","Recognize risks (infection, bleeding, dural puncture) and set realistic expectations (adjunct, not cure)","Reassess function after the procedure"],"pearls":["Interventional procedures (epidural steroids for radicular pain; medial branch blocks/radiofrequency ablation for facet pain) are ADJUNCTS that facilitate active rehab - not standalone cures","Epidural steroids give transient relief for radicular pain","Spinal cord stimulation is for refractory neuropathic/failed-back-surgery pain","Select patients carefully and pair procedures with exercise/function goals"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Interventional Pain Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Epidural Steroid Injections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470189/"}]},{"id":"pmr-amputation-prosthetics","name":"Amputation Rehabilitation & Prosthetics","sec":"pmr","present":["Limb loss (DYSVASCULAR/diabetic disease is the most common cause in adults; also trauma, cancer, congenital) - rehabilitation spans pre-operative through prosthetic training","The AMPUTATION LEVEL strongly influences function and the energy cost of walking","Phantom and residual limb pain are common"],"dx":["Assess the level (TRANSTIBIAL/below-knee preserves the KNEE - far better function and lower energy cost than transfemoral), healing, residual-limb shaping, and CONTRACTURE (knee/hip flexion)","Evaluate comorbidities and the VASCULAR status of the CONTRALATERAL limb (often also at risk); K-LEVELS classify functional potential for prosthetic prescription","Distinguish phantom limb pain from residual limb pain"],"tx":["Residual-limb care (shaping/shrinker, desensitization) and CONTRACTURE prevention (positioning - avoid prolonged knee flexion), prosthetic prescription/training, and gait training","Manage phantom vs residual limb pain; PROTECT the contralateral limb; energy conservation as level rises","Interdisciplinary, goal-based rehabilitation"],"pearls":["Preserving the KNEE (transtibial vs transfemoral) markedly improves function and lowers the energy cost of walking","Prevent knee/hip flexion CONTRACTURES in the residual limb (positioning; avoid a pillow under the knee)","K-levels guide prosthetic prescription; distinguish phantom limb pain from residual limb pain","Protect the contralateral limb (often also dysvascular)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Amputee Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Amputation Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560693/"}]},{"id":"pmr-orthotics-devices","name":"Orthotics, Bracing & Assistive Devices","sec":"pmr","present":["Orthoses are external devices that support, align, protect, or substitute for weak/unstable segments; assistive devices aid mobility","Orthoses are named by the JOINTS they cross (e.g., AFO = ankle-foot orthosis)","Correct prescription and fit prevent complications"],"dx":["Lower-limb orthoses: the AFO (ankle-foot orthosis) is the MOST COMMON - it manages FOOT DROP and controls the ankle (solid vs posterior-leaf-spring vs hinged); KAFO (knee-ankle-foot) for quadriceps weakness; spinal orthoses (TLSO)","ASSISTIVE gait devices span the stability/function trade-off: a CANE (least support), crutches, and a WALKER (most stability, least functional gait)","Assess the deficit the device must address and check skin/fit (especially insensate patients)"],"tx":["Prescribe the device that matches the functional deficit; a CANE is held in the hand OPPOSITE the affected leg (to offload it)","Ensure proper fit (pressure/skin checks) and provide training","Reassess as function changes"],"pearls":["An AFO is the most common lower-limb orthosis - it manages foot drop and controls the ankle","A cane is held in the hand OPPOSITE the affected leg (to offload it)","Walkers give the most stability but the least functional gait","Name orthoses by the joints they cross (AFO, KAFO); check skin/fit in insensate patients"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Orthotics / Assistive Devices","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Orthoses / Assistive Devices","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564325/"}]},{"id":"pmr-wheelchair-seating","name":"Wheelchair, Seating & Mobility","sec":"pmr","present":["Wheelchairs and seating systems provide mobility for those who cannot ambulate functionally (SCI, stroke, neuromuscular disease, amputation)","Proper prescription prevents complications (pressure injury, deformity)","The choice between manual and power depends on the individual"],"dx":["MANUAL vs POWER selection depends on upper-extremity function, endurance, cognition, and the environment","Seating assessment centers on PRESSURE distribution (to prevent injury), postural support, and function; measure for proper fit","Assess skin, posture, and transfer ability"],"tx":["Individualized prescription (manual for adequate upper-extremity function/fitness; power for those without); a PRESSURE-RELIEF cushion plus regular WEIGHT SHIFTS/pressure reliefs prevent ischial/sacral injuries","Provide postural support, skin monitoring, and training","Reassess with functional change"],"pearls":["Wheelchair seating must distribute PRESSURE to prevent ischial/sacral pressure injuries - prescribe a pressure-relief cushion and teach regular weight shifts","Manual chairs need adequate upper-extremity function/endurance; power chairs serve those without","Proper fit and postural support prevent deformity and skin breakdown","Individualize the prescription to function and environment"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Wheelchair / Seating Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Wheelchair Prescription","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560855/"}]},{"id":"pmr-pediatric-rehab","name":"Pediatric Rehabilitation & Cerebral Palsy","sec":"pmr","present":["Pediatric rehabilitation addresses childhood disability with attention to DEVELOPMENT, function, and participation (shares content with the pediatrics framing)","CEREBRAL PALSY (a non-progressive motor disorder, spastic type most common) is the prototype condition","Goals include maximizing function and preventing secondary musculoskeletal complications"],"dx":["Classify CP type/distribution and function (the GMFCS - Gross Motor Function Classification System, levels I-V)","Assess spasticity, CONTRACTURES, HIP surveillance (subluxation/dislocation risk, especially in non-ambulatory children), scoliosis, communication, and feeding","Cerebral palsy is non-progressive, but its musculoskeletal complications progress"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary, developmental, family-centered care: PT/OT/SLP, SPASTICITY management (botulinum toxin, intrathecal baclofen, selective dorsal rhizotomy), orthoses (AFO), and HIP SURVEILLANCE (serial radiographs)","Manage comorbidities and maximize function/participation","Anticipate and prevent contractures and hip displacement"],"pearls":["CP is non-progressive, but musculoskeletal complications progress - HIP SURVEILLANCE (radiographs) is essential in non-ambulatory children (silent subluxation/dislocation)","GMFCS classifies gross motor function (levels I-V)","Manage spasticity with botulinum toxin, intrathecal baclofen, or selective dorsal rhizotomy; use AFOs","Provide multidisciplinary, family-centered, developmental care"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Pediatric Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cerebral Palsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538147/"}]},{"id":"pmr-spina-bifida","name":"Spina Bifida (Myelomeningocele)","sec":"pmr","present":["Myelomeningocele (a neural tube defect) causes a spinal-cord-level motor/sensory deficit (shares content with the pediatrics/neurosurgery framings) plus neurogenic bladder/bowel and associated anomalies","Associated issues: HYDROCEPHALUS (Chiari II malformation/VP shunt), orthopedic deformities (clubfoot, hip, scoliosis), insensate skin, LATEX allergy, and TETHERED CORD","Function follows the neurologic level (as in SCI)"],"dx":["The neurologic LEVEL determines function; assess neurogenic bladder/bowel (to protect the KIDNEYS), shunt function, orthopedic status, and insensate skin","Watch for TETHERED CORD (new neurologic, functional, or urologic decline); LATEX allergy is common","Folic acid prevents neural tube defects (public health)"],"tx":["Multidisciplinary care: a neurogenic bladder/bowel program (protect the kidneys), level-appropriate mobility (bracing/wheelchair), insensate-skin protection, VP-shunt monitoring, and orthopedic management","LATEX PRECAUTIONS; monitor for tethered cord and intervene for decline","Lifelong, coordinated care"],"pearls":["Myelomeningocele function follows the neurologic level (like SCI) - manage neurogenic bladder/bowel to protect the KIDNEYS","LATEX ALLERGY is common - use latex precautions","Monitor the VP shunt (Chiari II/hydrocephalus) and watch for TETHERED CORD (new neurologic/urologic decline)","Folic acid prevents neural tube defects; protect insensate skin"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Spina Bifida Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Myelomeningocele / Spina Bifida","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545165/"}]},{"id":"pmr-cardiac-rehab","name":"Cardiac Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["A structured program after cardiac events (MI, CABG/PCI, heart failure, valve surgery) that reduces mortality and readmissions and improves function/quality of life (shares content with the internal medicine framing)","It is evidence-based but UNDERUTILIZED - referral matters","Care progresses from inpatient through maintenance phases"],"dx":["RISK-STRATIFY (often with an exercise test) and assess functional capacity in METs and comorbidities","Identify contraindications to exercise (unstable angina, decompensated heart failure, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, uncontrolled arrhythmia)","Screen for depression"],"tx":["SUPERVISED, progressive EXERCISE plus aggressive RISK-FACTOR modification (lipids, blood pressure, diabetes, SMOKING CESSATION, diet, weight), medication adherence, and psychosocial support","Phase I (inpatient) -> Phase II (monitored outpatient) -> Phase III (maintenance)","Monitor initially, then transition to independent exercise"],"pearls":["Cardiac rehab reduces mortality and readmissions after MI/revascularization and in heart failure - it's evidence-based but underutilized (refer!)","It combines supervised exercise with aggressive risk-factor modification and psychosocial support","Risk-stratify with an exercise test; measure capacity in METs","Hold for unstable angina, decompensated heart failure, or severe symptomatic aortic stenosis"],"refs":[{"t":"AHA/AACVPR Cardiac Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.heart.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537196/"}]},{"id":"pmr-pulmonary-rehab","name":"Pulmonary Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["A structured program for chronic lung disease (chiefly COPD; also interstitial lung disease and peri-lung-surgery/transplant) that improves dyspnea, exercise tolerance, and quality of life","It reduces hospitalizations and is centered on EXERCISE training","Care is multidisciplinary"],"dx":["Assess functional capacity (e.g., 6-MINUTE WALK test), dyspnea, and OXYGENATION (screen for EXERCISE DESATURATION to titrate oxygen)","Evaluate comorbidities and nutritional status","Identify goals and barriers"],"tx":["EXERCISE training (aerobic + strength) is the core; add BREATHING techniques (PURSED-LIP breathing, pacing), education, ENERGY CONSERVATION, secretion clearance, nutrition, SMOKING CESSATION, and oxygen as needed","Psychosocial support; multidisciplinary delivery","Titrate oxygen for exertional desaturation"],"pearls":["Pulmonary rehab (centered on EXERCISE training) improves dyspnea, exercise capacity, and quality of life and reduces hospitalizations in COPD","Pursed-lip breathing and pacing/energy conservation help dyspnea","Titrate oxygen for exertional desaturation","Combine with smoking cessation and education; it also benefits ILD and peri-surgical patients"],"refs":[{"t":"ATS/ERS Pulmonary Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.thoracic.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559253/"}]},{"id":"pmr-cancer-rehab","name":"Cancer Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Cancer and its treatment cause impairments: deconditioning, CANCER-RELATED FATIGUE, weakness, LYMPHEDEMA, chemotherapy neuropathy, pain, cognitive changes ('chemo brain'), and bone disease (metastases - fracture risk)","Rehabilitation spans prehabilitation, active treatment, survivorship, and palliative care","Function and quality of life are the goals"],"dx":["Identify TREATABLE impairments: cancer-related FATIGUE (very common), lymphedema, chemotherapy-induced neuropathy, and deconditioning","SCREEN for bone METASTASES/fracture risk before loading a limb or spine","Assess function across the cancer continuum"],"tx":["EXERCISE is safe and beneficial (reduces fatigue and improves function even DURING treatment); lymphedema is managed with COMPLETE DECONGESTIVE therapy; manage neuropathy/pain and use energy conservation","Modify exercise for bone metastases (fracture risk); prehab before treatment and palliative rehab both have roles","Interdisciplinary, goal-based care"],"pearls":["EXERCISE is safe and beneficial in cancer - it reduces cancer-related fatigue (the most common symptom) and improves function even during treatment","Screen for bone METASTASES/fracture risk before loading (modify exercise accordingly)","Lymphedema is managed with complete decongestive therapy","'Prehab' before treatment and palliative rehab both have roles; address chemo neuropathy and cognitive changes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Cancer Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cancer Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560937/"}]},{"id":"pmr-burn-rehab","name":"Burn Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Burns cause CONTRACTURES (from scar and protective positioning), HYPERTROPHIC scarring, pain, and functional/psychological impairment","Rehabilitation begins EARLY (in the ICU) and continues long-term","Preventing contractures is the central goal"],"dx":["Characterize burn depth, extent, and LOCATION (joints, hands, face, and neck carry high functional risk); assess ROM, scar, contracture risk, pain, and psychosocial status","Anticipate where contractures will form","Monitor scar maturation over months"],"tx":["EARLY, aggressive therapy: anti-contracture POSITIONING (OPPOSITE the anticipated contracture), splinting, ROM/stretching, and mobilization; PRESSURE GARMENTS for hypertrophic scarring","Pain control (especially to enable therapy) and psychological support; sustained scar management","Prevent contractures throughout the entire course"],"pearls":["Burn rehab is about preventing CONTRACTURES - position OPPOSITE the anticipated contracture ('the position of comfort is the position of contracture')","Splint and start ROM early; pressure garments manage hypertrophic scarring","Hands, face, and neck are high-risk areas for functional contractures","Aggressive early and sustained therapy plus psychological support are essential"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Burn Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Burn Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526118/"}]},{"id":"pmr-pressure-injuries","name":"Pressure Injuries (Pressure Ulcers)","sec":"pmr","present":["Localized damage to skin and underlying tissue over BONY PROMINENCES from sustained pressure with or without shear - a hallmark complication of immobility","High-risk sites: the SACRUM, HEELS, ISCHIUM, and trochanters; risk factors include immobility, insensate skin, malnutrition, incontinence, and poor perfusion","They are largely PREVENTABLE"],"dx":["STAGE by tissue depth: Stage 1 (non-blanchable erythema, intact skin), Stage 2 (partial-thickness/blister), Stage 3 (full-thickness into subcutaneous fat), Stage 4 (into muscle/bone/tendon); UNSTAGEABLE (obscured by eschar) and DEEP TISSUE injury (purple intact skin)","Use the BRADEN scale to identify at-risk patients","Assess for infection and nutritional status"],"tx":["PREVENTION is paramount: REPOSITION regularly (about every 2 hours), use pressure-redistributing surfaces/cushions, manage moisture/incontinence, optimize NUTRITION, and offload heels","Wound care by stage (debridement, appropriate dressings, treat infection); surgical flaps for advanced wounds","Offload and optimize nutrition to heal"],"pearls":["Pressure injuries are largely preventable - regular repositioning (about every 2 hours), pressure-redistribution surfaces, nutrition, and moisture control","Stage by tissue depth (1 = non-blanchable erythema -> 4 = muscle/bone; unstageable if covered by eschar)","The sacrum, heels, and ischium are highest-risk","Use the Braden scale to identify at-risk patients; offload and optimize nutrition to heal"],"refs":[{"t":"NPIAP Pressure Injury Staging / Guidelines","u":"https://npiap.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pressure Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553107/"}]},{"id":"pmr-vte-immobility","name":"Venous Thromboembolism & Immobility Complications","sec":"pmr","present":["Immobility (SCI, stroke, post-operative states, critical illness) predisposes to VENOUS THROMBOEMBOLISM and a broader constellation of complications","DVT (leg swelling/pain, often clinically SILENT) can lead to PE (dyspnea - potentially fatal)","Prevention is central to rehabilitation care"],"dx":["Maintain a HIGH index of suspicion (VTE risk is high in SCI, stroke, and trauma); Doppler ultrasound evaluates for DVT","Recognize the wider immobility syndrome: pressure injury, contracture, deconditioning, orthostatic hypotension, bone loss, and constipation","Watch for PE (dyspnea, tachycardia, hypoxia)"],"tx":["VTE PROPHYLAXIS (pharmacologic +/- mechanical) for at-risk rehab patients unless contraindicated; treat established DVT/PE with anticoagulation","EARLY MOBILIZATION reduces the whole spectrum of immobility complications; positioning, ROM, and skin/bowel-bladder care","Reassess prophylaxis as risk changes"],"pearls":["VTE risk is high after SCI, stroke, and trauma - use prophylaxis (pharmacologic +/- mechanical) unless contraindicated","DVT is often clinically silent; maintain suspicion for PE (dyspnea/tachycardia)","Early mobilization reduces the whole spectrum of immobility complications","The immobility syndrome spans VTE, pressure injury, contracture, deconditioning, orthostatic hypotension, and bone loss"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / CHEST VTE Prophylaxis","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: VTE Prophylaxis / Immobility","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534816/"}]},{"id":"pmr-heterotopic-ossification","name":"Heterotopic Ossification","sec":"pmr","present":["Abnormal formation of mature bone in SOFT TISSUE (typically around joints - hip, elbow, knee, shoulder) after SCI, TBI, burns, joint surgery, or trauma","Presents with DECREASED joint range of motion plus warmth, swelling, pain, and a low-grade fever - which can MIMIC DVT or infection","Early recognition preserves joint motion"],"dx":["Early markers: elevated ALKALINE PHOSPHATASE and a TRIPLE-PHASE BONE SCAN (the most sensitive early test, positive BEFORE plain-film changes)","Plain radiographs show mature bone later; differentiate from DVT and cellulitis","Track maturation before considering surgery"],"tx":["Maintain (gentle) ROM; NSAIDs (INDOMETHACIN) for prophylaxis/treatment; bisphosphonates; RADIATION prophylaxis in high-risk surgery (e.g., acetabular)","Surgical excision ONLY once the bone is MATURE (early excision risks recurrence)","Prevention in high-risk populations"],"pearls":["Suspect heterotopic ossification when a patient with SCI/TBI/burns loses joint ROM with warmth/swelling (it mimics DVT/infection)","A triple-phase bone scan detects it earliest (before X-ray changes); alkaline phosphatase rises","Maintain ROM and use NSAIDs (indomethacin)/radiation prophylaxis","Excise only after the bone matures (to avoid recurrence)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Heterotopic Ossification Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Heterotopic Ossification","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519029/"}]},{"id":"pmr-vestibular-rehab","name":"Vestibular Rehabilitation & Balance Disorders","sec":"pmr","present":["Dizziness, vertigo, and imbalance are common and drive falls; causes include BPPV (the most common peripheral cause), vestibular neuritis/labyrinthitis, unilateral vestibular hypofunction, and central disorders","Accurate diagnosis directs very different treatments","Vestibular rehabilitation restores balance and gaze stability"],"dx":["BPPV: brief POSITIONAL vertigo - the DIX-HALLPIKE maneuver reproduces characteristic positional nystagmus","Distinguish PERIPHERAL from CENTRAL causes (the HINTS exam flags concerning central signs); assess balance, gait, and fall risk","Characterize the vestibular deficit"],"tx":["BPPV: a canalith REPOSITIONING maneuver (EPLEY) - highly effective (NOT medications or generic exercises); vestibular hypofunction: VESTIBULAR REHABILITATION (gaze stabilization, habituation, balance/adaptation exercises)","AVOID prolonged vestibular SUPPRESSANTS (they impair central compensation); target fall prevention","Match therapy to the diagnosis"],"pearls":["BPPV is treated with a canalith REPOSITIONING maneuver (Epley) - not medications or generic exercises (Dix-Hallpike diagnoses it)","Vestibular rehabilitation (gaze stabilization, habituation, balance/adaptation) helps unilateral hypofunction","Avoid prolonged vestibular suppressants - they impair central compensation","Use HINTS to flag central causes; target fall prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Vestibular Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: BPPV / Vestibular Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470308/"}]},{"id":"pmr-deconditioning","name":"Deconditioning & Immobilization Syndrome","sec":"pmr","present":["Prolonged bed rest/inactivity (hospitalization, critical illness) causes MULTISYSTEM decline - deconditioning affects nearly every organ system","It compounds the primary illness and lengthens recovery","Prevention through early activity is the key principle"],"dx":["MUSCLE strength declines rapidly (roughly 1-3% per DAY of bed rest, faster in critical illness); cardiovascular deconditioning brings ORTHOSTATIC intolerance and reduced aerobic capacity","Other effects: contractures, bone loss, pressure injury, VTE, insulin resistance, and atelectasis","Recognize the pattern early"],"tx":["EARLY MOBILIZATION and activity (prevention far outperforms treatment); progressive reconditioning; upright positioning/tilt table for orthostatic intolerance; ROM and DVT/skin prophylaxis","AVOID unnecessary bed rest; ICU EARLY MOBILITY improves outcomes","Progress activity as tolerated"],"pearls":["Deconditioning affects every system - muscle strength drops ~1-3% per day of bed rest (faster in critical illness)","Early mobilization and avoiding unnecessary bed rest is the key (prevention beats treatment)","Reconditioning is progressive; a tilt table/upright positioning addresses orthostatic hypotension","ICU early mobility improves outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Deconditioning / Early Mobility","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Immobility / Deconditioning","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559097/"}]},{"id":"pmr-impairment-disability","name":"Impairment Rating, Disability & Return-to-Work Evaluation","sec":"pmr","present":["Physiatrists assess IMPAIRMENT and DISABILITY for work, benefits, and legal/administrative purposes","They also guide RETURN-TO-WORK planning after injury or illness","The role is objective and function-focused"],"dx":["Distinguish IMPAIRMENT (an objective loss of body structure/function) from DISABILITY (its contextual impact on activities, work, and roles)","Assign impairment at MAXIMAL MEDICAL IMPROVEMENT (MMI) using the AMA GUIDES; FUNCTIONAL CAPACITY EVALUATIONS (FCE) inform work restrictions","Define specific work restrictions and accommodations"],"tx":["Determine MMI, assign an impairment rating (AMA Guides), and define work restrictions/accommodations (per the ADA)","RETURN-TO-WORK planning uses modified/graded duty and addresses barriers","Keep the assessment objective and function-based"],"pearls":["Impairment (objective loss of function/structure) is distinct from disability (its contextual impact on work/roles)","Impairment rating is assigned at maximal medical improvement (MMI) using the AMA Guides","Functional capacity evaluations inform work restrictions","Return-to-work planning uses modified/graded duty and accommodations (ADA) - the physiatrist's role is objective and function-focused"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Impairment / Disability Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Disability Evaluation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559512/"}]},{"id":"pmr-geriatric-rehab","name":"Geriatric Rehabilitation & Polytrauma","sec":"pmr","present":["Older adults and multi-injury (POLYTRAUMA) patients need rehabilitation attentive to MULTIMORBIDITY, FRAILTY, cognition, and realistic functional goals","Geriatric syndromes (falls, delirium, polypharmacy, sarcopenia) complicate recovery","Care is individualized and interdisciplinary"],"dx":["COMPREHENSIVE assessment: function/ADLs, cognition (screen for DELIRIUM and dementia), FRAILTY, POLYPHARMACY, nutrition, mood, and social/home situation","POLYTRAUMA involves multiple injured systems (the model includes combined TBI, limb injury, and pain)","Prioritize and coordinate across impairments"],"tx":["Individualized, goal-oriented, interdisciplinary rehab: exercise and nutrition to target FRAILTY/sarcopenia, DELIRIUM prevention, deprescribing, fall prevention, and coordinated management of multiple impairments","Set REALISTIC function- and discharge-focused goals with caregiver support","Reassess as the patient progresses"],"pearls":["Geriatric rehab must account for multimorbidity, FRAILTY, cognition (screen delirium/dementia), and polypharmacy - not just the index injury","Exercise and nutrition target frailty/sarcopenia; prevent delirium and deprescribe","Polytrauma involves multiple injured systems requiring coordinated, prioritized rehab","Set realistic, function- and discharge-focused goals with caregiver support"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / AGS Geriatric Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Geriatric Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560531/"}]},{"id":"pmr-disorders-consciousness","name":"Disorders of Consciousness & TBI Prognosis","sec":"pmr","present":["After severe brain injury, patients occupy a spectrum of consciousness: COMA (no arousal or awareness, eyes closed), VEGETATIVE STATE/unresponsive wakefulness (arousal WITHOUT awareness - sleep-wake cycles, only reflexive responses), and the MINIMALLY CONSCIOUS STATE (inconsistent but REPRODUCIBLE awareness - visual tracking, command following)","Emergence is marked by functional communication or object use; LOCKED-IN syndrome (aware but paralyzed) is a MIMIC, not a disorder of consciousness","Accurate classification drives prognosis and care"],"dx":["Use a STANDARDIZED assessment (the Coma Recovery Scale-Revised, CRS-R) to distinguish states - MISDIAGNOSIS is common when relying on bedside impression","Separate AROUSAL from AWARENESS; rule out LOCKED-IN syndrome (pontine lesion - preserved vertical eye movement/blink) and covert consciousness","Consider etiology (traumatic vs anoxic) and time since injury"],"tx":["Prevent complications, maximize arousal/participation, and REASSESS serially; AMANTADINE has evidence for accelerating recovery in severe traumatic DOC","Prognosis is better for TRAUMATIC than anoxic injury and is time-dependent (recovery can continue for months) - avoid PREMATURE prognostication","Family-centered, goal-based care"],"pearls":["Distinguish vegetative/unresponsive wakefulness (arousal WITHOUT awareness) from the minimally conscious state (inconsistent but reproducible awareness) - use the CRS-R (misdiagnosis is common)","Locked-in syndrome is NOT a disorder of consciousness (aware but paralyzed, with preserved vertical gaze/blink)","Amantadine can accelerate recovery in traumatic DOC","Traumatic prognosis is better than anoxic; avoid premature prognostication (recovery can continue for months)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAN/AAPM&R Disorders of Consciousness Guideline","u":"https://www.aan.com"},{"t":"StatPearls: Disorders of Consciousness","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559123/"}]},{"id":"pmr-phantom-limb","name":"Phantom Limb & Residual Limb Pain","sec":"pmr","present":["After amputation, three distinct phenomena occur: non-painful PHANTOM SENSATION (awareness of the missing limb - nearly universal), PHANTOM LIMB PAIN (painful perception in the MISSING limb), and RESIDUAL LIMB PAIN (pain in the remaining stump)","Distinguishing them is essential because treatment differs","They can coexist and affect prosthetic use"],"dx":["Differentiate phantom limb PAIN (in the missing limb) from RESIDUAL limb pain (stump - often mechanical or from a NEUROMA, with a Tinel sign) and from non-painful phantom SENSATION","Assess prosthetic FIT (a common cause of residual limb pain)","Characterize pain quality and triggers"],"tx":["Phantom limb pain: MIRROR THERAPY, graded motor imagery, desensitization, neuropathic agents (gabapentinoids, TCAs), and TENS; RESIDUAL limb pain: address the cause (prosthetic REFIT, neuroma treatment)","Perioperative analgesia may reduce phantom pain; early prosthetic use can help","Reassure for non-painful phantom sensation"],"pearls":["Distinguish phantom limb PAIN (in the missing limb) from residual limb (stump) pain (often mechanical/neuroma) and non-painful phantom SENSATION (nearly universal)","Mirror therapy and graded motor imagery help phantom pain, along with neuropathic agents","Residual limb pain from a neuroma has a Tinel sign - address prosthetic fit","Non-painful phantom sensation needs only reassurance"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Amputee Pain Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Phantom Limb Pain","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448188/"}]},{"id":"pmr-lymphedema","name":"Lymphedema Management","sec":"pmr","present":["Lymphedema is the accumulation of protein-rich fluid from lymphatic dysfunction - PRIMARY (congenital) or SECONDARY (most commonly after CANCER treatment: node dissection/radiation; filariasis worldwide)","Presents with limb swelling, heaviness, skin changes, and a risk of recurrent CELLULITIS","It is controlled, not cured"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: pitting edema early progressing to non-pitting/FIBROTIC change; a positive STEMMER sign (inability to pinch the skin at the base of the toe/finger) is characteristic","Distinguish from venous edema, DVT, and systemic (usually bilateral) causes; secondary arm lymphedema commonly follows breast-cancer axillary treatment","Stage severity"],"tx":["COMPLETE DECONGESTIVE THERAPY is the mainstay: manual lymphatic drainage, multilayer COMPRESSION bandaging/garments, exercise, and meticulous SKIN care","Prevent cellulitis (avoid trauma/infection in the limb); weight management; maintenance compression","Lifelong self-management"],"pearls":["Complete decongestive therapy (manual lymphatic drainage + compression + exercise + skin care) is the mainstay - lymphedema is controlled, not cured","Secondary lymphedema after cancer node dissection/radiation is the most common cause in developed countries","A positive Stemmer sign (can't pinch the skin) is diagnostic","Meticulous skin care prevents cellulitis (a major complication); compression garments maintain control"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Lymphedema Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lymphedema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537239/"}]},{"id":"pmr-post-icu-syndrome","name":"Post-Intensive-Care Syndrome & Critical Illness Neuromyopathy","sec":"pmr","present":["POST-INTENSIVE-CARE SYNDROME (PICS) is new or worsening PHYSICAL, COGNITIVE, and MENTAL-HEALTH impairment after critical illness - and it also affects FAMILIES (PICS-F)","ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (critical illness myopathy and/or polyneuropathy) causes symmetric flaccid weakness and failure to wean from the ventilator","Risk factors include sepsis, prolonged ventilation/immobility, hyperglycemia, and corticosteroids"],"dx":["ICU-acquired weakness: diffuse SYMMETRIC weakness (proximal > distal) - preserved sensation suggests myopathy, sensory involvement suggests polyneuropathy; EMG/NCS characterize it","PICS: screen COGNITION, mental health (PTSD/anxiety/depression), and physical function","Assess families for PICS-F"],"tx":["PREVENTION: EARLY MOBILITY, minimizing sedation, glucose control, and treating sepsis; rehabilitation with progressive strengthening, functional/cognitive therapy, and psychological support","ICU FOLLOW-UP clinics; recovery is often prolonged","Address all three impairment domains"],"pearls":["Post-intensive-care syndrome spans PHYSICAL (ICU-acquired weakness), COGNITIVE, and MENTAL-HEALTH (PTSD/anxiety/depression) impairments - and affects families too","ICU-acquired weakness (critical illness myopathy/polyneuropathy) causes symmetric flaccid weakness and failure to wean","Prevention (early mobility, minimizing sedation, glucose control) is key","Recovery is often prolonged - screen and rehabilitate all three domains"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R / SCCM Post-ICU Care","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: ICU-Acquired Weakness","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564322/"}]},{"id":"pmr-pelvic-floor-rehab","name":"Pelvic Floor Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Pelvic floor dysfunction causes urinary/fecal incontinence, pelvic organ prolapse, pelvic pain, and dyspareunia (shares content with the OB/GYN framing)","Common settings: postpartum, post-prostatectomy, and neurologic disease","The key is distinguishing an UNDERACTIVE from an OVERACTIVE pelvic floor"],"dx":["Assess pelvic floor muscle STRENGTH and COORDINATION and the type of incontinence (STRESS vs URGE)","Distinguish an UNDERACTIVE (weak) floor (incontinence, prolapse) from an OVERACTIVE/NON-RELAXING floor (pelvic pain, voiding dysfunction, constipation)","Examine for prolapse"],"tx":["UNDERACTIVE: PELVIC FLOOR MUSCLE TRAINING (Kegels, with BIOFEEDBACK) - first-line for stress incontinence/weakness; bladder training for urge incontinence","OVERACTIVE/non-relaxing floor: DOWN-training/RELAXATION and manual therapy - Kegels can WORSEN it; behavioral strategies throughout","Match the program to the dysfunction type"],"pearls":["Pelvic floor muscle training (with biofeedback) is first-line for stress incontinence and weakness-related dysfunction","An OVERACTIVE/non-relaxing pelvic floor (pelvic pain, voiding dysfunction) needs DOWN-training/relaxation - Kegels can WORSEN it","Distinguish underactive from overactive BEFORE prescribing exercises","It addresses postpartum and post-prostatectomy incontinence (shares content with the OB/GYN entries)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Pelvic Floor Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pelvic Floor Dysfunction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559246/"}]},{"id":"pmr-contracture-management","name":"Contracture Prevention & Management","sec":"pmr","present":["A contracture is loss of joint range of motion from shortening of muscle, tendon, capsule, or skin - occurring after immobility, spasticity, burns, neurologic injury, or prolonged positioning","Contractures impair function, hygiene, and positioning","They are far easier to PREVENT than to reverse"],"dx":["Identify AT-RISK patients (immobile, spastic, burned, paralyzed) and assess PASSIVE range of motion","Distinguish a soft-tissue CONTRACTURE from a bony block/HETEROTOPIC OSSIFICATION (different treatment)","Recognize early, before fixed deformity develops"],"tx":["PREVENTION is paramount: regular ROM/stretching, POSITIONING/splinting, spasticity control, and early mobilization","Established contractures: stretching, SERIAL CASTING, splinting, and spasticity treatment (BOTULINUM TOXIN); surgery (tendon lengthening/release) for FIXED deformity","Treat the underlying spasticity"],"pearls":["Contractures are best PREVENTED - regular ROM, positioning/splinting, spasticity control, and early mobilization","Established contractures respond to stretching, serial casting, and splinting; surgery (tendon lengthening) is for fixed deformity","Treat the underlying spasticity (e.g., botulinum toxin)","Differentiate a soft-tissue contracture from a heterotopic ossification/bony block (different treatment)"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Contracture Management","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Joint Contractures","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551618/"}]},{"id":"pmr-sialorrhea-secretions","name":"Sialorrhea & Secretion Management","sec":"pmr","present":["Sialorrhea (drooling) and excess secretions arise from impaired swallowing/oromotor control - common in cerebral palsy, ALS, Parkinson disease, stroke, and TBI (shares content with the neurology framing)","Consequences: social impact, skin breakdown, and ASPIRATION risk","It usually reflects impaired CLEARANCE, not overproduction"],"dx":["Distinguish ANTERIOR drooling (spillage) from POSTERIOR secretion pooling (which raises ASPIRATION risk); assess swallowing, posture, and dentition","Identify the underlying neurologic cause","Characterize secretions as thin (saliva) vs thick/tenacious"],"tx":["Postural/behavioral measures and oromotor therapy; ANTICHOLINERGICS (glycopyrrolate, scopolamine patch) and BOTULINUM TOXIN injection to the salivary (parotid/submandibular) glands; suctioning","In ALS, BALANCE reducing thin saliva against thick tenacious secretions (avoid over-drying); salivary gland surgery/radiation for severe refractory cases","Tailor to the secretion type and aspiration risk"],"pearls":["Sialorrhea usually reflects impaired swallowing/clearance, not overproduction","Options include anticholinergics (glycopyrrolate, scopolamine) and botulinum toxin injection to the salivary glands","Posterior secretion pooling raises aspiration risk","In ALS, balance thin saliva against thick tenacious secretions (don't over-dry); common in CP, Parkinson, and ALS"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Sialorrhea Management","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sialorrhea","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560835/"}]},{"id":"pmr-adaptive-sports","name":"Adaptive Sports & Recreational Therapy","sec":"pmr","present":["Sports and recreation adapted for people with disabilities provide physical, PSYCHOSOCIAL, and community-participation benefits","Options include wheelchair sports, para-sport, handcycling, and adaptive equipment","Medical considerations differ by disability"],"dx":["Assess function, goals, and MEDICAL CLEARANCE (cardiac status; in SCI, autonomic and thermoregulatory risks); competitive CLASSIFICATION matches athletes by ability","Identify disability-specific risks before participation","Match the sport and adaptation to the individual"],"tx":["Prescribe sport/adaptation and EQUIPMENT (sport wheelchairs, prostheses, handcycles); address risks - AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA and impaired thermoregulation in SCI, PRESSURE injuries, and shoulder OVERUSE in wheelchair users","Recreational therapy targets participation and quality of life","Educate on safe participation"],"pearls":["Adaptive sports improve fitness, psychosocial health, and community participation","In SCI athletes, watch for autonomic dysreflexia - and 'BOOSTING' (deliberately inducing it to enhance performance) is dangerous and banned","Protect against impaired thermoregulation and pressure injuries in SCI, and shoulder overuse in wheelchair users","Recreational therapy targets participation and quality of life"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Adaptive Sports Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adaptive Sports / SCI Exercise","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568756/"}]},{"id":"pmr-occupational-rehab","name":"Occupational Rehabilitation & Ergonomics","sec":"pmr","present":["Occupational rehabilitation prevents and manages work-related injury and facilitates RETURN TO WORK","ERGONOMICS fits the job to the worker to reduce injury","Work-related musculoskeletal disorders are a major source of disability"],"dx":["Assess job demands (a functional/physical demands analysis) and the worksite; identify RISK FACTORS for work-related MSK disorders (REPETITION, FORCE, awkward POSTURE, vibration)","Determine candidacy for work conditioning/hardening programs","Match capacity to job demands"],"tx":["ERGONOMIC modification (workstation, tools, technique), WORK CONDITIONING/HARDENING programs for job-specific functional restoration, and GRADED return-to-work with modified duty","Injury-prevention education; early intervention reduces disability","Coordinate with employers on accommodations"],"pearls":["Ergonomics fits the job to the worker (workstation/tool/technique modification) to prevent work-related musculoskeletal disorders","Key risk factors are repetition, force, awkward posture, and vibration","Work conditioning/hardening programs restore job-specific function","Graded return-to-work with modified duty and early intervention reduce disability"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Occupational Rehabilitation","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Work-Related Musculoskeletal Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560860/"}]},{"id":"pmr-autonomic-orthostatic","name":"Autonomic Dysfunction / Orthostatic Intolerance in Rehabilitation","sec":"pmr","present":["Autonomic dysfunction is common in rehabilitation - after SCI (especially above T6), TBI, in Parkinson/multiple system atrophy, diabetes, GBS, and deconditioning","ORTHOSTATIC HYPOTENSION (a blood-pressure drop on standing with dizziness/syncope) LIMITS therapy participation; supine hypertension, temperature dysregulation, and tachycardia (POTS) also occur","Managing it enables rehabilitation"],"dx":["Check ORTHOSTATIC vitals: a fall of >=20 mmHg systolic or >=10 mmHg diastolic on standing defines orthostatic hypotension","Identify the cause (SCI, autonomic neuropathy, deconditioning, medications, volume depletion); POTS is a heart-rate rise >=30 WITHOUT a blood-pressure drop","Note that AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA is the hypertensive counterpart in SCI"],"tx":["NON-pharmacologic first: gradual position changes, COMPRESSION (abdominal binder/stockings), adequate FLUID/SALT intake, TILT-TABLE reconditioning, and avoiding triggers","Pharmacologic (MIDODRINE, fludrocortisone) for refractory orthostatic hypotension","Coordinate with the therapy team to maintain participation"],"pearls":["Orthostatic hypotension (a fall of >=20 systolic/>=10 diastolic on standing) is common after SCI/immobility and limits rehab participation","Treat first with gradual position changes, compression (abdominal binder/stockings), fluids/salt, and tilt-table reconditioning (midodrine/fludrocortisone if refractory)","POTS is a heart-rate rise >=30 without a blood-pressure drop","Autonomic dysreflexia is the hypertensive counterpart in SCI at/above T6"],"refs":[{"t":"AAPM&R Autonomic Dysfunction Resources","u":"https://www.aapmr.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Orthostatic Hypotension","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448192/"}]},{"id":"prev-study-designs","name":"Epidemiologic Study Designs","sec":"prev","present":["Study designs form a hierarchy of evidence for causation; choice depends on the question, outcome/exposure frequency, time, and ethics","INTERVENTIONAL: the randomized controlled trial (RCT) - randomization balances confounders and is the strongest design for efficacy/causation; OBSERVATIONAL: cohort, case-control, cross-sectional, and ecological","Board framing: match the design to the scenario (rare disease -> case-control; rare exposure -> cohort; prevalence snapshot -> cross-sectional; population-level correlation -> ecological)"],"dx":["COHORT (prospective or retrospective): groups defined by EXPOSURE and followed for OUTCOME - yields incidence and RELATIVE RISK; good for rare exposures and multiple outcomes, but slow/costly and poor for rare diseases","CASE-CONTROL: groups defined by OUTCOME (cases vs controls) looking BACK at exposure - efficient for RARE diseases and long latency, yields ODDS RATIO; prone to recall/selection bias, cannot compute incidence","CROSS-SECTIONAL: exposure and outcome measured at one time (prevalence, associations, but no temporality); ECOLOGICAL: population-level data (hypothesis-generating; the ECOLOGICAL FALLACY limits individual inference)"],"tx":["Pick the design by the question: efficacy of an intervention -> RCT; rare disease/long latency -> case-control; rare exposure or incidence needed -> cohort; disease burden at a point -> cross-sectional","Recognize the trade-offs (validity vs feasibility/cost/time/ethics) and where each design sits in the evidence hierarchy (systematic review/meta-analysis of RCTs at the top)","Use the strongest feasible design; when RCTs are unethical/impractical, well-designed cohort/case-control studies inform causation"],"pearls":["Case-control starts with the OUTCOME and gives an odds ratio (best for rare diseases); cohort starts with the EXPOSURE and gives relative risk (best for rare exposures)","Only cohort and RCT designs directly measure INCIDENCE and relative risk; case-control cannot","The ecological fallacy is inferring individual-level associations from population-level (ecological) data - a classic exam trap","RCT randomization controls for both known AND unknown confounders - its defining strength over observational designs"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Study Designs","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538321/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-disease-frequency","name":"Measures of Disease Frequency (Incidence & Prevalence)","sec":"prev","present":["Quantifying how much disease exists/arises in a population is the foundation of epidemiology and surveillance; the two core measures are INCIDENCE (new cases) and PREVALENCE (existing cases)","INCIDENCE reflects RISK of developing disease over time (etiology/acute processes); PREVALENCE reflects the BURDEN of existing disease (chronic disease, resource planning)","Board framing: distinguish incidence from prevalence and know the relationship between them"],"dx":["INCIDENCE: cumulative incidence (risk) = new cases / population at risk over a period; incidence RATE (density) = new cases / person-TIME at risk (handles varying follow-up and loss)","PREVALENCE: point prevalence = existing cases / total population at a point in time (a proportion, not a rate); period prevalence over an interval","Related rates: attack rate (outbreaks), and mortality/case-fatality measures"],"tx":["Use INCIDENCE to study causation/risk and acute disease, and to evaluate prevention (incidence should fall); use PREVALENCE for chronic-disease burden and health-services planning","Apply the relationship PREVALENCE approximately equals INCIDENCE x DURATION (in steady state) - anything that lengthens survival without cure (e.g., better treatment) RAISES prevalence even if incidence is unchanged","Choose person-time methods when follow-up time varies or there is loss to follow-up"],"pearls":["Prevalence is approximately incidence x duration - a treatment that prolongs life without curing INCREASES prevalence","Incidence measures new cases (risk/etiology); prevalence measures existing cases (burden) - do not confuse them","A rate has time in the denominator (person-time); a prevalence proportion does not","Improving survival raises prevalence - which can look like a 'rising epidemic' when incidence is flat"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Incidence and Prevalence","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430746/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-measures-association","name":"Measures of Association & Impact (RR, OR, HR, NNT)","sec":"prev","present":["Measures of association quantify the strength of the exposure-outcome relationship; measures of impact translate that into population/clinical effect","RELATIVE measures (relative risk, odds ratio, hazard ratio) describe strength of association; ABSOLUTE measures (attributable risk, risk difference, NNT) describe the actual magnitude of effect","Board framing: know which measure comes from which design and how to interpret each"],"dx":["RELATIVE RISK (RR, from cohort/RCT) = risk in exposed / risk in unexposed; ODDS RATIO (OR, from case-control) approximates RR when disease is rare; HAZARD RATIO (HR, from survival analysis) = instantaneous relative rate over time","ABSOLUTE: attributable risk / risk difference = risk in exposed - risk in unexposed; ATTRIBUTABLE RISK PERCENT and POPULATION attributable risk (impact of removing the exposure in the whole population)","NUMBER NEEDED TO TREAT (NNT) = 1 / absolute risk reduction; NUMBER NEEDED TO HARM (NNH) = 1 / absolute risk increase"],"tx":["Use RELATIVE measures to judge strength of association/causation; use ABSOLUTE measures (risk difference, NNT/NNH, population attributable risk) to judge clinical/public-health importance and to counsel patients","Interpret the null: RR/OR/HR = 1 means no association; a confidence interval crossing 1 is not statistically significant","A large relative risk can still be a small absolute effect if the baseline risk is low - always consider the absolute numbers for decisions"],"pearls":["Odds ratio comes from case-control and approximates relative risk only when the disease is rare","NNT = 1 / absolute risk reduction - the lower the NNT, the more effective the intervention","A high relative risk with a tiny baseline risk yields a small absolute benefit - relative measures can mislead without the absolute","Population attributable risk captures how much disease in the whole population a given exposure explains - key for prioritizing prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Relative Risk / Odds Ratio","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431098/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-test-characteristics","name":"Screening & Diagnostic Test Characteristics","sec":"prev","present":["Test performance determines the value of screening and diagnosis; the core metrics are SENSITIVITY, SPECIFICITY, predictive values, and likelihood ratios","SENSITIVITY/SPECIFICITY are intrinsic test properties (independent of prevalence); PREDICTIVE VALUES depend on PREVALENCE (pretest probability)","Board framing: build/interpret the 2x2 table and reason about how prevalence shifts predictive values"],"dx":["SENSITIVITY = true positives / all diseased (detects disease; a highly Sensitive test with a Negative result rules OUT - SnNout); SPECIFICITY = true negatives / all non-diseased (a highly Specific test with a Positive result rules IN - SpPin)","PREDICTIVE VALUES: PPV = true positives / all test-positives; NPV = true negatives / all test-negatives - both DEPEND ON PREVALENCE (higher prevalence raises PPV, lowers NPV)","LIKELIHOOD RATIOS (prevalence-independent): LR+ = sens/(1-spec), LR- = (1-sens)/spec; ROC curve and area-under-curve summarize discrimination across thresholds"],"tx":["Choose test purpose: a SENSITIVE test for SCREENING (minimize missed disease/false negatives) and a SPECIFIC/confirmatory test to rule in disease (minimize false positives) - screen sensitive, confirm specific","Account for PREVALENCE when interpreting a positive result: in low-prevalence screening, even a good test yields many FALSE POSITIVES (low PPV)","Use likelihood ratios and pretest probability to update to a posttest probability (independent of prevalence)"],"pearls":["SnNout / SpPin: a Sensitive test that is Negative rules OUT; a Specific test that is Positive rules IN","Predictive values depend on prevalence; sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratios do not","In a low-prevalence population, a positive screening test is often a false positive (low PPV) - the basis for screening harms","Lowering the cutoff raises sensitivity at the cost of specificity (and vice versa) - the ROC curve shows this trade-off"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Sensitivity and Specificity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557491/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Screening Evaluation","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-bias-confounding","name":"Bias, Confounding & Effect Modification","sec":"prev","present":["Systematic error threatens study validity; the resident must distinguish BIAS (systematic error in design/conduct), CONFOUNDING (a mixing of effects), and EFFECT MODIFICATION (a real difference in effect across strata)","Bias distorts the truth and cannot be fixed by more data; confounding can be controlled analytically; effect modification is a finding to report, not an error to remove","Board framing: recognize the classic biases and separate confounding from effect modification"],"dx":["SELECTION bias (who gets into/stays in the study - e.g., Berkson, healthy-worker, loss to follow-up, non-response) and INFORMATION/measurement bias (recall bias in case-control, interviewer bias, misclassification - differential vs non-differential); LEAD-TIME/length bias in screening","CONFOUNDING: a third variable associated with BOTH the exposure and the outcome and NOT on the causal pathway (classic: age, smoking); it can create, mask, or reverse an apparent association","EFFECT MODIFICATION (interaction): the exposure-outcome effect genuinely DIFFERS across levels of a third variable (different stratum-specific estimates) - a true biological/clinical phenomenon"],"tx":["Prevent BIAS by design (randomization, blinding, standardized/objective measurement, high follow-up, appropriate control selection) - it cannot be corrected after the fact","Control CONFOUNDING by design (randomization, restriction, matching) or analysis (stratification, standardization, multivariable regression)","For EFFECT MODIFICATION, report the STRATUM-SPECIFIC estimates separately (do not pool/adjust it away) - it is information, not error"],"pearls":["Bias is baked in by design and cannot be fixed with statistics; confounding can be adjusted for; effect modification should be reported by stratum, not removed","A confounder is linked to both exposure and outcome and is NOT on the causal pathway (a mediator on the pathway is different)","Randomization is uniquely powerful because it controls for unknown/unmeasured confounders too","Non-differential misclassification usually biases toward the null; differential misclassification can bias in either direction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Confounding Bias / Effect Modification","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430848/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-statistical-inference","name":"Statistical Inference (P-values, CIs, Power)","sec":"prev","present":["Statistical inference draws conclusions about a population from sample data, quantifying the role of chance; core tools are hypothesis testing, P-VALUES, CONFIDENCE INTERVALS, and POWER","The framework tests a NULL hypothesis (no difference/association) against an alternative; results are interpreted with attention to both statistical and clinical significance","Board framing: interpret p-values/CIs correctly and distinguish Type I from Type II error"],"dx":["P-VALUE = probability of observing results as/more extreme than seen IF the null were true; p < alpha (usually 0.05) -> reject the null (statistically significant) - it is NOT the probability the null is true","CONFIDENCE INTERVAL = range of plausible values for the true effect; a 95% CI excluding the null value (1 for ratios, 0 for differences) is statistically significant and also conveys precision and effect size","ERRORS: TYPE I (alpha) = false positive (reject a true null); TYPE II (beta) = false negative (fail to reject a false null); POWER = 1 - beta = probability of detecting a true effect (increased by larger sample size and effect size)"],"tx":["Report and interpret CONFIDENCE INTERVALS alongside (or instead of) p-values - they show effect magnitude and precision, not just a yes/no significance call","Ensure adequate POWER (typically >=80%) via sample-size calculation BEFORE the study; an underpowered 'negative' study cannot exclude a real effect","Separate STATISTICAL from CLINICAL significance - a tiny, unimportant effect can be statistically significant with a huge sample, and vice versa"],"pearls":["The p-value is NOT the probability the null hypothesis is true, nor the probability results are due to chance - it assumes the null is true","A 95% CI that crosses the null value (1 for RR/OR, 0 for a difference) is non-significant - and the CI also tells you effect size and precision","Type I = false positive (alpha); Type II = false negative (beta); power = 1 - beta, and bigger samples raise power","Statistical significance is not clinical importance - large samples make trivial effects 'significant'"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypothesis Testing, P Values, Confidence Intervals","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557421/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-regression-survival","name":"Regression & Survival Analysis Basics","sec":"prev","present":["Multivariable analysis models an outcome as a function of several predictors, adjusting for confounders and quantifying independent effects","The model type follows the OUTCOME: LINEAR regression (continuous outcome), LOGISTIC regression (binary outcome -> odds ratios), and COX proportional-hazards / survival analysis (time-to-event -> hazard ratios)","Board framing: match the outcome type to the regression model and interpret survival curves"],"dx":["LINEAR regression: continuous outcome, coefficients = change in outcome per unit predictor; LOGISTIC regression: binary outcome, exponentiated coefficients = ODDS RATIOS (used widely in case-control/adjusted analyses)","SURVIVAL analysis: time-to-event data handling CENSORING (incomplete follow-up); KAPLAN-MEIER curves estimate survival over time and the LOG-RANK test compares groups; COX regression yields adjusted HAZARD RATIOS (assumes proportional hazards)","Model choice also depends on count outcomes (Poisson) and repeated measures; assess assumptions and fit"],"tx":["Choose the model by outcome type: continuous -> linear; binary -> logistic (OR); time-to-event with censoring -> Kaplan-Meier/Cox (HR)","Use multivariable models to adjust for confounders and estimate INDEPENDENT associations; check key assumptions (linearity, proportional hazards) and avoid overfitting","Interpret hazard ratios as relative rates over the whole follow-up, and read Kaplan-Meier curves with numbers-at-risk"],"pearls":["Logistic regression gives odds ratios (binary outcome); Cox regression gives hazard ratios (time-to-event); linear regression gives mean differences (continuous outcome)","Survival analysis exists to handle CENSORING - patients with incomplete follow-up still contribute person-time","The Kaplan-Meier curve plus log-rank test compares survival between groups; Cox regression adjusts for covariates","The hazard ratio is a relative rate over time, not a simple risk ratio at one point"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Survival Analysis / Regression","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430841/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-systematic-reviews-grade","name":"Systematic Reviews, Meta-Analysis & Evidence Grading","sec":"prev","present":["A SYSTEMATIC REVIEW comprehensively identifies, appraises, and synthesizes all relevant studies on a question using explicit methods (PRISMA reporting); a META-ANALYSIS statistically pools their results","These sit atop the evidence hierarchy and underpin guidelines; the resident must appraise their quality and interpret pooled estimates","Board framing: interpret forest plots, heterogeneity, publication bias, and evidence grading (GRADE)"],"dx":["Meta-analysis produces a POOLED effect estimate (forest plot - individual studies with weights, and a diamond for the summary); assess HETEROGENEITY (I-squared / Cochran Q) - high heterogeneity means studies differ and pooling may be inappropriate (random-effects model)","Detect PUBLICATION BIAS (positive studies preferentially published) via FUNNEL PLOT asymmetry / Egger test - a major threat that inflates apparent effect","Appraise quality with tools (risk-of-bias assessment) and grade the certainty of evidence"],"tx":["Grade the CERTAINTY of evidence with GRADE (high/moderate/low/very low): start high for RCTs, downgrade for risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision, and publication bias; upgrade observational evidence for large effects/dose-response","Use fixed-effect vs random-effects models appropriately (random-effects when heterogeneity is present)","Base guideline strength on both the certainty of evidence AND the balance of benefits/harms/values/costs"],"pearls":["High heterogeneity (large I-squared) warns that studies are too different to pool cleanly - use random-effects and interpret cautiously","Funnel-plot asymmetry suggests publication bias - missing small negative studies inflate the pooled effect","GRADE rates certainty from high to very low: RCTs start high and get downgraded; observational studies start low and can be upgraded","A systematic review can be high quality even without a meta-analysis - pooling is not always appropriate"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430814/"},{"t":"GRADE Working Group / Cochrane","u":"https://www.cochrane.org"}]},{"id":"prev-causal-inference","name":"Causal Inference (Bradford Hill Criteria)","sec":"prev","present":["Association is not causation; causal inference is the reasoning that moves from an observed statistical association to a judgment of cause and effect","The BRADFORD HILL criteria are the classic framework for weighing evidence of causation from observational data (they are considerations, not a checklist/proof)","Board framing: apply the Hill criteria and recognize that temporality is the one essential requirement"],"dx":["Before invoking causation, exclude the alternatives to a real association: CHANCE (statistics), BIAS, and CONFOUNDING - a valid association must survive these","Assess Hill's considerations: TEMPORALITY (cause precedes effect - the ONLY one required), STRENGTH of association, DOSE-RESPONSE (biological gradient), CONSISTENCY across studies, SPECIFICITY, BIOLOGICAL PLAUSIBILITY, COHERENCE, EXPERIMENTAL evidence, and ANALOGY","Weigh the totality - no single criterion (except temporality) is necessary or sufficient"],"tx":["Establish TEMPORALITY first (exposure must precede outcome) - designs that ensure this (cohort, RCT) support causal claims more than cross-sectional data","Strengthen causal inference with a dose-response gradient, consistency across populations/designs, and experimental (RCT) confirmation where feasible","Translate causal judgments into prevention - removing/reducing a causal exposure should reduce incidence (the ultimate test)"],"pearls":["Temporality is the only Bradford Hill criterion that is truly required - the cause must come before the effect","The Hill criteria are aids to judgment, not a rigid proof or checklist - weigh the whole body of evidence","Always exclude chance, bias, and confounding before concluding an association is causal","A dose-response (biological gradient) and experimental confirmation are among the strongest supports for causation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bradford Hill Criteria / Causality","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430743/"},{"t":"CDC: Principles of Epidemiology","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-screening-principles","name":"Screening Principles & Biases","sec":"prev","present":["Screening applies a test to asymptomatic people to detect disease (or risk) early; it is only worthwhile under specific conditions and can cause net harm if applied carelessly","The WILSON-JUNGNER criteria define when screening is appropriate (the disease, test, treatment, and program must all qualify)","Board framing: apply the criteria and recognize the biases that make screening look better than it is"],"dx":["WILSON-JUNGNER criteria: the CONDITION is an important health problem with a recognizable latent/early stage and understood natural history; a suitable, ACCEPTABLE TEST exists; an accepted, more-effective-when-EARLY TREATMENT exists; facilities and an agreed policy on whom to treat exist; and the program is cost-effective and continuous","Screening BIASES that falsely inflate benefit: LEAD-TIME bias (earlier diagnosis lengthens apparent survival without changing death date), LENGTH bias (screening preferentially catches slow, indolent disease), and OVERDIAGNOSIS (detecting disease that never would have caused harm -> overtreatment)","The best evidence for screening benefit is reduced DISEASE-SPECIFIC MORTALITY in an RCT, not improved survival time"],"tx":["Only screen when Wilson-Jungner criteria are met and benefits outweigh harms (false positives, overdiagnosis/overtreatment, anxiety, cost) - defer to evidence-based bodies (USPSTF)","Judge screening programs by DISEASE-SPECIFIC MORTALITY reduction from RCTs, not by 5-year survival (which is distorted by lead-time and length bias)","Counsel patients on the harms as well as benefits (shared decision-making), especially where overdiagnosis is significant"],"pearls":["Wilson-Jungner: the disease must have a detectable early stage AND early treatment must improve outcomes - screening a disease you cannot treat earlier is useless","Lead-time bias makes survival look longer just by diagnosing earlier; length bias catches slow-growing disease; overdiagnosis finds disease that never would have mattered","Improved 5-year survival does NOT prove screening works - only reduced disease-specific mortality (ideally from an RCT) does","Screening low-prevalence populations produces many false positives - harms can outweigh benefits"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cancer Screening / Screening Principles","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556095/"},{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"}]},{"id":"prev-outbreak-investigation","name":"Outbreak Investigation & Epidemic Curves","sec":"prev","present":["An outbreak/epidemic is disease occurrence CLEARLY IN EXCESS of the expected baseline for a population/place/time; the field investigation is a core public-health function","Recognition triggers a systematic investigation to identify the source, mode of transmission, and population at risk and to STOP further cases","Board framing: know the ordered steps and interpret the EPIDEMIC CURVE"],"dx":["The classic STEPS (roughly ordered): confirm the diagnosis and the existence of an outbreak, establish a CASE DEFINITION, find cases and construct a line list, perform descriptive epidemiology (person/place/time - draw the EPIDEMIC CURVE), generate hypotheses, test them with analytic studies (retrospective COHORT for a defined group like a banquet; CASE-CONTROL for a dispersed population), and implement control measures","EPIDEMIC CURVE shapes: POINT-SOURCE (single sharp peak within one incubation period - common-source exposure), CONTINUOUS common-source (plateau), and PROPAGATED (successive peaks ~one incubation period apart - person-to-person spread)","Use the incubation period and curve to estimate the exposure time and likely agent"],"tx":["Implement CONTROL measures as soon as reasonable (do not wait for full analysis) - remove the source, interrupt transmission, and protect those at risk (e.g., recall food, isolate cases, prophylaxis)","Test hypotheses with the appropriate analytic design (cohort attack-rate comparison for a defined cohort; case-control for the general population) to identify the vehicle/source","Communicate findings, notify authorities, and evaluate/maintain control; write up for prevention"],"pearls":["A point-source outbreak shows a single sharp peak within one incubation period; a propagated outbreak shows successive peaks about one incubation period apart (person-to-person)","Establish a clear case definition EARLY - it standardizes case-finding and the line list","For a defined group (e.g., a wedding), use a retrospective cohort and compare attack rates by food; for a dispersed population, use case-control","Implement control measures as soon as the source is suspected - stopping the outbreak trumps waiting for perfect data"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Outbreak Investigation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459129/"},{"t":"CDC: Steps of an Outbreak Investigation","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-surveillance","name":"Public Health Surveillance & Notifiable Conditions","sec":"prev","present":["SURVEILLANCE is the ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, interpretation, and DISSEMINATION of health data for public-health action ('information for action')","It detects outbreaks, tracks disease trends, guides and evaluates interventions, and informs policy; NOTIFIABLE (reportable) diseases are legally mandated reports to health departments","Board framing: distinguish passive vs active and sentinel/syndromic surveillance and understand reporting"],"dx":["PASSIVE surveillance: providers/labs report as required (inexpensive, but UNDER-reporting is common); ACTIVE surveillance: health department proactively solicits reports (more complete, resource-intensive)","SENTINEL surveillance (selected reporting sites for trends, e.g., influenza) and SYNDROMIC surveillance (monitors pre-diagnostic indicators - symptoms, ED visits, OTC sales - for early outbreak detection)","NOTIFIABLE conditions are reported by clinicians/labs to local/state health departments, which forward nationally (NNDSS); reporting requirements vary by state"],"tx":["Choose the surveillance approach to fit the goal (routine trend monitoring vs early outbreak detection vs complete case ascertainment during an emergency)","Ensure the loop closes - DISSEMINATE data back to those who need it and translate surveillance into ACTION (control measures, resource allocation, policy)","Report notifiable conditions promptly per legal requirements; protect data confidentiality"],"pearls":["Surveillance is 'information for ACTION' - data collection without dissemination and response is incomplete","Passive surveillance is cheap but under-reports; active surveillance is more complete but costly","Syndromic surveillance uses pre-diagnostic signals (symptoms, ED visits) for EARLY outbreak detection, before lab confirmation","Reporting notifiable diseases to the health department is a legal obligation of clinicians and labs"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Public Health Surveillance","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441795/"},{"t":"CDC: National Notifiable Diseases Surveillance System","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-uspstf-framework","name":"USPSTF Framework & Recommendation Grades","sec":"prev","present":["The US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) is an independent, volunteer panel that makes EVIDENCE-BASED recommendations on clinical preventive services (screening, counseling, preventive medications) for ASYMPTOMATIC people","Its grades weigh the NET BENEFIT (benefit minus harm) and drive practice and coverage; under the ACA, grade A and B services must be covered without cost-sharing","Board framing: know the letter grades and what each means for practice"],"dx":["GRADES: A (high certainty of substantial net benefit - offer/provide), B (high certainty of moderate, or moderate certainty of moderate-to-substantial, net benefit - offer/provide), C (small net benefit - offer SELECTIVELY based on individual circumstances/judgment), D (no net benefit or harms outweigh - DISCOURAGE/do not provide), and I (INSUFFICIENT evidence to assess balance)","The grade reflects both the CERTAINTY (quality of evidence) and the MAGNITUDE of net benefit","USPSTF addresses only prevention in asymptomatic patients - not diagnostic testing in symptomatic patients or treatment of established disease"],"tx":["OFFER grade A and B services routinely (and they are covered without cost-sharing under the ACA); offer grade C selectively with shared decision-making","DO NOT routinely provide grade D services (discourage - harms outweigh benefits); for grade I, use clinical judgment and inform the patient about the uncertainty","Distinguish USPSTF (prevention in asymptomatic patients) from other guideline bodies (e.g., specialty societies, ACIP for vaccines)"],"pearls":["Grade A/B = provide (and ACA-covered without cost-sharing); C = offer selectively; D = don't do it (harms outweigh); I = insufficient evidence","USPSTF grades reflect NET BENEFIT (benefit minus harm) and the certainty of evidence, not benefit alone","USPSTF covers preventive services in ASYMPTOMATIC people - it does not address workup of symptomatic patients","A 'C' is not 'don't do it' - it means offer selectively based on the individual patient"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Grade Definitions","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Preventive Health Services","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560640/"}]},{"id":"prev-cvd-risk-aspirin-statin","name":"Cardiovascular Risk Assessment & Aspirin/Statin Primary Prevention","sec":"prev","present":["Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) is a leading cause of death and a central target of primary prevention; risk is estimated to guide preventive therapy in asymptomatic adults","Global RISK ESTIMATION integrates age, sex, blood pressure, lipids, diabetes, kidney function, and smoking to drive shared decisions - the AHA PREVENT equations (2023; race-agnostic, ages 30-79, add kidney/cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic factors) are now recommended by 2025 ACC/AHA guidance in place of the older race-based pooled cohort equations","Board framing: know current statin and aspirin primary-prevention thresholds and the shift away from routine aspirin"],"dx":["Estimate 10-YEAR ASCVD RISK in adults ~40-75 years using a validated calculator; assess and treat the modifiable risk factors (hypertension, dyslipidemia, diabetes, smoking, obesity/inactivity)","Consider risk-enhancing factors and, in intermediate-risk/uncertain cases, a coronary artery CALCIUM score to refine the decision","Separate PRIMARY prevention (no established ASCVD) from secondary prevention (established disease - different, more aggressive thresholds)"],"tx":["STATINS for primary prevention (per current guidance): patients with LDL >=190, diabetes aged 40-75, or intermediate/high 10-year ASCVD risk after a risk discussion; statins are the cornerstone lipid therapy","ASPIRIN primary prevention has NARROWED sharply - the USPSTF recommends INDIVIDUALIZED low-dose aspirin only for select adults ~40-59 with higher ASCVD risk and low bleeding risk (grade C), and recommends AGAINST initiating in adults >=60 (bleeding harm) - it is no longer a routine primary-prevention measure","Address all risk factors and lifestyle (diet, activity, smoking cessation, blood pressure, glucose)"],"pearls":["Routine aspirin for primary prevention is out - the USPSTF advises against starting it at age 60+ and only individualized use at 40-59 with high ASCVD/low bleeding risk","Statins are the primary-prevention cornerstone - indicated for LDL >=190, diabetes at 40-75, or elevated 10-year ASCVD risk","Use a validated 10-year ASCVD risk estimate (not single risk factors) to drive shared decisions; a coronary calcium score can refine intermediate risk","Secondary prevention (established ASCVD) uses far more aggressive statin/antiplatelet thresholds than primary prevention"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Aspirin & Statin Use","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576476/"}]},{"id":"prev-hypertension-screening","name":"Hypertension Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Hypertension is a highly prevalent, largely ASYMPTOMATIC ('silent') major risk factor for stroke, MI, heart failure, and kidney disease - an ideal screening target","The USPSTF recommends SCREENING for high blood pressure in adults (grade A), with confirmation before diagnosis; screening detects a treatable, high-impact condition","Board framing: know the screening interval and the requirement for out-of-office confirmation"],"dx":["Screen adults >=18 with office blood-pressure measurement; USPSTF recommends screening ADULTS (grade A) and CONFIRMING with measurements OUTSIDE the clinical setting (home or ambulatory monitoring) before diagnosis to avoid white-coat hypertension","Interval: adults >=40 (or those at increased risk - higher-normal BP, overweight, Black adults) annually; lower-risk adults 18-39 with normal BP every 3-5 years","Use proper technique (correct cuff size, seated/rested, averaged readings) and out-of-office confirmation"],"tx":["Confirm diagnosis with out-of-office (ambulatory/home) readings, then stage and manage per guidelines: lifestyle for all (DASH diet, sodium reduction, weight loss, activity, alcohol moderation) and pharmacotherapy by stage/risk","Screening itself is the preventive service; treatment thresholds/targets follow current hypertension guidelines and cardiovascular risk","Re-screen at the recommended interval and address coexisting ASCVD risk factors"],"pearls":["USPSTF gives hypertension screening in adults a grade A - it is a high-yield, evidence-based preventive service","Confirm the diagnosis with OUT-OF-OFFICE (home/ambulatory) monitoring to exclude white-coat hypertension before labeling and treating","Screen adults >=40 and higher-risk adults annually; lower-risk younger adults every 3-5 years","Hypertension is silent - screening, not symptoms, is how it is found"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Hypertension Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hypertension Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539859/"}]},{"id":"prev-dyslipidemia-screening","name":"Dyslipidemia Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Abnormal lipids (elevated LDL/non-HDL) are a modifiable driver of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease; screening identifies candidates for risk-based statin therapy","Screening is asymptomatic and feeds directly into the global ASCVD-risk/statin decision (see the CVD-risk entry)","Board framing: know who to screen and that screening informs statin primary prevention"],"dx":["Screen with a lipid panel (total, LDL, HDL, triglycerides; non-fasting is acceptable for many); the USPSTF frames lipid screening within STATIN primary prevention for adults ~40-75 as part of ASCVD risk assessment","Screen earlier/at any age with major risk factors or a family history suggesting FAMILIAL HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA (very high LDL, premature ASCVD, tendon xanthomas) - consider FH in markedly elevated LDL","Repeat screening periodically (e.g., every ~5 years, sooner if risk factors change)"],"tx":["Use results to estimate 10-year ASCVD risk and apply statin thresholds (LDL >=190, diabetes 40-75, or elevated calculated risk); lifestyle therapy (diet, activity, weight, smoking) for all","Suspected familial hypercholesterolemia -> more intensive management and CASCADE screening of relatives","Address secondary causes of dyslipidemia (hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, diabetes, medications)"],"pearls":["Lipid screening is a gateway to statin primary prevention - the number matters because it drives the ASCVD-risk-based statin decision","Suspect familial hypercholesterolemia with very high LDL, premature coronary disease, or tendon xanthomas - and screen the relatives (cascade)","Non-fasting lipid panels are acceptable for screening in most patients","Always look for secondary causes (hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, diabetes) of an abnormal lipid panel"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Statin Use / Lipid Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hyperlipidemia Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559182/"}]},{"id":"prev-diabetes-screening-dpp","name":"Type 2 Diabetes/Prediabetes Screening & Prevention","sec":"prev","present":["Type 2 diabetes and prediabetes are common, largely asymptomatic, and drivers of cardiovascular, renal, and microvascular disease - a high-value screening and PREVENTION target","Screening detects prediabetes, where an evidence-based lifestyle program can PREVENT or delay progression to diabetes","Board framing: know the USPSTF screening population and the Diabetes Prevention Program evidence"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening for prediabetes/type 2 diabetes in adults ~35-70 who are OVERWEIGHT or OBESE (grade B); screen earlier/others with additional risk factors (family history, high-risk race/ethnicity, gestational diabetes, PCOS)","Screening tests: fasting glucose, HbA1c, or oral glucose tolerance test; diagnostic thresholds define prediabetes (e.g., A1c 5.7-6.4%) and diabetes (A1c >=6.5%, fasting glucose >=126, etc.)","Rescreen at intervals (e.g., every ~3 years if normal)"],"tx":["PREDIABETES: refer to an intensive LIFESTYLE program - the DIABETES PREVENTION PROGRAM (DPP) showed ~7% weight loss and 150 min/week activity reduced progression to diabetes MORE than metformin; consider METFORMIN for higher-risk prediabetes (e.g., younger, higher BMI, prior gestational diabetes)","DIABETES: comprehensive management (glycemic, blood pressure, lipids, and cardiovascular/renal risk) per guidelines","Reinforce lifestyle change as the foundation of prevention and management"],"pearls":["The USPSTF screens adults 35-70 who are overweight/obese for diabetes/prediabetes (grade B) - and prediabetes is the actionable finding","The Diabetes Prevention Program (intensive lifestyle: ~7% weight loss + 150 min/week activity) beat metformin at preventing progression to diabetes","Metformin is an option for higher-risk prediabetes, but lifestyle change is first-line and more effective overall","A1c 5.7-6.4% is prediabetes; A1c >=6.5% is diabetes - screening catches the treatable pre-disease stage"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Prediabetes and Type 2 Diabetes Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"CDC: National Diabetes Prevention Program","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-obesity-screening","name":"Obesity Screening & Intensive Behavioral Intervention","sec":"prev","present":["Obesity is a prevalent, modifiable driver of diabetes, cardiovascular disease, cancer, and mortality; screening and effective behavioral intervention are core preventive services","BODY MASS INDEX (BMI) is the standard screening measure (with waist circumference as an adjunct); obesity defined as BMI >=30, overweight 25-29.9 in adults","Board framing: know the USPSTF recommendation for intensive behavioral intervention and pediatric screening"],"dx":["Screen all adults with BMI (grade B) and children/adolescents >=6 years with BMI percentiles (grade B); identify overweight/obesity and assess comorbidities and readiness to change","BMI has limitations (does not distinguish muscle/fat, varies by population) - use with clinical judgment and adjuncts (waist circumference)","Evaluate for and manage obesity-related conditions (diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, OSA)"],"tx":["Adults with obesity (BMI >=30): offer/refer to INTENSIVE, MULTICOMPONENT BEHAVIORAL interventions (diet, physical activity, behavior-change support - ideally >=12 sessions/year) - the evidence-based first-line (grade B)","Children/adolescents with obesity: refer to intensive (>=26 contact hours) comprehensive behavioral interventions","Pharmacotherapy and bariatric surgery are adjuncts for appropriate patients beyond behavioral therapy; treat comorbidities"],"pearls":["USPSTF recommends screening all adults with BMI and offering/referring those with obesity to intensive multicomponent behavioral interventions (grade B)","Intensive means high-contact (roughly >=12 sessions/year in adults; >=26 contact hours in children) - brief advice alone is insufficient","BMI is the screening standard but imperfect (muscle vs fat) - pair with waist circumference and clinical judgment","Behavioral intervention is first-line; medications and surgery are adjuncts for selected patients"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Obesity in Adults / Children","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Obesity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459357/"}]},{"id":"prev-breast-cancer-screening","name":"Breast Cancer Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Breast cancer is the most common non-skin cancer in women and a leading cancer death; mammographic screening reduces breast-cancer mortality","Screening is with MAMMOGRAPHY; the balance of benefit (mortality reduction) and harm (false positives, biopsies, overdiagnosis) drives age/interval recommendations","Board framing: know the current USPSTF age/interval and the role of risk-based/supplemental screening"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends biennial screening MAMMOGRAPHY for women aged 40-74 (grade B) - the starting age was lowered to 40 in the updated recommendation; screen every 2 years","Higher-risk women (BRCA/strong family history, prior chest radiation) warrant earlier/more intensive screening (often with MRI) and genetics referral - assess family history for BRCA risk (USPSTF recommends risk assessment/genetic counseling for those with concerning family history)","Harms include false positives, additional imaging/biopsies, and OVERDIAGNOSIS (detecting cancers that would never have caused harm)"],"tx":["Offer biennial mammography 40-74; individualize decisions at the margins with shared decision-making about benefits and harms","Refer high-risk women for enhanced screening (MRI) and BRCA genetic counseling/testing per risk-assessment tools; consider chemoprevention (tamoxifen/raloxifene) in high-risk women","Counsel on the harms (false positives, overdiagnosis) as part of informed screening"],"pearls":["USPSTF now recommends starting biennial mammography at age 40 (through 74) - a change from the prior age-50 start","Overdiagnosis (finding cancers that would never harm) is the key harm to weigh alongside the mortality benefit","Strong family history or known BRCA mutations shift to earlier, more intensive screening (add MRI) and genetics referral","Screening reduces breast-cancer mortality - the benefit is real, but so are false positives and overdiagnosis"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Breast Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Breast Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556050/"}]},{"id":"prev-cervical-cancer-screening","name":"Cervical Cancer Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Cervical cancer is caused by persistent high-risk HPV infection and is highly preventable through screening and HPV vaccination; screening detects precancer (CIN) for treatment before invasion","Screening uses cytology (Pap) and/or high-risk HPV testing; the strategy and interval depend on age","Board framing: know the age-based screening options/intervals and that screening pairs with HPV vaccination"],"dx":["USPSTF: begin screening at age 21; ages 21-29 - cytology every 3 years; ages 30-65 - cytology every 3 years, OR high-risk HPV testing every 5 years, OR co-testing (cytology + HPV) every 5 years (primary HPV testing is increasingly preferred)","Do NOT screen before age 21 or after 65 (if adequate prior negative screening and not high-risk); screening is not indicated after hysterectomy with cervix removal for benign disease","Abnormal results are managed by risk-based (ASCCP) algorithms with colposcopy/biopsy"],"tx":["Screen per the age-appropriate interval; manage abnormal results with risk-based referral to colposcopy and treat high-grade precancer (CIN 2/3) to prevent cancer","Reinforce HPV VACCINATION as primary prevention (it reduces high-grade disease and is reshaping incidence)","Do not over-screen (annual Pap is outdated) or screen outside the recommended age window"],"pearls":["Start at 21 regardless of sexual history; 21-29 is cytology every 3 years; 30-65 adds HPV testing options every 5 years - and stop at 65 with adequate prior negatives","Primary high-risk HPV testing (every 5 years) is increasingly the preferred strategy for ages 30-65","Screening plus HPV vaccination makes cervical cancer one of the most preventable cancers","Do not screen before 21 or annually - both are over-screening that causes harm"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Cervical Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556070/"}]},{"id":"prev-colorectal-cancer-screening","name":"Colorectal Cancer Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Colorectal cancer (CRC) is a leading cancer death that is highly preventable - screening detects and removes ADENOMATOUS POLYPS (interrupting the adenoma-carcinoma sequence) and finds early cancer","Multiple effective strategies exist (stool-based and direct-visualization tests) with different intervals and trade-offs; the best test is the one that gets done","Board framing: know the starting age (now 45) and the menu of tests/intervals"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening adults 45-75 (grade A for 50-75, grade B for 45-49 - the start age was LOWERED to 45); screening 76-85 is individualized (grade C)","OPTIONS: colonoscopy every 10 years (also therapeutic - removes polyps); annual FIT or high-sensitivity gFOBT; FIT-DNA (multitarget stool DNA) every 1-3 years; CT colonography or flexible sigmoidoscopy at defined intervals","A POSITIVE stool-based or radiologic test requires a follow-up COLONOSCOPY to complete screening; higher-risk patients (family history, IBD, hereditary syndromes) need earlier/more intensive screening"],"tx":["Offer any of the recommended strategies with shared decision-making; colonoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic (polypectomy prevents cancer), while stool-based tests are noninvasive but need colonoscopy if positive","Start at 45 for average-risk adults; individualize 76-85; screen high-risk groups (Lynch/FAP, IBD, strong family history) earlier and more often","Ensure abnormal noncolonoscopic tests get a timely colonoscopy - an unfollowed positive FIT is a failure of screening"],"pearls":["The colorectal cancer screening start age is now 45 (lowered from 50) for average-risk adults","Screening works by removing adenomatous polyps (adenoma-carcinoma sequence), not just finding cancer - colonoscopy is preventive and therapeutic","A positive stool-based test (FIT/FIT-DNA) or CT colonography must be followed by colonoscopy to complete screening","The best CRC test is the one the patient will actually do - offer the menu and match to preference"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Colorectal Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Colorectal Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570223/"}]},{"id":"prev-lung-cancer-screening","name":"Lung Cancer Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death; screening high-risk smokers with LOW-DOSE CT (LDCT) reduces lung-cancer mortality (National Lung Screening Trial, NELSON)","Screening targets a defined high-risk group by age and smoking (pack-year) history; benefit depends on selecting true high-risk patients","Board framing: know the eligibility criteria and that screening is coupled with smoking cessation"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends ANNUAL LDCT for adults aged 50-80 with a >=20 PACK-YEAR smoking history who currently smoke OR quit within the past 15 years (grade B) - eligibility broadened (age lowered to 50, pack-years to 20)","STOP screening when the person has not smoked for 15 years, develops a health problem that substantially limits life expectancy or the ability/willingness to have curative lung surgery","Manage findings with a structured nodule-reporting system (Lung-RADS); harms include false positives, incidental findings, radiation, and overdiagnosis"],"tx":["Screen eligible high-risk patients ANNUALLY with LDCT, integrate SMOKING CESSATION (the most important intervention for current smokers), and use Lung-RADS to guide follow-up of nodules","Ensure shared decision-making about benefits/harms and a system for follow-up and cessation support","Do not screen those outside eligibility or who could not/would not tolerate curative treatment"],"pearls":["Eligibility: age 50-80, >=20 pack-years, and currently smoking or quit within 15 years - annual low-dose CT","Screening does not replace cessation - quitting smoking is the highest-impact intervention, and screening is a teachable moment","Stop screening 15 years after quitting or when life expectancy/surgical candidacy no longer supports it","Lung-RADS standardizes nodule follow-up and limits unnecessary procedures from false positives"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Lung Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551659/"}]},{"id":"prev-prostate-cancer-screening","name":"Prostate Cancer Screening (Shared Decision-Making)","sec":"prev","present":["Prostate cancer is common and often INDOLENT; PSA-based screening can reduce prostate-cancer mortality modestly but causes substantial harm (overdiagnosis and overtreatment of cancers that would never have caused symptoms)","Because the benefit-harm balance is close and value-dependent, screening is a SHARED DECISION, not a routine test","Board framing: know the age-based USPSTF grades and the central role of shared decision-making"],"dx":["USPSTF: for men aged 55-69, the decision to screen with PSA should be an INDIVIDUAL one after a discussion of benefits and harms (grade C - offer selectively with shared decision-making)","For men aged 70 and older, USPSTF recommends AGAINST PSA-based screening (grade D - harms outweigh benefits)","Higher-risk men (Black men, strong family history) have higher risk and may weigh screening differently - individualize"],"tx":["ENGAGE in shared decision-making for men 55-69: discuss the small potential mortality benefit vs harms (false positives, biopsy complications, overdiagnosis, and overtreatment side effects like incontinence/erectile dysfunction)","Do NOT routinely screen men >=70; do not screen without a discussion","Where screening is chosen, use strategies to reduce overtreatment (active surveillance for low-risk disease)"],"pearls":["Men 55-69: PSA screening is a grade C shared decision - offer it, but only after discussing benefits and harms","Men 70+: USPSTF recommends AGAINST PSA screening (grade D)","The dominant harm is overdiagnosis/overtreatment of indolent cancer (incontinence, erectile dysfunction) - active surveillance mitigates this","Black men and those with strong family history are higher-risk and may value screening differently - individualize the discussion"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Prostate Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Cancer Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556081/"}]},{"id":"prev-osteoporosis-screening","name":"Osteoporosis Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Osteoporosis is a silent disease of low bone density that presents with FRAGILITY FRACTURES (hip, vertebral, wrist) causing major morbidity/mortality - screening identifies treatable low bone density before fracture","Bone density is measured by DUAL-ENERGY X-RAY ABSORPTIOMETRY (DXA); fracture risk is estimated with tools (FRAX)","Board framing: know who to screen and the T-score threshold"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening with bone-density testing (DXA) in WOMEN >=65 (grade B), and in POSTMENOPAUSAL women YOUNGER than 65 who are at increased fracture risk (by a formal risk assessment such as FRAX) (grade B); evidence is INSUFFICIENT for men (grade I)","DXA reports a T-SCORE: osteoporosis is T-score <= -2.5; osteopenia is -1.0 to -2.5; a fragility fracture also defines osteoporosis clinically","Use FRAX to estimate 10-year fracture probability and guide treatment in osteopenia"],"tx":["Screen women >=65 (and younger postmenopausal women at increased risk); treat osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5 or fragility fracture, or high-FRAX osteopenia) with bisphosphonates or other agents plus calcium/vitamin D and fall prevention","Address modifiable risks (smoking, alcohol, inactivity, low calcium/vitamin D) and secondary causes","Combine pharmacotherapy with FALL PREVENTION - most osteoporotic fractures result from falls"],"pearls":["Screen all women at age 65, and younger postmenopausal women with elevated fracture risk (FRAX) - evidence is insufficient to recommend routine screening in men","A T-score <= -2.5 defines osteoporosis; -1.0 to -2.5 is osteopenia; a fragility fracture is osteoporosis regardless of T-score","Use FRAX to decide treatment in the osteopenic range","Preventing fractures means treating bone AND preventing falls - the two go together"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Osteoporosis Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Osteoporosis Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441901/"}]},{"id":"prev-aaa-screening","name":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm Screening","sec":"prev","present":["An abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is a silent dilation of the abdominal aorta that can RUPTURE catastrophically (very high mortality); one-time ultrasound screening in high-risk men reduces AAA-related mortality","Risk factors: older age, MALE sex, and above all SMOKING (the strongest modifiable risk); family history also raises risk","Board framing: know the specific one-time-ultrasound screening population"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends a ONE-TIME screening ULTRASOUND for AAA in MEN aged 65-75 who have EVER SMOKED (grade B); for men 65-75 who never smoked, screen SELECTIVELY (grade C)","In WOMEN who never smoked and have no family history, USPSTF recommends AGAINST screening (grade D); evidence is insufficient (grade I) for women 65-75 who ever smoked or have a family history","Abdominal ultrasound is the accurate, noninvasive screening test; an aneurysm is generally an aortic diameter >=3 cm"],"tx":["Screen eligible men (65-75, ever-smokers) once with ultrasound; manage detected AAAs by SIZE - surveillance for small aneurysms and surgical/endovascular REPAIR at threshold size (~5.5 cm in men) or with rapid growth/symptoms","Reinforce SMOKING CESSATION and cardiovascular risk-factor control (smoking drives both AAA and growth/rupture)","Do not screen groups where harms outweigh benefits (e.g., never-smoking women)"],"pearls":["One-time ultrasound for AAA in men 65-75 who ever smoked (grade B) - smoking is the key risk factor","Screening is a ONE-TIME ultrasound, not repeated, in the eligible population","USPSTF recommends AGAINST screening never-smoking women with no family history (grade D)","Manage found aneurysms by size - repair around 5.5 cm in men, or for rapid growth/symptoms; keep counseling smoking cessation"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431075/"}]},{"id":"prev-depression-suicide-screening","name":"Depression, Anxiety & Suicide-Risk Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Mental health conditions are common, disabling, and frequently under-recognized; screening in primary care identifies treatable depression and anxiety and prompts appropriate support","Screening is only beneficial when coupled with systems to ensure accurate DIAGNOSIS, TREATMENT, and FOLLOW-UP - screening without a care pathway does not help","Board framing: know the USPSTF screening populations and that screening must link to care"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening for DEPRESSION in adults including pregnant/postpartum adults (grade B) and in adolescents 12-18 (grade B); screening for ANXIETY in adults including pregnant/postpartum (grade B) and in children/adolescents 8-18 (grade B)","Use validated tools (e.g., PHQ-2/PHQ-9 for depression, GAD-7 for anxiety; EPDS in perinatal patients) with adequate systems for diagnosis and follow-up","A positive screen requires clinical assessment of severity, safety, and comorbidity before treatment"],"tx":["Ensure screening is embedded in a system that provides accurate diagnosis, evidence-based TREATMENT (psychotherapy and/or pharmacotherapy), and FOLLOW-UP; screen only when this is in place","Assess safety in anyone with depression; connect patients experiencing distress to appropriate mental-health support and resources","For those in crisis, provide immediate access to help and support resources rather than delaying"],"pearls":["USPSTF recommends screening adults (including perinatal) and adolescents for depression, and adults and youth for anxiety - but only with systems to ensure diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up","Screening without a linked care pathway does not improve outcomes - the follow-through is the intervention","PHQ-2/PHQ-9 and GAD-7 are the common validated primary-care tools; the perinatal setting often uses the EPDS","A positive screen is a starting point for clinical assessment (severity, safety, comorbidity), not a diagnosis by itself. Mental health is a sensitive area - anyone in distress should be connected with appropriate professional support."],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Depression & Anxiety Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Depression Screening","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430847/"}]},{"id":"prev-sbirt-substance-use","name":"Unhealthy Substance Use Screening (SBIRT)","sec":"prev","present":["Unhealthy alcohol, tobacco, and drug use are leading preventable causes of morbidity/mortality; screening in primary care identifies risky use before dependence and enables early intervention","SBIRT (Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment) is the evidence-based framework for addressing unhealthy substance use in clinical settings","Board framing: know the USPSTF screening recommendations and the SBIRT structure"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening adults >=18 for UNHEALTHY ALCOHOL use (grade B) and for TOBACCO use (grade A); screening adults for illicit DRUG use (grade B) when accurate diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up can be offered","Validated tools: alcohol (AUDIT-C, single-item screen), drugs (single-item/brief screens); adolescents are screened with developmentally appropriate tools","A positive screen leads to assessment of severity and readiness to change"],"tx":["SBIRT: SCREEN, deliver a BRIEF INTERVENTION (nonjudgmental feedback and motivational counseling for risky use), and REFER TO TREATMENT for those with more severe use/substance use disorder","Brief interventions are effective for risky alcohol use; substance use disorders warrant evidence-based treatment (including medications for alcohol, opioid, and tobacco use disorders)","Pair screening with cessation/treatment resources and follow-up"],"pearls":["SBIRT = Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment - the framework for unhealthy substance use in clinical settings","USPSTF: screen adults for unhealthy alcohol (B) and tobacco (A) use; screen for drug use (B) when treatment/follow-up can be provided","Brief interventions have the best evidence for RISKY (not yet dependent) alcohol use; disorders need formal treatment","Screening must connect to intervention and treatment resources to be worthwhile"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Unhealthy Alcohol & Drug Use Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"SAMHSA: SBIRT","u":"https://www.samhsa.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-sti-hiv-screening","name":"STI & HIV Screening","sec":"prev","present":["Sexually transmitted infections (including HIV) are often ASYMPTOMATIC, so screening is essential to detect, treat, and interrupt transmission and prevent complications (PID/infertility, congenital infection, HIV progression)","Screening is risk- and age-based, with universal components (HIV) and targeted components (chlamydia/gonorrhea, syphilis)","Board framing: know the key USPSTF/CDC screening populations"],"dx":["HIV: USPSTF recommends screening everyone aged 15-65 at least once (grade A), and all PREGNANT persons; screen higher-risk individuals more often","CHLAMYDIA and GONORRHEA: screen all sexually active WOMEN <=24, and older women at increased risk (grade B); syphilis screening in persons at increased risk (grade A) and ALL pregnant persons (grade A)","Screen based on risk (new/multiple partners, MSM, HIV/other STI, injection drug use); offer partner services and additional STI testing as indicated"],"tx":["Screen per age/risk, treat detected infections promptly (with partner treatment) per CDC STI guidelines, and RESCREEN as indicated (e.g., test-of-cure/reinfection)","Offer HIV prevention (PrEP) to those at substantial risk and link HIV-positive patients to care/antiretroviral therapy","Integrate universal prenatal STI/HIV screening to prevent congenital/perinatal infection"],"pearls":["Screen everyone 15-65 for HIV at least once, and all pregnant persons - a grade A universal recommendation","Screen all sexually active women <=24 for chlamydia and gonorrhea; screen at-risk persons and all pregnant persons for syphilis","Most STIs are asymptomatic - screening, not symptoms, drives detection and prevents PID/infertility and congenital infection","Pair screening with treatment, partner services, and HIV prevention (PrEP) for at-risk patients"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: HIV & STI Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"CDC: STI Screening Recommendations","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-childhood-immunization","name":"Childhood & Adolescent Immunization Schedule","sec":"prev","present":["Childhood immunization is among the most effective public-health interventions, preventing serious infectious diseases and their complications; the schedule is set annually by the ACIP/CDC (with AAP/AAFP)","Vaccines are timed to provide protection when children are most vulnerable and to achieve population (herd) immunity","Board framing: know the general schedule structure and adolescent additions (Tdap, HPV, meningococcal)"],"dx":["The routine schedule includes (by infancy/early childhood): hepatitis B (birth), and the primary series of DTaP, Hib, PCV (pneumococcal conjugate), IPV (polio), and rotavirus; MMR and varicella at ~12-15 months; hepatitis A; annual influenza from 6 months","ADOLESCENT platform (~11-12 years): Tdap booster, HPV vaccine series, and meningococcal conjugate (MenACWY) with a booster at 16, plus MenB for older adolescents by shared decision-making; COVID-19 per current recommendations","Assess and CATCH UP missed vaccines using the catch-up schedule; document doses and contraindications"],"tx":["Follow the current ACIP/CDC schedule, use every visit to catch up missed doses, and address vaccine hesitancy with clear, empathetic, evidence-based counseling","Give the adolescent platform on time (Tdap, HPV, MenACWY at 11-12) - HPV vaccination is cancer prevention and works best before exposure","Screen for true contraindications (see vaccine-principles entry) but do not miss opportunities over false contraindications (minor illness is not a contraindication)"],"pearls":["The adolescent 11-12 visit anchors Tdap, HPV, and meningococcal (MenACWY) vaccination - with a MenACWY booster at 16","HPV vaccination is cancer prevention and is most effective given before sexual debut/exposure","Use every encounter to catch up missed vaccines - minor acute illness is NOT a contraindication","The ACIP/CDC schedule is updated annually - always check the current version"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/ACIP: Child & Adolescent Immunization Schedule","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Immunizations","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519028/"}]},{"id":"prev-adult-immunization","name":"Adult & Older-Adult Immunization Schedule","sec":"prev","present":["Adults require ongoing vaccination for waning immunity, age-related risk, and exposure/comorbidity; the adult schedule is set annually by ACIP/CDC and is often under-utilized","Vaccination prevents influenza, pneumococcal disease, shingles, and other serious infections that disproportionately harm older adults and those with chronic disease","Board framing: know the key age- and condition-based adult vaccines"],"dx":["Routine adult vaccines: annual INFLUENZA (all adults); Td/Tdap (Tdap once then Td/Tdap booster every 10 years, and Tdap in EACH pregnancy); COVID-19 per current guidance","AGE-based for older adults: recombinant ZOSTER vaccine (shingles) at >=50; PNEUMOCOCCAL vaccination for adults >=65 (and younger adults with risk conditions); RSV vaccine for older adults per current recommendations","CONDITION/risk-based: hepatitis B (e.g., diabetes, chronic liver disease, at-risk adults), HPV through age 26 (and shared decision-making to 45), pneumococcal/other vaccines for immunocompromise, asplenia, chronic heart/lung/liver/kidney disease"],"tx":["Vaccinate per the current ACIP adult schedule, prioritizing influenza (annual), Tdap (including each pregnancy), zoster (>=50), pneumococcal and RSV (older adults), and risk-based vaccines","Review vaccination status at visits, use standing orders/reminders to close gaps, and counsel on benefit","Tailor to immunocompromise (avoid LIVE vaccines) and pregnancy (see vaccine-principles)"],"pearls":["Give Tdap in EVERY pregnancy (optimally 27-36 weeks) to protect the newborn from pertussis","Recombinant zoster vaccine is recommended at age >=50; pneumococcal and RSV vaccines are key for older adults","Annual influenza is for all adults; a Td/Tdap booster is due every 10 years","Match vaccines to age AND risk conditions (asplenia, immunocompromise, chronic disease) - and avoid live vaccines in significant immunocompromise"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/ACIP: Adult Immunization Schedule","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adult Vaccinations","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442019/"}]},{"id":"prev-vaccine-principles","name":"Vaccine Principles, Contraindications & Safety","sec":"prev","present":["Vaccines induce immunity via LIVE-ATTENUATED (replicating, robust/durable immunity - e.g., MMR, varicella, rotavirus, intranasal influenza, yellow fever) or INACTIVATED/subunit/toxoid/mRNA (non-replicating, often needing boosters) platforms","The live vs non-live distinction drives the key contraindications (immunocompromise, pregnancy); understanding it prevents both harm and missed opportunities","Board framing: know true contraindications vs false ones, and the vaccine-safety monitoring system"],"dx":["TRUE contraindications: severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) to a prior dose or component; LIVE vaccines are contraindicated in significant IMMUNOCOMPROMISE and PREGNANCY; specific precautions per vaccine","FALSE contraindications (do NOT withhold): mild acute illness with/without low-grade fever, current antibiotic use, mild-to-moderate local reactions, prematurity (vaccinate by chronologic age), and breastfeeding","Screen for contraindications before each vaccine; separate live vaccines by 4 weeks if not given same-day"],"tx":["Administer per platform rules: give inactivated vaccines liberally; withhold LIVE vaccines in significant immunocompromise/pregnancy and give them same-day or >=4 weeks apart","Report adverse events to VAERS (Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System - a passive, hypothesis-generating surveillance system); the VACCINE INJURY COMPENSATION PROGRAM (VICP) provides no-fault compensation for covered injuries","Do not miss opportunities over false contraindications; counsel on expected mild reactions and true safety data"],"pearls":["Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, rotavirus, intranasal flu, yellow fever) are contraindicated in significant immunocompromise and pregnancy; inactivated vaccines are not","False contraindications - mild illness, antibiotics, prematurity, breastfeeding - are NOT reasons to defer vaccination","VAERS is passive, voluntary, hypothesis-generating surveillance (it detects signals, not causation); VICP is the no-fault injury compensation program","Give live vaccines the same day or at least 4 weeks apart if not simultaneous"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/ACIP: General Best Practice Guidelines for Immunization","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Vaccine Contraindications & Precautions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513293/"}]},{"id":"prev-travel-medicine","name":"Travel Medicine & Pre-Travel Counseling","sec":"prev","present":["Pre-travel consultation reduces travel-associated illness through risk assessment, vaccination, chemoprophylaxis, and counseling - ideally 4-6 weeks before departure","Risk depends on the DESTINATION, itinerary, duration, activities, and the traveler's health/immune status; the CDC Yellow Book and destination notices guide recommendations","Board framing: know the core components (vaccines, malaria prophylaxis, travelers' diarrhea) and yellow-fever specifics"],"dx":["Assess destination-specific risks and required/recommended vaccines: routine catch-up, and travel vaccines such as hepatitis A/B, typhoid, YELLOW FEVER (a live vaccine, required for entry to some countries with an official certificate), rabies (pre-exposure for high-risk), Japanese encephalitis, meningococcal (required for Hajj), and cholera per risk","Assess MALARIA risk and choose region-appropriate chemoprophylaxis (e.g., atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, or mefloquine by resistance/contraindications) plus bite avoidance","Consider special travelers (pregnant, immunocompromised, children) - live vaccines and certain drugs need caution"],"tx":["Provide destination-appropriate VACCINES (time live vaccines and yellow fever certificate needs), MALARIA chemoprophylaxis with adherence counseling, and TRAVELERS' DIARRHEA guidance (food/water precautions; stand-by antibiotic +/- loperamide for moderate-severe cases)","Counsel on insect-bite avoidance (repellents, nets), safe food/water, altitude, venous thromboembolism on long flights, safe sex, and injury prevention (the leading cause of preventable travel death is injury/road traffic)","Advise on post-travel evaluation of fever (consider malaria in any febrile returning traveler)"],"pearls":["Yellow fever vaccine is LIVE, may be required for entry (with an official certificate), and needs caution in immunocompromise/pregnancy/older age","Match malaria chemoprophylaxis to the destination's resistance pattern and start/continue per the drug's schedule; add bite avoidance","Injury/road-traffic crashes - not exotic infection - are the leading cause of preventable death in travelers","Fever in a returning traveler is malaria until proven otherwise - evaluate promptly"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC Yellow Book: Travelers' Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Travel Medicine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560506/"}]},{"id":"prev-chemoprevention","name":"Chemoprevention","sec":"prev","present":["Chemoprevention is the use of medications/supplements to PREVENT disease in at-risk but not-yet-affected people - a distinct preventive strategy from screening and vaccination","Common evidence-based examples: folic acid (neural tube defects), statins (ASCVD), PrEP (HIV), and risk-reducing agents for breast cancer; some once-common practices (routine aspirin, many vitamins) have narrowed","Board framing: know the well-supported chemoprevention recommendations and where evidence argues against"],"dx":["Identify the at-risk population and the intervention: FOLIC ACID 400-800 mcg daily for all people who could become pregnant (USPSTF grade A, to prevent neural tube defects); STATINS for ASCVD primary prevention (risk-based); PrEP (tenofovir-based) for those at increased HIV risk (grade A)","Breast-cancer risk reduction: tamoxifen/raloxifene/aromatase inhibitors for women at INCREASED risk and LOW risk of harm (grade B), not average-risk women","Recognize what the evidence does NOT support: routine beta-carotene/vitamin E (no benefit/possible harm) and, now, routine aspirin for primary CVD/colorectal prevention outside specific scenarios"],"tx":["Recommend folic acid to all who could become pregnant, offer PrEP to those at increased HIV risk, and apply risk-based statin and breast-cancer risk-reducing therapy after weighing benefits and harms","Do NOT recommend chemoprevention where evidence shows no benefit or net harm (routine vitamin supplements for chronic-disease/cancer prevention; routine aspirin outside narrow indications)","Individualize with shared decision-making, especially where benefit is modest and harms (e.g., bleeding, drug side effects) exist"],"pearls":["Folic acid for all who could become pregnant is a grade A chemoprevention (prevents neural tube defects) - start before conception","PrEP is grade A chemoprevention for people at increased HIV risk","Breast-cancer risk-reducing medications are for INCREASED-risk women, not average-risk women","The USPSTF recommends AGAINST beta-carotene/vitamin E for cancer/CVD prevention, and routine aspirin has been de-emphasized - more is not better"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Chemoprevention Recommendations","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chemoprophylaxis / Preventive Medication","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537043/"}]},{"id":"prev-post-exposure-prophylaxis","name":"Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (HIV, Rabies, Hepatitis B, Tetanus)","sec":"prev","present":["Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) prevents infection AFTER a known/suspected exposure - a time-critical secondary prevention used for occupational and community exposures","Key scenarios: bloodborne exposures (HIV, hepatitis B/C), animal bites (rabies), and wounds (tetanus); the window to act is short, so rapid assessment matters","Board framing: know the core PEP regimens and their urgency"],"dx":["Assess the exposure (source status, type/severity, timing) and the exposed person's immune/vaccination status; test source and exposed as appropriate (HIV, HBV/HCV serologies, tetanus history)","HIV: risk-assess percutaneous/mucosal exposures; RABIES: assess the animal/bite (bat exposure is high-concern even without an obvious bite); TETANUS: assess wound type and vaccination history; HEPATITIS B: assess vaccination/immune status and source","Time is critical - PEP effectiveness falls with delay"],"tx":["HIV PEP: start a 3-drug antiretroviral regimen ASAP (ideally within hours, and within 72 hours) for 28 days after significant exposure, with follow-up testing","RABIES PEP (for unvaccinated): wound cleansing plus rabies immune globulin (infiltrated at the wound) AND the rabies vaccine series; previously vaccinated persons get vaccine boosters (no RIG)","TETANUS: tetanus toxoid vaccine +/- tetanus immune globulin based on wound type and vaccination history; HEPATITIS B: hepatitis B vaccine +/- HBIG based on exposure and immune status"],"pearls":["HIV PEP must start ASAP (within 72 hours), is a 28-day 3-drug regimen, and is more effective the sooner it begins","Rabies PEP in the unvaccinated = wound washing + rabies immune globulin AT THE WOUND + vaccine series; bat exposures count even without a visible bite","Tetanus prophylaxis depends on wound type and vaccination status - give toxoid +/- immune globulin accordingly","Hepatitis B PEP uses vaccine +/- HBIG based on the source and the exposed person's immune status - and PEP is always time-sensitive"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (HIV, Rabies, Hepatitis)","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postexposure Prophylaxis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560581/"}]},{"id":"prev-tobacco-cessation","name":"Tobacco Cessation Counseling & Pharmacotherapy","sec":"prev","present":["Tobacco use is the leading preventable cause of death; cessation at any age produces major health gains, and clinicians markedly improve quit rates by intervening","Screening for tobacco use and offering cessation support is USPSTF grade A for adults; the combination of counseling AND pharmacotherapy works best","Board framing: know the 5 A's framework and first-line cessation medications"],"dx":["Screen ALL adults for tobacco use (grade A) and assess readiness to quit; use the '5 A's': ASK about use, ADVISE to quit (clear, personalized), ASSESS willingness, ASSIST with a plan/meds, and ARRANGE follow-up","For those not ready, use motivational strategies (the '5 R's'); document use and quit attempts","Screen pregnant persons and offer behavioral cessation support (USPSTF grade A; pharmacotherapy evidence in pregnancy is limited)"],"tx":["Combine BEHAVIORAL counseling (individual, group, or quitlines - 1-800-QUIT-NOW) WITH PHARMACOTHERAPY for the best quit rates","First-line MEDICATIONS: nicotine replacement therapy (patch + short-acting combination), VARENICLINE (often the most effective single agent), and bupropion; tailor to preferences/contraindications","Arrange follow-up to support the quit attempt and manage relapse (a normal part of quitting)"],"pearls":["The 5 A's (Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange) structure the office cessation intervention - and screening is USPSTF grade A","Counseling PLUS pharmacotherapy beats either alone; varenicline is among the most effective single agents","Offer behavioral cessation support to pregnant patients (grade A); pharmacotherapy data in pregnancy are limited","Relapse is common and part of the process - arrange follow-up and re-engage rather than giving up"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Tobacco Cessation","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Smoking Cessation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537066/"}]},{"id":"prev-alcohol-brief-intervention","name":"Unhealthy Alcohol Use Brief Intervention","sec":"prev","present":["Unhealthy alcohol use spans a spectrum from risky/hazardous drinking to alcohol use disorder and is a major preventable cause of injury, liver disease, cancer, and death","Screening plus BRIEF INTERVENTION in primary care effectively reduces risky drinking (a core SBIRT component); it is USPSTF-recommended for adults","Board framing: know the screening/brief-intervention evidence and what defines risky use"],"dx":["USPSTF recommends screening adults >=18 (including pregnant persons) for unhealthy alcohol use and providing brief behavioral counseling to those with risky use (grade B)","Use validated screens (AUDIT-C or a single-item screen) and define risky drinking by standard-drink thresholds (exceeding recommended limits); any alcohol is advised against in pregnancy","Assess for alcohol use DISORDER (loss of control, craving, tolerance/withdrawal) which needs more than brief intervention"],"tx":["Deliver a BRIEF INTERVENTION for risky use: nonjudgmental feedback, clear advice, and goal-setting using motivational interviewing (typically brief, sometimes with follow-up)","Refer patients with alcohol use disorder to treatment; evidence-based MEDICATIONS for alcohol use disorder include naltrexone and acamprosate (and disulfiram in selected patients)","Advise no alcohol in pregnancy; connect patients to support/treatment resources and follow up"],"pearls":["Screening plus brief intervention (USPSTF grade B) works best for RISKY drinking - the not-yet-dependent range","AUDIT-C is a common validated primary-care screen; brief intervention uses feedback and motivational interviewing","Alcohol use disorder (not just risky use) warrants formal treatment - medications include naltrexone and acamprosate","Advise against any alcohol during pregnancy - there is no known safe amount"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Unhealthy Alcohol Use","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Alcohol Use Screening & Brief Intervention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436003/"}]},{"id":"prev-nutrition-counseling","name":"Nutrition & Healthy-Diet Counseling","sec":"prev","present":["Diet quality is a leading modifiable determinant of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, obesity, and some cancers; dietary counseling is a core preventive service","Evidence-based dietary patterns (e.g., Mediterranean-style, DASH) emphasize vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts, and healthy fats while limiting sodium, added sugars, refined grains, and processed/red meat","Board framing: know the USPSTF behavioral-counseling recommendation and the evidence-based dietary patterns"],"dx":["Assess dietary patterns, cardiovascular/metabolic risk, and readiness to change; USPSTF recommends offering/referring adults with cardiovascular risk factors to intensive BEHAVIORAL COUNSELING to promote a healthy diet and physical activity (grade B)","For adults without known risk factors, the benefit of behavioral counseling is small - offer selectively (grade C)","Screen for food insecurity and social barriers that shape diet"],"tx":["Recommend an evidence-based dietary PATTERN (Mediterranean/DASH-style) rather than single nutrients; emphasize whole foods and limit sodium, added sugar, and ultra-processed foods","Refer higher-risk patients to intensive, multi-session behavioral counseling (often dietitian-led) for durable change; align nutrition and physical-activity counseling","Address social determinants (food access/insecurity) that constrain healthy eating"],"pearls":["USPSTF recommends intensive behavioral dietary/activity counseling for adults WITH cardiovascular risk factors (grade B); for those without, offer selectively (grade C)","Counsel on evidence-based dietary PATTERNS (Mediterranean, DASH), not isolated nutrients or fad diets","Reducing sodium and added sugars and increasing plants are high-yield, broadly recommended changes","Food insecurity and access shape diet - address the social context, not just the advice"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Healthy Diet & Physical Activity Counseling","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"StatPearls: Dietary Patterns & Health","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551512/"}]},{"id":"prev-physical-activity-counseling","name":"Physical Activity Counseling","sec":"prev","present":["Regular physical activity reduces all-cause mortality, cardiovascular disease, diabetes, several cancers, and depression, and improves function - one of the highest-yield preventive behaviors","Most adults do not meet activity guidelines; counseling (often bundled with diet) supports behavior change","Board framing: know the adult activity guideline targets and the counseling recommendation"],"dx":["Assess current activity level and barriers; the Physical Activity Guidelines recommend adults get at least 150-300 minutes/week of MODERATE (or 75-150 min VIGOROUS) aerobic activity PLUS MUSCLE-STRENGTHENING activity on >=2 days/week - and that moving more and sitting less benefits everyone","Older adults should add BALANCE training (fall prevention); children/adolescents need ~60 minutes/day","USPSTF frames activity counseling within behavioral interventions for cardiovascular risk (grade B for those with risk factors; grade C without)"],"tx":["Counsel toward the guideline targets (150-300 min/week moderate aerobic + 2 days strengthening), emphasizing that ANY increase from inactivity yields the largest relative benefit ('some is better than none')","Refer higher-risk patients to intensive behavioral counseling; individualize goals, and add balance training for older adults to prevent falls","Integrate activity with diet counseling and use behavior-change strategies (goal-setting, self-monitoring)"],"pearls":["Adult target: >=150-300 min/week moderate aerobic activity PLUS muscle-strengthening on >=2 days/week","The biggest relative gains come from moving from INACTIVE to somewhat active - 'some is better than none'","Add balance training for older adults - it reduces falls","Bundle physical-activity and diet counseling; intensive behavioral counseling is grade B for adults with cardiovascular risk factors"],"refs":[{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Behavioral Counseling (Diet/Activity)","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"},{"t":"Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans (HHS)","u":"https://health.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-behavior-change-models","name":"Behavior-Change Models & Motivational Interviewing","sec":"prev","present":["Effective prevention depends on behavior change; theoretical models explain and guide how people modify health behaviors (tobacco, diet, activity, adherence)","Key frameworks: the TRANSTHEORETICAL (Stages of Change) MODEL, the HEALTH BELIEF MODEL, social cognitive theory, and the counseling method of MOTIVATIONAL INTERVIEWING","Board framing: recognize the stages of change and the spirit/technique of motivational interviewing"],"dx":["TRANSTHEORETICAL (Stages of Change): precontemplation -> contemplation -> preparation -> action -> maintenance (with relapse as part of the cycle) - match the intervention to the person's stage","HEALTH BELIEF MODEL: behavior is driven by perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, cues to action, and self-efficacy","MOTIVATIONAL INTERVIEWING (MI): a collaborative, patient-centered method to elicit and strengthen the person's OWN motivation to change (resolving ambivalence), not to persuade/lecture"],"tx":["Tailor counseling to the STAGE (e.g., consciousness-raising for precontemplation; planning/goal-setting for preparation/action; relapse-prevention for maintenance)","Use MOTIVATIONAL INTERVIEWING skills - open questions, affirmations, reflective listening, summarizing (OARS), rolling with resistance, and eliciting change talk - rather than confrontation","Build SELF-EFFICACY (small achievable goals, self-monitoring) and address perceived barriers"],"pearls":["Match the intervention to the Stage of Change - pushing 'action' on a precontemplative patient backfires","Motivational interviewing draws out the patient's OWN reasons to change (resolving ambivalence) rather than lecturing - it is collaborative, not confrontational","The Health Belief Model highlights perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, and self-efficacy as change drivers","Relapse is expected in behavior change - plan for it as part of the cycle, not as failure"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Motivational Interviewing / Behavior Change","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436021/"},{"t":"CDC: Health Behavior & Promotion","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-injury-violence-prevention","name":"Injury & Violence Prevention","sec":"prev","present":["Unintentional injury is a leading cause of death, especially in younger people, and much of it is PREVENTABLE; violence (including intimate partner violence and firearm injury) is a major public-health problem","Prevention spans motor-vehicle safety, falls (especially older adults), poisoning, drowning, firearm safety, and screening for interpersonal/intimate partner violence","Board framing: know the high-yield injury-prevention counseling and USPSTF IPV screening"],"dx":["Assess age-relevant risks: MOTOR-VEHICLE (seat belts, child safety seats, no impaired/distracted driving), FALLS in older adults (a leading injury cause - assess gait/balance/meds/home hazards), poisoning, drowning, and FIREARM access/safe storage","USPSTF recommends screening women of reproductive age for INTIMATE PARTNER VIOLENCE and referring positives to support services (grade B)","Use the Haddon matrix/levels-of-prevention thinking (pre-event, event, post-event) to target interventions"],"tx":["Counsel and intervene: seat belts/car seats, older-adult FALL prevention (exercise/balance training, medication review, vitamin D where indicated, home-hazard reduction), safe firearm storage, and substance-use/impaired-driving prevention","Screen reproductive-age women for IPV and connect those affected to support services and safety planning","Apply population strategies (safe product design, policy) alongside individual counseling - environmental/engineering controls often outperform education alone"],"pearls":["Falls are a leading injury cause in older adults - exercise/balance training, medication review, and home-hazard reduction are evidence-based prevention","USPSTF recommends screening reproductive-age women for intimate partner violence and linking positives to services (grade B)","Passive/environmental protections (seat belts, safe storage, product design) often outperform education alone","Think in levels/Haddon matrix (pre-event, event, post-event) to find the best point to prevent injury"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Injury Prevention & Control","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"US Preventive Services Task Force: Intimate Partner Violence Screening","u":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org"}]},{"id":"prev-occupational-history","name":"Occupational & Exposure History","sec":"prev","present":["Many diseases are caused or worsened by workplace/environmental exposures that are missed without a deliberate history; the occupational history is the key diagnostic and preventive tool in occupational medicine","A good exposure history links a patient's illness to a job/exposure (enabling diagnosis, workers' compensation, and prevention for coworkers) and identifies sentinel cases","Board framing: know the components of the exposure history and the concept of the sentinel health event"],"dx":["Take a structured history: current and PAST jobs/tasks, specific exposures (dusts, fumes, solvents, metals, noise, radiation, biologics), use/availability of controls and PPE, and the TEMPORAL relationship of symptoms to work (better on weekends/vacations suggests a work cause)","Ask about hobbies, home, and environmental exposures; consider latency (some diseases appear decades after exposure - e.g., asbestos)","A SENTINEL HEALTH EVENT (a preventable disease/injury/death signaling a workplace hazard) should trigger investigation to protect other workers"],"tx":["Use the history to establish work-relatedness, remove/reduce the exposure (the definitive treatment), and report/refer for workers' compensation and workplace evaluation","Protect coworkers by investigating the hazard (an individual case may be a sentinel event); apply the hazard-control hierarchy (see that entry)","Coordinate surveillance (medical monitoring of exposed workers) and return-to-work planning"],"pearls":["Symptoms that improve away from work (weekends/vacations) and worsen on return point to an occupational cause - ask about the temporal pattern","'What is your job, and what are you exposed to?' - the occupational history is the tool that uncovers work-related disease","A sentinel health event (a preventable work-related illness/injury) is a signal to investigate and protect other workers","Remember latency - asbestos and many carcinogens cause disease decades after exposure"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Occupational Exposure Assessment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568707/"},{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Workplace Safety & Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"}]},{"id":"prev-occupational-lung-disease","name":"Occupational Lung Disease","sec":"prev","present":["Inhaled workplace agents cause a spectrum of lung disease; recognizing the exposure-disease links enables diagnosis, compensation, and prevention","PNEUMOCONIOSES (from inhaled mineral dusts): ASBESTOSIS (asbestos - also causes mesothelioma and lung cancer, with a long latency), SILICOSIS (silica - mining, sandblasting; raises TB risk), COAL WORKERS' pneumoconiosis, and berylliosis; plus OCCUPATIONAL ASTHMA and hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Board framing: know the classic exposure-disease pairings and their key complications"],"dx":["History of exposure plus imaging/PFTs: asbestosis (lower-zone fibrosis, pleural plaques; asbestos also -> MESOTHELIOMA and lung cancer, synergistic with smoking), silicosis (upper-zone nodules, eggshell-calcified hilar nodes; increased TB susceptibility), coal workers' pneumoconiosis","OCCUPATIONAL ASTHMA: new-onset or worsening asthma with a work-related temporal pattern (better away from work) from sensitizers/irritants; hypersensitivity pneumonitis from organic antigens (e.g., farmer's/bird-fancier's lung)","Confirm work-relatedness and screen exposed workers (surveillance spirometry, imaging as indicated)"],"tx":["The definitive intervention is PREVENTION - eliminate/reduce exposure (engineering controls, substitution, respiratory protection) and remove the worker from ongoing exposure; the pneumoconioses are largely irreversible, so prevention is paramount","Manage occupational asthma by removing the causative exposure (essential) plus standard asthma therapy; treat complications and screen for TB in silicosis","Report, arrange workers' compensation, and protect coworkers"],"pearls":["Asbestos causes asbestosis, pleural plaques, lung cancer, and mesothelioma (long latency) - and its lung-cancer risk is synergistic with smoking","Silicosis causes upper-zone nodules with eggshell-calcified hilar nodes and increases TB susceptibility","Occupational asthma is suggested by asthma that improves away from work - removing the exposure is essential treatment","The pneumoconioses are irreversible - prevention (dust control, substitution, respirators) is the only real cure"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pneumoconiosis / Occupational Lung Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555185/"},{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Respiratory Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"}]},{"id":"prev-noise-induced-hearing-loss","name":"Noise-Induced Hearing Loss & Hearing Conservation","sec":"prev","present":["Noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL) is a common, PERMANENT, and entirely PREVENTABLE occupational disease from chronic exposure to hazardous noise (manufacturing, construction, military, music)","It is sensorineural and irreversible, so a HEARING CONSERVATION program is the intervention; recreational noise also contributes","Board framing: know the audiometric signature and the OSHA action/exposure levels and program elements"],"dx":["NIHL is bilateral SENSORINEURAL hearing loss with a characteristic audiometric NOTCH at 3000-6000 Hz (classically 4000 Hz), which can progress with continued exposure; a temporary threshold shift can become permanent","OSHA requires a HEARING CONSERVATION program when noise exposure meets the action level (a time-weighted average of 85 dBA over 8 hours), with a permissible exposure limit of 90 dBA; noise dose scales with intensity and duration","Program elements: noise monitoring, baseline and annual AUDIOMETRY (to detect a standard threshold shift), hearing protection, training, and recordkeeping"],"tx":["PREVENT with the hazard-control hierarchy: engineering controls (reduce noise at the source), administrative controls (limit exposure time), and hearing PROTECTION (earplugs/muffs) as the last line","Run a hearing conservation program (monitoring, annual audiometry, protection, training) for exposed workers; identify a standard threshold shift early and intervene","Counsel on recreational noise; NIHL cannot be reversed, so protection and early detection are everything"],"pearls":["The audiometric hallmark of noise-induced hearing loss is a notch around 3000-6000 Hz (classically 4000 Hz), bilateral and sensorineural","OSHA's action level for a hearing conservation program is an 8-hour time-weighted average of 85 dBA (PEL 90 dBA)","NIHL is permanent but 100% preventable - engineering controls first, hearing protection last, with annual audiometry to catch shifts","Both occupational and recreational noise contribute - counsel on protection in all settings"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Noise & Hearing Loss Prevention","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"},{"t":"StatPearls: Noise-Induced Hearing Loss","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526094/"}]},{"id":"prev-ergonomics-msk","name":"Work-Related Musculoskeletal Disorders & Ergonomics","sec":"prev","present":["Work-related musculoskeletal disorders (WMSDs) - low back pain, neck/shoulder disorders, and upper-extremity conditions (e.g., carpal tunnel, tendinopathy) - are among the most common and costly occupational health problems","ERGONOMICS fits the job/task/tools to the worker to prevent these disorders; risk factors include repetition, forceful exertion, awkward/static postures, vibration, and heavy/manual lifting","Board framing: know the ergonomic risk factors and prevention approach"],"dx":["Identify ergonomic RISK FACTORS in the task: repetitive motions, high force, awkward or sustained postures, mechanical contact stress, vibration, and manual material handling (lifting/carrying)","Recognize common WMSDs (low back pain from lifting; carpal tunnel/tendinopathy from repetitive hand-intensive work) and their work-related temporal pattern","Assess the workstation/task (workstation setup, tool design, lifting demands) for corrective opportunities"],"tx":["Prevent via ERGONOMIC controls up the hierarchy: engineering (redesign workstations/tools, mechanical lifting aids, adjustable equipment), administrative (job rotation, rest breaks, training in safe lifting/posture), and behavioral supports","Treat established WMSDs with activity modification, therapy, and graded return-to-work; address the ergonomic cause to prevent recurrence","Involve worksite evaluation and return-to-work planning (see workers' comp entry)"],"pearls":["Ergonomic risk factors are repetition, force, awkward/static posture, contact stress, and vibration - reduce these to prevent WMSDs","Ergonomics fits the JOB to the WORKER (redesign the task), not the other way around","Engineering controls (workstation/tool redesign, lifting aids) outrank training/behavioral fixes in the hazard hierarchy","Treating a WMSD without fixing the ergonomic cause invites recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Ergonomics & Musculoskeletal Disorders","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"},{"t":"StatPearls: Work-Related Musculoskeletal Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560515/"}]},{"id":"prev-heavy-metal-toxicology","name":"Heavy-Metal & Chemical Toxicology","sec":"prev","present":["Heavy metals and toxic gases cause preventable occupational and environmental illness; recognizing exposure sources and syndromes enables diagnosis, chelation where appropriate, and source removal","Key agents: LEAD, MERCURY, ARSENIC, and CARBON MONOXIDE - each with characteristic sources, syndromes, and management","Board framing: know the classic exposure-syndrome-antidote triads and that source removal/prevention is central"],"dx":["LEAD (batteries, paint in older homes, radiators, mining/smelting): abdominal pain, anemia (basophilic stippling), peripheral neuropathy, and in CHILDREN neurodevelopmental/cognitive harm - screen at-risk children; diagnose by blood lead level","MERCURY (fish - methylmercury; industrial; elemental vapor): neurologic and renal effects; ARSENIC (contaminated water, pesticides, smelting): GI symptoms, peripheral neuropathy, skin changes (hyperkeratosis, Mees lines), and cancer risk","CARBON MONOXIDE (combustion, faulty heaters, fires): headache, dizziness, confusion (mimics flu); diagnose with CARBOXYHEMOGLOBIN (pulse oximetry reads falsely normal) - a leading cause of poisoning death"],"tx":["The cornerstone is SOURCE REMOVAL/exposure elimination and prevention (lead-safe housing, water safety, ventilation/CO detectors); report and investigate to protect others","CHELATION for significant metal poisoning (e.g., lead - succimer/EDTA/dimercaprol by severity; arsenic/mercury per agent) under toxicology guidance; treat symptomatically","CARBON MONOXIDE: high-flow 100% OXYGEN (hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases - loss of consciousness, neurologic deficits, pregnancy, high levels)"],"pearls":["Childhood lead exposure causes lasting neurodevelopmental harm - screen at-risk children and remove the source (old paint/housing); basophilic stippling is a clue","Carbon monoxide poisoning mimics the flu and gives a falsely normal pulse oximetry - measure carboxyhemoglobin and treat with 100% oxygen (hyperbaric for severe)","Arsenic causes GI symptoms, peripheral neuropathy, skin hyperkeratosis/Mees lines, and cancer; mercury causes neuro/renal effects","For all heavy metals, source removal and prevention matter more than chelation - chelate significant poisoning under toxicology guidance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Heavy Metal Toxicity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560920/"},{"t":"CDC/ATSDR: Toxic Substances","u":"https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-hazard-control-osha","name":"Hazard-Control Hierarchy, PPE & OSHA Basics","sec":"prev","present":["Occupational hazards are controlled through a defined HIERARCHY that prioritizes eliminating the hazard over relying on worker behavior/protective gear - the organizing principle of workplace safety","OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration) sets and enforces workplace safety standards; NIOSH is the research arm that makes recommendations","Board framing: know the order of the hazard-control hierarchy and why PPE is last"],"dx":["The HIERARCHY OF CONTROLS (most to least effective): 1) ELIMINATION (remove the hazard), 2) SUBSTITUTION (replace with something less hazardous), 3) ENGINEERING controls (isolate people from the hazard - ventilation, enclosures, guards), 4) ADMINISTRATIVE controls (change how people work - training, rotation, procedures), 5) PPE (personal protective equipment - the last line)","OSHA framework: employers must provide a safe workplace (General Duty Clause), meet permissible exposure limits, provide required PPE and training, and maintain records; workers have rights to a safe workplace and to report hazards","NIOSH recommends exposure limits and conducts health hazard evaluations"],"tx":["Apply controls from the TOP of the hierarchy down: try to eliminate/substitute the hazard, then engineer it out, then use administrative controls, and use PPE only as the last resort/supplement (PPE relies on correct, consistent use and fit)","Ensure OSHA compliance (exposure limits, hazard communication, training, PPE provision) and use NIOSH resources/health hazard evaluations","Combine controls (layered protection) where a single control is insufficient"],"pearls":["Hierarchy of controls, most to least effective: Elimination > Substitution > Engineering > Administrative > PPE","PPE is the LAST line of defense because it depends on correct, consistent use and fit - engineering out the hazard is more reliable","OSHA sets/enforces standards (and the General Duty Clause requires a safe workplace); NIOSH is the research/recommendation arm","Prefer controls that protect all workers automatically (engineering) over those that depend on individual behavior"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Hierarchy of Controls","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"},{"t":"OSHA: Workplace Safety Standards","u":"https://www.osha.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-workers-comp-fitness-duty","name":"Workers' Compensation, Fitness-for-Duty & Return-to-Work","sec":"prev","present":["Occupational medicine includes the systems that manage work-related injury/illness and a worker's capacity to work safely - workers' compensation, fitness-for-duty evaluations, and return-to-work","WORKERS' COMPENSATION is a no-fault insurance system providing medical care and wage replacement for work-related injuries/illnesses (in exchange, it generally limits employer liability)","Board framing: know the no-fault nature of workers' comp and the goals of fitness-for-duty/return-to-work"],"dx":["Establish WORK-RELATEDNESS (the occupational history) for a workers' compensation claim - it is NO-FAULT (benefits regardless of blame), covering medical treatment and partial wage replacement/disability","FITNESS-FOR-DUTY evaluation assesses whether a worker can safely perform essential job functions (with or without accommodation), balancing worker health, safety, and legal protections (ADA)","RETURN-TO-WORK planning matches the worker's current capacity to job demands, often with temporary restrictions/modified duty"],"tx":["Support timely, SAFE RETURN TO WORK - prolonged disability worsens outcomes, so use modified/transitional duty and functional restrictions to keep workers engaged when possible","Provide objective fitness-for-duty assessments and reasonable ACCOMMODATIONS (ADA) while protecting worker and public safety","Coordinate care, documentation, and communication among worker, employer, and the compensation system; treat the injury and address the workplace hazard to prevent recurrence"],"pearls":["Workers' compensation is a NO-FAULT system - it covers work-related injury/illness regardless of who was at fault, providing medical care and wage replacement","Establishing work-relatedness (via the occupational history) is the gateway to a compensation claim","Early, safe return to work with modified/transitional duty improves outcomes - prolonged disability is harmful","Fitness-for-duty evaluations balance the worker's health, safety, and legal protections (ADA reasonable accommodation)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Workers' Compensation / Return to Work","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544369/"},{"t":"CDC/NIOSH: Total Worker Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh"}]},{"id":"prev-environmental-health","name":"Environmental Health (Air, Water, Radiation, Climate)","sec":"prev","present":["Environmental exposures - air and water quality, radiation, and climate change - are major population-level determinants of health that operate outside the clinic and require policy/systems solutions","Effects are widespread and often affect vulnerable populations disproportionately (environmental justice)","Board framing: know the key environmental hazards and their health effects and controls"],"dx":["AIR: particulate matter (PM2.5) and ozone worsen cardiopulmonary disease and mortality; indoor hazards include RADON (a leading cause of lung cancer), secondhand smoke, and carbon monoxide; the EPA sets air-quality standards","WATER: contamination (LEAD from pipes, microbial pathogens, nitrates, arsenic, industrial chemicals) causes acute and chronic disease; safe drinking water and sanitation are foundational public-health achievements","RADIATION (ionizing - radon, medical imaging, occupational) raises cancer risk (minimize per ALARA); CLIMATE CHANGE drives heat-related illness, worsened air quality/allergens, extreme weather, vector-borne disease shifts, and food/water insecurity"],"tx":["Prevent through policy and systems: air-quality regulation and emissions control, safe drinking-water treatment/monitoring, radon testing/mitigation in homes, radiation minimization (ALARA), and climate mitigation/adaptation","Counsel individuals on modifiable exposures (radon testing, air-quality awareness for susceptible patients, water safety) and advocate for vulnerable communities (environmental justice)","Coordinate with public-health/environmental agencies for exposure assessment and remediation"],"pearls":["Radon is a leading cause of lung cancer (second to smoking) - home testing and mitigation are effective prevention","Particulate air pollution (PM2.5) increases cardiopulmonary disease and mortality - a major population-level exposure","Safe drinking water/sanitation is one of the greatest public-health achievements; lead in water (old pipes) remains a preventable hazard","Climate change is a health threat multiplier (heat, air quality, extreme weather, vector-borne disease) that hits vulnerable populations hardest"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Environmental Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Environmental Health / Air Pollution","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553131/"}]},{"id":"prev-levels-of-prevention","name":"Levels of Prevention","sec":"prev","present":["Prevention is classified by WHEN in the disease process it acts - a foundational framework organizing all preventive activity","The classic levels: PRIMORDIAL, PRIMARY, SECONDARY, and TERTIARY prevention (and quaternary - avoiding overmedicalization/harm from excess intervention)","Board framing: correctly categorize interventions by level - a very high-yield exam concept"],"dx":["PRIMORDIAL: prevent the emergence of RISK FACTORS in populations (e.g., policies for healthy environments, preventing obesity/smoking uptake at a societal level)","PRIMARY: prevent disease BEFORE it occurs by removing risk/adding protection in at-risk-but-healthy people (immunization, seat belts, smoking prevention, chemoprevention)","SECONDARY: detect and treat disease EARLY/pre-symptomatically to halt progression (SCREENING - mammography, Pap, blood pressure); TERTIARY: reduce complications/disability in ESTABLISHED disease (cardiac rehab, diabetic foot care, secondary-prevention statins)"],"tx":["Match interventions to the level: policy for primordial; vaccination/counseling/chemoprevention for primary; screening for secondary; rehabilitation/complication-prevention for tertiary","Recognize that SCREENING is SECONDARY prevention (early detection of existing but asymptomatic disease), a classic point of confusion","Consider quaternary prevention - protecting patients from unnecessary/harmful medical interventions (overdiagnosis/overtreatment)"],"pearls":["Screening is SECONDARY prevention (early detection of existing asymptomatic disease) - the most commonly tested distinction","Primary prevention stops disease before it starts (vaccines, seat belts); tertiary prevention limits complications of established disease (rehab)","Primordial prevention targets the risk factors themselves at a population/policy level - before they even develop","Quaternary prevention guards against the harms of over-intervention (overdiagnosis/overtreatment)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Levels of Prevention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537222/"},{"t":"CDC: Picture of Prevention","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-social-determinants","name":"Social Determinants of Health & Health Disparities","sec":"prev","present":["SOCIAL DETERMINANTS OF HEALTH (SDOH) are the non-medical conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, and age - economic stability, education, health-care access, neighborhood/built environment, and social/community context - that drive most health outcomes","HEALTH DISPARITIES are preventable differences in health burden across populations (by race/ethnicity, income, geography, etc.), rooted largely in these determinants and in structural inequities (HEALTH EQUITY is the goal)","Board framing: recognize that SDOH outweigh clinical care in shaping population health and how to address them"],"dx":["Recognize that medical care contributes only a fraction of health outcomes - SDOH and behaviors (shaped by those determinants) account for the majority; screen for social needs (food insecurity, housing, transportation, safety, finances) in clinical settings","Identify DISPARITIES in access, quality, and outcomes across populations and their structural drivers (poverty, racism, education, environment)","Distinguish health disparity (the difference), health equity (the goal of fair opportunity for health), and the determinants that produce them"],"tx":["Address social needs clinically (screen-and-refer to resources, community partnerships) AND advocate for upstream, structural/policy change (the higher-leverage intervention)","Design equitable programs and target resources to reduce disparities (proportionate universalism); collaborate across sectors (housing, education, food)","Practice culturally responsive, trauma-informed care and reduce access barriers"],"pearls":["Social determinants (economic stability, education, health-care access, environment, social context) drive the majority of health outcomes - more than medical care alone","Health disparities are PREVENTABLE differences across populations; the goal is health EQUITY (fair opportunity to be healthy)","Clinical screen-and-refer for social needs helps individuals, but upstream policy change is the higher-leverage lever","Structural factors (poverty, racism, environment) underlie disparities - addressing them requires cross-sector, population-level action"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Social Determinants of Health","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Social Determinants of Health","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560607/"}]},{"id":"prev-health-promotion-frameworks","name":"Health-Promotion Frameworks","sec":"prev","present":["Health promotion enables people and communities to increase control over and improve their health; theoretical frameworks operate beyond the individual to guide population-level programs","Whereas individual behavior-change models (stages of change, motivational interviewing) target one person, health-promotion frameworks span multiple LEVELS of influence","Board framing: recognize the socioecological model and the key population health-promotion frameworks and their levels"],"dx":["SOCIOECOLOGICAL MODEL: health behavior is shaped by nested levels - individual, interpersonal (family/peers), organizational, community, and policy/society; effective programs intervene at MULTIPLE levels, not just the individual","HEALTH BELIEF MODEL (individual perceptions - susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, cues, self-efficacy) and DIFFUSION OF INNOVATIONS (how new behaviors/ideas spread through populations via early adopters to late majority)","Distinguish downstream (individual clinical) from upstream (policy/environmental) approaches - upstream interventions typically have broader, more durable population impact"],"tx":["Design multi-level interventions using the socioecological model - combine individual education with interpersonal, organizational, community, and POLICY strategies for durable change","Favor UPSTREAM, structural/environmental and policy interventions (which reach whole populations automatically) alongside individual approaches","Apply diffusion principles (engage early adopters, opinion leaders) to spread healthy innovations through communities"],"pearls":["The socioecological model nests influences from individual to policy - the best programs act at MULTIPLE levels, not just on the individual","Upstream/structural and policy interventions reach entire populations and tend to be more durable than downstream individual counseling","Diffusion of innovations explains how new health behaviors spread (early adopters -> late majority) - use opinion leaders to accelerate it","Individual behavior-change models and population health-promotion frameworks are complementary, operating at different levels"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Health Promotion / Behavioral Models","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585085/"},{"t":"CDC: Health Promotion & Social-Ecological Model","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-program-planning-evaluation","name":"Program Planning & Evaluation","sec":"prev","present":["Public-health programs require systematic PLANNING (to design effective interventions) and EVALUATION (to determine whether they worked and how to improve them)","Structured frameworks organize needs assessment, objective-setting, implementation, and evaluation; the LOGIC MODEL and PRECEDE-PROCEED are core tools","Board framing: know the logic-model components and the types of evaluation"],"dx":["LOGIC MODEL links: INPUTS (resources) -> ACTIVITIES -> OUTPUTS (direct products) -> OUTCOMES (short/intermediate/long-term) -> IMPACT - a roadmap connecting resources to results","PRECEDE-PROCEED: a comprehensive planning framework that works backward from the desired outcome (PRECEDE - assess needs/determinants) then forward through implementation and evaluation (PROCEED)","EVALUATION types: FORMATIVE (during development), PROCESS (was it implemented as planned/reach and fidelity), OUTCOME (did it change knowledge/behavior/health), and IMPACT (long-term effects); use SMART objectives"],"tx":["Plan with a needs assessment and clear, measurable (SMART) objectives, then build a logic model to align resources, activities, and intended outcomes","Match the EVALUATION to the question: process evaluation to check implementation/reach and fidelity; outcome/impact evaluation to measure effect - and use findings to improve the program","Engage stakeholders and use the CDC program-evaluation framework (engage stakeholders, describe the program, focus the design, gather evidence, justify conclusions, use lessons learned)"],"pearls":["A logic model maps inputs -> activities -> outputs -> outcomes -> impact - the backbone of program planning and evaluation","Process evaluation asks 'was it done as planned (reach/fidelity)?'; outcome/impact evaluation asks 'did it work?'","PRECEDE-PROCEED plans backward from the desired health outcome, then forward through implementation","Set SMART objectives (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound) so the program can actually be evaluated"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Program Evaluation Framework","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Program Planning & Evaluation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555593/"}]},{"id":"prev-health-economics","name":"Health Economics & Cost-Effectiveness","sec":"prev","present":["Health resources are finite, so economic evaluation compares the costs and outcomes of interventions to inform allocation and policy - a core preventive-medicine competency","Several analysis types differ by how OUTCOMES are measured; cost-effectiveness and cost-utility analyses are the most used in health","Board framing: distinguish the analysis types and interpret cost-effectiveness (QALYs, ICER)"],"dx":["COST-EFFECTIVENESS ANALYSIS (CEA): outcomes in natural/health units (e.g., cost per life-year gained or per case prevented); COST-UTILITY ANALYSIS (a CEA subtype): outcomes in QUALITY-ADJUSTED LIFE-YEARS (QALYs, combining length and quality of life)","COST-BENEFIT ANALYSIS (CBA): both costs and outcomes in MONETARY terms (allows comparison across sectors but requires valuing health in dollars); COST-MINIMIZATION (when outcomes are equivalent)","The INCREMENTAL COST-EFFECTIVENESS RATIO (ICER) = (cost A - cost B) / (effect A - effect B) = extra cost per extra unit of health gained, compared against a willingness-to-pay threshold"],"tx":["Use CEA/cost-utility (QALYs, ICER) to compare interventions and prioritize those that deliver more health per dollar; interpret an ICER against a willingness-to-pay threshold","Recognize perspective (societal vs payer) and discounting of future costs/benefits; consider equity, not just efficiency, in allocation decisions","Apply results to prevention decisions (many preventive services are cost-effective or cost-saving) while acknowledging value judgments in prioritization"],"pearls":["Cost-EFFECTIVENESS measures outcomes in health units (life-years, cases prevented); cost-UTILITY uses QALYs; cost-BENEFIT converts everything to dollars","The ICER is the extra cost per extra unit of health (e.g., per QALY) gained - compared against a willingness-to-pay threshold","A QALY combines length AND quality of life into one outcome measure for cost-utility analysis","Cost-effective does not always mean cost-saving - most interventions cost money but may be worth it per QALY gained"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cost-Effectiveness Analysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557699/"},{"t":"CDC: Health Economics / Prevention Effectiveness","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"}]},{"id":"prev-quality-improvement-safety","name":"Quality Improvement & Patient Safety","sec":"prev","present":["Quality improvement (QI) systematically improves health-care processes and outcomes; patient safety focuses on preventing harm from care itself - both are population/systems-level prevention","QI uses iterative, data-driven methods; safety science recognizes that most errors arise from SYSTEM failures, not individual blame (a just-culture, systems approach)","Board framing: know the QI cycle, the domains of quality, and core safety concepts"],"dx":["The IOM/NAM defines quality across domains: SAFE, Timely, Effective, Efficient, Equitable, and Patient-centered (STEEEP); measures include structure, PROCESS, and OUTCOME measures (Donabedian)","QI METHODS: the PLAN-DO-STUDY-ACT (PDSA) cycle (small, rapid, iterative tests of change), Lean (reduce waste), and Six Sigma (reduce variation); run/control charts track change over time","PATIENT SAFETY: errors are analyzed with root-cause analysis (reactive) and failure mode and effects analysis (proactive); the SWISS-CHEESE model shows how latent system flaws align to cause harm - a SYSTEMS, not blame, approach"],"tx":["Improve with iterative PDSA cycles - small tests of change measured over time - rather than large untested overhauls; use QI data (run/control charts) to distinguish real improvement from noise","Build safety through SYSTEM design (checklists, standardization, forcing functions, redundancy) and a JUST CULTURE that reports errors/near-misses without individual blame","Address never events and use root-cause analysis to fix underlying system causes"],"pearls":["Quality has six domains (STEEEP: Safe, Timely, Effective, Efficient, Equitable, Patient-centered) - safety is one of them","PDSA (Plan-Do-Study-Act) is the iterative QI engine - small, rapid, measured tests of change","Patient safety takes a SYSTEMS view (Swiss-cheese model, just culture) - most errors are system failures, not bad individuals","Donabedian: measure structure, process, and outcome - process measures often change fastest and are most actionable"],"refs":[{"t":"AHRQ: Quality Improvement & Patient Safety","u":"https://www.ahrq.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Quality Improvement / Patient Safety","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559239/"}]},{"id":"prev-health-informatics","name":"Health Informatics & Data for Population Health","sec":"prev","present":["Health informatics applies information technology and data to improve individual and POPULATION health; data systems underpin surveillance, quality measurement, and population-health management","Sources include electronic health records (EHRs), registries, claims, vital statistics, and public-health reporting systems; interoperability and data quality are central challenges","Board framing: know the roles of key data systems and population-health data uses"],"dx":["ELECTRONIC HEALTH RECORDS enable clinical decision support, quality measurement, and (aggregated) population-health analytics; REGISTRIES track defined populations (e.g., cancer, immunization) for surveillance and outcomes","Population-health data uses: surveillance (see that entry), identifying disparities and high-risk groups, risk stratification, and measuring/reporting quality; vital statistics (births/deaths) and administrative/claims data support epidemiology","Key issues: INTEROPERABILITY (systems exchanging usable data), data QUALITY/completeness, standardization (coding/terminologies), and PRIVACY/security (HIPAA)"],"tx":["Use informatics tools for population-health management: registries and dashboards to find care gaps, risk-stratify, target outreach, and measure quality/equity","Support clinical decision support and public-health reporting (electronic case reporting, immunization registries); prioritize interoperability and data quality","Protect PRIVACY and security (HIPAA) while enabling appropriate data use for public health"],"pearls":["Registries and EHR data enable population-health management - finding care gaps, risk-stratifying, and measuring quality and disparities","Interoperability (usable data exchange between systems) and data quality are the central, persistent challenges of health informatics","Vital statistics, claims, and surveillance systems are core population data sources for epidemiology","HIPAA governs privacy/security - informatics must balance data use for population health against protecting individuals"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Public Health Informatics & Data","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Health Informatics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553900/"}]},{"id":"prev-infectious-disease-control","name":"Infectious-Disease Control (Isolation, Contact Tracing, Herd Immunity)","sec":"prev","present":["Controlling communicable disease is a core public-health function combining case detection, interruption of transmission, and population immunity","Tools include ISOLATION and QUARANTINE, CONTACT TRACING, prophylaxis, vaccination, and infection control; the goal is to break the chain of transmission","Board framing: know the key control measures, herd immunity, and the reproductive number"],"dx":["The CHAIN OF INFECTION (agent -> reservoir -> portal of exit -> mode of transmission -> portal of entry -> susceptible host) identifies points to interrupt; understand modes (contact, droplet, airborne, vehicle, vector)","ISOLATION separates people who are ILL/infected; QUARANTINE restricts people who are EXPOSED but not yet ill (a key distinction); CONTACT TRACING finds and manages exposed contacts","The BASIC REPRODUCTIVE NUMBER (R0) = average secondary cases from one case in a fully susceptible population; the effective R can be pushed below 1 (ending spread) by immunity/control - the HERD IMMUNITY THRESHOLD rises with R0"],"tx":["Interrupt transmission with the appropriate measures: isolate cases, quarantine exposed contacts, trace and manage contacts (test/prophylax/vaccinate), and apply infection-control precautions by transmission mode","Achieve HERD (community) IMMUNITY through vaccination to protect those who cannot be vaccinated - the threshold is higher for more contagious diseases (high R0, e.g., measles needs very high coverage)","Combine measures with surveillance, source control, and, when available, post-exposure prophylaxis/vaccination"],"pearls":["Isolation is for the ILL/infected; quarantine is for the EXPOSED-but-not-yet-ill - a classic tested distinction","Herd immunity protects the unvaccinated once enough of the population is immune - and the threshold is HIGHER for more contagious diseases (higher R0)","Driving the effective reproductive number below 1 stops an outbreak - vaccination and control measures both do this","Contact tracing (find, notify, and manage exposed contacts) is a cornerstone of communicable-disease control"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Isolation, Quarantine & Disease Control","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Herd Immunity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459297/"}]},{"id":"prev-emergency-preparedness","name":"Emergency Preparedness & Disaster Response","sec":"prev","present":["Public-health emergencies (natural disasters, outbreaks/pandemics, bioterrorism, mass-casualty events) require pre-planned, coordinated response to protect populations","Preparedness spans the disaster cycle - mitigation, PREPAREDNESS, RESPONSE, and RECOVERY - and uses standardized command structures","Board framing: know the disaster cycle, the incident command system, and triage principles"],"dx":["The DISASTER CYCLE: MITIGATION (reduce risk before), PREPAREDNESS (plan/train/stockpile), RESPONSE (act during), and RECOVERY (restore after) - a continuous cycle","Response is organized by the INCIDENT COMMAND SYSTEM (ICS) within the National Incident Management System - a scalable, standardized command/coordination structure","Mass-casualty TRIAGE (e.g., START) sorts patients by severity to do the greatest good for the greatest number when resources are overwhelmed - a shift from usual individual-focused care to population-focused allocation"],"tx":["Prepare through hazard-vulnerability assessment, planning, training/exercises, stockpiling (e.g., Strategic National Stockpile), and surveillance for early detection","Respond via the incident command system with coordinated communication, mass-casualty triage, surge capacity, and protection of responders/public; address at-risk populations","Recover by restoring services and infrastructure and applying lessons learned; integrate mental-health support for affected communities and responders"],"pearls":["The disaster cycle is mitigation -> preparedness -> response -> recovery - a continuous loop, not a one-time event","The Incident Command System provides a scalable, standardized structure to coordinate multi-agency disaster response","Mass-casualty triage shifts the goal from the individual to the greatest good for the greatest number under scarce resources","Preparedness (planning, training, stockpiling, surveillance) before an event determines how well the response goes"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Emergency Preparedness & Response","u":"https://emergency.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Disaster Preparedness","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470511/"}]},{"id":"prev-food-safety","name":"Food Safety & Foodborne Illness","sec":"prev","present":["Foodborne illness is common and largely preventable; recognizing agents, exposures, and control measures protects individuals and populations","Causes include bacterial (Salmonella, Campylobacter, E. coli O157, Listeria, Clostridium species), viral (norovirus - the most common cause), and toxin-mediated illness; presentations and incubation periods help identify the agent","Board framing: know the high-yield agent-exposure pairings and prevention"],"dx":["Match presentation/incubation to the agent: rapid-onset vomiting (hours) suggests preformed TOXIN (Staph aureus, Bacillus cereus); watery diarrhea; bloody diarrhea (E. coli O157 - risk of HEMOLYTIC UREMIC SYNDROME, avoid antibiotics; Shigella, Campylobacter); NOROVIRUS (explosive vomiting/diarrhea, highly contagious outbreaks on cruise ships)","Special exposures: LISTERIA (deli meats/soft cheeses - dangerous in pregnancy/immunocompromised), Vibrio (raw shellfish), botulism (improperly canned foods - neurotoxin), Salmonella (poultry/eggs/reptiles)","Public-health reporting and OUTBREAK investigation link cases to a common source"],"tx":["Prevent with the core measures: CLEAN (hand/surface hygiene), SEPARATE (avoid cross-contamination), COOK to safe temperatures, and CHILL promptly; safe water and pasteurization are foundational","Manage most cases supportively (rehydration); use antibiotics selectively (and AVOID them in E. coli O157 to reduce HUS risk); report notifiable foodborne illnesses","Protect high-risk groups (pregnant, immunocompromised, young, elderly) from higher-risk foods (Listeria, raw items)"],"pearls":["Norovirus is the most common cause of foodborne illness (explosive, highly contagious outbreaks); rapid vomiting within hours suggests a preformed toxin (S. aureus/B. cereus)","E. coli O157:H7 causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome - do NOT give antibiotics (they raise HUS risk)","Listeria from deli meats/soft cheeses is especially dangerous in pregnancy and immunocompromise","Prevention is Clean, Separate, Cook, Chill - plus safe water and pasteurization"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Food Safety & Foodborne Illness","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Foodborne Illness","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459388/"}]},{"id":"prev-global-health","name":"Global Health & Disease Eradication","sec":"prev","present":["Global health addresses health issues that transcend national borders - infectious disease, maternal/child health, nutrition, non-communicable disease, and health-system strengthening - with a focus on equity","Major players include the WHO and global partnerships; landmark achievements include the ERADICATION of smallpox and near-eradication of polio","Board framing: know the control-elimination-eradication distinction and major global-health concepts"],"dx":["Define the terms precisely: CONTROL (reduced incidence/prevalence to an acceptable level through ongoing effort), ELIMINATION (reduction to zero incidence in a defined GEOGRAPHIC area - still requires ongoing measures), and ERADICATION (permanent reduction to zero worldwide, so interventions can STOP - achieved only for SMALLPOX in humans)","Eradication requires specific features: an effective intervention, a sensitive diagnostic, no significant nonhuman reservoir, and humans essential to the life cycle (why smallpox was eradicable and why polio/guinea worm are targets)","Global burden shifts: infectious disease and maternal/child mortality remain major in low-income settings, while non-communicable diseases rise globally (the epidemiologic transition)"],"tx":["Pursue control/elimination/eradication with matched strategies (vaccination campaigns, surveillance, vector control, sanitation) and health-system strengthening; the leading global-health interventions include immunization, sanitation/clean water, and nutrition","Address the epidemiologic transition (rising non-communicable disease) alongside persistent infectious/maternal-child challenges; prioritize equity and the social determinants","Coordinate through global bodies (WHO), the Sustainable Development Goals, and partnerships"],"pearls":["Control (acceptable low level) vs Elimination (zero in a defined area, effort continues) vs Eradication (zero worldwide, interventions can stop) - smallpox is the only human disease eradicated","Eradicability requires an effective tool, good diagnostics, no nonhuman reservoir, and humans essential to transmission - polio and guinea worm are current targets","Vaccination, clean water/sanitation, and nutrition are among the highest-impact global-health interventions","The epidemiologic transition means non-communicable diseases are rising globally, even as infectious and maternal-child burdens persist"],"refs":[{"t":"WHO: Global Health & Disease Eradication","u":"https://www.who.int"},{"t":"StatPearls: Global Health","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555520/"}]},{"id":"prev-public-health-ethics-law","name":"Public Health Ethics & Law","sec":"prev","present":["Public health often acts for the COMMUNITY'S benefit, sometimes in tension with INDIVIDUAL autonomy/liberty; ethics and law define when and how such action is justified","Core tensions: individual rights vs collective good (e.g., mandatory vaccination, quarantine, reporting), and the ethics of screening programs","Board framing: know the ethical principles, the legal basis for public-health powers, and their limits"],"dx":["Ethical principles (autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, JUSTICE) apply, but public-health ethics adds a population/collective-good lens and principles like the LEAST-RESTRICTIVE ALTERNATIVE and proportionality","Legal basis: government POLICE POWER (largely at the state level in the US) authorizes actions to protect public health - communicable-disease reporting, ISOLATION/QUARANTINE, and vaccination mandates - subject to due process and constitutional limits (the classic precedent upheld compulsory vaccination for the public good)","Screening ethics: weigh benefits vs harms (false positives, overdiagnosis), informed consent, equity of access, and the duty not to screen when you cannot offer follow-up/treatment"],"tx":["Use the LEAST-RESTRICTIVE effective measure that achieves the public-health goal (prefer voluntary measures/education before coercive ones), applied proportionately and with due process","Balance individual liberty against community protection transparently; ensure equity, avoid stigmatization, and protect confidentiality while meeting reporting duties","Apply screening ethically - only screen when benefits outweigh harms and follow-up/treatment can be provided (Wilson-Jungner)"],"pearls":["Public-health powers (reporting, isolation/quarantine, vaccination mandates) rest on government police power and are balanced against individual rights with due process","The least-restrictive-alternative principle: use the minimum coercion needed to achieve the public-health goal","Mandatory public-health measures for the collective good have been legally upheld, but they are bounded by proportionality and due process","Ethical screening requires that benefits outweigh harms AND that follow-up/treatment is available - do not screen what you cannot act on"],"refs":[{"t":"CDC: Public Health Ethics & Law","u":"https://www.cdc.gov"},{"t":"StatPearls: Public Health Ethics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535402/"}]},{"id":"psych-mdd","name":"Major Depressive Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A common, disabling mood disorder defined by discrete MAJOR DEPRESSIVE EPISODES; leading cause of disability worldwide with substantial suicide risk","DSM-5-TR: >=5 of 9 symptoms for >=2 weeks, including at least one of DEPRESSED MOOD or ANHEDONIA, plus SIGECAPS features (Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicidality), causing impairment and not due to another cause","Recurrent and often chronic; screen with the PHQ-9; assess for bipolarity (past mania/hypomania) before treating"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR criteria; rule out MEDICAL mimics (hypothyroidism, anemia, B12 deficiency, medication effects, substance use) and screen for BIPOLAR disorder (a prior manic/hypomanic episode changes management)","Assess severity and SAFETY (suicidal ideation/plan) with every patient; note specifiers (anxious distress, melancholic, atypical, psychotic, peripartum, seasonal)","Consider comorbid anxiety, substance use, and grief; PHQ-9 tracks severity/response"],"tx":["First-line: SSRIs/SNRIs (e.g., sertraline, escitalopram) and evidence-based PSYCHOTHERAPY (CBT, IPT); combination is often best for moderate-severe disease; allow ~4-6 weeks at adequate dose for response","Match/switch by response and side effects; augment or switch for partial response; treat to REMISSION and continue for >=6-12 months after first episode (longer for recurrent)","Severe/psychotic/treatment-resistant or acutely suicidal -> consider ECT and neurostimulation (see treatment-resistant entry); address safety and monitor for activation, especially early in treatment. Suicidality is a medical emergency - ensure safety planning and appropriate support."],"pearls":["Always screen for a past manic/hypomanic episode before starting an antidepressant - unrecognized bipolar depression can be destabilized","Depressed mood OR anhedonia is required, plus SIGECAPS features, for >=2 weeks","Antidepressants take ~4-6 weeks for full effect at an adequate dose - do not abandon a trial too early, but reassess safety and activation early","Continue treatment 6-12 months after a first episode to prevent relapse; recurrent illness needs longer maintenance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Major Depressive Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559078/"},{"t":"NIMH: Depression","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-persistent-depressive","name":"Persistent Depressive Disorder (Dysthymia)","sec":"psych","present":["A CHRONIC form of depression: depressed mood MORE DAYS THAN NOT for at least 2 YEARS in adults (1 year in children/adolescents), with fewer acute symptoms than a major episode but greater chronicity","DSM-5-TR consolidates chronic major depression and dysthymia; patients often describe feeling depressed 'as long as I can remember'","'DOUBLE DEPRESSION' occurs when a superimposed major depressive episode develops on top of persistent depressive disorder"],"dx":["Diagnose by chronicity: depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for >=2 years, with >=2 of (appetite change, sleep change, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration, hopelessness), never symptom-free for >2 months","Rule out medical/substance causes and bipolar disorder; distinguish from major depressive disorder by the chronic, lower-grade course","Assess for double depression and comorbid anxiety/substance use"],"tx":["Treat like depression - antidepressants (SSRIs/SNRIs) PLUS psychotherapy; chronic depression responds well to specific psychotherapies (e.g., CBASP) combined with medication","Because it is chronic, expect longer treatment courses and emphasize maintenance","Address the demoralization/hopelessness and functional impairment that accrue over years"],"pearls":["The defining feature is CHRONICITY (>=2 years in adults, >=1 year in youth), not symptom severity","Double depression = a major depressive episode superimposed on persistent depressive disorder","Combined medication plus psychotherapy is especially valuable in chronic depression","Patients may not recognize they are depressed because it has been their baseline for years - ask about lifetime course"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Persistent Depressive Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541052/"},{"t":"NIMH: Depression","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-bipolar-i","name":"Bipolar I Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Defined by at least one MANIC episode (depressive episodes are common but not required); a lifelong, highly heritable illness with substantial morbidity and suicide risk","MANIA: >=1 week (or any duration if hospitalization is needed) of abnormally elevated/expansive or IRRITABLE mood plus increased energy, with >=3 DIGFAST features (Distractibility, Impulsivity/Indiscretion, Grandiosity, Flight of ideas, Activity increase, decreased Sleep need, Talkativeness) causing marked impairment/psychosis","Onset typically in late adolescence/early adulthood; a first 'depressive' presentation may later declare as bipolar"],"dx":["Diagnose from a manic episode (DSM-5-TR); assess for psychosis (mood-congruent/incongruent) and for depressive episodes; rule out substance/medication-induced mania and medical causes (e.g., hyperthyroidism)","Screen every depressed patient for a HISTORY of mania/hypomania - antidepressant monotherapy can precipitate mania/rapid cycling","Assess safety, insight, and function; note mixed features and rapid cycling"],"tx":["Acute MANIA: mood stabilizer (LITHIUM or VALPROATE) and/or a second-generation ANTIPSYCHOTIC, often in combination for severe mania; ensure safety (mania is often a psychiatric emergency); benzodiazepines adjunctively for agitation/sleep","Bipolar DEPRESSION: agents with evidence (quetiapine, lurasidone, lamotrigine, lithium, cariprazine) - AVOID antidepressant MONOTHERAPY (risk of switch/cycling)","MAINTENANCE: continue a mood stabilizer/antipsychotic long-term (lithium reduces suicide risk); psychoeducation and adherence support"],"pearls":["One manic episode = bipolar I - depressive episodes are common but not required for the diagnosis","DIGFAST captures the manic symptom cluster; mania lasting >=1 week (or needing hospitalization) with impairment/psychosis","Never treat bipolar depression with antidepressant monotherapy - it can trigger a manic switch or rapid cycling","Lithium is uniquely anti-suicidal in bipolar disorder - a key reason to consider it for maintenance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bipolar Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558998/"},{"t":"NIMH: Bipolar Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-bipolar-ii-cyclothymia","name":"Bipolar II Disorder & Cyclothymia","sec":"psych","present":["BIPOLAR II: at least one HYPOMANIC episode AND at least one MAJOR DEPRESSIVE episode, with NO full manic episode - the depressive episodes usually dominate the clinical burden","HYPOMANIA: >=4 days of elevated/irritable mood + increased energy with DIGFAST features that are OBSERVABLE but NOT severe enough to cause marked impairment, hospitalization, or psychosis (those would make it mania -> bipolar I)","CYCLOTHYMIA: >=2 years (1 in youth) of fluctuating subthreshold hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria"],"dx":["Diagnose bipolar II from a hypomanic PLUS a major depressive episode; carefully distinguish hypomania (no marked impairment/psychosis/hospitalization) from mania - the distinction defines II vs I","Bipolar II is frequently MISDIAGNOSED as unipolar depression because patients present when depressed and may not report hypomania as a problem - actively ask about past high-energy periods","Cyclothymia: chronic subthreshold mood instability; rule out substance/medical causes"],"tx":["Bipolar II DEPRESSION (the main burden): quetiapine, lithium, lamotrigine, and other evidence-based agents; use antidepressants cautiously (if at all) and generally with a mood stabilizer","MAINTENANCE mood stabilization (lithium, lamotrigine, quetiapine) and psychoeducation; treat hypomania and prevent progression/cycling","Cyclothymia: mood stabilizers and psychotherapy; monitor for progression to bipolar I/II"],"pearls":["Bipolar II = hypomania + major depression, never a full manic episode (that would be bipolar I)","Hypomania is observable but does NOT cause marked impairment, hospitalization, or psychosis - that threshold separates it from mania","Bipolar II is commonly mistaken for unipolar depression - ask specifically about past hypomanic periods in any depressed patient","Cyclothymia is chronic (>=2 years) subthreshold mood swings and can progress to bipolar I/II"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bipolar II Disorder / Cyclothymia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587350/"},{"t":"NIMH: Bipolar Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-perinatal-pmdd","name":"Perinatal & Premenstrual Mood Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["Reproductive-related mood disorders. The PERINATAL spectrum: transient 'BABY BLUES' (mild, self-limited, resolving within ~2 weeks) vs POSTPARTUM DEPRESSION (persistent, impairing, often within the first months) vs POSTPARTUM PSYCHOSIS (a psychiatric EMERGENCY, often bipolar-related)","PREMENSTRUAL DYSPHORIC DISORDER (PMDD): severe mood/irritability, affective lability, and physical symptoms in the LUTEAL phase that remit shortly after menses onset, causing significant impairment (more severe than PMS)","Perinatal mood/anxiety disorders are the most common complication of childbearing and a leading cause of maternal mortality (suicide)"],"dx":["Screen perinatally with a validated tool (Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale) and assess SAFETY (self-harm, thoughts of harming the infant); postpartum psychosis (rapid confusion, delusions, mood lability) is an emergency requiring immediate psychiatric care","PMDD: confirm the LUTEAL-phase timing with prospective symptom tracking across >=2 cycles (symptoms must remit after menses); rule out an underlying mood disorder that merely worsens premenstrually","Screen for bipolar disorder before starting an antidepressant, especially postpartum"],"tx":["Postpartum depression: psychotherapy for mild-moderate and SSRIs (sertraline is often first-line and breastfeeding-compatible) for moderate-severe; neuroactive-steroid options (brexanolone IV, zuranolone oral) target postpartum depression specifically","POSTPARTUM PSYCHOSIS: psychiatric EMERGENCY - hospitalize, ensure safety of mother and infant, and treat (antipsychotic/mood stabilizer, often ECT for severe cases)","PMDD: SSRIs (continuous or luteal-phase dosing) are first-line; combined hormonal contraceptives and lifestyle measures also help. Perinatal distress and any thoughts of self-harm or harming the infant warrant urgent professional support."],"pearls":["Baby blues resolve within ~2 weeks; persistent impairing symptoms are postpartum depression and need treatment","Postpartum psychosis is a psychiatric emergency (infanticide/suicide risk) - it is often bipolar-spectrum and needs immediate admission","PMDD requires luteal-phase timing with remission after menses - confirm prospectively; SSRIs are first-line and can be dosed only in the luteal phase","Screen for bipolar disorder before prescribing an antidepressant in the perinatal period"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Postpartum Depression / PMDD","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519070/"},{"t":"NIMH: Perinatal Depression","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-dmdd","name":"Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A childhood mood disorder characterized by CHRONIC, SEVERE IRRITABILITY and frequent TEMPER OUTBURSTS grossly out of proportion to the situation - created in DSM-5 to reduce overdiagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder","Severe recurrent temper outbursts (verbal/behavioral) >=3 times/week, with a persistently irritable/angry mood BETWEEN outbursts, present in >=2 settings","Onset before age 10; diagnosed between ages 6 and 18 (not before 6 or first after 18)"],"dx":["DSM-5-TR: chronic severe irritability with frequent outbursts for >=12 months across settings, onset before age 10; the mood between outbursts is persistently irritable, NOT episodic","Distinguish from BIPOLAR disorder (which is EPISODIC with distinct manic/hypomanic periods - DMDD irritability is chronic/non-episodic) and from oppositional defiant disorder (DMDD has more severe mood dysregulation; a child cannot be diagnosed with both - DMDD takes precedence)","Assess for comorbid ADHD, anxiety, and depression (common); rule out other causes of irritability"],"tx":["Limited specific evidence; management targets the irritability and comorbidities: psychosocial/behavioral interventions (parent training) and treatment of comorbid ADHD (stimulants) or depression/anxiety (SSRIs)","Avoid over-reliance on antipsychotics; use a measured, comorbidity-focused approach","Family-based interventions and school coordination are central"],"pearls":["DMDD is CHRONIC, non-episodic irritability - the opposite of the episodic mood shifts of bipolar disorder (it was designed to curb pediatric bipolar overdiagnosis)","Diagnose only between ages 6 and 18, with onset before age 10","DMDD trumps oppositional defiant disorder - you cannot diagnose both; DMDD reflects more pervasive mood dysregulation","Many children have comorbid ADHD/anxiety/depression - treat those, as DMDD lacks a specific drug"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551572/"},{"t":"NIMH: Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-treatment-resistant-depression","name":"Treatment-Resistant Depression & Neurostimulation","sec":"psych","present":["Treatment-resistant depression (TRD) is commonly defined as failure to respond to >=2 adequate antidepressant trials (adequate dose and duration) in a current episode","Before labeling TRD, confirm the DIAGNOSIS, ADHERENCE, adequate dose/duration, and rule out bipolarity and contributing medical/substance factors ('pseudoresistance')","Includes the severely ill, psychotic, catatonic, and acutely suicidal, where rapid, definitive treatment matters"],"dx":["Verify adequate prior trials (dose and >=4-6 weeks each) and adherence; reassess the diagnosis (missed bipolar disorder, undertreated anxiety/substance use, medical mimics like hypothyroidism)","Assess severity, psychosis, catatonia, and SUICIDE risk - these influence urgency and modality selection","Identify candidates for neurostimulation and newer rapid-acting agents"],"tx":["Pharmacologic strategies: OPTIMIZE, SWITCH (within/between classes), COMBINE antidepressants, or AUGMENT (lithium, second-generation antipsychotics, thyroid hormone)","NEUROSTIMULATION: ELECTROCONVULSIVE THERAPY (ECT) is the most effective treatment for severe, psychotic, catatonic, or acutely suicidal depression (rapid, highly effective); repetitive TRANSCRANIAL MAGNETIC STIMULATION (rTMS) is noninvasive for TRD; vagus nerve stimulation in refractory cases","Rapid-acting: intranasal ESKETAMINE / IV ketamine for TRD (rapid antidepressant and anti-suicidal effect, monitored administration). Acute suicidality is an emergency requiring immediate safety planning and support."],"pearls":["Rule out 'pseudoresistance' first - wrong diagnosis (bipolar), nonadherence, inadequate dose/duration, or untreated comorbidity","ECT is the MOST effective treatment for severe/psychotic/catatonic depression and for acute high suicide risk - not a last resort in those settings","Augmentation (lithium, atypical antipsychotics, thyroid) is a key strategy after partial response","Ketamine/esketamine act rapidly (hours-days) and reduce suicidality - a shift from the weeks antidepressants require"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Treatment-Resistant Depression","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585107/"},{"t":"NIMH: Brain Stimulation Therapies","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-gad","name":"Generalized Anxiety Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Excessive, difficult-to-control WORRY about multiple domains (health, finances, work, family) occurring more days than not for >=6 MONTHS, with physical and cognitive symptoms","DSM-5-TR requires >=3 of: restlessness, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance (only 1 needed in children), causing impairment","Common, chronic, frequently comorbid with depression and other anxiety disorders; often presents with somatic complaints"],"dx":["Diagnose by the pattern of pervasive, uncontrollable worry >=6 months with associated symptoms; screen with the GAD-7","Rule out medical mimics (HYPERTHYROIDISM, arrhythmia, caffeine/stimulant or substance use, medication effects) and other anxiety/mood disorders","Assess comorbid depression and substance use"],"tx":["First-line: SSRIs/SNRIs (e.g., escitalopram, venlafaxine, duloxetine) and CBT; buspirone is an option; combined medication plus CBT is effective","Avoid routine/long-term BENZODIAZEPINES (dependence, cognitive/fall risk) - reserve for short-term/bridging use; SSRIs take weeks to work","Address sleep, caffeine, and comorbidities; set expectations about the gradual onset of medication effect"],"pearls":["The core is uncontrollable worry across MULTIPLE domains for >=6 months - not tied to one specific trigger","Always check thyroid function and substance/caffeine use - they mimic GAD","SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT are first-line; benzodiazepines are not a long-term solution (dependence)","GAD-7 helps screen and track severity; comorbid depression is common"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Generalized Anxiety Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441870/"},{"t":"NIMH: Anxiety Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-panic-agoraphobia","name":"Panic Disorder & Agoraphobia","sec":"psych","present":["PANIC ATTACKS are abrupt surges of intense fear peaking within MINUTES with somatic symptoms (palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea, dizziness, paresthesias, derealization) and fear of dying/losing control","PANIC DISORDER: recurrent UNEXPECTED panic attacks PLUS >=1 month of anticipatory worry about attacks or maladaptive behavior change to avoid them","AGORAPHOBIA: marked fear/avoidance of >=2 situations (public transit, open/enclosed spaces, crowds, being outside alone) where escape/help feels difficult - often develops secondary to panic"],"dx":["Diagnose panic disorder from recurrent unexpected attacks plus persistent concern/behavior change; agoraphobia is a separate diagnosis defined by the situational fear/avoidance","Panic attacks mimic and must be distinguished from MEDICAL emergencies (acute coronary syndrome, arrhythmia, PE, hyperthyroidism, pheochromocytoma) and substance effects - especially at first presentation","Assess for comorbid depression and substance use; recurrent ED visits are common"],"tx":["First-line: SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT (with interoceptive exposure); CBT is highly effective and durable for panic","Benzodiazepines can abort acute attacks but are avoided as long-term therapy (dependence, and they can interfere with exposure); start SSRIs low (initial jitteriness can mimic panic)","Treat agoraphobia with exposure-based CBT plus pharmacotherapy; educate that panic attacks, though terrifying, are not dangerous"],"pearls":["Panic disorder requires UNEXPECTED attacks plus a month of anticipatory worry/avoidance - a single attack is not the disorder","At first presentation, exclude cardiac/pulmonary/endocrine mimics - but avoid endless workups once panic disorder is established","Start SSRIs at a LOW dose in panic disorder - initial activation can be mistaken for worsening panic","CBT with interoceptive exposure is first-line and durable; benzodiazepines are a short-term bridge at best"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Panic Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430973/"},{"t":"NIMH: Panic Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-social-anxiety","name":"Social Anxiety Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Marked, persistent FEAR of social or performance situations where the person may be SCRUTINIZED or negatively evaluated (speaking, eating, meeting people), with fear of embarrassment/humiliation","The situations are avoided or endured with intense distress, the fear is out of proportion, lasts >=6 months, and causes impairment; a PERFORMANCE-ONLY subtype exists (e.g., public speaking)","Typically begins in adolescence; commonly comorbid with depression and substance use (self-medication)"],"dx":["Diagnose from the fear of negative evaluation in social/performance situations with avoidance and impairment for >=6 months; distinguish from panic disorder (unexpected attacks), agoraphobia (fear of escape difficulty), and normal shyness","Assess the performance-only subtype and comorbid depression/alcohol use","Rule out avoidance better explained by another condition (e.g., autism, body dysmorphic disorder)"],"tx":["First-line: SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT (with social exposure); combined treatment is effective and durable","For PERFORMANCE-ONLY anxiety (e.g., stage fright), a BETA-BLOCKER (propranolol) or a benzodiazepine taken before the event can control autonomic symptoms","Avoid long-term benzodiazepines/alcohol reliance; encourage graded exposure"],"pearls":["The core fear is negative evaluation/scrutiny by others - not the situation itself but being judged","Performance-only (e.g., public speaking) social anxiety can be treated situationally with a beta-blocker (propranolol) beforehand","SSRIs/SNRIs plus exposure-based CBT are first-line for generalized social anxiety","Watch for alcohol misuse as self-medication - a common and important comorbidity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Social Anxiety Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555890/"},{"t":"NIMH: Social Anxiety Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-specific-phobia","name":"Specific Phobia","sec":"psych","present":["Marked, persistent FEAR of a SPECIFIC object or situation (animals, heights, flying, blood-injection-injury, enclosed spaces) that is out of proportion to actual danger","The phobic stimulus almost always provokes immediate anxiety and is actively avoided or endured with intense distress, lasting >=6 months with impairment","The BLOOD-INJECTION-INJURY subtype is distinctive - it can cause a VASOVAGAL fainting response (unlike other phobias, which raise heart rate/BP)"],"dx":["Diagnose from the circumscribed fear/avoidance of a specific stimulus with disproportionate anxiety and impairment for >=6 months; specify the type (animal, natural environment, blood-injection-injury, situational)","Distinguish from panic disorder (unexpected attacks), agoraphobia, and social anxiety (fear of scrutiny)","Note the blood-injection-injury type's vasovagal fainting tendency"],"tx":["EXPOSURE THERAPY (systematic desensitization/graded in-vivo exposure) is the first-line and highly effective treatment - the mainstay","Medications play a limited role; short-acting anxiolytics only for rare, unavoidable exposures (e.g., a one-time flight)","For blood-injection-injury phobia, teach APPLIED TENSION (tensing muscles to raise blood pressure) to prevent fainting during exposure"],"pearls":["Exposure therapy is the definitive, first-line treatment - medications are largely unhelpful for specific phobia","Blood-injection-injury phobia uniquely causes vasovagal SYNCOPE - manage with applied tension, not just exposure","The fear is circumscribed to a specific stimulus - distinguishing it from the broader anxiety disorders","Diagnosis requires >=6 months of disproportionate, impairing fear/avoidance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Specific Phobia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546626/"},{"t":"NIMH: Anxiety Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-separation-anxiety-mutism","name":"Separation Anxiety Disorder & Selective Mutism","sec":"psych","present":["SEPARATION ANXIETY DISORDER: developmentally excessive fear about SEPARATION from attachment figures - distress on separation, worry about losing them, refusal to be alone or to go to school/sleep away, nightmares, and somatic complaints","Though classically pediatric, it can occur in ADULTS; a leading contributor to SCHOOL REFUSAL in children","SELECTIVE MUTISM: consistent failure to speak in specific social situations (e.g., school) despite speaking normally in others (e.g., home), interfering with function - conceptualized as an anxiety disorder"],"dx":["Separation anxiety: symptoms >=4 weeks in children/adolescents (>=6 months typically in adults) with impairment; assess for school refusal and somatic presentations","Selective mutism: failure to speak in select settings for >=1 month (not limited to the first month of school), not due to a language/communication disorder or lack of knowledge of the language","Screen for comorbid anxiety disorders (very common) and rule out autism/language disorders"],"tx":["Both: CBT (child-focused, often family-involved) is first-line; for selective mutism, behavioral techniques and gradual exposure to speaking situations, with school collaboration","SSRIs are used for moderate-severe or refractory cases (both conditions)","Parent guidance to reduce accommodation and support graded separation/speaking"],"pearls":["Separation anxiety disorder is a major cause of school refusal - and is no longer considered pediatric-only (it occurs in adults)","Selective mutism is an ANXIETY disorder: the child CAN speak (does so at home) but fails to in specific settings like school","CBT/behavioral therapy with school and family involvement is first-line; SSRIs for more severe cases","Rule out a language/communication disorder or autism before diagnosing selective mutism"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Separation Anxiety / Selective Mutism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560793/"},{"t":"NIMH: Anxiety Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-ocd","name":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Characterized by OBSESSIONS (recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts/urges/images causing anxiety) and/or COMPULSIONS (repetitive behaviors or mental acts performed to neutralize the obsession or prevent a feared event)","Common themes: contamination/washing, checking, symmetry/ordering, and taboo/aggressive/sexual intrusive thoughts; the person typically recognizes the thoughts as excessive (insight varies)","Time-consuming (>1 hour/day) or impairing; onset often in adolescence/early adulthood"],"dx":["Diagnose from obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming or impairing; specify insight level; screen for the tic-related subtype","Distinguish from GAD (real-life worries vs irrational obsessions), OCPD (a personality style without true obsessions/compulsions), and psychosis (OCD patients usually retain insight)","Assess comorbid depression, tics, and, in youth, consider PANDAS/PANS in abrupt-onset cases"],"tx":["First-line: CBT with EXPOSURE AND RESPONSE PREVENTION (ERP - the most effective psychotherapy) and SSRIs, which for OCD often require HIGHER doses and LONGER trials (up to ~8-12 weeks) than for depression","Augment partial responders with antipsychotics; clomipramine (a TCA) is effective but has more side effects","Severe/refractory cases: intensive ERP, and rarely neurosurgical/neuromodulation options"],"pearls":["Exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the single most effective therapy - not generic supportive therapy","OCD often needs HIGHER SSRI doses and LONGER trials (8-12 weeks) than depression to respond","Distinguish OCD (ego-dystonic obsessions/compulsions, usually with insight) from OCPD (an ego-syntonic perfectionistic personality style)","Comorbid tics/Tourette suggest the tic-related subtype and may respond to antipsychotic augmentation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553162/"},{"t":"NIMH: Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-bdd-hoarding-bfrb","name":"Body Dysmorphic, Hoarding & Body-Focused Repetitive Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["The OCD-related disorders. BODY DYSMORPHIC DISORDER (BDD): preoccupation with perceived DEFECTS in appearance that are minimal/unobservable to others, with repetitive behaviors (mirror-checking, grooming, reassurance-seeking); high risk of depression and suicide","HOARDING DISORDER: persistent difficulty discarding possessions due to a perceived need to save them, causing clutter that impairs use of living spaces and function","BODY-FOCUSED REPETITIVE BEHAVIORS: TRICHOTILLOMANIA (recurrent hair-pulling) and EXCORIATION (skin-picking) disorder - repetitive self-grooming causing damage, with repeated attempts to stop"],"dx":["BDD: preoccupation with a nonexistent/slight appearance flaw plus repetitive behaviors; distinguish from an eating disorder (which centers on weight/shape) and normal appearance concern; screen for suicidality; note patients often seek COSMETIC procedures (which rarely help)","Hoarding: distinguish from normal collecting and from OCD (hoarding is ego-syntonic, no obsessions driving it); assess safety (fire, falls, sanitation)","Trichotillomania/excoriation: recurrent pulling/picking with attempts to stop and resulting damage; rule out dermatologic causes"],"tx":["BDD: SSRIs (often higher doses) and CBT tailored for BDD; AVOID cosmetic/dermatologic/surgical procedures (they do not resolve the preoccupation and can worsen it)","Hoarding: CBT specifically for hoarding (skills for sorting/discarding, cognitive work); SSRIs have modest benefit","Trichotillomania/excoriation: HABIT REVERSAL TRAINING (the first-line behavioral therapy); N-acetylcysteine and SSRIs are sometimes tried"],"pearls":["In BDD, patients seek cosmetic procedures that do NOT help - recognize it and treat with SSRIs + CBT instead of referring for surgery; screen for suicide risk","Hoarding disorder is distinct from OCD - it is ego-syntonic with no driving obsessions, and needs hoarding-specific CBT","Habit reversal training is first-line for trichotillomania and excoriation (skin-picking) disorder","These OCD-related disorders respond to SSRIs and targeted behavioral therapies, not generic reassurance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Body Dysmorphic Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555901/"},{"t":"NIMH: Obsessive-Compulsive & Related Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-ptsd","name":"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Develops after exposure to actual/threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence (directly, witnessed, or learned of in a close other), with symptoms in four clusters lasting >1 MONTH","CLUSTERS: INTRUSION (flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive memories), AVOIDANCE (of trauma reminders), negative alterations in COGNITION/MOOD (numbing, guilt, detachment), and AROUSAL/reactivity (hypervigilance, startle, irritability, sleep/concentration problems)","Symptoms <1 month with similar features = ACUTE STRESS DISORDER; a dissociative subtype exists"],"dx":["Diagnose from the trauma exposure plus the four symptom clusters for >1 month with impairment; symptoms lasting 3 days to 1 month are acute stress disorder","Screen for comorbid depression, substance use, TBI, and suicidality; distinguish from adjustment disorder (non-traumatic stressor) and primary mood/anxiety disorders","Assess the dissociative subtype (depersonalization/derealization)"],"tx":["First-line: TRAUMA-FOCUSED PSYCHOTHERAPY - cognitive processing therapy, prolonged exposure, and EMDR are the most effective treatments","Pharmacotherapy: SSRIs/SNRIs (sertraline, paroxetine, venlafaxine) are first-line medications; PRAZOSIN specifically targets trauma-related NIGHTMARES","AVOID benzodiazepines (ineffective for core PTSD and risky with comorbid substance use); treat comorbidities and ensure safety"],"pearls":["Four clusters - intrusion, avoidance, negative cognition/mood, arousal - for >1 month; <1 month is acute stress disorder","Trauma-focused psychotherapy (CPT, prolonged exposure, EMDR) is first-line and more definitive than medication","Prazosin is the go-to for PTSD-associated nightmares","Avoid benzodiazepines in PTSD - they do not treat the core disorder and carry dependence/substance-use risk"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559129/"},{"t":"NIMH: Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-acute-stress-adjustment","name":"Acute Stress & Adjustment Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["ACUTE STRESS DISORDER: PTSD-like symptoms (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal) beginning after a traumatic event and lasting 3 DAYS to 1 MONTH; if symptoms persist beyond a month, the diagnosis becomes PTSD","ADJUSTMENT DISORDER: emotional/behavioral symptoms (depressed mood, anxiety, conduct disturbance) out of proportion to an identifiable, NON-traumatic STRESSOR (job loss, divorce, illness), beginning within 3 months and resolving within 6 months of the stressor ending","Adjustment disorder is a common, less severe 'stress-response' diagnosis - a residual category when full criteria for another disorder are not met"],"dx":["Acute stress disorder: trauma exposure plus symptoms for 3 days to 1 month; adjustment disorder: a clear (usually non-traumatic) stressor with disproportionate symptoms not meeting criteria for another disorder","Distinguish adjustment disorder from normal stress reactions (impairment/excess distress required), major depression, and PTSD (traumatic stressor + full criteria)","Assess SUICIDE risk (adjustment disorder still carries suicide risk) and specify the subtype (with depressed mood, anxiety, mixed, or conduct disturbance)"],"tx":["Both are often self-limited; PSYCHOTHERAPY (supportive, problem-solving, CBT) is first-line, with attention to coping and the stressor","Acute stress disorder: trauma-focused CBT can reduce progression to PTSD; avoid routine benzodiazepines","Adjustment disorder: brief psychotherapy and support; medications only for specific persistent symptoms; reassess if it evolves into another disorder"],"pearls":["Acute stress disorder is the 3-day-to-1-month window after trauma; beyond 1 month it becomes PTSD","Adjustment disorder follows an identifiable stressor, begins within 3 months, and resolves within 6 months of the stressor's end","Adjustment disorder is a diagnosis of exclusion (symptoms do not meet criteria for another disorder) - but still assess suicide risk","Psychotherapy/support is first-line for both; avoid reflexive long-term medication"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Stress Disorder / Adjustment Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560815/"},{"t":"NIMH: Coping With Traumatic Events","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-schizophrenia","name":"Schizophrenia","sec":"psych","present":["A chronic psychotic disorder with POSITIVE symptoms (delusions, hallucinations - classically auditory, disorganized speech/behavior), NEGATIVE symptoms (flat affect, avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality), and cognitive impairment","DSM-5-TR: >=2 of (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized/catatonic behavior, negative symptoms) - at least one being a positive core symptom - for a significant portion of 1 month, with continuous signs for >=6 MONTHS","Onset typically late teens-20s (earlier in men); negative and cognitive symptoms drive long-term disability"],"dx":["Diagnose by criteria with >=6 months of continuous disturbance; RULE OUT mood disorder with psychosis, schizoaffective disorder, substance-induced psychosis, and medical/neurologic causes (a first psychotic episode needs a medical workup)","Distinguish by duration: <1 month = brief psychotic disorder; 1-6 months = schizophreniform; >6 months = schizophrenia","Assess safety, insight, substance use, and suicide risk (elevated in schizophrenia)"],"tx":["ANTIPSYCHOTICS are the mainstay (second-generation commonly first-line by tolerability); target positive symptoms and relapse prevention; negative/cognitive symptoms respond less well","CLOZAPINE for TREATMENT-RESISTANT schizophrenia (>=2 failed antipsychotic trials) and for reducing suicidality - requires ANC monitoring for agranulocytosis; LONG-ACTING INJECTABLES aid adherence","Combine with psychosocial treatment (assertive community treatment, supported employment, family psychoeducation, CBT for psychosis)"],"pearls":["Duration defines the psychotic disorders: <1 month brief psychotic, 1-6 months schizophreniform, >6 months schizophrenia","Negative and cognitive symptoms (not the hallucinations) cause most long-term disability and respond poorly to medication","Clozapine is uniquely effective for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reduces suicide - but requires ANC monitoring for agranulocytosis","Always work up and exclude substance-induced and medical causes at a first psychotic episode"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Schizophrenia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539864/"},{"t":"NIMH: Schizophrenia","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-schizoaffective","name":"Schizoaffective Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A disorder combining features of schizophrenia and a mood disorder: a MAJOR MOOD EPISODE (depressive or manic) concurrent with the criterion-A symptoms of schizophrenia","The defining requirement: delusions or hallucinations for >=2 WEEKS in the ABSENCE of a major mood episode at some point (this separates it from a mood disorder with psychotic features)","Mood symptoms are present for the MAJORITY of the total illness duration (this separates it from schizophrenia, where mood symptoms are brief if present)"],"dx":["Diagnose when psychotic and mood syndromes co-occur but psychosis ALSO occurs independently for >=2 weeks without mood symptoms; specify bipolar or depressive type","Differentiate from schizophrenia (mood episodes brief/absent), major depression/bipolar WITH psychotic features (psychosis occurs ONLY during mood episodes), and substance/medical causes","Assess longitudinal course to establish the mood-vs-psychosis timeline"],"tx":["Combine ANTIPSYCHOTICS with mood treatment: mood stabilizers/lithium for the bipolar type, antidepressants for the depressive type (with antipsychotic coverage)","Long-term antipsychotic maintenance plus mood management; consider clozapine for refractory cases and ECT for severe mood/psychotic presentations","Psychosocial support as in schizophrenia"],"pearls":["The key is psychosis WITHOUT mood symptoms for >=2 weeks (distinguishes it from psychotic depression/bipolar), while mood symptoms occupy the MAJORITY of the illness (distinguishes it from schizophrenia)","If psychosis occurs ONLY during mood episodes, it is a mood disorder with psychotic features - not schizoaffective disorder","Specify bipolar vs depressive type - it guides whether to add a mood stabilizer or antidepressant","Prognosis is generally intermediate between schizophrenia and mood disorders"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Schizoaffective Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541012/"},{"t":"NIMH: Schizophrenia","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-brief-psychotic-schizophreniform","name":"Brief Psychotic & Schizophreniform Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Shorter-duration psychotic disorders on a continuum with schizophrenia, separated by DURATION. BRIEF PSYCHOTIC DISORDER: >=1 psychotic symptom lasting >=1 DAY but <1 MONTH, with full return to premorbid function","SCHIZOPHRENIFORM DISORDER: the symptom picture of schizophrenia lasting 1 to 6 MONTHS (if it persists beyond 6 months, the diagnosis converts to schizophrenia)","Brief psychotic disorder may be triggered by a marked STRESSOR (including postpartum onset)"],"dx":["Diagnose by duration: brief psychotic disorder (1 day-1 month, full recovery) and schizophreniform (1-6 months); both require ruling out mood, substance-induced, and medical causes","Schizophreniform is often 'provisional' until duration/outcome is known - many progress to schizophrenia, some recover","Assess stressors (brief psychotic), postpartum onset, and safety"],"tx":["Antipsychotics for the acute episode; ensure safety and monitor course; brief psychotic disorder often resolves with short-term treatment and support","Schizophreniform: antipsychotic treatment with close follow-up; if symptoms exceed 6 months, reclassify and manage as schizophrenia","Provide psychoeducation; taper cautiously in true brief psychotic disorder after recovery"],"pearls":["Duration is everything: brief psychotic <1 month (with full recovery), schizophreniform 1-6 months, schizophrenia >6 months","Brief psychotic disorder can be triggered by a major stressor and can have a postpartum onset","Schizophreniform is provisional - a substantial proportion progresses to schizophrenia","All short psychotic presentations still require exclusion of substance-induced and medical causes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Brief Psychotic Disorder / Schizophreniform","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539900/"},{"t":"NIMH: Psychosis / Schizophrenia","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-delusional-disorder","name":"Delusional Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["Characterized by >=1 DELUSION for >=1 MONTH, WITHOUT other prominent psychotic symptoms - function is otherwise relatively PRESERVED and behavior is not obviously bizarre apart from the delusion's impact","Delusions are often NON-BIZARRE (plausible-sounding: being followed, deceived, loved, or having a disease); subtypes include persecutory, jealous, erotomanic, grandiose, and somatic","Contrasts with schizophrenia (which has hallucinations, disorganization, negative symptoms, and functional decline)"],"dx":["Diagnose from a persistent delusion (>=1 month) with otherwise intact functioning and NO prominent hallucinations/disorganization/negative symptoms; specify the subtype","Rule out schizophrenia, mood disorders with psychosis, substance/medical causes, and body dysmorphic/OCD (which can appear delusional)","These patients rarely present voluntarily and often have poor insight; a careful, non-confrontational history is key"],"tx":["Antipsychotics are used but response is often limited; building a therapeutic ALLIANCE is critical (patients are often mistrustful and reluctant)","Treat comorbid depression/anxiety; the somatic subtype may present to other specialties (dermatology, etc.)","Avoid directly challenging the delusion; focus on distress and function"],"pearls":["Delusional disorder features a single (often non-bizarre) delusion with otherwise preserved functioning - unlike schizophrenia","Subtypes: persecutory, jealous, erotomanic (belief another is in love with them), grandiose, and somatic","Antipsychotic response is often poor - the therapeutic alliance matters as much as medication","These patients rarely seek care and have limited insight - avoid confronting the delusion head-on"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Delusional Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539855/"},{"t":"NIMH: Psychotic Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-first-episode-psychosis","name":"First-Episode Psychosis & the Prodrome","sec":"psych","present":["The first episode of frank psychosis is often preceded by a PRODROME of attenuated symptoms (social withdrawal, declining function, odd beliefs/perceptual changes, suspiciousness) - a 'clinical high-risk' state","Early identification matters: a longer DURATION OF UNTREATED PSYCHOSIS is associated with worse outcomes, so prompt evaluation and treatment are priorities","A first psychotic episode is a broad differential, not automatically schizophrenia - it requires a thorough medical and substance workup"],"dx":["Work up a first episode thoroughly: history/exam, substance screen (cannabis, stimulants, hallucinogens), and evaluation for MEDICAL/NEUROLOGIC causes (autoimmune/anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis, CNS infection, metabolic, temporal lobe epilepsy, delirium) - imaging/labs as indicated","Differentiate primary psychotic disorders (by duration - brief psychotic/schizophreniform/schizophrenia), mood disorders with psychosis, and secondary causes","Assess the prodrome/clinical-high-risk state and safety"],"tx":["COORDINATED SPECIALTY CARE (team-based: low-dose antipsychotic, psychotherapy/CBT, family education, supported education/employment, case management) improves first-episode outcomes and is the standard of care","Start antipsychotics at LOW doses (first-episode patients are more responsive and more side-effect sensitive); minimize duration of untreated psychosis","For clinical-high-risk (prodromal) patients, monitor closely and treat comorbid depression/anxiety; avoid premature antipsychotic commitment unless indicated"],"pearls":["A longer duration of untreated psychosis predicts worse outcomes - early detection and treatment matter","Coordinated specialty care (medication + therapy + family + vocational support) is the evidence-based standard for first-episode psychosis","Always exclude substance-induced and medical/neurologic causes (e.g., anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis) at a first episode","Start antipsychotics low in first-episode patients - they respond to lower doses and are more sensitive to side effects"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: First-Episode Psychosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544365/"},{"t":"NIMH: First Episode Psychosis / RAISE","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-alcohol-use-disorder","name":"Alcohol Use Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A problematic pattern of alcohol use causing impairment/distress, defined by DSM-5-TR criteria (impaired control, social impairment, risky use, and pharmacologic tolerance/withdrawal); severity by criterion count","A leading preventable cause of morbidity/mortality (liver disease, trauma, cancers) frequently comorbid with mood/anxiety disorders","Screen with AUDIT-C or a single-item screen; ask about quantity/frequency nonjudgmentally"],"dx":["Diagnose by >=2 of 11 DSM-5-TR criteria over 12 months (mild 2-3, moderate 4-5, severe >=6); screen and assess withdrawal risk and medical complications","Check for comorbid psychiatric disorders, other substance use, and complications (liver disease, thiamine status); use biomarkers as adjuncts","Assess readiness to change and prior withdrawal history (predicts severity)"],"tx":["Combine PSYCHOSOCIAL treatment (motivational interviewing, CBT, 12-step facilitation, mutual-help groups) with MEDICATIONS for alcohol use disorder","FDA-approved medications: NALTREXONE (reduces craving/heavy drinking; avoid in acute hepatitis/opioid use), ACAMPROSATE (supports abstinence; renally dosed, good in liver disease), and DISULFIRAM (aversive - for motivated, supervised patients)","Manage withdrawal (see next entry), give THIAMINE, and treat comorbidities; brief intervention for risky-but-not-dependent use"],"pearls":["Naltrexone and acamprosate are first-line medications - naltrexone reduces heavy drinking; acamprosate is preferred in significant liver disease (renally cleared)","Disulfiram works only in motivated, supervised patients (aversive reaction with alcohol)","Always give thiamine BEFORE glucose in at-risk patients to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy","Combine medication with psychosocial treatment - neither alone is as effective"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Alcohol Use Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551577/"},{"t":"NIAAA: Alcohol Use Disorder","u":"https://www.niaaa.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-alcohol-withdrawal","name":"Alcohol Withdrawal & Delirium Tremens","sec":"psych","present":["Cessation/reduction in a physically dependent person produces autonomic hyperactivity and CNS excitability on a spectrum from mild tremor to life-threatening DELIRIUM TREMENS","TIMELINE: minor withdrawal (tremor, anxiety, sweating, tachycardia) within 6-24 hours; withdrawal SEIZURES ~12-48 hours; alcoholic hallucinosis ~12-24 hours; DELIRIUM TREMENS (confusion, severe autonomic instability, agitation) typically 48-96 hours","Delirium tremens is a medical EMERGENCY with significant mortality if untreated"],"dx":["Assess severity and risk (prior DTs/seizures, heavy use, comorbidity) and monitor with a symptom scale (CIWA-Ar); check electrolytes/magnesium/glucose and evaluate for concurrent illness/injury","Recognize the escalating timeline and identify high-risk patients who need inpatient/ICU care","Distinguish DTs (a hyperadrenergic delirium) from other causes of altered mental status; give thiamine to prevent Wernicke"],"tx":["BENZODIAZEPINES are the mainstay (they treat and prevent seizures and DTs) - symptom-triggered dosing (CIWA-guided) with long-acting agents (or lorazepam in liver disease); escalate for severe cases (ICU, phenobarbital, or adjunctive dexmedetomidine)","Supportive care: IV fluids, correct electrolytes (magnesium, potassium), and give THIAMINE before glucose (prevent Wernicke-Korsakoff)","Treat DTs aggressively with high-dose benzodiazepines and monitoring; identify precipitants/comorbid illness"],"pearls":["Benzodiazepines are the treatment for alcohol withdrawal - they prevent seizures and delirium tremens (symptom-triggered/CIWA dosing)","Timeline: tremor 6-24h, seizures 12-48h, delirium tremens 48-96h - DTs is a medical emergency","Give thiamine BEFORE glucose to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","In significant liver disease, use lorazepam (or oxazepam) - short-acting, non-oxidatively metabolized benzodiazepines"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Alcohol Withdrawal","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441882/"},{"t":"NIAAA: Alcohol Withdrawal","u":"https://www.niaaa.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-opioid-use-disorder","name":"Opioid Use Disorder & Overdose","sec":"psych","present":["A chronic, relapsing disorder of compulsive opioid use; a major driver of overdose deaths (especially with illicit fentanyl); defined by DSM-5-TR criteria (loss of control, craving, tolerance, withdrawal)","OVERDOSE: respiratory depression, PINPOINT (miotic) pupils, and depressed consciousness - the opioid toxidrome; a life-threatening emergency reversible with naloxone","WITHDRAWAL (not life-threatening but very distressing): dysphoria, myalgias, nausea/vomiting/diarrhea, lacrimation/rhinorrhea, yawning, piloerection, mydriasis"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR criteria; recognize the overdose toxidrome (respiratory depression + miosis) and the withdrawal syndrome (assess with COWS)","Screen for co-occurring disorders, other substances (benzodiazepines/alcohol raise overdose risk), and infectious complications (HIV, hepatitis, endocarditis) from injection","Confirm with history and appropriate testing; assess readiness for treatment"],"tx":["OVERDOSE: NALOXONE (opioid antagonist) plus airway/ventilatory support - repeat dosing may be needed (fentanyl); distribute take-home naloxone widely","MEDICATIONS FOR OPIOID USE DISORDER (the evidence-based standard, reduce mortality): BUPRENORPHINE (partial agonist - start in mild-moderate withdrawal to avoid precipitated withdrawal), METHADONE (full agonist, via opioid treatment programs), and naltrexone (antagonist, after full detox)","Combine with psychosocial support and harm reduction; treat withdrawal symptomatically (clonidine and adjuncts) when needed"],"pearls":["Overdose = respiratory depression + pinpoint pupils; treat with naloxone and ventilation (repeat dosing for fentanyl)","Buprenorphine and methadone reduce mortality - medications for opioid use disorder are first-line, not optional adjuncts","Start buprenorphine only when the patient is in withdrawal (its partial agonism can precipitate withdrawal if given too early)","Co-use of benzodiazepines/alcohol markedly increases overdose risk - and take-home naloxone saves lives"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Opioid Use Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553166/"},{"t":"NIDA: Opioid Use Disorder","u":"https://nida.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-stimulant-use-disorder","name":"Stimulant Use Disorder (Cocaine & Methamphetamine)","sec":"psych","present":["Problematic use of cocaine or amphetamines/methamphetamine; INTOXICATION produces euphoria, increased energy, and sympathetic activation (tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, MYDRIASIS), sometimes with psychosis, agitation, or seizures","Serious acute risks: cardiovascular events (MI, arrhythmia, aortic dissection), stroke, hyperthermia, and stimulant-induced PSYCHOSIS (paranoia, hallucinations)","WITHDRAWAL ('crash'): dysphoria, fatigue, hypersomnia, increased appetite, and vivid dreams - psychologically intense (with depression/suicidality) but not medically dangerous like alcohol withdrawal"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR criteria; recognize the sympathomimetic toxidrome and screen for cardiovascular/neurologic complications during intoxication","Distinguish stimulant-induced psychosis from a primary psychotic disorder (substance-induced typically resolves with abstinence)","Assess comorbid mood disorders, other substances, and injection-related infections; assess suicide risk during the crash"],"tx":["No FDA-approved medications for stimulant use disorder - PSYCHOSOCIAL treatments are the mainstay: CONTINGENCY MANAGEMENT (the most effective, rewarding abstinence), CBT, and the community reinforcement approach","Acute intoxication/agitation: BENZODIAZEPINES first-line for agitation, seizures, and to reduce cardiovascular strain; cool for hyperthermia; treat cardiac ischemia (avoid unopposed beta-blockade in acute cocaine toxicity)","Treat stimulant-induced psychosis with supportive care/antipsychotics as needed; manage comorbidities and support during withdrawal"],"pearls":["Contingency management is the most effective treatment for stimulant use disorder - there is no approved medication","Benzodiazepines are first-line for cocaine/methamphetamine agitation, seizures, and cardiovascular stress","Stimulant withdrawal is a psychologically severe 'crash' (depression, suicidality) but not medically life-threatening","Avoid unopposed beta-blockade in acute cocaine toxicity; treat sympathetic overdrive with benzodiazepines"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stimulant Use Disorder / Cocaine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430769/"},{"t":"NIDA: Cocaine & Methamphetamine","u":"https://nida.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-sedative-hypnotic","name":"Sedative-Hypnotic Use Disorder & Withdrawal","sec":"psych","present":["Problematic use of benzodiazepines, 'Z-drugs' (zolpidem), barbiturates, and related GABAergic sedatives; tolerance and physiologic dependence develop with sustained use","INTOXICATION resembles alcohol: sedation, slurred speech, ataxia, nystagmus, and impaired cognition; respiratory depression is the major danger, greatly amplified when COMBINED WITH OPIOIDS or alcohol","WITHDRAWAL mirrors alcohol withdrawal and is potentially LIFE-THREATENING: anxiety, tremor, autonomic hyperactivity, and, in severe cases, SEIZURES and delirium"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR criteria; recognize that sedative withdrawal, like alcohol, can be dangerous (seizures/delirium) - unlike opioid or stimulant withdrawal","Assess co-use of opioids/alcohol (compounds overdose risk), duration/dose of use, and prior withdrawal seizures","Distinguish intoxication from other causes of CNS depression; flumazenil is generally AVOIDED in chronic users (can precipitate seizures)"],"tx":["Manage dependence with a SLOW TAPER (often converting to a long-acting benzodiazepine like diazepam) rather than abrupt cessation, which risks seizures; phenobarbital protocols are an alternative","Overdose: supportive airway/ventilatory care; flumazenil is used cautiously and generally avoided in chronic/dependent users or mixed overdoses (seizure risk)","Combine taper with psychosocial support/CBT for insomnia or anxiety; address the underlying condition that led to use"],"pearls":["Sedative-hypnotic withdrawal, like alcohol, can cause seizures and delirium - taper slowly, never stop abruptly","The lethal danger is respiratory depression, hugely magnified by co-use with opioids or alcohol","Avoid flumazenil in chronic benzodiazepine users - it can precipitate withdrawal seizures","Convert to a long-acting agent (diazepam) and taper gradually; treat the underlying anxiety/insomnia driving use"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Benzodiazepine / Sedative Withdrawal","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470159/"},{"t":"NIDA: Prescription CNS Depressants","u":"https://nida.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-tobacco-cannabis","name":"Tobacco & Cannabis Use Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["TOBACCO use disorder: nicotine dependence is the leading preventable cause of death; withdrawal (irritability, anxiety, difficulty concentrating, increased appetite, craving) peaks early and drives relapse","CANNABIS use disorder: increasingly common with high-potency products; withdrawal (irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, decreased appetite) is real though not dangerous; heavy use is linked to psychosis risk in vulnerable individuals","CANNABINOID HYPEREMESIS SYNDROME: cyclic vomiting relieved by hot showers in chronic heavy users - an important recognition point"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR criteria; assess readiness to quit (tobacco) and functional impact (cannabis); screen for co-occurring psychiatric disorders","Recognize cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome (cyclic vomiting + compulsive hot bathing) and cannabis-associated psychosis risk","Quantify tobacco dependence (time-to-first-cigarette) to guide pharmacotherapy"],"tx":["TOBACCO: combine behavioral support with pharmacotherapy - VARENICLINE (most effective single agent), nicotine replacement (patch + short-acting, combination NRT), and BUPROPION; varenicline and NRT can be combined","CANNABIS: no FDA-approved medication - psychosocial treatments (CBT, motivational enhancement, contingency management) are the mainstay; treat comorbid anxiety/insomnia","Use the '5 A's' (Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange) framework; every tobacco user should be offered cessation help at each visit"],"pearls":["Varenicline is the most effective single agent for tobacco cessation; combining behavioral support with pharmacotherapy beats either alone","Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome = cyclic vomiting relieved by hot showers in chronic users - stop the cannabis","Cannabis use disorder has no approved medication - contingency management and CBT are first-line","Heavy high-potency cannabis use raises psychosis risk in vulnerable individuals"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nicotine / Cannabis Use Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499601/"},{"t":"NIDA: Tobacco, Nicotine & Cannabis","u":"https://nida.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-gambling-behavioral","name":"Gambling & Behavioral Addictions","sec":"psych","present":["GAMBLING DISORDER is the only formally recognized behavioral (non-substance) addiction in DSM-5-TR, classified with the substance-related and addictive disorders - reflecting shared reward-circuitry neurobiology","Features mirror substance addiction: preoccupation, tolerance (needing larger bets), 'chasing losses', failed attempts to cut back, lying, and jeopardizing relationships/work/finances","Other proposed behavioral addictions (internet gaming, compulsive internet/sexual behavior) are areas of ongoing study, not yet established disorders; high comorbidity with mood, anxiety, and substance disorders, and elevated suicide risk"],"dx":["Diagnose gambling disorder by DSM-5-TR criteria (>=4 of 9 over 12 months); screen for comorbid substance use, mood/anxiety disorders, and SUICIDALITY (markedly elevated)","Note association with dopamine agonist therapy (Parkinson disease) as a reversible iatrogenic cause of pathological gambling/impulse-control behaviors","Distinguish a manic episode (where excessive gambling can occur) from a primary gambling disorder"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY is first-line: CBT and motivational interviewing; peer support (Gamblers Anonymous)","No FDA-approved medication; naltrexone (opioid antagonist) shows benefit, especially with a family history of alcohol use disorder; treat comorbid depression/anxiety","If dopamine-agonist-induced, reduce/change the offending agent"],"pearls":["Gambling disorder is the only established behavioral addiction in DSM-5-TR - grouped with substance disorders by shared neurobiology","Dopamine agonists (Parkinson treatment) can cause pathological gambling and other impulse-control disorders - reversible with dose change","Suicide risk is markedly elevated in gambling disorder - screen for it","CBT is first-line; naltrexone can help, particularly with a family history of alcoholism"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gambling Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551587/"},{"t":"NIMH: Impulse-Control / Addictive Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-delirium","name":"Delirium","sec":"psych","present":["An ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention and awareness with cognitive change, caused by an underlying medical condition - a sign of acute brain dysfunction, not a primary psychiatric disorder","Hallmarks: ACUTE onset, FLUCTUATING course, INATTENTION, disorganized thinking, and altered consciousness; subtypes are HYPERACTIVE (agitated), HYPOACTIVE (quiet, often missed, worse prognosis), and mixed","Extremely common in hospitalized elderly and ICU patients; associated with increased mortality, longer stays, and long-term cognitive decline"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically (CAM - Confusion Assessment Method: acute onset/fluctuation + inattention + [disorganized thinking OR altered consciousness]); the priority is finding the CAUSE","Search for precipitants: infection (UTI, pneumonia), medications (anticholinergics, opioids, benzodiazepines), metabolic derangements, hypoxia, urinary retention/constipation, pain, substance withdrawal","Distinguish from DEMENTIA (chronic, stable, attention relatively preserved early) and depression; delirium can be superimposed on dementia"],"tx":["Treat the UNDERLYING CAUSE - this is the definitive management; medications are adjunctive","NON-PHARMACOLOGIC prevention/treatment is first-line: reorientation, sleep-wake normalization, early mobilization, sensory aids (glasses/hearing aids), hydration, minimizing tethers/restraints, and removing deliriogenic drugs","For dangerous agitation only, use LOW-dose antipsychotics short-term; AVOID benzodiazepines (they worsen delirium) EXCEPT in alcohol/sedative withdrawal delirium, where they are the treatment"],"pearls":["Delirium is acute, fluctuating inattention from a medical cause - always hunt for and treat the underlying etiology","Hypoactive delirium is quiet and frequently missed, yet carries a worse prognosis - screen actively (CAM)","Non-pharmacologic measures come first; antipsychotics are only for dangerous agitation, at low dose","Avoid benzodiazepines in delirium (they worsen it) - the exception is alcohol/sedative withdrawal delirium, where they treat it"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Delirium","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470399/"},{"t":"NIMH: Delirium / Cognitive Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-dementia-alzheimer","name":"Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Dementia) - Alzheimer & Related","sec":"psych","present":["Major neurocognitive disorder = acquired, PROGRESSIVE decline in >=1 cognitive domain that impairs independence, with a CLEAR SENSORIUM (distinguishing it from delirium)","Subtypes: ALZHEIMER (insidious memory-predominant decline, most common), VASCULAR (stepwise, vascular risk factors), LEWY BODY (fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, REM sleep behavior disorder, antipsychotic sensitivity), and FRONTOTEMPORAL (early personality/behavior or language change, younger onset)","Mild neurocognitive disorder (MCI) = decline WITHOUT loss of independence"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically with cognitive testing (MMSE, MoCA) and functional assessment; obtain collateral history; the pattern/course suggests the subtype","Work up REVERSIBLE contributors: B12/folate, thyroid, depression ('pseudodementia'), medication effects, neurosyphilis/HIV where indicated, and normal-pressure hydrocephalus (gait, urinary incontinence, cognitive decline); neuroimaging as indicated","Lewy body dementia: recognize severe ANTIPSYCHOTIC SENSITIVITY (avoid typical antipsychotics)"],"tx":["ALZHEIMER: CHOLINESTERASE INHIBITORS (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) for mild-moderate and MEMANTINE (NMDA antagonist) for moderate-severe; modest symptomatic benefit; newer anti-amyloid monoclonal antibodies (e.g., lecanemab) for early disease with monitoring for ARIA","Treat vascular risk factors (vascular dementia); manage behavioral symptoms non-pharmacologically first (see BPSD entry)","Support caregivers, advance-care planning, safety (driving, wandering); avoid deliriogenic/anticholinergic medications"],"pearls":["Dementia has a clear sensorium and chronic course; delirium has clouded, fluctuating attention and acute onset - and the two can coexist","Always exclude reversible causes (B12, thyroid, depression, medications, normal-pressure hydrocephalus) before attributing decline to a degenerative dementia","Lewy body dementia = fluctuating cognition + visual hallucinations + parkinsonism + REM sleep behavior disorder, with dangerous antipsychotic sensitivity","Cholinesterase inhibitors (mild-moderate) and memantine (moderate-severe) offer modest benefit; treat vascular risk factors in vascular dementia"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Alzheimer Disease / Dementia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499922/"},{"t":"NIA: Alzheimer's & Related Dementias","u":"https://www.nia.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-bpsd","name":"Behavioral & Psychological Symptoms of Dementia","sec":"psych","present":["The non-cognitive symptoms of dementia (agitation, aggression, psychosis, wandering, sleep disruption, apathy, disinhibition) that cause major caregiver distress and drive institutionalization","Very common across dementia subtypes; often triggered by unmet needs, pain, environmental factors, delirium, or medication effects rather than the dementia alone","A leading reason antipsychotics are (over)prescribed in the elderly - despite a BOXED WARNING for increased mortality in dementia"],"dx":["Evaluate for a TREATABLE precipitant first: pain, infection, constipation/urinary retention, delirium, dehydration, medication effects, and environmental/interpersonal triggers","Characterize the specific behavior (agitation vs psychosis vs apathy) - management differs; assess safety of patient and caregivers","Rule out superimposed delirium (acute change/fluctuation)"],"tx":["NON-PHARMACOLOGIC approaches are FIRST-LINE: identify/treat triggers, structured routines, activities, caregiver education, environmental modification","Reserve medications for severe/dangerous symptoms: antipsychotics carry a boxed warning (increased mortality/stroke in dementia) - use lowest dose, shortest duration, with informed consent; brexpiprazole is FDA-approved for agitation in Alzheimer dementia","AVOID typical antipsychotics in Lewy body dementia (severe sensitivity); minimize benzodiazepines (paradoxical agitation, falls); treat comorbid depression"],"pearls":["Always look for an unmet need or medical precipitant (pain, infection, constipation) before medicating behavior in dementia","Antipsychotics carry a boxed warning for increased mortality in dementia - non-pharmacologic measures are first-line, drugs a last resort","Lewy body dementia patients are dangerously sensitive to antipsychotics - avoid typical agents","Non-pharmacologic, trigger-focused care reduces both symptoms and caregiver burden"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Behavioral Symptoms of Dementia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551552/"},{"t":"NIA: Managing Dementia Behaviors","u":"https://www.nia.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-consult-liaison-capacity","name":"Consultation-Liaison Psychiatry & Decision-Making Capacity","sec":"psych","present":["Consultation-liaison (C-L) psychiatry addresses psychiatric issues in the medically ill: delirium, capacity questions, depression/anxiety in medical illness, somatic presentations, substance withdrawal, and agitation","DECISION-MAKING CAPACITY is a clinical, decision-SPECIFIC judgment (any physician can assess it) - distinct from COMPETENCY, which is a legal determination made by a court","Capacity can fluctuate and is specific to the decision at hand (a patient may have capacity for one decision but not another)"],"dx":["Assess capacity via FOUR components: (1) COMMUNICATE a choice, (2) UNDERSTAND the relevant information, (3) APPRECIATE the situation and consequences, and (4) REASON about options","A patient does NOT lack capacity merely because they refuse recommended care or make an 'unwise' choice - the reasoning process is what matters; higher-risk decisions warrant a higher threshold","Identify reversible impairments (delirium, intoxication, acute psychosis) that can be treated to restore capacity"],"tx":["If a patient LACKS capacity, involve a surrogate decision-maker (advance directive/healthcare proxy, then legal hierarchy) and treat reversible causes to restore capacity when possible","In emergencies, the emergency exception allows treatment to prevent serious harm when capacity is absent and no surrogate is available","C-L role: clarify the question, optimize the medical/psychiatric contributors, and document the capacity assessment clearly"],"pearls":["Capacity (clinical, decision-specific, any physician) is not competency (a legal/court determination)","Use the four abilities: communicate a choice, understand, appreciate, and reason - refusing care alone does not equal incapacity","Capacity is decision-specific and can fluctuate; higher-stakes decisions require a higher evidentiary bar","Treat reversible causes (delirium, psychosis, intoxication) - they can restore a patient's capacity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Decision-Making Capacity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441979/"},{"t":"NIH: Informed Consent & Capacity","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-medical-manifestations","name":"Psychiatric Manifestations of Medical Illness","sec":"psych","present":["Many medical conditions present with psychiatric symptoms - recognizing them prevents misdiagnosis of a primary psychiatric disorder and directs treatment to the cause","Classic examples: THYROID disease (hyperthyroid anxiety/mania, hypothyroid depression/'myxedema madness'), CUSHING/steroid (mood lability, psychosis), B12/folate deficiency (depression, cognitive change), pheochromocytoma (panic-like spells), Wilson disease, SLE, HIV, paraneoplastic/autoimmune encephalitis (anti-NMDA-receptor)","RED FLAGS for a medical/'organic' cause: late/atypical age of onset, abrupt onset, no psychiatric history, abnormal vitals or neuro exam, visual/tactile hallucinations, waxing-waning consciousness, or poor response to standard treatment"],"dx":["Maintain a broad differential for new psychiatric symptoms - screen with history, exam, vitals, and targeted labs (TSH, B12, metabolic panel, toxicology; further testing as indicated)","Weigh red flags that point away from a primary psychiatric disorder; delirium and substance effects are the most common medical mimics","Consider autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis (e.g., anti-NMDA-receptor) in young patients with rapid psychiatric + neurologic decline"],"tx":["Treat the UNDERLYING medical condition - psychiatric symptoms often resolve as the primary disease is corrected","Provide symptomatic psychiatric support (e.g., antipsychotic for steroid psychosis) while addressing the cause; coordinate with the primary medical team","Reassess the psychiatric diagnosis after the medical issue is treated"],"pearls":["New psychiatric symptoms with red flags (late/abrupt onset, abnormal exam/vitals, visual or tactile hallucinations, clouded consciousness) demand a medical workup","Check thyroid function and B12 in mood/cognitive presentations - common, reversible mimics","Anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis can masquerade as new psychosis in young patients - look for accompanying neurologic signs","Treating the underlying medical illness often resolves the psychiatric symptoms"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Organic / Secondary Psychiatric Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430685/"},{"t":"NIMH: Medical Conditions & Mental Health","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-adhd","name":"Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A neurodevelopmental disorder of INATTENTION and/or HYPERACTIVITY-IMPULSIVITY beginning in childhood, with several symptoms present before age 12 and in >=2 settings (home, school/work)","Presentations: predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, or combined; symptoms and impairment often PERSIST into adulthood (where hyperactivity may manifest as restlessness/disorganization)","Highly comorbid with learning disorders, oppositional defiant/conduct disorder, anxiety, depression, and substance use"],"dx":["Diagnose by DSM-5-TR: >=6 symptoms (>=5 for age >=17) of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity for >=6 months, onset before age 12, across >=2 settings, with impairment","Use collateral (parent/teacher rating scales, e.g., Vanderbilt); rule out mimics (sleep disorders, hearing/vision problems, thyroid, anxiety/mood, substance use, absence seizures)","Assess comorbidities that alter management (mood, anxiety, tics, substance use)"],"tx":["STIMULANTS (methylphenidate and amphetamine classes) are FIRST-LINE and most effective; monitor growth, appetite, sleep, blood pressure/heart rate, and screen cardiac history","NON-STIMULANTS: atomoxetine (SNRI), and alpha-2 agonists guanfacine/clonidine (useful with tics or when stimulants are unsuitable) - stimulants can unmask/worsen tics in some","Combine with behavioral interventions (parent training, classroom supports); in adults, address diversion risk and comorbid substance use"],"pearls":["Symptoms must appear before age 12 and impair >=2 settings - collateral from parents/teachers is essential","Stimulants are first-line and highly effective; monitor cardiovascular status, growth, sleep, and appetite","Atomoxetine and alpha-2 agonists (guanfacine/clonidine) are non-stimulant options - preferred with prominent tics or diversion concerns","ADHD frequently persists into adulthood and commonly coexists with mood, anxiety, and substance disorders"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441838/"},{"t":"NIMH: ADHD","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-autism","name":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A neurodevelopmental disorder defined by two core domains: (1) persistent deficits in SOCIAL COMMUNICATION and interaction, and (2) RESTRICTED, REPETITIVE patterns of behavior, interests, or activities (stereotypies, insistence on sameness, fixated interests, sensory sensitivities)","Symptoms present in EARLY development, though may not fully manifest until social demands exceed capacity; severity is specified by support needs","Wide range of intellectual/language ability; commonly co-occurs with intellectual disability, ADHD, anxiety, epilepsy, and GI issues"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically by DSM-5-TR core domains with early-developmental onset; use standardized tools (e.g., ADOS) and developmental history; universal toddler screening (e.g., M-CHAT) aids early detection","Evaluate for comorbid intellectual disability, language disorder, ADHD, anxiety, epilepsy, and genetic conditions (e.g., Fragile X) as indicated","Distinguish from social communication disorder (social deficits WITHOUT restricted/repetitive behaviors) and from intellectual disability alone"],"tx":["EARLY, intensive BEHAVIORAL/developmental intervention (e.g., applied behavior analysis, speech and occupational therapy) is the mainstay and improves outcomes; individualized educational support","No medication treats the core social deficits; medications target comorbid/associated symptoms - RISPERIDONE and ARIPIPRAZOLE are FDA-approved for irritability/aggression associated with autism; SSRIs/stimulants for comorbid anxiety/ADHD (used cautiously)","Family education and support, structured environments, and coordinated multidisciplinary care"],"pearls":["Two core domains: social-communication deficits AND restricted/repetitive behaviors, with early-developmental onset","Early intensive behavioral intervention is the evidence-based cornerstone - start as soon as possible","Risperidone and aripiprazole are approved only for irritability/aggression - no drug treats the core social deficits","Social communication disorder has the social deficits WITHOUT the restricted/repetitive behaviors that define autism"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Autism Spectrum Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525976/"},{"t":"NIMH: Autism Spectrum Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-odd-conduct","name":"Oppositional Defiant & Conduct Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["The disruptive, impulse-control, and conduct disorders. OPPOSITIONAL DEFIANT DISORDER (ODD): a pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, and vindictiveness toward authority - WITHOUT major violations of others' basic rights","CONDUCT DISORDER: a repetitive pattern VIOLATING the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate norms - aggression to people/animals, destruction of property, deceit/theft, and serious rule violations","Conduct disorder with onset in childhood and with 'limited prosocial emotions' (callous-unemotional traits) carries worse prognosis; conduct disorder can precede ANTISOCIAL personality disorder in adulthood"],"dx":["Differentiate by severity: ODD is defiance/irritability toward authority; conduct disorder involves violating others' rights/serious norm breaches; both require a persistent pattern with impairment","Conduct disorder before age 18 is a prerequisite for later diagnosing antisocial personality disorder; specify childhood vs adolescent onset and callous-unemotional traits","Screen heavily for comorbidity: ADHD (very common), mood/anxiety disorders, substance use, learning disorders, and trauma"],"tx":["PSYCHOSOCIAL interventions are first-line: PARENT MANAGEMENT TRAINING (for younger children), problem-solving skills training, and multisystemic therapy (for conduct disorder in adolescents)","Treat comorbid ADHD (stimulants can reduce aggression/impulsivity) and mood/anxiety; no medication is specifically approved - antipsychotics only for severe aggression, cautiously","Early intervention, school/community coordination, and addressing family/environmental factors improve outcomes"],"pearls":["ODD = defiance and irritability toward authority; conduct disorder = violating the rights of others - severity distinguishes them","Conduct disorder before 18 is required to later diagnose antisocial personality disorder","Parent management training (younger) and multisystemic therapy (adolescents) are first-line; treat comorbid ADHD","Callous-unemotional traits and early childhood onset predict a worse trajectory"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Oppositional Defiant / Conduct Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557443/"},{"t":"NIMH: Disruptive Behavior Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-tics-tourette","name":"Tic Disorders & Tourette Syndrome","sec":"psych","present":["Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic motor movements or vocalizations, typically preceded by a PREMONITORY URGE and temporarily SUPPRESSIBLE (with rebound); they wax and wane","TOURETTE SYNDROME: BOTH multiple motor tics AND >=1 vocal tic, present for >1 year, with onset before age 18; persistent (chronic) motor OR vocal tic disorder involves only one type","Onset in childhood, often peaking around ages 10-12 with improvement by adulthood; coprolalia (involuntary obscene speech) is uncommon and not required"],"dx":["Diagnose by type/duration: Tourette (motor + vocal, >1 year), persistent motor or vocal tic disorder (one type, >1 year), provisional tic disorder (<1 year); onset before 18","Screen for the very common comorbidities - ADHD and OCD - which often cause more impairment than the tics themselves","Distinguish tics (suppressible, premonitory urge) from other movement disorders (chorea, myoclonus, stereotypies, seizures)"],"tx":["Mild tics: education and reassurance; first-line behavioral therapy is COMPREHENSIVE BEHAVIORAL INTERVENTION FOR TICS (CBIT), which includes habit reversal","Medication for impairing tics: ALPHA-2 AGONISTS (guanfacine, clonidine) first (especially with comorbid ADHD); antipsychotics/VMAT2 inhibitors for severe tics","Treat comorbid ADHD and OCD - often the greater source of impairment; stimulants for ADHD are generally acceptable and do not meaningfully worsen tics for most"],"pearls":["Tourette = multiple motor tics + at least one vocal tic for >1 year, onset before 18; coprolalia is uncommon and NOT required","Tics have a premonitory urge and are briefly suppressible - distinguishing them from other movement disorders","CBIT (habit reversal) is first-line therapy; alpha-2 agonists are first-line medication, especially with comorbid ADHD","ADHD and OCD comorbidities frequently cause more impairment than the tics - treat them"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tourette Syndrome / Tic Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448200/"},{"t":"NINDS: Tourette Syndrome","u":"https://www.ninds.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-intellectual-disability","name":"Intellectual Disability & Learning/Communication Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["INTELLECTUAL DISABILITY (intellectual developmental disorder): deficits in intellectual functioning (reasoning, problem-solving) AND ADAPTIVE functioning (conceptual, social, practical domains), with onset during the developmental period","Severity (mild/moderate/severe/profound) is based on ADAPTIVE functioning and support needs, NOT IQ score alone","SPECIFIC LEARNING DISORDER: difficulty in a specific academic domain (reading/dyslexia, written expression, math/dyscalculia) despite adequate instruction; COMMUNICATION disorders include language, speech-sound, childhood-onset fluency (stuttering), and social (pragmatic) communication disorder"],"dx":["Diagnose intellectual disability from BOTH intellectual and adaptive deficits with developmental onset - adaptive function and support needs define severity (not IQ alone)","Pursue an etiologic workup (genetic/metabolic testing, e.g., Fragile X - the most common inherited cause; Down syndrome; fetal alcohol spectrum - a leading preventable cause)","Specific learning disorder: unexpected academic underachievement in a domain despite intact overall ability and adequate instruction; screen for comorbid ADHD"],"tx":["Individualized EDUCATIONAL and habilitative support (individualized education programs, special education services), skills training, and family support are the mainstay","Learning disorders: targeted educational remediation and accommodations; speech-language therapy for communication disorders","Treat comorbid psychiatric conditions (rates are elevated); coordinate multidisciplinary and community services and transition planning"],"pearls":["Intellectual disability requires BOTH intellectual and adaptive deficits with developmental onset - severity is graded by adaptive function/support needs, not IQ","Fragile X is the most common inherited cause; fetal alcohol spectrum disorder is a leading preventable cause","Specific learning disorder is domain-specific underachievement despite adequate instruction and intact general ability","Educational/habilitative supports are central; psychiatric comorbidity is common and treatable"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intellectual Disability","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560523/"},{"t":"NICHD: Intellectual & Developmental Disabilities","u":"https://www.nichd.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-child-mood-anxiety","name":"Child & Adolescent Mood, Anxiety & Suicidality","sec":"psych","present":["Mood and anxiety disorders in youth often present differently than in adults: pediatric depression may show IRRITABILITY rather than sadness; anxiety is the most common childhood psychiatric presentation","Suicide is a leading cause of death in adolescents - risk assessment is essential, and inquiry about suicidal thoughts does NOT increase risk","Developmental context matters: separation anxiety, school refusal, and somatic complaints are common expressions of distress in children"],"dx":["Use developmentally appropriate assessment with collateral (parents, school); screen adolescents for depression (e.g., PHQ-A) and for suicide risk with validated tools","Distinguish normal developmental variation from disorder by impairment/persistence; assess for bipolarity, trauma, ADHD, and substance use","Directly and calmly assess suicidal ideation, plan, access to means, and protective factors - asking does not plant the idea"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY (CBT for anxiety/depression, and interpersonal therapy for adolescents) is first-line, especially for mild-moderate illness; family involvement is key","Medication when indicated: FLUOXETINE and escitalopram have the strongest evidence for pediatric depression; SSRIs carry a BOXED WARNING for increased suicidal ideation in those <25 - monitor closely, especially early, but the benefit generally outweighs risk in appropriate use","For suicidality: ensure safety, restrict access to lethal means, create a safety plan, and arrange timely follow-up/appropriate level of care; connect families with professional and crisis support resources"],"pearls":["Pediatric depression often looks like irritability, not sadness; anxiety is the most common childhood presentation","Asking about suicide does not increase risk - screen directly and assess access to means and protective factors","Fluoxetine has the best evidence for pediatric depression; SSRIs carry a boxed warning for suicidal ideation in youth - start low, monitor closely early","Psychotherapy (CBT/IPT) with family involvement is first-line; combine with an SSRI for moderate-severe illness"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Depression / Suicide Risk","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534797/"},{"t":"NIMH: Child & Adolescent Mental Health","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-cluster-a-pd","name":"Cluster A Personality Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["The 'ODD/ECCENTRIC' cluster. PARANOID: pervasive distrust and suspiciousness, reading malevolence into others' motives. SCHIZOID: detachment from social relationships with restricted emotional expression - genuine preference for solitude. SCHIZOTYPAL: social/interpersonal deficits PLUS cognitive-perceptual distortions and eccentricity (odd beliefs, magical thinking, ideas of reference, unusual perceptions)","Personality disorders are enduring, pervasive, inflexible patterns of inner experience/behavior that deviate from cultural expectation, are stable from adolescence/early adulthood, and cause distress or impairment","Schizotypal personality disorder sits on the schizophrenia spectrum and can be a premorbid pattern"],"dx":["Diagnose from the enduring, pervasive trait pattern (not episodic); distinguish schizoid (prefers solitude, no psychotic-spectrum distortions) from schizotypal (eccentric, cognitive-perceptual distortions) from avoidant (WANTS relationships but fears rejection)","Schizotypal: differentiate from schizophrenia (no sustained frank psychosis) and autism; paranoid PD from delusional disorder (no fixed delusion) and psychosis","Assess comorbid depression/anxiety and, for schizotypal, transition risk toward psychotic-spectrum illness"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY is the foundation, though these patients often avoid or mistrust treatment; build a consistent, non-intrusive therapeutic alliance","Medications are adjunctive/symptom-targeted: low-dose antipsychotics for schizotypal cognitive-perceptual symptoms; antidepressants for comorbid depression/anxiety","Set realistic goals; respect the schizoid patient's autonomy and need for distance"],"pearls":["Cluster A = odd/eccentric: Paranoid (distrust), Schizoid (detached loner by preference), Schizotypal (eccentric with cognitive-perceptual distortions)","Schizoid PREFERS solitude; avoidant (Cluster C) WANTS closeness but fears rejection - a classic distinction","Schizotypal PD is on the schizophrenia spectrum; low-dose antipsychotics can help its cognitive-perceptual symptoms","Personality disorders are enduring and pervasive since early adulthood - not episodic states"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cluster A Personality Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556001/"},{"t":"NIMH: Personality Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-borderline-pd","name":"Borderline Personality Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["A Cluster B disorder marked by a pervasive pattern of INSTABILITY in relationships, self-image, and affect, plus marked IMPULSIVITY - beginning by early adulthood","Core features: frantic efforts to avoid ABANDONMENT, unstable intense relationships (idealization/devaluation - 'splitting'), identity disturbance, impulsivity, recurrent SELF-HARM/suicidal behavior, affective instability, chronic emptiness, intense anger, and transient stress-related paranoia/dissociation","Recurrent self-injury and suicidality are common and require ongoing risk awareness; high comorbidity with mood, PTSD, substance, and eating disorders"],"dx":["Diagnose from the enduring pattern (>=5 of 9 criteria); distinguish from bipolar disorder - borderline affective shifts are rapid, reactive to interpersonal triggers, and last hours, whereas bipolar episodes last days-weeks and are less interpersonally cued","Assess self-harm/suicide risk (distinguish chronic risk from acute escalation) and comorbid conditions (PTSD, substance use, eating disorders)","Recognize splitting and its effect on treatment teams"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY is the primary, evidence-based treatment: DIALECTICAL BEHAVIOR THERAPY (DBT), mentalization-based therapy, transference-focused therapy, and general good psychiatric management reduce self-harm and improve function","Medications are ADJUNCTIVE and symptom-targeted (mood instability, impulsivity, transient psychosis) - no drug is FDA-approved; avoid polypharmacy and be cautious with agents dangerous in overdose","Maintain clear, consistent boundaries; team communication counters splitting; prognosis is better than commonly assumed (many improve over time). Ongoing suicidality warrants safety planning and appropriate professional support."],"pearls":["DBT is the flagship evidence-based treatment - it specifically reduces self-harm and suicidal behavior","Distinguish from bipolar: borderline mood shifts are rapid, reactive, and interpersonally triggered (hours), not sustained episodes (days-weeks)","'Splitting' (idealization/devaluation) destabilizes treatment teams - consistent boundaries and communication are essential","Medications are adjunctive only; psychotherapy is primary, and long-term prognosis is often favorable"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Borderline Personality Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430883/"},{"t":"NIMH: Borderline Personality Disorder","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-cluster-bc-pd","name":"Cluster B & Cluster C Personality Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["CLUSTER B ('dramatic/erratic', besides borderline): ANTISOCIAL (disregard for/violation of others' rights, deceit, impulsivity, lack of remorse; requires age >=18 and conduct disorder before 15), NARCISSISTIC (grandiosity, need for admiration, lack of empathy), HISTRIONIC (excessive emotionality and attention-seeking)","CLUSTER C ('anxious/fearful'): AVOIDANT (social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy, hypersensitivity to rejection - wants relationships but fears them), DEPENDENT (excessive need to be cared for, submissive/clinging), OBSESSIVE-COMPULSIVE PERSONALITY DISORDER (OCPD: preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, and control - ego-syntonic, no true obsessions/compulsions)","Enduring, pervasive, inflexible patterns causing distress/impairment"],"dx":["Match the specific trait pattern; key distinctions: OCPD (perfectionistic personality style, ego-syntonic) vs OCD (ego-dystonic obsessions/compulsions); avoidant PD (wants closeness, fears rejection) vs schizoid (prefers solitude)","Antisocial PD requires age >=18 with evidence of conduct disorder before age 15; assess for comorbid substance use","Screen for comorbid mood/anxiety disorders across Cluster C"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY is first-line across these disorders (CBT and psychodynamic approaches); Cluster C disorders often respond relatively well to therapy","Treat comorbid depression/anxiety pharmacologically; no personality disorder has an approved specific medication","Antisocial PD is difficult to treat - focus on managing behavior, comorbid substance use, and harm reduction; avoid being manipulated"],"pearls":["Cluster B = dramatic (antisocial, narcissistic, histrionic, borderline); Cluster C = anxious (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive)","OCPD is an ego-syntonic perfectionistic style with NO true obsessions/compulsions - unlike OCD","Antisocial PD needs age >=18 plus conduct disorder before 15; it is notoriously treatment-resistant","Avoidant PD wants relationships but fears rejection (vs schizoid, who prefers solitude); Cluster C often responds well to psychotherapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Personality Disorders (Cluster B/C)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556058/"},{"t":"NIMH: Personality Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-anorexia","name":"Anorexia Nervosa","sec":"psych","present":["A serious eating disorder marked by restriction of intake leading to significantly low body weight for age/health, an intense fear of weight gain, and a disturbance in how body weight/shape is experienced","Subtypes: restricting, and binge-eating/purging; medically it is among the most lethal psychiatric disorders (from medical complications and suicide)","Physical signs reflect malnutrition: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, amenorrhea, lanugo, and, with purging behaviors, electrolyte disturbances and dental/parotid changes"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically (DSM-5-TR) from the restriction, fear of weight gain, and body-image disturbance; assess MEDICAL severity rather than focusing on any single number - vital signs, electrolytes, ECG (QTc), and hydration guide the level of care","Screen for the medical complications of malnutrition and for comorbid depression, anxiety, OCD, and suicidality","Distinguish from medical causes of weight loss and from bulimia/other eating disorders; use a nonjudgmental, collaborative approach"],"tx":["Treatment is MULTIDISCIPLINARY (medical, nutritional, psychiatric). NUTRITIONAL REHABILITATION and weight restoration are the foundation, with careful monitoring for REFEEDING SYNDROME (dangerous hypophosphatemia/electrolyte shifts) - refeeding is advanced gradually with electrolyte repletion and monitoring","PSYCHOTHERAPY is central: FAMILY-BASED TREATMENT (Maudsley) is first-line for adolescents; CBT for adults. Medications are adjunctive (no drug is a primary treatment; SSRIs are largely ineffective for core anorexia; olanzapine may help select patients)","Determine level of care by medical stability and safety; hospitalize for instability. This is a specialist-managed illness - connect patients and families with appropriate professional eating-disorder support (e.g., the National Alliance for Eating Disorders helpline)."],"pearls":["Anorexia nervosa has among the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder - from medical complications and suicide","Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia and electrolyte shifts) is the key danger when restoring nutrition - advance slowly with monitoring and repletion","Family-based treatment is first-line for adolescents; SSRIs are largely ineffective for the core illness","Assess medical severity (vitals, electrolytes, ECG) to set level of care - and manage with a multidisciplinary specialist team"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Anorexia Nervosa","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459148/"},{"t":"NIMH: Eating Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-bulimia-bed","name":"Bulimia Nervosa & Binge-Eating Disorder","sec":"psych","present":["BULIMIA NERVOSA: recurrent binge eating with recurrent inappropriate COMPENSATORY behaviors, with self-evaluation unduly influenced by body shape/weight; patients are often at normal or near-normal weight","BINGE-EATING DISORDER (BED): recurrent binge eating WITHOUT regular compensatory behaviors, associated with distress; it is the MOST COMMON eating disorder and is linked to obesity","Signs suggesting purging behaviors include dental enamel erosion, parotid (salivary gland) enlargement, calluses on the knuckles (Russell sign), and electrolyte abnormalities (e.g., hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis)"],"dx":["Diagnose bulimia from the binge-plus-compensatory pattern with overvaluation of shape/weight; BED from recurrent binges with distress and no regular compensation - both at a defined frequency over time (DSM-5-TR)","Screen for medical complications (electrolyte disturbances, especially with purging; cardiac risk) and comorbid depression, anxiety, substance use, and suicidality","Distinguish from anorexia binge/purge subtype (which involves significantly low weight)"],"tx":["BULIMIA: CBT (enhanced, CBT-E) is the first-line psychotherapy; FLUOXETINE is the FDA-approved medication (effective at 60 mg/day). Correct electrolyte disturbances and address medical complications","BINGE-EATING DISORDER: CBT/interpersonal therapy first-line; LISDEXAMFETAMINE is FDA-approved for moderate-severe BED, and SSRIs can help; address comorbid obesity and mood","Use a nonjudgmental, collaborative stance; connect patients with appropriate professional eating-disorder support (e.g., the National Alliance for Eating Disorders helpline)"],"pearls":["Bulimia patients are often normal weight - look for signs like dental erosion, parotid swelling, Russell sign, and hypokalemia/metabolic alkalosis","CBT-E is first-line for both; fluoxetine (60 mg/day) is the approved medication for bulimia","Lisdexamfetamine is FDA-approved for binge-eating disorder, the most common eating disorder","Bupropion is contraindicated in bulimia/purging disorders (increased seizure risk) - an important prescribing caution"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bulimia Nervosa / Binge-Eating Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562178/"},{"t":"NIMH: Eating Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-somatic-symptom","name":"Somatic Symptom & Related Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["Disorders featuring physical symptoms or health concerns with disproportionate distress/behavior. SOMATIC SYMPTOM DISORDER: >=1 distressing somatic symptom PLUS excessive thoughts/feelings/behaviors about it (the symptom may or may not have a medical explanation)","ILLNESS ANXIETY DISORDER: preoccupation with HAVING/acquiring a serious illness with minimal/no somatic symptoms and high health anxiety. FUNCTIONAL NEUROLOGICAL (CONVERSION) DISORDER: neurologic symptoms (weakness, nonepileptic seizures, sensory loss) incompatible with recognized disease","FACTITIOUS DISORDER: intentional falsification/induction of symptoms to assume the SICK ROLE (no external reward) - distinct from MALINGERING (feigning for external gain, e.g., money/drugs, which is not a mental disorder)"],"dx":["Diagnose by the pattern: excessive response to symptoms (somatic symptom disorder), health preoccupation (illness anxiety), neurologic incompatibility (functional neurological disorder, supported by positive signs like Hoover sign), or deception for the sick role (factitious)","Symptoms are NOT intentionally produced in somatic symptom/illness anxiety/conversion disorders (they are experienced as real); intentional production distinguishes factitious disorder and malingering (malingering = external incentive)","Avoid excessive testing while remaining alert to genuine medical disease; establish rapport"],"tx":["Build a strong therapeutic alliance with a SINGLE consistent physician, schedule REGULAR visits (not symptom-triggered), acknowledge suffering, and limit unnecessary tests/procedures","CBT is the most effective treatment; treat comorbid depression/anxiety (SSRIs/SNRIs); for functional neurological disorder, physiotherapy and education help","Set the goal as functioning/coping rather than eliminating symptoms; avoid confronting factitious patients punitively"],"pearls":["Somatic symptom disorder centers on the excessive REACTION to symptoms - the symptom itself may be medically explained or not","Factitious disorder = deception for the sick role (internal motive); malingering = feigning for external gain (not a mental disorder)","Regular scheduled visits with one physician, plus CBT, reduce excessive testing and improve function","Functional neurological disorder shows positive signs (e.g., Hoover sign) - it is a rule-in diagnosis, not merely exclusion"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Somatic Symptom Disorder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532253/"},{"t":"NIMH: Somatic Symptom & Related Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-dissociative","name":"Dissociative Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["Disruptions in the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception, and behavior - strongly associated with severe/chronic trauma","DISSOCIATIVE IDENTITY DISORDER: two or more distinct personality states with recurrent gaps in recall (amnesia) beyond ordinary forgetting. DISSOCIATIVE AMNESIA: inability to recall important autobiographical information (usually trauma-related), sometimes with dissociative fugue (travel/wandering with amnesia)","DEPERSONALIZATION/DEREALIZATION DISORDER: persistent/recurrent experiences of detachment from one's self (depersonalization) or surroundings (derealization), WITH INTACT reality testing"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically; take a careful trauma history; distinguish dissociative amnesia (psychological, autobiographical, often reversible) from neurologic/substance-induced amnesia (which impairs new learning and is not trauma-selective)","Depersonalization/derealization: reality testing remains intact (patients know the experience is not real) - distinguishing it from psychosis","Screen for comorbid PTSD, depression, and suicidality; rule out seizures, substance effects, and malingering"],"tx":["PSYCHOTHERAPY is the primary treatment: trauma-focused, phase-oriented therapy emphasizing safety, stabilization, and processing; establish grounding and coping skills","No specific FDA-approved medications - treat comorbid PTSD, depression, and anxiety pharmacologically","Ensure safety (self-harm risk), build a stable therapeutic relationship, and avoid retraumatization"],"pearls":["Dissociative disorders are strongly linked to severe, often early, trauma - take a careful trauma history","Depersonalization/derealization preserves intact reality testing - the person knows it feels unreal (unlike psychosis)","Psychotherapy (trauma-focused, phase-oriented) is primary; medications only target comorbidities","Distinguish dissociative amnesia (autobiographical, trauma-related) from organic amnesia (impaired new learning)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Dissociative Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568768/"},{"t":"NIMH: Dissociative Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-sleep-wake","name":"Sleep-Wake Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["Sleep disorders that commonly intersect with psychiatry. INSOMNIA DISORDER: dissatisfaction with sleep quantity/quality (difficulty initiating/maintaining sleep) despite adequate opportunity, with daytime impairment, >=3 nights/week for >=3 months","NARCOLEPSY: excessive daytime sleepiness with REM intrusion phenomena - cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone with emotion, in type 1 from orexin/hypocretin loss), sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations","Parasomnias (NREM: sleepwalking, night terrors; REM sleep behavior disorder - acting out dreams, a marker of future synucleinopathy), restless legs syndrome, and obstructive sleep apnea (which mimics depression/fatigue)"],"dx":["Diagnose insomnia clinically; evaluate for contributing psychiatric (depression, anxiety), medical, substance, and circadian factors; use sleep diaries; polysomnography for suspected apnea, narcolepsy, or REM behavior disorder","Narcolepsy: confirm with sleep studies (polysomnography + multiple sleep latency test); type 1 features cataplexy and low CSF orexin","Screen for obstructive sleep apnea (a common, treatable mimic of fatigue/depression) and for REM sleep behavior disorder (neurodegeneration risk)"],"tx":["INSOMNIA: cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is FIRST-LINE (sleep hygiene, stimulus control, sleep restriction); use hypnotics sparingly and short-term (dependence, falls in elderly)","NARCOLEPSY: scheduled naps plus wake-promoting agents (modafinil/armodafinil, stimulants); sodium oxybate for cataplexy and severe sleepiness","Treat obstructive sleep apnea (CPAP); manage restless legs (iron repletion if deficient, alpha-2-delta ligands/dopaminergics); ensure safety in REM behavior disorder"],"pearls":["CBT-I is first-line for chronic insomnia - not long-term hypnotics (which risk dependence and falls)","Narcolepsy type 1 = cataplexy + low CSF orexin (hypocretin); confirm with polysomnography and the multiple sleep latency test","REM sleep behavior disorder (acting out dreams) is a strong predictor of future Parkinson disease/synucleinopathy","Screen for obstructive sleep apnea in 'depression/fatigue' - it is a common, treatable mimic"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Insomnia / Sleep-Wake Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526136/"},{"t":"NHLBI: Sleep Disorders","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-sexual-paraphilic","name":"Sexual Dysfunctions & Paraphilic Disorders","sec":"psych","present":["SEXUAL DYSFUNCTIONS: clinically significant difficulty in sexual response or pleasure (e.g., erectile disorder, female sexual interest/arousal disorder, genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder, premature/delayed ejaculation, low desire), present for >=6 months with distress","Common contributors are medical (vascular, endocrine, neurologic), medication-related (notably SSRIs), substance use, and psychological/relational factors","PARAPHILIC DISORDERS: intense atypical sexual interests that cause DISTRESS/IMPAIRMENT or involve HARM/nonconsent (e.g., pedophilic, exhibitionistic, voyeuristic, frotteuristic, sexual sadism disorders) - an atypical interest alone is not a disorder unless it causes distress/impairment or harm to others"],"dx":["Sexual dysfunction: take a thorough medical, medication, substance, and psychosocial history; identify reversible causes (e.g., SSRI-induced dysfunction, hypogonadism, vascular disease); symptoms >=6 months with distress","Distinguish a paraphilia (interest) from a paraphilic DISORDER (distress/impairment or harm/nonconsent); disorders involving nonconsent/minors carry legal and safety obligations","Assess comorbid depression/anxiety and relationship factors"],"tx":["Sexual dysfunction: treat the cause - optimize medical conditions, adjust offending medications (e.g., switch from an SSRI or add an agent for SSRI-induced dysfunction), and offer sex therapy/CBT; PDE5 inhibitors for erectile disorder","Paraphilic disorders: CBT/relapse-prevention; for high-risk/harmful disorders, antiandrogen or GnRH-agonist therapy and SSRIs to reduce drive, within appropriate legal/ethical frameworks","Involve partners where appropriate; address safety and, where relevant, mandated-reporting and risk-management obligations"],"pearls":["SSRIs commonly cause sexual dysfunction - a frequent, reversible cause and a major reason for nonadherence","A paraphilia becomes a paraphilic DISORDER only when it causes distress/impairment or involves harm/nonconsent","Always evaluate medical, medication, and substance contributors to sexual dysfunction before assuming a psychological cause","Paraphilic disorders involving minors or nonconsent carry legal and safety obligations, including reporting duties"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Sexual Dysfunction / Paraphilic Disorders","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554459/"},{"t":"NIMH: Sexual & Gender-Related Health","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-gender-dysphoria","name":"Gender Dysphoria","sec":"psych","present":["Gender dysphoria is clinically significant DISTRESS arising from an incongruence between a person's experienced/expressed gender and their sex assigned at birth - the DISTRESS, not the gender identity itself, is the clinical focus","Gender incongruence/diverse gender identity is not in itself a mental disorder; the diagnosis exists to describe and address associated distress and to facilitate access to care","Presents across the lifespan; affected individuals have elevated rates of depression, anxiety, and suicidality, often related to minority stress, stigma, and lack of support"],"dx":["Diagnose from the marked incongruence and associated distress/impairment over time (DSM-5-TR criteria differ for children vs adolescents/adults)","Assess for and treat commonly co-occurring depression, anxiety, and suicidality; evaluate psychosocial supports and stressors","Use an affirming, patient-centered approach; a comprehensive assessment guides individualized care"],"tx":["Care is individualized and multidisciplinary, guided by established standards of care (e.g., WPATH): psychological support, and, for those who seek it, social affirmation and gender-affirming medical care (hormone therapy, and surgery for appropriate adult candidates)","Mental health treatment focuses on the distress and comorbid conditions and on support - NOT on attempting to change gender identity ('conversion' efforts are ineffective and harmful)","For youth, developmentally appropriate, family-involved, multidisciplinary assessment and care; ensure access to support and safety planning given elevated suicide risk"],"pearls":["The clinical target is the DISTRESS (dysphoria) - a diverse gender identity is not itself a mental disorder","Suicidality and depression/anxiety are elevated, largely from minority stress and lack of support - screen and support","Care follows established standards (WPATH) and is individualized and affirming; efforts to change gender identity are harmful and contraindicated","For minors, care is developmentally staged, family-involved, and multidisciplinary"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gender Dysphoria","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532313/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health of Sexual & Gender Minorities","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-suicide-risk-assessment","name":"Suicide Risk Assessment & Safety Planning","sec":"psych","present":["Suicide is a leading cause of death and a core psychiatric competency; risk assessment is a clinical judgment that synthesizes risk and protective factors - not a checklist that predicts individual acts","Ask directly about suicidal ideation, intent, and plan - inquiry does NOT increase risk and is essential; assess the presence of intent and access to lethal means","Recognize warning signs of acute risk (escalating hopelessness, agitation, withdrawal, giving away possessions) layered on chronic risk factors"],"dx":["Weigh STATIC risk factors (prior attempt - the strongest predictor; male sex for completion; older age; chronic illness; family history) and DYNAMIC/modifiable factors (active psychiatric illness, substance use, hopelessness, agitation, recent loss, access to lethal means)","Assess PROTECTIVE factors (social support, responsibility for children, help-seeking, reasons for living) and comorbid conditions (depression, bipolar, psychosis, substance use, borderline PD, PTSD)","Stratify risk (acute vs chronic; low/moderate/high) to determine the appropriate level of care; a prior attempt is the single strongest risk factor"],"tx":["Match the level of care to risk: from outpatient safety planning to hospitalization for acute high risk; treat the underlying psychiatric disorder (lithium and clozapine specifically reduce suicide risk in their populations)","SAFETY PLANNING (collaborative, patient-specific) plus MEANS RESTRICTION - counsel to reduce access to lethal means, a strongly evidence-based intervention - and mobilize supports and follow-up","Ensure timely follow-up (the post-discharge period is high-risk); provide crisis resources (e.g., the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline) and involve family/supports as appropriate. If someone is in immediate danger, emergency services/urgent evaluation are warranted."],"pearls":["A prior suicide attempt is the single strongest predictor of future suicide - always ask about attempt history","Asking about suicidal thoughts does not increase risk - direct, compassionate inquiry is essential","Means restriction and collaborative safety planning are among the most evidence-based interventions","Lithium (mood disorders) and clozapine (schizophrenia) uniquely reduce suicide risk; the post-discharge period requires close follow-up. This is a sensitive topic - connect patients with professional care and crisis resources such as the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline."],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Suicide Risk Assessment","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557428/"},{"t":"NIMH: Suicide Prevention","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-agitation-restraint","name":"Agitation & the Violent Patient - De-escalation & Restraint","sec":"psych","present":["Acute agitation is a psychomotor emergency ranging from restlessness to imminent violence; causes are broad - primary psychiatric (psychosis, mania), substance intoxication/withdrawal, and MEDICAL/delirium (never assume 'psychiatric' before excluding organic causes)","Recognize escalating warning signs (pacing, clenched fists, raised voice, invasion of space) to intervene early and protect patient and staff safety","A rapid, simultaneous approach to safety, evaluation, and treatment is required"],"dx":["Assess for medical causes and delirium FIRST (vitals, glucose, oxygenation, intoxication/withdrawal) - agitation is a symptom, not a diagnosis; obtain collateral","Gauge dangerousness and stratify the intervention; identify reversible contributors (hypoglycemia, hypoxia, pain, urinary retention)","Distinguish hyperactive delirium, substance states, akathisia (which can mimic agitation), and primary psychiatric agitation"],"tx":["VERBAL DE-ESCALATION is the first-line intervention - a calm, non-provocative, respectful approach, offering choices and space; the goal is the LEAST restrictive effective measure","If medication is needed: oral is preferred when feasible; for chemical restraint, ANTIPSYCHOTICS (e.g., haloperidol, droperidol, or a second-generation agent) and/or BENZODIAZEPINES - use benzodiazepines for suspected substance withdrawal/stimulant intoxication or unknown cause; avoid benzodiazepines as sole agent in delirium","PHYSICAL RESTRAINT is a LAST resort when others fail and danger is imminent - time-limited, with continuous monitoring, frequent reassessment, and prompt discontinuation; document indication and monitoring; treat the underlying cause"],"pearls":["Always exclude medical/delirium causes before labeling agitation 'psychiatric' - check glucose, oxygenation, and vitals","Verbal de-escalation is first-line; use the least restrictive effective intervention","Benzodiazepines are preferred for stimulant intoxication and alcohol/sedative withdrawal; avoid benzodiazepines alone in delirium (they worsen it)","Physical restraint is a last resort - time-limited, continuously monitored, and discontinued as soon as safe; akathisia can masquerade as agitation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Agitation Management","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576390/"},{"t":"NIMH: Psychiatric Emergencies","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-catatonia","name":"Catatonia","sec":"psych","present":["A psychomotor syndrome of marked disturbance, occurring with mood disorders (most commonly), psychotic disorders, and MEDICAL/neurologic conditions - it is a syndrome, not a disease, and is frequently underrecognized","Features (>=3): STUPOR, catalepsy, WAXY FLEXIBILITY, MUTISM, negativism, posturing, mannerisms, stereotypies, agitation, grimacing, ECHOLALIA (mimicking speech), and ECHOPRAXIA (mimicking movements)","MALIGNANT CATATONIA is a life-threatening variant with hyperthermia and autonomic instability - overlapping clinically with neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically; the BUSH-FRANCIS Catatonia Rating Scale aids recognition and severity; a positive LORAZEPAM CHALLENGE (rapid symptom improvement after IV lorazepam) supports the diagnosis and guides treatment","Work up underlying causes (psychiatric and medical/neurologic - including autoimmune encephalitis) and rule out neuroleptic malignant syndrome in the malignant form","Recognize that catatonia can be missed as 'uncooperative' or mistaken for stupor of other cause"],"tx":["BENZODIAZEPINES (lorazepam) are FIRST-LINE and often dramatically effective; escalate the dose as tolerated","ELECTROCONVULSIVE THERAPY (ECT) is definitive - the treatment of choice for benzodiazepine-refractory or MALIGNANT catatonia","AVOID/withdraw ANTIPSYCHOTICS when possible - they can worsen catatonia and precipitate neuroleptic malignant syndrome; treat the underlying disorder and support (hydration, VTE prophylaxis)"],"pearls":["Lorazepam is first-line (and a lorazepam challenge is both diagnostic and therapeutic); ECT is definitive, especially for malignant catatonia","Antipsychotics can WORSEN catatonia and trigger neuroleptic malignant syndrome - avoid or withdraw them","Waxy flexibility, mutism, negativism, echolalia, and echopraxia are hallmark signs","Catatonia most often accompanies MOOD disorders and can arise from medical/neurologic causes (e.g., autoimmune encephalitis)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Catatonia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459345/"},{"t":"NIMH: Catatonia / Mood & Psychotic Disorders","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-nms","name":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","sec":"psych","present":["A rare, life-threatening reaction to DOPAMINE ANTAGONISTS (antipsychotics; also antiemetics like metoclopramide) or to abrupt withdrawal of dopaminergic (Parkinson) medication","Classic TETRAD: HYPERTHERMIA, severe 'LEAD-PIPE' RIGIDITY, AUTONOMIC INSTABILITY (labile BP, tachycardia, diaphoresis), and ALTERED MENTAL STATUS; onset is typically over DAYS (slower than serotonin syndrome)","Labs: markedly ELEVATED CREATINE KINASE (rhabdomyolysis risk -> acute kidney injury), leukocytosis; higher risk with high-potency agents, rapid titration, dehydration, and depot formulations"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically from recent dopamine-antagonist exposure plus hyperthermia, rigidity, autonomic instability, and altered mental status with elevated CK","Distinguish from SEROTONIN SYNDROME (which features NEUROMUSCULAR HYPERACTIVITY - clonus/hyperreflexia, lower-limb predominant - rapid onset over hours, and a serotonergic drug) and from malignant catatonia and malignant hyperthermia","Rule out infection/sepsis and other causes of hyperthermia"],"tx":["STOP the offending dopamine antagonist immediately (or restart dopaminergic therapy if withdrawal-related); aggressive SUPPORTIVE care - cooling, IV fluids, and management of rhabdomyolysis/renal function in an ICU setting","Pharmacologic options for moderate-severe cases: DANTROLENE (muscle relaxant), and dopamine agonists BROMOCRIPTINE/amantadine; benzodiazepines for agitation and mild cases","ECT for refractory cases; reintroduce antipsychotics only cautiously after recovery (lower-potency agent, delayed)"],"pearls":["NMS = dopamine antagonist + hyperthermia + lead-pipe rigidity + autonomic instability + altered mental status, with high CK, evolving over days","Key contrast: serotonin syndrome has clonus/hyperreflexia and a faster (hours) onset; NMS has rigidity and bradyreflexia over days","Stop the antipsychotic and support aggressively; dantrolene and bromocriptine for severe cases","Watch for rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury from the elevated CK"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482282/"},{"t":"NINDS: Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","u":"https://www.ninds.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-serotonin-syndrome","name":"Serotonin Syndrome","sec":"psych","present":["A potentially life-threatening condition of serotonergic EXCESS, usually from combining serotonergic agents or overdose (SSRIs/SNRIs, MAOIs, tramadol, linezolid, triptans, dextromethorphan, St. John's wort, certain opioids like meperidine)","CLINICAL TRIAD: NEUROMUSCULAR HYPERACTIVITY (CLONUS - especially inducible/ocular, HYPERREFLEXIA, tremor, rigidity - LOWER-limb predominant), AUTONOMIC HYPERACTIVITY (hyperthermia, tachycardia, diaphoresis, mydriasis), and ALTERED MENTAL STATUS (agitation)","Onset is RAPID - typically within HOURS of a dose change or new agent (faster than NMS)"],"dx":["Diagnose clinically; the HUNTER CRITERIA (serotonergic agent plus findings such as spontaneous/inducible/ocular clonus, hyperreflexia with tremor, or hyperthermia with rigidity) are sensitive and specific","Key differentiator from NMS: serotonin syndrome has HYPERREFLEXIA/CLONUS and rapid onset; NMS has 'lead-pipe' rigidity, bradyreflexia, and a slower (days) course","Take a careful medication history (including OTC/supplements and recent MAOI use); rule out anticholinergic toxicity and infection"],"tx":["STOP all serotonergic agents; provide SUPPORTIVE care - IV fluids, active cooling for hyperthermia, and BENZODIAZEPINES for agitation, tremor, and autonomic symptoms","For moderate-severe cases, CYPROHEPTADINE (a serotonin antagonist) is the specific antidote; severe hyperthermia may require intubation/paralysis and ICU care","Prevent recurrence by observing appropriate MAOI washout periods (e.g., ~2 weeks; ~5 weeks after fluoxetine) before starting another serotonergic agent"],"pearls":["Serotonin syndrome = clonus + hyperreflexia (lower-limb predominant) + autonomic instability + altered mental status, onset within hours","Distinguish from NMS: hyperreflexia/clonus + fast onset (serotonin) vs rigidity/bradyreflexia + slow onset (NMS)","Stop serotonergic drugs, support, give benzodiazepines; cyproheptadine is the antidote for moderate-severe cases","Respect MAOI washout periods (2 weeks generally; 5 weeks after fluoxetine) to prevent it"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Serotonin Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482377/"},{"t":"NINDS: Serotonin Syndrome","u":"https://www.ninds.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-antidepressants","name":"Antidepressants (SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, MAOIs)","sec":"psych","present":["The major antidepressant classes differ in mechanism and safety. SSRIs (fluoxetine, sertraline, escitalopram) are FIRST-LINE by tolerability/safety; SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine) add noradrenergic action (helpful for neuropathic pain); atypicals include bupropion (no sexual dysfunction, lowers seizure threshold) and mirtazapine (sedation, weight gain)","TRICYCLICS (TCAs) are effective but dangerous in OVERDOSE (cardiotoxic); MAOIs are highly effective (esp. atypical/refractory depression) but require dietary and drug restrictions","All carry a BOXED WARNING for increased suicidal ideation in patients <25; effect takes ~4-6 weeks"],"dx":["Select by side-effect profile, comorbidity, safety in overdose, and interactions; SSRIs are first-line for most","Recognize DISCONTINUATION SYNDROME with abrupt stop of short-half-life agents (paroxetine, venlafaxine): flu-like symptoms, dizziness, 'brain zaps' - taper to avoid; fluoxetine's long half-life self-tapers","Monitor for activation/suicidality early, hyponatremia (SIADH, especially elderly), serotonin syndrome risk, and bleeding risk (with NSAIDs/anticoagulants)"],"tx":["Common SSRI/SNRI effects: GI upset, SEXUAL DYSFUNCTION (common, persistent - a key adherence issue), insomnia/activation or sedation; manage or switch (e.g., to bupropion) as needed","TCA OVERDOSE is a medical emergency - QRS widening/arrhythmia, anticholinergic and CNS effects, hypotension; treat with SODIUM BICARBONATE for cardiotoxicity","MAOIs: enforce a low-TYRAMINE diet (hypertensive crisis risk) and avoid serotonergic/sympathomimetic combinations (serotonin syndrome); observe washout periods when switching"],"pearls":["SSRIs are first-line; bupropion avoids sexual dysfunction but lowers the seizure threshold (avoid in eating disorders/seizure risk)","TCA overdose is cardiotoxic (wide QRS) - treat with sodium bicarbonate; this lethality-in-overdose limits TCA use","MAOIs require a tyramine-restricted diet and drug-washout discipline to avoid hypertensive crisis and serotonin syndrome","Taper short-half-life agents (paroxetine, venlafaxine) to avoid discontinuation syndrome; all antidepressants carry the youth suicidality boxed warning"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Antidepressants / SSRIs","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554406/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health Medications","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-antipsychotics","name":"Antipsychotics (Typical/Atypical) & Metabolic Monitoring","sec":"psych","present":["TYPICAL (first-generation) antipsychotics (haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine) block D2 receptors and cause EXTRAPYRAMIDAL SYMPTOMS (EPS) and hyperprolactinemia; ATYPICAL (second-generation) agents (risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, aripiprazole, ziprasidone) have broader receptor action and lower EPS but greater METABOLIC risk","EPS spectrum: acute DYSTONIA (hours-days; treat with anticholinergics/diphenhydramine), AKATHISIA (restlessness; treat with propranolol/lower dose), drug-induced PARKINSONISM, and TARDIVE DYSKINESIA (late, potentially irreversible involuntary movements; VMAT2 inhibitors)","CLOZAPINE is uniquely effective for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reduces suicide, but carries serious risks requiring monitoring"],"dx":["Select by efficacy, side-effect profile, and adherence needs (long-acting injectables aid adherence); baseline and periodic METABOLIC MONITORING (weight/BMI, waist, fasting glucose/HbA1c, lipids, blood pressure) for second-generation agents","Monitor for EPS, tardive dyskinesia (e.g., AIMS exam), QTc prolongation (esp. ziprasidone, IV haloperidol), and hyperprolactinemia (risperidone)","CLOZAPINE requires ANC monitoring (agranulocytosis) and vigilance for MYOCARDITIS, seizures, severe constipation/ileus, and sialorrhea"],"tx":["Treat EPS by type: dystonia -> anticholinergic (benztropine)/diphenhydramine; akathisia -> propranolol or dose reduction; parkinsonism -> reduce dose or switch; tardive dyskinesia -> VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine)","Manage metabolic effects proactively (diet/exercise, switch to a lower-risk agent, metformin); olanzapine and clozapine carry the highest metabolic burden, aripiprazole/ziprasidone the lowest","Reserve clozapine for treatment resistance (>=2 failed trials) and for reducing suicidality, with mandated ANC monitoring"],"pearls":["First-generation = more EPS/prolactin; second-generation = more metabolic syndrome - monitor weight, glucose, and lipids on atypicals","EPS management: dystonia (anticholinergic), akathisia (propranolol), tardive dyskinesia (VMAT2 inhibitor); tardive dyskinesia may be irreversible","Clozapine is the most effective agent for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reduces suicide - but requires ANC monitoring for agranulocytosis (also myocarditis, seizures, ileus)","Akathisia (inner restlessness) is easily mistaken for worsening agitation/anxiety - do not simply raise the antipsychotic dose"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Antipsychotic Medications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519503/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health Medications","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-mood-stabilizers","name":"Mood Stabilizers (Lithium, Valproate, Lamotrigine, Carbamazepine)","sec":"psych","present":["LITHIUM: a first-line mood stabilizer, uniquely ANTI-SUICIDAL, with a NARROW THERAPEUTIC INDEX requiring serum-level monitoring; excreted renally (levels rise with dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, thiazides)","VALPROATE: effective for acute mania and mixed states; LAMOTRIGINE: best for bipolar DEPRESSION/maintenance (not acute mania); CARBAMAZEPINE: an alternative with significant interactions","Each carries distinctive toxicities and teratogenic risks central to safe prescribing"],"dx":["LITHIUM monitoring: serum levels plus baseline/periodic RENAL and THYROID function; toxicity (tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures) is a medical emergency; chronic effects include nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, hypothyroidism, and CKD; teratogenic (Ebstein anomaly)","VALPROATE: monitor LFTs and platelets; risks - hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, thrombocytopenia, hyperammonemia, and HIGH teratogenicity (neural tube defects) - avoid in people who may become pregnant when possible","LAMOTRIGINE: slow titration to avoid STEVENS-JOHNSON SYNDROME/TEN; CARBAMAZEPINE: agranulocytosis/aplastic anemia, hyponatremia (SIADH), autoinduction of metabolism, and SJS risk with HLA-B*1502 (screen in at-risk ancestry)"],"tx":["Match agent to phase: lithium/valproate for mania; lamotrigine for bipolar depression and maintenance; use levels and organ monitoring to dose safely","Manage lithium toxicity with hydration and, if severe, hemodialysis; hold nephrotoxic/level-raising agents; counsel on hydration and consistent sodium intake","Counsel on teratogenicity and contraception; prefer lamotrigine over valproate in those who may become pregnant; titrate lamotrigine slowly and stop at any rash"],"pearls":["Lithium is uniquely anti-suicidal but has a narrow therapeutic index - monitor levels, renal, and thyroid function; toxicity is an emergency","Lamotrigine treats bipolar DEPRESSION/maintenance (not acute mania) - titrate slowly to avoid Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Valproate is highly teratogenic (neural tube defects) and hepatotoxic - avoid in potential pregnancy; check LFTs and platelets","Carbamazepine autoinduces its own metabolism, causes hyponatremia and marrow suppression, and needs HLA-B*1502 screening in at-risk populations"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mood Stabilizers / Lithium","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519062/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health Medications","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-anxiolytics-hypnotics-stimulants","name":"Anxiolytics, Hypnotics & Stimulants","sec":"psych","present":["BENZODIAZEPINES (GABA-A modulators) act rapidly for anxiety, agitation, alcohol/sedative withdrawal, and seizures, but cause tolerance, DEPENDENCE, sedation, falls (especially elderly), and dangerous respiratory depression with opioids/alcohol - not for long-term anxiety","Non-benzodiazepine options: BUSPIRONE (non-sedating, non-dependence-forming, for GAD; delayed onset), 'Z-DRUGS' (zolpidem/eszopiclone for insomnia; complex sleep behaviors), and others","STIMULANTS (methylphenidate, amphetamines) treat ADHD/narcolepsy - Schedule II controlled substances with cardiovascular effects, appetite/growth/sleep impact, and DIVERSION/misuse potential"],"dx":["Benzodiazepines: use short-term/situationally; screen for substance use and co-prescribed opioids (overdose risk); the elderly are especially vulnerable (falls, cognitive impairment - on Beers criteria)","Stimulants: screen cardiac history/symptoms before starting; monitor blood pressure, heart rate, growth, sleep, and appetite; assess diversion risk and use of controlled-substance safeguards","Distinguish appropriate therapeutic use from developing misuse/dependence"],"tx":["Prefer SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT for chronic anxiety; reserve benzodiazepines for short-term/bridging use and taper to avoid withdrawal (seizures); flumazenil is generally avoided in dependent users","Insomnia: CBT-I first-line; use hypnotics briefly; counsel on Z-drug complex sleep behaviors","Stimulants: titrate to effect for ADHD; consider non-stimulants (atomoxetine, alpha-2 agonists) when misuse/diversion or cardiac concerns exist; monitor controlled-substance use"],"pearls":["Benzodiazepines are for short-term use - dependence, falls in the elderly, and lethal synergy with opioids/alcohol are the key hazards","Buspirone is a non-sedating, non-dependence-forming anxiolytic for GAD, but has a delayed onset (not for acute relief)","Stimulants are Schedule II - screen cardiac history, monitor vitals/growth/sleep, and manage diversion risk","CBT-I (not chronic hypnotics) is first-line for insomnia; non-stimulants are alternatives when stimulant misuse is a concern"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Anxiolytics / Stimulant Pharmacology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538501/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health Medications","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-psychotherapy","name":"Psychotherapy Modalities","sec":"psych","present":["Evidence-based psychotherapies are first-line or co-equal treatments across many disorders. COGNITIVE BEHAVIORAL THERAPY (CBT): structured, present-focused, targets maladaptive thoughts/behaviors - strong evidence in depression and anxiety; EXPOSURE-based CBT (and ERP) is core for phobias, panic, OCD, and PTSD","DIALECTICAL BEHAVIOR THERAPY (DBT): CBT-derived, for borderline PD and chronic self-harm (mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness). INTERPERSONAL THERAPY (IPT): time-limited, links symptoms to interpersonal roles/transitions (depression, perinatal)","PSYCHODYNAMIC therapy: explores unconscious patterns and past relationships; SUPPORTIVE therapy: bolsters coping/functioning; MOTIVATIONAL INTERVIEWING: resolves ambivalence (substance use)"],"dx":["Match modality to disorder and patient: CBT/behavioral for anxiety/OCD/PTSD/depression; DBT for borderline PD and self-harm; IPT for depression and role transitions; motivational interviewing for substance use","Consider evidence base, patient preference, insight, and goals; therapy is often combined with medication for moderate-severe illness","Trauma-focused therapies (CPT, prolonged exposure, EMDR) are the first-line for PTSD"],"tx":["Deliver or refer to the indicated modality; ensure fidelity and adequate 'dose' (course length); measure outcomes and adjust","Combine with pharmacotherapy where evidence supports it (e.g., moderate-severe depression, OCD)","Attend to the therapeutic alliance - a robust predictor of outcome across all modalities"],"pearls":["Exposure and response prevention (a CBT technique) is first-line for OCD; exposure-based CBT anchors anxiety/phobia/PTSD treatment","DBT is the flagship therapy for borderline personality disorder and chronic self-harm","IPT is time-limited and interpersonally focused - strong evidence in depression and perinatal depression","The therapeutic alliance predicts outcome across every modality - technique alone is not enough"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Psychotherapy / Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470241/"},{"t":"NIMH: Psychotherapies","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"psych-forensic-legal","name":"Forensic & Legal Psychiatry","sec":"psych","present":["Forensic psychiatry addresses the interface of psychiatry and the law. CIVIL COMMITMENT (involuntary hospitalization) generally requires a mental illness plus DANGER TO SELF, DANGER TO OTHERS, or GRAVE DISABILITY (inability to meet basic needs) - standards vary by jurisdiction and are time-limited with due-process protections","Distinguish CAPACITY (clinical, decision-specific) from COMPETENCY (legal/court determination); COMPETENCY TO STAND TRIAL uses the DUSKY standard (rational and factual understanding of proceedings and ability to assist counsel)","The insanity defense addresses CRIMINAL RESPONSIBILITY at the time of the offense (e.g., M'NAGHTEN rule - not knowing the nature/wrongfulness of the act) - separate from competency to stand trial (present ability)"],"dx":["Apply the relevant legal standard to the clinical question: dangerousness/grave disability for commitment; Dusky for trial competency; the applicable insanity standard for criminal responsibility","TARASOFF: a DUTY TO PROTECT (often via warning the identifiable victim and/or notifying authorities) when a patient poses a serious threat of violence to an identifiable person - a key limit on confidentiality","Understand mandatory reporting (child/elder abuse) and other exceptions to confidentiality"],"tx":["Balance patient autonomy and rights with safety and legal obligations; use the LEAST restrictive alternative and ensure due process in commitment","Fulfill Tarasoff duties per jurisdiction (warn/protect) and mandatory-reporting obligations; document reasoning carefully","Recognize the difference between the treating and forensic-evaluator roles (avoid dual-role conflicts); restore competency (treatment) when a defendant is found incompetent to stand trial"],"pearls":["Civil commitment generally needs mental illness PLUS danger to self, danger to others, or grave disability - the least restrictive, time-limited option with due process","Competency to stand trial = the Dusky standard (understand proceedings + assist counsel), a PRESENT ability - separate from the insanity defense (criminal responsibility at the time of the act, e.g., M'Naghten)","Tarasoff created a duty to protect identifiable potential victims - a recognized limit on confidentiality","Capacity is clinical and decision-specific; competency is a legal determination by a court"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Involuntary Commitment / Forensic Psychiatry","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555922/"},{"t":"NIMH: Mental Health & the Law","u":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov"}]},{"id":"radonc-physics-fundamentals","name":"Radiation Physics Fundamentals","sec":"radonc","present":["External-beam radiotherapy mostly uses high-energy PHOTONS (x-rays) from a linear accelerator (typically 6-18 MV) and ELECTRONS for superficial targets; understanding how each deposits dose is foundational to safe planning","PHOTONS deposit dose via a build-up region then near-exponential attenuation - higher energies are more penetrating and more skin-sparing (dose maximum, Dmax, is deeper); ELECTRONS give a relatively uniform dose to a finite depth then a rapid fall-off, ideal for sparing deeper tissue","Key beam descriptors: percent depth dose (PDD), tissue-maximum ratio (TMR), and the inverse-square law governing dose vs distance from the source"],"dx":["Characterize beams by ENERGY (skin-sparing and penetration rise with photon energy) and by depth-dose behavior (PDD/TMR); electron practical range is approximately energy in MeV divided by 2 (in cm)","Dose is measured in GRAY (Gy = 1 J/kg); monitor units are calibrated so output is defined under reference conditions; the inverse-square law means dose falls with the square of distance from the source","Recognize where each modality excels: photons for deep targets, electrons for lesions within a few cm of the surface"],"tx":["Choose photon energy by depth (higher MV for deep/central targets, lower for superficial); use bolus to bring dose to the skin surface when the target is superficial (build-up otherwise spares skin)","Use electrons for superficial targets (skin, scar boost, nodes) - select energy so the prescribed isodose covers depth while sparing deeper structures","Combine beam arrangement, energy, and modifiers (wedges, bolus, blocks/MLC) to shape dose to the target while respecting the inverse-square and attenuation physics"],"pearls":["Gray (Gy) = 1 joule per kilogram - the SI unit of absorbed dose","Higher photon energy = more penetration AND more skin-sparing (deeper Dmax) - bolus is added to remove skin-sparing when the skin/surface is the target","Electron range (cm) is roughly energy (MeV)/2, with a sharp distal fall-off that spares deeper tissue","Dose obeys the inverse-square law - it falls with the square of the distance from the source"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiation Therapy Physics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564355/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy to Treat Cancer","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-brachytherapy-physics","name":"Brachytherapy Physics & Sources","sec":"radonc","present":["Brachytherapy places sealed radioactive SOURCES in or next to the tumor, exploiting the inverse-square law to deliver a very high local dose with rapid fall-off that spares nearby normal tissue","Delivery modes: LOW-DOSE-RATE (LDR, continuous over hours-days, e.g., prostate seeds), HIGH-DOSE-RATE (HDR, a stepping source over minutes via remote afterloading), and pulsed-dose-rate; placement can be interstitial, intracavitary, or surface","Common isotopes: Ir-192 (HDR), I-125 and Pd-103 (LDR prostate seeds), Cs-131, and historically Cs-137/Ra-226 (obsolete)"],"dx":["Know isotope properties - Ir-192 (~74 day half-life, HDR workhorse), I-125 (~60 days), Pd-103 (~17 days); half-life and photon energy determine shielding and dose delivery","Dose specification and reporting: modern GYN brachytherapy uses image-based (MRI/CT) volumetric planning (GEC-ESTRO) rather than the older Point A/Manchester system alone","The steep dose gradient is the defining advantage (and risk) - small displacements change dose substantially"],"tx":["HDR uses remote AFTERLOADING (source travels from a shielded safe into catheters) - this protects staff from exposure, a key radiation-safety advance over manual afterloading","Apply to appropriate sites: prostate (LDR seeds/HDR), cervix/endometrium (intracavitary), breast, skin, and interstitial implants; combine with EBRT where indicated (e.g., cervix)","Verify source position/applicator geometry with imaging before delivery; the inverse-square gradient demands precise placement"],"pearls":["Brachytherapy's power is the inverse-square dose fall-off - high tumor dose, rapid normal-tissue sparing","Ir-192 is the HDR workhorse; I-125 and Pd-103 are the LDR prostate seed isotopes","HDR remote afterloading protects staff by loading the source only after applicators are placed and staff have left the room","Image-based (MRI) volumetric planning has largely superseded the classic Point A system for cervical brachytherapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Brachytherapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562190/"},{"t":"NCI: Brachytherapy (Internal Radiation)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-radiobiology-5rs","name":"Radiobiology - The 5 R's & Cell Kill","sec":"radonc","present":["Ionizing radiation kills cells mainly by DNA damage - especially DOUBLE-STRAND BREAKS; photons act largely INDIRECTLY via free radicals (hydroxyl radicals from water), which is why molecular OXYGEN fixes damage and radiosensitizes","The differential effect of fractionation on tumor vs normal tissue is explained by the classic 5 R's of radiobiology","Cell survival is described by the linear-quadratic model; most tumor cells die a MITOTIC (clonogenic) death when they attempt to divide with unrepaired damage"],"dx":["The 5 R's: REPAIR (of sublethal damage between fractions - favors normal tissue), REASSORTMENT/redistribution (cells move into radiosensitive phases, G2/M sensitive, late S resistant), REPOPULATION (tumor regrowth during a course - accelerated after ~3-4 weeks), REOXYGENATION (hypoxic tumor regions reoxygenate between fractions), and RADIOSENSITIVITY","OXYGEN is the most important radiosensitizer (oxygen enhancement ratio ~2.5-3 for photons); hypoxic tumors are more resistant","Recognize how each R is exploited by fractionating dose over time"],"tx":["FRACTIONATION exploits the 5 R's: dividing dose lets normal tissue repair and repopulate while tumor undergoes reoxygenation and redistribution - widening the therapeutic window","Avoid unnecessary treatment PROLONGATION in rapidly proliferating tumors (e.g., head and neck, cervix) because accelerated repopulation reduces control - complete treatment on schedule","Strategies target the R's: hypoxic modifiers, altered fractionation (accelerated/hyperfractionated), and concurrent chemo as radiosensitizers"],"pearls":["The 5 R's: Repair, Reassortment (redistribution), Repopulation, Reoxygenation, Radiosensitivity","Oxygen is the key radiosensitizer - hypoxic cells are ~3x more resistant (photons act indirectly via free radicals)","Cells are most radiosensitive in G2/M and most resistant in late S phase","Accelerated repopulation begins ~3-4 weeks into treatment - do not prolong courses in rapidly proliferating tumors (head and neck, cervix)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiobiology","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551659/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy Biology","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-fractionation-lq","name":"Fractionation, Dose-Rate & the Linear-Quadratic Model","sec":"radonc","present":["The LINEAR-QUADRATIC (LQ) model describes cell survival with two components - alpha (linear, single-hit lethal, dominant at low dose/fraction) and beta (quadratic, accumulated sublethal damage, dominant at high dose/fraction)","The ALPHA/BETA RATIO captures fractionation sensitivity: LOW alpha/beta (~2-3 Gy) = late-responding normal tissues (spinal cord, lung, kidney) and some tumors (prostate, breast) that are sensitive to dose PER FRACTION; HIGH alpha/beta (~10 Gy) = early-responding tissues and most tumors","Biologically effective dose (BED) and the equivalent-dose-in-2-Gy-fractions (EQD2) let planners compare different fractionation schemes"],"dx":["Compute BED = nd x [1 + d/(alpha/beta)] to compare regimens and convert with EQD2; late-responding tissues (low alpha/beta) are disproportionately harmed by LARGE fraction sizes","Standard fractionation is ~1.8-2 Gy/fraction/day; HYPOFRACTIONATION (larger fractions, fewer days) exploits low tumor alpha/beta (prostate, breast); HYPERFRACTIONATION (smaller, more frequent fractions) spares late tissue while escalating tumor dose","Recognize that dose per fraction, not just total dose, drives late toxicity"],"tx":["Use hypofractionation where the tumor alpha/beta is low relative to late normal tissue (prostate ~1.5, breast ~4) - fewer, larger fractions maintain control with acceptable late toxicity","Use hyperfractionation/acceleration in select head and neck cancers to improve control while limiting late effects","Respect fraction-size limits for low-alpha/beta serial organs (e.g., spinal cord) - large fractions carry disproportionate late-toxicity risk"],"pearls":["Low alpha/beta (~3) = late-responding tissues and prostate/breast tumors - sensitive to dose PER FRACTION (favors hypofractionation)","High alpha/beta (~10) = early-responding tissue and most tumors","BED = nd[1 + d/(alpha/beta)] - the key equation to compare fractionation schemes","Large fraction sizes preferentially increase LATE toxicity in serial organs like the spinal cord"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiation Dose Fractionation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538292/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy Fractionation","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-normal-tissue-tolerance","name":"Normal-Tissue Tolerance & Organ-at-Risk Constraints","sec":"radonc","present":["Every treatment plan balances tumor dose against the tolerance of nearby ORGANS AT RISK (OARs); the QUANTEC reviews compiled dose-volume constraints that guide modern planning","Organ ARCHITECTURE predicts toxicity pattern: SERIAL organs (spinal cord, brainstem, bowel) fail if any small segment exceeds tolerance (max-dose limited), whereas PARALLEL organs (lung, liver, kidney, parotid) tolerate high dose to a portion if enough functional volume is spared (mean-dose/volume limited)","Toxicities are EARLY (acute, during/soon after treatment, in rapidly dividing tissue - mucosa, skin, marrow) or LATE (months-years, fibrosis/vascular, often irreversible)"],"dx":["Apply representative constraints (approximate, standard fractionation): spinal cord max ~50 Gy (myelopathy risk rises above), optic apparatus ~54-55 Gy, brainstem ~54 Gy, whole lung V20 <~30-35% and mean <~20 Gy, heart mean as low as achievable, kidney mean <~18 Gy (bilateral), parotid mean <~26 Gy (xerostomia)","Distinguish serial (limit MAX dose) from parallel (limit MEAN dose/volume) organs when setting objectives","Evaluate plans with dose-volume histograms (DVHs) against these thresholds"],"tx":["Design plans (IMRT/VMAT, arrangement, image guidance) to meet OAR constraints while covering the target - accept the highest achievable tumor dose within tolerance","Spare parallel organs by limiting the volume irradiated (e.g., parotid-sparing IMRT to reduce xerostomia; lung V20 to reduce pneumonitis); protect serial organs by capping maximum point dose","Track cumulative dose in re-irradiation, accounting for partial recovery and prior dose to serial organs"],"pearls":["Serial organs (cord, brainstem, bowel) are MAX-dose limited; parallel organs (lung, liver, kidney, parotid) are MEAN-dose/volume limited","Spinal cord tolerance is ~50 Gy at standard fractionation - exceeding it risks radiation myelopathy","Lung V20 (<~30-35%) and mean lung dose predict pneumonitis; parotid mean <~26 Gy limits xerostomia","Early toxicity hits rapidly dividing tissue (mucosa, skin, marrow); late toxicity (fibrosis) is often irreversible and driven by fraction size"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiation Therapy Toxicity / Tolerance","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576352/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy Side Effects","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy/side-effects"}]},{"id":"radonc-treatment-planning","name":"Treatment Planning & Delivery (3D-CRT, IMRT/VMAT, IGRT, Protons)","sec":"radonc","present":["Modern radiotherapy is planned on a CT SIMULATION (with immobilization and often IV/oral contrast and 4D-CT for moving targets); MRI/PET are fused for target definition","Target volumes (ICRU): GROSS TUMOR VOLUME (GTV, visible disease), CLINICAL TARGET VOLUME (CTV, GTV plus microscopic spread), PLANNING TARGET VOLUME (PTV, CTV plus setup/motion margin), and organs at risk","Delivery techniques range from 3D-CRT (shaped static fields) to INTENSITY-MODULATED RT (IMRT/VMAT, highly conformal with steep gradients) with IMAGE-GUIDED RT (IGRT) for daily positioning; protons offer a distinct dose distribution"],"dx":["Define volumes systematically: GTV -> CTV (anatomic/microscopic margin) -> PTV (geometric margin for setup/motion); internal target volume (ITV) accounts for respiratory motion on 4D-CT","Choose technique by conformality needs: IMRT/VMAT for concave targets near critical organs (head and neck, prostate); IGRT (cone-beam CT, fiducials, surface guidance) reduces setup error and enables tighter margins","Understand proton depth-dose (the BRAGG PEAK) - dose deposits at a defined depth with little exit dose, sparing distal tissue"],"tx":["Select the least complex technique that meets objectives: 3D-CRT for simple/palliative targets, IMRT/VMAT for complex conformal cases; add IGRT for precision and margin reduction","Manage motion (4D-CT, breath-hold, gating, abdominal compression) especially for thoracic/upper-abdominal targets; verify daily positioning with IGRT","Consider protons where the Bragg peak's normal-tissue sparing matters most (pediatrics, reirradiation, tumors adjacent to critical serial organs)"],"pearls":["Volume hierarchy: GTV (visible) -> CTV (+ microscopic) -> PTV (+ setup/motion margin); ITV adds respiratory motion","IMRT/VMAT gives highly conformal dose with steep gradients - ideal for concave targets wrapped around organs at risk","IGRT (cone-beam CT/fiducials) reduces setup uncertainty and permits smaller margins","Protons deposit dose at the Bragg peak with minimal exit dose - most valuable in pediatrics and near critical serial organs"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559034/"},{"t":"NCI: External Beam Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-sbrt-srs","name":"SBRT & Radiosurgery (SRS) Principles","sec":"radonc","present":["STEREOTACTIC RADIOSURGERY (SRS, intracranial) and STEREOTACTIC BODY RT (SBRT/SABR, extracranial) deliver very high doses in 1-5 fractions with sub-millimeter/millimeter precision and steep dose fall-off","Defining features: high dose per fraction (ablative), tight margins, rigorous immobilization and image guidance, and multiple beams/arcs converging on the target","Radiobiology at ablative doses may differ from the classic LQ model (possible vascular/endothelial and immunogenic effects) - the therapeutic ratio depends on precision, not fractionation-based sparing"],"dx":["Patient/target selection is critical: small, well-defined targets with limited motion; SRS for brain metastases (limited number), small AVMs, acoustic neuroma, trigeminal neuralgia, functioning/nonfunctioning adenomas; SBRT for early lung, liver, spine, oligometastases, and select prostate/pancreas","Require robust immobilization, image guidance (cone-beam CT, fiducials), and motion management; strict dose constraints because ablative doses are unforgiving","Verify no overlap with serial-organ tolerance (e.g., cord for spine SBRT, brainstem for SRS)"],"tx":["Deliver ablative regimens (e.g., single-fraction SRS for small brain metastases; ~3-5 fraction SBRT for lung/liver/spine); prescribe to a covering isodose with rapid dose gradient","Match dose/fractionation to site and adjacent organs (e.g., central lung tumors need risk-adapted multi-fraction SBRT to protect airways/vessels - avoid single-fraction near the proximal bronchial tree)","Use SBRT for medically inoperable early-stage disease and for oligometastatic ablation; follow with close imaging"],"pearls":["SBRT/SRS = ablative dose in 1-5 fractions with steep gradients - success depends on precision, immobilization, and image guidance","Central/ultra-central lung tumors require risk-adapted multi-fraction SBRT (not single-fraction) to protect the proximal bronchial tree and great vessels","SRS is standard for a limited number of brain metastases and for benign targets (acoustic neuroma, trigeminal neuralgia, AVM)","At ablative doses, vascular/endothelial and possible immunogenic mechanisms contribute beyond classic LQ cell kill"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564345/"},{"t":"NCI: Stereotactic Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-glioblastoma","name":"High-Grade Glioma (Glioblastoma) & the Stupp Protocol","sec":"radonc","present":["Glioblastoma (IDH-wildtype, CNS WHO grade 4) is the most common malignant primary brain tumor in adults - highly infiltrative, with a poor prognosis despite trimodality therapy","Presents with progressive neurologic deficits, headache, seizures, or signs of raised intracranial pressure; MRI shows a ring-enhancing lesion with surrounding non-enhancing infiltrative tumor and edema","Molecular markers are prognostic/predictive: IDH status (wildtype defines true glioblastoma), MGMT promoter METHYLATION (predicts temozolomide benefit)"],"dx":["Diagnose by MRI + maximal safe RESECTION for histology and molecular testing (IDH, MGMT, and integrated WHO 2021 classification); biopsy if unresectable","Assess extent of resection (prognostic), performance status, age, and MGMT methylation to guide therapy","Distinguish from brain metastasis, lymphoma, and abscess; note glioblastoma rarely metastasizes outside the CNS but spreads along white-matter tracts"],"tx":["STUPP PROTOCOL: maximal safe resection, then CONCURRENT chemoradiation - 60 Gy in 30 fractions with daily TEMOZOLOMIDE - followed by adjuvant temozolomide; tumor-treating fields can be added in the adjuvant setting","RT target is the resection cavity/residual enhancing tumor plus a margin covering infiltrative disease (guided by T2/FLAIR); typical ~2 cm CTV margin, respecting anatomic barriers","ELDERLY/poor performance status: HYPOFRACTIONATED RT (e.g., 40 Gy/15) with temozolomide (especially if MGMT methylated) improves tolerability"],"pearls":["Stupp: resection -> 60 Gy/30 with concurrent daily temozolomide -> adjuvant temozolomide","MGMT promoter methylation predicts temozolomide benefit and better prognosis; IDH-wildtype defines glioblastoma","Elderly/frail patients benefit from hypofractionated RT (40 Gy/15) - often with temozolomide if MGMT methylated","Glioblastoma is infiltrative - target FLAIR/T2 abnormality with margin; it recurs locally and rarely leaves the CNS"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Glioblastoma Multiforme","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558954/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult Central Nervous System Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-low-grade-glioma","name":"Low-Grade Glioma","sec":"radonc","present":["WHO grade 2 diffuse gliomas (e.g., IDH-mutant astrocytoma and oligodendroglioma) - infiltrative but slower-growing than high-grade tumors, typically in younger adults","Often present with SEIZURES and relatively preserved function; MRI shows a non-enhancing T2/FLAIR-hyperintense lesion (enhancement suggests higher grade)","MOLECULAR markers drive classification/prognosis: IDH mutation (favorable) and 1p/19q CODELETION (defines oligodendroglioma, better prognosis and chemosensitivity)"],"dx":["Diagnose with MRI and resection/biopsy for integrated histologic + molecular diagnosis (IDH, 1p/19q, ATRX, WHO 2021)","Risk-stratify (age, extent of resection, tumor size, neurologic function) into low vs high risk to guide timing of adjuvant therapy","Distinguish oligodendroglioma (IDH-mutant, 1p/19q codeleted) from astrocytoma (IDH-mutant, ATRX-lost, 1p/19q intact)"],"tx":["Maximal safe RESECTION is first-line and prognostic; low-risk fully-resected young patients may be observed","HIGH-RISK disease (older, subtotal resection, larger/symptomatic): postoperative RT (~50.4-54 Gy) followed by chemotherapy - the RTOG 9802 trial showed RT + PCV (procarbazine/CCNU/vincristine) improves survival over RT alone","Typical RT dose ~50.4-54 Gy in 1.8-2 Gy fractions to the T2/FLAIR abnormality plus margin; balance against long-term neurocognitive effects in these long-survivors"],"pearls":["1p/19q codeletion + IDH mutation defines oligodendroglioma - better prognosis and chemosensitivity","RTOG 9802: adding PCV chemotherapy to RT improves survival in high-risk low-grade glioma","Low-grade glioma dose is ~50.4-54 Gy - lower than glioblastoma's 60 Gy - to limit late neurocognitive toxicity in long survivors","Enhancement on MRI or rapid growth suggests transformation to higher grade"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Low-Grade Glioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560668/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult CNS Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-brain-metastases","name":"Brain Metastases - WBRT, SRS & Hippocampal Avoidance","sec":"radonc","present":["Brain metastases are the most common intracranial tumors in adults - commonly from lung, breast, melanoma, renal, and colorectal primaries","Present with headache, focal deficits, seizures, or cognitive/behavioral change; contrast MRI is the imaging standard (multiple enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction with edema)","Management depends on NUMBER and SIZE of lesions, primary histology/control, performance status, and systemic options"],"dx":["Characterize number, size, and location on MRI; assess extracranial disease control, performance status, and prognosis (e.g., GPA/graded prognostic assessment)","Consider resection for large/symptomatic single lesions (then cavity SRS); determine SRS vs whole-brain candidacy","Account for histology-specific factors (e.g., some drive leptomeningeal spread; targeted agents penetrate the CNS in select tumors)"],"tx":["LIMITED brain metastases: STEREOTACTIC RADIOSURGERY (SRS) - focal, brain-sparing, and preferred to avoid the neurocognitive decline of whole-brain RT; resect large symptomatic lesions and give postoperative CAVITY SRS","DIFFUSE/numerous metastases or leptomeningeal disease: WHOLE-BRAIN RT (e.g., 30 Gy/10 or 20 Gy/5); use HIPPOCAMPAL-AVOIDANCE WBRT with MEMANTINE to reduce neurocognitive toxicity (NRG CC001)","Integrate CNS-active systemic therapy where available; omit WBRT after SRS in many cases to preserve cognition (SRS alone with MRI surveillance)"],"pearls":["SRS is preferred for a limited number of brain metastases - it spares cognition versus whole-brain RT","Hippocampal-avoidance WBRT plus memantine (NRG CC001) reduces neurocognitive decline when WBRT is needed","Resect large symptomatic lesions, then treat the cavity with SRS to improve local control","Adding WBRT after SRS improves intracranial control but not survival and worsens cognition - often omitted with MRI surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Metastasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470246/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult CNS Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-meningioma","name":"Meningioma & Benign CNS Tumors","sec":"radonc","present":["Meningiomas arise from arachnoid cap cells and are the most common primary intracranial tumor - usually BENIGN (WHO grade 1), extra-axial, dural-based, and slow-growing; grade 2 (atypical) and grade 3 (malignant) are more aggressive","Present incidentally or with site-dependent deficits, seizures, or mass effect; MRI shows an enhancing dural-based mass with a DURAL TAIL","Other benign CNS targets for RT include vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) and pituitary adenoma"],"dx":["Diagnose by characteristic MRI (dural-based, homogeneously enhancing, dural tail); grade is established at resection when performed","Stratify by grade and resection extent (Simpson grade); grade drives adjuvant therapy decisions","Distinguish from dural metastasis, hemangiopericytoma/solitary fibrous tumor, and schwannoma"],"tx":["Grade 1: observation for small/asymptomatic lesions; RESECTION for symptomatic/growing tumors; RT (fractionated or SRS for small lesions) for unresectable, recurrent, or subtotally resected tumors - typical fractionated dose ~50.4-54 Gy","Grade 2 (atypical): resection, with postoperative RT (~54-60 Gy) especially after subtotal resection or recurrence; Grade 3 (malignant): resection + postoperative RT ~60 Gy","Vestibular schwannoma and secretory/nonsecretory pituitary adenoma: SRS or fractionated RT achieve high control while preserving function; respect optic apparatus tolerance"],"pearls":["Dural tail + extra-axial enhancing mass = meningioma; most are benign WHO grade 1","Small asymptomatic grade 1 meningiomas can be observed; RT (~50.4-54 Gy) or SRS treats unresectable/recurrent disease","Atypical (grade 2 ~54-60 Gy) and malignant (grade 3 ~60 Gy) meningiomas get higher postoperative doses","SRS treats vestibular schwannoma and pituitary adenoma with high control and function preservation - keep optic apparatus <~54-55 Gy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Meningioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560538/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult CNS Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-cord-compression-spine-sbrt","name":"Spinal Cord Compression & Spine SBRT","sec":"radonc","present":["MALIGNANT EPIDURAL SPINAL CORD COMPRESSION is an oncologic EMERGENCY - tumor (usually vertebral metastasis from lung, breast, prostate) compresses the cord/cauda equina, threatening irreversible neurologic loss","Presents with PROGRESSIVE BACK PAIN (often before neurologic signs), then weakness, sensory level, and bowel/bladder dysfunction; pre-treatment AMBULATORY status is the strongest predictor of outcome","Urgent MRI of the WHOLE spine is required (multilevel disease is common)"],"dx":["Obtain emergent MRI of the entire spine; start corticosteroids (dexamethasone) immediately on clinical suspicion to reduce edema","Assess spinal stability (SINS score) and the surgical candidacy of the patient; determine radiosensitivity of the histology","Time is critical - neurologic recovery depends on rapid decompression before deficits become fixed"],"tx":["Start DEXAMETHASONE at once; for a good-surgical-candidate with a stable-enough spine and single-level compression, SURGICAL DECOMPRESSION followed by RT outperforms RT alone (Patchell trial)","RT for most patients: conventional palliative regimens (e.g., 8 Gy x1, 20 Gy/5, or 30 Gy/10) provide pain relief and local control for radiosensitive tumors","SPINE SBRT delivers ablative dose for RADIORESISTANT histologies (renal, melanoma), oligometastatic disease, or reirradiation - requires strict cord dose constraints and stability assessment"],"pearls":["Malignant cord compression is an emergency - give dexamethasone immediately and get whole-spine MRI; ambulatory status at treatment predicts outcome","Patchell trial: surgery + RT beats RT alone in selected patients with a single area of compression and good performance status","Palliative RT (8 Gy x1 or 30 Gy/10) relieves pain; spine SBRT is for radioresistant/oligometastatic/reirradiation cases with strict cord constraints","New back pain in a cancer patient is cord compression until proven otherwise - image before deficits appear"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Cord Compression","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557604/"},{"t":"NCI: Spinal Cord Compression (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects"}]},{"id":"radonc-oropharynx-hpv","name":"Oropharyngeal Cancer (HPV-Associated) & Chemoradiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (tonsil, base of tongue, soft palate) is increasingly driven by HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS (HPV/p16-positive), which carries a substantially BETTER prognosis than HPV-negative/smoking-related disease","Presents with a painless neck mass (cystic node), sore throat, dysphagia, or otalgia; p16 immunohistochemistry is the standard surrogate for HPV status","HPV-positive disease has its own AJCC staging (downstaged relative to HPV-negative) reflecting its favorable outcome"],"dx":["Work up with exam/endoscopy, biopsy, p16/HPV testing, and cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) plus PET for staging; FNA of neck nodes","Stage per HPV-specific AJCC criteria; assess smoking history (worsens HPV-positive prognosis)","Distinguish HPV-positive (better prognosis) from HPV-negative disease - it changes prognosis and trial eligibility"],"tx":["Early-stage: single-modality (definitive RT or transoral surgery); LOCALLY ADVANCED: definitive CONCURRENT CHEMORADIATION - ~70 Gy in 35 fractions (2 Gy/day) with concurrent CISPLATIN (high-dose q3-weekly)","Use IMRT to spare parotids (xerostomia), constrictors (dysphagia), and cord; cover primary and bilateral neck as indicated","De-escalation for favorable HPV-positive disease is under active study but cisplatin should NOT be replaced by cetuximab in fit patients (RTOG 1016/De-ESCALaTE showed cetuximab is inferior)"],"pearls":["HPV/p16-positive oropharyngeal cancer has a markedly better prognosis and its own (downstaged) AJCC staging","Definitive chemoradiation = ~70 Gy/35 with concurrent high-dose cisplatin","Do NOT substitute cetuximab for cisplatin in cisplatin-eligible patients - RTOG 1016 showed inferior control with cetuximab","IMRT spares parotids and constrictors to reduce xerostomia and dysphagia; smoking worsens even HPV-positive outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Oropharyngeal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567715/"},{"t":"NCI: Oropharyngeal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/hp/adult/oropharyngeal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-larynx-hypopharynx","name":"Larynx & Hypopharynx Cancer - Organ Preservation","sec":"radonc","present":["Laryngeal (glottic, supraglottic, subglottic) and hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinomas are strongly SMOKING/alcohol-related; hypopharyngeal disease presents late with a worse prognosis","GLOTTIC cancers present EARLY with hoarseness (few lymphatics -> low nodal risk when early); supraglottic/hypopharyngeal tumors present with dysphagia, odynophagia, referred otalgia, or a neck mass and have rich nodal drainage","Organ (voice) preservation is a central goal of nonsurgical management"],"dx":["Evaluate with laryngoscopy, biopsy, and CT/MRI (plus PET for advanced disease); assess vocal cord mobility and cartilage invasion","Stage per AJCC; early glottic (T1-2N0) has excellent control; assess candidacy for larynx-preserving therapy vs total laryngectomy","Nodal risk is low for early glottic (elective neck often unnecessary) but high for supraglottic/hypopharyngeal disease"],"tx":["EARLY GLOTTIC (T1-2N0): definitive RT alone with a small field and HYPOFRACTIONATION (e.g., ~63 Gy/28 for T1, ~65.25 Gy/29 for T2, at ~2.25 Gy/fraction) gives high control with voice preservation; transoral laser surgery is an alternative","LOCALLY ADVANCED larynx: ORGAN-PRESERVATION concurrent chemoRT (~70 Gy with cisplatin) per RTOG 91-11 (concurrent chemoRT superior for larynx preservation to induction or RT alone); reserve total laryngectomy for bulky T4/cartilage destruction or salvage","Hypopharynx: chemoRT or surgery + adjuvant therapy; cover the primary and bilateral necks given high nodal risk"],"pearls":["Early glottic cancer is treated with small-field hypofractionated RT (higher dose per fraction, e.g., 2.25 Gy) for excellent control and voice preservation","RTOG 91-11: concurrent chemoRT gives the best larynx-preservation rate (superior to induction chemo or RT alone)","Early glottic tumors have low nodal risk (sparse lymphatics) - elective nodal RT often omitted; supraglottic/hypopharyngeal need bilateral neck coverage","Total laryngectomy is reserved for bulky T4 with cartilage invasion or for salvage after failed organ preservation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Laryngeal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526076/"},{"t":"NCI: Laryngeal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/hp/adult/laryngeal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-nasopharynx","name":"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","sec":"radonc","present":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is distinct from other head and neck cancers - strongly associated with EPSTEIN-BARR VIRUS (EBV) and endemic in Southern China/Southeast Asia; it is highly radiosensitive","Presents with a NECK MASS (early nodal spread), epistaxis/nasal obstruction, serous otitis media (Eustachian tube blockage), and cranial nerve palsies with skull-base extension","Plasma EBV DNA correlates with tumor burden and prognosis and is used for monitoring"],"dx":["Diagnose with nasopharyngoscopy and biopsy; MRI of the nasopharynx/skull base plus PET/CT for staging; measure plasma EBV DNA","Assess skull-base and intracranial extension and the (frequently bilateral, retropharyngeal) nodal spread","Stage per AJCC; the anatomy (deep, near critical structures) makes surgery impractical - RT is the mainstay"],"tx":["Definitive treatment is RADIOTHERAPY (IMRT) because of radiosensitivity and surgically inaccessible anatomy - ~70 Gy to gross disease with elective nodal coverage","LOCALLY ADVANCED disease: concurrent chemoRT with CISPLATIN plus adjuvant or induction chemotherapy improves outcomes (Intergroup 0099 established concurrent + adjuvant chemo)","IMRT is essential to cover the skull base while sparing brainstem, optic apparatus, temporal lobes, cochlea, and parotids; monitor EBV DNA for response/relapse"],"pearls":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is EBV-associated and highly radiosensitive - RT (IMRT) is the definitive local treatment, not surgery","Presenting signs: neck mass, epistaxis, unilateral serous otitis media, and cranial neuropathies","Intergroup 0099: concurrent cisplatin chemoRT plus adjuvant chemotherapy for locally advanced disease","Plasma EBV DNA tracks tumor burden and is used for prognosis and surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459256/"},{"t":"NCI: Nasopharyngeal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/hp/adult/nasopharyngeal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-oral-cavity","name":"Oral Cavity Cancer & Postoperative Radiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Oral cavity squamous cell carcinoma (tongue, floor of mouth, buccal mucosa, gingiva, hard palate, lip) is smoking/alcohol/betel-nut related; unlike oropharynx, it is primarily managed with SURGERY first","Presents with a non-healing ulcer/mass, pain, or bleeding; the oral tongue is the most common site and readily accessible for exam and biopsy","Nodal spread to levels I-III is common; depth of invasion predicts nodal risk"],"dx":["Evaluate with exam, biopsy, and CT/MRI; assess depth of invasion, bone involvement, and nodal status; PET for advanced disease","Determine surgical resectability - primary resection with neck dissection is standard; identify adverse pathologic features that mandate adjuvant therapy","Stage per AJCC (depth of invasion now incorporated into T category)"],"tx":["Primary SURGERY (resection + neck dissection) is the standard; POSTOPERATIVE RT (~60-66 Gy) is indicated for adverse features: positive/close margins, extranodal extension, multiple positive nodes, perineural/lymphovascular invasion, or advanced T stage","Add CONCURRENT CISPLATIN to postoperative RT for POSITIVE MARGINS or EXTRANODAL EXTENSION - the two 'high-risk' features from the EORTC 22931 / RTOG 9501 trials that mandate chemoradiation","Start postoperative RT promptly (ideally within ~6 weeks) and complete the whole package (surgery-to-RT-end) without undue delay to maximize control"],"pearls":["Oral cavity cancer is surgery-first (unlike oropharynx, which is often chemoRT)","Postoperative concurrent cisplatin is added for POSITIVE MARGINS or EXTRANODAL EXTENSION (EORTC 22931 / RTOG 9501)","Adverse features prompting postoperative RT: close/positive margins, extranodal extension, multiple nodes, perineural/lymphovascular invasion, advanced T","Minimize total 'package time' from surgery to RT completion - delays reduce control"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Oral Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567775/"},{"t":"NCI: Lip and Oral Cavity Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/hp/adult/lip-mouth-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-hn-emergencies-salivary-thyroid","name":"Head & Neck Emergencies & Salivary/Thyroid Tumors","sec":"radonc","present":["Head and neck RT carries specific EMERGENCIES: airway compromise from tumor/edema, CAROTID BLOWOUT (hemorrhage, especially after reirradiation or surgery), and severe mucositis/aspiration","SALIVARY GLAND tumors (parotid most common; smaller glands have higher malignancy rates) include mucoepidermoid and adenoid cystic carcinoma - the latter notorious for PERINEURAL SPREAD and late distant relapse","Well-differentiated THYROID cancer is usually treated with surgery and radioactive iodine; external-beam RT has a limited, selected role"],"dx":["Recognize impending airway obstruction and carotid blowout (sentinel bleeds) as emergencies; assess salivary tumor histology/grade and perineural involvement (MRI for named-nerve spread in adenoid cystic carcinoma)","For thyroid cancer, distinguish differentiated (papillary/follicular - iodine-avid) from medullary and anaplastic (aggressive) types","Stage and grade to determine the role of postoperative RT"],"tx":["Manage emergencies: secure the airway; carotid blowout requires immediate pressure/resuscitation and endovascular intervention","Salivary malignancies: SURGERY with postoperative RT for high grade, positive margins, perineural invasion, or advanced stage; adenoid cystic carcinoma often needs RT covering named nerves toward the skull base; neutron therapy has a historical role","THYROID: differentiated cancer -> thyroidectomy + radioactive iodine (I-131); reserve external-beam RT for gross unresectable/recurrent disease; ANAPLASTIC thyroid cancer needs urgent multimodality care including RT"],"pearls":["Carotid blowout (herald bleeds after surgery/reirradiation) and airway compromise are head and neck emergencies","Adenoid cystic carcinoma spreads PERINEURALLY and relapses late/distantly - cover named nerves toward the skull base with postoperative RT","Smaller salivary glands have a HIGHER proportion of malignant tumors than the parotid","Differentiated thyroid cancer uses I-131 (radioactive iodine), not external-beam RT, in most cases; anaplastic thyroid cancer is a multimodality emergency"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Salivary Gland Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563220/"},{"t":"NCI: Salivary Gland Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/head-and-neck/hp/adult/salivary-gland-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-nsclc","name":"Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer - Early-Stage & Locally Advanced","sec":"radonc","present":["Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC; adenocarcinoma, squamous, large cell) is staged by AJCC TNM and managed by stage: surgery for early operable disease, SBRT for inoperable early-stage, and concurrent chemoRT +/- immunotherapy for locally advanced (stage III)","Presents with cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, weight loss, or is found incidentally; workup requires tissue diagnosis, PET/CT, brain MRI, and molecular/PD-L1 testing","Mediastinal nodal status (often via EBUS) is pivotal for staging and treatment selection"],"dx":["Stage with PET/CT and brain MRI; obtain tissue for histology, driver mutations (EGFR, ALK, etc.), and PD-L1; evaluate mediastinal nodes (EBUS/mediastinoscopy)","Assess operability (pulmonary function, cardiac status) - medically inoperable early-stage patients are SBRT candidates","Distinguish resectable stage III from unresectable stage III (drives surgery vs definitive chemoRT)"],"tx":["EARLY-STAGE operable: surgery (lobectomy + nodal sampling); MEDICALLY INOPERABLE early-stage (T1-2N0): SBRT (see lung SBRT entry)","LOCALLY ADVANCED unresectable stage III: definitive CONCURRENT CHEMORADIATION - ~60 Gy in 30 fractions with platinum-doublet chemotherapy - followed by consolidation DURVALUMAB (anti-PD-L1) per the PACIFIC trial, which improved survival","Respect lung (V20, mean lung dose), heart, cord, and esophagus constraints; dose escalation beyond 60 Gy did not help (RTOG 0617 - 74 Gy arm was worse)"],"pearls":["Unresectable stage III NSCLC: ~60 Gy/30 concurrent chemoRT then consolidation durvalumab (PACIFIC trial)","RTOG 0617: escalating to 74 Gy was WORSE than 60 Gy - more is not better; watch heart dose","Medically inoperable early-stage NSCLC is treated with SBRT","Check lung V20/mean lung dose, esophagus, heart, and cord constraints to limit pneumonitis and late toxicity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562307/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-sclc-pci","name":"Small Cell Lung Cancer & Prophylactic Cranial Irradiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) is an aggressive neuroendocrine tumor, strongly smoking-related, with rapid growth, early metastasis, and high initial chemo/radiosensitivity; classified as LIMITED-stage (confined to one radiation field) or EXTENSIVE-stage","Presents with a central mass, rapid symptoms, and paraneoplastic syndromes (SIADH, Lambert-Eaton, ectopic ACTH); brain metastases are common","High propensity for brain relapse underlies the role of prophylactic cranial irradiation"],"dx":["Stage with PET/CT and brain MRI as limited vs extensive; obtain tissue diagnosis (neuroendocrine markers)","Assess for paraneoplastic syndromes and performance status; the disease responds fast but relapses","Determine candidacy for thoracic RT (limited-stage) and prophylactic cranial irradiation"],"tx":["LIMITED-STAGE: concurrent chemoRT with platinum/etoposide - thoracic RT ideally EARLY and often as ~45 Gy TWICE-DAILY (BID, hyperfractionated) over 3 weeks (Turrisi regimen) or high-dose once-daily; start RT early with chemotherapy","EXTENSIVE-STAGE: chemotherapy + immunotherapy; consolidative thoracic RT for responders in selected cases","PROPHYLACTIC CRANIAL IRRADIATION (PCI, ~25 Gy/10) reduces brain metastases and improves survival in responders (limited-stage; selected extensive-stage) - weigh neurocognitive effects and MRI surveillance alternatives"],"pearls":["Limited-stage SCLC: early concurrent chemoRT, classically 45 Gy BID (twice-daily) with platinum/etoposide (Turrisi)","Prophylactic cranial irradiation (~25 Gy/10) reduces brain metastases and improves survival in chemo-responders","SCLC is very chemo/radiosensitive initially but relapses quickly - start thoracic RT early","Watch for paraneoplastic syndromes: SIADH, Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, ectopic ACTH"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Small Cell Lung Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482458/"},{"t":"NCI: SCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-lung-sbrt","name":"Lung SBRT for Early-Stage/Inoperable Disease","sec":"radonc","present":["Stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT/SABR) is the standard for MEDICALLY INOPERABLE early-stage (T1-2N0) NSCLC and a strong option for operable patients who decline surgery, delivering ablative dose in a few fractions","Excellent local control (often >90%) for PERIPHERAL tumors with regimens like 54 Gy in 3 fractions or 48-50 Gy in 4-5 fractions","Requires motion management (4D-CT, ITV/gating) and image guidance because of the ablative dose and steep gradients"],"dx":["Confirm early-stage node-negative disease (PET/CT, often EBUS for central tumors) and biopsy when feasible; assess tumor location relative to the proximal bronchial tree/chest wall/vessels","Distinguish PERIPHERAL (safe for aggressive 3-fraction regimens) from CENTRAL/ULTRA-CENTRAL tumors (require risk-adapted fractionation)","Evaluate baseline pulmonary function and motion"],"tx":["PERIPHERAL early-stage: ablative SBRT (e.g., 54 Gy/3) with high local control and low toxicity","CENTRAL tumors: risk-adapted multi-fraction SBRT (e.g., 50 Gy/5 or 60 Gy/8) to protect the proximal bronchial tree/great vessels - AVOID single-/3-fraction ablation centrally (excess toxicity, as seen in early RTOG 0813-era experience)","Manage respiratory motion (gating, breath-hold, ITV) and use daily image guidance; monitor for radiation pneumonitis and chest-wall pain/rib fracture in peripheral cases"],"pearls":["Peripheral inoperable early-stage NSCLC: ablative SBRT (e.g., 54 Gy/3) achieves >90% local control","Central/ultra-central tumors need risk-adapted multi-fraction SBRT - do NOT use single/3-fraction regimens near the proximal bronchial tree","SBRT is the standard for medically inoperable early-stage disease and an option for operable patients declining surgery","Motion management and image guidance are mandatory given ablative doses; watch chest-wall toxicity for peripheral lesions"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570574/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-thymoma-meso-esophageal","name":"Thymoma, Mesothelioma & Esophageal Cancer","sec":"radonc","present":["Three thoracic entities. THYMOMA/thymic carcinoma: anterior mediastinal masses associated with MYASTHENIA GRAVIS and other paraneoplastic syndromes; staged by the Masaoka-Koga system","MALIGNANT PLEURAL MESOTHELIOMA: asbestos-related, encasing the pleura with effusion and chest pain - a challenging target given the large, complex pleural surface","ESOPHAGEAL cancer (squamous - upper/mid, more radiosensitive; adenocarcinoma - distal/GEJ) presents with progressive dysphagia and weight loss"],"dx":["Thymoma: CT of the chest, assess myasthenia (acetylcholine receptor antibodies); resectability determines management","Mesothelioma: biopsy (not just cytology) for subtype (epithelioid best prognosis); CT/PET/MRI for extent","Esophageal: endoscopy/biopsy, endoscopic ultrasound (T/N), PET/CT; note histology and GEJ location for treatment paradigm"],"tx":["THYMOMA: complete SURGICAL resection is primary; postoperative RT for incompletely resected or advanced (Masaoka III+) disease","MESOTHELIOMA: multimodality in selected patients (surgery, chemo, RT); hemithoracic RT is technically demanding - increasingly IMRT; RT also palliates","ESOPHAGEAL: NEOADJUVANT chemoRT (~41.4-50.4 Gy with carboplatin/paclitaxel) then surgery per the CROSS trial improves survival; definitive chemoRT (~50-50.4 Gy with cisplatin/5-FU) for nonoperative candidates or cervical esophagus"],"pearls":["Thymoma associates with myasthenia gravis - surgery is primary, with postoperative RT for advanced/incompletely resected disease","CROSS trial: neoadjuvant chemoRT (carboplatin/paclitaxel + ~41.4 Gy) before surgery improves esophageal cancer survival","Definitive esophageal chemoRT is ~50-50.4 Gy - escalating dose did not improve outcomes (INT 0123)","Epithelioid mesothelioma has the best prognosis; hemithoracic RT is complex and often IMRT-based"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophageal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459267/"},{"t":"NCI: Esophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-breast-early-hypofx","name":"Early-Stage Breast Cancer & Whole-Breast/Hypofractionation","sec":"radonc","present":["Early-stage invasive breast cancer is commonly treated with BREAST-CONSERVING THERAPY: lumpectomy plus whole-breast RT, which yields survival equivalent to mastectomy (NSABP B-06 and others)","Whole-breast RT after lumpectomy reduces local recurrence; a tumor-bed BOOST further lowers recurrence, especially in younger patients","HYPOFRACTIONATION is now the standard for most whole-breast treatment - fewer, slightly larger fractions with equal control and cosmesis"],"dx":["Confirm margins, receptor status (ER/PR/HER2), and nodal status; assess candidacy for breast conservation (tumor:breast ratio, multicentricity, prior RT, contraindications like pregnancy or certain connective-tissue disease)","Identify who can OMIT RT: selected older women (e.g., >=65-70) with small, node-negative, ER-positive tumors on endocrine therapy (per CALGB 9343/PRIME II - RT reduces local recurrence but not survival)","Determine boost need (younger age, high grade, close margins) and nodal RT indications"],"tx":["Whole-breast HYPOFRACTIONATION: ~40 Gy in 15 fractions or 42.5 Gy in 16 (START/Canadian trials); ULTRA-hypofractionation 26 Gy in 5 fractions (FAST-Forward) is an option","Add a tumor-bed BOOST (~10-16 Gy) for higher-recurrence-risk patients; use heart-sparing techniques (deep inspiration breath-hold, prone positioning) for left-sided disease","Combine with systemic therapy (endocrine, chemo, HER2-directed) per tumor biology; consider RT omission in selected favorable elderly patients"],"pearls":["Breast conservation (lumpectomy + whole-breast RT) equals mastectomy for survival (NSABP B-06)","Hypofractionation (40 Gy/15 or 42.5 Gy/16) is standard; FAST-Forward's 26 Gy/5 is an ultra-short option","Selected older women with small ER-positive node-negative tumors on endocrine therapy may OMIT RT (CALGB 9343 / PRIME II) - lower local recurrence, same survival","Use breath-hold/prone techniques to spare the heart in left-sided breast RT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Breast Cancer Conservation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547708/"},{"t":"NCI: Breast Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/hp/breast-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-breast-pmrt-rni","name":"Post-Mastectomy Radiation & Regional Nodal Irradiation","sec":"radonc","present":["POST-MASTECTOMY RADIATION THERAPY (PMRT) targets the chest wall and regional nodes to reduce locoregional recurrence and improve survival in higher-risk disease after mastectomy","REGIONAL NODAL IRRADIATION (RNI) covers the axilla, supraclavicular fossa, and internal mammary chain in node-positive or high-risk node-negative disease","Nodal RT decisions integrate the number of positive nodes, tumor size, and biology"],"dx":["Indications for PMRT: node-positive disease (classically >=4 positive nodes; strongly consider for 1-3 positive nodes), tumors >5 cm (T3/T4), or positive margins after mastectomy","RNI indications: node-positive disease and selected high-risk node-negative tumors - MA.20 and EORTC 22922 showed RNI improves disease control (and reduced breast-cancer mortality in some analyses)","Assess reconstruction status and laterality (heart/lung dose) in planning"],"tx":["Deliver ~50 Gy in 25 fractions (or a hypofractionated equivalent, increasingly supported) to the chest wall +/- comprehensive nodal volumes; bolus the chest wall as needed for skin coverage","Cover internal mammary nodes when indicated, balancing heart/lung dose (breath-hold, planning techniques); coordinate timing with reconstruction","Integrate with systemic therapy; the chest wall and nodal regions are treated to reduce locoregional recurrence"],"pearls":["PMRT is indicated for >=4 positive nodes, T3/T4 tumors, or positive margins - and is strongly considered for 1-3 positive nodes","MA.20 and EORTC 22922: regional nodal irradiation improves locoregional/disease control in node-positive and high-risk disease","Typical dose ~50 Gy/25 to chest wall +/- nodes, with chest-wall bolus for skin coverage","Balance internal-mammary nodal coverage against heart/lung dose using breath-hold and modern planning"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Postmastectomy Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564334/"},{"t":"NCI: Breast Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/hp/breast-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-dcis-pbi","name":"Ductal Carcinoma In Situ & Partial-Breast Irradiation","sec":"radonc","present":["DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU (DCIS) is non-invasive breast cancer confined to the ducts (no basement-membrane invasion) - a precursor detected mostly as mammographic MICROCALCIFICATIONS","After breast-conserving surgery, whole-breast RT roughly halves the risk of in-breast recurrence (about half of recurrences would be invasive)","ACCELERATED PARTIAL-BREAST IRRADIATION (APBI) treats only the tumor bed plus margin over a shorter course in SELECTED low-risk patients"],"dx":["Confirm DCIS on core biopsy/excision; assess grade, size, margins, and receptor status; the Van Nuys Prognostic Index and tools like the Oncotype DCIS score help estimate recurrence risk","Identify low-risk patients who may consider RT omission (small, low-grade, widely clear margins, older age) versus those who benefit clearly from RT","Determine APBI candidacy using consensus criteria (ASTRO/GEC-ESTRO 'suitable' groups: older age, small, node-negative, favorable biology)"],"tx":["Standard: breast-conserving surgery + WHOLE-BREAST RT (hypofractionation appropriate for most); endocrine therapy for ER-positive DCIS reduces recurrence","APBI options for suitable low-risk patients: external-beam APBI (e.g., 40 Gy/15 or select 5-fraction regimens), brachytherapy, or intraoperative RT - shorter and breast-tissue-sparing","Selected very-low-risk DCIS may omit RT with close follow-up +/- endocrine therapy (shared decision-making)"],"pearls":["DCIS is non-invasive (intact basement membrane) - whole-breast RT after lumpectomy halves in-breast recurrence","APBI treats only the tumor bed + margin and is limited to ASTRO/GEC-ESTRO 'suitable' low-risk patients (older, small, node-negative)","Endocrine therapy reduces recurrence in ER-positive DCIS","Very-low-risk DCIS may omit RT with shared decision-making and surveillance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ductal Carcinoma In Situ","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538331/"},{"t":"NCI: Breast Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/hp/breast-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-breast-locally-advanced","name":"Locally Advanced & Inflammatory Breast Cancer","sec":"radonc","present":["Locally advanced breast cancer (large tumors, chest-wall/skin involvement, or bulky/matted nodes; stage III) and INFLAMMATORY breast cancer require aggressive TRIMODALITY therapy","INFLAMMATORY breast cancer is a clinical diagnosis - rapid-onset breast erythema, edema (peau d'orange), and warmth from dermal lymphatic invasion - aggressive, with a worse prognosis, and by definition at least stage III (T4d)","Sequencing is neoadjuvant systemic therapy first, then surgery, then radiation"],"dx":["Confirm invasive cancer and receptor status; image extent (MRI, PET/CT); inflammatory breast cancer is diagnosed clinically (with skin punch biopsy showing dermal lymphatic involvement supporting it) - do not delay treatment awaiting a mass","Assess response to neoadjuvant therapy (guides surgery and prognosis)","Stage per AJCC; inflammatory disease is T4d, at least stage IIIB"],"tx":["Standard sequence: NEOADJUVANT systemic therapy (chemo +/- HER2-directed/immunotherapy per biology) -> SURGERY (mastectomy; inflammatory breast cancer requires MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY, not breast conservation) -> comprehensive POST-MASTECTOMY RT to chest wall and regional nodes (~50 Gy, sometimes with a chest-wall boost)","For inflammatory breast cancer, RT covers the chest wall and full nodal basins given the high recurrence risk; bolus ensures skin-surface dose","Do not omit any modality - trimodality therapy is essential for locoregional control"],"pearls":["Inflammatory breast cancer is a clinical diagnosis (peau d'orange from dermal lymphatic invasion), at least stage IIIB (T4d)","Inflammatory breast cancer is NOT a breast-conservation candidate - it requires modified radical mastectomy plus post-mastectomy RT","Standard sequence is neoadjuvant systemic therapy -> mastectomy -> comprehensive chest wall + nodal RT","Use chest-wall bolus to ensure adequate skin dose given the high risk of skin/dermal recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Inflammatory Breast Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564324/"},{"t":"NCI: Inflammatory Breast Cancer","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/ibc-fact-sheet"}]},{"id":"radonc-rectal","name":"Rectal Cancer - Neoadjuvant ChemoRT & Total Neoadjuvant Therapy","sec":"radonc","present":["Rectal adenocarcinoma (within ~15 cm of the anal verge) is managed differently from colon cancer because the pelvic location raises local-recurrence risk and makes RT central to care","Presents with bleeding, change in bowel habit, or obstruction; staging drives the neoadjuvant decision","Locally advanced disease (cT3-4 or node-positive) is the classic indication for preoperative therapy"],"dx":["Stage with pelvic MRI (T stage, mesorectal fascia/circumferential margin, nodes), endoscopic ultrasound, colonoscopy/biopsy, CT chest/abdomen, and CEA","Identify locally advanced disease (cT3-4 and/or N+) that benefits from neoadjuvant therapy; assess candidacy for organ preservation","Distinguish upper-rectal/rectosigmoid tumors (may be treated like colon) from mid/low rectal tumors"],"tx":["TOTAL NEOADJUVANT THERAPY (TNT) - chemotherapy plus chemoRT (or short-course RT) BEFORE surgery - is now standard for locally advanced disease (RAPIDO, PRODIGE 23), improving disease-free survival and pathologic response","RT options: LONG-COURSE chemoRT (~50.4 Gy/28 with capecitabine) or SHORT-COURSE RT (25 Gy/5); total mesorectal excision follows, or a WATCH-AND-WAIT nonoperative approach for a clinical complete response (organ preservation, per OPRA)","Deliver RT to the primary and mesorectal/pelvic nodes with bladder-filling and prone/belly-board techniques to spare small bowel"],"pearls":["TNT (all chemo + RT before surgery) is the modern standard for locally advanced rectal cancer (RAPIDO, PRODIGE 23)","Two RT approaches: long-course chemoRT 50.4 Gy/28 with capecitabine, or short-course 25 Gy/5","A clinical complete response can allow WATCH-AND-WAIT organ preservation (OPRA) instead of surgery","MRI defines the circumferential/mesorectal margin - the key driver of local recurrence"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rectal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493202/"},{"t":"NCI: Rectal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/colorectal/hp/rectal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-anal","name":"Anal Cancer - Definitive Chemoradiation (Nigro Regimen)","sec":"radonc","present":["Anal squamous cell carcinoma is strongly HPV-associated (and more common with HIV/immunosuppression); it is the classic ORGAN-PRESERVATION success story - cured with chemoRT, sparing the need for colostomy","Presents with rectal bleeding, a mass, pain, or pruritus; often mistaken for hemorrhoids, delaying diagnosis","Definitive chemoRT replaced upfront abdominoperineal resection - surgery is now reserved for salvage"],"dx":["Diagnose by biopsy; stage with exam (including inguinal nodes), anoscopy, pelvic MRI, and PET/CT; test HIV status","Assess inguinal and pelvic nodal involvement (influences target volumes)","Distinguish anal canal squamous carcinoma from rectal adenocarcinoma and from perianal skin cancers"],"tx":["Definitive CHEMORADIATION (the NIGRO regimen concept): concurrent 5-FU + MITOMYCIN-C with RT - IMRT to ~45-59 Gy depending on stage (RTOG 0529 established dose-painted IMRT to reduce toxicity), covering the primary and elective pelvic/inguinal nodes","Do NOT rush to surgery - allow months for full regression before declaring residual disease; SALVAGE abdominoperineal resection is reserved for persistent/recurrent disease","Manage significant acute skin/mucosal and hematologic toxicity (mitomycin); maintain treatment intensity when possible"],"pearls":["Anal cancer is cured with definitive chemoRT (5-FU + mitomycin-C) - surgery (APR) is salvage only","RTOG 0529: dose-painted IMRT reduces toxicity while covering primary + elective inguinal/pelvic nodes","Allow adequate time (months) for regression before calling it residual disease - avoid premature salvage surgery","Strongly HPV-associated; check HIV status, which affects tolerance and outcomes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Anal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538236/"},{"t":"NCI: Anal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/anal/hp/anal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-esophageal-gej","name":"Esophageal & Gastroesophageal Junction Cancer","sec":"radonc","present":["Esophageal cancer: SQUAMOUS cell carcinoma (upper/mid esophagus, alcohol/tobacco-related) and ADENOCARCINOMA (distal esophagus/gastroesophageal junction, arising from Barrett esophagus/reflux, rising in incidence)","GEJ tumors are classified by the SIEWERT system (types I-III), which influences whether they are managed as esophageal or gastric cancer","Presents with progressive solid-food dysphagia and weight loss; often locally advanced at diagnosis"],"dx":["Work up with endoscopy/biopsy, endoscopic ultrasound (T/N staging), and PET/CT; bronchoscopy for upper tumors near the airway","Classify GEJ tumors by Siewert type; assess resectability and nodal spread","Squamous histology is more radiosensitive than adenocarcinoma and can be cured with definitive chemoRT"],"tx":["NEOADJUVANT chemoRT then surgery (CROSS regimen: carboplatin/paclitaxel with ~41.4 Gy/23) is standard for resectable locally advanced disease and improves survival","DEFINITIVE chemoRT (~50-50.4 Gy with cisplatin/5-FU or carboplatin/paclitaxel) for cervical esophagus or nonoperative candidates - dose escalation beyond ~50.4 Gy did not help (INT 0123)","Use motion management and respect heart, lung, and cord constraints; adjuvant immunotherapy (nivolumab) for residual disease after trimodality therapy (CheckMate 577)"],"pearls":["CROSS: neoadjuvant carboplatin/paclitaxel + 41.4 Gy before surgery is the standard trimodality regimen","Definitive dose is ~50-50.4 Gy - INT 0123 showed higher dose did not improve outcomes","Squamous cell carcinoma is more radiosensitive than adenocarcinoma and can be cured non-surgically","Adjuvant nivolumab after trimodality therapy for residual disease (CheckMate 577); Siewert type guides esophageal-vs-gastric management of GEJ tumors"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophageal Cancer Staging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526091/"},{"t":"NCI: Esophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-pancreatic-hpb","name":"Pancreatic & Hepatobiliary Cancer (incl. Liver SBRT)","sec":"radonc","present":["Pancreatic adenocarcinoma has a poor prognosis and presents late (painless jaundice for head tumors, back pain, weight loss); most are unresectable or borderline at diagnosis","Resectability categories - RESECTABLE, BORDERLINE resectable, and LOCALLY ADVANCED unresectable - drive whether/when RT is used (often after induction chemotherapy)","Hepatobiliary targets include hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma, where LIVER SBRT provides local control when surgery/ablation is not feasible"],"dx":["Stage with pancreatic-protocol CT/MRI (vascular involvement defines resectability), CA 19-9, and biopsy; assess for metastases","For HCC, use LI-RADS imaging criteria and underlying liver function (Child-Pugh) - a key determinant of tolerance","Determine candidacy for surgery, ablation, or SBRT based on tumor size/location and liver reserve"],"tx":["Pancreatic: neoadjuvant/induction CHEMOTHERAPY first; chemoRT (~50.4 Gy) or SBRT (~30-40 Gy/5) may improve local control/resectability in borderline or locally advanced disease after chemo, though its survival role remains debated","LIVER SBRT for HCC/cholangiocarcinoma or liver metastases: ablative dose in ~3-5 fractions with strict MEAN LIVER dose limits and respiratory motion management - success depends on sparing enough functional liver (Child-Pugh A tolerates best)","Palliative RT relieves pain/bleeding/obstruction; coordinate with systemic therapy"],"pearls":["Pancreatic cancer: chemotherapy leads; RT/SBRT is used selectively for borderline/locally advanced disease after induction chemo","Liver SBRT requires sparing enough functional liver - mean liver dose limits and Child-Pugh class govern tolerance","Painless jaundice suggests a pancreatic head tumor; CA 19-9 aids monitoring","Respiratory motion management is essential for upper-abdominal SBRT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pancreatic Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518996/"},{"t":"NCI: Pancreatic Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/hp/pancreatic-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-gastric","name":"Gastric Cancer & Adjuvant Therapy","sec":"radonc","present":["Gastric adenocarcinoma is common worldwide (higher incidence in East Asia); risk factors include H. pylori, smoking, and diet; SIGNET-RING/diffuse (linitis plastica) histology is aggressive","Presents late with weight loss, early satiety, dyspepsia, anemia, or obstruction; often node-positive or metastatic at diagnosis","Management is multimodal - surgery with perioperative chemotherapy is the backbone, with RT in selected adjuvant settings"],"dx":["Stage with endoscopy/biopsy, endoscopic ultrasound, CT, PET, and staging laparoscopy (to detect peritoneal disease); assess HER2/PD-L1 for systemic therapy","Assess resectability and nodal burden; adequate D2 lymphadenectomy influences the role of adjuvant RT","Distinguish intestinal from diffuse (signet-ring) types"],"tx":["Standard is PERIOPERATIVE CHEMOTHERAPY (FLOT regimen) with surgery; adjuvant CHEMORADIATION (~45 Gy with 5-FU-based chemo, the INT 0116/Macdonald approach) is used when lymphadenectomy was limited (D0/D1) or margins/nodes are high-risk","Deliver RT to the gastric bed, anastomosis, and regional nodes with attention to kidney, liver, bowel, and heart dose","Palliative RT controls bleeding and obstruction in advanced disease"],"pearls":["Perioperative FLOT chemotherapy + surgery is the backbone; adjuvant chemoRT (INT 0116, ~45 Gy) benefits patients with inadequate (D0/D1) nodal dissection","Signet-ring/diffuse type (linitis plastica) is aggressive with a worse prognosis","Adjuvant RT covers the gastric bed, anastomosis, and regional nodes - watch kidney and liver dose","Palliative RT is effective for tumor bleeding and obstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gastric Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459142/"},{"t":"NCI: Gastric Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/stomach/hp/stomach-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-prostate-ebrt","name":"Prostate Cancer - Risk Groups, EBRT & Dose Escalation","sec":"radonc","present":["Prostate adenocarcinoma is stratified into NCCN RISK GROUPS (low, favorable/unfavorable intermediate, high, very high) using PSA, Gleason grade group, and T stage - risk group drives treatment intensity and androgen-deprivation duration","Often asymptomatic, detected via PSA screening; the low alpha/beta (~1.5) makes prostate cancer well suited to hypofractionation","Options include active surveillance (low risk), surgery, EBRT, and brachytherapy"],"dx":["Risk-stratify with PSA, biopsy Gleason grade group, and stage; use MRI and, increasingly, PSMA PET for staging higher-risk disease","Match treatment intensity to risk group; determine androgen-deprivation therapy (ADT) duration by risk","Assess urinary/bowel/sexual baseline function and comorbidity"],"tx":["EBRT with DOSE ESCALATION (~78-80 Gy conventional, or moderate hypofractionation ~60 Gy/20, or SBRT ~36.25 Gy/5 per HYPO-RT-PC/PACE-B) improves control; IGRT with fiducials/rectal spacer reduces toxicity","Add ANDROGEN-DEPRIVATION THERAPY by risk: none for low risk, SHORT-COURSE (~4-6 months) for intermediate, and LONG-COURSE (~18-36 months) for high/very-high risk (improves survival)","Cover pelvic nodes in higher-risk disease (POP-RT supports nodal RT in node-risk patients); active surveillance for low-risk disease"],"pearls":["Prostate's low alpha/beta (~1.5) makes it ideal for hypofractionation/SBRT (36.25 Gy/5, PACE-B/HYPO-RT-PC)","ADT duration scales with risk: none (low), ~4-6 months (intermediate), ~18-36 months (high/very high)","Dose escalation to ~78-80 Gy (or hypofractionated equivalent) improves biochemical control","Rectal spacers and IGRT with fiducials reduce rectal toxicity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470550/"},{"t":"NCI: Prostate Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/prostate/hp/prostate-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-prostate-brachy-postop","name":"Prostate Brachytherapy (LDR/HDR) & Post-Op Radiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Prostate BRACHYTHERAPY delivers a highly conformal, dose-escalated treatment via implanted sources: LOW-DOSE-RATE (LDR) permanent seeds (I-125, Pd-103) or HIGH-DOSE-RATE (HDR) temporary Ir-192","Brachytherapy (alone for low/favorable-intermediate risk, or as a BOOST with EBRT for higher-risk disease) exploits the steep dose gradient to spare the rectum and bladder","After prostatectomy, RT is used adjuvantly or as SALVAGE for recurrence"],"dx":["Select brachytherapy candidates by risk group, gland size, urinary function (IPSS), and prior TURP; a very large gland or high obstructive symptoms are relative contraindications","For post-prostatectomy patients, distinguish adjuvant (adverse pathology) from salvage (rising PSA) settings","Assess dosimetry goals and organ-at-risk (urethra, rectum) constraints"],"tx":["LDR monotherapy (e.g., I-125 ~145 Gy) or HDR monotherapy for low/favorable-intermediate risk; brachytherapy BOOST plus EBRT for unfavorable-intermediate/high risk improves biochemical control (ASCENDE-RT, at the cost of more urinary toxicity)","POST-PROSTATECTOMY: early SALVAGE RT at PSA rise (~0.2 and rising) is now generally favored over adjuvant RT (RADICALS-RT, RAVES, GETUG-AFU 17 showed similar control with less toxicity via a salvage approach); typical dose ~66-70 Gy to the prostate bed +/- nodes/ADT","Manage urinary toxicity; protect the urethra and rectum with careful dosimetry"],"pearls":["Brachytherapy boost + EBRT improves biochemical control for higher-risk disease (ASCENDE-RT) but increases urinary toxicity","Early SALVAGE RT (at PSA ~0.2 and rising) is now preferred over adjuvant RT after prostatectomy (RADICALS-RT/RAVES/GETUG-AFU 17)","Large gland size, high IPSS, or prior TURP are relative contraindications to seed implantation","LDR uses permanent seeds (I-125/Pd-103); HDR uses a temporary Ir-192 source"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Brachytherapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560506/"},{"t":"NCI: Prostate Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/prostate/hp/prostate-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-bladder","name":"Bladder Cancer - Trimodality Bladder Preservation","sec":"radonc","present":["Muscle-invasive bladder cancer (usually urothelial carcinoma) is traditionally treated with radical cystectomy, but TRIMODALITY THERAPY offers organ (bladder) preservation with comparable outcomes in selected patients","Presents with painless hematuria; smoking is the dominant risk factor","Bladder preservation suits patients with a solitary tumor, no extensive carcinoma in situ, no hydronephrosis, and good bladder function/capacity, or those unfit for/declining cystectomy"],"dx":["Stage with TURBT (maximal resection - the first step of trimodality), cross-sectional imaging (CT urogram), and assessment for nodal/metastatic disease","Select bladder-preservation candidates: complete/maximal TURBT, unifocal tumor, absence of extensive CIS or hydronephrosis","Distinguish muscle-invasive (needs definitive therapy) from non-muscle-invasive disease (intravesical therapy)"],"tx":["TRIMODALITY THERAPY: maximal TURBT -> concurrent CHEMORADIATION (radiosensitizing chemo, e.g., cisplatin-based or 5-FU/mitomycin per BC2001) with RT to the bladder +/- pelvic nodes (~60-64 Gy, or hypofractionated 55 Gy/20)","Perform CYSTOSCOPIC reassessment; salvage cystectomy for non-responders or muscle-invasive recurrence - close surveillance is essential","Neoadjuvant/systemic chemotherapy and immunotherapy are integrated per stage and fitness"],"pearls":["Trimodality therapy (maximal TURBT + concurrent chemoRT) preserves the bladder with outcomes comparable to cystectomy in selected patients","Ideal candidates: solitary tumor, complete TURBT, no extensive CIS, no hydronephrosis, good bladder function","BC2001 showed adding chemotherapy (5-FU/mitomycin) to RT improves local control","Reserve salvage cystectomy for non-responders; lifelong cystoscopic surveillance is required"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536923/"},{"t":"NCI: Bladder Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/bladder/hp/bladder-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-seminoma","name":"Seminoma & Testicular Cancer","sec":"radonc","present":["Testicular germ cell tumors are SEMINOMA or non-seminomatous; seminoma is exquisitely RADIOSENSITIVE and CHEMOSENSITIVE with excellent cure rates even when metastatic","Presents as a painless testicular mass in a young man; spreads predictably via lymphatics to the RETROPERITONEAL (para-aortic) nodes","Serum tumor markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) aid diagnosis/staging - a pure seminoma does NOT elevate AFP (if AFP is up, treat as non-seminoma)"],"dx":["Diagnose with radical INGUINAL orchiectomy (never trans-scrotal biopsy - risks altering lymphatic drainage); stage with CT abdomen/pelvis and markers","Confirm pure seminoma histology and normal AFP; stage I (confined) vs stage II (retroperitoneal nodes)","Assess for the predictable para-aortic nodal spread pattern"],"tx":["STAGE I seminoma: after orchiectomy, SURVEILLANCE is preferred; alternatives are single-agent carboplatin or adjuvant RT to the para-aortic strip (~20 Gy) - the modern trend favors surveillance to avoid late RT toxicity/second malignancy","STAGE II (nodal): RT to the para-aortic + ipsilateral pelvic nodes ('dog-leg', ~30-36 Gy) for lower-bulk disease, or chemotherapy (BEP) for bulkier disease","Non-seminoma: primarily surgery (retroperitoneal lymph node dissection) and chemotherapy, not RT"],"pearls":["Diagnose via radical INGUINAL orchiectomy - never a trans-scrotal biopsy","Pure seminoma does NOT raise AFP - an elevated AFP means treat as non-seminoma","Stage I seminoma: surveillance is now preferred over adjuvant RT/carboplatin to avoid late toxicity and second cancers","Seminoma is highly radiosensitive; nodal disease spreads predictably to the para-aortic/retroperitoneal region"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Seminoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441937/"},{"t":"NCI: Testicular Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/testicular/hp/testicular-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-renal-penile-urethral","name":"Renal, Penile & Urethral Cancers","sec":"radonc","present":["Less common GU malignancies where RT has selected roles. RENAL cell carcinoma is relatively RADIORESISTANT to conventional fractionation but responds to ablative SBRT and is a common source of radioresistant metastases (bone/brain)","PENILE cancer is usually squamous cell carcinoma (HPV-associated), spreading to inguinal nodes; organ preservation is a goal for small distal lesions","Primary URETHRAL carcinoma is rare, with histology varying by location"],"dx":["Renal: diagnose by imaging (many resected without biopsy); SBRT is an option for medically inoperable primaries and for oligometastases; conventional palliative RT controls symptomatic metastases","Penile: biopsy and assess inguinal nodes (the key prognostic factor); stage with exam and imaging","Urethral: biopsy and imaging; management individualized by site/histology/stage"],"tx":["RENAL: ablative SBRT for inoperable primary tumors and oligometastatic/symptomatic sites (renal cell carcinoma responds to high dose-per-fraction despite conventional radioresistance)","PENILE: organ-preserving options for small lesions include brachytherapy or EBRT (with surgery for larger tumors); manage inguinal nodes (surgery +/- RT) given their prognostic weight","URETHRAL: multimodality (surgery, chemoRT) tailored to location and stage"],"pearls":["Renal cell carcinoma is radioRESISTANT to conventional fractionation but responds to ablative SBRT (high dose per fraction)","Penile cancer (HPV-related squamous cell) spreads to inguinal nodes - nodal status is the key prognostic factor; brachytherapy can preserve the organ for small lesions","RCC brain/bone metastases are classically radioresistant - favor SRS/SBRT (high dose per fraction) over conventional fractionation","Primary urethral carcinoma is rare and managed with individualized multimodality therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470336/"},{"t":"NCI: Renal Cell Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/kidney/hp/kidney-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-cervix","name":"Cervical Cancer - Definitive ChemoRT & Brachytherapy","sec":"radonc","present":["Cervical cancer (usually HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma or adenocarcinoma) is a leading cause of cancer death where screening is limited; it spreads locally to the parametria and to pelvic/para-aortic nodes","Presents with abnormal bleeding, postcoital bleeding, or discharge; staging incorporates imaging and nodal status (FIGO 2018)","Locally advanced disease is cured with definitive chemoRT plus BRACHYTHERAPY - an irreplaceable component"],"dx":["Stage per FIGO 2018 (now allows imaging/pathology); MRI defines local extent and PET/CT assesses nodes; biopsy confirms histology","Assess parametrial and nodal (pelvic/para-aortic) involvement to set fields and dose","Early-stage disease may be surgical; locally advanced disease is definitive chemoRT"],"tx":["LOCALLY ADVANCED: definitive concurrent CHEMORADIATION with weekly CISPLATIN - external-beam pelvic RT (~45 Gy, extended-field to para-aortics if involved) FOLLOWED BY BRACHYTHERAPY boost to bring point-A/high-risk CTV dose to ~80-85+ Gy EQD2","BRACHYTHERAPY is essential and cannot be replaced by external-beam boost - omitting it worsens survival; complete all treatment within ~8 weeks (prolongation reduces control, given accelerated repopulation)","Image-guided adaptive brachytherapy (EMBRACE/GEC-ESTRO) individualizes dose to the high-risk CTV while sparing bladder/rectum/sigmoid"],"pearls":["Brachytherapy is IRREPLACEABLE in cervical cancer - external-beam boost cannot substitute, and omitting brachy worsens survival","Definitive treatment: ~45 Gy pelvic chemoRT with weekly cisplatin THEN brachytherapy to ~80-85+ Gy EQD2 to the high-risk target","Finish all treatment within ~8 weeks - prolongation reduces control (accelerated repopulation)","Extend fields to the para-aortics when those nodes are involved (PET-guided)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cervical Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431093/"},{"t":"NCI: Cervical Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/cervical/hp/cervical-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-endometrial","name":"Endometrial Cancer - Adjuvant Radiation (PORTEC)","sec":"radonc","present":["Endometrial (uterine) adenocarcinoma is the most common gynecologic malignancy in developed countries, usually presenting EARLY with POSTMENOPAUSAL BLEEDING","Primary treatment is SURGERY (hysterectomy/BSO with nodal assessment); adjuvant RT is guided by recurrence-risk factors and, increasingly, molecular classification (POLE, MMR/MSI, p53)","The PORTEC trials defined the role and type of adjuvant radiation"],"dx":["Diagnose by endometrial biopsy; stage surgically (FIGO); risk-stratify by grade, depth of myometrial invasion, LVSI, age, and molecular subtype","Identify low, intermediate, high-intermediate, and high-risk groups to select observation vs vaginal brachytherapy vs external-beam RT +/- chemo","Molecular classification (POLE-mutated favorable; p53-abnormal unfavorable) increasingly refines risk"],"tx":["HIGH-INTERMEDIATE risk: VAGINAL CUFF BRACHYTHERAPY alone controls the vaginal vault with far less toxicity than pelvic EBRT and equal survival (PORTEC-2)","HIGH risk / advanced: pelvic EXTERNAL-BEAM RT, and CHEMOTHERAPY combined with RT improves outcomes for high-risk disease (PORTEC-3, especially p53-abnormal)","Low-risk disease is observed after surgery; tailor therapy by molecular profile going forward"],"pearls":["PORTEC-2: vaginal brachytherapy alone equals pelvic EBRT for vaginal control in high-intermediate-risk disease, with less toxicity","PORTEC-3: adding chemotherapy to RT benefits high-risk disease (notably p53-abnormal tumors)","Postmenopausal bleeding is endometrial cancer until proven otherwise - biopsy","Molecular classification (POLE, MMR/MSI, p53) is reshaping adjuvant decisions"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Endometrial Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525981/"},{"t":"NCI: Endometrial Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/uterine/hp/endometrial-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-vulvar-vaginal","name":"Vulvar & Vaginal Cancer","sec":"radonc","present":["VULVAR cancer (usually squamous cell carcinoma, HPV-related or from lichen sclerosus) spreads to inguinofemoral nodes - the single most important prognostic factor; presents with a lump, pruritus, or an ulcer","VAGINAL cancer is rare (most vaginal malignancies are metastatic/extension from cervix or vulva); primary vaginal cancer is usually squamous and HPV-related","Both frequently rely on RT for organ preservation or when surgery would be morbid/disfiguring"],"dx":["Biopsy the primary; assess INGUINOFEMORAL nodes clinically and with imaging (and sentinel node for early vulvar cancer)","Stage per FIGO; nodal status drives prognosis and treatment fields","Distinguish primary vaginal cancer from secondary involvement by cervix/vulva/endometrium"],"tx":["VULVAR: surgery for early disease (with inguinofemoral node assessment); definitive or adjuvant CHEMORADIATION for locally advanced or node-positive disease, or to avoid exenteration; cover inguinal nodes when indicated","VAGINAL: definitive RT (EBRT + brachytherapy) is standard for most primary vaginal cancers, individualized by location/stage","Manage skin/mucosal toxicity in these sensitive regions; nodal coverage decisions hinge on inguinofemoral involvement"],"pearls":["Inguinofemoral nodal status is the dominant prognostic factor in vulvar cancer","Most vaginal malignancies are secondary (from cervix/vulva/endometrium) - true primary vaginal cancer is rare","Chemoradiation preserves organs/avoids exenteration in locally advanced vulvar cancer","Primary vaginal cancer is usually treated with combined EBRT and brachytherapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vulvar Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567798/"},{"t":"NCI: Vulvar Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/vulvar/hp/vulvar-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-gyn-brachytherapy","name":"Cervical & Endometrial Brachytherapy Techniques","sec":"radonc","present":["Gynecologic brachytherapy delivers a highly conformal boost that no external-beam technique can match, exploiting the inverse-square gradient to escalate tumor dose while sparing bladder, rectum, and sigmoid","CERVIX (intact uterus): INTRACAVITARY applicators - TANDEM AND OVOIDS (or tandem and ring) placed through the cervical os; interstitial needles added for bulky/asymmetric disease (hybrid applicators)","ENDOMETRIUM (post-hysterectomy): a VAGINAL CYLINDER treats the vaginal cuff/vault"],"dx":["Cervix dose historically specified at POINT A (2 cm superior and 2 cm lateral to the cervical os along the tandem); modern practice uses MRI-based volumetric planning to the high-risk CTV (GEC-ESTRO/EMBRACE)","Report bladder and rectal reference points/dose-volume constraints (ICRU); verify applicator geometry with imaging before each delivery","Choose intracavitary vs interstitial/hybrid based on tumor width and topography"],"tx":["Deliver HDR (Ir-192, multiple fractions) or LDR/pulsed regimens; for cervix, combine external-beam (~45 Gy) with brachytherapy to reach ~80-85+ Gy EQD2 to the high-risk CTV","Post-hysterectomy endometrial cancer: vaginal cylinder brachytherapy (e.g., a few HDR fractions) treats the cuff with minimal toxicity (PORTEC-2 approach)","Constrain bladder/rectum/sigmoid (D2cc limits) using image-guided adaptive planning"],"pearls":["Tandem and ovoids/ring = intracavitary cervix brachytherapy; a vaginal cylinder treats the post-hysterectomy vaginal cuff","Point A is the classic cervix prescription point (2 cm up, 2 cm lateral from the os) - now supplemented by MRI-based high-risk CTV planning","Add interstitial needles (hybrid applicator) for bulky or asymmetric cervical tumors","Image-guided D2cc constraints for bladder/rectum/sigmoid minimize late toxicity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gynecologic Brachytherapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562169/"},{"t":"NCI: Cervical Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/cervical/hp/cervical-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-ovarian-rare-gyn","name":"Ovarian & Rare Gynecologic Tumors","sec":"radonc","present":["Epithelial OVARIAN cancer presents late with abdominal distension/ascites and spreads transcoelomically throughout the peritoneum - which is why RT (which cannot safely cover the whole abdomen at tumoricidal dose) plays only a limited role compared with surgery and chemotherapy","Management centers on CYTOREDUCTIVE surgery and platinum/taxane CHEMOTHERAPY (with maintenance PARP inhibitors/bevacizumab per biology)","RT is used mainly for PALLIATION (e.g., symptomatic masses, bleeding, brain metastases)"],"dx":["Diagnose/stage surgically; CA-125 and imaging aid assessment; the peritoneal spread pattern limits curative RT","Identify palliative RT indications (localized symptomatic disease) and rare radiosensitive histologies (dysgerminoma - the ovarian counterpart of seminoma, highly radiosensitive but treated with chemo to preserve fertility)","Assess BRCA/HRD status for systemic maintenance therapy"],"tx":["Standard curative therapy is surgery + platinum/taxane chemotherapy - NOT radiotherapy; RT is reserved for palliation of localized symptoms (pain, bleeding, obstruction, brain metastases)","Historical whole-abdominal RT is largely abandoned due to toxicity and the efficacy of chemotherapy","For radiosensitive germ-cell tumors (dysgerminoma), chemotherapy is preferred to preserve fertility, reserving RT for select relapse"],"pearls":["Ovarian cancer spreads transcoelomically across the peritoneum - RT has a limited (mainly palliative) role; surgery + platinum/taxane chemo is standard","Whole-abdominal RT is largely obsolete due to toxicity and effective chemotherapy","Dysgerminoma is highly radiosensitive (ovarian analog of seminoma) but treated with chemo to preserve fertility","Palliative RT helps localized symptomatic ovarian disease and brain metastases"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ovarian Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567760/"},{"t":"NCI: Ovarian Epithelial Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/ovarian/hp/ovarian-epithelial-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-hodgkin","name":"Hodgkin Lymphoma - Involved-Site RT & Combined Modality","sec":"radonc","present":["Classic Hodgkin lymphoma (Reed-Sternberg cells) typically affects young adults, presents with painless supradiaphragmatic (cervical/mediastinal) lymphadenopathy, and spreads in a CONTIGUOUS nodal pattern","B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) and staging (Ann Arbor/Lugano) guide therapy; it is highly curable with combined-modality treatment","Modern RT is greatly reduced in field and dose (from historical extended-field) to limit late toxicity"],"dx":["Diagnose by excisional node biopsy; stage with PET/CT (Lugano) and assess bulk and B symptoms","Use INTERIM PET (Deauville score) to adapt therapy - response-adapted approaches may omit or reduce RT","Stratify early favorable, early unfavorable, and advanced disease"],"tx":["Combined-modality therapy: ABVD CHEMOTHERAPY followed by INVOLVED-SITE RT (ISRT) - modern low-dose (~20-30 Gy) RT to originally involved sites replaced toxic extended-field techniques","Response-adapted therapy (interim PET/Deauville) may omit RT in PET-negative patients to spare late effects, balancing relapse risk","Minimize late risks (second cancers - breast/lung, cardiac disease) with small fields, low dose, and modern techniques (breath-hold, proton/butterfly for mediastinum)"],"pearls":["Hodgkin lymphoma spreads contiguously and is highly curable - modern RT is INVOLVED-SITE (ISRT), low-dose (~20-30 Gy)","Interim PET (Deauville score) drives response-adapted therapy, sometimes omitting RT","Late effects dominate survivorship: second malignancies (breast/lung) and cardiac disease - minimize field/dose","ABVD chemotherapy is the standard backbone; RT targets originally involved sites only"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499969/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult Hodgkin Lymphoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lymphoma/hp/adult-hodgkin-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-nhl","name":"Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma & Indolent Lymphomas","sec":"radonc","present":["Non-Hodgkin lymphomas are diverse. AGGRESSIVE types (e.g., diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, DLBCL) are potentially curable with chemoimmunotherapy; INDOLENT types (e.g., FOLLICULAR lymphoma) are radiosensitive and, when localized, can be cured with low-dose RT","Unlike Hodgkin lymphoma, NHL often presents at advanced stage with non-contiguous/extranodal spread","RT roles: consolidation after chemo (bulky/DLBCL), definitive for early-stage indolent disease, and highly effective PALLIATION"],"dx":["Diagnose by biopsy with immunophenotyping; stage with PET/CT (Lugano); assess bulk, extranodal sites, and histologic grade","Distinguish aggressive (curable, chemo-driven) from indolent (radiosensitive, often incurable but slow) lymphomas","Identify localized (stage I-II) indolent disease treatable with RT alone"],"tx":["DLBCL/aggressive: R-CHOP chemoimmunotherapy is the backbone; consolidative ISRT for bulky or partially responding disease","LOCALIZED FOLLICULAR (indolent) lymphoma: definitive low-dose RT (~24 Gy, or the very-low-dose 4 Gy 'BOOM'/2x2 Gy for palliation) can be curative for stage I-II disease","Very-low-dose 2 x 2 Gy is remarkably effective palliation for indolent lymphomas (excellent response, minimal toxicity)"],"pearls":["Localized (stage I-II) follicular lymphoma can be CURED with low-dose RT alone (~24 Gy)","2 x 2 Gy ('boom-boom') is highly effective, low-toxicity palliation for indolent lymphomas","DLBCL is treated with R-CHOP; RT consolidates bulky or incompletely responding sites","NHL often presents advanced/non-contiguous - unlike the contiguous spread of Hodgkin lymphoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559328/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult NHL Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lymphoma/hp/adult-nhl-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-tbi-plasma-cell","name":"Total Body Irradiation & Plasma Cell Malignancies","sec":"radonc","present":["TOTAL BODY IRRADIATION (TBI) is part of the conditioning regimen before allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant - it provides immunosuppression (to prevent graft rejection) and eradicates malignant cells (e.g., in acute leukemia)","MULTIPLE MYELOMA and solitary plasmacytoma are radiosensitive plasma cell malignancies where RT provides excellent local control and palliation","Plasmacytoma (solitary bone or extramedullary) can be treated definitively with RT"],"dx":["TBI: determine the regimen (myeloablative vs reduced-intensity) with the transplant team; lung dose is the critical constraint (pneumonitis)","Myeloma: diagnose with serum/urine protein electrophoresis, free light chains, marrow, and imaging (skeletal survey/MRI/PET); identify painful lytic lesions or cord compression for RT","Solitary plasmacytoma: confirm a single lesion without systemic myeloma"],"tx":["TBI: delivered in fractionated regimens (e.g., ~12 Gy in 6 fractions) with LUNG SHIELDING/dose limits to reduce fatal pneumonitis; combined with chemotherapy (e.g., cyclophosphamide) for conditioning","MYELOMA: low-dose PALLIATIVE RT (~8-30 Gy, often ~20 Gy/5-10) rapidly relieves bone pain and treats cord compression or impending fracture - myeloma is very radiosensitive","SOLITARY PLASMACYTOMA: definitive RT (~40-50 Gy) achieves high local control (many later progress to systemic myeloma - monitor)"],"pearls":["TBI conditions patients for allogeneic transplant (immunosuppression + tumor kill); LUNG dose limits are critical to prevent fatal pneumonitis","Myeloma is very radiosensitive - low-dose palliative RT (~20 Gy) quickly relieves bone pain and cord compression","Solitary plasmacytoma is treated definitively with RT (~40-50 Gy) but frequently progresses to systemic myeloma","Fractionated TBI (~12 Gy/6) with lung shielding reduces toxicity versus single-fraction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Total Body Irradiation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568795/"},{"t":"NCI: Plasma Cell Neoplasms Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/myeloma/hp/myeloma-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-peds-cns-csi","name":"Pediatric CNS Tumors & Craniospinal Irradiation","sec":"radonc","present":["CNS tumors are the most common solid tumors of childhood. MEDULLOBLASTOMA (a posterior-fossa embryonal tumor) is the prototype requiring CRANIOSPINAL IRRADIATION (CSI) because it seeds the entire neuraxis via CSF","EPENDYMOMA is often treated with maximal resection plus involved-field RT; other tumors include low-grade glioma and germ-cell tumors","Late effects (neurocognitive, endocrine, growth, second cancers) are a paramount concern in these long survivors - driving proton use"],"dx":["Diagnose with MRI of the whole brain AND spine plus CSF cytology (to detect neuraxis dissemination) before treatment; molecular subgrouping (e.g., medulloblastoma WNT/SHH/group 3/4) refines risk","Risk-stratify medulloblastoma (average vs high risk) by residual disease, dissemination, and molecular subtype","Assess age - RT is often deferred/avoided in very young children (chemotherapy bridging) to protect the developing brain"],"tx":["MEDULLOBLASTOMA: maximal safe resection -> CRANIOSPINAL IRRADIATION (average-risk ~23.4 Gy, high-risk ~36 Gy to the neuraxis) with a POSTERIOR FOSSA/tumor-bed BOOST to ~54-55.8 Gy, plus chemotherapy","Use PROTON therapy for CSI when available to spare the heart, lungs, thyroid, and bowel anterior to the spine and reduce late effects; defer RT in infants","EPENDYMOMA: maximal resection + involved-field RT (~54-59.4 Gy); coordinate multidisciplinary late-effects surveillance"],"pearls":["Medulloblastoma requires craniospinal irradiation because it seeds the whole neuraxis via CSF - image brain + spine and sample CSF before treatment","Average-risk CSI ~23.4 Gy, high-risk ~36 Gy, with a posterior-fossa/tumor-bed boost to ~54-55.8 Gy","Protons for CSI spare anterior organs (heart, thyroid, bowel) and reduce late toxicity in children","Avoid/defer RT in infants and very young children to protect neurocognitive development"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Medulloblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431069/"},{"t":"NCI: Childhood CNS Embryonal Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/child-cns-embryonal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-wilms-neuroblastoma","name":"Wilms Tumor, Neuroblastoma & Pediatric Abdominal Tumors","sec":"radonc","present":["WILMS TUMOR (nephroblastoma) is the most common pediatric renal tumor, presenting as a painless abdominal mass in a young child; it is highly curable with multimodality therapy","NEUROBLASTOMA arises from sympathetic/adrenal tissue, presents as an abdominal mass (may cross midline) with possible paraneoplastic features (opsoclonus-myoclonus), and ranges from spontaneously regressing to high-risk metastatic disease","RT is integrated by stage/risk into surgery + chemotherapy backbones"],"dx":["Wilms: imaging and surgery/biopsy; stage per COG (US) or SIOP; histology (favorable vs anaplastic) and stage drive RT use","Neuroblastoma: diagnose with catecholamines (urine VMA/HVA), imaging, MIBG scan, and marrow; risk-stratify (age, stage/INRG, MYCN amplification)","Both: assess extent, nodal/margin status, and risk group to decide on RT"],"tx":["WILMS: surgery (nephrectomy) + chemotherapy; add FLANK/abdominal RT for higher stage/unfavorable histology (relatively LOW doses, ~10-20 Gy, given radiosensitivity) - whole-abdomen RT for diffuse spill/peritoneal disease","NEUROBLASTOMA: HIGH-RISK disease gets multimodal therapy including RT to the primary site and persistent metastatic sites, plus MIBG-based therapy in some protocols; low/intermediate-risk often needs no RT","Use modern techniques to limit dose to the remaining kidney, liver, spine (symmetric to avoid scoliosis), and gonads"],"pearls":["Wilms tumor is very radiosensitive - relatively LOW RT doses (~10-20 Gy) are used, added for higher stage/anaplastic histology","Neuroblastoma risk hinges on MYCN amplification, age, and stage; RT is reserved for high-risk disease","Treat the spine symmetrically in children to avoid growth asymmetry/scoliosis, and spare the contralateral kidney and gonads","Both cancers are managed by multidisciplinary surgery + chemotherapy with stage/risk-directed RT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Wilms Tumor / Nephroblastoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442004/"},{"t":"NCI: Wilms Tumor Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/kidney/hp/wilms-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-rhabdomyosarcoma","name":"Rhabdomyosarcoma & Pediatric Considerations/Late Effects","sec":"radonc","present":["Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft-tissue sarcoma of childhood, arising in the head/neck (including parameningeal), genitourinary, and extremity sites; EMBRYONAL (favorable) and ALVEOLAR (unfavorable, often FOXO1 fusion-positive) are the main subtypes","Presents as a mass with site-specific effects (e.g., proptosis for orbit, cranial neuropathies for parameningeal); risk group integrates stage, group (postsurgical), histology, and fusion status","Treatment is always multimodal (chemotherapy + local control by surgery and/or RT)"],"dx":["Biopsy for histology and fusion status (FOXO1); stage with imaging and marrow/nodal assessment; classify by IRS group and risk","Identify PARAMENINGEAL primaries (skull-base extension raises CNS risk and alters RT timing/volumes)","Risk-stratify (low/intermediate/high) to set chemotherapy and RT dose"],"tx":["Multimodal therapy: chemotherapy (e.g., vincristine/actinomycin/cyclophosphamide) plus LOCAL CONTROL with RT and/or surgery; RT dose is risk/response-adapted (roughly ~36-50.4 Gy)","ORBITAL rhabdomyosarcoma has an excellent prognosis with chemo + RT (organ preservation - avoid exenteration); parameningeal disease needs prompt RT for skull-base/intracranial extension","Emphasize LATE-EFFECT mitigation (growth, endocrine, second cancers) with modern conformal/proton techniques in these children"],"pearls":["Alveolar (FOXO1 fusion-positive) rhabdomyosarcoma is higher-risk than embryonal","Orbital rhabdomyosarcoma has an excellent prognosis with chemo + RT - preserve the eye rather than exenterate","Parameningeal primaries with skull-base/intracranial extension need prompt RT and CNS-directed attention","Late effects drive technique choice (conformal/proton) in pediatric sarcoma survivors"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Rhabdomyosarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430818/"},{"t":"NCI: Childhood Rhabdomyosarcoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/rhabdomyosarcoma-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-skin-melanoma","name":"Non-Melanoma Skin Cancer & Melanoma","sec":"radonc","present":["NON-MELANOMA SKIN CANCER (basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma) is very common and usually cured by surgery; RT is an effective alternative for nonsurgical candidates or cosmetically/functionally sensitive sites (eyelid, nose, lip, ear)","MELANOMA is relatively radioRESISTANT to conventional fractionation but RT has defined ADJUVANT and PALLIATIVE roles; wide local excision +/- sentinel node is the primary treatment, with immunotherapy/targeted therapy for advanced disease","RT decisions weigh local control against skin/soft-tissue toxicity"],"dx":["Diagnose skin cancers by biopsy; assess size, depth, histology, and perineural invasion (high-risk squamous cell carcinoma) and site","Melanoma: obtain Breslow depth, ulceration, and sentinel node status; identify high-risk features for adjuvant nodal RT","Determine surgical candidacy and whether RT is definitive, adjuvant, or palliative"],"tx":["NON-MELANOMA: definitive RT for nonsurgical candidates or function/cosmesis-sensitive sites; POSTOPERATIVE RT for positive margins, perineural invasion, or recurrent/aggressive squamous cell carcinoma","MELANOMA: consider adjuvant RT to the primary/nodal bed for high-risk features (though systemic immunotherapy has reduced its use); melanoma/RCC brain metastases respond better to SRS (high dose per fraction) than to conventional fractionation given radioresistance","Palliative RT (including hypofractionated regimens) controls symptomatic or unresectable melanoma"],"pearls":["RT cures non-melanoma skin cancer when surgery is unsuitable and preserves function/cosmesis at sensitive sites (eyelid, nose, ear, lip)","Melanoma is relatively radioresistant to conventional fractionation - favor SRS/high-dose-per-fraction for brain metastases","Postoperative RT for skin squamous cell carcinoma with positive margins, perineural invasion, or recurrence","Adjuvant melanoma RT is now less used given effective immunotherapy/targeted agents"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Basal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482439/"},{"t":"NCI: Skin Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/skin/hp/skin-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-soft-tissue-sarcoma","name":"Soft-Tissue Sarcoma - Pre- vs Post-Op Radiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Soft-tissue sarcomas are a heterogeneous group (liposarcoma, undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, leiomyosarcoma, synovial sarcoma, etc.) most commonly arising in the EXTREMITY; limb-sparing surgery + RT has replaced amputation","Present as an enlarging, often painless deep mass; grade and size drive prognosis and the need for RT","RT (pre- or post-operative) improves local control for intermediate/high-grade or large/deep tumors"],"dx":["Image with MRI of the primary and biopsy along a planned resection axis (CORE needle, oriented so the tract can be excised); grade and stage (FNCLCC grade, size, depth)","Assess resectability for limb-sparing surgery; determine RT timing (pre- vs post-op) with the surgeon","Chest CT for pulmonary metastasis surveillance (the lung is the dominant metastatic site)"],"tx":["PREOPERATIVE RT (~50 Gy): smaller field and lower dose, better long-term function/fibrosis, but HIGHER acute WOUND-COMPLICATION rate (the classic NCIC SR2 trade-off)","POSTOPERATIVE RT (~60-66 Gy): larger field/higher dose (more late fibrosis/edema/stiffness) but fewer acute wound problems; used when surgery is done first or margins are close/positive","Limb-sparing surgery + RT yields local control comparable to amputation; retroperitoneal sarcoma often uses preoperative RT (STRASS informs selective use)"],"pearls":["The pre- vs post-op trade-off (NCIC SR2): preoperative RT (50 Gy) causes more acute WOUND complications but less late fibrosis; postoperative RT (60-66 Gy) is the reverse","Limb-sparing surgery + RT replaced amputation with equivalent local control","Biopsy along the planned resection axis so the tract can be excised en bloc","The lung is the dominant metastatic site - survey with chest CT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Soft Tissue Sarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538232/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult Soft Tissue Sarcoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/adult-soft-tissue-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-merkel-cutaneous-lymphoma","name":"Merkel Cell Carcinoma & Cutaneous Lymphoma","sec":"radonc","present":["MERKEL CELL CARCINOMA is a rare, aggressive neuroendocrine skin cancer (associated with Merkel cell polyomavirus and UV/immunosuppression) that is highly RADIOSENSITIVE and prone to nodal and local recurrence","CUTANEOUS T-CELL LYMPHOMA (mycosis fungoides/Sezary syndrome) presents with patches/plaques progressing to tumors; it is exquisitely radiosensitive","RT is central to both - adjuvant for Merkel cell carcinoma, and definitive/palliative (including total-skin therapy) for cutaneous lymphoma"],"dx":["Merkel cell carcinoma: biopsy (CK20 dot-like positivity), sentinel node biopsy, and staging imaging; high propensity for regional nodal spread","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma: skin biopsy and staging (TNMB, including blood involvement in Sezary syndrome)","Assess extent to plan local vs regional vs total-skin treatment"],"tx":["MERKEL CELL CARCINOMA: wide excision with ADJUVANT RT to the primary bed AND draining nodes (high recurrence risk) - it is very radiosensitive; definitive RT for unresectable disease; immunotherapy (anti-PD-1/PD-L1) for advanced disease","CUTANEOUS T-CELL LYMPHOMA: localized lesions respond to LOW-DOSE local RT (even ~8-12 Gy); extensive skin disease is treated with TOTAL SKIN ELECTRON BEAM THERAPY (TSEBT), using electrons to treat the whole skin surface while sparing deeper organs","Palliate symptomatic lesions with brief low-dose RT (excellent response)"],"pearls":["Merkel cell carcinoma is aggressive but very radiosensitive - give adjuvant RT to the primary bed AND draining nodes","CK20 dot-like staining supports Merkel cell carcinoma; anti-PD-1/PD-L1 immunotherapy treats advanced disease","Total skin electron beam therapy (TSEBT) treats whole-skin cutaneous T-cell lymphoma using electrons that spare deep organs","Cutaneous lymphomas respond to very low RT doses (~8-12 Gy) - highly effective palliation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Merkel Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441960/"},{"t":"NCI: Merkel Cell Carcinoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/skin/hp/merkel-cell-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-benign-conditions","name":"Benign Conditions Treated with Radiation","sec":"radonc","present":["Low-dose RT effectively treats several BENIGN (non-cancer) conditions by targeting proliferating cells or aberrant tissue - always weighing the small long-term second-cancer risk, especially in younger patients","Classic indications: KELOID (post-excision RT prevents recurrence), HETEROTOPIC OSSIFICATION prophylaxis (after hip arthroplasty/acetabular surgery), and GRAVES ORBITOPATHY (thyroid eye disease)","Others include pterygium recurrence, Dupuytren/Ledderhose disease, and aggressive fibromatosis (desmoid)"],"dx":["Confirm the benign diagnosis and that RT is appropriate (failed/insufficient conservative options, high recurrence risk); counsel on the small second-malignancy risk","For heterotopic ossification, identify high-risk patients (prior HO, ankylosing spondylitis) for prophylaxis","For Graves orbitopathy, confirm active moderate-severe disease with a component amenable to RT"],"tx":["KELOID: excision followed promptly by RT (e.g., a few fractions, ~12-20 Gy total, or single-fraction) markedly reduces recurrence","HETEROTOPIC OSSIFICATION prophylaxis: a SINGLE ~7-8 Gy fraction within ~24-72 hours around surgery (pre- or early post-op) prevents ectopic bone (as effective as NSAIDs)","GRAVES ORBITOPATHY: low-dose orbital RT (~20 Gy in 10 fractions) reduces inflammation/proptosis, often with steroids; desmoid/fibromatosis: RT for unresectable/recurrent disease"],"pearls":["Heterotopic ossification prophylaxis: a single ~7-8 Gy fraction perioperatively (within ~24-72 h) prevents ectopic bone","Keloids: post-excision RT dramatically lowers recurrence versus excision alone","Graves orbitopathy responds to low-dose orbital RT (~20 Gy/10), often with corticosteroids","Always weigh the small long-term second-cancer risk when irradiating benign disease, especially in young patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Heterotopic Ossification","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519029/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy Overview","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-bone-mets-palliation","name":"Bone Metastases - Palliative Fractionation","sec":"radonc","present":["Bone metastases are a leading cause of cancer pain (common from breast, prostate, lung, kidney, myeloma) and can cause pathologic fracture, hypercalcemia, and cord compression","Palliative RT provides effective PAIN RELIEF in most patients, with onset over days to a few weeks","The central board question is SINGLE-FRACTION versus MULTI-FRACTION treatment"],"dx":["Confirm the painful lesion corresponds to imaging (X-ray/CT/MRI/bone scan); assess fracture risk (e.g., Mirels score) and rule out cord compression","Identify whether the site is weight-bearing or at risk of pathologic fracture (may favor multi-fraction or surgical stabilization first)","Distinguish uncomplicated painful metastasis from complicated (impending fracture, cord compression, prior RT)"],"tx":["UNCOMPLICATED painful bone metastasis: SINGLE-FRACTION 8 Gy x1 gives EQUIVALENT pain relief to multi-fraction regimens (20 Gy/5, 30 Gy/10) - it is more convenient but has a HIGHER RETREATMENT rate (retreatment is straightforward, ~8 Gy again)","Multi-fraction (30 Gy/10) is often chosen for weight-bearing sites, soft-tissue components, or after surgical stabilization; postoperative RT follows fixation of a pathologic fracture","SBRT for oligometastatic or radioresistant spine/bone disease and for reirradiation; add bone-modifying agents (bisphosphonates/denosumab) and treat hypercalcemia"],"pearls":["Single-fraction 8 Gy equals multi-fraction regimens for pain relief in uncomplicated bone metastases - just with a higher (easily repeated) retreatment rate","Use 8 Gy x1 for convenience/poor prognosis; consider 30 Gy/10 for weight-bearing sites or a soft-tissue component","Assess fracture risk (Mirels) and rule out cord compression before palliating","Reirradiation and SBRT are options for recurrent or radioresistant bone disease"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bone Metastasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507911/"},{"t":"NCI: Cancer Pain / Bone Metastases (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/pain"}]},{"id":"radonc-brain-mets-palliation-reirradiation","name":"Brain Metastases Palliation & Re-Irradiation Principles","sec":"radonc","present":["Beyond focal SRS (covered separately), brain metastases often require PALLIATIVE whole-brain RT for diffuse disease and raise the question of RE-IRRADIATION when disease progresses after prior brain RT","Symptomatic edema causes headache and deficits; corticosteroids give rapid symptom relief while RT is arranged","Re-irradiation weighs cumulative normal-tissue dose (especially serial organs) against tumor control and prognosis"],"dx":["Assess number/size of lesions, prior RT dose/date, performance status, and extracranial control to choose SRS vs whole-brain RT vs best supportive care (prognostic tools like GPA help)","For re-irradiation, tally CUMULATIVE dose to serial structures (brainstem, optic apparatus, cord) accounting for partial tissue recovery over time","Distinguish tumor progression from radiation NECROSIS (advanced MRI/perfusion, sometimes biopsy) - a key post-SRS dilemma"],"tx":["Start CORTICOSTEROIDS (dexamethasone) for symptomatic edema/mass effect; whole-brain RT (30 Gy/10 or 20 Gy/5) palliates diffuse disease, with hippocampal avoidance + memantine to protect cognition when appropriate","RE-IRRADIATION: focal SRS is preferred over repeat whole-brain RT to limit neurocognitive toxicity; respect cumulative serial-organ limits and accept higher radionecrosis risk","Manage radiation necrosis (steroids, bevacizumab, sometimes surgery); integrate CNS-active systemic therapy and consider best supportive care for poor prognosis"],"pearls":["Dexamethasone rapidly relieves symptomatic peritumoral edema while definitive RT is arranged","Whole-brain palliation is 30 Gy/10 or 20 Gy/5; use hippocampal avoidance + memantine to spare cognition when appropriate","Re-irradiation favors focal SRS over repeat WBRT and requires tallying cumulative serial-organ dose (with partial recovery)","Post-SRS, distinguish tumor progression from radiation necrosis - they are managed very differently"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Whole Brain Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574499/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult CNS Tumors Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-oncologic-emergencies","name":"Oncologic Emergencies in Radiation Oncology","sec":"radonc","present":["Several oncologic emergencies require URGENT radiotherapy (often alongside steroids, surgery, or endovascular care). The core radiation-treated emergencies are malignant SPINAL CORD COMPRESSION, SUPERIOR VENA CAVA (SVC) syndrome, symptomatic BRAIN metastases with mass effect, and uncontrolled TUMOR BLEEDING/hemorrhage","Airway obstruction from a central tumor and malignant hemoptysis are life-threatening and may respond rapidly to RT","Rapid recognition and multidisciplinary triage determine outcome"],"dx":["CORD COMPRESSION (see the dedicated entry): new back pain + neurologic signs -> emergent whole-spine MRI + dexamethasone; ambulatory status at treatment predicts recovery","SVC syndrome: face/arm swelling, distended neck veins, dyspnea -> CT to define the cause/level and obtain tissue (histology dictates therapy)","Tumor hemorrhage (hemoptysis, hematuria, vaginal/GI bleeding) and airway compromise: assess severity, secure airway/hemodynamics, and identify the bleeding tumor"],"tx":["Cord compression: immediate dexamethasone; surgery + RT for suitable single-level cases (Patchell), otherwise RT (8 Gy x1 or 30 Gy/10) or spine SBRT for radioresistant/oligometastatic disease","SVC syndrome: histology first when stable (chemosensitive tumors - small cell lung cancer, lymphoma, germ cell - respond to systemic therapy); ENDOVASCULAR STENTING gives the fastest relief for severe symptoms, with RT for many solid tumors","Tumor bleeding: hemostatic PALLIATIVE RT (often hypofractionated, e.g., a few large fractions) controls hemoptysis/hematuria/gynecologic bleeding; embolization/surgery as needed"],"pearls":["The radiation-treated oncologic emergencies: cord compression, SVC syndrome, symptomatic brain mets, and tumor hemorrhage","Cord compression - give dexamethasone and image the WHOLE spine immediately; ambulatory status predicts outcome","SVC syndrome is rarely immediately life-threatening - get tissue first when stable (chemosensitive histologies respond to systemic therapy); stenting is fastest for severe cases","Hemostatic hypofractionated RT effectively controls tumor bleeding (hemoptysis, hematuria, gynecologic hemorrhage)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Oncologic Emergencies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560907/"},{"t":"NCI: Cardiopulmonary Syndromes (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects"}]},{"id":"radonc-palliative-symptom-control","name":"Palliative RT for Symptom Control","sec":"radonc","present":["Palliative radiotherapy relieves symptoms from incurable cancer - PAIN, BLEEDING (hemostasis), OBSTRUCTION (airway, esophagus, bowel), neurologic compromise, and ulcerating/fungating masses - typically with short, hypofractionated courses","The goal is rapid, durable symptom relief with minimal treatment burden and toxicity, matched to prognosis","A cornerstone of supportive oncology and often delivered alongside systemic therapy and palliative care"],"dx":["Define the symptomatic target and clarify the goal (pain, hemostasis, decompression); estimate prognosis to choose fractionation (shorter courses for poorer prognosis)","Confirm the symptom is radiation-responsive and localizable; weigh prior RT and normal-tissue tolerance","Coordinate with palliative care, interventional, and systemic teams"],"tx":["Use SHORT, hypofractionated regimens tailored to symptom and prognosis: single 8 Gy for bone pain, a few fractions for hemostasis, and short courses for obstructing lesions (e.g., endobronchial/esophageal obstruction, sometimes with brachytherapy)","Match intensity to life expectancy - avoid protracted courses in patients with short prognosis; escalate/lengthen only when durable control justifies the burden","Integrate steroids, stents, and analgesia; re-treat when appropriate as symptoms recur"],"pearls":["Palliative RT targets pain, bleeding, and obstruction with short hypofractionated courses matched to prognosis","Single-fraction 8 Gy is standard for uncomplicated bone pain; a few fractions achieve hemostasis for bleeding tumors","Endobronchial/esophageal obstruction can be relieved by external-beam RT +/- brachytherapy (and stents)","Right-size the course to life expectancy - shorter for poorer prognosis to minimize burden"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Palliative Radiation Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564342/"},{"t":"NCI: Palliative Care in Cancer","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet"}]},{"id":"radonc-svc-obstruction","name":"Superior Vena Cava Syndrome & Airway/Visceral Obstruction","sec":"radonc","present":["SUPERIOR VENA CAVA (SVC) SYNDROME results from obstruction of the SVC (most often by lung cancer or lymphoma, or by thrombus around indwelling catheters) - producing face/neck/arm swelling, distended neck and chest wall veins, plethora, headache, and dyspnea","Malignant AIRWAY obstruction (endobronchial/tracheal tumor) causes dyspnea, stridor, and post-obstructive pneumonia; malignant ESOPHAGEAL/GI obstruction causes dysphagia or bowel compromise","These obstructive syndromes are managed by relieving the blockage - RT, stenting, and/or systemic therapy per histology"],"dx":["Image with contrast CT to define the level/cause of SVC obstruction; obtain TISSUE diagnosis when the patient is stable, because chemosensitive histologies (small cell lung cancer, lymphoma, germ cell tumors) change management","Assess airway compromise urgently (may need bronchoscopy/stent); characterize the obstructing lesion for RT/stent/surgery","Evaluate for catheter-associated thrombus (managed with anticoagulation +/- catheter removal, not RT)"],"tx":["SVC syndrome: if stable, establish histology first - CHEMOSENSITIVE tumors (small cell lung cancer, lymphoma, germ cell) respond quickly to systemic therapy; for most solid tumors, RT relieves obstruction; ENDOVASCULAR STENTING provides the fastest relief in severe/threatening cases","Malignant AIRWAY obstruction: urgent measures may include bronchoscopic debulking/stenting, then external-beam RT +/- endobronchial brachytherapy; steroids and airway management as needed","Esophageal/visceral obstruction: stenting and/or RT (external-beam +/- brachytherapy) restores patency"],"pearls":["SVC syndrome is usually NOT an immediate emergency - obtain tissue first when stable, since chemosensitive tumors (small cell, lymphoma, germ cell) respond to systemic therapy","Endovascular stenting gives the fastest SVC relief for severe or life-threatening presentations","Catheter-associated SVC thrombus is treated with anticoagulation, not radiation","Malignant airway/esophageal obstruction is relieved with stenting and/or RT (external-beam +/- brachytherapy)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Superior Vena Cava Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441872/"},{"t":"NCI: Cardiopulmonary Syndromes (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects"}]},{"id":"radonc-acute-toxicity","name":"Acute Radiation Toxicity by Site & Management","sec":"radonc","present":["ACUTE toxicities occur DURING or within ~90 days of RT, arising in rapidly dividing tissues (mucosa, skin, bone marrow, GI epithelium); they are usually self-limited and REVERSIBLE, and are graded by CTCAE","Site-specific patterns: head/neck -> MUCOSITIS, xerostomia, dysgeusia, dermatitis; thorax -> esophagitis, cough (early pneumonitis); abdomen/pelvis -> nausea, diarrhea/enteritis, cystitis, proctitis; skin -> erythema then moist desquamation","Fatigue is near-universal; myelosuppression occurs with large marrow volumes or concurrent chemotherapy"],"dx":["Grade toxicity with CTCAE and examine the treated site; distinguish expected acute reactions from infection or progression","Anticipate site-specific effects and screen proactively (weight/nutrition in head-neck and GI RT; blood counts with large fields/chemoRT)","Identify early pneumonitis (cough/dyspnea/low-grade fever weeks after thoracic RT) versus infection"],"tx":["MUCOSITIS: analgesia (including topical/systemic), oral care, nutrition support (feeding tube if needed); manage candidiasis; esophagitis - analgesia, acid suppression, nutrition","SKIN: gentle care and topical agents for dermatitis; moist desquamation - non-adherent dressings; GI - antidiarrheals, hydration, antiemetics; cystitis/proctitis - symptomatic care","Early radiation PNEUMONITIS: corticosteroids for symptomatic cases; support nutrition and treat symptomatically throughout, aiming to avoid unplanned treatment breaks (which compromise tumor control)"],"pearls":["Acute toxicity hits rapidly dividing tissue (mucosa, skin, marrow, GI) and is generally reversible; grade with CTCAE","Manage mucositis and esophagitis aggressively (pain control + nutrition) to avoid treatment breaks that reduce tumor control","Symptomatic early radiation pneumonitis (weeks after thoracic RT) is treated with corticosteroids","Moist desquamation is an acute skin reaction managed with non-adherent dressings and gentle skin care"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiation Therapy Acute Toxicity","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563217/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Therapy Side Effects","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy/side-effects"}]},{"id":"radonc-late-effects-second-malignancy","name":"Late Radiation Effects & Second Malignancies","sec":"radonc","present":["LATE effects appear MONTHS TO YEARS after RT, arise in slowly proliferating/late-responding tissues, are often IRREVERSIBLE (fibrosis, vascular damage, atrophy), and are driven by dose per FRACTION and total dose","Classic late toxicities: FIBROSIS, radiation MYELOPATHY (cord), pneumonitis/fibrosis, xerostomia, telangiectasia, lymphedema, bowel stricture/fistula, infertility, cataract, hypothyroidism, and osteoradionecrosis (notably mandible after head/neck RT)","SECOND MALIGNANCIES are a serious very-late risk, especially after childhood/young-adult treatment (e.g., breast/lung cancer after Hodgkin lymphoma RT)"],"dx":["Attribute late effects to the irradiated volume and dose; monitor at-risk organs (thyroid function, cardiac and pulmonary surveillance after thoracic RT, breast screening after chest RT in young patients)","Recognize osteoradionecrosis risk before dental extractions in prior head/neck fields; assess for radiation-induced second cancers in long-term survivors","Distinguish late normal-tissue injury from tumor recurrence"],"tx":["PREVENTION is primary: minimize dose/volume to organs at risk, control fraction size for late-responding tissues, and use conformal/proton techniques, especially in children and young adults","Manage established late effects supportively: pentoxifylline-vitamin E for fibrosis, hyperbaric oxygen/surgery for osteoradionecrosis, hormone replacement for hypothyroidism, and lymphedema therapy","SURVIVORSHIP: risk-based screening for second malignancies and organ dysfunction (e.g., earlier breast MRI/mammography after chest RT in young women; cardiac follow-up)"],"pearls":["Late effects are often irreversible and driven by dose PER FRACTION - the reason serial organs have strict fraction-size limits","Second malignancies are a major very-late risk after young-age RT (breast/lung cancer after Hodgkin lymphoma) - do risk-based survivorship screening","Osteoradionecrosis of the mandible - address dental health BEFORE head/neck RT and avoid post-RT extractions in the field","Protons/conformal techniques in children reduce integral dose and late toxicity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiation-Induced Late Effects","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563157/"},{"t":"NCI: Late Effects of Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/late-effects-hp-pdq"}]},{"id":"radonc-particle-therapy","name":"Particle Therapy (Protons & Carbon Ions)","sec":"radonc","present":["PARTICLE therapy uses charged particles (protons; and carbon ions at select centers) that deposit dose at a defined depth - the BRAGG PEAK - with little or NO EXIT dose beyond the target, unlike photons","This reduces integral (low-dose) radiation to surrounding normal tissue, the main rationale in situations where sparing developing or critical tissue matters most","Carbon ions add higher LET (linear energy transfer) with greater biological effect (higher RBE), of interest for radioresistant tumors"],"dx":["Identify the best candidates: PEDIATRIC tumors (reduce late effects and second cancers), tumors near critical serial organs (skull-base chordoma/chondrosarcoma, spinal/paraspinal tumors, ocular melanoma), and selected reirradiation","Understand proton RBE (~1.1) and range UNCERTAINTY - small changes in tissue density shift the Bragg peak, demanding robust planning","Weigh evidence and access/cost - benefit is clearest where normal-tissue sparing is decisive"],"tx":["Use protons where reducing integral dose changes outcomes: children (fewer late effects/second malignancies), skull-base and paraspinal tumors near the cord/brainstem, ocular melanoma, and select reirradiation","Plan robustly for range uncertainty (density heterogeneity, motion) - proton dose distributions are more sensitive to setup/anatomy changes than photons","Carbon-ion therapy (limited centers) for selected radioresistant tumors leveraging higher LET/RBE"],"pearls":["The Bragg peak gives protons a sharp dose fall-off with no exit dose - reducing integral dose to normal tissue","Protons are most valuable in pediatrics (fewer second cancers/late effects) and for tumors abutting critical serial organs (skull base, spine)","Proton RBE is ~1.1; range uncertainty from tissue density makes robust planning essential","Carbon ions add high-LET/high-RBE biology for select radioresistant tumors at specialized centers"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Proton Beam Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560867/"},{"t":"NCI: Proton Therapy","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-radiopharmaceuticals","name":"Radiopharmaceuticals & Systemic Radiotherapy","sec":"radonc","present":["Radiopharmaceuticals deliver radiation SYSTEMICALLY by targeting tumor tissue biologically, using primarily ALPHA or BETA emitters bound to a targeting molecule or taken up metabolically","Key agents: RADIUM-223 (alpha, bone-seeking, for symptomatic bone-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer - improves survival, ALSYMPCA); LUTETIUM-177 (beta) as Lu-177-DOTATATE for somatostatin-receptor NETs (NETTER-1) and Lu-177-PSMA-617 for metastatic prostate cancer (VISION)","IODINE-131 (RAI) for differentiated thyroid cancer and hyperthyroidism; YTTRIUM-90 microspheres (radioembolization/SIRT) for liver tumors; Sr-89/Sm-153 for bone-pain palliation"],"dx":["Confirm target expression/uptake (e.g., PSMA or somatostatin-receptor PET, radioiodine avidity) before therapy; assess marrow, renal, and hepatic reserve","Select the agent by tumor type and biodistribution; radium-223 is for BONE-predominant prostate disease (not visceral-dominant)","Plan radiation-safety precautions appropriate to the isotope and emission type"],"tx":["RADIUM-223 for symptomatic bone-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (survival benefit, favorable safety - an alpha emitter with short range sparing marrow); Lu-177-PSMA-617 (VISION) and Lu-177-DOTATATE (NETTER-1) for PSMA+ prostate cancer and SSTR+ NETs respectively","I-131 for thyroid remnant ablation/metastatic differentiated thyroid cancer and for hyperthyroidism; Y-90 radioembolization for hepatocellular carcinoma/liver metastases; Sr-89/Sm-153 palliate multifocal bone pain","Apply radiation-safety and excretion precautions (especially I-131) and monitor for marrow/renal toxicity"],"pearls":["Radium-223 (alpha emitter) improves survival in bone-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (ALSYMPCA) - for bone-predominant, not visceral, disease","Lu-177-PSMA-617 (VISION) for PSMA+ metastatic prostate cancer; Lu-177-DOTATATE (NETTER-1) for somatostatin-receptor+ neuroendocrine tumors","I-131 treats differentiated thyroid cancer/hyperthyroidism; Y-90 microspheres treat liver tumors (radioembolization)","Confirm target uptake (PSMA/SSTR PET, iodine avidity) before selecting a radiopharmaceutical"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiopharmaceuticals","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576385/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiopharmaceuticals in Cancer Therapy","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"radonc-radiation-safety","name":"Radiation Safety, Emergencies & Accidental Exposure","sec":"radonc","present":["Radiation PROTECTION rests on ALARA (As Low As Reasonably Achievable) and the three principles: TIME, DISTANCE, and SHIELDING; exposure limits protect workers and the public, with special limits for the pregnant worker/fetus","Distinguish EXPOSURE/irradiation (no residual radioactivity) from CONTAMINATION (radioactive material on/in the body, requiring decontamination) - a key emergency concept","ACUTE RADIATION SYNDROME (ARS) follows high whole-body doses over a short time, with dose-dependent subsyndromes"],"dx":["ARS subsyndromes by increasing dose: HEMATOPOIETIC (marrow suppression, the earliest/most treatable), GASTROINTESTINAL (mucosal loss, dehydration, sepsis), and CEREBROVASCULAR/neurovascular (very high dose, rapidly fatal); the TIME TO ONSET OF VOMITING and the lymphocyte-depletion kinetics estimate dose/prognosis","In an exposed patient, determine whether contamination is present (survey/decontaminate) versus pure external exposure (patient is not radioactive and poses no risk to staff)","Recognize localized high-dose injuries (radiation burns) from source mishandling"],"tx":["Radiation emergencies: ensure life-threatening medical issues take priority, then DECONTAMINATE (remove clothing - removes most contamination - and wash) and manage per health-physics guidance; a purely externally IRRADIATED patient is not radioactive","ARS: supportive care - growth factors/cytokines and transfusion support for the hematopoietic syndrome, and consider stem-cell transplant for very high marrow doses; aggressive GI/infection support for the GI syndrome","Occupational protection: apply time/distance/shielding and ALARA; protect the pregnant worker (fetal dose limits); ensure sealed-source security and inventory in brachytherapy"],"pearls":["ALARA plus time, distance, and shielding are the core radiation-protection principles","Externally IRRADIATED (exposed) patients are NOT radioactive; CONTAMINATED patients need decontamination - removing clothing eliminates most contamination","ARS by dose: hematopoietic (lowest, most survivable) -> gastrointestinal -> cerebrovascular (highest, rapidly fatal); time-to-vomiting and lymphocyte kinetics estimate dose","Manage the hematopoietic syndrome with cytokines/growth factors and transfusion support; medical stabilization precedes decontamination"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Radiation Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441915/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiation Emergencies / Safety","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"rads-radiography-fluoroscopy","name":"Radiography & Fluoroscopy - Image Formation & Safety","sec":"rads","present":["Projection RADIOGRAPHY forms images from differential X-ray attenuation by tissue (bone > soft tissue > fat > air/gas - the five basic radiographic densities: metal, bone/calcium, soft tissue/water, fat, air)","FLUOROSCOPY provides real-time imaging for dynamic and guided procedures (barium studies, angiography, line placement), delivering continuous or pulsed X-rays","Digital detectors have replaced film; image quality balances against radiation dose"],"dx":["Recognize the five radiographic densities and how superimposition/summation and magnification affect projectional images; two orthogonal views are standard to localize findings","Fluoroscopic dose depends on time, beam-on technique (pulsed vs continuous), magnification, and geometry; the image intensifier/flat-panel and last-image-hold reduce dose","Optimize technique (kVp/mAs) for the clinical question while limiting dose"],"tx":["Minimize fluoroscopy dose: use PULSED fluoroscopy, last-image-hold, collimation, minimal magnification, and keep the image receptor close to the patient with the X-ray tube far (increase source-to-skin distance)","Monitor cumulative skin dose in long procedures - DETERMINISTIC skin injury (erythema, epilation, necrosis) can occur above threshold doses; document peak skin dose","Apply operator protection: distance, lead shielding/aprons, and the inverse-square law (scatter drops steeply with distance)"],"pearls":["The five radiographic densities (increasing attenuation): air, fat, soft tissue/water, bone/calcium, metal","Pulsed fluoroscopy, collimation, and last-image-hold cut dose substantially - keep the tube away from and the detector close to the patient","Fluoroscopic skin injury is a DETERMINISTIC effect (threshold dose) - erythema, epilation, ulceration in prolonged procedures","Scatter to the operator falls with the inverse-square law - step back and shield"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiographic Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547721/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: X-ray (Radiography)","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ct-physics-dose","name":"CT Physics, Radiation Dose & ALARA","sec":"rads","present":["Computed tomography reconstructs cross-sectional images from many X-ray projections; attenuation is quantified in HOUNSFIELD UNITS (HU): water = 0, air = -1000, fat ~ -100, soft tissue ~ +40, acute blood ~ +50-70, bone/calcium/contrast are high positive","Windowing (level/width) displays the HU range relevant to the tissue of interest (lung, soft tissue, bone windows)","CT is the largest medical source of population radiation dose - stewardship is essential"],"dx":["Interpret HU to characterize tissue (e.g., simple fluid ~0, fat negative, acute hemorrhage hyperdense, calcification/contrast very high); use appropriate windows","Dose metrics: CTDIvol and dose-length product (DLP); effective dose estimates stochastic risk; multiphase/repeat scanning multiplies dose","Match protocol (phases, coverage, kVp) to the clinical question to avoid unnecessary exposure"],"tx":["Apply ALARA: justify every CT, use the lowest dose consistent with diagnostic quality (iterative/deep-learning reconstruction, automatic tube-current modulation, lower kVp), and limit multiphase acquisitions","Be especially dose-conscious in children and pregnancy - consider ultrasound/MRI alternatives when appropriate","Avoid duplicate imaging; use prior studies and appropriateness criteria to justify scans"],"pearls":["Hounsfield units: water 0, air -1000, fat ~-100, soft tissue ~+40, acute blood ~+50-70 - the basis of CT tissue characterization","CTDIvol and DLP are the reported dose metrics; effective dose estimates stochastic (cancer) risk","ALARA: justify, then optimize (tube-current modulation, iterative reconstruction, appropriate kVp) - fewer phases, lower dose","Children are more radiosensitive - 'image gently' and prefer ultrasound/MRI where they answer the question"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Computed Tomography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567796/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: CT Safety","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-mri-physics-safety","name":"MRI Physics, Sequences & Safety","sec":"rads","present":["MRI images hydrogen-proton behavior in a strong magnetic field using radiofrequency pulses; contrast derives from T1, T2, and proton-density weighting plus many specialized sequences (DWI, FLAIR, GRE/SWI, STIR, MRA)","Basic signal rules: on T1 fat is bright and fluid/water is dark; on T2 fluid/water is bright; FLAIR nulls CSF (bright lesions near ventricles); DWI shows restricted diffusion (acute stroke, abscess, hypercellular tumor)","No ionizing radiation, but powerful magnetic-field SAFETY hazards demand rigorous screening"],"dx":["Choose sequences by question: DWI for acute infarct/abscess, FLAIR for periventricular disease, GRE/SWI for blood/calcium (blooming), STIR/fat-sat for marrow/edema, MRA for vessels; gadolinium for enhancement","Recognize characteristic signal (T1 bright: fat, subacute blood, melanin, proteinaceous/contrast; T2 bright: water/edema/most tumors)","Screen every patient for implants/foreign bodies before entry"],"tx":["SAFETY: rigorously screen for MRI-incompatible devices/implants and metallic foreign bodies (e.g., intraocular metal); the projectile/missile effect is lethal - the magnet is ALWAYS ON; control ferromagnetic objects in Zone IV","GADOLINIUM: avoid or use caution in severe renal impairment (risk of NEPHROGENIC SYSTEMIC FIBROSIS, mainly with older linear agents); note gadolinium retention considerations","Manage acoustic noise (hearing protection), and quench/cryogen emergencies; use conditional-device parameters when scanning approved implants"],"pearls":["T1: fat bright, water dark; T2: water/edema bright; FLAIR nulls CSF; DWI restricts in acute stroke, abscess, and hypercellular tumor","GRE/SWI 'blooms' with blood and calcium; STIR/fat-sat highlights marrow edema","The MRI magnet is always on - the projectile effect is deadly; screen exhaustively for implants and intraocular metal","Gadolinium can cause nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in severe renal failure (older linear agents) - use caution/avoid"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: MRI Physics / Safety","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551523/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: MRI Safety","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ultrasound-physics","name":"Ultrasound Physics, Doppler & Artifacts","sec":"rads","present":["Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound and echo timing/amplitude to form real-time images with NO ionizing radiation; higher-frequency transducers give better resolution but less penetration (and vice versa)","Tissue appearance: fluid is ANECHOIC (black) with posterior acoustic ENHANCEMENT; bone/stones/gas are ECHOGENIC with posterior SHADOWING","DOPPLER assesses flow (color/spectral) - direction and velocity - for vascular and organ perfusion questions"],"dx":["Select transducer frequency by depth (high-frequency linear for superficial; low-frequency curvilinear for deep abdominal); optimize gain/depth/focus","Recognize key artifacts and use them diagnostically: posterior ACOUSTIC SHADOWING (stones/calcification), posterior ENHANCEMENT (cysts/fluid), REVERBERATION/comet-tail, RING-DOWN, and MIRROR-IMAGE artifacts; twinkle artifact on Doppler suggests calculi","Apply Doppler correctly (angle <60 degrees for velocity accuracy); assess flow presence/direction (e.g., testicular/ovarian torsion, DVT, portal flow)"],"tx":["Use ultrasound as a first-line, radiation-free tool (pregnancy, pediatrics, gallbladder, thyroid, DVT, procedural guidance, point-of-care/FAST)","Follow ALARA for acoustic output (thermal/mechanical indices), especially in obstetric/fetal imaging","Leverage its real-time nature for image-guided procedures (biopsy, drainage, vascular access)"],"pearls":["Anechoic + posterior acoustic enhancement = fluid/cyst; echogenic + posterior shadowing = stone/calcification/gas","Higher frequency = better resolution but less penetration - pick the transducer for the depth","Keep the Doppler angle <60 degrees for accurate velocities; 'twinkle' artifact suggests calculi","Ultrasound is first-line where avoiding radiation matters (pregnancy, pediatrics) and for real-time procedural guidance"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ultrasound Physics","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570593/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Ultrasound","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-contrast-media","name":"Contrast Media - Iodinated & Gadolinium Reactions","sec":"rads","present":["IODINATED contrast (for CT/angiography/fluoroscopy) and GADOLINIUM-based contrast (for MRI) improve lesion/vessel conspicuity but carry risks of adverse reactions and organ effects","Reactions are ALLERGIC-LIKE (anaphylactoid - urticaria to bronchospasm/hypotension/anaphylaxis, NOT true IgE-mediated) or PHYSIOLOGIC (warmth, nausea, vasovagal)","Prior contrast reaction is the strongest risk factor for a future reaction; 'shellfish/iodine allergy' is NOT a specific contraindication (a common myth)"],"dx":["Risk-stratify: prior moderate/severe contrast reaction, asthma, and atopy raise risk; assess renal function for iodinated contrast where relevant","Recognize and grade reactions (mild/moderate/severe) and distinguish allergic-like from physiologic; be ready to treat anaphylaxis","Understand contrast-associated acute kidney injury is less common than historically feared; gadolinium and nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk relates to severe renal impairment and agent type"],"tx":["PREMEDICATE at-risk patients (prior allergic-like reaction) with corticosteroids +/- antihistamine before elective contrast; use a different agent when possible","Treat reactions: EPINEPHRINE is first-line for anaphylaxis/severe bronchospasm/laryngeal edema/hypotension; oxygen, IV fluids, bronchodilators, antihistamines as appropriate - know weight-based/adult epinephrine dosing and routes","Mitigate renal risk with hydration in selected patients; avoid high-risk gadolinium agents in severe renal impairment"],"pearls":["Contrast reactions are anaphylactoid (allergic-LIKE), not true IgE allergy - and 'shellfish allergy' does NOT contraindicate iodinated contrast","The strongest predictor of a reaction is a PRIOR reaction to the same class - premedicate at-risk patients","Epinephrine is first-line for severe/anaphylactic contrast reactions","Gadolinium's nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk is tied to severe renal failure and older linear agents - screen renal function"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Contrast-Induced Reactions","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482170/"},{"t":"ACR: Contrast Manual","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-stroke-imaging","name":"Acute Ischemic Stroke Imaging & Thrombectomy Selection","sec":"rads","present":["Acute stroke imaging must rapidly answer: is there HEMORRHAGE, is there a LARGE-VESSEL OCCLUSION, how much brain is already infarcted (core) vs salvageable (penumbra), and is there a treatable target","NON-CONTRAST CT is first to EXCLUDE HEMORRHAGE before thrombolysis; early ischemic signs include the hyperdense MCA sign, loss of gray-white differentiation, insular ribbon sign, and sulcal effacement (ASPECTS scoring)","CT ANGIOGRAPHY detects large-vessel occlusion; CT/MR PERFUSION and DWI estimate core vs penumbra for extended-window thrombectomy"],"dx":["Non-contrast CT first (rule out hemorrhage; assess early ischemic change with ASPECTS); CTA head/neck to find large-vessel occlusion; perfusion or DWI-based mismatch for late-window selection","MRI DWI is most sensitive for early infarct (restricted diffusion); FLAIR-DWI mismatch estimates stroke age for unknown-onset stroke","Identify large-vessel occlusion candidates and quantify core/penumbra (DEFUSE 3 / DAWN criteria used perfusion or clinical-core mismatch)"],"tx":["IV THROMBOLYSIS eligibility hinges on excluding hemorrhage and time window; ENDOVASCULAR THROMBECTOMY for anterior large-vessel occlusion - standard within 6 hours, and up to 24 hours in selected patients by perfusion/clinical mismatch (DAWN, DEFUSE 3)","Communicate critical findings immediately to the stroke team; a small established core with large penumbra favors intervention","Post-treatment imaging monitors for hemorrhagic transformation"],"pearls":["Non-contrast CT's first job is to EXCLUDE hemorrhage before thrombolysis; ASPECTS quantifies early ischemic change","Hyperdense MCA sign, insular ribbon loss, and gray-white blurring are early infarct signs","DWI is the most sensitive sequence for acute infarct (restricted diffusion)","Late-window thrombectomy (up to 24 h) is selected by core/penumbra mismatch (DAWN, DEFUSE 3)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Stroke Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553153/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Stroke Imaging","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-intracranial-hemorrhage-trauma","name":"Intracranial Hemorrhage & Head Trauma","sec":"rads","present":["Non-contrast head CT is the first-line study for acute head trauma and suspected intracranial hemorrhage - acute blood is HYPERDENSE","Extra-axial collections by shape/anatomy: EPIDURAL hematoma is biconvex/LENTIFORM, does NOT cross sutures (arterial, classically middle meningeal artery, with a lucid interval); SUBDURAL hematoma is CRESCENTIC, crosses sutures but not midline (bridging veins)","SUBARACHNOID hemorrhage fills sulci/cisterns; intraparenchymal hemorrhage and diffuse axonal injury (gray-white junction microhemorrhages, best on SWI) round out the trauma spectrum"],"dx":["Distinguish epidural (lentiform, respects sutures, crosses midline/dura) from subdural (crescentic, crosses sutures, respects midline); look for mass effect, midline shift, and herniation","Non-traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage warrants CTA to find an aneurysm; hemorrhage age changes density (hyperacute/acute hyperdense -> chronic hypodense)","Apply decision rules for CT in minor head injury; use SWI/GRE for microhemorrhage and diffuse axonal injury"],"tx":["Communicate emergent findings (herniation, large/expanding hematoma, significant midline shift) immediately to neurosurgery - these are time-critical","CTA for suspected aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage; follow-up imaging to assess for expansion","Recognize herniation patterns (subfalcine, uncal, tonsillar) that mandate urgent intervention"],"pearls":["Epidural = biconvex/lentiform, does NOT cross sutures (middle meningeal artery, lucid interval); subdural = crescentic, crosses sutures but not midline (bridging veins)","Acute blood is hyperdense on CT; chronic collections become hypodense over weeks","Non-traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage needs CTA to find the aneurysm","SWI/GRE best detect microhemorrhages of diffuse axonal injury; herniation and midline shift are emergent, communicate at once"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intracranial Hemorrhage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560818/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Head CT","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-brain-tumors","name":"Brain Tumors & Ring-Enhancing Lesions","sec":"rads","present":["Brain masses are characterized by location (INTRA- vs EXTRA-axial), enhancement, diffusion, and demographics; extra-axial masses (meningioma - dural-based with a tail) displace rather than infiltrate brain","RING-ENHANCING lesions have a classic differential (mnemonic MAGIC DR: Metastasis, Abscess, Glioblastoma, Infarct/Inflammatory, Contusion, Demyelination, Radiation necrosis)","Advanced imaging (DWI, perfusion, spectroscopy) helps separate tumor from abscess and recurrence from treatment effect"],"dx":["Localize intra- vs extra-axial; assess enhancement, edema, mass effect, and multiplicity (multiple lesions at gray-white junction suggest metastases)","ABSCESS shows central RESTRICTED DIFFUSION (bright DWI, low ADC) - a key discriminator from necrotic tumor (which usually does not restrict centrally)","Use perfusion (elevated in high-grade tumor/recurrence, low in radiation necrosis) and spectroscopy to refine the differential"],"tx":["Communicate new mass with significant edema/herniation urgently; recommend appropriate next steps (contrast MRI, biopsy/resection for tissue, staging for suspected metastasis)","For suspected abscess, DWI restriction supports the diagnosis and prompts source workup/antibiotics and possible drainage","Distinguish tumor recurrence from radiation necrosis with perfusion/advanced imaging to guide management"],"pearls":["Ring-enhancing differential (MAGIC DR): Metastasis, Abscess, Glioblastoma, Infarct/Inflammation, Contusion, Demyelination, Radiation necrosis","Abscess RESTRICTS diffusion centrally (bright DWI/low ADC) - a key sign separating it from necrotic tumor","Extra-axial dural-based mass with a dural tail = meningioma; multiple gray-white junction lesions = metastases","Perfusion helps separate recurrent tumor (high) from radiation necrosis (low)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Brain Neoplasm Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470272/"},{"t":"NCI: Adult CNS Tumors (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/brain/hp/adult-brain-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"rads-spine-imaging","name":"Spine Imaging - Degenerative, Cord Compression & Cauda Equina","sec":"rads","present":["Spine imaging spans degenerative disease (disc, facet, stenosis), trauma, infection, and neoplasm; MRI is the modality of choice for the cord, discs, marrow, and soft tissues","RED-FLAG presentations demand urgent MRI: suspected cord compression, CAUDA EQUINA SYNDROME (saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction, bilateral leg symptoms), spinal infection, or malignancy","CT excels for bony detail/fractures; radiographs for alignment and screening"],"dx":["MRI for cord/nerve compression, disc herniation, spinal stenosis, infection (discitis-osteomyelitis: disc + endplate signal change, epidural collection), and metastasis/marrow replacement","Recognize cauda equina syndrome as a surgical emergency needing immediate MRI; distinguish epidural abscess/malignant cord compression (see radonc entries) from degenerative stenosis","Use CT for fracture characterization and surgical planning; assess stability"],"tx":["Communicate cord compression, cauda equina, epidural abscess, or unstable fracture emergently - these are time-critical surgical/oncologic issues","Correlate imaging with symptoms (degenerative findings are common and often incidental) to avoid over-attribution","Guide management: urgent decompression for cauda equina/cord compression; source control for infection"],"pearls":["Cauda equina syndrome (saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction) is a surgical emergency - get urgent MRI","MRI is the modality of choice for cord, discs, marrow, and infection; CT is best for bony fracture detail","Discitis-osteomyelitis: disc-space + adjacent endplate signal change with possible epidural abscess","Degenerative findings are common and frequently incidental - correlate with symptoms before attributing"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Spinal Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551598/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Spine MRI","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-head-neck-temporal","name":"Head, Neck & Temporal Bone Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["Head and neck imaging is anatomy-intensive, organized by SPACES (parapharyngeal, carotid, masticator, parotid, retropharyngeal, etc.); CT and MRI are complementary, with CT best for bone/calcification and MRI for soft tissue/perineural spread","Common questions: neck mass/lymphadenopathy characterization, infection (peritonsillar/retropharyngeal abscess), and staging of head and neck cancers","TEMPORAL BONE CT evaluates hearing loss, cholesteatoma, and trauma with thin-section bone detail"],"dx":["Localize a lesion to a space and characterize (cystic vs solid, enhancement, nodal features - central necrosis/extranodal extension suggests malignancy)","Recognize emergencies: deep-neck-space abscess with airway compromise, and complications like Lemierre syndrome (internal jugular thrombophlebitis)","Temporal bone: identify cholesteatoma (erosion of scutum/ossicles), otosclerosis, and fracture patterns (longitudinal vs transverse)"],"tx":["Communicate airway-threatening deep-neck infections urgently; recommend contrast CT for suspected abscess and MRI for perineural tumor spread/skull-base involvement","Guide oncologic staging and biopsy planning; assess nodal levels systematically","Use MRI to trace perineural spread along named nerves (e.g., in adenoid cystic carcinoma)"],"pearls":["Head and neck imaging is organized by anatomic SPACES - localize first, then characterize","Nodal central necrosis and extranodal extension suggest malignant/metastatic nodes","Cholesteatoma erodes the scutum and ossicles on temporal bone CT","MRI best demonstrates perineural tumor spread along named nerves and skull-base invasion"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Head and Neck Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555987/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Head and Neck","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-chest-radiograph","name":"Chest Radiograph - Lines, Tubes & Acute Findings","sec":"rads","present":["The chest radiograph is the most common imaging study; a systematic approach (airway, breathing/lungs, cardiac/mediastinum, diaphragm, bones/soft tissue, and lines/tubes) prevents missed findings","LINE AND TUBE positions are a core inpatient task: endotracheal tube tip ~3-5 cm above the carina, central venous catheter tip at the cavoatrial junction, nasogastric tube below the diaphragm/in the stomach, chest tube position","Acute findings include consolidation, pleural effusion, PNEUMOTHORAX, pulmonary edema, and pneumomediastinum"],"dx":["Verify device positions and screen for complications (e.g., PNEUMOTHORAX after line placement - a deep sulcus sign on supine films); a malpositioned endotracheal tube in the right mainstem causes contralateral collapse","Recognize consolidation (air bronchograms), effusion (meniscus/blunted costophrenic angle, layering on decubitus), and edema (cephalization, Kerley B lines, perihilar 'bat-wing')","Detect TENSION pneumothorax (mediastinal shift away, depressed hemidiaphragm) - a clinical emergency; use the silhouette sign to localize disease"],"tx":["Communicate critical findings immediately - misplaced airway/tubes, large/tension pneumothorax, and free intraperitoneal air under the diaphragm require urgent action","Recommend corrective steps (reposition tube, decompress pneumothorax) and confirmatory imaging as needed","Correlate with clinical status; obtain upright/decubitus or CT when the radiograph is equivocal"],"pearls":["Endotracheal tube tip should sit ~3-5 cm above the carina; central line tip at the cavoatrial junction","Deep sulcus sign = pneumothorax on a supine film; tension pneumothorax shifts the mediastinum AWAY and is an emergency","The silhouette sign localizes disease (loss of a border indicates an adjacent-lobe process)","Free air under the diaphragm on an upright film means perforation - communicate urgently"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chest Radiograph Interpretation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564428/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Chest X-ray","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pulmonary-nodule","name":"Pulmonary Nodule & Lung Cancer Screening","sec":"rads","present":["Pulmonary nodules are common incidental and screen-detected findings; management balances malignancy risk (size, growth, morphology, patient risk factors) against the harms of overinvestigation","LOW-DOSE CT LUNG CANCER SCREENING is recommended for high-risk adults (per USPSTF: ages ~50-80 with a significant smoking history who currently smoke or quit within 15 years)","Structured frameworks standardize management: the FLEISCHNER SOCIETY criteria (incidental nodules) and LUNG-RADS (screening)"],"dx":["Characterize nodules: size, SOLID vs SUBSOLID (ground-glass/part-solid), margins (spiculated = suspicious), calcification pattern (benign: central, laminated, popcorn, diffuse), and growth over time","Apply FLEISCHNER criteria for incidental nodules (by size, solid/subsolid, risk) and LUNG-RADS for screening to set follow-up vs workup","Persistent part-solid nodules and spiculated margins raise malignancy concern; fat within a nodule suggests hamartoma"],"tx":["Recommend the appropriate interval CT follow-up or workup (PET/biopsy) per Fleischner/Lung-RADS category; some small low-risk nodules need no follow-up","For screen-detected suspicious nodules (higher Lung-RADS), advance to diagnostic evaluation (PET/CT, tissue sampling)","Ensure structured reporting and tracking so nodules are not lost to follow-up"],"pearls":["Fleischner criteria = incidental nodules; Lung-RADS = screening CT - know which framework applies","Benign calcification patterns: central, laminated, 'popcorn' (hamartoma), diffuse; spiculation suggests malignancy","USPSTF lung screening: high-risk adults ~50-80 with heavy smoking history (current or quit within 15 years)","Persistent subsolid/part-solid nodules and interval growth raise malignancy risk - track carefully"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Solitary Pulmonary Nodule","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556143/"},{"t":"ACR: Lung-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pulmonary-embolism-aorta","name":"Pulmonary Embolism (CTPA) & Thoracic Aortic Emergencies","sec":"rads","present":["CT PULMONARY ANGIOGRAPHY (CTPA) is the first-line test for suspected pulmonary embolism (PE) - a filling defect in a contrast-opacified pulmonary artery; right-heart strain (RV enlargement, septal bowing) flags severity","Clinical decision tools (Wells, PERC) and D-dimer determine who needs CTPA, limiting overuse; V/Q scanning is an alternative when CTPA is contraindicated","THORACIC AORTIC emergencies (acute aortic syndrome) - DISSECTION, intramural hematoma, penetrating ulcer, and aneurysm rupture - are diagnosed with CT angiography"],"dx":["CTPA: identify intraluminal filling defects; assess clot burden and RV:LV ratio (right-heart strain predicts adverse outcome); optimize contrast timing to avoid mixing artifacts","Aortic DISSECTION: CT angiography shows an intimal flap/two lumens - classify Stanford A (ascending, surgical emergency) vs B (descending, often medical)","Recognize intramural hematoma (crescentic high-attenuation wall) and contained/active rupture; use ECG-gating to reduce motion at the aortic root"],"tx":["Communicate PE with right-heart strain and any aortic emergency IMMEDIATELY - these are life-threatening","Stanford A dissection is a surgical emergency (communicate to cardiothoracic surgery); type B is often managed medically (blood-pressure control) with intervention for complications","Guide anticoagulation/thrombolysis decisions for PE by severity (massive/submassive)"],"pearls":["CTPA is first-line for PE; an elevated RV:LV ratio signals right-heart strain and worse prognosis","Use Wells/PERC and D-dimer to select patients and avoid unnecessary CTPA","Stanford A dissection (involves ascending aorta) is a surgical emergency; type B (descending only) is usually medical","Acute aortic syndrome also includes intramural hematoma (crescentic wall) and penetrating ulcer - CT angiography is the test"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Embolism Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560551/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: CT Angiography","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-interstitial-lung-disease","name":"Interstitial & Diffuse Lung Disease (HRCT Patterns)","sec":"rads","present":["HIGH-RESOLUTION CT (HRCT) characterizes diffuse/interstitial lung disease by PATTERN and DISTRIBUTION, narrowing a broad differential and guiding multidisciplinary diagnosis","Key patterns: USUAL INTERSTITIAL PNEUMONIA (UIP - subpleural/basal HONEYCOMBING and traction bronchiectasis, suggesting idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis), nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (ground-glass, basal, subpleural sparing), hypersensitivity pneumonitis (mosaic/air-trapping, centrilobular nodules), and sarcoidosis (perilymphatic nodules, hilar adenopathy)","Distribution (upper vs lower, central vs peripheral) is as informative as the pattern itself"],"dx":["Assess pattern (reticulation, ground-glass, nodules, cysts, honeycombing) and distribution; a definite UIP pattern (subpleural basal honeycombing) can support idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis without biopsy","Recognize signature patterns: perilymphatic nodules + symmetric hilar adenopathy (sarcoidosis), mosaic attenuation/air-trapping (hypersensitivity pneumonitis/small-airways disease), crazy-paving (alveolar filling)","Integrate with clinical/serologic data in a multidisciplinary discussion"],"tx":["Provide a pattern-based differential and recommend correlation (occupational/drug/exposure history, serologies) or biopsy when the pattern is indeterminate","Recommend follow-up HRCT to assess progression/treatment response; flag acute exacerbations","Support antifibrotic vs immunosuppressive treatment decisions through accurate pattern characterization"],"pearls":["UIP pattern = subpleural, basal-predominant honeycombing + traction bronchiectasis (suggests idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis)","Perilymphatic nodules + symmetric hilar adenopathy = sarcoidosis; mosaic attenuation/air-trapping suggests hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Distribution (upper vs lower, central vs peripheral) is as diagnostic as the pattern","A confident HRCT UIP pattern can avoid surgical lung biopsy in the right clinical setting"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Interstitial Lung Disease Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541084/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Chest CT","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-cardiac-ct","name":"Coronary CTA, Calcium Scoring & CAD-RADS","sec":"rads","present":["CARDIAC CT is a leading noninvasive tool for coronary artery disease: the CORONARY ARTERY CALCIUM (CAC) score risk-stratifies asymptomatic patients, and CORONARY CT ANGIOGRAPHY (CCTA) evaluates stenosis and plaque in symptomatic patients","CCTA has high NEGATIVE PREDICTIVE VALUE - excellent for RULING OUT significant coronary disease in low-to-intermediate-risk chest pain","CAD-RADS standardizes reporting of stenosis severity and recommended management"],"dx":["CAC scoring (non-contrast, Agatston units): higher scores predict cardiovascular risk (0 = very low risk; escalating scores guide statin/preventive therapy)","CCTA: assess stenosis severity and plaque (calcified/non-calcified/high-risk features - positive remodeling, low attenuation, spotty calcification, napkin-ring sign); requires heart-rate control and ECG gating to limit motion","Report with CAD-RADS categories tied to management recommendations"],"tx":["Use CCTA's high negative predictive value to safely exclude significant coronary disease and avoid invasive angiography in low-intermediate-risk chest pain","Translate CAD-RADS category into management (preventive therapy, functional testing, or invasive angiography); CT-derived FFR can assess lesion-specific ischemia","Guide preventive therapy from CAC score (e.g., statin decisions in intermediate-risk patients)"],"pearls":["Coronary artery calcium score risk-stratifies asymptomatic patients (0 = very low risk); CCTA evaluates symptomatic chest pain","CCTA's strength is a very high negative predictive value - it excludes significant coronary disease well","CAD-RADS standardizes stenosis reporting and links to management; high-risk plaque features include the napkin-ring sign and positive remodeling","Heart-rate control and ECG gating are essential to reduce coronary motion artifact"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Coronary CT Angiography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544319/"},{"t":"ACR: CAD-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-cardiac-mri","name":"Cardiac MRI & Great-Vessel Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["CARDIAC MRI is the reference standard for ventricular volumes/function and for MYOCARDIAL TISSUE CHARACTERIZATION, using cine imaging, T1/T2 mapping, and LATE GADOLINIUM ENHANCEMENT (LGE)","LGE PATTERN distinguishes causes of cardiomyopathy: SUBENDOCARDIAL/transmural in an arterial territory = infarct/ischemic; MID-WALL = nonischemic (e.g., dilated cardiomyopathy); EPICARDIAL/patchy = myocarditis; other patterns for infiltrative disease","MRI/CT also evaluate the great vessels, congenital heart disease, cardiac masses, and pericardial disease"],"dx":["Quantify function/volumes with cine; use LGE to identify scar/fibrosis and its pattern (ischemic vs nonischemic distribution)","Characterize tissue: T2 for edema (acute myocarditis/infarct), T2* for iron overload, and mapping for infiltration (amyloid - diffuse LGE with abnormal nulling; sarcoid - patchy)","Assess the aorta/great vessels and congenital anatomy with MR/CT angiography"],"tx":["Provide etiologic diagnosis of cardiomyopathy from LGE pattern to guide management (e.g., ischemic workup vs myocarditis vs infiltrative disease)","Recommend viability assessment (transmurality of LGE predicts recovery after revascularization) and risk stratification (scar burden and arrhythmia risk)","Guide great-vessel and congenital management with accurate anatomic/functional data"],"pearls":["LGE localizes and characterizes scar: subendocardial/transmural in a coronary territory = ischemic; mid-wall = nonischemic; epicardial = myocarditis","Cardiac MRI is the reference standard for ventricular volumes and function","T2 shows myocardial edema (acute injury); T2* quantifies iron overload; mapping detects amyloid/infiltration","LGE transmurality predicts myocardial viability/recovery after revascularization"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac MRI","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556123/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Cardiac MRI","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-acute-abdomen","name":"Acute Abdomen - Appendicitis, Obstruction & Perforation","sec":"rads","present":["CT is the workhorse for the acute abdomen; ultrasound is first-line in children/pregnancy and for gallbladder/gynecologic causes","APPENDICITIS: dilated (>6 mm), non-compressible, fluid-filled appendix with wall thickening, periappendiceal fat stranding, and possible appendicolith; complications include abscess/perforation","BOWEL OBSTRUCTION (dilated loops with transition point; small-bowel feces sign) and PERFORATION (free intraperitoneal air) are core emergencies"],"dx":["Appendicitis: CT/ultrasound criteria as above; obstruction: identify the transition point and cause (adhesions, hernia, mass), and look for CLOSED-LOOP obstruction and ISCHEMIA (bowel wall non-enhancement, pneumatosis, portal venous gas - ominous signs)","Perforation: free air (best seen on upright/decubitus radiograph or CT lung windows); localize the source (e.g., extraluminal air/contrast)","Also recognize diverticulitis, cholecystitis, pancreatitis, and mesenteric ischemia as acute-abdomen causes"],"tx":["Communicate emergent findings (free air, closed-loop obstruction, bowel ischemia, abscess) immediately to surgery","Recommend the appropriate modality (ultrasound first in kids/pregnancy) and next steps (drainage for abscess, surgery for perforation/ischemia)","Guide conservative vs operative management (e.g., uncomplicated vs complicated diverticulitis)"],"pearls":["Appendicitis on imaging: appendix >6 mm, non-compressible, wall thickening, periappendiceal fat stranding +/- appendicolith","Closed-loop obstruction and signs of ischemia (non-enhancing wall, pneumatosis, portal venous gas) are surgical emergencies","Free intraperitoneal air = perforation - best seen on upright/decubitus film or CT lung windows","Use ultrasound first for appendicitis in children and pregnancy to avoid radiation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Abdomen Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459328/"},{"t":"ACR: Appropriateness - Acute Abdomen","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-liver-lirads","name":"Liver Imaging & LI-RADS (HCC)","sec":"rads","present":["Liver lesion characterization uses multiphase contrast CT/MRI; in patients AT RISK for HEPATOCELLULAR CARCINOMA (cirrhosis, chronic hepatitis B), the LI-RADS system standardizes diagnosis and can confirm HCC NONINVASIVELY (without biopsy)","The HCC hallmark is ARTERIAL-PHASE HYPERENHANCEMENT with WASHOUT on portal-venous/delayed phases and an enhancing capsule","Common benign lesions have signatures: hemangioma (peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement, progressive fill-in), FNH (central scar), cyst (fluid, no enhancement)"],"dx":["Apply LI-RADS major features in at-risk patients: size, arterial hyperenhancement, washout, capsule, and threshold growth -> LR categories (LR-5 = definite HCC)","Characterize benign lesions by their enhancement pattern (hemangioma fill-in, FNH central scar); use hepatobiliary contrast agents to problem-solve","Distinguish HCC from intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (progressive/delayed enhancement, capsular retraction) and metastases"],"tx":["An LR-5 lesion establishes HCC WITHOUT biopsy, enabling treatment (transplant listing, resection, ablation, TACE/TARE - see IR entries) per stage/liver function","Recommend surveillance ultrasound (+/- AFP) in cirrhosis and diagnostic multiphase CT/MRI for detected lesions","Communicate findings to the multidisciplinary tumor board; benign lesions (hemangioma, FNH, cysts) typically need no intervention"],"pearls":["HCC hallmark: arterial-phase hyperenhancement + washout + enhancing capsule; LI-RADS LR-5 diagnoses HCC noninvasively","Hemangioma: peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement with progressive centripetal fill-in; FNH has a central scar","LI-RADS applies only to at-risk patients (cirrhosis, chronic hepatitis B)","Cholangiocarcinoma shows progressive delayed enhancement and capsular retraction - unlike HCC washout"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hepatocellular Carcinoma Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559177/"},{"t":"ACR: LI-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pancreaticobiliary","name":"Pancreaticobiliary Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["Pancreaticobiliary imaging spans pancreatitis, biliary obstruction, and pancreatic/biliary neoplasms; ultrasound screens the biliary tree, CT stages disease, and MRCP (MR cholangiopancreatography) noninvasively maps ducts","ACUTE PANCREATITIS is a clinical/biochemical diagnosis - CT is for complications and severity (necrosis, collections) and is often best a few days in; the revised Atlanta classification defines collection types","Biliary obstruction: identify the LEVEL and CAUSE (stone, stricture, tumor) - 'double-duct sign' (dilated common bile and pancreatic ducts) suggests a pancreatic head/periampullary mass"],"dx":["Acute pancreatitis: assess for necrosis (non-enhancing pancreas), peripancreatic collections (acute fluid collection, pseudocyst, walled-off necrosis), and vascular complications (pseudoaneurysm, splenic vein thrombosis)","Biliary obstruction: MRCP maps ductal anatomy; distinguish choledocholithiasis from stricture/mass; the double-duct sign raises concern for periampullary malignancy","Characterize pancreatic cystic lesions (IPMN communicates with the duct; mucinous cystic neoplasm; serous cystadenoma) and solid masses"],"tx":["Communicate complications (necrosis, hemorrhage/pseudoaneurysm, obstruction with cholangitis) promptly; recommend drainage for infected collections and IR/endoscopic biliary decompression","Guide workup of the double-duct sign toward pancreatic malignancy; recommend endoscopic ultrasound/biopsy as needed","Recommend surveillance vs resection for cystic lesions per worrisome features (mural nodule, main-duct dilation)"],"pearls":["Acute pancreatitis is a clinical diagnosis - CT is for severity/complications and is often most useful a few days in","Double-duct sign (dilated common bile + pancreatic ducts) suggests a pancreatic head/periampullary tumor","MRCP noninvasively maps the biliary/pancreatic ducts (stones, strictures, anatomy)","IPMN communicates with the pancreatic duct; main-duct involvement and mural nodules are worrisome for malignancy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pancreatitis Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538337/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: MRCP","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-bowel-fluoroscopy","name":"Bowel Imaging - IBD, Luminal Disease & Fluoroscopy","sec":"rads","present":["Luminal GI imaging combines FLUOROSCOPIC contrast studies (barium swallow/upper GI/small-bowel follow-through, contrast enema) with cross-sectional CT/MR ENTEROGRAPHY for inflammatory bowel disease and obstruction","CROHN DISEASE: transmural, SKIP lesions, terminal-ileum predilection, mural hyperenhancement, 'comb sign' (engorged vasa recta), strictures, fistulae; ULCERATIVE COLITIS: continuous, mucosal, from the rectum proximally","Fluoroscopy also evaluates swallowing (modified barium swallow), leaks/perforation (water-soluble contrast), and strictures"],"dx":["CT/MR enterography: assess active inflammation (mural enhancement/thickening, comb sign), complications (abscess, fistula, stricture), and disease distribution; MR avoids radiation for young IBD patients needing repeat imaging","Differentiate Crohn (skip lesions, transmural, fistulizing) from ulcerative colitis (continuous, mucosal, rectal); recognize toxic megacolon (a clinical/imaging emergency)","Use WATER-SOLUBLE contrast (not barium) when perforation/leak is suspected (barium peritonitis is harmful)"],"tx":["Communicate complications (abscess -> drainage, high-grade obstruction, perforation, toxic megacolon) urgently","Recommend enterography for IBD staging/surveillance and modified barium swallow for aspiration risk","Guide medical vs surgical management and IR drainage of collections"],"pearls":["Crohn = transmural, skip lesions, terminal ileum, fistulae, comb sign; ulcerative colitis = continuous mucosal disease from the rectum","Use WATER-SOLUBLE contrast (not barium) if perforation/leak is suspected - barium peritonitis is dangerous","MR enterography avoids radiation for young IBD patients requiring repeated imaging","Toxic megacolon (dilated colon, wall abnormality, systemic toxicity) is an emergency"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Inflammatory Bowel Disease Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551692/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: GI Fluoroscopy","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-renal-adrenal","name":"Renal & Adrenal Masses (Bosniak, Adrenal Washout)","sec":"rads","present":["Renal and adrenal masses are frequently incidental; standardized frameworks estimate malignancy risk and guide follow-up vs intervention","RENAL cystic lesions are classified by the BOSNIAK system (I-IV) on contrast CT/MRI, based on septations, wall thickening, calcification, and ENHANCEMENT (enhancing soft tissue is the key malignancy indicator)","ADRENAL 'incidentalomas' are characterized by CT attenuation and CONTRAST WASHOUT to separate benign adenomas from other lesions"],"dx":["BOSNIAK: I (simple cyst, benign) and II (few thin septa) are benign; IIF needs follow-up; III (thick/irregular enhancing walls/septa) and IV (enhancing soft-tissue components) carry rising malignancy risk and usually warrant intervention","ADRENAL adenoma: low unenhanced attenuation (<=10 HU = lipid-rich adenoma) or, if indeterminate, ABSOLUTE/relative contrast WASHOUT (high washout favors adenoma); pheochromocytoma and metastases show different behavior","Enhancing solid renal masses raise concern for renal cell carcinoma; fat within a renal mass suggests angiomyolipoma (benign)"],"tx":["Recommend management per category: benign Bosniak I/II need no follow-up, IIF surveillance, III/IV urology referral/intervention; solid enhancing renal masses -> urologic evaluation (surgery/ablation)","Adrenal: a lipid-rich adenoma (<=10 HU) needs no further workup; indeterminate lesions get washout CT or MRI chemical-shift; consider biochemical evaluation (and exclude pheochromocytoma before any biopsy)","Communicate suspicious features and recommend the appropriate next study/specialist"],"pearls":["Bosniak category rises with septations/wall thickening/enhancement; ENHANCING soft tissue (III/IV) is the key malignancy indicator","Adrenal adenoma: <=10 HU on unenhanced CT = lipid-rich adenoma; otherwise use contrast washout","Macroscopic FAT in a renal mass = angiomyolipoma (benign); an enhancing solid renal mass suggests renal cell carcinoma","Exclude pheochromocytoma biochemically before biopsying or intervening on an adrenal mass"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Mass Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558975/"},{"t":"ACR: Incidental Findings - Renal/Adrenal","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-prostate-mri","name":"Prostate MRI (PI-RADS) & GU Oncologic Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["MULTIPARAMETRIC PROSTATE MRI (T2, DIFFUSION/DWI-ADC, and dynamic contrast enhancement) detects and localizes clinically significant prostate cancer and guides TARGETED BIOPSY","PI-RADS (v2.1) scores lesions 1-5 by likelihood of clinically significant cancer; DIFFUSION is the dominant sequence in the PERIPHERAL zone and T2 in the TRANSITION zone","MRI also stages GU malignancies (extracapsular extension, seminal-vesicle invasion, nodes) and characterizes bladder/renal tumors"],"dx":["Score with PI-RADS: peripheral-zone lesions are driven by DWI/ADC (restricted diffusion is suspicious), transition-zone lesions by T2 morphology (lenticular, non-circumscribed, 'erased charcoal')","PI-RADS 4-5 lesions warrant targeted (MRI-fusion) biopsy; assess local staging (extracapsular extension, seminal vesicle invasion) for treatment planning","Correlate with PSA/PSA density; recognize benign mimics (BPH nodules, prostatitis, post-biopsy hemorrhage)"],"tx":["Recommend MRI-targeted +/- systematic biopsy for PI-RADS 4-5; MRI can support active surveillance decisions and biopsy avoidance in low-suspicion cases","Provide local staging to guide surgery vs radiotherapy (see radonc prostate entries) and nodal assessment","Communicate suspicious extracapsular/seminal-vesicle findings that change management"],"pearls":["PI-RADS: diffusion (DWI/ADC) is dominant in the peripheral zone; T2 morphology dominates in the transition zone","PI-RADS 4-5 lesions warrant MRI-targeted biopsy; MRI-fusion biopsy improves detection of clinically significant cancer","Restricted diffusion (low ADC) in the peripheral zone is a hallmark of significant prostate cancer","Recent biopsy causes hemorrhage that mimics/obscures tumor - timing and T1 review matter"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate MRI","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576440/"},{"t":"ACR: PI-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ob-gyn-imaging","name":"Obstetric & Gynecologic Imaging (O-RADS, Ectopic)","sec":"rads","present":["Pelvic ULTRASOUND (transabdominal + transvaginal) is first-line for gynecologic and early-obstetric questions, using no ionizing radiation; MRI problem-solves adnexal/uterine masses and placental disorders","EARLY PREGNANCY: correlate beta-hCG with findings; an empty uterus at a discriminatory hCG level with an adnexal mass raises concern for ECTOPIC pregnancy - an emergency; know landmarks of a normal intrauterine pregnancy","ADNEXAL MASSES are risk-stratified by O-RADS (ultrasound and MRI) to separate benign from malignant lesions"],"dx":["Confirm intrauterine pregnancy (gestational sac with yolk sac/embryo) vs findings suggesting ectopic (adnexal ring/mass, free fluid, no intrauterine pregnancy at appropriate hCG); recognize pregnancy of unknown location","O-RADS categorizes adnexal lesions by size, solid components, septations, and vascularity; simple cysts are benign, solid/vascular/complex features raise risk","Evaluate uterine pathology (fibroids, adenomyosis, endometrial thickening) and, with MRI, placenta accreta spectrum and Mullerian anomalies"],"tx":["Communicate suspected ectopic pregnancy or ovarian TORSION (enlarged ovary, abnormal/absent Doppler flow, peripheralized follicles) IMMEDIATELY - both are emergencies","Recommend O-RADS-based management (follow-up vs gynecologic oncology referral) for adnexal masses; MRI for indeterminate lesions","Guide obstetric management (accreta planning, anomaly assessment) with appropriate MRI"],"pearls":["No intrauterine pregnancy at a discriminatory hCG + an adnexal mass/free fluid = concern for ectopic pregnancy (emergency)","Ovarian torsion: enlarged edematous ovary with peripheralized follicles and abnormal/absent Doppler flow - a surgical emergency","O-RADS risk-stratifies adnexal masses; simple cysts are benign, solid/vascular complexity raises malignancy risk","Transvaginal ultrasound is first-line for early pregnancy and adnexal evaluation - no ionizing radiation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ectopic Pregnancy Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539860/"},{"t":"ACR: O-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-mammography-birads","name":"Mammography & Breast Cancer Screening (BI-RADS)","sec":"rads","present":["Mammography is the backbone of breast cancer SCREENING (reduces mortality); digital breast tomosynthesis improves cancer detection and lowers recall in dense breasts","Suspicious features: a SPICULATED mass, and PLEOMORPHIC/fine-linear-branching or segmentally distributed MICROCALCIFICATIONS (can indicate DCIS); architectural distortion is also concerning","BI-RADS standardizes reporting and, crucially, links each category to a MANAGEMENT recommendation and an implied malignancy likelihood"],"dx":["Assess masses (shape, margins - spiculated/irregular are suspicious) and calcifications (benign: coarse, popcorn, rim/eggshell, vascular; suspicious: fine pleomorphic, fine-linear-branching)","Assign BI-RADS: 0 (incomplete - need more imaging), 1 (negative), 2 (benign), 3 (probably benign, <=2% - short-interval follow-up), 4 (suspicious - biopsy), 5 (highly suggestive, >=95% - biopsy), 6 (known cancer)","Use additional views/ultrasound/tomosynthesis and MRI for problem-solving and high-risk screening"],"tx":["Translate BI-RADS into action: category 0 -> additional imaging; 3 -> short-interval follow-up; 4/5 -> tissue biopsy; 2 -> routine screening","Recommend supplemental screening (MRI/ultrasound) for high-risk or dense-breast patients per guidelines","Ensure results communication and tracking so category 3/4/5 findings are followed to resolution"],"pearls":["BI-RADS ties each category to management: 3 = short-interval follow-up, 4/5 = biopsy, 0 = needs additional imaging","Suspicious findings: spiculated mass, fine pleomorphic or fine-linear-branching microcalcifications, architectural distortion","Benign calcifications: coarse/popcorn (fibroadenoma), rim/eggshell, vascular, large rod-like","Tomosynthesis improves detection and reduces recall, especially in dense breasts"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mammography","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559294/"},{"t":"ACR: BI-RADS","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-breast-mri-biopsy","name":"Breast MRI & Image-Guided Breast Biopsy","sec":"rads","present":["BREAST MRI is the most SENSITIVE test for invasive breast cancer and is used for HIGH-RISK SCREENING (e.g., BRCA carriers/strong family history), extent-of-disease evaluation, problem-solving, and monitoring neoadjuvant response","It relies on dynamic contrast enhancement - malignancy typically shows rapid enhancement with washout kinetics and suspicious morphology (irregular/rim-enhancing masses, suspicious non-mass enhancement)","IMAGE-GUIDED BIOPSY (stereotactic for calcifications, ultrasound for masses, MRI-guided for MRI-only lesions) provides tissue diagnosis"],"dx":["Interpret breast MRI by morphology and kinetics (washout curve is suspicious); high sensitivity comes with lower specificity - correlate to avoid unnecessary biopsy","Select biopsy modality by how the lesion is best seen: ULTRASOUND-guided for sonographic masses, STEREOTACTIC for mammographic microcalcifications, MRI-guided for MRI-only findings","Ensure radiologic-pathologic CONCORDANCE after biopsy; discordant benign results warrant re-biopsy/excision"],"tx":["Recommend MRI for high-risk screening, extent of disease in newly diagnosed cancer, and neoadjuvant response assessment","Perform the appropriate image-guided biopsy for suspicious findings; place a clip to mark the site for future localization/surgery","Communicate concordance status and next steps (surveillance, surgical excision) based on pathology"],"pearls":["Breast MRI is the most sensitive modality - used for high-risk screening (BRCA), disease extent, and neoadjuvant monitoring; specificity is lower, so correlate","Biopsy modality follows visibility: ultrasound (masses), stereotactic (calcifications), MRI-guided (MRI-only lesions)","Always confirm radiologic-pathologic concordance - a discordant benign result needs re-biopsy or excision","Place a marker clip after biopsy to localize the site for surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Breast MRI / Biopsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564396/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Breast MRI","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-msk-trauma","name":"MSK Trauma & Fracture Patterns","sec":"rads","present":["Radiographs are first-line for skeletal trauma (two orthogonal views, joint above and below); CT characterizes complex/intra-articular fractures and MRI detects occult/radiographically negative fractures and soft-tissue injury","Key named fractures/patterns carry management implications: scaphoid (risk of avascular necrosis/nonunion), Colles/Smith (distal radius), Jones (5th metatarsal), Salter-Harris (pediatric physis), and Lisfranc (tarsometatarsal)","Detect effusions/lipohemarthrosis (a fat-fluid level indicates an intra-articular fracture) as indirect signs"],"dx":["Describe fractures systematically: location, pattern (transverse/oblique/spiral/comminuted), displacement, angulation, intra-articular extension, and open vs closed","Recognize occult fractures (scaphoid, hip, radial head) needing MRI or follow-up when radiographs are negative but suspicion is high; a posterior fat-pad/'sail sign' at the elbow indicates effusion/occult fracture","Identify high-consequence patterns: Lisfranc injury (subtle malalignment), hip fracture in the elderly, and any physeal (Salter-Harris) involvement in children"],"tx":["Communicate fractures needing urgent care (open, displaced/angulated, intra-articular, neurovascular threat, or dislocations requiring reduction)","Recommend CT for surgical planning (intra-articular/complex) and MRI for occult fractures or ligament/tendon injury","Guide follow-up for high-nonunion/AVN fractures (e.g., scaphoid) and physeal injuries needing precise reduction"],"pearls":["A fat-fluid level (lipohemarthrosis) or an elbow posterior fat pad ('sail sign') indicates an intra-articular fracture, even if the fracture line is not seen","Scaphoid fractures risk avascular necrosis/nonunion - treat clinically and re-image/MRI if the first film is negative","Salter-Harris classifies physeal fractures in children (SALTR: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed/crush)","Lisfranc injuries are subtle - malalignment at the tarsometatarsal joint needs weight-bearing views/CT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Fracture Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518967/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: X-ray Bone","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-bone-tumors","name":"Bone Tumors - Aggressive vs Non-Aggressive Features","sec":"rads","present":["Radiographs remain the cornerstone for characterizing a bone lesion; the goal is to judge AGGRESSIVENESS (likely benign vs likely malignant) and generate a focused differential using lesion features and patient AGE","Aggressiveness is read from the MARGIN/ZONE OF TRANSITION (narrow/sclerotic = non-aggressive; wide/ill-defined = aggressive), PERIOSTEAL REACTION (solid = benign; lamellated/'onion-skin', spiculated/'sunburst', Codman triangle = aggressive), and cortical destruction/soft-tissue mass","Age is a powerful discriminator (e.g., metastasis/myeloma in older adults; primary sarcomas peak in youth)"],"dx":["Apply Lodwick grading of the transition zone and assess matrix (chondroid rings-and-arcs = cartilage tumor; cloud-like/ground-glass osteoid; ground-glass = fibrous dysplasia)","Use age: <30 consider primary tumors (osteosarcoma about the knee, Ewing sarcoma - permeative/onion-skin); >40 favor metastasis and myeloma; know classic benign lesions (non-ossifying fibroma, osteochondroma, enchondroma, giant cell tumor - epiphyseal, after physeal closure)","Recognize the 'do not touch' benign lesions to avoid unnecessary biopsy"],"tx":["Provide an aggressiveness assessment and differential; recommend MRI for local staging (marrow/soft-tissue extent) and referral to an orthopedic-oncology/sarcoma center BEFORE biopsy (biopsy tract must be planned by the treating surgeon)","Recommend chest CT and bone/PET staging for suspected malignancy; correlate with labs (myeloma workup) in older patients","Communicate aggressive features prompting urgent specialist referral"],"pearls":["Aggressiveness = wide zone of transition + aggressive periosteal reaction (sunburst, onion-skin, Codman triangle) + cortical breakthrough/soft-tissue mass","Patient AGE is one of the best discriminators - >40 think metastasis/myeloma; young patients get primary sarcomas","Chondroid matrix shows rings-and-arcs; fibrous dysplasia is ground-glass; know the 'do not touch' benign lesions","Refer suspected primary bone sarcoma to a sarcoma center before biopsy - a poorly placed tract compromises limb salvage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bone Tumor Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559250/"},{"t":"NCI: Bone Cancer","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/bone/hp"}]},{"id":"rads-arthritis-patterns","name":"Arthritis Patterns (RA, OA, Crystal, Seronegative)","sec":"rads","present":["Radiographic arthritis is diagnosed by PATTERN and DISTRIBUTION: which joints, symmetry, erosions vs productive bone changes, and characteristic signs","OSTEOARTHRITIS (degenerative): asymmetric NON-uniform joint-space loss, OSTEOPHYTES, subchondral sclerosis and cysts, no true erosions - targets DIP/PIP, first CMC, hips, knees, spine","RHEUMATOID ARTHRITIS (inflammatory): symmetric UNIFORM joint-space loss, marginal EROSIONS, periarticular osteopenia, soft-tissue swelling - targets MCP/PIP, wrists, and spares DIPs (opposite of OA)"],"dx":["Distinguish erosive/inflammatory (RA, psoriatic, gout) from degenerative (OA) and from CPPD/crystal disease; note symmetry and joint targets","GOUT: well-defined 'punched-out' erosions with OVERHANGING EDGES and preserved joint space, often first MTP, with soft-tissue tophi; CPPD: CHONDROCALCINOSIS (cartilage calcification) and OA-like changes in atypical joints (wrist/MCP)","SERONEGATIVE spondyloarthropathies: psoriatic ('pencil-in-cup', DIP disease, new bone), reactive, and ankylosing spondylitis (sacroiliitis, syndesmophytes/'bamboo spine', enthesitis)"],"tx":["Provide a pattern-based diagnosis and recommend correlation (serologies, joint aspiration for crystals); early RA erosions on MRI/ultrasound support prompt rheumatology treatment","Recommend appropriate joints/views (e.g., sacroiliac imaging for suspected spondyloarthropathy) and MRI for early inflammatory disease","Guide treatment urgency by identifying erosive inflammatory arthritis early"],"pearls":["OA = osteophytes, asymmetric joint-space loss, subchondral sclerosis/cysts, DIP involvement; RA = symmetric erosions, periarticular osteopenia, MCP/PIP with DIP sparing","Gout = punched-out erosions with overhanging edges and preserved joint space (first MTP); CPPD = chondrocalcinosis","Psoriatic arthritis = 'pencil-in-cup' deformity, DIP disease, and new bone formation","Ankylosing spondylitis = sacroiliitis + syndesmophytes ('bamboo spine') + enthesitis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Arthritis Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555978/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Arthritis Imaging","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-msk-mri","name":"MSK MRI - Internal Derangement & Soft-Tissue Masses","sec":"rads","present":["MRI is the modality of choice for internal joint derangement (menisci, ligaments, cartilage, labrum, tendons), bone marrow, and soft-tissue masses","KNEE: meniscal tears (abnormal signal contacting the articular surface) and cruciate/collateral ligament tears (ACL - altered course/discontinuity, with pivot-shift bone contusions); SHOULDER: rotator cuff and labral tears (MR arthrography aids labral/ligament assessment)","MARROW-replacing processes (metastasis, myeloma, infection) and soft-tissue masses are characterized by signal and enhancement"],"dx":["Interpret fluid-sensitive sequences (T2/STIR/PD fat-sat) for edema/tears; assess marrow for infiltration (low T1 replacing normal fatty marrow)","Soft-tissue masses: most cannot be reliably called benign vs malignant by MRI alone (exceptions: lipoma follows fat on all sequences; simple cyst/ganglion follows fluid) - indeterminate/deep/large masses need sarcoma referral and biopsy","Recognize osteomyelitis (marrow edema, low T1, enhancement) and abscess (rim-enhancing, diffusion-restricting)"],"tx":["Recommend MRI for internal derangement and for characterizing/staging soft-tissue masses; recommend MR arthrography for labral/ligament questions","Refer indeterminate or aggressive-appearing soft-tissue masses to a sarcoma center BEFORE biopsy (tract planning)","Guide surgical management of tears and confirm/exclude osteomyelitis"],"pearls":["A meniscal tear shows abnormal signal contacting the articular surface; ACL tear shows fiber discontinuity/abnormal course with characteristic bone contusions","On MRI, lipoma follows fat and a ganglion/cyst follows fluid - but most soft-tissue masses cannot be safely called benign by imaging alone","Marrow replacement = low T1 signal replacing bright fatty marrow (metastasis, myeloma, infection)","Refer indeterminate/deep/large soft-tissue masses to a sarcoma center before biopsy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Musculoskeletal MRI","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560574/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: MSK MRI","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pediatric-nai","name":"Pediatric Imaging Safety & Non-Accidental Injury","sec":"rads","present":["Pediatric imaging prioritizes RADIATION MINIMIZATION ('Image Gently') - use ultrasound and MRI preferentially, and dose-optimize CT for the smaller, more radiosensitive child","NON-ACCIDENTAL INJURY (child abuse) has HIGH-SPECIFICITY imaging findings that the radiologist must recognize and report: classic metaphyseal lesions ('corner'/'bucket-handle' fractures), POSTERIOR RIB fractures, fractures of varying ages, and certain skull/complex fractures","A skeletal survey (and follow-up survey) plus neuroimaging are standard when abuse is suspected"],"dx":["Recognize high-specificity abuse findings: classic metaphyseal (corner/bucket-handle) lesions, posterior rib fractures, scapular/sternal/spinous-process fractures, and multiple fractures of DIFFERENT AGES","Order the appropriate work-up: skeletal survey (not a 'babygram'), head CT/MRI for suspected abusive head trauma (subdural hematomas of varying ages, retinal correlation), and a follow-up skeletal survey in ~2 weeks","Correlate the injury pattern with the stated mechanism and developmental stage (injuries inconsistent with history/age are red flags)"],"tx":["The radiologist has a duty to identify and clearly communicate findings suspicious for abuse and to ensure appropriate reporting/child-protection pathways are engaged","Recommend the complete standardized work-up (skeletal survey + follow-up, neuroimaging) rather than isolated films","Use ultrasound/MRI first and dose-optimized CT throughout pediatric imaging (Image Gently)"],"pearls":["High-specificity abuse fractures: classic metaphyseal (corner/bucket-handle) lesions, POSTERIOR rib fractures, and fractures of different ages","Injuries inconsistent with the history or the child's developmental stage are red flags - report suspected abuse","Use a formal skeletal survey (+ follow-up survey ~2 weeks later), not a single 'babygram'","'Image Gently': prefer ultrasound/MRI and dose-optimize CT in children"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Non-Accidental Trauma Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441788/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Pediatric Imaging Safety","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pediatric-abdomen","name":"Pediatric Abdomen - Intussusception, Pyloric Stenosis, Malrotation, NEC","sec":"rads","present":["Pediatric abdominal emergencies have age-specific presentations and imaging: ULTRASOUND is first-line for many, sparing radiation","INTUSSUSCEPTION (classically ileocolic, ~6 months-3 years): the 'TARGET/DONUT' sign on ultrasound; presents with intermittent pain, vomiting, and 'currant-jelly' stools","HYPERTROPHIC PYLORIC STENOSIS (~2-8 weeks, non-bilious projectile vomiting): elongated, thickened pylorus on ultrasound; MALROTATION with MIDGUT VOLVULUS (bilious vomiting in a neonate) is a surgical EMERGENCY; NECROTIZING ENTEROCOLITIS (NEC) affects premature infants"],"dx":["Intussusception: ultrasound target sign (diagnostic); assess for lead point (in older/atypical children) and complications (obstruction, ischemia, free air)","Pyloric stenosis: ultrasound measurements of pyloric muscle thickness/channel length; MALROTATION: an upper GI series showing an abnormally positioned duodenojejunal junction (fails to cross midline) and a 'CORKSCREW'/'whirlpool' suggests midgut volvulus","NEC: abdominal radiograph shows PNEUMATOSIS INTESTINALIS (bowel-wall gas), portal venous gas, and pneumoperitoneum (perforation)"],"tx":["INTUSSUSCEPTION: if no perforation/peritonitis, image-guided AIR (or contrast) ENEMA REDUCTION is both diagnostic and therapeutic (surgery if reduction fails or complications present)","MALROTATION with volvulus: emergent surgery (Ladd procedure) - communicate immediately; pyloric stenosis: correct electrolytes, then pyloromyotomy","NEC: supportive care and surgery for perforation; communicate pneumatosis/portal venous gas/free air urgently"],"pearls":["Intussusception: ultrasound 'target/donut' sign; treat with air/contrast enema reduction if no perforation - it is diagnostic AND therapeutic","Bilious vomiting in a neonate = malrotation with midgut volvulus until proven otherwise - an emergency (upper GI: DJ junction fails to cross midline, corkscrew)","Pyloric stenosis: non-bilious projectile vomiting at 2-8 weeks; ultrasound shows a thickened, elongated pylorus","NEC: pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas on radiograph; free air means perforation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Intussusception","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431078/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Pediatric Imaging","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pediatric-chest","name":"Pediatric Chest, Airway & Congenital","sec":"rads","present":["Pediatric chest imaging addresses respiratory distress, stridor, and congenital anomalies; radiography is first-line, with attention to airway and age-specific conditions","AIRWAY: croup ('STEEPLE sign' - subglottic narrowing on frontal view), epiglottitis ('THUMB sign' - swollen epiglottis on lateral, a clinical emergency - do not agitate the child), retropharyngeal abscess (widened prevertebral soft tissue), and foreign body (air-trapping on decubitus/expiratory films)","CONGENITAL lung lesions (congenital pulmonary airway malformation, sequestration, congenital lobar overinflation) and neonatal chest disease (surfactant deficiency, transient tachypnea, meconium aspiration) have characteristic patterns"],"dx":["Airway: recognize the steeple sign (croup) vs thumb sign (epiglottitis) vs retropharyngeal widening; assess for radiopaque/lucent foreign bodies and unilateral air-trapping","Neonatal: distinguish surfactant deficiency disease (low volumes, granular/ground-glass), transient tachypnea (fluid, prominent vessels), and meconium aspiration (patchy, hyperinflation)","Congenital masses: characterize with CT/MR (systemic arterial supply defines sequestration)"],"tx":["Communicate airway emergencies (epiglottitis - keep the child calm, involve ENT/anesthesia; airway foreign body) immediately","Recommend the appropriate next imaging (CT/MR for congenital lesions and vascular supply) and correlate with neonatal history","Guide management of foreign body (bronchoscopy) and congenital lesions (surgical planning)"],"pearls":["Croup = subglottic 'steeple sign'; epiglottitis = 'thumb sign' (lateral) and is an emergency - do NOT agitate the child","Airway foreign body causes unilateral air-trapping - use decubitus/expiratory films in young children","Pulmonary sequestration has a SYSTEMIC arterial supply (from the aorta) - the defining feature","Neonatal surfactant deficiency shows low lung volumes with a granular/ground-glass pattern"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pediatric Airway Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441954/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Pediatric Chest","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-trauma-imaging","name":"Trauma Imaging - FAST, Whole-Body CT & Solid-Organ Injury","sec":"rads","present":["Major trauma imaging is protocol-driven: the FAST ultrasound (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) rapidly screens for free fluid (hemoperitoneum, pericardial effusion), and CONTRAST-ENHANCED WHOLE-BODY CT ('pan-scan') is the definitive survey in stable patients","Solid-organ (spleen, liver, kidney) injuries are graded (AAST) and assessed for ACTIVE EXTRAVASATION (a contrast blush indicating ongoing hemorrhage) and pseudoaneurysm","Unstable patients go to the operating room/angiography rather than CT"],"dx":["FAST checks the hepatorenal (Morison pouch), splenorenal, pelvic, and pericardial spaces for fluid; it is fast but operator-dependent and misses solid-organ and retroperitoneal injury","CT identifies and grades solid-organ injuries and detects ACTIVE BLEEDING (contrast blush/pooling), vascular injury, bowel/mesenteric injury (an important, sometimes subtle finding), and pelvic fractures with bleeding","Use arterial + portal-venous phases (+/- delayed/excretory phase for urinary tract) as appropriate"],"tx":["Communicate active extravasation, high-grade organ injury, vascular injury, and hollow-viscus injury immediately - these change management (embolization vs surgery)","Recommend IR EMBOLIZATION for active arterial bleeding (splenic/hepatic/pelvic) in appropriately selected patients (see IR entries)","Triage by stability: unstable -> operating room/angio; stable -> CT-guided management"],"pearls":["FAST screens for free fluid (Morison pouch, pericardium) but misses solid-organ and retroperitoneal injury - a negative FAST does not exclude injury","A contrast BLUSH on CT = active extravasation/ongoing hemorrhage - communicate for embolization or surgery","Unstable trauma patients go to the operating room/angiography, not the CT scanner","Bowel/mesenteric injury can be subtle (free fluid without solid-organ injury, mesenteric stranding) - look carefully"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Trauma Imaging / FAST","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470479/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Trauma CT","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-vascular-emergencies","name":"Vascular Emergencies - Aortic Aneurysm & Mesenteric Ischemia","sec":"rads","present":["CT angiography is the workhorse for acute vascular emergencies of the abdomen: ABDOMINAL AORTIC ANEURYSM (AAA) and its rupture, and ACUTE MESENTERIC ISCHEMIA","AAA: an infrarenal aorta >=3 cm; RUPTURE (or contained/impending rupture) presents with pain, hypotension, and a pulsatile mass - a surgical/endovascular emergency","ACUTE MESENTERIC ISCHEMIA (embolic/thrombotic arterial, venous, or nonocclusive) has high mortality and requires prompt recognition - 'pain out of proportion to exam'"],"dx":["AAA rupture signs on CT: retroperitoneal/periaortic hemorrhage, and warning signs of instability (hyperdense crescent in the wall, 'draped aorta', focal contour discontinuity); measure size and plan for repair","Mesenteric ischemia: CT angiography shows arterial occlusion (SMA embolus/thrombus) or venous thrombosis; bowel findings include wall thickening or thin non-enhancing wall, PNEUMATOSIS, portal venous gas, and mesenteric edema","Distinguish arterial embolic (sudden, cardiac source) from thrombotic, venous, and nonocclusive ischemia"],"tx":["Communicate ruptured/impending-rupture AAA and mesenteric ischemia IMMEDIATELY - both are life-threatening","AAA: emergent open or ENDOVASCULAR repair (EVAR - see the aortic-endovascular entry) depending on anatomy/stability","Mesenteric ischemia: urgent revascularization (surgical or endovascular) and resection of nonviable bowel; nonocclusive ischemia needs treatment of the underlying low-flow state"],"pearls":["AAA = infrarenal aorta >=3 cm; a hyperdense crescent, draped aorta, or contour discontinuity signals impending/active rupture","Acute mesenteric ischemia = 'pain out of proportion to exam' - CT angiography is the test; pneumatosis and portal venous gas are ominous","SMA embolus (cardiac source, sudden onset) vs thrombosis (on prior atherosclerosis) vs venous vs nonocclusive - the subtype guides therapy","Both AAA rupture and mesenteric ischemia are communicate-immediately emergencies"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470237/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: CT Angiography Abdomen","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-appropriateness-reporting","name":"Imaging Appropriateness, Structured Reporting & Critical Results","sec":"rads","present":["Choosing the RIGHT test is a core radiology competency: the ACR APPROPRIATENESS CRITERIA rate imaging options by clinical scenario, and CLINICAL DECISION SUPPORT guides ordering to reduce low-value imaging","The RADIOLOGY REPORT communicates findings and, critically, an IMPRESSION with actionable recommendations; STRUCTURED reporting and standardized lexicons (the RADS systems) improve clarity and consistency","Timely communication of CRITICAL/URGENT and unexpected findings is a patient-safety and medicolegal imperative"],"dx":["Match modality to question using appropriateness criteria (e.g., ultrasound first for suspected appendicitis in children/pregnancy; MRI for cord compression); weigh radiation, contrast, cost, and yield","Write a clear impression that answers the clinical question and gives specific follow-up recommendations (e.g., Fleischner for nodules, incidental-findings algorithms)","Categorize findings by urgency to trigger appropriate communication pathways"],"tx":["COMMUNICATE critical/emergent findings directly and promptly (documented closed-loop communication) - e.g., tension pneumothorax, free air, PE with strain, ectopic pregnancy, cord compression, malpositioned tubes","Use structured reports and standardized lexicons to reduce ambiguity and ensure actionable recommendations reach the ordering clinician","Support value: avoid duplicate/unnecessary imaging via decision support and prior-study review"],"pearls":["ACR Appropriateness Criteria + clinical decision support guide the RIGHT test and reduce low-value imaging","The IMPRESSION should answer the clinical question and give specific, actionable follow-up recommendations","Critical/unexpected findings require direct, timely, documented (closed-loop) communication - a patient-safety and medicolegal duty","Standardized lexicons (BI-RADS, LI-RADS, Lung-RADS, etc.) make reports consistent and actionable"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiology Reporting / Communication","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564323/"},{"t":"ACR: Appropriateness Criteria","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-incidental-findings","name":"Incidental Findings Management","sec":"rads","present":["Cross-sectional imaging frequently reveals INCIDENTALOMAS unrelated to the presenting complaint (adrenal, renal, hepatic, pancreatic, pulmonary, thyroid nodules); most are benign, but a rational approach prevents both missed cancers and harmful over-investigation","The ACR publishes WHITE PAPERS/algorithms for managing common incidental findings by size and features","The goal is proportionate follow-up: reassure and stop when features are benign; recommend workup when features or size warrant"],"dx":["Characterize the incidental lesion with the same feature-based logic used electively (e.g., adrenal <=10 HU adenoma; simple renal cyst = Bosniak I; benign-pattern liver lesion; sub-centimeter low-risk pulmonary nodule)","Apply the relevant ACR incidental-findings algorithm to decide follow-up interval vs further imaging vs no follow-up, factoring patient risk and life expectancy","Avoid the incidentaloma 'cascade' - reflexive follow-up of clearly benign findings"],"tx":["Give a clear, guideline-based recommendation in the report (e.g., 'no follow-up needed', a specific interval, or a defined next test), reducing ambiguity for the ordering clinician","Weigh comorbidity/life expectancy - not every finding warrants pursuit in every patient","Ensure findings that do warrant follow-up are tracked so they are not lost"],"pearls":["Most incidentalomas are benign - use feature-based characterization and ACR white-paper algorithms for proportionate follow-up","State a clear recommendation (no follow-up / specific interval / defined next test) to prevent ambiguity and the follow-up 'cascade'","Benign signatures stop the workup: adrenal <=10 HU adenoma, simple renal cyst, macroscopic-fat renal angiomyolipoma, classic hemangioma","Weigh life expectancy/comorbidity before recommending pursuit of a low-risk incidental finding"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Incidental Radiologic Findings","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574526/"},{"t":"ACR: Incidental Findings White Papers","u":"https://www.acr.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ir-fundamentals","name":"IR Fundamentals - Access, Seldinger & Periprocedural Care","sec":"rads","present":["Interventional radiology performs minimally invasive, image-guided procedures via vascular and percutaneous access; the SELDINGER TECHNIQUE (needle -> wire -> exchange over wire for sheath/catheter) is the foundation of vascular access","Image guidance (ultrasound, fluoroscopy, CT, cone-beam CT) enables precise targeting; moderate sedation and periprocedural monitoring are routine","Safe practice requires managing COAGULATION status, and appropriate consent, planning, and post-procedure care"],"dx":["Plan access (common femoral or radial artery; jugular/femoral vein) using ultrasound guidance to reduce complications; review cross-sectional imaging for the roadmap","Assess and correct bleeding risk per procedure category (SIR guidelines): check/optimize INR, platelets, and manage anticoagulants/antiplatelets before higher-risk procedures","Screen renal function/contrast allergy and plan sedation/analgesia and monitoring"],"tx":["Use ULTRASOUND-GUIDED access and the Seldinger technique; achieve hemostasis with manual compression or closure devices; monitor the access site for complications (hematoma, pseudoaneurysm, retroperitoneal bleed)","Follow periprocedural coagulation management (hold/bridge anticoagulants per bleeding-risk category) and provide post-procedure monitoring and instructions","Recognize/manage complications: access-site bleeding, pseudoaneurysm (ultrasound-guided thrombin), contrast reactions, and sedation events"],"pearls":["Seldinger technique (needle -> wire -> sheath over wire) is the foundation of vascular access","Ultrasound-guided common femoral (or radial) access reduces complications - avoid punctures too high (retroperitoneal bleed) or too low (pseudoaneurysm)","Stratify and correct coagulation by procedure bleeding-risk category (SIR guidelines) before intervention","Post-procedure, watch the access site for hematoma, pseudoaneurysm, and retroperitoneal hemorrhage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Seldinger Technique / Vascular Access","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559090/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Interventional Radiology","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-embolization-principles","name":"Angiography & Embolization Principles","sec":"rads","present":["Catheter ANGIOGRAPHY maps vascular anatomy and enables targeted therapy; EMBOLIZATION intentionally occludes vessels to stop bleeding, treat tumors/vascular malformations, or redistribute flow","Embolic AGENTS are chosen by goal and permanence: temporary (GELFOAM), particles (PVA/microspheres - for tumors/flow reduction), COILS/plugs (mechanical, permanent, for discrete vessels), and LIQUIDS (glue/Onyx, sclerosants) for malformations","The endpoint (stasis vs subtotal) and level (proximal vs distal) depend on the indication"],"dx":["Perform diagnostic angiography to identify the target (bleeding vessel/extravasation, tumor supply, malformation nidus) and plan the safest effective occlusion","Match agent to task: coils for a focal pseudoaneurysm/discrete vessel; particles for tumor/diffuse bleeding; liquid embolics for arteriovenous malformation nidus; consider non-target embolization risk","Understand collateral anatomy and 'danger' anastomoses to avoid ischemic complications"],"tx":["Deliver superselective embolization to achieve the endpoint (hemostasis, devascularization) while sparing normal tissue; verify with post-embolization angiography","Anticipate and prevent NON-TARGET embolization (ischemia of normal tissue) through careful catheter position and agent selection","Manage POST-EMBOLIZATION SYNDROME (pain, fever, nausea after tumor embolization - usually self-limited)"],"pearls":["Match the embolic agent to the goal: coils/plugs (discrete vessels), particles (tumor/diffuse bleeding), liquids/glue (AVM nidus), Gelfoam (temporary)","Superselective catheterization minimizes NON-TARGET embolization and ischemic injury","Post-embolization syndrome (pain, low-grade fever, nausea) is common after tumor embolization and usually self-limited","Post-embolization angiography confirms the endpoint (stasis/hemostasis)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Embolization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560510/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Catheter Angiography","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-hemorrhage-embolization","name":"GI & Trauma Hemorrhage Embolization","sec":"rads","present":["Endovascular embolization controls acute hemorrhage that is not amenable to or has failed endoscopic/conservative management - GI bleeding, post-traumatic solid-organ and pelvic bleeding, and postpartum hemorrhage","CT angiography localizes active EXTRAVASATION and guides the interventional plan; catheter angiography then targets the bleeding vessel","Embolization is often preferred over surgery for many bleeding sources in appropriately selected patients"],"dx":["Localize bleeding: acute GI bleeds usually need a rate of active hemorrhage detectable angiographically (CT angiography/tagged-RBC scan can localize first); identify the culprit vessel (e.g., gastroduodenal artery in a bleeding duodenal ulcer)","Trauma: CT-identified splenic/hepatic/renal/pelvic arterial blush directs selective embolization","Distinguish arterial from venous/nonlocalizable bleeding (venous/diffuse sources may not be embolizable)"],"tx":["Perform SELECTIVE/superselective embolization of the bleeding vessel (coils, particles, glue) - e.g., gastroduodenal artery for duodenal ulcer bleeding, splenic artery for splenic trauma (splenic salvage), internal iliac branches for pelvic trauma/postpartum hemorrhage","Balance hemostasis against ischemia (the bowel's dual supply usually tolerates selective embolization; be cautious to avoid infarction)","Communicate and coordinate with surgery/endoscopy/obstetrics; monitor for rebleeding and non-target ischemia"],"pearls":["Embolization controls GI, traumatic solid-organ, pelvic, and postpartum hemorrhage - often preferred over surgery in selected patients","CT angiography localizes active extravasation and plans the intervention","Splenic artery embolization enables splenic salvage in trauma; gastroduodenal artery is the target for bleeding duodenal ulcers","Selective technique balances hemostasis against ischemic complications (bowel tolerates selective embolization owing to collaterals)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Gastrointestinal Bleeding Embolization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564399/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Embolization","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-bronchial-artery-embolization","name":"Bronchial Artery Embolization (Hemoptysis) & Pulmonary Vascular IR","sec":"rads","present":["MASSIVE HEMOPTYSIS is usually of BRONCHIAL ARTERY (systemic) origin (~90%), not pulmonary artery - so BRONCHIAL ARTERY EMBOLIZATION (BAE) is the mainstay of control; causes include bronchiectasis, tuberculosis/fungal disease, and malignancy","Pulmonary vascular IR also includes treatment of pulmonary arteriovenous malformations (embolization, e.g., in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia) and catheter-directed therapy for select pulmonary embolism","CT angiography maps the bleeding source and abnormal bronchial/systemic collaterals before embolization"],"dx":["Localize with CT angiography (hypertrophied bronchial arteries, the culprit lung/segment) and bronchoscopy as needed; identify a SPINAL ARTERY (anterior spinal/artery of Adamkiewicz) origin off the bronchial circulation - a key hazard","For pulmonary AVMs, identify the feeding artery (screen in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia; risk of paradoxical embolism/stroke and brain abscess)","Assess massive hemoptysis for airway protection and lateralization"],"tx":["BRONCHIAL ARTERY EMBOLIZATION with particles is first-line for massive/recurrent hemoptysis - protect the airway first (position bleeding side down, secure airway) and avoid embolizing a spinal artery origin (risk of cord infarction/paralysis)","PULMONARY AVM: coil/plug embolization of the feeding artery reduces paradoxical embolism and hemorrhage risk","Recognize hemoptysis can recur (revascularization/collaterals) and may need repeat embolization; coordinate with pulmonology/surgery"],"pearls":["Massive hemoptysis is usually BRONCHIAL (systemic) arterial in origin - bronchial artery embolization is the mainstay","Avoid embolizing a spinal artery (artery of Adamkiewicz) arising from the bronchial circulation - risk of cord infarction/paraplegia","Protect the airway and position the bleeding side DOWN to protect the good lung","Pulmonary AVMs (hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia) are embolized to prevent paradoxical stroke/brain abscess and hemorrhage"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hemoptysis / Bronchial Artery Embolization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538330/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Embolization Procedures","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ufe-pae","name":"Uterine Fibroid & Prostate Artery Embolization","sec":"rads","present":["UTERINE FIBROID (ARTERY) EMBOLIZATION (UFE/UAE) is a uterus-sparing treatment for symptomatic fibroids (menorrhagia, bulk symptoms), embolizing both uterine arteries to infarct fibroids while sparing the myometrium","PROSTATE ARTERY EMBOLIZATION (PAE) treats benign prostatic hyperplasia (lower urinary tract symptoms) by embolizing the prostatic arteries to shrink the gland - a minimally invasive alternative to surgery","Both rely on preserved collateral supply to normal tissue and careful arterial mapping"],"dx":["UFE workup: MRI characterizes fibroid number/size/location and viability, excludes adenomyosis/leiomyosarcoma mimics, and confirms candidacy; assess fertility goals (counsel accordingly)","PAE: assess prostate size and symptoms; CT/MR angiography maps the often-complex prostatic arterial anatomy and dangerous anastomoses (to bladder/rectum/penis)","Exclude contraindications (active infection, suspected malignancy where inappropriate)"],"tx":["UFE: bilateral uterine artery embolization with particles causes fibroid infarction and symptom relief while preserving the uterus; expect POST-EMBOLIZATION SYNDROME (pain, low-grade fever) and counsel on fertility implications","PAE: superselective prostatic artery embolization improves lower urinary tract symptoms with low sexual-dysfunction risk; meticulous technique avoids NON-TARGET embolization (bladder/rectum/penile ischemia)","Provide post-procedure analgesia and follow-up imaging/symptom assessment"],"pearls":["UFE embolizes BOTH uterine arteries to infarct fibroids while sparing the uterus - a uterus-preserving option for symptomatic fibroids","Always image (MRI) before UFE to exclude leiomyosarcoma/adenomyosis mimics and confirm candidacy","PAE treats BPH symptoms with low risk of sexual dysfunction, but the prostatic arterial anatomy is complex - avoid non-target embolization","Post-embolization syndrome (pain, low-grade fever) is expected after UFE - manage with analgesia"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Uterine Artery Embolization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560657/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Uterine Fibroid Embolization","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-interventional-oncology","name":"Interventional Oncology - TACE/TARE & Tumor Ablation","sec":"rads","present":["Interventional oncology treats tumors locoregionally. TRANSARTERIAL CHEMOEMBOLIZATION (TACE) and RADIOEMBOLIZATION (TARE, yttrium-90) exploit the hepatic tumor's ARTERIAL supply (normal liver is mostly portal-fed) to deliver chemo/radiation while embolizing","THERMAL ABLATION (radiofrequency/RFA, MICROWAVE, cryoablation) destroys tumors in situ - used for small HCC, liver/lung/kidney/bone tumors and oligometastases","Selection depends on tumor size/number/location, liver function, and vascular anatomy"],"dx":["Confirm diagnosis/staging (e.g., LI-RADS LR-5 HCC) and assess liver function (Child-Pugh), tumor burden, and portal vein status (portal vein thrombosis affects TACE safety)","For TARE, perform mapping angiography with a technetium MAA study to quantify LUNG SHUNT FRACTION and prevent radiation pneumonitis, and to coil-protect non-target vessels","Select ablation candidates by size (best for small tumors) and location (avoid heat-sink near large vessels; protect adjacent bowel/nerves)"],"tx":["TACE (drug-eluting/conventional) and TARE (Y-90) for intermediate-stage HCC and select liver metastases; combine with systemic therapy and transplant/resection pathways","THERMAL ABLATION (RFA/microwave/cryo) for small HCC, colorectal liver metastases, renal (small RCC), lung, and painful bone metastases; achieve an ablative margin","Manage post-embolization/ablation syndrome and monitor imaging response (mRECIST); avoid non-target injury (bowel, gallbladder, diaphragm, nerves)"],"pearls":["Liver tumors are ARTERIALLY fed (normal liver is portal-fed) - the basis for TACE/TARE selectivity","Before Y-90 (TARE), a technetium-MAA mapping study measures the lung shunt fraction to prevent radiation pneumonitis","Thermal ablation is best for SMALL tumors; a large adjacent vessel causes a heat-sink effect (incomplete ablation)","Protect adjacent structures (bowel, nerves) during ablation (hydrodissection); assess response with mRECIST"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Transarterial Chemoembolization","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537306/"},{"t":"NCI: Liver Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/liver/hp/adult-liver-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"rads-biliary-abscess-drainage","name":"Biliary Intervention & Percutaneous Abscess Drainage","sec":"rads","present":["PERCUTANEOUS BILIARY intervention relieves obstruction/infection when endoscopic (ERCP) access fails or is unavailable: PERCUTANEOUS TRANSHEPATIC CHOLANGIOGRAPHY (PTC) and biliary DRAIN placement, and cholecystostomy for acute cholecystitis in poor surgical candidates","PERCUTANEOUS ABSCESS/FLUID DRAINAGE is a core IR service - image-guided catheter drainage of intra-abdominal, pelvic, and other collections, often avoiding surgery","Both require a safe access route and management of infected/obstructed systems"],"dx":["Biliary: define the level/cause of obstruction (stone, stricture, tumor) and the presence of CHOLANGITIS (infected, obstructed biliary tree - an emergency needing urgent drainage)","Abscess: confirm a drainable collection (mature, accessible) on CT/ultrasound and plan a safe percutaneous route avoiding bowel/vessels","Assess coagulation and antibiotic coverage before drainage of infected collections"],"tx":["Biliary: PTC with external/internal-external drain (or cholecystostomy) decompresses obstruction/cholangitis; later definitive therapy (stent, stone removal, surgery) follows; give antibiotics and avoid over-manipulation in sepsis","ABSCESS: image-guided catheter drainage (Seldinger or trocar) with a safe window; place a drain, flush, and monitor output; some small collections are aspirated","Manage catheters (patency, output, exchanges) and coordinate source control/definitive treatment"],"pearls":["Cholangitis (infected, obstructed bile duct) is an emergency - decompress urgently (ERCP first-line; percutaneous PTC drain when ERCP fails)","Percutaneous cholecystostomy temporizes acute cholecystitis in poor surgical candidates","Image-guided percutaneous drainage treats most accessible, mature abscesses and often avoids surgery","Choose a safe access route avoiding bowel/vessels/pleura; cover with appropriate antibiotics"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Percutaneous Abscess Drainage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459332/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Biliary Drainage","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-gu-intervention","name":"Genitourinary Intervention - Nephrostomy & Ureteral Stenting","sec":"rads","present":["PERCUTANEOUS NEPHROSTOMY relieves urinary tract obstruction and provides access for further intervention; it is urgent in an OBSTRUCTED, INFECTED collecting system (pyonephrosis) or obstruction causing renal failure","Antegrade URETERAL STENTING and nephroureteral catheters manage ureteral obstruction (stones, tumor, stricture) when retrograde (cystoscopic) approaches fail","Percutaneous access also enables stone therapy (for percutaneous nephrolithotomy) and biopsy"],"dx":["Confirm HYDRONEPHROSIS/obstruction and identify infection (pyonephrosis - pus in an obstructed system, an emergency); assess the level/cause of obstruction","Evaluate coagulation and plan a posterolateral calyceal puncture (avoiding the colon, pleura, and major vessels) under ultrasound/fluoroscopic guidance","Determine goal: decompression alone (nephrostomy) vs internalization (antegrade stent/nephroureteral catheter)"],"tx":["Perform PERCUTANEOUS NEPHROSTOMY to decompress an obstructed/infected system emergently (pyonephrosis, obstruction with sepsis or acute kidney injury) and to gain access for stone/stent procedures","Place antegrade ureteral stents/nephroureteral catheters for ureteral obstruction not manageable retrograde (e.g., malignant obstruction)","Manage catheters and watch for complications (bleeding, sepsis after manipulating an infected system, catheter dislodgement)"],"pearls":["Pyonephrosis (infected, obstructed kidney) is a urologic emergency - decompress urgently with percutaneous nephrostomy","Nephrostomy provides drainage AND access for stents, nephroureteral catheters, and stone therapy","Use a posterolateral calyceal puncture to avoid the colon, pleura, and major vessels","Manipulating an infected obstructed system risks sepsis - drain, cover with antibiotics, and minimize manipulation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Percutaneous Nephrostomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493184/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Nephrostomy","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-venous-access","name":"Central Venous & Dialysis Access","sec":"rads","present":["IR places and manages long-term VENOUS ACCESS: tunneled central catheters, PORTS (for chemotherapy), PICCs, and tunneled dialysis catheters - using ultrasound and fluoroscopic guidance for safe placement and correct tip position","IR also MAINTAINS hemodialysis ARTERIOVENOUS FISTULAE/GRAFTS - treating stenosis (angioplasty) and thrombosis (thrombectomy) to preserve access longevity","Correct catheter tip position (cavoatrial junction) and sterile technique reduce complications"],"dx":["Select access by need/duration (port for intermittent chemo, tunneled catheter for longer-term/dialysis, PICC for shorter courses); ultrasound-guide venipuncture to avoid pneumothorax/arterial puncture","Confirm tip at the cavoatrial junction fluoroscopically; recognize malposition and complications (pneumothorax, arterial injury, arrhythmia)","Dialysis access: identify the failing fistula/graft (stenosis at the venous anastomosis is common) via clinical signs and fistulography"],"tx":["Place tunneled catheters/ports/PICCs with ultrasound + fluoroscopy; ensure correct tip position and manage catheter complications (infection, thrombosis, fibrin sheath - treated with stripping or exchange)","Maintain dialysis access: ANGIOPLASTY of stenoses and pharmacomechanical THROMBECTOMY of clotted fistulae/grafts to restore function and prolong access life","Follow infection-prevention bundles; recognize catheter-related bloodstream infection needing removal"],"pearls":["Central catheter tip belongs at the cavoatrial junction; ultrasound-guided venipuncture reduces pneumothorax/arterial puncture","Choose access by duration/use: port (intermittent chemo), tunneled catheter (dialysis/long-term), PICC (shorter courses)","Dialysis fistula/graft failure is usually from venous-anastomotic stenosis - treat with angioplasty; declot thrombosed access","A fibrin sheath causing catheter dysfunction is managed by stripping or catheter exchange"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Central Venous Access","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557798/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Vascular Access Procedures","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-ivc-filter-venous","name":"IVC Filters & Venous Thrombolysis","sec":"rads","present":["INFERIOR VENA CAVA (IVC) FILTERS prevent pulmonary embolism by trapping emboli when anticoagulation is contraindicated or has failed in venous thromboembolism; retrievable filters should be removed once no longer needed","CATHETER-DIRECTED THROMBOLYSIS and pharmacomechanical thrombectomy treat extensive/limb-threatening DVT (e.g., iliofemoral, phlegmasia) and selected massive/submassive PE to reduce clot burden and post-thrombotic complications","May-Thurner syndrome (left iliac vein compression) is a treatable cause of left-leg DVT"],"dx":["IVC filter INDICATIONS: venous thromboembolism with a contraindication to anticoagulation, or recurrent PE despite therapeutic anticoagulation; document indication and a retrieval plan","Identify candidates for catheter-directed therapy: acute iliofemoral DVT with low bleeding risk, limb-threatening thrombosis, or high-risk PE; screen for May-Thurner in young women with left-leg DVT","Assess bleeding risk before thrombolysis"],"tx":["Place a RETRIEVABLE IVC filter when anticoagulation is not possible - and RETRIEVE it promptly once anticoagulation can resume (retained filters cause complications: migration, fracture, penetration, IVC thrombosis)","Catheter-directed/pharmacomechanical thrombolysis for extensive iliofemoral DVT and selected PE; stent an underlying May-Thurner lesion","Monitor for bleeding during thrombolysis; anticoagulate when feasible as the definitive therapy"],"pearls":["IVC filter indications: venous thromboembolism with a contraindication to (or failure of) anticoagulation - not a substitute for anticoagulation when it can be given","Retrieve retrievable filters promptly - retained filters migrate, fracture, penetrate the cava, and cause IVC thrombosis","Catheter-directed thrombolysis is for extensive iliofemoral/limb-threatening DVT and selected high-risk PE","Consider and stent May-Thurner (left iliac vein compression) in young women with left-leg DVT"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Inferior Vena Cava Filter","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470201/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: IVC Filter","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-pad-intervention","name":"Peripheral Arterial Disease - Angioplasty & Stenting","sec":"rads","present":["Endovascular therapy treats symptomatic PERIPHERAL ARTERIAL DISEASE - lifestyle-limiting claudication refractory to medical therapy and CRITICAL LIMB(-THREATENING) ISCHEMIA (rest pain, tissue loss/ulcers/gangrene), a limb-salvage emergency","Options include balloon ANGIOPLASTY (including drug-coated balloons), STENTING, and atherectomy, guided by lesion location, length, and calcification","CT/MR angiography and duplex map disease; the ankle-brachial index quantifies severity"],"dx":["Localize and grade disease (aortoiliac vs femoropopliteal vs infrapopliteal) with duplex and CT/MR angiography; correlate with the ankle-brachial index and symptoms (claudication vs critical limb ischemia)","Identify critical limb-threatening ischemia (rest pain, non-healing ulcer, gangrene) requiring prompt revascularization for limb salvage","Distinguish ACUTE limb ischemia (sudden, the 6 P's - a true emergency needing urgent thrombolysis/thrombectomy/surgery) from chronic disease"],"tx":["Endovascular revascularization (angioplasty +/- stent, drug-coated balloon, atherectomy) for suitable lesions - especially aortoiliac disease and for limb salvage in critical limb ischemia","ACUTE limb ischemia: urgent catheter-directed thrombolysis/thrombectomy or surgery - communicate immediately (viable vs threatened vs irreversible limb)","Pair with medical therapy (antiplatelets, statin, smoking cessation, supervised exercise for claudication)"],"pearls":["Critical limb-threatening ischemia (rest pain, tissue loss) is a limb-salvage emergency needing revascularization","Acute limb ischemia (the 6 P's: pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, poikilothermia, paralysis) demands urgent thrombolysis/thrombectomy/surgery","Endovascular therapy is favored for aortoiliac disease and limb salvage; drug-coated balloons reduce restenosis in the femoropopliteal segment","Ankle-brachial index quantifies severity; always add medical therapy (antiplatelet, statin, smoking cessation, exercise)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Peripheral Arterial Disease Intervention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430745/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Angioplasty and Stenting","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-tips","name":"TIPS & Portal Hypertension","sec":"rads","present":["A TRANSJUGULAR INTRAHEPATIC PORTOSYSTEMIC SHUNT (TIPS) creates a low-resistance channel between a hepatic vein and the portal vein (through the liver parenchyma) to DECOMPRESS PORTAL HYPERTENSION","Main indications: refractory VARICEAL BLEEDING (not controlled endoscopically) and refractory ASCITES; also Budd-Chiari and hepatic hydrothorax in selected patients","The trade-off is diverting portal flow away from the liver - which can precipitate or worsen HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY"],"dx":["Confirm the indication (refractory variceal hemorrhage or refractory ascites) and assess liver reserve/prognosis (MELD), cardiac function, and encephalopathy risk","Screen for CONTRAINDICATIONS: severe hepatic failure/high MELD, right heart failure/pulmonary hypertension, severe/uncontrolled encephalopathy, and polycystic liver disease","Image the portal/hepatic venous anatomy and rule out obstructing masses along the tract"],"tx":["Create the shunt (hepatic vein to portal vein) and place a covered stent, reducing the portosystemic gradient (target < ~12 mmHg to control variceal bleeding) - decompressing varices and ascites","Anticipate and manage NEW/worsened HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY (the major complication) with lactulose/rifaximin; a shunt can be reduced if severe","Monitor shunt patency (duplex surveillance) and liver/cardiac status"],"pearls":["TIPS decompresses portal hypertension for refractory variceal bleeding and refractory ascites","The major complication is new/worsened hepatic encephalopathy (portal flow bypasses the liver) - manage with lactulose/rifaximin","Contraindications include severe hepatic failure/high MELD, right heart failure/pulmonary hypertension, and severe encephalopathy","Target a portosystemic gradient < ~12 mmHg to control variceal bleeding; surveil shunt patency with duplex"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567756/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: TIPS","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-image-guided-biopsy","name":"Image-Guided Biopsy & Drainage","sec":"rads","present":["Image-guided percutaneous BIOPSY (ultrasound-, CT-, or MRI-guided) obtains tissue from masses and organs with minimal invasiveness - core-needle for histology, fine-needle aspiration for cytology","Guidance choice depends on target visibility and access: ultrasound (real-time, no radiation, superficial/solid organs), CT (deep/complex targets, lung), and fluoroscopy/MRI for specific cases","Safe planning avoids intervening bowel, vessels, and pleura, and accounts for bleeding risk"],"dx":["Plan the safest trajectory to a representative part of the target (avoid necrotic center); use ultrasound for real-time guidance and CT for deep or poorly visualized lesions","Assess and correct COAGULATION per bleeding-risk category; identify high-risk biopsies (e.g., adrenal - exclude pheochromocytoma first; highly vascular lesions)","Match modality/needle to the diagnostic need (core for architecture/lymphoma subtyping vs FNA for cytology)"],"tx":["Perform biopsy with real-time or intermittent guidance; for lung biopsy, counsel on and monitor for PNEUMOTHORAX (the main complication) and manage with observation/aspiration/chest tube as needed","Ensure adequacy (on-site cytology when available) and radiologic-pathologic concordance; place markers when relevant","Drain associated collections when indicated; manage post-procedure bleeding and monitoring"],"pearls":["Ultrasound guidance is real-time and radiation-free (superficial/solid organs); CT suits deep or poorly seen targets","Pneumothorax is the main complication of percutaneous LUNG biopsy - counsel, monitor, and manage per severity","Exclude pheochromocytoma before adrenal biopsy; correct coagulation by bleeding-risk category first","Sample a representative (non-necrotic) region; core biopsy is preferred for architecture/lymphoma, FNA for cytology"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Image-Guided Biopsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556053/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Needle Biopsy","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-evar-tevar","name":"Aortic Endovascular Repair (EVAR/TEVAR)","sec":"rads","present":["ENDOVASCULAR ANEURYSM REPAIR (EVAR, abdominal aorta) and THORACIC endovascular repair (TEVAR) exclude aneurysms/dissections by deploying a covered STENT-GRAFT via femoral access - less morbid than open repair in suitable anatomy","Indications: AAA meeting size/growth thresholds (typically ~5.0-5.5 cm or rapid growth/symptoms), ruptured AAA in stable-enough anatomy, and complicated type B aortic dissection (TEVAR)","Durable success depends on anatomy (adequate proximal/distal 'landing zones' and access vessels) and lifelong surveillance"],"dx":["Plan with CT angiography: measure neck length/angulation, aneurysm extent, landing zones, and iliofemoral access; assess candidacy vs open repair","For dissection, define the entry tear and branch-vessel involvement (TEVAR covers the entry tear in complicated type B dissection)","Establish a SURVEILLANCE plan (post-EVAR CT/ultrasound) to detect endoleaks"],"tx":["Deploy the stent-graft to exclude the aneurysm/dissection; confirm exclusion and screen for ENDOLEAKS (persistent flow into the sac): Type I (seal-zone leak - needs treatment), Type II (branch backflow, often observed), Type III (graft defect - treat), and monitor sac size","Provide lifelong SURVEILLANCE imaging (endoleak, migration, sac growth) - a key difference from open repair","Coordinate emergent repair for ruptured AAA in suitable anatomy"],"pearls":["EVAR/TEVAR exclude aneurysms/dissections with a stent-graft - lower upfront morbidity than open repair but requires lifelong surveillance","ENDOLEAK types: I (seal-zone, treat), II (branch backflow, often watch), III (graft defect, treat) - detected on surveillance CT","AAA repair thresholds: ~5.0-5.5 cm, rapid growth, or symptoms; TEVAR treats complicated type B dissection by covering the entry tear","Adequate proximal/distal landing zones and iliofemoral access anatomy determine EVAR/TEVAR candidacy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Endovascular Aortic Repair","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499911/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Aortic Stent Graft","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-carotid-neurovascular","name":"Carotid & Neurovascular Intervention","sec":"rads","present":["Neurointervention (performed by neuro-IR/neuroradiology/neurosurgery) treats cerebrovascular disease endovascularly: MECHANICAL THROMBECTOMY for acute large-vessel-occlusion stroke, ANEURYSM COILING, arteriovenous malformation/fistula embolization, and CAROTID stenting","MECHANICAL THROMBECTOMY (stent retriever/aspiration) is standard for anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion, within 6 hours and up to 24 hours in selected patients by perfusion/clinical mismatch (DAWN, DEFUSE 3)","CAROTID artery stenting is an alternative to endarterectomy for selected patients with significant carotid stenosis"],"dx":["Confirm large-vessel occlusion on CTA and estimate salvageable tissue (perfusion/DWI mismatch) to select thrombectomy candidates (see the diagnostic stroke entry for imaging workup)","Aneurysm: characterize size/morphology/neck (CTA/DSA) to choose coiling vs clipping; ruptured aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage needs prompt securing to prevent rebleeding","Carotid stenosis: quantify degree (duplex/CTA) and symptom status; weigh stenting vs endarterectomy by anatomy and risk"],"tx":["MECHANICAL THROMBECTOMY (stent retriever/aspiration) rapidly recanalizes large-vessel-occlusion stroke - time-critical; coordinate with the stroke team and IV thrombolysis pathways","ANEURYSM: endovascular COILING (+/- flow diverters/stents) secures many aneurysms, especially in poor surgical candidates; treat AVMs/dural fistulae with embolization (+/- radiosurgery/surgery)","CAROTID stenting (with embolic protection) for suitable patients; manage periprocedural stroke/reperfusion risks"],"pearls":["Mechanical thrombectomy is standard for anterior large-vessel-occlusion stroke - up to 24 h in selected patients by mismatch (DAWN, DEFUSE 3)","Endovascular coiling secures many intracranial aneurysms (vs surgical clipping) - especially useful in poor surgical candidates","Ruptured aneurysm (aneurysmal SAH) must be secured promptly to prevent rebleeding","Carotid stenting (with embolic protection) is an alternative to endarterectomy in selected patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mechanical Thrombectomy / Neurointervention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560796/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Stroke Intervention","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-vertebral-augmentation","name":"Vertebral Augmentation & MSK/Pain IR","sec":"rads","present":["VERTEBROPLASTY (cement injection) and KYPHOPLASTY (balloon cavity creation then cement) stabilize painful VERTEBRAL COMPRESSION FRACTURES - osteoporotic or malignant - and can relieve pain and, for kyphoplasty, partially restore height","MSK/pain IR also includes image-guided joint/nerve injections, ablation of osteoid osteoma (radiofrequency) and painful bone metastases, and biopsy","Patient selection (fracture acuity, correlation of pain to level, edema on MRI) is key to outcomes"],"dx":["Confirm the symptomatic level: MRI (STIR/marrow edema) or bone scan identifies an ACUTE/active fracture that correlates with pain - old healed fractures are not good targets","Exclude contraindications (uncorrected coagulopathy, infection at site, retropulsed fragment/cord compression that needs surgery); evaluate for malignancy where relevant","For osteoid osteoma, identify the nidus (CT) for ablation"],"tx":["VERTEBROPLASTY/KYPHOPLASTY: inject bone cement under fluoroscopic/CT guidance to stabilize and reduce pain in appropriately selected acute/subacute compression fractures; kyphoplasty creates a cavity to reduce cement leak and restore some height","Watch for CEMENT LEAK/EMBOLISM (venous/epidural) - the key complication; meticulous technique and low-pressure injection reduce risk","Radiofrequency ablation of osteoid osteoma (highly effective) and palliative ablation +/- cementoplasty for painful bone metastases; perform image-guided joint/nerve injections for pain"],"pearls":["Vertebral augmentation targets ACUTE/active compression fractures (marrow edema on MRI/uptake on bone scan) that correlate with pain - not old healed ones","Kyphoplasty creates a balloon cavity (may restore some height and reduce cement leak) vs direct-injection vertebroplasty","Cement leak/embolism is the key complication of vertebral augmentation","Radiofrequency ablation is highly effective for osteoid osteoma; ablation +/- cementoplasty palliates painful bone metastases"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vertebroplasty and Kyphoplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555962/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Vertebroplasty","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-nuclear-physics","name":"Nuclear Physics & Radiopharmaceuticals","sec":"rads","present":["Nuclear medicine images FUNCTION/physiology by detecting gamma rays from administered RADIOPHARMACEUTICALS (a radionuclide + a targeting molecule); the gamma camera (SPECT) and PET scanner form the images","TECHNETIUM-99m is the workhorse radionuclide (~6-hour half-life, 140 keV gamma - ideal for imaging) and is eluted on demand from a MOLYBDENUM-99/Tc-99m GENERATOR ('milking' the cow)","PET uses POSITRON emitters (F-18, Ga-68, Rb-82) detected by 511 keV annihilation-photon coincidence"],"dx":["Match radiopharmaceutical to the physiologic target (e.g., Tc-99m MDP -> bone, Tc-99m sestamibi -> myocardium/parathyroid, F-18 FDG -> glucose metabolism, I-123/I-131 -> thyroid)","Understand image formation: SPECT (single-photon, gamma camera, collimated) vs PET (coincidence detection, higher resolution/quantitation, SUV); hybrid SPECT/CT and PET/CT add anatomy","Consider half-life and dosimetry for imaging quality and radiation safety"],"tx":["Apply radiation SAFETY/dosimetry principles (ALARA, patient/staff/public dose, pregnancy and BREASTFEEDING precautions - e.g., interruption after certain agents, and special handling for I-131 therapy)","Ensure correct radiopharmaceutical, dose, and timing; recognize free-pertechnetate and other quality-control issues that alter biodistribution","Coordinate generator elution and radiopharmacy quality control for reliable imaging"],"pearls":["Tc-99m is the workhorse: ~6-hour half-life, 140 keV gamma, eluted from a Mo-99/Tc-99m generator","Nuclear medicine images FUNCTION; SPECT uses single gamma photons, PET uses positron annihilation (511 keV coincidence, quantitative SUV)","Match tracer to target: MDP (bone), sestamibi (heart/parathyroid), FDG (glucose metabolism), iodine (thyroid)","Apply ALARA and specific pregnancy/breastfeeding precautions - especially for I-131 therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radiopharmaceuticals / Nuclear Medicine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557652/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Nuclear Medicine","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-bone-scintigraphy","name":"Bone Scintigraphy & Infection/Inflammation Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["The BONE SCAN (Tc-99m MDP/HDP) images OSTEOBLASTIC activity and blood flow - highly SENSITIVE for skeletal metastases, occult fractures, osteomyelitis, and other bone turnover, though nonspecific","A THREE-PHASE bone scan (flow, blood-pool, delayed) distinguishes osteomyelitis (positive on all three phases at the site) from cellulitis (soft-tissue activity without focal bone uptake on delayed images)","Whole-body distribution patterns aid diagnosis: multiple random foci = metastases; a 'SUPERSCAN' (diffuse intense skeletal uptake with faint kidneys) signals extensive metastatic/metabolic bone disease"],"dx":["Interpret three-phase scans for osteomyelitis vs cellulitis; recognize the FLARE phenomenon (transient increased uptake in healing/treated metastases - do not mistake for progression)","For infection where the bone scan is nonspecific (e.g., prosthesis, diabetic foot), add labeled-WBC scanning (In-111 or Tc-99m HMPAO WBC) +/- marrow imaging, or Ga-67 for spinal osteomyelitis","Correlate with radiographs/CT/MRI; false negatives can occur in aggressive lytic lesions (e.g., some myeloma) with little osteoblastic response"],"tx":["Recommend the appropriate agent for the question: three-phase bone scan (osteomyelitis, complex regional pain, stress fracture), labeled-WBC/marrow (prosthetic joint infection), Ga-67 (spinal infection)","Guide oncologic assessment (skeletal metastasis burden) and correlate flare with treatment response to avoid misreading it as progression","Recommend cross-sectional correlation (MRI) for suspected cord compression or when specificity is needed"],"pearls":["Three-phase bone scan: osteomyelitis is positive on all three phases (flow/blood-pool/delayed); cellulitis lacks focal delayed bone uptake","'Superscan' = diffuse intense skeletal uptake with faint/absent kidneys (extensive metastatic or metabolic bone disease)","The FLARE phenomenon (transiently increased uptake in healing metastases) can mimic progression - correlate clinically","For prosthesis/diabetic-foot infection add labeled-WBC (+/- marrow) imaging; Ga-67 helps in spinal osteomyelitis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bone Scan / Scintigraphy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559097/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Bone Scan","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-fdg-pet","name":"FDG PET/CT Oncologic Imaging","sec":"rads","present":["F-18 FDG (a glucose analog) PET/CT images GLUCOSE METABOLISM - most tumors are hypermetabolic - and is central to oncologic STAGING, restaging, treatment response, and detecting occult/recurrent disease","Uptake is semi-quantified by the STANDARDIZED UPTAKE VALUE (SUV); PET/CT fuses metabolic and anatomic data","Correct preparation (fasting, controlling blood glucose) is essential - hyperglycemia competes with FDG and reduces tumor uptake"],"dx":["Recognize PHYSIOLOGIC uptake (brain, myocardium, liver, GI, renal/urinary excretion) and BENIGN causes of uptake (infection/inflammation, granulomatous disease, brown fat, recent surgery/radiation) that cause FALSE POSITIVES","Know FALSE NEGATIVES: low-FDG-avid tumors (many well-differentiated/mucinous tumors, some prostate cancer, low-grade lymphoma, small lesions below resolution)","Use standardized response criteria (e.g., Deauville for lymphoma, PERCIST) and compare to prior scans; wait an appropriate interval after treatment (inflammation) before restaging"],"tx":["Recommend FDG PET/CT for staging/restaging of FDG-avid malignancies (lymphoma, many solid tumors), treatment response, and localizing recurrence (e.g., rising tumor markers)","Optimize preparation (fasting, glucose control, warm environment to reduce brown fat) and timing after therapy to reduce pitfalls","Recommend alternative tracers when FDG is limited (e.g., PSMA PET for prostate, DOTATATE for neuroendocrine tumors - see theranostics)"],"pearls":["FDG PET images glucose metabolism; control blood glucose/fasting - hyperglycemia reduces tumor uptake","False positives: infection/inflammation, granulomatous disease, brown fat, post-surgery/radiation; false negatives: low-grade/mucinous tumors, small lesions","Use Deauville (lymphoma)/PERCIST for response; allow time after treatment before restaging to avoid inflammatory uptake","Non-FDG-avid tumors (some prostate, neuroendocrine) need dedicated tracers (PSMA, DOTATATE)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: PET Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534789/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: PET/CT","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-cardiac-nuclear","name":"Cardiac Nuclear - Myocardial Perfusion & MUGA","sec":"rads","present":["MYOCARDIAL PERFUSION IMAGING (MPI) assesses coronary ischemia by comparing STRESS and REST perfusion of the myocardium using SPECT (Tc-99m sestamibi/tetrofosmin or Tl-201) or PET (rubidium-82, N-13 ammonia)","A REVERSIBLE defect (abnormal at stress, normal at rest) indicates ISCHEMIA (viable, at-risk myocardium); a FIXED defect (abnormal at both) indicates infarct/SCAR","MUGA (multigated acquisition, Tc-99m-labeled red cells) precisely and reproducibly measures LEFT-VENTRICULAR EJECTION FRACTION - classically for monitoring cardiotoxic chemotherapy"],"dx":["Interpret MPI: reversible (ischemia) vs fixed (scar) vs normal; assess the extent/severity and territory of the defect and post-stress function (transient ischemic dilation suggests severe/multivessel disease)","Recognize attenuation ARTIFACTS (breast, diaphragm) that mimic defects - use attenuation correction, prone imaging, and gating (a defect with preserved wall motion suggests artifact)","MUGA: quantify LVEF reproducibly; PET adds absolute myocardial blood flow/flow reserve and viability assessment"],"tx":["Use MPI results to guide management: reversible ischemia (especially large/multivessel) supports revascularization; fixed scar without ischemia does not benefit from revascularization for ischemia","Recommend viability assessment (PET FDG or Tl-201 redistribution) where hibernating myocardium may recover after revascularization","Use MUGA LVEF to guide cardiotoxic chemotherapy decisions (hold/adjust for a significant drop)"],"pearls":["Reversible perfusion defect = ischemia (viable at-risk myocardium); fixed defect = infarct/scar","Transient ischemic dilation on MPI suggests severe/multivessel disease","Breast/diaphragm attenuation artifacts mimic defects - gating (preserved wall motion) and attenuation correction help distinguish them","MUGA gives a reproducible LVEF - classically to monitor cardiotoxic chemotherapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Myocardial Perfusion Imaging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556130/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Cardiac Nuclear Medicine","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-thyroid-nuclear","name":"Thyroid & Parathyroid Imaging + Radioiodine Therapy","sec":"rads","present":["Thyroid SCINTIGRAPHY (I-123 or Tc-99m pertechnetate) evaluates nodule function and the cause of HYPERTHYROIDISM by uptake pattern; RADIOACTIVE IODINE UPTAKE (RAIU) quantifies gland avidity","Hyperthyroidism patterns: DIFFUSE increased uptake (Graves disease), a single HOT nodule (toxic adenoma), multiple hot/cold areas (toxic multinodular goiter), and LOW uptake (thyroiditis, exogenous hormone, iodine load)","PARATHYROID imaging (Tc-99m SESTAMIBI +/- SPECT/CT) localizes an adenoma before surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism"],"dx":["Characterize nodules by function: 'COLD' (nonfunctioning) nodules carry a higher malignancy risk and warrant biopsy; 'HOT' (autonomously functioning) nodules are almost always benign","Determine the cause of hyperthyroidism from uptake (high/diffuse = Graves; focal hot = toxic adenoma; low = thyroiditis) to guide therapy","Parathyroid: sestamibi (retained in adenomas) with SPECT/CT (or 4D-CT) localizes the culprit gland for minimally invasive parathyroidectomy"],"tx":["RADIOIODINE (I-131) THERAPY: definitive treatment for hyperthyroidism (Graves/toxic nodular disease) and for thyroid-remnant ablation/metastatic differentiated thyroid cancer (see radonc); apply pregnancy exclusion and radiation-safety/precaution counseling","Recommend FNA for cold/suspicious nodules; radioiodine is ineffective in low-uptake thyroiditis","Localize parathyroid adenoma preoperatively (sestamibi/4D-CT) to enable focused surgery"],"pearls":["'Cold' thyroid nodules have higher malignancy risk (biopsy); 'hot' nodules are almost always benign","Hyperthyroid uptake patterns: diffuse (Graves), focal hot (toxic adenoma), LOW (thyroiditis/exogenous/iodine load) - low uptake will not respond to radioiodine","I-131 treats hyperthyroidism and ablates/treats differentiated thyroid cancer - with strict pregnancy exclusion and radiation precautions","Tc-99m sestamibi (+ SPECT/CT) localizes a parathyroid adenoma before minimally invasive surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thyroid Scintigraphy / Radioiodine","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537327/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Thyroid Nuclear Medicine","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-vq-scan","name":"V/Q Scanning & Pulmonary Nuclear Medicine","sec":"rads","present":["The VENTILATION/PERFUSION (V/Q) SCAN evaluates suspected pulmonary embolism by comparing lung ventilation (inhaled agent) with perfusion (Tc-99m MAA injected); PE causes PERFUSION DEFECTS in NORMALLY VENTILATED lung ('MISMATCH')","V/Q is a key alternative to CT pulmonary angiography when contrast/radiation is a concern - notably in PREGNANCY, renal impairment, or contrast allergy","Results are reported by probability (PIOPED criteria: normal, low, intermediate, high probability)"],"dx":["Interpret V/Q: multiple segmental MISMATCHED defects (perfusion absent, ventilation intact) = high probability for PE; MATCHED defects (both abnormal) suggest parenchymal disease (e.g., pneumonia, COPD)","A NORMAL perfusion scan effectively excludes PE; intermediate/indeterminate scans require further evaluation (often clinical probability plus other tests)","Prefer V/Q in patients with a normal chest radiograph and contraindications to CTPA (best test characteristics in that setting)"],"tx":["Recommend V/Q over CTPA when avoiding iodinated contrast/limiting dose matters (pregnancy - lower fetal/breast dose; renal failure; contrast allergy)","Integrate V/Q probability with clinical pretest probability to reach a management decision (anticoagulation)","Recognize that a normal perfusion study reliably excludes PE, avoiding unnecessary anticoagulation"],"pearls":["PE on V/Q = perfusion defect with preserved ventilation (MISMATCH); matched defects suggest parenchymal lung disease","A normal perfusion scan effectively excludes PE","V/Q is preferred over CTPA in pregnancy, renal impairment, and contrast allergy (especially with a normal chest radiograph)","Report by PIOPED probability (normal/low/intermediate/high) and combine with clinical pretest probability"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ventilation-Perfusion Scan","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470291/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Lung VQ Scan","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-renal-hida-meckel","name":"Renal, Hepatobiliary & GI Scintigraphy","sec":"rads","present":["Functional nuclear studies of the abdomen answer physiologic questions imaging cannot: RENAL scans assess function/drainage, HEPATOBILIARY (HIDA) assesses the gallbladder/bile flow, and dedicated scans localize GI bleeding and ectopic gastric mucosa","RENAL: Tc-99m MAG3 (dynamic - split function, obstruction, transplant evaluation) and Tc-99m DMSA (cortical - scarring/pyelonephritis); a LASIX (diuretic) renogram distinguishes obstruction from a dilated non-obstructed system","HIDA (cholescintigraphy): NONVISUALIZATION of the gallbladder is the hallmark of ACUTE (cystic-duct-obstruction) CHOLECYSTITIS"],"dx":["Renal: MAG3 for obstruction (diuretic renogram - persistent retention = true obstruction) and split function; DMSA for cortical scars/pyelonephritis; captopril renography for renovascular hypertension","HIDA: nonvisualization of the gallbladder at delayed/post-morphine imaging = acute cholecystitis; HIDA also detects BILE LEAKS and biliary atresia (in neonates)","GI: a Tc-99m-RBC bleeding scan localizes active lower-GI bleeding (more sensitive than angiography for slow bleeds); a MECKEL scan (Tc-99m pertechnetate) detects ectopic GASTRIC mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum"],"tx":["Recommend the diuretic (Lasix) renogram to separate obstruction from a baggy non-obstructed collecting system, guiding urologic intervention","Use HIDA when ultrasound is equivocal for acute cholecystitis, or to find a suspected bile leak; use the bleeding scan to localize before angiography/endoscopy","Meckel scan in a child with painless lower-GI bleeding (ectopic gastric mucosa)"],"pearls":["HIDA: nonvisualization of the gallbladder = acute cholecystitis (cystic duct obstruction); HIDA also finds bile leaks","MAG3 diuretic (Lasix) renogram distinguishes true obstruction (persistent retention) from a dilated non-obstructed system; DMSA shows cortical scars","Tc-99m-RBC bleeding scan localizes slow/intermittent lower-GI bleeding better than angiography","Meckel scan (Tc-99m pertechnetate) detects ectopic gastric mucosa - painless GI bleeding in a child"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hepatobiliary (HIDA) Scan","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539781/"},{"t":"RadiologyInfo: Renal & Hepatobiliary Nuclear Medicine","u":"https://www.radiologyinfo.org"}]},{"id":"rads-theranostics","name":"Theranostics & Radionuclide Therapy","sec":"rads","present":["THERANOSTICS pairs a diagnostic tracer with a therapeutic radionuclide against the SAME molecular target ('see it, treat it') - imaging confirms target expression, then a therapy radiopharmaceutical delivers targeted radiation","Prostate cancer: Ga-68/F-18 PSMA PET images disease, and Lu-177 PSMA-617 TREATS PSMA-positive metastatic disease (VISION); neuroendocrine tumors: Ga-68 DOTATATE PET images, and Lu-177 DOTATATE treats somatostatin-receptor-positive disease (NETTER-1)","Other radionuclide therapies: RADIUM-223 (bone-metastatic prostate cancer), I-131 (thyroid), I-131 MIBG (neuroblastoma/pheochromocytoma), and Y-90 (liver radioembolization) - overlapping with radiation oncology's radiopharmaceuticals"],"dx":["Confirm target EXPRESSION with the companion PET tracer before therapy (PSMA PET for prostate; DOTATATE PET for neuroendocrine tumors; iodine avidity for thyroid; MIBG avidity for neuroblastoma/pheochromocytoma)","Assess marrow, renal, and hepatic reserve and disease distribution to select and dose therapy; PET tracers also include F-18 amyloid/tau (dementia) and F-18 FDG (metabolism)","Plan dosimetry and radiation-safety precautions specific to each therapeutic isotope"],"tx":["Deliver TARGETED radionuclide therapy: Lu-177 PSMA-617 (metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, VISION), Lu-177 DOTATATE (SSTR-positive neuroendocrine tumors, NETTER-1), radium-223 (bone-metastatic prostate cancer, ALSYMPCA), I-131 (thyroid), Y-90 (liver)","Apply radiation-safety/excretion precautions and monitor for marrow/renal toxicity; coordinate with radiation oncology/nuclear medicine (cross-reference the radonc radiopharmaceuticals entry)","Use the companion diagnostic to select patients and to monitor response"],"pearls":["Theranostics = matched diagnostic + therapeutic radiopharmaceutical to one target: PSMA (prostate) and DOTATATE (neuroendocrine) are the prototypes","Confirm target expression with the companion PET tracer BEFORE giving therapy (Lu-177 PSMA after PSMA PET; Lu-177 DOTATATE after DOTATATE PET)","Radium-223 (bone-metastatic prostate), I-131 (thyroid), I-131 MIBG (neuroblastoma/pheochromocytoma), Y-90 (liver) are established radionuclide therapies","Theranostic therapy overlaps radiation oncology's radiopharmaceuticals - coordinate care and radiation-safety precautions"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Theranostics / Radionuclide Therapy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK580521/"},{"t":"NCI: Radiopharmaceuticals","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/radiation-therapy"}]},{"id":"thoracic-anatomy-access","name":"Thoracic Surgical Anatomy, Incisions & Access","sec":"thoracic","present":["Thoracic operations are defined by their ACCESS: posterolateral THORACOTOMY (classic lung access), median STERNOTOMY (cardiac and anterior mediastinum), and minimally invasive VATS/RATS (video/robot-assisted) ports","Key anatomy: the intercostal neurovascular bundle runs along the INFERIOR margin of each rib (enter over the top of the rib below to avoid it); the thoracic duct, azygos vein, phrenic and recurrent laryngeal nerves, and vagus are at-risk structures","Approach selection balances exposure, morbidity, and the operation planned"],"dx":["Match approach to target: posterolateral thoracotomy (lung/esophagus), sternotomy (heart/great vessels/anterior mediastinum/bilateral lung), clamshell (bilateral), VATS/RATS (most lung resections, mediastinal, pleural)","Plan single-lung isolation and positioning (lateral decubitus for thoracotomy/VATS; supine for sternotomy)","Identify structures to protect (phrenic, recurrent laryngeal, vagus, thoracic duct, long thoracic nerve)"],"tx":["Choose the least invasive approach that achieves the operative goal - VATS/RATS reduce pain, length of stay, and pulmonary complications versus open thoracotomy for appropriate resections","Reserve open thoracotomy for large/central tumors, dense adhesions, chest wall involvement, or when minimally invasive fails","Use sternotomy for cardiac/great-vessel and anterior mediastinal access"],"tech":["Posterolateral thoracotomy: lateral decubitus, incision below the tip of the scapula, divide latissimus (spare serratus when possible), enter the pleura over the top of a rib to spare the intercostal bundle, place a rib spreader","Median sternotomy: midline incision, divide the sternum with a saw in the midline, retract; VATS/RATS: place camera and working ports through intercostal spaces (over the rib), CO2 insufflation optional, avoid the neurovascular bundle","Close thoracotomy with pericostal sutures (avoid entrapping the nerve); close sternotomy with wires"],"after":["Multimodal analgesia (epidural/paravertebral/intercostal block) is central after thoracotomy to enable deep breathing and prevent atelectasis/pneumonia","Chest tube(s) to drainage; early mobilization and pulmonary toilet; monitor for air leak and bleeding","Intercostal nerve injury causes post-thoracotomy pain/neuralgia - minimized by nerve-sparing closure and MIS approaches"],"pearls":["The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs along the INFERIOR rib margin - enter over the TOP of the rib below to avoid it","VATS/RATS reduce pain, complications, and length of stay vs open thoracotomy for suitable resections","Sternotomy accesses the heart, great vessels, and anterior mediastinum; thoracotomy accesses lung/esophagus","Protect the phrenic, recurrent laryngeal, and vagus nerves and the thoracic duct during dissection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555919/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Median Sternotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537018/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-preop-pulmonary-eval","name":"Preoperative Pulmonary Evaluation & Risk","sec":"thoracic","present":["Before lung resection, physiologic assessment predicts whether the patient will tolerate loss of functional lung - the central question in operability","The workhorse tests are SPIROMETRY (FEV1) and DIFFUSING CAPACITY (DLCO), converted to PREDICTED POSTOPERATIVE (ppo) values based on the number of segments to be removed","Cardiac risk assessment and, when needed, exercise testing (VO2 max) refine the estimate"],"dx":["Calculate ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO (% predicted) using the segment method (19 total segments); ppo values <~40% predicted flag high risk and prompt further testing","When FEV1/DLCO are borderline, obtain CARDIOPULMONARY EXERCISE TESTING - VO2 max >~20 mL/kg/min supports resection (even pneumonectomy), while <~10-15 mL/kg/min indicates high risk","Assess cardiac risk (revised cardiac risk index, functional status), pulmonary hypertension, and smoking/COPD status; quantitative perfusion scans help estimate split function"],"tx":["Optimize before surgery: SMOKING CESSATION, treat COPD/infection, pulmonary rehabilitation, and manage cardiac risk","Match resection extent to reserve (a marginal patient may tolerate a sublobar resection but not a pneumonectomy)","Consider non-surgical options (SBRT - see radonc) when physiologic risk is prohibitive"],"tech":["Use the segment-counting method for ppo calculations (subtract functional segments removed); incorporate quantitative perfusion when perfusion is heterogeneous","Stage exercise testing after borderline PFTs to avoid denying resection to fit patients","Document a stepwise algorithm (PFTs -> ppo values -> exercise testing) for operability"],"after":["Anticipate higher pulmonary complication risk in low-reserve patients: aggressive pulmonary toilet, early mobilization, and analgesia","Monitor for respiratory failure and right-heart strain after major resection/pneumonectomy","Reassess function and rehabilitate postoperatively"],"pearls":["ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO drive operability - values <~40% predicted flag high risk and prompt exercise testing","VO2 max >~20 mL/kg/min supports major resection; <~10-15 is high risk","DLCO is independently predictive - never rely on FEV1 alone","Smoking cessation and prehabilitation measurably reduce postoperative pulmonary complications"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Preoperative Pulmonary Evaluation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546636/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lung-isolation","name":"Lung Isolation & One-Lung Ventilation","sec":"thoracic","present":["Most thoracic operations require LUNG ISOLATION so the operative lung deflates (giving exposure) while the dependent lung is ventilated - ONE-LUNG VENTILATION (OLV)","Isolation is achieved with a DOUBLE-LUMEN ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE (DLT) or a BRONCHIAL BLOCKER; correct positioning is confirmed by fiberoptic bronchoscopy","OLV in the lateral decubitus position creates predictable ventilation-perfusion changes and hypoxemia risk"],"dx":["Choose DLT (fast lung collapse, ability to suction/CPAP each lung; left-sided DLT is default) vs bronchial blocker (useful for difficult airway, existing single-lumen tube, or selective lobar isolation)","Confirm placement with bronchoscopy (avoid malposition - too deep/shallow causes failed isolation or airway obstruction); reconfirm after repositioning to lateral decubitus","Anticipate hypoxemia during OLV (shunt through the non-ventilated lung)"],"tx":["Manage OLV hypoxemia stepwise: increase FiO2, verify tube position, apply CPAP to the non-dependent (operative) lung and/or PEEP to the dependent lung, and, if needed, intermittently reinflate or clamp the operative pulmonary artery","Use lung-protective ventilation on the dependent lung (lower tidal volumes, PEEP) to reduce acute lung injury","Communicate with the surgeon (operative-lung CPAP interferes with exposure - coordinate)"],"tech":["Place a left-sided DLT and confirm the bronchial cuff position below the carina in the left mainstem with bronchoscopy; for a right DLT, align the Murphy eye with the right upper lobe orifice","Bronchial blocker: pass through/alongside a single-lumen tube and inflate in the target mainstem under bronchoscopic guidance","Recheck position after turning the patient lateral (tubes migrate)"],"after":["Return to two-lung ventilation and re-expand the operative lung under direct vision before closure (check for air leaks)","Watch for post-OLV lung injury/edema (especially after pneumonectomy - keep fluids judicious)","Ensure adequate re-expansion and oxygenation before extubation"],"pearls":["Left-sided double-lumen tube is the default; confirm ALL tube/blocker positions with fiberoptic bronchoscopy (and recheck after lateral positioning)","OLV hypoxemia: increase FiO2, verify position, then CPAP to the operative lung / PEEP to the dependent lung","Bronchial blockers suit difficult airways, in-situ single-lumen tubes, and selective lobar isolation","Use lung-protective ventilation and judicious fluids to prevent post-resection lung injury"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: One-Lung Ventilation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534198/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Double-Lumen Endotracheal Tube","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538318/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-chest-tubes","name":"Chest Tubes & Pleural Drainage","sec":"thoracic","present":["Chest tubes (thoracostomy) evacuate air (pneumothorax) and fluid (effusion, hemothorax, chyle, empyema) and re-expand the lung; they are ubiquitous after thoracic surgery and in chest trauma/critical care","A standard drainage system has three chambers: COLLECTION, WATER SEAL (one-way valve; bubbling here = AIR LEAK), and SUCTION control","Management questions - suction vs water seal, when to remove - are daily decisions after thoracic surgery"],"dx":["Assess the system: continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber indicates an ONGOING AIR LEAK (or a system leak); tidaling (fluctuation with respiration) confirms patency and pleural communication","Quantify and characterize output (serosanguineous vs frank blood vs chylous vs purulent); a large sudden bloody output signals hemorrhage","Image (chest radiograph) to confirm tube position and lung re-expansion"],"tx":["Set to SUCTION or WATER SEAL per situation: water seal often promotes air-leak resolution and is used before removal; large air leaks/incomplete expansion may need suction","Remove the tube when the lung is expanded, there is NO air leak, and drainage is acceptably low/non-chylous/non-infected (thresholds vary by institution)","Troubleshoot: a persistent air leak may be from the patient (parenchyma) or the system (loose connection) - check the whole circuit"],"tech":["Insert in the 'safe triangle' (anterior to latissimus, lateral to pectoralis, above the 5th intercostal space/nipple level), over the top of the rib, with blunt dissection into the pleura","Direct posteriorly/apically for air and basally for fluid; secure and connect to the drainage system; confirm position radiographically","Remove at end-expiration or Valsalva with a prompt occlusive dressing"],"after":["Watch for re-expansion pulmonary edema after rapid drainage of a large effusion/pneumothorax; monitor output and air leak trends","Recurrent pneumothorax after removal or a persistent large air leak may require re-intervention (pleurodesis/surgery)","Provide analgesia and encourage deep breathing to aid re-expansion"],"pearls":["Water-seal chamber bubbling = air leak; tidaling = patent tube communicating with the pleural space","Insert in the 'safe triangle' over the top of the rib to avoid the intercostal bundle and abdominal organs","Remove when the lung is up, there is no air leak, and output is acceptable; water seal often speeds air-leak resolution","Rapid drainage of a large effusion can cause re-expansion pulmonary edema"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chest Tube (Thoracostomy)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459199/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pleural Drainage","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555899/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-postthoracotomy-care","name":"Post-Thoracotomy Care & Complications","sec":"thoracic","present":["The post-thoracotomy/lung-resection course centers on PAIN CONTROL, pulmonary toilet, and vigilance for characteristic complications","Common complications: PROLONGED AIR LEAK (>5-7 days, the most frequent), atelectasis/pneumonia, ATRIAL FIBRILLATION (very common after thoracic surgery), and, less commonly, bronchopleural fistula and post-pneumonectomy complications","Effective analgesia is not just comfort - it enables coughing/deep breathing that prevents pulmonary complications"],"dx":["Monitor air leak (water-seal bubbling), oxygenation, and chest imaging for expansion/effusion; new fever/purulent secretions suggest pneumonia","Detect atrial fibrillation (telemetry) and evaluate for BRONCHOPLEURAL FISTULA (sudden large air leak, new air-fluid level, expectoration of fluid - especially post-pneumonectomy) and EMPYEMA","Watch for post-pneumonectomy pulmonary edema and cardiac herniation (rare, catastrophic)"],"tx":["Optimize MULTIMODAL ANALGESIA (thoracic epidural or paravertebral block plus non-opioids) to enable pulmonary toilet; treat atelectasis with mobilization, incentive spirometry, and secretion clearance","Manage prolonged air leak (water seal, portable drainage, Heimlich valve, pleurodesis, or reoperation if persistent); treat pneumonia with antibiotics","Manage post-op atrial fibrillation (rate/rhythm control, anticoagulation risk-benefit); treat bronchopleural fistula/empyema urgently (drainage, antibiotics, surgery)"],"tech":["Place thoracic epidural/paravertebral catheters for analgesia; use lung-protective strategies and judicious fluids (especially after pneumonectomy)","For prolonged air leak, transition to water seal and portable/ambulatory drainage; escalate to pleurodesis/surgery if it persists","Early ambulation and chest physiotherapy protocols"],"after":["Bronchopleural fistula after pneumonectomy is a surgical emergency (aspiration risk) - position operative side DOWN, secure the airway, drain the space, and repair","Prevent/treat atrial fibrillation and monitor for empyema; ensure adequate nutrition and mobilization","Surveillance for oncologic patients after resection"],"pearls":["Prolonged air leak is the most common complication after lung resection; atrial fibrillation is very common - anticipate both","Bronchopleural fistula (sudden air leak, expectorated fluid, new air-fluid level) post-pneumonectomy is an emergency - operative side DOWN, secure airway, drain","Multimodal analgesia (epidural/paravertebral) enables the pulmonary toilet that prevents pneumonia","Keep fluids judicious after pneumonectomy to avoid post-pneumonectomy pulmonary edema"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Postoperative Thoracic Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK540969/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bronchopleural Fistula","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430893/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-mis-eras","name":"Minimally Invasive Thoracic Surgery & Enhanced Recovery","sec":"thoracic","present":["VIDEO-ASSISTED (VATS) and ROBOTIC-ASSISTED (RATS) thoracoscopic surgery accomplish most lung resections, mediastinal, and pleural procedures through small incisions without rib spreading, reducing pain and pulmonary complications","ENHANCED RECOVERY AFTER SURGERY (ERAS) pathways bundle prehabilitation, minimally invasive access, opioid-sparing analgesia, early mobilization, and early chest-tube removal to shorten recovery","These approaches have become the default for anatomic lung resection in suitable patients"],"dx":["Select MIS candidates: most early-stage lung cancers and benign disease; relative limits include large/central tumors, chest-wall invasion, dense adhesions, and hilar nodal disease requiring open control","Assess for conversion risk and plan analgesia (paravertebral blocks often replace epidural in MIS)","Confirm the physiologic and oncologic appropriateness of a minimally invasive resection"],"tx":["Prefer VATS/RATS for anatomic resection when feasible - lower morbidity and equivalent oncologic outcomes for early-stage disease; convert to open promptly for safety when needed","Apply ERAS elements: minimize drains/lines, opioid-sparing multimodal analgesia, early feeding and ambulation, and early chest-tube removal","Reserve open thoracotomy for cases where exposure/control demands it"],"tech":["VATS: camera port plus 1-3 working ports; anatomic dissection of hilar structures with staplers; RATS adds wristed instruments and 3D vision for fine hilar/nodal dissection","Perform systematic nodal dissection/sampling as in open surgery; retrieve the specimen in a bag to avoid port-site seeding","Have a low threshold to convert for bleeding or unclear anatomy"],"after":["Early chest-tube removal, mobilization, and multimodal analgesia drive faster discharge; paravertebral blocks manage MIS pain well","Monitor for the same complications as open surgery (air leak, arrhythmia) but expect fewer pulmonary complications","Enroll in ERAS pathways with defined discharge criteria"],"pearls":["VATS/RATS give equivalent oncologic outcomes with less pain and fewer pulmonary complications than open thoracotomy for early-stage lung cancer","ERAS bundles (MIS access, opioid-sparing analgesia, early mobilization/chest-tube removal) shorten recovery","Convert to open promptly and without stigma for bleeding, unclear anatomy, or oncologic safety","Systematic nodal dissection is still required in minimally invasive resections"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Video-Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564388/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lung-cancer-staging","name":"Lung Cancer Staging & Invasive Mediastinal Staging","sec":"thoracic","present":["Accurate staging drives lung cancer treatment; the TNM system defines T (size/invasion), N (nodal), and M (metastasis), and NODAL status is pivotal for surgery vs multimodality therapy","Noninvasive staging (contrast CT, PET/CT, brain MRI) is complemented by INVASIVE MEDIASTINAL STAGING when nodal involvement must be confirmed - because PET/CT can be falsely positive/negative","EBUS-TBNA (endobronchial ultrasound) is the first-line invasive nodal test; mediastinoscopy remains a standard for confirmation"],"dx":["Stage with PET/CT + brain MRI; sample mediastinal nodes when enlarged/PET-avid, for central tumors, or for larger tumors, since N2/N3 disease changes management","EBUS-TBNA samples paratracheal, subcarinal, and hilar stations (a negative EBUS in high-suspicion cases may need confirmatory mediastinoscopy); mediastinoscopy accesses stations 2/4/7","Confirm histology and molecular/PD-L1 markers; assess resectability by T and N"],"tx":["Resectable N0/N1 disease -> surgery (often with adjuvant therapy); confirmed N2 (mediastinal) disease generally -> multimodality therapy (chemoradiation +/- surgery in selected cases) rather than upfront resection","N3 (contralateral/supraclavicular) disease is not surgically resectable","Use staging to select candidates for neoadjuvant therapy (increasingly chemo-immunotherapy)"],"tech":["EBUS-TBNA: bronchoscopic ultrasound-guided needle aspiration of mediastinal/hilar nodes station by station (systematic sampling, contralateral first for staging)","Mediastinoscopy: small suprasternal incision, pretracheal dissection to biopsy paratracheal and subcarinal nodes; know the aberrant anatomy (innominate artery, azygos)","Combine modalities for a complete nodal map when needed"],"after":["EBUS is outpatient with minimal recovery; mediastinoscopy risks (rare) include bleeding, recurrent laryngeal nerve injury, and airway/esophageal injury","Use the confirmed stage to route to surgery vs multimodality therapy in a multidisciplinary tumor board","Restage after neoadjuvant therapy when planning resection"],"pearls":["Nodal (N) status is the key surgical determinant - confirmed N2 usually means multimodality therapy, N3 is unresectable","EBUS-TBNA is first-line invasive mediastinal staging; mediastinoscopy confirms and samples stations 2/4/7","PET/CT has false positives (infection/inflammation) and false negatives - tissue-confirm suspicious nodes","Always obtain brain MRI in staging (occult brain metastases) and molecular/PD-L1 testing"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Cancer Staging","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558925/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lobectomy","name":"Anatomic Lobectomy","sec":"thoracic","present":["LOBECTOMY with systematic mediastinal nodal dissection is the standard operation for early-stage NSCLC with adequate pulmonary reserve - removing the tumor-bearing lobe along its anatomic vascular and bronchial pedicles","It is increasingly done by VATS/RATS; the oncologic principles (anatomic resection + nodal dissection) are identical regardless of approach","Lobar anatomy (arterial branches, veins, bronchus, and fissures) must be understood to resect safely"],"dx":["Confirm resectable early-stage disease with adequate ppoFEV1/DLCO; localize the tumor to a lobe and plan the vascular/bronchial anatomy","Rule out N2/N3 disease (invasive staging as needed); confirm the patient can tolerate lobectomy","Plan approach (VATS/RATS vs open) by tumor size/location and surgeon judgment"],"tx":["Perform anatomic LOBECTOMY (division of the lobar artery, vein, and bronchus) with systematic mediastinal lymph node dissection/sampling for accurate staging and local control","Adjuvant chemotherapy/immunotherapy per final pathologic stage and biomarkers","Consider sublobar resection for very small peripheral tumors or limited reserve (see next entry)"],"tech":["Individually dissect, ligate, and divide the lobar PULMONARY ARTERY branches, the pulmonary VEIN, and the lobar BRONCHUS (staplers); complete the fissure with staplers to avoid air leaks","Perform systematic nodal dissection (hilar and mediastinal stations); test the bronchial staple line underwater for air leak","Ensure the remaining lobes re-expand to fill the hemithorax"],"after":["Chest tube management for air leak/fluid; multimodal analgesia and pulmonary toilet; monitor for atrial fibrillation and prolonged air leak","Adjuvant therapy per pathology; oncologic surveillance imaging","Early mobilization within an ERAS pathway"],"pearls":["Lobectomy + systematic nodal dissection is the standard for fit patients with early-stage NSCLC","Divide the artery, vein, and bronchus anatomically; complete fissures with staplers to limit air leaks","VATS/RATS lobectomy gives equivalent oncologic outcomes with less morbidity than open","Nodal dissection is required for accurate staging even when nodes look normal"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lobectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555957/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-sublobar-resection","name":"Sublobar Resection - Segmentectomy & Wedge","sec":"thoracic","present":["SUBLOBAR resection (anatomic SEGMENTECTOMY or non-anatomic WEDGE) removes less lung than lobectomy - used for small peripheral early-stage NSCLC and for patients with limited pulmonary reserve or metastasectomy","Recent trials (JCOG0802, CALGB 140503) established that for SMALL (<=2 cm) peripheral node-negative NSCLC, sublobar resection is NON-INFERIOR to lobectomy - a practice-changing shift","Wedge is non-anatomic (for diagnosis, metastasectomy, or poor reserve); segmentectomy is anatomic with nodal assessment"],"dx":["Select candidates: small (<=2 cm) peripheral tumors, node-negative (confirm N0), or compromised pulmonary reserve; segmentectomy preferred over wedge for primary lung cancer (better margins/nodal assessment)","Ensure adequate margins (margin >= tumor size, or >=2 cm) to limit local recurrence","Confirm nodal status intraoperatively (sample hilar/mediastinal nodes)"],"tx":["ANATOMIC SEGMENTECTOMY for small peripheral NSCLC (non-inferior to lobectomy per JCOG0802/CALGB 140503) with nodal sampling; WEDGE for metastasectomy, diagnosis, or very limited reserve","Preserve lung function while achieving adequate margins and staging","Adjuvant therapy per final pathology"],"tech":["Segmentectomy: identify and divide the segmental artery, vein, and bronchus; define the intersegmental plane (inflation-deflation line or indocyanine-green fluorescence) and divide with staplers","Wedge: staple across the parenchyma to excise the lesion with a margin (no named vascular/bronchial division)","Sample nodes; ensure margin adequacy (frozen section if needed)"],"after":["Preserved pulmonary function aids recovery; standard chest-tube and air-leak management","Closer surveillance for local recurrence given smaller margins; adjuvant therapy per pathology","Monitor for the same complications as lobectomy"],"pearls":["JCOG0802/CALGB 140503: sublobar (segmentectomy) is non-inferior to lobectomy for <=2 cm peripheral node-negative NSCLC","Segmentectomy (anatomic, better margins/nodal assessment) is preferred over wedge for primary lung cancer","Ensure a margin >= tumor diameter (or >=2 cm) to reduce local recurrence","Wedge resection is best for metastasectomy, diagnosis, or severely limited pulmonary reserve"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Segmentectomy / Wedge Resection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560916/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-pneumonectomy-sleeve","name":"Pneumonectomy & Sleeve Resection","sec":"thoracic","present":["PNEUMONECTOMY (removal of an entire lung) is reserved for central tumors not resectable by lesser resection; it carries higher morbidity/mortality and demands careful physiologic selection","SLEEVE resection (bronchial and/or vascular sleeve lobectomy) removes a bronchial/arterial segment and reanastomoses, PRESERVING lung parenchyma - a lung-sparing alternative that avoids pneumonectomy when feasible","Right pneumonectomy carries higher risk than left (larger vascular bed, bronchopleural fistula risk)"],"dx":["Confirm that a central tumor cannot be managed by sleeve/lobar resection before committing to pneumonectomy; verify adequate reserve for whole-lung loss (VO2 max, ppo values)","Assess bronchial/arterial involvement to plan a sleeve; rule out N2/N3 disease","Weigh sleeve lobectomy (preferred when oncologically adequate) over pneumonectomy"],"tx":["Prefer SLEEVE resection over pneumonectomy when it achieves negative margins - it preserves function with equivalent oncologic outcomes and lower morbidity","Pneumonectomy for central tumors requiring whole-lung removal; buttress/cover the bronchial stump to reduce bronchopleural fistula","Adjuvant therapy per stage"],"tech":["Sleeve lobectomy: resect the involved bronchial (and/or arterial) segment and perform an end-to-end anastomosis, covering it with vascularized tissue (intercostal muscle/pericardial fat)","Pneumonectomy: divide the main pulmonary artery, both pulmonary veins, and the mainstem bronchus; cut the bronchus short and cover the stump with a vascularized flap","Test anastomoses/stump for air leak; avoid excessive fluid administration"],"after":["Manage the post-pneumonectomy space (avoid overfilling with fluid - post-pneumonectomy pulmonary edema is often fatal); monitor for bronchopleural fistula and cardiac herniation","Sleeve anastomosis surveillance (bronchoscopy) for stenosis/dehiscence; standard pulmonary toilet","Judicious fluids and close respiratory monitoring after pneumonectomy"],"pearls":["Prefer sleeve resection over pneumonectomy when margins allow - it preserves lung function with equivalent oncology and lower morbidity","Right pneumonectomy carries higher mortality and bronchopleural fistula risk than left - buttress the bronchial stump","Post-pneumonectomy pulmonary edema (from fluid overload/injury) is highly lethal - keep fluids judicious","Cover bronchial stumps/anastomoses with vascularized tissue to prevent fistula"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pneumonectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555920/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-locally-advanced-pancoast","name":"Locally Advanced NSCLC & Superior Sulcus (Pancoast) Tumors","sec":"thoracic","present":["Locally advanced NSCLC (T3-4 or N2) often requires MULTIMODALITY therapy; the surgeon's role is defined after induction therapy and precise staging","SUPERIOR SULCUS (PANCOAST) tumors arise at the lung apex and invade the chest wall/brachial plexus/subclavian vessels, causing shoulder/arm pain, Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis), and hand muscle wasting","These apical tumors are managed with induction chemoradiation then en-bloc resection in selected patients"],"dx":["Fully stage (PET/CT, brain MRI, invasive nodal staging); MRI of the thoracic inlet defines brachial plexus/vessel/vertebral involvement for Pancoast tumors","Confirm N status - N2 disease and vascular/vertebral extent determine resectability after induction","Assess resectability and physiologic fitness for often-extensive resections"],"tx":["PANCOAST: INDUCTION CHEMORADIATION followed by en-bloc resection (lung + chest wall +/- structures) in appropriately selected patients (Rusch/SWOG approach) improves outcomes","Locally advanced N2 disease: multidisciplinary multimodality therapy; surgery in selected responders (neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy is increasingly used)","Unresectable disease: definitive chemoradiation + consolidation immunotherapy (see radonc)"],"tech":["Superior sulcus resection: anterior (Dartevelle) or posterior (Shaw-Paulson) approach to access the thoracic inlet; en-bloc resection of the involved chest wall, apical lung, and involved structures, protecting/repairing the subclavian vessels and assessing the plexus","Reconstruct the chest wall as needed; achieve negative margins","Combine with lobectomy and nodal dissection"],"after":["Monitor for the specific morbidity of apical/chest-wall resection (neurologic deficits, vascular issues, CSF leak if vertebral); intensive analgesia and pulmonary care","Adjuvant therapy per protocol; rehabilitation for shoulder/arm function","Surveillance imaging"],"pearls":["Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumors present with shoulder/arm pain, Horner syndrome, and hand muscle wasting from apical invasion","Standard Pancoast management: induction chemoradiation then en-bloc resection in selected patients (SWOG/Rusch)","MRI of the thoracic inlet defines brachial plexus/subclavian/vertebral involvement and resectability","Neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy is increasingly used for resectable locally advanced NSCLC"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pancoast Tumor","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470249/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-neuroendocrine-carcinoid","name":"Neuroendocrine Lung Tumors & Carcinoid Resection","sec":"thoracic","present":["Pulmonary neuroendocrine tumors span a spectrum: TYPICAL CARCINOID (low grade, indolent), ATYPICAL CARCINOID (intermediate), and the high-grade large-cell neuroendocrine carcinoma and small cell lung cancer","Carcinoids are often CENTRAL/endobronchial (causing recurrent obstruction/pneumonia, hemoptysis, wheeze) and are typically resected; carcinoid syndrome is uncommon in bronchial carcinoids","Surgery is the mainstay for localized typical/atypical carcinoid"],"dx":["Diagnose by bronchoscopy/biopsy (vascular - beware bleeding) and imaging; DOTATATE PET (somatostatin-receptor) images well-differentiated carcinoids; distinguish typical from atypical (mitoses/necrosis)","Stage and assess nodal status; carcinoids can metastasize (atypical more so)","Assess central airway involvement for resection planning"],"tx":["Surgical RESECTION (anatomic, lung-sparing when possible - sleeve/segmentectomy for central carcinoid) with nodal dissection is the treatment of choice for localized typical/atypical carcinoid","Preserve parenchyma via bronchoplastic/sleeve techniques for central tumors; systematic nodal assessment","High-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas (small cell/large-cell) are treated more like small cell lung cancer (chemo-based, see radonc)"],"tech":["Sleeve/bronchoplastic resection for central endobronchial carcinoid to spare distal lung; anatomic resection with nodal dissection for the rest","Handle vascular tumors gently (bleeding risk) and obtain clear margins","Bronchoscopic debulking may temporize obstruction before definitive resection"],"after":["Excellent prognosis for typical carcinoid after complete resection; surveillance for atypical carcinoid (higher recurrence)","Standard post-resection care; monitor for recurrence with imaging (and DOTATATE PET when indicated)","Manage any carcinoid syndrome perioperatively (rare in bronchial carcinoid)"],"pearls":["Typical carcinoid is low-grade and indolent with excellent prognosis after complete resection; atypical carcinoid recurs more","Central carcinoids are best treated with lung-sparing sleeve/bronchoplastic resection","DOTATATE PET (somatostatin-receptor) images well-differentiated carcinoids","High-grade neuroendocrine tumors (small cell/large-cell) are managed chemotherapeutically, not primarily surgically"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bronchial Carcinoid Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537090/"},{"t":"NCI: Lung Carcinoid Tumor Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/lung-carcinoid-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-metastasectomy","name":"Pulmonary Metastasectomy","sec":"thoracic","present":["The lung is a common site of metastasis; surgical PULMONARY METASTASECTOMY can offer prolonged survival in SELECTED patients with resectable pulmonary metastases and controlled primary disease","Classic selection criteria (Thomford): the PRIMARY tumor is CONTROLLED, metastases are COMPLETELY RESECTABLE, there is NO extrathoracic disease (or it is controllable), and the patient can tolerate resection","Common primaries: colorectal, sarcoma, renal cell, melanoma, germ cell, and head/neck"],"dx":["Confirm controlled primary and absence/controllability of extrathoracic disease (PET/CT, cross-sectional imaging); assess number/location of nodules and resectability","Weigh tumor biology and disease-free interval (longer interval and fewer nodules predict better outcomes)","Confirm adequate pulmonary reserve for parenchyma-sparing resection"],"tx":["Perform PARENCHYMA-SPARING resection (usually WEDGE resections; segmentectomy/lobectomy if needed) to remove all metastases with clear margins while preserving lung","Repeat metastasectomy is reasonable for re-recurrence in selected patients; integrate with systemic therapy per tumor type","Note the randomized PulMiCC trial questioned survival benefit in colorectal metastases - selection and multidisciplinary judgment matter"],"tech":["Wedge excision (staplers) of nodules with clear margins; palpate the lung (open/some MIS) to detect additional nodules not seen on imaging","Spare maximal parenchyma to allow future re-resections; nodal sampling in selected cases","Localization techniques (wire/dye/fluorescence) for small/deep nodules"],"after":["Preserve function for potential repeat metastasectomy; standard chest-tube/air-leak care","Ongoing systemic therapy and surveillance per primary tumor","Monitor for pulmonary recurrence"],"pearls":["Metastasectomy criteria: controlled primary, completely resectable metastases, no uncontrollable extrathoracic disease, adequate reserve","Wedge resection preserves parenchyma and allows repeat metastasectomy for recurrence","Longer disease-free interval and fewer nodules predict better outcomes","PulMiCC questioned benefit in colorectal metastases - careful multidisciplinary selection is essential"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pulmonary Metastasectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560945/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-solitary-nodule-surgical","name":"Solitary Pulmonary Nodule - Surgical Evaluation","sec":"thoracic","present":["A SOLITARY PULMONARY NODULE (<=3 cm, surrounded by lung, no adenopathy/effusion) prompts a probability-based decision among surveillance, biopsy, and surgical resection","The surgeon weighs malignancy risk (size, spiculation, growth, upper-lobe location, smoking/age) against operative risk; part-solid/persistent subsolid nodules and growth raise concern","Tissue diagnosis vs 'diagnostic resection' is a common crossroads"],"dx":["Estimate malignancy probability (clinical + imaging features; see the rads nodule entry for Fleischner/Lung-RADS); compare prior imaging for stability/growth","Choose among surveillance CT (low probability), biopsy (intermediate - CT-guided or navigational bronchoscopic), or upfront surgical resection (high probability/growing)","Localize small/deep nodules preoperatively for wedge resection"],"tx":["High-probability nodules: proceed to VATS WEDGE resection with intraoperative FROZEN SECTION - if malignant, complete an anatomic resection (lobectomy or segmentectomy) with nodal dissection in the same setting","Intermediate-probability: obtain a diagnosis (biopsy) or resect diagnostically per shared decision-making","Benign diagnoses (hamartoma, granuloma) need no further resection"],"tech":["Preoperative/intraoperative LOCALIZATION for small/deep nodules (wire, microcoil, dye, or fluorescence) enables wedge excision; palpation for accessible lesions","VATS wedge with frozen section; convert to anatomic resection + nodal dissection if cancer is confirmed","Send nodes for staging when proceeding to cancer resection"],"after":["If benign, reassure and stop; if malignant and resected, adjuvant therapy per stage and surveillance","Standard post-resection care (chest tube, analgesia)","Continued nodule surveillance if a non-resected nodule remains"],"pearls":["High-probability nodule: VATS wedge + frozen section, then complete anatomic resection + nodal dissection if cancer is confirmed - all in one anesthetic","Preoperative localization (wire/coil/dye/fluorescence) is key for small or deep nodules","Compare with prior imaging first - 2-year stability of a solid nodule suggests benignity","Match strategy to malignancy probability and operative risk (surveillance vs biopsy vs resection)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Solitary Pulmonary Nodule","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430866/"},{"t":"NCI: NSCLC Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-bronchoscopy","name":"Bronchoscopy - Diagnostic & Therapeutic","sec":"thoracic","present":["Bronchoscopy is central to thoracic practice - FLEXIBLE bronchoscopy for diagnosis (airway inspection, biopsy, BAL, EBUS staging, navigational biopsy of peripheral nodules) and RIGID bronchoscopy for therapeutic airway work","THERAPEUTIC uses: relief of central airway OBSTRUCTION (tumor debulking, stenting), foreign-body removal, control of bleeding, and management of stenosis/fistula","Rigid bronchoscopy provides airway control and a large working channel for interventions"],"dx":["Diagnostic: airway survey, endobronchial/transbronchial biopsy, BAL, EBUS-TBNA (nodal staging), and navigational/robotic bronchoscopy for peripheral lesions","Assess central airway obstruction (intrinsic tumor vs extrinsic compression vs mixed) to plan the therapeutic approach","Evaluate stenosis, fistula, and hemoptysis source"],"tx":["Relieve malignant/benign central airway OBSTRUCTION with mechanical/laser/electrocautery/APC debulking and AIRWAY STENTS (silicone via rigid scope, or self-expanding metallic)","Control airway bleeding (topical agents, tamponade, laser); remove foreign bodies (rigid scope preferred for large/complex)","Manage stenosis with dilation/stenting; palliate or bridge to surgery"],"tech":["RIGID bronchoscopy under general anesthesia secures the airway and permits ventilation while debulking/stenting; combine with flexible scope through the rigid barrel","Debulk with laser/electrocautery/APC/mechanical coring; deploy stents across the lesion; achieve hemostasis","For peripheral nodules, navigational/robotic bronchoscopy improves diagnostic yield"],"after":["Monitor for airway edema, bleeding, stent complications (migration, mucus plugging, granulation), and pneumothorax after transbronchial biopsy","Airway toilet and follow-up bronchoscopy for stent surveillance","Definitive oncologic therapy after diagnosis/palliation"],"pearls":["Rigid bronchoscopy is the tool for therapeutic airway work (debulking, stenting, massive-bleeding control) - it secures the airway and gives a large working channel","EBUS-TBNA (flexible) provides mediastinal nodal staging; navigational/robotic bronchoscopy reaches peripheral nodules","Central airway obstruction from tumor can be rapidly palliated with debulking + stenting","Watch stents for migration, mucus plugging, and granulation tissue"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bronchoscopy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448152/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Rigid Bronchoscopy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563192/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-airway-surgery","name":"Airway Surgery - Tracheal Resection & Stenosis","sec":"thoracic","present":["Tracheal disease includes post-intubation/tracheostomy STENOSIS (the most common benign cause), primary tracheal tumors (adenoid cystic and squamous cell carcinoma), and tracheomalacia","TRACHEAL RESECTION AND RECONSTRUCTION removes the diseased segment and performs a primary end-to-end anastomosis; the key constraint is the LENGTH resectable while achieving a tension-free anastomosis (roughly up to ~half the trachea with release maneuvers)","Airway management is shared intimately with anesthesia (cross-field ventilation)"],"dx":["Characterize the lesion (bronchoscopy + CT with airway reconstruction): location, length, and degree of stenosis; assess vocal cord function","Distinguish benign stenosis from tumor; plan the length to be resected and release maneuvers needed for a tension-free anastomosis","Optimize the airway (treat infection/inflammation) before definitive resection"],"tx":["Resect the diseased segment and reconstruct with a primary tension-free anastomosis; benign stenosis may first be managed by dilation/stenting, with resection as definitive therapy","Primary tracheal tumors (adenoid cystic - perineural spread; squamous cell): resection with negative margins +/- adjuvant radiation","Avoid tracheostomy through a planned resection segment when possible"],"tech":["Cervical/collar incision (+/- sternotomy for lower lesions); circumferentially resect the diseased trachea; perform an end-to-end anastomosis with absorbable sutures","Use RELEASE maneuvers (suprahyoid laryngeal release, hilar release) and neck FLEXION to reduce anastomotic tension","A GUARDIAN ('chin') suture keeps the neck flexed postoperatively to protect the anastomosis"],"after":["Keep the neck FLEXED postoperatively (guardian suture) to protect the anastomosis; avoid hyperextension","Watch for anastomotic complications (dehiscence, restenosis, granulation) and recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","Early extubation is preferred to avoid pressure on the anastomosis; bronchoscopic surveillance"],"pearls":["Post-intubation stenosis is the most common benign tracheal lesion requiring resection","Tension-free anastomosis is paramount - use laryngeal/hilar release maneuvers and neck flexion; a guardian (chin-to-chest) suture protects it postoperatively","Adenoid cystic carcinoma spreads perineurally/submucosally - margins can be deceptive","Resectable tracheal length is limited (~half the trachea with releases) - avoid over-resection tension"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tracheal Stenosis / Resection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544272/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Tracheal Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519057/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-pneumothorax","name":"Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Blebectomy & Pleurodesis","sec":"thoracic","present":["PRIMARY spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in young, tall, thin (often smoking) patients from rupture of apical BLEBS/BULLAE without underlying lung disease; SECONDARY spontaneous pneumothorax occurs with underlying disease (COPD, etc.) and is more dangerous","Presents with sudden pleuritic pain and dyspnea; TENSION pneumothorax (hemodynamic collapse, tracheal shift) is a clinical emergency requiring immediate decompression","Surgery is indicated for recurrence, persistent air leak, or high-risk occupation/first-episode features"],"dx":["Diagnose on chest radiograph/CT; assess size and stability; identify tension physiology clinically (do not wait for imaging)","Determine primary vs secondary; CT identifies apical blebs/bullae and underlying disease","Indications for surgery: recurrent (ipsilateral second, or contralateral/bilateral) pneumothorax, persistent air leak (>~3-5 days), non-re-expansion, hemopneumothorax, or occupation/lifestyle risk (pilots, divers) - and often the first episode in secondary disease"],"tx":["Initial management is now SYMPTOM-based (not size-based): conservative/observation for minimally symptomatic primary pneumothorax REGARDLESS of size (Brown 2020; BTS 2023 / ERS-EACTS-ESTS 2024), with ambulatory management an option; needle aspiration or chest tube if significantly symptomatic or unsuitable for conservative care; chest tube for secondary pneumothorax; IMMEDIATE needle/finger decompression for tension","Definitive surgery (usually VATS): BLEBECTOMY/bullectomy (staple the culprit apical blebs) PLUS a PLEURODESIS procedure (mechanical pleural abrasion or pleurectomy, +/- chemical) to prevent recurrence","Pleurodesis alone (chemical via tube) for non-surgical candidates"],"tech":["VATS: staple-resect apical blebs/bullae; perform MECHANICAL pleurodesis (apical pleural abrasion or apical pleurectomy) to create adhesions - lowest recurrence when combined with blebectomy","Chemical pleurodesis (talc/doxycycline) as an adjunct or for poor surgical candidates","Leave a chest tube; confirm full re-expansion"],"after":["Chest-tube management for air leak/re-expansion; recurrence risk is low after blebectomy + pleurodesis (vs high after a first pneumothorax managed conservatively)","Counsel on smoking cessation and (for pleurodesis patients) future implications if lung surgery is ever needed","Activity/altitude/diving counseling"],"pearls":["Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis - decompress immediately, do not wait for imaging; for stable primary pneumothorax, current guidelines are symptom-based (conservative care for minimally symptomatic disease regardless of size)","Surgery (VATS blebectomy + pleurodesis) is indicated for recurrence, persistent air leak, non-re-expansion, or high-risk occupation","Blebectomy PLUS mechanical pleurodesis (abrasion/pleurectomy) gives the lowest recurrence","Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax (underlying lung disease) is more dangerous and has a lower threshold for intervention"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Spontaneous Pneumothorax","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441885/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pleurodesis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560685/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-empyema-decortication","name":"Pleural Effusion & Empyema - VATS Decortication","sec":"thoracic","present":["A PARAPNEUMONIC effusion can progress through stages: EXUDATIVE (free-flowing) -> FIBRINOPURULENT (loculated, thick) -> ORGANIZING (thick peel trapping the lung); EMPYEMA is frank pus in the pleural space","Management depends on stage: simple effusions drain; complicated/loculated empyemas need drainage +/- fibrinolytics or surgery; organized empyema with a trapped lung needs DECORTICATION","Diagnostic thoracentesis (Light criteria; pH, glucose, LDH, culture) guides the need for drainage"],"dx":["Thoracentesis: exudate by Light criteria; pleural pH < ~7.2, low glucose, positive Gram stain/culture, or frank pus indicate a complicated effusion/empyema needing drainage","Imaging (CT/ultrasound) defines loculations, pleural thickening/enhancement (split-pleura sign), and trapped lung","Stage the empyema to choose chest tube +/- intrapleural fibrinolytics/DNase vs surgery"],"tx":["EXUDATIVE: chest tube drainage + antibiotics; FIBRINOPURULENT/loculated: chest tube + intrapleural tPA/DNase (MIST2), or VATS if that fails; ORGANIZING with trapped lung: DECORTICATION (remove the fibrous peel to re-expand the lung)","Early VATS is effective for fibrinopurulent empyema and increasingly favored; open decortication for advanced organization","Treat the underlying pneumonia and drain the source"],"tech":["VATS: evacuate pus/debris, break down loculations, and DECORTICATE the visceral peel to free the lung; place chest tubes to the apex and base","Open decortication (thoracotomy) for dense, mature peel; ensure full lung re-expansion","Send cultures; irrigate the space"],"after":["Ensure full lung re-expansion (obliterate the space); continue antibiotics per cultures; chest-tube management until drainage/leak resolves","Monitor for persistent space/residual empyema; nutrition and source control","Late presentation with a trapped lung may need staged management"],"pearls":["Complicated parapneumonic effusion/empyema signs: pH <7.2, low glucose, positive culture/Gram stain, or frank pus - these need drainage","Intrapleural tPA + DNase (MIST2) helps loculated empyema; VATS is favored when tube drainage/fibrinolytics fail","Organized empyema with a trapped lung requires decortication (peel removal) to re-expand the lung","The 'split-pleura sign' on contrast CT indicates empyema"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Empyema","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459237/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pleural Effusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448189/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-mesothelioma","name":"Malignant Pleural Mesothelioma - Surgical Management","sec":"thoracic","present":["Malignant pleural mesothelioma is an ASBESTOS-related pleural malignancy with a long latency, presenting with dyspnea, chest pain, and a pleural effusion/rind; the EPITHELIOID subtype has the best prognosis","Diagnosis requires adequate TISSUE (thoracoscopic pleural biopsy - cytology alone is insufficient); it spreads along the pleura and encases the lung","Management is multimodal and controversial; surgery is part of selected multimodality regimens, not a stand-alone cure"],"dx":["Obtain a generous tissue diagnosis (VATS pleural biopsy) for subtype (epithelioid vs biphasic/sarcomatoid) and immunohistochemistry; stage with CT/PET/MRI (chest wall/diaphragm/mediastinal involvement) and often surgical staging","Assess resectability and physiologic fitness; sarcomatoid histology generally precludes aggressive surgery","Multidisciplinary evaluation is essential"],"tx":["Surgery in selected epithelioid patients as part of MULTIMODALITY therapy: PLEURECTOMY/DECORTICATION (lung-sparing, removing the pleural tumor rind - now generally preferred) or EXTRAPLEURAL PNEUMONECTOMY (removes lung, pleura, pericardium, diaphragm - higher morbidity, less favored)","Combine with chemotherapy (platinum/pemetrexed); first-line nivolumab + ipilimumab is standard for UNRESECTABLE disease (CheckMate 743, OS benefit regardless of histology); +/- radiation in some protocols","Palliation (pleurodesis/indwelling catheter) for unresectable disease and effusion control"],"tech":["Pleurectomy/decortication: strip the parietal and visceral pleural tumor to free the lung (macroscopic complete resection) while preserving the lung","Extrapleural pneumonectomy: en-bloc removal of lung, parietal/visceral pleura, ipsilateral pericardium, and hemidiaphragm with prosthetic reconstruction","Aim for macroscopic complete resection within a multimodality plan"],"after":["High morbidity - especially after extrapleural pneumonectomy (respiratory/cardiac complications); intensive perioperative care","Adjuvant therapy per protocol; palliative care integration given overall prognosis","Effusion/space management and surveillance"],"pearls":["Mesothelioma is asbestos-related with long latency; epithelioid subtype has the best prognosis, sarcomatoid the worst","Diagnosis needs adequate TISSUE (thoracoscopic biopsy) - cytology alone is insufficient","Lung-sparing pleurectomy/decortication is preferred over extrapleural pneumonectomy (largely abandoned) - though MARS-2 (2024) questioned the survival benefit of radical surgery + chemo, so surgery's role is debated","Surgery is only one part of multimodality therapy - it is not curative alone"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Malignant Mesothelioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519530/"},{"t":"NCI: Malignant Mesothelioma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/mesothelioma/hp/mesothelioma-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-malignant-effusion","name":"Malignant Pleural Effusion - Pleurodesis & Indwelling Catheter","sec":"thoracic","present":["Malignant pleural effusion (from lung, breast, lymphoma, and other cancers) causes dyspnea and signals advanced disease; the goals are symptom relief and definitive fluid control","Two mainstays after confirming a symptomatic recurrent effusion and that the lung RE-EXPANDS: chemical PLEURODESIS and TUNNELED INDWELLING PLEURAL CATHETER (IPC)","A TRAPPED (non-expandable) LUNG changes strategy - pleurodesis fails if the lung cannot appose the chest wall"],"dx":["Confirm malignant etiology (cytology/pleural biopsy); assess whether the lung RE-EXPANDS after drainage (imaging) - essential for choosing pleurodesis vs catheter","Gauge symptom relief with drainage (does dyspnea improve?) and prognosis","Identify trapped lung (failure to re-expand, ex-vacuo pneumothorax)"],"tx":["EXPANDABLE lung: talc PLEURODESIS (poudrage at thoracoscopy or slurry via tube) OR an indwelling pleural catheter; the TIME2/AMPLE-type evidence shows IPCs reduce hospital days and are preferred for trapped lung or patient preference","TRAPPED lung: indwelling pleural catheter (pleurodesis will fail); some achieve auto-pleurodesis over time with an IPC","Treat the underlying malignancy; integrate palliative care"],"tech":["Talc pleurodesis: instill talc (poudrage via thoracoscope or slurry via chest tube) after full drainage/lung re-expansion to fuse the pleural surfaces","Indwelling pleural catheter: tunneled catheter for intermittent ambulatory drainage at home; educate the patient/caregiver on drainage","Ensure lung apposition for pleurodesis to work"],"after":["IPCs allow outpatient management and can lead to spontaneous pleurodesis; monitor for infection/blockage","Pleurodesis: chest tube until drainage falls, then remove; monitor for fever/pain post-talc","Ongoing oncologic and palliative care"],"pearls":["Choose the strategy by lung re-expansion: pleurodesis needs an EXPANDABLE lung; a TRAPPED lung -> indwelling pleural catheter","Talc is the most effective pleurodesis agent; indwelling catheters reduce hospital time and suit trapped lung/patient preference","Malignant effusion signals advanced disease - integrate palliative care","Confirm symptom relief with drainage before committing to definitive management"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Malignant Pleural Effusion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541020/"},{"t":"NCI: Cardiopulmonary Syndromes (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lvrs-emphysema","name":"Lung Volume Reduction Surgery, Bullectomy & Emphysema","sec":"thoracic","present":["Surgery for emphysema improves function by removing the most destroyed lung so healthier lung and the diaphragm work better. LUNG VOLUME REDUCTION SURGERY (LVRS) resects hyperinflated, poorly-functioning (typically UPPER-LOBE) tissue","BULLECTOMY removes a GIANT BULLA (occupying >~1/3 of the hemithorax) that compresses adjacent normal lung","The landmark NETT trial defined who benefits and who is harmed by LVRS"],"dx":["LVRS candidacy (NETT): severe emphysema with UPPER-LOBE-predominant disease and LOW exercise capacity benefit most; assess with PFTs, high-resolution CT (distribution), and cardiopulmonary exercise testing","NETT identified a HIGH-RISK group with NO benefit and high mortality: FEV1 <=20% predicted AND either homogeneous emphysema or DLCO <=20% predicted - avoid surgery in them","Bullectomy: identify a giant bulla compressing functional lung with preserved underlying parenchyma"],"tx":["LVRS for upper-lobe-predominant emphysema with low exercise capacity - improves survival, function, and quality of life in that subgroup; avoid in the NETT high-risk group","BULLECTOMY for giant bullae compressing normal lung; ENDOBRONCHIAL VALVES are a less-invasive option for selected patients without collateral ventilation","Optimize medical therapy, pulmonary rehabilitation, and smoking cessation first"],"tech":["LVRS: bilateral (usually VATS) stapled resection of ~20-30% of the most emphysematous (upper-lobe) tissue, buttressing staple lines to reduce air leak","Bullectomy: staple-resect the bulla at its base, preserving compressed normal lung","Endobronchial valves placed bronchoscopically for selected patients (target lobe without collateral ventilation)"],"after":["PROLONGED AIR LEAK is the dominant complication (emphysematous lung) - buttressed staple lines and careful drainage; continued pulmonary rehab","Monitor respiratory status closely; the benefit is functional improvement over months","Avoid LVRS in NETT high-risk patients (high mortality, no benefit)"],"pearls":["NETT: upper-lobe-predominant emphysema + low exercise capacity benefits from LVRS; FEV1 <=20% with homogeneous disease or DLCO <=20% is a high-risk group (harm, no benefit)","Giant bulla (>~1/3 hemithorax) compressing normal lung is an indication for bullectomy","Prolonged air leak is the main complication - buttress staple lines","Endobronchial valves are a less-invasive alternative for selected patients without collateral ventilation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Volume Reduction Surgery","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544268/"},{"t":"NHLBI: COPD","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/copd"}]},{"id":"thoracic-infectious-lung-surgery","name":"Infectious Lung Disease Surgery","sec":"thoracic","present":["Surgery for pulmonary infection is reserved for complications and destroyed lung: localized BRONCHIECTASIS, ASPERGILLOMA (fungus ball in a cavity), refractory LUNG ABSCESS, and destroyed lobes from prior TB/infection","The common thread is recurrent hemoptysis, uncontrolled infection, or a destroyed segment/lobe not responding to medical therapy","Aspergilloma classically causes hemoptysis (which can be massive) within a pre-existing cavity"],"dx":["Localize disease (CT) and confirm it is anatomically limited enough for resection; assess pulmonary reserve","ASPERGILLOMA: cavitary fungus ball (the 'air-crescent'/monod sign), positive Aspergillus serology; a source of hemoptysis","Optimize medical therapy first (antibiotics/antifungals, drainage); reserve surgery for failure/complications"],"tx":["Resect localized, destroyed, or complicated disease: segmentectomy/lobectomy for focal bronchiectasis or a destroyed lobe; resection for symptomatic aspergilloma (definitive) or refractory abscess (most abscesses respond to drainage/antibiotics)","Bronchial artery embolization first for active massive hemoptysis (see rads) to stabilize before/instead of surgery","Continue targeted antimicrobial therapy perioperatively"],"tech":["Anatomic resection of the involved segment/lobe; dense inflammatory adhesions and hilar fibrosis make these technically demanding (higher bleeding/BPF risk)","Buttress bronchial stumps (vascularized tissue) in infected/irradiated fields to prevent bronchopleural fistula","Aspergilloma resection: avoid spillage; manage the residual space"],"after":["Higher complication rates (bleeding, prolonged air leak, bronchopleural fistula, empyema) in infected fields - vigilant drainage and antimicrobials","Ensure space obliteration and lung re-expansion; nutrition","Continue antifungal/antibiotic therapy as indicated"],"pearls":["Surgery for pulmonary infection targets localized/destroyed lung or complications (recurrent hemoptysis, refractory infection)","Aspergilloma (fungus ball in a cavity) causes hemoptysis - definitive treatment is resection; embolize active massive bleeding first","Most lung abscesses resolve with antibiotics/drainage - surgery is for refractory cases","Infected/fibrotic fields raise the risk of bleeding and bronchopleural fistula - buttress stumps"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aspergilloma / Bronchiectasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430950/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Abscess","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537034/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-hemoptysis-surgery","name":"Massive Hemoptysis - Surgical Management","sec":"thoracic","present":["MASSIVE hemoptysis (large-volume, e.g., >~200-600 mL/24h or any amount threatening the airway) is life-threatening primarily by ASPHYXIATION, not exsanguination - airway protection is the first priority","Most bleeding is from the high-pressure BRONCHIAL (systemic) circulation; causes include bronchiectasis, TB/fungal disease (aspergilloma), and malignancy","Management is multidisciplinary: airway control, localization, bronchial artery embolization, and surgery for refractory/localized disease"],"dx":["Stabilize first (airway/breathing), then LOCALIZE the bleeding side/source (bronchoscopy and CT angiography); protect the good lung","Identify surgically treatable, LOCALIZED disease vs diffuse/unresectable causes","Assess pulmonary reserve for potential resection"],"tx":["AIRWAY first: position the bleeding side DOWN, secure the airway (selective intubation/lung isolation to protect the healthy lung), and resuscitate","BRONCHIAL ARTERY EMBOLIZATION is first-line to control bleeding (see the rads entry); SURGERY (resection of the bleeding segment/lobe) for localized disease refractory to embolization or for definitive treatment (e.g., aspergilloma)","Reverse coagulopathy; treat the underlying cause"],"tech":["Emergent rigid bronchoscopy can localize, protect the airway, and tamponade/treat bleeding; lung isolation (DLT/blocker) protects the non-bleeding lung","Definitive resection (lobectomy/segmentectomy) of the bleeding source once stabilized and localized","Coordinate with IR for embolization"],"after":["Recurrence is common (collateral revascularization after embolization) - definitive resection may be needed for durable control","Monitor respiratory status and for rebleeding; treat the underlying disease","Optimize before any elective definitive surgery"],"pearls":["Massive hemoptysis kills by ASPHYXIATION - airway protection (bleeding side down, lung isolation) comes first","Bleeding is usually from the bronchial (systemic) arteries - bronchial artery embolization is first-line control","Surgery is for localized disease refractory to embolization or for definitive cure (e.g., aspergilloma)","Rigid bronchoscopy is invaluable for localizing, protecting the airway, and controlling bleeding"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Massive Hemoptysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513222/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Hemoptysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430812/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-esophageal-cancer-staging","name":"Esophageal Cancer - Staging & Surgical Approach","sec":"thoracic","present":["Esophageal cancer (SQUAMOUS - upper/mid, alcohol/tobacco; ADENOCARCINOMA - distal/GEJ, from Barrett/reflux) is staged by TNM; depth (T) and nodal (N) status determine endoscopic vs surgical vs multimodality therapy","Accurate staging uses endoscopy/biopsy, ENDOSCOPIC ULTRASOUND (best for T and regional N), PET/CT (distant disease), and sometimes staging laparoscopy for GEJ tumors","The GEJ location (Siewert type) influences the surgical approach"],"dx":["Stage with EUS (T/N), PET/CT (M), and endoscopy; classify GEJ tumors by Siewert type; assess resectability and fitness","Early disease (T1a, limited to mucosa) may be cured ENDOSCOPICALLY (EMR/ESD); deeper/node-positive disease needs surgery +/- neoadjuvant therapy","Determine candidacy for neoadjuvant chemoradiation (CROSS) or perioperative chemotherapy (FLOT for adenocarcinoma)"],"tx":["T1a mucosal cancer: endoscopic resection (EMR/ESD) can be curative; T1b-T3/N+ resectable disease: esophagectomy, usually after NEOADJUVANT therapy (CROSS chemoradiation, or perioperative FLOT for adenocarcinoma)","Definitive chemoradiation for cervical esophageal cancer or non-operative candidates (see radonc)","Multidisciplinary planning is essential"],"tech":["Approach depends on tumor location and Siewert type (see the esophagectomy entry): transthoracic (Ivor Lewis/McKeown) vs transhiatal, open or minimally invasive","Perform a complete lymphadenectomy for accurate staging and local control","Restage after neoadjuvant therapy before resection"],"after":["Post-esophagectomy care and complication surveillance (see the complications entry); nutritional support is central","Adjuvant therapy per pathology (e.g., adjuvant nivolumab for residual disease after trimodality therapy, CheckMate 577)","Surveillance for recurrence"],"pearls":["EUS is best for T and regional N staging; PET/CT detects distant disease; stage before choosing therapy","T1a mucosal cancer can be cured endoscopically (EMR/ESD) - not everyone needs esophagectomy","Resectable locally advanced disease: neoadjuvant chemoradiation (CROSS) or perioperative FLOT, then esophagectomy","Siewert type (GEJ location) guides the surgical approach and esophageal-vs-gastric management"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophageal Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470127/"},{"t":"NCI: Esophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-esophagectomy","name":"Esophagectomy - Ivor Lewis, Transhiatal, McKeown & MIE","sec":"thoracic","present":["Esophagectomy removes the esophagus (and tumor) and reconstructs GI continuity, usually with a GASTRIC CONDUIT (stomach tubularized and pulled up); the approach is chosen by tumor location, needed lymphadenectomy, and surgeon expertise","Main approaches: IVOR LEWIS (abdomen + right chest, intrathoracic anastomosis - for mid/distal tumors), McKEOWN (abdomen + right chest + neck, cervical anastomosis - for more proximal tumors), and TRANSHIATAL (abdomen + neck, no thoracotomy)","MINIMALLY INVASIVE esophagectomy (MIE) reduces pulmonary complications (TIME trial)"],"dx":["Select the approach by tumor level and lymphadenectomy needs; confirm conduit suitability (prior gastric surgery may require colon/jejunal interposition)","Assess nutritional status and physiologic fitness; optimize before this high-risk operation","Plan the anastomotic location (intrathoracic vs cervical) - each has a different leak profile"],"tx":["IVOR LEWIS (intrathoracic anastomosis) for mid/distal and GEJ tumors; McKEOWN (cervical anastomosis) for more proximal tumors; TRANSHIATAL avoids thoracotomy (cervical anastomosis, less complete thoracic lymphadenectomy)","MINIMALLY INVASIVE esophagectomy lowers pulmonary complications and is increasingly standard (TIME trial)","Perform a complete lymphadenectomy; construct a well-vascularized gastric conduit"],"tech":["Tubularize the stomach on the right gastroepiploic arcade (preserve this pedicle - it is the conduit's blood supply); mobilize the esophagus; create the anastomosis (stapled/hand-sewn) intrathoracically (Ivor Lewis) or in the neck (McKeown/transhiatal)","Place a feeding jejunostomy; a pyloric drainage procedure is often added; protect the recurrent laryngeal nerves during cervical/upper dissection","Ensure conduit perfusion (assess with indocyanine-green angiography)"],"after":["Watch for ANASTOMOTIC LEAK (cervical leaks are more frequent but often less catastrophic than intrathoracic), conduit ischemia/necrosis, chylothorax, respiratory complications, recurrent laryngeal nerve injury, and delayed gastric emptying","Nutrition via jejunostomy until oral intake is safe (contrast study to check the anastomosis); aggressive pulmonary toilet","Long-term: reflux, dumping, and stricture (dilatable) management"],"pearls":["The gastric conduit is perfused by the RIGHT GASTROEPIPLOIC artery - protect this pedicle at all costs","Ivor Lewis (intrathoracic anastomosis) for mid/distal tumors; McKeown (cervical) for proximal; transhiatal avoids thoracotomy","Cervical anastomotic leaks are more common but usually less catastrophic than intrathoracic leaks","Minimally invasive esophagectomy (TIME trial) reduces pulmonary complications"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophagectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539819/"},{"t":"NCI: Esophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-esophagectomy-complications","name":"Esophagectomy Complications","sec":"thoracic","present":["Esophagectomy is among the highest-morbidity elective operations; the feared complications are ANASTOMOTIC LEAK, conduit (gastric) ISCHEMIA/NECROSIS, CHYLE LEAK (thoracic duct injury), and pulmonary complications","Anastomotic leak presents with fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, and drain findings (saliva/enteric content), typically around post-op days 5-10; intrathoracic leaks can cause mediastinitis/sepsis","Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury (hoarseness, aspiration) and delayed gastric emptying also occur"],"dx":["Suspect leak with sepsis/systemic signs; confirm with contrast esophagram (water-soluble) and/or CT with oral contrast; endoscopy assesses conduit viability","Chyle leak: high-output, MILKY drain fluid (rises with enteral fat); confirm with elevated triglycerides/chylomicrons in fluid","Assess conduit perfusion (endoscopy/CT); evaluate for recurrent laryngeal nerve injury (voice, swallow)"],"tx":["LEAK management by severity/location: contained/small leaks -> NPO, drainage, antibiotics, nutrition (jejunostomy) +/- endoscopic stent/vacuum; large/uncontained or conduit NECROSIS -> reoperation (drainage, conduit revision/diversion with esophagostomy)","CHYLE LEAK: low-fat/MCT or NPO+TPN; octreotide; thoracic duct LIGATION (or embolization) for high-output/persistent leaks","Manage delayed gastric emptying (prokinetics, pyloric intervention) and aspiration risk"],"tech":["Endoscopic stent or endoluminal VACUUM therapy for contained anastomotic leaks; reoperative drainage and conduit takedown/diversion for conduit necrosis","Thoracic duct ligation (mass ligation of tissue between aorta, azygos, and spine at the diaphragmatic hiatus) or lymphangiography-guided embolization for chylothorax","Ensure wide drainage of infected collections"],"after":["Nutrition is central (jejunostomy feeding) until the anastomosis heals; monitor drains, sepsis, and nutritional status","Chyle leaks deplete lymphocytes/protein - watch immune/nutritional consequences; escalate to duct ligation if conservative measures fail in a few days","Long-term: anastomotic stricture (endoscopic dilation), reflux, and dumping"],"pearls":["Anastomotic leak (POD 5-10: fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis) is confirmed by water-soluble esophagram/CT; conduit necrosis needs reoperation/diversion","Chyle leak = milky high-output drainage with high triglycerides - trial conservative (low-fat/MCT, octreotide), then thoracic duct ligation/embolization","The gastric conduit's blood supply is the right gastroepiploic artery - ischemia there causes conduit necrosis","Jejunostomy nutrition supports healing; long-term anastomotic strictures respond to dilation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophagectomy Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544277/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chylothorax","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459206/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-gerd-antireflux","name":"GERD & Antireflux Surgery","sec":"thoracic","present":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) causes heartburn, regurgitation, and complications (esophagitis, stricture, Barrett esophagus, and respiratory/laryngeal symptoms); most is managed medically (PPIs, lifestyle)","ANTIREFLUX SURGERY (fundoplication) is considered for medication-refractory disease, medication intolerance/preference, large hiatal hernias, or severe regurgitation","NISSEN (360-degree) fundoplication is the standard; partial wraps (Toupet 270 posterior, Dor anterior) reduce dysphagia in patients with impaired motility"],"dx":["Confirm GERD objectively before surgery: upper endoscopy (esophagitis/Barrett), and pH/impedance monitoring (document acid exposure), plus ESOPHAGEAL MANOMETRY to assess motility (avoid a full Nissen in poor motility)","Barium swallow defines anatomy/hiatal hernia; exclude achalasia (which mimics reflux/regurgitation) and other motility disorders","Correlate symptoms with objective reflux"],"tx":["Offer fundoplication for refractory/complicated GERD or patient preference after objective confirmation; choose Nissen (normal motility) vs partial wrap (impaired motility) to limit postoperative dysphagia","Magnetic sphincter augmentation (LINX) is an alternative in selected patients","Repair any associated hiatal hernia and restore intra-abdominal esophageal length"],"tech":["Laparoscopic Nissen: mobilize the esophagus, reduce any hernia, close the crura, and wrap the gastric fundus 360 degrees around the distal esophagus over a bougie (a 'floppy', short wrap prevents dysphagia)","Divide short gastric vessels as needed to create a tension-free wrap; ensure adequate intra-abdominal esophageal length","Partial wrap (Toupet/Dor) for impaired peristalsis"],"after":["Early dysphagia and gas-bloat/inability to belch are common and usually improve; advise a stepwise diet","Complications: wrap herniation/slippage, dysphagia, and recurrence; watch for pneumothorax/perforation intraoperatively","Long-term reflux control is good; some recurrence over years"],"pearls":["Get objective confirmation (endoscopy, pH study) AND manometry before antireflux surgery - and rule out achalasia","Match the wrap to motility: Nissen (360) for normal peristalsis, partial (Toupet/Dor) for impaired motility to avoid dysphagia","A 'floppy', short Nissen over a bougie prevents postoperative dysphagia","Gas-bloat and early dysphagia are common and usually transient"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nissen Fundoplication","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539853/"},{"t":"StatPearls: GERD","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441938/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-hiatal-hernia","name":"Hiatal & Paraesophageal Hernia Repair","sec":"thoracic","present":["Hiatal hernias are classified I-IV: TYPE I (sliding, the GE junction migrates above the diaphragm - most common, associated with reflux) and TYPES II-IV (PARAESOPHAGEAL, where the fundus/other organs herniate alongside the esophagus)","Large paraesophageal hernias can cause obstructive symptoms, postprandial pain, dysphagia, anemia (Cameron erosions), and, dangerously, GASTRIC VOLVULUS/incarceration/strangulation","Acute gastric volvulus (Borchardt triad: epigastric pain, retching without vomiting, inability to pass an NG tube) is a surgical emergency"],"dx":["Characterize with barium swallow, endoscopy, and CT; classify type and assess for volvulus/organoaxial rotation","Symptomatic paraesophageal hernias warrant repair; acute obstruction/volvulus/strangulation is an emergency","Assess reflux and motility (manometry) to plan concomitant fundoplication"],"tx":["Repair SYMPTOMATIC paraesophageal hernias (asymptomatic, incidental ones in frail patients may be watched); emergency repair for volvulus/strangulation/obstruction","Reduce the hernia and sac, close the crura (mesh reinforcement selectively), restore intra-abdominal esophageal length, and add a FUNDOPLICATION (also anchors the stomach and controls reflux)","Gastropexy in selected high-risk patients"],"tech":["Laparoscopic approach: reduce contents, EXCISE the hernia SAC, mobilize the mediastinal esophagus for adequate intra-abdominal length, close the crura posteriorly, and perform a fundoplication (+/- Collis gastroplasty for a short esophagus)","Selective mesh for large crural defects (weigh mesh complications near the esophagus)","Emergency: reduce/assess viability, resect necrotic stomach if strangulated"],"after":["Recurrence is common with large hernias; watch for acute dysphagia, wrap issues, and recurrence","Early postoperative anti-nausea measures to protect the repair from retching","Dietary progression; long-term follow-up for recurrence"],"pearls":["Type I = sliding (reflux); Types II-IV = paraesophageal (risk of volvulus/incarceration/strangulation)","Borchardt triad (pain, retching without vomiting, inability to pass an NG tube) signals acute gastric volvulus - a surgical emergency","Repair symptomatic paraesophageal hernias: excise the sac, close the crura, restore intra-abdominal esophagus, and add a fundoplication","Recurrence is common with large hernias; a short esophagus may need a Collis gastroplasty"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hiatal Hernia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562200/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Paraesophageal Hernia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544270/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-achalasia","name":"Achalasia & Esophageal Motility Disorders","sec":"thoracic","present":["ACHALASIA is failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax with ABSENT esophageal peristalsis, from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons - causing progressive dysphagia to solids AND liquids, regurgitation, and weight loss","Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus with a 'BIRD'S-BEAK' tapering; manometry (the gold standard) shows incomplete LES relaxation and aplastic/absent peristalsis (Chicago classification subtypes I-III)","Always EXCLUDE PSEUDOACHALASIA (malignancy at the GE junction mimicking achalasia) with endoscopy, especially in older patients with rapid weight loss"],"dx":["Diagnose with barium swallow (bird's beak), high-resolution MANOMETRY (gold standard - integrated relaxation pressure elevated, absent peristalsis), and ENDOSCOPY to exclude pseudoachalasia/tumor","Subtype by Chicago classification (type II - pan-esophageal pressurization - responds best; type III spastic is hardest)","Assess nutritional status and esophageal dilation"],"tx":["Definitive treatments reduce LES tone: LAPAROSCOPIC HELLER MYOTOMY (with partial fundoplication to limit reflux) or PER-ORAL ENDOSCOPIC MYOTOMY (POEM); pneumatic dilation is an effective non-surgical option","POEM is especially effective for type III (spastic) achalasia (longer myotomy) but causes more reflux; botulinum toxin injection is reserved for poor operative candidates (temporary)","Match therapy to subtype, fitness, and reflux considerations"],"tech":["Heller myotomy: divide the circular/longitudinal muscle across the LES onto the stomach, then add a PARTIAL fundoplication (Dor/Toupet) to reduce post-myotomy reflux","POEM: submucosal tunnel endoscopically, then myotomy of the inner circular muscle across the LES - no anti-reflux wrap (hence more reflux)","Take care to avoid mucosal perforation during myotomy"],"after":["Monitor for reflux after myotomy (especially POEM - often needs PPI); watch for mucosal leak/perforation early","Dysphagia usually improves markedly; some need retreatment over time","Surveillance for reflux complications; nutrition recovery"],"pearls":["Achalasia = absent peristalsis + non-relaxing LES; barium 'bird's beak', manometry is the gold standard","Always exclude PSEUDOACHALASIA (GE junction cancer) with endoscopy - especially older patients with rapid weight loss","Heller myotomy + partial fundoplication or POEM are definitive; POEM excels for type III (spastic) but causes more reflux","Botulinum toxin is a temporary option for poor operative candidates"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Achalasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441911/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Heller Myotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560540/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-esophageal-perforation","name":"Esophageal Perforation & Boerhaave Syndrome","sec":"thoracic","present":["Esophageal perforation is a life-threatening surgical emergency; the most common cause is IATROGENIC (endoscopy/dilation), followed by BOERHAAVE SYNDROME (spontaneous transmural rupture from forceful vomiting, classically the left posterolateral distal esophagus)","Presentation: chest/epigastric pain, dysphagia, subcutaneous emphysema, and sepsis; Mackler triad (vomiting, chest pain, subcutaneous emphysema) suggests Boerhaave","Contamination of the mediastinum/pleura causes rapidly progressive MEDIASTINITIS and sepsis - time to treatment strongly affects survival"],"dx":["Confirm with water-soluble contrast esophagram (then thin barium if negative and suspicion remains) and/or CT with oral contrast (extraluminal air/contrast, effusion, mediastinal fluid)","Localize the perforation (cervical vs thoracic vs abdominal) and assess contamination; pleural effusion with high amylase/low pH suggests perforation","Assess timing (early <24h vs late) and physiologic stability - both guide management"],"tx":["Resuscitate, NPO, broad-spectrum antibiotics + antifungals, and drain contamination; the perforation must be controlled and the space drained","Options by stability/timing/location: primary surgical REPAIR (buttressed) with wide drainage (best for early, stable), ENDOSCOPIC stent/clip/vacuum for contained/selected leaks, and drainage/diversion or esophagectomy for extensive necrosis or unstable/late presentations","Contained cervical perforations may be managed with drainage/antibiotics"],"tech":["Primary repair: debride, close in layers over the mucosal defect (extend the myotomy to expose the full mucosal injury), and BUTTRESS with vascularized tissue (intercostal muscle/pleura/pericardial fat); drain widely","Endoscopic stenting/vacuum for contained thoracic perforations; T-tube for controlled fistula","Esophageal diversion (cervical esophagostomy) + drainage for unsalvageable esophagus, with later reconstruction"],"after":["ICU care for sepsis; wide drainage and nutrition (jejunostomy/TPN); NPO until a contrast study confirms healing","Monitor for ongoing leak, empyema, and mediastinitis; escalate to reoperation if not controlled","Late reconstruction after diversion"],"pearls":["Iatrogenic (endoscopic) perforation is most common; Boerhaave is spontaneous rupture from vomiting (left posterolateral distal esophagus)","Diagnose with water-soluble esophagram +/- CT; pleural fluid with high amylase/low pH suggests perforation","Early diagnosis and wide drainage drive survival - primary buttressed repair for early/stable, stent/drainage for contained, diversion for unsalvageable","Broad-spectrum antibiotics + antifungals and aggressive source control from the outset"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Esophageal Perforation / Boerhaave","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430848/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Boerhaave Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430814/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-benign-esophageal","name":"Benign Esophageal Disease","sec":"thoracic","present":["Benign esophageal conditions include DIVERTICULA (Zenker's - upper/pharyngoesophageal; epiphrenic - distal, motility-associated; midesophageal - traction), benign tumors (LEIOMYOMA - the most common, intramural), and strictures/webs/rings","ZENKER DIVERTICULUM (a pulsion diverticulum through Killian triangle from upper-esophageal-sphincter dysfunction) presents with dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, and aspiration in older adults","Leiomyoma is a smooth, submucosal mass - do NOT biopsy through intact mucosa (risks complicating enucleation)"],"dx":["Barium swallow characterizes diverticula (location/size) and rings/strictures; endoscopy and manometry assess associated motility (epiphrenic diverticula accompany motility disorders)","Leiomyoma: endoscopic ultrasound shows a hypoechoic intramural mass arising from muscularis propria - avoid mucosal biopsy","Evaluate strictures for underlying cause (reflux, eosinophilic esophagitis, malignancy)"],"tx":["ZENKER: cricopharyngeal MYOTOMY is essential (treats the underlying sphincter dysfunction) + diverticulum management (endoscopic stapling/diverticulotomy for suitable size, or open diverticulectomy/diverticulopexy + myotomy)","EPIPHRENIC diverticulum: myotomy of the associated motility disorder (+/- diverticulectomy) - address the motility problem, not just the pouch","LEIOMYOMA: ENUCLEATION (leave mucosa intact) for symptomatic/large lesions; treat rings/strictures with dilation (+/- reflux therapy)"],"tech":["Zenker: endoscopic stapled diverticulotomy (divides the common wall/cricopharyngeus) for appropriately sized pouches, or open transcervical diverticulectomy + cricopharyngeal myotomy","Leiomyoma enucleation: split the muscle, shell out the tumor preserving mucosa (VATS/robotic), and reapproximate muscle","Epiphrenic: long myotomy across the LES + diverticulectomy with partial fundoplication"],"after":["Zenker: watch for leak/mediastinitis and recurrent laryngeal nerve injury; contrast study before feeding","Leiomyoma enucleation: check mucosal integrity intraoperatively; low recurrence","Monitor for leak after any esophageal myotomy/resection"],"pearls":["Zenker diverticulum: cricopharyngeal MYOTOMY is the essential step (treats the cause) - not just removing the pouch","Epiphrenic diverticula are driven by a motility disorder - treat with myotomy, not diverticulectomy alone","Esophageal leiomyoma is submucosal - do NOT biopsy through intact mucosa; treat symptomatic ones by enucleation","Evaluate strictures/rings for reflux, eosinophilic esophagitis, and malignancy before dilation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Zenker Diverticulum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430794/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Esophageal Leiomyoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551669/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-mediastinal-masses","name":"Mediastinal Masses by Compartment","sec":"thoracic","present":["Mediastinal masses are localized to COMPARTMENTS, which powerfully narrows the differential; the modern (ITMIG) scheme uses PREVASCULAR (anterior), VISCERAL (middle), and PARAVERTEBRAL (posterior) compartments","ANTERIOR (prevascular) - the '4 T's': THYMOMA/thymic tumors, TERATOMA/germ cell tumors, 'Terrible' LYMPHOMA, and THYROID (retrosternal goiter)","MIDDLE (visceral) - foregut/bronchogenic/pericardial cysts, lymphadenopathy, vascular; POSTERIOR (paravertebral) - NEUROGENIC tumors (schwannoma, neurofibroma, ganglioneuroma, neuroblastoma)"],"dx":["Localize on CT/MRI; use serum markers for anterior masses (AFP/beta-hCG for nonseminomatous germ cell tumors, LDH), and assess for myasthenia (thymoma)","Choose the diagnostic route: many need biopsy (core/EBUS/mediastinoscopy/anterior mediastinotomy) - EXCEPT a resectable anterior mass suspicious for thymoma (resect without transpleural biopsy to avoid seeding)","MRI for posterior/neurogenic masses (spinal canal extension - 'dumbbell' tumors)"],"tx":["Treat by diagnosis: resect thymoma and most neurogenic tumors; germ cell tumors and lymphoma are treated medically (chemo +/- residual mass resection); drain/resect symptomatic cysts","Do NOT biopsy a resectable suspected thymoma transpleurally (seeding risk) - resect it; DO biopsy suspected lymphoma/germ cell tumors (medical treatment)","Plan approach by compartment and size"],"tech":["Resect anterior masses via sternotomy or minimally invasive (VATS/robotic) thymectomy; posterior neurogenic tumors via VATS (assess spinal extension - combined neurosurgery for dumbbell tumors)","Obtain tissue via the least invasive adequate route when medical treatment is likely (EBUS, anterior mediastinotomy/Chamberlain)","Protect the phrenic nerve during anterior resection"],"after":["Compartment- and diagnosis-specific: monitor for phrenic nerve injury (anterior), CSF leak/neuro deficit (posterior dumbbell), and chylothorax","Adjuvant therapy per pathology (e.g., thymoma stage); surveillance","Manage myasthenia perioperatively for thymoma"],"pearls":["Compartment localizes the differential: anterior '4 T's' (Thymoma, Teratoma/germ cell, Terrible lymphoma, Thyroid); posterior = neurogenic tumors","Check AFP/beta-hCG for anterior masses (germ cell tumors) and assess for myasthenia (thymoma)","Do NOT transpleurally biopsy a resectable suspected thymoma (seeding) - resect it; biopsy suspected lymphoma/germ cell tumors instead","Posterior 'dumbbell' neurogenic tumors extend into the spinal canal (MRI) - plan combined neurosurgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mediastinal Mass","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560737/"},{"t":"NCI: Thymoma and Thymic Carcinoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/thymoma/hp/thymoma-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-thymoma-thymectomy","name":"Thymoma & Thymectomy (Myasthenia Gravis)","sec":"thoracic","present":["THYMOMA is the most common anterior mediastinal tumor in adults; ~30-50% of thymoma patients have MYASTHENIA GRAVIS, and thymoma is staged by the Masaoka-Koga system (capsular/invasion-based)","Conversely, many myasthenia patients (even without thymoma) benefit from THYMECTOMY; the MGTX randomized trial showed thymectomy improves outcomes in nonthymomatous generalized AChR-antibody myasthenia","Thymoma can also associate with other paraneoplastic syndromes (pure red cell aplasia, hypogammaglobulinemia)"],"dx":["Image with CT (chest); test for myasthenia (acetylcholine receptor antibodies) in all thymoma patients and optimize before surgery","Stage by Masaoka-Koga (invasion of capsule/adjacent structures); do NOT transpleurally biopsy a resectable thymoma (seeding) - resect","Assess for paraneoplastic cytopenias/immunodeficiency"],"tx":["Complete surgical RESECTION (total thymectomy with the tumor and thymic tissue) is the mainstay for resectable thymoma; postoperative radiation for incompletely resected/invasive (Masaoka III+) disease (see radonc)","THYMECTOMY for generalized AChR-antibody myasthenia (MGTX) improves symptoms and reduces steroid need - even without thymoma","OPTIMIZE myasthenia preoperatively (anticholinesterases, steroids, IVIG/plasma exchange) to prevent perioperative crisis"],"tech":["Total thymectomy via sternotomy or minimally invasive (VATS/robotic/transcervical) - remove the thymus and mediastinal fat en bloc, protecting BOTH phrenic nerves (their injury causes diaphragm paralysis)","For invasive thymoma, resect involved structures (pericardium, lung, vein) to achieve complete resection","Avoid capsular disruption/seeding"],"after":["Watch for MYASTHENIC CRISIS postoperatively (respiratory failure) - continue myasthenia therapy, monitor respiratory status, and avoid precipitating drugs","Phrenic nerve injury -> diaphragm paralysis; monitor pulmonary function","Adjuvant radiation per stage; long-term surveillance (thymoma can recur late)"],"pearls":["Thymoma is the most common anterior mediastinal tumor; ~30-50% have myasthenia gravis - test AChR antibodies in all","MGTX trial: thymectomy benefits generalized AChR-antibody myasthenia even WITHOUT thymoma","Optimize myasthenia before surgery (IVIG/plasma exchange) to prevent perioperative myasthenic crisis; protect both phrenic nerves","Do not transpleurally biopsy a resectable thymoma (seeding) - complete resection is the goal; radiation for invasive/incomplete resection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thymoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559113/"},{"t":"NCI: Thymoma and Thymic Carcinoma Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/thymoma/hp/thymoma-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"thoracic-mediastinitis","name":"Mediastinitis & Descending Necrotizing Infection","sec":"thoracic","present":["Mediastinitis is a life-threatening infection of the mediastinal space. ACUTE mediastinitis arises from esophageal perforation, POST-STERNOTOMY wound infection (a dreaded cardiac surgery complication), or DESCENDING NECROTIZING mediastinitis from oropharyngeal/deep-neck infection tracking down fascial planes","Post-sternotomy mediastinitis presents with sternal instability/drainage, fever, and pain; descending necrotizing mediastinitis follows odontogenic/pharyngeal infection with rapid spread","High mortality without prompt drainage, debridement, and antibiotics"],"dx":["CT of the neck/chest defines the extent, fluid/gas, and source; identify the etiology (esophageal leak, sternal wound, descending neck infection)","Post-sternotomy: assess sternal stability and wound; blood/wound cultures (often staph)","Recognize descending necrotizing mediastinitis early (neck infection + mediastinal extension) - it spreads fast"],"tx":["Prompt SOURCE CONTROL: surgical DRAINAGE and DEBRIDEMENT of infected/necrotic tissue, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and treatment of the source (repair esophageal perforation, drain neck spaces)","Post-sternotomy mediastinitis: debridement, often NEGATIVE-PRESSURE wound therapy, and muscle/omental FLAP reconstruction; rewiring vs flap closure by tissue viability","Descending necrotizing mediastinitis: aggressive cervical AND transthoracic drainage of all involved compartments"],"tech":["Sternal wound: debride nonviable bone/tissue, vacuum-assisted closure, then vascularized flap (pectoralis/omental) coverage","Descending necrotizing mediastinitis: combined cervical drainage + thoracic drainage (VATS/thoracotomy) of the posterior/lower mediastinum - inadequate thoracic drainage is a common cause of failure","Wide drainage of all contaminated spaces"],"after":["ICU sepsis management; serial debridement as needed; nutritional support and long antibiotic courses","Monitor for recurrent collections and inadequate drainage (re-image with a low threshold)","Reconstruct once infection is controlled"],"pearls":["Post-sternotomy mediastinitis is a dreaded cardiac surgery complication - debride, vacuum therapy, and muscle/omental flap closure","Descending necrotizing mediastinitis from a neck/dental infection requires BOTH cervical and thoracic drainage - inadequate thoracic drainage causes failure","Esophageal perforation is a leading cause of acute mediastinitis - control the source","Prompt drainage, debridement, and broad-spectrum antibiotics are the pillars; delay raises mortality"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mediastinitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557551/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Sternal Wound Infection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537043/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-chest-wall-tumors","name":"Chest Wall Tumors - Resection & Reconstruction","sec":"thoracic","present":["Chest wall tumors may be PRIMARY (benign: osteochondroma, fibrous dysplasia, chondroma; malignant: CHONDROSARCOMA - the most common primary malignancy, Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma) or SECONDARY (metastasis, direct invasion from lung/breast)","They present as an enlarging (often painful) mass; wide RESECTION with adequate margins is the principle for primary malignancies, followed by RECONSTRUCTION of the skeletal defect and soft-tissue coverage","The reconstructive challenge is restoring chest wall rigidity (to prevent paradoxical motion) and coverage"],"dx":["Image with CT/MRI (extent, invasion) and biopsy appropriately (core/incisional along a resectable axis - plan so the tract is excised); stage for malignancy","Chondrosarcoma is relatively radio/chemo-resistant - WIDE surgical resection with clear margins is key; Ewing/osteosarcoma involve chemotherapy","Assess the size/location of the anticipated defect for reconstruction planning"],"tx":["WIDE en-bloc resection with adequate margins (typically resect the involved rib(s) plus a margin above/below and involved structures) for primary chest wall malignancy","RECONSTRUCT: skeletal stabilization (mesh - e.g., prosthetic mesh, or methyl-methacrylate 'sandwich', or rigid titanium plates) for large anterolateral defects, plus soft-tissue/muscle or myocutaneous flap coverage","Multimodality therapy for chemo/radiosensitive histologies (Ewing/osteosarcoma)"],"tech":["Resect the tumor with wide margins (rib segments + involved lung/soft tissue); reconstruct skeletal defects >~5 cm or anterolateral defects with mesh/methyl-methacrylate or titanium plating to prevent flail","Cover with muscle (latissimus, pectoralis, rectus) or omental flaps; posterior defects under the scapula may not need rigid reconstruction","Coordinate plastic surgery for large soft-tissue coverage"],"after":["Monitor for paradoxical chest wall motion/respiratory compromise if reconstruction is inadequate; manage pain and pulmonary function","Watch for flap/mesh complications (infection, seroma); adjuvant therapy per pathology","Oncologic surveillance"],"pearls":["Chondrosarcoma is the most common primary chest wall malignancy and is radio/chemo-resistant - WIDE resection with clear margins is essential","Reconstruct large/anterolateral skeletal defects (mesh, methyl-methacrylate, or titanium plates) to prevent paradoxical (flail) motion","Cover with muscle/myocutaneous or omental flaps; posterior scapular-covered defects often need no rigid reconstruction","Plan biopsy so the tract is excised en bloc; Ewing/osteosarcoma need multimodality therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chest Wall Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556114/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chest Wall Reconstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560721/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-pectus","name":"Pectus Excavatum & Carinatum","sec":"thoracic","present":["PECTUS EXCAVATUM ('funnel chest') is the most common congenital chest wall deformity - a posterior depression of the sternum/costal cartilages; PECTUS CARINATUM ('pigeon chest') is anterior protrusion","Most are cosmetic/psychosocial concerns, but severe excavatum can cause exertional dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance, and cardiac compression/displacement","Severity is quantified by the HALLER INDEX (transverse chest width / anteroposterior distance on CT; > ~3.25 is severe)"],"dx":["Assess with exam, CT (Haller index/asymmetry, cardiac compression), echocardiography, and pulmonary function/exercise testing when symptomatic","Rule out associated connective tissue disease (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos) and scoliosis","Document objective indications (Haller >3.25, cardiac/pulmonary compromise, progression) beyond cosmesis"],"tx":["PECTUS EXCAVATUM: minimally invasive NUSS procedure (substernal bar) is the standard for most, especially adolescents; open RAVITCH (cartilage resection + sternal osteotomy) for complex/asymmetric or older patients","PECTUS CARINATUM: BRACING is first-line in flexible chests of growing patients; surgery (modified Ravitch) for rigid/severe cases","Timing is ideally around adolescence when the chest is still malleable"],"tech":["NUSS: thoracoscopically guided placement of a curved retrosternal metal bar that is flipped to elevate the sternum, left in place ~2-3 years, then removed","RAVITCH: resect abnormal costal cartilages (preserve perichondrium), perform a sternal osteotomy, and stabilize (often with a temporary support bar)","Take care to avoid cardiac injury passing the substernal Nuss bar (thoracoscopic guidance)"],"after":["Nuss: significant early pain (thoracic epidural/cryoablation of intercostal nerves helps); watch for bar displacement, pericardial/cardiac injury, and pneumothorax","Bar removal after ~2-3 years; good long-term correction and quality-of-life improvement","Carinatum bracing requires adherence over months-years"],"pearls":["Haller index >~3.25 on CT defines severe pectus excavatum; look for cardiac compression and exercise limitation, not just cosmesis","Nuss (minimally invasive substernal bar) is standard for pectus excavatum; Ravitch (open cartilage resection) for complex/older cases","Pectus carinatum responds to bracing in growing, flexible chests - surgery only for rigid/severe cases","Cardiac injury is the feared Nuss complication - thoracoscopic guidance mitigates it; postoperative pain control is a major issue"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pectus Excavatum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430929/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Pectus Carinatum","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556107/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-thoracic-outlet","name":"Thoracic Outlet Syndrome & First-Rib Resection","sec":"thoracic","present":["Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) is compression of the neurovascular structures (brachial plexus, subclavian artery/vein) at the thoracic outlet; it is subclassified as NEUROGENIC (>90%), VENOUS, or ARTERIAL","NEUROGENIC TOS: arm/hand pain, paresthesias, weakness, and hand intrinsic wasting (ulnar-predominant, from lower-plexus/C8-T1 compression)","VENOUS TOS (Paget-Schroetter, 'effort thrombosis'): acute arm swelling/cyanosis after exertion from subclavian vein thrombosis; ARTERIAL TOS: ischemia/emboli, often with a cervical rib"],"dx":["Neurogenic TOS is largely CLINICAL (provocative maneuvers; electrodiagnostics/imaging to exclude other causes); imaging (dynamic CT/MR angiography, venography) confirms vascular subtypes","Identify anatomic causes: CERVICAL RIB or anomalous first rib/fibrous bands, scalene hypertrophy","Arterial TOS: assess for subclavian aneurysm/stenosis (often cervical-rib related) and distal emboli"],"tx":["NEUROGENIC TOS: physical therapy first-line; surgical DECOMPRESSION (first-rib resection + scalenectomy, remove cervical rib/bands) for refractory disease","VENOUS TOS (Paget-Schroetter): catheter-directed THROMBOLYSIS then first-rib resection/decompression (+/- venous reconstruction) to remove the compressive cause","ARTERIAL TOS: rib/cervical-rib resection and arterial repair/reconstruction (aneurysm, emboli)"],"tech":["First-rib resection via transaxillary, supraclavicular, or infraclavicular approach with scalenectomy and removal of cervical rib/fibrous bands to decompress the outlet","Protect the brachial plexus, subclavian vessels, and (on the left) the thoracic duct; long thoracic/phrenic nerves at risk","Vascular reconstruction as needed for arterial/venous subtypes"],"after":["Monitor for pneumothorax, plexus/nerve injury, chylothorax (left side), and vascular injury; physical therapy for recovery","Venous TOS: anticoagulation and vein patency surveillance after decompression","Symptom improvement is generally good with correct patient selection"],"pearls":["Neurogenic TOS (>90%) is largely clinical - lower-plexus (C8-T1) compression causes ulnar-side symptoms and hand intrinsic wasting","Paget-Schroetter (venous/effort thrombosis): thrombolysis THEN first-rib resection to remove the compressive cause","A cervical rib is a common anatomic cause, especially of arterial TOS (subclavian aneurysm/emboli)","First-rib resection + scalenectomy decompresses; protect the plexus, subclavian vessels, and thoracic duct (left)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracic Outlet Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538279/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Paget-Schroetter Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482240/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-diaphragm","name":"Diaphragm - Eventration, Paralysis & Hernia","sec":"thoracic","present":["Diaphragmatic disorders include PARALYSIS (from phrenic nerve injury - surgical, birth, tumor, idiopathic), EVENTRATION (a thin, permanently elevated but intact diaphragm from muscular deficiency), and non-hiatal HERNIAS (congenital Bochdalek/Morgagni, or traumatic)","Unilateral paralysis/eventration causes an elevated hemidiaphragm and may be asymptomatic or cause dyspnea (worse supine); bilateral paralysis causes severe orthopnea and respiratory failure","CONGENITAL diaphragmatic hernia (neonatal) causes pulmonary hypoplasia and is a neonatal emergency"],"dx":["Chest radiograph shows an elevated hemidiaphragm; the SNIFF (fluoroscopic) TEST or ultrasound confirms paralysis (paradoxical upward motion on sniff)","Distinguish paralysis (denervated) from eventration (intact but thin); evaluate the cause (phrenic nerve injury, mediastinal tumor)","Bochdalek (posterolateral, left) vs Morgagni (anterior, retrosternal) hernias on imaging; assess for herniated viscera"],"tx":["Symptomatic unilateral diaphragmatic paralysis/eventration: diaphragm PLICATION (fold and suture the redundant diaphragm taut) to improve mechanics and dyspnea; often minimally invasive","Diaphragmatic hernias: surgical repair (reduce viscera, close the defect +/- mesh); congenital diaphragmatic hernia needs neonatal stabilization (often ECMO) then repair","Asymptomatic unilateral paralysis is often observed"],"tech":["PLICATION: imbricate/fold the flaccid diaphragm with sutures to flatten and tension it (VATS/laparoscopic/open), lowering it out of the chest to increase lung volume","Hernia repair: reduce contents, close the defect primarily or with mesh; congenital diaphragmatic hernia repair after physiologic stabilization","Diaphragm pacing (phrenic nerve stimulation) in selected paralysis (e.g., high spinal cord injury with intact phrenic nerve)"],"after":["Plication improves dyspnea and pulmonary function; monitor for recurrence and effusion","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia: outcome depends on the degree of pulmonary hypoplasia/hypertension","Watch for phrenic nerve injury during nearby thoracic/cardiac surgery (a common iatrogenic cause of paralysis)"],"pearls":["The sniff test confirms diaphragmatic paralysis (paradoxical upward movement); eventration is intact-but-thin, paralysis is denervated","Symptomatic unilateral paralysis/eventration is treated by plication (fold and tension the diaphragm)","Bochdalek hernia = posterolateral (usually left); Morgagni = anterior/retrosternal","Phrenic nerve injury during cardiac/thoracic surgery is a common cause of iatrogenic paralysis - protect it"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Diaphragmatic Paralysis / Eventration","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534090/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Diaphragmatic Hernia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470195/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-chylothorax","name":"Thoracic Duct & Chylothorax Management","sec":"thoracic","present":["CHYLOTHORAX is accumulation of lymphatic fluid (chyle) in the pleural space from THORACIC DUCT injury/obstruction - iatrogenic (esophagectomy, mediastinal/cardiac surgery, neck dissection), traumatic, or from malignancy (lymphoma obstructing the duct)","The thoracic duct runs from the cisterna chyli up the right posterior mediastinum, crossing to the left around T5-6 to drain into the left subclavian/jugular junction - so LOW duct injuries cause RIGHT chylothorax and HIGH injuries cause LEFT","Chyle is MILKY (fat) - drainage increases with enteral fat; chronic loss depletes lymphocytes, protein, and fat-soluble vitamins (immune/nutritional compromise)"],"dx":["Confirm chyle: milky pleural fluid with triglycerides >110 mg/dL and chylomicrons present","Identify the cause (recent surgery, trauma, or evaluate for malignancy - lymphoma - if non-traumatic); lymphangiography/MR lymphangiography can localize the leak","Quantify output (high vs low) to guide conservative vs interventional management"],"tx":["CONSERVATIVE first for low-output leaks: low-fat/MEDIUM-CHAIN-TRIGLYCERIDE diet or NPO + TPN (reduces chyle flow), plus OCTREOTIDE and chest drainage","INTERVENTION for high-output (>~1 L/day) or persistent leaks: thoracic duct LIGATION (surgical) or percutaneous LYMPHANGIOGRAPHY-GUIDED EMBOLIZATION of the duct/cisterna chyli","Pleurodesis or pleuroperitoneal shunt for refractory cases; treat underlying malignancy if that is the cause"],"tech":["Surgical ligation: mass ligation of the tissue between the aorta, azygos vein, and spine just above the diaphragmatic hiatus (low, en-masse ligation captures the duct even if anatomy varies)","Thoracic duct embolization: percutaneous cannulation of the cisterna chyli/duct under lymphangiographic guidance and coil/glue embolization","Fibrin sealant/clipping of an identified leak; pre-op cream/fat load can help visualize the leak"],"after":["Monitor nutritional and immune status (lymphopenia, hypoproteinemia, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency) during prolonged leaks","Escalate from conservative to ligation/embolization if a high-output leak does not settle within a few days","Confirm resolution before removing drains and advancing diet"],"pearls":["Chylothorax = milky pleural fluid, triglycerides >110 mg/dL, chylomicrons present; output rises with dietary fat","Duct anatomy: LOW injury -> RIGHT chylothorax; HIGH injury (above the T5-6 crossover) -> LEFT chylothorax","Low-output leaks: low-fat/MCT diet or TPN + octreotide; high-output/persistent -> thoracic duct ligation or embolization","Chronic chyle loss depletes lymphocytes, protein, and fat-soluble vitamins - watch nutrition/immunity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Chylothorax","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560405/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracic Duct Anatomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482049/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-svc-syndrome","name":"Superior Vena Cava Syndrome & Mediastinal Vascular Involvement","sec":"thoracic","present":["SUPERIOR VENA CAVA (SVC) SYNDROME is obstruction of SVC flow - usually by MALIGNANCY (lung cancer, lymphoma) or, increasingly, by indwelling catheters/pacemaker-lead thrombosis","Presents with face/neck/arm swelling, distended neck and chest-wall veins, plethora, headache, and dyspnea; symptoms worsen when leaning forward/lying down","From a thoracic-surgery standpoint, the questions are tissue diagnosis, relief of obstruction, and, rarely, SVC RESECTION/RECONSTRUCTION when resecting invasive mediastinal tumors (e.g., invasive thymoma, some lung cancers)"],"dx":["CT venography defines the level/cause and collaterals; obtain TISSUE (chemosensitive tumors - small cell lung cancer, lymphoma, germ cell - change management) when the patient is stable","Assess airway/cerebral edema severity (true emergencies are uncommon but occur with airway compromise or cerebral edema)","Evaluate catheter-associated thrombus vs tumor invasion"],"tx":["Most cases: treat the underlying malignancy (chemo/radiation for chemosensitive/radiosensitive tumors) - SVC syndrome is usually not an immediate emergency; ENDOVASCULAR STENTING gives the fastest symptom relief for severe/threatening cases","Catheter-associated thrombosis: anticoagulation +/- catheter removal (not surgery)","SVC RESECTION AND RECONSTRUCTION (patch or graft) as part of en-bloc resection of invasive mediastinal tumors in selected patients"],"tech":["Endovascular stenting of the SVC for rapid decompression; anticoagulation for thrombotic components","Surgical SVC reconstruction: partial resection with patch, or replacement with a ringed PTFE/spiral vein graft during en-bloc tumor resection (heparinize; manage clamp-related venous hypertension)","Protect the phrenic nerves and airway during mediastinal dissection"],"after":["Monitor graft/stent patency (anticoagulation), cerebral/airway edema, and reperfusion; treat the underlying malignancy","After reconstruction, surveillance for thrombosis and tumor recurrence","Head-of-bed elevation and supportive measures for symptom relief"],"pearls":["SVC syndrome is usually NOT an immediate emergency - get tissue first when stable, since chemosensitive tumors (small cell, lymphoma, germ cell) respond to systemic therapy","Endovascular stenting provides the fastest relief for severe/threatening SVC obstruction","Catheter/lead-associated SVC thrombosis is treated with anticoagulation +/- catheter removal, not surgery","SVC resection/reconstruction (patch or ringed graft) enables en-bloc removal of invasive mediastinal tumors in selected patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Superior Vena Cava Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441872/"},{"t":"NCI: Cardiopulmonary Syndromes (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects"}]},{"id":"thoracic-chest-trauma","name":"Blunt & Penetrating Chest Trauma; Rib Fractures & Flail Chest","sec":"thoracic","present":["Chest trauma is a leading cause of trauma mortality; immediately life-threatening injuries are found in the primary survey: TENSION pneumothorax, OPEN pneumothorax, massive HEMOTHORAX, FLAIL chest, and cardiac TAMPONADE","RIB FRACTURES cause pain-related hypoventilation, atelectasis, and pneumonia (especially in the elderly); FLAIL CHEST (>=3 contiguous ribs fractured in >=2 places) produces a free segment with paradoxical motion and underlying pulmonary CONTUSION","Most chest trauma is managed non-operatively (analgesia, chest tubes, pulmonary toilet); a minority need surgery"],"dx":["Primary survey identifies immediate threats (decompress tension pneumothorax, cover open wounds, drain massive hemothorax); chest radiograph, eFAST, and CT characterize injuries","Flail chest: recognize the free segment and the underlying pulmonary contusion (the contusion often drives respiratory failure more than the mechanical flail)","Assess for associated injuries (great vessels, tracheobronchial, diaphragm, cardiac) and the number/pattern of rib fractures"],"tx":["Cornerstones: ANALGESIA (thoracic epidural/paravertebral/intercostal blocks), pulmonary toilet, and chest tubes; avoid over-resuscitation of the contused lung","SURGICAL STABILIZATION OF RIB FRACTURES (SSRF) improves outcomes in selected flail chest/severely displaced fractures (less ventilator time, fewer pulmonary complications)","Operative exploration (thoracotomy) for massive/ongoing hemothorax, or specific injuries (see the next entries)"],"tech":["Chest tube for pneumo/hemothorax; SSRF: open reduction and internal fixation of ribs with plates for flail segments/severely displaced painful fractures","Resuscitative thoracotomy in extremis (penetrating trauma with recent loss of vitals) for aortic cross-clamp, open cardiac massage, and relief of tamponade","Damage-control principles in unstable patients"],"after":["Aggressive pain control and pulmonary toilet prevent pneumonia (particularly in elderly rib-fracture patients - a high-risk group)","Monitor for delayed hemothorax, empyema, and respiratory failure from pulmonary contusion","Early mobilization; incentive spirometry"],"pearls":["Immediately life-threatening chest injuries: tension/open pneumothorax, massive hemothorax, flail chest, cardiac tamponade - found in the primary survey","Flail chest = >=3 contiguous ribs broken in >=2 places; the underlying pulmonary contusion often drives respiratory failure","Analgesia + pulmonary toilet are the cornerstone; surgical rib fixation (SSRF) helps selected flail/displaced fractures","Elderly patients with rib fractures have high pneumonia/mortality risk - aggressive analgesia and monitoring"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Blunt Chest Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493172/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Flail Chest","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560515/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-aortic-injury","name":"Traumatic Aortic & Great-Vessel Injury","sec":"thoracic","present":["BLUNT THORACIC AORTIC INJURY results from rapid deceleration (high-speed collisions, falls); the classic site is the aortic ISTHMUS just distal to the left subclavian artery (tethered by the ligamentum arteriosum)","It is often immediately fatal; survivors to hospital have a contained injury that can rupture - a can't-miss diagnosis","Widened mediastinum on chest radiograph raises suspicion; CT ANGIOGRAPHY is the diagnostic test"],"dx":["Suspect with the mechanism (deceleration) and chest radiograph signs (widened mediastinum, abnormal aortic contour, left apical cap, depressed left mainstem); CONFIRM with CT angiography","Grade the injury (intimal tear -> intramural hematoma -> pseudoaneurysm -> rupture); identify other great-vessel injuries","Assess associated injuries (common in polytrauma) that affect timing"],"tx":["IMPULSE CONTROL: strict anti-impulse therapy (beta-blockade/blood-pressure and heart-rate control, target lower systolic pressure) to reduce aortic wall stress while planning repair","THORACIC ENDOVASCULAR AORTIC REPAIR (TEVAR) is now the preferred treatment for most blunt aortic injuries (lower morbidity/mortality than open repair)","Repair timing: minimal/low-grade injuries may be managed medically with surveillance; higher-grade injuries are repaired (often after stabilizing other life-threatening injuries - delayed selective repair)"],"tech":["TEVAR: endovascular stent-graft coverage of the injured isthmus via femoral access (may cover the left subclavian origin - assess need for revascularization)","Open repair (rare now): left thoracotomy, clamp-and-sew or interposition graft with distal perfusion strategies","Coordinate anti-impulse therapy and injury prioritization in polytrauma"],"after":["Monitor blood pressure/anti-impulse therapy and, after TEVAR, for endoleak, stent complications, and (rarely) spinal ischemia","Surveillance imaging of the repair; manage concomitant injuries","Left subclavian coverage may cause arm/posterior-circulation symptoms - assess"],"pearls":["Blunt aortic injury classically occurs at the isthmus (distal to the left subclavian, at the ligamentum arteriosum) from deceleration","Widened mediastinum on chest radiograph prompts CT angiography - the diagnostic test","Immediate anti-impulse therapy (heart-rate/blood-pressure control) reduces rupture risk while planning repair","TEVAR is now preferred over open repair for most blunt thoracic aortic injuries"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Traumatic Aortic Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430932/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Blunt Aortic Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470387/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-tracheobronchial-injury","name":"Tracheobronchial & Diaphragmatic Injury","sec":"thoracic","present":["TRACHEOBRONCHIAL INJURY (rare, high-mortality) follows blunt or penetrating trauma; blunt airway rupture classically occurs within ~2-3 cm of the CARINA (often the proximal mainstem bronchi)","Signs: persistent large air leak, pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema, and the 'FALLEN LUNG' sign; a pneumothorax with a PERSISTENT AIR LEAK that fails to re-expand after chest tube placement suggests airway injury","DIAPHRAGMATIC INJURY (more common on the LEFT) may present late with herniation of abdominal contents into the chest"],"dx":["Bronchoscopy is the definitive test for airway injury; CT shows pneumomediastinum, airway discontinuity, and the fallen-lung sign","A large persistent air leak and failure to re-expand after chest tube are hallmark clues - pursue bronchoscopy","Diaphragmatic injury: CT (herniated viscera, 'collar/dependent viscera' signs) or findings at laparotomy; a nasogastric tube in the chest is diagnostic"],"tx":["TRACHEOBRONCHIAL: small injuries may be managed conservatively, but most significant airway ruptures require SURGICAL REPAIR (primary repair of the airway); secure the airway distal to/beyond the injury for ventilation","DIAPHRAGMATIC injury: surgical REPAIR (reduce herniated viscera, close the defect +/- mesh) - it will not heal spontaneously and risks late strangulation","Manage associated injuries and ensure lung isolation for airway repair"],"tech":["Airway repair: expose (right thoracotomy for most; the injury site guides approach), debride, and primary repair with absorbable sutures, buttressing with vascularized tissue; lung-isolation/cross-field ventilation with anesthesia","Diaphragm repair: reduce contents, close the defect primarily (non-absorbable) or with mesh for large defects; via laparotomy (acute, to address abdominal injuries) or thoracotomy (chronic)","Confirm distal airway ventilation intraoperatively"],"after":["Airway: monitor for anastomotic dehiscence, stenosis, and fistula; early extubation when possible to protect the repair","Diaphragm: watch for recurrence and pulmonary complications","Bronchoscopic surveillance of airway repairs"],"pearls":["Blunt tracheobronchial rupture usually occurs within ~2-3 cm of the carina; suspect it with a large persistent air leak and non-re-expanding lung after chest tube","Bronchoscopy is the definitive diagnostic test for airway injury; the 'fallen lung' sign is characteristic","Diaphragmatic injury (more common on the left) needs surgical repair - it will not heal and risks late herniation/strangulation","Secure the airway beyond the injury and use lung isolation for airway repair"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Tracheobronchial Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560543/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Diaphragmatic Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459305/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-hemothorax","name":"Hemothorax & Retained Hemothorax","sec":"thoracic","present":["HEMOTHORAX (blood in the pleural space) commonly follows chest trauma (intercostal/internal mammary vessels, lung, great vessels) but also occurs from iatrogenic or spontaneous causes","MASSIVE HEMOTHORAX (>~1500 mL initial output, or ongoing >~200 mL/h for several hours, or hemodynamic instability) is an indication for OPERATIVE exploration (thoracotomy/VATS)","RETAINED HEMOTHORAX (clot not evacuated by the initial chest tube) predisposes to EMPYEMA and fibrothorax/trapped lung"],"dx":["Diagnose on eFAST/chest radiograph/CT; quantify chest-tube output and trend it; assess hemodynamic status","Massive hemothorax criteria (immediate >1500 mL, or persistent >200 mL/h x 2-4 h, or instability) indicate surgery","Retained hemothorax: residual pleural collection on CT after initial drainage"],"tx":["Initial: large-bore CHEST TUBE drainage (evacuates blood, monitors output, and re-expands the lung); resuscitate","OPERATIVE control (VATS/thoracotomy) for massive or ongoing bleeding - identify and control the source (systemic arterial bleeding - intercostal/internal mammary - often does not stop spontaneously)","RETAINED hemothorax: early VATS EVACUATION (within ~3-7 days) prevents empyema/fibrothorax; intrapleural fibrinolytics are an alternative in selected cases"],"tech":["Chest tube (large-bore, directed posteriorly/basally) for drainage; VATS for early retained hemothorax evacuation and clot removal","Thoracotomy for massive hemothorax to control the bleeding source (vessel ligation, lung repair/resection)","Autotransfusion of collected blood in selected trauma settings"],"after":["Ensure complete evacuation and lung re-expansion; watch for empyema and fibrothorax if clot is retained","Monitor ongoing output for the need to operate; treat coagulopathy","Retained collections detected late may need decortication"],"pearls":["Massive hemothorax (>1500 mL immediately, or >200 mL/h for hours, or instability) is an indication for operative exploration","Systemic arterial bleeding (intercostal/internal mammary) often will not stop on its own - operate to control it","Retained hemothorax predisposes to empyema and trapped lung - evacuate early by VATS (within days)","Large-bore chest tube is the initial step; it drains, monitors output, and re-expands the lung"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hemothorax","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538219/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retained Hemothorax","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557661/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-ecmo","name":"ECMO & Mechanical Cardiopulmonary Support","sec":"thoracic","present":["EXTRACORPOREAL MEMBRANE OXYGENATION (ECMO) provides temporary heart/lung support by draining venous blood, oxygenating/removing CO2 across a membrane, and returning it - a bridge to recovery, decision, or transplant/durable device","VENO-VENOUS (VV) ECMO supports the LUNGS (severe respiratory failure/ARDS - e.g., EOLIA trial) with native cardiac output intact; VENO-ARTERIAL (VA) ECMO supports the HEART AND lungs (cardiogenic shock, cardiac arrest)","Cannulation and complication management are core cardiothoracic/critical-care skills"],"dx":["Select the mode by the physiologic deficit: VV for isolated respiratory failure (adequate cardiac function), VA for cardiogenic shock/arrest (circulatory support)","Assess candidacy (reversible or bridgeable process; exclude futility) and cannulation targets (femoral/jugular vessels)","Anticipate mode-specific complications (see below)"],"tx":["VV ECMO for refractory severe ARDS/respiratory failure (support oxygenation/CO2 removal while the lungs rest and heal); VA ECMO for cardiogenic shock/refractory arrest (eCPR)","Anticoagulate (usually heparin) and manage the circuit; wean as native function recovers","Bridge to recovery, durable device (LVAD), or transplant as appropriate"],"tech":["VV: drainage and return both in the venous system (e.g., femoral-to-jugular, or a dual-lumen single cannula); VA: venous drainage + arterial return (central, or peripheral femoral artery/vein)","Peripheral VA ECMO risks LIMB ISCHEMIA (place a distal perfusion cannula) and NORTH-SOUTH/Harlequin syndrome (differential hypoxia - well-oxygenated lower body, hypoxic upper body/coronaries/brain if native lungs are poor)","LV distension on VA ECMO may need venting/unloading"],"after":["Complications: bleeding (anticoagulation), thrombosis, hemolysis, stroke, infection; VA-specific: limb ischemia, LV distension, differential hypoxia","Monitor end-organ perfusion, upper-body oxygenation (right radial ABG) on VA ECMO, and daily circuit assessment","Structured weaning and neuro/functional assessment"],"pearls":["VV ECMO supports the LUNGS (respiratory failure, native circulation intact); VA ECMO supports HEART and lungs (cardiogenic shock/arrest)","Peripheral VA ECMO: place a distal perfusion cannula to prevent limb ischemia; watch for LV distension and differential hypoxia (Harlequin)","EOLIA informed VV ECMO use in severe ARDS; ECMO is a bridge to recovery, decision, transplant, or durable device","Bleeding and thrombosis (from anticoagulation and the circuit) are the major ongoing complications"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556152/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Venoarterial ECMO","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499804/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-cpb","name":"Cardiopulmonary Bypass & Myocardial Protection","sec":"thoracic","present":["CARDIOPULMONARY BYPASS (CPB) diverts venous blood to a machine that oxygenates and returns it, allowing a still, bloodless field for cardiac surgery while an oxygenator and pump replace heart/lung function","MYOCARDIAL PROTECTION during the aortic cross-clamp period uses CARDIOPLEGIA (high-potassium solution, warm or cold, antegrade and/or retrograde) to arrest the heart in diastole and minimize ischemic injury","CPB triggers a systemic inflammatory response, hemodilution, and coagulopathy - understanding its physiology underlies safe cardiac surgery"],"dx":["Plan cannulation (arterial - usually ascending aorta; venous - right atrium/cavae) and confirm adequate anticoagulation (ACT) before initiating CPB","Monitor on-bypass parameters (flow, perfusion pressure, temperature, ACT, blood gases, hematocrit); assess for aortic insufficiency (affects antegrade cardioplegia delivery)","Determine cardioplegia strategy (antegrade vs retrograde, blood vs crystalloid, warm vs cold) by the operation and coronary anatomy"],"tx":["Deliver CARDIOPLEGIA to arrest and protect the myocardium during cross-clamp; use retrograde (coronary sinus) delivery when severe coronary stenoses limit antegrade distribution or during aortic valve surgery","Systemic heparinization before cannulation (reverse with protamine after weaning); manage hemodilution/temperature","Wean from bypass as cardiac function recovers, then decannulate and reverse anticoagulation"],"tech":["Cannulate the ascending aorta (arterial return) and right atrium/venae cavae (venous drainage); heparinize (ACT typically >400-480 s) before cannulation","Cross-clamp the aorta and deliver cardioplegia antegrade (aortic root) and/or retrograde (coronary sinus); vent the LV to prevent distension","Wean with gradual volume/rewarming; give PROTAMINE to reverse heparin after separating from bypass"],"after":["Post-bypass issues: bleeding/coagulopathy (platelet dysfunction, residual heparin), vasoplegia, arrhythmias, and low cardiac output; watch for protamine reactions","Neurocognitive effects and systemic inflammatory response; monitor end-organ perfusion (renal, cerebral)","ICU management of hemodynamics, rewarming, and bleeding"],"pearls":["CPB provides a still, bloodless field; cardioplegia (high-K, arrests in diastole) protects the myocardium during the aortic cross-clamp","Retrograde (coronary sinus) cardioplegia helps when severe coronary stenoses limit antegrade delivery or during aortic valve surgery","Heparinize (high ACT) before cannulation; reverse with protamine after weaning - watch for protamine reactions","CPB causes a systemic inflammatory response, hemodilution, and coagulopathy - anticipate post-bypass bleeding and vasoplegia"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiopulmonary Bypass","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482190/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardioplegia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549789/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-cabg","name":"Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG)","sec":"thoracic","present":["CORONARY ARTERY BYPASS GRAFTING revascularizes the myocardium by routing grafts around obstructive coronary lesions; it is favored over PCI for LEFT MAIN and complex MULTIVESSEL disease, especially with diabetes or reduced LV function (SYNTAX, FREEDOM trials)","The LEFT INTERNAL MAMMARY ARTERY (LIMA) to the LEFT ANTERIOR DESCENDING (LAD) is the single most important graft - its long-term patency drives survival benefit","Additional conduits include the radial artery, right internal mammary, and saphenous vein"],"dx":["Define coronary anatomy (angiography) and lesion complexity (SYNTAX score); assess LV function, viability, and comorbidity","Heart-team decision (CABG vs PCI): CABG generally preferred for left main/triple-vessel disease, diabetics, and reduced ejection fraction","Plan conduits and targets; assess the LIMA and other conduit availability"],"tx":["Perform CABG for left main/complex multivessel disease and in diabetics with multivessel disease (survival/MACE benefit over PCI); always graft the LIMA to the LAD when possible","On-pump (arrested heart) vs off-pump (beating heart) per patient/surgeon; total arterial revascularization improves long-term patency in selected patients","Optimize secondary prevention (antiplatelets, statins, risk-factor control) postoperatively"],"tech":["Median sternotomy; harvest conduits (LIMA pedicle, radial artery, saphenous vein); establish CPB and cardioplegic arrest (or off-pump stabilization)","Construct distal anastomoses to coronary targets beyond the stenoses and proximal anastomoses to the aorta (vein/free grafts); LIMA-to-LAD in situ","Confirm graft flow (transit-time flowmetry) before closing"],"after":["Monitor for perioperative MI, low cardiac output, atrial fibrillation, bleeding, stroke, sternal wound infection/mediastinitis, and renal dysfunction","Aggressive secondary prevention (aspirin, high-intensity statin, risk-factor modification) and cardiac rehabilitation","Graft patency depends on conduit (arterial > vein) and target quality"],"pearls":["CABG is preferred over PCI for left main and complex multivessel disease, and in diabetics with multivessel disease (SYNTAX, FREEDOM)","The LIMA-to-LAD graft is the key to the survival benefit - its long-term patency is excellent","Total/multi-arterial revascularization improves long-term patency versus vein grafts","Postoperative secondary prevention (antiplatelet, statin, risk-factor control) is essential for graft longevity"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507796/"},{"t":"NHLBI: Coronary Artery Disease","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease"}]},{"id":"thoracic-aortic-valve","name":"Aortic Valve Disease - SAVR vs TAVR","sec":"thoracic","present":["Aortic STENOSIS (commonly calcific/degenerative, or from a bicuspid valve) causes exertional dyspnea, angina, and syncope; symptomatic severe AS or AS with reduced LV function warrants valve replacement","Aortic REGURGITATION (from root dilation, bicuspid valve, endocarditis) is treated by replacement/repair when symptomatic or with LV dilation/dysfunction","Replacement is by SURGICAL aortic valve replacement (SAVR) or TRANSCATHETER aortic valve replacement (TAVR); the choice hinges on surgical risk, age, and anatomy"],"dx":["Grade severity by echocardiography (valve area, mean gradient, jet velocity); assess symptoms and LV function; low-flow/low-gradient AS may need dobutamine echo","CT for TAVR planning (annular sizing, access, coronary heights, calcification); evaluate surgical risk (STS score) and frailty","Heart-team evaluation to choose SAVR vs TAVR"],"tx":["TAVR is now first-line for many patients across the risk spectrum (high, intermediate, and selected low risk - PARTNER, Evolut trials), especially older patients; SAVR is favored for younger/low-risk patients, bicuspid anatomy, concomitant surgery, or unfavorable TAVR anatomy","Mechanical vs bioprosthetic valve choice at SAVR balances durability against lifelong anticoagulation (mechanical - warfarin; bioprosthetic - no long-term anticoagulation but limited durability); guided by age/patient preference","Manage aortic regurgitation surgically (repair possible in selected cases)"],"tech":["SAVR: sternotomy (or minimally invasive), CPB and cardioplegic arrest, excise the diseased valve, and implant a prosthesis (mechanical or bioprosthetic)","TAVR: transcatheter (usually transfemoral) deployment of a bioprosthesis within the native valve without removing it","Choose valve type/size and access per imaging and heart-team plan"],"after":["TAVR watch: paravalvular leak, conduction block needing PACEMAKER, vascular access complications, and stroke; SAVR watch: bleeding, arrhythmia, wound issues","Mechanical valves require lifelong warfarin (INR targets by position); bioprosthetic valves risk structural degeneration over time","Endocarditis prophylaxis and echocardiographic surveillance"],"pearls":["Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis (or with LV dysfunction) needs valve replacement - medical therapy does not change its natural history","TAVR is first-line for many patients (high/intermediate/selected low risk - PARTNER, Evolut); SAVR favored for young/low-risk, bicuspid, or concomitant surgery","Mechanical valves (durable, need lifelong warfarin) vs bioprosthetic (no long-term anticoagulation, less durable) - choose by age/preference","TAVR risks include new conduction block requiring a pacemaker and paravalvular leak"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Valve Replacement","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507860/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Stenosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557628/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-mitral-valve","name":"Mitral Valve Disease - Repair vs Replacement","sec":"thoracic","present":["Mitral REGURGITATION is PRIMARY/degenerative (leaflet/chordal pathology - e.g., prolapse, flail) or SECONDARY/functional (from LV dilation/ischemia with structurally normal leaflets); mitral STENOSIS is usually rheumatic","For PRIMARY (degenerative) severe MR, mitral REPAIR is strongly preferred over replacement - better preservation of LV function, lower operative mortality, and no need for long-term anticoagulation","Timing and repair durability depend on mechanism, symptoms, LV size/function, and center expertise"],"dx":["Echocardiography (often transesophageal) defines MR mechanism, severity, leaflet/chordal anatomy, LV size/function, and repairability","Indications for intervention: symptomatic severe primary MR, or asymptomatic severe MR with LV dilation/dysfunction, new atrial fibrillation, or pulmonary hypertension","Distinguish primary (repair the valve) from secondary/functional MR (treat the LV/ischemia; intervention more nuanced)"],"tx":["PRIMARY degenerative MR: mitral REPAIR (leaflet resection/neochordae + annuloplasty ring) is the treatment of choice - refer to experienced repair centers","Replacement when repair is not feasible; secondary/functional MR - optimize guideline-directed heart-failure therapy/revascularization first, with transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (COAPT) or surgery in selected patients","Rheumatic mitral stenosis: percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty (suitable anatomy) or valve replacement"],"tech":["Repair: annuloplasty ring plus leaflet techniques (triangular/quadrangular resection, neochordae) via sternotomy or minimally invasive (right mini-thoracotomy); test with saline and intraoperative TEE","Replacement: preserve the subvalvular apparatus when possible (protects LV function); mechanical vs bioprosthetic per age/anticoagulation considerations","Transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (clip) for selected high-risk/secondary MR"],"after":["Repair avoids long-term anticoagulation (with a ring, short-term only) and preserves LV function; monitor for residual/recurrent MR","Replacement: anticoagulation per valve type; watch for LV dysfunction, arrhythmia, and prosthesis issues","Echocardiographic surveillance and endocarditis prophylaxis"],"pearls":["For primary/degenerative severe MR, REPAIR beats replacement - better LV preservation, lower mortality, no lifelong anticoagulation","Intervene in symptomatic severe MR, or asymptomatic severe MR with LV dilation/dysfunction, new AF, or pulmonary hypertension","Secondary/functional MR: optimize heart-failure therapy first; transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (COAPT) helps selected patients","Preserve the subvalvular apparatus during replacement to protect LV function"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Mitral Valve Repair / Replacement","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564376/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Mitral Regurgitation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430907/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-aortic-aneurysm-dissection","name":"Aortic Root/Ascending/Arch Aneurysm & Type A Dissection","sec":"thoracic","present":["Thoracic aortic aneurysms of the ROOT/ASCENDING aorta and ARCH arise from degeneration, connective tissue disease (MARFAN, Loeys-Dietz), or bicuspid-valve aortopathy; repair thresholds are size-based (generally ~5.5 cm ascending, lower ~4.5-5.0 cm for connective tissue disease/bicuspid or rapid growth)","ACUTE TYPE A AORTIC DISSECTION (involving the ascending aorta) is a SURGICAL EMERGENCY - untreated mortality rises ~1-2% per hour - presenting with sudden tearing chest/back pain +/- malperfusion, tamponade, or aortic regurgitation","Type B dissection (descending only) is usually managed medically (see the descending aorta/TEVAR entry)"],"dx":["CT angiography is the diagnostic test (intimal flap, true/false lumen, extent, tamponade, malperfusion); echocardiography assesses aortic valve/root and tamponade","Type A (ascending involved) = emergency surgery; classify extent and complications (coronary, arch vessels, malperfusion)","Screen for connective tissue disease/bicuspid aortopathy and family history in aneurysms"],"tx":["ACUTE TYPE A DISSECTION: EMERGENCY surgical repair (replace the ascending aorta +/- root/arch; resuspend or replace the aortic valve) to prevent rupture/tamponade/death - meanwhile control blood pressure/heart rate (anti-impulse therapy)","ANEURYSM: elective replacement at size thresholds (lower for Marfan/Loeys-Dietz/bicuspid or rapid growth); root replacement options include a valve-sparing root replacement (David) or a composite valve-graft (Bentall)","Manage malperfusion syndromes and involve the heart/aortic team"],"tech":["Ascending/arch repair on CPB with cardioplegia; arch work uses DEEP HYPOTHERMIC CIRCULATORY ARREST with cerebral perfusion (antegrade/retrograde) to protect the brain","Root: valve-sparing (David) reimplantation when the valve is salvageable, or Bentall composite valve-graft with coronary reimplantation; hemiarch/total arch as needed (elephant trunk for extensive disease)","Restore true-lumen flow and address malperfusion"],"after":["Watch for bleeding (coagulopathy after circulatory arrest), stroke/neurologic injury, malperfusion, renal failure, and tamponade","Lifelong blood-pressure control and surveillance imaging (residual dissection/aneurysm can progress); genetic evaluation/family screening in connective tissue disease","Reoperation may be needed for distal aortic progression"],"pearls":["Acute type A dissection (ascending aorta) is a surgical emergency - mortality rises ~1-2%/hour untreated; type B (descending) is usually medical","Repair aneurysms at size thresholds (~5.5 cm ascending - lowered to ~5.0 cm at experienced multidisciplinary aortic centers per the 2022 ACC/AHA guideline; ~4.5-5.0 cm for Marfan/Loeys-Dietz/bicuspid or rapid growth >=0.5 cm/yr)","Arch repair requires deep hypothermic circulatory arrest with cerebral perfusion to protect the brain","Root options: valve-sparing (David) if the valve is salvageable, or Bentall composite graft; screen for connective tissue disease and family history"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Dissection","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441963/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracic Aortic Aneurysm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431109/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-descending-aorta-tevar","name":"Descending & Thoracoabdominal Aorta; Type B Dissection & TEVAR","sec":"thoracic","present":["Disease of the DESCENDING thoracic and THORACOABDOMINAL aorta includes degenerative aneurysms and type B aortic dissection (dissection NOT involving the ascending aorta/arch)","UNCOMPLICATED type B dissection is managed MEDICALLY (anti-impulse therapy - heart-rate/blood-pressure control); COMPLICATED type B dissection (malperfusion, rupture, refractory pain/hypertension, rapid expansion) requires intervention","Thoracoabdominal aneurysms are classified by the CRAWFORD system by extent; repair thresholds are size/growth/symptom based"],"dx":["CT angiography defines extent, true/false lumen, branch-vessel involvement, malperfusion, and rupture; classify type B (descending only) vs type A (ascending - emergency surgery)","Assess malperfusion syndromes (visceral, renal, limb, spinal) and connective tissue disease","For aneurysms, use Crawford extent classification and plan spinal cord protection"],"tx":["UNCOMPLICATED type B: optimal medical therapy (strict anti-impulse control) with surveillance; many stabilize","COMPLICATED type B: THORACIC ENDOVASCULAR AORTIC REPAIR (TEVAR) is first-line - cover the primary entry tear to redirect flow into the true lumen and treat malperfusion/rupture","Thoracoabdominal aneurysm: open repair, endovascular (branched/fenestrated), or hybrid, with spinal cord protection strategies"],"tech":["TEVAR: transfemoral stent-graft coverage of the proximal entry tear; may need branch revascularization or fenestrated/branched devices for visceral vessels","Open thoracoabdominal repair: thoracoabdominal incision, sequential clamping, reattach intercostal/visceral vessels; SPINAL CORD PROTECTION (CSF drainage, permissive proximal hypertension, distal perfusion, reimplant critical intercostals)","Manage left subclavian coverage (revascularize when needed)"],"after":["Feared complication is SPINAL CORD ISCHEMIA/paraplegia - CSF drainage, blood-pressure augmentation, and monitoring; also watch endoleak, stroke, renal/visceral ischemia","Lifelong blood-pressure control and surveillance imaging (false-lumen aneurysmal degeneration can progress after type B dissection)","Reintervention may be needed for endoleak or distal progression"],"pearls":["Uncomplicated type B dissection is medical (anti-impulse therapy); complicated type B (malperfusion, rupture, refractory pain/HTN) -> TEVAR","TEVAR covers the primary entry tear to redirect flow into the true lumen - now first-line for complicated type B","Spinal cord ischemia/paraplegia is the feared complication of descending/thoracoabdominal repair - CSF drainage and blood-pressure augmentation are protective","Thoracoabdominal aneurysms use the Crawford extent classification; false-lumen degeneration can progress after type B dissection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Aortic Dissection (Type B)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532980/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Thoracoabdominal Aortic Aneurysm","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562161/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-endocarditis-surgery","name":"Infective Endocarditis - Surgical Indications","sec":"thoracic","present":["Infective endocarditis (IE) is infection of the heart valves/endocardium; most cases are treated with prolonged antibiotics, but a subset needs SURGERY","Surgical indications (any of): HEART FAILURE from valve dysfunction, UNCONTROLLED INFECTION (persistent bacteremia/fever despite appropriate antibiotics, abscess, fistula, or difficult organisms - fungal/multidrug-resistant), and PREVENTION OF EMBOLISM (large mobile vegetations, especially >10 mm with prior embolism)","Prosthetic valve endocarditis and perivalvular abscess frequently require surgery"],"dx":["Diagnose with the modified DUKE criteria (blood cultures + echocardiographic evidence); transesophageal echo defines vegetations, abscess, leaflet destruction, and prosthetic involvement","Identify the organism and complications (heart failure, abscess/fistula, emboli - including septic cerebral emboli that affect surgical timing)","Assess for the three main surgical indications above"],"tx":["Foundation is targeted, prolonged IV ANTIBIOTICS; operate for the accepted indications (heart failure, uncontrolled infection, high embolic risk)","SURGERY = radical debridement of infected tissue and valve REPAIR or REPLACEMENT (+/- root/abscess reconstruction); timing is often early when strong indications are present","Manage septic emboli and coordinate neuro assessment when cerebral emboli affect timing of cardiac surgery"],"tech":["Excise all infected/necrotic tissue, drain/obliterate abscesses, and repair or replace the valve; extensive destruction/abscess may need homograft/root replacement","Debride perivalvular abscess and reconstruct the annulus; prosthetic valve endocarditis usually needs replacement","Intraoperative TEE to confirm repair and rule out residual infection/regurgitation"],"after":["Complete the full antibiotic course; monitor for recurrent infection, heart block (from perivalvular/abscess involvement near the conduction system - may need a pacemaker), and valve dysfunction","Watch for embolic complications and manage anticoagulation per prosthesis","Dental/procedural endocarditis prophylaxis and source control afterward"],"pearls":["Three surgical indications in endocarditis: heart failure from valve dysfunction, uncontrolled infection (persistent sepsis, abscess, resistant/fungal organisms), and high embolic risk (large mobile vegetations)","Surgery = radical debridement + valve repair/replacement; perivalvular abscess may need root/homograft reconstruction","Perivalvular abscess near the conduction system can cause heart block (watch for a new need for pacing)","Prosthetic valve endocarditis usually requires surgery; TEE defines vegetations, abscess, and leaflet destruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Infective Endocarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557641/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Endocarditis Surgery Indications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542249/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lvad-mcs","name":"Advanced Heart Failure Surgery - LVAD & Mechanical Support","sec":"thoracic","present":["MECHANICAL CIRCULATORY SUPPORT (MCS) treats advanced (stage D) heart failure refractory to medical therapy; a LEFT VENTRICULAR ASSIST DEVICE (LVAD) is a continuous-flow pump that unloads the LV and pumps blood to the aorta","LVAD strategies: BRIDGE TO TRANSPLANT (BTT), DESTINATION THERAPY (permanent, for transplant-ineligible patients), or bridge to decision/recovery","Temporary MCS (IABP, Impella, ECMO) supports acute cardiogenic shock as a bridge to recovery or durable device"],"dx":["Select candidates by heart-failure staging, hemodynamics (right heart catheterization), end-organ function, and RIGHT ventricular function (RV failure is a major post-LVAD risk)","Assess reversibility, transplant candidacy, and psychosocial/support factors","For acute shock, choose the temporary device by the degree/mechanism of support needed"],"tx":["Durable continuous-flow LVAD (current devices are magnetically levitated centrifugal pumps - e.g., MOMENTUM 3 established the newer device as superior) for advanced HF as BTT or destination therapy","Temporary MCS (IABP, Impella, VA ECMO) for acute cardiogenic shock as a bridge to recovery/decision/durable device","Optimize RV function and anticoagulation; total artificial heart in selected biventricular failure"],"tech":["LVAD implant: inflow cannula in the LV apex, pump, and outflow graft to the ascending aorta; usually via sternotomy on CPB (some less-invasive approaches)","Manage RV function (avoid RV failure - inotropes, pulmonary vasodilators, sometimes temporary RVAD); driveline exits the skin to the controller/batteries","Careful de-airing and speed optimization"],"after":["Major complications: bleeding, driveline/pump infection, pump thrombosis, STROKE (embolic/hemorrhagic), and RIGHT HEART FAILURE; require anticoagulation (warfarin + antiplatelet)","GI bleeding from acquired von Willebrand deficiency and arteriovenous malformations is common with continuous-flow devices","Blood-pressure control (MAP targets) and infection prevention are central to long-term management"],"pearls":["Continuous-flow LVADs treat advanced (stage D) HF as bridge to transplant or destination therapy; magnetically levitated centrifugal pumps (MOMENTUM 3) improved outcomes","Right heart failure is the major post-LVAD risk - assess RV function before implant","Continuous-flow complications: pump thrombosis, stroke, driveline infection, and GI bleeding (acquired von Willebrand + AVMs)","Temporary MCS (IABP, Impella, VA ECMO) bridges acute cardiogenic shock to recovery, decision, or a durable device"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Left Ventricular Assist Device","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499841/"},{"t":"NHLBI: Heart Failure","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure"}]},{"id":"thoracic-adult-congenital","name":"Adult Congenital Heart Disease - Common Lesions Overview","sec":"thoracic","present":["Adult congenital heart disease (ACHD) includes lesions surviving to or newly presenting in adulthood; the common ones are ATRIAL SEPTAL DEFECT (ASD), VENTRICULAR SEPTAL DEFECT (VSD), PATENT DUCTUS ARTERIOSUS (PDA), and COARCTATION of the aorta","Left-to-right shunts (ASD/VSD/PDA) cause volume overload and, if untreated and large, pulmonary hypertension progressing to EISENMENGER physiology (shunt reversal - a contraindication to closure)","Coarctation causes upper-extremity hypertension with a brachial-femoral pulse delay and can present in adulthood"],"dx":["Echocardiography defines the lesion, shunt direction/magnitude (Qp:Qs), chamber enlargement, and pulmonary pressures; CT/MRI and catheterization refine anatomy and hemodynamics","ASD signs: fixed split S2, RV volume overload; coarctation: differential blood pressure/pulses, rib notching on radiograph","Assess for Eisenmenger physiology (irreversible pulmonary hypertension - closure contraindicated)"],"tx":["ASD: close (device or surgical) for significant left-to-right shunt (RV enlargement, Qp:Qs elevated) before pulmonary hypertension becomes fixed - secundum ASDs are often device-closed","VSD/PDA: close hemodynamically significant defects (device or surgical) unless Eisenmenger physiology has developed","COARCTATION: repair (surgical or catheter stent/angioplasty) for significant gradient/hypertension"],"tech":["Secundum ASD and many PDAs: transcatheter device closure; sinus venosus/primum ASD and large/complex VSDs: surgical patch closure on CPB","Coarctation: resection with end-to-end anastomosis, patch/graft, or endovascular stenting depending on age/anatomy","Manage associated lesions and pulmonary vascular disease"],"after":["Long-term ACHD follow-up for arrhythmia, residual shunt, pulmonary hypertension, and (coarctation) re-coarctation/aneurysm and persistent hypertension","Endocarditis considerations and pregnancy counseling in reproductive-age patients","Lifelong specialized ACHD surveillance"],"pearls":["Common ACHD lesions: ASD (fixed split S2, RV overload), VSD, PDA, and coarctation (upper-extremity HTN, brachial-femoral delay, rib notching)","Close significant left-to-right shunts BEFORE fixed pulmonary hypertension develops - Eisenmenger physiology contraindicates closure","Secundum ASDs and many PDAs are device-closed; sinus venosus/primum ASD and complex VSDs need surgery","Coarctation causes upper-extremity hypertension; repair for significant gradient - watch for re-coarctation and persistent HTN"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Septal Defect","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535440/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Coarctation of the Aorta","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430913/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-pericardial-disease","name":"Pericardial Disease - Tamponade, Constriction & Pericardiectomy","sec":"thoracic","present":["Pericardial disease relevant to surgery includes cardiac TAMPONADE (pericardial fluid compressing the heart) and CONSTRICTIVE pericarditis (a rigid, often calcified pericardium limiting diastolic filling)","TAMPONADE presents with Beck's triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus, and obstructive shock - a life-threatening emergency","CONSTRICTION presents with right-heart failure signs (elevated JVP, edema, ascites), Kussmaul's sign, and a pericardial knock; it can mimic restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"dx":["Tamponade: echocardiography shows effusion with diastolic chamber collapse (RA/RV), IVC plethora, and respiratory flow variation; it is a clinical/echo diagnosis","Constriction: imaging (echo with ventricular interdependence/septal bounce, CT/MRI showing pericardial thickening/calcification) and catheterization (equalization of diastolic pressures, dip-and-plateau)","Distinguish constriction from restrictive cardiomyopathy (management differs - surgery vs medical)"],"tx":["TAMPONADE: urgent DRAINAGE - pericardiocentesis or a surgical PERICARDIAL WINDOW; treat the underlying cause; avoid excessive positive-pressure ventilation before drainage in unstable patients","Recurrent/loculated effusions: surgical pericardial window (subxiphoid or thoracoscopic) for drainage and pericardial biopsy","CONSTRICTION: definitive treatment is surgical PERICARDIECTOMY (removal of the constricting pericardium) for symptomatic patients"],"tech":["Pericardial window: subxiphoid or VATS approach to drain and create a communication (to pleura/peritoneum), with pericardial biopsy for diagnosis","Pericardiectomy: radical resection of the parietal (and often visceral) pericardium, freeing the ventricles; can be done off-pump or on CPB - meticulous dissection off the epicardium/coronaries","Manage the sometimes densely adherent, calcified pericardium carefully to avoid myocardial/coronary injury"],"after":["After pericardiectomy, watch for LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT (the deconditioned myocardium may take time to recover), bleeding, and arrhythmia","Tamponade drainage: monitor for reaccumulation; treat the underlying etiology (malignancy, infection, uremia, post-cardiac injury)","Pericardiectomy for advanced/calcific constriction carries meaningful operative risk"],"pearls":["Tamponade = Beck's triad + pulsus paradoxus + echo diastolic chamber collapse - urgent pericardiocentesis or surgical window","Constrictive pericarditis: elevated JVP, Kussmaul's sign, pericardial knock, ventricular interdependence; definitive treatment is pericardiectomy","Distinguish constriction (surgical) from restrictive cardiomyopathy (medical) - catheterization and imaging help","After pericardiectomy, low cardiac output can occur as the chronically constrained myocardium recovers"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Tamponade","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431090/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Constrictive Pericarditis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459307/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-cardiac-tumors","name":"Cardiac Tumors - Myxoma & Others","sec":"thoracic","present":["Primary cardiac tumors are rare; MOST are BENIGN, and MYXOMA is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults - typically arising in the LEFT ATRIUM from the interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis","Myxoma presents with a triad of OBSTRUCTIVE (mitral-valve obstruction mimicking mitral stenosis, positional dyspnea/syncope), EMBOLIC (systemic emboli - stroke), and CONSTITUTIONAL (fever, weight loss, elevated inflammatory markers) features","METASTATIC tumors to the heart are far more common than primary tumors; the most common primary MALIGNANT cardiac tumor is angiosarcoma. In children, rhabdomyoma (associated with tuberous sclerosis) is most common"],"dx":["Echocardiography (often transesophageal) identifies the mass, attachment, and mobility; CT/MRI characterize tissue and extent","Myxoma: a mobile, pedunculated left atrial mass attached to the septum; evaluate for embolic sources and obstruction","Differentiate benign (myxoma, papillary fibroelastoma, rhabdomyoma, fibroma) from malignant (angiosarcoma) and metastatic disease"],"tx":["MYXOMA: prompt surgical RESECTION (risk of embolization and obstruction/sudden death) - excise the tumor with a margin of attachment (interatrial septum), repairing the resulting septal defect","Papillary fibroelastoma: resect if symptomatic/embolic or large/mobile; malignant tumors (angiosarcoma) have a poor prognosis - resection +/- multimodality therapy in selected cases","Metastatic involvement: treat the underlying malignancy; surgery is usually palliative"],"tech":["Resect myxoma on CPB via atriotomy; remove the tumor with a full-thickness cuff of the interatrial septum to reduce recurrence, then patch/repair the septum","Avoid tumor fragmentation/embolization during manipulation; inspect all chambers/valves","Reconstruct the valve if involved"],"after":["Myxoma resection is usually curative with low recurrence; sporadic myxomas recur rarely, but FAMILIAL/CARNEY COMPLEX myxomas recur more often (surveillance)","Monitor for arrhythmia and residual septal defect/valve dysfunction","Angiosarcoma: poor prognosis, high recurrence despite resection"],"pearls":["Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults - usually left atrial, from the interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis","Classic triad: obstruction (mitral-valve, mimics MS), embolism (stroke), and constitutional symptoms - resect promptly given embolic/obstructive risk","Excise with a septal margin and repair the defect; Carney complex/familial myxomas recur more often (surveillance)","Metastases to the heart are commoner than primaries; angiosarcoma is the most common primary malignant cardiac tumor"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Myxoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470198/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Tumors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534801/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-surgical-af-ablation","name":"Surgical Atrial Fibrillation Ablation - Maze Procedure","sec":"thoracic","present":["Atrial fibrillation (AF) is common and causes symptoms, stroke risk, and (long-term) atrial/ventricular dysfunction; SURGICAL ablation addresses AF, most often as a CONCOMITANT procedure during other cardiac surgery (valve, CABG)","The COX-MAZE procedure creates a set of scar lines in the atria that block the reentrant circuits sustaining AF, restoring organized conduction from the sinus node to the AV node","The modern COX-MAZE IV uses radiofrequency/cryoablation to create the lesion set (rather than the original cut-and-sew), plus LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE management"],"dx":["Characterize the AF (paroxysmal vs persistent/long-standing persistent), left atrial size, and stroke risk (CHA2DS2-VASc); persistent AF and larger atria predict lower ablation success","Evaluate concomitant cardiac disease driving the operation (mitral valve disease is strongly associated with AF)","Assess anticoagulation status and left atrial appendage thrombus"],"tx":["Offer surgical (Cox-Maze) ablation concomitantly during cardiac surgery in patients with AF, especially mitral surgery; standalone minimally invasive ablation for selected symptomatic drug/catheter-refractory AF","LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE closure/excision reduces stroke risk and is a key part of the procedure","Coordinate with rhythm management and postoperative anticoagulation"],"tech":["Cox-Maze IV: create the biatrial lesion set with radiofrequency and/or cryoablation (pulmonary vein isolation plus connecting left- and right-atrial lines), replacing the original cut-and-sew Maze","Manage the LEFT ATRIAL APPENDAGE (excision or closure) to lower embolic risk","Perform during the concomitant cardiac operation on CPB"],"after":["Early postoperative atrial arrhythmias are common; continue anticoagulation and antiarrhythmics during the healing/blanking period","Some patients need a permanent PACEMAKER (sinus node dysfunction); assess rhythm over follow-up","Success is higher for paroxysmal AF and smaller atria than for long-standing persistent AF"],"pearls":["The Cox-Maze creates atrial scar lines that interrupt AF reentry; the modern Maze IV uses radiofrequency/cryoablation plus left atrial appendage management","Surgical AF ablation is most often concomitant with other cardiac surgery (especially mitral valve disease, strongly linked to AF)","Left atrial appendage closure/excision is integral - it reduces stroke risk","Success is higher for paroxysmal AF/smaller atria; watch for early postoperative arrhythmias and possible need for pacing"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Maze Procedure","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556141/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Atrial Fibrillation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526072/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-lung-transplant","name":"Lung Transplantation","sec":"thoracic","present":["Lung transplantation treats end-stage lung disease (COPD, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, cystic fibrosis, pulmonary arterial hypertension, and others) when maximal therapy fails and prognosis is poor","Options are SINGLE- or BILATERAL-lung transplant (bilateral preferred for suppurative disease like cystic fibrosis and for pulmonary hypertension); allocation uses a lung allocation score prioritizing urgency and benefit","Donor lungs are the most fragile solid-organ graft (primary graft dysfunction risk); careful donor selection and preservation matter"],"dx":["Assess candidacy: disease severity/prognosis, absence of major contraindications (active malignancy, severe other-organ dysfunction, active infection, non-adherence), and rehabilitation potential","Determine single vs bilateral by disease (bilateral for infection/CF and PAH); size-match and crossmatch donor-recipient","Evaluate right heart function and pulmonary pressures"],"tx":["Perform single or bilateral (sequential) lung transplant; intraoperative support (CPB/ECMO) as needed, especially for pulmonary hypertension","Lifelong IMMUNOSUPPRESSION (calcineurin inhibitor + antimetabolite + steroid) with infection prophylaxis","Manage primary graft dysfunction and early rejection surveillance"],"tech":["Recipient pneumonectomy and implantation with bronchial, pulmonary artery, and left-atrial (pulmonary vein) anastomoses; bilateral done as sequential single transplants (often via clamshell or bilateral thoracotomy/sternotomy)","Lung preservation and reperfusion strategy to limit primary graft dysfunction; ECMO/CPB support as needed","Meticulous bronchial anastomosis (airway healing/ischemia is a vulnerability)"],"after":["Early: PRIMARY GRAFT DYSFUNCTION (ischemia-reperfusion injury), airway/anastomotic complications, and infection; acute rejection surveillance (transbronchial biopsy)","Late: CHRONIC LUNG ALLOGRAFT DYSFUNCTION - bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome - is the main limit on long-term survival; infection (CMV, fungal) with immunosuppression","Lifelong immunosuppression, rejection/infection monitoring, and spirometric surveillance"],"pearls":["Bilateral lung transplant is preferred for suppurative disease (cystic fibrosis) and pulmonary hypertension; single is an option for COPD/fibrosis","Primary graft dysfunction (ischemia-reperfusion) is the major early threat; the lung is the most fragile solid-organ graft","Chronic lung allograft dysfunction (bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome) is the leading limit on long-term survival","Airway/bronchial anastomotic healing is a vulnerability; lifelong immunosuppression brings CMV/fungal infection risk"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Lung Transplantation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430826/"},{"t":"NHLBI: Lung Transplant","u":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/lung-transplant"}]},{"id":"thoracic-heart-transplant","name":"Heart Transplantation & Rejection","sec":"thoracic","present":["Heart transplantation is the definitive treatment for selected end-stage (stage D) heart failure refractory to medical and device therapy; many recipients are bridged with an LVAD","Candidacy requires severe prognosis with acceptable other-organ function and absence of major contraindications (fixed severe pulmonary hypertension, active malignancy/infection, non-adherence)","REJECTION (hyperacute, acute cellular, antibody-mediated, and chronic - cardiac allograft vasculopathy) and immunosuppression complications shape outcomes"],"dx":["Assess candidacy including RIGHT-heart catheterization for pulmonary vascular resistance (fixed severe pulmonary hypertension is a contraindication - the donor RV fails against it)","Donor-recipient matching (size, ABO, crossmatch); evaluate reversibility and psychosocial support","Rejection surveillance with endomyocardial biopsy and noninvasive tools"],"tx":["Orthotopic heart transplant (bicaval technique is standard); lifelong IMMUNOSUPPRESSION (induction sometimes, then calcineurin inhibitor + antimetabolite + steroid taper)","Treat acute cellular rejection with corticosteroids (escalate for higher grades); antibody-mediated rejection with plasmapheresis/IVIG/rituximab-based regimens","Manage the transplanted (denervated) heart's physiology and infection prophylaxis"],"tech":["Orthotopic implantation with BICAVAL anastomoses (superior/inferior vena cavae), plus left atrial, pulmonary artery, and aortic anastomoses; the bicaval technique reduces atrial arrhythmias/tricuspid regurgitation versus the older biatrial method","Protect the donor heart (preservation/ischemic time); manage RV function against recipient pulmonary pressures","De-air and wean from CPB with inotropic/RV support"],"after":["The DENERVATED heart has a higher resting rate and no reflex tachycardia - it responds to circulating catecholamines, not vagal maneuvers/atropine (use direct agents like isoproterenol/epinephrine)","Acute rejection (endomyocardial biopsy surveillance) early; CARDIAC ALLOGRAFT VASCULOPATHY (accelerated diffuse coronary disease, often silent due to denervation) limits long-term survival","Immunosuppression complications: infection, renal dysfunction, malignancy (PTLD)"],"pearls":["Fixed severe pulmonary hypertension is a contraindication to heart transplant - the donor RV fails against high resistance","The transplanted heart is DENERVATED: higher resting rate, no response to atropine/vagal maneuvers - use direct-acting agents (isoproterenol, epinephrine)","Bicaval anastomotic technique is standard (fewer atrial arrhythmias/less TR than biatrial)","Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (accelerated, often silent coronary disease) is the main long-term limit; surveil with biopsy for acute rejection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Heart Transplantation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535406/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cardiac Allograft Vasculopathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560923/"}]},{"id":"thoracic-cardiac-postop-critical-care","name":"Cardiothoracic Postoperative Critical Care","sec":"thoracic","present":["Post-cardiac-surgery ICU care manages the patient recovering from CPB and cardiac repair; the core problems are LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT syndrome, BLEEDING/coagulopathy, TAMPONADE, arrhythmias, and respiratory/renal issues","LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT SYNDROME (LCOS) is transient myocardial dysfunction after bypass - managed with a systematic approach to preload, afterload, contractility, rate/rhythm, and mechanical support","POSTOPERATIVE TAMPONADE can be atypical (localized clot) and should be suspected with falling output, rising filling pressures, and increased/then suddenly decreased chest-tube drainage"],"dx":["Monitor hemodynamics (arterial line, central/PA pressures or cardiac output monitoring), chest-tube output, lactate, and echocardiography for tamponade/ventricular function","Diagnose the cause of low output: hypovolemia/bleeding, tamponade, ventricular dysfunction, vasoplegia, arrhythmia, or mechanical (graft/valve) problem","Bleeding: assess surgical vs coagulopathic (platelet dysfunction, residual heparin, hypothermia, factor depletion) with point-of-care testing"],"tx":["LCOS: optimize preload, correct rate/rhythm (pace if needed), reduce afterload, add inotropes, and escalate to mechanical support (IABP, then ECMO/VAD) if refractory","BLEEDING: normothermia, reverse residual heparin (protamine), correct coagulopathy (targeted products via point-of-care testing); RE-EXPLORE for excessive/ongoing bleeding or suspected tamponade","TAMPONADE: urgent re-exploration/evacuation; manage vasoplegia (vasopressors +/- vasopressin/methylene blue) and arrhythmias (postoperative AF is common)"],"tech":["Epicardial pacing wires for bradycardia/rhythm control; IABP for afterload reduction/coronary perfusion; ECMO/VAD for refractory failure","Chest re-exploration in the ICU or OR for tamponade or surgical bleeding (a decisive, time-critical maneuver)","Optimize ventilation/sedation weaning and renal protection"],"after":["Watch for postoperative atrial fibrillation, acute kidney injury, delirium/stroke, sternal wound infection, and pericardial effusion/late tamponade","Structured weaning of support (inotropes, ventilator), mobilization, and secondary prevention","Recognize that suddenly decreased chest-tube output with rising filling pressures may signal clot/tamponade, not resolved bleeding"],"pearls":["Low cardiac output syndrome after CPB: work through preload, afterload, contractility, rate/rhythm, then mechanical support (IABP -> ECMO/VAD) if refractory","Suspect postoperative tamponade with falling output, rising/equalizing filling pressures, and chest-tube drainage that increases then abruptly stops - re-explore urgently","Postoperative bleeding: correct hypothermia, residual heparin, and coagulopathy (point-of-care guided); re-explore for excessive/ongoing bleeding","Postoperative AF is very common; epicardial pacing wires manage bradyarrhythmias - watch for AKI, stroke, and sternal wound infection"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Low Cardiac Output Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559075/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Postoperative Cardiac Surgery Care","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542179/"}]},{"id":"uro-hematuria-evaluation","name":"Hematuria Evaluation","sec":"uro","present":["Hematuria is GROSS (visible) or MICROSCOPIC (>=3 RBC/hpf on microscopy of a properly collected specimen); GROSS hematuria carries a much higher malignancy risk and always warrants full evaluation","Key history: painless gross hematuria is bladder cancer until proven otherwise; timing (initial = urethral, terminal = bladder neck/prostate, total = bladder/upper tract) localizes the source","Risk factors for urothelial malignancy: age >35, smoking, occupational exposures (aromatic amines), prior pelvic radiation/cyclophosphamide, chronic indwelling catheter"],"dx":["Confirm true hematuria with microscopy (not dipstick alone - dipstick detects myoglobin/hemoglobin); exclude benign causes (UTI, menstruation, vigorous exercise, recent instrumentation)","Risk-stratify per AUA microhematuria guideline (low/intermediate/high) to determine workup intensity; distinguish glomerular (dysmorphic RBCs, casts, proteinuria -> nephrology) from urologic bleeding","Upper-tract imaging: CT UROGRAPHY is the gold standard for high-risk patients; CYSTOSCOPY evaluates the bladder/urethra (the test for lower-tract source)"],"tx":["Direct management at the identified cause: urothelial cancer, stones, BPH, infection, or glomerular disease","Negative full evaluation: reassure; consider surveillance/repeat evaluation for persistent or high-risk gross hematuria","Manage anticoagulation-associated hematuria by still completing an appropriate evaluation (anticoagulation does not exempt workup)"],"tech":["Flexible cystoscopy in the office (local anesthesia) to inspect the bladder and urethra; rigid cystoscopy under anesthesia for biopsy/resection when a lesion is found","CT urography protocol: non-contrast, nephrographic, and excretory (urographic) phases to assess parenchyma and the collecting system/ureters","Retrograde pyelography or ureteroscopy for indeterminate upper-tract findings"],"after":["Ensure the source is definitively identified for gross hematuria; a single negative workup may not suffice for persistent visible bleeding","Counsel on smoking cessation (a major modifiable urothelial cancer risk)","Arrange surveillance for any malignancy found and follow up incidental findings"],"pearls":["Painless gross hematuria = bladder cancer until proven otherwise; it mandates cystoscopy + upper-tract imaging","Dipstick can be falsely positive (myoglobin/hemoglobin) - confirm with microscopy (>=3 RBC/hpf)","CT urography is the gold-standard upper-tract imaging; cystoscopy evaluates the bladder/urethra","Dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, and proteinuria point to a glomerular (nephrologic) source, not urologic bleeding"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hematuria","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534213/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Microscopic Hematuria","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448105/"}]},{"id":"uro-urinalysis-imaging","name":"Urinalysis, Urine Microscopy & Urologic Imaging Basics","sec":"uro","present":["Urinalysis is the core urologic screening test: dipstick (blood, leukocyte esterase, nitrites, protein, glucose, pH, specific gravity) plus microscopy (RBCs, WBCs, casts, crystals, bacteria, epithelial cells)","Nitrites indicate nitrate-reducing organisms (e.g., E. coli); leukocyte esterase indicates pyuria; the combination supports UTI. Sterile pyuria suggests TB, chlamydia, stones, or a partially treated infection","Urine pH and crystal type hint at stone chemistry (acidic urine -> uric acid; alkaline urine + struvite crystals -> infection stones)"],"dx":["Interpret dipstick with microscopy; a proper specimen (clean-catch midstream or catheterized) reduces contamination (squamous epithelial cells suggest contamination)","Imaging selection: non-contrast CT (stones), CT urography (hematuria/urothelial), ultrasound (hydronephrosis, renal masses, post-void residual, pediatric/pregnant), MRI (prostate mpMRI, complex masses)","Renal ultrasound is first-line for hydronephrosis and avoids contrast/radiation; KUB radiograph shows radiopaque stones"],"tx":["Use urinalysis/culture to guide antimicrobial therapy; treat the underlying condition identified (infection, stone, malignancy)","Order the imaging matched to the question (stone vs mass vs obstruction vs cancer staging) to minimize radiation/contrast","Send urine cytology for high-risk hematuria/urothelial surveillance"],"tech":["Clean-catch midstream collection technique; straight catheterization for accurate specimens or retention assessment; suprapubic aspiration in infants","Bladder scan/ultrasound for post-void residual; renal ultrasound with Doppler for perfusion/obstruction","Contrast-enhanced CT phases tailored to the diagnostic question"],"after":["Repeat/confirm abnormal findings (e.g., microhematuria) before extensive workup; correlate imaging incidental findings","Track culture results and antibiotic sensitivities; de-escalate therapy accordingly","Follow up indeterminate imaging with the appropriate next study"],"pearls":["Dipstick nitrites (nitrate-reducing bacteria) + leukocyte esterase (pyuria) support UTI; sterile pyuria suggests TB, chlamydia, or stones","Squamous epithelial cells on microscopy indicate a contaminated (poorly collected) specimen","Non-contrast CT is best for stones; CT urography for hematuria/urothelial cancer; ultrasound for hydronephrosis and in pregnancy/children","Urine pH and crystals hint at stone type: acidic -> uric acid; alkaline + struvite -> infection stones"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Urinalysis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557685/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Casts","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559162/"}]},{"id":"uro-obstructive-uropathy","name":"Obstructive Uropathy & Hydronephrosis","sec":"uro","present":["Obstructive uropathy is functional/anatomic obstruction to urine flow causing hydronephrosis (collecting-system dilation) and, if unrelieved, renal impairment; it may be acute or chronic, unilateral or bilateral","Causes by level: intraluminal (stones, clots, tumor), intramural (stricture, UPJ obstruction, tumor), or extrinsic (BPH, pelvic/retroperitoneal mass, retroperitoneal fibrosis, pregnancy)","BILATERAL obstruction or obstruction of a solitary kidney causes post-renal ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY and can be life-threatening (hyperkalemia, volume overload)"],"dx":["Renal ultrasound detects hydronephrosis (first-line, no contrast/radiation); non-contrast CT localizes the level/cause; a MAG3 diuretic renogram distinguishes obstructive from non-obstructive dilation and quantifies split function","Assess renal function (creatinine, potassium) and for infection (obstruction + infection = emergency)","Post-void residual and prostate assessment for bladder outlet obstruction"],"tx":["Relieve obstruction: URETERAL STENT or PERCUTANEOUS NEPHROSTOMY for upper-tract obstruction; urethral or suprapubic CATHETER for bladder outlet obstruction","OBSTRUCTION WITH INFECTION (obstructive pyelonephritis) is a urologic EMERGENCY - urgent decompression (stent or nephrostomy) plus antibiotics; do not delay for definitive stone treatment","Watch for POST-OBSTRUCTIVE DIURESIS after relieving bilateral/high-grade obstruction - monitor and replace fluids/electrolytes"],"tech":["Retrograde ureteral stent placement cystoscopically, or percutaneous nephrostomy under imaging when retrograde access fails or sepsis demands the most direct drainage","Bladder decompression with urethral catheter (or suprapubic if urethral access impossible)","Definitive treatment of the cause (stone removal, stricture repair, tumor management) after decompression"],"after":["Monitor for post-obstructive diuresis (large urine output after relief) with fluid/electrolyte replacement in high-grade bilateral obstruction","Assess renal recovery; prolonged obstruction may cause permanent nephron loss","Plan definitive treatment of the underlying cause and stent/nephrostomy exchange or removal"],"pearls":["Obstruction WITH infection (obstructive pyelonephritis) is an emergency - decompress urgently (stent or nephrostomy) plus antibiotics before definitive stone treatment","Ultrasound is first-line for hydronephrosis; MAG3 diuretic renogram distinguishes true obstruction from non-obstructive dilation","Bilateral obstruction (or a solitary kidney) causes post-renal AKI - relieve promptly","Anticipate post-obstructive diuresis after relieving high-grade bilateral obstruction - replace fluids/electrolytes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hydronephrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563217/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Obstructive Uropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436038/"}]},{"id":"uro-complicated-uti-pyelonephritis","name":"Complicated UTI & Pyelonephritis","sec":"uro","present":["A COMPLICATED UTI occurs with a structural/functional abnormality (obstruction, stone, catheter, stent, neurogenic bladder, immunocompromise, pregnancy, male sex, or renal disease); ACUTE PYELONEPHRITIS is upper-tract infection with fever, flank pain, and costovertebral-angle tenderness","Uncomplicated cystitis (dysuria, frequency, urgency in a healthy nonpregnant woman) contrasts with complicated/upper-tract disease requiring broader workup and often imaging","A RENAL or PERINEPHRIC ABSCESS should be suspected when fever/symptoms persist beyond 48-72 hours of appropriate antibiotics"],"dx":["Urinalysis (pyuria, bacteriuria, nitrites) and URINE CULTURE with sensitivities (obtain before antibiotics in complicated disease); blood cultures if septic","Imaging (CT with contrast, or ultrasound) for complicated pyelonephritis, suspected obstruction/stone, abscess, or non-response - to find a drainable focus or obstruction","Identify the complicating factor (stone, obstruction, catheter, diabetes, immunosuppression)"],"tx":["Empiric antibiotics guided by local resistance, then TAILOR to culture; relieve any OBSTRUCTION (stent/nephrostomy) - antibiotics alone fail if the system is obstructed","DRAIN abscesses (percutaneous) that do not respond to antibiotics; remove/exchange infected catheters/stents when feasible","Admit for sepsis, inability to tolerate oral intake, obstruction, pregnancy, or failure of outpatient therapy"],"tech":["Percutaneous drainage of renal/perinephric abscess under imaging; ureteral stent or nephrostomy for obstructive pyelonephritis","Exchange or remove infected hardware (catheters, stents) as a source-control measure","Definitive treatment of the underlying stone/obstruction after infection controlled"],"after":["Confirm clinical improvement; re-image if fever/symptoms persist (occult abscess or obstruction)","Complete the tailored antibiotic course; address the complicating factor to prevent recurrence","Follow renal function and arrange definitive management of stones/obstruction"],"pearls":["Complicated UTI = infection plus a structural/functional abnormality (obstruction, stone, catheter, neurogenic bladder, pregnancy, male, immunocompromise)","Obstructive pyelonephritis needs urgent DECOMPRESSION plus antibiotics - antibiotics alone fail against an obstructed, infected system","Persistent fever beyond 48-72 hours of appropriate antibiotics suggests a renal/perinephric abscess or undrained obstruction - re-image","Always obtain urine culture (and blood cultures if septic) before antibiotics in complicated disease and tailor therapy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Pyelonephritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519537/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Complicated Urinary Tract Infections","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436013/"}]},{"id":"uro-urosepsis-emphysematous","name":"Urosepsis & Emphysematous GU Infections","sec":"uro","present":["UROSEPSIS is sepsis arising from a urinary source; it commonly follows obstructing infected stones, instrumentation, or catheter-associated infection and can progress rapidly to septic shock","EMPHYSEMATOUS PYELONEPHRITIS is a necrotizing gas-forming renal parenchymal infection (usually E. coli/Klebsiella), overwhelmingly in DIABETICS - a life-threatening emergency; EMPHYSEMATOUS CYSTITIS is gas within the bladder wall","FOURNIER'S GANGRENE (covered separately) is the necrotizing soft-tissue infection of the perineum/genitalia in the same at-risk population"],"dx":["Recognize sepsis physiology (hypotension, tachycardia, altered mentation, elevated lactate); obtain blood and urine cultures and identify the source/obstruction urgently","CT reveals gas in the renal parenchyma/collecting system (emphysematous pyelonephritis - classified by extent, e.g., Huang-Tseng) or bladder wall, and any obstructing stone","Assess glycemic control and immune status; look for an obstructed, infected upper tract needing drainage"],"tx":["Immediate SEPSIS RESUSCITATION (fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, source control) plus urgent DECOMPRESSION of any obstruction (stent/nephrostomy)","EMPHYSEMATOUS PYELONEPHRITIS: aggressive resuscitation, antibiotics, glycemic control, and percutaneous drainage; NEPHRECTOMY for extensive necrosis/failure of conservative management (historically high mortality)","Emphysematous cystitis: antibiotics, bladder drainage, and glycemic control; correct the underlying obstruction/diabetes"],"tech":["Percutaneous nephrostomy or ureteral stent for the obstructed infected kidney - the most critical source-control step in obstructive urosepsis","Percutaneous drainage of localized gas/abscess; nephrectomy for extensive emphysematous pyelonephritis unresponsive to drainage","ICU-level support for septic shock"],"after":["Monitor in an ICU for septic shock; continue tailored antibiotics and optimize diabetes control","Reassess for source control adequacy (repeat imaging if not improving); plan definitive stone/obstruction treatment after recovery","Emphysematous pyelonephritis carries significant mortality - early drainage improves kidney salvage vs upfront nephrectomy"],"pearls":["Obstructive urosepsis: resuscitate and DECOMPRESS urgently (stent/nephrostomy) - this source control is life-saving; do not delay for definitive stone treatment","Emphysematous pyelonephritis is a gas-forming necrotizing renal infection almost always in diabetics - a true emergency (CT shows parenchymal gas)","Early percutaneous drainage plus antibiotics can salvage the kidney; nephrectomy is reserved for extensive necrosis/failure","Tight glycemic control and ICU support are integral to managing emphysematous GU infections"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Emphysematous Pyelonephritis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544344/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urosepsis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482427/"}]},{"id":"uro-nephrolithiasis-workup","name":"Nephrolithiasis - Workup & Metabolic Evaluation","sec":"uro","present":["Kidney stones are common and recurrent; types by frequency: CALCIUM OXALATE (most common), calcium phosphate, URIC ACID (radiolucent, forms in acidic urine), STRUVITE (infection/magnesium-ammonium-phosphate, forms staghorn calculi in alkaline urine), and CYSTINE (hereditary cystinuria)","Presentation ranges from asymptomatic to renal colic; recurrent stone formers, solitary kidney, and metabolically active stones warrant metabolic evaluation","Risk factors: low urine volume, diet (sodium, animal protein, oxalate), obesity, gout (uric acid), primary hyperparathyroidism (calcium), and recurrent UTIs (struvite)"],"dx":["NON-CONTRAST CT is the gold standard for diagnosis (size, location, density in Hounsfield units, and skin-to-stone distance guide treatment); ultrasound in pregnancy/children","Stone analysis of passed/retrieved fragments guides prevention; basic labs (calcium, uric acid, creatinine, electrolytes) and urinalysis/culture","METABOLIC WORKUP for recurrent/high-risk stone formers: 24-hour urine collection (volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, sodium, pH, cystine) and serum studies (rule out hyperparathyroidism if hypercalcemia)"],"tx":["Prevention: increase fluid intake (target urine output >2.5 L/day), reduce dietary sodium and animal protein, maintain normal dietary CALCIUM (low-calcium diets worsen oxalate stones)","Targeted therapy: THIAZIDES for hypercalciuria, POTASSIUM CITRATE for hypocitraturia/uric acid stones (alkalinizes urine), ALLOPURINOL for hyperuricosuria; uric acid stones can DISSOLVE with urinary alkalinization","Struvite (infection) stones require complete surgical removal plus treating the infection; treat primary hyperparathyroidism surgically"],"tech":["Surgical removal modality chosen by stone size/location/composition (see ureteroscopy, SWL, and PCNL entries)","24-hour urine-directed medical therapy for recurrence prevention","Parathyroidectomy for stone-causing primary hyperparathyroidism"],"after":["Follow-up 24-hour urine to confirm the metabolic effect of interventions; imaging surveillance for recurrence/residual fragments","Emphasize durable fluid and dietary changes (adherence drives recurrence reduction)","Monitor for medication effects (thiazide - hypokalemia; potassium citrate - hyperkalemia in CKD)"],"pearls":["Non-contrast CT is the gold standard; stone density (HU) and skin-to-stone distance predict SWL success","Maintain NORMAL dietary calcium - low-calcium diets increase oxalate absorption and worsen calcium oxalate stones","Uric acid stones are radiolucent, form in acidic urine, and can be dissolved by urinary alkalinization (potassium citrate)","Struvite staghorn stones (infection, alkaline urine) require complete removal + infection control; thiazides treat hypercalciuria, citrate treats hypocitraturia"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nephrolithiasis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442014/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cystinuria","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470527/"}]},{"id":"uro-renal-colic","name":"Acute Renal Colic Management","sec":"uro","present":["Renal colic is acute, severe, colicky flank pain radiating to the groin from an obstructing ureteral stone, often with nausea/vomiting, hematuria, and an inability to find a comfortable position (writhing, unlike peritonitis)","Location-based referral: proximal ureteral stones give flank pain; distal/ureterovesical-junction stones give lower-quadrant pain and irritative voiding (frequency/urgency)","The urgent concern is an OBSTRUCTED, INFECTED system (fever, sepsis) - a surgical emergency"],"dx":["NON-CONTRAST CT confirms the stone, its size, and location; ultrasound is used in pregnancy and to reduce radiation in young/recurrent patients","Check for infection (urinalysis/culture, fever) and renal function; assess stone size (predicts spontaneous passage - most <5 mm pass, >10 mm rarely do)","Bilateral obstruction or a solitary kidney raises urgency"],"tx":["Pain control: NSAIDs are first-line (superior to opioids for renal colic) unless contraindicated (renal impairment); antiemetics and hydration","MEDICAL EXPULSIVE THERAPY with an ALPHA-BLOCKER (tamsulosin) aids passage of distal ureteral stones (especially >5 mm); most small stones pass spontaneously with observation","URGENT DECOMPRESSION (stent/nephrostomy) for obstruction with infection/sepsis, intractable pain/vomiting, AKI, or a solitary/transplant kidney"],"tech":["Ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy for emergent decompression of the obstructed infected kidney","Definitive stone treatment (ureteroscopy, SWL, PCNL) electively after the acute episode/infection resolves","Trial of passage with alpha-blocker and analgesia for suitable small stones"],"after":["Strain urine to capture the stone for analysis; arrange follow-up imaging to confirm passage or plan intervention","Counsel on recurrence prevention (fluids, diet) and metabolic evaluation for recurrent formers","Return precautions for fever, uncontrolled pain, or anuria"],"pearls":["NSAIDs are first-line analgesia for renal colic (more effective than opioids), if renal function allows","Most stones <5 mm pass spontaneously; alpha-blocker medical expulsive therapy helps distal ureteral stones (especially >5 mm)","Obstruction with infection/sepsis is a surgical emergency - urgent decompression (stent/nephrostomy), not just antibiotics","Non-contrast CT is the diagnostic test; use ultrasound in pregnancy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nephrolithiasis (Acute Renal Colic)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559227/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteral Calculi","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560674/"}]},{"id":"uro-ureteroscopy-laser","name":"Ureteroscopy & Laser Lithotripsy","sec":"uro","present":["Ureteroscopy (URS) with laser lithotripsy passes a semirigid or flexible scope up the ureter (and into the kidney - retrograde intrarenal surgery) to directly visualize and fragment/extract stones","URS is highly effective across ureteral stones and small-to-moderate renal stones, with high stone-free rates and the ability to treat regardless of stone density (unlike SWL)","It is the preferred approach for many distal ureteral stones, stones resistant to SWL, and in patients who cannot undergo SWL (pregnancy considerations, bleeding, obesity)"],"dx":["Confirm stone burden/location on CT; assess for active infection (treat before elective URS - stone manipulation risks urosepsis)","Evaluate anatomy (strictures, prior surgery) and coagulation status","Plan flexible vs semirigid scope and access strategy"],"tx":["URS with HOLMIUM laser (or thulium fiber laser) lithotripsy fragments or dusts the stone; basket extraction of fragments","Ureteral stent placement afterward is common (not always required) to prevent obstruction from edema/fragments","Preferred for distal ureteral stones and as an alternative to PCNL for smaller renal stones"],"tech":["Cystoscopic access, guidewire placement, and passage of the ureteroscope (a ureteral access sheath aids flexible renal work); holmium:YAG laser dusting/fragmentation; basket retrieval","Ureteral stent placed post-procedure when there is edema, residual fragments, solitary kidney, or a difficult procedure","Preoperative stenting or staged procedures if the ureter is too narrow for access"],"after":["Stent-related symptoms (irritative voiding, flank discomfort, hematuria) are common; remove the stent on schedule (do not forget a retained stent - a serious pitfall)","Watch for post-op infection/urosepsis, ureteral injury/perforation, and (rarely) ureteral avulsion or stricture","Confirm stone-free status and send fragments for analysis"],"pearls":["Ureteroscopy + holmium (or thulium fiber) laser has high stone-free rates and works regardless of stone density (unlike SWL)","Preferred for distal ureteral stones and many small-to-moderate renal stones; treat active infection BEFORE elective URS to avoid urosepsis","A forgotten/retained ureteral stent is a classic serious pitfall - track and remove stents on schedule","Ureteral avulsion is a rare but catastrophic complication of basket extraction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteroscopy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560538/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Holmium Laser Lithotripsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560644/"}]},{"id":"uro-swl","name":"Shockwave Lithotripsy (SWL)","sec":"uro","present":["Extracorporeal SHOCKWAVE LITHOTRIPSY (SWL) fragments stones with externally generated, focused acoustic shockwaves - a noninvasive outpatient option requiring no scope","Best for smaller (<=~2 cm), radiopaque, lower-density stones in favorable locations (renal pelvis, upper ureter); less effective for large, dense (>~1000 HU), cystine, or lower-pole stones with unfavorable anatomy","Success depends on stone size, density (Hounsfield units), skin-to-stone distance (obesity reduces efficacy), and location"],"dx":["Non-contrast CT to size, localize, and measure stone density (HU) and skin-to-stone distance - the key predictors of SWL success","Exclude contraindications: PREGNANCY, uncorrected COAGULOPATHY, active UTI, distal obstruction below the stone, and aortic/renal artery aneurysm near the field","Treat any infection before SWL"],"tx":["SWL for appropriately selected small radiopaque stones; alpha-blocker afterward may aid clearance of fragments (especially ureteral)","Pre-stenting is generally NOT required for small stones but is considered for larger burdens (weigh steinstrasse risk)","Alternative (URS/PCNL) if SWL is contraindicated or the stone is unfavorable"],"tech":["Fluoroscopic/ultrasound targeting of the stone at the shockwave focal point; graded ramp-up of shockwave energy to reduce renal injury","Fragments then pass spontaneously over days-weeks; repeat SWL sessions may be needed","Ureteral stent for large burdens to reduce obstructive complications"],"after":["Watch for STEINSTRASSE ('street of stones' - a column of fragments obstructing the ureter), renal hematoma, and incomplete fragmentation requiring retreatment or URS","Strain urine and follow up imaging to confirm clearance; fragments clear over weeks","Bruising and transient hematuria are expected; significant perinephric hematoma is a recognized complication"],"pearls":["SWL is noninvasive but works best for smaller (<=2 cm), radiopaque, lower-density (<1000 HU) stones with a short skin-to-stone distance","Contraindications: pregnancy, uncorrected coagulopathy, active UTI, obstruction distal to the stone, and nearby aneurysm","Steinstrasse (a column of obstructing fragments) is a classic post-SWL complication of larger stone burdens","Cystine and lower-pole/dense stones respond poorly - choose URS/PCNL instead"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Extracorporeal Shockwave Lithotripsy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574505/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Steinstrasse","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560522/"}]},{"id":"uro-pcnl","name":"Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy (PCNL)","sec":"uro","present":["Percutaneous NEPHROLITHOTOMY (PCNL) removes stones through a percutaneous tract into the kidney; it is the treatment of choice for LARGE (>2 cm) renal stones, STAGHORN calculi, and complex/lower-pole burdens","It achieves the highest stone-free rates for large stone burdens in a single setting but is more invasive than URS/SWL","Staghorn (usually struvite/infection) stones require complete clearance to control infection and preserve renal function"],"dx":["CT defines stone burden, collecting-system anatomy, and the optimal calyx for access; assess renal function and split function when relevant","Treat infection preoperatively and culture the stone/renal urine (infection stones seed sepsis)","Plan access (often a posterior lower/interpolar calyx) and evaluate bleeding risk"],"tx":["PCNL for large/staghorn/complex renal stones; combine with flexible nephroscopy or ureteroscopy for scattered fragments (endoscopic combined intrarenal surgery)","Antibiotic prophylaxis directed by culture; staged or multi-tract PCNL for extensive staghorn disease","Nephrostomy tube vs 'tubeless' PCNL depending on the case"],"tech":["Percutaneous access into the desired calyx (fluoroscopic/ultrasound-guided), tract dilation, nephroscope insertion, and stone fragmentation (ultrasonic/pneumatic/laser lithotripsy) with fragment extraction","Nephrostomy tube and/or ureteral stent at the end; tubeless approaches in selected cases","Multi-tract access for complex staghorn stones"],"after":["Major risk is BLEEDING (transfusion or, rarely, angioembolization for arteriovenous fistula/pseudoaneurysm); also urosepsis, pleural injury/hydrothorax (upper-pole access), and injury to adjacent organs (colon)","Obtain post-op imaging for residual fragments (complete clearance matters especially for infection stones)","Monitor for sepsis and hemorrhage; manage the nephrostomy/stent"],"pearls":["PCNL is the treatment of choice for large (>2 cm) renal stones and staghorn calculi - highest stone-free rates for big burdens","Bleeding is the major complication (may need transfusion or angioembolization for pseudoaneurysm/AV fistula)","Upper-pole access risks pleural injury/hydrothorax; complete clearance is essential for infection (struvite) staghorn stones","Treat infection and obtain stone cultures preoperatively - PCNL on infected stones can precipitate urosepsis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563219/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Staghorn Calculi","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564394/"}]},{"id":"uro-prostate-cancer-screening","name":"Prostate Cancer Screening, PSA & Biopsy","sec":"uro","present":["Prostate cancer is the most common non-skin cancer in men; screening centers on PROSTATE-SPECIFIC ANTIGEN (PSA) +/- digital rectal exam, with SHARED DECISION-MAKING given the risk of overdiagnosis of indolent disease","PSA is prostate-specific but NOT cancer-specific - it rises with BPH, prostatitis, instrumentation, and age; use PSA density, velocity, free/total ratio, and adjunct markers to refine risk","Screening is generally discussed for men ~55-69 (individualized earlier for high risk: Black men, BRCA/family history)"],"dx":["Elevated/rising PSA or abnormal DRE prompts risk assessment; secondary markers (free PSA, 4Kscore, PHI) and MULTIPARAMETRIC PROSTATE MRI (PI-RADS) refine the need for biopsy and target lesions","MRI-targeted plus systematic biopsy (transrectal or transperineal) yields the diagnosis and GLEASON/Grade Group; transperineal biopsy lowers infection risk","Stage with the tissue grade, PSA, and (for higher risk) bone scan/PSMA PET and cross-sectional imaging"],"tx":["Management flows from risk stratification (see localized prostate cancer): active surveillance for low-risk, definitive therapy for higher-risk","Address the harms of overdiagnosis by favoring surveillance for low-grade disease","Counsel using shared decision-making about screening benefits (mortality reduction) vs harms (biopsy complications, overtreatment)"],"tech":["MRI-fusion targeted biopsy combined with systematic cores; TRANSPERINEAL approach reduces sepsis risk versus transrectal","Antibiotic prophylaxis and, increasingly, transperineal technique to combat fluoroquinolone-resistant infection","PSMA PET for staging in higher-risk disease/biochemical recurrence"],"after":["Post-biopsy: watch for infection/sepsis, hematuria, hematospermia, and urinary retention","Enroll appropriate patients in active surveillance protocols (serial PSA, MRI, repeat biopsy)","Continue shared decision-making about further testing/treatment"],"pearls":["PSA is prostate-specific, not cancer-specific - it rises with BPH, prostatitis, and instrumentation; use density/velocity/free-total and MRI to refine risk","Multiparametric MRI (PI-RADS) guides targeted biopsy and reduces detection of indolent cancers","Transperineal biopsy lowers infection/sepsis risk versus transrectal","Screening is a shared decision (roughly ages 55-69), individualized earlier for Black men and BRCA/strong family history"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Prostate Cancer Screening (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/prostate/hp/prostate-screening-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Specific Antigen","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557495/"}]},{"id":"uro-localized-prostate-cancer","name":"Localized Prostate Cancer - Risk Stratification","sec":"uro","present":["Localized prostate cancer is risk-stratified by PSA, GRADE GROUP (Gleason), and clinical T stage into low, intermediate (favorable/unfavorable), and high risk (D'Amico/NCCN)","Most is adenocarcinoma; management balances cancer aggressiveness against life expectancy and treatment morbidity (urinary, sexual, bowel)","Many low-risk cancers are indolent and best observed rather than treated (avoiding overtreatment)"],"dx":["Integrate PSA, biopsy Grade Group, percentage of positive cores, MRI, and clinical stage; genomic classifiers can refine intermediate-risk decisions","Stage higher-risk disease with PSMA PET/bone scan/CT to exclude nodal/metastatic spread","Assess life expectancy and comorbidity (treatment benefit requires sufficient longevity)"],"tx":["LOW-RISK: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE (serial PSA, MRI, repeat biopsy) is preferred - defer treatment while monitoring for progression","INTERMEDIATE/HIGH-RISK: definitive treatment - RADICAL PROSTATECTOMY or RADIATION (external beam +/- brachytherapy), with ANDROGEN DEPRIVATION added to radiation for higher risk","Compare modalities on cancer control (similar for many) and side-effect profiles (surgery - incontinence/ED; radiation - bowel/urinary irritation, later ED)"],"tech":["Definitive local therapy options detailed under radical prostatectomy and (radiation oncology) prostate radiotherapy/brachytherapy","Active surveillance protocols with defined triggers for intervention (grade/volume progression)","Focal therapy is investigational/selective"],"after":["Surveillance: monitor for reclassification/progression and convert to treatment when indicated","After definitive therapy: monitor PSA for biochemical recurrence and manage functional outcomes (continence, potency)","Support decision regret reduction through thorough pre-treatment counseling"],"pearls":["Active surveillance is preferred for low-risk prostate cancer - it avoids overtreatment of indolent disease","Grade Group (Gleason), PSA, and clinical T stage drive D'Amico/NCCN risk categories","Add androgen deprivation to radiation for high-risk (and unfavorable intermediate-risk) disease","Surgery vs radiation often give similar cancer control - choose by side-effect profile and patient factors/life expectancy"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Prostate Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/prostate/hp/prostate-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prostate Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553117/"}]},{"id":"uro-radical-prostatectomy","name":"Radical Prostatectomy","sec":"uro","present":["Radical prostatectomy removes the entire prostate and seminal vesicles with vesicourethral anastomosis; it is a definitive option for localized intermediate/high-risk prostate cancer in men with adequate life expectancy","Most are done ROBOT-ASSISTED laparoscopically; pelvic lymph node dissection is added for higher-risk disease","The key functional trade-offs are URINARY CONTINENCE and ERECTILE FUNCTION, which depend on nerve-sparing feasibility"],"dx":["Confirm localized disease and risk category; assess candidacy (life expectancy, comorbidity) and baseline continence/potency","Plan nerve-sparing based on cancer location/grade (oncologic safety first); decide on lymph node dissection by nodal risk (nomograms)","Discuss expected functional recovery and management options for ED/incontinence"],"tx":["Radical prostatectomy (robot-assisted, laparoscopic, or open retropubic) with vesicourethral anastomosis +/- pelvic lymph node dissection for higher risk","NERVE-SPARING (preserving the neurovascular bundles) when oncologically safe to preserve erectile function","Adjuvant/salvage radiation +/- ADT for adverse pathology or biochemical recurrence"],"tech":["Robot-assisted radical prostatectomy: bladder-neck dissection, seminal vesicle mobilization, control of the dorsal venous complex, nerve-sparing along the prostate, apical dissection, and watertight vesicourethral anastomosis","Extended pelvic lymph node dissection for staging in higher-risk disease","Meticulous apical/urethral length preservation aids continence"],"after":["Urethral catheter for the anastomosis to heal; complications include urinary INCONTINENCE (usually improves over months), ERECTILE DYSFUNCTION, anastomotic stricture, and lymphocele","Post-op PSA should become UNDETECTABLE - a detectable/rising PSA signals biochemical recurrence (consider salvage radiation +/- ADT)","Pelvic floor rehabilitation and ED management (PDE5 inhibitors, penile rehabilitation)"],"pearls":["After radical prostatectomy the PSA should be undetectable; a rising PSA indicates biochemical recurrence -> consider salvage radiation +/- ADT","Nerve-sparing preserves erectile function but is subordinate to oncologic control (positive margins matter)","Main functional trade-offs are incontinence (usually improves over ~12 months) and erectile dysfunction","Add pelvic lymph node dissection for higher-risk disease per nodal-risk nomograms"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Radical Prostatectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563184/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prostatectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556089/"}]},{"id":"uro-advanced-prostate-cancer","name":"Advanced & Metastatic Prostate Cancer","sec":"uro","present":["Advanced prostate cancer includes biochemically recurrent, locally advanced, and metastatic disease; it is driven by androgen-receptor signaling, so ANDROGEN DEPRIVATION THERAPY (ADT) is the backbone","Metastatic disease is initially hormone-sensitive (mCSPC) and later becomes CASTRATION-RESISTANT (mCRPC) despite castrate testosterone; bone is the dominant metastatic site","Presentation: bone pain, obstructive symptoms, or asymptomatic rising PSA; complications include spinal cord compression and pathologic fractures"],"dx":["Confirm metastases with imaging (bone scan/CT or PSMA PET); monitor PSA and testosterone (confirm castrate level on ADT)","At castration resistance, reassess disease burden and consider molecular testing (BRCA/HRR, mismatch repair) for targeted therapy eligibility","Watch for cord compression (an oncologic emergency) in symptomatic patients"],"tx":["ADT (GnRH agonist/antagonist or bilateral orchiectomy) is the foundation; for metastatic hormone-sensitive disease, INTENSIFY upfront with an androgen-receptor pathway inhibitor (abiraterone, enzalutamide, apalutamide) +/- docetaxel (doublet/triplet therapy improves survival)","mCRPC: sequence AR-pathway inhibitors, taxane chemotherapy (docetaxel, cabazitaxel), radium-223 (bone-predominant), PARP inhibitors (HRR/BRCA mutations), and PSMA-targeted radioligand therapy (lutetium-177 PSMA)","Manage bone health (denosumab/zoledronic acid) and treat emergencies (cord compression - steroids, radiation/surgery)"],"tech":["Bilateral orchiectomy is a definitive surgical castration option (rapid, avoids ongoing injections); most use medical ADT","Palliative surgical/endoscopic procedures for obstruction (channel TURP); decompression for cord compression","Radioligand and radiopharmaceutical delivery handled with oncology"],"after":["Monitor for ADT side effects: hot flashes, osteoporosis/fractures, metabolic syndrome, cardiovascular risk, sarcopenia, and cognitive/mood effects - manage bone health and cardiometabolic risk","Surveillance of PSA/imaging to detect progression and guide sequencing","Palliative and supportive care for symptom control"],"pearls":["ADT is the backbone; for metastatic hormone-sensitive disease, INTENSIFY upfront (add an AR-pathway inhibitor +/- docetaxel) - doublet/triplet improves survival over ADT alone","Castration-resistant disease progresses despite castrate testosterone - confirm the castrate level before labeling CRPC","Test for BRCA/HRR mutations (PARP-inhibitor eligibility); PSMA radioligand therapy (Lu-177) is an option in mCRPC","ADT causes osteoporosis, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular risk - manage bone and cardiometabolic health; watch for cord compression"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Prostate Cancer Treatment - Advanced (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/prostate/patient/prostate-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Castration Resistant Prostate Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556069/"}]},{"id":"uro-bladder-cancer-nmibc","name":"Bladder Cancer - NMIBC & TURBT","sec":"uro","present":["Bladder cancer is usually UROTHELIAL carcinoma; SMOKING is the dominant risk factor (also occupational aromatic amines, cyclophosphamide, pelvic radiation; schistosomiasis for squamous cell)","It presents with PAINLESS GROSS HEMATURIA; ~75% is NON-MUSCLE-INVASIVE (NMIBC: Ta, T1, and carcinoma in situ) at diagnosis","NMIBC is stratified into low/intermediate/high risk by stage, grade, size, multifocality, and presence of CIS - risk drives treatment intensity and surveillance"],"dx":["CYSTOSCOPY with biopsy/resection is the cornerstone; urine cytology detects high-grade disease/CIS; CT UROGRAPHY evaluates the upper tracts (synchronous urothelial tumors)","TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT) provides diagnosis, staging (must include DETRUSOR MUSCLE in the specimen to assess invasion), and treatment of NMIBC","Restaging (repeat) TURBT for high-risk/T1 or when muscle is absent"],"tx":["Complete TURBT + a single immediate postoperative intravesical CHEMOTHERAPY instillation (e.g., mitomycin/gemcitabine) reduces recurrence for low/intermediate-risk NMIBC","HIGH-RISK NMIBC (high-grade T1, CIS): induction + maintenance intravesical BCG immunotherapy; radical cystectomy for BCG-unresponsive disease","Risk-adapted surveillance with cystoscopy and cytology"],"tech":["TURBT: resect the tumor in fractions ensuring detrusor muscle is sampled; fulguration of small lesions; consider blue-light (enhanced) cystoscopy to improve CIS/tumor detection","Restaging TURBT ~4-6 weeks later for high-risk/T1 tumors or if muscle was absent","Intravesical instillation technique (BCG/chemo) via catheter"],"after":["Watch for bladder perforation (extraperitoneal usually managed with catheter drainage), bleeding, and BCG side effects (irritative symptoms, rare BCG sepsis)","Structured surveillance cystoscopy/cytology (frequent early, then spaced) given high recurrence rates","Reinforce smoking cessation (reduces recurrence/progression)"],"pearls":["Painless gross hematuria is bladder cancer until proven otherwise - evaluate with cystoscopy + CT urography + cytology","TURBT specimens MUST contain detrusor muscle to assess muscle invasion - repeat TURBT if absent or high-risk T1","A single immediate post-TURBT intravesical chemo instillation lowers recurrence in low/intermediate-risk NMIBC","High-risk NMIBC (high-grade T1, CIS) gets induction + maintenance BCG; cystectomy for BCG-unresponsive disease"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Bladder Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/bladder/hp/bladder-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536923/"}]},{"id":"uro-muscle-invasive-bladder","name":"Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer & Radical Cystectomy","sec":"uro","present":["MUSCLE-INVASIVE bladder cancer (MIBC, >=T2, invading the detrusor) is aggressive with substantial metastatic potential and requires radical treatment","Standard of care is NEOADJUVANT cisplatin-based CHEMOTHERAPY followed by RADICAL CYSTECTOMY with pelvic lymph node dissection and urinary diversion","Bladder-preserving TRIMODAL THERAPY (maximal TURBT + chemoradiation) is an alternative in selected, well-counseled patients"],"dx":["TURBT establishes muscle invasion; stage with CT/MRI of abdomen/pelvis and chest imaging (+/- bone scan/PSMA-type imaging as indicated) to assess nodes/metastases","Assess cisplatin eligibility (renal function, performance status) for neoadjuvant chemotherapy","Evaluate suitability/preference for cystectomy vs bladder preservation"],"tx":["NEOADJUVANT cisplatin-based chemotherapy THEN radical cystectomy + bilateral pelvic lymph node dissection + urinary diversion (survival benefit over surgery alone)","Radical cystectomy: in men removes bladder/prostate/seminal vesicles; in women the anterior exenteration (bladder, uterus, adnexa, anterior vaginal wall) - tailored with organ-sparing when appropriate","URINARY DIVERSION: ileal conduit (incontinent) or continent diversion/orthotopic neobladder (patient-selected)"],"tech":["Radical cystectomy (open or robotic) with extended pelvic lymphadenectomy; construct the chosen diversion (ileal conduit vs neobladder/continent pouch) using detubularized bowel","Ureteroenteric anastomoses; protect ureteral blood supply to prevent stricture","Nerve/organ-sparing techniques in selected candidates"],"after":["Major surgery with significant complication rates: ileus, infection, ureteroenteric stricture, metabolic derangement (from bowel segment), and diversion-specific issues","Neobladder patients need training for continence/emptying (some require intermittent catheterization); lifelong metabolic and B12 monitoring","Oncologic surveillance imaging and upper-tract monitoring"],"pearls":["Muscle-invasive bladder cancer: neoadjuvant cisplatin-based chemotherapy THEN radical cystectomy improves survival over surgery alone","Cystectomy includes pelvic lymph node dissection; diversion options are ileal conduit (simplest) vs continent/orthotopic neobladder","Trimodal bladder preservation (maximal TURBT + chemoradiation) is an option for selected, motivated patients","Bowel used for diversion causes metabolic issues and B12 deficiency - monitor long term; ureteroenteric stricture is a common late complication"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Bladder Cancer Treatment - MIBC (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/bladder/patient/bladder-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Radical Cystectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562148/"}]},{"id":"uro-upper-tract-urothelial","name":"Upper-Tract Urothelial Carcinoma","sec":"uro","present":["Upper-tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC) affects the renal pelvis and ureter; it is less common than bladder urothelial cancer but often higher-stage at diagnosis","Risk factors overlap with bladder cancer (smoking, aromatic amines) plus LYNCH SYNDROME (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer) and aristolochic acid exposure","Presents with hematuria or flank pain/obstruction; there is field-change risk of synchronous/metachronous bladder tumors"],"dx":["CT UROGRAPHY shows a filling defect/enhancing mass; urine cytology (selective, from the affected side helps); URETEROSCOPY with biopsy provides tissue and assesses grade","Cystoscopy to exclude concurrent bladder tumors; stage with cross-sectional imaging","Consider Lynch syndrome screening in appropriate patients"],"tx":["Standard for high-risk UTUC: RADICAL NEPHROURETERECTOMY (kidney, entire ureter, and a bladder cuff) with a peri-operative single intravesical chemotherapy instillation to reduce bladder recurrence","KIDNEY-SPARING (endoscopic ablation or segmental ureterectomy) for low-risk, low-volume tumors, solitary kidney, or renal insufficiency","Neoadjuvant/adjuvant platinum-based chemotherapy for high-risk disease (adjuvant improves outcomes per POUT-type data)"],"tech":["Radical nephroureterectomy: remove the kidney and full-length ureter WITH A BLADDER CUFF (incomplete distal ureter/cuff removal risks recurrence)","Kidney-sparing endoscopic laser ablation for select low-risk tumors; segmental ureterectomy for distal ureteral tumors","Perioperative intravesical chemotherapy instillation to reduce intravesical recurrence"],"after":["Lifelong surveillance of the bladder (cystoscopy) and the contralateral upper tract (field-change recurrence risk)","Monitor renal function after nephroureterectomy; manage chemotherapy eligibility with the remaining kidney","Genetic counseling if Lynch syndrome suspected"],"pearls":["Radical nephroureterectomy for high-risk UTUC must include a BLADDER CUFF (remove the entire distal ureter) to prevent stump recurrence","A single perioperative intravesical chemo instillation reduces subsequent bladder recurrence","Kidney-sparing options suit low-risk tumors, a solitary kidney, or renal insufficiency","Consider Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) in UTUC; lifelong bladder and contralateral upper-tract surveillance is needed"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Upper Tract Urothelial Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562252/"},{"t":"NCI: Transitional Cell Cancer of Renal Pelvis/Ureter (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/kidney/hp/transitional-cell-treatment-pdq"}]},{"id":"uro-renal-mass-rcc","name":"Renal Cell Carcinoma & Renal Mass Workup","sec":"uro","present":["Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is the most common solid renal malignancy; CLEAR CELL is the dominant subtype (papillary and chromophobe are others)","Most are now found INCIDENTALLY on imaging; the classic triad (flank pain, hematuria, palpable mass) is late/uncommon; paraneoplastic syndromes and varicocele (left, from renal vein involvement) can occur","Risk factors: smoking, obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic kidney disease (dialysis), and hereditary syndromes (VON HIPPEL-LINDAU, hereditary papillary RCC, Birt-Hogg-Dube)"],"dx":["Characterize the mass with contrast CT or MRI (enhancement indicates a solid, likely malignant mass); the BOSNIAK classification stratifies cystic renal masses' malignancy risk","Renal mass biopsy in selected cases (indeterminate masses, before ablation, or when it changes management); stage with chest imaging and labs","Assess for venous (renal vein/IVC) tumor thrombus and metastases"],"tx":["Localized: PARTIAL nephrectomy (nephron-sparing) preferred for small/T1 tumors; RADICAL nephrectomy for large/complex tumors; THERMAL ABLATION or ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE for small masses in select/frail patients","Locally advanced: radical nephrectomy +/- IVC tumor thrombectomy","Metastatic RCC: systemic therapy (immune checkpoint inhibitor combinations, VEGF tyrosine kinase inhibitors); cytoreductive nephrectomy in selected patients"],"tech":["Partial vs radical nephrectomy per tumor size/location (see next entry); IVC thrombectomy for venous extension (may need cardiopulmonary bypass for high-level thrombus)","Percutaneous/laparoscopic thermal ablation (cryo/radiofrequency) for small masses","Cytoreductive nephrectomy in selected metastatic disease"],"after":["Surveillance imaging per stage/grade for recurrence; monitor renal function (especially after radical nephrectomy or in solitary kidneys)","Manage systemic therapy toxicities for metastatic disease","Genetic evaluation if a hereditary syndrome is suspected (young, multifocal, bilateral, syndromic features)"],"pearls":["Most RCCs are incidental; enhancement on contrast imaging indicates a solid (likely malignant) mass - the Bosniak system risk-stratifies cystic masses","Nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy is preferred for small (T1) tumors to preserve renal function","RCC can extend as venous tumor thrombus (renal vein/IVC) and cause a left varicocele or paraneoplastic syndromes","Metastatic RCC is treated with immune checkpoint inhibitor combinations/VEGF TKIs; consider hereditary syndromes (VHL) in young/multifocal/bilateral disease"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Renal Cell Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/kidney/hp/kidney-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Cell Carcinoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470336/"}]},{"id":"uro-nephrectomy","name":"Partial vs Radical Nephrectomy","sec":"uro","present":["Surgical removal of renal tumors is by PARTIAL nephrectomy (excise the tumor, preserve the kidney) or RADICAL nephrectomy (remove the whole kidney +/- adrenal, Gerota's fascia)","Choice balances oncologic control against preserving renal function (avoiding chronic kidney disease and its cardiovascular consequences)","Approach can be open, laparoscopic, or robotic depending on tumor complexity (e.g., nephrometry score) and surgeon expertise"],"dx":["Assess tumor size, location (hilar, endophytic), and complexity (e.g., R.E.N.A.L. nephrometry) to judge partial-nephrectomy feasibility","Evaluate baseline renal function, solitary kidney, bilateral tumors, and comorbidity (CKD risk)","Stage to exclude nodal/venous/metastatic disease"],"tx":["PARTIAL nephrectomy (nephron-sparing) is preferred for small (T1a/T1b) and imperative cases (solitary kidney, bilateral tumors, CKD) - equivalent cancer control with better renal preservation","RADICAL nephrectomy for large/complex/locally advanced tumors or when partial is not feasible","Thermal ablation/active surveillance for small masses in select patients"],"tech":["Partial nephrectomy: hilar control, tumor excision with a margin, and reconstruction (renorrhaphy); minimize WARM ISCHEMIA time during hilar clamping (or use off-clamp/selective clamping) to protect function","Radical nephrectomy: ligate the renal artery then vein, remove the kidney within Gerota's fascia (adrenal spared unless involved)","Robotic/laparoscopic or open per complexity"],"after":["Partial: watch for urine leak, bleeding/pseudoaneurysm (may need angioembolization), and positive margins; radical: monitor renal function (CKD risk) in the remaining kidney","Surveillance imaging by stage/grade","Optimize cardiovascular/renal risk after nephron loss"],"pearls":["Partial (nephron-sparing) nephrectomy is preferred for small (T1) tumors - equivalent oncologic control with preserved renal function","Radical nephrectomy is for large/complex/locally advanced tumors or when partial is unsafe","Minimize warm ischemia time during partial nephrectomy hilar clamping to protect renal function","Nephron-sparing is imperative for a solitary kidney, bilateral tumors, or baseline CKD"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Nephrectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555981/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Partial Nephrectomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572081/"}]},{"id":"uro-testicular-cancer","name":"Testicular Cancer, Germ Cell Tumors & RPLND","sec":"uro","present":["Testicular cancer is the most common solid malignancy in young men (~15-35); most are GERM CELL TUMORS, divided into SEMINOMA and NON-SEMINOMATOUS germ cell tumors (NSGCT) - management differs","Presents as a painless testicular mass/swelling; risk factors include cryptorchidism, prior testicular cancer, and family history","It is highly curable, even when metastatic, because of platinum-based chemotherapy sensitivity"],"dx":["Scrotal ULTRASOUND characterizes the mass; SERUM TUMOR MARKERS (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) aid diagnosis, staging, and monitoring (AFP is NOT elevated in pure seminoma - an elevated AFP means treat as NSGCT)","Do NOT perform a transscrotal biopsy (seeding risk) - diagnosis is by RADICAL INGUINAL ORCHIECTOMY; stage with CT abdomen/pelvis (retroperitoneal nodes) and chest imaging","Offer sperm banking before treatment (fertility preservation)"],"tx":["RADICAL INGUINAL ORCHIECTOMY is both diagnostic and therapeutic; further treatment by histology/stage","SEMINOMA: stage I - surveillance (preferred), or single-agent carboplatin/radiation; advanced - platinum-based chemotherapy (radiosensitive/chemosensitive)","NSGCT: stage I - surveillance, chemotherapy, or nerve-sparing RETROPERITONEAL LYMPH NODE DISSECTION (RPLND); advanced - BEP chemotherapy then RPLND for residual masses"],"tech":["Radical INGUINAL orchiectomy with high cord ligation (never transscrotal); place a prosthesis if desired","RPLND (nerve-sparing when possible to preserve antegrade ejaculation) for NSGCT staging or post-chemotherapy residual masses (teratoma/viable tumor)","Post-chemotherapy RPLND for residual NSGCT masses; residual seminoma masses often watched/PET-assessed"],"after":["Follow tumor markers and imaging on a surveillance schedule; rising markers indicate relapse","RPLND risk: loss of antegrade ejaculation (mitigated by nerve-sparing), chylous ascites, and vascular injury","Monitor for late chemotherapy effects (cardiovascular, secondary malignancy, neuropathy); support fertility counseling"],"pearls":["Diagnose testicular cancer by radical INGUINAL orchiectomy - NEVER transscrotal biopsy (seeding risk)","AFP is not elevated in pure seminoma; an elevated AFP mandates treating as NSGCT regardless of pathology","Tumor markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) guide staging, prognosis, and relapse detection; offer sperm banking first","Testicular germ cell tumors are highly curable (platinum-sensitive); nerve-sparing RPLND preserves antegrade ejaculation"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Testicular Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/testicular/hp/testicular-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Testicular Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563159/"}]},{"id":"uro-penile-cancer","name":"Penile Cancer","sec":"uro","present":["Penile cancer is usually SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA, often arising on the glans/prepuce; HPV (especially 16/18) and lack of circumcision, phimosis, chronic inflammation, and smoking are risk factors","It presents as a non-healing penile lesion, ulcer, or mass; delayed presentation is common due to embarrassment","Spread is predictably via INGUINAL LYMPH NODES first - nodal status is the strongest prognostic factor"],"dx":["Biopsy the primary lesion for diagnosis/grade; examine and image the INGUINAL nodes (palpable nodes and imaging guide nodal management)","MRI/ultrasound assesses local invasion (corpora); cross-sectional imaging/PET for nodal and distant staging","Assess HPV status and stage by primary invasion and nodal involvement"],"tx":["PRIMARY: organ-sparing options (topical therapy/laser/glansectomy/wide local excision) for low-stage/superficial disease; partial or total penectomy for invasive tumors, preserving length when oncologically safe","NODAL: inguinal lymph node dissection (or dynamic sentinel node biopsy) is critical - early lymphadenectomy for node-positive disease improves survival; management is guided by risk of nodal spread even when nodes are clinically negative but high-risk","Advanced disease: multimodal chemotherapy +/- surgery/radiation"],"tech":["Penile-preserving surgery (Mohs/laser/glansectomy) for select superficial tumors; partial/total penectomy for invasive disease","INGUINAL LYMPHADENECTOMY (or sentinel node biopsy) for staging/treatment of nodal disease - the key determinant of outcome","Reconstruction (perineal urethrostomy after total penectomy)"],"after":["Inguinal lymphadenectomy has notable morbidity (wound complications, LYMPHEDEMA, lymphocele) - manage meticulously","Psychosexual support and reconstruction counseling; surveillance for local/nodal recurrence","HPV counseling and smoking cessation"],"pearls":["Penile cancer is usually squamous cell; HPV, phimosis/lack of circumcision, and chronic inflammation are risk factors","Inguinal lymph node status is the strongest prognostic factor - do not delay nodal evaluation/lymphadenectomy in high-risk or node-positive disease","Organ-sparing treatment is appropriate for low-stage disease; penectomy for invasive tumors","Inguinal lymphadenectomy carries significant morbidity (lymphedema, wound issues); sentinel node biopsy reduces this in clinically node-negative patients"],"refs":[{"t":"NCI: Penile Cancer Treatment (PDQ)","u":"https://www.cancer.gov/types/penile/hp/penile-treatment-pdq"},{"t":"StatPearls: Penile Cancer","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559046/"}]},{"id":"uro-adrenal-mass","name":"Adrenal Masses & Incidentaloma","sec":"uro","present":["An adrenal INCIDENTALOMA is an adrenal mass found on imaging done for another reason; the two questions are: is it FUNCTIONAL (hormone-secreting) and is it MALIGNANT?","Functional tumors: cortisol-secreting (Cushing/subclinical hypercortisolism), aldosterone-secreting (primary aldosteronism/Conn), and catecholamine-secreting (PHEOCHROMOCYTOMA)","Most incidentalomas are benign non-functioning adenomas, but adrenocortical carcinoma and metastases must be excluded"],"dx":["BIOCHEMICAL workup for all: overnight dexamethasone suppression test (cortisol), plasma/urine metanephrines (pheochromocytoma), and aldosterone-to-renin ratio if hypertensive/hypokalemic","IMAGING characterization: benign adenomas are lipid-rich (low HU on non-contrast CT, <=10 HU) with rapid contrast washout; large size (>4 cm), irregular/heterogeneous features, and high unenhanced HU raise malignancy concern","Do NOT biopsy until PHEOCHROMOCYTOMA IS EXCLUDED (biopsy can precipitate a hypertensive crisis)"],"tx":["Resect FUNCTIONAL tumors (pheochromocytoma, aldosteronoma with lateralization, cortisol-secreting causing Cushing) and masses SUSPICIOUS FOR MALIGNANCY (large/indeterminate/growing)","Non-functioning, benign-appearing small masses: surveillance imaging/biochemistry","Medical management of hormone excess as indicated (e.g., mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist for non-surgical aldosteronism)"],"tech":["Laparoscopic ADRENALECTOMY for most functional/benign-indeterminate masses; open adrenalectomy for suspected adrenocortical carcinoma or very large tumors (avoid capsule rupture/tumor spillage)","Pre-operative preparation is essential for pheochromocytoma (alpha-blockade - see the pheochromocytoma entry)","Cortical-sparing surgery for bilateral/hereditary disease in selected cases"],"after":["After cortisol-secreting tumor removal, expect adrenal insufficiency of the contralateral gland - give perioperative/tapering glucocorticoids","Surveillance for non-resected masses (imaging/biochemistry over a defined period)","Monitor blood pressure/electrolytes post-adrenalectomy"],"pearls":["Every adrenal incidentaloma needs biochemical screening (cortisol via dexamethasone suppression, metanephrines, and aldosterone-renin if hypertensive)","Benign adenomas are lipid-rich (<=10 HU unenhanced) with rapid washout; size >4 cm and indeterminate features suggest malignancy","NEVER biopsy an adrenal mass until pheochromocytoma is biochemically excluded (crisis risk)","After removing a cortisol-secreting adenoma, treat the resulting (contralateral) adrenal insufficiency with glucocorticoids"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Adrenal Incidentaloma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493229/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Adrenal Adenoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538262/"}]},{"id":"uro-pheochromocytoma","name":"Pheochromocytoma - Surgical Management","sec":"uro","present":["Pheochromocytoma is a CATECHOLAMINE-secreting tumor of the adrenal MEDULLA (extra-adrenal tumors are paragangliomas); it causes episodic or sustained hypertension with the classic triad of HEADACHE, PALPITATIONS, and DIAPHORESIS","It follows a 'rule of 10s' teaching heuristic (roughly 10% bilateral, extra-adrenal, malignant, hereditary - though hereditary fractions are now known to be higher)","Associated hereditary syndromes: MEN2 (RET), von Hippel-Lindau, neurofibromatosis type 1, and SDH-related paraganglioma syndromes"],"dx":["Biochemical confirmation: elevated plasma free METANEPHRINES or 24-hour urine metanephrines/catecholamines (metanephrines are the most sensitive test)","Localize with CT/MRI; functional imaging (MIBG or DOTATATE/PET) for extra-adrenal, multifocal, or metastatic disease","Offer GENETIC testing/counseling (high heritability); screen for associated syndromic features"],"tx":["Definitive treatment is surgical resection (adrenalectomy), but PREOPERATIVE MEDICAL PREPARATION IS MANDATORY to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis","ALPHA-BLOCKADE FIRST (e.g., phenoxybenzamine or a selective alpha-1 blocker) for ~10-14 days with volume expansion/liberal salt; ADD beta-blockade ONLY AFTER adequate alpha-blockade (beta-blockade first risks unopposed alpha vasoconstriction and crisis)","Manage hypertensive crises and coordinate anesthesia for hemodynamic swings"],"tech":["Laparoscopic adrenalectomy is standard for most; minimize tumor manipulation to limit catecholamine surges; ligate the adrenal VEIN early to reduce catecholamine release","Open resection for large/malignant/invasive tumors; cortical-sparing surgery for bilateral/hereditary disease","Close intraoperative hemodynamic monitoring (expect swings, and hypotension after tumor removal)"],"after":["After resection, anticipate HYPOTENSION (loss of catecholamine drive) and HYPOGLYCEMIA (rebound insulin) - support blood pressure/glucose","Monitor for residual/recurrent/metastatic disease with metanephrines and imaging; lifelong surveillance in hereditary disease","Genetic counseling and family screening"],"pearls":["Diagnose with plasma free or urinary metanephrines (most sensitive); localize with CT/MRI (+/- MIBG/DOTATATE for extra-adrenal/metastatic)","MANDATORY preoperative ALPHA-blockade with volume expansion for ~10-14 days; add beta-blockade ONLY after alpha-blockade (never beta first - unopposed alpha causes crisis)","Ligate the adrenal vein early and minimize tumor handling to limit catecholamine surges","After removal, expect hypotension and hypoglycemia; high heritability warrants genetic testing (MEN2, VHL, NF1, SDH)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pheochromocytoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589700/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Paraganglioma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549834/"}]},{"id":"uro-retroperitoneal-masses","name":"Retroperitoneal Masses & Fibrosis","sec":"uro","present":["Retroperitoneal masses relevant to urology include primary retroperitoneal SARCOMAS (e.g., liposarcoma, leiomyosarcoma), lymphoma, germ cell metastases, and benign lesions, plus RETROPERITONEAL FIBROSIS","RETROPERITONEAL FIBROSIS is a fibro-inflammatory process encasing the aorta/iliac vessels and URETERS, causing medial ureteral deviation and obstruction (uni- or bilateral); it may be idiopathic (often IgG4-related) or secondary (drugs, malignancy, radiation)","Presentation: vague back/flank/abdominal pain, and features of ureteral obstruction/renal failure"],"dx":["CT/MRI characterize the mass or the periaortic fibrotic rind and ureteral involvement (classic medial ureteral deviation and hydronephrosis in RPF)","Biopsy for tissue diagnosis of masses (sarcoma vs lymphoma vs other) and to exclude malignancy in atypical/secondary fibrosis; check inflammatory markers and IgG4","Assess renal function and degree of obstruction"],"tx":["RETROPERITONEAL FIBROSIS: relieve ureteral obstruction (stents/nephrostomy) and treat the inflammation with CORTICOSTEROIDS (+/- other immunosuppression); URETEROLYSIS (surgical freeing of the ureters, often with omental wrap/intraperitonealization) for refractory/severe cases; stop offending drugs and treat secondary causes","Retroperitoneal SARCOMA: en-bloc surgical resection (often with adjacent organs) is the mainstay; radiation/chemo as adjuncts per histology","Lymphoma/germ cell: systemic therapy (not primarily surgical)"],"tech":["Ureteral stent or nephrostomy for obstruction; ureterolysis with omental wrap or lateral/intraperitoneal transposition to protect ureters in RPF","Radical en-bloc resection for retroperitoneal sarcoma (may include kidney/colon) with attention to margins","Image-guided or open biopsy for diagnosis"],"after":["RPF: monitor renal function, inflammatory markers, and imaging response to steroids/immunosuppression; watch for relapse and stent-related issues","Sarcoma: surveillance for local recurrence (common) per histology/grade","Manage immunosuppression side effects"],"pearls":["Retroperitoneal fibrosis classically causes MEDIAL ureteral deviation with obstruction (vs lateral deviation from most masses) - often idiopathic/IgG4-related","Treat RPF with ureteral decompression + corticosteroids/immunosuppression; ureterolysis (with omental wrap) for refractory cases; exclude malignancy/secondary causes","Retroperitoneal sarcoma is treated with en-bloc surgical resection - histology (liposarcoma most common) guides adjuvant therapy","Always get tissue for a new retroperitoneal mass to distinguish sarcoma, lymphoma, and germ cell metastasis (very different treatments)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Retroperitoneal Fibrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482409/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Retroperitoneal Sarcoma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544365/"}]},{"id":"uro-stents-nephrostomy","name":"Upper-Tract Obstruction - Ureteral Stents & Nephrostomy","sec":"uro","present":["Upper-tract drainage is achieved by a retrograde URETERAL STENT (double-J, placed cystoscopically) or an antegrade PERCUTANEOUS NEPHROSTOMY (placed through the flank under imaging) - core urologic decompression skills","Indications: obstructing stones (especially with infection), malignant ureteral obstruction, ureteral injury/stricture, and to protect a ureteral repair or anastomosis","Choice depends on the clinical setting: sepsis, access feasibility, stone burden, and whether antegrade access is needed"],"dx":["Confirm obstruction and its level (ultrasound/CT); assess for infection (obstruction + infection = emergency decompression)","Determine retrograde feasibility (stent) vs need for percutaneous access (nephrostomy) - e.g., impassable stricture, hostile bladder, or the need for the most direct drainage in sepsis","Evaluate coagulation status before percutaneous access"],"tx":["Ureteral STENT for many obstructions (internal drainage, no external bag); NEPHROSTOMY when retrograde access fails, for the most reliable drainage in severe sepsis, or when antegrade access is needed for later procedures","For obstructive pyelonephritis, either stent or nephrostomy relieves the infected system - do it urgently with antibiotics","Definitive treatment of the cause after decompression"],"tech":["Retrograde stent: cystoscopy, guidewire up the ureter, and double-J stent placement (confirm coiling in renal pelvis and bladder)","Percutaneous nephrostomy: image-guided (ultrasound/fluoroscopy) puncture of a posterior calyx, tract dilation, and drainage catheter placement","Antegrade stenting or nephroureteral stents when combined access is useful"],"after":["STENT symptoms (irritative voiding, flank pain on voiding, hematuria) are common; both stents and nephrostomies can obstruct, migrate, or become infected/encrusted","EXCHANGE indwelling stents/nephrostomies on schedule and NEVER FORGET a stent - a retained, encrusted stent is a serious avoidable complication","Monitor drainage/renal function and plan definitive management"],"pearls":["Obstruction with infection is an emergency - decompress urgently with either a ureteral stent or a percutaneous nephrostomy, plus antibiotics","Choose nephrostomy when retrograde access fails, for the most reliable drainage in severe sepsis, or when antegrade access is needed","A forgotten/retained ureteral stent encrusts and obstructs - always track and exchange/remove on schedule","Stent-related irritative symptoms and hematuria are expected; counsel patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteral Stent","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574569/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Percutaneous Nephrostomy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493185/"}]},{"id":"uro-bph-luts-evaluation","name":"BPH & LUTS - Evaluation","sec":"uro","present":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is histologic proliferation of the transition zone; it causes bladder outlet obstruction and LOWER URINARY TRACT SYMPTOMS (LUTS) - both STORAGE (frequency, urgency, nocturia) and VOIDING (weak stream, hesitancy, intermittency, incomplete emptying)","Symptom burden does not correlate with prostate SIZE; small prostates can obstruct and large prostates can be asymptomatic","Complications of untreated obstruction: urinary retention, recurrent UTIs, bladder stones, hematuria, and (late) upper-tract deterioration/renal failure"],"dx":["Quantify symptoms with the IPSS/AUA symptom score; history to exclude other LUTS causes (overactive bladder, nocturnal polyuria, neurologic disease, medications)","Exam (DRE for size/nodules), urinalysis (exclude infection/hematuria), PSA (counsel - BPH raises PSA and size affects it), and post-void residual; uroflowmetry and pressure-flow studies in selected cases","Assess for red flags: hematuria, elevated PVR/retention, recurrent UTI, stones, or renal insufficiency"],"tx":["Mild symptoms: watchful waiting and lifestyle measures (fluid timing, caffeine/alcohol reduction, avoid antihistamines/decongestants that worsen voiding)","Bothersome symptoms: medical therapy (see next entry); surgery for refractory symptoms or complications","Treat/rule out contributing overactive bladder or nocturnal polyuria separately"],"tech":["Diagnostic uroflowmetry and pressure-flow urodynamics distinguish true obstruction from detrusor underactivity when unclear","Cystoscopy to assess anatomy (median lobe, prostatic urethra) before surgical planning","Bladder scan/ultrasound for post-void residual"],"after":["Reassess symptoms (IPSS), PVR, and complications over time; escalate therapy for progression","Counsel that symptom severity, not gland size alone, drives management","Monitor for retention, stones, and upper-tract effects in high-PVR/obstructed patients"],"pearls":["Symptom severity does not correlate with prostate size - small glands can obstruct, large glands can be silent","Use the IPSS/AUA symptom score to quantify LUTS and track response","Red flags mandating further workup/surgery: retention, recurrent UTI, bladder stones, gross hematuria, renal insufficiency","Rule out mimics: overactive bladder, nocturnal polyuria, neurologic disease, and offending medications (anticholinergics, decongestants)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558920/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538283/"}]},{"id":"uro-bph-medical-therapy","name":"BPH - Medical Therapy","sec":"uro","present":["Medical therapy is first-line for bothersome BPH/LUTS; the main classes are ALPHA-BLOCKERS (rapid symptom relief) and 5-ALPHA-REDUCTASE INHIBITORS (5-ARIs, size reduction over months)","Alpha-blockers relax prostatic/bladder-neck smooth muscle (dynamic obstruction); 5-ARIs shrink the gland (static obstruction) and reduce progression/retention risk in larger prostates","Adjuncts: antimuscarinics/beta-3 agonists for storage-predominant symptoms, PDE5 inhibitors (tadalafil) for LUTS + erectile dysfunction, and combination therapy"],"dx":["Match therapy to symptom pattern and prostate size: alpha-blocker for rapid relief; add/choose 5-ARI for larger glands (>~30-40 mL or elevated PSA as a size surrogate)","Assess for storage-predominant symptoms (consider antimuscarinic/beta-3 agonist, checking PVR first)","Review cataract/eye-surgery status (alpha-blockers cause intraoperative floppy iris syndrome)"],"tx":["ALPHA-BLOCKERS (tamsulosin, alfuzosin, silodosin - uroselective; or doxazosin/terazosin - also lower BP): fast symptom relief within days","5-ARIs (finasteride, dutasteride): shrink the prostate over 3-6 months, lower PSA by ~50%, and reduce retention/surgery risk in larger glands","COMBINATION (alpha-blocker + 5-ARI) for larger prostates and progression risk (MTOPS/CombAT); add tadalafil for concurrent ED; antimuscarinic/beta-3 agonist for persistent storage symptoms"],"tech":["No procedure; optimize/sequence drugs and set expectations for onset (alpha-blocker days vs 5-ARI months)","Check PVR before adding antimuscarinics (retention risk)","De-prescribe/adjust based on response and side effects"],"after":["Alpha-blocker side effects: orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, RETROGRADE EJACULATION (especially silodosin/tamsulosin), and intraoperative FLOPPY IRIS SYNDROME (warn ophthalmology before cataract surgery)","5-ARI effects: decreased libido, ED, gynecomastia; 5-ARIs HALVE PSA (double the measured value when screening) and take months to work","Reassess symptoms and adherence; escalate to surgery if refractory"],"pearls":["Alpha-blockers give rapid relief (dynamic obstruction); 5-ARIs shrink the gland over months and reduce progression/retention in larger prostates (static obstruction)","5-ARIs lower PSA by ~50% - double the measured PSA when interpreting for cancer screening","Alpha-blockers cause intraoperative floppy iris syndrome - warn the ophthalmologist before cataract surgery; retrograde ejaculation is common with uroselective agents","Combination therapy (alpha-blocker + 5-ARI) benefits larger glands (MTOPS/CombAT); add tadalafil if ED coexists"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: 5-Alpha-Reductase Inhibitors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555930/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Alpha-1 Adrenergic Blockers","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556066/"}]},{"id":"uro-turp-surgical-bph","name":"TURP & Surgical BPH Options","sec":"uro","present":["Surgery is indicated for BPH refractory to medical therapy or with complications: refractory retention, recurrent UTI, bladder stones, recurrent gross hematuria, or renal insufficiency from obstruction","TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF THE PROSTATE (TURP) is the historical gold standard; many alternatives now exist tailored to gland size and patient factors","Size/anatomy guides choice: smaller glands (TURP, laser, minimally invasive), larger glands (laser enucleation, simple prostatectomy)"],"dx":["Confirm bladder outlet obstruction and gland size (cystoscopy, imaging/ultrasound); assess median lobe and prostatic urethral length","Evaluate anticoagulation, sexual-function priorities (ejaculation preservation), and fitness for anesthesia","Rule out prostate cancer and neurogenic causes"],"tx":["TURP (monopolar or BIPOLAR) for moderate glands; LASER options (HoLEP - holmium enucleation, effective for all sizes; photoselective vaporization/GreenLight - good for anticoagulated patients)","EJACULATION-PRESERVING/minimally invasive options (prostatic urethral lift/UroLift, water vapor therapy/Rezum, aquablation, prostatic artery embolization) for suitable patients","SIMPLE PROSTATECTOMY (open/robotic/laser enucleation) for very large glands"],"tech":["TURP: endoscopic resection of prostatic adenoma in chips; BIPOLAR TURP uses normal saline irrigation (reducing TUR syndrome risk vs monopolar's hypotonic glycine)","HoLEP enucleates the adenoma off the capsule (size-independent, durable, low bleeding); GreenLight vaporization suits anticoagulated patients","Minimally invasive/ejaculation-sparing procedures for select anatomy"],"after":["Classic TURP complications: bleeding, TUR SYNDROME (dilutional hyponatremia from hypotonic irrigant absorption - largely avoided with bipolar/saline), RETROGRADE EJACULATION (common), urethral/bladder-neck stricture, and transient incontinence","Ejaculation-preserving procedures (UroLift, Rezum) trade some efficacy for preserved antegrade ejaculation","Monitor for retention resolution and stricture; catheter then voiding trial"],"pearls":["TURP is the classic benchmark; BIPOLAR TURP uses saline irrigation and avoids the dilutional hyponatremia (TUR syndrome) seen with monopolar/glycine","HoLEP is size-independent, durable, and low-bleeding - useful for very large glands and anticoagulated patients (as is GreenLight)","Retrograde ejaculation is common after TURP; UroLift/Rezum preserve ejaculation for suitable anatomy","Surgery is indicated for refractory retention, recurrent UTI/hematuria, bladder stones, or obstructive renal insufficiency"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Transurethral Resection of the Prostate","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560529/"},{"t":"StatPearls: TURP Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559089/"}]},{"id":"uro-acute-urinary-retention","name":"Acute Urinary Retention","sec":"uro","present":["Acute urinary retention (AUR) is the sudden, painful inability to void with a palpable/distended bladder - a urologic emergency requiring prompt drainage","Most common cause in men is BPH; precipitants include medications (anticholinergics, sympathomimetics, opioids), constipation, UTI/prostatitis, alcohol, and postoperative/immobility states","Other causes: urethral stricture, clot retention, neurologic disease, and (in both sexes) obstructing masses; CHRONIC retention may be painless with high residual volumes and overflow incontinence"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis (suprapubic pain/distension, inability to void) confirmed by bladder scan and immediate relief with catheter drainage (record the drained volume)","Identify the precipitant (medications, infection, constipation, neurologic signs); check renal function and potassium (high-pressure chronic retention causes post-renal AKI)","Distinguish acute (painful) from acute-on-chronic/high-pressure chronic retention (large volumes, hydronephrosis, renal impairment)"],"tx":["Immediate bladder DECOMPRESSION with a urethral catheter (suprapubic catheter if urethral catheterization fails/contraindicated); relieve the obstruction and treat precipitants","Start an ALPHA-BLOCKER before a trial without catheter (TWOC) to improve the chance of successful voiding","Treat infection/constipation, stop offending drugs, and plan definitive BPH management if that is the cause"],"tech":["Urethral catheterization (coude catheter may help past an enlarged prostate); suprapubic catheter if the urethra cannot be catheterized","Trial without catheter after a few days on an alpha-blocker; definitive surgery (TURP, etc.) for failed TWOC/recurrent retention","Slow/monitored drainage awareness (see below)"],"after":["Watch for POST-OBSTRUCTIVE DIURESIS and DECOMPRESSION HEMATURIA after draining a large-volume/high-pressure bladder - monitor urine output, fluids, and electrolytes","High-pressure chronic retention with renal impairment needs continued drainage and monitoring of renal recovery/diuresis","Recurrent retention or failed TWOC typically leads to surgical management of the obstruction"],"pearls":["Acute urinary retention is an emergency - decompress immediately with a catheter; BPH is the most common male cause","Start an alpha-blocker before a trial without catheter to improve voiding success","After draining a large-volume/high-pressure bladder, watch for post-obstructive diuresis and decompression hematuria (fluid/electrolyte monitoring)","Look for reversible precipitants: medications (anticholinergics, opioids, decongestants), constipation, UTI/prostatitis, and alcohol"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acute Urinary Retention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459301/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Retention","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538499/"}]},{"id":"uro-bladder-outlet-obstruction","name":"Bladder Outlet Obstruction","sec":"uro","present":["Bladder outlet obstruction (BOO) is impaired flow at the bladder neck/urethra; in men BPH predominates, but causes include urethral stricture, bladder-neck dysfunction/contracture, prostate cancer, and (both sexes) neurogenic and functional obstruction (detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia)","Chronic BOO causes detrusor changes (hypertrophy, trabeculation, diverticula), then decompensation with incomplete emptying, retention, and upper-tract risk","In women, BOO is less common (prior anti-incontinence surgery, prolapse, primary bladder-neck obstruction, or functional causes)"],"dx":["PRESSURE-FLOW URODYNAMICS is the definitive test distinguishing true obstruction (high pressure, low flow) from detrusor UNDERACTIVITY (low pressure, low flow) - critical when the cause is unclear","Uroflowmetry, post-void residual, cystoscopy (stricture, median lobe, bladder-neck), and imaging localize the level/cause","Assess upper tracts and renal function in chronic/high-pressure obstruction"],"tx":["Relieve obstruction by cause: BPH (medical/surgical), urethral stricture (urethroplasty/dilation), bladder-neck contracture (incision), and treat prostate cancer obstruction appropriately","Functional/neurogenic BOO (detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia): manage per neurourology (catheterization, botulinum toxin, medications)","Protect the upper tracts and bladder function; treat before decompensation"],"tech":["Definitive procedures by cause: TURP/enucleation (BPH), direct-vision internal urethrotomy or urethroplasty (stricture), bladder-neck incision (contracture/primary bladder-neck obstruction)","Clean intermittent catheterization or sphincter-directed therapy for functional/neurogenic obstruction","Urodynamic confirmation before irreversible outlet surgery when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"after":["Confirm relief of obstruction (improved flow, reduced PVR) and monitor for recurrence (e.g., stricture, bladder-neck contracture)","A chronically decompensated (underactive) detrusor may not recover even after relieving obstruction - counsel accordingly","Upper-tract and renal surveillance in high-pressure chronic obstruction"],"pearls":["Pressure-flow urodynamics distinguishes true obstruction (high pressure/low flow) from detrusor underactivity (low pressure/low flow) - essential before irreversible outlet surgery when unclear","Chronic BOO remodels the bladder (trabeculation, diverticula) and can decompensate to an underactive detrusor that may not recover","BPH is the leading male cause; also consider urethral stricture, bladder-neck contracture, and prostate cancer","In women, BOO is uncommon - consider prior incontinence surgery, prolapse, or functional/neurogenic causes"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Outlet Obstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563238/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urodynamic Studies","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562256/"}]},{"id":"uro-erectile-dysfunction","name":"Erectile Dysfunction","sec":"uro","present":["Erectile dysfunction (ED) is the consistent inability to attain/maintain an erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual activity; it is largely VASCULAR and is an early marker of systemic endothelial/cardiovascular disease","Causes: vasculogenic (atherosclerosis, diabetes, hypertension - most common), neurogenic (pelvic surgery/radiation, spinal cord, diabetes), hormonal (hypogonadism), medication-induced, and psychogenic (sudden onset, situational, preserved nocturnal erections)","ED shares risk factors with coronary disease - new ED warrants cardiovascular risk assessment"],"dx":["History (onset, nocturnal/morning erections, psychosocial factors) and validated questionnaires (IIEF/SHIM); exam (genital, secondary sex characteristics, peripheral pulses)","Labs: morning TESTOSTERONE (plus fasting glucose/HbA1c, lipids); further testing (penile Doppler ultrasound) for select/complex or medicolegal cases","Screen for cardiovascular risk (ED as a harbinger of CAD)"],"tx":["Lifestyle/risk-factor modification and treat reversible causes; PDE5 INHIBITORS (sildenafil, tadalafil) are first-line oral therapy","Second-line: intracavernosal or intraurethral vasoactive agents (alprostadil), vacuum erection devices; third-line: PENILE PROSTHESIS implantation for refractory ED","Address hypogonadism (testosterone if deficient) and psychological factors; switch offending medications where possible"],"tech":["Penile Doppler ultrasound (with vasoactive injection) evaluates arterial inflow and venous leak in select cases","Penile PROSTHESIS (inflatable multi-component or malleable) for refractory ED; intracavernosal injection teaching","Counsel/coordinate with cardiology for CV risk"],"after":["PDE5 INHIBITORS ARE CONTRAINDICATED WITH NITRATES (life-threatening hypotension) - a critical teaching point; caution with alpha-blockers","Counsel on priapism risk with injection therapy; prosthesis complications include infection and mechanical failure","Reassess response, adherence, and cardiovascular risk over time"],"pearls":["ED is predominantly vascular and an early marker of cardiovascular disease - new ED warrants CV risk assessment","PDE5 inhibitors are first-line and ABSOLUTELY CONTRAINDICATED with nitrates (severe hypotension)","Sudden-onset, situational ED with preserved nocturnal/morning erections suggests a psychogenic cause","Escalate first-line orals -> injections/vacuum device -> penile prosthesis for refractory ED; check morning testosterone"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Erectile Dysfunction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562253/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Phosphodiesterase-5 Inhibitors","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549843/"}]},{"id":"uro-male-infertility","name":"Male Infertility","sec":"uro","present":["Infertility affects ~15% of couples; a male factor contributes in about half. Evaluate the male partner in parallel with the female workup","Causes span PRE-TESTICULAR (hypothalamic-pituitary/hormonal), TESTICULAR (varicocele, cryptorchidism, genetic - Klinefelter/Y-microdeletion, gonadotoxins, orchitis), and POST-TESTICULAR (obstruction - vasectomy/CBAVD, ejaculatory dysfunction)","AZOOSPERMIA (no sperm) is subdivided into obstructive vs non-obstructive - a key branch point"],"dx":["Two SEMEN ANALYSES (count, motility, morphology, volume) are the cornerstone; history/exam (testicular volume, varicocele, vas deferens presence)","Hormonal panel (FSH, LH, testosterone) localizes the level; low volume/azoospermia prompts checking for obstruction (post-ejaculate urine, absent vas)","GENETIC testing for severe oligospermia/azoospermia: karyotype (Klinefelter 47,XXY), Y-chromosome microdeletions, and CFTR mutations (congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, CBAVD)"],"tx":["Treat reversible causes: VARICOCELE repair (improves parameters in selected men), correct endocrine disorders, stop gonadotoxins, and manage ejaculatory/obstructive issues","Obstructive azoospermia: surgical reconstruction (vasovasostomy/vasoepididymostomy) or sperm retrieval; non-obstructive azoospermia: microdissection TESTICULAR SPERM EXTRACTION (microTESE)","Assisted reproduction (IUI, IVF/ICSI) using ejaculated or surgically retrieved sperm"],"tech":["Sperm retrieval: microTESE for non-obstructive azoospermia; percutaneous/open epididymal or testicular retrieval for obstructive causes","Microsurgical vasectomy reversal (vasovasostomy) or vasoepididymostomy; microsurgical varicocelectomy","Coordinate retrieved sperm with IVF/ICSI"],"after":["CBAVD (absent vas) is associated with CFTR mutations - offer genetic counseling to the couple before ICSI","Klinefelter and Y-microdeletions have implications for retrieval success and offspring - counsel accordingly","Avoid empiric testosterone for fertility - EXOGENOUS TESTOSTERONE SUPPRESSES SPERMATOGENESIS (a common harmful error)"],"pearls":["Never give exogenous testosterone to a man trying to conceive - it suppresses the HPG axis and spermatogenesis (acts as a contraceptive)","Two semen analyses are the cornerstone; FSH/LH/testosterone localize the level of the problem","Severe oligospermia/azoospermia needs genetic testing: karyotype (Klinefelter), Y-microdeletions, and CFTR (CBAVD)","Non-obstructive azoospermia -> microTESE; obstructive -> reconstruction or retrieval; varicocele repair helps selected men"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Male Infertility","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562258/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Azoospermia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578204/"}]},{"id":"uro-varicocele","name":"Varicocele","sec":"uro","present":["A varicocele is dilation of the pampiniform plexus veins; it is the most common CORRECTABLE cause of male infertility and can cause a dull scrotal ache and testicular atrophy","It is far more common on the LEFT (the left gonadal vein drains at a right angle into the left renal vein - higher pressure/longer course); a classic 'bag of worms' that decompresses when supine","A NEW right-sided or non-decompressing (isolated, sudden) varicocele warrants imaging to exclude a retroperitoneal mass/renal vein or IVC obstruction"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis on standing exam with Valsalva (graded I-III); scrotal ultrasound with Doppler confirms and sizes when the exam is equivocal","Assess testicular volume/asymmetry and semen parameters when fertility is a concern","Image the retroperitoneum for an isolated right-sided or acute non-reducible varicocele (rule out obstructing mass/thrombus)"],"tx":["Repair indications: infertility with abnormal semen parameters and a palpable varicocele, testicular pain, or adolescent testicular growth arrest/atrophy - not asymptomatic subclinical varicoceles","VARICOCELECTOMY (microsurgical subinguinal is preferred - lowest recurrence/complication rates) or percutaneous embolization","Observation for asymptomatic varicoceles without fertility concern"],"tech":["MICROSURGICAL subinguinal varicocelectomy: ligate the internal spermatic veins under magnification while preserving the testicular artery and lymphatics (reduces hydrocele/recurrence)","Percutaneous retrograde/antegrade embolization as an alternative","Laparoscopic or retroperitoneal (Palomo) approaches are alternatives"],"after":["Complications: hydrocele (from lymphatic injury - minimized by microsurgery), recurrence/persistence, and rare testicular artery injury","Recheck semen parameters ~3-6 months post-repair (spermatogenesis cycle) to assess improvement","Adolescents: monitor catch-up testicular growth"],"pearls":["Varicoceles are usually LEFT-sided (left gonadal vein anatomy); a new right-sided or non-decompressing varicocele warrants imaging for a retroperitoneal/renal mass","It is the most common surgically correctable cause of male infertility; repair for abnormal semen + palpable varicocele, pain, or adolescent growth arrest","Microsurgical subinguinal varicocelectomy has the lowest recurrence/hydrocele rates (preserves artery and lymphatics)","Do not repair asymptomatic subclinical varicoceles; recheck semen ~3-6 months after repair"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Varicocele","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448113/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Varicocele Repair","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553124/"}]},{"id":"uro-peyronie-disease","name":"Peyronie's Disease","sec":"uro","present":["Peyronie's disease is a fibrotic PLAQUE of the tunica albuginea causing penile CURVATURE, pain, deformity (narrowing/hinge), and often erectile dysfunction - it can impair intercourse and cause distress","It has an ACUTE (active) inflammatory phase (evolving deformity, pain) and a CHRONIC (stable) phase (stable curvature, calcified plaque, usually painless)","Associated with penile trauma/microtrauma, Dupuytren's contracture, and ED; a subset follows a documented injury"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis: palpable plaque and curvature; document deformity with photographs and/or an in-office injection-induced erection (objective angle/plaque mapping)","Assess erectile function and psychosocial impact; ultrasound can characterize/calcify plaque if needed","Distinguish active vs stable phase (guides timing of treatment)"],"tx":["ACTIVE phase: nonsurgical management and stabilization (oral agents have limited evidence); intralesional COLLAGENASE clostridium histolyticum for suitable curvature, plus traction therapy","SURGERY is reserved for STABLE disease (>~12 months, stable curvature) impairing intercourse: plication (tunical shortening on the convex side), plaque incision/excision with grafting (for severe curvature/short penis), or penile PROSTHESIS when ED coexists","Set realistic expectations (deformity improvement, not perfection)"],"tech":["Intralesional collagenase injections (with modeling) for the stable/active plaque per protocol; penile traction as adjunct","Surgical correction: Nesbit/plication for milder curvature, plaque incision-and-grafting for severe deformity, or inflatable penile prosthesis (+/- modeling) when ED is significant","Operate only in stable disease"],"after":["Plication may shorten the penis; grafting risks ED/sensory change; prosthesis carries infection/mechanical risks - counsel on trade-offs","Counsel that some residual curvature/shortening is common; psychological support","Do not operate during the active phase (deformity still evolving)"],"pearls":["Distinguish active (evolving, painful) from stable (>~12 months, painless) phases - operate only when stable","Surgery options: plication (may shorten), plaque incision/grafting (severe curvature), or penile prosthesis when ED coexists","Intralesional collagenase + traction is a nonsurgical option for suitable curvature","Association with Dupuytren's contracture and penile trauma; ED frequently coexists and drives management"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Peyronie Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562207/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Penile Curvature","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574531/"}]},{"id":"uro-hypogonadism-testosterone","name":"Male Hypogonadism & Testosterone Therapy","sec":"uro","present":["Male hypogonadism is deficient testosterone with symptoms (low libido, ED, fatigue, decreased muscle mass, mood changes, osteoporosis); classify as PRIMARY (testicular failure - high LH/FSH) or SECONDARY (hypothalamic-pituitary - low/inappropriately normal LH/FSH)","Causes: primary (Klinefelter, orchitis, trauma, chemo/radiation), secondary (pituitary tumor/hyperprolactinemia, opioids, obesity, systemic illness, exogenous steroids)","Symptoms are nonspecific - biochemical confirmation is essential before treating"],"dx":["Confirm with at least TWO MORNING (fasting) total testosterone levels below the reference range PLUS symptoms; measure LH/FSH to localize (primary vs secondary) and prolactin","Check for secondary causes (prolactinoma, iron overload/hemochromatosis, opioids); consider free testosterone when SHBG is abnormal (obesity, aging)","Baseline hematocrit, PSA, and bone density as indicated before therapy"],"tx":["Treat symptomatic, biochemically confirmed hypogonadism with TESTOSTERONE replacement (gels, injections, pellets) after discussing risks/benefits and fertility plans","If FERTILITY is desired, do NOT give exogenous testosterone (it suppresses spermatogenesis) - use hCG, clomiphene, or gonadotropins instead","Address reversible secondary causes (weight loss, stop opioids, treat prolactinoma)"],"tech":["No procedure; select formulation and monitor; testosterone pellets are implanted subcutaneously","Coordinate fertility-preserving alternatives (hCG/clomiphene) when childbearing is desired","Treat underlying pituitary pathology as needed"],"after":["Monitor testosterone response, HEMATOCRIT (erythrocytosis is common - hold/adjust if elevated), PSA/prostate symptoms, and symptom improvement","Counsel that exogenous testosterone suppresses fertility and testicular size; do not use it in men seeking conception","Reassess cardiovascular and prostate considerations per current guidance"],"pearls":["Confirm hypogonadism with two morning total testosterone levels + symptoms before treating; LH/FSH separate primary (high) from secondary (low/normal) causes","Exogenous testosterone suppresses spermatogenesis - use hCG/clomiphene/gonadotropins if fertility is desired","Erythrocytosis (rising hematocrit) is the most common testosterone-therapy adverse effect - monitor and adjust","Evaluate secondary hypogonadism for prolactinoma, opioids, obesity, and hemochromatosis"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypogonadism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532933/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Testosterone","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526128/"}]},{"id":"uro-priapism","name":"Priapism","sec":"uro","present":["Priapism is a prolonged erection (>4 hours) unrelated to stimulation; the critical split is ISCHEMIC (low-flow) vs NON-ISCHEMIC (high-flow)","ISCHEMIC priapism is a compartment-syndrome-like EMERGENCY - a painful, rigid, poorly oxygenated corpora; prolonged ischemia causes fibrosis and permanent ED","NON-ISCHEMIC (high-flow) priapism follows perineal/genital trauma (a cavernous artery fistula); it is non-painful, not fully rigid, and NOT an emergency"],"dx":["Differentiate with CORPORAL BLOOD GAS: ischemic shows hypoxic, hypercapnic, acidotic blood (dark); non-ischemic shows well-oxygenated (bright red) blood; color Doppler ultrasound confirms (absent vs present/turbulent flow, fistula)","Identify causes: ischemic (sickle cell disease, PDE5 inhibitors/injection therapy, medications, malignancy infiltration); non-ischemic (straddle/perineal trauma)","History of trauma points to high-flow; sickle cell/injection points to ischemic"],"tx":["ISCHEMIC = EMERGENCY: aspirate corporal blood and inject an intracavernosal SYMPATHOMIMETIC (PHENYLEPHRINE); escalate to surgical SHUNT if refractory; treat sickle cell disease systemically (hydration, oxygen, exchange transfusion) but do NOT delay local treatment","NON-ISCHEMIC: usually observation (many resolve); selective arterial EMBOLIZATION of the fistula if persistent/bothersome - not an emergency","Time is tissue in ischemic priapism - prompt detumescence preserves erectile function"],"tech":["Corporal aspiration +/- irrigation and intracavernosal phenylephrine (dilute, with cardiovascular monitoring); distal or proximal surgical shunt for refractory ischemic priapism","Selective cavernous artery embolization for high-flow priapism fistula","Penile prosthesis consideration for prolonged ischemia with impending/established fibrosis"],"after":["Ischemic priapism risks permanent ED from cavernosal fibrosis - counsel and follow erectile function; monitor for recurrence (stuttering priapism in sickle cell)","Phenylephrine can cause hypertension/reflex bradycardia - monitor vitals","Treat the underlying cause (sickle cell, offending drugs) to prevent recurrence"],"pearls":["Ischemic (low-flow) priapism is a compartment-syndrome emergency - corporal blood gas shows hypoxic/acidotic (dark) blood; treat immediately with aspiration + intracavernosal phenylephrine","Non-ischemic (high-flow) priapism follows perineal trauma (arterial fistula), is non-painful, and is managed by observation +/- embolization - not an emergency","Prolonged ischemic priapism causes fibrosis and permanent ED - time is tissue","Sickle cell disease and intracavernosal/PDE5 therapies are common causes of ischemic priapism (including stuttering recurrences)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Priapism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459178/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ischemic Priapism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574546/"}]},{"id":"uro-testicular-torsion","name":"Testicular Torsion","sec":"uro","present":["Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord cutting off testicular blood flow - a TIME-CRITICAL surgical emergency; the testis is best salvaged within ~6 hours of onset","Classically ADOLESCENTS/young men with SUDDEN severe unilateral scrotal pain, nausea/vomiting, a high-riding testis with a horizontal lie, and an ABSENT CREMASTERIC REFLEX","A BELL-CLAPPER deformity (inadequate testicular fixation) predisposes and is typically BILATERAL - so the contralateral testis must also be fixed"],"dx":["This is primarily a CLINICAL diagnosis - if suspicion is high, go to the OR without delay (do not wait for imaging)","Color Doppler ultrasound (decreased/absent testicular flow) supports the diagnosis when the picture is equivocal, but should not delay surgery in a clear case","Differentiate from epididymitis (more gradual, positive Prehn's sign/relief with elevation, intact cremasteric reflex) and torsion of the appendix testis ('blue dot' sign)"],"tx":["Emergent surgical EXPLORATION with DETORSION and ORCHIOPEXY (fixation) if viable; ORCHIECTOMY if non-viable - plus contralateral orchiopexy in the same setting (bell-clapper is bilateral)","MANUAL detorsion (often 'open book,' medial-to-lateral) can be attempted as a temporizing bridge while arranging surgery, but definitive fixation is still required","Do not delay for imaging when clinical suspicion is high - time is testis"],"tech":["Scrotal exploration, detorsion, assessment of viability (warm, reperfuse), and ORCHIOPEXY (multi-point fixation) of a viable testis; orchiectomy if necrotic","BILATERAL orchiopexy - always fix the contralateral testis given the bilateral bell-clapper predisposition","Manual detorsion at bedside as a temporizing measure only"],"after":["Counsel on salvage rates falling steeply with time (excellent <6 h, poor >24 h); document viability and fixation","Even after successful detorsion, subfertility/atrophy can occur; follow testicular size","Return precautions and understanding that fixation reduces but does not fully eliminate recurrence risk"],"pearls":["Testicular torsion is a clinical, time-critical emergency - explore within ~6 hours; do NOT delay for imaging when suspicion is high","Absent cremasteric reflex, high-riding horizontal testis, and sudden severe pain suggest torsion; epididymitis is more gradual with an intact reflex and positive Prehn's sign","Always perform CONTRALATERAL orchiopexy - the predisposing bell-clapper deformity is bilateral","Manual detorsion ('open book') is only a temporizing bridge to definitive surgical fixation"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Testicular Torsion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448199/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Scrotal Pain (Acute)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534831/"}]},{"id":"uro-fournier-gangrene","name":"Fournier's Gangrene","sec":"uro","present":["Fournier's gangrene is NECROTIZING FASCIITIS of the perineum/genitalia/perianal region - a life-threatening surgical emergency, typically polymicrobial (aerobic + anaerobic synergy)","Risk factors: DIABETES (most common), immunosuppression, alcohol use, obesity, and local trauma/infection (perianal, urethral, or skin sources)","Presents with severe pain (often OUT OF PROPORTION to exam findings early), erythema/crepitus, skin necrosis, systemic toxicity/sepsis, and a foul odor"],"dx":["Largely CLINICAL - do not delay surgery for imaging; findings include pain out of proportion, crepitus/subcutaneous gas, skin necrosis, and rapid progression","CT can define extent/gas if it does NOT delay debridement; labs support severity (e.g., leukocytosis, acidosis; scoring tools like LRINEC are adjuncts, not gatekeepers)","Blood/tissue cultures and glucose; assess septic physiology"],"tx":["The three pillars: EARLY AGGRESSIVE SURGICAL DEBRIDEMENT (the definitive, life-saving intervention), BROAD-SPECTRUM antibiotics (cover gram-positive, gram-negative, and anaerobes), and hemodynamic RESUSCITATION","Repeated debridements (return to OR) are usually necessary until viable tissue margins; diverting colostomy/suprapubic catheter for fecal/urinary diversion as needed","Glycemic control and intensive supportive care"],"tech":["Wide surgical debridement of all necrotic fascia/tissue back to healthy bleeding margins; serial second-look debridements","Fecal (colostomy) or urinary (suprapubic) diversion for source control/wound protection when indicated","Delayed reconstruction/skin grafting after infection control"],"after":["ICU-level sepsis management; plan staged reconstruction of large soft-tissue defects after the infection is controlled","High mortality despite treatment - driven by delay to debridement; optimize diabetes and comorbidities","Nutritional support and wound care (sometimes negative-pressure therapy)"],"pearls":["Fournier's gangrene is necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum/genitalia - EARLY aggressive surgical debridement is the life-saving step; do not delay for imaging","Pain out of proportion to exam, crepitus/gas, and rapid progression are hallmark; diabetes is the leading risk factor","Three pillars: emergent debridement + broad-spectrum antibiotics (including anaerobes) + resuscitation; expect repeat debridements","Consider fecal/urinary diversion for source control; mortality tracks with time to surgery"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Fournier Gangrene","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549821/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Necrotizing Fasciitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430756/"}]},{"id":"uro-renal-trauma","name":"Renal Trauma","sec":"uro","present":["The kidney is the most commonly injured genitourinary organ; most (blunt) renal trauma is managed NON-OPERATIVELY, especially in hemodynamically stable patients","Mechanisms: blunt (deceleration, direct flank impact - most common) and penetrating; graded by the AAST renal injury scale (I-V)","Suspect with flank pain/ecchymosis, rib fractures, and HEMATURIA - though the degree of hematuria does not reliably predict injury severity (major injuries can have minimal hematuria)"],"dx":["CT with IV contrast INCLUDING A DELAYED (excretory) PHASE is the imaging standard - it grades parenchymal injury and detects urinary EXTRAVASATION and collecting-system/ureteral injury","Imaging indications: gross hematuria, or microscopic hematuria with hypotension, or significant mechanism/associated injuries; hemodynamic status drives management","Grade the injury (AAST I-V) including vascular pedicle injury and devascularized segments"],"tx":["HEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE (most, including many high-grade injuries): NON-OPERATIVE management with observation, bed rest, serial hematocrit, and imaging","INTERVENTION for hemodynamic instability from renal bleeding, expanding/pulsatile hematoma at laparotomy, or ongoing transfusion needs: angioembolization (preferred for bleeding when feasible) or surgery","Urinary extravasation/urinoma often resolves or is managed with a ureteral stent/drainage; renovascular pedicle injuries are time-critical for salvage"],"tech":["ANGIOEMBOLIZATION for active arterial bleeding in stable/selected patients (kidney-sparing); ureteral stent/percutaneous drainage for persistent urinary extravasation/urinoma","Surgical exploration (renorrhaphy or, if unsalvageable, nephrectomy) for instability/expanding hematoma; obtain proximal vascular control","Damage-control principles in the unstable polytrauma patient"],"after":["Watch for delayed bleeding, urinoma/abscess, hypertension, and (late) loss of function; follow-up imaging for high-grade injuries","Most stable injuries heal without surgery; preserve renal parenchyma when possible","Monitor blood pressure long-term (post-traumatic renovascular hypertension)"],"pearls":["Most blunt renal trauma - even high-grade - is managed non-operatively if the patient is hemodynamically stable","CT with a DELAYED excretory phase is essential to detect urinary extravasation and collecting-system/ureteral injury","Hematuria degree does not reliably predict injury severity - image based on mechanism, hemodynamics, and hematuria together","Angioembolization is preferred for renal bleeding in stable patients; surgery/nephrectomy for instability or expanding hematoma"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470425/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Genitourinary Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559182/"}]},{"id":"uro-bladder-trauma","name":"Bladder Trauma","sec":"uro","present":["Bladder injury usually results from blunt pelvic trauma and is strongly associated with PELVIC FRACTURES; it presents with GROSS HEMATURIA and suprapubic pain/inability to void","Rupture is EXTRAPERITONEAL (most common; associated with pelvic fractures - urine extravasates into the pelvis/perivesical space) or INTRAPERITONEAL (a burst of the dome, often from a full bladder + blunt force, spilling urine into the peritoneum)","The combination of pelvic fracture + gross hematuria warrants bladder imaging"],"dx":["CT CYSTOGRAPHY (or conventional retrograde cystography) with ADEQUATE BLADDER DISTENSION (retrograde filling) is the diagnostic test - a passively filled/clamped catheter can miss a rupture","Extraperitoneal: flame-shaped perivesical contrast extravasation; intraperitoneal: contrast outlining bowel loops/paracolic gutters","Evaluate for concurrent urethral injury before catheterization when a urethral injury is suspected"],"tx":["EXTRAPERITONEAL rupture: usually managed NON-OPERATIVELY with catheter drainage (heals with an indwelling catheter); repair if there are complicating factors (bladder-neck involvement, bone fragments in the wall, concurrent operative pelvic fixation)","INTRAPERITONEAL rupture: SURGICAL REPAIR (peritoneal urine causes chemical peritonitis and will not heal with drainage alone)","Catheter drainage and follow-up cystography to confirm healing before removal"],"tech":["Surgical repair (usually a multilayer closure) for intraperitoneal rupture, complicated extraperitoneal injury, or when the pelvis is being opened for orthopedic fixation","Catheter drainage (urethral +/- suprapubic) for simple extraperitoneal rupture","Retrograde CT cystography technique with full distension for diagnosis"],"after":["Confirm healing with a cystogram before catheter removal (typically ~1-2 weeks)","Watch for urinary leak, pelvic abscess/urinoma, and (with pelvic fracture) associated injuries","Ensure urethral injury is excluded/addressed"],"pearls":["Diagnose with CT/retrograde cystography using ADEQUATE retrograde bladder distension - a passively drained bladder can miss the rupture","Extraperitoneal rupture (most common, pelvic-fracture associated) usually heals with catheter drainage; intraperitoneal rupture needs surgical repair","Pelvic fracture + gross hematuria = image the bladder (and consider urethral injury)","Confirm healing with a follow-up cystogram before removing the catheter"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470409/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Rupture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560686/"}]},{"id":"uro-ureteral-injury","name":"Ureteral Injury","sec":"uro","present":["Ureteral injuries are most often IATROGENIC (gynecologic, colorectal, and pelvic surgery, plus ureteroscopy) rather than external trauma; external ureteral trauma is uncommon (penetrating > blunt, except pediatric UPJ avulsion)","The distal ureter near the pelvic brim/uterine vessels is the most common iatrogenic site; injuries include ligation, transection, crush, devascularization, and thermal damage","DELAYED recognition is common - presenting later with flank pain, urinoma, fistula (urine leak from wound/vagina), fever, or a rising creatinine"],"dx":["Intraoperative recognition is ideal (direct inspection, retrograde/antegrade dye - indigo carmine/methylene blue extravasation); high suspicion in relevant pelvic surgery","Delayed: CT UROGRAPHY (delayed excretory phase) shows extravasation/obstruction/urinoma; retrograde pyelography localizes the level/extent","Assess ureteral length, viability, and the level of injury (guides repair)"],"tx":["Management depends on timing, level, and length: URETERAL STENT for partial/small injuries; primary repair over a stent for clean transections","DISTAL injuries: URETERONEOCYSTOSTOMY (reimplant), with a psoas hitch or BOARI FLAP for longer distal gaps; MID injuries: ureteroureterostomy (tension-free, spatulated, stented)","Extensive loss: ileal ureter or autotransplant; temporize with nephrostomy if the patient/tissues are unfavorable for immediate repair"],"tech":["Repair principles: debride to healthy tissue, tension-free, SPATULATED, watertight anastomosis over a STENT, with drainage and preserved blood supply","Distal reconstruction: ureteroneocystostomy +/- psoas hitch/Boari flap; proximal/mid: ureteroureterostomy; long defects: ileal interposition or autotransplantation","Nephrostomy diversion as a temporizing measure for delayed/hostile cases"],"after":["Leave the stent and drain; confirm healing (imaging) before stent removal; watch for stricture, leak/urinoma, and recurrent obstruction","Recognize that a missed intraoperative injury presents later as urinoma/fistula/obstruction - maintain suspicion","Monitor renal function and treat any urinoma/abscess"],"pearls":["Most ureteral injuries are iatrogenic (pelvic/gynecologic/colorectal surgery, ureteroscopy) - the distal ureter is the classic site","Repair principles: tension-free, spatulated, watertight anastomosis over a stent with drainage; preserve blood supply","Distal injuries -> ureteroneocystostomy +/- psoas hitch/Boari flap; mid -> ureteroureterostomy; long gaps -> ileal ureter/autotransplant","Missed injuries present in a delayed fashion (urinoma, urine leak/fistula, rising creatinine) - keep a high index of suspicion"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteral Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507817/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteral Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562155/"}]},{"id":"uro-urethral-trauma","name":"Urethral Trauma","sec":"uro","present":["Urethral injuries are largely male and split into POSTERIOR (membranous/prostatic urethra, associated with PELVIC FRACTURES) and ANTERIOR (bulbar/penile, from straddle injury or penetrating/instrumentation) types","Classic signs: BLOOD AT THE URETHRAL MEATUS, inability to void, a high-riding/'boggy' or non-palpable prostate, and perineal/genital hematoma","Suspecting a urethral injury is critical BEFORE catheterization - blind catheterization can convert a partial tear into a complete disruption"],"dx":["If urethral injury is suspected, perform RETROGRADE URETHROGRAPHY before any catheterization - extravasation indicates injury and its level/completeness","Do NOT attempt blind urethral catheterization when injury is suspected (risk of worsening the tear)","Assess associated pelvic-fracture injuries (posterior) and evaluate for bladder injury"],"tx":["POSTERIOR (pelvic-fracture) disruption: initial SUPRAPUBIC CATHETER for urinary diversion, with DELAYED urethroplasty (definitive repair after the pelvis/hematoma stabilizes); some centers do early endoscopic realignment","ANTERIOR injuries: suprapubic or careful realignment depending on severity; straddle bulbar injuries often need delayed urethroplasty for stricture","Avoid early open repair of complex posterior disruptions (higher stricture/incontinence/ED rates) in favor of diversion + delayed reconstruction"],"tech":["Suprapubic cystostomy for urinary diversion in significant disruptions; delayed anastomotic URETHROPLASTY for posterior disruption strictures","Primary endoscopic realignment over a catheter in selected cases; anterior urethroplasty (anastomotic or graft) for bulbar strictures","Retrograde urethrogram to plan reconstruction"],"after":["STRICTURE is the main long-term sequela; erectile dysfunction and incontinence are risks of posterior injuries/repairs - counsel and follow","Ensure adequate diversion until definitive repair; image the reconstructed urethra","Long-term follow-up for recurrent stricture (may need repeat intervention)"],"pearls":["Blood at the meatus, inability to void, and a high-riding prostate suggest urethral injury - do RETROGRADE URETHROGRAPHY BEFORE any catheterization","Never blindly catheterize a suspected urethral injury (can convert a partial to a complete disruption)","Posterior (pelvic-fracture) disruption: suprapubic diversion + delayed urethroplasty is the classic approach; anterior from straddle injury","Stricture is the main long-term complication; posterior injuries also risk ED and incontinence"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Urethral Injury","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493222/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urethral Trauma","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560888/"}]},{"id":"uro-urethral-stricture","name":"Urethral Stricture & Urethroplasty","sec":"uro","present":["A urethral stricture is a fibrotic narrowing of the (anterior) urethral lumen from scarring; causes include idiopathic, iatrogenic (catheterization, instrumentation, TURP), inflammatory (lichen sclerosus), infectious (gonococcal), and traumatic (straddle)","It causes obstructive voiding (weak/spraying stream, straining, incomplete emptying) and can lead to UTIs, retention, and bladder decompensation","Location matters: bulbar strictures are most common; penile/lichen sclerosus strictures behave differently (grafts favored over local flaps)"],"dx":["RETROGRADE URETHROGRAPHY (+/- voiding cystourethrography) defines stricture location, length, and number - the key for planning; uroflowmetry shows a flattened (plateau) curve","Cystoscopy assesses the lumen; ultrasound urethrography can gauge spongiofibrosis","Assess prior interventions and etiology (lichen sclerosus changes management)"],"tx":["ENDOSCOPIC (dilation or direct-vision internal URETHROTOMY) has HIGH RECURRENCE rates - best for short, single, first-time bulbar strictures; repeated endoscopic treatment of recurrent strictures is discouraged","URETHROPLASTY (open reconstruction) is the DEFINITIVE, durable treatment for recurrent, long, or complex strictures: excision and primary anastomosis (short bulbar) or substitution urethroplasty with a BUCCAL MUCOSA graft (longer strictures)","Lichen sclerosus strictures: graft-based repair (avoid genital skin flaps)"],"tech":["Direct-vision internal urethrotomy/dilation for select short first-time strictures; anastomotic urethroplasty (excise stricture, spatulate, tension-free anastomosis) for short bulbar strictures","Substitution urethroplasty with buccal mucosa graft (dorsal/ventral onlay) for longer strictures; staged repair for complex/penile disease","Suprapubic diversion if needed pre-reconstruction"],"after":["Endoscopic treatments recur frequently - counsel that repeated urethrotomy is low-yield and urethroplasty is more durable","Post-urethroplasty: monitor for recurrence, and (graft harvest) oral/donor-site issues; confirm patency with flow/imaging","Manage lichen sclerosus long-term (recurrence risk)"],"pearls":["Retrograde urethrography defines stricture length/location/number - the essential planning study","Internal urethrotomy/dilation has high recurrence; reserve it for short, single, first-time bulbar strictures - do not repeat endlessly","Urethroplasty (anastomotic for short bulbar; buccal mucosa graft for longer) is the durable definitive treatment","Lichen sclerosus strictures should be repaired with grafts, not genital skin flaps"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Urethral Stricture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564297/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urethroplasty","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560606/"}]},{"id":"uro-ureteral-stricture","name":"Ureteral Stricture & Reconstruction","sec":"uro","present":["A ureteral stricture is a narrowing causing obstruction; causes include iatrogenic (ureteroscopy, prior surgery, radiation), ischemic, impacted stones, malignancy, and (proximal) ureteropelvic junction obstruction","UPJ OBSTRUCTION is a specific proximal stricture/functional obstruction at the renal pelvis-ureter junction, often congenital (crossing vessel) or acquired, causing flank pain (classically with diuresis) and hydronephrosis","Untreated obstruction risks progressive renal functional loss"],"dx":["CT urography/retrograde or antegrade pyelography defines the site/length; MAG3 DIURETIC RENOGRAM confirms functional obstruction and quantifies SPLIT RENAL FUNCTION (whether the kidney is worth reconstructing)","Rule out malignancy (biopsy/cytology) for suspicious strictures; assess stone/radiation/iatrogenic history","Evaluate location (UPJ vs mid vs distal) and length to choose repair"],"tx":["Relieve obstruction (stent/nephrostomy) then reconstruct based on site/length: UPJ obstruction -> PYELOPLASTY (dismembered Anderson-Hynes, often robotic - the gold standard); distal strictures -> reimplant (+/- psoas hitch/Boari flap)","Short mid-ureteral strictures -> ureteroureterostomy; long defects -> ileal ureter or autotransplantation; endoscopic incision (endopyelotomy) for select short strictures","Nephrectomy if the kidney is nonfunctional/not worth salvage (low split function)"],"tech":["Dismembered pyeloplasty for UPJ obstruction (excise the narrowed segment, transpose an anterior crossing vessel, and create a dependent, tension-free, spatulated anastomosis over a stent)","Distal reconstruction (ureteroneocystostomy +/- psoas hitch/Boari flap); ileal ureter/autotransplant for extensive loss","Stented, spatulated, tension-free, watertight anastomoses with preserved blood supply"],"after":["Leave a stent; confirm resolution of obstruction on follow-up renogram/imaging before and after stent removal; watch for restricture and leak","Preserve/monitor split function; a poorly functioning kidney may warrant nephrectomy rather than reconstruction","Long-term surveillance for recurrent obstruction"],"pearls":["MAG3 diuretic renogram confirms functional obstruction AND gives split function - deciding whether to reconstruct or remove the kidney","Dismembered (Anderson-Hynes) pyeloplasty is the gold standard for UPJ obstruction; watch for a crossing lower-pole vessel","Reconstruction principles mirror ureteral injury: tension-free, spatulated, stented, watertight, well-vascularized","Biopsy suspicious strictures to exclude malignancy before benign reconstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteral Stricture","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572100/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563285/"}]},{"id":"uro-urinary-fistula","name":"Urinary Fistula","sec":"uro","present":["A urinary fistula is an abnormal communication between the urinary tract and another epithelialized surface; common types: VESICOVAGINAL, ureterovaginal, vesicoenteric (colovesical), and vesicocutaneous","VESICOVAGINAL fistula presents with continuous urinary incontinence (constant vaginal wetness); in high-resource settings it usually follows pelvic/gynecologic surgery (hysterectomy), while globally OBSTRUCTED LABOR is the leading cause","VESICOENTERIC (colovesical) fistula (often from DIVERTICULITIS, also Crohn's or malignancy) presents with PNEUMATURIA and FECALURIA and recurrent polymicrobial UTIs"],"dx":["Localize the fistula: cystoscopy and vaginal exam +/- dye tests (methylene-blue/tampon test) for vesicovaginal; CT cystography/CT for vesicoenteric (bladder-wall thickening, air in the bladder without instrumentation)","CT urography and retrograde/antegrade studies for ureterovaginal fistula; assess for malignancy/radiation and rule out a ureteral component","Identify the underlying cause (surgery, diverticulitis, Crohn's, malignancy, radiation, obstructed labor)"],"tx":["Some small, uncomplicated vesicovaginal fistulae close with catheter drainage; most need SURGICAL REPAIR (transvaginal or transabdominal) with tension-free, multilayer closure and interposition of healthy vascularized tissue (e.g., MARTIUS flap or omentum)","VESICOENTERIC (colovesical) fistula: treat the bowel disease - resection of the affected bowel segment (e.g., sigmoid for diverticular disease) with bladder repair","Ureterovaginal fistula: ureteral stent for select cases or ureteral reimplantation; radiation/malignancy-related fistulae are complex (may need diversion)"],"tech":["Vesicovaginal repair: excise the tract, multilayer tension-free closure, and interpose vascularized tissue (Martius labial fat flap or omental flap) with catheter drainage","Colovesical fistula: segmental bowel resection (treat diverticulitis/Crohn's/tumor) + bladder closure +/- omental interposition","Ureteral reimplantation/stenting for ureterovaginal fistula; urinary diversion for irreparable radiation fistulae"],"after":["Maintain adequate catheter drainage after repair to protect the closure; confirm healing (cystogram) before catheter removal","Radiation-associated and malignant fistulae have higher failure rates - vascularized tissue interposition improves success","Manage the underlying disease (diverticulitis/Crohn's/malignancy) to prevent recurrence"],"pearls":["Continuous urinary leakage after pelvic/gynecologic surgery suggests a vesicovaginal (or ureterovaginal) fistula - localize with cystoscopy and dye testing","Pneumaturia + fecaluria = colovesical fistula, most often from diverticulitis (also Crohn's, malignancy); CT shows air in the bladder without instrumentation","Repair principles: tension-free, multilayer closure with interposition of healthy vascularized tissue (Martius/omental flap) and good drainage","Treat the underlying cause (resect diseased bowel for colovesical fistula); globally, obstructed labor is the leading vesicovaginal cause"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vesicovaginal Fistula","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564389/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Colovesical Fistula","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560576/"}]},{"id":"uro-neurogenic-bladder","name":"Neurogenic Bladder","sec":"uro","present":["Neurogenic bladder is lower-urinary-tract dysfunction from neurologic disease/injury; the pattern depends on the LEVEL of the lesion","SUPRASPINAL (e.g., stroke, Parkinson's): detrusor overactivity with urgency/urge incontinence; SUPRASACRAL SPINAL CORD (e.g., SCI above the sacral cord, MS): detrusor overactivity WITH DETRUSOR-SPHINCTER DYSSYNERGIA (DSD - simultaneous bladder contraction against a closed sphincter, generating HIGH PRESSURES); SACRAL/INFRASACRAL (cauda equina, pelvic surgery, diabetes): detrusor areflexia/underactivity with retention/overflow","The paramount goal is PROTECTING THE UPPER TRACTS from high storage pressures (which cause hydronephrosis and renal failure)"],"dx":["VIDEO-URODYNAMICS is the key test - it measures storage/voiding pressures, detects DSD, and identifies high-risk features (poor compliance, high detrusor leak-point pressure, vesicoureteral reflux)","Baseline and periodic renal function and upper-tract imaging (ultrasound) for surveillance","Correlate the neurologic level/diagnosis with the urodynamic pattern"],"tx":["Protect the upper tracts by ensuring LOW-PRESSURE storage and adequate emptying: CLEAN INTERMITTENT CATHETERIZATION (CIC) is the cornerstone for emptying; ANTIMUSCARINICS/beta-3 agonists and BOTULINUM TOXIN (detrusor injection) reduce detrusor overactivity/pressure","Manage DSD and outlet as needed; augmentation cystoplasty or urinary diversion for refractory high-pressure/low-compliance bladders","Treat/prevent complications (UTIs, stones, autonomic dysreflexia)"],"tech":["Clean intermittent catheterization for emptying; intradetrusor onabotulinumtoxinA injections for neurogenic detrusor overactivity; augmentation cystoplasty (bowel) for refractory low-compliance/high-pressure bladders","Sphincterotomy/outlet procedures or diversion in selected cases","Sacral neuromodulation in appropriate non-DSD patients"],"after":["Lifelong upper-tract SURVEILLANCE (renal function, imaging, periodic urodynamics) to catch rising pressures/reflux before renal damage","Watch for AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA (in lesions >=T6: a hypertensive emergency triggered by bladder/bowel distension) - relieve the trigger urgently","Prevent stones/UTIs; manage catheter care and bowel program"],"pearls":["The overriding goal is protecting the upper tracts from high storage pressures - low-pressure storage + reliable emptying (often CIC) prevents renal failure","Suprasacral cord lesions cause detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia (high-pressure, dangerous); sacral/infrasacral lesions cause areflexia/retention","Video-urodynamics identifies high-risk features (poor compliance, high detrusor leak-point pressure, DSD, reflux)","Autonomic dysreflexia (lesions >=T6) is a hypertensive emergency from bladder/bowel distension - relieve the trigger immediately"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Neurogenic Bladder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560617/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Detrusor Sphincter Dyssynergia","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560491/"}]},{"id":"uro-overactive-bladder","name":"Overactive Bladder","sec":"uro","present":["Overactive bladder (OAB) is a symptom syndrome of URGENCY (usually with frequency and nocturia), WITH or WITHOUT urge incontinence, in the absence of infection or other identifiable pathology","It reflects detrusor overactivity/urgency and is common with aging; it substantially affects quality of life","Distinguish from stress incontinence (leak with cough/exertion) - the two can coexist (mixed incontinence)"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis after excluding UTI, hematuria, and other causes; urinalysis, a bladder diary (frequency/volume), and post-void residual","Urodynamics is reserved for complex, refractory, or diagnostically unclear cases","Screen for contributing factors (fluids/caffeine, medications, poorly controlled diabetes, neurologic disease)"],"tx":["FIRST-LINE: behavioral therapy (bladder training, timed voiding, fluid/caffeine management, pelvic floor exercises)","SECOND-LINE pharmacotherapy: ANTIMUSCARINICS (oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin) or BETA-3 AGONISTS (mirabegron, vibegron) - beta-3 agonists avoid anticholinergic side effects","THIRD-LINE (refractory): intradetrusor BOTULINUM TOXIN, SACRAL NEUROMODULATION, or percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation"],"tech":["Intradetrusor onabotulinumtoxinA injections (cystoscopic) for refractory OAB; sacral neuromodulation (implanted device); percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation","No procedure for first/second-line therapy (behavioral + oral)","Reserve urodynamics/cystoscopy for atypical or refractory cases"],"after":["Antimuscarinic side effects (dry mouth, constipation, cognitive effects in the elderly - anticholinergic burden and dementia-risk concerns) favor beta-3 agonists in older patients","Botulinum toxin risks urinary retention (counsel about possible need for CIC) and UTI","Reassess response with the bladder diary; escalate stepwise"],"pearls":["OAB is urgency +/- urge incontinence with frequency/nocturia, after excluding UTI and other pathology; behavioral therapy is first-line","Beta-3 agonists (mirabegron/vibegron) avoid the anticholinergic burden of antimuscarinics - preferred in older adults (cognitive/dementia concerns)","Refractory OAB: intradetrusor botulinum toxin (retention/CIC risk), sacral neuromodulation, or tibial nerve stimulation","Distinguish from (and screen for coexisting) stress incontinence - mixed incontinence is common"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Overactive Bladder","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539542/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urge Incontinence","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560627/"}]},{"id":"uro-stress-incontinence","name":"Stress Urinary Incontinence","sec":"uro","present":["Stress urinary incontinence (SUI) is involuntary urine leakage with increases in intra-abdominal pressure (cough, sneeze, laugh, exertion) - due to urethral hypermobility and/or intrinsic sphincter deficiency","Risk factors in women: vaginal childbirth, aging/menopause, obesity, and pelvic surgery; in men, SUI most often follows RADICAL PROSTATECTOMY (sphincter injury)","It is distinct from urge incontinence (OAB); mixed incontinence combines both"],"dx":["History and a cough stress test (observed leakage with cough) confirm SUI; assess urethral mobility and pelvic floor; post-void residual and urinalysis","Urodynamics for complex/mixed/refractory cases or before surgery in select patients (assess for intrinsic sphincter deficiency and coexisting detrusor overactivity)","Distinguish/quantify any urge component (mixed incontinence)"],"tx":["FIRST-LINE: PELVIC FLOOR MUSCLE TRAINING (Kegels), weight loss, and lifestyle measures; a pessary/continence device or vaginal support in women","SURGICAL (women): MIDURETHRAL SLING (synthetic mesh) is the standard; alternatives include autologous fascial (pubovaginal) sling, Burch colposuspension, and urethral bulking agents","MEN (post-prostatectomy SUI): urethral sling for mild-moderate; ARTIFICIAL URINARY SPHINCTER is the gold standard for moderate-severe SUI"],"tech":["Midurethral sling placement (retropubic or transobturator) in women; autologous pubovaginal sling or Burch colposuspension as mesh-free options; urethral bulking injections","Artificial urinary sphincter implantation for men with significant post-prostatectomy incontinence (cuff, pump, reservoir)","Pelvic floor physical therapy for first-line management"],"after":["Sling complications: mesh exposure/erosion, voiding dysfunction/retention, and pain; counsel on synthetic-mesh risks and alternatives","Artificial urinary sphincter risks: mechanical failure, erosion, infection, and atrophy; patients must have the dexterity to operate the pump","Reassess continence and voiding; treat any coexisting urge component"],"pearls":["SUI = leakage with cough/exertion (urethral hypermobility/intrinsic sphincter deficiency); confirm with a cough stress test","Pelvic floor muscle training is first-line; midurethral sling is the standard surgical option in women (counsel on mesh risks; autologous slings/Burch are mesh-free alternatives)","Post-prostatectomy male SUI: artificial urinary sphincter is the gold standard for moderate-severe leakage; slings for milder cases","Distinguish from urge incontinence and treat coexisting components (mixed incontinence)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Stress Incontinence","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559078/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Incontinence","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559095/"}]},{"id":"uro-pelvic-organ-prolapse","name":"Pelvic Organ Prolapse","sec":"uro","present":["Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) is descent of pelvic organs (bladder - CYSTOCELE, uterus, rectum - RECTOCELE, small bowel - enterocele, or vaginal vault) through weakened pelvic floor support","Risk factors: vaginal childbirth (parity), aging/menopause, obesity, chronic straining, and prior hysterectomy; it presents with vaginal bulge/pressure, and may cause voiding/defecatory dysfunction or coexisting incontinence","Advanced anterior prolapse can kink the urethra and mask (or unmask after repair) stress incontinence"],"dx":["Examine with a speculum/Valsalva and stage with the POP-Q system; identify which compartment(s) are involved (anterior/posterior/apical)","Assess for concurrent SUI (occult/masked incontinence that may appear after prolapse repair) and voiding dysfunction; post-void residual","Correlate symptoms with exam (asymptomatic mild prolapse may not need treatment)"],"tx":["Conservative: observation for asymptomatic/mild POP; PESSARY (vaginal support device) and pelvic floor physical therapy","SURGICAL for symptomatic prolapse: RECONSTRUCTIVE (native-tissue repair, apical suspension - sacrospinous fixation or uterosacral ligament suspension; abdominal/robotic SACROCOLPOPEXY for apical/vault prolapse) or OBLITERATIVE (colpocleisis) in select non-sexually-active patients","Address concurrent SUI at the time of repair as indicated"],"tech":["Pessary fitting for nonsurgical support; native-tissue vaginal repairs (anterior/posterior colporrhaphy, apical suspensions); sacrocolpopexy (mesh) for apical/vault prolapse via abdominal/robotic approach","Colpocleisis (obliterative) for frail, non-sexually-active patients (high success, low morbidity)","Concomitant anti-incontinence procedure when indicated"],"after":["Apical support is key to durable repair - inadequate apical suspension predisposes to recurrence","Counsel on transvaginal mesh concerns (regulatory restrictions) vs native-tissue and sacrocolpopexy options","Monitor for prolapse recurrence, de novo SUI/urgency, and pessary-related erosion (needs periodic removal/cleaning)"],"pearls":["Stage prolapse with POP-Q by compartment (cystocele/rectocele/apical); treat symptomatic prolapse, not incidental mild descent","Pessary and pelvic floor therapy are effective nonsurgical options; sacrocolpopexy is durable for apical/vault prolapse","Restoring APICAL support is central to durable repair; screen for masked stress incontinence that may appear after repair","Colpocleisis (obliterative) is a low-morbidity option for frail, non-sexually-active patients"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Pelvic Organ Prolapse","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563229/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Cystocele","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534773/"}]},{"id":"uro-interstitial-cystitis","name":"Interstitial Cystitis / Bladder Pain Syndrome","sec":"uro","present":["Interstitial cystitis/bladder pain syndrome (IC/BPS) is chronic bladder/pelvic PAIN, PRESSURE, or discomfort perceived to be related to the bladder, with urinary urgency/frequency, lasting >6 weeks WITHOUT infection or other identifiable cause","It is a diagnosis of EXCLUSION; a subset has HUNNER LESIONS (distinct inflammatory patches) on cystoscopy - a phenotype that responds to lesion-directed therapy","It disproportionately affects women and overlaps with other chronic pain conditions"],"dx":["EXCLUDE mimics: UTI (culture), bladder cancer/carcinoma in situ (especially with hematuria - cystoscopy/cytology), overactive bladder, endometriosis, and other pelvic pathology","Symptom assessment, bladder diary, and pelvic exam; cystoscopy identifies HUNNER LESIONS (which change management) and excludes malignancy; potassium sensitivity/hydrodistension are adjuncts of limited/variable use","There is no single confirmatory test - it is clinical after exclusion"],"tx":["STEPWISE, multimodal per AUA: begin with education, behavioral/dietary modification (avoid trigger foods), stress management, and pelvic floor physical therapy (avoid pelvic floor strengthening/Kegels - manual therapy is preferred)","Escalate: oral agents (amitriptyline, pentosan polysulfate - counsel on retinal-toxicity concerns), intravesical instillations (e.g., DMSO, heparin/lidocaine cocktails)","HUNNER LESIONS: cystoscopic fulguration/resection or triamcinolone injection; refractory disease: neuromodulation, cyclosporine, or (rarely) major reconstructive surgery/diversion; AVOID long-term opioids"],"tech":["Cystoscopy for Hunner-lesion identification and treatment (fulguration/resection/steroid injection); hydrodistension (diagnostic/therapeutic in some)","Intravesical instillation therapy; sacral neuromodulation for refractory cases","Pelvic floor physical therapy (myofascial release) as a core treatment"],"after":["Set expectations for a chronic, relapsing course managed with multimodal therapy; avoid escalating to opioids and reserve major surgery for refractory, well-selected cases","Pentosan polysulfate: counsel about reported pigmentary maculopathy (retinal) risk and ophthalmologic monitoring","Address overlapping chronic pain/psychosocial factors and flares (trigger avoidance)"],"pearls":["IC/BPS is a diagnosis of exclusion (rule out UTI, bladder cancer/CIS, endometriosis) - chronic bladder pain + urgency/frequency >6 weeks without infection","Cystoscopy identifies Hunner lesions, a distinct phenotype treated by fulguration/resection or steroid injection","Management is stepwise/multimodal (behavioral, pelvic floor PT with manual therapy - NOT Kegels, oral/intravesical agents); avoid long-term opioids","Pentosan polysulfate carries a reported retinal maculopathy risk - counsel and arrange eye monitoring"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Interstitial Cystitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570588/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Bladder Pain Syndrome","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563297/"}]},{"id":"uro-vesicoureteral-reflux","name":"Vesicoureteral Reflux","sec":"uro","present":["Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) is retrograde flow of urine from the bladder up the ureter (+/- to the kidney) due to an incompetent ureterovesical junction (short intramural ureter tunnel); it is PRIMARY (congenital) or SECONDARY (from high bladder pressures - posterior urethral valves, neurogenic bladder)","It predisposes to febrile UTIs/pyelonephritis and, when combined with infection or high pressures, to renal scarring (reflux nephropathy) with risk of hypertension and chronic kidney disease","It is often detected during evaluation of a febrile UTI or antenatal hydronephrosis"],"dx":["VOIDING CYSTOURETHROGRAM (VCUG) diagnoses and GRADES reflux (I-V by the International Reflux Study grading); renal-bladder ultrasound screens anatomy; DMSA renal scan detects scarring/pyelonephritis","Evaluate after a febrile UTI in young children (imaging per age/guidelines); assess for bladder-bowel dysfunction (a major modifiable factor)","Exclude secondary causes (posterior urethral valves in boys, neurogenic bladder)"],"tx":["Many low-grade cases RESOLVE SPONTANEOUSLY with growth - observation +/- CONTINUOUS ANTIBIOTIC PROPHYLAXIS (reduces recurrent febrile UTIs, per RIVUR), plus treating bladder-bowel dysfunction","SURGERY (ureteral REIMPLANTATION or endoscopic bulking injection - e.g., Deflux) for high-grade reflux, breakthrough febrile UTIs on prophylaxis, or progressive renal scarring","Treat secondary reflux by relieving the cause (valve ablation, bladder management)"],"tech":["Endoscopic subureteric bulking-agent injection (minimally invasive) for lower/select grades; open or robotic ureteral REIMPLANTATION (lengthen the intramural tunnel) for definitive correction of high-grade/refractory reflux","Manage bladder-bowel dysfunction (constipation, voiding habits) - it drives breakthrough infections and reflux persistence","Ablation of posterior urethral valves for secondary reflux"],"after":["Monitor for breakthrough febrile UTIs, renal growth/scarring (imaging), blood pressure, and resolution over time","Treat bladder-bowel dysfunction aggressively; counsel families on UTI recognition and prophylaxis adherence","Long-term follow-up for reflux nephropathy (hypertension, proteinuria, CKD)"],"pearls":["VCUG diagnoses and grades VUR (I-V); low-grade reflux commonly resolves spontaneously with growth","Continuous antibiotic prophylaxis reduces recurrent febrile UTIs (RIVUR); treat coexisting bladder-bowel dysfunction (a key modifiable factor)","Surgery (reimplantation or endoscopic Deflux) for high-grade reflux, breakthrough infections, or progressive scarring","Reflux nephropathy (scarring) risks hypertension and CKD - monitor renal growth, blood pressure, and function"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Vesicoureteral Reflux","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560573/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Reflux Nephropathy","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559189/"}]},{"id":"uro-cryptorchidism","name":"Undescended Testis (Cryptorchidism)","sec":"uro","present":["Cryptorchidism is failure of testicular descent into the scrotum; it is the most common male genital birth anomaly and is more frequent in premature infants","The testis may be palpable (inguinal) or non-palpable (intra-abdominal, absent/vanishing); distinguish a true undescended testis from a RETRACTILE testis (which can be brought to and stays in the scrotum - normal, no surgery)","Untreated cryptorchidism carries increased risks of INFERTILITY and TESTICULAR CANCER, plus torsion and inguinal hernia"],"dx":["Diagnosis is CLINICAL by examination (palpable vs non-palpable); imaging is generally NOT reliable/recommended for localization","For BILATERAL non-palpable testes, check karyotype/hormones (rule out disorders of sex development and anorchia - hCG stimulation, MIS/AMH levels)","Non-palpable unilateral testis: diagnostic laparoscopy locates an intra-abdominal testis or confirms absence"],"tx":["ORCHIOPEXY (surgical relocation and fixation of the testis in the scrotum) is the treatment - IDEALLY BY ~6-18 MONTHS OF AGE (early surgery optimizes fertility potential and eases cancer surveillance); spontaneous descent after ~6 months is unlikely","Hormonal therapy is not routinely recommended for descent","Two-stage (Fowler-Stephens) orchiopexy for high intra-abdominal testes"],"tech":["Inguinal orchiopexy for palpable testes (mobilize the cord, ligate the patent processus vaginalis, and fix the testis in a dartos pouch); laparoscopy for non-palpable testes (locate and mobilize)","Fowler-Stephens (single- or two-stage, dividing the testicular vessels to gain length) for high intra-abdominal testes","Orchiectomy for a clearly atrophic/nubbin testis"],"after":["Even after orchiopexy, the increased testicular cancer risk persists - teach TESTICULAR SELF-EXAM at puberty for lifelong awareness","Monitor testicular position/growth and fertility counseling as appropriate","Watch for surgical complications (testicular atrophy, ascent/re-do)"],"pearls":["Orchiopexy is ideally done by ~6-18 months - early surgery improves fertility potential and enables cancer surveillance (a scrotal testis is examinable)","Distinguish a true undescended testis from a retractile testis (normal - stays in the scrotum when positioned; no surgery)","Cryptorchidism increases risks of infertility AND testicular cancer (risk persists after orchiopexy - teach self-exam)","Bilateral non-palpable testes need endocrine/genetic evaluation (DSD/anorchia); imaging is unreliable - use exam +/- laparoscopy"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Cryptorchidism","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470270/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Undescended Testicle","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560640/"}]},{"id":"uro-hypospadias","name":"Hypospadias","sec":"uro","present":["Hypospadias is a congenital anomaly where the urethral MEATUS opens on the VENTRAL penis (glanular, penile, or more proximal penoscrotal) rather than at the tip, from incomplete urethral fold fusion","It is commonly associated with VENTRAL CHORDEE (penile curvature) and a DORSAL HOODED foreskin (incomplete ventral prepuce)","Severity ranges from mild distal (most common) to severe proximal forms; proximal/severe or associated cryptorchidism raises concern for a disorder of sex development"],"dx":["Clinical diagnosis at birth: note meatal position, chordee, and the hooded foreskin; assess for associated cryptorchidism/inguinal hernia","Proximal hypospadias WITH bilateral non-palpable testes or ambiguous genitalia warrants a DSD workup (karyotype/endocrine)","Document penile size/anatomy for surgical planning"],"tx":["Surgical REPAIR (urethroplasty) is the treatment, typically around 6-18 months of age; goals are a straight penis, a slit-like glanular meatus, and normal voiding/sexual function","DO NOT CIRCUMCISE a newborn with hypospadias - the foreskin is often needed as graft/flap tissue for the repair","Distal repairs are often single-stage (e.g., TIP/Snodgrass); severe proximal cases may need staged repair"],"tech":["Distal repair: tubularized incised plate (TIP/Snodgrass) urethroplasty with meatal advancement/glansplasty; correct chordee (degloving, dorsal plication, or ventral grafting)","Proximal/severe: staged repairs using preputial or buccal mucosa grafts/flaps","Preserve the foreskin for reconstruction (hence no prior circumcision)"],"after":["Complications: urethrocutaneous FISTULA, meatal stenosis, urethral stricture, and diverticulum - counsel families that revision may be needed","Monitor voiding stream and cosmesis; follow for delayed complications","Fistula and stricture are the classic long-term issues to watch for"],"pearls":["Do NOT circumcise a newborn with hypospadias - the foreskin is needed for the repair","Hypospadias triad: ventral meatus, ventral chordee (curvature), and a dorsal hooded foreskin; repair usually at ~6-18 months","Distal repair (TIP/Snodgrass) is often single-stage; severe proximal forms may be staged with grafts","Proximal hypospadias + bilateral non-palpable testes suggests a disorder of sex development - do a DSD workup; watch for postoperative fistula/stricture"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hypospadias","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482122/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chordee","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563246/"}]},{"id":"uro-posterior-urethral-valves","name":"Posterior Urethral Valves & UPJ Obstruction","sec":"uro","present":["POSTERIOR URETHRAL VALVES (PUV) are obstructing membranous folds in the posterior (prostatic) urethra - the most common cause of congenital lower-urinary-tract obstruction in BOYS, and a major cause of pediatric renal failure","PUV causes high bladder pressures with bladder wall thickening, bilateral hydroureteronephrosis, secondary VUR, and (from in-utero obstruction) renal dysplasia and oligohydramnios/pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence)","URETEROPELVIC JUNCTION (UPJ) OBSTRUCTION is the most common cause of congenital upper-tract (antenatal) hydronephrosis, often from an intrinsic narrowing or a crossing lower-pole vessel"],"dx":["PUV: often detected antenatally (bilateral hydronephrosis, thick-walled bladder, dilated posterior urethra - 'keyhole' sign, oligohydramnios); confirmed postnatally by VCUG (dilated posterior urethra, valves, +/- reflux)","UPJ obstruction: renal ultrasound shows pelvicaliceal dilation without ureteral dilation; MAG3 diuretic renogram confirms obstruction and split function","Assess renal function (nadir creatinine after birth predicts long-term outcome in PUV)"],"tx":["PUV: initial bladder DRAINAGE (catheter) and stabilization of the neonate (fluids/electrolytes, treat pulmonary issues), then endoscopic VALVE ABLATION; vesicostomy/diversion if the infant is too small or unstable","Ongoing BLADDER MANAGEMENT is crucial in PUV - 'valve bladder' (poor compliance/dysfunction) drives long-term renal decline even after ablation","UPJ obstruction: observe many antenatal cases (many improve); PYELOPLASTY (dismembered Anderson-Hynes) for symptomatic obstruction, decreasing function, or high-grade obstruction"],"tech":["PUV: transurethral endoscopic valve ablation; temporary vesicostomy or higher diversion for small/unstable neonates or persistent upper-tract deterioration","UPJ: dismembered pyeloplasty (excise the narrowed segment, transpose a crossing vessel, tension-free spatulated anastomosis over a stent), open or robotic","Manage secondary VUR and bladder dysfunction"],"after":["PUV: LIFELONG surveillance of renal function and bladder dynamics (urodynamics) - many progress to CKD/transplant despite valve ablation because of renal dysplasia and valve bladder","UPJ: post-pyeloplasty renogram to confirm relief; monitor renal growth/function","Blood pressure and growth monitoring; multidisciplinary (nephrology) care for PUV"],"pearls":["Posterior urethral valves are the commonest congenital lower-tract obstruction in boys and a major cause of pediatric renal failure - VCUG shows a dilated posterior urethra","PUV management: drain the bladder, stabilize the neonate, then ablate the valves; long-term outcome hinges on renal dysplasia and BLADDER management ('valve bladder')","UPJ obstruction is the commonest cause of antenatal hydronephrosis (dilated pelvis, non-dilated ureter); MAG3 renogram confirms obstruction/function","Dismembered pyeloplasty is the gold standard for symptomatic/functionally significant UPJ obstruction"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Posterior Urethral Valve","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560904/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction (Congenital)","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563250/"}]},{"id":"uro-pediatric-gu-anomalies","name":"Pediatric GU Anomalies - Overview","sec":"uro","present":["Pediatric urology covers a range of congenital anomalies beyond VUR/PUV/hypospadias/cryptorchidism, including ANTENATAL HYDRONEPHROSIS (the most common finding), duplex collecting systems (+/- ureterocele or ectopic ureter), multicystic dysplastic kidney, and the bladder exstrophy-epispadias complex","ANTENATAL HYDRONEPHROSIS is graded (e.g., SFU grade or the UTD classification) and prompts postnatal evaluation to distinguish transient/physiologic dilation from significant obstruction/reflux","Ectopic ureters and ureteroceles (often with duplex systems) can cause obstruction, reflux, incontinence (continuous dampness from an ectopic ureter draining below the sphincter in girls), or infection"],"dx":["Postnatal renal-bladder ULTRASOUND (timed after the first days to avoid the newborn oliguric period) is the first study for antenatal hydronephrosis; VCUG for reflux/ureterocele/PUV suspicion; MAG3 renogram for obstruction/function","Characterize duplex systems (Weigert-Meyer rule: the upper-pole ureter inserts ectopically/obstructs, the lower-pole ureter refluxes) and ureteroceles","Evaluate bladder exstrophy/epispadias and cloacal anomalies at birth (obvious anatomic defects)"],"tx":["Observe/monitor mild antenatal hydronephrosis (much resolves); intervene for significant obstruction (pyeloplasty for UPJ, see PUV entry) or high-grade reflux","Duplex-system anomalies: manage per pathology (ureterocele incision, upper-pole heminephrectomy, ureteral reimplantation, or ureteroureterostomy) based on function and symptoms","Bladder exstrophy-epispadias complex: staged surgical reconstruction (bladder closure, epispadias repair, bladder-neck reconstruction) at specialized centers"],"tech":["Endoscopic ureterocele incision; upper-pole heminephrectomy or ureteral reimplant/ureteroureterostomy for duplex anomalies; pyeloplasty for UPJ obstruction","Staged reconstruction of bladder exstrophy (primary/complete closure approaches) at high-volume centers","Antibiotic prophylaxis and structured surveillance for high-risk anomalies"],"after":["Structured imaging surveillance (ultrasound/renogram) and UTI prophylaxis where indicated; monitor renal growth/function","Continence outcomes are a long-term focus in exstrophy/ectopic anomalies; multidisciplinary follow-up","Family counseling on prognosis and staged interventions"],"pearls":["Antenatal hydronephrosis is the most common prenatal GU finding - most is transient/physiologic; grade it and image postnatally (timed ultrasound, then VCUG/renogram as indicated)","Weigert-Meyer rule for duplex systems: upper-pole ureter inserts ectopically (obstructs/ureterocele), lower-pole ureter refluxes","A girl with continuous dampness despite normal voiding suggests an ectopic ureter draining below the sphincter","Bladder exstrophy-epispadias complex needs staged reconstruction at specialized high-volume centers"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Antenatal Hydronephrosis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563279/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Duplicated Collecting System","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563164/"}]},{"id":"uro-renal-transplant-urologic","name":"Renal Transplant - Urologic Aspects","sec":"uro","present":["Kidney transplantation is the best treatment for end-stage renal disease; the urologist's role includes the URETERAL (ureteroneocystostomy) anastomosis, managing urologic complications, and pre-transplant lower-urinary-tract evaluation","The graft is placed EXTRAPERITONEALLY in the ILIAC FOSSA with anastomoses to the iliac vessels and a ureteroneocystostomy to the bladder","Pre-transplant urologic issues (bladder dysfunction, outlet obstruction, reflux/recurrent UTI, prior bladder augmentation) must be addressed to protect the graft"],"dx":["Pre-transplant: evaluate lower-tract function (urodynamics if dysfunction suspected), treat outlet obstruction/BPH, and manage native-kidney issues (e.g., symptomatic polycystic kidneys, chronic infection)","Post-transplant complications: URINE LEAK and URETERAL STRICTURE (ureteroneocystostomy), lymphocele (compressing the ureter/graft), vascular issues, and VUR into the graft","Rising creatinine + hydronephrosis suggests a ureteral/obstructive complication - image with ultrasound"],"tx":["Ureteral leak/stricture: percutaneous nephrostomy/stent then repair (ureteral reimplant or ureteropyelostomy to the native ureter); LYMPHOCELE causing obstruction -> drainage/peritoneal window (fenestration)","Manage recurrent UTIs, BK/CMV infection considerations (with the transplant team), and graft VUR/obstruction","Address symptomatic native kidneys (nephrectomy for chronic infection, refractory hematuria, or space/symptoms in polycystic disease)"],"tech":["Extraperitoneal iliac-fossa transplant with vascular anastomoses and ureteroneocystostomy (a ureteral stent is commonly used to reduce leak/stricture); antireflux tunnel technique","Repair ureteral complications (reimplant, native ureteroureterostomy/pyelostomy); laparoscopic lymphocele fenestration","Pre-transplant outlet/bladder optimization"],"after":["Immunosuppression raises infection (UTI, BK virus) and malignancy risks - coordinate surveillance with the transplant team","Monitor graft function; a rising creatinine with hydronephrosis prompts evaluation for a urologic (obstructive) cause","Stent removal on schedule (avoid retained-stent complications)"],"pearls":["The transplant kidney is placed extraperitoneally in the iliac fossa with a ureteroneocystostomy - urologic complications are urine leak and ureteral stricture","Rising creatinine + graft hydronephrosis suggests a ureteral/obstructive complication (or a compressing lymphocele) - image and decompress","Optimize the lower urinary tract (outlet obstruction, bladder dysfunction) BEFORE transplant to protect the graft","Immunosuppression increases UTI/BK virus and malignancy risk - surveil with the transplant team; remove stents on time"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Renal Transplantation","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557459/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Kidney Transplant Complications","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564349/"}]},{"id":"uro-ckd-dialysis-gu","name":"CKD & Dialysis-Related GU Issues","sec":"uro","present":["Chronic kidney disease and dialysis create several urologic issues: ACQUIRED CYSTIC KIDNEY DISEASE (ACKD) in long-term dialysis patients raises the risk of RENAL CELL CARCINOMA (surveillance consideration)","Dialysis/ESRD patients also have higher rates of nephrolithiasis-related and infectious complications, and management of symptomatic native kidneys (chronic infection, refractory hematuria, stones)","Urologists may also address obstructive uropathy causing renal decline and evaluate lower-tract function before transplant"],"dx":["Screen/evaluate for ACKD-associated renal masses in long-term dialysis patients (ultrasound/CT/MRI); characterize any solid enhancing mass (RCC risk)","Work up hematuria in dialysis/ESRD patients (native-kidney malignancy, ACKD, cysts) - anuria does not exclude urologic pathology","Assess obstructive causes of renal decline (imaging) and lower-tract function pre-transplant"],"tx":["Resect symptomatic or suspicious native renal masses (nephrectomy for RCC arising in ACKD, chronic infection, or refractory hematuria)","Relieve obstructive uropathy contributing to renal decline (stent/nephrostomy then definitive treatment); manage stones in the ESRD/transplant setting","Optimize the urinary tract for transplant candidacy"],"tech":["Native nephrectomy (open/laparoscopic) for RCC in ACKD, chronic pyelonephritis, refractory hematuria, or symptomatic polycystic kidneys","Decompression (stent/nephrostomy) for obstruction; angioembolization for refractory hematuria in select cases","Lower-tract optimization procedures pre-transplant"],"after":["Ongoing surveillance for ACKD-related renal tumors in long-term dialysis patients; coordinate with nephrology","Monitor for infection and hematuria; manage transplant candidacy and native-kidney symptoms","Follow renal recovery after relieving obstruction"],"pearls":["Long-term dialysis causes acquired cystic kidney disease, which increases renal cell carcinoma risk - consider surveillance of native kidneys","Anuria in ESRD does not exclude urologic pathology - work up hematuria (native malignancy, ACKD)","Native nephrectomy is indicated for RCC in ACKD, chronic infection, refractory hematuria, or symptomatic polycystic kidneys","Relieve obstructive uropathy to preserve/recover renal function and optimize the tract before transplant"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Acquired Cystic Kidney Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557857/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Chronic Kidney Disease","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535404/"}]},{"id":"uro-urologic-infections","name":"Urologic Infections - Epididymo-Orchitis & Prostatitis","sec":"uro","present":["EPIDIDYMO-ORCHITIS is inflammation/infection of the epididymis (+/- testis) with gradual-onset scrotal pain/swelling; the cause is AGE-DEPENDENT - in sexually active young men, sexually transmitted organisms (chlamydia, gonorrhea); in older men/children, coliform urinary pathogens (E. coli)","PROSTATITIS syndromes (NIH classification): I acute bacterial, II chronic bacterial, III chronic pelvic pain syndrome (most common, inflammatory or non-inflammatory), IV asymptomatic inflammatory","The key acute mimic to exclude for scrotal pain is TESTICULAR TORSION (sudden, severe, absent cremasteric reflex) - do not miss it"],"dx":["Epididymo-orchitis: gradual onset, positive PREHN'S SIGN (pain relief with elevation), intact cremasteric reflex, and pyuria; urinalysis/culture and STI testing (NAAT) by age/risk; ultrasound if torsion cannot be excluded","Acute bacterial prostatitis: fever, perineal/pelvic pain, obstructive/irritative voiding, an exquisitely tender prostate (AVOID vigorous prostate massage - bacteremia risk); urinalysis/culture","Chronic prostatitis/CPPS: symptom assessment (NIH-CPSI), localization cultures for chronic bacterial prostatitis"],"tx":["Epididymo-orchitis: antibiotics targeted by likely organism - STI coverage (ceftriaxone + doxycycline) in young sexually active men, or a fluoroquinolone/coliform coverage in older/coliform-driven cases; supportive care (elevation, NSAIDs)","Acute bacterial prostatitis: systemic antibiotics with good prostate penetration (fluoroquinolone or TMP-SMX) for a prolonged course; drain a prostatic abscess and relieve retention (suprapubic if needed, avoiding transurethral catheter through an inflamed prostate)","Chronic bacterial prostatitis: prolonged antibiotics; CPPS: multimodal (alpha-blockers, anti-inflammatories, pelvic floor therapy, neuromodulators) - a chronic pain syndrome, not a simple infection"],"tech":["Drainage of a prostatic or scrotal abscess; suprapubic catheter for retention in acute prostatitis","Generally no surgery for uncomplicated epididymo-orchitis; explore if torsion cannot be excluded","Localization (pre/post-massage) cultures for chronic bacterial prostatitis"],"after":["Ensure clinical response; consider abscess or missed torsion if not improving","Treat sexual partners for STI-related epididymo-orchitis and counsel on prevention","CPPS requires long-term multimodal management and expectation-setting (relapsing course)"],"pearls":["Epididymo-orchitis cause is age-dependent: STIs (chlamydia/gonorrhea) in young sexually active men, coliforms (E. coli) in older men/children - and always exclude torsion","Prehn's sign (relief with elevation) and an intact cremasteric reflex favor epididymitis over torsion, but torsion is the can't-miss diagnosis","Acute bacterial prostatitis: avoid vigorous prostate massage (bacteremia); use prostate-penetrating antibiotics and drain any abscess","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (NIH category III) is the most common prostatitis - manage multimodally (it is not a simple infection)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Epididymitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430823/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Prostatitis","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519032/"}]},{"id":"uro-scrotal-masses","name":"Scrotal Masses - Hydrocele, Spermatocele & Others","sec":"uro","present":["Scrotal masses/swellings include HYDROCELE (fluid between the tunica vaginalis layers - transilluminates), SPERMATOCELE/epididymal cyst (cystic, above/posterior to the testis), VARICOCELE (dilated veins - covered separately), and solid TESTICULAR masses (cancer until proven otherwise)","The essential first branch point is INTRA-testicular (concerning for malignancy) versus EXTRA-testicular (usually benign) and cystic versus solid","Hydroceles are communicating (congenital, patent processus vaginalis) or non-communicating (adult, often idiopathic or reactive to epididymitis/tumor)"],"dx":["Examination (transillumination for hydrocele; ability to palpate above the mass suggests scrotal, not inguinal, origin) and SCROTAL ULTRASOUND to characterize solid vs cystic and intra- vs extra-testicular","Any SOLID intratesticular mass -> treat as testicular cancer (tumor markers, radical inguinal orchiectomy - never transscrotal biopsy)","A new hydrocele in an adult warrants ultrasound to exclude an underlying testicular tumor"],"tx":["Asymptomatic benign hydroceles/spermatoceles: observation; INTERVENE for discomfort, large size, or diagnostic uncertainty","Hydrocelectomy (excision/eversion of the sac) for symptomatic hydroceles; spermatocelectomy for symptomatic spermatoceles (counsel on fertility risk if the epididymis is disrupted)","Communicating (pediatric) hydroceles: many resolve by ~1-2 years; repair persistent ones via an inguinal approach (ligate the patent processus, like a hernia)"],"tech":["Hydrocelectomy (Jaboulay eversion or plication of the sac) via a scrotal approach for adults; inguinal approach for pediatric communicating hydroceles/associated hernia","Spermatocelectomy (excise the cyst preserving the epididymis when possible)","Radical INGUINAL orchiectomy for a solid intratesticular mass (never transscrotal)"],"after":["Hydrocelectomy complications: hematoma, infection, and recurrence; counsel accordingly","Spermatocelectomy can affect ipsilateral fertility (epididymal disruption) - counsel men concerned about fertility","Ensure any solid mass is managed as potential malignancy (do not aspirate/biopsy transscrotally)"],"pearls":["Any SOLID intratesticular mass is testicular cancer until proven otherwise - tumor markers + radical INGUINAL orchiectomy, never transscrotal biopsy","Hydrocele transilluminates and is extratesticular; a new adult hydrocele needs ultrasound to exclude an underlying tumor","Spermatocele/epididymal cyst is a benign cystic mass above/behind the testis; spermatocelectomy can impair ipsilateral fertility","Pediatric communicating hydroceles often resolve by ~1-2 years and are repaired via an inguinal approach (like a hernia)"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Hydrocele","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559125/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Spermatocele","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470415/"}]},{"id":"uro-urinary-diversion","name":"Urinary Diversion & Neobladder Management","sec":"uro","present":["Urinary diversion (usually after radical cystectomy) reroutes urine using a bowel segment; the main types are the INCONTINENT ILEAL CONDUIT (urine to a stoma/bag), the CONTINENT CUTANEOUS pouch (catheterizable stoma), and the ORTHOTOPIC NEOBLADDER (a bowel reservoir anastomosed to the urethra, voided per urethra)","Choice depends on patient factors: renal/hepatic function, urethral/oncologic status (neobladder needs a cancer-free urethra and adequate renal/hepatic function), dexterity, and preference","All bowel-based diversions carry METABOLIC and mechanical consequences that require lifelong management"],"dx":["Assess candidacy: renal function (adequate for a diversion using bowel - poor function worsens metabolic acidosis), hepatic function, urethral margin (neobladder), bowel disease, and manual dexterity/motivation (self-catheterization for continent forms)","Counsel on the trade-offs (body image/stoma vs continence and self-catheterization/night-time leakage with neobladder)","Baseline and ongoing metabolic and B12 status"],"tx":["Construct the chosen diversion with detubularized bowel (to lower pressure/improve capacity); ileal conduit is simplest/most reliable, neobladder offers voiding per urethra but requires training and may need intermittent catheterization","Manage the classic METABOLIC problem: ileal/colonic segments reabsorb urinary solutes causing HYPERCHLOREMIC METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (and, long-term, B12 deficiency from terminal ileum use)","Prevent/treat stones, infections, ureteroenteric strictures, and (continent forms) difficulty catheterizing"],"tech":["Ileal conduit (short ileal segment to a stoma); Studer/Hautmann orthotopic neobladder (detubularized ileal reservoir to the urethra); continent cutaneous pouch with a catheterizable channel","Ureteroenteric anastomoses with attention to blood supply (stricture prevention)","Detubularization to create a low-pressure, higher-capacity reservoir"],"after":["Lifelong monitoring: HYPERCHLOREMIC METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (may need oral alkali), VITAMIN B12 deficiency (years later, from terminal ileum resection), ureteroenteric strictures, stones, recurrent UTIs, and renal function","Neobladder: training for continence/emptying; some (especially women) need clean intermittent catheterization; nocturnal incontinence is common","Stoma care/education for conduits; regular oncologic and upper-tract surveillance"],"pearls":["Bowel-based diversions cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (solute reabsorption) and long-term B12 deficiency (terminal ileum) - monitor and supplement lifelong","Orthotopic neobladder needs a cancer-free urethra and adequate renal/hepatic function; some patients require intermittent catheterization and have nocturnal leakage","Ileal conduit is the simplest, most reliable diversion; continent options require dexterity and motivation for self-catheterization","Ureteroenteric stricture, stones, and recurrent infections are common long-term issues - surveil the upper tracts"],"refs":[{"t":"StatPearls: Urinary Diversion","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563239/"},{"t":"StatPearls: Ileal Conduit","u":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560877/"}]}];
